fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-538", author: "claude", content: "Deferred 'prove-pass gate' (the Tier-B / standalone proof-VERIFICATION half) filed as WI-558, a prerequisite of WI-539 Part 2. WI-538's delivered inline Tier-A by-derivation discharge stands unchanged; WI-558 is the follow-on that verifies recorded Pending ProofRecords (top-level + Tier-B) and updates them Pending->Discharged/Failed. Confirmed unbuilt: typing.rs:3599-3603 names the gate as follow-on; grep prove_pass/verify_proof = zero.", at: "2026-06-24T08:52:43Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-539", author: "claude", content: "Part 2 (proof <op>.<clause> contract proofs, e.g. push.ensures) is now FORMALLY blocked on WI-558 (the prove-pass gate), filed as a prerequisite per user 2026-06-24. Verified via Explore: push.ensures already PARSES (dotted proof target) and LOADS (ProofRecord Pending + placeholder witness) but is never verified; no body-symbolic-execution, no implicit-rule-from-contract. Part 2's own remaining scope on top of WI-558 = the <op>.<clause> target classifier + contract-clause->goal extraction + body-symbolic-execution to build the contract-proof Gamma. Part 1 (call-site contracts) stays delivered; ticket stays OPEN.", at: "2026-06-24T08:52:44Z")
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-559", name: "wi027-followup")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-027", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. Collection-bounded quantification in rule bodies: (forall ?x in xs: P(?x)) = finite conjunction (nil=vacuous true); (some ?x in xs: P(?x)) = finite disjunction (nil=fail). Distinct from the Harrop forall_impl (WI-105/108) which skolemises; this iterates a concrete list's elements, eliminating hand-written recursive list walks. LAYERS: (1) grammar.js bounded_quantification = '(' (forall|some) ?x 'in' <term> ':' rule_body ')', parenthesised so the comma-body doesn't bleed into the enclosing conjunction; wired into _non_name_atom_term. (2) convert.rs convert_bounded_quantification lowers to forall_in/some_in(binder, collection, tuple(body)); binder shares VarId with body uses (get_or_create_var); anonymous '?' binder REJECTED loudly (would bind nothing). (3) print.rs round-trips both occurrence + term printer paths. (4) resolve.rs: marker dispatch (step_init) -> bounded_quant_kind -> step_bounded_quant: forall = flatten per-element bodies into a conjunction + frame-replace (like step_forall_impl); some = ChoicePoint with one Continuation per element. Binder substituted STRUCTURALLY per element via subst_globals (NOT bound in sigma), so a free ?y in the body threads across elements through sigma; non-ground collection -> delay_goal -> floundered residual (WI-519, never silently decided). bounded_list_elements walks cons/nil/ListLiteral by SHORT NAME (resolve_sym, since a value list and the resolver carry distinct cons/nil Symbols sharing the name), handles nil-as-Ref AND nil-as-Fn, with a cycle guard (non-occurs-checked sigma can carry a cyclic spine -> bail to delay vs hang). (5) node_occurrence.rs: FIXED a pre-existing silent-data-loss bug the feature exposed — the 'ListLiteral' term->occurrence build used collect_list_visits (a cons/nil walker) which returned EMPTY for a ListLiteral (elements live in pos_args); now reads pos_args. Added a try_occurrence_to_term ListLit arm reifying to the ListLiteral term twin. KEY DEBUG FINDINGS (root causes, in order): elements lost = ListLiteral build via cons/nil walker; ?y not threaded = item() test helper used make_name_term (nullary Fn) vs facts' Ref -> use Term::Ref; variable collection None = cons matched by Symbol-equality vs a 2nd 'cons' symbol -> match by name; nil collection None = nil rides as Ref not Fn. TESTS: tests/include/bounded_quant_test.rs (11: parse/load, round-trip, forall all-hold/one-fail/nil-vacuous/var-bound/free-var-threaded, some one-holds/none/nil-fail, non-ground->undecided) + 3 tree-sitter corpus cases. SPEC: kernel-language.md §5.3 bounded-quantification subsection + binder-hygiene caveat (no per-quantifier scoping; binder must be a fresh name). CODE REVIEW (high, 3 finders): no new correctness bug in the feature; cyclic-list hang FIXED; anon-binder diagnostic ADDED; some-witness over-dedup REFUTED (goal_fingerprint reifies body through sigma so distinct enclosing bindings differentiate). FOLLOW-UP WI-559: SetLiteral/TupleLiteral have the IDENTICAL pre-existing build bug + try_occurrence_to_term lacks SetLit/TupleLit reify arms + ListLiteral [h|t] tail dropped at occurrence-build. anthill-core 1907/0; tree-sitter 172/0; full workspace green.", at: "2026-06-24T09:40:33Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-027", description: some(value: "forall quantifier in rule bodies: forall ?x in list: P(?x) eliminates recursive list walking. Extend grammar and resolver to support quantified goals in rule bodies, not just constraints."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-029"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-06-24T09:40:38Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-552", description: some(value: "Canonicalize binder/parameter references to var_ref(name); retire the WI-067 discharge normalize pass. SURFACED BY WI-067 (effect discharge): a reference to a LOCAL binder / operation parameter has TWO representations that do not unify — var_ref(name) (the canonical form, minted by binder_ref_value for Γ facts + occurrence_to_term / Expr::VarRef, settled by WI-550) vs bare Ref(sym) (minted by the effect-rekey substitute_ref_syms, the Modify[c] -> Modify[s] rename, which never got the var_ref memo). So a guard eq(b,0), after the rekey rewrites b to the caller binder, is eq(Ref(caller_b),0) while Γ holds neq(var_ref(caller_b),0) — they do not unify. WI-067 papers over this with TWO consumer-side patches: (1) normalize_param_refs_to_var_ref (rewrite the guard's Ref -> var_ref at discharge time) and (2) the resolver's value_has_open_world_ref recognizing var_ref-ONLY as the open-world binder (a bare Ref must stay decidable so a reflective builtin a proof chases — scope/is_entity_of — still resolves). Three sync points for one concept; binder_ref_value's own doc-contract ('if the canonical binder form ever moves, both producers move with it') is ALREADY VIOLATED by the rekey. ROOT CAUSE: bare Ref(sym) is OVERLOADED — it means both 'a global (sort/op/const/constructor — closed, decide)' and 'a local binder (open-world, flounder)'; var_ref is precisely the disambiguator and one producer just does not emit it. REFACTOR: make var_ref(name) the SINGLE canonical form for a local binder/param everywhere, Ref(sym) reserved for globals. Concretely substitute_ref_syms emits var_ref(arg) (not Ref(arg)) when it rewrites a param to a binder argument — and its targets ARE binder refs (it is fed by extract_var_ref_sym_node), so this is semantically exact, not a hack. Then guards carry var_ref natively, unify with Γ with NO normalize pass, and the resolver's open-world rule becomes a principled 'var_ref => open-world' instead of 'gated on the gamma overlay + relies on a pre-normalize having run'. CATCH (blast radius — why deferred not done): the Modify[.] resource readers — effect-region masking (WI-351/353 keep/flow), eval, cpp-gen, the effect-row printer/canonicalizer — currently read the resource as a bare Ref(s); if the rekey emits var_ref(s) for LABELS too (a blind whole-value rekey cannot tell guard-position from label-position) each must learn var_ref. Smaller alternative: var_ref-ify ONLY the guard sub-term at the producer (teach substitute_ref_syms guard-vs-label position-awareness) — net-even with the current normalize-at-consumer, so the real win is the full label+guard canonicalization. BENEFIT: retire normalize_param_refs_to_var_ref; collapse three sync points to one (KnowledgeBase::make_var_ref_term + binder_ref_value); the resolver's open-world floundering becomes principled instead of gamma-gated-plus-pre-normalized; restores binder_ref_value's one-canonical-form contract. NON-BLOCKING: the current WI-067 fix is sound and gamma-scoped so it cannot leak into non-discharge resolution — this is altitude/maintainability, not a correctness fix. TRIGGER: WI-539 (call-site requires/ensures over Γ) is the same-shaped SECOND consumer (its preconditions are the same guard shape) — doing this before/with WI-539 avoids adding a fourth patch. Acceptance: cargo-test green; normalize_param_refs_to_var_ref removed and the resolver open-world recognition principled (var_ref = open-world, no gamma-pre-normalize dependency), WI-067's discharge tests still green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-06-24T10:30:30Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-552", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED 2026-06-24 (commit 085b6c9 + merge with origin WI-027; full workspace suite green 2282). Approach A (chosen by user after a var_ref-vs-Ref design discussion): a binder/parameter occurrence IS a VARIABLE, so it is canonicalized to var_ref(name) at the PRODUCER, with bare Ref reserved for closed globals. The bare Ref(b) the effect-rekey emitted was the mis-representation that forced the WI-067 discharge-time normalize patch.\n\nWHAT (load.rs + typing.rs): (1) PRODUCER -- requires/ensures clauses (convert_clause_list) and guarded-effect guards (EffectGuarded arm) store their op-frame VARIABLE occurrences as var_ref at LOAD via wrap_places_as_var_ref. Variable set = this signature's params+result (exact symbols, signature_place_types) PLUS callback parameter/result places (unambiguous CallbackParam/CallbackResult kinds). Globals (sort/op/const/constructor) stay decidable bare Ref -- strictly MORE precise than the old normalize's \"any non-constructor ref\" net, which over-wrapped globals. (2) sigma -- substitute_ref_terms is now a proper VARIABLE substitution: replaces a whole var_ref(name:Ref(s)) node when s is in the sigma domain (never recurses into the wrapper -> no var_ref(name:0) corruption); a malformed name child trips a debug_assert (loud-over-silent). (3) Deleted normalize_param_refs_to_var_ref + normalize_param_refs_rec + their 3 discharge call sites. Guards/preconditions carry var_ref natively, unify with Gamma, flounder as the open-world parameter -- no consumer-side normalize.\n\nACCEPTANCE MET LITERALLY: normalize_param_refs_to_var_ref removed; resolver value_has_open_world_ref recognition is principled (var_ref => open-world, no pre-discharge normalize dependency -- doc restored); WI-067 discharge + WI-539 contract + WI-347 refinement tests green.\n\nLABELS STAY BARE Ref: the ticket's \"full label+guard canonicalization\" framing was a misread -- a Modify[c] resource label is a SortRef read by extract_effect_resource_sym, NOT a binder; var_ref-ifying it would break region masking. Only GUARD/clause goal refs are var_ref'd. The call-site effect rekey (substitute_ref_syms) preserves var_ref automatically by renaming the inner symbol of var_ref(name:Ref(c))->var_ref(name:Ref(s)); WI-347 substitute_clause compares var_ref clauses like-to-like.\n\n/code-review (5 finders, high effort): NO correctness bugs. Caught + fixed before delivery: (a) callback-place COMPLETENESS gap -- the old normalize wrapped callback places too; extended the wrap predicate to CallbackParam/CallbackResult kinds (latent, no current trigger, but closes the removed safety net); (b) loud None arm (debug_assert) on a malformed var_ref reaching sigma. Confirmed sound: WI-347 like-to-like, the sigma layer (reflect-term substitution, rightly distinct from the resolver logic-var machinery -- folding would violate the WI-348 boundary), term_references_any finds a surviving param inside its var_ref wrapper. Altitude confirmed right depth: op-BODY binders already become var_ref via occurrence_to_term's VarRef arm (WI-537), so wrapping only the two clause/guard contexts that flowed through bare convert_term is principled, not a bandaid.\n\nKEY CONCEPTUAL FRAMING (user-clarified): var_ref = a VARIABLE identified by name (the reflect Expr.var_ref constructor); bare Ref(sym) = a reference to a closed named entity (a global). The open-world/flounder behavior is a CONSEQUENCE of being a variable (NAF/closed-world reasoning is invalid over a runtime-unknown), NOT its definition.\n\nMerged cleanly with origin WI-027 (bounded quantification); both touched resolve.rs (auto-merged, different regions).", at: "2026-06-24T10:31:06Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-559", description: some(value: "SetLiteral / TupleLiteral occurrence-build drops all elements (pre-existing; surfaced by WI-027 code review). The term->occurrence build arms for 'SetLiteral' and 'TupleLiteral' in node_occurrence.rs visit_fn still use collect_list_visits(kb, Some(t)) -> list_to_vec, a cons/nil spine walker that returns EMPTY for these functors (their elements live in pos_args for SetLiteral, named_args for TupleLiteral — NOT a cons/nil spine). Result: Expr::SetLit([]) / Expr::TupleLit{empty} — every element silently dropped (loud-over-silent violation). WI-027 fixed the IDENTICAL bug for ListLiteral (read pos_args directly) but deliberately left the siblings to keep scope tight. ALSO: try_occurrence_to_term gained a ListLit reify arm (-> ListLiteral term twin) but SetLit/TupleLit still fall to '_ => None' -> occurrence_to_term debug_assert/Bottom, so a set/tuple literal reaching reify hits the assert (debug) or silent Bottom (release). Fix: (a) SetLiteral build reads pos_args (mirror ListLiteral); (b) TupleLiteral build reads named_args (its element carrier per convert.rs); (c) add SetLit + TupleLit arms to try_occurrence_to_term reifying to anthill.reflect.SetLiteral / TupleLiteral term twins (TupleLit via named_args). ALSO note (separate, lower pri): a ListLiteral with a 'tail:' named arg ([h|t] surface) is dropped at occurrence-build because Expr::ListLit has no tail slot — the resolver's bounded_list_elements DOES chase a ListLiteral tail, so the term round-trip disagrees with the SLD view (latent dedup-collision for [h|t] literals); fixing needs an Expr::ListLit representation change or a cons/nil desugar of head-tail literals. Acceptance: a {a,b} set literal and (a,b) tuple literal round-trip through occurrence_to_term without dropping elements; cargo-test green; add a focused round-trip test per literal kind."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-06-24T10:20:17Z"))
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-560", name: "wi027-followup")
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-561", name: "collection-patterns")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-561", description: some(value: "Collection deconstruction syntax (first-class, type-directed patterns). DESIGN (settled in WI-560 discussion): a collection PATTERN [], [h | t], [a, b], [a, b | t] should destructure ANY collection by desugaring (type-directed) to the abstract Iteration API -- the dual of construction. The API ALREADY EXISTS: anthill.prelude.Iteration.split(i) -> Option[(Element, Iterator)] effects Effect (prelude/iteration.anthill), provided by List purely (split(xs:List)->Option[(xs.T,List)]); construction dual = PersistentCollection.empty/insert (prelude/collection.anthill). Pattern semantics: [] == split(c)=none; [h|t] == split(c)=some((h,t)); [a,b] == split(c)=some((a,c1)),split(c1)=some((b,c2)),split(c2)=none. So NO new collection vocabulary is needed -- the gap is entirely in the PATTERN MACHINERY. KEY BOUNDARIES (load-bearing): (1) Two roles by POSITION, opposite operations: a collection literal in EXPRESSION/value position is CONSTRUCTION -> Collection.insert/empty (List: cons/nil); a collection literal in PATTERN position is DESTRUCTURING -> Iteration.split. split is a destructor and NEVER appears in list-literal/expression position. (2) Logical vs operational: rule-head destructuring is matched by UNIFICATION, which can only take apart concrete inductive structure (cons/nil) -> rule-head collection patterns are intrinsically List-only (desugar to cons; abstract carriers cannot be unified because unification cannot RUN split). Operational destructuring (match case / op body) CAN run split -> works for any Iteration/Stream carrier, but is EFFECTFUL (split's effect row: {} for List, s.E for Stream) -- so matching a Stream pattern is an effectful guard the typer must attribute. (3) For LIST, split is never needed at all: cons/nil serves both construction and (unification) destructuring. split is purely an abstract-collection, match-position, operational feature. (4) Set has NO Iteration -> head-tail over Set is a correct type error (Set only has membership/union). CURRENT STATE (why this is greenfield): patterns today are PURELY STRUCTURAL (Pattern enum = Var/Wildcard/Literal/Constructor/Tuple in node_occurrence.rs; eval/pattern.rs matches constructor symbol + recurses, NEVER calls an operation). [h|t] is NOT in the _pattern grammar (was expression-only and is now removed entirely, WI-560). match cases destructure lists via explicit case cons(h,t) -> (see stdlib/cli/parse.anthill, help.anthill). So there is NO view/extractor/active-pattern mechanism and NO syntactic entry point for split-based destructuring. STAGING: occurrence-build happens at LOAD (materialize_from_handle), BEFORE the typer (type_check_sorts, load.rs ~3041). The loader does a List-ONLY construction desugar (convert_term_with_expected/find_list_element_type/build_list). The typer types ListLit as List[T] but does NOT desugar. The principled home for the type-directed pattern desugar is the TYPER (only stage with the expected type for abstract carriers; already tree-producing for ListLit via reassemble_group). IMPLEMENTATION SKETCH (when prioritized): (a) add collection patterns to the _pattern grammar ([], [h|t], [a,b], [a,b|t]); (b) add Pattern::Collection{elements, tail} OR a general Pattern::View{extractor, args} (Scala unapply-style) to the Pattern enum + parse IR + loaders + pattern_to_term; (c) typer/pattern-compiler desugars type-directed: List -> cons/nil structural pattern (no split); abstract Iteration/Stream -> run split, match the Option[(E,Rest)] structurally (view pattern), attributing split's effect row; (d) rule-head collection patterns stay List/cons-only (unification limit); (e) law: split(insert(h,t))=some((h,t)), split(empty())=none as a provider obligation. SCOPE DECISION when picked up: MVP could be List-only structural ([h|t] -> cons pattern in match + rule head, no split, no effects); full version adds the view-pattern mechanism + effectful matching for abstract carriers. Likely warrants its own proposal (it is a language feature with the logical/operational + effect boundaries above). REFERENCES: docs/kernel-language.md 4.6 (revised by WI-560), docs/proposals/011-type-resolution.md OQ3.4 (Collection.add/empty + Iteration.split, effect-parameterized), prelude/iteration.anthill, prelude/collection.anthill, prelude/stream.anthill (splitFirst), prelude/list.anthill (split/splitFirst/cons/nil), prelude/set.anthill (no iteration). Acceptance (high-level, refine at pickup): [h|t]/[]/[a,b] usable as patterns in match (and rule heads for List); type-directed desugar via Iteration.split (abstract) / cons (List); effect attribution for effectful carriers; cargo-test green; tests per pattern shape + carrier."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: PreOpened)
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-560", author: "user", content: "RESOLVED BY REMOVAL, not by the originally-filed 'carry the tail faithfully' fix (direction (a)). Root finding from a design pass with the project owner: the [h|t] head-tail surface is PARSE-ONLY with zero end-to-end semantics and zero real usage (only parse_test assertions + grammar corpus + docs; stdlib destructures lists via explicit cons(?h,?t) / case cons(h,t)). Two load-bearing facts drove the removal: (1) at PARSE time we cannot know whether a collection literal is in rule-head (pattern) or value (construction) position, so the tail-bearing ListLiteral is a position-agnostic surface that the parser commits to before the role is known; (2) the meaning of [h|t] is opposite by role -- construction (insert/cons) in value position vs destructuring (Iteration.split) in pattern position -- and split is a destructor that NEVER belongs in list-literal/expression position. For LIST, cons/nil already serves both construction and unification-destructuring, so the sugar was pure redundancy. REMOVAL (this delivery): grammar.js collection_literal drops the '| tail' alternative; convert.rs drops has_tail plumbing (build arm now alloc_fn_term, like SetLiteral); load.rs folds build_list_with_tail into build_list (no tail param) and the ListLiteral desugar drops the tail read; resolve.rs bounded_list_elements ListLiteral arm drops the tail chase; reflect.anthill + kernel-language.md 4.6 revised (destructure via cons; head-tail removed); parse_test head-tail tests replaced with head_tail_literal_surface_removed (| is a plain infix op, so [?h|?t] now parses as ListLiteral(or(?h,?t)), NOT a head-tail); tree-sitter corpus head-tail cases removed. Proper first-class type-directed collection deconstruction patterns (the real feature: [h|t]/[]/[a,b] as PATTERNS desugaring to Iteration.split for abstract carriers / cons for List, effectful for Stream, with the logical-vs-operational boundary) filed separately as WI-561 (PreOpened, tag collection-patterns). Tree-sitter 170/0; anthill-core full suite green (1908).", at: "2026-06-24T12:20:24Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-560", description: some(value: "ListLiteral [h|t] tail dropped at occurrence-build (surfaced by WI-559). A head-tail list literal [a, b | t] parses to a ListLiteral term with heads in pos_args and the tail in a 'tail:' named arg (convert.rs CollectionLiteral build ~load 1469-1493 / parse). The term->occurrence builder (node_occurrence.rs visit_fn 'ListLiteral' arm ~3915-3927) reads pos_args only and DROPS the 'tail:' named arg, because Expr::ListLit (node_occurrence.rs:688) is a flat Vec with NO tail slot; reify (try_occurrence_to_term ListLit arm ~2330-2333, occ_build_fn with empty named_args) likewise emits no tail. CONSEQUENCE: the occurrence view of [a | t] is just [a], diverging from the SLD/resolver view -- bounded_list_elements (resolve.rs ~1100-1108) DOES chase the ListLiteral 'tail:' arg, so it sees a AND everything in t. Two concrete harms: (1) lossy round-trip -- [a | t] -> occurrence -> term yields ListLiteral(a), breaking the WI-425 occurrence<->term isomorphism for this shape; (2) latent dedup collision -- distinct source literals [a] and [a | t] both build the identical Expr::ListLit([a]) occurrence, so any occurrence cache/key (e.g. cached_term) silently aliases semantically different lists. Today this is LATENT not active: in a List-typed context the WI-007 loader desugar (convert_term_with_expected, load.rs:5881; List-only trigger find_list_element_type load.rs:5811; rewrite build_list_with_tail load.rs:4649 which threads the tail into the cons spine terminator CORRECTLY) turns [a|t] into a proper cons(a, t) spine -- which has a tail slot, so nothing is lost. The tail-bearing ListLiteral term only survives un-desugared where there is no List-typed context (e.g. a bounded-quant collection slot, the case WI-027 added). REJECTED FIX (per project owner): do NOT desugar [h|t] to cons/nil at occurrence-build time. Collection literals are TYPE-DIRECTED -- the same ListLiteral(h, tail: t) is meant to desugar to whatever collection the expected type names (List -> cons/nil today; conceptually Set/Stream via Collection.add/empty + Iteration.split per proposal 011 OQ3.4, parameterized by an effect set so effectful Stream literals work; see docs/kernel-language.md:430-432, docs/design/kernel-vocab-provenance.md:110-132). The occurrence builder has no expected-type context, so hardcoding cons/nil there would bake in List semantics and be wrong for non-List collections (and note: SetLiteral is NEVER loader-desugared today -- no Set branch in convert_term_with_expected -- it stays SetLiteral, exactly the WI-559 path). PREREQUISITE INVESTIGATION: understand how type-dependent collection-literal desugaring works end to end before changing the representation -- map convert_term_with_expected (load.rs:5881) + find_list_element_type (only List-shaped triggers) + build_list_with_tail; the typer TypeBuildFrame::ListLit/SetLit (typing.rs:4717/4753) which TYPES a literal as List[T]/Set[T] but does NOT spine-desugar; proposal 011 OQ3.4 + WI-007/WI-285/WI-393 history; and where (if anywhere) the broader Collection/Iteration desugar is meant to live vs the current List-only loader special-case. RECOMMENDED FIX DIRECTION (a): give Expr::ListLit an optional tail slot (e.g. tail: Option<Rc<NodeOccurrence>>) and thread it through build (visit_fn 'ListLiteral'), reify (try_occurrence_to_term), the printer (persistence/print.rs Expr::ListLit ~407), for_each_child + drain_expr_children (~265/983), and the typer ListLit frame -- keeping the literal carrier-neutral and faithful to the term, and leaving type-directed desugaring at its proper site (the loader / typer). Acceptance: a [a | t] head-tail list literal round-trips through occurrence_to_term WITHOUT dropping the tail (the round-trip term equals the original); [a] and [a | t] build DISTINCT occurrences (no dedup collision); cargo-test green; add a focused round-trip test."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-06-24T12:31:52Z"))
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-562", name: "prop025_1")
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-563", name: "prop025_1")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-562", author: "user", content: "FINAL APPROACH CORRECTION (supersedes the \"dispatch-layer fix is WRONG\" line in the description): the fix IS a dispatch-layer filter in candidate_sub_goals_owned, made sound by a keep-condition refinement I initially missed. Keep a carrier self-requires entry iff its spec is RELEVANT to the dispatched spec: (a) it equals the dispatched spec_sort — the conditional self-provision case (EqList provides Eq[List[A]] requires Eq[A]: a list is Eq iff its elements are, so Eq[A] IS the provision condition and must be checked), OR (b) it is in the dispatched spec's transitive requires_chain (a genuine dependency, e.g. Ordered requires Eq). An UNRELATED self-requires (List's Eq, for member, while dispatching IndexedSeq) is dropped. The naive filter (keep only requires_chain(spec_sort)) was wrong because requires_chain(Eq) is empty and dropped EqList's same-spec Eq; the \"== spec_sort\" clause fixes that. NO stdlib change needed (List keeps its sort-level requires Eq[T]). An earlier op-scoped-member detour (member[E] requires Eq[E] + a typer op-requires-coverage guard) was REVERTED because it broke WI-420 eta-dict capture (which keys on sort-requires) and was far more invasive. Final diff: ~25 lines in candidate_sub_goals_owned (add spec_sort param + relevant-set filter) plus tests. Tests: wi562_indexed_seq_nth_dispatch_test (nth on List[NonEq] dispatches; conditional Eq-provider still requires element Eq).", at: "2026-06-24T23:36:44Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-562", author: "user", content: "FINAL APPROACH (supersedes BOTH earlier notes — the dispatch-filter idea AND the op-type-param-member idea). Two dead ends ruled it out: (1) a dispatch-layer filter that drops a carrier's \"unrelated\" self-requires is IMPOSSIBLE — a carrier's genuine provision requirement (CarrierB requires DiamondA, providing DiamondB) is structurally identical to an incidental one (List requires Eq, for member), and wi224 diamond-coherence pins that a carrier's sort-requires ARE threaded at every dispatch; (2) moving member's element to an OP-TYPE-PARAM (member[E] requires Eq[E]) breaks eta-expansion (a bare `member` value typed as Bool, not the arrow) and the WI-420 eta-dict path.\n\nWHAT SHIPPED: keep member on the SORT param T but move its Eq need from the LIST SORT to an OP-SCOPED requires (WI-448): `operation member(x: T, l: List) -> Bool requires Eq[T]`. List drops its sort-level `requires Eq[T]`. Typer (kb/typing.rs): TypingEnv gains an `op_requires` snapshot (set per op-body in check_operation_bodies from op_requires_entries); check_apply, BEFORE provider dispatch, licenses a spec-op call whose spec the enclosing op's own op-requires covers (leaving it as the spec op for value-directed eval). NO change to candidate_sub_goals_owned (diamond coherence intact). NO change to attach_eta_dispatch_dict needed — member still eta-expands as a sort-param op, and its `eq` value-directs at eval (member is only ever evaluated on concrete lists), so no requirement dict is threaded. Diff: ~30 lines in typing.rs + the 1-line stdlib member requires + tests. Tests: wi562 (nth on List[NonEq] dispatches; op-scoped requires licenses abstract eq in a body; no-requires still rejected). Full anthill-core suite green; member eval (wi431, eval_test) + eta (wi420) + diamond (wi224) all pass.", at: "2026-06-25T04:15:39Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-562", author: "user", content: "PLACEMENT REFINEMENT (post code-review): the op-requires coverage guard lives in the FAILED dispatch arms (DispatchOutcome::NoMatch and the abstract NoCandidates arm), NOT pre-dispatch. Helper op_requires_covers(kb, env.op_requires(), spec_sort). Rationale (code-review finding): a pre-dispatch guard also short-circuited CONCRETE calls of an op-required spec, bypassing the WI-365 impl effect-grounding (an effectful override would surface as ?_ instead of the named effect). Placing it in the failure arms means only a genuinely-abstract call (member's eq on the abstract element, which fails dispatch — NoCandidates in plain scope, NoMatch when a parameterized provider like fact Eq[List[A]] false-matches) is licensed; a concrete call resolves Unique and keeps effect-grounding + precise return. wi365 + full anthill-core suite green.", at: "2026-06-25T04:48:13Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-562", description: some(value: "Fix IndexedSeq.nth dispatch regression: spec-op dispatch via a carrier wrongly imposed the carrier's UNRELATED self-requires. `List requires Eq[T]` (added 2026-05-29 by WI-325 so the abstract `member` body's `eq(head,x)` type-checks) interacted with the WI-224 dispatch sub-goal check (candidate_sub_goals_owned recurses the impl sort's full requires chain), so dispatching ANY spec op through List demanded Eq on the element. `nth(pts, i)` on a `List[Waypoint]` (Waypoint not Eq) in examples/webots-modelling/lf1/leader.anthill failed with 'IndexedSeq.nth.dispatch: no impl matches', aborting the whole lf1 load and breaking discharge.sh. A dispatch-layer fix (filter the carrier's self-requires to those the dispatched spec itself requires) is WRONG: it can't distinguish an incidental self-requires (List's Eq, for member) from a conditional-provider's requires (EqList: `requires Eq[A]` guarding `fact Eq[List[A]]`) — structurally identical. Correct fix: (1) stdlib — move Eq from the List sort to the `member` op as an op-scoped requires over an op-type-param (`member[E](x: E, l: List[T=E]) -> Bool requires Eq[E]`, WI-448), so List[NonEq] is well-formed for element-agnostic ops (nth/length/append/IndexedSeq/Stream/Iterable/PersistentCollection) and member's Eq is checked at its call site via E inference; (2) typer — kb/typing.rs NoCandidates arm now lets an operation's OWN op-scoped requires cover its body's abstract spec-op calls (op-scoped dual of the sort-level defer-to-requirement). Tests: wi562_indexed_seq_nth_dispatch_test (nth on List[NonEq] dispatches; sort-param no-requires still rejected). NOT CAUGHT because no cargo test type-checks the lf1 example end-to-end (only discharge.sh, a shell script not in cargo test) and lf1_real_spec_test.rs discards load errors (let _ = load_all) — it only emits SMT for specific safety rules, never type-checking leader.anthill's operation bodies."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-06-25T05:05:13Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-103", description: some(value: "Proposal 025.1 Phase 8 — Cleanup, docs, migration. Deliverables: delete lf1_transponder_excursion_ranking_function_manual test (Phase 5 obsoletes it); audit anthill-smt-gen/tests/ to use new helpers (run_z3, common::z3_available); add cache_hit assertions where applicable; stdlib README for the new tactic catalog with worked examples; update discharge.sh documentation to mention --show-cache and the warm-cache CI pattern; tighten the proposal doc with anything implementation revealed. Acceptance: cargo test all green; docs link the worked examples; lf1_real_spec_test.rs has 3 tests (manual ranking gone). See §Implementation plan Phase 8."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-100"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-06-25T05:43:03Z"))
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-564", name: "p048")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-558", description: some(value: "Proof-verification pass (the \"prove-pass gate\" deferred by WI-538): actually CHECK recorded proofs instead of recording them Pending-and-unverified. WI-538 delivered in-body 'by derivation' discharge INLINE in the typer (prove_from_gamma over Gamma + KB, typing.rs ~3593) and explicitly deferred the rest -- the comment at typing.rs:3599-3603 says an external proof's conclusion may be assumed only once the \"Gamma-snapshot + prove-pass gate\" verifies it (follow-on); until then it stays conservatively undischarged, never a silent drop. Today the loader (load.rs ~11293-11505) records EVERY proof (by derivation, by z3, structured/031, hints) as a ProofRecord fact with result Pending and a placeholder witness TrustedAxiom(\"pending -- not yet discharged\"), and NOTHING ever verifies it (grep prove_pass/verify_proof = zero). Two consequences: (1) TOP-LEVEL proofs (proof length by derivation, proof push_pop by derivation) are never checked at all -- WI-538's inline Tier-A fires only for IN-BODY proof statements, not top-level rule companions; (2) Tier-B strategies (by z3 / using / external) are no-ops (typer returns discharged=false). SCOPE: a post-typing/load pass that walks Pending ProofRecord facts and verifies each, updating result Pending -> Discharged | Failed with a REAL witness (SLD trace for derivation, external result for Tier-B) -- never silently Discharged on failure (loud + conservative). by-derivation: discharge via the SLD resolver over the proof's context (scope/inherited/definition rules, plus the Gamma-snapshot where the proof is in-body -- WI-538 names \"Gamma-snapshot + prove-pass gate\" together, so carrying the local Gamma at the proof point into the pass is part of this). Tier-B: dispatch to the external tool/driver (anthill-cli already has top-level proof-dispatch scaffolding to reuse). A Discharged conclusion may then be assumed into Gamma (the deferred half of WI-538's in-body modification rule) and discharge its obligation. RELATIONSHIP: foundation for WI-539 Part 2 (proof <op>.<clause> contract proofs, e.g. push.ensures) -- Part 2 adds the <op>.<clause> target classifier + contract-clause-to-goal extraction + body-symbolic-execution to BUILD the contract-proof Gamma, then hands goal+Gamma to THIS pass for discharge; it also completes WI-538's deferred Tier-B half. DISTINCT FROM proposal 050's self-hosted endgame gate (tabled/bottom-up holds(fact,point) evaluator), which is deferred-by-design and NOT needed here -- this is a Rust-side verification driver over the existing SLD resolver + external dispatch. Depends on WI-538 (in-body proof + Tier-A, delivered) and WI-537 (Gamma substrate, delivered)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-538"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-06-25T06:15:10Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-539", author: "user", content: "Part 2 (<op>.<clause> body-vs-contract proof, e.g. push.ensures) is now UNBLOCKED: WI-558 (proof-verification pass) DELIVERED 2026-06-24 — anthill-core kb/proof_verify.rs exposes discharge_by_derivation + set_proof_result + verify_proofs, the discharge entry point Part 2 hands its constructed goal+Gamma to. Part 2 still owes: the <op>.<clause> target classifier + contract-clause-to-goal extraction + body-symbolic-execution to BUILD the contract-proof Gamma. NOTE: the auto-chaining-into-check half (OQ-A/B) was deferred from WI-558 and filed as WI-564 (not a blocker for Part 2).", at: "2026-06-25T06:15:12Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-563", author: "user", content: "Diagnosis corrected during implementation (user-confirmed). The 4 proofs did NOT fail because position_distance is uninterpreted — it HAS a defining rule body (safety_common.anthill:264, added after the predicate-status table was written). They failed because position_distance was not IMPORTED in safety_gps/safety_transponder, so the functor never resolved to the qualified ...common symbol and try_inline_rule_call returned false → loud 'unhandled body goal functor' error (the short name in the error was the tell). These pointwise refutation proofs REQUIRE inlining position_distance to the concrete initial geometry (?d=4.0) for lt(?d,d_min=1.0) to be unsat; the WI's prescribed declare-fun/uninterpreted-relation path would leave ?d free → spurious SAT → still failing, AND would be a bug-masking silent fallback contrary to the repo 'loud error over silent skip' principle. Fix delivered: (1) import position_distance in safety_gps + safety_transponder; (2) corrected the stale predicate-status table (line 502: 'UNINTERPRETED/No rule body' → DEFINED+UNFOLDED-by-pointwise-proofs) and the inductive-proof note at safety_gps:276 that misled the WI author; (3) new smt-gen test cross_namespace_inline_test.rs guarding cross-namespace inline-to-unsat AND that an unresolved functor stays a loud error (no silent uninterpreted fallback) — this replaces the WI's 'uninterpreted-relation body goal' test, which would have encoded the wrong behavior. discharge.sh now 14 proved / 0 failed; full anthill-smt-gen suite green.", at: "2026-06-25T06:23:28Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-563", description: some(value: "anthill-smt-gen v0: handle uninterpreted-function body goals. After WI-562 unblocked the lf1 load, discharge.sh reports 4 failing proofs: safety_max_distance, safety_min_distance, safety_no_collision_transponder, safety_bounded_excursion_transponder — all 'v0: unhandled body goal functor position_distance' (anthill-smt-gen/src/lib.rs:616). position_distance is documented (safety_gps.anthill:276) as an UNINTERPRETED free function symbol; the smt-gen body-goal translator has no path for a functor with no ground facts and no defining body — it should emit a Z3 (declare-fun position_distance ...) and assert the goal as an uninterpreted relation. These 4 proofs were ADDED AFTER WI-100 (Apr 28, when discharge.sh reported 7 proved) and were NEVER validated because discharge.sh had been broken by the WI-562 regression since 2026-05-29 — so they are pre-existing-unvalidated, not a WI-562 regression. Acceptance: discharge.sh reports all proofs proved (currently 10 proved, 4 failed); add an smt-gen test for an uninterpreted-relation body goal. See also the spec's documented predicate-status table in safety_common.anthill."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-06-25T06:23:33Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-539", author: "claude", content: "PART 2 DELIVERED 2026-06-25 (commit d35a89e; suite 2299). proof <op>.ensures by derivation now proves an op BODY satisfies its PREDICATE postcondition. USER MODEL (clarified): requires/ensures each split into a TYPE kind (->typing, a 'return member') and a PREDICATE kind (->proof); Part 2 owns the predicate ensures. Part 1 ASSUMES ensures at the call (use side, abstract+concrete); Part 2 PROVES it (concrete side only). LOAD: contract_proof_target_qn interns <op-qn>.<clause> for ProofRecord.rule (no more UnresolvedName). DISCHARGE (kb/proof_verify.rs discharge_contract_proof): skolemize params to fresh ground consts, bind result<-body[params:=skolems] (symbolic exec), seed Gamma from predicate requires, prove each predicate-ensures conjunct via prove_from_gamma (eigenvariable reading). Reuses WI-558 set_proof_result/make_sld_witness. ABSTRACT op (no body) ensures = spec axiom callers USE -> Deferred, never silently Discharged. /code-review (4 angles) caught a CRITICAL false-discharge: is_value_precondition_clause accepts Value::Entity but contract_clause_conjuncts handled only Term/Node -> empty conjuncts -> silent Discharge; fixed via value_to_term total boundary + ensures-loop fails CLOSED on zero conjuncts. DEFERRED FOLLOW-ONS (focused inline-body slice, user-chosen): (a) the proposal-025 separate-rule-in-provides body form; (b) helper ops whose bodies are Expr::DotApply field access (resolver reduce_dot_value folds only the field_access builtin form a contract clause lowers to, NOT the DotApply an op body lowers to) -> so eq(top(result),x) with a separate top op does NOT yet discharge, but eq(result.value,x) does; (c) requires-contract proofs (a precondition is assumed, not proved). WI-558 was the unblocker.", at: "2026-06-25T09:40:27Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-539", description: some(value: "Proposal 050 \"operation call\" modification rule + 025 §\"Proof for operation contracts\" — call-site operation contracts (requires/ensures) over Γ. A SIBLING consumer of WI-537 (the 050 substrate); NOT on WI-067's critical path (effect discharge rides branch conditions and Γ, not contracts) and therefore does NOT block WI-067 — filed because it is part of proposal 050 and the main thing that richly POPULATES Γ. At a call y = callee(args), build σ (callee params ↦ actual args, result ↦ y), then: (1) requires IS CHECKED — the callee's precondition σ(requires) must follow from Γ_in, discharged by the SAME resolver query (and, failing the trivial flow, the same in-body-proof fallback) used for a guarded effect; an undischarged precondition is an obligation/error, never a silent pass. (2) ensures IS ASSUMED — Γ_out = Γ_in ∪ { σ(ensures) }: the callee's postcondition (result ↦ y) becomes a known fact for the code after the call. This is how contract knowledge flows: an op that \"ensures neq(result,0)\" lets a later div(_, y) refute its eq(y,0) guard straight from Γ with no branch test written; ensures facts are in practice the main populator of Γ beyond branch conditions and bindings. Plus 025 §\"Proof for operation contracts\": a proof targeting the implicit contract rule via the <operation>.<clause> naming convention (e.g. push.ensures). DISTINCT FROM WI-347 (DELIVERED), which checks OVERRIDE refinement (a providing sort's op vs the spec op — precondition no stronger, postcondition no weaker); THIS is CALL-SITE checking, which is unimplemented — op requires_clause/ensures_clause exist in the IR (OperationInfo.requires/ensures) but are unused across stdlib/examples and never checked at a call. Depends on WI-537 (needs Γ + the resolver bridge). Acceptance: cargo-test green; a test shows a callee's requires checked against Γ at a call site (error when undischarged, pass when Γ entails it) and its ensures available as a Γ fact after the call."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-537", "WI-558"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-06-25T09:40:28Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-557", description: some(value: "Scope the WI-539 call-site value-precondition requires-check to op-body context (it currently leaks into rule-body dot-dispatch). The WI-539 requires-check lives in check_apply_iter, which is ALSO reached by the rule-body dot-dispatch pass (dispatch_rule_body_dots -> dispatch_dots_in_occ -> type_check_node -> check_apply_iter) with a STRUCTURALLY EMPTY Gamma. A value precondition over a rule-body variable then floats (definite_only) -> spurious TypeError::UnsatisfiedPrecondition, which is currently SWALLOWED by the pre-existing Err(_) wildcard in dispatch_dots_in_occ (typing.rs). Doubly latent today (no value-precondition requires exist in stdlib; the error is swallowed) but conceptually wrong: a rule body is SLD/relational, not imperative-with-Gamma, so the op-body Hoare requires-check does not apply there. Two candidate approaches (from user): (a) handle the value-precondition discharge in req_insertion::run -- the post-typecheck requires-threading pass that already walks OP BODIES (kb.op_bodies) and add/removes requires, so it is naturally scoped to op-body context and never sees rule bodies; CAVEAT: Gamma is built during the typing pass (check_apply_iter / the iterative typer), so a req_insertion-time check needs Gamma available or rebuilt (the inline check has it for free) -- weigh persisting/recomputing Gamma vs the inline approach. (b) simpler: in the rule-body dot-dispatch context just MARK the precondition succeeded (gate the check off) -- needs an op-body-vs-rule-body discriminator threaded to check_apply_iter (e.g. a flag on TypingEnv set by dispatch_rule_body_dots' empty env, or gate on an enclosing-op marker). Deferred from WI-539 (Part 1) because a careless inline gate risks a SILENT OVER-SKIP that the contract-less test suite cannot catch (no value-precondition requires to regress on). Acceptance: a test where a value-precondition op is invoked in a RULE body raises NO spurious UnsatisfiedPrecondition, while op-body requires-checking (the WI-539 tests) is unchanged."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-539"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-06-25T10:33:30Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-564", description: some(value: "Chain the WI-558 proof-verification pass into `anthill check` (the prove-pass gate's auto-trigger, local-proof.md OQ-A/B — deferred by WI-558 whose USER-chosen scope was L1 core + prove write-back only). GOAL: `anthill check` runs load -> type -> discharge-pending in one invocation so a green check MEANS verified, with no separate `anthill prove` step. WHERE: orchestrated in anthill-cli (z3 lives in anthill-smt-gen, downstream of anthill-core), reusing run_prove's dispatch over both tiers (by-derivation via core discharge_by_derivation; z3 via smt-gen) + the delivered set_proof_result in-KB write-back. OQ-B: when a relied-upon proof is pending/missing/refuted or z3 is unavailable/times out, check COMPLETES but emits a loud warning ('relied on N unverified proof(s); not fully verified') -- does NOT silently trust and does NOT hard-block by default; a strict --require-proofs flag escalates to an error for airtight CI. Reuses the proof cache (state_hash/sidecar/WI-177 epoch). DISTINCT FROM proposal 050's self-hosted endgame gate (deferred-by-design). Builds on WI-558 (verify_proofs + set_proof_result + cli prove write-back, DELIVERED)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-558"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-06-25T11:00:36Z"))
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-566", name: "p048")
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-567", name: "p048")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-566", author: "claude", content: "Design refinement (2026-06-25): the criterion for a Phase-4 subject is that the failure condition be a VALUE-DECIDABLE guard — literal-foldable / verbatim flow-fact over the ARGUMENTS, like div's eq(b,0) — NOT an opaque or effectful call to another operation. A guard phrased as another op (isEmpty(s)) discharges only when that op reduces to the evaluable fragment on a concrete constructor (isEmpty(cons)->false) or matches verbatim in Gamma; it gets NO cross-operation reasoning (gt(length,0) does NOT refute isEmpty). So head is NOT a clean example: it returns Option (total) today, and a guarded isEmpty on an abstract/lazy receiver never discharges. The concrete head/headOption split (guarded head on CONCRETE List only) is filed separately as WI-567.", at: "2026-06-25T12:09:36Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-088", author: "user", content: "REOPENED: the reader is built on an ad-hoc mechanism and must use the standard one. MarshalTable::from_kb scans free-floating top-level TypeMapping facts via kb.rules_by_functor(TypeMapping), keyed only by anthill_type in a flat map with NO profile and NO binding scope — a parallel reading path running alongside the (still-unbuilt) standard LanguageMapping reader. The data model is right (TypeMapping with optional lift/lower); the READER is wrong. Correction: there is ONE type-mapping reader (the LanguageMapping reader WI-089 establishes); renames and marshalled entries flow through it identically; the rename-vs-marshalled distinction is adapter-presence WITHIN a single map — exactly WI-088 own thesis (a coercion is just a TypeMapping entry whose value-level conversion is not identity / collapse into ONE concept), which the implementation then re-split at the reading layer. The only force that pushed marshalling to a free-floating fact was SCOPE (a global Vec3->const double* would lower the struct/pure-ops/fields). The correct answer to scope is a SCOPED language mapping, not a loose fact: the profile LanguageMapping (cpp_std) is the global base; the foreign binding (webots realization) owns an OVERLAY carrying the adapter-bearing entries. The standard reader composes base+overlay; boundary-only application stays at the dispatch site (synthesise_body_for); declared-signature lowering (sort_to_cpp) reads the same map but ignores adapters. Retire MarshalTable into the WI-089 standard reader and migrate the entries from free-floating facts into the binding scoped LanguageMapping. Bonus: the current global rules_by_functor scan has a latent collision (two bindings mapping the same type differently, last-wins) that a scope-aware reader fixes.", at: "2026-06-25T12:44:10Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-088", author: "user", content: "APPROACH UPDATE (supersedes the scope-aware-reader wording in the prior feedback): WI-089 makes type-mapping an ORDINARY KB QUERY over KEYED entries (keys, NOT tags) and retires MarshalTable with NO replacement reader. So the WI-088 migration is: give the webots Vec3/EulerAngles TypeMapping entries a key (webots, reused from NamespaceMapping.host_module) so the standard lower_type query picks them up at carrier boundaries; delete MarshalTable. No bespoke marshalling reader survives.", at: "2026-06-25T12:44:20Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-089", author: "user", content: "APPROACH (agreed in design discussion): make type-mapping lookup an ORDINARY KB QUERY, not a Rust reader. Retire hardcoded prim_lower/param_lower and do NOT add a TypeMapTable; the WI-088 MarshalTable is retired too (NO replacement reader). (1) KEYED ENTRIES (keys, NOT tags): every type mapping carries a single key that selects it. Renames keyed by profile (cpp17-stl/cpp20-stl); marshalling keyed by binding (webots). The key generalizes what profile already does (it IS the selector). Marshalling and renames are the SAME shape (TypeMapping with optional lift/lower), differing only by key and adapter-presence -- WI-088 one-concept thesis, finally uniform. (2) STANDARD QUERY: a relation lower_type(?keys, ?anthill_type, ?host, ?lift, ?lower) resolved by SLD against the keyed entries; cpp-gen calls kb.resolve(lower_type(active_keys, T, ...)) and reads host + adapters. The discrimination tree IS the table; the rule IS the overlay logic. This DELETES reader code instead of adding a third reader. (3) ACTIVE KEYS come from cpp-gen context, ordered most-specific-first. Always includes the compilation profile (Generated.profile). At a carrier-dispatch boundary, PREPEND the binding key, reusing NamespaceMapping.host_module (carrier sort namespace anthill.examples.lf1.webots -> host_module webots). So: declared-signature position (sort_to_cpp) uses active_keys=[profile] -> Vec3 misses -> generated struct (adapters invisible); carrier-boundary position (synthesise_body_for) uses active_keys=[binding, profile] -> Vec3 hits webots -> const double* + lift/lower. Boundary-only marshalling FALLS OUT of which keys are active where -- no special overlay rule. Carrier (global sort replacement via Implementation.carrier) stays an earlier, separate precedence step than the type-mapping query (it is a global replacement, not a boundary-only coercion). (4) PRIORITY is the only non-vanilla bit: binding-shadows-base = first-match over ordered keys. Encode either caller-side (cpp-gen tries keys in order, each query vanilla) or in-language (lower_type picks first key via member + NAF). Caller-side is simplest. OPEN FORK = entry representation: NESTED (keep renames in LanguageMapping.type_map + a key, project to a queryable relation via member(?type_map, TypeMapping(...)); VERIFY the resolver binds entity fields inside a list member) vs FLAT (top-level fact TypeMapping(key, anthill_type, host_type, lift, lower); more query-friendly, direct discrim hit, no list walk, but LanguageMapping no longer groups its type_map). Decide on whether member-over-list-entity-fields resolves cleanly. Also move INCLUDE_PROBES (host-type -> header) into the same keyed/queryable form, else fact-driven is half-done. WI-088 (depends on this) migrates the webots Vec3/EulerAngles entries to carry key webots and deletes MarshalTable.", at: "2026-06-25T12:45:11Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-089", author: "user", content: "ADDENDUM (predicate naming): the query predicates must be FULLY QUALIFIED. lower_type (and the projection relation, if the nested representation is used) live in anthill.realization -- the base realization namespace, sibling to LanguageMapping/TypeMapping/NamespaceMapping -- so every language mapping can use them. cpp-gen builds the query term with the full qualified name: kb.resolve(anthill.realization.lower_type(...)), matching how the existing reads resolve Implementation/TypeMapping via try_resolve_symbol(anthill.realization.X). Rationale: a bare short name is NOT scanned as a definition and would be interned as an unresolved symbol rather than resolving to the intended rule -- a silent mismatch. Full qualification keeps resolution unambiguous and consistent with the load.rs convention (all resolve_symbol calls use full paths).", at: "2026-06-25T12:45:15Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-089", author: "user", content: "CROSS-REF: cut (!) is now ticketed as WI-568 (kernel control primitive, proposal 033 §Future work / 033.1). It is the eventual clean form for the calculate_type_mapping priority -- TypeMapping(...)!, default instead of the NAF existence-wrapper. WI-089 does NOT depend on WI-568: ship on NAF (or caller-side first-match) now; adopt ! once WI-568 lands.", at: "2026-06-25T12:45:23Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-567", author: "claude", content: "STEP 3 (guard discharge) BLOCKED on the WI-502/WI-292 cluster; type-level split (steps 1-2) DELIVERED + committed (b58aeb8): Stream.head -> headOption (Option, total) + new head(s) -> s.T effects { s.E, Error[EmptyStream] :- isEmpty(s) } + EmptyStream payload; 3 Option-asserting caller tests migrated to headOption. EMPIRICAL FINDING (throwaway probes, removed): NO form of the emptiness guard discharges today. head(cons(1,nil)) in a pure op (literal tier), if isEmpty(l) then.. else head(l) (flow tier), AND the eq(l,nil) variant in BOTH ground-cons and flow forms all leave Error stuck; only native-scalar guards discharge (eq(b,0) over Int64 = the div baseline, clean). ROOT CAUSE: a guard discharges iff its predicate bottoms out in NATIVE scalar builtins. isEmpty and Eq[List].eq are anthill-RULE-defined (structural / via splitFirst), so refuting them needs the rules FIRED on the prove path, which the type-erased resolver cannot do = WI-502 (typed terms -> type-directed equational/[simp] firing). So eq(l,nil) is the SAME wall as isEmpty, NOT a shortcut: there is NO concrete-enum path below WI-502. Resume the discharge half once WI-502 lands; head currently carries Error[EmptyStream] unconditionally in practice (conservative, sound). WI-567 unclaimed -> Open, now depends on WI-502.", at: "2026-06-25T14:36:42Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-566", author: "claude", content: "Criterion sharpened (via WI-567 probes): 'value-decidable guard' precisely means the predicate bottoms out in a NATIVE SCALAR BUILTIN (Int eq/neq/lt...). A guard over any anthill-RULE-defined predicate (isEmpty, Eq[List].eq, structural ops) does NOT discharge today -- empirically confirmed, neither literal nor flow-fact nor eq(l,nil) refutes it -- because the type-erased resolver cannot fire the defining rules on the prove path (= WI-502/WI-292). So a Phase-4 op is dischargeable NOW only if its failure condition is a native scalar predicate over arguments; emptiness/bounds guards over rule-defined predicates are gated on WI-502.", at: "2026-06-25T14:36:44Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-089", author: "user", content: "PROGRESS (increment landed, commit b2137d95; WI stays Claimed — full WI not yet delivered): the CORE type-map query is done, reviewed (/code-review, one finding fixed), green (cpp-gen 117/0, anthill-core clean). Delivered: (1) TypeMapping + lang/key Option fields; (2) cpp_std.anthill 10 flat keyed base renames (key=none = language base, all 6 fields explicit so arity-6 by construction); (3) cpp_base_host_type resolves host types via kb.query_view over an allocation-free Value::Entity pattern — ORDINARY KB QUERY, no reader / no TypeMapTable, works against cpp-gens immutable &KnowledgeBase (cannot kb.resolve / hash-cons); (4) prim_lower/param_lower deleted, 4 call sites rewired. DEFERRED follow-on increments: (a) profile threading + active-key priority + binding OVERLAYS — the WI-088 enabler (webots Vec3 -> key: some(webots)); (b) NamingConvention fact (camelCase) + effect_map facts (still hardcoded); (c) move INCLUDE_PROBES (host-type -> header) into the keyed/queryable form. Code-review notes judged acceptable: is_body_emittable leaf-widening (bare container unreachable), host_type continue (schema-required field), placeholder VarId scheme (query-side wildcards, bindings discarded).", at: "2026-06-25T13:40:57Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-568", description: some(value: "Add cut (!) as a kernel control primitive in the SLD resolver. Implements proposal 033 §Future work (Cut and the barrier mechanism); promote that section to a child proposal 033.1. Cut commits to the current rule invocation: given a(?x) :- b(?x), !, c(?x), when ! fires it discards (i) other clauses of a still queued at the parent ChoicePoint and (ii) every ChoicePoint created during resolution of b(?x) (push_choice Continuations and nested rule unfoldings); choice points created AFTER ! (during c) are untouched. DESIGN (proposal 033 lines 207-222): (1) per-invocation BarrierId allocated in with_fresh_vars alongside the fresh Globals -- BarrierId is to cut what VarId is to a query variable: a fresh, monotonic, per-invocation tag. (2) ChoicePoint gains barrier_at_creation: BarrierId, tagged when each choice point is pushed (push_choice, multi-alt rule unfold, occurrence backtracking). (3) cut(?barrier) builtin: the ! body syntax opens to cut(B) where B is the enclosing rule barrier (parallels DeBruijn-var opening); semantics = walk the candidate / choice-point stack and remove every alternative (Rule, Occurrence, Continuation, Assumption alike) whose barrier_at_creation is B or descended from B. (4) Disjunction-cut interaction falls out for free = Prolog transparent-to-outer-cut, opaque-to-inner-cut: a cut in a body that contains or(...) prunes the push_choice Continuations of that or (tagged with the outer barrier transitively); a cut inside the body of or itself (or any inner rule) prunes only within its own barrier. SCOPE CORRECTION (load-bearing): proposal 033 Requirement 3 claims cut is expressible as rewrites without core changes -- over-optimistic and contradicted by its own §Future work. push_choice CREATES choice points; cut PRUNES them back to a barrier (the inverse), which genuinely requires the two CORE resolver additions above (BarrierId allocator in with_fresh_vars + barrier_at_creation on ChoicePoint). The no-core-changes claim holds only for the DERIVED combinators. FILES: kb/resolve.rs (ChoicePoint field, candidate-stack prune, step handling of cut alongside Not / HoApply / PushChoice), kb/mod.rs (with_fresh_vars BarrierId alloc, BuiltinTag::Cut, register anthill.kernel.cut), grammar (tree-sitter + fastparse: parse ! as a body goal) + parse IR, kernel.anthill (operation cut). DERIVED FOLLOW-ONS (separate WIs, each a pure rewrite over cut, no further core): once(g) = (g, !) lifted into a synthesized rule; if-then-else(c, t, e) = (c, !, t) ; e; findall = meta-call plus collection. SUBSTRATE ALREADY DELIVERED: push_choice (WI-075, Verified) supplies the ChoicePoint / Continuation machinery that cut prunes. TESTS: commit-to-first-clause (a(?x):-b,!,c discards later a-clauses); inner choice-point pruning (! after a branching b drops the alternatives of b); disjunction transparency (outer cut prunes inner or; inner cut opaque to outer or); cut + NAF (cut within a not(...) scope); cut + delay / residualization (a barrier whose owning frame floundered). IMMEDIATE CONSUMER (NON-BLOCKING): WI-089 anthill.realization.calculate_type_mapping uses a NAF existence-wrapper for the specific-key-wins-else-default priority; once cut lands it simplifies to the clean TypeMapping(...)!, default form. WI-089 does NOT depend on this. scaland parity (grammar + loader; resolver port if scaland resolves) tracked as a follow-up."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-075"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-06-26T07:25:06Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-502", author: "claude", content: "Design specced 2026-06-26 in docs/design/constrained-term-substrate.md (committed 18471bc): a CONSTRAINT substrate of which type is one kind, carrier-centric; untyped kernel by stratification; type-specificity = an untyped guard over type-terms; type caches on the carrier (never the shared TermId); functional model kept (explicit wakeup, no cells). Two shapes (A monomorphize at boundary, B guard over the type that flows from the redex). Staged Step0..6; check+suspend now, label later. Step 0 (persistent imbl substitution, O(1) frame.subst.clone) = WI-569, DELIVERED (commit 18471bc).", at: "2026-06-26T11:52:25Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-569", description: some(value: "WI-502 Step 0 — persistent substitution substrate. Replace Substitution.bindings (HashMap<VarId,Value>) with imbl::HashMap (persistent HAMT, O(1) structural clone; imbl is already a direct dep, precedent eval/map_arena.rs) so the per-frame frame.subst.clone() at each resolution step becomes O(1) instead of an O(bindings) deep copy — eliminating the O(depth^2) copy that would otherwise bottleneck carrying a growing constraint store (the WI-537 Gamma-OOM shape). Behavior-preserving: same lookup/insert semantics; rewrite bind_compressed's in-place iter_mut path-compression as collect-keys-then-functional-insert; KEEP the parent chain (frame substs are parent=None, so it does not compromise O(1); parent removal deferred). Prerequisite for WI-502 (constrained-term substrate; see docs/design/constrained-term-substrate.md, Step 0). Acceptance: cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-06-26T11:52:28Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-169", description: some(value: "Synth-rule lifetime — memoize, don't retract (framing corrected 2026-06-25). `synthesize_conjunction_rule` (kb/execute.rs:288), reached from `coerce_to_single_goal_value` (execute.rs:325), mints a uniquely-named `_synth_{rules.len()}` rule on every multi-goal disjunction/negation lowering (proposal 033 §M4 / WI-076). The name is unique by construction, so hash-consing never collapses two structurally-identical synths, and each `KB.execute` (eval/builtins.rs:755 -> execute_logical_query, execute.rs:608) of such a query permanently grows the symbol table, the append-only `rules` Vec, and the discrim tree. REAL but LATENT: the only entry point is one-shot today (anthill CLI, anthill-todo); no daemon/REPL/server issues queries in a loop, so nothing triggers the aggregate growth. Priority stays LOW until a long-running query consumer lands (proposal 047 effect loop / a query server). CORRECTION to the original framing — the two retract-based options it preferred are the weak ones. (a) 'retract on Drop' is architecturally impossible: `SearchStream` (resolve.rs:431) does NOT own or borrow the KB — it threads `&mut kb` through split_first/step per pump — so a Drop impl has no KB handle to call retract. (b) 'bulk-retract after stream exhaustion' misses abandoned streams: lazy streams are routinely dropped half-consumed (take(1), short-circuit, walk_solutions stopping early) and never reach an exhaustion hook. Worse, `KnowledgeBase::retract` (kb/mod.rs:1556 — the original '710' ref is stale) only flips entry.retracted=true and cleans indexes/discrim/refcounts; it does NOT reclaim the RuleEntry slot (the Vec is append-only, RuleIds must stay stable). So (a) and (b) cannot fix RuleEntry growth — the very thing the WI calls the larger leak. CHOSEN FIX — option (c), structural-dedup memoization. Cache the synth head functor keyed on a canonical form of the body. The rule is already stored De Bruijn (assert_rule_debruijn_with_nodes abstracts over the fresh free-vars), so key on (canonical De Bruijn body, arity); a repeated query reuses one rule, re-allocating only the head application with the current query's fresh free-var args. This converts the leak from unbounded-in-#queries to bounded-by-#distinct-multi-goal-bodies (a static program-text quantity), needs NO retraction (sidesteps both the Drop-can't-reach-KB and abandoned-stream problems — the synth rule is a memoized lowering artifact, not garbage), and is a small perf win on hot repeated reflect queries. Residual: the interned synth symbol still can't be reclaimed (no symbol GC), but #distinct-bodies-bounded interning is acceptable. Acceptance: issue the SAME multi-goal disjunction/negation query N times and assert KB rule count is CONSTANT after the first (zero growth, not merely sub-linear); plus N distinct bodies -> growth <= N. Exercises the synth path via tests/eval_q3_test.rs::q3_disjunction_multi_goal_branch_lifts_via_synthesized_rule. NOTE: a duplicate `id: WI-169` (the Delivered scaland forward-mapping item) is a pre-existing data dup, tracked in WI-174 feedback; not addressed here."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-076"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-06-26T12:37:43Z"))
