fact WorkItem(id: "WI-592", description: some(value: "Constructor argument decidable in Γ-discharge: a nullary-constructor used as a bare-identifier argument is lowered for a Γ goal as var_ref(name: ctor) (op-body load wraps every bare identifier as var_ref), and the Γ floundering gate treats every var_ref as an open-world variable, so a guarded-effect guard / requires-precondition over a constructor argument never refutes. Fix at the goal-lowering boundary: try_occurrence_to_term lowers a constructor var_ref to a ground Ref (decidable), leaving the carried occurrence's var_ref shape intact (so the typer's param_to_arg_sym re-key is unaffected); plus substitute_ref_syms (the effect param-rename) no longer recurses into a var_ref name (the WI-552 corruption, here for the rename). Const excluded (a const names a value; structural Ref(const) compare without folding would be unsound). Unblocks WI-573 spec-op guard discharge over a concrete constructor carrier; also corrects WI-067/WI-539 Γ-discharge over constructor args. Acceptance: cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-06-29T06:56:59Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-573", author: "claude", content: "Prerequisite WI-592 DELIVERED (constructor args now decidable in Γ-discharge: try_occurrence_to_term lowers a constructor var_ref→Ref; substitute_ref_syms no longer recurses into var_ref). REMAINING WI-573 SCOPE (Layer 2, verified by probe): a guard whose predicate is a spec op resolves to the generic anthill.prelude.Eq.eq, which is ALSO a registered structural builtin (BuiltinTag::Eq). So a NATIVE-Eq carrier (structural, no override) now discharges soundly via the builtin (post-WI-592). But a carrier with a CUSTOM eq override discharges via the STRUCTURAL builtin too — UNSOUND (ignores the override). The SLD resolver does NOT dispatch user spec-op overrides (confirmed: dispatch is a typer/eval concern, resolve.rs only handles Eq.eq/Eq.neq builtins). So WI-573 = detect when the carrier overrides the spec op and either (a) dispatch the guard goal to the carrier impl via the carried type (value_type_term, WI-578) = runtime monomorphization, or (b) suspend (keep effect) when an override exists but can't be dispatched. The isEmpty/head CASCADE (isEmpty default body calls splitFirst, another spec op) = WI-567, not WI-573. ALSO: the occurrence-twin substitute_ref_syms_occ lacks the var_ref skip WI-592 added to the term path — benign/pre-existing (guard goals are Value::Term so untriggered), worth symmetry if a Node-carried guard ever stores a re-keyable var_ref.", at: "2026-06-29T06:57:26Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-581", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED 2026-06-29 (commit 2b6e03b) with a DEVIATION from the prescribed assert, user-approved. The ticket prescribed a STRICT assert by_qualified_name[qn(f)]==Some(f) on the premise 'heads carry the resolved canonical symbol by construction'. A non-fatal probe at push_value_head_entry over the FULL workspace suite FALSIFIED that premise: 1096 DISTINCT head functors are legitimately bare short-name interns (by_qn=None) -- undeclared 'fact foo(...)' predicates (stdlib file_extension/needs_rebuild), datalog-style test facts (ancestor/parent/...), and generated _synth_N memo rules (execute.rs:377 uses intern, not define) -- and ZERO are a genuine same-FQN divergence (by_qn=Some(other)). The strict form would panic ~7317x. A bare ad-hoc predicate is SOUND without being canonical: the fact head and every query goal bare-intern the same string to the same Symbol via intern_map dedup, so the discrim tree matches. SHIPPED FORM (lenient): debug_assert_eq!(canonical_sym(f), f) -- fires only when a QN IS registered but maps to a DIFFERENT symbol, i.e. a same-FQN COPY (scan-time twin under a different u32), which is the realistic WI-502 typed-rule/typed-value producer drift and exactly the canonical_sym-bridgeable case the ticket's DECIDED FIX + CAVEAT center on. Passes the whole suite (2360/0). DOCUMENTED RESIDUAL GAP: a bare-head / FQN-goal asymmetry (head bare-interns a name the goal FQN-resolves) is UNDETECTABLE from the head symbol alone at this choke point -- a benign ad-hoc bare head and a 'should-have-resolved' bare head are indistinguishable, so no choke-point assert can separate them without false-positiving on all 1096. Acceptance: (1) producers enforced via the assert; (2) loud debug_assert added; (3) caveat documented (assert comment + canonical_sym doc); (4) canonical_sym general alias added, canonical_sort_sym delegates; (5) unindex_functor/rules_by_functor doc corrected (enumeration index vs discrim-tree resolution index; SLD drops eqs via is_equation filter). /code-review (2 finders): correctness []=clean (re-ran full suite + cargo check), doc-accuracy verified all 7 behavioral claims TRUE + 1 minor locator nit fixed. STILL DEFERRED (ticket's opt-in): symmetric canonical_sym normalization INSIDE the lookup (query_view top-edge + rules_by_functor()), only if a non-canonical goal path appears that cannot be fixed at the producer.", at: "2026-06-29T07:44:35Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-581", description: some(value: "Guardrail (WI-502 sub-item): canonical head/goal functor symbols for the two rule-firing indexes. Both indexes key on the RAW functor Symbol read off the head TermView — the discrimination tree via DiscrimKey::Functor(s)/Ref(s) (the resolution-time matcher, query_view at resolve.rs:905) and rules_by_functor via TermView::head().functor_sym() (kb/mod.rs:995,1013; the enumeration index) — with NO by_qualified_name canonicalization, unlike sort_ops which normalizes via canonical_sort_sym on BOTH insert and lookup. Sound TODAY only because heads and goals carry the resolved canonical symbol by construction. RISK: WI-502 typed-rule-pattern machinery (the ?x: T binder grammar + loader, plus any head rekeying) and the typed-value producers (WI-578) will synthesize or rekey head and goal functor symbols; a non-canonical copy of the same name (a fresh intern, an intern_unique twin per WI-550, a scan-time copy, or a SHORT-name intern) would index or query under a divergent u32 and SILENTLY no-match (no fallback). DECIDED FIX: producer-side canonical-by-construction is PRIMARY — producers resolve the functor to the canonical resolved symbol (the by_qualified_name entry); because both the stored head and the query goal flow from producers, this covers BOTH index sides at once (insert-only canonicalization does NOT, since the goal/query path has no insert choke point). ENFORCE with a loud debug_assert at the shared insert choke point KnowledgeBase::push_value_head_entry, which feeds both rules_by_functor (~kb/mod.rs:1013) and the discrim insert (~:1019) off one head_functor: assert by_qualified_name[qualified_name_of(f)] == Some(f). CAVEAT: canonical_sort_sym (by-QN) only bridges same-FQN copies; a short-name or mis-qualified intern is NOT bridged (falls through to identity), so it MUST be fixed at the producer, never papered over by a canonicalize call. ACCEPTANCE: (1) typed-rule head/goal producers yield the canonical resolved symbol; (2) loud assert at push_value_head_entry catches a drifting producer; (3) caveat documented; (4) a general canonical_sym alias added since canonical_sort_sym now also serves rule head functors; (5) the stale comment at kb/mod.rs:1816 (unindex_functor, which calls rules_by_functor the SLD goal-resolution index) corrected — the resolution index is the discrim tree, rules_by_functor is the enumeration index; cargo-test green. DEFERRED (opt-in): symmetric canonical_sym normalization INSIDE the lookup (query_view top-edge + rules_by_functor()) mirroring sort_ops, only if a non-canonical goal path appears that cannot be fixed at the producer."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-06-29T07:44:45Z"))
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-593", name: "finiteness")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-585", description: some(value: "Finiteness Phase A (proposal docs/proposals/library/003-finite-collection.md, RE-SCOPED): ADDITIVE — add the sorts, remove NOTHING (suite stays green; the removal is Phase C / WI-589). Add sort FiniteCollection (requires Iterable): collect(c)->List[Element] is the finiteness PRIMITIVE (body-less, each carrier provides), with size = List.length(collect(c)) and foldLeft/foldRight = List.{foldLeft,foldRight}(collect(c),...) as default bodies. size is count renamed (property name is sound on a guaranteed-finite carrier). Add sort FiniteStream: provides Stream[T,E] + provides FiniteCollection; its splitFirst returns a FiniteStream TAIL (the load-bearing invariant — makes collect a well-founded recursion). List provides FiniteStream (=> FiniteCollection + Stream transitively); add concrete List.foldLeft/foldRight (List.length exists; List.collect = identity). Map provides FiniteCollection directly (collect = entries; keeps its own O(1) size override). New prelude files MUST be added to both embedded-stdlib lists (anthill-cli + anthill-todo stdlib_embedded.rs). KERNEL DEP: covariant-return provision — FiniteStream provides Stream.splitFirst via a more-specific (FiniteStream) tail; type_compatible/WI-344 must admit it (akin to WI-347 override refinement). Acceptance: stdlib loads clean; FiniteCollection.size/foldLeft/foldRight + collect eval on a List and a Map. Follow-ups: WI-588 (Phase B finite map/filter + dispatch), WI-589 (Phase C removal), WI-590 (Phase D consolidation), WI-591 (doc-fix)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-06-29T07:21:36Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-585", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED 2026-06-29 (finiteness Phase A, proposal library/003). ADDITIVE: new prelude/finite_collection.anthill (FiniteCollection requires Iterable; collect primitive + size=List.length(collect(c)) + foldLeft/foldRight defaults delegating to List's concrete folds). list.anthill: concrete foldLeft/foldRight (recurse via splitFirst NOT cons — bare cons tail loses element type -> unconstrained EffP) + provides FiniteCollection + collect=identity. map.anthill: provides FiniteCollection + collect=entries. Registered in anthill-stl/src/stdlib.rs AND scaland EmbeddedStdlib.scala (shared .anthill load in both impls). Test wi585_finite_collection (List full eval; Map size eval; custom FiniteBag full eval). wi345 clean-stdlib-load updated for 3 TRANSITIONAL FiniteCollection->Iterable shadow warnings (clear in Phase C). Verified: anthill-core 886/0, full workspace green, scaland 30/30, /code-review xhigh (5 findings fixed incl. HIGH scaland-missing-entry). SCOPE: FiniteStream deferred to Phase B/WI-588; Map collect/fold deferred to WI-593 (compound-Element threading). Nothing removed (removal = Phase C/WI-589).", at: "2026-06-29T07:21:39Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-584", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED 2026-06-29 (commit 7db93b1, suite 2364). apply_eq_rules matched eq via query(eq(current,?result)) then reify(result_var), which for a DeBruijn rule (loaded [simp] form, arity>0) returns the UNOPENED DeBruijn RHS template -> only ground-RHS identities rewrote; pick(?a,?b)=?a made simplify(pick(5,7)) yield Var(DeBruijn(0)) not 5. (Arity-0 Global-var equations via assert_fact never hit it -- LHS match binds the Global directly, reify resolves.) FIX: new instantiate_eq_rhs opens the RHS template DeBruijn vars to fresh globals (term_from_debruijn) + substitutes the synthetic VarId(u32::MAX-n) LHS-match values in tree_subst -- the SAME instantiation with_fresh_vars does for a body. Ground RHS / non-term-RHS fallback behavior-preserving. CORE ASSUMPTION verified (code-review finder 3, traced discrim.rs/persist_subst.rs/walk_view): the query var binds to the raw DeBruijn(0) term and reify never chases a DeBruijn through the synthetic encoding, so the RHS var and the LHS-matched var are independent subst entries -- the fix bridges them; test passes for the right reason. /code-review (3 finders, max effort): correctness []=clean (6 concerns traced: synthetic-detection byte-identical to with_fresh_vars, term_from_debruijn bounds safe since RHS indices<arity, fresh vars strictly newer than tree_subst keys so 2nd reify cannot capture, fuel-bounded); cascade []=clean (activation reaches ONLY the proof-verify simplify pass -- config.simplify default false, sole prod setter discharge_by_derivation; dangerous Set/Map commutative/idempotent rules stay requires-guarded=skipped, validated by resolver_skips_requires_guarded_equation; activated requires-free rules Pair/List/Int64/Float/Eq/String are sound terminating identities; no cross-sort collision per WI-581 qualified-functor guardrail); 1 maintainability finding APPLIED -- the u32::MAX-n synthetic-VarId decode (was inline in BOTH with_fresh_vars and instantiate_eq_rhs) centralized in new Var::synthetic_debruijn_index next to its encoder Var::as_vid, eliminating the 3-file silent-desync risk. Test: apply_eq_rules_instantiates_var_rhs. UNBLOCKS WI-292 (now its only remaining blocker is cleared).", at: "2026-06-29T08:19:28Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-584", description: some(value: "Resolver equational [simp] fallback (apply_eq_rules) instantiates VARIABLE-RHS rules correctly. Today the query + reify(result_var) shortcut (resolve.rs ~2244-2289) returns the rule UNOPENED DeBruijn RHS var, so only GROUND-RHS identities rewrite: a var-RHS rule pick(?a,?b)=?a makes simplify(pick(5,7)) yield Var(DeBruijn(1)) instead of 5 (probe-verified; GENERAL -- reproduces with a NON-requires-guarded sort, so pre-existing rewriter debt, not type-directed). The discrim match tree_subst already carries the LHS bindings as SYNTHETIC VarId(u32::MAX - n) entries (one per DeBruijn n); with_fresh_vars (mod.rs ~2848, body_rename loop ~2929-2937) opens a rule head+body via term_from_debruijn + those bindings. Fire the equation by instantiating the eq head RHS template the same way (open its DeBruijn vars to fresh globals, bind them to the synthetic LHS-match values, reify) instead of reifying the raw result_var. CASCADE RISK: any var-RHS equation now instantiates -- run the full suite. Acceptance: a non-requires-guarded var-RHS [simp] rule fires to its substituted RHS via simplify; cargo-test green. Discovered while implementing WI-292 (type-directed [simp] firing), which depends on this."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-06-29T08:19:38Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-292", description: some(value: "Type-directed [simp] firing (CONSUMER of the WI-502 typed-term substrate). A requires-guarded [simp] rule (add(?x,0)=?x scoped to Numeric) fires by READING the carried type of the redex rather than being skipped (today: the typer fires via simp_fire_guard_holds over op bodies; the resolver equation_is_requires_guarded-skips). With typed values (WI-502) the matcher reads the redex carried type and checks subsort(type, Numeric) as an SLD query over sort/instance facts: a concrete carrier holds via a provides fact, an abstract carrier via the in-scope requires in the constraint store; fire / do-not-fire / SUSPEND as residual C when under-determined (never NAF-decide, WI-067 hazard). NO min_sort builtin (min_sort is REMOVED per WI-502, replaced by typed(value,env)); read the carried type. Depends on WI-502. See docs/design/constrained-term-substrate.md. Acceptance: cargo-test green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-283", "WI-572", "WI-578", "WI-584"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-06-29T09:26:24Z"))
