fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-588", author: "user", content: "DEFERRED 2026-06-29 (depends WI-594). Phase B stdlib drafted then REVERTED: the finite-preserving combinators need effect-row threading the typer lacks. Approach that works once WI-594 lands: map/filter on FiniteCollection (List provides it DIRECTLY → coherence beats transitive Iterable.map), effect = FiniteCollection's SORT param E (grounds via provision, no projection), body wraps a finite-stream view of the receiver. New sorts needed: FiniteStream (provides Stream covariant-return + FiniteCollection), FiniteMappedStream/FiniteFilteredStream carriers (source field typed FiniteStream → unconditional provision). Covariant-return provision VERIFIED working (MappedStream precedent). The finiteIterator(c)->FiniteStream[Element,E] indirection was a workaround for WI-594 but cascaded backing-burden onto every carrier — drop it once WI-594 lets fmapped(s,f) over a bare receiver thread the effect. Reproducer of the blocker: wi594 test (bare-receiver map over MappedStream).", at: "2026-06-29T08:41:09Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-594", author: "user", content: "REPRODUCER CONFIRMED + GENERALIZED 2026-06-29: the bug is NOT finite-specific. wi594 test (anthill-core/tests/include/wi594_finite_map_effect_threading_test.rs, #[ignore]d) reproduces it on the EXISTING lazy carrier MappedStream: a bare-receiver 'map(s: Stream, f) = mapped(s, f)' yields 'MappedStream[ES = ??_, Src = s.T]' — source element threads, source effect does NOT. The lazy combinators never hit it only because Iterable.map routes through iterator(c) -> Stream[Element, E] (explicit type-args carry the effect). NOTE: a SINGLE-field carrier (one Stream source, element FROM that source) threads the effect fine; the gap needs the TWO-field map shape (source + transform fn, result element from fn) — confirmed by a passing single-field control. Acceptance flip = remove #[ignore] from the wi594 test.", at: "2026-06-29T08:41:23Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-594", description: some(value: "Typer BUG (surfaced by WI-588 finiteness Phase B): thread the access-EFFECT row — not just the element — when a PROVIDER value flows into a carrier's spec-typed field, so the finite combinators FiniteStream.map/filter can be written WITHOUT the finiteIterator indirection. ROOT: an asymmetry — the element threads but the effect row does not — in three sites. (1) CONSTRUCTION: fmapped(s, f) over a bare s: FiniteStream binds Src = s.T (element) but leaves ES = ??_ (effect unbound). (2) DISPATCH GROUNDING: an op-type-param effect (EffS in 'map[S,EffS](s: FiniteStream[S,EffS], ...)') does NOT ground from the carrier's provision at dispatch on a List, while a SORT-param effect E (Iterable.map's) DOES — inconsistent (args not checked vs param types, WI-385). (3) PROVISION-RETURN CHECK: the body's FiniteMappedStream is matched against the declared FiniteStream return for an op-param effect row {EffS,EffP} (works, cf. MappedStream.map) but NOT when the row carries a PROJECTION {s.E, EffP} (the raw carrier head is compared instead of applying the provision). WORKAROUND in WI-588: a body-less finiteIterator(c) -> FiniteStream[Element, E] on FiniteCollection routes the effect through an explicit return type; the parallel of Iterable.iterator. ACCEPTANCE: the ignored reproducer test flips to passing AND FiniteStream.map/filter can be defined directly (fmapped(s,f) over a bare receiver threads the effect), letting finiteIterator be removed from FiniteCollection. Reproducer: anthill-core/tests/include/wi594_finite_map_effect_threading_test.rs (#[ignore]d)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-06-29T09:53:15Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-594", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED 2026-06-29 (anthill-core 1967/0, full workspace 2365/0). ROOT cause (empirically pinned, NOT the Explore agent's provider-fact theory): in check_constructor_iter the field-unify of a bare spec arg (s: Stream) against a parameterized field (source: Stream[Src, ES]) bound NOTHING — the element only APPEARED to thread because the sibling fn field's declared type writes the projection (f: (x: s.T) -> Dst pins Src = s.T); the source effect, written nowhere a value flows, stayed ??_ and the provided Stream[E={ES,EF}] then failed to match the declared Stream[E={s.E,EffP}] return. FIX (face 1 CONSTRUCTION + face 3 PROVISION-RETURN, both): new bare_spec_arg_self_projection rebuilds the bare arg's type as the receiver self-projection B[p = s.p] keyed by the FIELD's own binding symbols (so unify_parameterized_view's by-symbol match threads every param), used in both arg-unify loops. An EFFECT-ROW param binds the single-label ROW {s.E} (build_canonical_effects_rows) not the bare projection — the field effect param is a row TAIL but a projection in a row is a present ATOM, so the row must wrap it to match the source-written {s.E,EffP}; a SORT param threads the bare s.T. Effect-vs-sort discrimination = sort_param_is_effect_row via the sort's DIRECT requires EffectsRuntime[Effects=member] anchor (WI-320 desugaring of effects E=?). Altitude: construction-site is the only layer holding BOTH the field param structure and the receiver occurrence — unify_parameterized_with_sort_ref sees only type views (no receiver identity), and global env-expansion is rejected (param_projection_cycle would flag s: Stream[T=s.T] as a self-cycle). /code-review xhigh (2 independent finder agents converged on ONE finding): sort_param_is_effect_row used the TRANSITIVE requires_chain + short-name match -> a transitively-required spec's effects A could misclassify the sort's own sort-param A; FIXED -> direct_requires_chain (the effects-anchor is always a DIRECT requires). Reproducer flipped (removed #[ignore]). FiniteStream Phase B removal stays WI-588 (deferred).", at: "2026-06-29T09:54:00Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-582", description: some(value: "WI-502 typed rule pattern SYNTAX — the explicit ?x: T binder (new grammar), distinct from the implicit requires path (WI-292). Add the EXPLICIT surface for typed rule patterns per docs/design/constrained-term-substrate.md §\"Typed rule patterns — surface and matching\": a type-variable introducer [E] on a rule head (operation conventions — rule add[T](?x: T, 0) = ?x :- Numeric[T]) and a : T annotation on a pattern variable in a rule LHS (inline rule add(?x: Numeric, 0) = ?x). SEMANTICS (desugaring): head(…, ?x: T, …) :- body MEANS head(…, ?x, …) :- conforms(typeof(?x), T), body — conforms = subsort (sort bound) / provides (spec bound), an ordinary SLD goal over the carried type. REALIZATION: the loader installs the bound as a Type(T) CONSTRAINT on the rule variable (the Step-1 store, WI-570), keyed by the DeBruijn index on the rule template and opened to the fresh VarId at with_fresh_vars; matching then rides the EXISTING Step-2 bind_waking (WI-571) + the carried type (WI-578) — the SAME wakeup the implicit path (WI-292) wires, so NO new matcher. A type-variable bound is declared ONCE: a non-linear p[T](?x: T, ?x) shares one T across both occurrences (must bind alike AND type alike); a conflicting re-annotation is a LOUD load error. The body-guard form (conforms(typeof(?x), T) as an explicit goal) is the equivalent fallback for a bound spanning several variables. SCOPE: grammar.js ([T] type-params after a rule-head functor + : Type on a rule-LHS pattern var — remember the WI-517 gotcha: a parenthesized single typed binder must be a NAMED node, pattern_typed) + lowering + loader Type-constraint installation. Reuses the dormant Pattern::Var.type_ann channel (WI-318 created it, WI-517 used it for lambda binders — prior art). NOT in scope: the implicit requires firing (WI-292) and the typed-value carrier (WI-578); this CONSUMES both. PRODUCER of new rule-head/goal functor symbols -> MUST honor the WI-581 canonicalization guardrail (canonical resolved symbol, never a fresh/short/unique intern). ACCEPTANCE: rule add[T](?x: T, 0) = ?x :- Numeric[T] and the inline rule add(?x: Numeric, 0) = ?x both parse, load (installing the Type constraint on the variable), and FIRE by reading the carried type (subsort/provides discharged; suspend when under-determined, never NAF-decided); cargo-test green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-292"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-06-29T12:39:09Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-573", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED 2026-06-29 (commit f8e4dd7, full workspace suite green) — the SOUND FLOOR (ticket option (b)): detect a custom eq/neq override and SUSPEND (keep the effect), do NOT let the structural Eq/Neq builtin decide it. THE HAZARD is exactly the eq/neq family: negate_goal swaps eq<->neq into a POSITIVE goal the structural builtin DECIDES; every other predicate negates via not(..) which prove_from_gamma's definite_only already suppresses (kept soundly — so isEmpty/head guards were never the unsound-DROP path; that cascade = WI-567, the DISCHARGE side). New gate in guarded_atom_refuted (typing.rs): for an eq/neq conjunct, scan BOTH operands' FULL value structure (value_carrier_overrides_eq_family_deep mirrors views_structurally_equal's positional + named recursion) and suspend if ANY reachable carrier has its own eq/neq member (carrier_override_op). Extracted eq_neq_functors shared with negate_goal so swap+gate can't diverge. 7 tests (top-level keep / native drop / declarable / conservative-suspend-not-dispatch / nested-element keep / nested-field keep / nested-native drop). /code-review (high) caught the NESTED-carrier hole (a top-level-only check missed eq(some(Green),some(Red)) over Option[Color] = unsound drop) -> fixed via the deep scan; reuse finding -> eq_neq_functors. DEFERRED (option (a)): runtime-monomorphization DISPATCH of the override so a custom-override guard can still DISCHARGE (not just suspend) — this is the discharge capability WI-567/WI-566 need. RESIDUAL (separate, review Finding C): the direct prove_from_gamma sites (typing.rs ~3669, proof_verify.rs ~602) decide eq over a custom carrier structurally too — a potential soundness gap in the WI-538/WI-558 PROOF paths, out of WI-573's guarded-effect-discharge scope, candidate follow-on.", at: "2026-06-29T13:46:13Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-573", description: some(value: "Guarded-effect guard discharge over spec-op / type-directed guards (CONSUMER of the WI-502 typed-term substrate). Extend the WI-067 discharge from native-scalar guards (eq(b,0) over Int64) to guards whose predicate is a spec op ({ Error :- eq(b,0) } over a non-native carrier; { Error[EmptyStream] :- isEmpty(s) }) by refuting them through the carried type on the argument: a concrete carrier (typed value) dispatches the spec op via its requires dictionary (runtime monomorphization), making the guard evaluable/refutable; an abstract carrier stays present (sound). Depends on WI-502. Unblocks WI-567 (concrete head discharge) and WI-566 (048 Phase-4 discharge over rule-defined predicates). RE-SCOPED 2026-06-27 from Shape A monomorphization (dropped). See docs/design/constrained-term-substrate.md. Acceptance: cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-572", "WI-578", "WI-592"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-06-29T13:46:21Z"))
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-599", name: "finiteness")
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-598", name: "finiteness")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-588", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED 2026-06-29 (finiteness Phase B, proposal library/003). Finite-preserving map/filter on FiniteCollection returning FiniteStream, so a finite pipeline stays consumable. NEW: prelude/finite_stream.anthill (FiniteStream provides Stream covariant-tail + FiniteCollection) + prelude/finite_combinators.anthill (FiniteMappedStream/FiniteFilteredStream, mirror the lazy carriers; recursive fmapped(rest,fn) bare re-wrap = the WI-594 case). FiniteCollection gains finiteIterator (finite dual of iterator) + map/filter = fmapped/ffiltered(finiteIterator(c),.). List provides FiniteStream + finiteIterator=l; Map finiteIterator=entries. Registered in anthill-stl/src/stdlib.rs AND scaland EmbeddedStdlib.scala. COHERENCE (the load-bearing piece): map/filter live on FiniteCollection so a Map (finite non-stream) gets them too, but a Map provides Iterable AND FiniteCollection BOTH DIRECTLY (equal provision-graph distance) so dot-dispatch m.filter(p) tied to the LAZY Iterable.filter. FIXED by a resolver tie-break in find_spec_op_for_provided_sort (typing.rs): keep nearest-distance definers, then on a tie prefer the requires-REFINEMENT (FiniteCollection requires Iterable -> wins); kernel-language.md 8.7 documents both stages. (The stdlib alternative -- FiniteCollection PROVIDES Iterable -- was tried and rejected: a concrete sub-spec iterator entangles Iterable's abstract bodies, breaking pure-Iterable carriers, wi495.) Verified: full rust 897/0/3, scaland 187/0, /code-review xhigh (caught the Map dot gap; typer tie-break reviewed clean). Tests: wi588 (List map/filter/fold eval; dispatch coherence; Map dot map/filter finite + eval); wi492 rewired to finite + added a qualified-Iterable lazy-carrier test; wi278 rewired to finite (consume via FiniteCollection.foldLeft); wi345 expects 5 finite shadows (3 transitional size/foldL/foldR + 2 PERMANENT map/filter); wi585 FiniteBag backs finiteIterator. GOTCHAS: collect(xs.map(f)) -- direct body-less primitive on the dot-chain's abstract FiniteStream -- fails dispatch (use size/foldLeft); -> WI-598. Follow-ups: WI-599 (thin no-finiteIterator design: needs WI-594 generalized to FiniteCollection fields + WI-593), WI-598.", at: "2026-06-29T13:25:26Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-588", description: some(value: "Finiteness Phase B (proposal library/003): finite-preserving combinators + dispatch coherence. Add FiniteCollection.map/filter returning FiniteStream (new finite mapped/filtered carriers whose source FIELD is typed FiniteStream, so they unconditionally provide FiniteStream — no conditional instance). Dispatch coherence (kernel dep 2, VERIFIED mechanism): the kernel selects among same-named ops from different specs by PROVISION-GRAPH DISTANCE (find_spec_op_for_provided_sort typing.rs:2942-2993, BFS direct-before-transitive, first match) — NOT a requires/sub-spec ranking (pick_most_specific typing.rs:8912 is impls-of-SAME-spec by ground-binding count). So shape the provides graph so FiniteCollection.map is strictly CLOSER than Iterable.map on a List (FiniteCollection depth 1 via FiniteStream; Iterable depth 2 via Stream->Iterable; Stream defines no map) and ADD A TEST pinning it — equal distance is order-dependent / ambiguity error. Doc gap: kernel-language.md §8.7 is silent on the two-different-specs-same-op case. Rewire wi492_transitive_provision_test to the finite path (xs.map(inc).size() => List->FiniteStream->size). Additive: Stream/Iterable consumers still present, suite stays green. Depends on WI-585 (Phase A)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-585", "WI-594"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-06-29T13:25:26Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-589", author: "user", content: "Phase B did NOT rewire the consumers (the WI premise is wrong): removing the eager consumers from Stream/Iterable breaks 46 tests across 16 files. ~half are mechanical import swaps (Iterable.{size,fold}/Stream.{collect,fold} -> FiniteCollection); the other half call collect on an abstract map/filter/iterator/rewrap result and hit WI-598 (collect on an abstract FiniteStream: 'no impl matches per-call bindings'). Several of those (wi368/wi411/wi424-bump/wi439/wi461) test collect-returns-List element-precision, which can't be faithfully kept without WI-598. Also wi461/wi411 helpers return a BARE Stream (would need to become FiniteStream). Decision (user, 2026-06-29): deliver WI-598 first, so added depends_on WI-598; then Phase C is a clean mechanical rewire. The spec+bridge removal itself is straightforward and verified to compile (regenerated stream.rs drops the ops, bridge SearchStreamAdapter drops count/fold_left/fold_right/collect + retires fold_solutions). Per-file rewiring notes for when WI-598 lands: wi345 = drop count to 2 (map/filter only, its own comment predicts this); wi495 IntBag must 'provides FiniteCollection' (collect=items, finiteIterator=items) mirroring Map; wi492 iterable_size_on_lazy_mapped_stream tests a REMOVED capability (sizing a lazy mapped stream) -> repurpose to find/isEmpty; wi374 Stream.count(s) on a bare Stream -> length(takeN(s,1)) or similar; wi365 collect_wrong_element_return currently passes only because the import fails (false positive) -> rewire to FiniteCollection.collect.", at: "2026-06-29T14:32:17Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-591", description: some(value: "Doc-fix: docs/design/operation-call-model.md:551 claims 'fact Eq[T = List[?A]] :- Eq[?A] already works via Horn-clause facts; SLD resolution handles it natively'. This is INACCURATE vs the implementation — rule-bodied fact/provides are not parsed/loaded/dispatched (collect_provides_candidates skips non-fact rules; lookup is by functor index, not SLD). Correct it to the actual mechanism: conditional instances via the witness-sort + requires pattern (sort EqList { sort A=?; requires Eq[T=A]; fact Eq[T=List[T=A]] }), see wi224_sld_resolution_test. Found during proposal library/003."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: []), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-06-29T14:48:45Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-595", author: "claude", content: "PARTIAL (2026-06-29, commits 69db1a3 part5 + 27be6d3/merge 5601894 part4+refine; full suite green). DELIVERED the TYPE-READ COMPLETENESS half: (part 5) folded the double lookup_spec_op_dispatch -- simp_guard_holds_core now takes the resolved spec_sort, each caller (typer/resolver) keeps its own non-spec-op default; (part 4) apply_eq_rules now takes the live frame subst (the resolver loop threads self.stack.last().subst, an O(1) imbl clone; simplify+tests pass empty), so a constraint-store-typed carrier var in the redex is decidable (C1); (NEW, via design dialogue) var_type_term refines a BOUND var: meets the bound value's own type with the carrier var's store constraint(s) over the resolved chain. /code-review (focused) caught a DORMANT soundness defect -- meet_types returns the CONSTRAINT when it is a strict SUBSORT ((false,true)=>b), so a List value + NonEmptyList bound would move the head and wrong-fire; FIXED by PINNING the refine to value_ty's authoritative sort head (apply only meets that keep the head = sharpen type-params; never change it; headless stays headless; deterministic chain order). 2 tests (wi578: refines nil+List[Int64]->List[Int64]; ignores Int64+bogus-List[Int64]). KEY FINDINGS for the REMAINING parts: the Value::ty CARRY machinery is INERT in prod (typed() has ZERO prod callers -> value_type_term always recomputes structurally; the carry-sites drop None->None as no-ops); the refine only sharpens type-PARAMS (sort head invariant) so it is inert for the head-reading simp consumer but correct for param-readers; reify's fn_value COLLAPSES an all-Term redex back to a Value::Term (not an Entity), so part 1 must build the typed Entity EXPLICITLY (not via reify) to get per-arg ty; the carry-sites are NOT on the apply_eq_rules path (part 1's typed Entity is used locally), so they are separate infra. REMAINING (pure efficiency, no behavior change): (1) compute-once typed Entity at the apply_eq_rules entry so per-arg carrier sorts compute once across candidates; (2) the 4 carry-sites (reify/fn_value, close_value_head_debruijn, rewrite_ref_value, walk_value_to_resolved) sigma-REFINE ty (user chose sigma-refine over verbatim; helpers close_value_type/subst_value_type/open_value_type exist); (3) cache the sort_provides SortProvidesInfo edge-set (rebuilt per call today). Still Claimed/in-progress.", at: "2026-06-29T15:05:18Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-595", author: "claude", content: "PART 1 DELIVERED 2026-06-29 (commit cc5ecfd; suite 2386 green; /code-review high, 4 finders): apply_eq_rules computes the requires-guard (simp_requires_guard_holds) at most ONCE per redex (lazy memo over the candidate loop) instead of per requires-guarded candidate -- the decision reads only current+subst (rid never enters it), so behavior-identical (all finders + suite confirm). Parts 2+3 DEFERRED after review. (2) carry-sites sigma-refine is INERT: typed()/Value::typed_term have ZERO prod callers, so ty is always None and the 4 reconstruction sites (reify_value, close_value_head_debruijn, rewrite_ref_value, walk_value_to_resolved) carry None->None; sigma-refine = untestable dead code, revived only by a future ty-stamping producer. (3) sort_provides edge-set memo was implemented (count-keyed) then REVERTED: 3 independent finders confirmed a latent ABA soundness hole -- a balanced RUNTIME retract+assert of DISTINCT SortProvidesInfo facts (Store.retract/insert builtins) restores the active provider-rule count, so a count-keyed memo serves a STALE edge-set; also O(providers)-per-hit (not O(1)) and the invalidate_resolve_cache backstop is dead in prod. A correct cache needs a monotonic fact-mutation EPOCH at the assert/retract choke-point (or invalidation wired at the provides sites) -- a central-fact-path change not justified by the UNMEASURED per-call rebuild cost. OPEN DECISION (user): pursue the part-3 epoch (touches core path; unmeasured gain) OR close WI-595 with part-3 as a future profiling-driven ticket. Recommendation: close (part-2 inert; part-3 deferred-pending-measurement). Builds on parts 4+5+refine (69db1a3/27be6d3).", at: "2026-06-29T16:03:30Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-593", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. Root: bind_spec_params_from_carrier_param skipped a compound provider binding (Element=Pair[A=K,B=V]) — neither a bare carrier-param ref nor ground — leaving Element abstract → MissingRequiresForSpecOp when it escapes into collect's result or a fold/map/filter callback. Fix: a new arm substitutes the receiver's type-args (K->Int64,V->Int64) into the compound and binds if fully ground. TWO subtleties found via tests: (1) must SKIP the spec's carrier param C — its provision binding is the carrier application (C->Map, or transitive C->SortView[T=T] for a List); grounding it pinned C wrong and conflicted with arg unification (regressed List/Iterable). Threaded carrier_pvid from carrier_param_receiver to skip it. (2) the compound binding is stored as a reflect.SortView WRAPPER (SortView(Pair,A=K,B=V)), NOT a plain Pair[...]; substituting in-place kept the SortView wrapper, which then mismatched user-written Pair in result/callback positions (regressed Map.map/filter dot-dispatch wi588) AND blocked fold EffP inference (SortView!=Pair param unify bailed before the effect-row tail). substitute_carrier_params now UNWRAPS SortView -> plain Fn{base,named}. With that, collect/size/foldLeft/foldRight/map/filter on a Map ALL typecheck+eval with NO explicit type args. WI-601 (filed then DELETED) was a symptom of the SortView bug, not a real gap. Tests: wi585 finite_collection_on_map_eval (collect/size/foldL/foldR, bare) + finite_collection_map_wrong_element_callback_rejected (soundness: Pair[String,Bool] callback rejected, proves grounding is the concrete Pair[Int64,Int64]). Short-name matching deepened -> tracked by WI-600. Full suite green; code-review (3 angles) clean.", at: "2026-06-29T16:06:20Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-600", author: "user", content: "WI-593 added one more site to subsume: substitute_carrier_params now ALSO rebuilds a reflect.SortView wrapper into a plain Fn{base,named} during grounding (the provider fact stores compound bindings as SortView(Pair,A=K,B=V), not Pair[...]). The canonical-identity normalization should make provider-view compound bindings carry the plain parameterized term directly, removing both the short-name keying AND this SortView->Fn rebuild.", at: "2026-06-29T16:06:42Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-593", description: some(value: "Compound-Element threading on a non-Stream FiniteCollection/Iterable provision. FiniteCollection.collect / foldLeft / foldRight (and Iterable.find/fold) on a Map fail to ground the provision's COMPOUND Element=Pair[A=K,B=V] from a concrete Map[K=Int64,V=Int64] receiver when Element ESCAPES into the result type (collect -> List[Element]) or the callback (fold x: Element) — the typer raises MissingRequiresForSpecOp ('requires FiniteCollection[Element=…] covering abstract type parameter'). size works (Element consumed internally -> Int64). WI-357's bind_spec_params_from_carrier threads simple/Stream elements but not a compound Element on a direct (non-transitive) provision. Found in WI-585 Phase A (test wi585_finite_collection: Map collect/fold deferred, concrete-element FiniteBag carrier works). Fix = thread the provision sort-params (K,V) from the receiver type args into the compound Element binding at dispatch."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-585"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-06-29T16:20:44Z"))
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-601", name: "finiteness")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-598", description: some(value: "Typer: a body-less FiniteCollection primitive (collect) fails to DISPATCH on a value whose STATIC type is an abstract spec that provides+defines it (the dot-chain result xs.map(f).filter(p) is typed as the abstract FiniteStream, map/filter's declared return). 'collect(xs.map(f))' raises 'FiniteCollection.collect.dispatch: no impl matches per-call bindings', while size/foldLeft (DEFAULTED ops, whose bodies call collect on the spec's own C param via WI-365 self-typing) work. So a finite dot-chain must currently be consumed via size/foldLeft, not direct collect. Found in WI-588 (wi278/wi492 consume via foldLeft to work around). Also: Stream.collect's PROJECTION effect s.E does not ground through the 2-hop transitive provision FiniteX->FiniteStream->Stream (FiniteCollection's sort-param E does). ACCEPTANCE: collect(xs.map(f)) on a List typechecks+evals; cargo-test green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-588"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-06-29T17:39:17Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-598", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED 2026-06-29. Root: a body-less FiniteCollection carrier-param op (collect) on a value whose STATIC type is the abstract spec FiniteStream (the declared return of finite map/filter). FiniteStream provides FiniteCollection DIRECTLY (transitive==false) so the WI-496 transitive escape did not fire; concrete dispatch picked the FiniteStream impl then recursed into FiniteStream provides Stream -> Stream requires EffectsRuntime[E], unsatisfiable at the abstract access row E -> spurious DispatchNoMatch. Fix (typing.rs check_apply_iter ~6271): extend the carrier-param dispatch escape to ALSO defer when the carrier sort is itself an abstract spec — new helper carrier_is_abstract_spec = !sort_has_constructors(canon) && spec_has_any_providers(canon). A value of such a sort is some concrete provider at runtime, so leave the call as the spec op and let eval value-directed dispatch resolve it (the carrier-param analogue of the abstract self-receiver arm at 6221). resolved_ret is already Element-threaded and E bound by bind_spec_params_from_carrier_param, so the deferred return type/effects match the pre-existing transitive branch. Tests (wi598): collect_on_finite_map_chain_eval (prefix collect over map/filter/chain) + dot_collect_on_finite_chain_eval (fully-dotted .map().collect()/.filter().collect()). The 'Also' note (Stream.collect's s.E projection effect not grounding through 2-hop) is routed around: .collect() lands on FiniteCollection.collect (sort-param E grounds), not Stream.collect; that Stream-path limitation is mooted by Phase C/WI-589. code-review xhigh (2 agents): no correctness bug; applied cleanups (lazy || short-circuit, canonical sort_has_constructors, canonicalized carrier sym). ALTITUDE finding (self-receiver receiver_carrier uses the narrower base==spec_sort test, same latent gap for a Stream self-receiver op on a FiniteStream value) filed as WI-601. Full suite 2389/0.", at: "2026-06-29T17:39:18Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-589", description: some(value: "Finiteness Phase C (proposal library/003): remove the unsound homes. Delete count/foldLeft/foldRight/collect from stream.anthill and size/foldLeft/foldRight from iterable.anthill. Regenerate the WI-553 Stream trait (anthill-stl/build.rs -> OUT_DIR/stream.rs drops them) and remove the matching SearchStreamAdapter impls in anthill-stl/src/reflect/bridge.rs (count, fold_left, fold_right, collect; retire fold_solutions if unused). Rewire eval_test wi064 + wi424_iterable_members_test to the FiniteCollection ops. IMPORTANT: removal must be in BOTH spec AND Rust host. Suite stays green because Phase B already rewired the consumers. Depends on Phase B."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-588", "WI-598"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-06-30T14:55:29Z"))
