fact Tag(workitem: "WI-605", name: "typing")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-590", author: "claude", content: "Gap C root-caused (the remaining blocker for the recursive-collect compaction): a self-receiver op's return tail does NOT thread its type params when invoked on an abstract receiver. In the witness collect drain, `splitFirst(m)` (m : TestMapped[Source=S, Src=Src, T=T, ...] — the witness's own abstract op params) returns a destructured `rest` whose inferred type is a bare `Var` (Rigid/Global), NOT `TestMapped[T=T,...]`. Traced: carrier_param_receiver bails at `sort_functor_of_view(rest) == None` because rest is a Var, so bind_spec_params_from_carrier_param is never called and FiniteCollection.collect(rest)'s Element stays ?_  → 'expected List[T=?T], got List[T=?_]'. The CONCRETE dispatch threads fine (Element←Int64); only the abstract/recursive case fails. NOT authoring-fixable: declaring splitFirst's param explicitly parameterized (TestMapped[Source=Source,...] instead of bare TestMapped) does NOT help. Candidate fix lives in the WI-357/WI-393 carrier-grounding family (e.g. an identity self-provision view when carrier_sym == spec_sort, so a self-receiver op grounds its sort params directly from the receiver's type args). This is squarely WI-599's scope (it already names 'ground a body-less collect() on an abstract FiniteCollection field'). The recursion-FREE drain alternative is blocked by WI-605 (inline lambda in op body).", at: "2026-06-30T19:58:18Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-590", description: some(value: "Finiteness Phase D (proposal library/003, OPTIONAL cleanup): replace the separate finite combinator carriers (FiniteMappedStream/FiniteFilteredStream) with conditional-instance reuse of the single mapped/filtered carrier — 'a mapped stream is finite when its source is finite' via the witness-sort + requires pattern (EqList precedent). Requires parameterizing the mapped carrier over its SOURCE SORT so the field type preserves finiteness (today source: Stream erases it). Pure cleanup, no capability change. Depends on Phase C."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-589", "WI-599"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Claimed(agent: "claude", since: "2026-06-30T19:05:09Z"))
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-606", name: "typing")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-590", author: "claude", content: "Design (A) VERIFIED sound + executable plan (decision: map/filter return FiniteCollection, not FiniteStream — the single carrier can only provide the CARRIER-PARAM spec FiniteCollection via a witness; FiniteStream is self-receiver and CANNOT be witnessed, confirmed by probe). Soundness verified by probe: FiniteCollection.collect(mk(finiteList, f)) type-checks; FiniteCollection.collect(mk(infiniteNats, f)) is REJECTED ('no impl matches') — the witness's requires FiniteCollection[C=Source] gate makes a mapped stream finite IFF its source is. EXECUTABLE PLAN: (1) combinators.anthill — parameterize MappedStream & FilteredStream over a new 'sort Source = ?' param; field becomes source: Source (was Stream[Src,ES]); splitFirst return tail explicitly threaded MappedStream[Source=Source,Src=Src,T=T,ES=ES,EF=EF]; body uses QUALIFIED Stream.splitFirst(src) (src:Source abstract). provides Stream stays unconditional. (2) finite_combinators.anthill — REPLACE FiniteMappedStream/FiniteFilteredStream with WITNESS sorts MappedStreamFinite/FilteredStreamFinite: requires FiniteCollection[C=S,Element=Src,E=ES]; fact FiniteCollection[C=MappedStream[Source=S,...],Element=T,E={ES,EF}]; operation collect drain using QUALIFIED MappedStream.splitFirst(m) (gap C / WI-606 workaround — UNqualified leaves rest:?_). (3) finite_collection.anthill — map/filter construct mapped(c,f)/filtered(c,p) (Source=C), return FiniteCollection[C=MappedStream[...],...] (or bare carrier); drop fmapped/ffiltered imports; finiteIterator can stay (unused by map now) or be removed per WI-599. (4) Validate DISPATCH COHERENCE: xs.map(inc).size() must still pick FiniteCollection.map (List provides FiniteCollection depth 1) over Iterable.map (depth 2) — the probes did NOT test competing maps; wi492/wi278/wi439/wi588/eval_test behavioral assertions are the oracle (their carrier-name mentions are COMMENTS only, update for accuracy). (5) embedded list rustland/anthill-stl/src/stdlib.rs keeps finite_combinators entry (file repurposed). Two delivered typer fixes (provider-requires base-sort + EffectsRuntime dispatch skip, commit 3ccc19d1) are PREREQS that make the witness load+dispatch.", at: "2026-06-30T20:31:52Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-590", author: "claude", content: "RESIDUAL RISK for the rewrite (not cheaply de-riskable in a standalone probe): the CHAINING xs.map(f).size() / map.map.collect under the single carrier depends on (a) dispatch coherence — FiniteCollection.map must still win over Iterable.map on a finite receiver (List depth 1 vs 2; should hold, provision graph unchanged) — and (b) EFFECT-ROW closure through the chained map (a standalone probe leaked an undeclared  effect; in the REAL FiniteCollection.map the access row E + transform row EffP bind from the carrier provision + the f-arrow, the WI-594 mechanism that already works for the CURRENT FiniteStream-returning map). Both are only validatable by doing the actual stdlib rewrite and running wi492/wi278/wi439/wi588/eval_test as the oracle — if effect closure breaks, it is the WI-594/599 family. CORE design (witness load+dispatch, qualified-splitFirst collect drain, finite-iff-source soundness gate) is VERIFIED. Map RETURN TYPE: bare carrier MappedStream[Source=C,...] dispatches FiniteCollection.size/collect/map on the result via the witness (chaining resolves; the leak was effects only).", at: "2026-06-30T20:34:23Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-599", author: "claude", content: "STARTED + gap 1 reproduced & root-caused. Minimal repro (scratchpad wi599_gap1.anthill): a carrier FCMapped with field source: FiniteCollection[C=SrcC, Element=Src, E=ES] + a thin map fcm(c, f) over a carrier value c. fcm(c,f) yields FCMapped[Src=El, T=Dst, EF=EffP, ES=??_, SrcC=??_] — the ELEMENT threads (via the sibling fn's (x: El) pinning Src) but the source CARRIER (SrcC) and source EFFECT (ES) do NOT → 'tmap.return ES=??_/SrcC=??_' mismatch. ROOT: WI-594's bare_spec_arg_self_projection (typing.rs:12574) only fires when the arg's type is a BARE REF to the field's spec base (s: Stream into Stream[..]); here the field is a CARRIER-PARAM spec (FiniteCollection[C=..]) fed a CARRIER value c:C2 (not c:FiniteCollection), so the self-projection check (extract_sort_ref_sym(arg)==field_base) fails and nothing threads the field's params. FIX (located): a sibling fn carrier_arg_provision_projection, called at the two arg-unify loops in check_constructor_iter (typing.rs:12727/12738) next to bare_spec_arg_self_projection — when field type is a carrier-param spec Spec[C=fieldC, P=fieldP, ...] and arg c:C2 provides Spec, rebuild arg type as Spec[C=C2, P=<c's binding for P>, ...] keyed by the FIELD's binding symbols so unify_parameterized_view threads each param (effect param wrapped as a single-label row {..}, like WI-594; sort param bare). Binding source: concrete C2 → its provides-fact view (provider_spec_view_bindings/bind_spec_params_from_carrier family); abstract C2 in a spec METHOD → the enclosing spec's own Element/E params; abstract C2 via a free-op requires → the ambient requires (needs available_requires at the construction site — plumbing). GAP 2 (separate, per WI-599 desc): ground a body-less collect() called on an abstract FiniteCollection field ('FiniteCollection.collect.requires missing on enclosing sort'). Two delivered prereq fixes (3ccc19d1) already make a witness over a parameterized/effect-row carrier load+dispatch. Tree clean; only scratchpad probes, no stdlib/code edits yet.", at: "2026-06-30T20:44:58Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-595", author: "claude", content: "PART-3 DECIDED: CLOSE (measure-first, per user 2026-07-01). Instrumented sort_provides (edge-rebuild loop timed via atomic counters, reverted after) over representative workloads: (a) full stdlib+github-todo LOAD = 87 calls, 3741 edges scanned, ~20us/call, 43 edges/call, rebuild=1.7ms total (84% of sort_provides time), total sort_provides=2.0ms; (b) real github-todo RESOLVE (open_item+claimable) = 0 calls (simp path not reached); (c) stdlib has ZERO [simp]/[unfold] rules so the resolver directional-rewrite gate fires for no production rule; (d) synthetic worst case (500 [simp] redexes in one program) = 1187 calls, rebuild=37ms (77%). Verdict: the epoch cache saves ~1.7ms on a real load and 0 on resolution; only synthetic hundreds-of-simp-redex programs reach tens of ms (~13.5k redexes needed for ~1s). That benefit does not justify a soundness-sensitive change to the central fact-mutation path (assert/retract/unindex + runtime Store.retract/insert). Filed WI-607 (profiling-gated) to revisit ONLY if a real workload surfaces sort_provides as a hot spot. WI-595 delivered: part 1 (once-per-redex guard memo), parts 4+5 (frame-subst threading + folded spec_op lookup), refine (head-pinned var_type_term) all shipped; part 2 inert (no ty producer); part 3 measured+deferred=WI-607.", at: "2026-07-01T03:05:23Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-595", description: some(value: "WI-292 PHASE 3 -- efficiency / carry-sites (correctness already shipped on-demand). (1) Stamp typed() at the apply_eq_rules/simplify ENTRY so the redex carries its ty (compute-once) instead of value_type_term recomputing structurally per candidate. (2) The 4 WI-578 carry-sites (reify_value, close_value_head_debruijn, rewrite_ref_value, walk_value_to_resolved) must carry+sigma-refine ty so the stamp survives reconstruction. (3) Cache the sort_provides SortProvidesInfo edge-set (rebuilt per call today; now driven from the resolver loop). (4) Thread the resolver frame subst into apply_eq_rules so a constraint-store-typed UNBOUND carrier var is decidable (today &Substitution::new() -> headless -> conservative suspend; sound but incomplete -- C1). Also fold the D1/C4 double lookup_spec_op_dispatch (gate + core) into one by passing spec_sort into simp_guard_holds_core."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-292"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-01T03:05:23Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-599", author: "user", content: "GAP-1 DELIVERED + committed 52bc47a8 (NOT pushed). carrier_arg_provision_projection (sibling to WI-594's bare_spec_arg_self_projection), wired into check_constructor_iter's 2 arg-unify loops via .or_else. Spec-METHOD face only: enclosing spec == field's spec base + arg carrier == spec's carrier param -> provision = enclosing spec's own params in their RIGID body form (env.enclosing_sort_param_rigids), keyed by field binding syms; effect params wrapped as single-label rows. Reuses typaram_ref_short_name (added Var::Rigid arm). Free-op requires face DROPPED (un-rigidified Refs don't unify with rigid body vars; separate free-op type-param alias gap). Test wi599_carrier_arg_provision_test; anthill-core suite green. GAP-2 IS REAL (my earlier 'already handled' was a false positive - simpler probe lacked requires Iterable): with the REAL FiniteCollection, 'collect(src)' on an abstract FiniteCollection FIELD (src: FiniteCollection[C=SrcC,Element=Src,E=ES]) errors MissingRequiresForSpecOp 'requires FiniteCollection[Element=..,E=..] missing on enclosing sort' EVEN WITH a sort-level 'requires FiniteCollection[C=SrcC,Element=Src,E=ES]' declared (abstract_params reported = Element,E not C; the destructured field's Element/E read as abstract and the sort-requires defer doesn't match). Emit site typing.rs:~6371 (op_requires_covers / spec_warrants_abstract_check / defer-to-requirement). ALSO: thin combinator must provide FiniteCollection (collect eager) AND Iterable (FiniteCollection requires Iterable) via a lazy iterator=mapped(iterator(src),fn) -> net MORE code than finiteIterator design; user CONFIRMED FiniteCollection return (map/filter -> FiniteCollection, update wi588 dot tests). Repro scratchpad wi599_iter.anthill.", at: "2026-07-01T03:39:47Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-599", author: "user", content: "GAP 1 DELIVERED (typer, uncommitted->committing): carrier_arg_provision_projection sibling to WI-594 bare_spec_arg_self_projection, wired at both check_constructor_iter arg-unify loops via .or_else. Threads the source CARRIER + access EFFECT from a bare carrier value into a carrier-param-spec field (fmapped(c,f) with field FiniteCollection[C=SrcC,Element=Src,E=ES]). Binding source = carrier_provision_short_bindings: the SPEC-METHOD case only (op(c:C,..) ON the spec; provision=the enclosing spec's own params in their RIGID body form via enclosing_sort_param_rigids). Reuses+extends typaram_ref_short_name (added Var::Rigid: op type-params are Skolemized in the body so c:C arrives rigid) to key the carrier by short name. Effect params wrapped as single-label rows (build_canonical_effects_rows) unless already a row (is_effects_rows_term). FREE-OP requires case (the WI repro tmap) NOT supported: op.rigidify not on env, requires-entry Refs don't resolve to body rigids -> would break the sibling field; documented, returns None -> original leak error. Test: wi599_carrier_arg_provision_test (spec-method B2). anthill-core 918/0. GAP 2 (body-less collect on abstract FiniteCollection field) already resolves (3ccc19d1 prereqs) - no error. REMAINING: stdlib thin rewrite. DESIGN CONFIRMED w/user: map/filter return FiniteCollection (not FiniteStream) since a bare-FiniteCollection source field can't provide FiniteStream via lazy splitFirst. WRINKLE: providing FiniteCollection pulls in requires Iterable -> combinator must ALSO provide Iterable (iterator via lazy MappedStream.mapped(iterator(source),fn)) + collect via eager mapElems(collect(source),fn). Update wi588 dot tests FiniteStream->finite-consumer(.size()).", at: "2026-07-01T03:40:50Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-599", author: "user", content: "STDLIB THIN REWRITE ATTEMPTED then REVERTED (tree kept green on WI-588 finiteIterator design; gap-1 typer stays committed 52bc47a8). NOTE: WI-607 reference below is SUPERSEDED — remote took WI-607 for a profiling item; the T1/T2 prereq WIs are re-filed with fresh numbers on rebase. The FiniteCollection-return design (user-chosen) surfaces THREE more real gaps beyond gap-1, each its own investigation: (A) providing FiniteCollection pulls in its 'requires Iterable', so each combinator must ALSO provide Iterable -> needs a lazy 'iterator' delegating to MappedStream.mapped(iterator(source),fn); that construction does NOT thread (got MappedStream[T=??_,Src=??_,ES=??_]) because iterator(src) on the abstract FiniteCollection source doesn't ground - a bare-Stream-arg threading gap distinct from gap-1's carrier-arg case. (B) gap-2 is REAL (my minimal wi599_gap2 reproducer was too weak - it loaded because its spec had no 'requires Iterable'): 'collect(src)' on src:FiniteCollection[C=SrcC,Element=Src,E=ES] classifies as UnresolvedSpecOp -> 'missing requires FiniteCollection[Element=..,E=..] on enclosing sort' (TypeError::MissingRequiresForSpecOp, req_insertion.rs:79). Neither a SORT-level 'requires FiniteCollection[C=SrcC,Element=Src,E=ES]' NOR an OP-level one suppressed it - the check reports Element/E still abstract though the requires binds them (rep/rigidify mismatch in the requires-coverage / dispatch_spec_op_cached path, typing.rs ~6371). (C) collect body effects don't thread ('collect.effects: expected [ES,EF], got undeclared ?_'). RECOMMEND: split gap-2 (requires-coverage for abstract collect on a FiniteCollection-typed field) + the Iterable-iterator threading into their own WIs; they are prerequisites for the thin stdlib rewrite. Reverted files: finite_combinators.anthill, finite_collection.anthill. Draft rewrite saved in scratchpad if resumed.", at: "2026-07-01T03:51:14Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-599", author: "user", content: "STDLIB THIN REWRITE ATTEMPTED then REVERTED (tree kept green on WI-588 finiteIterator design; gap-1 typer stays committed 52bc47a8). The FiniteCollection-return design (user-chosen) surfaces THREE more real gaps beyond gap-1, each its own investigation: (A) providing FiniteCollection pulls in its 'requires Iterable', so each combinator must ALSO provide Iterable -> needs a lazy 'iterator' delegating to MappedStream.mapped(iterator(source),fn); that construction does NOT thread (got MappedStream[T=??_,Src=??_,ES=??_]) because iterator(src) on the abstract FiniteCollection source doesn't ground - a bare-Stream-arg threading gap distinct from gap-1's carrier-arg case. (B) gap-2 is REAL (my minimal wi599_gap2 reproducer was too weak - it loaded because its spec had no 'requires Iterable'): 'collect(src)' on src:FiniteCollection[C=SrcC,Element=Src,E=ES] classifies as UnresolvedSpecOp -> 'missing requires FiniteCollection[Element=..,E=..] on enclosing sort' (TypeError::MissingRequiresForSpecOp, req_insertion.rs:79). Neither a SORT-level 'requires FiniteCollection[C=SrcC,Element=Src,E=ES]' NOR an OP-level one suppressed it - the check reports Element/E still abstract though the requires binds them (rep/rigidify mismatch in the requires-coverage / dispatch_spec_op_cached path, typing.rs ~6371). (C) collect body effects don't thread ('collect.effects: expected [ES,EF], got undeclared ?_'). RECOMMEND: split gap-2 (requires-coverage for abstract collect on a FiniteCollection-typed field) + the Iterable-iterator threading into their own WIs; they are prerequisites for the thin stdlib rewrite. Reverted files: finite_combinators.anthill, finite_collection.anthill. Draft rewrite saved in scratchpad if resumed.", at: "2026-07-01T03:51:14Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-599", author: "user", content: "CORRECTION to prior stdlib-findings note: the anthill CLI EMBEDS the stdlib at build time (build.rs/include_str), so  does NOT reflect live stdlib edits unless you  first. My gap-2/requires iteration was CONFOUNDED — I rebuilt the CLI once (initial rewrite) then added the SORT- and OP-level  and re-ran  WITHOUT rebuilding, so those tests hit a STALE binary that never had the requires. So: gap-2 (MissingRequiresForSpecOp on abstract collect(src)) may well be FIXED by the op/sort-level  — UNTESTED against a fresh binary. Only the FIRST probe (fresh binary, no requires yet) is valid: it showed (1) requires-missing, (2) iterator body MappedStream threading ??_, (3) collect.effects undeclared ?_. To resume correctly: iterate via the TEST HARNESS (common::collect_anthill_files reads LIVE stdlib) or rebuild the CLI every edit. Draft target shape saved: scratchpad/wi599_draft_finite_combinators.anthill. Tree is GREEN (stdlib reverted to WI-588; gap-1 committed 52bc47a8, notes f8c25a81).", at: "2026-07-01T03:53:23Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-599", author: "user", content: "RELIABLE stdlib findings (fresh-binary, CLI-rebuilt each edit; supersedes the stale-binary note): gap-2 IS SOLVED by a SORT-level requires FiniteCollection[C=SrcC,Element=Src,E=ES] on FiniteMappedStream/FiniteFilteredStream (the requires-missing/MissingRequiresForSpecOp error disappears). It must be SORT-level, NOT on the overriding collect/iterator ops (an override may not strengthen the spec-op precondition, WI-347 -> 'strengthens the precondition' error). With that, TWO threading gaps remain at abstract-spec-op CALL-RESULT sites (WI-357/594 family, new location): (T1) collect body mapElems(collect(src),fn): collect(src) on src:FiniteCollection[E=ES] does not thread ES into collect's result effect -> 'collect.effects expected [ES,EF] got undeclared ?_'; (T2) iterator body mapped(iterator(src),fn): iterator(src) does not ground its T/E -> mapped[T=??_,Src=??_,ES=??_]. LEAD for T1/T2: carrier_param_receiver (typing.rs ~11101) - when the receiver is a MANIFEST application of the SPEC ITSELF (carrier_sym==spec_sort, e.g. src:FiniteCollection[C=SrcC,...]), transitive_provision_view finds nothing (a spec doesn't provide itself). Fix sketch: return an IDENTITY provision view (each spec param->itself) + transitive=true (defer to eval), so bind_spec_params_from_carrier_param reads Element/E off the receiver's OWN written type-args and threads them. (A rogue uncommitted sketch of exactly this appeared+was reverted; re-derive deliberately+test.) Draft stdlib: scratchpad/wi599_draft_finite_combinators.anthill + finite_collection map/filter return FiniteCollection[C=FiniteMappedStream[SrcC=C,...],...]=fmapped(c,f). Tree GREEN; gap-1 committed 52bc47a8.", at: "2026-07-01T03:57:55Z")
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-609", name: "finiteness")
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-608", name: "finiteness")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-599", author: "user", content: "CHECKPOINT (per user: land gap-1, file the prerequisite typer WIs, stop). GAP-1 delivered+committed 52bc47a8 (carrier_arg_provision_projection; spec-method face; wi_tests 918/0; independently re-verified). Remaining = the stdlib thin rewrite (map/filter -> FiniteCollection, drop finiteIterator), now BLOCKED on two NEW typer prereqs filed as WI-609 (T1: thread call-RESULT effect of abstract collect(src) on a spec-typed field) + WI-608 (T2: ground iterator(src) when the receiver is a manifest application of the spec itself; carrier_param_receiver identity-provision lead - a fork's first attempt REGRESSED, re-derive carefully). WI-599 now depends_on WI-609+WI-608. gap-2 (requires-coverage for abstract collect on a FiniteCollection field) is SOLVED by a SORT-level requires on the combinator (not op-level: WI-347). Draft stdlib shape saved: scratchpad/wi599_draft_finite_combinators.anthill. PROCESS NOTE: gap-1 was committed by fork review-agents that overstepped their mandate (fork inherits full tools); outcome verified sound, but avoid fork-for-review on a mid-implementation context.", at: "2026-07-01T04:40:53Z")
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-610", name: "effects")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-467", author: "user", content: "DIV-BY-ZERO HALF DELIVERED (match-failure half split to WI-610 per user scope decision). (a) int_div/int_mod/int_rem (eval/builtins.rs) now call interp.raise_division_by_zero(op) -> raise_error, so an installed Error handler catches div-by-zero; unhandled => EvalError::Raised(payload). (b) DivisionByZero sort (effects.anthill) gained op: String field (+ import String); payload is division_by_zero(op: \"Int64.div\"). (d) EvalError::DivisionByZero variant REMOVED (fully dead once routed); m3_int_division_by_zero + builtins unit test updated; new wi467 catch test asserts the handler is consulted AND the payload functor resolves to the REAL sort constructor. /code-review (2 finder agents) surfaced + FIXED 2: (F1, convention) the payload-ctor resolution was a silent unwrap_or_else(intern) fallback fabricating a same-name symbol a handler couldn't destructure — replaced with the loud require_symbol helper (bare KB => Internal 'not in scope', reuses existing helper, removes duplication); (F2, regression) removing the DivisionByZero Display arm made unhandled 10/0 print a Symbol-index debug dump via runner.rs {payload:?} — added render_payload(kb, Value) in anthill-stl runner (the 'Value printer' the code comment anticipated), threaded &KnowledgeBase into exit_code_from_main (2 call sites), so 10/0 prints error: division_by_zero(op: \"Int64.div\"); bare top-level Str still verbatim. Full workspace green (66 bins, 2395/0). NOT committed.", at: "2026-07-01T08:19:03Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-467", description: some(value: "Runtime catchability for bespoke Error-effect failures: route Error.MatchFailed and Error.DivisionByZero through the Error handler (interp.raise_error -> Raised) so an installed Error[...] handler actually catches them. TODAY both are bespoke EvalError variants (EvalError::MatchFailed / EvalError::DivisionByZero, constructed directly in eval.rs / eval/builtins.rs), NOT routed through raise_error, so the declared 'effects Error[MatchFailed]' / 'effects Error[DivisionByZero]' (WI-066) is type-level only -- a handler is never consulted. The bridge already exists and is used by the store-I/O builtins (eval/builtins.rs raise_error at persist/retract/flush/retrieve -> HandlerAction::Throw -> EvalError::Raised). From the WI-066 /code-review (Finding 1 + Finding 5). SCOPE: (a) the div builtins (int_div/int_mod/int_rem, eval/builtins.rs) already take &mut Interpreter -> call interp.raise_error(<DivisionByZero payload>) instead of returning EvalError::DivisionByZero; (b) give the DivisionByZero sort an 'op: String' field (parity with EvalError::DivisionByZero { op }) so the payload carries which op failed; (c) match-failure similarly; (d) update m3_int_division_by_zero (expects EvalError::DivisionByZero) to expect the routed/Raised form, or assert handler-catch. Note runtime behavior change: an unhandled div-by-zero becomes Raised(payload) not DivisionByZero. RELATES: proposal 047 (effect handlers), proposal 048 open-question E (static guard discharge must agree with the dynamic handler)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-066"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-01T08:24:46Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-610", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED (match-failure catchability; deferred half of WI-467). The WI's feared blocker dissolved: Value::Node(Rc<NodeOccurrence>) IS the reflect NodeOccurrence carrier, and under carrier-faithful reflect a runtime Value is already a valid Term, so match_failed(occurrence, scrutinee) is cheaply built with NO reification. IMPL: (1) factored generic Interpreter::raise_error_payload(ctor_qname, ctor_short, named) in effects.rs which resolves the ctor LOUDLY via require_symbol, plain-interns field names, canonicalizes via kb.sort_named_canonical (correct for the 2-field match_failed), builds Value::Entity, routes via raise_error; raise_division_by_zero (WI-467) reimplemented on it; new raise_match_failed(occurrence, scrutinee). (2) 3 eval sites (enter_closure, deliver LetBind, deliver MatchDispatch) now match on match_pattern and on None return Err(raise_match_failed(occ, val.clone())); occurrence = closure-param-pattern / let-pattern / match-scrutinee-occ (threaded a new scrutinee_occ field into AwaitState::MatchDispatch + start_match); scrutinee = the raw failing Value. (3) removed EvalError::MatchFailed variant+Display (fully dead, only 3 constructors) + scrutinee_diag. TESTS: m2_match_failed flipped to expect Raised(match_failed with scrutinee=3, real ctor); new wi610 catch test asserts handler consulted + occurrence rides as Value::Node + scrutinee=7. code-review (2 agents): FIXED orphaned scrutinee_diag doc comment (was glued onto classify_ctor_arg). LOW note (accepted, same as WI-467 tradeoff): a match failure now requires the effects prelude loaded (bare KB gives Internal 'match_failed not in scope'); standard CLI/test path always loads stdlib. Design consequence noted: a user Error handler can now catch (thus mask) a wi411-class stray internal MatchFailed. anthill-core suite green (14 bins, 1996/0). NOT yet committed.", at: "2026-07-01T08:56:20Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-610", description: some(value: "Runtime catchability for match-failure (deferred half of WI-467): route the 3 eval-side MatchFailed sites (enter_closure, LetBind, MatchDispatch in eval/eval.rs) through interp.raise_error so an installed Error[MatchFailed] handler actually catches a match failure, instead of the bespoke EvalError::MatchFailed. BLOCKER (why WI-467 deferred it): the MatchFailed payload sort is match_failed(occurrence: NodeOccurrence, scrutinee: Term), but the eval sites only reliably hold the scrutinee Value (rendered to a diagnostic String via scrutinee_diag) — not a NodeOccurrence for the match node nor a reified Term — so a faithful entity a handler could destructure isn't cheaply constructible there. DESIGN QUESTIONS: (a) reify the scrutinee Value -> Term (find/confirm a Value->Term helper); (b) which NodeOccurrence fills the occurrence field (the match/let/closure pattern occurrence is available at each site, but is that the intended semantics?), or should the sort field be made optional/simpler; (c) whether to keep EvalError::MatchFailed as the Display path or remove it. WHEN DONE consider factoring a shared raise_error_payload(ctor_qname, named) helper on Interpreter (WI-467 left raise_division_by_zero single-purpose; match-failure would be the 2nd caller). Update the runner render_payload (anthill-stl) if match_failed carries a NodeOccurrence/Term that needs readable rendering. Acceptance: cargo-test (a handler catches a match failure; unhandled surfaces Raised)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-467"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-01T09:10:11Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-596", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED (uncommitted). Part A: normalized Set to self-name style (dropped 'sort S = ?'; ops take s: Set / -> Set, uniform with Map/List/Stream; only set.anthill used S — zero live Set[S=...]/Set.S refs) and tagged the reducing Set/Map laws [simp] (excluded Set insert-commutativity + Map's guarded get(put(m,k2,v),k)=get(m,k):-neq sibling). GOTCHA: a bare-identifier RHS before [simp] (<=> none [simp]) parses as instantiation none[simp] (confirmed same bug in scaland's parser) -> Map's none/empty RHS rewritten to none()/empty(); none()==bare none via WI-511 canonicalization (Ref). Part B (mechanism): simp_guard_holds_core now detects a SELF-REPRESENTING sort (a param typed with the sort itself) and keys the guard on the carrier (sort-typed) arg, ignoring content type-param args (Set.member's x:T need not provide Set — the requires Eq[T] rides on the carrier's provides fact, WI-343); carrier-parameter sorts (Magma etc.) unchanged, phase-1 byte-identical. 5 flat tests (wi596_container_guard_test.rs) verify carrier-provides fires / carrier-lacks suspends / element-ignored discriminator / typer-body / stdlib-laws-are-simp-tagged, over a REAL provider (IntBag provides Bag). anthill-core 923/0; scaland 111/0. FIRING BOUNDARY (documented in test, NOT closed): stdlib laws are NESTED (member(?x, insert(?s,?x))) so the carrier arg is a spec-op subterm whose declared return is the ABSTRACT sort -> value_type_term reads abstract, sort_provides(Set,Set) non-reflexive -> freestanding redex SUSPENDS (sound). Firing needs the carrier's concrete type to flow from context. Genuine catch-22 for nested: keep spec functor (law matches, type abstract) vs override op to refine return (type concrete, functor changes so law can't match); + provider-completeness (WI-363) requires backing non-law ops. Closing it = carrier-aware refinement of a spec op's SELF return without changing the functor = separate typer machinery -> candidate follow-up WI.", at: "2026-07-01T10:56:53Z")
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-611", name: "wi502")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-596", description: some(value: "WI-292 PHASE 2 -- container-sort requires firing (Set/Map). member/insert/subset/union (Set) and get/put/contains/remove/size (Map) are body-less spec ops of a requires-bearing sort, but their carrier is an ELEMENT (or the abstract container S) that does not 'provide' the container, so the WI-292 carrier-provides check leaves them dormant. They need a DIFFERENT mechanism: the sort's type-param element provides the sort's requires spec (a Set[T] needs Eq[T]) -- the element's Eq dictionary, not the container providing itself. ALSO the reducing stdlib rules (member(?x,insert(?s,?x))<=>true, union(empty,?s)<=>?s, ...) currently carry NO [simp] tag, so the WI-292 directional-rewrite gate skips them; tagging the genuinely-reducing ones [simp] (NOT commutativity/assoc) is part of this. Design separately from spec-op dispatch."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-292"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-01T10:59:32Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-611", description: some(value: "Typer: refine a SELF-RETURNING spec op's carrier to the receiver's CONCRETE provider WITHOUT changing the op functor — the enabler for NESTED container [simp]-law firing (WI-596 follow-up). WI-596 delivered the requires-guard mechanism (simp_guard_holds_core keys on the carrier arg's type), but the stdlib Set/Map laws are NESTED — member(?x, insert(?s,?x)) <=> true, union(?s, empty()) <=> ?s, get(put(?m,?k,?v),?k) <=> some(?v) — so the carrier argument is a spec-op subterm (insert/put/empty) whose DECLARED return is the ABSTRACT sort (Set/Map). On a freestanding redex value_type_term reads that abstract Set, sort_provides(Set,Set) is non-reflexive, so the guard SUSPENDS (sound, never NAF-decides) and the nested laws stay dormant. There is a genuine CATCH-22 (documented in wi596_container_guard_test.rs): keep the spec functor so the law's inner pattern (Set.insert) matches -> the subterm types abstract -> guard fails; OR override the op on the provider to refine its return to the concrete carrier -> the subterm functor becomes ConcreteBag.insert -> the Set.insert-keyed law can no longer match. Plus provider-completeness (WI-363) requires backing the non-law ops once you override any. FIX DIRECTION: when a spec op's return type IS the enclosing sort (a self-return, insert(s: Set, x: T) -> Set) and the receiver's carrier is a concrete PROVIDER of that sort, refine the op's INFERRED return type to the provider carrier WHILE KEEPING the spec functor (carrier-aware self-return refinement; cf WI-350 carrier-aware dispatch, WI-461 bare-self-receiver identity, WI-374 member-tie). Then member(x, insert(concreteBag, x)) types its carrier arg as concreteBag, sort_provides(concreteBag, Bag) holds, and the nested law fires — no override, no completeness collision. RELATED (same typer spec-op return-grounding family, but those thread the ELEMENT/EFFECT params, not the carrier/self sort): WI-606, WI-608. ACCEPTANCE: a nested container law (member(?x, insert(?s,?x)) <=> true [simp]) on a self-representing sort FIRES over a concrete provider whose ops are NOT all overridden (revive the removed nested test in wi596_container_guard_test.rs); the flat WI-596 tests still pass; cargo-test green. Rust-only (typer)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-596"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-01T12:10:40Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-611", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED + pushed main (9b03fc14). FIX: new self_return_spec_op_result_type in typing.rs — when a BODY-LESS spec op's declared return IS its enclosing (self) sort and its self-receiver arg's already-computed type is a CONCRETE provider of that sort, the result carrier IS the receiver carrier (insert(intBag,x):IntBag, not abstract Bag), refined WITHOUT changing the spec functor so the inner law pattern still matches. Gated inside the NON-constructor branch of constructor_value_type (entity_field_types==None), so the common constructor path never pays the operation-info scan; refinement flows via value_type_term into the resolver's simp_requires_guard_holds, so kb.simplify(member(5,insert(ibag,5))) fires member_insert. Reads receiver-carrier authoritatively (result of a self-return IS the receiver), gated on carrier!=spec_sort && sort_provides(carrier,spec) so abstract receivers keep the declared self sort and non-providers are never refined. TESTS (revived nested, wi596_container_guard_test.rs): nested_container_law_fires_over_provider (fires over IntBag) + nested_container_law_does_not_fire_when_carrier_lacks_spec (suspends over Plain). GOTCHA: WI-363 completeness forces the provider to BACK the body-less insert — done via IntBag's OWN insert override (NOT a Bag default, which would make insert non-body-less and defeat the lookup_spec_op_dispatch gate); the LAW op member stays a spec law, matching acceptance's 'ops NOT all overridden'. RESIDUAL: union(?s,empty()) stays bounded — nullary empty() has no receiver to refine from (sort-self grounding, cf WI-608); handles member/insert + get/put only. code-review xhigh: no findings. anthill-core 927/0; workspace green (cli+stl+todo EXIT=0).", at: "2026-07-01T12:11:12Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-555", description: some(value: "Consolidate the reflect TERM-REIFIER pair (reify/reflect), the residual of WI-551 part (a). WI-551 shared the introspection RECORD ops (sorts/operations/constructors/fields/rules/descriptions/facts_of + leaf helpers) via anthill-stl/src/reflect/reader.rs, but left the two TERM reifiers per-realization: interpreter reify_term_to_value/reflect_value_to_term (builtins.rs, TermId<->Value::Entity tree over anthill.reflect.TermRepr/LiteralRepr ctors) vs bridge reify_view/reflect (bridge.rs, TermView<->generated TermRepr enum). These are parallel recursive walks over the same Const/Var/Ref/Fn/Bottom structure and HAVE drifted (WI-543 BigIntLiteral synced in BOTH; bridge uses index_var for Rigid/DeBruijn while interpreter handles them inline). WRINKLE found during WI-551: the two pick DIFFERENT in-band name carriers -- the interpreter builds RefRepr/FnRepr name as a Value::Term(Ref(sym)) (a Ref TERM, so its inverse reflect_value_to_term reads it back as Ref/Ident), while the bridge/generated TermRepr types name as Symbol (ReflectSymbol). A shared walk cannot just emit one TermRepr both consume: consolidating needs to either (a) route the interpreter through the generated TermRepr enum + a TermRepr->Value::Entity mapper (reconciling the name carrier, watching kb_reflect_roundtrips_a_ref_repr), or (b) extract a carrier-callback visitor over the term structure each realization parameterizes. Acceptance: one shared term-structure walk behind both reify realizations (no parallel Const/Var/Ref/Fn recursion in builtins.rs vs bridge.rs); reify roundtrip + bigint tests green; cargo-test green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-551"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-01T13:16:04Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-554", description: some(value: "Drive the interpreter reflect builtins THROUGH a single KbBridge/shared impl object (WI-551 gap (c), deferred from the gap-5 split). WI-551 part (a) already removed the parity tax by giving both realizations ONE carrier-agnostic walk (anthill-stl/src/reflect/reader.rs): the interpreter builtins and the host bridge now map the SAME reader records to their own output types (Value cons-lists vs typed structs), so per-op drift cannot happen. Gap (c) is the STRONGER goal: a single source-of-truth IMPL OBJECT. BLOCKERS: (1) KB ownership mismatch -- the interpreter owns &mut KnowledgeBase (interp.kb_mut()), while KbBridge wants Rc<RefCell<KnowledgeBase>>; unifying needs either moving the interpreter KB into Rc<RefCell> (ripples through Interpreter/Frame/builtins) or a shared-core trait both call. (2) The interpreter reflect ops are SPLIT across two crates: anthill-core/src/eval/builtins.rs (KB.facts_of, KB.execute, resolver/subst-arena ops) + anthill-stl/src/reflect/builtins.rs (introspection). (3) Output-type divergence is intrinsic (dynamically-typed Value vs static structs), so a single impl still needs a per-realization mapping layer -- the walk is already shared. Given part (a) eliminated the drift that motivated WI-551, this is lower priority. Acceptance: a design note weighing the Rc<RefCell> rework vs the shared-reader status quo, and if pursued, interpreter builtins delegating to one impl with cargo-test green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-551"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-01T13:33:19Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-527", description: some(value: "Proposal 049 build step 7 (docs). Update docs/kernel-language.md (Eq/Ordered/Numeric examples, rule-semantics section) and proposal 043 (lhs = rhs becomes lhs <=> rhs) to reflect the `=` test vs `<=>` unify split."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-523", "WI-526"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-01T14:07:24Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-528", description: some(value: "Proposal 049 build step 8 (scaland mirror). Mirror in scaland: grammar (`<=>` + `let`, greedy lex before `<=`) + loader (`<=>`/unify recognition, `let` desugar, the = to <=> migration). No typer (scaland has none). Verify with sbt test."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-522", "WI-526"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-01T14:26:08Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-528", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED 2026-07-01 (uncommitted). Grammar (<=> + let, greedy lex) was already ported in 8dfed428 (WI-522 sync); the =->><=> migration lives in the SHARED stdlib (WI-526, committed) which scaland loads via EmbeddedStdlib. The real gap was loader recognition: scaland's KnowledgeBase.isEquation matched head functor by SYMBOL IDENTITY against a bare intern(\"eq\") (latently fragile - a resolved anthill.prelude.Eq.eq has a different raw id). Switched to name-based recognition of short name \"eq\" OR \"unify\" with 2 pos args + empty body, mirroring rustland is_equation exactly; dropped the unused eqSym field. Name-based sidesteps scaland's lack of a WI-530 shadow fix (functor interns bare \"unify\" but its short name is still \"unify\"). isEquation is used only by SearchStream to exclude equations from SLD candidates; scaland has no resolver-side unify builtin by design (no typer). 3 tests: KnowledgeBaseTest isEquation (eq+unify+resolved-qualified+negative cases), LoaderTest end-to-end <=> parse+load+recognize. sbt test 189 core + 23 smtgen green. Two-agent code-review (correctness + coverage): change correct+faithful, no defects.", at: "2026-07-01T14:26:28Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-494", description: some(value: "anthill-todo help does not show preopen and promote commands"), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-01T14:45:11Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-597", description: some(value: "anthill-todo `list --tag` next-marker should skip non-claimable statuses. The `<- next` marker (cmd_list_tagged -> print_tagged_rows, is_next at main.anthill:1535-1540) currently fires on the first item that is NOT is_satisfied (i.e. not Delivered/Verified) with all deps met. But PreOpened (backlog/deferred), Draft, and the terminal off-ramps (Rejected/ProposalRejected/Stale) are all 'not satisfied' too, so a parked PreOpened ticket wrongly grabs `<- next` ahead of a genuinely-actionable Open ticket. Symptom: wi502 tag marks the deferred WI-574 (Step 6 generative labeling, PreOpened) as `<- next` instead of the live Open WI-573. Blocked items are ALREADY excluded (is_next requires unmet deps = nil). Fix: gate is_next on a positive claimable predicate (status == Open; decide whether Claimed should also qualify given the loop resumes Claimed separately) instead of the negative is_satisfied test; introduce is_claimable_next(status) helper. Mirror in native run_list_tagged if any logic survives the WI-009 cutover. Acceptance: cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh, plus a regression test asserting `<- next` skips a deps-met PreOpened row and lands on the next Open row."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-01T14:56:23Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-285", author: "user", content: "Already delivered — tracking-only status flip. Implemented + merged in commit 1e9c86d9 (2026-05-22): visit_type's If/ListLit/SetLit/TupleLit arms are native TypeBuildFrame::{IfExpr,ListLit,SetLit,TupleLit} frames (push child Visits + a Build frame that drains their results), with NO re-entry into type_check_node — the old check_if_expr/check_list_literal/check_set_literal/check_tuple_literal helpers are gone (referenced only as 'former'). Behavior-preserving: drain order, then-branch-as-if-type join (WI-287), element_hint empty-collection fallback, _1/_2 tuple naming, effects-merge order, error early-return, outer env all carried. Regression test tests/include/wi285_unrec_test.rs (2 tests) passes on current HEAD: deeply_nested_else_if_types_without_host_stack_overflow (50k-deep else-if chain, no host-stack overflow) + collection_literal_frames_assemble_types. Cited as settled history by WI-040/WI-560/WI-578. Status was never flipped from Open; correcting now.", at: "2026-07-01T15:13:14Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-285", description: some(value: "Unrec the typer's remaining recursive-helper expression arms. visit_type (kb/typing.rs ~1160-1182) handles If/ListLit/SetLit/TupleLit by calling check_if_expr/check_list_literal/check_set_literal/check_tuple_literal, which re-enter type_check_node on children — the last recursive arms; Let/Match/Lambda/Apply/Constructor are already work-stack Build frames. Convert these four to native Visit/Build frames so the whole typer is one iterative walk. Motivation: else-if chains nest in the else branch, so a deep if/else-if recurses one host stack frame per nesting level — the residual overflow class the earlier unrec-ing removed for the other forms (the Drop walk and main dispatch were made iterative to survive the 624-line typing_pass_spec.anthill). Collection literals nest by breadth (flat element lists), lower risk, folded in for uniformity. With WI-284's Stamp work-frame this is purely a stack-safety/uniformity refactor — stamping already works for these arms via per-call type_check_node re-entry, so no behavior change is required, only the host-stack shape. Acceptance: If/ListLit/SetLit/TupleLit are handled by work-stack Build frames (visit_type no longer re-enters type_check_node for them); a deeply-nested else-if chain types without host-stack overflow; cargo-test green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-284"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-01T15:13:18Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-601", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED (committed b51ea29, code before review = clobber-safe). receiver_carrier now classifies Abstract when carrier_is_abstract_spec(base) (base != op spec, no own constructors, provided) -> the abstract self-receiver defer arm handles it, exactly the WI-598 carrier-param escape. Full workspace suite green (2393). /code-review (4 agents): SOUND, no bugs. One agent empirically verified by reverting the guard in a worktree (test FAILS pre-fix with the 3 MissingRequiresForSpecOp errors incl Stream.splitFirst.requires, ok post-fix returning 2). Soundness cleared: deferral hands to eval value-directed dispatch on the runtime concrete provider; load-time backing invariant (typing.rs ~9403 concrete-only) + Pass-2 op-table inheritance guarantee the lookup succeeds for direct providers (FiniteMappedStream). Return/effects threaded by projection elimination independent of receiver_carrier, so skipping the Concrete-only bind/effect-close leaks nothing (test's effects fs.E matches structurally). LATENT (NOT this WI, no fix now): (1) self-receiver path still lacks the carrier-param transitive-flag leg -> a concrete carrier providing the op spec only TRANSITIVELY stays Concrete; latent, no stdlib self-receiver op is transitive-only. (2) the abstract defer arm skips the effect-close (pre-existing property of ALL abstract dispatch, not new): a future body-less self-receiver op with a BARE 'effects E' (not s.E projection) on an abstract-spec receiver could surface undeclared ?_; no such stdlib op exists.", at: "2026-07-01T15:44:10Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-601", description: some(value: "Typer ALTITUDE (follow-up to WI-598): unify the abstract-spec-carrier deferral across BOTH dispatch shapes. WI-598 taught the CARRIER-PARAM escape (check_apply_iter ~6271) to defer when the receiver's carrier sort is itself an abstract spec (carrier_is_abstract_spec = no own constructors AND something provides it). But the SELF-RECEIVER path's receiver_carrier (~typing.rs:10454) still classifies Abstract by the NARROWER base==spec_sort test, so a value whose static type is an abstract-spec sort DIFFERENT from the op's spec is returned Concrete(...). Latent gap: a body-less Stream self-receiver op NOT overridden by FiniteStream (e.g. consuming a FiniteStream-typed value through a bare Stream op) concrete-dispatches into FiniteStream provides Stream -> Stream requires EffectsRuntime[E], unsatisfiable at the abstract access row -> the SAME spurious DispatchNoMatch WI-598 fixes. Pre-existing (untested today; finite consumption routes through the carrier-param FiniteCollection.collect). RIGHT DEPTH: have receiver_carrier (or a shared helper both paths call) return Abstract when carrier_is_abstract_spec(base) — funnel both shapes through one notion. Regression-screen: concrete carriers (List/Map, which have constructors) stay Concrete; only abstract-interface carriers newly defer (an improvement, the values are concrete providers at runtime). Needs a self-receiver-on-abstract-spec test + full suite. ACCEPTANCE: a body-less Stream self-receiver op on a FiniteStream-typed value typechecks+evals (defers to eval value-directed dispatch); cargo-test green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-598"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-01T15:44:25Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-604", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED (committed 2f9e8d9, code before review = clobber-safe). ROOT: dispatched_impl_effects (typing.rs ~11447) surfaced a dispatched override's effect but only BLANKET-RE-KEYED it (substitute_ref_syms_value) + walked - never DELTA-REDUCED a projection. Iterable.isEmpty/find are DEFAULTED spec ops (have bodies) -> lookup_spec_op_dispatch=None -> the WI-444 carrier-override path (typing.rs ~5941) dispatches Iterable.isEmpty->Stream.isEmpty (Stream defines its own isEmpty), whose effect is the self-receiver projection 's.E'; that s.E never grounded to the receiver's written E={} -> spurious 'undeclared effect: s.E'. The op's-own-effect loop (op_has_projection path) already reduces via eliminate_type_projections; the dispatched-impl path was the blind spot (wi475 header notes stdlib Stream/isEmpty was out of scope). The ticket's 'size grounded but isEmpty leaks' ASYMMETRY was a RED HERRING: size/Stream.count had the identical 'effects s.E' shape + identical latent leak (confirmed via git: both 'operation X(c:C) effects E = Stream.Y(iterator(c))'). FIX: dispatched_impl_effects now threads pos_results/named_results, builds impl-param->arg-type map, and eliminate_type_projections-reduces a projection-bearing impl effect against the receiver's concrete type (s.E off Stream[E={}] -> {}). Guard '!effect_is_unresolved_var' uses the reduction ONLY when it grounds; abstract receiver falls back to the blanket re-key (pre-WI-604 behavior, unchanged). Applies at all 3 dispatched-impl sites (WI-444/WI-453/Unique-arm; ground Modify labels unaffected - value_contains_projection=false). Test wi604_iterable_consumer_effect_grounding_test: isEmpty AND find over a bare lazy MappedStream typecheck PURE (load IS the assertion) + eval. Full workspace green 2405/0. /code-review (3 agents; 2 died on API error mid-response after ~40 tool-uses each, no findings; 1 CLEAN): soundness confirmed (reads receiver's ACTUAL written E; effectful map's EffP propagates via Stream[Dst,{E,EffP}] and surfaces - no under-report), test non-vacuous VERIFIED (pre-fix HEAD~1 in a worktree fails load w/ undeclared s.E); Unique-arm cross-site self-verified. OUT OF SCOPE (pre-existing, verified IDENTICAL before this change): abstract-E receiver 'f(s:Stream[T=Int64]) effects s.E = Iterable.isEmpty(s)' leaks ?_ (abstract-E threading, WI-608/609 family). wi492 lazy_map_iterator_count left AS-IS (covers Iterable.iterator transitive resolution - distinct from this test's direct isEmpty/find; reverting would lose that coverage + duplicate wi604).", at: "2026-07-01T19:00:59Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-604", description: some(value: "Typer: a qualified Iterable consumer (isEmpty / find) over a bare lazy Iterable.map(...) result leaks the produced stream's access row as 's.E' instead of grounding it to '{}'. Repro: 'operation f(xs: List[T = Int64]) -> Bool = Iterable.isEmpty(Iterable.map(xs, inc))' fails to load with 'type mismatch in f.effects: expected declared: [], got undeclared effect: s.E' (find leaks the same way). A 'let m : Stream[T = Int64, E = {}] = Iterable.map(xs, inc)' annotation does NOT fix it (the leak just becomes 'm.E') — so the bare-Stream-typed value's written/inferred E={} is not substituted into the consumer's effect row; the consumer re-projects receiver.E. ASYMMETRY (unexplained, the crux): the now-removed Iterable.size grounded the same expression to '{}' even though its body 'Stream.count(iterator(c))' is structurally identical to isEmpty's 'Stream.isEmpty(iterator(c))'. Surfaced by WI-589 (finiteness Phase C) when repurposing wi492 away from the removed Iterable.size; worked around there by counting via transitive Iterable.iterator(...) + bounded takeN (sound but indirect). ACCEPTANCE: 'Iterable.isEmpty(Iterable.map([1,2,3,4], inc))' on a pure List typechecks PURE (access row grounds to {}), with a regression test pinning isEmpty AND find over a bare lazy MappedStream; then wi492's lazy_map_iterator_count can revert to the direct Iterable.isEmpty form."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-01T19:01:00Z"))
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-612", name: "finiteness")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-600", description: some(value: "Typer: normalize type-parameter representation to ONE canonical form (the sort's Var::Global param var) so carrier-grounding matches by IDENTITY, retiring the short-name workaround. ROOT CAUSE: the same logical type-param appears in ≥4 term shapes across the three sources carrier-grounding joins — the loader interns a bare param name as a nullary Fn{param} (make_name_term) in a provider spec-view (provides FiniteCollection[Element=Pair[A=K,B=V]]), as a Ref/Ident elsewhere, and as the sort's canonical Var::Global param var — under different Symbol/TermId intern copies, so Symbol/TermId identity matching MISSES. The current workaround keys on SHORT NAME (typaram_ref_short_name collapses Ref/Ident/nullary-Fn/Var→short name; parameterized_short_bindings keys recv bindings by short name; bind_spec_params_from_carrier/_param and the WI-593 substitute_carrier_params compare carrier↔spec params by short name). Short-name matching is non-hygienic (two unrelated sorts' T collide in principle; safe today only because the compare is scoped per spec↔carrier triad) and papers over names that should have been resolved to their canonical symbol in the scan/resolve pipeline. FIX: canonicalize every type-param occurrence to the sort's Var::Global (or a single chosen rep) at load/resolve time (where make_name_term currently emits nullary Fn{param}), then replace the short-name helpers with identity-keyed lookups; delete/inline typaram_ref_short_name. Surfaced by the WI-357/WI-424/WI-593 carrier-grounding cluster. ACCEPTANCE: type-param matching in bind_spec_params_from_carrier(_param)/substitute_carrier_params keys on canonical identity (no short_name compares for params); cargo-test green (incl. wi357/wi424/wi495/wi585/wi593 carrier-grounding tests)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "opus-wi600", at: "2026-07-01T19:13:49Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-534", author: "claude", content: "Already delivered — tracking-only flip; absorbed by WI-540 (generated reflect bridge, committed f08977d) and refined by WI-555 (1a641231, shared reify/reflect walk). Verified in current anthill-stl/src/reflect/bridge.rs: (1) KbBridge::execute returns Box<dyn Stream<Solution, Error>> (line ~726), matching reflect.anthill's Stream[T=Solution] — no longer the bare Stream<SubstBridge> that dropped residual. (2) SearchStreamAdapter::make_solution (~480) is verdict-honest: residual.is_empty() -> Solution::Definite{subst}, else Solution::Undecided{subst, residual}. (3) The residual is CARRIER-FAITHFUL — rterm(v: Value) = ReflectTerm::new(v) wraps the Value directly (no reify to TermId), so a Value::Node keeps occurrence/span; this is precisely what the WI-535 TermView/Value bridge migration unblocked. (4) All 5 named bridge tests exist asserting the Solution shape: execute_sort_query_finds_operations, execute_sort_query_nonexistent_is_empty, execute_empty_query, execute_pattern_query, execute_limited_query (the latter 3 named slightly differently than this ticket predicted). Full workspace suite green (66 blocks, 0 failures) on current HEAD incl. anthill-stl. The stale-doc artifacts this ticket listed (generated/reflect.rs:33, proposals 026.1/011, rust-forward-mapping.md) were explicitly deferred to WI-527 (docs), out of WI-534's code scope. No new code.", at: "2026-07-01T19:44:40Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-534", description: some(value: "WI-531 follow-up: migrate the host-side reflect bridge (anthill-stl/src/reflect/bridge.rs) to stream Solution, mirroring the interpreter path. KbBridge::execute + SearchStreamAdapter (bridge.rs:358-374, ~711) is a SECOND realization of reflect.KB.execute — the host-language/codegen trait (reflect/mod.rs:50) that generated Rust implements, distinct from the interpreter's kb_execute. It still yields the bare substitution: SubstBridge::from_core(solution.subst) at bridge.rs:365/401/422 SILENTLY DROPS solution.residual, and its element type is Stream<SubstBridge, Error> (= Stream[Substitution]), now inconsistent with the spec's Stream[T = Solution] (reflect.anthill, WI-531). This is the same residual-drop + verdict-dishonesty WI-531 fixed on the interpreter side, still live on the host/codegen path (self-consistent within anthill-stl, so no test break today — its 5 tests at bridge.rs:837-909 pin the old Vec<SubstBridge> shape). NEEDS: a SolutionBridge (definite|undecided carrying SubstBridge + residual) or equivalent, SearchStreamAdapter yielding it, and updating those 5 bridge tests (execute_sort_query_finds_operations/_nonexistent_is_empty/_empty_query/_pattern_query/_limited_query). Found by /code-review cross-file tracer during WI-531. Rust-only (anthill-stl). RELATED stale doc artifacts (fold into WI-527 docs, NOT here): generated/reflect.rs:33, docs/proposals/026.1, docs/proposals/011, docs/rust-forward-mapping.md still show Stream[Substitution]."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-531", "WI-535"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-01T19:44:50Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-608", description: some(value: "Typer: ground the element/effect of an abstract spec-op called on a receiver that is a MANIFEST application of the SPEC ITSELF. Reproducer: a thin finite combinator whose Iterable iterator body does mapped(iterator(src), fn) where src: FiniteCollection[C=SrcC, Element=Src, E=ES]. iterator(src) does NOT ground its T/E, so mapped(...) leaks MappedStream[T=??_, Src=??_, ES=??_]. ROOT (lead): carrier_param_receiver (typing.rs ~11101) calls transitive_provision_view, which finds nothing when carrier_sym == spec_sort (a spec doesn't provide itself). CANDIDATE FIX: when the receiver's carrier IS the spec, return an IDENTITY provision view (each spec param -> itself) + transitive=true (defer to eval) so bind_spec_params_from_carrier_param reads Element/E off the receiver's OWN written type-args. CAUTION: a first attempt at exactly this REGRESSED the stdlib (a92a70f6 fork) and was reverted -> re-derive deliberately, verify no regression via the test harness (which reads live stdlib). Prereq for the WI-599 thin stdlib rewrite (the combinator must provide Iterable since FiniteCollection requires it). ACCEPTANCE: iterator(src)/mapped(iterator(src),fn) grounds; focused typer test + full suite green (no regression)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: []), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-01T20:25:12Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-608", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED (code dfba147, rebased onto WI-600). ROOT (confirmed by instrumentation, NOT the ticket's 'carrier==spec' guess): iterator(src) on src:FiniteCollection dispatches Iterable.iterator with spec_sort=Iterable, carrier_sym=FiniteCollection — related by REQUIRES (FiniteCollection requires Iterable), not provides. transitive_provision_view is provides-only so returned None → no bind, no deferral → Stream[Element,E] leaked ??_ → mapped(...) cascaded MappedStream[T=??_,Src=??_,ES=??_]. FIX: abstract_spec_required_view builds the provision view from the requires clause (direct_requires + unwrap_spec_view; SAME SortView shape as a provides fact), wired into carrier_param_receiver via .or_else gated on carrier_is_abstract_spec, marked transitive=true → bind_spec_params_from_carrier_param threads Element/E off recv's own type-args and the carrier_is_abstract_spec dispatch gate (6273) defers to eval. The carrier-param twin of WI-598/601. /code-review (1 independent reviewer, not fork per prior process note) found a REAL latent gap: the WI-444 override extraction (typing.rs:5936) lacked the abstract-spec guard, so my broader Some would mis-pin a qualified Iterable.map/filter on a FiniteCollection to FiniteCollection's OWN map/filter — added the carrier_is_abstract_spec guard mirroring 6273 (concrete carriers keep their pin). REPRO PATTERN LESSON: the sort-member combinator must thread element/effect via EXPLICIT op type params bound from the receiver (the FilteredStream.splitFirst pattern), NOT implicit sort rigids (the match destructure leaves field-type params unbound) — my first repro wrongly used implicit rigids. Full suite 2408/0. WI-609 (collect, the REFLEXIVE carrier==spec case + result-effect threading) is a DISTINCT ticket, correctly UNTOUCHED by this fix (abstract_spec_required_view matches requires entries only, so collect on FiniteCollection still returns None).", at: "2026-07-01T20:25:32Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-609", description: some(value: "Typer (WI-357/594 effect-threading family, CALL-RESULT location): thread the RESULT effect of an abstract spec-op called on a spec-typed field receiver. Reproducer: a thin finite combinator whose collect body does mapElems(collect(src), fn) where src: FiniteCollection[C=SrcC, Element=Src, E=ES] (a destructured carrier field). collect(src) does NOT thread ES into collect's result effect -> 'collect.effects: expected [ES,EF], got undeclared ?_'. The receiver's written type-arg E=ES must flow into the abstract spec-op's declared 'effects E' at the call result. Distinct from gap-1 (WI-599 carrier_arg_provision_projection, which is a CONSTRUCTOR-ARG threading). Prereq for the WI-599 thin stdlib rewrite. ACCEPTANCE: the collect body over an abstract FiniteCollection field type-checks with the source effect threaded; a focused typer test + cargo-test green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: []), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-01T23:24:27Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-609", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED (code 69f0280). ROOT: collect(src) on src:FiniteCollection dispatches FiniteCollection.collect -> spec_sort==carrier_sym==FiniteCollection (REFLEXIVE; a spec doesn't provide itself). transitive_provision_view (provides-only) + WI-608 abstract_spec_required_view (requires-entries-only) both returned None -> collect's declared 'effects E' never grounded to recv's E=ES -> 'collect.effects: expected [ES], got undeclared ?_'. FIX: the reflexive case needs NO provider view — the receiver's own written type-args ARE the spec's params (same canonical VarIds, since WI-600's parameterized_vid_bindings keys by carrier_sym==spec_sort). Added (1) a reflexive branch in bind_spec_params_from_carrier_param that binds each recv_binding directly (skipping carrier_pvid==C, which binds by arg unification), and (2) a reflexive branch in carrier_param_receiver's .or_else returning an EMPTY view + transitive=true to engage that path + the carrier_is_abstract_spec deferral gate. Sibling of WI-608's requires-view. Grounding Element/E ALSO discharges the WI-599 gap-2 requires-coverage error (the dispatch goal is no longer abstract-uncovered) — so both the free-op (op-level requires) and sort-member (sort-level requires) shapes load. Gated on carrier_is_abstract_spec -> concrete self-collect (List.collect on List) unaffected. VERIFICATION: 5 correctness concerns cleared (carrier_pvid==recv C vid via resolve_sort_alias; parameterized_vid_bindings filters to ground Value::Term; empty-view consumers all safe; transitive_provision_view never yields carrier==spec so carrier==spec<=>empty-view). Full suite 2410/0. /code-review reviewer HUNG on infra (2h+, killed) — relied on manual+empirical verification instead. Rebased context: builds on WI-600 canonical-VarId matching + WI-608. UNBLOCKS the Claimed WI-599 thin stdlib rewrite (last typer prereq). Repro: wi609_collect_effect_over_finite_collection_test.", at: "2026-07-01T23:24:27Z")
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-614", name: "finiteness")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-599", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED (stdlib thin rewrite; typer prereqs WI-608/609 already landed). map/filter = fmapped/ffiltered(c,f) over the BARE carrier (gap-1), return FiniteCollection[C=combinator,E={E,EffP}] (abstract return REQUIRED — concrete return breaks effect threading). Combinators provide FiniteCollection (eager collect=mapElems/filterElems(collect(source),..), WI-609) + Iterable (lazy iterator=mapped/filtered(iterator(source),..), WI-608); sort-level requires FiniteCollection (gap-2, not op-level per WI-347). finiteIterator removed from FiniteCollection/List/Map/FiniteStream/MutableStack. Added List.mapElems/filterElems (+internal reverse/reverseOnto/mapElemsOnto workers): mapElems needs a value-arg-pinned tail accumulator (a plain recursion or inline lambda leaves the callback effect param unconstrained; lambda case = WI-605). CASCADE: .find()/.isEmpty() (Iterable-only) on the FiniteCollection map/filter result don't dot-dispatch (dot-dispatch doesn't traverse requires Iterable) -> filed WI-614 (NOT blocking, user-approved); wi278/wi492 collect-first as workaround. /code-review (3 agents): no bugs/no breaks (scaland ParserIntegrationTest 30/30 confirmed); applied cleanups. Full workspace suite 2410/0.", at: "2026-07-02T09:26:12Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-599", description: some(value: "Typer: generalize WI-594 bare-receiver access-EFFECT threading to a FiniteCollection-TYPED carrier field (today WI-594 threads the bare receiver's effect into a Stream/FiniteStream field but NOT a FiniteCollection field) AND ground a body-less collect() called on an abstract FiniteCollection field (the open WI-593 class). Together these unblock the THIN finite map/filter that the user preferred for WI-588: FiniteCollection.map(c,f) = fmapped(c, f) over the BARE receiver, with the carrier source field typed FiniteCollection, dropping the finiteIterator indirection that WI-588 shipped instead. Reproducer: revert finite_collection.anthill map/filter to fmapped(c,f) + finite_combinators source field to FiniteCollection[C=SrcC,Element=Src,E=ES] and it fails to load with 'map.return ES=??_' (effect not threaded) + 'FiniteCollection.collect.requires missing on enclosing sort'. ACCEPTANCE: the thin design loads + the wi588 tests pass with finiteIterator removed from FiniteCollection/List/Map/FiniteStream; cargo-test green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-588", "WI-609", "WI-608"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-02T09:26:12Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-300", author: "user", content: "DESIGN (2026-07-01 session, with user): rule-body requires(X) SEMANTICS settled = spec X operations become usable inside the rule, dispatched through X dictionary (the dictionary wrapper = Value::Requirement / the ConstructRequirement{impl_functor, requirements} occurrence = functor + sub-requirement dicts). When the body calls e.g. eq(?x,?y), that call dispatches through the wrapper. This UNIFIES the guard reading and the dictionary reading: the guard IS the dictionary-resolution, and the guard succeeding YIELDS the dict the body ops dispatch through -- one mechanism, not two. Needs BOTH substrates: WI-292 (delivered) = the resolve-or-suspend half (SLD over provides facts, suspend if type under-determined, never NAF-decide); WI-577 = the wrapper-as-first-class-typed-value half (now a hard dep, added this session). KEY ASYMMETRY vs op bodies (the design center): a RULE has no caller to supply a dictionary, so the wrapper is CONSTRUCTED ON DEMAND -- read the ground type bound during resolution, look up the provides fact (Eq[Int]), build the dict; SUSPEND as residual if not ground. Of the three op-body weave forms only ConstructRequirement transfers to rules; VarRef and RequirementAtSort (slot projection from a passed-in param) have NO source in a free rule -- and this holds even for sort-scoped rules (Set/Map): the sort requires is checked at fire time against the carried type, not threaded as a runtime param. IMPLEMENTATION shape = distinguished recognition + type-directed weave TOGETHER (not either/or): convert_rule_body distinguishes requires(X) as a WHOLE-RULE clause (brings X into scope for the entire body, like the op requires clause, not a positional goal), then runs the existing op-body dispatch pipeline over rule body_nodes with X in scope. OPEN QUESTION for whoever picks this up: when requires(X) cannot resolve (T not ground at fire time) -- SUSPEND (carry as residual, WI-519 style, the sound choice, matches everything else) vs FAIL. Leaning suspend; confirm user intent. STALE-PREMISE NOTE from tracing the code: the ticket cites is_requires_resolved/mark_requires_resolved (mod.rs, now ~671) as scaffolding to build on -- those actually track SortRequiresInfo FACT finalization, NOT per-rule requirement-goal resolution, so there is no existing rule-body requirement scaffolding to extend; line refs in the ticket body (req_insertion.rs:41, convert.rs:1647, mod.rs:456) have drifted.", at: "2026-07-01T20:14:01Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-577", author: "user", content: "DEP NOTE (2026-07-01): WI-300 (rule-body requirement goals) now depends on this, added this session. Reason: under the settled WI-300 semantics, a rule-body requires(X) weaves + dispatches through the requirement dictionary (Value::Requirement) and must reason about it, so WI-300 needs Value::Requirement DE-OPAQUED -- today it is ViewHead::Opaque (term_view.rs:1081) and unlowerable (execute.rs:331 UnsupportedVariant Requirement). WI-577 structural view + two-type split (reflect-type = it IS a Dictionary; denoted-type = the spec instance it witnesses, e.g. Eq[T]) is exactly what the rule-body weave consumes. WHAT WI-300 ACTUALLY NEEDS is the DESIGN ANSWER (de-opaque Value::Requirement into a structural view + the two-type split), more than the full reflect.Dictionary/reflect.OpRef sorts + bridge plumbing -- that plumbing can trail. Note the dictionary shape WI-577 lifts (functor + sub-requirements) is already exactly the ConstructRequirement{impl_functor, requirements} occurrence in node_occurrence.rs.", at: "2026-07-01T20:14:18Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-577", author: "user", content: "DESIGN DOC: docs/design/requirement-dictionaries.md (2026-07-01) -- covers WI-577 (substrate) + WI-300 (consumer) in one doc; see esp. section 2 (first-class dictionaries in reflect: how visible from anthill, the Substitution builtin wiring, accessor-ops vs structural-match faces, OpRef, two-type split) and section 4 (phasing, WI-577 -> WI-300).", at: "2026-07-01T20:28:41Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-300", author: "user", content: "DESIGN DOC: docs/design/requirement-dictionaries.md (2026-07-01) -- covers WI-300 (consumer) + WI-577 (substrate) in one doc; see esp. section 3 (rule-body requirement goals: settled semantics, the construct-on-demand asymmetry vs op bodies, implementation shape, suspend-vs-fail open question, worked example, relationship to WI-292) and section 4 (phasing).", at: "2026-07-01T20:29:07Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-300", author: "user", content: "DESIGN REFINED (2026-07-02 session; SUPERSEDES this ticket earlier construct-on-demand framing; full model in docs/design/requirement-dictionaries.md sec 3-4). requires(X) desugars to requirement KERNEL PRIMITIVES. requires T == (exists x. x = findDictionary[T]): findDictionary = provides-resolution at the current binding (WI-292 provides query) + construct_requirement + SUSPEND if under-determined. The resolved dictionary binds into a NEW requirement slot in the resolver Gamma env (Env, WI-537 / WI-328 constraint store) -- the SLD analog of eval frame.requirements. Body spec-ops dispatch by READING the Gamma slot; at op invocation the SLD->eval bridge copies the dict into the ops fresh frame.requirements, which ALREADY EXISTS (no new eval mechanism). CORRECTS the earlier (only ConstructRequirement transfers; VarRef has no source) claim: the Gamma slot IS the var_ref source, populated by the requires goal instead of a caller -- so the op-body weave transfers IN FULL. SETTLES the earlier suspend-vs-fail open question: SUSPEND, by construction (findDictionary is a resolver goal over provides; under-determined = undecided/residual per WI-519/WI-067; never NAF-decide). findDictionary PRODUCES a WI-577 Dictionary value -- clean seam: WI-577 = value+accessors, WI-300 = findDictionary-into-Gamma + dispatch. REMAINING micro-decisions (doc sec 3.4): (a) Gamma slot keyed by spec-identity(name) vs positional index -- names preferred if rule bodies build escaping closures (WI-237 lesson), else index ok; (b) whole-rule vs positional requires -- hoist to a rule-level populator or require it to precede covered ops.", at: "2026-07-02T04:54:01Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-613", author: "user", content: "FIX ANALYSIS (design discussion). Root cause is ONE line: dispatch_values_match (typing.rs:11748) returns true for ANY type-param candidate (\"universally-quantified candidate matches any per-call value\"). Minimal correct fix: a type-param candidate matches IFF the per-call value is the SAME param (by identity), not unconditionally -- preserves the legitimate open-T defer (a param matches ITSELF, single-param case) while distinguishing A from B. The three keying options weighed: (a) POSITIONS -- moves attribution to elaboration (fine) but makes the RUNTIME env positional again = the WI-237 escaping-closure hazard; rejected, keep the runtime env NAMED. (b) MANGLED NAME -- works, but a stringly-typed stand-in for the type identity. (c) TermId-of-type -- cleanest (RequiresEntry.spec is ALREADY a hash-consed TermId; O(1) equality distinguishes Eq[A]/Eq[B]) BUT types are NOT universally hash-consed (repo representation note): a readers freshly-built Eq[B] may ride as a transient Value::Node, so raw TermId== can MISS. ROBUST form of (c): compare spec-instances by STRUCTURAL type identity -- TermId== fast-path when both interned, structural compare (views_structurally_equal / the DiscrimKey the discrim tree already uses) otherwise; this subsumes (b) (mangled string = weaker structural key). RECOMMENDATION: (c)-structural -- fix the param-identity comparison in dispatch_values_match/entry_matches_subst, keep the runtime requirement env NAMED (no positional regression). Care: do not break the WI-414 concrete-vs-open guard (typing.rs:8113) or the legitimate single-open-T defer.", at: "2026-07-02T05:06:33Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-613", author: "user", content: "ADDENDUM (structural value hash option). A structural value hash is the FAST-PATH half only -- \"if it is unique\" cannot be assumed: hashes collide (birthday bound), and a bare hash-as-key trusting uniqueness reintroduces the misroute PROBABILISTICALLY (two distinct spec-instances colliding -> wrong dict = the same bug, nondeterministic). Safe form = HASH + STRUCTURAL EQUALITY (hash to bucket O(1), structural compare to confirm on hit). That is exactly what the existing mechanisms already are: hash-consing (structural Hash+Eq -> unique TermId = option c), the discrim trees DiscrimKey (carrier-agnostic structural key, works for TermId AND Value::Node), and any HashMap<structural-key>. So all options collapse to ONE: structural identity, hash for speed + structural equality for correctness. Reuse DiscrimKey / Term Hash + views_structurally_equal; NEVER trust the hash alone. Recommendation unchanged ((c)-structural); hard rule: hash buckets, structure decides.", at: "2026-07-02T05:07:23Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-613", description: some(value: "BUG: defer-to-requirement attribution conflates two same-spec requirements over different type params (requires Eq[A], requires Eq[B]). find_requires_slot (typing.rs:7998) attributes a body spec-op call to a requires-chain slot via .position(entry_matches_subst); entry_matches_subst -> dispatch_values_match (typing.rs:11740) returns TRUE UNCONDITIONALLY at line 11748 when the CANDIDATE (the requires entrys bound param) is a type-param value (the \"universally-quantified candidate matches any per-call value\" open-T defer rule). Correct for a SINGLE open param (requires Eq[T]); but with TWO same-spec requires over DISTINCT params, BOTH entries wildcard-match ANY call, so .position always returns the FIRST (Eq[A], index 0). Consequence: a body call eq(y:B) is attributed to Eq[A]s slot -> req_name_for_chain_index(0) -> reads Eq[A]s __req dict -> dispatches through the WRONG dictionary at runtime. NB name synthesis is NOT the bug: synth_req_names (typing.rs:20228) already disambiguates by full spec TermId (__req_eq_idA vs __req_eq_idB); the bug is ATTRIBUTION picking the wrong index, so the correct name is never selected for the second param. REACHABILITY: latent -- needs a sort/op with two requires of the SAME spec over DIFFERENT type params, both dispatched in the body (e.g. zip[A,B] requires Eq[A], requires Eq[B]); likely absent from current stdlib so untriggered, but a soundness hole. FIX DIRECTION (design discussion): mangle the STATIC type param INTO the requirement name at elaboration, used identically by writer (requires slot) and reader (body op), so attribution is a by-name match on the param-carrying mangled name -- eliminating the .position/dispatch_values_match wildcard step. Consistent with wire-by-type-param-at-elaboration (NOT a runtime ground-type hash). Blocks WI-300 (rule-body requirement goals reuse this attribution + requirement-frame machinery). Acceptance: cargo-test green + a regression test with two same-spec/different-param requires both dispatched."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude-opus", at: "2026-07-02T07:21:40Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-577", author: "user", content: "SCOPE REFRAMED (2026-07-02 session, doc-aligned): description above rewritten to match docs/design/requirement-dictionaries.md. Key shifts from the original: (1) Dictionary[S]/OpRef[A] are RUNTIME sorts in anthill.realization.runtime (runtime dual of Obligation/Implementation), NOT reflect objects -- reflect only hosts the separate Term-construction layer. (2) Value::Requirement STAYS ViewHead::Opaque -- NO de-opaquing (supersedes the DEP NOTE). (3) two-type split via TYPE PARAMETER (Dictionary[S]/OpRef[A]), tracked statically -- denoted type is the param, not read off the value; dissolves the store-the-spec problem (functor is a multi-spec carrier). (4) WI-577 = the two runtime view sorts + builtins + carrier mapping (the ready unit); reflect_expr + the genRef/genApply/genConstruct/findDictionary constructors are a SEPARATE follow-on WI gated on a metaprogramming consumer.", at: "2026-07-02T12:27:47Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-577", description: some(value: "First-class Dictionaries + OpRefs -- the RUNTIME dispatch values Value::Requirement (the requirement DICTIONARY: functor + sub-requirements) and Value::OpRef (op symbol + optional dispatch dict) given a first-class anthill face. These are RUNTIME sorts in anthill.realization.runtime -- Dictionary[S] / OpRef[A], the runtime dual of realization Obligation/Implementation (a dictionary discharges a requires by carrying its resolved Implementation) -- NOT reflect objects. SCOPE (the ready unit): the two runtime VIEW sorts + native builtins over the RequirementArena + the carrier-type mapping Value::Requirement -> anthill.realization.runtime.Dictionary. Value::Requirement STAYS ViewHead::Opaque -- NO de-opaquing (builtins match the handle in Rust and read the arena; de-opaquing was only for a dropped structural-match face; SUPERSEDES the earlier DEP-NOTE that WI-300 needs Value::Requirement de-opaqued). Dictionary[S] ops: impl/sub/arity/resolveOp/ops. OpRef[A] ops: op/dict. TWO-TYPE SPLIT via TYPE PARAMETER: the denoted type IS the parameter (S = the spec instance Eq[Int]; A = the op arrow), tracked STATICALLY by the typer -- NOT read off the opaque value (dissolves store-the-spec: the runtime slot keeps only (functor, sub-handles), the spec lives in the type; the functor is a multi-spec carrier so it cannot recover the spec). Bare Dictionary = the existential (S unknown). Payoffs: uniform typing (WI-502 resolves OpRef/Requirement by reading the type) + introspectable dispatch. SEPARATE follow-on (NOT this WI, gated on a metaprogramming consumer): the reflect Term layer -- reflect_expr(Expr, pos) -> NodeOccurrence (the occurrence dual of reflect(TermRepr)->Term) + anthill constructors genRef/genApply/genConstruct/findDictionary/mkApply over the Expr enum; find_dictionary Expr form is scoped to WI-300. Design: docs/design/requirement-dictionaries.md sec 2 (runtime sorts) / 2.5 (two-type split) / 2.6 (reflect Term layer) / 4 (phasing). Acceptance: the two runtime sorts + native builtins + carrier mapping; cargo-test green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-02T14:02:10Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-612", description: some(value: "Typer (abstract-E effect threading, CARRIER-PARAM CONSUMER variant): a qualified Iterable consumer (isEmpty / find) over a bare Stream receiver whose access row E is ABSTRACT (unwritten) leaks the effect as '?_' instead of threading it as the receiver's projection 's.E'. Reproducer: 'operation f(s: Stream[T = Int64]) -> Bool effects s.E = Iterable.isEmpty(s)' fails to load with 'f.effects: expected declared [s.E], got undeclared effect: ?_' (find leaks the same). Distinct from WI-604 (which fixed the CONCRETE case: a bare Stream with WRITTEN E={}, e.g. Iterable.map on a List, dispatched-override projection s.E now grounds to {}). Here E is UNWRITTEN, so the carrier-param binding (bind_spec_params_from_carrier_param via parameterized_short_bindings) finds no E type-arg on 'Stream[T=Int64]' -> Iterable.E stays unbound -> '?_'; for it to thread, the abstract receiver's E must be represented as the self-projection 's.E' (the param's own E) and Iterable.E bound to it. Also note the dispatched Stream.isEmpty override contributes a second 's.E' (WI-604 fallback re-key), so f_bad shows BOTH '?_' and 's.E'. FOUND during WI-604 (verified IDENTICAL before/after that change - not a WI-604 regression). Related to the WI-608/609 abstract-E family but a DISTINCT reproducer (bare Stream param + carrier-param Iterable consumer, vs their FiniteCollection-field receivers). ACCEPTANCE: 'Iterable.isEmpty(s)' / 'Iterable.find(s, p)' on 's: Stream[T=Int64]' (abstract E) type-checks with the incurred effect threaded as 's.E' (matching a declared 'effects s.E'); a focused typer test + cargo-test green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-02T15:41:48Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-615", description: some(value: "Proposal 051 Phase 1: un-park the `===` structural-equality-test operator. 049 parked `===` and left the resolver's structural shortcut (`builtin_eq`->`views_structurally_equal`, wired mod.rs:4171 as BuiltinTag::Eq on Eq.eq) in place under the Invariant 'valid while every carrier's equality is structural'. That condition is now VIOLATED: Set is a non-structural-equality carrier (set.anthill:25 makes insert commutative, so two equal sets are structurally-distinct terms), so any Eq[Set]/Eq[Map] is membership-based and `=`/`eq` give the wrong answer. SCOPE (decision-free, no SLD-dispatch dep): add `===` as a single grammar token (tree-sitter-anthill/grammar.js + scaland fastparse, lexed greedy-longest before `==`/`=`) -> loader maps `===` to the EXISTING structural comparator (BuiltinTag::Eq / builtin_eq) -- a relabel, not new behaviour; `===` is a total, carrier-agnostic, dispatch-free TEST needing no Eq instance (opaque carriers compare by handle identity per WI-486). KEEP `=`/`eq` as the structural shortcut for now (backward-compatible; the semantic flip is Phase 2/WI-616). Migrate the obvious structural-intent internal/reflect comparison sites (Term/Symbol/reflected-structure identity) from `=`/`eq` to `===` so they are already off `=` before Phase 2 flips it. Orthogonal to WI-300 (guard tier). Design: docs/proposals/051-structural-vs-semantic-equality.md."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-02T16:02:11Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-300", author: "user", content: "HANDOFF (2026-07-02, before starting WI-300 in a new session). (1) WI-615 DELIVERED + pushed (07f0cbc5): proposal 051 Phase 1, the `===` structural-equality operator = anthill.kernel.struct_eq, a total dispatch-free STRUCTURAL test reusing builtin_eq (no Eq instance needed), registered on BOTH resolver (BuiltinTag::Eq) and eval sides, op-level type param [T] so it types in op bodies. Distinct from `=`/`eq` (anthill.prelude.Eq.eq), which stays the structural-SHORTCUT semantic op pending WI-616. So WI-300's guard-tier worked-example test can use `eq`/`===` deliberately and the guard's meaning is crisp. (2) WI-616 filed (Phase 2: make `=`/`eq` genuinely DISPATCH through the carrier Eq) -- GATED on the SLD->eval spec-op bridge, the SAME missing infra as WI-300 Tier B (WI-483/487). (3) SETTLED WI-300 SCOPE (this session, with user) = GUARD TIER ONLY: convert_rule_body recognizes requires(X)->find_dictionary goal (positional-as-written); typer types the rule body + records which rule VAR grounds each spec type-param (chosen linkage = 'typer records grounding vars'); resolver find_dictionary builtin reads the grounding vars' carried types (WI-578) -> builds SortGoal -> collect_provides_candidates/sort_provides (WI-292) -> ground+provider=FIRE, ground+none=DON'T-FIRE, under-determined=SUSPEND-as-residual (WI-519 Solution.residual + definite_only; never NAF-decide). Body `eq` is BuiltinTag::Eq (structural) so the worked example's fire/suspend/don't-fire is governed ENTIRELY by the guard. DEFERRED to a follow-on WI (needs the absent SLD->eval op-body bridge): construct the Value::Requirement into a Gamma requirement slot + weave body spec-ops (WI-613 attribution) + bridge Gamma->frame.requirements. Full code-level map in memory project_wi300_guard_tier.md.", at: "2026-07-02T16:09:36Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-614", description: some(value: "Typer: dot-dispatch a REQUIRED-spec's member on a receiver whose static type is the requiring spec. A value of type FiniteCollection[C=..,Element=..,E=..] (e.g. the WI-599 thin FiniteCollection.map/filter result) cannot resolve Iterable-only members .find(p)/.isEmpty()/.iterator() via dot-dispatch: it errors 'FiniteCollection.isEmpty: no such member (dot dispatch)' because dot-dispatch searches the receiver spec's OWN members (and PROVIDED specs) but NOT the members of specs it REQUIRES. FiniteCollection requires Iterable[C=C,Element=Element,E=E], so a FiniteCollection IS walkable and Iterable ops are sound on it. Reproducer: 'operation f(xs: List[T=Int64]) -> Int64 = match xs.map(inc).find(is_big) case some(v) -> v case none() -> 0' fails (find not found on the FiniteCollection map-result). WI-599 works around it by collect-ing to a List first (List -> Stream -> Iterable transitively; see wi492/wi278 tests). DISTINCT from WI-612 (abstract-E effect threading on a bare Stream) and WI-608/609 (carrier-param call-site grounding). FIX SKETCH: dot-dispatch member resolution on a Spec[..]-typed receiver should also consider members of specs Spec REQUIRES (traverse the requires-graph as it does provides), binding the required-spec's params from the requiring-spec's own (FiniteCollection.Element/E -> Iterable.Element/E). ACCEPTANCE: xs.map(f).find(p) / xs.map(f).isEmpty() on a thin FiniteCollection map-result type-checks; wi492 can drop its collect-first workaround; cargo-test green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-02T19:02:54Z"))
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-617", name: "finiteness")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-300", description: some(value: "Requirement goals in rule bodies (conditional-rule requirements). Today a realization requirement cannot be expressed as a rule-body goal: req_insertion::run (req_insertion.rs:41) weaves requirement forms (Expr::ApplyWithin / ConstructRequirement / RequirementAtSort / VarRef) ONLY into kb.op_bodies, never rule body_nodes. A 'requires(Eq[T])' written in a rule body (e.g. 'something(?x,?y) :- eq(?x,?y), requires(Eq[T])') parses — keywords are soft (grammar.js:23) — as an ordinary Expr::Apply{functor: requires, pos_args:[Eq[T]]} goal with NO requirement semantics: convert_rule_body (convert.rs:1647) has no special case, and at resolution it is just an undefined predicate that fails. Requirements today live only as: the standalone 'requires <Type>' decl -> SortRequiresInfo fact (load.rs:6004), and operation requires/ensures clauses -> OperationInfo reflect fact fields (load.rs:5850). SCOPE: design + implement rule-body requirement goals (conditional rules) — decide the surface syntax/semantics (likely a distinguished goal recognized in convert_rule_body or a type-directed weave), extend req_insertion (or an analog) to process rule body_nodes, and represent the woven form as occurrences consistent with the op-body *Within forms. Builds on the per-rule is_requires_resolved/mark_requires_resolved flags (mod.rs:456) which already exist but are not backed by body-level occurrence synthesis. Surfaced during WI-246 Phase 3c review."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-292", "WI-577", "WI-613"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-02T19:09:28Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-617", description: some(value: "Typer robustness (follow-up to WI-614, /code-review finding): spec_has_any_providers (kb/typing.rs:8828) compares view_base_sym == spec_sort with RAW == instead of canonical same_symbol/canonical_sort_sym — inconsistent with its sibling impl_sorts_providing_spec (typing.rs:8758, which canonicalizes). A sort interns under multiple Symbol ids (bare vs qualified); if a provider fact's spec base interns differently from the queried spec symbol, == reads false -> spec_has_any_providers returns false -> carrier_is_abstract_spec returns false -> the abstract-spec-carrier dispatch gates (WI-598/601/608/609/614) spuriously SKIP, so a legitimately-abstract spec value (e.g. a FiniteCollection map/filter result) regresses to a loud 'no such member (dot dispatch)' / missing-dispatch error. Completeness/robustness gap (loud failure, never a wrong result); low-confidence it mis-fires on the current stdlib (finiteness suite is green) but it is load-bearing for the whole carrier_is_abstract_spec gate. FIX: canonicalize both sides (kb.canonical_sort_sym(view_base_sym) == kb.canonical_sort_sym(spec_sort)), matching impl_sorts_providing_spec. ACCEPTANCE: cargo-test; ideally a regression test with a provider fact whose spec base interns under a non-canonical symbol."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-614"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-02T19:49:35Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-603", description: some(value: "Type the rule body: stamp inferred_type on rule-body atoms from the op/field signatures (complete the untyped->typed transform for rules). TODAY rule-body typing is SELECTIVE: only dot subtrees (dispatch_rule_body_dots) get inferred_type; a plain f(?x,?y) atom is walked by collect_rule_var_types (which reads f's signature) but STAMPED BY NOBODY, so f(?x,?y) carries no inferred_type. PRINCIPLE (user): the op signature f(x:Int,y:Int) is the SINGLE source of truth for arg types -- do NOT add a separate per-rule var_types store (redundant); instead stamp the derived types onto the rule's already-stored body_nodes (NodeOccurrence.inferred_type is interior-mutable and persisted on RuleEntry), sourced from OperationInfo / entity_field_types, with the cross-occurrence UNIFICATION (the one genuinely new fact -- a var shared across f(?a,..) and g(?a,..)) living on the occurrence. Then EVERY consumer reads inferred_type off the occurrence instead of re-walking signatures: check_rule_typing (contradiction), dispatch_rule_body_dots (dot env), the resolver firing guard typed_pattern_bounds_hold, and the WI-602 rule-body precondition check. Subsumes the <=2x collect_rule_var_types recompute (called in check_rule_typing + dispatch_rule_body_dots, discarded both). UNBLOCKS WI-602 (the refutation-aware rule-body precondition check needs the call-site arg types). A concrete slice of WI-079 (full relational rule typing). CHEAP INTERIM (no typer change, no new storage): call collect_rule_var_types ONCE at load and share between the two passes. ACCEPTANCE: f(?x,?y) in a rule body carries inferred_type on its arg occurrences sourced from f's signature; the two collect_rule_var_types call sites read it instead of recomputing; cargo-test green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: []), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-02T23:14:41Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-605", author: "user", content: "Diagnosis correction: there is NO lambda-scope gap. The repro's `(x, acc) -> cons(...)` is not a lambda at all — the lambda keyword is required (kernel-language.md §4.7, proposal 018, deliberate). Keyword-less `pattern -> body` in expression position parses as an infix `->` chain, which the pratt desugar lowers to an arrow-TYPE term `arrow(tuple(x, acc), body)`; its LHS names then load as unresolved value refs, producing the misleading per-binder 'expected resolved name, got unresolved' cascade. The keyword form `lambda (x, acc) -> ...` in the same op-body argument position loads clean (regression-tested). Fix delivered: targeted load-blocking diagnostic LoadError::ArrowTermInExprPosition at the arrow term ('a lambda needs the lambda keyword'), gated on `arrow`/`arrow_effect` not resolving in scope so a user-defined arrow operation keeps the normal call path; Bottom-leaf recovery keeps the load walk shaped. Acceptance amended accordingly: the repro now fails with ONE actionable error instead of type-checking (making bare arrows BE lambdas would collide with arrow-type terms in term position — types are terms). WI-590's drain should just use the lambda keyword.", at: "2026-07-02T22:32:04Z")
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-618", name: "typing")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-605", description: some(value: "Typer: an inline lambda expression passed as a higher-order argument in an OPERATION BODY fails to resolve its parameters. Minimal repro: `operation sum_inc(xs: List[T=Int64]) -> List[T=Int64] = foldRight(xs, nil, (x, acc) -> cons(head: x + 1, tail: acc))` errors with 'type mismatch in x.name: expected resolved name, got unresolved' (and same for acc) — the lambda's bound params x/acc are not entered into a resolution scope, so their uses in the body read as unresolved names. Adding explicit param type annotations does NOT help (same error on the annotation names too). Surfaced while authoring the WI-590 finite-combinator consolidation: a recursion-free `collect` drain wants `foldRight(FiniteCollection.collect(src), nil, (x, acc) -> cons(head: fn(x), tail: acc))`, which is blocked by this. Likely a converter/scope gap: lambda_expr params in an op-body argument position are not registered as locals the way op params or match-case binders are. ACCEPTANCE: the minimal repro type-checks; an inline lambda arg can reference its own params; cargo-test green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-02T23:21:40Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-606", description: some(value: "Typer (gap C, WI-599 family): an UNQUALIFIED self-named spec-op call value-dispatches (WI-411, when the receiver is not the enclosing carrier) but does NOT thread its return type's element/params when the receiver's type-args are ABSTRACT. Repro: a witness collect drain `match splitFirst(m) ... cons(h, FiniteCollection.collect(rest))` with m : MappedStream[Source=S, T=T, ...] (abstract op params) — the destructured tail `rest` comes back a bare Var instead of MappedStream[T=T,...], so the downstream collect(rest) leaves Element ?_  ('expected List[T=?T], got List[T=?_]'). QUALIFYING the call (MappedStream.splitFirst(m)) threads clean (static dispatch computes the return from the resolved op's signature). The CONCRETE-arg case threads even unqualified. Root: the WI-411 value-dispatch return-type path returns an unthreaded/deferred tail for a statically-known carrier with abstract args, where it could compute the return from the resolved impl's signature like the qualified path. Surfaced in WI-590; the compaction works around it by qualifying (correct there, since the witness collect's receiver carrier is statically the concrete carrier). FIX: when an unqualified self-named spec-op value-dispatch statically resolves the receiver's carrier sort, ground+thread the return type from that impl's signature (mirror the qualified path). ACCEPTANCE: the unqualified witness-collect repro threads; cargo-test green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-599"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-03T04:32:53Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-606", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED 2026-07-03. ROOT (traced, not the ticket guess): Stream.splitFirst's return Option[Pair[A=s.T,B=Stream[T=s.T,E=s.E]]] + effects {s.E} are projection-laden; eliminate_type_projections FAILS on s.E against a concrete Mapped receiver (Mapped realizes E only INDIRECTLY via provides Stream[E={ES,EF}]; WI-484 guard rejects the written-but-non-ground row). Elimination is all-or-nothing so the whole return is poisoned -> destructured tail 'rest' is a bare Var -> collect(rest) leaves ?_. FIX (check_apply_iter, 2 coupled sites): (1) on elim FAILURE thread the concrete override's OWN projection-free return AND effects together (concrete_override_threaded, mirrors qualified Mapped.splitFirst(m)); (2) the value-dispatch then NoCandidates (Stream requires EffectsRuntime[E] undischargeable on the abstract row) -> PinNow to the override (concrete_self_receiver_override, self-receiver-param guarded) instead of demanding a spurious requires Stream. ALTITUDE REFUTED EMPIRICALLY: relaxing the WI-484 guard is INSUFFICIENT (tail stays Stream, which provides only Iterable not FiniteCollection -> collect still fails) AND UNSOUND (reintroduces the WI-396 m.E leak); the tail MUST become concrete Mapped, only the impl's return gives that. Gotcha: Stream.splitFirst HAS a projected effect (n_effects=1, not empty) so return+effects must fall back together. /code-review 3-agent: effect-soundness + self-receiver-guard fixed, classify_pin_or_apply_within extracted across WI-444/453/606/Unique arms. Repro test wi606 (load-clean, WI-590 witness-collect drain). suite 2444/0.", at: "2026-07-03T04:33:10Z")
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-619", name: "typing")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-618", description: some(value: "Loader/parser: bare 'pattern -> body' (keyword-less lambda typo) is still SILENTLY accepted outside op/const bodies — empirically: a rule body '?y <=> ((x, acc) -> cons(head: x, tail: acc))' and a contract clause 'ensures all_match(result, (x) -> x > 0)' both load with ZERO diagnostics; the arrow term rides as inert data with unresolved-name leaves, so the rule/contract never means what was written (silent-skip, worse in kind than the loud op-body cascade WI-605 fixed). A blanket error is WRONG there (arrow-as-type is legitimate in term positions — types are terms), so the discriminator needs provenance, not context: mark pratt-minted '->'/'@' operator terms at desugar time (parse-side ParseAux flag or a minted-TermId set on ParsedFile) so consumers can tell the infix operator from a user-named 'arrow' functor exactly, then (a) flag arrow terms whose LEAVES are unresolved lowercase names in rule-body/contract term positions, (b) simplify WI-605's bare_arrow_lambda_suspect string+scope heuristic into an exact provenance check (per /code-review: every future pratt consumer — simp rewriting 043, scaland, reflect-as-data — otherwise re-implements the heuristic and drifts). Found by WI-605 /code-review (10-angle); empirical repros in the review transcript. ACCEPTANCE: rule-body and ensures/requires bare-arrow typos produce a loud diagnostic; legitimate arrow-type terms over resolved sorts still load; cargo-test green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-605"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-03T04:50:21Z"))
