fact Tag(workitem: "WI-624", name: "typing")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-620", description: some(value: "Grammar: parenthesized lambda patterns don't parse as contract-clause call arguments: 'ensures all_match(xs, lambda x -> gt(x, 0))' parses, but 'ensures all_match(xs, lambda (x) -> gt(x, 0))' is a syntax error (same for tuple/annotated params, which REQUIRE parens — so multi-param and annotated lambdas are unwritable in requires/ensures argument position; rule bodies accept both spellings). Found empirically during WI-618 /code-review. Fix in tree-sitter grammar: admit the parenthesized lambda-param form wherever contract-clause arguments admit lambda_expr. ACCEPTANCE: both spellings parse in requires/ensures; grammar corpus + cargo-test green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-03T07:19:35Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-580", author: "user", content: "Design note landed: docs/design/abstract-interpreter-and-rules.md — supersedes scope item 1 (materialized derivation): body views come from ONE on-demand specializer (derived rule == one-step abstract interpretation of the body). Typer site = threshold-gated inlining at the WI-283 hook (GHC-inliner heuristic); SLD site = one-step unfold minting case-split choice points (NO threshold — completeness never heuristic-gated; gated on WI-246/WI-502 substrate); prover extracts defining equations on demand; materialization demoted to a body-keyed cache. member's eq-vs-unification fix falls out (eq evaluated/suspended per branch, undecided -> WI-519 residual). Scope items 2-4 (retire duplicates, laws=theorems, hand-written only for bodyless relations) unchanged.", at: "2026-07-03T08:24:14Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-370", description: some(value: "Custom unification / custom search at a discrimination-tree node: let a node delegate matching of a subterm to on-demand unification (over TermView) instead of materializing that subterm's structure into the trie. Today insert_walk materializes the FULL nested structure of every fact head as DiscrimNodes (multi-level structural dispatch) and queries descend the same way; for a non-discriminating / structurally-large / opaque-headed subterm this is wrong+wasteful. MOTIVATING CASE: an operation BODY (an Expr occurrence) nested in an OperationInfo fact -- its control-flow forms (let/if/match) read Opaque in occ_head (no DiscrimKey), so deep-indexing it (a) builds a per-body structural MIRROR in the trie that NO query prunes on, (b) needs occ_head to mirror the whole Expr enum (duplicating occurrence_term's reflection; occ_head is shared with the simp matcher), and (c) risks deep recursion (the simp engine had to go iterative for the same body depth, WI-278). THE FEATURE: at a node, switch to a custom-unification hook -- read the candidate's subterm via TermView on demand and unify it against the query's subterm -- so values like bodies can live in facts AND support discrim-level shape queries (e.g. 'operations whose body starts with let') WITHOUT a materialized trie mirror or an occ_head change. Two related themes: (a) custom unification (a discrim node delegates to a unify routine); (b) custom search (query descent driven by custom logic, not trie structure). DRIVING CONSUMER: the op_bodies -> OperationInfo.body collapse (WI-348 follow-up). The body is keyed data (op Symbol -> body) that should ALSO be a fact (everything-is-facts / proposal 022 / typing-as-transform). Until this feature exists, op_bodies stays as the keyed store behind the operation_body relation (body relationally readable today via the builtin; typer still mutates the side-table on the [simp] write-back). Design principle from the dialogue: a 'side index' should itself be a relation / the discrim tree, never a bespoke Rust map -- and the discrim tree already IS the Symbol->fact index via name: Ref(op). Acceptance: feature design + impl; cargo-test green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: []), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: PreOpened)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-619", description: some(value: "WI-582 introducer scan mis-targets 2-ary rule heads: collect_rule_tvar_names (load.rs) treats ANY 2-positional-arg head Fn as an equational head and reads type-args off pos_args[0] (the first ARGUMENT), so a '[t]' introducer on a 2-ary head (rule same_ty[t](?x, ?y) :- Eq[t], ...) is never collected — the guard doesn't fold, the tvar isn't bounded, and the rule silently loses its WI-582 desugar (found empirically during WI-618 /code-review: 1-ary and 3-ary heads work, exactly 2-ary fails). Fix: recognize the equational-head LHS by the head functor (eq/unify/struct_eq shape), not by arity==2. ACCEPTANCE: introducers collected on 2-ary heads; existing equational-head rules unaffected; cargo-test green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-03T16:13:25Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-078", description: some(value: "Land proposal 027 (Effect handlers and standard effects): see docs/proposals/027-effect-handlers-and-standard-effects.md. Substrate confirmation (verified 2026-04-23): the defunctionalized eval (activation stack with explicit Frame/AwaitState in eval/frame.rs and eval/eval.rs, per 026/M1) already exists — no evaluator refactor needed. Sequencing per proposal §Migration plan, in two phases. PHASE A (substrate, do first): (a) add Clone to Frame and ActivationStack in eval/frame.rs after auditing AwaitState fields for cloneability; (b) define HandlerAction carrier (Pure | Throw | Fail | Choice) in eval/effects.rs and migrate existing handlers to return it; (c) add RuntimeAPI snapshot_eval_state / resume_with / push_choice / fail_branch operations and wire Choice interpretation; (d) WI-075 push_choice resolver-side prerequisite (parallel). PHASE B (handlers + ergonomics): (e) default Branch handler returning Choice(a, alt_b) for branch / Fail for fail (this is WI-054's scope, recast on the carrier model); (f) default Error handler returning Throw(payload) — propagates to EvalError (this is WI-070's scope on the carrier model); (g) Reader sort + handler in stdlib + Rust default; (h) push/pop handler stack: HashMap<Symbol, Vec<Handler>>, push_handler/pop_handler/with_handler in RuntimeAPI, optional 'with handler H for E do body end' grammar; (i) bracket(setup, body, cleanup) stdlib op via with_handler. Kernel-side stdlib changes: add 'operation branch(a: T, b: T) -> T effects Branch' to Branch sort + sub-effect derivation 'rule Effect[T = Suspension] :- Effect[T = Branch]' to effects.anthill. Tests: per-handler unit tests; integration test for branch inside let yielding LogicalStream; nested with-handler tests; bracket success+error path tests. Relates: WI-050 (M5 framework, landed); WI-053 (Suspension, sibling — overlaps phase A's snapshot/resume API); WI-054 (Branch, replaced by phase B step e); WI-070 (Modify follow-up, parallel — already planned); WI-077 (long-stream stress test, harness reusable for MPlus over Branch)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-075"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Claimed(agent: "claude", since: "2026-07-03T16:29:06Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-078", author: "claude", content: "GROUND-TRUTH audit 2026-07-03 (plan's 2026-04-23 status is stale): Phase A (b) HandlerAction carrier is DONE (enum Pure|Throw|Fail|Choice|Suspend in eval/effects.rs; EffectHandler returns Result<HandlerAction,EvalError>; console/Modify/Error handlers migrated); (d) WI-075 push_choice DONE/Verified; Phase B (f) Error handler DONE (default_error_handler Throw->EvalError::Raised, register_standard_effect_handlers). STILL MISSING: (a) [now DONE this session], (c) RuntimeAPI snapshot_eval_state/resume_with/fail_branch + Choice/Fail/Suspend interpretation (today invoke_effect_handler rejects them via EvalError::UnsupportedHandlerAction) — the hard eval<->resolve crux; (e) Branch handler; (g) Reader sort+handler; (h) push/pop handler stack (registry is flat HashMap<Symbol,Option<EffectHandler>>, no with-handler grammar); (i) bracket; kernel: branch(a,b) op (Branch sort has only fail(), needs sort T=? param) + sub-effect rule Effect[T=Suspension]:-Effect[T=Branch] (today two independent facts). INCREMENT (a) DELIVERED this session: Clone derive-chain MatchBranch->AwaitState->Frame->ActivationStack (only blocker was MatchBranch #[derive(Debug)]-only; all other fields already Clone/Copy); frame.rs clone_snapshots_stack_independently test. NEXT: either kernel-stdlib decls (low risk, but typer implications on sub-effect rule) or (c) the snapshot/resume RuntimeAPI (the crux).", at: "2026-07-03T16:35:59Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-626", description: some(value: "scaland: mirror WI-616 semantic-eq dispatch (proposal 051 Phase 2). Rust delivered: Eq.eq/Eq.neq resolver tags SemEq/SemNeq (struct_eq keeps the structural comparator); on ground structurally-distinct operands the builtin reads the operand's carrier sort SHALLOWLY (entity ctor parent / algebra-op parent spec sort) and rewrites the goal to the carrier's OWN short-named eq op from the sort-ops table (Set.eq/Map.eq), resolved as ordinary SLD rules; reflexivity fast-path keeps every current ground answer; neq wraps the dispatch in NAF not(...). stdlib side (shared .anthill files already carry it — scaland only needs the RESOLVER flip + must load the new set.anthill/map.anthill rules): Set provides Eq[T=Set] + eq/subset/member relational rules (element compare = spec Eq.eq, dispatches recursively); Map provides Eq[T=Map] + eq->map_eq delegator + binds/strip_is helpers (delegator exists because is_equation classifies a BODYLESS 2-ary eq-short-named head as an equational law — mirror that gotcha; also mirror the WI-624 nonlinear-head caveat: Map.binds keeps a LINEAR head + <=> output bind). Rust refs: kb/resolve.rs (BuiltinTag::SemEq/SemNeq, BuiltinResult::ReplaceGoal, builtin_sem_eq/builtin_sem_neq/sem_eq_override_goal/shallow_carrier_sort), kb/mod.rs register_standard_builtins, tests/include/wi616_semantic_eq_test.rs. NB scaland has no typer — only grammar/loader/resolver mirror applies."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "sbt-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-626", author: "user", content: "DESIGN CHANGED post-review (mirror THIS, not the first cut): no ReplaceGoal / no NAF wrapper. SemEq/SemNeq share sem_eq_core(positive): (1) reflexivity fast path (structural-equal => verdict); (2) O(1) probe of a LOAD-TIME eq-dispatch index (build_sort_ops_table pass 3: for each sort with a genuine OWN eq per typing::carrier_own_op — parent-gated, body-agnostic — key its entity constructors + SELF-RETURNING ops to the target; Map.get must NOT key Map); (3) hit + both operands DEEP-GROUND => prove <carrier>.eq(a,b) in a CLOSED bounded sub-resolution (fresh SearchStream, SEM_EQ_SUB_DEPTH=100k; first definite proof => verdict; only-residual OR truncated => Delay; exhausted-complete => opposite verdict) — bindings never escape, eq stays semi-deterministic; (4) hit + non-ground => Delay; (5) no hit but an overriding carrier REACHABLE inside an operand (value_reaches_eq_override walk) => Delay (buried some({1,2}) vs some({2,1}) must not decide structurally); (6) else structural verdict. SearchStream gains a truncated flag (set at the depth pop). BuiltinTag::Neq deleted (dead). carrier_own_op extracted in typing.rs; carrier_overrides_eq_family (WI-573 gate) now uses it (body-agnostic) so rules-backed overrides block structural guard refutation. See wi616_semantic_eq_test.rs incl. the 3 soundness-guard tests.", at: "2026-07-03T16:54:07Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-625", author: "user", content: "Two more gaps for the bridge, from the WI-616 /code-review: (5) EVAL-side silent structural divergence — eval/builtins.rs still registers Eq.eq/Eq.neq to the STRUCTURAL builtin_eq/builtin_neq, so an op body's eq over symbolic Set/Map values that stays on the abstract spec op (e.g. List.member's eq(head,x)) answers structurally at eval while the same goal answers semantically at SLD; kernel-language.md documents the caveat. (6) typed PinNow — WI-210/WI-350 call-site dispatch pins a concrete-Set/Map-receiver eq(...) to the bodyless rule-backed Set.eq/Map.eq, which eval cannot invoke: typechecks, then EvalError UnknownOperation at runtime (loud but late; a load-time diagnostic or an eval fallback-to-builtin-when-rule-backed would be kinder). Also NOTE the mechanism update: the resolver dispatch is now a closed bounded sub-resolution keyed by a load-time eq-dispatch index (see WI-626 feedback for the full shape); 'sem_eq_override_goal' named in this WI's body no longer exists — the instance-fact tier gate now lives in build_sort_ops_table pass 3 + sem_eq_core.", at: "2026-07-03T16:54:24Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-616", description: some(value: "Proposal 051 Phase 2: make `=`/`eq` genuinely DISPATCH through the carrier's Eq instance (semantic equality), replacing the structural shortcut 049 deferred; add the first non-structural Eq carriers (Set/Map membership equality). `=`/`eq` = the Eq.eq spec op: concrete arg -> carrier-pinned impl (WI-350; Int->IntEq.eq stays an i64 compare so every current ground use keeps its answer), abstract arg -> requirement dictionary (WI-300 Tier B). GATED ON spec-op dispatch at SLD, which does NOT exist today: a rule-body `eq(1,1)` works only because it is builtin_eq; unbinding it needs the resolver to dispatch Int->IntEq.eq at resolution -- the same SLD->eval bridge that gates WI-300 Tier B (WI-483/487 line). Also completes the intent audit: split remaining `=`/`eq` sites into structural (->`===`, done in Phase 1) vs semantic (stay `=`, gain dispatch); `neq` pairs with semantic `=`. Design: docs/proposals/051-structural-vs-semantic-equality.md. NB the SLD->eval op-body dispatch bridge is a prerequisite and should be scoped as its own WI first."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-615"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-03T17:32:02Z"))
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-630", name: "resolver")
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-630", name: "reflect")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-515", description: some(value: "Constraint/guard enforcement: var-quantified queries spuriously match synthetic entity field-type DECLARATION facts. The loader asserts a per-entity declaration fact carrying the field TYPES in the slots (per kb_query_test: e.g. Person(name: sort_ref(String), role: sort_ref(String)); confirm exact carrier — appeared as a Value::Node-bound candidate during WI-512 debugging). A CONCRETE query value does not unify with the type slot, so concrete-discriminator constraints (the WI-023 cross-atom tests) correctly exclude it; but a fully-VAR self-referential constraint like  has ?p unify with the (same) type in both slots, so the declaration fact is counted as a match and the constraint is ALWAYS spuriously violated — independent of WI-512's non-linearity fix (which only drops a≠b conflicts). This blocks WI-023 quantified constraints over fully-var self-referential patterns (functional-dependency / self-loop / uniqueness shapes). SCOPE: (1) confirm the exact declaration-fact representation + which sort/domain it is asserted under; (2) exclude these non-DATA facts from constraint/guard enforcement — e.g. tag declaration facts and filter them in the guard's resolve, scope check_all_guards/eval_count_guard queries to the data domain, or stop asserting field-type declarations as same-functor facts. ACCEPTANCE: a fully-var self-referential constraint (no ?p -: edge(from:?p,to:?p)) holds when no DATA fact is a self-loop and is violated only by a real self-loop data fact; cargo-test green. Surfaced isolating WI-512 (the constraint-level self-loop test was confounded by this, so WI-512 was verified at the resolver level instead)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-04T14:20:31Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-515", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED (295ef1e4). ROOT confirmed live via CLI before the fix: 'no ?p -: edge(from: ?p, to: ?p)' over self-loop-free data reported VIOLATED, and 'anthill query edge(from: ?p, to: ?p)' returned exactly the phantom row edge(from: Node, to: Node) [?p = Node]. Representation (scope item 1): load_entity asserted <ctor>(field: <type-term>, ...) under sort Entity — same functor as data, discrim-indexed, sort-blind at SLD. FIX = the ticket's option 3 (stop asserting): declaration data = entity_field_types registry + EntityInfo facts (sort-body entities); readers re-pointed through NEW canonical KB::resolve_entity_functor (tier 1 exact qualified via try_resolve_symbol, tier 2 short-name scan, min-qualified-name tie-break). 10-finder+verify+sweep /code-review drove 15 findings, key ones: (a) first-cut helper compared resolve_sym = SHORT names -> qualified matching dead + nondeterministic pick on stdlib collisions (guarded/Unknown); (b) FIVE tests had encoded the phantom row as expected contract (eval m4 count=3, banking by_sort(Entity), typing ?-field decode x2, bridge facts_of 3/2); (c) wi515 test's Debug-contains assertion was vacuous (derived Debug prints TermIds) -> structural bound-symbol assert; (d) wi182 'alice'||'no-string' disjunct masked regressions -> tightened; (e) unwrap_or_default silent-empty schema -> loud expect; (f) typer skip-list fully dead -> deleted; (g) ~12 stale schema-fact comments across tests/smt-gen/anthill-todo bundles. ACCEPTED behavior changes: ambiguous short-name resolution now deterministic min-qualified (was load-order); find_entity_schema serves value-in-type entities; user-defined 'sort Entity' data facts now type-checked. Bug CLASS (sort-blind resolve vs future same-functor metadata) + namespace-level EntityInfo gap -> WI-630. No scaland mirror (scaland emits entity_of, never the schema fact). Suite 2504/0.", at: "2026-07-04T14:22:01Z")
