fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-630", author: "user", content: "CAVEAT for scope item 2 (namespace-level EntityInfo), from the WI-515 dialogue: EntityInfo emission is coupled to entity_of SEMANTICS, not just accidentally placed in load_sort_with_body. typing.anthill:26-35 derives the parent sort from the entity's SCOPE ('rule is_entity_of(?A,?B) :- EntityInfo(name: ?A), scope(?A, ?B)'; same for entity_of) — for a namespace-level entity the scope is the NAMESPACE, so naively emitting EntityInfo for it would make entity_of(Account, ?sort) bind the namespace as a bogus parent; typing_test.rs entity_of_standalone_entity_has_no_parent pins that standalone entities have NO parent (0 solutions). Emitting namespace-level EntityInfo therefore requires either a sort-guard conjunct in the typing.anthill rules (e.g. SortInfo(name: ?B)) or a distinct fact shape for parentless entities. That coupling is WHY the gap exists: EntityInfo was built as the entity-subtyping record for sort-body entities; the schema fact accidentally covered the rest of fact space until WI-515 removed it.", at: "2026-07-04T14:33:05Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-631", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED (3166b824). Three-way = intern::ResolveResult REUSED (Found/Ambiguous/NotFound; gained PartialEq/Eq) — no new parallel enum, per review. Resolution policy in ONE place: reader::read_entity_fields returns (functor, fields); find_entity_schema consumes it (first cut had re-inlined it). Diagnostic uses the codebase 'candidates' vocabulary and says 'use a FULLY qualified name' (review caught: a partial qualification like Alpha.dup is NotFound, which is SILENT on all three surfaces — the candidate strings are the exact retry inputs). Surfaces: KB.fields -> EvalError::Internal (kept for file-wide consistency; Internal Displays as 'internal evaluator error' which misframes a user-input problem — a UserError EvalError variant would be a separate sweep); bridge SortQuery -> Err(Error); bridge fields() -> panic (facts_of precedent; let-bind before unwrap so the RefCell Ref releases before the closure). ACCEPTED EDGE (documented NB): in a namespace-less file a top-level entity's qualified name IS its short name, so it wins the exact tier and is never reported ambiguous vs a same-named sort-body ctor. PARITY NOTE for WI-632: interpreter-side KB.execute sort_query (execute.rs ~639) resolves try_resolve_symbol-only (loud UnknownSort on ANY short name) while the bridge SortQuery accepts unique short names — pre-existing surface divergence, exactly WI-632's scope. Suite 2511/0.", at: "2026-07-04T15:12:23Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-631", description: some(value: "unique-or-loud short-name entity resolution (WI-515 follow-up, user-directed). KB::resolve_entity_functor currently resolves an AMBIGUOUS short entity name by silently picking the minimal qualified name (the WI-515 compat tie-break) — real stdlib collisions exist (entity 'guarded' in anthill.prelude vs anthill.reflect.LogicalQuery; 'Unknown' in prelude/meta vs realization/witness), so a caller silently gets one sort's schema. Per the loud-over-silent principle: make resolution three-way (Unique / Ambiguous(candidates, qualified-name order) / NotFound); a short name resolves ONLY when unique; ambiguity surfaces loudly listing the candidates and asking for a qualified name — EvalError from the interpreter KB.fields builtin, Err through the bridge's SortQuery lowering (query_to_goals has the Result channel), panic on the bridge's Vec-returning fields() surface (precedent: facts_of panics on caller type errors). Exact qualified-name resolution stays O(1) and unaffected. Rationale discussion: the short-name string surface is a display-name convenience with no scope context — the principled endgame is WI-632 (typed refs / scoped resolution); this WI just removes the silent-arbitrary-pick hazard cheaply. ACCEPTANCE: ambiguous short name errors loudly on all three surfaces (tests incl. a two-sorts-same-ctor fixture); unique short and qualified names unchanged; cargo-test green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-04T15:12:27Z"))
