fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-626", author: "user", content: "WI-624 landed rust-side; two notes for the mirror. (1) The 'Map.binds keeps a LINEAR head + <=> output bind' caveat REMAINS CORRECT but its REASON changed: the DeBruijn answer-leak is fixed (WI-624), yet the linear form is still required because a nonlinear head imposes STRUCTURAL identity (discrim synthetic double-bind -> contradiction drop), not unification, on doubly-concrete matches — binds(put(empty,1,some(?x)),1,some(42)) would false-fail nonlinear (WI-633 tracks unification-complete nonlinear heads; map.anthill's NB comment now says this). (2) scaland does NOT need the WI-624 fix pair itself: its withFreshVars already threads answer links through the rename (KnowledgeBase.scala:300-310, the rust legacy-path structure) and it has no DeBruijn/synthetic-vid split — but IF the mirror adopts the rust DeBruijn representation, port the pair: two-pass body_rename-before-links + apply-rename-to-links + occurs-check->contradiction in withFreshVars, AND the fact fast-path gate is_fact && arity==0 in the resolver (plus the WI-624 regression tests: forward-orientation nonlinear head, bodyless and bodied, and the occurs-cycle query).", at: "2026-07-04T19:43:57Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-624", description: some(value: "Resolver: nonlinear rule-head output var leaks unopened DeBruijn into the answer (silent wrong binding). REPRO (found during WI-616, test wi616_semantic_eq_test::nonlinear_head_output_var_repro, currently #[ignore]d on this WI): rule unbox0(box(v: ?v), ?v) — same head var ONCE inside a compound arg subpattern and ONCE as a whole top-level arg; query unbox0(box(42), ?out) yields 1 solution but ?out reifies to Var(DeBruijn(0)) instead of 42 (bodyless and bodied variants both fail; the goal also 'succeeds' while leaving downstream consumers of ?out floundering — Map.binds originally hit this: eq(10, ?_) residualized and a FALSE map equality surfaced as an undecided solution). SUSPECT: the discrim-tree head match + with_fresh_vars answer_links path — the query-var linkage records the rule var slot but the rule var's own concrete binding (from the OTHER, compound occurrence of the same DeBruijn var) is not threaded through (the 'only query-var linkages go into answer_links, not synthetic fresh->concrete bindings' O(n2)-avoidance invariant, kb/mod.rs instantiate/with_fresh_vars area). WORKAROUND used in stdlib map.anthill Map.binds: keep heads LINEAR and bind the output via an explicit body unify (?v <=> ?v2); note List.member's nonlinear head (list.anthill:52) is test-only ground so it never observes the leak; Map.strip_is's COMPOUND-output nonlinear head works (the leak is specific to a BARE repeated var as the whole output arg). Acceptance: un-ignore the repro test; full suite green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-04T20:06:10Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-633", description: some(value: "Resolver: nonlinear rule-head match completeness — repeated head vars impose STRUCTURAL identity, not unification, on doubly-concrete occurrences, and the inverse orientation leaks an unbound output. Found by the WI-624 /code-review verify pass (both empirically reproduced). (1) DOUBLY-CONCRETE: head p(box(v: ?v), ?v) queried p(box(v: some(?x)), some(42)) — true semantics binds ?x=42 (unify the two ?v matches); today the discrim var edges double-bind the synthetic MAX-n vid (discrim.rs:816 as_bind_value), bind_term's re-bind check is views_structurally_equal (structural, no unification), the mismatch flags is_contradiction and resolve.rs drops the candidate: SILENT 0 solutions. This is why stdlib Map.binds rule 1 must keep its LINEAR head + explicit '?v <=> ?v2' body unify (see the map.anthill NB comment; the WI-624 session briefly reverted it to nonlinear and the verify pass caught the false failure). (2) INVERSE ORIENTATION: query var buried in the compound occurrence with a concrete term at the bare occurrence — binds(put(empty,1,?y), 1, 42) / unbox0(box(v: ?out2), 42) — yields a DEFINITE solution with the output var left silently UNBOUND (the WI-624 leak shape through the route the fix pair does not cover; WI-624 fixed the forward orientation: concrete in compound, flex at bare). Ignored repro tests: wi616_semantic_eq_test::{nonlinear_head_doubly_concrete_unifies, nonlinear_head_inverse_orientation_binds}. Direction: either thread rule-var double-binds through real unification at the leaf/with_fresh_vars (turning the structural-conflict drop into a unify attempt and threading inverse links through the synthetic-entry direction), or gate/lint nonlinear heads at load. NB: whichever lands must keep WI-624's occurs check (with_fresh_vars pass 2 -> answer_links.contradiction) intact."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-05T07:38:00Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-634", author: "user", content: "WI-633 review surfaced the load-bearing distinction for this twin: resolve_leaf is shared by SLD resolution (query_resolved_value, unify-on-rebind) AND one-directional matching (query_resolved -> match_view, structural-identity-on-rebind, drives typer simp_rewrite::try_fire + assumed_facts discharge + external rows). WI-633 threaded a unify_rebind flag so only the SLD path unifies. apply_eq_rules is the RESOLVER's simp entry (unify path) and is already guarded against inexpressible query-var/unify-produced entries (the WI-633 loud gate at resolve.rs ~2355). The typer's match_view simp path needs NO guard because matching (unify_rebind=false) already refuses to fire a nonlinear LHS on distinct-but-unifiable subterms. So WI-634's remaining completeness work is narrower than filed: instantiate_eq_rhs need only thread the SYNTHETIC-entry rename it already builds PLUS any query-var link the guard currently skips; the match_view path is out of scope. Regression locked by match_term_nonlinear_is_matching_not_unification (kb/mod.rs) + apply_eq_rules_skips_{severing_var_redex,inexpressible_query_var_links} (kb/resolve.rs).", at: "2026-07-05T07:38:15Z")
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-637", name: "scala")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-634", description: some(value: "simp/eq-rewrite: instantiate_eq_rhs drops query-var links — the WI-624 twin in the eq-rules path (CONFIRMED by the WI-624 /code-review verify pass; pre-existing, untouched by WI-624's fix pair). instantiate_eq_rhs (resolve.rs ~2453-2470) threads ONLY synthetic u32::MAX-n entries into its RHS rename; a query-var -> Var(DeBruijn n) link entry (recorded when a query var meets an LHS var position, discrim.rs:611-613 Path extraction) is never consumed. Consequences: (a) '[simp] pick(?a,?b) = ?a' firing over redex pick(?q, 7): no synthetic entry exists for DeBruijn 0 in that direction, the opened RHS fresh global stays disconnected, and the rewritten goal SEVERS ?q — whatever solves the rewritten goal never binds the caller's var (reachable: step_init ~940-956 fires eq rewriting on any term goal when candidates are all-eq; no groundness gate — the 2310-2357 gates are tag/requires/typed-bounds only). (b) reify(rhs, &tree_subst) at ~2384 chases a query var through the raw q -> Var(DeBruijn n) entry and leaks an unopened DeBruijn — but only into EqChange.rewritten (the change RECORD, 2386-2390); the goal continuation uses rhs/final_term, so (b) corrupts provenance not resolution. (c) the rename walks iter_terms() only, so a Value-bound synthetic entry (non-Term carrier) is silently skipped — same class as WI-635-to-be / with_fresh_vars gap. NOTE the missing-is_contradiction claim from the same review was REFUTED: query()/query_view already drops contradictory matches (kb/mod.rs:2282-2284) before apply_eq_rules sees them. Fix direction: mirror WI-624's with_fresh_vars pass structure in instantiate_eq_rhs (build the synthetic rename first, then thread query-var links through it — or refuse to fire on redexes whose var links the rename cannot express, a loud gate per the loud-over-silent principle)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-05T11:19:26Z"))
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-638", name: "typing")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-638", description: some(value: "Named-tuple component field access (t.x): a dot projection t.x where t types as a named tuple ((x: A, y: B), TypeExtractor.NamedTuple) does NOT resolve today — verified it errors with no-such-member (dot dispatch) on BOTH a tuple literal ((x:10, y:20).x) and a statically-typed param (t: (x: Int64, y: Int64)). kernel-language.md 6.7 dot-projection has only two dispatch modes: (1) entity-constructor fields, (2) sort components; a named tuple is neither — its functor is TupleLiteral whose entity_field_types is empty Some([]), and the component names live in the named-args of the value plus the TypeExtractor.NamedTuple type, which the field resolver never consults. Adjacent DELIVERED WIs do not cover this: WI-013 (field access in expression bodies, entity/sort), WI-262 (TYPE-level projection Modify[c.field], explicitly out-of-scope for term-level field access), WI-490 (free-standing-entity field access via field_constructors_of_sort). FIX: add a THIRD dispatch mode — when the receiver synthesized type is a TypeExtractor.NamedTuple, resolve .field against its element names. TOUCH-POINTS: typer field resolution — resolve_field_type (typing.rs ~11565) and the DotApply field-fallback (typing.rs ~3565): add a NamedTuple arm reading the element (name,type) list, typing the access at the field type; eval field_access — read Value::Tuple.named by field name (the named_arg lookup in term_view already does this). Also support positional projection t._1 / t._2 since positional tuples desugar to _N names. DRIVER: proposal 052 (Relations as first-class values, docs/proposals/052) — its relational-algebra lambda surface reads columns as row.x; without this it falls back to tuple destructuring, awkward for a two-row join. ACCEPTANCE: (x:10, y:20).x evaluates to 10 and .y to 20; t.x on a param typed (x: Int64, y: Int64) type-checks and evaluates; positional ._1 works; existing entity/sort field access unaffected; cargo test green with a test paralleling the diagnostic probe in tests/include/tuple_order_test.rs."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-05T12:49:48Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-640", description: some(value: "Simp firing convergence: make apply_eq_rules carrier-neutral (&Value -> (Value, changes)) and fire it via a corrected ONE-WAY matcher, so `anthill prove` simplifies Value::Node occurrence goals (was silently term-goal-only) and the WI-624/633/634 query-var-threading apparatus (instantiate_eq_rhs, the query-link classification, the linearity gate) is DELETED. Root cause fixed: match_view was WRONG for a redex carrying flex-Global query vars — it ran the discrim QUERY path which self-binds a flex-Global TARGET var and scrambles the match. Added a match_mode to the discrim query so a target flex-Global var is inert (one-way); kept match_view WILDCARD (assumed-fact/reflect discharge relies on target-var binding, resolve.rs:1023) and added match_view_oneway used only by fire_simp_equation. /code-review (high, 8 angles) found+fixed 2 correctness bugs (the match_view hypothesis-discharge regression from flipping it globally one-way — A/C/G converged; and a value-headed-equation rule_head panic) + 2 efficiency regressions (WI-595 guard-result cache, unified functor pre-filter); regression test match_view_binds_target_var_but_oneway_does_not. Phase 1 of the convergence; Phase 2 (driver merge) is the follow-up. Full workspace 2523/0. Related WI-283 (converge the two simp sites, delivered), WI-634."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-05T15:05:55Z"))
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-640", name: "rust")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-641", description: some(value: "Simp rewriter convergence PHASE 2: merge the two [simp] drivers into ONE carrier-neutral engine. After WI-640 the FIRING is converged (typer simp_rewrite::try_fire and resolver fire_simp_equation both match one-way via match_view_oneway + share the guard/bounds helpers) but the DRIVERS still coexist: the typer's ITERATIVE bottom-up simp_rewrite::rewrite/visit_node/build_node (work-stack, made iterative by WI-278 to survive deep occurrence trees) vs the resolver's apply_eq_rules_occurrence RECURSIVE walk (added in WI-640). Tasks: (1) route the resolver's occurrence redex through ONE iterative driver (parameterize its firing over fire_simp_equation) so a deep Node prove-goal can't overflow the host stack; (2) share the opener — open_equation returns fresh so fire_simp_equation stops re-inlining it and try_fire reuses it; (3) make the requires-guard carrier-neutral (fire_simp_equation reifies a Node redex to a term for simp_requires_guard_holds(TermId), discarding the occurrence's WI-578 carried type + interning a transient term — the typer uses occurrence-native simp_fire_guard_holds(&occ)); (4) align the descend gate (apply_eq_rules_occurrence uses ViewHead::Functor vs the typer's is_rewritable Expr whitelist). Branch base: wi640-simp-rewriter-convergence. Related WI-640 (Phase 1), WI-283, WI-278. ACCEPTANCE: one shared iterative occurrence driver + one opener; requires-guard carrier-neutral; cargo test green; a deeply-nested Node prove-goal that would overflow the recursive walk now simplifies."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-640"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-05T18:43:30Z"))
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-641", name: "rust")
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-643", name: "rust")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-642", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. New typer pass check_rule_body_requirements in kb/typing.rs (run in type_check_sorts_typed after record_find_dictionary_grounding): walks every non-fact rule body for spec-op calls (Apply/Constructor/Instantiation), aligns args to param order (align_call_args_to_params) and reads each carrier's stamped inferred_type, then shares simp_guard_holds_core (the find_dictionary_guard core) for the 3-way WI-292 decision -- Fire=clean, Suspend(under-determined/abstract carrier)=clean, DontFire(concrete no-provision)=MissingRequiresForSpecOp. KEY DEVIATION from the literal acceptance: the 'eq' example does NOT error -- Eq.eq/neq and Ordered.gt/lt/gte/lte are registered structural BUILTINs (is_builtin) that resolve WITHOUT a dictionary, so flagging them would break the stdlib (needs_rebuild's gt on two Timestamps). The check targets genuine non-builtin spec ops (user typeclasses). Gates: skip is_builtin, skip no-carrier-param, skip !spec_warrants_abstract_check (host builtins), skip in-body declared requires(Spec) (find_dictionary base, element-agnostic). Two carrier shapes read as headless->Suspend: sort-params (is_sort_param_symbol) and abstract-spec sorts (carrier_is_abstract_spec, the splitFirst-on-Stream self-receiver). CONSERVATIVE BOUNDARY: sort_provides misses WI-450 witness-sort and denoted/value-fact provisions (same blind spot as the runtime find_dictionary guard) -- rare false-positive risk, escape is declare requires. /code-review high (3 finder agents) -> fixed named-arg misalignment via align_call_args_to_params + added Constructor/Instantiation forms + dropped read-only body clone. Tests: wi642_rule_body_requires_test.rs (6 tests). Full suite green 2535+.", at: "2026-07-05T16:19:54Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-642", description: some(value: "Extend the `MissingRequiresForSpecOp` static check from operation bodies to RULE / relation clause bodies, so a clause that calls a `requires`-carrying spec-op whose requirement is neither declared nor satisfiable is a LOAD error, not a silent resolution-time failure.\n\nGAP: today the requirement-diagnostic pass `req_insertion::run` (rustland/anthill-core/src/kb/req_insertion.rs:46-50) walks ONLY `kb.op_bodies` (op_bodies_iter) and emits `MissingRequiresForSpecOp` (WI-325, req_insertion.rs:79) for OPERATION bodies. Rule / relation clause bodies (`body_nodes`) are NOT walked, so a rule-body spec-op call with an unsatisfiable requirement is caught only at RESOLUTION -- the WI-300 `find_dictionary(X)` goal (resolve.rs:3909) just fails / floats -- never at load. This violates the repo principle \"prefer a loud error over a silent skip.\"\n\nFIX DIRECTION (kb/req_insertion.rs + typing.rs): walk each rule's clause body (`body_nodes`) for spec-op calls (the same UnresolvedSpecOp / spec-op-dispatch shape req_insertion already recognizes in op bodies), and for each resolve the requirement against (a) a `requires` declared on the enclosing rule/sort, or (b) an available provision (WI-300 / WI-292 provides-resolution); if NEITHER, emit `MissingRequiresForSpecOp` (or the direct-call analogue at typing.rs:2614) at load. Reuse the op-body machinery -- a coverage extension of req_insertion (or a sibling pass over body_nodes), not new diagnostic logic.\n\nLOAD-BEARING DISTINCTION (WI-292): a STATICALLY-MISSING requirement (no provision can satisfy it AND it is not declared) is an ERROR; an UNDER-DETERMINED requirement (the grounding type is not yet bound at the current substitution) is NOT an error -- it SUSPENDS as a residual (WI-292 resolve-or-suspend / WI-067, never NAF-decide). The static check must report ONLY the former: a requirement unsatisfiable for ALL groundings AND undeclared -- never one that is merely not-yet-ground, and never a polymorphic rule that legitimately propagates `requires X` to its callers.\n\nDRIVER: proposal 052 (docs/proposals/052-rules-as-stream-valued-operations.md, section \"Requirements in a clause body -- the rule-body dictionary (WI-300), and checking a missing one\"). A relation is a rule; its clause bodies calling spec-ops should fail at LOAD when a requirement can never be met. The runtime path is WI-300 (delivered); this is its STATIC face.\n\nACCEPTANCE: a rule whose body calls a spec-op (e.g. `eq` from `Eq[T]`) with T a concrete sort that provides NO `Eq` instance, and with no `requires Eq[T]` on the enclosing rule/sort, is a LOAD error (`MissingRequiresForSpecOp` or the direct-call analogue) -- verified it errors at load, not only at query time; a rule that DECLARES `requires Eq[T]` (propagating) loads clean; a polymorphic rule whose grounding is under-determined SUSPENDS (does not error); existing op-body MissingRequiresForSpecOp diagnostics are unchanged; cargo test green. REF: WI-325 (MissingRequiresForSpecOp, the op-body diagnostic this extends), WI-300 (rule-body requirement goals / find_dictionary, delivered -- the runtime path this statically checks), WI-292 (resolve-or-suspend -- the under-determined-vs-missing distinction), WI-420 (bare-op unsatisfiable requirement analogue), req_insertion.rs:46-79, typing.rs (MissingRequiresForSpecOp at 106/287/467, no-instance error at 6331). Rust-only (typer; scaland has no typer)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-300"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-05T16:20:08Z"))
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-646", name: "rust")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-643", description: some(value: "Simp rewriter convergence PHASE 3: unite the TERM and OCCURRENCE carriers onto ONE iterative driver. After WI-641 the two OCCURRENCE rewriters share one iterative work-stack (simp_rewrite::rewrite, parameterized over the SimpFirer trait), but apply_eq_rules still has TWO drivers split by carrier: a Value::Node redex routes through the iterative simp_rewrite::rewrite, while a Value::Term redex takes a hand-written RECURSIVE walk (resolve.rs apply_eq_rules steps 1-2: recurse apply_eq_rules(child, fuel-1) per subterm, rebuild Term::Fn, then top-fire via fire_simp_equation, fixpoint-recurse). The iterative driver itself is hardcoded to Rc<NodeOccurrence> (SimpFirer::fire and rewrite's Visit/Build/reassemble/for_each_child/is_rewritable are all occurrence-specific).\n\nTWO CONSEQUENCES this removes: (a) the term carrier is HOST-RECURSIVE, so it can overflow on a deeply-nested term redex (no iterative heap walk like the Node path's WI-641 fix); (b) the two carriers bound fuel DIFFERENTLY and necessarily so today -- the recursive term walk must spend fuel on DESCENT DEPTH (fuel-1 per level) to avoid overflow, so a term nested deeper than fuel stops rewriting, whereas the iterative Node driver descends the whole tree and spends fuel only on the fire->refire chain (documented at resolve.rs apply_eq_rules doc, WI-641). Uniting removes both: the term carrier becomes iterative (no overflow) and both carriers get fire-chain-only fuel (same firing DECISIONS, same depth reach).\n\nAPPROACH: generalize simp_rewrite::rewrite (and Visit/Build/visit_node/build_node) over a CARRIER abstraction -- child iteration, reassembly, and the leaf/descend test -- so both a term (TermId / Value::Term, via get_term children + alloc Term::Fn reassembly) and an occurrence (Rc<NodeOccurrence>, via for_each_child + simp_rewrite::reassemble) drive the SAME iterative loop; SimpFirer::fire becomes carrier-neutral (&Value or a TermView) firing via fire_simp_equation. Then apply_eq_rules's Value::Term branch routes through the unified driver too, deleting the recursive steps 1-2. Keep firing decisions identical (fire at every node incl. bare leaves -- the WI-641 fire/descend split -- and match_view_oneway is unchanged). NOTE the driver already fires carrier-neutrally at the leaf (fire_simp_equation handles term + Node), so only the DESCENT/REASSEMBLY needs the carrier abstraction.\n\nBUNDLED (optional, same convergence story -- split out if they grow): (i) add a has_simp_equations(kb) gate so apply_eq_rules short-circuits the whole walk when the KB has no indexed [simp]/[unfold] equations (simp_rewrite::run already gates once; needs an entry/inner split so the recursion doesn't re-check per level), and consider caching the indexed simp-rule id list instead of the per-node double rules_by_functor(eq)+rules_by_functor(unify) alloc in fire_simp_equation (pre-existing, amplified by WI-641's per-node firing). (ii) merge the resolver's simp_requires_guard_holds(&Value, non-spec-op->false) and the typer's simp_fire_guard_holds(&NodeOccurrence, non-spec-op->true) into one carrier-neutral guard -- blocked today by the &mut kb (value_type_term) vs &kb (typer holds immutable) split AND the different non-spec-op defaults (typer fires monomorphic identities unconditionally; resolver only reaches its guard behind equation_is_requires_guarded); resolve the borrow split first.\n\nACCEPTANCE: one iterative driver handles BOTH carriers (the recursive term walk in apply_eq_rules is gone); fuel bounds only the fire->refire chain on both carriers; a deeply-nested TERM redex (analogous to apply_eq_rules_deep_node_goal_does_not_overflow's 200k Node depth) simplifies without host-stack overflow (new test); cargo test green. REF: WI-641 (Phase 2 -- occurrence-driver merge + SimpFirer trait, delivered 7d428b63), WI-640 (Phase 1), WI-283, WI-278 (why the occurrence walk was made iterative). Rust-only (simp_rewrite.rs + resolve.rs; scaland has no typer/simp)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-641"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-05T21:11:03Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-646", description: some(value: "Simp rewriter convergence follow-up (efficiency + DRY after WI-643 united the term/occurrence carriers onto one iterative Value-carrier driver). Five items, all in rustland/anthill-core (resolve.rs + simp_rewrite.rs), no behavior change intended: (1) A CORRECT O(1) simp-firing gate as a KB-cached bit computed at load — mirror equation_is_directional_rewrite ([simp] OR [unfold]) over BOTH eq and unify functors, so apply_eq_rules can short-circuit a no-directional-rewrite KB WITHOUT the per-call rules_by_functor bucket scan (2 Vec allocs) that made WI-643's naive gate net-negative, and WITHOUT the [simp]-only/eq-only bug that silently skipped unfold-only or <=>-only KBs (WI-643 removed that gate entirely rather than ship it wrong). (2) Gather the eq+unify simp rule-id list ONCE per simp_rewrite::rewrite walk and thread it into the SimpFirer, instead of fire_simp_equation/try_fire re-gathering rules_by_functor(eq)+rules_by_functor(unify) at EVERY node (amplified by WI-641/643 per-node firing). (3) Extract the triplicated eq+unify selection (has_simp_equations + fire_simp_equation + try_fire all spell 'rids = rules_by_functor(eq); if unify != eq { extend(rules_by_functor(unify)) }') into ONE simp_equation_rids(kb) helper so the gate can never drift from the fire sites again — that drift is exactly what caused the WI-643 regression (has_simp_equations was eq-only while the fire sites scan eq+unify). (4) Give reassemble_value's Value::Term arm an unchanged-check (compare rewritten children to the originals by TermId, like the Node arm's ChildCursor.changed/Rc::ptr_eq) so it skips kb.alloc(Term::Fn) + the two SmallVec builds on an unchanged subtree — now hit on every node since WI-643 removed the fuel-as-depth cutoff. (5) Fix the pre-existing has_simp_equations eq-only + [simp]-only narrowness that leaves the typer's simp_enabled (typing.rs; simp_enabled = has_simp_equations(kb) || kb.has_dot_applies) UNDER-firing for a KB whose [simp] laws are all <=>-headed (the stdlib case: 14/14) and which has no dot-applies. REF: WI-643 (Phase 3, driver unification), WI-641 (Phase 2), WI-640 (Phase 1). Rust-only. Found by /code-review during WI-643."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-643"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "opus-wi646", at: "2026-07-05T22:19:02Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-644", author: "user", content: "CORRECTION (per 'no ticket-spinoff for open work'): this ticket is NOT superseded -- it IS the implementation driver for library proposal 004 (design: docs/proposals/library/004-partial-vs-total-equality-and-ordering.md). The earlier plan to spin off a separate driver (WI-647) was reverted and WI-647 deleted; WI-644's description now carries the build steps. The design evolved from this ticket's original 'drop the universal Eq default' framing to the principled PartialEq/Eq + PartialOrd/Ord split.", at: "2026-07-05T21:22:13Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-645", author: "user", content: "Principled resolution filed: library proposal 004 (PartialEq/Eq/PartialOrd/Ord split) + driver WI-644 -- this is the ticket's DIRECTION B. library-004 build-step 2 IS the fix (gate sem_eq_core's reflexivity fast-path to reflexive-Eq carriers; eval builtin_eq IEEE-for-Float) and un-ignores the wi645 repro test. The interim direction A (make Float eq IEEE without the spec split) is unnecessary if library-004 lands, since the split is what JUSTIFIES the IEEE answer (Float provides PartialEq, not lawful Eq). Prior art confirmed against Lean: it withholds LawfulBEq Float for non-reflexivity AND non-congruence (-0.0).", at: "2026-07-05T21:22:13Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-622", description: some(value: "rule-body dot obligations should be relational-aware (float vs definite), generalizing WI-602 beyond value-preconditions. WI-602 made value-preconditions refutation-aware in rule-body dot dispatch and inverted dispatch_dots_in_occ to surface ALL type_check_node errors except DotDispatchNoMatch{receiver_sort:None} (removing a blanket Err(_) silent-skip). WI-557 premise: a rule body is relational, so op-body call-site obligations do not apply to symbolic rule-body vars. That premise is BROADER than value-preconditions: two OTHER op-body-only obligations now surface as potentially-SPURIOUS hard load errors for a rule-body value-method-dot. (1) spec-op DispatchNoMatch -- an abstract-spec-op dot (?x.compare(?y)) cannot be licensed because the rule-body env has empty op_requires (an op body requires Ordered[T] licenses it). (2) UnconstrainedTypeParam -- dispatch_dots_in_occ types the dot with expected=None, so an op whose type-param appears ONLY in its return type cannot be pinned (an op-body call site supplies expected via a let-annotation / return position), though it would resolve by unification at resolution time. Lower-confidence third case: argument-conformance TypeMismatch when a rule var infers to reflect Term (the pair.anthill self-typed-access weakness). UNREACHABLE today (no value-method-dot exists in any rule body in stdlib/tests -- suite is green), LATENT for future user programs. FIX TEMPLATE (per WI-602): make each obligation relational-aware -- definite violation raises, a float defers to runtime value-directed dispatch -- rather than an unconditional hard-error. Discovered by the WI-602 /code-review (3 finder angles converged on dispatch_dots_in_occ)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-602"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-06T06:28:26Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-629", description: some(value: "Resolver NAF over carrier-neutral (Value-carried) goals: two verdict-honesty gaps (pre-existing; found during WI-616 /code-review, finders A/E). (1) value_is_ground (resolve.rs ~2605) has NO Value::Entity/Tuple arm — falls to '_ => true' — so step_naf's groundness gate reads a not(Value::Entity{...}) wrapper goal (the shape lower_query synthesizes via make_goal_value for not/or) as GROUND even when unbound vars sit inside its children; NAF then runs and can decide/flounder where it should delay-and-rotate (contrast value_has_open_world_ref_inner, which HAS the Entity recursion arm — value_is_ground needs the same; WI-616's value_deep_ground in resolve.rs is a ready-made deep checker to unify with). (2) step_naf's ground/inner-floundered arm (~1735) pops the frame and yields Solution{residual:[not(P)]} WITHOUT checking whether the frame still holds TAIL goals — goals[1..] are silently dropped from the yielded solution, so a conjunction reads satisfied-modulo-residual while a conjunct was never attempted (the Delay branch at ~1666 correctly rotates when tail goals exist). Fix both together: deep groundness for value-carried inners + carry-or-rotate the tail on the floundered yield; add regression tests with not(Entity) wrappers from LogicalQuery paths."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-06T08:48:57Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-649", author: "user", content: "OCCURS-CHECK ANALYSIS + preferred fix direction (from design discussion). Occurs-check = before binding ?v |-> rhs, verify ?v does NOT occur in rhs (else the bind builds an infinite/cyclic term). SOUND form must walk rhs THROUGH sigma, not just the literal rhs -- otherwise it misses MUTUAL cycles: bind ?w|->E{?v} (ok, ?w not in it), later bind ?v|->E{?w} (ok, ?v not in it), yet sigma is now cyclic ?v->E{?w}->E{E{?v}}->... . THE CODEBASE ALREADY HAS THE SOUND TRANSITIVE CHECK: occurs_in_value(vid, value, work) at resolve.rs:4179 -- it chases work.resolve_as_value(w) so it catches the mutual case, and is carrier-agnostic (walks Value::Entity/Tuple children via the TermView pos_arg/named_arg interface), i.e. it already covers exactly the entity carriers WI-629 made reify_value descend into. The unifier already gates on it (resolve.rs:3969/4089/4096) and WI-624 uses the same idea to drop a cyclic answer_link. THE GAP is the DIRECT fast-path binds that bypass unification: in step_choice_point's fact/external-row match, bind_compressed (term fast path) and bind_waking (the Value::Entity external-row path, ~resolve.rs:1949-1958) bind WITHOUT calling occurs_in_value. That skip is deliberate (fact-binding is the hot inner loop; occurs-check is O(rhs)) and is precisely why a cyclic sigma can form. TRADEOFF: reify-side guard (option a) pays per REIFY; bind-side occurs-check (option b) pays per BIND -- and binds vastly outnumber reifies, so blanket occurs-checking the hot term fast-path is the more expensive choice the original design intentionally avoided. PREFERRED DIRECTION = HYBRID: occurs-check ONLY the rare path that can actually carry an entity cycle -- the bind_waking / Value::Entity external-row bind -- and leave the hot term fast-path alone. On a positive occurs_in_value, drop the candidate as contradictory (exactly the WI-624 answer_link move). This closes the one reachable route to a cyclic entity sigma, is cheap (external-row binds are uncommon), gives the strong 'sigma stays acyclic' invariant, and means reify_value needs NO per-call guard. A reify/reify_value depth-cap (REACH_DEPTH_CAP-style) then becomes just an optional cheap belt-and-suspenders backstop rather than the primary mechanism. Regression test still applies: construct the cyclic external-row bind and assert the candidate is dropped (no cyclic sigma reaches reify), rather than a stack overflow.", at: "2026-07-06T09:03:59Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-627", description: some(value: "Equation classification is SHORT-NAME based and silently swallows own-op eq rules (sibling of WI-619). is_equation (kb/mod.rs ~2931) = head functor SHORT-NAMED eq/unify + 2 positional args + empty body; is_equational_head (load.rs ~4487) = short name =/eq/unify. Consequence found in WI-616: a carrier's OWN bodyless 2-ary eq rule — rule eq(empty(), empty()) on Map.eq, a genuinely RELATIONAL base case under the WI-444 short-name override convention — is classified as an equational LAW, unindexed (WI-139 cite-required) and retain-filtered out of SLD candidates (resolve.rs ~933): the base case silently never fires (worked around in map.anthill via the eq->map_eq delegator; see its comment). ALSO: the bodied Set.eq/Map.eq rules ARE unindexed from rules_by_functor by is_equational_head at load (they fire only because SLD candidates come from the discrim query which retains them) — fragile. FIX DIRECTION: classify by RESOLVED SYMBOL — the equality-connective heads carry the canonical anthill.prelude.Eq.eq / anthill.kernel.unify symbols (WI-530 removed the <ns>.eq goal-shadow minting), so compare functor == those symbols (with the bare-name fallback only for kernel-less unit KBs) instead of short-name matching; a rule on a DIFFERENT op that merely shares the short name (Set.eq, Map.eq, any future carrier Eq instance) is then a normal rule. Per the loud-over-silent principle, if any ambiguity remains, the loader should WARN when it reclassifies a user rule head into a cite-required law. Acceptance: rule eq(empty(), empty()) inside Map fires as an SLD base case (drop the map_eq delegator or keep it and pin with a new test); WI-139 behavior unchanged for genuine =/<=> equations; full suite green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-06T11:17:18Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-650", description: some(value: "Map: drop the O(n^2) relational eq/binds/strip_is apparatus and back Eq[T=Map] at the operation level (KEEP provides Eq[T=Map] + a BODYLESS operation eq(a:Map,b:Map)->Bool; the provision is backed because the spec op Eq.eq is a builtin — op_backed path 3 — so the stdlib still loads). Consequence to guard: WI-616 SemEq dispatch (sem_eq_core -> sem_eq_dispatch, resolve.rs ~3592) sub-resolves the declared Map.eq override, finds NO rules, exhausts, and returns sem_verdict(false) = SILENTLY 'not equal' (order-sensitive structural-ish wrong answer). BuiltinResult has no error channel (Success/Delay/Failure only), so make it a LOUD TYPE-TIME diagnostic instead: an =/eq/neq (Eq.eq/Eq.neq) application whose operand sort declares its OWN eq override that has NO backing (no non-fact rules, no runnable body) is a compile error 'Eq[<sort>] declared but unimplemented (pending WI-625 host bridge)'. Per use-site: the stdlib never compares maps so it stays clean; only user code comparing maps errors. HOOK: mirror check_one_spec_op_requirement (typing.rs ~24636) which already reads each operand's inferred sort head via inferred_type()->sort_functor_of_view; but it returns early on is_builtin and Eq.eq IS a builtin, so add an eq-specific check BEFORE that skip (or a sibling pass) in BOTH the rule-body-goal pass and the op-body (WI-325) pass. Reuse an op_backed-style predicate for the carrier's OWN override (not the spec-op builtin path, which would mask it). TEST FALLOUT (wi616_semantic_eq_test.rs): DELETE map_eq_ignores_put_order / map_eq_honors_put_shadowing / map_eq_distinguishes_values_and_domains / map_binds_unifies_non_ground_stored_value (test the removed rules; general WI-624/633 invariants stay covered by the unbox0/unbox1 Box tests); re-vehicle dispatch_on_non_ground_operand_suspends_and_never_binds onto Set (keeps its eq); ADD a test asserting eq(map,map) in a rule/op body is a LOUD type error; update comments mentioning Map.binds. Follow-up to WI-627. Acceptance: Map has no relational eq/binds/strip_is; stdlib loads; a rule/op body comparing two Maps fails to load with the new diagnostic; Set eq unaffected; enums/ADTs keep structural eq; full suite green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-07T08:01:21Z"))
fact StoreFormat(version: 1)
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-505", author: "user", content: "WI-434 half B (template-stamp) folded here (2026-07-07, user decision): WI-434 delivered its data-format (StoreFormat) versioning half; the template-drift half — version-stamping the scaffolded rules.anthill/domain.anthill — is SUPERSEDED by this WI's bundling. Bundling the domain/rules version-locks them structurally (no per-project copy to drift), the same way WI-412 (WI-434's named twin) was resolved by consolidation rather than stamp-and-hash. So do NOT build a TemplateInfo stamp+hash mechanism; when bundling lands here, template drift is closed by construction. WI-434's StoreFormat versioning (version.anthill entity + version_check + migrate) is the reference model for reporting a bundle-vs-project version mismatch loudly.", at: "2026-07-07T09:15:38Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-434", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED (2026-07-07) the DATA-FORMAT half only; template-stamp half (B) folded into WI-505 (user decision — bundling version-locks templates structurally, superseding TemplateInfo stamp+hash; driver's silent never-match already made loud by WI-433). WHAT LANDED: anthill-native (no host prescan). New version.anthill bundle file defines entity StoreFormat(version:Int64) — bundle-owned so it resolves regardless of project domain age (store.anthill/main.anthill import it; if it lived in a stale project domain the whole bundle would fail load on exactly the old projects the check targets). main.anthill: version_check() runs before each bundle command (query over facts_of StoreFormat) — empty=pre-versioning warn, version!=current_store_format()(=1) warn stale; SKIPPED for migrate (which owns its messaging). New migrate subcommand stamps a pre-versioning project THROUGH the store (WorkItemStore.stamp_format persists fact StoreFormat), idempotent, refuses un-migratable version bumps loudly (explicit data-migrators = future work). init stamps new workitems.anthill via host const CURRENT_STORE_FORMAT_VERSION, paired to bundle current_store_format() by fresh_init test. New reflect builtin term_as_int (mirrors term_as_string). Tests: cmd_version_stamp_test.rs (7). Fixed 10 anthill-core fixture tests that load store/main without version.anthill (added it to load sets: wi204_smoke/sort_param/let_ctor_env, wi219, wi236, wi237). /code-review high: fixed migrate double-warn + corrected false 'facts_of yields declaration head' comments across 4 files. Dogfooded: migrated this repo's own tracker. Full suite green.", at: "2026-07-07T09:16:11Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-434", description: some(value: "anthill-todo: per-project VERSIONING + startup PRESCAN of the project's todo files (user request 2026-06-11; driver = the 9fa0da6 incident: a broken rules.anthill template copy persisted SILENTLY in every project scaffolded by an older 'anthill-todo init', breaking claim/next with no diagnostic). Two halves, one mechanism: (1) VERSION STAMPS -- 'anthill-todo init' stamps every scaffolded file (rules.anthill, domain.anthill, ...) AND the workitems data format with a version fact (e.g. fact TemplateInfo(file: \"rules\", version: N) / fact StoreFormat(version: N)); the binary embeds the current versions next to its include_str! templates. (2) PRESCAN at CLI startup, before any subcommand executes against the project: compare stamped versions vs the binary's; on mismatch warn LOUDLY (loud-error principle -- the motivating bug was a silent never-match) naming file, found/current versions, and the upgrade path; a missing stamp = pre-versioning project, warn once. (3) UPGRADE path: 'anthill-todo migrate' (or init --refresh) rewrites an outdated template ONLY if unmodified (hash against known historical template versions; locally-edited files are reported, never overwritten); data-format bumps get explicit migrators. RELATION: rides naturally AFTER WI-009 (prescan belongs in the anthill bundle, not new native run_* logic); not a cutover blocker. Project-side twin of WI-412 (embedded-stdlib drift) and sibling of WI-433 (the never-match the stale template carried)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-07T09:16:27Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-652", author: "user", content: "PERF MEASUREMENT CORRECTION + user-reported regression (2026-07-07): WI-652's own text says 'no perf impact measured -- WI-650 added ~0.2s to a ~9s debug stdlib load'. That is UNDERSTATED. On the anthill-todo CLI (embedded stdlib+bundle, debug), check_eq_override_backing costs ~1.5s: disabling it drops type_check_sorts from 3.63s -> 2.09s (ANTHILL_LOAD_TIMING=1, repo project, 114 sorts). User observed the slowdown directly ('was not a problem yesterday', pre-WI-650 07-06) and pinpointed the cause as 'why load stdlib 4 times?' -- i.e. the 4 whole-KB body walks in type_check_sorts_typed: check_operation_bodies + record_find_dictionary_grounding(WI-300) + check_rule_body_requirements(WI-642) + check_eq_override_backing(WI-650). The DEDUP (shared walk_body_occurrences collapsing all four) is therefore a real perf win (~1.5s recoverable from WI-650's walk alone, more from sharing the other three), NOT just an altitude cleanup. NOTE for the dedup: check_eq_override_backing only fires on user/project code (the stdlib never compares Maps by WI-650's design), so scoping its walk to non-stdlib bodies alone would recover the ~1.5s for CLI consumers. Deferred by user (2026-07-07): document-only, no fix this session.", at: "2026-07-07T10:09:56Z")
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-653", name: "perf")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-653", author: "user", content: "USER DIRECTIVE (2026-07-07): type-checking must NOT be disabled/skipped -- it must be made FAST while staying fully enabled. This REJECTS the earlier fix direction (b) 'skip the pure-check typer passes for trusted stdlib'. Revised directions, all keeping full type-checking: (1) DEDUP the 4 whole-KB body walks into one shared traversal (WI-652) -- one pass instead of four. (2) MAKE check_eq_override_backing's walk genuinely cheap: 1.5s to walk stdlib bodies once is too slow for an O(nodes) traversal -- profile the per-node cost (occurrence-tree walk, rules_by_functor Vec alloc in the backing probe even though memoized, is_eq_call symbol compares) and cut it; the check stays, just fast. (3) BIG LEVER, type-checking-preserving: the embedded stdlib+bundle is IMMUTABLE (baked into the binary) -- type-check it ONCE and CACHE/memoize the resulting KB (serialize a pre-checked KB image, or a build-time artifact) so each CLI invocation reuses it instead of re-checking 3.63s of unchanged code every run. This AMORTIZES the check rather than disabling it -- aligned with 'should be fast'. Only the per-project workitems.anthill changes between runs and needs (incremental) checking. Prefer (3) for the durable win + (1)/(2) as they compose. Acceptance unchanged: full type-checking still runs (map_eq_error_test.rs green), status drops toward pre-WI-650 timing, full suite green.", at: "2026-07-07T10:11:18Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-653", author: "user", content: "USER CLARIFICATION (2026-07-07): the type phase is not pure checking -- it applies TRANSFORMATIONS THE RUNTIME REQUIRES: [simp] rewriting runs INSIDE the typer (typing::build_type, tree-producing -> set_op_body_node writes rewritten bodies back), plus dispatch_rewrites + req_insertion::run (WI-231/WI-325) and flow_derive. Eval/resolution consume these rewritten bodies. So 'skip the typer for trusted stdlib' (rejected direction b) is doubly wrong: it would break RUNNING (the rewrites wouldn't happen), not merely relax checking. This CONSTRAINS the cache design (direction 3): the memoized artifact must be the FULLY-LOADED, TYPED, AND REWRITTEN KB image (post build_type, post req_insertion), not the raw parsed stdlib -- so a cache hit yields a KB that both passed checks AND carries the run-ready transformed bodies. Invalidation key = the embedded stdlib+bundle content hash (baked into the binary, so bumps only on rebuild); the per-project workitems.anthill loads/typechecks incrementally on top. Directions (1) dedup walks and (2) make check_eq_override_backing's walk cheap remain valid independent speedups that also help the one-time cache-build cost.", at: "2026-07-07T10:11:53Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-651", description: some(value: "neq(map, map) SILENT-MISDECIDE hazard escapes the WI-650 load check. check_eq_override_backing catches eq(map,map) because the typer stamps the eq operand's inferred_type as the concrete carrier Map; but for a neq(?a,?b) rule-body goal over two Maps the typer UNDER-DETERMINES each operand's inferred_type to the ABSTRACT param anthill.prelude.Map.K (neq's dictionary threading picks up Map's 'requires Eq[T=K]' rather than concretizing the receiver to Map, unlike eq which is a direct SemEq builtin). So operand_unbacked_eq_carrier reads Map.K (a sort-param symbol, correctly declined), the check does NOT fire, and neq(map1,map2) — which resolves as not(eq(map1,map2)) over the empty Map.eq override — silently returns the WRONG Bool at runtime (equal maps read as neq=true). This is the same silent-misdecide class WI-650 exists to prevent, for neq instead of eq; narrow (only Map, only neq, only unbacked-override carriers). ROOT CAUSE is the typer's neq operand-type inference (likely the neq(?a,?b) <=> not(eq(?a,?b)) unfold interacting with Map's requires), NOT the WI-650 check itself (which already matches Eq.neq via is_eq_call and would fire if the operand concretized). FIX DIRECTIONS: (a) make the typer concretize a neq operand to its receiver carrier the way eq does (preferred — closes it at the source, and would let map_eq_error_test.rs add a neq-in-rule-body case, currently omitted with a NOTE); (b) as a fallback, have check_eq_override_backing recognize a neq/eq operand typed as a carrier type-PARAM and probe the enclosing dictionary — brittle, rejected unless (a) is infeasible. Acceptance: neq(map,map) in a rule/op body fails to load with the EqOverrideUnbacked diagnostic (mirror the eq tests); full suite green. Follow-up to WI-650."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-07T11:43:50Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-654", description: some(value: "Carrier-aware transitive op-requires coverage (DELIVERED). op_requires_covers licensed an abstract spec-op call when the enclosing op's declared requires TRANSITIVELY reached the call's spec, but compared only spec SYMBOLS -- wrongly licensing e.g. a Bar-op over the sibling param of requires Foo[A,B] whose Foo requires Bar[B]. Fix (consumer-side, no storage change): the carrier link IS present in the stored op-requires (held positionally), so thread it through the requires graph -- op_requires_entry_carrier_map + compose_reached_carrier_map + reached_carrier_matches_call -- composing then sigma-aligning the reached carrier against the deferred call's carrier. NOTE: this is the work committed as 'WI-653' on the wi-644-partial-total-eq-ordering branch (commits 6eccc6e1, 28da049e); that branch-local WI-653 label COLLIDED with main's WI-653 (anthill-todo perf regression) at merge time, so re-filed here as a fresh id. Code + commit messages retain the WI-653 label. Unblocks WI-625."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-07T13:50:31Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-636", author: "claude", content: "Ticket's LATENT/no-live-producer premise DISPROVEN during /code-review: the WI-625 eval->SLD eq/neq bridge feeds raw ground Value::Entity{ctor,[Value::Tuple...]} operands into a rule-backed eq's head match, reaching with_fresh_vars. A first-cut panic on the un-reifiable carrier would abort the PROCESS on legit user code -> pivoted to graceful candidate-drop (contradiction=true), so eq falls back to its structural verdict. FIX: top normalization (precedes arity split) routes EVERY non-Term tree_subst carrier via value_to_term -- reify structural subset (Node/Entity-with-scalars/scalar/Var; Entity-with-scalars now FIRES the rule, was silently dropped), drop the whole candidate on the un-reifiable residue (Tuple/opaque/Unit/Entity-carrying-one). answer_links is Term-only BY CONSTRUCTION (all .bind->Value::Term); the resolver debug_assert is a construction tripwire, NOT a WI-636 completeness guard (a dropped link passes all(is_term) vacuously). WI-634(c)'s instantiate_eq_rhs ref is STALE (deleted -> fire_simp_equation/match_view); refreshed 3 comment refs. Deeper fix = reify eq operands at the producer (eq-bridge) = WI-625 follow-up, not a new ticket.", at: "2026-07-07T18:44:45Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-636", description: some(value: "with_fresh_vars: non-Term Value-carrier tree_subst entries are silently dropped (LATENT — PLAUSIBLE-no-live-producer per the WI-624 /code-review verify pass; parity/loud-over-silent hardening). Both WI-624 passes walk tree_subst.iter_terms() (Value::Term-only, subst.rs:560-565): (a) a SYNTHETIC u32::MAX-n entry bound to a non-Term Value (scalar/Entity via BindValue::Value, term_view.rs ~1135) never enters body_rename — for a nonlinear head the query-var link then dangles on a never-bound fresh var, the WI-624 symptom on the Value carrier; (b) non-Term query-var LINK entries never reach answer_links at all, while the fact fast-path preserves them via bind_waking (resolve.rs ~1900-1908, proposal 026.1 lineage) — a bind_waking-parity gap for the bodyless arity>0 rules WI-624 rerouted. WHY LATENT: the top-of-function normalization rescues Value::Node (the realistic carrier — occurrence goals convert sigma-bound scalars to Expr::Const Node leaves, node_occurrence.rs ~3579, and panic LOUDLY on Entity); Term goals stay term-land (Path extraction vs a Term head always yields TermId); scalar Value children reaching a DeBruijn head match need a Value-carrier rule head with vars — none constructible today (bodyless value heads are always arity 0 per push_value_head_entry kb/mod.rs:1062; REFUTED-reachability in the review). ACTION when it becomes reachable (WI-342-P3 value-head-with-vars territory, or any new Value-carrier producer): extend the normalization to reify/route ALL non-Term entries or fail LOUD on the unhandled carrier instead of the silent iter_terms skip; also give answer_links a bind_waking-parity path in the resolver rule branch. Related: instantiate_eq_rhs has the same iter_terms skip (noted in WI-634 (c))."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-07T18:44:48Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-653", author: "user", content: "CORRECTED DIAGNOSIS (2026-07-07, current main, 117 sorts, debug): the ~1.5s attributed to check_eq_override_backing (WI-650) does NOT reproduce. Measured per-pass breakdown of type_check_sorts (2.75s total) via temp instrumentation inside type_check_sorts_typed (added then reverted): per-sort check_operation_bodies (type inference + simp rewriting) = 2.34s / 85%; type_rule_bodies = 0.24s / 9%; free-op check_operation_bodies = 0.13s / 5%; check_rule_body_requirements (WI-642) = 30ms; check_operation_signatures = 4ms; record_find_dictionary_grounding (WI-300) = 0.5ms; check_eq_override_backing (WI-650) = 2ms / 0.07%. A/B disabling the WI-650 pass entirely: 2.77s to 2.77s, no change. CONSEQUENCES: direction (2) make-check_eq_override_backing-cheap is MOOT (already 2ms; it is a plain O(nodes) structural scan with no mechanism to cost 1.5s while the same-shaped WI-642 walk is 30ms -- the filing-time figure was a measurement confound, or was erased by the WI-644 / WI-625 / WI-651 landings that all touched typing.rs after the measurement, and type_check_sorts itself is already down from the filed 3.63s to 2.75s). Direction (1) dedup-the-4-walks is NEGLIGIBLE as perf: the three cheap post-walks (WI-300 0.5ms + WI-642 30ms + WI-650 2ms) sum to ~33ms; a shared walk_body_occurrences saves at most that -- keep it as the WI-652 ALTITUDE cleanup, not a perf item. The real 90% is check_operation_bodies = build_type + simp rewriting, which the user directives require to STAY ENABLED (it produces the runtime-consumed rewritten bodies). Note WI-650 also REMOVED Map O(n^2) relational eq rules, so on net it likely did not regress type-check time at all. Also debug-only: the WI notes debug is a 10-50x amplifier; a release CLI masks all of it (total load ~4.3s debug here, sub-second in release). RE-SCOPE (user chose option A, 2026-07-07): the PERF-REGRESSION framing is RETIRED. WI-653 is narrowed to direction (3) ONLY -- cache the fully-typed+rewritten KB image (post build_type, post req_insertion), keyed on the embedded stdlib+bundle content hash, so each CLI invocation reuses it instead of re-running the ~2.47s check_operation_bodies; the per-project workitems.anthill loads/typechecks incrementally on top. WI-653 is now a dev-time load-latency optimization judged on that payoff, NOT a regression fix. Directions (1) and (2) are dropped from WI-653.", at: "2026-07-07T16:14:49Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-652", author: "user", content: "PERF-CORRECTION SUPERSEDED (2026-07-07, current main, 117 sorts, debug): the earlier 2026-07-07 note on this item -- that the dedup is a real perf win with ~1.5s recoverable from check_eq_override_backing -- is itself now corrected by direct per-pass measurement (see WI-653 corrected-diagnosis feedback). check_eq_override_backing costs 2ms, not 1.5s; the three cheap post-walks (WI-300 0.5ms + WI-642 30ms + WI-650 2ms) sum to ~33ms, so collapsing them into a shared walk_body_occurrences saves at most ~33ms. The dedup reverts to its ORIGINAL framing: an ALTITUDE / DRY cleanup, NOT a perf win. The real WI-652 content is unchanged and still valuable on correctness grounds: closing the coverage gaps in check_eq_override_backing (compound operands + dot-form a.eq(b) + constraint/guard bodies are the reachable ones), plus the single parameterized backed-by(kind) helper unifying carrier_has_unbacked_eq_override with op_backed. No perf urgency; scope this on correctness/altitude.", at: "2026-07-07T16:14:54Z")
