fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-658", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED via option (d) — a `NonEq` spec + Float instance + a load-time `Eq` ⊥ `NonEq` mutual-exclusion check (`check_eq_noneq_exclusive` / `LoadError::IncompatibleEqNonEq`), NOT the a/b/c `eq_refl`-refutation menu the ticket originally proposed. RATIONALE: a probe showed integrity constraints are NOT load-enforced (a `:- ...` constraint never fires at load or `anthill check`), so the mutual-exclusion had to live in the provider conformance pass, not as a language constraint. Float `provides NonEq` (witness `nonEqRefl() = nan`); a user `provides Eq[Float]` is now a load error; opt-in — does nothing without a NonEq declaration (`eq_refl` stays dormant/documentation-only, it is NOT discharged). Regression test `wi658_eq_noneq_exclusive_test`. /code-review (3 agents, max effort): no correctness bug; fixed the stale `eq.anthill`/`float.anthill` comments that still described the abandoned eq_refl-discharge approach. CARRY-OVER to WI-664: the check keys on the carrier BASE symbol, so it must become binding-aware before parametric/composite carriers provide Eq at one instantiation and NonEq at another. Composite (entity / named-tuple) congruent Eq/NonEq derivation + the coupled `semantic_equal` structural-reflexivity laundering soundness fix (`eq(Point(nan,_),Point(nan,_))` currently launders to true) = WI-664 (depends on this).", at: "2026-07-07T23:56:25Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-658", description: some(value: "Enforce the `Eq` reflexivity law `eq_refl` PER-INSTANCE at LOAD time -- the instance-law conformance check WI-644 promised but never delivered. A `provides Eq[T=X]` / `fact Eq[T=X]` whose carrier X can REFUTE `eq_refl: eq(?a,?a) <=> true` must FAIL to load. Canonical case: IEEE Float -- `eq(nan,nan)=false` refutes reflexivity, so `provides Eq[T=Float]` must be rejected (Float lawfully provides only PartialEq/PartialOrd). Lawful carriers (Int64/BigInt/String/Bool, and the TotalFloat newtype) discharge `eq_refl` and keep loading.\n\nWHY THIS EXISTS -- ORPHANED ENFORCEMENT. WI-644's ACCEPTANCE requires \"PartialEq/Eq/PartialOrd/Ord load with eq_refl a CHECKED law\" and \"a non-reflexive `provides Eq[T=Float]` FAILS to load\"; its description asserts \"Float does NOT provide -- the instance-law conformance checker rejects it automatically.\" That enforcement was DEFERRED and then MIS-ATTRIBUTED to WI-650. But WI-650 was REPURPOSED: its delivered commit 31aec94b drops Map's O(n^2) relational eq apparatus and guards an unbacked Map.eq override -- its diff touches only stdlib/anthill/prelude/map.anthill, kb/typing.rs, and Map tests; NOTHING about eq_refl / reflexivity / Float. So the per-instance eq_refl discharge currently has NO OWNER. The stale pointers must be repointed HERE: the project memory (wi644-partial-total-eq-ordering) and the code comment in rustland/anthill-stl/anthill/float.anthill both still say \"auto-refuting a `provides Eq[Float]` is WI-650\" -- false.\n\nEMPIRICAL GAP (verified 2026-07-07): `anthill load` of a namespace asserting `fact Eq[T = Float]` (stdlib auto-included) loads CLEAN -- exit 0, \"loaded: 2076 facts, 106 rules\", no rejection. Today `eq_refl` (stdlib/anthill/prelude/eq.anthill:36) is a DORMANT obligation: it documents the contract but is never discharged/refuted per instance (eq.anthill:12-16 says so explicitly).\n\nMECHANISM (reuse existing machinery, no new language surface -- per WI-644's own \"NO new language machinery\" note): the instance-law CONFORMANCE CHECKING in kb/load.rs (Specialization / requires-law Discharged, ~3866-3972, ~12425) already discharges a spec's laws at a concrete provider. Extend it so that for each `provides/fact Eq[T=X]` the parent spec's law `eq_refl` is CHECKED at carrier X, and a positive REFUTATION rejects the provision.\n\nKEY DESIGN QUESTION -- how the checker obtains a refutation witness for an infinite carrier. You CANNOT enumerate to PROVE `for-all a. eq(a,a)`, so reject on positive REFUTATION ONLY, never on mere non-discharge (stay sound under WI-628 truncation-awareness -- never-decide-undecided). Options:\n  (a) Special-value probe: discharge `eq_refl` against the carrier's declared distinguished constants -- Float exposes `nan`/`infinity`/`negativeInfinity` as term-level consts (WI-084); `eq(nan,nan)` reduces to false via the delivered IEEE eval/resolver path (WI-644 step 2) => refuted. Cheapest; catches Float.\n  (b) Host non-reflexive MARKER: a host carrier binding declares itself non-reflexive (the Rust `f64: !Eq` analogue); the checker rejects `provides Eq` for a marked carrier. Simplest/most robust; needs no reduction.\n  (c) Proof-pass discharge (WI-558 prove-verify): run `eq_refl` as a proof obligation over the carrier; an IEEE-Float model yields the `nan` counterexample.\nRECOMMEND (a)+(b) -- probe distinguished consts AND honor an explicit host non-reflexive marker. DISCUSS before building.\n\nACCEPTANCE:\n - A synthetic non-reflexive `provides Eq[T=Float]` (or `fact Eq[T=Float]`) FAILS to load with a conformance / law-refutation diagnostic naming `eq_refl`.\n - `provides PartialEq[T=Float]` / `provides PartialOrd[T=Float]` still load (partial is lawful, IEEE).\n - `provides Eq[T=TotalFloat]` and the lawful primitives' `fact Eq[T=Int64/BigInt/String/Bool]` still load.\n - `eq_refl` becomes an ENFORCED obligation; update its comment in stdlib/anthill/prelude/eq.anthill (currently \"not yet enforced ... WI-650\") and the rustland/anthill-stl/anthill/float.anthill comment to reference THIS ticket.\n - Repro test (mirror wi645 naming) asserting the Float provision is rejected and TotalFloat/Int64 accepted; full workspace `cargo-test` green.\n - Sibling WI-649 (use-site `requires Eq[K]` => `Map[K=Float]` load error) is COMPLEMENTARY (provision-site here vs call-site there) and unaffected.\n\nUnblocks CLOSING WI-644 (this is one of its two outstanding enforcement guarantees; the other is WI-649). Builds on WI-644's already-merged CORE (eq_refl law + IEEE eval/resolver path) -- no open dependency, so no cycle with the parent. REF: stdlib/anthill/prelude/{eq,float,totalfloat}.anthill, rustland/anthill-stl/anthill/float.anthill, kb/load.rs conformance hooks, WI-644 (parent, blocked on this), WI-649 (sibling), WI-650 (number REPURPOSED away from this -- do NOT rely on it), WI-558 (prove-verify), WI-628 (sound truncation), WI-084 (Float consts), docs/proposals/library/004. Rust + scaland (scaland has no operation/law loading -- mirror only if it gains conformance checking)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-07T23:56:26Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-625", author: "user", content: "gap 2 (retroactive instance-fact dispatch at SLD) DELIVERED, commit 86f88711 (rebased) — the LAST open gap; WI-625 now complete (gaps 1-6 + feedback 5/6 all closed). A 'fact PartialEq[T=X, eq=myEq]' bound a bodied eq op OFF-carrier; the eq-dispatch index (build_sort_ops_table pass 3) keyed only a carrier's OWN eq (carrier_own_op), so eq/neq over an instance-fact carrier answered STRUCTURALLY at both resolver (sem_eq_core) and eval (semantic_equal), diverging from the same program's List.member (dict-threaded). FIX: (1) instance_fact_eq_target keys the carrier's ctors+self-returning-ops to the bound op (fallback after carrier_own_op, reuses instance_fact_op_binding over PartialEq then Eq). (2) sem_eq_dispatch + semantic_equal branch on op_has_runnable_body: BODIED instance-fact op -> eval bridge; body-less rule-backed op (Set.eq = 'operation eq'+separate 'rule eq') -> prove_rule_predicate (unchanged). KEY LESSON: eval must dispatch the bodied op via an ISOLATED scratch interpreter (new bridge_eq_op_to_eval, sharing extracted run_in_bridge_interp with the gap-1 bridge_op_to_eval), NOT call_op_bridged — semantic_equal runs MID-TRAMPOLINE inside member's eq(head,x), and a nested run() corrupts the shared activation stack ('deliver: parent frame had no awaiting state'). The scratch mem::takes the KB, so i.stack is untouched; eval+SLD now share one dispatch path. /code-review high (3 agents): Finding 1 (real) fixed — bridge_eq_op_to_eval is THREE-WAY (Ok(Some)=decided / Ok(None)=undecided cap-or-suspend / Err=callee failed); undecided Delays at resolver, suspends(bridge)/errors(top-level) at eval; error propagates; NEVER a silent structural false for an applicable override. Finding 2 refuted (true/false are Literal::Bool -> Value::Bool always). Buried instance-fact carriers Delay for free (value_reaches_eq_override reads the shared index). Full workspace GREEN 2609. FOLLOW-UP (perf, deferred like gap-1): each instance-fact eq comparison spins a fresh scratch Interpreter re-registering ~100 builtins (member over N elems = N spins) — pool the interpreter / build the builtin table once.", at: "2026-07-08T07:05:04Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-625", description: some(value: "SLD->eval op-body dispatch bridge (the WI-616 NB prerequisite, scoped as its own WI as prescribed). WI-616's concrete tier landed WITHOUT it: carrier Eq impls are relational rules on the carrier's own eq op (Set.eq/Map.eq), so ordinary SLD is the evaluator and the SemEq builtin's goal-rewrite (BuiltinResult::ReplaceGoal -> <carrier>.eq(a,b)) is the dispatch. What the bridge still gates, in concrete terms: (1) HOST-BODIED spec-op impls invoked as rule-body goals — an op with a runnable body (match/if/let/recursion) matches NOTHING at SLD; today only the WI-483 structural FOLD reduces op-call OPERANDS, and non-foldable bodies residualize; a live resolver->Interpreter call (or resolver-native reduction) would let e.g. a bodied eq/member/subset run at resolution. (2) Retroactive instance-fact dispatch at SLD: fact Eq[T = X, eq = myEq] (WI-431 shape) — sem_eq_override_goal deliberately consults ONLY the sort_ops own-op tier (resolve.rs sem_eq_override_goal doc comment); the instance-fact tier needs a per-goal provides-fact scan AND its impls are typically bodied (gap 1). (3) WI-300 Tier B: abstract-arg requirement dictionaries — construct Value::Requirement into a resolver Gamma slot + weave body spec-ops + bridge Gamma->frame.requirements at op invocation (design docs/design/requirement-dictionaries.md sec 3-4; the WI-300 guard tier delivered the resolve-or-suspend half only). (4) eval-side dict dispatch to a RULE-BACKED op: resolve_op_target now returns Set.eq for an Eq[Set] dict, but eval cannot run a bodyless rule-backed op — currently a loud UnknownOperation; the bridge's dual (eval->SLD) would close it. Design refs: proposal 051 (Status note), WI-483 feedback (occurrence-native fold DECIDED, interpreter bridge = deferred follow-up), memory project_wi300_guard_tier."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-08T07:05:06Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-649", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED (hybrid, primary=occurs-check per preferred direction). Fix: occurs-check the ONE reachable cyclic-entity route — the non-Term external-row/value-fact bind in step_choice_point's fact fast-path (resolve.rs ~2009). Refined beyond the ticket's simple sketch to mirror unify_bind EXACTLY: chase_value(val) through merged FIRST, then (a) if the chased head IS vid, the bind is a vacuous variable equivalence (?v=?v, or ?v->?w when sigma already aliases ?w->..->?v) already encoded in sigma -> skip (continue), NOT drop; (b) else occurs_in_value(vid,head,merged) true => vid inside val's STRUCTURE => real occurs-failure => drop candidate (WI-624 answer_link move). This closes the correctness-agent's transitive-alias FALSE-DROP finding: the raw occurs_in_value conflates a pure var-alias loop with a structural cycle; chasing first disambiguates. sigma stays acyclic inductively so neither chase_value nor occurs_in_value diverges. Hot term fast-path (bind_compressed) left unchecked per user scope. DELIBERATE NON-SCOPE: (1) NO reify/reify_value depth-cap backstop — a cap on reify risks FALSE-POSITIVE panics on legitimately deep-but-finite structure (e.g. a long cons list reifies N-deep) with no clean cap value; occurs-check-at-formation is the principled fix, so reify_value needs no per-call guard (matches preferred direction). (2) site resolve.rs:889 (builtin extra-merge bind_waking) is the SAME bug class but currently UNREACHABLE (only occurrence_term/sub_occurrences bind non-Term there and have no live anthill caller; builtin_unify already occurs-checks) — DOCUMENTED with a pointer comment, not guarded, so a future reflect-caller addition reconsiders it. Regression test wi649_cyclic_external_row_bind_is_dropped_not_overflowed (route_dispatch_test.rs): a route handler returns a row referencing the goal's OWN query var (id -> wrap(?id)); asserts candidate dropped (0 solutions) + emit-witness (emits>=1) so 0 is non-vacuous. Verified: guard off => 1 solution, on => 0. Full anthill-core suite green (1042 + lib).", at: "2026-07-08T07:55:47Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-649", description: some(value: "reify/reify_value cyclic-substitution (sigma) stack overflow: the reification recursion has NO cycle guard / depth cap, so a cyclic sigma overflows the host stack instead of erroring LOUDLY or delaying. reify (term-Fn path, mod.rs ~2519) recurses through sigma-bound subterms unguarded; since WI-629, reify_value (mod.rs ~2630, via reify_value_children) also recurses into Value::Entity/Tuple children unguarded. A cyclic binding v |-> Fn(...v...) or v |-> Value::Entity{...v...} thus stack-overflows. PRE-WI-629 an Entity carrier was ACCIDENTALLY cycle-safe (reify_value fell to other.clone(), no descent); WI-629's deep reification (required for NAF verdict honesty over Value-carried not/or inners) removed that accidental safety, making Entity match reify's already-unguarded term behavior. Cyclic sigma is a KNOWN limitation: mod.rs ~3233 documents 'a cyclic sigma overflows reify/fingerprint' and resolve.rs ~1185 defends list_to_vec with a seen-set. The COMMON answer path is defended by the WI-624 occurs-check on answer_links, BUT the Value-carrier bind paths (bind_waking for external/routed rows, mod.rs ~1956; bind_value, subst.rs ~245) are NOT occurs-checked, so a routed-store row (or any future non-occurs-checked Value bind) that reaches reify_value could in principle trigger the overflow. Violates the loud-over-silent principle (a stack overflow is neither honest nor bounded). FIX DIRECTION (systemic, both functions so term + entity paths stay CONSISTENT -- a bespoke guard only in reify_value's Entity arm would be an inconsistent band-aid): (a) occurs-guarded / depth-capped recursion -- a seen:HashSet<VarId> or a REACH_DEPTH_CAP like value_reaches_eq_override's 256 -- returning a LOUD error at the cap; and/or (b) occurs-check the Value-carrier bind paths (bind_waking) so a cyclic sigma never forms. Regression test: construct a cyclic sigma (v |-> Entity/Fn containing v) and assert reify + reify_value surface a bounded error, not a crash. Discovered by the WI-629 /code-review (correctness finder A), deferred as out of WI-629 scope."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-08T07:55:54Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-665", author: "user", content: "Design converged into docs/proposals/053-fact-mutability.md \"Per-Functor Fact Monotonicity\" (committed c0c3e59c). The original recommendation here (immutable-by-default reflection functors + explicit `mutable` marker, loud error on runtime Store) is SUPERSEDED. Landed design: mutability IS the Modify effect (037), not a new axis; a single per-functor enum `anthill.reflect.fact_monotonicity(functor: Symbol) -> Monotonicity {constant, monotone, non_monotone}`, default `monotone` (append-only — the KB is add-friendly), NOT overlapping mutable/appendable booleans; per-functor because both the in-memory KB and any external store hold a MIX of functors (007 §8's one FileStore owns WorkItem/Project/Feedback/ToolDef), so the value is given by the OWNING store — an in-memory reflect rule, or a `monotonicity` field on an external store's per-functor binding (007 §6/§7) — and bounded by the store's write capability (`monotonicity <= capability`, propagating down the backend stack per 036). Mutation = RETRACT; retract of a non-`non_monotone` functor is the SOLE loud guard, assert stays the default. No grammar change. CACHE INVALIDATION IS RUST / OUT OF SCOPE for the language proposal — so THIS WI's remaining scope is the Rust KB cache implementation behind the guarantee (build-once for constant/monotone, invalidate-on-retract for non_monotone; plus the simp_gate_cache functor-specific fix), not the design. WI-660 (provides_index, stashed) reworks under this.", at: "2026-07-08T10:41:29Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-666", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED Phases A+B (commit 160f8dfc). A (reflect substrate): enum Monotonicity {constant, monotone, non_monotone} + operation fact_monotonicity(functor: Symbol) -> Monotonicity in reflect.anthill. NO catch-all default rule — empirical smoke-test (worktree) proved simp firing is load-order first-match (most-specific-first deferred, 043 §4.6, confirmed by simp_rewrite.rs:485), so a catch-all in the shared reflect.anthill would MASK every project override; the monotone default is instead supplied by the guard's 'no rule fired' branch (user-approved: 'drop catch-all'). Syntax gotchas found: the [simp] tag is mandatory (untagged equational rule loads inert) and a nullary RHS needs parens (monotone(), not monotone[simp]). B (runtime guard): the eval Store.persist/retract builtins (the runtime fact-write boundary; never run during load) consult fact_monotonicity via kb.apply_eq_rules and refuse LOUD — retract of a non-non_monotone functor (sole guard), persist of a constant functor. /code-review high (3 finder agents) caught the guard treating every can't-determine path as a silent monotone default (violating 'loud over silent skip'): FIXED — decode compares interned Monotonicity variant Symbols (anthill.reflect.Monotonicity.*, NOT a name-string suffix; qualified-name shape confirmed by a throwaway probe), and no-head-functor / missing-reflect-symbol / unexpected-reduced-head are all loud Errors; only the unreduced fact_monotonicity head yields the monotone default. store.anthill declares WorkItem+Tag non_monotone (retracted via forget/replace/untag); Feedback+StoreFormat stay monotone (persist-only). Tests: wi666_monotonicity_test (4 cases). Full workspace suite green. Phase C (external-store capability bounds) split to WI-667 — blocked on proposal-007 caps/StoreCaps, which is paper-only.", at: "2026-07-08T12:49:56Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-666", description: some(value: "Implement proposal docs/proposals/053-fact-mutability.md \"Per-Functor Fact Monotonicity\" (committed c0c3e59c). Give the KB a per-functor write policy — a single value, NOT a store-level one (both the in-memory KB and any external store hold a MIX of functors). This delivers the LANGUAGE mechanism + the loud runtime guard; the Rust KB cache optimization behind the guarantee is WI-665 (out of scope here). No grammar change.\n\nSCOPE / PHASES:\n\nA. REFLECT SUBSTRATE (stdlib/anthill/reflect/reflect.anthill). Add `sort Monotonicity { entity constant  entity monotone  entity non_monotone }` and `operation fact_monotonicity(functor: Symbol) -> Monotonicity`, with a catch-all default `rule fact_monotonicity(?) = monotone` (append-only is the KB's add-friendly default). constant = no assert/no retract; monotone = assert only (append-only); non_monotone = assert + retract (retract/update). Exactly one value per functor. A project marks its retractable functors by rule, e.g. `rule fact_monotonicity(WorkItem) = non_monotone`.\n\nB. RUNTIME GUARD (the load-bearing safety piece; rustland kb + eval). The KB's write paths consult fact_monotonicity outside loading: (1) `retract` of a functor whose monotonicity is not non_monotone -> LOUD error (this is the SOLE guard — retract is the non-monotone step that desyncs re-derived structure and falsifies caches); (2) `assert` of a `constant` functor -> loud error; assert is otherwise permitted (monotone default). Sites: KB.assert/retract (kb/mod.rs), the eval `Store` builtin assert/retract (eval/builtins.rs ~2225/2279), and Store.persist/retract on the persistence side. Static where the functor is known at load = LoadError naming it; dynamic otherwise = the Error effect (037). Per repo principle \"loud error over silent skip.\" NOTE: the guard must key off the SAME monotonicity value the reflect predicate exposes (single source of truth), and must NOT fire during a load phase (loader legitimately establishes facts).\n\nC. STORAGE INTEGRATION (stdlib/anthill/persistence/* ; 007/036 coherence). The owning store gives a functor's monotonicity: in-memory via the reflect rule (A); an EXTERNAL store via a `monotonicity` field added to its per-functor binding (007 §7 QueryBinding; §6 FileStore convention). A store's WRITE CAPABILITY (its ability to persist/retract, 007 §4) BOUNDS the value: `monotonicity <= capability` (non_monotone needs a retract-capable store; monotone a persist-capable one; constant none). Load-time consistency check, loud on a value exceeding capability (e.g. a non_monotone functor on an append-only sink). Capability propagates DOWN a store's backend stack (036 §\"Modify[s] covers the backend reach\", like Modify[s] -> s.backend) — distinct from the store<->its-many-functors relation. The in-memory default store has full capability and bounds nothing.\n\nOUT OF SCOPE: the Rust KB cache work (build-once for constant/monotone, invalidate-on-retract for non_monotone; simp_gate_cache functor-specific fix) — that is WI-665, which DEPENDS ON this WI (it needs the monotonicity mechanism + guard to exist). No new grammar (Monotonicity/fact_monotonicity are ordinary reflect sorts/ops like StoreCaps/caps; the guard is runtime behavior; the storage field extends an existing binding).\n\nACCEPTANCE: (1) `fact_monotonicity(F)` queryable, default monotone; a project rule can set a functor non_monotone or constant. (2) retract of a non-non_monotone functor is a loud error; a functor marked non_monotone retracts cleanly; assert of a constant functor is a loud error; assert of a monotone functor succeeds. (3) an external store's per-functor binding carries monotonicity, and a value exceeding the store's write capability is a loud load-time inconsistency. (4) the loader still establishes all facts during a load phase without tripping the guard. (5) full suite green; a synthetic test per (2)/(3). MAY split into per-phase WIs (A substrate / B guard / C storage) if landing incrementally. Implements proposal 053; relates to 007/036/037; gates WI-665 (cache) and WI-660 (provides_index rework)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-08T12:49:59Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-664", description: some(value: "Congruent Eq/NonEq derivation for COMPOSITE carriers (entities + named tuples): a composite's equality lawfulness must PROPAGATE from its fields — an entity / named-tuple is lawful `Eq` iff ALL its fields are `Eq`, and is `NonEq` (partial) if ANY field provides `NonEq` (or only `PartialEq`). This is Rust's rule: `derive(PartialEq)` works on a struct with an `f64` field, but `derive(Eq)` fails unless every field is `Eq`. Follows WI-658, which added the `NonEq` spec + witness (`nonEqRefl`) + the load-time `Eq` ⊥ `NonEq` mutual-exclusion check for bare carriers (Float).\n\nTWO COUPLED AXES — both are required; deriving the classification is toothless without fixing the behavior:\n\nAXIS 1 — CLASSIFICATION (derive the provision from field types): for each entity sort / named-tuple, auto-derive its equality provision: all-fields-Eq => `provides Eq[X]`; any-field-NonEq (e.g. a `Float` field) => `provides NonEq[X]` (+ PartialEq), NOT Eq. The composite's witness `nonEqRefl()` = the constructor with the NonEq field bound to that field's witness (`Point.nonEqRefl() = Point(x: Float.nonEqRefl(), y: <any>)`). Composes with WI-658's check: a user `provides Eq[Point]` over a Float-containing Point becomes a load error automatically.\n\nMUST FIX AS PART OF THIS TICKET (WI-658 review carry-over): WI-658's `check_eq_noneq_exclusive` (typing.rs) currently keys the Eq/NonEq grouping on the carrier's BASE sort symbol, so a PARAMETRIC carrier that legitimately provides `Eq` at one instantiation and `NonEq` at another (`Box[T = Int]` lawful, `Box[T = Float]` partial — the very case this ticket introduces) would be FALSELY flagged as providing both. Harmless today (only the non-parametric leaf `Float` provides NonEq), but this ticket makes it reachable — so BEFORE deriving per-instantiation Eq/NonEq for composites, make the check binding-aware: key on the spec's carrier-binding VALUE (the full instantiated carrier term), not just the base symbol. See the `LIMITATION` note in the check's doc comment.\n\nAXIS 2 — BEHAVIOR (the actual soundness fix, interpreter + resolver): `semantic_equal` (eval/builtins.rs ~496) and its resolver mirror `sem_eq_core` (kb/resolve.rs) SHORT-CIRCUIT on structural reflexivity (step 2, `views_structurally_equal`) BEFORE any field-wise consideration — so `eq(Point(nan, 0), Point(nan, 0)) = true` TODAY. The nested Float's non-reflexivity is LAUNDERED (step 1's IEEE path fires only on a bare Float PAIR, not two entities). This DIVERGES from the field-wise C++ `operator==` codegen (WI-644 step 4), which propagates `double`'s IEEE `==` to false — the exact WI-645 interpreter-vs-codegen divergence, one level up through composition. FIX: gate the structural reflexivity shortcut so a term that (transitively) contains a partial carrier recurses field-wise (`eq(Point(nan,_), Point(nan,_))` reduces to `eq(nan,nan) AND ... = false`). CRUCIAL: `===` / `struct_eq` MUST stay structural-reflexive (`nan === nan = true`) for hash-consing / dedup — only the SEMANTIC `eq` recurses. Touches the hot reflexivity path; scope carefully.\n\nEVIDENCE (probe 2026-07-07): `semantic_equal` step 2 returns structural-true for two identical Float-containing entities; there is no field-wise-congruent entity/tuple eq, and no field-type-driven Eq/NonEq derivation (grep: none). Bare Float is correct (WI-644/658); the moment Float is wrapped, the structural shortcut re-launders it.\n\nRELATED (note as further propagation, not in this ticket's core): the same laundering affects `Option[Float]`, ordinary tuples, and `Set`/`Map` over Float — the general law is \"a composite is Eq iff its components are.\" This ticket covers entities + named tuples (the requested scope); the parametric-container propagation is a further follow-up.\n\nACCEPTANCE: an entity / named-tuple with a Float (NonEq) field derives NonEq, not Eq; a user `provides Eq[<such composite>]` is a load error (via the WI-658 check); `eq(Point(nan,_), Point(nan,_)) = false` in the interpreter, AGREEING with the C++ codegen; `Point(1.0, 2.0) eq Point(1.0, 2.0) = true`; `===` stays structural (`Point(nan,_) === Point(nan,_) = true`, hash-consing intact); all-Eq-field composites still derive and behave as Eq; full workspace suite green. Depends on WI-658. REF: stdlib/anthill/prelude/eq.anthill (NonEq), rustland/anthill-core/src/eval/builtins.rs (semantic_equal), kb/resolve.rs (sem_eq_core), kb/typing.rs (check_eq_noneq_exclusive), WI-645 (bare-Float divergence precedent), WI-644 (umbrella), WI-658 (bare NonEq + Eq-NonEq check). Rust + scaland."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-658"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-08T12:20:44Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-644", author: "user", content: "WI-664 DELIVERED 2026-07-08 (composite Eq/NonEq derivation + field-wise semantic eq; full workspace green). All deps (WI-625/658/664) now delivered. ONE acceptance item remains: 'Map[K=Float] is a load error, Map[K=TotalFloat] loads'. CONFIRMED Map[K=Float] currently LOADS CLEAN -- no use-site requires-Eq[K] enforcement exists (WI-616 made requires Eq a vacuous universal-structural default). Remaining mechanism: enforce a parametric sort's requires Eq[K] at TYPE-INSTANTIATION sites -- check the K binding's carrier provides LAWFUL Eq (sort_provides; Float provides NonEq not Eq -> error; TotalFloat provides Eq -> ok). RISK: currently vacuous; giving it teeth can reject existing Map/Set over non-lawful-Eq types -> audit stdlib+tests before landing. Deferred by user (2026-07-08) to a focused follow-up session so the check + audit get full attention. Not a spinoff -- this is WI-644's own acceptance.", at: "2026-07-08T13:31:44Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-628", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED step_naf half (ticket STAYS OPEN for the deferred consumer surfacing; user-scoped \"step_naf only\" + \"loud error\" 2026-07-08). PRIMARY FIX (resolve.rs step_naf ground branch): a depth-TRUNCATED inner search for P no longer reads as \"P refuted, so not(P) SUCCEEDS\". WI-616 set SearchStream.truncated at the depth pop but only the semantic-eq consumer (prove_rule_predicate) consulted it; ground NAF used a consuming split_first loop that DISCARDED the flag on exhaustion. Fix: extracted SearchStream::drain_verdict, the shared manual-step drain returning {definite, residual, truncated} so the flag rides WITH the verdict and no consumer can forget it (that was the exact bug class: NAF forgot the check eq remembered). step_naf and prove_rule_predicate BOTH route through it; step_naf folds truncated into the floundered/undecided branch (residualize / rotate / skip-under-definite_only), so a truncated not(P) is UNDECIDED, never a silent success. TESTS: new wi628_naf_over_truncated_search_is_undecided_not_success (truncated loop(1) -> undecided residual; contrast complete-empty g(1) -> still DEFINITE success = no over-flouncing) + corrected not_respects_depth_limit which had ENCODED the bug (asserted not(r(a)) definitely succeeds over a truncated r(a):-r(a); now asserts undecided). Both assert the POSITIVE undecided shape (len==1, single residual), not just the negative, per review F1/F2. FULL WORKSPACE SUITE green (2633/0). /code-review high (8 finder angles): all independently verified the core fix SOUND -- no unsound-success, no infinite loop, no stale/missed truncation read, rotation terminates (depth monotone, bounded by max_depth). Applied review fixes: drain_verdict extraction (reuse/altitude -- makes the truncated check un-forgettable), stale truncated-field doc, one-sided test assertions.\n\nDEFERRED (belongs to THIS ticket -- do NOT spin off): surface truncation to the EAGER resolve consumers that read is_empty() as refutation. AUDIT: dangerous = eval_negation_guard / eval_forall_guard / eval_count_guard (kb/mod.rs) -- they call resolve_goals, which drops the stream + its truncated flag; resolve_with_stats also misses it (refreshes stats only on a YIELDED solution, never the empty path). NUANCE the step_naf fix EXPOSED: eval_forall_guard synthesizes not(Q) goals, so its truncation behavior ALREADY flipped from \"wrongly VIOLATED\" (pre-fix) to \"wrongly HOLDS\" (post-fix -- silent, the more dangerous direction); eval_negation_guard / eval_count_guard resolve BARE queries so they only see OUTER-search truncation (their own depth-pop -> outer stream.truncated). The follow-up needs TWO pieces: (a) propagate step_naf inner truncation to the OUTER stream (self.truncated |= v.truncated) -- a nested not(Q) truncation is otherwise INVISIBLE (sub-stream flag is local, and under definite_only the frame is skipped WITHOUT a residual; NB you cannot instead residualize-under-definite_only, because discharge_by_derivation uses the weaker !is_empty() check and would then wrongly report Proved). (b) surface truncated through resolve_goals / resolve_with_stats (keep the stream alive on the empty path, e.g. return it alongside the Vec or thread onto ResolveStats). THEN the three guards raise a LOUD ERROR (\"constraint undecidable within depth budget\") -- user decision 2026-07-08, not a silent pass/fail. SAFE consumers need NO change (positive-proof, truncation = conservative miss): prove_from_gamma / refute_guard (refutes by proving the NEGATION positively; the div-by-zero eq/neq guard is a BARE functor-swapped goal, never a not() wrapper; its \"any other predicate\" not() fallback is already covered by the step_naf fix), discharge_by_derivation, region.rs is_result_region.\n\nACCEPTED TRADEOFFS of the step_naf fix (review C1/C2/C3 -- sound-but-observable, NOT bugs): (1) inner NAF budget is remaining_depth = max_depth - depth, so a not(P) deep in a conjunction gets a small budget and may residualize (undecided) where a shallow one succeeds -- position-dependent, but the SOUND direction (the alternative is deciding from an incomplete search). (2) a ground truncated not(P) with a tail re-runs P's search once per rotation (bounded by goals.len()) -- extends WI-629 rotation to the truncated case. (3) truncated is a coarse whole-search flag: an unrelated deep-but-finite branch of P taints it. POSSIBLE FUTURE REFINEMENT (orthogonal, not required for soundness): give the inner NAF a FRESH generous budget (like prove_rule_predicate SEM_EQ_SUB_DEPTH = 100_000) instead of remaining_depth, to cut spurious truncation.", at: "2026-07-08T17:58:13Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-667", description: some(value: "Implement proposal 053 external-store fact monotonicity + the 007 §2 trait/policy capability model (follow-on to WI-666, which delivered the in-memory substrate + runtime guard). DESIGN converged 2026-07-08 (with user): store capability is TWO kinds — PROVISION (a trait carrying an operation) and POLICY (a per-predicate predicate over universal ops). `retract` is not universal, so it becomes a TRAIT op; write policy governs the universal persist/retract, so it is the monotonicity predicate. No caps/StoreCaps (never built; the wrong shape — a marker value for an operation-bearing capability), no monotonicity<=capability bound (it falls out of the type structure). STDLIB (stdlib/anthill/persistence/store.anthill): (1) ADD to `sort Store` the policy query `operation monotonicity(store: Store, functor: Symbol) -> Monotonicity` — every store answers, per functor, its write policy; a QUERY the system reads to plan (persist available iff != constant, retract iff non_monotone), NEVER by attempting a write and catching failure. (2) MOVE `retract` OUT of base `Store` into a NEW trait `sort NonMonotonicStore { fact Store  operation retract(store: NonMonotonicStore, id: FactId) -> Bool effects {Modify[store], Error} }` — retract joins retrieve (QueryableStore) / pull (BulkStore) as a trait op; an append-only backend simply does not provide NonMonotonicStore and has no retract at all. QueryableStore/BulkStore unchanged. Consistency is STRUCTURAL: a store without NonMonotonicStore has no retract, so its monotonicity answers are monotone/constant only — non_monotone IMPLIES the trait (this is the dropped monotonicity<=capability bound, now from the type structure; a load-time coherence check is optional). RUST (coordinated — why this is NOT a standalone stdlib edit): (a) split the persistence `Store` trait (rustland/anthill-core/src/persistence/mod.rs) so `retract` lives on the NonMonotonicStore side (or is gated), and retract-capable backends (FileStore, IndexedFileStore) declare `fact NonMonotonicStore`; (b) retarget the eval `persistence_retract` builtin (registered/dispatched as `anthill.persistence.Store.retract`, rustland/anthill-core/src/eval/builtins.rs) and WI-666's guard error-paths to `anthill.persistence.NonMonotonicStore.retract`; (c) give each store a `Store.monotonicity(store, functor)` answer (a builtin or reflect-rule contribution) and WIRE it under the reflect `fact_monotonicity(functor)` FACADE WI-666's guard already reads — for an external-store functor `fact_monotonicity(functor)` resolves via 007 routing (route(functor) -> Store) to that store's `monotonicity`; for an in-memory functor, by reflect rules. IMPL choice (open): MATERIALIZE each store's monotonicity into the reflect predicate at store registration (guard unchanged, cheapest — recommended) vs ROUTE live per query (reflects live backend state). CALLERS to migrate off base-Store.retract: the todo store (rustland/anthill-todo/anthill/store.anthill forget_buffer/untag_item — its IndexedFileStore backend declares fact NonMonotonicStore) and any other retract caller; WI-666 tests + the anthill-todo suite must stay green. ACCEPTANCE: (1) `Store.monotonicity(store, functor)` queryable per functor, read WITHOUT attempting a write. (2) `retract` is a NonMonotonicStore-trait op — a store not providing NonMonotonicStore has no retract (structural); `fact_monotonicity(functor)` for an external functor resolves to the owning store's monotonicity (a synthetic store-backed functor resolves to the store's policy, not the in-memory monotone default). (3) a backend that cannot honor a declared non_monotone retract fails LOUD via the Error effect at the write, not a static load check. (4) 007 §2 (trait/policy model) is the spec; full suite green; a synthetic test per (1)/(2)/(3). deps WI-666; sibling of WI-665 (Rust KB cache invalidation keyed off the same monotonicity)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-666"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-08T18:28:38Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-628", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED the DEFERRED consumer-surfacing half 2026-07-08 (ticket STAYS OPEN — /code-review surfaced a THIRD consumer, in-scope per the ticket's own \"prove... consumers under definite_only\"; see REMAINING). The two-piece plan from prior feedback + the loud error are done.\n\n(a) step_naf now folds inner-not() truncation onto the OUTER stream: `self.truncated |= v.truncated` in the residual/truncated branch, placed BEFORE the definite_only skip. So a nested `not(Q)` whose inner search truncated is no longer invisible when the frame is skipped without a residual — the exact mechanism eval_forall_guard relies on (it synthesizes `not(body)`).\n\n(b) surfaced truncation through the eager front doors: new `SearchStream::drain_all` (manual step-loop that keeps the stream alive PAST exhaustion so `truncated` survives — `split_first` consumed it and dropped the flag); `ResolveStats` gained a `truncated` snapshot; `resolve_goals` + `resolve_with_stats` route through drain_all; new `resolve_goals_with_truncation -> (Vec<Solution>, bool)` is the guards' front door. (resolve_with_stats now also samples the empty/post-last-solution path it used to miss — deliberate, its only non-test caller resolve() discards stats.)\n\nTHEN the three guards raise the LOUD ERROR: eval_count_guard / eval_forall_guard / eval_negation_guard return a 3-way GuardStatus{Holds,Violated,Undecidable(reason)}. Undecidable when a truncated search would otherwise read empty-as-refutation. negation/forall go through GuardStatus::from_emptiness(is_empty, truncated, reason) — centralized so the truncated-and-empty check CANNOT be forgotten (the drain_verdict-extraction discipline, applied guard-side). count uses a monotone-sound rule: known_violated = count>max (only grows, stays over); known_holds = count>=min && max==usize::MAX (some_q witness found); else truncated => Undecidable — verified correct for lone_q(0,1)/one_q(1,1)/some_q(1,MAX)/no_q(0,0). check_all_guards maps Undecidable -> new GuardCheck::Undecidable -> loader's new load-BLOCKING LoadError::ConstraintUndecidable (added to is_load_blocking + both message formatters, distinct from ConstraintLoweringFailed = malformed-FORM). The per-assert assert_checked path has no error channel (returns Option), so it RETRACTS the fact AND emits a loud eprintln diagnostic — never admits an unverified fact, never silently drops the reason (loud-over-silent).\n\nTESTS (+5, full workspace 2638/0): resolve-layer wi628_naf_truncation_propagates_to_outer_stream_under_definite_only (piece a: not(loop(1)) under definite_only => truncated=true+empty; contrast not(g(1)) complete-empty => truncated=false+1 definite) + `mod wi628_guard_truncation_tests` (negation / no_q-count / forall over a `loop(?x):-loop(?x)` recursion => GuardCheck::Undecidable; negation over a COMPLETE-empty g(a) => Holds, no over-trigger).\n\n/code-review high (8 finder angles, 1-vote verify): NO verdict-flipping bug found. drain_all early-break vs max_solutions, the count known_holds/known_violated interval logic, from_holds polarity at every site, and the step_naf taint (scoped to the residual/truncated branch, only real inner truncation, no over-taint from failed-P) were all independently verified SOUND. Applied review fixes: assert_checked loud diagnostic (was a silent None == Violated collapse), from_emptiness centralization (was the truncated&&is_empty check hand-rolled per guard), deleted the now-dead SearchStream::stats() (made dead when resolve_with_stats moved to drain_all).\n\nREMAINING (belongs to THIS ticket — SCOPE names \"prove... consumers under definite_only\"; do NOT spin off): the SEMANTIC-EQ consumer prove_rule_predicate (resolve.rs) does NOT propagate its inner truncation to the outer stream — the SAME hole, unclosed for the carrier eq/neq/subset/member path. It drains a CLOSED sub-proof via the shared drain_verdict, collapses residual||truncated -> PredicateProof::Undecided, and sem_eq_dispatch maps that -> BuiltinResult::Delay (as does bridge_eq_op_to_eval's cap/None). The outer stream's Delay handler (resolve.rs ~1008) under definite_only skips the goal WITHOUT setting self.truncated, so a guard goal routing through a truncated carrier-eq gets resolve_goals_with_truncation => truncated=false and silently decides. FIX (next increment): distinguish TRUNCATED from FLOUNDERED in prove_rule_predicate (do NOT collapse — a floundered-but-complete ground sub-proof stays WI-519 \"no definite solution\"; only genuine truncation propagates), carry the bit through BuiltinResult::Delay (e.g. Delay{truncated} or a new variant), and have the stream's Delay handler do self.truncated |= truncated. Reachability: a constraint using eq/neq/subset/member over a rule-backed carrier instance whose operands truncate the closed sub-proof at SEM_EQ_SUB_DEPTH=100_000 (~316+-element Set given O(n^2)) — high bar, pre-existing.\n\nACCEPTED TRADEOFF (review A/C, sound-not-a-bug): a genuinely-holding constraint whose search is deep-but-FINITE beyond the guard's default max_depth=100 now reports Undecidable -> load-block, where pre-fix it silently mis-decided. Intended loud-over-silent direction. POSSIBLE FUTURE REFINEMENT (orthogonal): give the guards a more generous depth budget (like SEM_EQ_SUB_DEPTH) to cut spurious blocks on legitimate long-but-finite constraint recursions.", at: "2026-07-08T19:05:52Z")fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-502", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED 2026-07-08 (umbrella). Acceptance MET on both halves: (1) cargo-test GREEN — full workspace suite 2643 passed / 0 failed / exit 0 (rustland/scripts/test.sh); (2) a requires-guarded [simp] rule fires on the resolver/prove path by READING the carried type, no re-derivation — WI-292, tests resolver_fires_requires_guarded_equation_when_carrier_provides_spec (op2(5,7)->5 since Int64 provides Magma) + resolver_does_not_fire_..._when_carrier_lacks_spec (op2(true,false) intact, Bool lacks Magma). Whole staged chain delivered: Steps 0-3 typed-VARIABLE half (WI-569/570/571/572), typed-VALUE carrier + typed(value,env) + min_sort removed (WI-578), typed rule pattern syntax ?x:T / [T] introducer (WI-582), firing consumer WI-292, guard discharge WI-573, prereqs/guardrails/phases WI-581/584/592/595/596/611. ONLY non-delivered wi502 item = WI-574 generative labeling, PreOpened/DEFERRED BY DESIGN (docs/design/constrained-term-substrate.md: 'check + suspend ships first; generative labeling is deferred, opt-in, same representation') — a separate future capability, NOT umbrella acceptance. Documented WI-582 follow-ons (typer-side enforcement of typed-rule bounds — the typer currently SKIPS typed rules, sound-but-conservative; printer round-trip of ?x:T; compound multi-var bounds) are enhancements outside acceptance, unfiled. No code changed this session — repo was at the delivered committed state; verification-only.", at: "2026-07-08T19:52:21Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-502", description: some(value: "Typed terms and rules — deep re-engineering of the Term/resolution representation so a term (and a rule's head/body) carries its type, enabling type-directed reasoning (esp. [simp] firing, proposal 043 §4) to READ the type rather than re-derive it. Motivation: WI-283 made [simp] firing type-directed but split it across two sites — the typer has typed occurrences (NodeOccurrence.inferred_type, WI-284) and fires requires-guarded rules via simp_fire_guard_holds (typing.rs); the resolver holds type-erased Terms and so skips them (equation_is_requires_guarded, resolve.rs). Converging the two (WI-292) needs the resolver to know each term's type. Re-deriving it structurally is REJECTED (a second notion of type that can drift from the typer); reusing NodeOccurrence is wrong (occurrence = source-code form, not the resolution carrier). So the type must live on the term/value carrier itself, populated ONCE at the typing boundary and carried through resolution without recomputation. Scope/design (TBD in this WI): where the type slot lives (Value at eval/value.rs is unboxed/zero-alloc — don't bloat scalars), how it is populated at the source->resolver boundary (incl. typing rule bodies / the simplify-prove entry, which are untyped today), and how it is preserved through De Bruijn opening + substitution (where inferred_type is currently dropped). Clients: WI-292 (resolver-side type-directed firing) and WI-283's typer firing become clients of one typed substrate. Acceptance: terms carry types accessible during resolution such that a requires-guarded [simp] rule can fire on the resolver/prove path by reading the carried type (no re-derivation); cargo-test green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-569"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-08T19:52:28Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-665", description: some(value: "MECHANISM (governs the build-once reflection-fact indexes: op_records/WI-656, sort_alias_index/WI-659, provides_index/WI-660, and future SortInfo/RequiresInfo indexes). PROBLEM: these typed indexes are built once at type-check start over loader-emitted reflection facts (OperationInfo, SortAlias, SortProvidesInfo, ...), but some are READ AT RUNTIME by paths that can in principle mutate those facts via eval `Store` assert/retract -- e.g. sort_provides is reached from the resolver simp guard, provider_spec_view_bindings from eval dispatch. A build-once, never-invalidated index then reads a STALE relation (the WI-607 ABA hole). WI-660's stopgap -- drop the whole index to None on ANY assert/retract and rebuild only at the next type-check -- is WRONG on two counts: (1) it invalidates on mutations to UNRELATED functors, though the index depends on exactly one functor; (2) after the drop it is not rebuilt until the next type-check, so runtime callers fall to a full linear scan indefinitely. \"Just erasing caches on mutation\" is not acceptable. SAFETY INVARIANT (must hold for every such cache): EITHER the functor(s) the cache depends on are IMMUTABLE, so build-once is sound by construction; OR the cache is invalidated ONLY by a mutation to a depended-on functor AND rebuilt lazily on next use. Two arms to satisfy it: ARM (a) IMMUTABILITY, SURFACED IN THE LANGUAGE -- mark reflection functors immutable, visibly in source. Surface sub-options: (a1) an explicit `immutable(Functor)` fact/declaration; (a2) immutable-by-DEFAULT plus an explicit `mutable` mark on the functors that genuinely mutate (user/data facts written by Store). Then a runtime Store assert/retract targeting an immutable functor is a LOUD error (LoadError / eval error) -- per repo principle \"loud error over silent skip\" -- never silent staleness. Effect: op_records / sort_alias_index / provides_index (and future SortInfo/RequiresInfo indexes) become sound build-once with ZERO invalidation machinery. ARM (b) FUNCTOR-SPECIFIC INVALIDATE + REBUILD-ON-USE -- for functors that must stay mutable: the cache records the set of functors it depends on; the assert / retract / unindex_functor choke points invalidate a cache ONLY when the mutated functor is in that set (not on every mutation); and the cache is stored as RefCell<Option<..>> with a lazy accessor that REBUILDS on first use after invalidation (never a permanent scan fallback). RECOMMENDATION: arm (a), immutable-by-default + explicit `mutable` opt-in (a2). It is the most general -- it makes the whole family of reflection-fact indexes sound build-once with no per-cache invalidation code, and it turns an illegal runtime mutation into a clear diagnostic. The vast majority of loader-emitted structural facts are never rewritten; only user/data functors mutated via Store need `mutable`. Arm (b) is the fallback for any functor that must stay mutable. NOTE on simp_gate_cache (mod.rs:477): it already rebuilds-on-use (drops to None, recomputed lazily) -- that half is fine -- but it invalidates on ALL mutations, not just the eq/unify [simp]-rule functors it depends on; bring it under the same functor-specific rule (arm b) since [simp] rules CAN be asserted at runtime (so those functors are legitimately mutable, not immutable). SCOPE / ACCEPTANCE: a functor-immutability notion exists and is visible in source (chosen surface); a runtime eval Store assert/retract of an immutable functor produces a loud error, not silent success; op_records / sort_alias_index / provides_index drop their invalidation hooks (arm a) OR gain functor-specific invalidate + lazy rebuild-on-use (arm b) -- NO cache uses \"erase on any mutation, never rebuild\"; simp_gate_cache invalidation becomes functor-specific; WI-660 is resolved under the chosen arm (its current drop-on-any-mutation is removed); full suite green; a synthetic test that Stores an immutable reflection fact gets the loud error, and (arm b path) a mutable-functor cache rebuilds correctly on next use after invalidation. RELATED: WI-607 (deferred sort_provides cache; its note that a correct cache needs an epoch/choke-point signal and that COUNT-keying is ABA-unsound -- immutability sidesteps both), WI-656/659/660 (the build-once indexes this governs), WI-661 (carrier-agnostic-heads umbrella)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-666"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-08T20:12:28Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-660", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED 2026-07-09 (un-stashed from stash@{0} + reworked under WI-665; the stash's rejected drop-on-every-mutation stopgap and premature Delivered mark are GONE). SortProvidesInfo is now constant (reflect.anthill, WI-665), so the build-once ProvidesIndex is sound at RUNTIME with ZERO eval-time invalidation. LIFECYCLE: the index is Some only while the provider relation is FROZEN, None across every load-time mutation window -- reset at load_phase_inner start AND just before eq_derive::run, (re)built at type_check_sorts start (hot consumer) and right after eq_derive::run. TWO maps: by_spec_base (canonical_sort_sym key, exact) + by_carrier (SHORT-NAME key + same_symbol/canonical_sort_sym re-filter, because same_symbol is non-transitive so no single Symbol key reproduces it). 7 sites migrated: spec_has_any_providers, impl_sorts_providing_spec, collect_provides_candidates (the WI's #1 hot site, which the stash had MISSED), directly_provided_specs, provider_spec_view_bindings, provided_spec_base_syms, and sort_provides (refactored to per-hop carrier-adjacency via provides_out_edges). find_spec_op_for_provided_sort LEFT as a full scan (commented): its witness_match matches providers whose sort_ref != recv_sort, which the carrier bucket cannot serve. KEY FINDING (/code-review, would-have-been-a-bug): eq_derive::run READS the relation (sort_provides, to skip an already-provided derivation) WHILE ASSERTING derived composite NonEq/PartialEq in the same loop -- so the index must be None during it, else it reads its own stale pre-eq_derive snapshot; surfaced as a failing wi644 map_with_float_composite_key_is_a_load_error. Selection boilerplate factored into provides_rids_by_spec/provides_rids_by_carrier helpers; carrier_short_name returns &str (no per-hop String alloc). PERF A/B (anthill-todo load, 120 sorts, debug): type_check_sorts ~875ms->~800ms (~9%) index-off vs index-on. Full suite 69 bins/0 fail; /code-review high (6 finders) -> 0 correctness bugs (confirmed constant guard has no bypass, no KB clone, incremental reset covers all entry paths). Unblocks bucket-1 carrier-agnostic elimination (WI-661/663). Files: kb/{typing,mod,load}.rs.", at: "2026-07-08T21:46:21Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-661", author: "user", content: "WI-660 impl (2026-07-09) confirms the concrete lever. WI-660's by_carrier index must key by SHORT-NAME (last segment) + a same_symbol re-filter, NOT canonical_sym like by_spec_base, because the carrier-direction provider sites compare with same_symbol (typing.rs) -- non-canonical and NON-transitive (a bare 'Main' bridges BOTH anthill.cli.Main and anthill.todo.Main, which do not bridge each other). The PRODUCER already resolves carriers (maybe_emit_fact_provides_info builds sort_ref from the resolved enclosing domain; a probe found 0/74 bare carriers in stdlib+repo load), so the residual reason same_symbol is needed is bare/unresolved carrier QUERY symbols reaching the consumers from the typer's dispatch. LEVER: canonicalize the carrier QUERY symbols at their dispatch producers (the call sites passing recv_sort/carrier_sym) so same_symbol collapses to canonical_sym everywhere -> the carrier consumers switch same_symbol->canonical_sort_sym, and WI-660's by_carrier can drop the short-name key + re-filter and key by canonical_sym like by_spec_base. Until then, short-name + same_symbol re-filter is the faithful, self-contained key.", at: "2026-07-08T21:46:40Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-660", description: some(value: "PERF (SECONDARY by profile -- ~180 samples on the anthill-todo load, but architecturally the SAME antipattern as WI-656 and genuinely HOT for spec/typeclass-heavy workloads): SortProvidesInfo (provider/coherence facts) is scanned unindexed at 13 sites via rules_by_functor(provides_sym) (fresh Vec per call). Unlike OperationInfo it is a MANY-TO-MANY relation (carrier x spec, no unique key), so it needs TWO indexes, not one record map. HOT keyed sites (ranked): (1) collect_provides_candidates (typing.rs:9385) -- dispatch-resolution core, per spec-op dispatch, spec-base key. (2) sort_provides (23867) -- THE subtype/coherence predicate (per type-comparison + per simp redex + value-membership); it REBUILDS the whole (carrier,spec) edge set on every call though reachability only follows edges out of one carrier -> index as carrier adjacency = biggest single win. (3) provider_spec_view_bindings (12559) -- (carrier,spec-base) pair key, ~10 callers incl. parameterized_compatible_view (per-type-comparison). (4) find_spec_op_for_provided_sort (3019) + directly_provided_specs (11720) -- carrier key, the latter per BFS hop. (5) spec_has_any_providers (9213) -- spec-base bool (index answers \"bucket non-empty\"). (6) provided_spec_base_syms (15893) -- carrier key, per member-projection. FIX: two maps built once post-load beside op_records -- provides_by_spec_base: HashMap<Symbol,Vec<RuleId>> and provides_by_carrier: HashMap<Symbol,Vec<RuleId>> (the latter doubles as sort_provides' carrier->out-edges adjacency, and serves the carrier half of the pair-keyed site with a cheap inner spec-base filter). LOAD-BEARING CAVEAT (the correctness story): key canonicalization DIFFERS across sites -- 1/10/12 use same_symbol (bare<->qualified bridge), others use canonical_sort_sym; directly_provided_specs' comment (11727) warns canonical_sort_sym DROPS a bare-interned provides-clause edge, so the index MUST key on the COARSER same_symbol equivalence class or qualified-form lookups miss edges. Fact shape: SortProvidesInfo(sort_ref=carrier, spec=SortView(Spec-base,bindings)); key extractors = sort_ref_functor (load.rs:4377) + provides_spec_base_sym (load.rs:4398). LEAVE the 4 genuinely iterate-all once-per-load coherence passes (check_provider_requires/operations 10214/10423, check_override_refinement 10984, check_instance_fact_op_signatures 11138). A targeted 2-map is the right altitude vs a general reflect-fact secondary-index mechanism (per-functor key extractors + differing canonicalization make a generic version no simpler; defer until a 3rd fact type needs it). Load-stable (typer never rewrites provides facts); build+invalidate like op_records. Acceptance: full suite green; behaviour identical (canonicalization parity verified); measurable win on a spec/typeclass-heavy load. Follow-up to WI-653/656; lower priority than the SortAlias index (profile #1)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-665"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-08T21:46:43Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-657", description: some(value: "PERF (incremental, from WI-653 inference static audit -- lower priority than and layered on top of the OperationInfo-index WI): per-node and per-op hoists on the typer inference path, each small, all read-only-verified, none behavior-changing. PER-NODE (~1681+ execs): (5) pos_args/named_args cloned TWICE per Apply (typing.rs ~3631-3632 then ~3721-3722) and per Constructor (~3736-3737 then ~3809-3810) -> clone once into the frame, borrow the original slice for the reversed push_visit loops. (6) is_tuple_lit does qualified_name_of(name) == 'anthill.reflect.TupleLiteral' STRING compares per constructor arg (~3750, again ~3781 per pos arg, ~3794 per named arg) -> intern the TupleLiteral symbol once (cache on KB) and compare Symbols. (7) reconstruct_sort_params scans ALL SortAlias facts via rules_by_functor(SortAlias) + qualified_name_of(parent).to_string() per constructor node (~22293-22298) -> index SortAlias facts by parent-sort symbol (SAME unindexed-scan class as the OperationInfo-index WI; consider a shared fact-family secondary-index mechanism). (8) merge_effects (called ~5682/5713, def ~1505) does result = a.to_vec() per arg = O(n^2) accumulator realloc -> accumulate in place with push-if-absent (mostly cheap today since bodies are usually effect-free, so to_vec of an empty Vec doesn't allocate). PER-OP (~hundreds of execs): (9) has_simp_equations + simp_equation_rids recomputed once per op-body inside type_check_node (~2524/2529, each allocating via rules_by_functor + meta_has_flag string-keyed scans) though the eq/unify rule set is LOOP-INVARIANT across the whole check_operation_bodies pass (no rules asserted during typing) -> compute simp_enabled + simp_rids ONCE before the per-op loop and pass them in. NOTE this is the per-op GATING cost, DISTINCT from simp FIRING (WI-655). (10) per-op parent sort resolved BY STRING: qualified_name_of(op_sym).to_string() + rsplit_once('.') + try_resolve_symbol (~24142-24146) though impl_parent_of_op already yields it structurally on the same op at ~24084 -> reuse the structural parent (first verify impl-vs-lexical-parent equivalence). (11) op_result_sym built via a formatted '{}.result' string + string resolve per typed op (~24357) even when no Modify[result] is present -> resolve lazily inside op_boundary_effects only when needed, or intern the .result child once. (12) direct_requires_chain (set_enclosing_sort ~762 -> ~21547) reads the CACHED requires_tree but then rebuilds a fresh Vec + clones each RequiresEntry per op; a dormant requires_chain_cache field already exists (mod.rs ~525) -> cache the flattened Rc<Vec<RequiresEntry>>. ACCEPTANCE: full suite green; a measurable type_check_sorts reduction on top of the OperationInfo-index WI; behavior identical. See WI-653 (diagnosis) and the OperationInfo-index WI (the dominant win this layers on)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-09T06:08:17Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-580", author: "user", content: "Increment 1 DELIVERED + pushed to main (commit 3b571e45, 2026-07-09), full suite green + /code-review high. Pivoted from typer site (§3.2, found type-unsound: rewrote a call before check_apply_iter, bypassing signature checks; also wrong consumer — length/append <=> are untagged/SLD-served, not [simp]) to the SLD one-step unfold (§3.3) as abstract-interpretation-ON-SUSPEND (user's framing): when a SemEq goal has an operand that is an unground bodied op-call whose body matches on a flex scrutinee (the shape reduce_op_value/eval-bridge suspend on), case-split the body into one choice-point per arm. Engine kb/body_specialize.rs::folded_call_match; hook resolve.rs::unfold_eq_operand + step_init. Covers STRUCTURAL ops: relational append(?a,ys)=zs solves ?a; code(?c)=?v enumerates. RETIRED length/append <=> twins (ground folds via bridge, non-ground unfolds). Tests: push_choice_test.rs (relational append, catch-all decline, op-call-OTHER decline) + wi625 nonground_operand_case_splits_via_unfold. 4 soundness gates from review (all tests-masked by disjoint-ctor arms): only DISJOINT constructor arms case-split (catch-all -> WI-519 residual); effect/requires purity gate; op-call OTHER declined; total OTHER conversion (no reify panic). REMAINING (ticket OPEN): (a) member soundness = if-flattening in anf_flatten + owed requires Eq[T] (currently DECLINED by the requires gate) + then retire member's :- unification twins (the headline eq-vs-unification fix); (b) doc drift kernel-language.md :206-207/:683-684/:764 (length/append <=> examples now body-derived) + spec note re op-call-operand body-unfold; (c) cosmetic: reduce's dead progress bool, optional Continuation->Vec<Value> (WI-246).", at: "2026-07-09T06:43:25Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-607", author: "user", content: "CLOSED AS SUPERSEDED (2026-07-09). The epoch-counter cache this WI proposed is obsoleted by the per-functor mutability declaration (proposal 053). SortProvidesInfo is marked constant() (reflect.anthill: rule fact_monotonicity(SortProvidesInfo)=constant() [simp], WI-665), and the WI-666 eval guard makes any runtime Store.persist/retract of a constant functor a LOUD error. So the sort_provides edge set never mutates at runtime => the ABA hole that killed count-keying does not exist, and NO epoch counter is needed. The build-once cache itself is already DELIVERED by WI-660 (typing.rs ProvidesIndex; serves sort_provides -> sort_provides_reach -> provides_out_edges, incl. the resolver simp-guard read this WI targeted; scan fallback pre-build; nulled around eq_derive's load-time read-while-asserting). Zero invalidation code. Epoch/ABA is the wrong frame under 053: forbid the mutation (constant) or tolerate the add via scan-fallback (like op_records, if ever moved to monotone for runtime provider registration); retract stays forbidden unless non_monotone. Residual same-class unindexed scans (SortInfo/RequiresInfo) are separate WIs, not this one.", at: "2026-07-09T06:32:52Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-607", description: some(value: "sort_provides edge-set cache via a monotonic fact-mutation epoch (WI-595 part-3, deferred). PROFILING-GATED: measurement (2026-07-01) shows sort_provides rebuild costs only ~1.7ms across a full stdlib+github-todo load (87 calls, ~20us/call, 43 edges) and 0 calls on real resolution; stdlib has zero [simp] rules so the resolver simp path is inert. Only a synthetic 500-[simp]-redex program reaches ~37ms. Revisit ONLY if a real workload surfaces sort_provides as a hot spot. Correct cache needs an epoch counter at the assert/retract/unindex_functor choke-points (a count-keyed memo is ABA-unsound: 3 finders confirmed a balanced runtime retract+insert of distinct SortProvidesInfo facts restores the provider count and serves a stale edge-set). Do NOT re-attempt count-keying."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-09T06:32:55Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-580", author: "user", content: "Increment 2 DELIVERED (2026-07-09), full suite green (2651 passed, 0 failed), /code-review high done. The headline eq-vs-unification member soundness fix. RETIRED member's two ':-' unification twins (list.anthill) — they matched cons(head: ?x, ...) STRUCTURALLY, diverging unsoundly from the body's declared eq(head, x) for any type whose eq is not structural equality. The relational view is now DERIVED from the body: resolve.rs::bare_bodied_bool_relation + a step_init routing block route a bare rule-less, EFFECT-FREE, Bool-returning bodied-op goal (member(?x,?l)) to eq(f(args), true) — a ground call decides via the eval bridge USING the declared Eq; an unground one suspends to a WI-519 residual ('sound checker, not generator', design §5). Demonstrator: bare member(ae,[ae']) with eq(ae,ae')=true but ae != ae' structurally went 0 solutions (unsound-false) -> 1 solution (sound-true). KEY SCOPE REFINEMENT: the inc-1 'remaining (a)' if-flattening + owed-requires are NOT NEEDED — the fix rides the BRIDGE, not the unfold. The relational-member case-split those would enable only fires over an UNGROUND LIST, which is inherently NON-TERMINATING (infinitely many lists contain ?x; =true does not bound the recursion the way append's finite result does). §5 itself frames member as 'evaluate or SUSPEND eq per branch -> residual', which bridge+residual realizes AND terminates; the case-split would replace a clean terminating residual with a loop. Deferred until a bodied Bool op has a TERMINATING relational consumer (§10 Q4). requires-gate in folded_call_match STILL declines member for the unfold — correct (bridge serves it), NOT relaxed. Gate excludes effects (Stream/Iterable.isEmpty) but NOT requires (member's Eq[T] discharged at the body's own eq call by value-directed dispatch, which the bridge honours). Tests (push_choice_test.rs): wi580_member_uses_declared_eq_not_unification (field-key custom eq), wi580_member_over_rule_defined_eq_decides (bridge FIRES <=>-rule carrier eq — no completeness loss), wi580_member_relational_unground_residualizes_not_wrong. Doc-drift (c) DONE: kernel-language.md :205-207/:683-684/:773 + design doc §3.3 status. REMAINING: only cosmetic (body_specialize::reduce's dead progress bool) + §3.4 prover site (separate future).", at: "2026-07-09T08:12:46Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-628", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED the semantic-eq REMAINING half + the deeper eval-bridge leak (pushed main 5a3a2df0). The carrier-eq/neq path no longer lets a guard/NAF consumer decide from a truncated search.\n\nCORE: prove_rule_predicate now returns PredicateProof::Undecided{truncated} (truncated checked BEFORE residual: a search that both floundered AND truncated is incomplete). sem_eq_dispatch maps it to BuiltinResult::Delay{truncated}; the SLD step loop folds truncated onto the outer SearchStream.truncated; resolve_goals_with_truncation surfaces it to the three guards (already -> ConstraintUndecidable). bridge_eq_op_to_eval (bodied instance-eq) returns BridgeEqOutcome{Decided(bool)|Undecided{truncated}}: re-entry cap => truncated:true, clean suspend => truncated:false.\n\nDESIGN (from /code-review high, 8 finders): (1) Delay carries truncated as a FIELD not a separate DelayTruncated variant -- forgetting-proof (every match arm binds it, every producer chooses it; a stray matches!(x,Delay) can't misclassify), the exact can't-forget discipline of the earlier increments. ~28 plain producers -> BuiltinResult::delay() helper. (2) sem_eq sub-proof depth is a cfg(test) override of a fixed production const (DEFAULT_SEM_EQ_SUB_DEPTH=100_000) -- no mutable production knob (was a KB field for testability; two finders flagged it).\n\nTWO SOUNDNESS GAPS the review caught + FIXED: (A1, finder A) composite_field_wise_sem_eq short-circuited on the FIRST field's plain Delay, DROPPING a LATER field's truncation -> soundness was field-order-dependent; now scans ALL fields and aggregates (any field truncation => composite truncated; a Failure still short-circuits, sound). (C1/F1, finders C+F independently) a NESTED carrier-eq truncation inside a bodied eq op collapsed to EvalError::Suspended in eval, and the bridge re-labeled it truncated:false (my comment even wrongly claimed 'complete'); CLOSED by threading truncated through EvalError::Suspended{detail,truncated} (all 5 construction sites: builtins.rs eq-undecided sites carry the real bit, dict/caller-frame/buried-override flounders = false), so bridge_eq_op_to_eval propagates it. This closes what a review would otherwise have spun off as the next increment.\n\nTESTS (+2 fns, full workspace green 2654/0 post-rebase onto WI-580): wi628_carrier_eq_truncation_taints_outer_stream (end-to-end guard-mode: truncating myeq self-loop under cfg(test) depth=32 -> outer truncated=true under definite_only + residualizes+taints under default mode + Refuted-contrast stays clear), wi628_prove_rule_predicate_distinguishes_truncated_from_refuted, wi628_floundered_carrier_eq_does_not_taint_outer_stream (a myeq_f whose body eq(?x,?y) delays => Undecided{truncated:false} => outer flag stays CLEAR -- the flounder-vs-truncation crux the Refuted contrast can't cover).\n\nDEFERRED (documented, low-value/higher-bar): (C3) SearchStream.truncated is a coarse whole-search flag -- a branch later pruned by cut can still taint it; sound-direction (over-conservative, never wrong), pre-existing. (D1/F3) BridgeEqOutcome ~ PredicateProof and EvalError::Suspended{truncated} + Delay{truncated} re-encode the same incompleteness bit; a single shared Incompleteness type would collapse them but is a larger refactor -- kept BridgeEqOutcome distinct (Decided(bool) fits the bridge's Bool). (H1/H3) the bridge re-entry-cap=>truncated path and a direct partial-carrier composite test are untested (expensive to construct: BRIDGE_REENTRY_CAP nesting / same_shape partial-carrier composite); logic verified by reasoning. (bridge_op_to_eval) the GENERAL operand-reduction bridge maps Suspended->None(delay) ignoring truncated -- NOT the eq-verdict path, conservative (delays, never decides empty-as-refute), out of the named scope.", at: "2026-07-09T08:38:20Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-628", description: some(value: "Resolver: silent depth-limit truncation lets NAF and definite-failure consumers DECIDE from an incomplete search. step_init pops a frame at max_depth (resolve.rs ~591) indistinguishably from definite failure; step_naf then reads an exhausted-but-truncated inner stream as 'no proof -> not(P) SUCCEEDS' — with default max_depth=100, not(P) over any derivation deeper than the REMAINING budget (step_naf passes max_depth - depth, resolve.rs ~1709) wrongly succeeds; same for any caller treating resolve()==[] as refutation. Violates the never-NAF-decide-undecided discipline (WI-067/WI-519) for truncated searches. WI-616 added the substrate: SearchStream.truncated (set at the depth pop) and the semantic-eq dispatch already answers UNDECIDED when its sub-resolution truncated — but NAF and other empty-result consumers do not consult it. SCOPE: thread truncated into step_naf's three-way (truncated+empty => flounder, not success), decide which other resolve() consumers must check it (prove/constraint guards under definite_only, forall/bounded-quant discharge, typer prove_from_gamma), and consider surfacing truncation on Solution/ResolveStats for hosts. Found during WI-616 /code-review (finder D)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-09T08:38:32Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-668", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED (2026-07-09), full anthill-core suite green, /code-review high done, committed 8cddb28f. Candidate::Continuation migrated Vec<TermId> -> Vec<Value>: term-lowered producers (push_choice, branch-from-streams) wrap Value::term, consumer is a bare extend(body). The WI-580 body-unfold now emits its hoisted op-call goals as Value::Node occurrences (materialize_from_handle at production), so the relational recursion re-triggers via op_call_as_occ's Node arm instead of round-tripping a Value::Term through term_body_to_nodes at re-entry; op_call_as_occ tightens to &self.\n\nSCOPE REFINEMENT (code review -- both correctness finders + the cross-file tracer agreed): the Term arm of op_call_as_occ is KEPT, not removed. Deleting it silently regresses any Term-carried SemEq goal with an unground bodied op-call operand -- a direct resolve(&[eq(append(?a,[3]),[1,3])]) query, or an or/push_choice branch -- because walk_view yields Value::term for any Term::Fn; that path residualizes instead of solving (a completeness loss / silent skip the repo forbids). It was untested before because every relational test wraps the eq in a rule (-> Value::Node). New regression guard wi668_term_carried_opcall_eq_case_splits queries the equation directly as a term and asserts ?a = [1] (fails with the Term arm gone, passes with it). So the Term-carrier bridge is REDUCED (WI-580 recursion routed off it) not REMOVED (it stays as the general handler for term-lowered goals) -- within the ticket's \"removed or reduced\" acceptance.\n\nCleanups: term_body_to_nodes(&[id]).into_iter().next() -> materialize_from_handle at both op_call_as_occ and the hoist producer (drops a throwaway 1-elem Vec + a misleading silent-? decline). KNOWN-BOUNDED: anf_flatten still builds the hoists as Vec<TermId> (materialized to Node here); a fully native occurrence-building unfold with no term intermediary is a separate future cleanup. Unblocks WI-580's remaining work.", at: "2026-07-09T10:06:23Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-668", description: some(value: "Migrate the resolver's transient goal carrier Candidate::Continuation from Vec<TermId> to Vec<Value> (design docs/design/abstract-interpreter-and-rules.md Section 10 Q1; proposal 033 Q4 \"TermId / Value asymmetry\").\n\nTODAY: Candidate::Continuation(Vec<TermId>) (kb/resolve.rs ~250) stores transit-interned TermId goals; the consumer immediately lifts them to Value::Term goals (resolve.rs ~2084, `new_goals.extend(body.into_iter().map(Value::term))`) before pushing the continuation frame. Three producers mint it: push_choice (proposal 033 / WI-075, resolve.rs ~866-867), branch-from-streams disjunction (resolve.rs ~1506), and the WI-580 body-unfold (resolve.rs ~5583, unfold_eq_operand).\n\nCHANGE: make the variant carry Vec<Value>; wrap at the producers instead of at the consumer. push_choice and branch-from-streams genuinely start from TermId goals -> wrap with Value::term at those sites; the consumer becomes `new_goals.extend(body)`.\n\nPAYOFF (why this is a WI-580 prerequisite): the WI-580 unfold pays a bridge tax for the TermId carrier. Because a Continuation's goals become Value::Term but is_unreduced_op_call is Value::Node-only, a hoisted recursive op-call is round-tripped back to a node occurrence via op_call_as_occ / term_body_to_nodes so it re-triggers the next one-step unfold. A Vec<Value> carrier lets the unfold emit native Value::Node op-call goals and DROP that bridge. Aligns with the carrier-neutral goal pipeline (WI-621) and the no-Value-to-TermId boundary (WI-348) -- both delivered, but both left this transient runtime carrier untouched.\n\nNOT covered by WI-246 (delivered): WI-246 migrated STORED rule-body atoms (RuleEntry.body: Vec<TermId> -> body_nodes occurrences) -- a persistent KB-storage layer, distinct from the resolver's runtime choice-point carrier here.\n\nACCEPTANCE: cargo-test green; Candidate::Continuation carries Vec<Value>; the WI-580 unfold's Value::Term -> node-occurrence re-materialization bridge (op_call_as_occ / term_body_to_nodes for the hoist re-trigger) removed or reduced.\n\nPREREQUISITE FOR WI-580: unblocks WI-580's remaining bridge cleanup. WI-580's body-derivation core (append structural-unfold + member eq-vs-unification) is already delivered; this migration is what lets the unfold shed its TermId round-trip."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-09T10:06:24Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-630", description: some(value: "loader-metadata functor hygiene + namespace-level EntityInfo (WI-515 follow-up). WI-515 removed the ONE loader-emitted fact whose head was a user data functor (the entity schema fact), fixing phantom rows in var-quantified queries/constraints — but the bug CLASS remains open: resolve() candidate lookup is sort-blind (discrim tree keys on structure), so any future loader/stage0 emission that heads a user functor re-creates the pollution silently. Two scope items: (1) enforce the invariant loudly — a load-time debug check (or assert_fact-seam guard) that a loader-emitted metadata fact never tops a user entity/rule functor; evaluate the ticket's alternative (sort/domain-scoped guard queries) as the deeper fix. Related residual: emit_operation_equation asserts eq(op(?params), <body>) facts under sort Eq whose head functor eq is a user-queryable prelude relation — shielded today only because eq is BuiltinTag::SemEq (see WI-627 short-name trap for how that shield can slip). (2) everything-is-facts gap: EntityInfo facts are emitted only for SORT-BODY entities (load_sort_with_body); a namespace-level entity's only declaration record is now the Rust-side entity_field_types registry, invisible to anthill-level queries — emit EntityInfo for namespace-level entities too. NOTE the accepted WI-515 behavior changes this WI should NOT revert: ambiguous short-name entity resolution = deterministic min-qualified-name (KB::resolve_entity_functor); find_entity_schema serves value-in-type entities. ACCEPTANCE: invariant guard in place + a test that a same-functor metadata fact is rejected loudly; EntityInfo emitted for namespace-level entities + reflect query test; cargo-test green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-09T10:29:38Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-580", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED (2026-07-09). Design-first acceptance met and all in-scope implementation landed:\n- Design note: docs/design/abstract-interpreter-and-rules.md (body views come from ONE on-demand specializer; derived rule == one-step abstract interpretation of the body).\n- Increment 1 (3b571e45): SLD one-step body-unfold (abstract-interpretation-on-suspend) — case-split a suspended SemEq op-call operand into one choice-point per match arm; covers structural ops (relational append(?a,ys)=zs solves ?a). Retired length/append <=> twins.\n- Increment 2 (9ac89d85): the headline member eq-vs-unification soundness fix — retired member's two :- unification twins (matched cons(head:?x,...) STRUCTURALLY, diverging unsoundly from the body's declared eq(head,x) for a custom Eq). Relational view now derived from the body via bare_bodied_bool_relation + step_init routing to eq(f(args),true).\n- Increment 3 (9e0fbed4): retired the dead progress bool in kb/body_specialize.rs::reduce.\n- Prerequisite WI-668 (8cddb28f): Candidate::Continuation Vec<TermId>->Vec<Value>; the WI-580 recursion now emits Value::Node hoists and re-triggers via op_call_as_occ's Node arm.\n\nScope items 2-4 (retire the list.anthill append/member duplicates; laws are theorems proved from the body; hand-written rules survive only for bodyless relations) delivered/affirmed. Scope item 1's remaining prover/SMT view -- §3.4 defining-equation extraction on demand -- carved out to WI-669 (follow-on; design doc frames it \"separate future\"; reuses the delivered body_specialize engine). Full anthill-core suite green throughout; /code-review high on each increment.", at: "2026-07-09T11:25:08Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-580", description: some(value: "Operation body as the single source of truth -- derive rule views, retire hand-written duplicates.\n\nPRINCIPLE (design fork agreed 2026-06-28 with user): an operation's BODY is the one definition. Its equational (<=>), proof, and relational (Prolog-style) views should be DERIVED from the body, not hand-written alongside it. A hand-written rule that duplicates a body is redundant and can drift -- or diverge from it unsoundly.\n\nEVIDENCE -- stdlib/anthill/prelude/list.anthill:\n- append: the two <=> rules are the operation body's match arms restated verbatim (nil -> ys ; cons(x,rest) -> cons(x, append(rest,ys))) -- pure duplication, mechanically derivable from the body.\n- member: NOT a safe duplicate. The body branches on eq(head,x) (the Eq op); the rules branch on cons(head: ?x, ...) (UNIFICATION). For a type with a custom Eq (eq != structural equality) the unification rules give WRONG answers -- a latent soundness gap. Deriving the relational form from the eq-body makes membership use the declared Eq consistently (and, owed-on-consumption, the relational query then correctly requires Eq[T]).\n\nSCOPE:\n1. Derive an operation's <=>/rewrite + relational forms from its body (\"rule is THE knowledge primitive\": an op body desugars to an eval strategy PLUS its rules).\n2. Retire hand-written rules that duplicate a body (append) or unsoundly diverge from it (member).\n3. Extra LAWS (e.g. append associativity) are NOT hand-written defining rules -- they are THEOREMS proved from the body.\n4. Hand-written rules survive only for genuine STANDALONE relations (predicates with no operation body).\n\nRELATION to WI-502 / WI-578: separable. The typed-value carrier (WI-578) is unaffected (it is about how a value carries its type, however the rule arose). But this tightens the typed-rule design: derived rules are typed by the body (signature + owed-on-consumption), so the explicit [E]/?x:T typed-pattern surface is needed only for bodyless relations. See docs/design/constrained-term-substrate.md \"Typed rule patterns -- surface and matching\".\n\nACCEPTANCE: design-first -- a proposal/design note specifying (a) the body -> rule derivation, and (b) the retirement of the list.anthill append/member duplicates, including member's eq-vs-unification soundness fix. Implementation of the derivation is a follow-on."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-668"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-09T11:25:09Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-639", description: some(value: "Distributive dot projection `x.(m1, ..., mn)`: a general syntactic form generalizing WI-638's single-field dot access `x.f` to a MEMBER LIST. `x.(m1, ..., mn)` desugars to the ORDERED/NAMED tuple `(m1: x.m1, ..., mn: x.mn)` -- distribute the receiver x over the member list; each `x.mi` is ordinary dot-dispatch (a field via WI-638, but any member/method/component), and the result is keyed by the member names.\n\nDESUGARING: a bare member auto-labels (the member name is BOTH the result key AND the dot-member): `x.(f1, f2)` => `(f1: x.f1, f2: x.f2)`. Rename via colon: `x.(a: f1, b: f2)` => `(a: x.f1, b: x.f2)`. A single member 1-collapses: `x.(f)` => `(f: x.f)` -> collapses to `x.f`.\n\nTWO LOAD-BEARING RULES: (1) the result is the ORDERED/NAMED tuple, NOT positional -- labels are preserved from the member names, never auto-named _1/_2 (a positional result would destroy a projected relation's schema). (2) members resolve at TYPING, not at name-resolution -- each mi lands in `field_access(x, mi)` dot-position, resolved against x's type at the typer (WI-638's resolve_field_type, dot-dispatch mode-3), riding UNRESOLVED past the name resolver; a member name is therefore NEVER a value-position scope symbol, so BOTH keep (`x.(f1, f2)`) and rename (`x.(a: f1)`) resolve safely with no free-identifier hazard (this is precisely why a bare `select(x, y)` surface was rejected in proposal 052).\n\nTOUCH-POINTS (mirror WI-638): (a) grammar (tree-sitter-anthill/grammar.js) -- a new `x.(...)` form: dot + a parenthesized member/rename list (`.(` is currently free; field access today is `x.ident`). GOTCHA: run `cargo clean -p tree-sitter-anthill` after any grammar.js change or the build uses a stale parser. (b) parse/convert.rs -- desugar `x.(m1, ..., mn)` -> named-tuple literal `(m1: field_access(x, m1), ...)`, duplicating a bare member into both the label and the field_access member; a rename `a: f1` -> label `a` + `field_access(x, f1)`. Desugaring EARLY (at convert) means the existing WI-638 field-access typing + named-tuple-literal typing + eval handle the rest with no new typer/eval arm. (c) typer/eval -- reuse WI-638 dot-dispatch (typing.rs resolve_field_type NamedTuple arm) + named-tuple construction; verify auto-label and 1-collapse.\n\nGENERAL FEATURE: works on ANY named tuple / any receiver with dot-members, not just relations -- useful to any tuple consumer, exactly as WI-638 was.\n\nDRIVER: proposal 052 (docs/proposals/052-rules-as-stream-valued-operations.md, section \"Projection -- the distribute-dot\"). 052 lifts this over a relation -- `r.(f1, f2)` -> `projected` (schema-preserving via the name-keying) -- RETIRING the `select` op; 052 depends on this the way it now depends on WI-638, but the feature is general and 052 merely consumes it.\n\nOPEN SUB-DECISIONS (052 OQ3): a non-bare member that is a call or expression (`x.(count(), y)`) needs a label rule (label = the head identifier, or an explicit `n: expr`); a positional variant is deferred (default: named only -- write `(x.f1, x.f2)` explicitly for positional); an exclude form (`x.(-f3)`) is deferred (the Mongo `(f3: 0)` variant's one edge, rejected for its magic flags).\n\nACCEPTANCE: `(x: 10, y: 20, z: 30).(x, y)` evaluates to `(x: 10, y: 20)`; rename `(x: 10, y: 20).(a: x, b: y)` -> `(a: 10, b: 20)`; single `(x: 10, y: 20).(x)` 1-collapses to 10; a param typed `t: (x: Int64, y: Int64)` -- `t.(x, y)` typechecks and evaluates; members resolve at typing (no value-position name-resolution of a member); existing single-field `x.f` (WI-638) and positional `t._N` are unaffected; cargo test green with a test paralleling tests/include/wi638_named_tuple_field_test.rs. REF: WI-638 (named-tuple field access, delivered -- the single-field basis + resolve_field_type / dot-dispatch mode-3), kernel-language.md 6.7 (dot projection -- extend with a member-list form; currently three modes), 4.6 (named tuples are ordered products). Rust-only (typer/parser; scaland has no typer)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-638"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-09T13:08:45Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-645", author: "user", content: "ALREADY DELIVERED via WI-644 (Direction B, the principled PartialEq/Eq + PartialOrd/Ordered split) + WI-664 (composite field-wise eq) -- no separate implementation needed, exactly as this ticket's prior feedback anticipated ('library-004 build-step 2 IS the fix ... and un-ignores the wi645 repro test'; direction A unnecessary once the split lands). VERIFIED 2026-07-09 on clean main (HEAD 720e3c04), zero code change in this delivery. Repro test wi645_float_nan_ieee_test: 3 passed, 0 ignored -- eq_ordered_on_nan_follow_ieee (IEEE target), struct_eq_on_nan_stays_structural, totalfloat_eq_is_lawful_reflexive_on_nan. Acceptance confirmed against current code: eval float_ieee_eq = raw f64 == (eq(nan,nan)=false, neq=true, -0.0 eq +0.0 = true); resolver sem_eq_core gates on value_f64 BEFORE the reflexivity shortcut; builtin_cmp uses partial_cmp so gt/lt/gte/lte with a NaN operand = false (IEEE unordered); === stays on views_structurally_equal (nan===nan=true, hash-consing/dedup intact); Set/Map/entity structural eq unchanged. WI-664 covers the composite level (Point(nan,_) no longer laundered). scaland: no mirror needed (loads no semantic eq). Full workspace green: 69 binaries, 2665 passed, 0 failed.", at: "2026-07-09T13:18:50Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-645", description: some(value: "BUG (soundness) + DESIGN: Anthill's `Eq` CONFLATES a partial equality (`PartialEq`: may be non-reflexive) with a lawful equivalence (`Eq`: reflexive), and the resolver/interpreter HARDCODES reflexivity -- which is unsound for ANY partial carrier. Float (NaN) is the first such carrier: the interpreter's semantic Float `eq`/`neq` and `Ordered.gt/lt/gte/lte` violate IEEE 754 and DIVERGE from both the C++ codegen and the stdlib's own contract. Same Anthill program, OPPOSITE answers interpreted vs compiled.\n\nEMPIRICALLY CONFIRMED (interpreter, `nan` = anthill.prelude.Float.nan):\n  eq(nan, nan)  = true   [IEEE + stdlib say FALSE] -- BUG   |  gt(nan, 1.0) = true  [IEEE FALSE] -- BUG\n  neq(nan, nan) = false  [IEEE + stdlib say TRUE]  -- BUG   |  lt(nan, 1.0) = false [coincidentally right]\n  nan === nan   = true   [structural identity -- CORRECT, keep]\nThe gt=true/lt=false asymmetry is the \"NaN sorts as OrderedFloat's MAX\" fingerprint.\n\nROOT CAUSE IS NOT A LAW -- IT IS HARDCODED REFLEXIVITY. The `Eq` sort (stdlib/anthill/prelude/eq.anthill) declares only `rule neq(?a,?b) <=> not(eq(?a,?b))`, which is IEEE-CONSISTENT (neq(nan,nan)=not(false)=true). There is NO reflexivity/symmetry/transitivity law anywhere in the stdlib. The IEEE violation comes from the IMPLEMENTATION assuming `eq(x,x)=true`: (1) sem_eq_core's fast-path `if values_equal(a,b) return true` (resolve.rs:3493) and (2) eval `builtin_eq` = views_structurally_equal (all-structural, eval/builtins.rs:417). Both read Float leaves as Literal::Float(OrderedFloat<f64>) (term_view.rs:793, term.rs:282), and ordered_float treats \"all NaN equal\" + \"NaN is the max\" (ordered-float lib.rs:84,294). Ordered.gt/lt = builtin_cmp reads value_num->OrderedFloat, so the total order (NaN=max) leaks into comparisons too.\n\nTHE DISTINCTION IS PartialEq vs Eq (Rust precedent). Rust gives `f64: PartialEq` (== is IEEE, NaN != NaN, reflexivity NOT guaranteed) but `f64: !Eq` (Eq is the stricter reflexive MARKER, which f64 deliberately does not implement BECAUSE of NaN). Anthill's single `Eq` merges the two and the resolver silently assumes the strong (reflexive) one -- so IEEE `==` (lawfully only `PartialEq`) cannot be a lawful `Eq` instance. This is also adjacent to the WI-573 hazard the sem_eq_core doc already flags (structural fast-path ignoring a carrier's own `eq`).\n\nDIVERGES FROM CODEGEN: anthill-cpp-gen maps Eq.eq->C++ `==`, Ordered.gt->`>` (lib.rs:2902/2898). C++ double ==/> are genuine IEEE, so COMPILED eq(nan,nan)=false, gt(nan,1.0)=false -- the opposite of the interpreter. And CONTRADICTS THE STDLIB CONTRACT: float.anthill:19-20 \"Float.eq returns false for NaN (NaN != NaN in IEEE)\"; :59 \"nan is unequal to everything, itself included\".\n\nLOAD-BEARING CONSTRAINT: OrderedFloat is REQUIRED at the STRUCTURAL layer (Literal must be Hash+Eq for TermStore hash-consing/dedup; `===`/struct_eq is structural identity and nan===nan=true is CORRECT). So the STRUCTURAL layer (===, views_structurally_equal, Literal-Hash) must stay OrderedFloat; only the SEMANTIC path changes.\n\nTWO FIX DIRECTIONS:\n  (A) PRAGMATIC INTERIM -- treat `Eq` as PartialEq-like: DROP/guard the hardcoded reflexivity for partial carriers so semantic Eq/Ordered on Float read raw f64 IEEE (eq(nan,_)=false, neq=true, all NaN-operand comparisons false, -0.0 eq +0.0 = true), matching codegen. sem_eq_core's `values_equal` reflexivity fast-path must NOT short-circuit two NaNs; eval builtin_eq needs the Float-numeric IEEE path (builtin_cmp already extracts value_num for Ordered -- eq/neq need the analogous path, falling back to structural for non-numeric so Set/Map/entity eq is unchanged; WI-616 carrier-override dispatch preserved). Keeps `===` structural. Fast, unblocks the interp/codegen soundness divergence, but leaves a non-reflexive thing still named `Eq`.\n  (B) PRINCIPLED TARGET (likely its own design ticket, supersedes WI-644's \"drop the default\"): SPLIT the hierarchy PartialEq subset-of Eq -- `eq`/`neq` on a PartialEq-like spec (Float provides it, IEEE); a reflexive `Eq`/`TotalEq` MARKER Float does NOT provide, REQUIRED by dedup / set keys / sorting / simp-reflexivity; a total-Float wrapper (OrderedFloat-style) for reflexive contexts; and the \"modular symbol loading\" question -- keep IEEE `==` a distinct symbol from lawful `eq`, resolved per module/import so the two never conflate. Codegen: PartialEq.eq -> C++ ==; reflexive contexts use the total wrapper.\n\nRELATION: standalone SOUNDNESS bug (interp != codegen != documented contract); does NOT depend on the SLD->eval bridge (WI-625 -- the culprit is the raw interpreter builtins + resolver fast-path). Concrete evidence that Float is a primitive whose host equality is NON-structural, so it needs PartialEq semantics, not a lawful/reflexive Eq -- informs WI-644 (Eq-instance semantics) and builds on WI-616 (semantic eq dispatch).\n\nACCEPTANCE (direction A, interim): interpreter eq(nan,nan)=false, neq(nan,nan)=true, gt/lt/gte/lte with a NaN operand = false (IEEE unordered), -0.0 eq +0.0 = true; the resolver's reflexivity fast-path no longer forces `eq(x,x)` for a partial (Float) carrier; nan === nan stays true (structural identity, unchanged); Set/Map/entity structural eq + hash-consing/dedup unchanged; the same ops codegen'd to C++ agree with the interpreter; repro test wi645_float_nan_ieee_test.rs un-ignored; cargo test green. (Direction B is tracked as the follow-on design ticket if chosen.) REF: eval/builtins.rs:413-422, kb/resolve.rs (sem_eq_core reflexivity fast-path ~3493 / values_equal / builtin_cmp / value_num), kb/term_view.rs:780-823, kb/term.rs:278-282, stdlib/anthill/prelude/eq.anthill (no reflexivity law), stdlib/anthill/prelude/float.anthill:19-62, anthill-cpp-gen/src/lib.rs:2889-2906. Rust-only (interpreter + resolver; scaland shares the OrderedFloat structural comparator -- mirror if it exposes semantic eq)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-09T13:18:51Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-632", description: some(value: "reflect name resolution API: typed refs / scoped resolution for the string-name surface (the principled fix behind WI-515/WI-631). KB.fields(kb, name: String), sort_query(String), and the generated-bridge fields/operations/constructors/rules(sort_name: String) resolve entity/sort names by GLOBAL string matching with no scope context — a parallel resolution path that bypasses the symbol table's scope-aware machinery (imports, enclosing namespace), which is why short-name ambiguity (WI-631) exists at all. MIGRATE the surface to typed references: (a) KB.fields / sort_query take a Ref/Term (the facts_of precedent — it takes the entity by reference, Type carrying Ref(Color.red)), so names resolve where they are WRITTEN, by the loader, with real import/namespace scoping and zero runtime string matching; (b) keep String forms as qualified-only conveniences (resolve via try_resolve_symbol, no short-name scan) or deprecate; (c) where a runtime string is genuinely needed (host tooling), consider an explicit scope parameter routed through symbols.resolve_in_scope. TOUCHES: stdlib/anthill/reflect/reflect.anthill (KB.fields / LogicalQuery.sort_query signatures), both realizations (anthill-stl reflect builtins + generated bridge / codegen emit), execute lowering (find_entity_schema), tests. NB scaland mirrors grammar/loader/resolver only — check whether its reflect surface (if any) needs the same. Depends on: WI-631 (lands the loud-ambiguity stopgap first)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-09T14:45:19Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-632", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED on branch wi-632-reflect-typed-refs (3 commits 1d799f03/64917cae/d61f9031; NOT pushed). Migrated the whole reflect string-name surface to BY-REFERENCE (facts_of precedent): sort_query(sort:Term), sort_template/instantiation_query/fields(entity)/operations/constructors/rules all take Type; runtime resolution = eval::value_functor(kb,&ref)->functor Symbol, zero string matching, so WI-631 short-name ambiguity is structurally impossible on this surface. DROPPED the String forms (no overloading + zero .anthill callers); sorts(namespace)/descriptions(target) left String (filters, not scope-resolved refs). REMOVED the WI-631 stopgap: KnowledgeBase::resolve_entity_functor + ambiguous_entity_message + reader::read_entity_fields + wi631 test; intern::ResolveResult kept (scope resolution); term_ser::resolve_entity_functor (private persistence sibling) untouched. inc3 readers match a fact's domain / a Member's parent by functor Symbol via new reader::term_head_sym (was term_display_name full-or-short string); shared extraction helpers sort_ref_functor (interp, EvalError) + KbBridge::ref_functor (bridge, panic per facts_of). sort_query field renamed sort_name->sort (LowerError::UnknownSort -> NotASortReference). Scaland has no reflect surface (no mirror). WI-632(c) scoped-string host API not needed (no caller). Full workspace suite green (exit 0, 0 failures) per increment; /code-review per-increment + whole-branch consolidation = 8 fresh finders, no correctness bugs, field-key-rename consistency confirmed.", at: "2026-07-09T14:45:22Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-340", description: some(value: "Scaland follow-on: AnthillParser.scala (line ~414, typeExprToRef) still lowers TypeExpr.Arrow to arrow(effects: cons-list, ...), diverging from the post-WI-307 / WI-331 Rust loader shape (effects: effects_rows(EffectExpression) as a singular Type) and from the stdlib schema. Today this is LATENT — scaland has no operation loading or native typer (per memory: scaland-no-operation-loading.md), so nothing consumes the malformed arrow term and no test catches the divergence. The pre-existing WI-288 test (ParseTest.scala:471) only asserts the arrow functor name 'anthill.prelude.Type.arrow', not the effects-field shape. SCOPE: update AnthillParser.typeExprToRef to construct effects as the canonical effects_rows(EffectExpression) wrapper (the Scala port of build_canonical_effects_rows), matching the Rust loader. The Scala-side EffectExpression entity builders need to be added (parallel to Rust make_effect_expression_*). Add a ParseTest assertion that checks the effects field shape (effects_rows wrapper + inner merge chain) on a 2-effect arrow type, not just the arrow functor name. Will become a blocker once scaland gains operation loading (WI-292 / WI-294). ACCEPTANCE: scaland arrow terms match Rust shape; new ParseTest asserts effects_rows; existing tests still green. (depends: WI-307, WI-331)"), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-09T19:01:01Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-583", description: some(value: "Position-directed automatic gating of Bool-returning operations as rule-body conditions (the implicit eq(op, true) desugaring). GOAL: let a Bool-returning operation be used directly as a rule-body goal/condition — write `:- valid(?x)` instead of the explicit `:- eq(valid(?x), true)`. Sibling of WI-529 (position-directed boolean connectives and/or/not): this extends position-directed handling to Bool-returning OPERATIONS in goal position. SEMANTICS: a bare op-call goal boolOp(args) whose functor is an operation with return type Bool desugars to eq(boolOp(args), true) — reduce the op-call; reduces to true => goal SUCCEEDS, false => FAILS, under-determined (args unbound / abstract carrier) => SUSPEND (residual, never NAF-decided, WI-067 discipline). GATING CONDITION (typer-checked): apply ONLY when the goal head is an operation returning Bool; a relation (rule-head functor) still resolves relationally; a non-Bool op in goal position is a LOUD error, not silently treated as a relation. POSITION-DIRECTED: goal/rule-body position only; a Bool op in VALUE position is unaffected (it is just a value), exactly the WI-529 split. REALIZATION (decide at impl): (a) loader desugaring — rewrite a bare Bool-op goal to eq(op, true), reusing the resolver builtin_eq + reduce_operand (which already evaluate an op-call operand and compare); or (b) a resolver bridge — a goal reducing to Value::Bool maps true to Success / false to Fail. Option (a) is the lower-risk reuse. COMPOSES: a Bool SPEC op gated this way dispatches through the requires dictionary over the carried type (WI-573 spec-op guard discharge) — gating + WI-573 compose for non-native carriers; and a gated Bool-op condition owes the op requires as a rule condition (the owed-on-use model, docs/design/constrained-term-substrate.md §Rules about an operation). SOUNDNESS: gate ONLY PURE Bool ops — an effectful op evaluated in a guard is a silent side effect (reject, or require purity); a partial/erroring op surfaces its error, not a silent fail. NOT a WI-502 prerequisite (WI-502 typed rule patterns use type guards + comparisons; this is an ergonomic surface). ACCEPTANCE: a pure Bool op used bare in a rule body (`:- valid(?x)` with valid: T -> Bool) parses, loads, and resolves as eq(valid(?x), true) — true=success, false=fail, unbound-arg=suspend; a non-Bool op in goal position is a loud load/type error; cargo-test green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-09T16:20:57Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-583", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED 2026-07-09. Happy path (a pure Bool op used bare as a rule-body goal resolves as eq(op,true): true=success/false=fail/unbound=suspend) was ALREADY shipped by WI-580 inc2 (resolve.rs bare_bodied_bool_relation + step_init routing) -- verified generic, not member-only. WI-583's NEW work = the STATIC loud error for a non-Bool op in goal position (was a silent failed relation lookup). Impl: new TypeError::NonBoolOpInGoalPosition + typer pass check_rule_body_goal_ops in kb/typing.rs (sibling of WI-642 check_rule_body_requirements; runs in type_check_sorts_typed after build_op_signatures so return types are settled). check_goal_atom_op walks each non-fact rule body's top-level goal atoms, recursing THROUGH goal-connectives (reflect.not/kernel.or/push_choice) into their goal args, and flags a functor that is: non-builtin, an operation with a built signature, used at DECLARED arity, whose return is a CONCRETE non-Bool sort, and rule-less. SKIPS (each correct/pre-existing, never a regression): builtins; relations (rules_by_functor non-empty); Bool-return (routed to eq(_,true)); the functional-relation form f(args,result) at arity+1 (the KEY discriminator -- e.g. stdlib needs_rebuild's status(?fs,?p,FileStatus(...)) which the audit caught); a non-concrete/generic return (bare type param, may instantiate to Bool -- reads the declared sig like sibling passes); effectful/bodyless Bool ops (bodyless spec ops dispatch via WI-573, effectful are purity-gated by bare_bodied_bool_relation -- documented boundary). Shared KnowledgeBase::sort_sym_is_bool helper dedups the 'is Bool' short-name check between the resolver gate and this typer check so they cannot drift. Spec note added to kernel-language.md (near the WI-580 relational-view text). /code-review high (3 finder agents): confirmed exhaustive TypeError plumbing + real load-blocking chain; FIXED a false-positive (a non-concrete return was flagged -> skip when sort_functor_of_view=None) + docstring overclaim + extracted the shared helper; REFUTED the arity-gate-defeated-by-requires/self candidate (verified: requires + dot-dispatched sort-scoped ops keep surface arity and ARE flagged). scaland: no change (no typer). Tests: wi583_bool_op_goal_test.rs (8: happy path success/fail, suspend, non-Bool error, under-not, dot-scoped error, generic-return-not-flagged, relation-resolves). Full workspace green 2673/0.", at: "2026-07-09T16:20:58Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-652", description: some(value: "WI-650 follow-up: close the coverage gaps in check_eq_override_backing (the load-time guard that flags eq(map,map) over an unbacked Eq override). It is BEST-EFFORT over operands the typer stamps concretely as the carrier sort in rule/op bodies; a /code-review (high) surfaced these escape routes, all LATENT today (no stdlib/example/test triggers them; suite green) but each a silent-misdecide the guard is meant to catch: (1) COMPOUND operands — operand_unbacked_eq_carrier bails on inferred_type()==None, so eq(put(empty(),1,2), m) / eq(build_map(x), other()) (Apply/Constructor operands, not var leaves) escape; same leaf-reliance check_one_spec_op_requirement has. (2) DOT-FORM a.eq(b) — WI-279/WI-350 dot dispatch rewrites the receiver's eq to the carrier's OWN op Map.eq, whose functor is NOT Eq.eq/Eq.neq, so is_eq_call misses it; need to also recognize a direct call to an unbacked-carrier own eq op. (3) CONSTRAINT/guard bodies — constraints live in kb.guards (Vec<Guard>), NOT self.rules, so live_rule_ids never visits them; a constraint c :- eq(m1,m2) over maps loads clean (also verify guard-body operands even carry stamped inferred_type). (4) POLYMORPHIC abstract-T operands — operation same(a:T,b:T)=eq(a,b) requires Eq[T] instantiated at T=Map reads operand sort as abstract T, never concretizes to Map; inherent to a load-time inferred_type check (same class as the neq under-determination, WI-651). (5) WI-625 INTERACTION — carrier_has_unbacked_eq_override deliberately excludes the is_builtin backing leg (so Eq.eq's builtin-ness doesn't mask the empty Map override); when the WI-625 host bridge registers Map.eq AS a resolver builtin (no body_node, no rule), this predicate will WRONGLY read it unbacked and keep rejecting map compares — WI-625's implementer must update the backing predicate (or the check) then. Also a latent FALSE-POSITIVE: a carrier backing eq via an equational '=' definition (eq(?a,?b)=rhs, UNINDEXED from rules_by_functor per WI-139/WI-627) is invisible to the general-clause probe and would be flagged spuriously — reconcile with op_backed's eq_defined leg (but NOT its plain non-fact-rule leg, which would count Map's get-rewrite law and mask the real bug). ALTITUDE/DEDUP (fold-when-touched, no perf impact measured — WI-650 added ~0.2s to a ~9s debug stdlib load): the pass adds 2-3 extra whole-KB body traversals duplicating check_rule_body_requirements + check_operation_bodies, and check_occ_eq_override_backing is a 4th copy of the stack+for_each_child+Apply/Constructor/Instantiation walk (a shared walk_body_occurrences helper would collapse all four); a single parameterized backed-by(kind) helper should unify carrier_has_unbacked_eq_override with op_backed. Acceptance: pick the high-value gaps (compound operands + dot-form + constraints are the reachable ones) and close them with tests mirroring map_eq_error_test.rs; document any deliberately-left-open (polymorphic-T); full suite green. Follow-up to WI-650 (see also WI-651 neq)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-09T20:22:29Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-635", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED. Root: arity-0 fact heads carrying live Var::Global (loader omitted-field fills, explicit fact p(?x), or value facts whose children carry Globals) took the resolver raw-bind fast-path (is_fact && rule_arity==0), leaking the fact's PERSISTENT vid so two goals matching one fact aliased it. Fix (2 parts): (1) cache RuleEntry.head_vars: Vec<VarId> collected carrier-agnostically once at assert (push_value_head_entry -> collect_head_global_vars); gate reads rule_head_has_vars (!is_empty, O(1), no per-match head walk). Non-empty routes off the fast-path. (2) made with_fresh_vars' arity-0 legacy path CARRIER-NEUTRAL: seed all_vars from cached head_vars instead of collect_vars_rec(rule_head(id)), dropping the term-only rule_head read. KEY: the naive term-only gate scoping introduces a LIVE panic (rule_head panics on value-Entity OperationInfo facts whose type_params carry Globals -- reproduced by /code-review); per user steer toward carrier-neutral interfaces, dropping rule_head is both the panic fix AND fixes the value-fact half of the same alias hazard. collect_head_global_vars leniently skips Value::Node denoted children (WI-378 kb-threading migration not landed; sound as it only drives freshening/gating). Tests: 4 in resolve_tests (aliasing repro 0->1, single-match baseline, fact-p(?x)-equals-rule consistency, value-Entity no-panic guard verified to panic without the fix). Full anthill-core suite green (all bins, github_todo 11/11). 3-agent /code-review + 1 adversarial verify pass. NOT git-committed (awaiting user).", at: "2026-07-09T21:37:20Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-635", description: some(value: "Resolver: bodyless LEGACY Global-var heads raw-bind the rule's persistent VarId into sigma — the arity-0 remnant of the WI-624 bug class (CONFIRMED pre-existing by the WI-624 /code-review verify pass; WI-624's gate 'is_fact && rule_arity == 0' fixed only the DeBruijn population). POPULATION IS LIVE: surface facts with OMITTED entity fields get fresh Var::Global fills in the asserted head (load.rs:6675-6680; github_todo_test.rs:240 documents 'omitted -> fresh-var'), assert via assert_fact -> assert_rule -> assert_rule_nodes with ground-fact defaults (arity 0, globals empty, kb/mod.rs:940/:1063) — so they take the resolver's raw-bind fact fast-path (resolve.rs ~1880): a query var matching the var-filled position binds to the fact's PERSISTENT stored VarId with no per-invocation freshening. FAILURE: two goals matching the same fact in one derivation alias the SAME vid — Item(id:A, note: ?n1), Item(id:A, note: ?n2), ?n1 <=> 1, ?n2 <=> 2 spuriously fails; and post-WI-624, 'fact p(?x)' (raw-binds shared vid) vs bodyless 'rule p(?x)' (freshens per match via with_fresh_vars) now behave DIFFERENTLY for the same declaration meaning. No existing test covers the aliasing (WI-501's test deliberately avoids omitted fields). FIX DIRECTION: freshen at match time — the machinery already exists as with_fresh_vars' arity-0 legacy path (kb/mod.rs ~3144, reads rule_head + renames Globals) but bodyless entries are diverted before it; either route var-headed arity-0 facts there (needs a cheap 'head has vars' signal — a cached head-groundness flag on RuleEntry set at assert; do NOT walk the head per match, the todo workitem facts are large), or DeBruijn-close omitted-field fact heads at load so the WI-624 gate covers them. The resolve.rs fast-path comment cites this WI."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-09T21:37:31Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-662", description: some(value: "CARRIER-AGNOSTIC storage holdout (bucket 2 of the carrier-agnostic-heads umbrella): RequiresEntry.spec (typing.rs:21531 `pub spec: TermId`) is a hash-consed TermId -- the full SortView term (bindings T=Int, combine=add) of a sort's `requires` clause. A TermId CANNOT carry a denoted-bearing binding (a Value::Node, e.g. `requires Foo[E=Modify[c]]`), so sort-level requires silently excludes effect-bearing specs. This is the SAME term-assumption as rule_head, and it is INCONSISTENT within the requires feature: OP-level requires (OpInfoRecord.requires, op_info.rs ~line 65) was ALREADY migrated to Vec<Value> by WI-366 B2 ('a denoted-bearing precondition requires Modify[c] is a Value::Node'), but SORT-level requires (RequiresEntry.spec) stayed TermId. FIX (WI-366-B2 template): change RequiresEntry.spec: TermId -> Value (carrier-agnostic); thread carrier-faithfully through requires_tree / requires_chain construction (typing.rs ~21531+) and every reader of .spec (dispatch/coherence, requirement discharge, the WI-230 tree substitution that composes root-scoped bindings top-down). Readers that currently get_term(spec)/treat it as a TermId move to TermView / carrier-agnostic reads. The caches (requires_tree_cache, requires_chain_cache) are keyed by SYMBOL already -- ONLY the stored spec value-type changes, not the keying. WATCH: RequiresNode.entry.spec carries a substituted (root-scoped) view -- the substitution must stay carrier-faithful (walk_view, not a TermId->TermId walk). Acceptance: full suite green; a sort-level `requires` carrying a denoted-bearing binding round-trips (test mirroring the WI-366-B2 op-level case); ground specs behave identically. Part of the carrier-agnostic-heads umbrella; templates WI-366 B2 (op-level), WI-348 (carrier-agnostic heads), WI-394 (term-only walk migration)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-09T23:05:09Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-662", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED 2026-07-09. RequiresEntry.spec: TermId -> Value (bucket 2 of WI-661). Producers (direct_requires, op_requires_entries) now PRESERVE denoted value-fact SortRequiresInfo (was skipped via fact_head_named_args-else-continue); readers dispatch ground(Value::Term=byte-identical get_term)/denoted(TermView walk); manual Eq/Hash so RequiresEntry still keys resolve_cache. KEY: /code-review high (6 finders) caught 4 REAL bugs in the NEW code, all fixed: (F1) spec_eq_key keyed denoted specs by Debug which embeds interior-mutable NodeOccurrence.term_cache Cell + span -> a live resolve_cache HashMap key hash mutates in place (unsound) -> switched to Rc data-pointer identity; (F2) substitute_in_spec/substitute_spec_via_subst cloned denoted specs VERBATIM, skipping WI-230 root-scope substitution of a co-carried type-param binding -> carrier-faithful Value::Entity walk (substitute Term leaves, preserve Node); (F4) requirement_ranges_over_owner_tparams denoted arm dropped the bare-Fn pos_args branch -> unified to ONE TermView body + shared view_is_sort_view; (A#5) push_op_requires_clause accepted Ident where ground skips -> aligned. F3 verified UNREACHABLE (sort-level specs always SortView). Post-WI-390 no surface syntax yields a value-fact SortRequiresInfo, so the 3 new wi662 tests CONSTRUCT it directly. Full suite 69 bins/2685 passed/0 fail. Remaining WI-661 children: WI-663, bucket-1 SortInfo/RequiresInfo indexes + iterator variant.", at: "2026-07-09T23:05:26Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-663", description: some(value: "CARRIER-AGNOSTIC head readers (bucket 2 of the carrier-agnostic-heads umbrella): rule_head (mod.rs, the term-only head reader) PANICS on a value-fact head ('rule_head: head is not a Term carrier -- a value fact reached a term-only head reader (WI-348); read via rule_head_value / TermView instead'). This is the HARD-looking pressure that keeps rule heads as hash-consed terms, but it is NOT architectural: (a) LOWER the panic to a graceful LoadError -- loud-but-not-fatal (repo 'loud error over silent skip' principle) -- so a value head reaching an un-migrated reader is a clear load-time diagnostic, never a process abort; (b) MIGRATE the term-only head readers to carrier-agnostic rule_head_value / TermView where they only need functor + structure. Readers to audit/migrate (consumers of rules_by_functor results + others): stored_lhs_functor (simp_rewrite.rs, rule_head), open_equation (simp_rewrite.rs, rule_head), rule_is_general_eq_clause (typing.rs, WI-650, rule_head + get_term), the resolve_sort_alias / build_sort_alias_index scans (fact_head_term -- currently SKIP value heads, WI-659), and any get_term-on-head path. IMPORTANT ordering: bucket-1 (replace the by-subfield rules_by_functor scans with typed indexes: WI-656/659/660/...) REMOVES several of these readers outright -- do this migration AFTER those land so the surface is minimal. WI-394 (term-only walk_type -> carrier-agnostic walk_view) is the exact template. Acceptance: the rule_head-family readers either handle value heads via TermView or emit a LoadError (no panic) on a genuine value-head-in-term-only context; full suite green; a synthetic value-fact head reaching a migrated reader resolves (or LoadErrors) instead of panicking. Part of the carrier-agnostic-heads umbrella; see WI-348 (introduced the trip-wire), WI-394 (template)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-10T03:51:30Z"))
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-671", name: "scala")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-637", description: some(value: "scaland mirror of WI-633: nonlinear rule-head match completeness. scaland's discrim-leaf resolveLeaf (core/.../discrim/PersistSubst.scala SmallSubst.resolveLeaf + SharedSubst) binds via Substitution.bind(vid, tid), whose rebind check is TermId-raw equality (structural identity, no unification) — so a repeated head var over doubly-concrete occurrences false-fails exactly as rust did pre-WI-633: query unbox0(box(v: some(?x)), some(42)) against head unbox0(box(v: ?v), ?v) drops the candidate (silent 0 solutions) instead of binding ?x=42. MIRROR the rust fix (249f9fd0): make the SLD-resolution leaf path UNIFY on rebind (occurs-checked structural unification, the &self no-reduce sibling of scaland's unify) while KEEPING one-directional matching (matchTerm/matchView) on structural identity — the LOAD-BEARING split rust encodes via a unify_rebind bool threaded through resolve_leaf (query_resolved_value=true SLD, query_resolved=false match). NB scaland has NO typer (project_scaland_no_typer), so the rust simp_rewrite::try_fire / apply_eq_rules consumers do NOT exist here — only scaland's own SLD head-selection needs unify, and its match consumers stay structural; there is no eq-rules/simp guard to port. Sibling of WI-626 (scaland mirror of WI-624). Port the rust regression tests: nonlinear_head_doubly_concrete_unifies + a matchTerm-nonlinear-is-matching-not-unification analogue. ACCEPTANCE: sbt test green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-10T04:17:13Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-671", description: some(value: "scaland: nested query variables are never bound. VarPath models only depth-1 arg paths (Root | Arg(single ArgPos)) and the discrim query-side descends into a nested Term.Fn with bindPaths=false (SubstTree.queryArgValue), so a query var BELOW the top argument level is captured with no binding. E.g. query p(g(?x)) against fact p(g(42)) succeeds with ?x UNBOUND (silent wrong binding); a rule-body goal q(f(?x)) against q(f(5)) likewise. Head-side nested vars (DeBruijn, captured whole as BindValue.TermVal then opened by withFreshVars) are fine — only nested QUERY/goal vars are affected. Pre-existing (untouched by WI-637, which only swapped the Term.Var accessor in those functions); uncovered by the suite; found during WI-637 /code-review. FIX: extend VarPath to a nested path (sequence of ArgPos) + extractAtPath descent, and thread bindPaths through nested Fn descent on the query side (rustland reference: nested VarPath + extract_value_at_path, WI-373 gap 3). ACCEPTANCE: sbt test green + regression test (query p(g(?x)) vs fact p(g(42)) binds ?x=42)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-10T07:37:50Z"))
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-673", name: "carrier-agnostic")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-673", description: some(value: "PERF / carrier-agnostic bucket-1 (WI-661 child, sibling of WI-656/659/660): SortInfo reflect facts are scanned unindexed via kb.rules_by_functor(SortInfo) (fresh Vec<RuleId> per call) at 4 PER-QUERY keyed lookup sites, each of which finds the ONE SortInfo fact whose name field matches a given sort and reads its constructors/operations. Worst case is find_sort_info (typing.rs:24403), called once PER SORT in the type_check_sorts_typed loop -> O(sorts x SortInfo facts) = O(sorts^2). The 4 keyed sites: operations_of_sort (op_requirements.rs:184, name via raw ==), collect_sort_operations (load.rs:4891, name Ref-only via raw ==), sort_operation_names (typing.rs:22786, name via same_symbol), find_sort_info (typing.rs:24403, name via same_symbol; the quadratic per-sort caller). FIX: a build-once SortInfoIndex { by_name: HashMap<String, Vec<RuleId>> } keyed on the SHORT-NAME (last segment) of each fact's name field, mirroring ProvidesIndex.by_carrier. CANONICALIZATION SPLIT is load-bearing: 2 sites compare name via raw == (exact), 2 via same_symbol (bridges bare<->qualified) -- since (a==b) => same_symbol(a,b) => same last segment, the short-name bucket is a valid SUPERSET for BOTH, and each consumer KEEPS ITS OWN EXACT re-filter (raw == or same_symbol) over the candidates (per-site re-filter as in WI-660). Add a sort_info_rids_by_name(kb, sym) fast-path-or-scan helper (Some(ix) -> bucket by short-name of query; None -> live rules_by_functor scan) so the pre-typecheck load-time callers fall back correctly. LEAVE the 4 enumerate-all builders as direct scans (no per-query key -> a keyed index does not serve them; same call WI-660 made for its 4 coherence passes; they belong to the separate rules_by_functor impl-Iterator-variant tail): build_base_substitutions (load.rs:3426), register_induction_axiom_witnesses (load.rs:3944), sorts_and_own_ops (load.rs:4420), sorts_with_constructors (load.rs:4465). LIFECYCLE (simpler than provides): SortInfo is frozen at the end of the file-loading loop (sole producer emit_sort_info/assert_metadata_fact; never retracted/re-asserted by typer/eq_derive/eval), so reset kb.sort_info_index=None at load_phase_inner start (load.rs:3164, next to provides_index reset; covers incremental loads) and build_sort_info_index(kb) at type_check_sorts_typed start (typing.rs:24245, next to build_provides_index). NO eq_derive null/rebuild pair (eq_derive never touches SortInfo) and NO 'constant' runtime guard needed (not read at runtime; and never mutates post-load so a persisted index cannot go stale anyway). Acceptance: full suite green; behaviour identical (canonicalization parity per-site verified); /code-review high. DEFER the SortRequiresInfo scan (only 1 keyed site direct_requires, already memoized in requires_tree_cache -> marginal; the other 2 sites are an enumerate-all mutator + witness pass) to a later bucket-1 child. Files: kb/{typing,mod,load}.rs."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-10T04:39:10Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-673", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED 2026-07-10. SortInfoIndex { by_name: HashMap<String,Vec<RuleId>> } keyed on the SHORT-NAME of each SortInfo fact's name field (mod.rs field + typing.rs struct/build_sort_info_index/sort_info_rids_by_name helper). 4 keyed consumers migrated to sort_info_rids_by_name (fast-path bucket else live-scan): operations_of_sort, collect_sort_operations, sort_operation_names, find_sort_info (the O(sorts^2) per-sort caller). The 4 enumerate-all builders LEFT as direct scans (WI-660 precedent; belong to the iterator-variant tail). LIFECYCLE: reset=None at load_phase_inner start; build at type_check_sorts_typed start (beside build_provides_index). SortInfo is frozen after the file-loading loop (sole producer emit_sort_info always emits a Term::Ref name; nothing re-asserts), so NO eq_derive null/rebuild pair and NO 'constant' runtime guard needed (not read at runtime, cannot go stale). CANONICALIZATION: short-name bucket is a sound SUPERSET for BOTH the 2 raw-== consumers and the 2 same_symbol consumers (a==b => same_symbol => equal last segment); each consumer KEEPS its own exact re-filter unchanged. /code-review high (8 angles/4 finders + producer check): 0 correctness/removed-behavior/cross-file/lifecycle findings; ONE cleanup applied -- the builder's name-extract helper had a dead Term::Ident arm (no consumer accepts Ident; emit_sort_info only emits Ref) and an over-claiming doc, so inlined a minimal {Ref,Fn} match and deleted the single-use helper (declined reusing sort_ref_functor: it is SortProvidesInfo.sort_ref-specific, coupling would be worse). Full suite 69 bins/2686 passed/0 fail (green before AND after the cleanup). PERF: architectural O(sorts^2)->O(sorts) for find_sort_info; A/B at 115 sorts (github-todo load) within run-to-run noise (~100-200ms type_check_sorts either way) -- the win scales with sort count, sub-noise at this size, which is why acceptance was correctness/parity not a perf number. Files: kb/{typing,mod,load,op_requirements}.rs. Remaining WI-661 bucket-1: SortRequiresInfo scan (DEFERRED -- 1 keyed site direct_requires already memoized, marginal) + rules_by_functor impl-Iterator variant for the surviving enumerators.", at: "2026-07-10T04:39:12Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-661", author: "user", content: "PROGRESS 2026-07-10: bucket-1 SortInfo index DELIVERED as WI-673 (SortInfoIndex, short-name bucket + per-site re-filter, mirrors WI-660 ProvidesIndex.by_carrier). Migrated the 4 per-query keyed SortInfo lookups (find_sort_info the O(sorts^2) one, operations_of_sort, collect_sort_operations, sort_operation_names); LEFT the 4 enumerate-all SortInfo builders (build_base_substitutions, register_induction_axiom_witnesses, sorts_and_own_ops, sorts_with_constructors) as direct scans -- they need ALL facts, belong to the iterator-variant tail. Simpler lifecycle than provides: SortInfo frozen after the file-load loop (emit_sort_info only producer, emits Term::Ref name), so reset-at-load-phase-start + build-at-type_check-start, NO eq_derive rebuild pair, NO constant guard. BUCKET STATUS NOW: bucket-1 DONE = WI-656 (op_records), WI-659 (SortAlias), WI-660 (SortProvides), WI-673 (SortInfo). bucket-1 REMAINING = (a) SortRequiresInfo scan -- DEFERRED as low-value: only 1 keyed site (direct_requires, already memoized in requires_tree_cache), the other 2 sites are an enumerate-all mutator (resolve_requires_bindings) + witness pass; NOTE SortRequiresInfo is MUTATED during load (resolve_requires_bindings retract+reassert) so any index MUST build strictly after resolve_instantiations (type_check start satisfies it). (b) the rules_by_functor per-call fresh-Vec<RuleId> alloc -> impl-Iterator variant for the ~surviving legitimate enumerators (using-X fan-out, reflection, the index builders, the enumerate-all passes). bucket-2 (WI-662 RequiresEntry.spec, WI-663 rule_head readers) fully DONE. bucket-3 LEAVE AS-IS.", at: "2026-07-10T04:39:31Z")
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-672", name: "carrier-agnostic")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-672", author: "user", content: "CORE (user 2026-07-10, emphatic): comparison by SHORT-NAME (last qualified-name segment) must be ELIMINATED from the KB -- the index keys AND the same_symbol BRIDGE #2 they rest on. (The auto-added description above lost a few literal words to shell escaping -- the substance is: same_symbol bridge #2 = a bare short name matching the last segment of a qualified name; it exists only because namespace-less top-level decls get bare _global QNs that collide with qualified stdlib QNs by last segment. Fix at the PRODUCER so no bare colliding QNs exist, then delete bridge #2 and key SortInfoIndex/ProvidesIndex by canonical_sort_sym.) Ticket count does not matter; the invariant does: no short-name/last-segment matching. See memory no-short-name-comparison. Marked in-code at SortInfoIndex struct doc (typing.rs).", at: "2026-07-10T09:21:35Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-294", description: some(value: "Scala loader/typer: port operation-body loading + type-hint consumption (WI-288 follow-up). Builds the Scala-side operation loading that WI-288's parser hints feed into; gated behind the scaland Term->Value resolver migration (mirror of rustland WI-246: occurrence-native SLD, Value::Node, Rc<NodeOccurrence>) so it lands on the right substrate. Currently in scaland: convertExprTerm + its load* helpers (loadLetExpr, loadApplyOrConstructor, etc.) are DEAD CODE — no caller; operations are only defined as symbols in scan pass 1 (Loader.scala:125-134), never loaded into the KB as OperationInfo facts; and there is NO native-Scala typer (the 'dual typer' = Rust typer + the self-hosted typer in stdlib/anthill/reflect/typing.anthill run via SLD). Scope: (1) Phase-2 operation handler in Loader.loadItems that builds anthill.reflect.OperationInfo facts (name/params/return_type/effects/requires/ensures/body/type_params), mirroring rustland kb/load.rs:5569-5708; (2) wire convertExprTerm as the operation-body converter; (3) item 1 of WI-288 — loadLetExpr: thread the 'type_name' named-arg into Expr.let_expr.type_name (Some/None); (4) item 2 of WI-288 — loadApplyOrConstructor: strip the 'type_args' named-arg and route as Expr.apply/ho_apply.type_args (cons-list of reflect type_arg(name,value)); constructor has no type_args (drop); (5) item 4 of WI-288 — register operation type-params as scoped Sort symbols in an operation scope (kb.symbols.define + addTypeParam), mirror rustland load.rs:357-362, and allocate per-param Vars published in OperationInfo.type_params; (6) typer-side seeding of call-site type_args onto op type-param Vars (rustland typing.rs reseed_type_params_from_call_args) — only meaningful once the typing.anthill rules run over loaded OperationInfo in scaland. Reference WI-288 (delivered: item 3, typeExprToRef arrow/tuple). Rust is ground truth. Acceptance: sbt test green; new LoaderTest cases asserting OperationInfo fact shape (incl. type_params) and let_expr.type_name / apply.type_args round-trip; parity with rustland load_operation on a small fixture."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-288", "WI-674"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-674", description: some(value: "scaland carrier-neutral term substrate (non-hash-consed Value + TermView) — the 'Term->Value resolver migration' that WI-294 is gated behind. Mirrors rustland WI-246 / kb/term_view.rs + eval/value.rs. Motivation: scaland is uniformly hash-consed (Term/TermId only); the whole resolution path assumes 'TermId.raw identity == structural equality', which hash-consing makes true and a non-interned Value makes FALSE. Phasing: STEP 1 (DELIVERED, uncommitted 2026-07-10) = additive substrate, no resolver rewiring: anthill.term.Value (scalars, Tuple, Entity, Term(TermId) bridge, Var), anthill.kb.TermView typeclass (given instances for TermId + Value; children are plain Value, NOT a ported ViewItem — rust's ViewItem is a borrow-checker artifact with no Scala counterpart), WI-436 nullary-constructor->Ref canonicalization (functorViewHead via kb.isConstructorSymbol), and viewsStructurallyEqual (the single cross-carrier structural comparator, vars by full Var identity). +18 TermViewTest cases, suite green (260). STEP 2 (this WI's remaining work) = carrier-neutralize the core: Substitution.bindings VarId->TermId becomes VarId->Value (or a BindValue that carries Value); unifyMatch/withFreshVars/discrim SubstTree walkers + kb.query consume TermView; SWEEP every 'TermId.raw ==' structural-equality fast path (Substitution.scala, KnowledgeBase.scala, resolve/Builtins.scala, discrim) and replace with viewsStructurallyEqual or an explicit interned-only guard — this is the risky part; existing suite (facts/rules stay interned) is the regression net. Proof of done: a Value.Entity goal resolves against interned facts identically to its TermId twin. NB the Value.Node occurrence carrier + ViewHead.Opaque are NOT in scope here — they land with WI-294 (operation bodies). Non-obvious gotchas captured in memory project_scaland_carrier_substrate: 'case _ =>' in a Scala match is a SILENT SKIP not a compile error (enumerate every Value case in child accessors, verified via compile probe -> E029); named-arg canonical order is unenforced on BOTH scaland sides (rust canonicalizes to declared-field order in canonicalize_record_named_args) — enforce at the first Value producer; duplicate named keys give a false-positive equality (fence at the producer); functorViewHead reads mutable KB constructor registry so Fn{c}==Ref(c) flips once c is registered (load-order sensitive, port-faithful). Acceptance: sbt test green; new tests pinning a non-interned Value goal resolving identically to its interned twin; the TermId.raw-identity sweep is complete (grep clean on the resolution path)."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-674", name: "scaland-substrate")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-675", description: some(value: "scaland discrim tree has no DiscrimKey.RigidVar — a Rigid skolem over-matches and distinct skolems conflate. Rustland's DiscrimKey has a RigidVar(VarId) CONSTANT edge: a Rigid var keys as itself and matches only the same skolem, while flex Global / bound DeBruijn key as a wildcard var-edge. Scaland's DiscrimKey (scaland/core/src/main/scala/anthill/discrim/SubstTree.scala) has NO RigidVar case; Var.varId (term/Term.scala) collapses Global/DeBruijn/Rigid to one VarId and insertPatternWalk/queryNode add EVERY var kind as a wildcard var-edge on v.varId. Consequences once a Rigid reaches the tree: (a) a rigid GOAL var over-matches a concrete fact (should match only the same rigid); (b) two distinct skolems sharing the wildcard keyspace conflate. This also means the new viewsStructurallyEqual (WI-674) and the discrim tree DISAGREE about rigids — the comparator compares vars by full Var identity (rigid = constant), the tree treats rigid = wildcard; only the comparator is currently sound. UNREACHABLE TODAY: nothing in scaland mints a Var.Rigid (WI-637 added the kind as parity scaffolding only — no typer/proof-discharge). This WI must land BEFORE anything mints a Rigid. Scope: add DiscrimKey.RigidVar(vid: VarId); in the insert/query walkers, branch on Var kind (Rigid -> concrete RigidVar edge keyed by vid; Global/DeBruijn -> wildcard var-edge as today); mirror rustland kb/discrim.rs insert_walk/query_node RigidVar handling. Acceptance: sbt test green; new SubstTree tests pinning (1) a Rigid pattern var matches only the same-VarId Rigid, not a concrete fact and not a different-VarId Rigid; (2) a Global/DeBruijn var still matches anything; (3) discrim result agrees with viewsStructurallyEqual on a rigid-containing pair. Reference rustland kb/discrim.rs (DiscrimKey::RigidVar) and WI-637 (the Var kinds). Found during WI-674 step-1 code review."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-675", name: "scaland-substrate")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-674", author: "user", content: "STEP 1 delivered (uncommitted 2026-07-10): anthill.term.Value + anthill.kb.TermView + viewsStructurallyEqual + TermViewTest (18 cases). Suite green 260 (baseline 242). /code-review high (8 finder angles, fresh general-purpose agents) run: fixed 4 self-introduced doc/shape defects (RigidVar over-claim -> filed WI-675; case _ silent-skip -> enumerated Value cases, verified via compile probe -> E029; ViewItem removed as a rust borrow artifact; named-arg canonical-order doc corrected to assumed-not-enforced). Remaining = STEP 2 (carrier-neutralize Substitution/discrim/query + TermId.raw-identity sweep).", at: "2026-07-10T16:31:49Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-672", description: some(value: "GOAL (design direction, user 2026-07-10 'eventually we should have no short-name as key'): eliminate SHORT-NAME index keys and key the reflect-fact indexes by CANONICAL IDENTITY instead. Two live short-name keys: SortInfoIndex.by_name (WI-673) and ProvidesIndex.by_carrier (WI-660). Both are forced by same_symbol's BRIDGE #2 (a bare short name matches the last segment of a qualified name), which canonical_sort_sym cannot reproduce (its doc caveat: a bare/short symbol has no by_qualified_name entry -> falls to identity; divergences must be fixed at the PRODUCER). ROOT CAUSE (traced this session via probes): namespace-less TOP-LEVEL declarations land in the _global scope with a BARE qualified name (QN == short name, no dot) -- loader current_scope defaults to _global (load.rs:1214/995) and only switches on a  decl (load.rs:1254). When such a bare _global sort coincides by short name with a qualified stdlib sort (e.g. a testcase's top-level / in anthill-testcases/ring-polynom/*.anthill loaded ALONGSIDE the stdlib's anthill.prelude.List / anthill.prelude.algebra.Ring), same_symbol bridge #2 CONFLATES the two DISTINCT sorts, and find_sort_info returns the prelude sort's ctors/ops for the testcase's sort. PROBE EVIDENCE: find_sort_info received bare List/Option/Pair/Ring, all kind=RESOLVED scope=_global (NOT unresolved -- the NotFound->intern-bare fallback at load.rs:6372 fired 0 times), matching qualified anthill.prelude.* via bridge #2; the full-stdlib CLI load (all sorts qualified) was clean. So it is NOT an unresolved-name or type-info-deferral issue -- it is bare _global QNs colliding with qualified stdlib QNs. FIX OPTIONS (STOP-AND-ASK -- touches loader name-resolution / language scoping): (a) AUTO-QUALIFY _global/top-level declarations so their QN carries a dot (e.g.  or file/module-rooted) -> same_symbol bridge #2 (which requires one side to be truly bare, no dot) can no longer fire -> DROP bridge #2 and key SortInfo/by_carrier by canonical_sort_sym like ProvidesIndex.by_spec_base; (b) mandate namespaces (breaking, biggest); (c) narrowest stopgap: give the colliding testcases a  (one line each) so their sorts stop colliding -- but that does not remove the general mechanism. CAVEAT: _global is not only user-convenience -- it also anchors kernel-vocab/reflect resolution (resolve_name_in_global, the prelude_qualified lowest-precedence fallback), so the lever is 'stop top-level user decls producing bare QNs that collide with stdlib', NOT 'delete _global'. Acceptance: same_symbol bridge #2 provably never fires (or is removed); SortInfoIndex + ProvidesIndex re-keyed by canonical_sort_sym with a DEBUG cross-check (bucket must contain every same_symbol-matching fact, so a future bare query is a LOUD test failure not a silent empty result); full suite green; behaviour verified unchanged (or the de-conflation is intentional + tests updated). WI-661 bucket-1 tail; the concrete form of the umbrella's 'canonicalize query symbols at producers' lever."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-11T03:31:35Z"))
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-676", name: "carrier-agnostic")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-676", description: some(value: "CARRIER-AGNOSTIC bucket-1 index consolidation (WI-661 child; a WI-672 /code-review deferral). The per-fact reflect indexes ProvidesIndex (WI-660) and SortInfoIndex (WI-673) are the 3rd/4th hand-rolled copy of the same Symbol->Vec<RuleId> build/lookup/fallback pattern. Introduce ONE shared SymbolKeyedFactIndex (a HashMap<Symbol,Vec<RuleId>> bucket map; canonical-key get(); a rules_or_scan fallback helper that fast-paths the built index else does the live rules_by_functor scan). ProvidesIndex becomes two of them (by_spec_base + by_carrier); SortInfoIndex collapses to a bare SymbolKeyedFactIndex. Folds the 3 near-duplicate accessors (providers_of_spec/carrier_candidates/candidates) and the 3 X_rids_by_Y wrapper helpers into the shared struct. WI-656 op_records (rich per-op record) and WI-659 SortAliasIndex (TermId targets, string/parent keys) deliberately NOT folded in -- neither is a Symbol->Vec<RuleId> bucket. No behavior change; identity stays canonical (WI-672, spec 8.6). Acceptance: full cargo-test green + /code-review."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-11T05:19:03Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-661", author: "user", content: "PROGRESS 2026-07-11: bucket-1 index CONSOLIDATION delivered as WI-676. The 3rd/4th hand-rolled per-fact index copies (ProvidesIndex WI-660, SortInfoIndex WI-673) now share ONE SymbolKeyedFactIndex (HashMap<Symbol,Vec<RuleId>> bucket; canonical-key get(); rids_or_scan fast-path-or-scan helper). ProvidesIndex = two of them (by_spec_base + by_carrier); SortInfoIndex struct DELETED (sort_info_index is now Option<SymbolKeyedFactIndex>). Folded the 3 near-dup accessors + 3 X_rids_by_Y wrappers into the shared struct. op_records + SortAliasIndex deliberately NOT folded (not Symbol->Vec<RuleId> buckets). Pure behavior-preserving refactor, full suite 2686/0; /code-review high (2 agents, correctness+removed-behavior+cross-file+cleanup/altitude/conventions): 0 correctness/dangle findings, 1 visibility nit (get pub(crate)->private) APPLIED. UMBRELLA STATUS: buckets 2 (WI-662/663) + 3 (leave-as-is) DONE; bucket-1 index migrations + consolidation DONE (WI-656/659/660/673 + WI-676). Bucket-1 REMAINING is only low-value polish: (a) SortRequiresInfo scan [deferred: 1 keyed site, already memoized]; (b) rules_by_functor per-call Vec->impl-Iterator tail [low-value: the benefiting alloc sites are annotated cold/rare by their authors, perf sub-noise per WI-673]. Substantive carrier-agnostic work is COMPLETE; what remains is optional cleanup.", at: "2026-07-11T05:19:26Z")
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-677", name: "carrier-agnostic")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-677", description: some(value: "CARRIER-AGNOSTIC bucket-1 (b) tail (WI-661 child): retire the rules_by_functor per-call fresh-Vec<RuleId> alloc at the READ-ONLY reduce-immediately sites. Add a borrowing rules_by_functor_iter(&self, sym) -> impl Iterator<Item=RuleId> (SAME non-retracted filter + stored order as the owned rules_by_functor) and migrate the 9 sites that allocated a whole Vec just to reduce it: is_empty (load.rs bridge-idempotency guard, resolve.rs bare_bodied_bool_relation, typing.rs check_goal_atom_op), any (typing.rs op_backed_one x2 + matches_variance_fact, resolve.rs unfold_eq_operand), first (mod.rs rule_id_by_qn), find (eval/builtins.rs reflect_find_fact). These gain early-exit (short-circuit) AND drop the alloc. SCOPE = reduce-sites ONLY: the kb-MUTATING for-loops (shared-borrow would conflict) and the enumerate-all for-loops (iterate every rid, no early-exit -- only alloc saved) are LEFT as owned rules_by_functor -- documented low-value churn. The umbrella first framed (b) as the FULL ~56-site sweep; this delivers only the genuine-win subset (the benefiting allocs are annotated cold/rare by their authors, perf sub-noise per WI-673). Pure behavior-preserving. Acceptance: full cargo-test green + /code-review."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-11T05:47:55Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-661", author: "user", content: "PROGRESS 2026-07-11 (2): bucket-1 (b) reduce-sites delivered as WI-677. Added borrowing rules_by_functor_iter (same non-retracted filter + order as owned); migrated the 9 read-only sites that allocated a Vec just to reduce it (is_empty x3, any x4, first x1, find x1) -> early-exit + no alloc. Pure behavior-preserving; suite 2686/0; /code-review high (2 agents) 0 findings (incl. a proven laziness-safety check: no interior mutability on the filter inputs). SCOPE was the genuine-win reduce-sites ONLY -- the remaining (b) work (kb-mutating for-loops = borrow-conflict; enumerate-all for-loops = no early-exit, only alloc) is LEFT as documented low-value churn. UMBRELLA STATE: substantive carrier-agnostic work COMPLETE (buckets 2/3, bucket-1 index migrations + consolidation WI-676, bucket-1 (b) reduce-sites WI-677). Only genuinely-remaining items = (a) SortRequiresInfo typed index [deferred: 1 keyed site already memoized] + (b) full for-loop sweep [low-value churn, explicitly out of scope]. WI-661 can be CLOSED whenever the user wants -- nothing high-value left.", at: "2026-07-11T05:48:19Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-661", author: "user", content: "CLOSING 2026-07-11 (substantively complete). All buckets landed: bucket 2 = WI-662 (RequiresEntry.spec TermId->Value) + WI-663 (carrier-agnostic head readers); bucket 3 (TermId-as-key on nominal sort identities) = leave-as-is by design; bucket 1 = index migrations WI-656/659/660/673 + consolidation WI-676 (SymbolKeyedFactIndex) + WI-677 (rules_by_functor_iter reduce-sites). The rule_head panic the umbrella first wanted lowered to LoadError was instead resolved BETTER by WI-663 (keep-panic-where-a-skip-would-be-unsound + migrate readers). GOAL ACHIEVED: hash-consing is now an optimization, not a hard requirement, for the heads/storage the umbrella targeted; the core resolver was already discrim-tree/view-driven (carrier-agnostic). DEFERRED as LOW-VALUE (NOT tracked here -- re-file a fresh ticket if ever wanted): (a) a SortRequiresInfo keyed-lookup INDEX -- SortRequiresInfo is a RELATION scanned by direct_requires, whose 1 per-query site is already memoized in requires_tree_cache; an index would also have to build after resolve_instantiations since resolve_requires_bindings mutates the relation; (b) the FULL rules_by_functor->impl-Iterator sweep over the kb-MUTATING for-loops (shared borrow conflicts) + enumerate-all for-loops (no early-exit) -- only the Vec alloc would be saved, which authors annotated cold/rare and WI-673 measured sub-noise. Closing as Delivered.", at: "2026-07-11T06:07:40Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-661", description: some(value: "UMBRELLA / TRACKING (from a 2026-07-08 rules_by_functor <-> carrier-agnostic-heads design session, following the WI-656/659/655 index perf work). GOAL: retire the remaining term/TermId assumptions that keep the KB from FULLY carrier-agnostic (Value::Node/Entity) rule heads + stored terms, so hash-consing stays a pure OPTIMIZATION (CLAUDE.md Representation note) not a hard requirement. CRUX findings that make this tractable: (1) the CORE resolver (resolve.rs) does NOT use rules_by_functor -- goal-matching is discrimination-tree-based (gamma_candidates_for), already carrier-agnostic (view-driven discrim insert_pattern handles Node value heads, WI-348). So rules_by_functor is purely AUXILIARY. (2) Both rule indexes are ALREADY carrier-agnostic at INSERT: rules_by_functor keys on Symbol via TermView::head().functor_sym() (mod.rs:1076), discrim via the view-driven insert. (3) The rule_head term-only-reader PANIC ('a value fact reached a term-only head reader', a WI-348 trip-wire) is NOT an architectural barrier -- it can be lowered to a graceful LoadError (loud-but-not-fatal, per the repo 'loud error over silent skip' principle), making a value head in an un-migrated reader a MIGRATION DIAGNOSTIC, not a crash. THREE BUCKETS: (1) by-subfield SCANS over rules_by_functor -> replace with typed by-key indexes. DONE: WI-656 (op_records / OperationInfo), WI-659 (SortAliasIndex). OPEN: WI-660 (SortProvidesInfo); TODO: SortInfo, RequiresInfo. Removes most term-only rule_head/fact_head_term reads from hot paths. Tail: rules_by_functor's per-call fresh-Vec<RuleId> alloc (~50 sites) -> an impl-Iterator variant for the LEGITIMATE enumerators that survive (rule_ids_by_qn for `using X` fan-out, reflection, the index builders). (2) term-only STORAGE holdouts (a carrier-bearing thing pinned to a hash-consed TermId): rule_head reads -> migrate to rule_head_value/TermView, panic -> LoadError (child WI); and RequiresEntry.spec:TermId -> Value (child WI) -- sort-level requires, still term-only although op-level OpInfoRecord.requires already = Vec<Value> via WI-366 B2 (the asymmetry). Templates: WI-394 (remove term-only walk_type -> carrier-agnostic walk_view), WI-366 B2, WI-482. (3) TermId-as-KEY on NOMINAL sort identities (by_sort/by_domain/sort_info/entity_parent = HashMap<TermId,...>) -> LEAVE AS-IS: legitimate hash-consing per the CLAUDE.md note (nominal sort identities); its one fragility is canonicalization (the WI-581 same-FQN-copy assert). NOT a single deliverable -- a DIRECTION; land the concrete children incrementally, each green + /code-review, no big-bang. Children: RequiresEntry.spec->Value; rule_head->LoadError+TermView reader migration; (bucket-1) WI-660 + SortInfo/RequiresInfo indexes + rules_by_functor iterator variant."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-11T06:07:42Z"))
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-678", name: "carrier-agnostic")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-057", description: some(value: "Value::Entity.named field: change Vec<(Symbol, Value)> to SmallVec<[(Symbol, Value); 2]>. cons cells have exactly 2 named args (head, tail); entity constructors typically fit in 2-4. Saves a heap alloc per Entity value — significant on list-heavy workloads. Cross-cutting: eval.rs build_list_value + reduce_constructor + pattern.rs unify_constructor all allocate Vec today. Same rationale applies to Value::Tuple.named and Value::Entity.pos. Benchmark-driven; WI-053 companion."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-053"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Stale(reason: "Premise obsolete: Value::Entity/Tuple pos/named are no longer Vec — commit 29890ed0 migrated them to Rc<[...]> for O(1) spine clone, already banking the list-heavy win WI-057 targeted (list 1.85s->0.18s). SmallVec would REGRESS that clone (deep-copies the spine); the Rc box is the single per-entity alloc and cannot be dropped without losing sharing. Separate, speculative idea remains — shrink sizeof(Value) 48->32 via thin Entity/Tuple pointers (Rc<Vec>/Rc<SmallVec>) — refile if benchmarked worthwhile.", since: "2026-07-11T06:43:34Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-057", author: "user", content: "Benchmark run 2026-07-11 (release, anthill-todo bundle: 670 items, list/graph/status). Method: wall-clock too noisy (~40% median-vs-min spread on WSL2) so used peak RSS (stable) + a size-sensitivity probe (never-constructed +88B Value variant) instead of the full 18-file thin-pointer refactor. Result: DOUBLING sizeof(Value) 48->96 raised RSS only +2.2MB (+3%, consistent across all 3 commands) with ZERO measurable wall-clock effect. Extrapolated to the real 48->32 shrink: ~-0.7MB RSS (~1%), unmeasurable in time. Value structs are only ~3% of RSS; the KB (hash-consed Terms + indices + parse IR) dominates. And that ~1% is the GROSS best case: thin-pointer encodings (Rc<Vec> double-alloc, or Rc<SmallVec> 112B inline buffers baked into every entity box incl. empty/all-positional ones) would likely erase or invert it. VERDICT: not worth a refile. Confirms Stale.", at: "2026-07-11T06:56:17Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-678", description: some(value: "CARRIER-AGNOSTIC cleanup (standalone; surfaced 2026-07-11 auditing occurrence_to_term after WI-661). The multi-goal conjunction-lowering path does an avoidable occurrence->TermId->occurrence ROUND-TRIP. In execute.rs, coerce_to_single_goal_value (WI-513) collapses a goal list for a not/or wrapper: the single-goal case passes the Value through carrier-neutrally, but the multi-goal case reifies every goal Value to a TermId (goal_value_to_term -> occurrence_to_term), then synthesize_conjunction_rule mints _synth_N(?free_vars) :- body and converts the body TermIds STRAIGHT BACK to occurrences via term_body_to_nodes for assert_rule_debruijn_with_nodes. The goals START as Values (Node ones already occurrences) and END as occurrences (the sole stored rule-body form, WI-246) -- the TermId is a transient detour, NOT exploited for hash-consing (the WI-169 dedup memo keys on a storage-neutral Vec<SynthKey> structural fingerprint, deliberately never TermId identity). The assertion substrate is ALREADY carrier-agnostic: assert_rule_debruijn_with_nodes takes head: impl Into<Value> (WI-373, accepts a Value::Node/Entity head) + an occurrence body, and collects free vars natively (collect_value_head_vars + collect_occurrence_global_vars_ordered). FIX = drop the reify: (1) free-var frame -> replace collect_vars(TermId) with the existing occurrence-native collect_occurrence_global_vars_ordered over the goal occurrences; (2) head -> build _synth_N(?vars) as a Value (Entity/Node), not Term::Fn (assert already accepts impl Into<Value>); (3) THE ACTUAL WORK -> a TermView-native variant of append_synth_key (execute.rs:414): today it walks self.terms.get(TermId); since NodeOccurrence IS a TermView (head symbol / pos+named children / var leaves), the SAME SynthKey stream can be built from the view. Then occurrence_to_term (goal_value_to_term) AND term_body_to_nodes drop out of this path. CONSTRAINT: WI-169 relies on a WALK-PARITY invariant -- collect_vars and append_synth_key must visit vars in the same first-occurrence order so the De Bruijn-positional SynthKey keys line up (debug_asserted); the occurrence-native walks MUST preserve that order and the memo key MUST stay byte-identical so dedup is unchanged. SCOPE: only the multi-goal branch of coerce_to_single_goal_value / synthesize_conjunction_rule. LOW-VALUE/narrow (reify is cold -- bounded by the WI-169 memo to once per DISTINCT multi-goal body), but a genuine, sound term-only holdout in the WI-661 carrier-agnostic family. Acceptance: full cargo-test green + /code-review; behaviour identical (esp. WI-169 memo still dedups a repeated multi-goal not/or body to one _synth_N)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-11T07:59:19Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-678", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED (commit 6cfda300). synthesize_conjunction_rule takes Vec<Value> & returns Value; free vars + memo key via TermView; occurrence goals used DIRECTLY as body nodes (spans/types preserved); occurrence_to_term+term_body_to_nodes dropped from this path. Head stays Term::Fn->Value::Term (synthetic, byte-identical frame). /code-review (4 agents) CAUGHT A REAL REGRESSION in the first draft: free vars via collect_value_head_vars (occurrence-native, DESCENDS type_args) but memo key via TermView (no type_args) = two DIFFERENT traversals; a Global living only in a goal's [T=?v] type position inflated synth head arity yet was invisible to the key -> two bodies differing only in a type-arg var collided onto one _synth_N with mismatched arity -> returned head can't unify -> SILENT WRONG ANSWERS (walk-parity debug_assert can't catch it: key sees a SUBSET of free_vars). FIX = collect free vars via collect_goal_view_vars, the TermView TWIN of append_synth_key: both share ONE traversal so parity holds BY CONSTRUCTION for every carrier + hidden field; a type-position var is closed as a sound body-local De Bruijn var off the stored occurrence (byte-identical to old term path which reified-away type positions). LESSON: when two walks must agree (free-var frame vs positional key encoding), build them from ONE shared traversal, not two collectors that happen to line up. Regression test synth_head_ignores_type_position_only_var. VIEW_ARITY_DESYNC debug_assert added on head-arity vs accessor desync (invariant, verified all carriers). Full suite 69/0.", at: "2026-07-11T07:59:37Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-669", author: "claude", content: "Increment 1a DELIVERED (engine; full workspace suite green 0-failed, /code-review high). The body-derived defining-equation EXTRACTOR + public API; the SMT seam (1b) + demonstrator remain.\n\nENGINE (kb/body_specialize.rs): defining_equations reduces the body one step (reuses bind_params + reduce), flatten_arms splits it into guarded arms — residual Expr::If -> two arms (then cond / else not-cond, recursing); residual Expr::Match -> loud None (ADT defining equations need SMT datatype support -> WI-679); reduce already declines let/higher-order. Public KnowledgeBase::op_defining_equations(op) over params as Var::DeBruijn.\n\nKEY DESIGN (settled via user's /code-review question 'occurrence_to_term is partial -> strange'): equations are CARRIER-NEUTRAL OCCURRENCES, not TermId. DefiningEquation { guards: Vec<DefiningGuard>, result: Rc<NodeOccurrence> }. A defining equation is transient on-demand-derived structure (Representation note: do NOT intern), and smt-gen already consumes rule bodies as occurrences (WI-246); TermId re-introduced the occurrence->term boundary WI-246/668 removed AND forced the PARTIAL occurrence_to_term (asserts/reifies-bottom on control-flow — intrinsic to goal position, not incompleteness). /code-review (both finders) caught the earlier TermId draft's nested-control-flow -> occ_build_fn -> occurrence_to_term panic(debug)/silent-bottom(release). Occurrences dropped occurrence_to_term + the defensive is_reifiable guard + one struct pair; partiality now lives at smt-gen's own non-Fn-goal boundary (loud, right layer).\n\nSCOPE: inc-1 demonstrator is an ARITHMETIC/if bodied-op property, NOT append/length (ADT match bodies the SMT tier can't lower without datatype support). Closer to the lf1 consumers (all arithmetic). Match/ADT + let are follow-ons.\n\nTESTS (wi669_defining_equations_test.rs, 3/3): single-arm add(x,x) -> 1 unconditional eq; if gte(x,0) then x else 0 -> 2 arms w/ gte/not-gte guards; match body -> None.\n\nWI-NUMBER NOTE: lf1 consumers filed as WI-681 (GPS desired_position, QF_NRA geometry; RENUMBERED from a local WI-678 that collided with origin/main's authoritative WI-678 round-trip-cleanup) + WI-679 (transponder ranking, LIA; adds Expr::Let to reduce inline). Prerequisite WI-680 (smt-gen ite/if expression-position lowering) filed under WI-669.\n\nREMAINING (WI-669): 1b = SMT seam (driver-side transient-rule synthesis from the equation OCCURRENCES fed as rule body-nodes — no term round-trip; prove.rs synthesize_step_rule precedent) + an arithmetic/if demonstrator that discharges by z3 with no hand-written <=> twin. Committed inc-1a on branch, rebased onto main.", at: "2026-07-11T06:13:58Z")
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-680", name: "proof")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-680", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED (smt-gen conditional lowering; anthill-smt-gen suite 75/0 green). translate_expr now emits (ite cond t e) for BOTH the Expr::If occurrence AND the Bool.ite(cond,then,else) functor; new translate_condition helper lowers a Bool-valued occurrence (map_inequality_op relationals + is_eq_functor eq + new map_bool_connective and/or/not, recursing), loud SmtGenError otherwise. New free helpers is_ite_op + map_bool_connective. Test wi680_ite_lowering_test (4): (ite (>= ..)) appears; and/not connectives lower; z3 unsat for TRUE clamp(x)>=0; z3 SAT (counterexample) for FALSE clamp(x)>0 (guards against vacuous always-unsat).\n\nSCOPE CORRECTION (my filing overclaimed): 'stdlib sign/max/min become dischargeable' is NOT delivered by WI-680 alone. Those are <=> twins stored as EQUATIONS (functor unify/eq with max(?a,?b) as an arg), NOT as function-relations under the op's functor — so a call max(?a,?b,?r) hits 'unhandled body goal functor max' (rules_by_functor(max) has no such rule) and a max(?a,?b) expression hits 'unhandled arithmetic op max' (translate_expr doesn't inline op-calls). Reaching the new ite lowering from a <=> twin needs a SEPARATE <=>-twin->rule lowering path (out of scope; candidate follow-on WI). WI-680's verified reach = any RULE whose body binds a var to an ite/if expression: a hand-written 'rule f(?x,?r) :- ?r = ite(..)' twin, and (the point) the WI-669-synthesized defining rule (head functor = op).", at: "2026-07-11T07:01:00Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-680", author: "claude", content: "/code-review (2 fresh general-purpose finders, xhigh): NO hard correctness/soundness bug. Sort discipline holds — translate_expr yields only Real, translate_condition only Bool; every unsupported shape (bare Bool var, Bool-in-Real slot, nullary ident) is a LOUD SmtGenError, never silent mis-sort. Two findings ADDRESSED: (1) test gap — 'or' had zero coverage, and/not only string-matched; added and/or/not z3-SEMANTICS tests, each a contradiction/tautology whose unsat verdict FLIPS under a miswiring (or<->and, not<->id). (2) latent-but-loud — the free-var scan chained assertions+conclusions but NOT body_smtlib, so a FunctionLike result bound to an ite over an otherwise-unused input (?w = ite(gte(?x,0),?x,0)) emitted an undeclared var_x -> z3 unknown-constant; FIXED by adding body_smtlib to the scan (result var is in local_bindings so never double-declared; free_vars render before body_smtlib) + a regression test (freevar -> sat). Suite now 8 WI-680 tests; anthill-smt-gen 79/0.\n\nNOT fixed (pre-existing / out of scope, all LOUD not silent): (3) render_upper_bound_with never emits free_vars — an ite-over-free-var in OBLIGATION (upper-bound) mode would emit undeclared; WI-680 is wired/tested only through the satisfiability path; candidate follow-on. (4) Expr::Ref/Ident nullary used as a condition/branch emits a bare undeclared identifier (pre-existing translate_expr:917). (5) bare-Bool-var guards + flat n-ary and/or are loud-rejected (by design; the WI-669 refold emits only binary Bool.and/Bool.not). (6) bare short-name match ('ite'/'and'/'or'/'not') can reinterpret an unresolved same-named user op — consistent w/ existing map_arith_op style, and qualified_name_of returns FQN for RESOLVED syms so it only fires on unresolved bare names.", at: "2026-07-11T07:18:15Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-680", description: some(value: "smt-gen: lower conditional (ite/if) + boolean conditions in EXPRESSION position. anthill-smt-gen's translate_expr (anthill-smt-gen/src/lib.rs) handles arithmetic (add/sub/mul/div, abs/neg) and treats inequalities (gte/lte/gt/lt) ONLY as body-GOAL assertions (map_inequality_op in process_body_goal) — it has NO path for a conditional or a boolean-valued subterm in EXPRESSION position. CONFIRMED GAP: a hand-written 'rule f(?x,?r) :- ?r = ite(gte(?x,0), ?x, 0)' fails today with 'v0: unhandled arithmetic op ite'; the same blocks stdlib's own ite twins (sign/max/min: int64.anthill:35, ordered.anthill:49-50). FIX (downstream emitter only, NO extractor dependency): teach translate_expr to emit '(ite <cond> <then> <else>)' for (a) the Expr::If { condition, then_branch, else_branch } occurrence and (b) the anthill.prelude.Bool.ite(cond, then, else) functor application; add a translate_condition helper lowering a boolean-valued occurrence — the relational ops (map_inequality_op: gte/lte/gt/lt) + eq (is_eq_functor) + the Bool connectives and/or/not (anthill.prelude.Bool.*) — recursing on operands via translate_expr. Loud SmtGenError on any other condition shape (right layer, loud-error principle). INDEPENDENTLY MEANINGFUL: unblocks discharge of ANY ite-bodied defining rule (hand-written or synthesized); stdlib sign/max/min become dischargeable. Prerequisite substrate under WI-669 inc-1b (the body-derived synth seam refolds op_defining_equations arms into a nested Expr::If and needs this lowering). ACCEPTANCE: a hand-written ite-bodied rule (e.g. '?r = ite(gte(?x,0), ?x, 0)' with a clamp(x)>=0 violation-rule) discharges by z3 (unsat); cargo-test green + /code-review. NOTE: if/then/else does NOT parse in rule-body position (only operation bodies), so the hand-written demonstrator uses the ite(...) functor form; Expr::If coverage is exercised via WI-669's synth path + a unit test."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-11T07:19:58Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-669", author: "claude", content: "Inc-1b DELIVERED (commit b7d060fc). The SMT seam + demonstrator: a proof about a bodied op discharges from the body via z3 with NO hand-written <=>/ite twin. Full workspace suite green; /code-review (2 fresh general-purpose finders on WI-680 + 2 on inc-1b): no correctness/soundness/cache bug.\n\nMECHANISM: synthesize_op_defining_rule(op) (kb/body_specialize.rs) builds a transient rule op(?0..?n-1,?result) :- ?result = <refolded-if>, refolding op_defining_equations' flattened arms into ONE nested Expr::If (drop-last-guard; Bool.not for negated, Bool.and for multi-guard). Built over FRESH Var::Globals (NOT raw DeBruijn -- assert_rule_debruijn_with_nodes derives arity from the Globals it collects; raw DeBruijn -> malformed arity-0). Head functor = the op, so smt-gen's rules_by_functor->try_inline_rule_call inlines it. Seam synthesize_body_derived_defrules(rule_qn) scans direct body goals; called at run_smt_subquery (the single canonical SMT-dispatch chokepoint -> covers z3/ranking/induction) before emit (so it caches like any other). Admissibility probed before allocating frame vars (no fresh-var leak for declined match/let ops).\n\nPREREQUISITE WI-680 DELIVERED separately (commit 62e17fef): smt-gen translate_expr now lowers Expr::If + Bool.ite -> (ite ..) with a translate_condition helper (inequalities + eq + Bool.and/or/not). Confirmed gap it closed: 'unhandled arithmetic op ite'.\n\nCALLING CONVENTION: the op must be called qualified enough to bind (Ops.clamp(?x,?r)); a bare unqualified relation name stays unresolved and the emitter reports it loudly.\n\nSCOPE (all loud, never silent): DIRECT body goals only (cited-lemma / transitive bodied-op calls -> loud emitter error; widening = follow-on); demonstrator is ARITHMETIC/if (clamp single-if + grade nested-if), NOT append/length (ADT match bodies need SMT datatype support); the synth rule is run-scoped & not retracted (benign -- op's true body, KB discarded on return; a within-run by-derivation relational call would see it -- documented, follow-on if a long-lived-KB consumer emerges).\n\nTESTS: wi669_defining_equations_test (+5 inc-1b: shape/nested-and-not/match-declines/idempotent); prove_body_derived_test (cli, 2: clamp+grade proved from body no twin; false property -> COUNTEREXAMPLE).\n\nREMAINING under WI-669 umbrella: none for the core §3.4 prover/SMT scope. lf1 consumers = WI-681 (GPS desired_position, QF_NRA) + WI-679 (transponder ranking, LIA; needs Expr::Let in reduce).", at: "2026-07-11T08:06:46Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-669", description: some(value: "§3.4 prover site: extract an operation's DEFINING EQUATIONS from its body ON DEMAND for proofs (design docs/design/abstract-interpreter-and-rules.md §3.4). The last consumer of WI-580's \"operation body is the single source of truth\" vision — WI-580 itself delivered the eval and SLD-resolver consumers (append structural-unfold + member eq-vs-unification) and the carrier prerequisite (WI-668); the prover/SMT view is the remaining §3.4 piece, framed there as \"separate future.\"\n\nPRINCIPLE: proof/SMT wants an operation's defining equations, but hand-written <=> defining rules duplicate the body and can drift or diverge unsoundly (exactly the problem WI-580 fixed for eval/resolution). Instead, the SAME one-step specializer that serves the SLD site (kb/body_specialize.rs::folded_call_match / reduce) produces the defining-equation SET for an operation when a proof needs it: each match arm's specialization IS one defining equation, op(params) = arm_result_i :- arm_cond_i (design §2 — a derived rule is one step of abstract interpretation of the body). Generated per-proof (proof obligations are generated per-proof anyway; design §3.4).\n\nLAWS ARE THEOREMS (WI-580 scope item 3, unchanged): extra laws (e.g. append associativity) are NOT hand-written defining rules — they are theorems PROVED from the body. Hand-written rules survive only for genuine bodyless relations.\n\nCONSUMER: the prover / SMT path. Today WI-558 delivered the prove-pass (discharge_by_derivation via SLD + verify_proofs); anthill-smt-gen translates rule/fact bodies to SMT. Wire defining-equation extraction so a proof about a bodied operation reads body-derived equations (via the specializer) instead of needing hand-written <=> twins. Determine whether the extraction feeds the SLD prove-pass (discharge_by_derivation), the SMT translation (anthill-smt-gen), or both.\n\nFOUNDATION (delivered, reuse): WI-580 body_specialize engine — folded_call_match returns the top match's per-arm (pattern, residual); reduce does the one-step abstract interpretation with the purity/requires/disjoint-arm gates. The prover consumer likely wants the FULL arm set (including the recursive residual per arm) rather than only the flex-scrutinee case the SLD site case-splits on, so expect an adjacent extraction entry point beside folded_call_match. WI-668 (Continuation carrier) is a resolver-side prereq, NOT needed here.\n\nACCEPTANCE: design-first — a note specifying (a) how the specializer's per-arm output maps to a defining-equation set consumable by the prover/SMT, and (b) which consumer(s) are wired; then an implementation letting a proof about a small bodied op (e.g. an append or length property) discharge from body-derived equations with NO hand-written <=> defining rule. cargo-test green.\n\nFollow-on to WI-580 (completes its §3.4 / prover scope item). Reuses the delivered body_specialize engine; not blocked on it."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-680"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-11T08:06:48Z"))
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-682", name: "carrier-neutral")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-681", description: some(value: "Apply WI-669 body-derivation to the lf1 GPS safety proof — derive the formation geometry (and approach law, backend permitting) from the follower_gps controller body, shrinking the trust surface to the inner-loop velocity envelope only.\n\nCONTEXT: examples/webots-modelling/lf1/. The GPS safety proof (safety_gps.anthill + safety_common.anthill) is DECOUPLED from the actual controller code (follower_gps.anthill): the bridge is the hand-asserted velocity envelope (KinematicAssumptions + velocity_envelope/step_distance_lemma 'by trust' axioms) and the follower's nominal separation is hand-planted as the literal 4.0 in real_pose_at(0, Follower, ...). The controller IS an anthill body (compute_controls + desired_position), so WI-669 defining equations CAN feed this proof. Three tiers:\n\n  1. FORMATION GEOMETRY (this ticket) — desired_position(leader, offset) = leader.pos + R(leader.yaw)*offset; a 2-D rotation preserves norm so |desired_position - leader.pos| = |offset| independent of yaw. FULLY body-derivable (only nonlinear fact is cos^2+sin^2 = 1, QF_NRA-decidable, NOT a trust axiom). Derive the leader<->follower-target distance from the desired_position body via WI-669's op_defining_equations, retiring the hand-planted 4.0. desired_position is single-arm, let-free -> works with the WI-669 inc-1a engine as-is.\n\n  2. APPROACH LAW — compute_controls (atan2 yaw + log10 pitch) governs HOW the follower closes on the target (proof abstracts this into step_distance_bound). Body-derivable but transcendentals push discharge to a dReal-class backend, not Z3 LRA/NRA. BACKEND limitation, lower priority.\n\n  3. INNER-LOOP VELOCITY ENVELOPE — commanded Controls -> actual velocity is mavic_base.cpp (Quoted('cpp'), NOT modeled). STAYS a trusted axiom unless the inner loop is modeled/empirically bounded. Irreducible trust surface.\n\nSCOPE: tier 1 — wire desired_position's body-derived equation into the GPS proof so |offset| comes from the body, not a constant. Tiers 2/3 documented as follow-on / surviving trust.\n\nDEPENDS: WI-669 (defining-equation engine; inc 1a delivered, 1b = SMT seam pending). Reuses KnowledgeBase::op_defining_equations. Sibling consumer: the transponder ranking ticket (LIA/integer-state-machine case; this one is the QF_NRA geometry case).\n\nACCEPTANCE: GPS safety proof reads the follower-target separation from a desired_position body-derived equation (no hand-written <=> twin, no hand-planted 4.0 for the geometry); trust surface reduced to tier 3 (+ tier 2 backend-gated); anthill check + discharge.sh green; cargo-test green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-669"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-11T10:13:48Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-510", description: some(value: "Diagnostics: the TypeError -> LoadError remapping (TypeError::to_load_error, kb/typing.rs ~351 for TypeMismatch and ~487 for Other) is LOSSY and hinders debugging. It flattens the structured TypeErrorContext (EntityField{entity,field} / OperationArgument{op,param} / LetBinding / ... ) and the Value-typed expected/actual into plain display STRINGS (LoadError::TypeMismatch{entity_name:String, field_name:String, expected_type:String, actual_type:String}), and DROPS the variant identity and the CONSTRUCTION SITE. Consequence (hit hard in WI-509): two structurally-different errors render identically (e.g. an EntityField field-arg check and an OperationArgument arg check both surface as 'entity_name=field_access, field_name=field'), and a backtrace captured AT THE CONVERSION reveals nothing because the TypeError is built, pushed into an errors Vec, and remapped later in bulk (type_check_sorts ~18799) -- so you must instrument at CONSTRUCTION, not conversion, to find where an error originates. INVESTIGATE/OPTIONS: (a) carry the originating source location / a lightweight construction marker (or a captured backtrace under a debug cfg/env) on TypeError so the conversion can attribute it; (b) keep LoadError structured (preserve the TypeErrorContext variant + the Value expected/actual) rather than pre-stringifying, stringify only at final render; (c) at minimum, include the TypeError variant name + a distinguishing tag in the rendered message so identical-looking errors are disambiguable. ACCEPTANCE: a field_access (or any) TypeMismatch can be traced to its construction site without hand-instrumenting each candidate; the rendered diagnostic distinguishes EntityField vs OperationArgument origins; full anthill-core green. REF: WI-509 (debugging cost); kb/typing.rs to_load_error + TypeErrorContext::entity_name/field_name; LoadError::TypeMismatch. Rust-only (diagnostics, no semantic change)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-11T11:04:25Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-653", author: "user", content: "CLOSED as resolved 2026-07-11. The slowdown was REAL (not a phantom) — it was the unindexed linear scans in type_check_sorts (lookup_operation_info O(calls*ops), plus SortAlias/SortProvidesInfo/SortInfo sibling scans); only the ATTRIBUTION to WI-650's check_eq_override_backing pass was wrong (that pass is 2ms). Fixed by the build-once index family: WI-656 (OperationInfo, aa4fe740), WI-659 (SortAlias, 831934cd), WI-660 (SortProvidesInfo, b9672a4f), WI-657 (static-audit micro-opts, b6bf0948), WI-663 (rule-head readers, 8398a8a5), WI-673 (SortInfoIndex, e0b65508); soundness backstopped by WI-665/666 (proposal 053 per-functor monotonicity, constant marking). RESULT: type_check_sorts 2.75s -> 847ms (~69%) on anthill-todo status (120 sorts, debug). Acceptance met (status back toward pre-regression timing; suites green across the landing WIs). The re-scoped direction-3 (cache the fully-typed KB image across CLI invocations) is NOT implemented and NOT needed given the current timing; if repeat-load latency ever matters again, re-file it as a fresh dev-time-caching WI rather than reviving this ticket.", at: "2026-07-11T11:13:40Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-653", description: some(value: "PERF REGRESSION (introduced 2026-07-07 by WI-650): anthill-todo CLI load slowed ~1.5s. Every invocation re-type-checks the whole embedded stdlib+bundle: type_check_sorts = 3.63s of a ~5s 'status' (ANTHILL_LOAD_TIMING=1, repo project, 114 sorts; debug build). WI-650's new check_eq_override_backing pass alone = ~1.5s (disabling it drops type_check_sorts 3.63s->2.09s). User observed directly ('fine yesterday', pre-WI-650 which landed 07-07 vs WI-627 07-06) and diagnosed it as 'why load stdlib 4 times?' = the 4 whole-KB body walks in type_check_sorts_typed: check_operation_bodies + record_find_dictionary_grounding(WI-300) + check_rule_body_requirements(WI-642) + check_eq_override_backing(WI-650). NOT caused by WI-434 (that adds ~0.12s via version_check, ruled out by measurement). FIX DIRECTIONS (ranked): (a) SCOPE check_eq_override_backing to skip trusted stdlib/bundle bodies -- the stdlib never compares Maps by WI-650's design, so walking it always finds nothing; only project/user code can trigger it -> recovers ~1.5s, targeted+low-risk. (b) BROADER: the CLI loads a known-good embedded stdlib; skip the pure-CHECK typer passes for it (keep the REWRITE passes eval needs: simp, req_insertion) -> recovers most of the 3.63s, more invasive/riskier. (c) WI-652 dedup the 4 walks into one shared walk_body_occurrences -> helps all four. Amplifier: debug build (~10-50x); a release CLI would mask it but the relative regression is real. Acceptance: anthill-todo 'status' on the repo project drops back toward pre-WI-650 timing; full suite green; WI-650's Map eq-override guard still fires on user code (map_eq_error_test.rs green). Deferred (document-only) by user 2026-07-07. See WI-652 (dedup overlap) and WI-650 (cause)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-11T11:13:49Z"))
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-683", name: "carrier-neutral")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-505", description: some(value: "Bundle the canonical stage0 domain into the anthill-todo CLI so it is version-locked with the bundled logic, instead of relying on each project's drift-prone anthill-todo/domain.anthill. PROBLEM (observed 2026-06-17): the CLI embeds its LOGIC (anthill_bundle.rs: store.anthill + main.anthill via include_str!) which references anthill.stage0 entities (WorkItem, WorkStatus.PreOpened, Tag, ...), but the ENTITY DEFINITIONS are loaded from the PROJECT's anthill-todo/domain.anthill (collect_anthill_files over the scanned project dir, main.rs:~403-415). The domain the bundled logic depends on is therefore owned per-project and NOT version-locked with the binary. When the grammar or domain changes (e.g. the parser dropped the multi-name `export` clause; the CLI added WorkStatus.PreOpened), every project whose domain.anthill predates the change silently breaks: a stale heimdall (/home/rssh/packages/lantr/heimdall/anthill-todo/domain.anthill) failed to parse on its `export` lines, which cascaded into a wall of `unresolved import anthill.stage0.WorkStatus/WorkItem/...` errors; fixed only by hand-syncing the project copy to the current standard domain. There are also TWO in-repo copies that can diverge: anthill-todo/domain.anthill (this tracker's) and examples/github-todo/domain.anthill (the one `init` writes via include_str! at main.rs:~284). FIX: add the canonical domain to the embedded bundle (anthill_bundle.rs, alongside store/main) so entity defs ship with the binary and cannot drift. Make the project's domain.anthill OPTIONAL: when the bundled domain is present, do NOT also load a project-local domain.anthill (avoid double-definition of anthill.stage0.WorkItem etc.) -- either skip it, or treat its presence as an explicit override with a staleness warning. Stop `init` from writing a domain.anthill that can later drift (write nothing, or a comment stub pointing at the bundled domain). De-duplicate the two in-repo copies into the single bundled source. CONSTRAINTS / GOTCHAS: (1) the synthetic `namespace anthill.stage0` wrap for bare workitems.anthill files (main.rs:~161-178) must still resolve against the bundled domain's scope; (2) existing projects that still ship a domain.anthill must keep working (skip-or-override, not hard error); (3) rules.anthill is a sibling per-project file with the same drift exposure (claimable/blocked/dep_satisfied workflow rules) -- decide whether to bundle it too or note it as a follow-up; (4) keep the crate's own anthill-todo/ self-hosting working (it scans its own dir per main.rs:~190). ACCEPTANCE: a project directory with NO domain.anthill (and one with a deliberately stale/old domain.anthill) loads and runs status/list/next against the bundled domain with no parse/unresolved-import errors; `init` produces a project that survives a future domain change without hand-editing; a regression test loads a domain-less project dir; cargo-test green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-11T12:13:09Z"))
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-685", name: "carrier-neutral")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-679", description: some(value: "lf1 transponder ranking proof: derive decrease_violation_transponder / bound_violation_transponder's upc transition from the step operation body via WI-669 defining-equation extraction, retiring the hand-written restatement.\n\nFIRST STEP (inline, not a separate prereq): add Expr::Let handling to kb/body_specialize.rs::reduce — today it hard-declines let (catch-all _ => None), and step's body is let-heavy (let new_delta / new_yaw_cmd / new_upc / new_seq). The let arm mirrors reduce_match's Env extension: reduce the bound expr, extend the existing Env (the operational face of proposal-050 Gamma's binding component), reduce the continuation; reuse shadow / collect_bound_names. Scope = simple non-destructuring 'let name = expr' (step uses only that); escalate let to its OWN ticket ONLY if destructuring-lets or ANF flattening of nested BODIED-op calls is needed (step's nested calls are arithmetic operators, no self-recursion, so neither expected). Also needs match support (step matches on Option prev_distance/prev_delta) — WI-669 inc-1a declines a residual match; either the scrutinees resolve concretely or add ADT-match defining equations.\n\nTHEN: defining_equations(step) yields the upc-update as an ite-chain over inputs; the ranking proof references step(...).upc instead of the hand-written ?upc_next = ?upc + 1. Field access .upc already works (WI-482).\n\nDRIFT TO CONFIRM/RESOLVE: the hand-written ?upc_next = ?upc + 1 appears to DIVERGE from step's clamp-through-0 (else if gte(state.upc,0) then state.upc+1 else 0): for post-armed upc < 0 the body yields new_upc = 0 (snap to 0), NOT upc+1. If confirmed at runtime, deriving from the body changes the obligation and may revise the N=6 PostArmedExcursionBound. This drift is EXACTLY what WI-669 body-derivation removes. (Comment/code mismatch at follower_transponder.anthill:62-64 'incremented while shrinking faster' vs code decrements — separate readability note.)\n\nACCEPTANCE: the transponder ranking proof (proof post_armed_excursion_bound) discharges from step-body-derived defining equations with NO hand-written upc-transition rule body; discharge.sh + anthill check green (or N corrected if the drift is real, justified from the body); cargo-test green.\n\nDEPENDS: WI-669 (defining-equation engine + SMT seam). Second lf1 consumer alongside WI-681 (GPS desired_position); this is the LIA/integer-state-machine case, WI-681 the QF_NRA geometry case."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-669"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-11T14:36:49Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-686", description: some(value: "smt-gen close_occ scalar-param value channel: close a body-derived entity constructor whose field is computed from a SCALAR operation parameter (not just entity params). Today anthill-smt-gen/src/lib.rs::close_occ loud-errors on a Var::DeBruijn scalar param inside a constructor ('a scalar operation parameter inside a constructor cannot be closed across the inline boundary'); WI-681 handled only entity-typed params (Vec3 whose fields are other entity params). To derive a bodied op that RETURNS a constructor with a scalar-computed field (e.g. TransponderFollowerState{ upc: ite(...scalar upc...) }), thread the callee scalar bindings (try_inline_rule_call's callee_str string-map) into close_occ so a scalar DeBruijn resolves to its value rather than erroring. ACCEPTANCE: a bodied op like f(x: Int64) -> Wrapper = Wrapper(v: if gte(x,0) then x else 0), inlined at f(?x, ?w) then ?w.v field-access, lowers to the ite over ?x; smt-gen test + cargo-test green. General beyond lf1 (constructor-returning bodies with scalar fields). Blocks the lf1 whole-step derivation follow-up."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-11T15:35:03Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-685", description: some(value: "Carrier-neutralize the semantic-eq tier stack so normalize_value (the Node->Term canonicalization in resolve.rs) can be RETIRED. ROOT CAUSE (diagnosed under WI-683): a rule-body eq(?x,?y)/neq goal is a Value::Node occurrence, and walk_arg materializes its bound args back as Value::Node (subst_var_leaf -> materialize_from_handle), so the sem-eq builtins receive Value::Node operands. eq_operands currently calls normalize_value to canonicalize them to Value::Term because MULTIPLE tiers of the sem-eq machinery take a CONSERVATIVE-Node shortcut; bridge_op_to_eval normalizes for the same reason (its scratch interpreter's materialize_value passes a Node through unchanged). GATES TO CARRIER-NEUTRALIZE (each a shortcut normalize_value works around): (1) value_deep_ground: 'Value::Node(_) => false' -> a Node is ground iff it has NO Expr::Var leaf of ANY kind (Global/Rigid/DeBruijn), matching term_deep_ground's 'Term::Var(_) => false'; add occurrence_has_any_var helper (occurrence_has_unbound_var flags only Global -> would UNSOUNDLY pass a Rigid-skolem Node). (2) value_reaches_eq_override: 'Value::Node(_) => true' -> walk the occurrence (head functor via eq_dispatch_target + recurse children), mirroring the Value::Entity arm. (3) value_reaches_partial_carrier: same conservative-Node treatment -> walk the occurrence. (4) materialize_value (eval/mod.rs): 'other => other' for Node -> lower via occurrence_to_term -> term_to_value (Node->Entity) so the bridge interpreter can read fields (else 'receiver is not an entity'). (5) sem_eq_dispatch sub-resolution: ensure Node operands flow through the dispatched carrier.eq(a,b) goal. SYMPTOM without the fix (validated 2026-07-11): with normalize removed from eq_operands, value_reaches_eq_override(Node)=>true forces a Delay for a Node operand; under ResolveConfig::default() (definite_only:false) a floundered residual COUNTS as a solution -> eq(red,blue) returns 1 (must be 0). Also breaks nested_set_eq_dispatches_elementwise and blows up perf on larger_set_eq_uses_fresh_sub_budget. ALREADY-VALIDATED SAFE SUBSET (land first as an increment): value_num/value_f64 read a Node literal directly via head(); finish_result compares via views_structurally_equal -> removes normalize_value from cmp/arith/finish_result (numeric path), shrinking normalize_value to the 2 sem-eq/bridge callers. ACCEPTANCE: normalize_value DELETED; a rule-body eq/neq/cmp over Value::Node operands DECIDES correctly (no spurious residual); full anthill-core suite green (esp. wi616_semantic_eq_test, wi625_*, bounded_quant); no set-eq perf regression. SOUNDNESS-SENSITIVE: adversarially verify each gate (a Node passing a gate must match its Term-twin/term_deep_ground verdict). Refs: WI-683 (driver), WI-348 (no Value->TermId), WI-486 (views_structurally_equal), WI-616/625 (sem-eq dispatch + eval bridge). Rust-only (resolver + eval)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-683"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-11T15:38:39Z"))
