fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-698", author: "user", content: "BROKEN DOWN into children; WI-698 is now the UMBRELLA carrying the design record (2026-07-12). RATIONALE: the scope items live in four different subsystems with very different size and risk, and -- decisively -- what WI-437 increment 3 actually waits on is NEITHER of the hardening gates. Scope (d) the proposal and (e) persistence-stays-as-is were already DONE; (a)+(b) are nearly free, the row-param refinement mechanism being delivered machinery already proven by the 8-test smoke against a stand-in effect; and (c) is not ONE gate but THREE, in three subsystems, one of which is a GENERAL soundness hole unrelated to External. CHILDREN: WI-699 (A -- declare External in prelude/external.anthill, retarget the smoke, pin the ER/EW read/write split; no deps; THE WHOLE CRITICAL PATH TO WI-437). WI-700 (B -- effect-row conformance and lacks enforcement at explicit row-instantiation shapes: `shield[EffP = {}](poke3)` LOADS today although poke3 declares {Outside}, so a non-empty row escapes into an op declaring `effects {}`; root cause localized to validate_callback_effect_row reading a row-param tail as OPEN-hence-ABSORBING instead of resolving the explicit instantiation through the substitution. NOT External-specific -- the hole exists for Modify, Error and Clock alike -- and it is the RISKY item, a behavior change that will stop some currently-loading code from loading, so it lands EARLY per the repo preference rather than at the tail). WI-701 (C -- the Branch x External co-occurrence gate; statically writable NOW, since Branch is already a declared effect kind, and it forbids nothing we can RUN, since the Branch runtime is still a placeholder; depends on WI-700 because the end-state gate is a -External lacks-constraint; WI-329 handler-discharge, still Open, is the follow-on that makes it compositional rather than blunt). WI-702 (D -- purity-consumer LOUDNESS at the explicit defining-equation request sites, plus the one real formation hole: load-time validation of a user-written [simp]-tagged equation mentioning an External-rowed op; soundness is already free via the blanket body_specialize.rs:192 gate). WI-703 (E -- the EffectsRuntime import wart) is filed as a SIBLING and deliberately NOT an umbrella dependency: it is a diagnostic defect in the neighbouring WI-320 anchor, not part of what it means for External to be delivered, and the umbrella must not be held open by it. EDGE REWIRED: WI-437 depended on WI-698; that edge now points at WI-699, so the tracker backend is no longer blocked behind hardening gates it does not need. Cluster tagged wi698. SEQUENCE: WI-699 first (cheap, unblocks the consumer), then WI-700 (risky substrate), then WI-701 and WI-702 in either order; WI-703 anytime.", at: "2026-07-12T10:55:10Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-698", author: "user", content: "CORRECTION to the third feedback above (applied 2026-07-12 during the pre-commit /code-review). That entry stated: `validate_callback_effect_row fires only at the WI-440 -Modify[x] shape`. THAT DIAGNOSIS IS WRONG, and it would send the WI-700 implementer hunting for a check at a call shape that is not the problem. The truth: validate_callback_effect_row (rustland/anthill-core/src/kb/typing.rs:14393) IS wired into the ORDINARY call path -- both argument loops call it (typing.rs:5983 positional, typing.rs:6011 named). It fails to fire because its open-vs-closed decision reads a DECLARED row whose tail is a row PARAMETER (`{EffP, -Outside}`) as OPEN, hence ABSORBING, and never resolves the explicit `[EffP = {...}]` instantiation through the substitution first. CONSEQUENCE: WI-700 is a fix at ONE DECISION POINT, not a new check at a new call shape -- smaller than the third feedback implied, though the blast radius (code that loads today stops loading) is unchanged. The corrected diagnosis is carried in WI-700 and in proposal 054 section Mechanism. Also note, for the record: that third feedback says the smoke was `7 green` with a single combined gap test; the fourth feedback supersedes it -- the suite is 8, with the gap pin split into two independent flip targets.", at: "2026-07-12T11:02:39Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-704", author: "user", content: "RENUMBERED from WI-697 to WI-704 (2026-07-12, at rebase). This item was filed locally as WI-697 in the 2026-07-12 WI-437 design session and never pushed; meanwhile origin/main published a DIFFERENT WI-697 (entity_parent index re-keyed TermId to Symbol; is_entity_of carrier-neutral, delivered in 29d41393). Two distinct items claimed one id. The published id wins, so this one moved to WI-704 -- the next free id above the WI-698..703 External cluster. Nothing referenced this item (no depends_on edge, no doc, no feedback), so the renumber was a single-line change. ROOT CAUSE, worth not repeating: the id was allocated without fetching first. Fetch before filing.", at: "2026-07-12T11:17:50Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-437", author: "user", content: "DESIGN DOC POINTER, refreshed (2026-07-12, post-push). The full specification is rustland/anthill-todo/docs/design/backend-github-coordination.md, now COMMITTED AND PUSHED in 890e1399 -- it was still untracked when the earlier DESIGN DOC feedback was written. READ THIS FIRST: the DESCRIPTION of this item still cites examples/github-todo/docs/pluggable-backend.md, which is SUPERSEDED; backend-github-coordination.md is the current spec. Increments are its section 14: (1) store-factory substrate, (2) StateDirs + fsck, (3) Mirror capability + fake, (4) coordinated add + provisional ids, (5) sync (reconcile / repair / push / tombstone), (6) migrate. DEPENDENCY REWIRE (same session): this item used to depend on WI-698; WI-698 was split into an umbrella plus children, and the edge now points at WI-699 -- declare the generic External effect -- which is the ENTIRE critical path to increment 3, whose Mirror carrier ops need the {External, Error} rows. The hardening gates (WI-700 row conformance at explicit instantiation, WI-701 Branch x External, WI-702 purity-consumer gates) are NOT on this path and must not be re-added as blockers on this item. The effect design is proposal 054 (docs/proposals/054-external-effect.md); its section Mechanism carries the ER/EW carrier-tied row-param split that section 8.3 of the design doc relies on.", at: "2026-07-12T11:28:04Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-699", description: some(value: "WI-698 child A (proposal 054 scope items (a)+(b)): DECLARE the generic External effect and pin its row surface. New stdlib/anthill/prelude/external.anthill declaring `sort External` + `fact Effect[T = External]` via the Clock convention (mirror stdlib/anthill/prelude/time.anthill, whose header already states that Clock exists to mark operations non-deterministic by virtue of consulting external state -- the concept 054 names once); wire the file into the prelude load chain. Scope item (b) -- the typer threads External as an ordinary row member -- is expected FREE, and the mechanism is DELIVERED machinery: the 2026-07-12 smoke (rustland/anthill-core/tests/include/wi698_row_param_refinement_test.rs, 8 green) proved carrier-tied row-param refinement end to end using a STAND-IN effect `Outside` declared exactly this way. TASKS: (1) the prelude declaration; (2) retarget that test file from `Outside` to the real `External` -- 6 of 8 tests transfer verbatim, while the two `*_today` gap pins belong to WI-700 and stay as they are; (3) add the 054 section-Mechanism READ/WRITE SPLIT as a new pinned test, since the smoke probed only a SINGLE row param: `effects ER = ?` and `effects EW = ?` on one spec, the real carrier instantiating ER = EW = {External}, the fake ER = {} and EW = {Modify[reg]}, and a store threading E = {ER, EW} -- the WI-441 decoupled-rows precedent shipped as MappedStream ES/EF, so the same substitution should carry it with no new typer work (confirm that). BY-CATCH: a user-namespace `effects X = ?` currently needs `import anthill.prelude.EffectsRuntime` (WI-703); the test file already carries the import. THIS IS THE WHOLE CRITICAL PATH TO WI-437 increment 3 (the Mirror carrier): the WI-698 hardening gates (WI-700, WI-701, WI-702) are NOT prerequisites for it, which is why WI-437 depends on THIS item rather than on the WI-698 umbrella. Value beyond the tracker: any future host binding (http, db, external tools) reuses the same effect."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-12T17:22:34Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-700", author: "user", content: "BY-CATCH from WI-699 /code-review (empirical finder): the carrier provides-conformance check check_override_refinement (typing.rs ~11780-11849) is FAIL-OPEN for denoted/parametric effect rows -- it runs only when every effect is a confident ground Value::Term, and 'parametric/denoted (Modify[c]) effect refinement is deferred'; denoted-bearing provisions are skipped entirely. CONSEQUENCE proven live: a carrier 'provides Spec[C=X, ER={}, EW={Modify[Reg]}]' whose concrete ops declare DIFFERENT rows loads uncaught (a finder swapped FakeRW's ER/EW binding in wi698_row_param_refinement_test and the test still passed). This is a DISTINCT shape from WI-700's stated call-site/lacks focus (it is provides-declaration-time, not shield[EffP=...] call-site), so it may be a sibling rather than in-scope -- flagging because it is the same 'effect-row conformance fail-open' family and the WI-699 test (Wrap2RW) had to route around it to pin the two-param mechanism via the ENFORCED call-site substitution path. The fail-open provides path is documented in that test's invariant-6 docstring.", at: "2026-07-12T17:23:50Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-700", author: "user", content: "CORRECTED ROOT CAUSE (verified empirically 2026-07-12 by instrumenting typing.rs; SUPERSEDES the ticket's 'reads declared row tail as OPEN' hypothesis, which is WRONG): the explicit [EffP={...}] instantiation IS already resolved -- seed_op_type_args (typing.rs:5743, before the arg loops) binds EffP into subst via unify_types. PROOF: decompose_effect_row on the declared callback row returns None because it is SELF-CONTRADICTORY ({Outside,-Outside}); if EffP were still an open tail it would return Some(([],[EffP],[Outside])). The two probes slip through for two DIFFERENT real reasons: (a) the substituted declared row {Outside,-Outside} is self-contradictory -> decompose returns None -> validate_callback_effect_row's ? SWALLOWS it as no-error; (b) a bare NULLARY op ref (poke/poke3) synthesizes to its RETURN type Int64 (arrow + effect row both vanish; ty=Int64 effects=[]), so arrow_parts(actual)=None bails before any row comparison. A lambda callback keeps ty=()->Int64; a NON-nullary op ref (WI-469 badcb(x:Int64)) also keeps its arrow -- only nullary op refs collapse (operation_as_function_value returns None for nullary at typing.rs:2796). FIX CHOSEN BY USER = Option B (root fix): eta-lift nullary op refs in arrow position to ()->ret@row so the existing WI-440/469 machinery enforces the row; plus an explicit self-contradiction reject for probe (a). Option B subsumes the 'nullary op-as-callback drops its effect row' by-catch, so that hole is WI-700's core fix, not a separate ticket.", at: "2026-07-12T18:12:03Z")
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-705", name: "wi698")
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-706", name: "wi698")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-700", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED (code af269943). Mechanism (Option B, user-chosen): (b) eta-lift a bare NULLARY op ref in a callback/arrow slot to ()->ret@row (drop the nullary exclusion in operation_as_function_value; empty-NamedTuple () param) so its effect row is no longer dropped -- typer marks EVERY eta site with CallClass::EtaOpRef (dict now Option<TermId>, None at requires-free/nullary sites) and eval reads the marker to mint the arity-0 OpRef selectively; (a) validate_callback_effect_row reads the DECLARED row RAW (new decompose_effect_row_raw split from the filtering wrapper, sharing row_self_contradiction) and rejects the self-contradictory instantiated callback row. Both wi698 *_today pins flipped to expect_reject; +nullary-eta eval round-trip test +nullary-returns-function regression guard. Full suite 2750/0; purely additive (no existing nullary-op-as-callback usages). /code-review (max effort, 5 finder agents) ran: fixed a REGRESSION it found (a nullary op whose RETURN type is a function -- make_inc()->Function[..] -- was newly rejected; check_bare_ref now prefers the return-type reading when ret conforms to the expected arrow, nullary-only); resolved REUSE (shared row_self_contradiction) + SIMPLIFICATION (shared eval eta_marker accessor); corrected a FALSE doc-comment that claimed lambda callbacks are 'checked elsewhere'. DEFERRED (loud siblings, both verified LOADING on HEAD): WI-705 (self-contradiction at signature altitude -- op's OWN row g[E={X}] + lambda self-contradiction; hoist to post-seed_op_type_args, subsumes the eta-scoped reject) and WI-706 (lambda-callback actual-vs-declared conformance -- distinct mechanism, pre-existing).", at: "2026-07-12T19:13:10Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-700", description: some(value: "Effect-row SOUNDNESS HOLE, GENERAL -- not External-specific (surfaced by the WI-698 smoke, 2026-07-12). At EXPLICIT row-instantiation call shapes -- `op[EffP = {...}](callback)` -- the DECLARED callback row is not enforced, so a non-empty effect row escapes into an operation declaring `effects {}`. Two independent probes, both pinned TODAY-AS-LOADING in rustland/anthill-core/tests/include/wi698_row_param_refinement_test.rs, both to be FLIPPED to expect-reject by this WI: (a) `lacks_unenforced_at_explicit_instantiation_today` -- `shield[EffP = {Outside}](poke)` loads although shield declares its callback `-Outside`, so the lacks-constraint parses but does not enforce; (b) `row_conformance_unchecked_at_explicit_instantiation_today` -- `shield[EffP = {}](poke3)` loads although poke3 declares `{Outside}`; there is no self-contradictory row in (b), so the eventual reject must come from actual-vs-declared conformance ITSELF, not from row self-inconsistency. ROOT CAUSE, localized: `validate_callback_effect_row` (rustland/anthill-core/src/kb/typing.rs:14393) IS wired into the ordinary call path -- both argument loops call it (typing.rs:5983 positional, typing.rs:6011 named) -- but its open-vs-closed decision reads a declared row whose tail is a row PARAM (`{EffP, -Outside}`) as OPEN, hence ABSORBING, and never resolves the explicit `[EffP = {...}]` instantiation through the substitution first. Its own doc comment already claims bound row-tail resolution; the explicit-instantiation binding does not reach it. FIX: resolve an explicitly-instantiated row param before the open/closed decision. This escape exists today for EVERY effect -- Modify, Error, Clock -- so it is worth fixing on its own terms; the WI-698 External is merely its first demanding client (the 054 Branch gate is a `-External` lacks-constraint and therefore rests on this check, hence WI-701 depends on this item). RISK / why this lands EARLY: it is a BEHAVIOR CHANGE -- code that loads today will stop loading -- so budget for fallout across existing explicit-instantiation call sites in stdlib and tests, and review the blast radius. ACCEPTANCE: both `*_today` tests flipped to expect-reject and green; full cargo-test green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-12T19:13:11Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-455", author: "user", content: "ACCEPTANCE SUPERSEDED. The filed acceptance -- 'a synthetic deep eta-chain surfaces StepsExhausted (named) not a native overflow' -- is NOT met as written, and cannot be: such a chain is no longer CONSTRUCTIBLE. The chain was never a legitimate feature that merely recursed too deep. It existed only because dispatch re-resolved an OpRef's already-resolved operation Symbol by SHORT NAME against the caller's locals (an OpRef hop pushes no frame, so those locals were still on top). A caller-local merely SHARING the callee op's name therefore hijacked the call -- dynamic scoping of a resolved reference. Its quiet form was a SILENT WRONG ANSWER, worse than the crash: apply_it(f, double) called as apply_it(double, triple) ran triple (3) where f denotes double (2). The cycle was just two such locals pointing at each other. Fixed at the root instead: dispatch split into dispatch_call_with_requirements_inner (a source-level NAME, which a local callable may shadow -- that is how f(x) on a local lambda works, and it stays) and dispatch_resolved_operation (an already-resolved Symbol, which no local may shadow; the OpRef arm goes here). An OpRef application now resolves in exactly ONE HOP BY CONSTRUCTION, so there is no chain to bound. StepOutcome::Dispatch was therefore NOT shipped: it was built and proven green first, then dropped -- it would bound a chain that can no longer occur, at the cost of a Box + trampoline round-trip on every HOF call. Note for 027.2/047, which cite this WI as the precedent for StepOutcome::Suspend: that precedent does not exist; introduce Suspend on its own merits. By-catch fixed: resolved dispatch skips the wrapper feeding the recent-dispatch ring, so OpRef-reached ops (i.e. every HOF call) went missing from StepsExhausted -- the one diagnostic this WI exists to protect. Same gap was already present on the WI-444 carrier-override and WI-350 spec-op-by-value arms; all now note the impl they actually run. Code 354356ce, pushed to main. Suite green on the rebased tree (73 binaries, 2754 passed), incl. upstream WI-700, which now mints OpRefs for NULLARY ops straight into the rewritten arm. Four tests, each verified to fail with its fix removed.", at: "2026-07-12T20:09:48Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-455", description: some(value: "Typer/eval: route the OpRef / spec-op REDISPATCH through the run() trampoline so host call depth stays O(1). After the WI-179 iterative-eval transform the value-cascade is trampolined (StepOutcome::Deliver), but dispatch_call_with_requirements_inner's OpRef arm (eval.rs ~1042/1047) and the spec-op-by-value redispatch still tail-call dispatch_call_with_requirements NATIVELY (do NOT return to run() between hops), so a pathological eta-chain recurses on the host Rust stack and can overflow before step_cap ticks. Narrow edge (eta-chains are 1 hop), documented in-code. FIX: add StepOutcome::Dispatch{target,args,requirements,type_args} re-entered by run() (like Deliver) and have the redispatch RETURN it instead of native-tail-calling. Acceptance: cargo-test green; a synthetic deep eta-chain surfaces StepsExhausted (named) not a native overflow. Rust-only (eval)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-12T20:09:58Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-701", description: some(value: "WI-698 child C (proposal 054, section Branch and External): the typer REJECTS an effect row carrying BOTH `Branch` and `External`. WHY (054): 027 / 037 / 047 section 8 give a tracked resource exactly two lawful branch-interaction contracts -- ranked ABOVE Branch it is snapshotted on entry and rolled back on backtrack (register_undo), ranked BELOW it survives across branches -- and BOTH rest on one premise: the runtime mediates every change to the resource. External names precisely the state for which that premise fails, so NEITHER contract is available. ABOVE IS IMPOSSIBLE: there is no register_undo for the world -- a branch that fails after create_issue cannot un-create the issue; the id is burnt, the notification sent, another writer may already have read the registry. Search would leave debris in the world on behalf of computations that officially never happened. BELOW IS UNSOUND: a solver multi-shot-resumes the continuation once per solution (the 027.2 eval-SLD switch), so an External call reached after a `reflect` runs ONCE PER BRANCH -- create_issue inside a ten-solution region mints ten issues -- and even pure READS break the search, since recent_issues re-executed per branch can answer differently in different branches, so the branches explore different worlds and the solutions are no longer answers to one question. 027 already rules plain sticky-under-Branch a soundness HAZARD for tracked resources that merely LACK snapshot hooks today; External is the state for which the hooks are impossible IN PRINCIPLE, so the hazard is permanent. WRITABLE NOW, statically: Branch is already a declared effect kind (`fact Effect[T = Branch]`, stdlib/anthill/prelude/effects.anthill:136), so rows can carry it and a load-time co-occurrence check can reject it; and the gate forbids nothing we can currently RUN, since the Branch / suspend-resume runtime is still a placeholder (rustland/anthill-core/src/eval/effects.rs:42 and :438 -- WI-075 delivered push_choice, the SUBSTRATE, not the Branch effect). SHAPE: the end-state gate is a LACKS-CONSTRAINT -- a solver region row demanding `-External`, the `-Modify[x]` surface of Iterable.find -- which is exactly why this item depends on WI-700: lacks-constraints parse but do not ENFORCE at explicit row-instantiation shapes today. FOLLOW-ON, not in scope: WI-329 (handler discharge, Open) is what makes the rule COMPOSITIONAL and exact -- a solver reify discharges Branch from the row, so External becomes legal again at precisely the point where the search has committed to its solutions. That is the sound sandwich: read the world before the search, search over tracked state only, write the world after the commit (WI-437 does this twice -- buffered flush, and sync reconcile). Until WI-329 lands, this gate is a blunt co-occurrence reject with no discharge path; that is acceptable and intended."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-699", "WI-700"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-12T20:17:06Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-701", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED (code 5a8d4922). Blunt load-time co-occurrence reject: check_branch_external_exclusion in type_check_sorts_typed (beside check_operation_signatures) walks every OperationInfo fact via new all_operation_effects and rejects any op whose declared row PRESENTS both Branch and External. effect_label_names_sort classifies a label via type_head + same_sort_canonical (canonical QN identity, no short-name); it peeks past a guarded(label,guard) atom to its label (guarded = conservatively present, matching decompose_effect_row_raw) but NOT past absent (-External is not performed). Purely additive: no runnable code declares both (Branch runtime is still a placeholder); full suite 2756/0. /code-review (3 finder agents, max effort): (1) guarded-External silently missed -> FIXED (the guarded-unwrap; verified non-vacuous by neutering) + test; (2) all_operation_params/all_operation_effects now share one operation_info_fact_heads walk (DRY); (3) added present-vs-absent test ({Branch,-External} loads) and a guarded reject test. Both rejects verified non-vacuous by breaking the mechanism. DELIBERATE SCOPE LIMITS (all consistent with blunt/not-compositional): (a) OP-LEVEL declared rows only -- a row var instantiated to External inside a Branch region, or a callback ARROW-param row carrying both, is not caught here (compositional/lacks-constraint end-state is WI-329, riding WI-700 instantiation enforcement); (b) per-declared-row: a {Branch}-spec/{External}-impl split across two facts of one op symbol is not merged (effectively unreachable -- redefinition, caught by check_override_refinement); (c) guarded co-occurrence conservatively rejected pre-discharge (WI-067/WI-329 refine). FOLLOW-ON: WI-329 makes it compositional and exact.", at: "2026-07-12T20:18:44Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-206", author: "user", content: "Delivered (code 107f6e31), with one deviation from the acceptance line, agreed with the user before implementing. ACCEPTANCE AS WRITTEN said `is_modifiable(Cell[Int])` returns true from source; the op's SEMANTICS does that (a value-level test builds Cell[V = Int64] and asserts true), but WRITING a parameterized type in an argument slot is a language surface that does not exist for any `Type`-taking reflect op (facts_of(kb(), List[T = Int64]) fails identically, and always has) -- split out as WI-707. Source-level acceptance met for the BARE form: is_modifiable(Cell) = true, is_modifiable(KB) = true, is_modifiable(Int64) = false.\n\nTWO THINGS THE WI TEXT GOT WRONG, both now settled in code:\n\n(1) The suggested implementation -- 'a Rust builtin that wraps a KB query for the Modifiable fact functor with the type as the T parameter binding' -- CANNOT satisfy the acceptance. The stdlib fact is `Modifiable[T = Cell]`, whose T is a bare Ref(Cell); that does not unify with `Cell[V = Int64]`, so a literal query answers FALSE for the parameterized case the acceptance calls out. The op matches the HEAD SORT of the fact's T binding instead, so a parameterized instance answers as its base does.\n\n(2) 'Add to reflect.anthill alongside the existing introspection ops' understated it: a bare SORT name had NO value denotation at all. Only a free-standing ENTITY could be passed to a `Type` param (facts_of(kb(), WorkItem)); a sort name was an UnresolvedName error, so is_modifiable(Cell) did not type-check. Delivery therefore includes the sort-as-Type reading: typer check_bare_ref + eval reduce_var, gated on the slot expecting a `Type` (new type_slot_arg_hint), so a sort name in an ordinary value slot stays a loud error (test pins it).\n\nLANDMINE for anyone touching this: do NOT reuse region::region_sorts as the 'is modifiable' test, even though it reads the same facts. It over-approximates ON PURPOSE (every sort reachable anywhere in the fact head), because effect-masking must keep a Modify when the result type merely MENTIONS a region-bearing sort. As an exact predicate it INVERTS the answer: under a parameterized `Modifiable[T = Box[V = Int64]]` it collects Int64 (a subterm) but not Box (a Fn's functor is excluded from subterms()) -- so is_modifiable(Box)=false, is_modifiable(Int64)=true. Found in /code-review, fixed by reading the T binding via extract_type_param; regression test wi206 pins it (verified it fails against the old reader).", at: "2026-07-12T22:16:06Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-206", description: some(value: "Reflect operation: is_modifiable(t: Type) -> Bool. Add to stdlib/anthill/reflect/reflect.anthill alongside the existing introspection ops (sort_template, occurrences_of, etc.). Body: queries the KB for fact Modifiable[T = t] (the marker introduced by WI-205); returns true iff such a fact exists. Use cases: (a) defensive programming — a function that takes any type and only mutates if its modifiable, queries is_modifiable before deciding to call set/get; (b) reflection-driven UIs and dispatchers — listing all modifiable resources at runtime; (c) typer-side helpers in user code that mirror the kernel typer rule (the kernel typer enforcing Modify[T] requires Modifiable[T] is WI-205 internal; user code can introspect via this reflect op). Implementation: a Rust builtin that wraps a KB query for the Modifiable fact functor with the type as the T parameter binding. Acceptance: anthill.reflect.is_modifiable(Cell[Int]) returns true after WI-205 lands; is_modifiable(Int) returns false; cargo test green. Independent of WI-205 implementation order — could land before WI-205 (returning false for everything until Modifiable facts are registered) or after."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-205"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-12T22:16:09Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-702", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED (code 44b0d24b). Two parts + one doc. (1) LOUDNESS: the SOUNDNESS half already existed (blanket effect/requires gate in defining_equations); WI-702 makes the effectful-op equation decline LOUD. KEY DESIGN CORRECTION found by /code-review: the shared reducer defining_equations is reached from the TOLERANT bulk sweep (synthesize_body_derived_defrules), so a loud eprintln there double-fires + spams. FIX: defining_equations stays a SILENT primitive; the one production consumer (the goal-closure sweep) emits the loud, per-op-deduped diagnostic (naming op+row via new pub effect_row_blocking_equations) and skips synth. (2) FORMATION HOLE: new load-time check_simp_effectful_ops rejects a [simp]/[unfold] rewrite whose sides mention an effectful op. SCOPE DECISIONS (evidence-forced, not preference): keyed on the EFFECT ROW only, NOT requires -- Set/Map carry sort-level requires Eq[T] into member/insert/get and the stdlib's own simp laws mention them, so a requires-inclusive gate would REJECT THE STDLIB (verified). Effect-general (function-hood), not External-only (design-doc-endorsed + verified safe repo-wide). CODE-REVIEW (high, 3 finder angles) caught a real COVERAGE HOLE: the gate must run AFTER type_rule_bodies so a dot-syntax guard (?x.op()) is dispatched to Apply -- else the un-dispatched DotApply slips the walk (ordering break-verified non-vacuous). Reused walk_calls_node. LESSON: verify each gate by BREAKING it (part-1 gate, part-2 gate, and the dispatch ORDERING each reddens a distinct test). Full suite 2772/0.", at: "2026-07-12T22:50:54Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-702", description: some(value: "WI-698 child D (proposal 054, section Consumers that must decline it -- loudly): the purity-consumer gates for External. The SOUNDNESS half ALREADY EXISTS and is BLANKET: the defining-equation family routes through a shared purity gate (rustland/anthill-core/src/kb/body_specialize.rs:192 -- an effectful body is not an equation; it declines ANY op with a non-empty effect row or `requires`), with twins on the SLD unfold path (folded_call_match, body_specialize.rs:80) and on the relational-view grant (bare_bodied_bool_relation, kb/resolve.rs:5763). An External-rowed op is therefore declined FOR FREE, and the gate predicate is already the principled one -- function-hood, not the absence of one named effect: a Modify-carrying body is not an equation either, and the blanket check knows it. So this WI changes LOUDNESS plus ONE FORMATION HOLE, not soundness. (1) LOUDNESS: the existing declines are a silent `return None`. That is correct on BULK ENUMERATION paths, but where a consumer SPECIFICALLY REQUESTS equations for an op -- a proof tactic citing them; the request sites are op_defining_equations (body_specialize.rs:974), synthesize_op_defining_rule (:1003) and synthesize_op_defining_rule_at (:1129) -- an effectful, and notably External, row must produce a LOUD DIAGNOSTIC naming the offending op and its row, not a quiet absence. Repo principle: prefer a loud error over a silent skip. (2) THE FORMATION HOLE: the simp rewriter checks no effect rows at the FIRING site (fire_simp, kb/typing.rs:4289; fire_simp_equation, kb/resolve.rs:2902) -- sound today only because effectful ops never BECOME simp equations, formation being gated as above. The one real hole is a USER-WRITTEN `[simp]`-tagged equation rule whose sides mention an External-rowed operation symbol: firing it would duplicate, reorder, or drop the call by rewriting. That gate belongs at LOAD-TIME tag validation (kb/load.rs), NOT at the firing site. (3) Record the rule where it will be found: any future memoization or CSE joins the same decline list. MAY SPLIT: the load-time simp-tag check lives in a different subsystem from the request-site loudness, so if (2) turns out gnarly, split it out rather than growing this item."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-699"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-12T22:50:55Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-698", description: some(value: "substrate: generic External effect -- ONE language-level effect marking dependence on state outside the tracked heap (forge/network, entropy, out-of-store filesystem): non-deterministic across calls, non-replayable, results never equational. Motivation (WI-437 design doc rustland/anthill-todo/docs/design/backend-github-coordination.md section 8.3): the Mirror carrier ops need coverage Modify/Error cannot express; the load-bearing case is a READ -- recent_issues(m) mutates nothing in-process, so today's convention types it Error-only (like WorkItemStore.lookup), but Error-only is honest for KB reads (KB changes only via tracked Modify) and dishonest for a forge read (another writer changes the registry between two calls with NO tracked mutation in between). Semantics: External revokes the pure-code licenses -- no re-run, no reorder, no dedup/CSE, no drop, no equational use; any non-empty row already pins sequencing, External adds precisely: two calls may disagree without any tracked Modify between them. Deliberately GENERIC, one effect not per-capability (no Mirror/Http/Db effects): the distinctions between external capabilities are AUTHORITY, which lives in carriers (the value you must hold; IndexedFileStore precedent) and later in a capability system if we build one -- so the row vocabulary stays stable as backends multiply. Scope: (a) declare the effect + row surface {External}; (b) typer threads it as an ordinary row member; (c) guard integration: fire_simp, op_defining_equations, synthesize_op_defining_rule(_at) DECLINE ops whose row carries External -- loud decline, not silent skip; (d) short proposal in docs/proposals/ recording the semantics + the effects-vs-capability split + why Console/Clock are left as-is for now (proto-External markers; folding/aliasing is a later decision); (e) persistence stays absorbed under Modify[s] (works today; honest External migration optional, later). First consumers: WI-437 increment 3 -- Mirror carrier ops typed {External, Error}, alloc_id/commit spec rows {Modify[s], External, Error}, fresh_token() entropy op. Value beyond the tracker: any future host binding (http, db, external tools) reuses the same effect."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-699", "WI-700", "WI-701", "WI-702"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-13T05:37:49Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-703", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED (code 0768850d). Chose FIX option 1 -- emit the anchor by its CANONICAL qualified name at the PRODUCER (convert_effects_sort_item builds a 3-segment anthill.prelude.EffectsRuntime Name, not a bare one), per the 'fix at producer, key by canonical' direction. Import-independent: a qualified ref resolves straight to the pre-registered prelude symbol, so all the symbol-identity/qualified-name EffectsRuntime exemptions fire and the bare name no longer lands unresolved + misreported as does-not-provide. Per user direction, codegen (rust.rs) recognizes the anchor by its FULL canonical name (new type_expr_qualified_name), NOT the short-name != EffectsRuntime compare (which would also wrongly drop a user sort merely NAMED EffectsRuntime). CRITICAL: /code-review (5 finder angles) caught a real WI-422-class REGRESSION the naive fix introduced -- once the anchor RESOLVES, scan_items_pass2 wired anthill.prelude (via the anchor's enclosing scope) as a resolution PARENT of EVERY effects E=? sort, so a user 'sort Option' referenced inside an effects-bearing sort collided with anthill.prelude.Option => ambiguous-symbol load error (the bare name previously dodged this by FAILING to resolve). Fix = the same EffectsRuntime scope-parent exemption every other subsystem has (load.rs RequiresDecl arm skips the anchor). LESSON: a make-it-resolve fix can trigger a side effect (scope-parent wiring) that accidental-non-resolution masked; break-verified each mechanism (2 regression tests, each RED without its fix: does-not-provide no-import + scope-parent ambiguity). Flipped the wi698 smoke's 3 workaround imports. Full suite 2774/0. FOLLOW-UPS (noted, NOT WI-703 scope; ticket named only the wi698 file): ~12 other test files still carry now-INERT EffectsRuntime workaround imports (wi475/481/599/413/608/459/609/606/411/392/365/590); a shared pub const for the anthill.prelude.EffectsRuntime literal would centralize ~11 sites; scaland AnthillParser effectsSortItem doc-comment cross-refs the old short-name desugar (stale, no behavioral divergence -- scaland omits the anchor).", at: "2026-07-13T06:27:31Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-703", description: some(value: "Diagnostic wart in the WI-320 effect-row-param anchor (surfaced by the WI-698 smoke, 2026-07-12; INDEPENDENT of External, hence deliberately NOT a WI-698 umbrella dependency). A user-namespace `effects X = ?` declaration REQUIRES `import anthill.prelude.EffectsRuntime`; without it the load fails with a confusing `does not provide EffectsRuntime` instead of naming the real cause. MECHANISM: the `effects X = ?` desugar emits `requires EffectsRuntime[Effects = X]`, and the provider-requires check (WI-343) exempts that anchor BY SYMBOL IDENTITY (rustland/anthill-core/src/kb/typing.rs, around line 10836). Without the import the user-namespace occurrence is a DIFFERENT Symbol, the exemption misses, and the check reports the anchor as an unsatisfied requirement of the user sort. FIX -- pick at implementation time: resolve the anchor by canonical NAME rather than by imported-symbol identity; or auto-import the anchor at the desugar site; or, as a minimum, emit a targeted diagnostic naming the missing import as the cause. WORKAROUND today: import it explicitly -- rustland/anthill-core/tests/include/wi698_row_param_refinement_test.rs carries the import, with a comment, for exactly this reason."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-13T06:27:31Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-707", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED (code 2c4b5fc2). All acceptance forms load and evaluate from source: is_modifiable(Cell[V = Int64]) = true (the ORIGINAL WI-206 acceptance line, now reachable from source), is_modifiable(Cell[Int64]) positional = true, is_modifiable(List[T = Int64]) = false, nested Map[K = String, V = Cell[V = Int64]] loads (proving the Type hint recurses), facts_of(kb(), List[T = Int64]) loads. Also closed the constructor asymmetry: a Type-declared entity FIELD takes a sort too (ResourceRow(t: Cell)).\n\nBuilt as sketched: typer hints each arg of a sort-headed apply as Type + types the node as Type (ahead of [simp] firing and the spec-op redirect -- a type application is neither a redex nor a receiver); eval adds a SortTypeArgs frame that pumps args like constructor fields, then assembles.\n\nLANDMINE #1 (caught in /code-review, would have shipped): do NOT hand-roll the type term as Term::Fn in eval. Use kb.make_parameterized_type -- the SAME canonical builder the loader lowers a written type with -- or an evaluated type and a written one hash-cons to DIFFERENT TermIds (distinct types to unification and the discrim index). Hand-rolling diverges three ways, ALL invisible to consumers that read only the head sort (which is all of them today, so the tests passed anyway): (a) named-arg ORDER -- make_entity_term canonicalizes via canonicalize_record_named_args (declared-field order / symbol index), NOT by spelling; (b) EMPTY bindings must map to Ref(S), never a degenerate no-arg Fn{S}, which type_head classifies as Error, losing the base sort; (c) POSITIONALS must bind the sort's DECLARED type params in order (the loader's rule), so Cell[Int64] IS Cell[V = Int64] -- kept positional they are structurally different terms and the binding is invisible to extract_type_param. The wi707 test now pins TermId identity against the canonical builder; it FAILS against the hand-rolled version.\n\nLANDMINE #2: both declared-type lookups that FEED the hint were shape-gated and missed a bare sort -- the constructor field lookup on call/tuple shapes, the op-param lookup on Lambda/VarRef shapes. A sort named as a plain Expr::Ref fell through to UnresolvedName. Both now also fire on a sort-naming arg (shared O(1) arg_names_sort).\n\nKNOWN GAP -> WI-708 (filed with reproducer): inside a GENERIC op, Cell[V = T] builds Cell[V = Ref(T)] -- the type param is NOT substituted. The frame's type-arg channel carries T => Int64 but is keyed by the declared-name symbol in OperationInfo.type_params, a DIFFERENT interning from the op-scoped symbol a body ref resolves to, so find_type_arg's identity match always misses. The WI-272 channel is effectively dead for body-side reads. Harmless today only because no consumer reads type ARGUMENTS.\n\nNOT fixed (deliberate): a sort application's type-arg NAMES are not validated against the declared params, so Cell[W = Int64] rides an undeclared param into the term. A WRITTEN type has stray bindings dropped at load, so eval is not merely lenient but divergent -- worth settling for both positions at once rather than half-fixing in eval.", at: "2026-07-13T06:57:51Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-707", description: some(value: "Language surface: a PARAMETERIZED type expression as a value argument -- `is_modifiable(Cell[V = Int64])`, `facts_of(kb(), List[T = Int64])`. Today a `Type`-declared parameter accepts only a BARE reference: a free-standing entity (`facts_of(kb(), WorkItem)`) or, since WI-206, a bare sort (`is_modifiable(Cell)`). A parameterized type in the same slot fails to load: the parser already produces the right shape (`Cell[V = Int64]` -> `Fn{Cell, V: Ref(Int64)}`), but the loader converts it to an Expr::Apply whose args are typed as VALUE expressions, so the inner `Int64` dies as UnresolvedName. This gap is shared by EVERY `Type`-taking reflect op (facts_of / sort_template / operations / fields / rules / assert / is_modifiable), none of which can currently be handed a parameterized type from source. Note none of them would USE the type arguments -- they all key on the head sort -- so this is about the surface not FAILING, not about extra expressive power. Implementation sketch: (a) typer -- in visit_type's Expr::Apply arm, when `expected` is `anthill.prelude.Type` and the functor is a SymbolKind::Sort, push a `Type` hint into every argument (so nested bare sort names read as types via check_bare_ref's WI-206 arm), and in build_type's Apply frame return a `Type` result for a sort-headed apply instead of falling through to operation dispatch; (b) eval -- a sort-headed apply must build the parameterized type TERM from its evaluated args rather than dispatching a call, which needs a new frame variant mirroring `start_constructor` (evaluate args, then assemble), because named args are otherwise bound to callee params and a sort has none. Also worth covering in the same pass: the Expr::Constructor arg chain, so an entity field declared `Type` accepts a bare sort name too (today `ResourceRow(t: Cell)` still fails while the operation-parameter form works -- an asymmetry users hit in the reflection-driven-listing use case). Acceptance: `is_modifiable(Cell[V = Int64])` written in anthill source loads and returns true; `facts_of(kb(), List[T = Int64])` loads; cargo test green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-13T06:57:54Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-708", author: "user", content: "Related: WI-709 (stray/undeclared type-arg names + positional arity are unvalidated, and the written vs evaluated positions disagree). The two overlap: WI-708 makes type ARGUMENTS actually carry their bindings, which is precisely when WI-709's unvalidated/divergent bindings start being observable. If both are taken, do WI-709 first (or together) -- a channel that finally substitutes correctly should not be substituting an undeclared param.", at: "2026-07-13T07:02:51Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-705", description: some(value: "Effect-row self-contradiction at SIGNATURE altitude (WI-700 sibling / altitude follow-up, found by WI-700 /code-review). WI-700 rejects a self-contradictory instantiated CALLBACK-PARAM row (shield[EffP={Outside}](poke) -> {Outside,-Outside}) but only for an eta'd OP-REF callback arg, inside the per-arg validate_callback_effect_row. Two shapes of the SAME uninhabitable-row bug slip through (both verified LOADING on HEAD af269943): (a) an op's OWN instantiated row -- operation g[E]() -> Int64 effects {E, -Outside} called g[E={Outside}]() loads with an uninhabitable {Outside,-Outside} signature (no callback param, so validate_callback_effect_row never inspects it); (b) a LAMBDA callback with a self-contradictory instantiated declared row -- shield[EffP={Outside}](lambda () -> 5) loads (validate_callback_effect_row bails at the arg_op_sym extraction for non-var-ref args). FIX: hoist the self-contradiction check to a per-call signature validation right after seed_op_type_args (typing.rs), decomposing the op's OWN declared effect row AND each arrow/Function-typed param's row (after subst) via decompose_effect_row_raw + the shared row_self_contradiction predicate -- catching all callback shapes and non-callback rows uniformly, at the right altitude. SUBSUMES WI-700's eta-scoped self-contradiction reject (can then be removed). ACCEPTANCE: g[E={X}] own-row + lambda self-contradiction both reject with a targeted diagnostic; full cargo-test green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-13T07:51:23Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-705", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED (code daa72975). Replaced WI-700's eta-scoped per-arg self-contradiction reject (in validate_callback_effect_row) with ONE per-call check_signature_self_contradiction over the op's OWN effect row + every arrow-typed param row (actual-agnostic). PLACEMENT is the crux and DEVIATES from the ticket's 'right after seed_op_type_args': that point sees only EXPLICIT [E={..}] args, but WI-700's per-arg reject ALSO ran POST-unify and thus caught an INFERENCE-bound row param (multi-arg op where one param infers EffP={X} and a -X-lacks callback param inherits it) -- placing the check only after seed would SILENTLY REGRESS that. So it runs AFTER the arg-unify loops (subst carries full instantiation, explicit+inferred), before validate_callback_effect_row (reverted to the FILTERED decompose, now owns only actual-vs-declared conformance). /code-review (high, 3 finder angles) FLAGGED the regression; the correctness finder THEORIZED it impossible (bind_row_tail lacks-gate), but an EMPIRICAL probe proved it REAL (HEAD rejects f(src,src); ticket-placement loads) -- the lacked label flows via a DIFFERENT param's row (no lacks registered) then the lacks-bearing row inherits it, bypassing the tail gate. LESSON: verify a suspected regression empirically, don't trust the static theory. Gate = any op type param BOUND (explicit or inferred), subst-based (not occ.kind -> no drift with seed). GUARDED false-positive (also /code-review-found + break-verified): guarded(X,g) present must NOT hard-clash with -X (WI-067 discharge may drop it) -- MULTISET-counted so a literal X alongside a guarded X still rejects. FOUR break-verified mechanisms (own-row branch, param branch, post-unify placement, guarded exclusion -- each toggled RED). Full suite 2778/0, then rebased over WI-707 (also touched typing.rs; clean, re-tested 2783/0). KNOWN LIMITATION (documented, NOT a WI-700 regression): return-position + nested/curried inner arrow rows not decomposed. WI-706 (lambda-callback CONFORMANCE) remains -- distinct mechanism.", at: "2026-07-13T07:51:45Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-706", description: some(value: "Lambda-callback effect-row CONFORMANCE (WI-700 sibling / pre-existing hole, found by WI-700 /code-review). The WI-440/469 callback-row conformance check (validate_callback_effect_row) fires ONLY for an eta'd OP-REF callback arg -- it bails at arg_op_sym = extract_var_ref_sym_node(arg_occ)? for any non-var-ref arg, so a LAMBDA callback is unchecked against the declared callback row's lacks/closed constraints. Verified LOADING on HEAD af269943: shield[EffP={}](lambda () -> poke()) (poke rowed {Outside}, shield's callback declared () -> Int64 @ {EffP, -Outside}) loads with the lambda body's {Outside} escaping a -Outside slot into an effects {}-declared op; the eta twin ...(poke) correctly rejects. This is actual-vs-declared conformance for LAMBDAS -- DISTINCT from the self-contradiction shape of WI-705: here the declared row {-Outside} is consistent, but the lambda's INFERRED row {Outside} violates it. For External this is the memoize/CSE/Branch unsoundness proposal 054 section 'Branch and External' forbids, and a -External solver region is naturally an inline lambda. The false doc-comment claiming lambdas are 'checked elsewhere' was already corrected in WI-700 (typing.rs validate_callback_effect_row header). FIX: infer the lambda's effect row and run the same lacks/closed conformance, aligning the lambda's binder places to the declared callback param places. ACCEPTANCE: the lambda escape rejects; the eta/lambda twins agree; full cargo-test green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-13T08:56:19Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-709", description: some(value: "A sort application's type-argument NAMES are not validated against the sort's declared type params -- and the WRITTEN and EVALUATED positions disagree about what to do with a stray one. Repro: `Cell` declares one type param `V`. Written in TYPE position, `c: Cell[W = Int64]` has its stray `W` binding DROPPED at load (see make_parameterized_type's own comment: 'also covers an over-applied non-parametric sort whose stray bindings were dropped at load'). Written in VALUE position (WI-707), `is_modifiable(Cell[W = Int64])` type-checks and eval builds `Fn{Cell, named: [(W, Int64)]}` -- a type term carrying an UNDECLARED parameter. So the two positions do not merely differ in strictness, they build DIFFERENT TERMS for the same written type, which is exactly the hash-consing divergence WI-707 otherwise closed by routing eval through the canonical make_parameterized_type builder. Nothing observes it today only because no consumer reads type ARGUMENTS (every Type-taking reflect op keys on the head sort); it is a live trap for the first one that does (and for WI-708, which will make type args actually carry bindings). Decide ONE rule and apply it to BOTH positions -- do not half-fix it in eval, which is why WI-707 deliberately left it. Options: (a) LOUD (preferred, per CLAUDE.md loud-over-silent): an undeclared type-param name is an error at load, in type position AND in value position -- a typo like Cell[W = Int64] is a bug the user wants to hear about, and the current silent drop hides it; (b) keep dropping, but drop in BOTH positions so the terms agree. Under (a), also check the ARITY of positionals: eval already errors loudly when a positional type arg has no declared param to bind (finish_sort_type), but the typer accepts it -- so the diagnostic arrives at eval instead of load. Scope note: the natural home is the typer's sort-application build arm (kb/typing.rs, the WI-707 arm that returns the Type result), which can validate binding names + positional arity against type_params_of_sort at type-check time, making eval's runtime guard unreachable-by-construction rather than the only check. Acceptance: Cell[W = Int64] behaves identically in a type annotation and in a value argument (both loud, or both dropped); a positional type arg with no declared param to bind is diagnosed at LOAD, not at eval; cargo test green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-13T08:45:47Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-712", description: some(value: "Query capabilities to make the list report a QUERY not a hand-rolled fold: aggregation in value-binding (producer) position. CONTEXT: follow-up to WI-711. Even after WI-711 folds the blocked classification into the rule, list (cmd_list_plain) stays a functional report because one thing cannot be expressed relationally today: the [unblocks N] count (number of transitive dependents), which stays as operations (build_dep_map+count_transitive, main.anthill:946/1084). CORRECTION to an earlier framing -- aggregation is NOT absent: the language already has cardinality quantifiers forall/some/one/lone/no AND aggregate keywords count/sum/min/max (grammar quantifier_keyword/aggregate_keyword; ir.rs:576,592). But they live in CONSTRAINT/boolean-test position: one/lone/no lower to guard queries (one_q/lone_q/no_q, ir.rs:580), enforced via the general guard path (WI-020=discrim optimization, WI-556=first example, both open); forall/some ... in xs ALREADY resolve as rule-body goals (some_in/forall_in, resolve.rs:1437) -- the existential/universal WI-711's classification uses; count/sum/min/max are PARSED-BUT-LOAD-REJECTED -- 'aggregation constraint is not yet enforced (the guard engine cannot evaluate count/sum/min/max)' (load.rs:548). THE GAP (narrow): list needs a COUNT bound to a VALUE for projection into a Row -- the PRODUCER dual of the existing count-in-constraint-position (count(...) op bound, a boolean test). Two ways forward: (a) promote count from unenforced constraint-position to an enforced value-binding rule-body goal, e.g. unblocks(?id,?n) with ?n=|transitive dependents|, dovetailing the open WI-023/020/556 aggregate/cardinality thread rather than being greenfield; (b) sidestep -- if the exact N in [unblocks N] is not load-bearing, degrade to a boolean [unblocks]=(some ?x: depends(?x,?id)), expressible TODAY with the existing bounded-quant, no new capability. With aggregation-as-producer (or (b)), plus the standing fact that ORDER BY/GROUP BY are always post-query folds (true even in SQL -- chrono_topo+status grouping stay a final step), the whole list report collapses to query(item(?id,?status,?desc,?deps,?blocked,?unblocks,?has_fb)) -> order -> group -> render: the 'everything is facts / typing-as-transform' thesis applied to the CLI's own read path. Overlaps WI-370 (custom unification/search at a discrim node) and WI-580 (body-derived relational views on demand). ACCEPTANCE: a design proposal in docs/proposals/ settling (i) aggregation surface in value-binding position (how ?n binds; relation to the WI-023 constraint-position count/sum/min/max), (ii) interaction with NAF + undecided/floundered honesty (WI-519/531), (iii) whether counts are cached/incremental, (iv) migration of cmd_list_plain onto a query, and the (a)-vs-(b) decision for [unblocks N]; NOT a shipped impl (that is a follow-on). Backlog / future direction."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: PreOpened)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-713", description: some(value: "Migrate anthill-todo's reflect-query call sites to proposal 052 Relation[T]; retire the make_fn/execute/Solution-walk boilerplate. CONTEXT: follow-up to WI-711 + proposal 052 (docs/proposals/052-rules-as-stream-valued-operations.md -- relations as first-class Relation[T] values that provide LogicalStream[T]). WI-711 added query_id_set + collect_id_set to anthill-todo/anthill/main.anthill, which query the workflow rules (dep_satisfied) the ONLY way available today: the low-level reflect API -- fresh_var[Term](\"id\") + make_fn(\"anthill.stage0.workflow.dep_satisfied\", cons(id_var,nil())) (a STRING-named goal) + pattern_query + execute(kb(),...) + a hand-written Solution-stream walk (collect_id_set) that pulls the ?id binding by string key via extract_string_field. cmd_next's walk_solutions (querying claimable) is the SAME boilerplate. This is EXACTLY proposal 052's three motivating pain points: (1) you reach the relation by hand-assembling a LogicalQuery, not by naming a rule; (2) the element is a raw untyped Substitution read by string key; (3) there is no typed relation value to fold/compose. GAP: once 052 lands (sort Relation[T] provides LogicalStream[T, E=Error]; a rule cited by name resolves to Relation[T]; schema T = named tuple of free head vars, 1-collapsing for a single free var), dep_satisfied becomes Relation[String] (one free var ?id -> String) usable directly through the inherited Stream API -- so query_id_set collapses to an ordinary stream fold/collect (e.g. dep_satisfied.toList or a set-accumulate over the provided stream), and collect_id_set + extract_string_field + cmd_next's walk_solutions retire entirely. SCOPE: after 052 core is delivered (Relation[T] + provides LogicalStream + the rule-reference resolution arm, per 052 Build path), migrate anthill-todo's query call sites (query_id_set/collect_id_set in main.anthill, and cmd_next/walk_solutions) from the raw reflect API to the Relation stream API and delete the boilerplate helpers; also list these call sites in 052 as concrete motivating consumers. BLOCKED ON: proposal 052 delivery (currently Draft; core rests on landed 026.1 execute + WI-638 named-tuple field access, but the Relation[T] sort + provides edge + rule-reference resolution are not yet built). ACCEPTANCE: query_id_set/collect_id_set/extract_string_field/walk_solutions replaced by Relation[T] + inherited Stream ops; behavior unchanged (list blocked/ready split + next claimable identical, verified against the WI-711 regression test); cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh. Backlog / future direction."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-714"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: PreOpened)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-711", description: some(value: "Unify list's ready/blocked classification onto the KB rule; delete the is_satisfied/any_unmet operation mirror. PROBLEM: two copies of dependency-satisfaction/blocking logic exist and have already drifted. list (cmd_list_plain, main.anthill:1449) classifies rows via hand-rolled OPERATIONS is_satisfied(status)+any_unmet(deps,row_map)+partition_ready/blocked (main.anthill:911-937); the comment at main.anthill:917 admits it 'Mirrors the KB claimable rule'. next (cmd_next, main.anthill:2748) instead uses the KB RULE via execute(kb(),pattern_query(claimable(?id,?desc))) (main.anthill:2756), which leans on all_deps_satisfied/dep_satisfied in rules.anthill. DRIFT: the KB blocked rule gates on status:Open only (rules.anthill:54) whereas the op partition_blocked runs in EVERY status group (print_group_body applies it per group), so a PreOpened/Claimed item with unmet deps gets a '-- blocked --' line the rule would never emit -- two definitions that disagree. NOT rust-vs-anthill: both paths run entirely in the anthill bundle over KB facts (the mirror is anthill operations vs anthill rule); list sources rows via WorkItemStore.all -> facts_of(kb(),WorkItem) (eager whole-relation List[Term], store.anthill:228), next via execute(...) -> lazy Stream[Solution]; Rust supplies only leaf verbs (facts_of, execute, pattern_query, term_*, Map, Cell, println). So this is an anthill-only change: swap facts_of+hand-classify for execute(a blocked/has_unmet_deps goal), no host change. FIX: (1) rules.anthill add a STATUS-AGNOSTIC rule has_unmet_deps(?id):-WorkItem(id:?id,depends_on:?deps),(some ?d in ?deps: not(dep_satisfied(?d))), and redefine blocked as Open+has_unmet_deps; the bounded-quant (some/forall ?d in ?deps: ...) ALREADY resolves as a rule-body goal (some_in/forall_in, resolve.rs:1437), so all_deps_satisfied can also drop its hand-written all_deps_satisfied_rest recursion (rules.anthill:32-36) for a one-line forall-in -- no new capability needed. (2) cmd_list_plain runs has_unmet_deps(?id) ONCE via execute(kb(),pattern_query(...)) -- the single-query-collects-a-set pattern cmd_next already uses (main.anthill:2756-2759) -- collects the blocked id-set, and partition_ready/blocked classify rows by set membership (one SLD query for the whole set, not per-row). (3) delete is_satisfied+any_unmet and inline callers; KEEP the genuinely-functional row pipeline (unblocks aggregate via build_dep_map/count_transitive, has_feedback join via feedback_set, chrono_topo ordering, status grouping) -- those are not relational and stay (see backlog follow-up for turning them into queries too). DECISION: encode status-agnostic has_unmet_deps so every group's blocked partition is rule-derived (matches today's list behavior, removes the rule's Open-only limitation). depends-on-PreOpened=>blocked is already true in both list and next (empirically verified: an Open item depending on a PreOpened item shows under '-- blocked --', is refused by next, and is excluded from --unblocked); this WI must keep that invariant. ACCEPTANCE: one definition of dependency-satisfaction (the rule); list and next agree by construction; regression test pinning depends-on-PreOpened=>blocked in BOTH list and next; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh; /code-review run."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-13T13:19:15Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-710", description: some(value: "A sort application in a RULE BODY bypasses both WI-709 type-argument checks: `convert_term` builds it as a plain `Term::Fn` at load, a THIRD lowering path that reaches neither the loader's `type_expr_to_child` (type position) nor the typer's sort-application arm (value position). Repro (probed while delivering WI-709): `rule bad(?x) :- eq(?x, is_modifiable(Cell[W = Int64]))` LOADS and RESOLVES to a solution, with the undeclared `W` riding into the term — the very divergence WI-709 closed for the other two positions. Nothing observes it today only because `is_modifiable` (like every Type-taking reflect op) keys on the head sort and ignores type ARGUMENTS; it is a live trap for the first consumer that reads them, and for WI-708. Scope note: in a rule body a sort-headed application is not syntactically distinguished from an ordinary data-constructor term, so the check must be keyed on the functor's kind (`kind_of(f) == SymbolKind::Sort`) at the `convert_term` site, and must tolerate LOGIC VARIABLES in argument positions (`List[T = ?x]` is a legitimate rule-body type pattern — reflect rules write them), which the two value/type-position callers never see. The rule itself already exists and is shared: `KnowledgeBase::check_sort_type_args`. Acceptance: the repro above fails at LOAD with the same `InvalidTypeArgument` diagnostic the other two positions raise; a rule-body parameterized type with variable arguments still loads; cargo test green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-13T14:02:07Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-268", description: some(value: "Document entity ↔ term runtime mapping in docs/design/. Five load-bearing rules across the entity-term boundary live only in inline code comments today and bit cmd_X (WI-267) hard during port: (1) Value::Entity → Term::Fn lowering (kb/execute.rs alloc_from_value): recursive field allocation, named_args sorted to declaration order. (2) Term::Fn → Value::Entity materialization (eval/builtins.rs materialize_entity / term_as_entity): walks entity_field_types, finds value in named_args or pos_args by index, defaults missing Option-typed fields to none(), recurses via term_to_value; treats Var-valued Option fields as semantically absent (WI-267 fix). (3) Loader partial-named-arg expansion (kb/load.rs:2752 + :3492): every absent named slot gets fresh_var(field_name) stuffed in so the discrim tree can index uniformly; positional args count as provided (WI-267 fix). (4) Printer 0-arg Fn ↔ Ref round-trip asymmetry (persistence/print.rs): Fn(X, []) prints as bare 'X' (no parens), parser loads back as Term::Ref(X) — pattern matcher now accepts Ref/Ident as 0-arg Fn (WI-267 fix). (5) is_option_type peels Type entity wrapper — load_entity stores field types as parameterized(base: sort_ref(name: Option), ...) not bare Option (WI-267 fix). Acceptance: docs/design/entity-term-mapping.md exists, covers all five rules with examples (WorkItem fact load → materialize → with_X → alloc_from_value → persist round-trip), cross-links to the relevant source files; kernel-language.md §6.3 gets a 'see also' pointer. cargo test green. Driver: WI-267 spent ~half its time rediscovering these rules; future bundle authors should not pay that cost."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-267"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-13T17:48:54Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-268", author: "user", content: "Delivered: docs/design/entity-term-mapping.md — a compact reference for the five Value::Entity<->Term::Fn runtime rules (1 alloc_from_value lowering; 2 materialize_entity/term_to_value; 3 loader partial-named-arg expansion; 4 0-ary Ref canon; 5 is_option_type), plus a value-vs-pattern-position section, a worked WorkItem round-trip, and a file:line source-map table. Documented to HEAD (not frozen at WI-267): includes WI-500/WI-511 evolution. kernel-language.md §6.3 got a concise \"see also\" pointer, not a spec chapter — the mapping is internal-representation with no surface-language effect. Verified by independent source fact-checkers (every file:line citation exact; a few precision nits fixed) and cargo-test green. Rewritten compact per user preference (tables + one-liners over prose). NOTE: this doc's review is what surfaced the WI-716 optional-none-fill soundness bug.", at: "2026-07-13T17:48:55Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-716", description: some(value: "Loader stores an absent OPTIONAL entity field as an unbound fresh var in GROUND FACTS, which is unsound under SLD unification. Root cause (per user): the fresh-var fill in the loader's partial-named-arg expansion (kb/load.rs:6969 fact/rule path + :5314 query path) is the PATTERN representation of an entity — \"specify a few fields, the rest match anything\" — and it has leaked into ENTITY (fact/value) representation. In a ground fact an absent optional field means none(), but it is stored as Var(fresh), so the fact is logically \"forall v. Entity(field: v)\" and unifies with some(?), none(), or anything.\n\nCONFIRMED REPRO (anthill query --resolve): entity Thing(id: String, note: Option[String]); fact Thing(id: \"A\", note: some(\"hello\")); fact Thing(id: \"B\") with note OMITTED; rule has_note(?id, ?n) :- Thing(id: ?id, note: some(?n)). Query has_note(?id, ?n) returns 2 solutions — {A, \"hello\"} (correct) AND {B, ?_} (SPURIOUS, ?n unbound) — instead of 1. B has no note yet matches some(?n).\n\nIMPACT: any rule/query matching an Option field by some/none structure is unsound against a fact that omits that field. Latent in examples/github-todo only because its queried optionals (description, depends_on) are always written explicitly; the omitted optionals (context, generates, requires_capability) are stored as vars but never matched. rules.anthill matches \"description: some(?desc)\" (:39/44/50) and \"depends_on: nil()/cons(...)\" (:20/23) — these misfire the moment an Open item omits the field.\n\nNOTE the existing materialize->none() promotion (materialize_entity, eval/builtins.rs:1454, the WI-267 Var-valued-Option->none() branch) does NOT fix this: it only corrects the value READ path (Term->Value::Entity); SLD unifies the stored TERM, whose optional slot is still a var.\n\nFIX DIRECTION: split the entity VALUE conversion from the entity PATTERN conversion.\n- entity value (fact-assert path, load.rs:6969 when lowering a `fact` head — thread a \"this is a ground fact\" flag, or normalize the fact term at assert time): absent optional field -> none() (detect via kb::typing::is_option_type); absent REQUIRED field -> loud load error, not a silent var.\n- entity pattern (convert_query_term load.rs:5314; rule-body atoms): KEEP var-fill — \"unspecified = matches anything\" is correct for a pattern.\nBoth stay discrim-tree aligned: a none()-valued fact slot still unifies a pattern's var (so Thing(id: ?) finds B), but correctly fails note: some(?n).\n\nOnce facts store none() directly, evaluate whether materialize_entity's WI-267 Var-valued-Option branch can be narrowed (keep it only for residual var-bearing rule/pattern terms).\n\nDOC: documented in docs/design/entity-term-mapping.md (Rule 3 and section 3 fact-vs-query). That doc currently presents the var-fill as intended for both fact and pattern — update it once this lands (and until then it should flag the smell).\n\nACCEPTANCE: the repro query returns exactly 1 solution (only A); an integration regression test asserts an omitted-optional fact does NOT match a some(?) pattern (and a none() pattern DOES match); cargo-test green. Surfaced during WI-268's doc review."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-13T17:49:10Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-716", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. Root fix: the loader's absent-named-field fill now keys on VALUE vs PATTERN position, not \"is a fact\". In a value position an absent OPTIONAL field is filled with none() (value semantics); a pattern keeps the fresh-var fill (\"matches anything\"). Mechanism: a `Loader.in_value_position` flag (renamed from an initial fact-only `in_fact_value`), set by load_fact (fact head) AND load_rule (each rule head), read by the partial-named-arg expansion (kb/load.rs). none() is built as Fn{Option.none} which alloc canonicalizes to Ref(none) (WI-511), identical to a user-written none(). Absent REQUIRED fields still var-fill (unchanged; malformed-fact hardening left out of scope).\n\nSCOPE GREW DURING /code-review — two adversarial reviewers refuted the initial fact-only fix on two counts, both confirmed empirically and now fixed:\n (Concern 1) an entity-DERIVING rule head (`rule Thing(id: ?id) :- src(?id)`) also produces a value, so its omitted optionals must be none() too — the fact-only fix left the same bug reachable through rule heads. Fixed by setting in_value_position for rule heads.\n (Concern 3) a reflect `Term`-typed field holds a QUOTED PATTERN, not a value (e.g. FactHolds(pattern: E(id: ?x))); none()-filling inside it over-restricts the pattern to none()-valued matches — a regression the fact-only fix introduced. Fixed by `convert_arg_value`, which clears in_value_position when recursing into a reflect.Term field. Applied at all three reflect-Term positions: a bare `Term` field, an `Option[Term]` payload (via the named-arg path), and a `List[Term]` element (via the list-element path).\n\nThe value flag propagates through the whole value recursion (so nested value entities like ToolPasses.params get none()) but resets to pattern at every reflect.Term boundary.\n\nTESTS: 4 regression tests in tests/include/wi716_optional_fact_none_fill_test.rs — (1) omitted-optional fact does NOT match some(?) [core]; (2) a pattern omitting the field still matches all [no over-restriction]; (3) entity-deriving rule head stores none() [Concern 1]; (4) reflect Term field keeps a var, not none() [Concern 3]. List[Term] element routing verified empirically (anthill query) + covered by the broad suite. Golden transcript updated: `show`'s acceptance now prints `ToolPasses(tool: \"cargo-test\", params: none)` instead of the leaked loader var `params: ?params` — the bug had been surfacing in user-facing CLI output. is_reflect_term_type made pub(crate).\n\nVERIFIED: full workspace suite green (74 binaries, 2801 passed, 0 failed) across the final change. Empirical anthill-query repros confirm: fact omitted-optional => 1 match (was 2); entity-deriving rule => 0 (was 1); reflect Term / List[Term] patterns keep their var.\n\nNOTES / possible follow-ups (not filed):\n - rules.anthill matches optionals structurally (`depends_on: nil()`/`cons(...)`, `description: some(?)`). Now that an OMITTED optional is none() (not a wildcard var), such a rule won't match a fact that omits the field. Currently latent — all 196 workitems.anthill facts write depends_on and description explicitly. If a future fact omits one, the rule needs a `none()` case (or the fact should write the value).\n - A pre-existing WI-697 order-dependence (a nullary constructor keys as Fn{c} before / Ref(c) after option.anthill registers `none` as a constructor): WI-716 removes the spelling-independence that made omitted-field fills immune. Not triggered under normal alphabetical load (user code loads after the full prelude; no pre-option.anthill facts have Option fields). Robust fix would register some/none as constructors in register_prelude.\n\nDesign doc docs/design/entity-term-mapping.md updated (section 3 + Rule 3) to describe value-position vs pattern-position semantics.", at: "2026-07-13T17:49:11Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-708", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. Root cause (verified live): the WI-272 eval type-arg channel was keyed by op.type_params' BARE symbol (kb.intern(\"T\")) but a body value-ref to T resolves through the op scope to the op-scoped <ns>.<op>.T; find_type_arg's identity match always missed -> Cell[V = T] delivered a dangling Ref(T). Fix: re-key ONLY the eval channel to the op-scoped symbol at the typer write site (new helper op_scoped_type_param_symbol in kb/typing.rs). The WI's 'preferred' re-key of op.type_params ITSELF is WRONG and would break the repro: build_call_type_args (load.rs:8253) interns explicit [T = Int64] call-site labels bare too, and seed_op_type_args matches them against op.type_params by identity -> changing it breaks the explicit-binding path (verified the repro currently loads only because both sides are the bare symbol). GATE: op_scoped_type_param_symbol resolves only when is_type_param(op_scope, short) -- op.type_params also carries mint_bare_spec_carrier '?P's (named after a spec member, NOT scope locals); a bare resolve_in_scope walks ENCLOSING parents and could bind such a name to a visible sibling sort of the same name, keying the channel onto a body-readable symbol and HIJACKING a body read of that real sort (find_type_arg runs ahead of the WI-206 bare-sort arm). This was surfaced by /code-review and A/B-confirmed (un-gated: body read of colliding 'State' returned the inferred carrier 'WIS'). Tests: 3 in wi708_body_type_arg_read_test.rs (repro -> Cell[V=Int64]; per-call distinctness; carrier-collision no-hijack). Full workspace: 2811 passed, 0 failed. frame.rs SortTypeArgs KNOWN-GAP comment updated.", at: "2026-07-13T18:53:11Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-708", description: some(value: "Eval type-arg channel is keyed by the WRONG symbol: a body reference to an operation's type parameter never resolves through it. Repro (anthill-core, needs WI-707): `operation ty[T]() -> Type = Cell[V = T]` + `operation ty_int() -> Type = ty[T = Int64]()` evaluates to `Cell[V = Ref(T)]`, NOT `Cell[V = Int64]` -- the type param is left dangling. Diagnosis: the typer writes `occ.set_resolved_type_args` (typing.rs, WI-272) keyed by the declared-name Symbol carried in `OperationInfo.type_params`; eval installs that verbatim on `Frame.type_args` and `reduce_var` looks it up with `find_type_arg`, which matches by SYMBOL IDENTITY. But the symbol a BODY reference to `T` resolves to is a different interning (the op-scoped `<ns>.<op>.T`) than the one in `type_params` -- observed live: body sym Symbol(2241) qualified `test.ty.T` vs channel key Symbol(2256), both short-named `T`. So the lookup always misses and the reference falls through (today: to WI-206's bare-sort arm, which delivers a bare `Ref(T)`; before WI-206 it would have been an UnresolvedName). Impact: the WI-272 frame type-arg channel is effectively dead for body-side reads -- any feature that wants to read a type param as a VALUE in a body silently gets a dangling ref instead of the binding. Currently harmless ONLY because no consumer reads type ARGUMENTS: every Type-taking reflect op keys on the head sort. It is a live trap for the next one that does. Fix: make the two keyings agree -- either key `type_params` by the op-scoped symbol the loader defines for the param (preferred: symbol identity stays the single source of truth, per the typed-wrapper/by-reference direction of WI-632/WI-352), or resolve body-side type-param refs to the same symbol `type_params` holds. Do NOT paper over it by matching on short name in find_type_arg -- that reintroduces name-based resolution the codebase has been moving away from. Acceptance: the repro above evaluates to `Cell[V = Int64]`; a test pins that a body reference to a type param reads its binding; cargo test green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-13T18:53:19Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-715", author: "user", content: "Fixed per direction (b): pass/fail decided by process COMPLETION + exit status; wall-clock is only a finite backstop against a non-terminating regression, no longer a performance deadline. Backstop set to 20s (user chose 20s; 120s was flagged as too big). Verified green: full workspace suite (74/74 binaries) passed twice; the test itself completed in ~1.5s standalone and 1.56s under full-suite PARALLEL load on this box (~13x headroom under 20s). TRADEOFF ON RECORD: WI's worst-observed loaded time was 10-20s, so 20s has a thinner margin than 120s -- if it flakes again under heavier load, bump the backstop (the comment + panic message tell a maintainer to do exactly that). The finite backstop preserves the original infinite-loop guard: an unbalanced-paren find_fact_block regression never terminates and still trips it.", at: "2026-07-13T22:46:17Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-715", description: some(value: "anthill-todo test `claim_completes_when_description_has_unbalanced_paren` (cmd_claim_unbalanced_paren_test.rs) is LOAD-SENSITIVE and fails spuriously under parallel test execution: it spawns the CLI and panics if it has not exited within a hard 10-SECOND wall-clock budget, but the operation takes ~2.7s on an idle machine -- barely 3.7x headroom. Under `scripts/test.sh` (many test binaries in parallel) the box reaches load average 13-33 and the same run takes 10-20s, so the test fails. Observed while delivering WI-710: it failed in the full suite, passed 3/3 standalone on a quiet machine (2.67/2.73/2.78s), and an interleaved A/B on an idle box showed NO load-time regression from WI-710 (medians 2.61/2.74s vs baseline 2.70/2.77s) -- i.e. the failure is purely scheduling noise, and the wall-clock budget is the defect. The test exists to guard a real past bug (a `find_fact_block` infinite loop on an unbalanced `(` in a description), so the GUARD must stay -- but a wall-clock deadline is the wrong instrument for 'did not hang': it conflates a hang with a busy CI machine, and it will keep producing false red. Fix directions: (a) raise the budget substantially (60s+) so it still catches an infinite loop but not a loaded box; (b) better, assert on COMPLETION rather than elapsed time -- the regression was a non-terminating loop, so any finite exit passes, and a generous timeout is only a backstop against hanging the suite forever. Acceptance: the test cannot fail on a machine that is merely busy; it still fails if find_fact_block regresses to a non-terminating loop; cargo test green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-13T22:46:23Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-714", author: "user", content: "C1 DELIVERED + committed/pushed to main (2dd73fdf). Runtime-materialization foundation of proposal 052 Relation[T]. stdlib: LogicalStream now carries the search access-effect row E (splitFirst threads s.E through effect+tail; fact Stream[T=T,E=E] so the override refines not widens); new sort Relation[T] provides LogicalStream[T,E] (relation.anthill), registered in the anthill-stl embed list. runtime (eval): StreamSource::MaterializedResolver + materialize_solution -- the one place a relation answer materializes: projects a Solution subst onto the relation free vars (canonical resolve_as_value, reified via term_to_value) into a named-tuple row, 1-collapse to the element / 0 to Unit. dispatcher: an omitted effect-row spec param is non-discriminating on the concrete self-receiver path too (keyed on sort_param_is_effect_row), so head/find resolve on a Relation/LogicalStream carrier that binds Stream.E=E. tests: wi714_relation_materialize_test (multi-column, 1-collapse, 0-Unit, empty). Full workspace green 2815; /code-review fixed a parent-frame subst-lookup bug (local-only scan -> resolve_as_value). REMAINING (WI-714 stays OPEN): C2 rule-reference resolution (bare queens, bare-qualified Sort.rule, applied Sort.rule(args) -> Relation value; 3 positions), C3 schema typing (free head params -> named tuple, lub across clauses, thread T+E through the provides edge), then 6 algebra increments join/where/union/negate/project/fix. C2/C3 insertion-point map in memory wi714-c1-relation-foundation. WI-642 already delivered the static-requires check face.", at: "2026-07-14T07:57:01Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-714", author: "user", content: "C2/C3 DELIVERED + committed to main (633b6de6). A bare rule reference is now a Relation[T] value that provides LogicalStream and consumes via the Stream API. C2: Value::Relation{query,columns} (native carrier of the constructor-less abstract sort Relation, the Stream/Map/Cell pattern; opaque) + reduce_var/build_relation_value (open the rule head with fresh vars -> free columns -> pattern_query) + Relation.splitFirst host builtin (execute_logical_query -> C1 MaterializedResolver). C3: check_bare_ref -> Relation[T=schema, E={Error}] (free head params -> named tuple, 1-collapse/0-Unit, column-type lub across clauses aligned by head-SLOT identity SlotKey). Dispatch: the 2-hop Relation->LogicalStream->Stream needed transitive_provider_spec_view_bindings (self-receiver view composition) + collect_provides_candidates transitive-carrier FALLBACK (gated on !carrier_directly_provides so List/Map direct providers unaffected; caught+fixed a wi357 regression). /code-review (2 cloud agents) found 3 edge-case defects, ALL FIXED+TESTED: compound head arg (some(?x)) -> loud load error (was silent 0 solutions); heterogeneous multi-clause -> loud error (was unsound silent-drop); nonlinear head var (twin(?n,?n)) -> dedup to one column. Tests: wi714_relation_reference_test (12). Full workspace green 2823. STAYS OPEN: bare-qualified Sort.rule + applied Sort.rule(args) citation positions (2 of 3 remain; bare-unqualified done) + 6 algebra increments (join/where/union/negate/project/fix). Insertion-point map in memory wi714-relation-value-repr + wi714-c1-relation-foundation.", at: "2026-07-14T10:19:08Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-714", author: "claude", content: "APPLIED position DELIVERED + committed to main (8aff2915). A rule applied to args (queens(board), qualified ns.rule(args)) is a Relation[T] with supplied args BINDING columns (subtracted -> narrows T). CRUX: binding is on the ordered dedup'd COLUMNS (schema), NOT raw head slots -- so a nonlinear head column (twin(?n,?n)) binds as ONE param -> Relation[Unit] (not an echo column), and a ground slot before a free var (rankOne(1,?who)) doesn't block positional binding. typer: check_apply_iter arm (gated !in_rule_body) -> relation_reference_type_applied (dedup column_names + resolve_relation_arg_columns + per-arg type-check via ONE shared subst catching correlated-column contradictions rel(?x,?y):-eq(?x,?y) + free cols resolved through subst; UNCONSTRAINED raw-var column bound to arg type = accept+narrow, not types_compatible which rejects a non-TypeVar var). eval: Expr::Apply kind arm (Goal/Rule) -> start_relation_apply + AwaitState::RelationArgs (mirrors SortTypeArgs) -> build_relation_value splice/freshen. NEW shared rule_head_var_slots(kb,rid) = the ONE head-slot enumeration for BOTH typer+eval (closes divergence; retires typer-continue/eval-Err asymmetry). Tests +11 in wi714_relation_reference_test (24 total). /code-review 8-angle + adversarial verify drove the columns-not-slots redesign (found nonlinear echo + ground-slot + typer/eval divergence + per-arg subst + over-rejection of unconstrained columns). LESSON: a verify agent's git-checkout CLOBBERED my uncommitted TESTS (git-clobber hazard) -> commit promptly. STAYS OPEN: bare-qualified Sort.rule (1 of 3 positions; parses as field_access(Sort,rule), needs CallClass::RelationRef mark) + 6 algebra increments.", at: "2026-07-14T14:26:49Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-714", author: "user", content: "BARE-QUALIFIED position DELIVERED + committed to main (d43eba80 + review hardening bb3d2f8e). ALL 3 citation positions now land (bare-unqualified, applied, bare-qualified). Instead of the planned typer arm + CallClass::RelationRef, the LOADER collapses the field_access Ident-chain to the SAME var_ref(Ref(rule)) the bare-unqualified name lowers to (visit_load op-body-expr path, after try_identifier_dot_field): try_qualified_rule_ref -> field_access_dotted_name -> resolve_qualified_rule_readonly (Goal/Rule kind gate) -> mk_var_ref, reusing check_bare_ref (C3) + reduce_var (C2) -- NO new typer/eval code. Review (4 finder agents) hardening: shared read-only resolve_dotted_by_head probe so the bare + applied Sort.rule halves resolve identically (can't drift) + qualified_visible gate + explicit Ambiguous arm (defensive; rules never internal). NEW head-functor-uniformity guard in relation_columns_across_clauses: a LABEL grouping clauses with DIFFERENT head functors (rule L: foo(?x) / rule L: bar(?y)) is a LOAD error -- was a SILENT typer/eval divergence (typer unioned both functors in the schema, eval ran only rids.first()'s functor, dropping the rest); pre-existing C2 gap reachable via bare-unqualified too. Tests +7 in wi714_relation_reference_test (bare ns-qualified, sort-scoped Queen.find, value-receiver guard, labeled-rule ref, labeled same-functor multiclause, differing-functor reject, cross-sort/cross-file rule-body subgoal). Full workspace green 2842. STAYS OPEN: 6 algebra increments (join/where/union/negate/project/fix) are the ONLY remaining work -- start join->conjunction. SIDE THREAD (not WI-714 scope): recorded associated-(dispatched)-relations design in proposals 052 (Future direction) + 044; LESSON requires=SORT COMPOSITION not name-import -> NO ops/rules asymmetry (both bare-reachable iff required sort is entity-LESS).", at: "2026-07-14T20:17:21Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-721", description: some(value: "Associated (dispatched) relations: per-instance rule clauses as spec members (proposal 052 Future direction + 044). A spec may declare an ASSOCIATED relation -- a rule head with NO clauses (a relational signature, like an abstract operation); each PROVIDER of the spec supplies its own clauses; generic code that 'requires' the spec cites the relation and it dispatches to the DISCHARGED instance's clauses. The relational dual of a spec operation. Ref: docs/proposals/052-rules-as-stream-valued-operations.md 'Future direction -- associated (dispatched) relations' + 044. CORRECTED SCOPE (load-bearing framing -- do not re-derive the old wrong premise): this is NOT a name-visibility feature. 'requires' is SORT COMPOSITION and ALREADY exposes a spec's rules bare, UNIFORM with its operations, subject to the entity-variant-exposure filter (an entity-bearing required sort leaks only its constructors, hiding ops AND rules; an entity-LESS spec leaks everything) -- verified; there is NO ops/rules asymmetry. The genuine gap is PER-INSTANCE DISPATCH of rule clauses; name resolution / loader is UNTOUCHED. TWO IMPLEMENTATION PIECES: (1) PROVIDER-SCOPED CLAUSES -- a provider's 'rule edge ...' associated with ITS provision, not the global rules_by_functor / rules_by_label index every rule lands in today; the spec's own 'edge' is a clause-less signature. (2) REQUIREMENT-DIRECTED CLAUSE SELECTION (the load-bearing engine capability) -- bare 'edge' in a 'requires Graph' clause fires the DISCHARGED Graph instance's clauses, read from the resolver's local-interpretation env (the SLD analog of a frame's requirements, already threaded for rule-body requirements by WI-300 / find_dictionary). This is the clause-level parallel of WI-222 defer-to-requirement (which does exactly this for operation IMPLS). COHERENCE: (a) an associated rule = a clause-less spec head; a spec's ordinary clause-bearing rule stays STATIC (one shared relation, composed in unchanged) -- distinguished by the spec head having clauses or not, NOT a new visibility rule; (b) AMBIGUITY = LOAD ERROR (two required specs declaring the same associated-rule name -> reject at load, qualify to disambiguate; decidable statically at the requires site, so loud -- unlike a plain unqualified rule miss which is a silent 0-solutions); (c) a sort's OWN rule of that name OVERRIDES the associated one (mirror the operation override policy, WI-444 / WI-411). OUT OF SCOPE: any name-resolution / loader visibility change (already works via requires composition); the Relation[T] VALUE surface (that is WI-714, orthogonal -- this feature is SUBGOAL-position dispatch of an associated relation cited in a rule body, not relations-as-values). DEPENDS on delivered machinery it reuses/parallels: WI-300 (rule-body requirement dictionaries / find_dictionary -- the local-interpretation channel it rides) and WI-222 (defer-to-requirement -- the operation-dispatch parallel). Independent of WI-714. ACCEPTANCE (the Graph/edge/reachable example runs PER-INSTANCE): spec Graph declares clause-less 'rule edge(?a, ?b)'; sort SocialNet provides Graph with 'rule edge(?a,?b) :- follows(?a,?b)'; sort RoadMap provides Graph with 'rule edge(?a,?b) :- road(from:?a, to:?b)'; generic 'sort Reach requires Graph' with 'rule reachable(?a,?b) :- edge(?a,?b)' + 'rule reachable(?a,?b) :- edge(?a,?c), reachable(?c,?b)'; then reachable resolved against a SocialNet instance yields follows-transitive-reachability and against a RoadMap instance yields road-transitive-reachability -- DISTINCT per instance (the crux); PLUS cross-spec ambiguity (two required specs both declaring 'edge') is a loud LOAD error; a sort's local 'edge' overrides the associated one; cargo-test green via rustland/scripts/test.sh with tests per piece. Rust-only (loader storage + resolver dispatch; scaland has no operation loading)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-300", "WI-222"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: PreOpened)
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-722", author: "user", content: "DESIGN CORRECTED (043.1 updated): NO [macro] attribute — classify by SIGNATURE (declared result is an occurrence NodeOccurrence/Expr), per 043 section 4.2 argument-domain classification applied to the result. Same op is an ordinary fn at a runtime call site, a macro only AS a [simp] RHS. Drop the (marker) piece; is_macro(op) = returns-occurrence. Also: NO 043 non-goal revision — the (no new macro concept) non-goal is correct and STANDS; macros come organically (an occurrence-returning op in a [simp] RHS), no new concept. Open edge: exclude Option[NodeOccurrence] container so a runtime reflect op (operation_body) is not misread.", at: "2026-07-15T05:21:01Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-722", author: "user", content: "DEPENDENCY RESOLVED (probed): occurrences already carry the typer-stamped inferred_type (node_occurrence.rs:564; typing.rs:5111 set_inferred_type; WI-502 typed-term substrate; carried through rebuilds). 043 design 5.4 (type discarded) is STALE. So NO substrate prerequisite / hidden blocker -> BUILDABLE NOW. Reflect surface to add: occurrence_expr(occ)->Expr (read structure) + occurrence_type(occ)->Option[Type] over inferred_type (read type) + make_occurrence(e: Expr, from: NodeOccurrence)->NodeOccurrence (build; from = a parsed sub-part of an argument, for precise diagnostics). Engine: is_macro(op)=returns-occurrence + fire-hook in simp_rewrite.rs try_fire + scratch-interp eval + RE-TYPE spliced subtree via type_check_node (043 Appendix A). 043.1 fully specified.", at: "2026-07-15T05:53:25Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-717", description: some(value: "Domain rules match Option fields by their some/nil/cons SHAPE but have no `none()` case, so after WI-716 a WorkItem fact that OMITS such an optional (now stored as `none()`, not a wildcard var) matches NO rule and falls through. This is the domain-side consequence of the WI-716 soundness fix.\n\nCONCRETELY (rustland/anthill-todo/anthill/rules.anthill): `depends_on: Option[List[String]]` and `description: Option[Term]` are matched structurally —\n  :25  all_deps_satisfied(?wi) :- WorkItem(id: ?wi, depends_on: nil())\n  :28  all_deps_satisfied(?wi) :- WorkItem(id: ?wi, depends_on: cons(head: ?dep, tail: ?rest)), ...\n  :53/:58/:63/:68  ... WorkItem(..., description: some(?desc))\n(the WI-408 some-coercion wraps the bare nil()/cons() patterns to some(nil())/some(cons(...))). The example examples/github-todo/rules.anthill has the same shapes.\n\nBEFORE WI-716 an omitted optional was a wildcard var that unified ALL of these (unsoundly — exactly the bug WI-716 fixed: an omitted depends_on matched BOTH the nil() and the cons() rule). AFTER WI-716 an omitted optional is `none()`, which unifies NONE of them. EFFECT: a WorkItem that omits `depends_on` is classified as neither unblocked (no nil() match) nor has-deps (no cons() match) — it drops out of claimable/ready/blocked; one that omits `description` vanishes from open_item/claimable/needs_review.\n\nCURRENTLY LATENT: all workitems.anthill facts write depends_on and description explicitly, so nothing omits them today. Surfaces the moment a hand-written or tool-written fact omits one of these optionals.\n\nFIX (decide the domain semantics first — is \"omitted depends_on\" = \"no deps\" or \"unspecified\"?). Most likely: an omitted optional list should read as empty/no-deps, so add a `none()` case beside the nil() case, e.g.:\n  all_deps_satisfied(?wi) :- WorkItem(id: ?wi, depends_on: none())\nand, for description, decide whether an item with no description is still open/claimable (probably yes — match `description: none()` too, or drop the description guard from those rules). Alternatively normalize on the writer side so omitted optionals are persisted with an explicit empty value (`depends_on: []`), but the loud/robust fix is handling `none()` in the rules.\n\nACCEPTANCE: a WorkItem fact that OMITS depends_on is classified as unblocked/claimable (not dropped); same for an omitted description where the domain intends the item to remain listed; an integration/todo test loading such a fact and asserting claimable/ready; cargo-test green. Driver: surfaced by WI-716 (omitted optional now none(), not a wildcard)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-716"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-14T22:43:27Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-717", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED (098beb75). Domain semantics decided as the WI suggested: omitted depends_on = no deps (matches CLI normalize_deps); omitted description = item stays listed. Mechanism: (a) all_deps_satisfied/all_deps_verified gain a 'depends_on: none()' case beside nil() — safe because the WI-408 coercion skips Option.none-headed patterns, so the written none() matches the WI-716 fill exactly; (b) description_view(some(?d),?d)/(none(),none()) helper joined into open_item/claimable/blocked/needs_review — a description-less item surfaces with ?desc = none (deliberately heterogeneous: term when present, none atom when absent; CLI extract_string_field falls back to '' so next prints 'WI-x — '). needs_review added to the EXAMPLE rules (bundle parity) so it has coverage. Tests: 4 wi717 tests in github_todo_test with EXACT sorted-id sets (these also pin the WI-716 cross-file none()-fill: a var-fill would double-match description_view and duplicate solutions) + both description_view cases asserted + CLI next/list test vs bundled rules. Docs: kernel-language.md omission-semantics callout (value=none()/pattern=wildcard/reflect-Term=pattern), building.md walkthrough refreshed. NOTES: (1) verification via 'anthill query -p stdlib -p <proj>' is INERT for this fix — anthill-cli globally sorts files across -p dirs so project files convert before option.anthill registers ctors (WI-697 order-dependence); filed WI-719; test-harness and anthill-todo bundle load stdlib-first and are correct. (2) A hand-written var slot (description: ?) in a store fact still double-matches — pathological input, pre-existing class, accepted. (3) description_view is duplicated in the two intentionally-parallel rule files; hoisting a generic option-view to the prelude is a possible future cleanup.", at: "2026-07-14T22:43:30Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-719", description: some(value: "anthill-cli collect_anthill_files sorts ALL collected files GLOBALLY across -p dirs (rustland/anthill-cli/src/main.rs:389 files.sort()), so a project dir that sorts lexicographically BEFORE the stdlib dir (e.g. 'examples/...' < 'stdlib/...', or any absolute path) has its files CONVERTED before stdlib/anthill/prelude/option.anthill registers nil/none/some as constructors. Consequence (the WI-697 nullary-ctor order-dependence, triggered by a real documented usage): structural Option/List patterns in project rules key as Fn{c} while facts key Ref(c) (or vice versa), so they never match — 'anthill query anthill.stage0.workflow.claimable(?id,?desc) --resolve -p stdlib/anthill -p examples/github-todo' returns NO solutions on main even though github_todo_test (which loads stdlib FIRST, then the example) proves the same query resolves to WI-AUTH-001. Also omitted-optional fields in facts converted pre-option.anthill get VAR fills instead of the WI-716 none() fill (observed empirically: ?desc = ?_ duplicates from a scratch -p dir). This silently breaks the documented query workflow in examples/github-todo/rules.anthill's header comment. FIX DIRECTION: either load stdlib-registered files first (sort per -p dir, preserving the -p argument order, mirroring tests' collect order), or robustly: register some/none/nil/cons as constructors in register_prelude (the WI-697 note's 'robust fix'), making conversion order-independent. ACCEPTANCE: the header-comment query above returns WI-AUTH-001 solutions; a -p dir sorting before stdlib gets WI-716 none() fills; cargo-test green. Surfaced verifying WI-717."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-15T06:01:11Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-722", author: "user", content: "INC 1 DELIVERED (dbad43dc), STAYS OPEN. Mechanism end-to-end: an occurrence->occurrence op in a [simp] RHS is now evaluated at compile time + its returned occurrence spliced. PIECES: (1) typing::is_macro/is_occurrence_type -- signature classifier (params+return NodeOccurrence/Expr, >=1 param so nullary is constructor-like not macro, Option/List container EXCLUDED so operation_body->Option[NodeOcc] isn't a macro). (2) eval::builtins::reflect_make_apply + reflect.anthill decl -- occurrence BUILD builtin make_apply(name,args,from)->NodeOccurrence wrapping synthesized_expr; cons-walk shares new reflect_cons_to_vec helper factored from make_fn. (3) simp_rewrite::try_expand_macro + fire hook in try_fire -- after substitute_to_occurrence builds template, if head is_macro run body via kb.run_in_bridge_interp(call_op_bridged) with Value::Node args, splice result; push_visit(fuel-1) re-types splice FREE. run_in_bridge_interp made pub(super). (4) load::check_macro_purity -- declared effect row <= Error, PER-FACT (spec op + impl are separate OperationInfo facts, WI-701) so impure impl can't hide behind pure spec-op fact. ARCHITECTURE (USER directed 'register reflect builtins on eval side'): call_op_bridged dispatches self.builtins FIRST args UNtouched; the flatten (materialize_value) is ONLY in the resolver value-domain bridge_op_to_eval, NOT the normal op-call -- so macro path keeps Value::Node args (a lambda-body arg would else lower to Bottom). READ side collapses to occurrence_term+sub_occurrences (no Expr-enum decode; ReflectedExpr lens is Const-only, irrelevant); only BUILD side new. Per-shape builder chosen (043.1 sec7), NOT single make_occurrence entity-decoder, NOT make_fn+convert. TESTS wi722_compile_time_macro_test (4): body rewritten to wrapped() at compile time, re-types+evals->105, classifier incl operation_body open-edge, impure macro load-reject. Full workspace green 2862/76 binaries. /code-review high (2 finder agents) -> 5 findings FIXED: purity false-negative (per-fact), make_fn/make_apply cons-walk dedup, per-rewrite pre-gate clone removed, unwrap_or_default purity fallback removed, Internal eval error now loud (mirrors bridge_op_to_eval). INC 2 REMAINING (where/join-ready, gates WI-714): eval-side occurrence READ builtins occurrence_term/sub_occurrences on Value::Node + occurrence_type over inferred_type (node_occurrence.rs:521); then WI-714 where/join client (052).", at: "2026-07-15T07:41:09Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-722", author: "user", content: "INC 2 DELIVERED (97804629) — the occurrence-READ side, completing the macro read/build surface. Three eval-side reflect builtins (eval/builtins.rs, surface A), the value-domain complement of the resolver's occurrence_term/sub_occurrences GOAL handlers: (1) occurrence_term(occ)->Term via shared try_occurrence_to_term (apply->Fn, arg-less dot_apply->term, literal->Const); a child-bearing/binder form (lambda/if/let/match) has no flat term -> Bottom, the documented signal to navigate STRUCTURALLY via sub_occurrences (complements, does NOT replace, the resolver's Const-only ReflectedExpr pattern-match lens). (2) sub_occurrences(occ)->List[NodeOccurrence] via for_each_child, identity-preserving children; list SPINE is the eval-side Value::Entity cons (build_value_list) that make_apply's cons-walk consumes, NOT the resolver's Value::Node occ-cons. (3) occurrence_type(occ)->Option[Type] over inferred_type + reflect.anthill decl. ARCHITECTURE: the two builtin registries are independent (interp.builtins vs kb.builtins); resolver NEVER bridges a BuiltinTag op to eval (HeadCheck::BodiedOpCall gate = builtins.get(f).is_none()), so goal-domain semantics provably unchanged. TEST wi722_read_builtins_test (3): each macro reads its arg via one builtin, rebuilds wrapped(5)->105 (a misbehaving read yields 5). EMPIRICAL WIN: the occurrence_type test confirms a REUSED pattern-var occurrence IS typed at macro-fire time (substitute_to_occurrence reuses the same Rc in place, inferred_type on RefCell preserved) — the exact property where/join needs to read a relation's schema. Full workspace green 2865/77. /code-review high (2 finder agents): NO reachable bug — flagged panic (try_occurrence_to_term->resolve_symbol) is UNREACHABLE (register_if_present fires only once reflect.anthill scanned, interning all sibling meta-constructors), noted as doc precondition; cross-file agent confirmed all SAFE. REMAINING for where/join (WI-714 consumer-driven): additional BUILD shapes as where/join needs them (make_dot_apply?, column-var/guarded/conjunction goal builders) — the READ side is now complete.", at: "2026-07-15T08:24:44Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-722", description: some(value: "Compile-time macros: extend the [simp] rewrite engine to CALL macro operations (proposal 043.1, docs/proposals/043.1-compile-time-macros.md). A macro = an operation marked [macro] whose params/result are occurrences (NodeOccurrence / reflect Expr) that the [simp] engine EVALUATES AT COMPILE TIME when a fired rule RHS calls it -- the user-authored generalization of 043 section 4.2 expression-accepting builtins (min_sort/constant_fold/reflect-Expr builtins). REVISES 043 non-goal (No new macro concept), narrowly: NO kernel/grammar change; a macro is an ordinary op + the existing [simp] firing phase + a marker. MOTIVATION: 043 walls the expression-accepting bridge to ENGINE builtins only and never enumerates them; there is no way for user code to author a compile-time syntax-to-syntax transform. VERIFIED BASELINE (absent today): a user op m(x: NodeOccurrence) -> NodeOccurrence placed in a [simp] RHS is typed as an ORDINARY value-domain call -- the pattern var arrives as its evaluated-value type (Int64), not the occurrence; substitute_to_occurrence builds the RHS by template substitution only, never evaluating the op. MECHANISM (3 pieces): (1) MARKER -- parse/store a [macro] attribute + KnowledgeBase::is_macro(op) [lean: attribute; open alternatives = signature-domain classification per 043 section 4.2, or a macro keyword]. (2) FIRE HOOK -- in kb/simp_rewrite.rs try_fire, after substitute_to_occurrence (which ALREADY reuses each matched pattern-var child occurrence in place, so m(?a,?b) builds apply(m,[occ a, occ b]) with the real occurrences as args), detect a macro-head result and route it through compile-time eval instead of leaving the template. (3) COMPILE-TIME EVAL -- reuse the WI-625 scratch-interpreter precedent (bodied op evaluated at compile time); bind the macro params to the argument occurrences as Value::Node; run the body (which reads args via reflect sub_occurrences/occurrence_term/the Expr entities and builds a result via make_fn/as_term); take the Value::Node result; splice it; continue the bottom-up rewrite fuel-bounded (043 section 4.5) with the Synthesized.from ancestor-loop check. PURITY/TERMINATION: macros run at compile time so they must be pure + terminating (043 discipline extends unchanged); a macro may raise a compile-time Error diagnostic at the redex span; open = load-time reject of an impure effect row. NOT WI-189 quote: a macro CONSUMES existing occurrences (the lambda already IS the quotation), it does not manufacture un-evaluated code, so staging is NOT a prerequisite. SCOPE: occurrence-side (typer call site) only -- a Term goal at the logical-engine site is not a syntax bracket. PREREQUISITE FOR: WI-714 / proposal 052 where-to-guarded + join-to-conjunction row-lambda crossing (where(r, c -> eq(c.x,1)) becomes guarded(r.query, eq(?col_x,1)) via a [macro] guarded_of that reads the lambda body occurrence, maps c.x to the column logic-var, eq to a goal atom). ACCEPTANCE: a minimal [macro] that rewrites a marker occurrence at compile time (the inverse of the verified-absent baseline) passes; the reflect occurrence read+build API is audited/closed for the where/join transform; cargo-test green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. Rust-only (simp engine + typer; scaland has no simp engine)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-15T08:30:26Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-714", author: "claude", content: "BUILD-SIDE NOTE for the where/join algebra increments (from WI-722 delivery): the macro mechanism (WI-722, DELIVERED) gives where/join its compile-time engine, but the occurrence-BUILD surface is currently make_apply ONLY (builds Expr::Apply). where(r,(a)->cond) lowers via a macro guarded_of(r,cond)->NodeOccurrence that BUILDS guarded(query_of(r), <goal>) as a spliced+re-typed occurrence. make_apply covers the apply-shaped nodes (guarded, eq/lt/..., query_of); but the goal's COLUMN LOGIC-VAR (?col_x in eq(?col_x,1)) is a VARIABLE occurrence make_apply cannot build, and fresh_var/make_fn build Term-level values NOT occurrences — so where/join MUST add >=1 new occurrence-build builtin: a variable/column-ref occurrence builder (make_var / column-ref), and likely make_dot_apply if r.query is a dot form. join needs the same for two row binders -> conjunction merging columns. The EXACT set is discoverable only once the row-lambda -> goal design is worked out (the 'HARD symbolic-eval' part): the macro READS the lambda body via WI-722's occurrence_term/sub_occurrences/occurrence_type (DELIVERED) and must MAP each param.field to the relation's column var (schema from occurrence_type(r)) — that mapping is where the new var-occurrence builder is consumed. These builders are WI-714 scope (consumer-driven, per the WI-722 close decision), added as inc work alongside join->conjunction / where->guarded, NOT a separate ticket.", at: "2026-07-15T09:10:51Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-720", description: some(value: "General fix for cross-file nullary-constructor discrim order-dependence: WI-719 pre-registered only the 4 stdlib constructors (some/none/nil/cons), but the SAME WI-697 order-dependence bites any USER-defined nullary constructor whose fact file sorts (lexicographically, under anthill-cli's global collect_anthill_files sort) BEFORE its sort-declaration file — the fact keys the bare ctor Fn{c,0,0} (is_constructor_symbol false at conversion, alloc canon off) while a later-loaded rule keys Ref(c), a silent discrim miss. Deep fix: populate constructor_symbols during scan_definitions pass 1 (all entity-kind child symbols are defined before any body load), so the alloc/discrim Fn{c,[],[]}->Ref(c) canon is order-independent for EVERY constructor, retiring the per-constructor register_prelude special case. Verify: a 2-file project (fact file sorting before the enum-declaration file) resolves a bare-ctor rule; cargo-test green. Surfaced by WI-719 code review."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-15T10:14:21Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-720", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED + pushed (code b51a29fb, rebased onto origin/main after two syncs totaling 16 commits — my WI-719/720 filings were already in origin; the OTHER session renumbered THEIR local WI-719/720 to WI-721/722, so no collision). Fix: scan_definitions pass 1 marks every sort-nested entity as a constructor (KnowledgeBase::mark_constructor_symbol), settling is_constructor_symbol — and the alloc/discrim Fn{c,[],[]}->Ref(c) canon — before any body converts, load-order-independent for EVERY ctor. REPRODUCING SURFACE: a WRITTEN bare 'red' lowers to Ref(red) directly (already order-independent); only Fn-BUILT nullary ctors bit — empty-parens 'red()' or fill/literal paths — which is why WI-719's stdlib-only pre-reg missed user ctors. KEPT WI-719 register_prelude pre-reg as bootstrap guarantee for register_prelude-only setups (no stdlib scan). CONSEQUENCE: constructor identities at load (EntityInfo.name) now canonicalize to WI-511 Ref(c); eval_q3_test read made carrier-agnostic; new wi720_ctor_order_test pins both orders (counterfactual-verified). Full workspace suite 0 failures on the rebased tree (incl. origin's WI-722 macros + WI-714).", at: "2026-07-15T10:14:24Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-714", author: "claude", content: "WHERE/JOIN DESIGN (converged in a design session; implement next). FRAMING: a Relation is a QUERY (intensional, IQueryable), NOT a collection of rows — running it yields rows (extensional); the algebra composes queries->queries and rows appear only on consumption (head/toList). This is LINQ's IQueryable pattern: a query composed with lambdas, translated to a backend query, materialized only on enumerate.\n\nMECHANISM for where(r, c -> eq(c.x,1)) -> guarded(r.query, goal):\n(1) CONDITION = a lambda over a row. The lambda's SOLE job is the BINDER: it makes columns TYPED FIELD ACCESS (c.x resolved against the row schema, WI-638), never free logic vars; it is NEVER applied. REJECT the bare-logic-var / Prolog form eq(?x,1): binding ?x to r's column would be a cross-scope SHORT-NAME comparison (var name vs column name across scopes) = exactly what WI-672 eliminated -> unsound. The binder is load-bearing precisely because it converts a column reference from a name-match into a positional/typed access.\n(2) COMPILE-TIME: parse the lambda's expression tree -> build a carrier-neutral QUERY-TERM RECIPE (a constructor, or equivalently a quoted query-term with column HOLES), parameterized over the columns. tree->query mapping (= LINQ tree->SQL; target = the LogicalQuery ADT 026.1 already in kb/execute.rs): atomic predicate eq/lt/le/ge/... -> goal atom; &&/||/! -> conjunction/disjunction/negation; c.x -> the column variable (by schema POSITION, not name); literal -> term constant. where -> guarded(r.query, goal); join -> conjunction (merge both rows' columns). PARTIAL: only the goal-expressible Bool subset; anything else (an if, a non-predicate call) is a compile error — the analog of LINQ's 'cannot translate to SQL'.\n(3) RUNTIME: 'extract the query term' = instantiate the recipe with the relation's REAL column VarIds (fill the holes). The columns are the SEAM: holes at compile-time, filled at runtime. This is what makes it CODEGEN-SAFE — the recipe is plain code/data in generated Rust/C++, extraction is a plain call, NO lambda reflected on at runtime. PHASE RULE (established): the tree->query translation runs wherever the lambda-tree survives to runtime (interpreter closure.body, C# Expression<>, Scala Expr — LINQ-style); it is FORCED to anthill-compile-time only for a host that drops the tree (plain Rust Fn). anthill targets codegen -> compile-time.\n(4) TERM = carrier-neutral representation (a Value spine: Value::Entity LogicalQuery constructors, VarId columns, Value::Term/Node leaves) — NOT a hash-consed TermId (WI-348 boundary). Same representation build_logical_query_value + rename_query_vars already use for negate/union.\n(5) negate/union are the DEGENERATE case (no lambda -> no recipe; build the query term directly at runtime from operand query VALUES; DELIVERED). where/join ADD the compile-time recipe because they carry a lambda.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION LOCUS (confirm at start of next session): RECOMMEND a COMPILE-TIME transform in the TYPER (Rust), reading the lambda's already-typed NodeOccurrence (c.x resolved against schema) and building the carrier-neutral LogicalQuery Value — uniform with negate/union (Rust builtins). The WI-722 anthill macro is the ALTERNATIVE user-EXTENSIBLE route (relational algebra authored in anthill via reflect) — it carries the alignment + new occurrence-build-builtin cost flagged in the prior WI-714 note and is NOT needed for BUILT-IN where/join. Column alignment is POSITIONAL (schema position -> the relation value's column VarId), never by name.\n\nSEQUENCE: where->guarded (single row) FIRST; then join->conjunction (two row binders, merge column sets). project/fix stay separate (project = distribute-dot r.(f1,f2) -> projected, no lambda; fix = guarded+project sugar by named key). This DESIGN SUPERSEDES the earlier 'build-side needs new occurrence-build builtins' note IF the typer-pass locus is chosen (a Rust pass builds the carrier-neutral Value directly, so no anthill-level make_var/make_dot_apply builtins are needed); those builtins are needed ONLY on the WI-722-macro route.", at: "2026-07-15T12:17:04Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-714", author: "claude", content: "CORRECTION to the where/join design note, piece (1). The reject of the logic-variable where-FORM (where(r, eq(?x,1)) with raw logic vars as the argument) STANDS, but the REASON I gave (WI-672 cross-scope short-name comparison / unsound) was WRONG — RETRACTED. It is NOT a WI-672 issue and NOT a rejection of RULES. Correct framing = 052's DIVISION OF LABOR (two LAYERS, both present): (a) logic-variable relational work is DEFINED as a RULE at the Prolog/metalevel (rule pCell(y) :- cells(x,y), p(x)) — the foundation, never rejected; a rule reference IS a Relation value (WI-714 C2). (b) the FUNCTIONAL API (where/join) OPERATES ON those relation-values, and its condition is a LAMBDA — columns read as TYPED FIELD ACCESS on the row value (c.x, WI-638), so the condition stays in the functional world with no logic variables. The ONLY thing rejected is a raw logic-variable GOAL as the functional where argument: it would smuggle the metalevel (logic vars / rule-scoping) into functional expression syntax, and the clean alternative (write a RULE) already exists — layer-separation, NOT soundness. The lambda's binder is therefore the FUNCTIONAL way to name columns (field access on a row value), not a device to avoid a name-match. Everything else in the design note (compile-time expr-tree -> carrier-neutral query-term recipe over LogicalQuery, runtime column-VarId fill seam, positional alignment, typer-pass locus, sequence) is UNCHANGED.", at: "2026-07-15T12:25:33Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-718", description: some(value: "Register `some`/`none` (and ideally all prelude constructors) as CONSTRUCTORS in register_prelude, so nullary-constructor discrim keying is stable from the start — closing a WI-697 order-dependence that WI-716 newly exposed.\n\nMECHANISM: a nullary constructor keys in the discrim tree as `Functor(c)+Arity(0)` BEFORE its sort registers it as a constructor, and canonicalizes to `Ref(c)` AFTER (the WI-511 alloc flip at kb/mod.rs:1074-1078 and the WI-436 functor_view_head at kb/term_view.rs:94 both gate on `is_constructor_symbol`). `is_constructor_symbol` is set only in `register_entity_of` (kb/mod.rs:2438) during the LOAD phase via `load_sort_with_body` — NOT in `register_prelude`, which defines the `none`/`some` symbols (kb/load.rs:2699/2712) but not the constructor flag. So `none` becomes a \"constructor\" only when option.anthill loads. WI-697 documented this pre/post-registration two-TermId-identity order-dependence.\n\nWHY WI-716 EXPOSES IT: the old omitted-field fresh-var fill was spelling-independent (a var unifies any spelling of `none`), so it was immune to the split. After WI-716 an omitted Option field is a CONCRETE `none()` whose stored key depends on whether `none` is a constructor at assert time. A ground fact with an omitted Option field asserted BEFORE option.anthill registers `none` would key its none-slot as `Functor(none)+Arity(0)` while later `f: none()` queries key `Ref(none)` — a SILENT discrim-tree miss (`resolve.rs` trusts the tree as complete).\n\nCURRENTLY NOT TRIGGERED (latent): files load alphabetically (anthill-cli main.rs `files.sort()`); the only pre-option.anthill facts are spec witnesses with no Option-typed fields (verified cell/effects/console/delay/external/list/logical_stream.anthill — Option appears only in operation RETURN types, never as an omitted entity/spec field); and user code always loads after the full prelude. So no realistic load order triggers it today.\n\nFIX: set `is_constructor_symbol` for `some`/`none` (and, more generally, for every prelude-defined constructor) at register_prelude time rather than only when their defining file loads, so the Fn{c}<->Ref(c) canonicalization is stable from the first assert. This also closes the general WI-697 order-dependence for these constructors independent of WI-716. (Broader: consider making is_constructor_symbol set at scan-pass-1 for ALL sort-body entities, so no fact can ever be asserted with a not-yet-constructor functor.)\n\nACCEPTANCE: a ground fact with an omitted Option field asserted BEFORE option.anthill loads (or under a partial / non-alphabetical load) is found by a `f: none()` query — no silent miss; a regression test exercising the pre-registration assert order; cargo-test green. Driver: adversarial review of WI-716 (reviewer-2 latent hazard); relates to WI-697."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-716"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-15T15:41:04Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-718", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED (test-only; production fix already shipped). WI-718's requested change — mark some/none (and all prelude ctors) as constructors in register_prelude so the Fn{c,[],[]}->Ref(c) alloc/discrim canon is settled before the first assert — was ALREADY delivered under WI-719 (register_prelude_constructor at load.rs:2762-2763 for some/none, :2652-2653 for nil/cons) and generalized to EVERY sort-nested entity at scan pass 1 under WI-720 (mark_constructor_symbol at load.rs:1811-1813). The number split came from a WI-719 collision/renumber. So no production change was needed; the outstanding acceptance item was the regression test, which WI-719 shipped without and neither the WI-720 test (user enum, explicit red()) nor the WI-716 tests (full stdlib, benign order) covered for the prelude Option/none pre-registration order.\n\nAdded tests/include/wi718_prelude_ctor_preregistration_test.rs (commit 15322d6d), 3 tests, registered in wi_tests.rs:\n(1) register_prelude_marks_prelude_constructors — direct invariant: none/some/nil/cons answer is_constructor_symbol right after register_prelude, before any .anthill file loads. VERIFIED DISCRIMINATING: fails when WI-719's register_prelude_constructor calls are reverted (WI-720's scan marking cannot cover a register_prelude-only setup, so this isolates WI-719).\n(2) omitted_optional_fact_before_option_anthill_found_by_none_query — the literal acceptance: a fact omitting an Option field, converted before option.anthill (project source ordered ahead of stdlib), is found by a none() query (asserts exact id B). VERIFIED DISCRIMINATING: reproduces the exact WI-697 silent miss (Got 0) when BOTH WI-719 and WI-720's marking are reverted.\n(3) ..not_matched_by_some_query — WI-716 soundness dual (asserts exact id A).\n\nKEY INSIGHT recorded during impl: a register_prelude-ONLY behavioral test is NON-discriminating — both the fact's none() fill and the none() query build Fn{none} symmetrically, so they match whether or not none is a constructor. The WI-697 bug needs an ASYMMETRY (fact keys Fn early, query keys Ref late), which only arises when the constructor flag flips mid-load. Hence the pairing: a direct-invariant unit test for WI-719 + a front-loaded-order behavioral test for the end-to-end miss.\n\nFull workspace suite green (78 binaries, 2870 passed, 0 failed). Inline /code-review run; its one finding (cardinality-only assertions) fixed by asserting exact matched ids.", at: "2026-07-15T15:41:25Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-565", description: some(value: "Improve the diagnostic for a bare (unqualified) call to a sort MEMBER operation from outside its defining sort. Today guarded(box(...), n) where guarded is a member of sort Box, written at namespace scope, reports the terse 'type mismatch in guarded.apply: expected known operation or arrow-typed variable, got unknown functor' (TypeError::UnknownApplyFunctor in typing.rs). The BEHAVIOR is correct member scoping — a member's bare name is in scope only WITHIN its defining sort (a sibling member CAN bare-call it; verified), and from outside it must be qualified Box.guarded(...) or dot-dispatched receiver.guarded(...) (both verified working). Only the message is poor: it should recognize the unresolved functor IS a known member of some sort and suggest the qualified/dot form, e.g. 'guarded is a member of sort Box; call it as Box.guarded(...) or via receiver.guarded(...)'. Surfaced during WI-557 (a malformed test wrote a bare member call); independent of the precondition/requires machinery (a member with no requires fails identically). Acceptance: a bare out-of-scope member-op call yields a diagnostic naming the owning sort and the qualified/dot remedy; existing UnknownApplyFunctor tests for genuinely-unknown names unchanged."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-15T19:19:53Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-714", author: "user", content: "F1 (WI-723 review, user-confirmed 2026-07-15): a method call on a FULLY-QUALIFIED rule-ref value 'Queen.find.where(lambda ...)' is NOT re-routed to a dot-call and fails loudly (UnresolvedName). This is WI-714 surface (method call on a bare-qualified Sort.rule relation value), NOT a WI-443 limitation (WI-443 = local-identifier-rooted dot calls; its deferred multi-segment case is the local value-CHAIN p.x.m()). Narrow + workaround-able: bare in-scope 'person_row.where(l)' AND 'let r = Queen.find; r.where(l)' both work (WI-723). FIX when wanted: in load.rs try_identifier_dot_call, on single-segment miss split on the LAST dot and try resolve_qualified_rule_readonly(prefix) on the multi-segment prefix.", at: "2026-07-15T19:08:07Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-723", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED (pending commit). ROOT CAUSE was mis-diagnosed: named-tuple schema elimination ALWAYS worked (a let-local receiver 'let pr = person_row; pr.where(l)' typed c:(name,age) correctly all along). The real bug: a bare RULE-reference receiver 'person_row.where(l)' MISLOWERED to a qualified name 'person_row.where' applied to the lambda (never a dot-call), because the WI-443 method-call re-route only fired for local/param heads. So where's arrow param (c: r.T)->Bool never hinted the lambda -> c stayed a fresh var -> c.name = <unresolved receiver>. The ticket's 'scalar resolves, named-tuple does not' was a SYMPTOM of the same mislowering (scalar eq(c,30) survives via unification; named-tuple c.name needs the type up front). FIX (2 edits, general, no where-special-casing): (A) load.rs try_identifier_dot_call: .or_else(resolve_qualified_rule_readonly(head)) so a rule head re-routes to a DotApply on the Relation sort; (B) typing.rs varref_arg_env_type: fall back to arg.inferred_type() so the pre-typed dot-call receiver's schema eliminates the callback projection (a rule ref is not in the value env). Tests wi723_row_lambda_binder_test.rs (4): named-tuple binder types + c.name concrete String + c.age concrete Int64 (negatives) + let-local parity. Full workspace green 2871/0. /code-review high (2 finders+verify): no correctness/soundness bug; watch-items F1 (WI-714 surface, recorded there) + F5 (theoretical false-reject, prevented by env-first+fresh-stamp).", at: "2026-07-15T19:08:23Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-723", description: some(value: "Typer: a Relation named-tuple schema (r.T) does not resolve as a ROW-LAMBDA BINDER type — blocks WI-714 where/join. A row lambda c -> eq(c.x, v) passed to an op whose param projects the receiver schema (where(r, cond: (c: r.T) -> Bool)) needs the binder c typed at the CONCRETE schema so c.x (WI-638 named-tuple field access) type-checks. Diagnosed empirically in the WI-714 where increment: (a) a PREFIX call where(r, lam) does not resolve r.T at all — c stays at the abstract r.T; a DOT call r.where(lam) (receiver resolves the projection, like xs.find(lam)) is required. (b) even via dot-call, r.T resolves for a SCALAR (1-collapse Int64) schema but NOT for a NAMED-TUPLE (multi-column) schema — it stays abstract, so c.x fails: type mismatch in unresolved-receiver.x, no such member (dot dispatch). Acceptance: person_row.where(lambda c -> eq(c.name, x)) with person_row : Relation[(name: String, age: Int64)] type-checks with c : (name, age) and c.name : String. Likely a WI-638 / path-dependent-schema gap in lambda-binder context-typing. Rust typer only."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-15T19:18:52Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-724", author: "user", content: "RESOLVED BY WI-723 (no new impl needed) — root cause was NOT a stdlib provides-overlap (the ticket's guess), verified empirically in an isolated worktree. There is exactly ONE fact PartialEq[T=Int64]; the numeric hierarchy (Eq/Ordered/Numeric requires ... PartialEq) is resolved by the requires-recursion in resolve_inner, NOT collected as extra candidates. The real cause was an ABSTRACT row-lambda binder: pre-WI-723 c typed as an unresolved projection var, and dispatch_values_match/types_lesseq accepts an abstract per-call value against EVERY concrete PartialEq provider (Int64/String/Bool/BigInt/Set/Map/TotalFloat) -> 'multiple impls match'. WI-723 (concrete-schema binder) makes c:Int64 concrete, so eq's per-call value matches only the Int64 provider -> unique. PROOF: at a73d2297 (WI-723 parent) the exact scenario 'ages.where(lambda c -> eq(c,30))' yields the exact ticket error 'PartialEq.eq.dispatch ... multiple impls match (coherence rule)'; on WI-723 (main) it loads clean. Suite-wide sweep: ZERO multi-candidate eq dispatches across 1180+ tests. Acceptance (eq(30,30) and eq(c,30) c:Int64 type-check with a unique impl) now HOLDS. Guarded by new regression tests include/wi724_eq_int64_coherence_test.rs (3 cases: scalar literal, single-column row binder = exact scenario, valid Int64 column) — the case set the WI-723 suite omitted (it tests only String + the mismatching eq(c.age,'thirty')).", at: "2026-07-15T19:47:20Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-724", description: some(value: "Typer/stdlib: eq on Int64 fails impl-coherence in a value context (multiple impls match) — blocks WI-714 where/join over Int64 columns. Diagnosed in the WI-714 where increment: eq(c, 30) with c : Int64 fails to load with: type mismatch in anthill.prelude.PartialEq.eq.dispatch, expected unique impl for per-call bindings, got multiple impls match (coherence rule). Multiple PartialEq[Int64] impls (or overlapping provides via the numeric hierarchy Eq/Ordered/Numeric) match, so eq per-call impl dispatch is ambiguous. Observed for Int64 in the scalar diagnostic; String may be unaffected (unconfirmed — the named-tuple path failed earlier on the r.T binder gap, WI-723). Acceptance: eq(30, 30) and eq(c, 30) (c : Int64) type-check with a unique impl. Determine whether it is a stdlib provides-overlap (fix the numeric eq provisions) or a typer coherence-resolution gap. Rust typer/stdlib."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-15T19:47:37Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-714", author: "claude", content: "WHERE increment — CORRECTED diagnosis (empirically bisected this session with instrumentation; supersedes the earlier where->guarded LOCUS note). The macro route (WI-722 guarded_of [simp]) is the RIGHT path and is NOT fundamentally broken; my prior double-typing / NodeOccurrence-re-type story was WRONG (repeated from a stale handoff note without verifying — the [simp] engine EVALUATES the macro via try_expand_macro, it never type-checks guarded_of(r,lambda)). The real failure is TWO sequential blockers, neither about WI-722: (1) BLOCKER 1 = WI-725 (the [simp] firing guard rejects a self-representing carrier typed at the spec sort itself: sort_provides(Relation, Relation) = false -> DontFire -> the rewrite never fires -> bare where reaches eval as UnknownOperation). This is a GENERAL guard bug, own ticket, WI-714 now depends on WI-725. (2) BLOCKER 2 = INLINE WI-714 work (the row-lambda->goal compiler): compile_operand (eval/builtins.rs, behind relation_guarded_of) matches c.x as Expr::DotApply, but the TYPED lambda lowers c.name (WI-638 named-tuple field access) to Apply{functor: anthill.reflect.field_access, [VarRef(c), Const(String(\"name\"))]} — the field name rides as a String constant. So the compiler must recognize field_access(binder, Const(String(field))) and mint a column hole named by the INTERNED field symbol (not the raw DotApply). Surfaces only after WI-725 (blocker 1 always killed the rewrite first, so compile_condition/compile_operand had never actually run against a real typed lambda). No Rust typer pass; no removal of the guarded_of macro. Locus DECISION SETTLED: macro route (WI-722), per user Socratic review.", at: "2026-07-15T21:36:33Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-725", author: "claude", content: "CORRECTED FIX (the ticket FIX DIRECTION was WRONG, verified empirically). The proposed reflexive-guard change (accept same_sort_canonical(carrier, spec_sort) in simp_guard_holds_core) IS too broad: it fires EVERY self-representing spec law on an abstract carrier, so a value-RHS reducing law get(put(m,k,v),k) <=> some(v) rewrites a Map op body to some(1) which breaks at eval (UnknownOperation some) — the exact WI-611 catch-22 the WI-596 authors deliberately avoided (refine-to-provider, NOT reflexive-fire). With the reflexive fix the full suite went 2 red (map_builtins proposal_acceptance_fixture + wi724 single-column). ACTUAL FIX = distinguish a definitional LOWERING from a conditional typeclass LAW by the RHS: a [simp] rule whose RHS head is a MACRO (is_macro; occurrence->occurrence; e.g. where <=> guarded_of) is a compile-time syntax->syntax lowering — the macro is its own validity check — so it BYPASSES the spec-op carrier guard; a value-RHS law stays gated (WI-611 preserved). LOCUS = kb/simp_rewrite.rs try_fire: new stored_rhs_functor(rid) (sibling of stored_lhs_functor; reads the stored equation RHS head at pos_args[1], no DeBruijn open) + is_macro, checked ONLY when the carrier guard failed (guard_ok/guard_failed memoized; guard stays BEFORE open_equation, WI-655 cost model kept). Sound: the guard is side-effect-free; a macro reads matched occurrences and can decline (return non-Node -> template kept -> loud downstream error). simp_guard_holds_core itself is UNCHANGED (reverted). Test wi725_self_carried_spec_simp_test.rs: a macro-RHS lowering on a constructor-less abstract self-carrier (Widget; sort_provides(Widget,Widget) false) fires (use_flip body rewrites flip(w) -> macro output noted(w)); counterfactual-verified (bypass off -> flip stays dormant -> MissingRequires load error). The value-RHS dual (a Map reducing law still does NOT fire on an abstract carrier) is pinned by map_builtins_test::proposal_acceptance_fixture. Full workspace suite 2885/0. [[verify-before-declaring-blocker]]", at: "2026-07-16T05:35:03Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-714", author: "claude", content: "WHERE INCREMENT DELIVERED (pending commit + code-review) via the WI-722 MACRO route (NOT a Rust typer pass — my earlier typer-pass recommendation rested on a MIS-diagnosis; the macro route works). Two blockers, both resolved: (1) BLOCKER 1 = WI-725 (a macro-RHS [simp] lowering bypasses the spec-op carrier guard) — the ONLY reason where <=> guarded_of never fired (sort_provides(Relation,Relation) false -> guard DontFire -> bare where -> eval UnknownOperation). See WI-725. (2) BLOCKER 2 (WI-714 inline, eval/builtins.rs): (a) compile_operand reads a column ref through the SAME field_access_parts contract as the op-body specializer — post-typing c.x is field_access(c,'x'), an Apply carrying the field as a String const, NOT a raw DotApply; the field interns to the canonical Symbol so where_run hole/column match is exact-symbol (not WI-672 short-name). Made field_access_parts pub(crate). (b) SINGLE-COLUMN (1-collapse): a BARE binder c (eq(c,30) = whole row = sole column) mints a reserved WHOLE_ROW_HOLE that where_run maps to the one column (loud error if schema not single-column). (c) guarded_of STAMPS the spliced where_run(r,cond) arg inferred_type to reflect Term — the Expr::Spliced typer arm reads inferred_type else expected and errored BottomExpr when both absent (synthesized_expr resets it; the constructor supplies it). Tests wi714_where_test.rs (4, all EVAL end-to-end): multi-column keeps/drops + single-column whole-row keeps/drops — proves the column hole fills with the real column var and the guard actually constrains (drops -> empty, not vacuously true). wi724_eq_int64 single-column now loads via the real where path. Full workspace green 2885/0. REMAINING WI-714 algebra: join->conjunction (two row binders, merge columns — same macro route), project (distribute-dot r.(f1,f2)), fix (guard-from-named-key). Locus SETTLED: macro route (WI-722), per user Socratic review. [[verify-before-declaring-blocker]]", at: "2026-07-16T05:35:24Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-725", description: some(value: "The [simp] type-directed firing guard (simp_guard_holds_core in kb/typing.rs, shared by the typer simp_fire_guard_holds and the resolver simp_requires_guard_holds) refuses to fire a <=>/= [simp] rewrite whose spec-op carrier is typed at the SPEC SORT ITSELF — a constructor-less abstract sort used DIRECTLY as its own carrier. VERIFIED failure (WI-714 where increment, empirically bisected with instrumentation): where(r: Relation, cond) is a member of the abstract sort Relation, so lookup_spec_op_dispatch(where) = Relation and the guard classifies it as a spec op; the guard then requires every carrier argument sort to sort_provides the spec sort Relation. The carrier person_row : Relation has sort head Relation, and sort_provides(Relation, Relation) = false (a sort does not provide itself — Relation provides LogicalStream, not Relation). So the guard returns DontFire, try_fire returns None, the where <=> guarded_of [simp] rewrite NEVER fires, and the body-less where op reaches eval as UnknownOperation. ASYMMETRY: the other self-representing containers (Set/Map/List/Stream) have CONCRETE carriers that PROVIDE the abstract spec so the guard passes; Relation is the first spec sort used DIRECTLY as its own carrier (a rule-reference relation value is typed Relation[schema]), so its carrier sort IS the spec sort. FIX DIRECTION: in the self-representing branch of simp_guard_holds_core (typing.rs ~25494, the Some(carrier) if sort_provides(...) arm), also accept self_representing && same_sort_canonical(carrier, spec_sort) — a value typed at the abstract sort directly IS a valid carrier for a law declared on that sort. Empirically verified: with this one-line change the guard fires and the WI-714 where macro (guarded_of) runs to its next stage. Sound: a value whose static sort IS the spec sort trivially carries the spec. Blast radius = the shared guard (typer + resolver), so verify BOTH phases. ACCEPTANCE: cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh; a minimal <=> [simp] rule whose LHS is a member operation of a constructor-less abstract sort, invoked on a value typed directly at that sort, fires the rewrite (regression test); the existing spec-op-law guard behavior is unchanged (a concrete carrier whose sort does NOT provide the spec still DontFires). Blocks WI-714 where/join (proposal 052); surfaced empirically by the where increment. Rust-only (typer + resolver simp guard)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-16T05:56:18Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-670", description: some(value: "Floundered rule-goal counted as a solution in default resolve mode, bypassing hard-failing conjuncts. The open-time whole-body caller-var pre-check (body_builtins_delay_on_caller_vars_nodes, resolve.rs ~5879, feeding the delay-propagation gate at resolve.rs ~2310) delays the ENTIRE rule before its body opens whenever any body builtin's first positional arg resolves to an unbound CALLER var — so earlier hard-failing conjuncts (a 0-candidate fact lookup; a Failure-returning builtin like scope() on _global) never run to refute the branch. The residualization gate (resolve.rs ~756-775) then yields the still-delayed rule-goal as a NON-DEFINITE Solution (residual = [the rule goal]) under the default definite_only:false, so kb.resolve() reports a SPURIOUS solution. Repro (found during WI-630): rule entity_of(?x,?sort) :- nonvar(?x), EntityInfo(name:?x), scope(?x,?sort), kind(?sort,\"Sort\") — querying entity_of(Color,?s) for a top-level sort Color (no EntityInfo(name:Color) fact; scope(Color)=_global fails) returns 1 solution with ?s UNBOUND, instead of 0. Removing the trailing kind(?sort,...) builtin → correct 0, because then no body builtin delays on a caller var and the pre-check lets the body open so EntityInfo/scope refute the branch. WI-630 worked around it by keeping the guard's FIRST arg bound (is_entity_of(?x,?sort), ?x bound → pre-check does not flag it). ADJACENT-BUT-DISTINCT: WI-519 [R] residual-honesty bites in definite_only contexts (guards/quantifiers/NAF), NOT the default kb.resolve path; WI-322/WI-504 are the pre-check UNDER-detecting caller vars (opposite class). This is the OVER-eager whole-rule delay + residual-counted-as-solution in DEFAULT mode. FIX OPTIONS (STOP-AND-ASK, touches core SLD delay/residual semantics): (a) extend the WI-519 residual-honesty backstop to the default kb.resolve path — a rule-goal residual left ONLY by the open-time pre-check must not count as a definite solution (drop or flag it); (b) before flouncing the whole rule at the pre-check, verify the rule's non-delayed conjuncts are individually satisfiable first. Acceptance: the repro returns 0; existing delay-and-rotate legit cases (entity_of enumeration mode, computational rule bodies eq(?v, expr)) stay green; cargo-test green. SCOPE: resolver, Rust-only."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-16T08:09:23Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-670", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED (2026-07-16), commit 43c21e11, full workspace suite green (79 binaries, 0 failed), /code-review xhigh done (no correctness bugs). Chose neither option (a) nor (b) verbatim but their sound refinement: at the open-time whole-rule delay pre-check, before delaying the rule on a caller var, run body_refuted_by_ground_conjunct over the freshly-opened body and DROP the candidate (StepResult::Continue) if any non-builtin conjunct with a concrete functor has ZERO non-equation discrim candidates under the head-match subst. query_view over-approximates + KB fixed during resolution => zero candidates is a hard fact no later var binding can reverse, so this never drops a satisfiable rule (sound under NAF). This is stricter/cheaper than option (b)'s 'individually satisfiable' (only refutes on a hard 0-candidate, never speculatively delays), and unlike option (a) it fixes the bug at the SOURCE rather than filtering residuals downstream (an honest stuck-builtin residual and an honest delayed-but-satisfiable rule residual both still count).\n\nKEY LANDMINES handled: refutation DISABLED for every resolution path query_view can't see — assumed_facts (WI-108) / gamma overlay (WI-537) / [simp] folded into context_opaque at the SearchStream caller (KnowledgeBase has no config); and per-functor route_handler_for (external rows), bare_bodied_bool_relation (routed to eq(f(args),true), WI-580), and scoping/quantifier markers forall_impl/forall_in/some_in/__pop_assumption — each a 0-candidate goal step_init nonetheless resolves, so treating it as refuted would FALSELY refute. Marker names extracted into shared is_scoping_marker_name so step_init's marker dispatch and the refutation skip can't drift. Note context_opaque lives on SearchStream/ResolveConfig, not KnowledgeBase (E0609 during impl).\n\nREGRESSION guard tests/wi670_floundered_rule_test.rs (3): dead rule (refuting conjunct) => 0 solutions; stuck builtin nonvar(?s) alone => 1 residual (kept); honest delayed rule check(?x):-nonvar(?x),is_thing(name:?x) with fact is_thing(name:\"hello\") => 1 residual (kept — the case a naive 'drop any un-opened rule-goal residual' fix, my first (a) attempt, wrongly discarded and which broke delay_propagation_residualizes_when_unresolvable). WI-630's workaround (keep the guard's FIRST arg bound) is no longer required for CORRECTNESS.", at: "2026-07-16T08:10:03Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-714", author: "user", content: "JOIN increment DELIVERED + committed to main (6185dbc2). join(r1, r2, lambda (c,q) -> eq(c.x, q.y)) combines two relations end-to-end via the WI-722 macro route: [simp] join <=> conjoin_of (two-binder macro) splices join_run, which freshens r2's columns, fills the goal recipe holes over the merged column set cols1++cols2', and wraps guarded(conjunction(r1.query, r2.query), <goal>). Merged schema T = Concat[A = r1.T, B = r2.T]: Concat is a bare type CONSTRUCTOR (sort.anthill) REDUCED by an INTERNAL typer op (reduce_concat_in_type/concat_named_tuple_types) at the return-type normalization boundary; NOT a stdlib operation and NOT op-name-keyed (USER-steered). Two-binder plumbing: eliminate_node_projections NamedTuple arm now DESCENDS (was a loud bail) so cond:(c:r1.T,q:r2.T) eliminates nested projections at hint time; relation_ref_arg_type computes a bare rule-ref arg's Relation[T] on demand. join stays a Relation MEMBER (dot form), which required the WI-726 typer fix (same commit). Scope (first increment): a single atomic predicate over two MULTI-column relations with DISJOINT names; collision / 1-collapse / abstract operand / Concat-in-non-arg-position are LOUD errors (deferred). Tests: wi714_join_test.rs (5). /code-review (5 agents): no correctness bug in join; CONFIRMED the WI-726 same_label panic (fixed) + doc/altitude notes (addressed). Full workspace green 2891. REMAINING WI-714 algebra: project (distribute-dot r.(f1,f2)), fix (guard-from-named-key). DEFERRED review follow-ups: computed-relation operands at hint time; Concat abstract-operand NEUTRAL behavior; relation_ref_arg_type->stamp cleanup.", at: "2026-07-16T12:03:17Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-726", description: some(value: "Typer: an operation's explicit type param bound to its parent sort's own type param (Relation[T = L] on a member of sort Relation) is aliased onto the sort's CANONICAL shared T var at load, so the explicit param never binds independently. Two such params (join[L,R](r1: Relation[T=L], r2: Relation[T=R])) collapse onto one canonical Relation.T: r1 binds it to schema-1, r2 to schema-2 -> contradiction (enforce_member_tie) / the type params surface as UnconstrainedTypeParam. This is the parametricity-tie (WI-374) firing where it should NOT: a member op that DECLARES distinct explicit type params for its self-sort refs intends them INDEPENDENT (unlike union(a: Relation, b: Relation) which shares bare Relation and WANTS the tie). DIAGNOSIS (WI-714 join): unify_parameterized_view unifies the T slot fine, but the param's T var was already aliased to canonical Relation.T at load, so bind_resolved targets canonical-T (name 'T'), never the declared L/R; check_unconstrained_type_params then flags L. FIX DIRECTION: when a member op's self-sort-ref param carries an EXPLICIT type-param binding (Relation[T = L]), keep L distinct from the sort's canonical T (honor the explicit param) instead of collapsing it onto the canonical channel; then enforce_member_tie must not tie the two independent params. Blocks WI-714 join as a Relation MEMBER with the dot form (person_row.join(member_row, lambda (c,q) -> eq(c.name, q.who))); a namespace-level (non-member, prefix) join sidesteps it via foreign-sort expansion. Repro: the [L,R] signature in stdlib relation.anthill + wi714_join_test.rs."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-16T12:03:37Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-726", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED + committed (6185dbc2, with WI-714 join). ROOT CAUSE differed from this ticket's original text (a WRONG early hypothesis): the real cause, bisected with per-symbol instrumentation, is unify_parameterized_view matching binding params by raw SYMBOL IDENTITY (p==param), missing the canonical-key vs bare-key split -- a Relation[T=schema] VALUE keys T as anthill.prelude.Relation.T while a Relation[T=L] SIGNATURE keys it bare T -- so the T slot was silently skipped and op type param L stayed UnconstrainedTypeParam. FIX (USER chose the surgical gate over canonicalizing the 4 bare-key loader sites): same-base-gated same_label -- same_sort_canonical(a_base,b_base) ? same_label : identity. A code-review agent EMPIRICALLY CONFIRMED an UNGATED same_label PANICS on cross-base unify (List provides Stream passes the base check, then same_label(Stream.T, List.T) trips its debug_assert; release = silent mis-unify). Reachable here (Relation provides LogicalStream). Mirrors parameterized_compatible_view. Full workspace green 2891.", at: "2026-07-16T12:03:39Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-714", author: "user", content: "PROJECT increment DELIVERED + committed to main (07e15fdb). Relation projection via the distribute-dot r.(f1, f2) (proposal 052 algebra): r.f / r.(f) -> single column (1-collapse to Relation[<col>]); r.(f1, f2) -> multi (rename r.(a: f1, b: f2)). NO macro (project has no lambda): the typer synthesizes project_run(r, spec) DIRECTLY off the dot form + stamps the projected schema; 'projected' is a resolver PASS-THROUGH so the column restriction happens at MATERIALIZATION -- relation_project_run rebuilds the relation's materialized columns to the kept/renamed set, query UNCHANGED, so a dropped column is still SOLVED (bag projection, OQ6). stdlib: body-less project_run(r: Relation, spec: Term). eval: relation_project_run matches source->column by canonical interned symbol (the where_run seam), loud error if a source names no column. typer: a 4th DotApply dispatch mode (single) + a check_tuple_literal_constructor pre-check (multi), sharing projected_relation_type/build_relation_projection; schema assembly shared with the citation-position path via NEW assemble_relation_type; E threaded from receiver (floored to {Error}). SCOPE (as where/join first increments): a bare rule-ref receiver needs a let (the WI-443/F1 dot-access limit shared with where/join); a let-bound computed receiver works, the inline chain r.where(..).(a,b) is a LOUD error. Deferred: multi-column over a computed receiver; dup result keys (surface-guarded at parse, convert.rs:1164). /code-review (4 finders): FIXED shared assemble_relation_type (dedup) + {Error} E-floor + comment accuracy; latent findings (String-round-trip column match, silent computed-receiver misfire) VERIFIED NOT REACHABLE (named-arg-head + let-bound-computed-receiver tested working; inline chain is loud); dup-keys pre-existing+surface-guarded; Project[T,keep]-reducer-vs-stamp is a deferred design note (not a bug -- field_access INC-1b precedent legitimizes stamp+idempotency-handler). Tests: wi714_project_test (10). Full workspace green 1210. REMAINING WI-714 algebra: ONLY fix (guard-from-named-key), the last increment.", at: "2026-07-16T13:58:59Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-727", author: "user", content: "SETTLED SURFACE (converged with user over a Socratic thread; supersedes the OQ #1 open-marker framing in the description): operation fix[R](p: Relation, ...args: R) -> Relation[T = Without[T = p.T, Drop = R]]. [R] = an ORDINARY explicit type parameter (042), INFERRED from the captured args exactly as join[L,R] infers from its operands (NOT a new row-var syntax). ...args = the ONE new grammar token, the rest/capture marker. R = the captured record's type, a first-class handle the return type consumes via Without[Drop=R]. NO inline open-record type (?R); NO effect-set {} for args. KEY: effects FLATTEN ({X,Y}=set union) but argument records DON'T (named+structured) -> capture rides record () not set {}, and schema combine/drop is an EXPLICIT name-aware type constructor (Concat/Without), never {}-algebra; join's own signature already splits E={r1.E,r2.E} (flatten) vs T=Concat[A,B] (explicit). Prior art: Without=TS Omit<T,K> (project=Pick), ...args capture=TS object-rest {...rest}; Scala 3 named tuples have subset pattern match but NO rest-capture / NO open rows -> the internal 045 row precedent carries this. fix[R] dodges WI-726 (R types the args capture, not Relation.T). Proposal 056 §2-3 updated to this. RESOLVED OQs: #1 marker=...args:R + [R]; #2 positional leftovers unify into R as _N (one mechanism covers named+positional).", at: "2026-07-16T19:07:57Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-727", author: "user", content: "GRAMMAR PROBED conflict-free (empirical, this session, tree-sitter 0.25.10): the ...args capture marker is a single FUSED lexer token, added as optional(field('rest', token('...'))) on the param rule -- the exact technique the distribute-dot .( uses (token(seq('.','('))) to diverge at the LEXER from . / .( / A.B and avoid GLR/precedence fights. Results: tree-sitter generate = clean/no conflicts; parsing fix[R](p: Relation, ...args: R) attaches the rest marker to exactly the ...args param (p: Relation untouched), NO ERROR/MISSING; x.field + rel.(name,age) + Ns.Sort.member coexist; FULL 176-test corpus passes. So the grammar side is a one-line param-rule change, already validated -- no design/tokenization risk remains for the ... marker.", at: "2026-07-16T20:18:09Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-727", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED on branch wi-727-variadic-capture (94dbe449 impl + 3594585a review fixes). Full workspace green 2915/0; 11 wi727_fix tests + grammar corpus (177/177). Pieces: (1) grammar ...args fused-token marker on param; (2) parse Param.rest + loader at-most-one/trailing validation + KB side-table op_capture_params (gated on conformance); (3) typer normalize_variadic_capture folds leftover named args into ONE named-tuple record (Expr::Constructor{TupleLiteral}) bound to the capture param, single-pass at the top of check_apply_iter Path 1 — all downstream matching (R inference, coverage, node reorder) runs UNCHANGED; (4) Without[T,Drop] type ctor = dual of Concat, membership+type checks live in the reduction (they give the unconstrained capture its meaning), residual 1-collapses; (5) fix[R](p: Relation, ...args: R) -> Relation[T=Without[T=p.T,Drop=R], E=p.E] + host builtin (guarded eq + column drop, bag semantics). NO op-identity-keyed typer code (code-review altitude agent explicitly PASSED this). /code-review high (4 agents): 1 real finding (capture recorded even when validation failed -> gated) + conventions (silent Err drop -> explicit; None==None Unit test -> zip-guarded) + dedup refactor (reduce_concat/reduce_without -> shared reduce_binary_type_ctor + BinaryTypeCtor config; type_mentions_sort + one combined gate walk; collapse_schema; eval find_column). DEFERRED (proposal 056 s5, no client): positional-leftover capture; the rule-head rest-pattern face (s2.3, optional for fix); polymorphic-column type-check in Without is best-effort. KNOWN EDGE (loud error, not silent): a relation column literally named like the capture param (args) hits the idempotency guard -> Without 'drop operand must be a record' error.", at: "2026-07-17T05:00:40Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-727", description: some(value: "Variadic argument capture (proposal 056, docs/proposals/056-variadic-argument-capture.md): collect the named arguments NOT matched to a declared parameter into a single named-tuple (record) value, so an operation or rule whose arguments are a VARIADIC set of names (not a fixed parameter list) resolves through the ordinary path with NO op-identity-keyed typer code. TWO FACES of one capability: (1) OPERATION parameter -- a trailing capture param whose type is an open/row record (the record analog of an open effect row, 045); ordinary DotApply arg-matching, after binding declared params, collects the leftover named args into it as a named tuple typed field-by-field from the call, reaching the runtime as an ordinary Value::Tuple (no compile-time spec-splicing). (2) RULE-HEAD rest-pattern -- rule f(?r, ?rest..) <=> ... binds the leftover args as a record OCCURRENCE a [simp] macro (043.1) reads (the variadic analog of join's fixed (?r1,?r2,?cond) head). PLUS the Without[T, Drop] type constructor (dual of 052 join's Concat, WI-726): reduces at the return-type normalization boundary, dropping from named-tuple T every field named in record Drop; the membership + constant-type checks (a Drop field naming no T field, a mismatched type) are LOAD errors there, so the capture itself stays unconstrained/generic. DRIVING CLIENT: WI-714 fix -- r.fix(x: 1, z: 2) restricts relation columns to constants and drops them; the keys x/z are DYNAMIC columns of the receiver relation, not declared params, so there is nothing for ordinary arg-matching to bind them against. Without 056, fix needs an op_sym==Relation.fix typer recognizer -- the per-op recognizer the join->Concat 'no op-name-keyed typer code' discipline forbids; with 056, fix is an ordinary operation (capture param + Without in the return type + one host builtin that reads the record and wraps guarded + drops columns), nothing in the typer keyed on fix. AFFECTS: grammar (capture marker on a param / a rule-head rest-pattern), loader+typer (arg-match collects the residue into a named tuple; the Without reduction), docs/kernel-language.md (par.4 parameters, par.8.3 rule heads); runtime: none new (an ordinary named-tuple value). OPEN QUESTIONS: marker spelling (open-record (..) vs a row var (x:T,..R) vs a keyword); named-only vs positional variadic (fix needs NAMED; positional has no client yet -- design symmetric, defer); at most one capture param, trailing. ACCEPTANCE: an operation with a capture parameter resolves a call carrying extra named args, binding them as a named tuple typed from the call; the Without type constructor reduces a relation schema (drop + 1-collapse/Unit, load error on a missing field); WI-714 fix reimplemented as an ordinary op with no op-identity-keyed typer code; cargo-test green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. Rust-only (grammar/loader/typer; scaland has no typer). Draft proposal 056 written this session from the WI-714 fix design thread."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-17T05:00:42Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-714", author: "user", content: "FIX increment DELIVERED via WI-727 (branch wi-727-variadic-capture: 94dbe449 + 3594585a) — the LAST of the 6 relational-algebra increments (negate/union/where/join/project/fix all done). fix is now an ORDINARY operation: 'operation fix[R](p: Relation, ...args: R) -> Relation[T = Without[T = p.T, Drop = R], E = p.E]' in relation.anthill + one host builtin (relation_fix: guarded eq(col,const) per captured field + column drop, column stays SOLVED in the query = bag semantics like project). The dynamic column args ride the WI-727 variadic capture (...args: R -> a named-tuple record, R inferred like join's L/R); the schema narrows via the Without[T,Drop] type constructor (dual of join's Concat, reduced at the same return-type boundary; its membership + constant-type checks are the LOAD errors). NOTHING in the typer is keyed on fix's identity — the op_sym==Relation.fix recognizer the feedback-typer-no-nongeneric-code discipline forbids is GONE. Verified by the /code-review altitude agent. Tests: wi727_fix_test (11) incl. restrict+drop 1-collapse, drop-1-of-3 (2-col residual), two constants, empty-capture identity, named-arg-head, mixed prefix fix(p: rel, age: 30), + unknown-column/type-mismatch load errors. Full workspace green 2915/0.", at: "2026-07-17T05:01:32Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-728", description: some(value: "WI-714 follow-up: enforce negate's MEMBERSHIP-relation operand at LOAD time, not only at runtime. CONTEXT: proposal 052 / WI-714 negate increment (ac5f8a8d). negate(r) builds the negation(query: r.query) LogicalQuery, which the resolver lowers to NAF. NAF over a relation with a FREE column FLOUNDERS (the resolver cannot decide not p(?x) with ?x unbound, resolve.rs:993) -> a silent wrong answer, so the operand must be a MEMBERSHIP relation (0 columns: a bare 0-column rule, or an applied relation binding every column). GAP: that requirement is enforced only by the HOST BUILTIN at runtime (relation_negate rejects a non-empty columns set). The SIGNATURE cannot express it today: 'operation negate(r: Relation) -> Relation[T = Unit, E = r.E]' leaves the operand's T unconstrained because the arg-vs-param check SKIPS a non-ground param (validate_arg_against_param, typing.rs:14913), and pinning E to make the param ground both over-narrows E and trips a param-order canonicalization in types_compatible. So 'negate(person_name)' (a 1-column relation) type-checks and fails only when the stream is pulled. Note this is the single-param case: an op with 2+ params of the SAME parameterized sort gets cross-param consistency FREE via the op-type-params tie (enforce_member_tie) -- which is why union's schema match needs no custom check but negate's T=Unit does. FIX DIRECTION: a dedicated load-time operand-SCHEMA check (the typer knows the operand's Relation[T] schema at the call site -- T=Unit iff 0 columns), raised as a LOAD error naming the offending columns and pointing at the fix (bind the columns via application, or project/exists). Keep it GENERAL per the feedback-typer-no-nongeneric-code discipline -- do NOT key it on op_sym == Relation.negate; express it as a checkable constraint the signature carries (the Without/Concat type-constructor precedent: a type-level predicate the return-type normalization boundary reduces). ACCEPTANCE: negate(person_name) (1 free column) is a LOAD error, not a runtime one; negate(has_alice) (0 columns) still loads and runs; the existing wi714 negate tests stay green; nothing in the typer keyed on negate's identity; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-714"]), status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-731", description: some(value: "WI-714 follow-up: join -- support COLUMN-NAME COLLISION and 1-collapsed/membership operands (the merged-schema cases the first increment rejects). CONTEXT: proposal 052 join increment (6185dbc2). join(r1, r2, cond) wraps guarded(conjunction(r1.query, r2.query), goal); the merged schema T = Concat[A = r1.T, B = r2.T], a bare type CONSTRUCTOR reduced by an internal typer op (reduce_concat_in_type / concat_named_tuple_types) at the return-type normalization boundary. FIRST increment scope: a single atomic predicate over two MULTI-column relations with DISJOINT column names. DEFERRED (today a LOUD error, correctly -- never a silent wrong schema): (a) a SHARED column name across the two rows ('eq(c.x, q.x)' when both have x) -- the merged schema would have to QUALIFY or RENAME the clashing column, and join_run's hole fill (by name, disjoint in this increment) would need the same disambiguation; (b) a 1-COLLAPSED or membership operand -- a 1-collapse DROPPED the column name at the schema boundary, so Concat has no name to merge and it would have to be RECOVERED. FIX DIRECTION: decide the collision surface first (qualify by operand -- 'c.x'/'q.x' -> left.x/right.x -- vs require an explicit rename via project before the join); then teach Concat's reducer + join_run's fill the same rule, sharing ONE name-resolution contract so typer and eval cannot drift (the rule_head_var_slots precedent). For (b), the 1-collapse is done at the typer's schema boundary (OQ5 keeps the stored element as the 1-field record), so the name may be recoverable there rather than reconstructed. ACCEPTANCE: a self-join r.join(r, ...) and a shared-column-name join produce a correct, unambiguous merged schema and run end-to-end; a 1-collapsed operand either works or stays a loud error with a message naming the fix; nothing keyed on join's identity in the typer (Concat stays a general type constructor); existing wi714_join tests green; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-714"]), status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-733", description: some(value: "Stream.head / headOption / tail RESOLVE under SLD but do NOT EVALUATE -- a rule-backed spec op has no interpreter path. CONTEXT: surfaced (again) closing WI-714's consumption story; a general Stream/eval gap, NOT relation-specific. stream.anthill defines the consumption laws equationally -- 'rule head(?s) = fst(?p) :- splitFirst(?s) = some(?p)' (stream.anthill:97-104) -- and stream.anthill:78-80 states the consequence outright: 'the interpreter has no equational-rewrite fallback, so the law rules below alone make isEmpty resolvable but not evaluable'. eval.rs:1624-1633 implements it: a spec op whose ONLY definition is equational rules has no own sort_ops entry, so the inherited entry points back at the body-less spec op (impl_target == target), the 'if impl_target != target' guard at eval.rs:1634 skips it, and the call falls through to a loud UnknownOperation. The one eval->SLD bridge (WI-625, eval.rs:1653-1662) cannot rescue it: eq_bridge_target (eval.rs:1755) returns None unless args.len() == 2, so arity-1 head(s) is out immediately, and its own doc says functional rule-backed ops 'carry no predicate goal to prove and stay a loud UnknownOperation'. CONSEQUENCE: on any Stream carrier that does not OVERRIDE them with a body (a Relation, a LogicalStream), '.head' / '.headOption' / '.tail' type fine and then fail at eval -- the ops that DO run are the body-backed ones (takeN / find / isEmpty / map / filter) and the primitive splitFirst. Ops with a default BODY (takeN, isEmpty) were given one for exactly this reason (the WI-362 pattern: 'the body is the same one splitFirst step'). Every WI-714 test hand-rolls headOption out of splitFirst as a result. FIX DIRECTIONS (pick one): (a) give head/headOption/tail DEFAULT BODIES over splitFirst, the WI-362 pattern already used for takeN/isEmpty/find -- smallest, uniform, keeps the laws as laws; (b) an equational-rewrite fallback in eval -- much larger, and the interpreter deliberately has none. RELATED: WI-567 (split head into a total headOption + a guarded-Error head on concrete List) is a DIFFERENT ticket -- it is about the guarded-Error TYPING of an empty head, and notes 'the eval-side raise of an empty head is a WI-567 follow-on, NOT yet wired'. This ticket is the plain evaluability of the happy path on an abstract carrier. ACCEPTANCE: 'r.headOption' / 'r.head' on a Relation (and on any LogicalStream) EVALUATE, returning the first materialized row; the empty case behaves per the declared type (headOption -> none); 052's NotFound table and relation.anthill's doc comment can drop their 'does not evaluate, use splitFirst' caveats; the wi714 tests can spell .headOption directly; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-735", description: some(value: "WI-714/WI-727 follow-up: fix -- accept NON-LITERAL constant arguments (a const-folded expression / a term-level const binding), beyond the literal Int/Float/Bool/String the first increment allows. CONTEXT: proposal 052 'fix is sugar' + proposal 056 (variadic capture), delivered as WI-727 (94dbe449). 'r.fix(x: 1, z: 2)' restricts columns to constants and DROPS them (= where(eq(x,1)) + project). fix is an ORDINARY operation -- 'operation fix[R](p: Relation, ...args: R) -> Relation[T = Without[T = p.T, Drop = R], E = p.E]' -- whose dynamic column arguments ride the ...args: R variadic capture into a named-tuple record; the host builtin (relation_fix) reads the args record VALUE and wraps guarded(query, eq(col, const)) per captured field, then drops the column (which stays SOLVED in the query = bag semantics, like project). SCOPE of the first increment: LITERAL constants only. GAP: a captured arg that is a const-folded expression or a term-level const (proposal 039 / WI-084, which already carries a LOAD-time purity gate) is rejected even though it is statically a constant. FIX DIRECTION: relax the constant check to accept any value the const/purity machinery already certifies as a compile-time constant -- REUSE WI-084's existing gate rather than adding a second notion of constant-ness; the eq is a GUARDED SEMANTIC eq (PartialEq.eq, WI-616), so any value with a lawful eq works at runtime; the constraint is about compile-time knowability, not the eq. Keep the Without[T, Drop] membership + constant-TYPE checks as the LOAD errors, and keep NOTHING keyed on fix's identity in the typer (the feedback-typer-no-nongeneric-code discipline -- the op_sym == Relation.fix recognizer was explicitly rejected by the user and removed). ACCEPTANCE: 'r.fix(age: someConstOp())' / a term-level const binding restricts + drops correctly end-to-end; a non-constant (a runtime value) stays a LOUD load error naming why; existing wi727_fix tests (11) green; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-714", "WI-727"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Open)
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-714", author: "claude", content: "CONSUMPTION increment DELIVERED + pushed to main (10552e6c) — the LAST acceptance item ('a relation folds/collects as a stream'). THE ACCEPTANCE PROSE WAS THE BUG: it named 'dep_satisfied.toList', and there is NO toList — it never existed in the stdlib on ANY sort, only in prose (WI-714's own description x4, proposal 052:100/:368, a SHIPPED relation.anthill doc comment, and 3 Rust comments). Note the structured acceptance field was only ToolPasses(cargo-test); toList was never a checkable criterion. It CANNOT be added either: a relation is MAYBE-INFINITE (a recursive rule enumerates unboundedly), and the eager drains (collect/size/foldLeft/foldRight) live on FiniteCollection rather than Stream precisely because they walk to the end and diverge on a maybe-infinite carrier (stream.anthill:63-68, WI-589 / proposal library/003 Phase C). 'Providing collect IS the finiteness guarantee' (finite_collection.anthill:27-29) and a relation cannot honor it: Relation's provision closure is exactly {LogicalStream, Stream, Iterable} and stops short of FiniteCollection (FiniteCollection REQUIRES Iterable — a precondition, not a provision; nothing says Iterable provides FiniteCollection). The prose predated the Stream/FiniteCollection split (WI-589) and was never reconciled. USER CONFIRMED: 'takeN is a right step'.\n\nREJECTED ALTERNATIVE (tempting + wrong): 'Relation provides FiniteCollection, justified by the resolver's depth cap (max_depth 100, resolve.rs:317/351), with WI-628 truncation raising into E (already >= {Error})'. The depth cap makes a drain TERMINATE, but 'terminates' is not 'can be fully consumed' — a truncated drain silently returns an INCOMPLETE answer (the silent-skip the repo principles forbid), and papering it with an Error means collect is no longer the finiteness primitive. It would also erode the discipline (the same 'just cap it' argument would put collect back on Stream) and would INHERIT FiniteCollection.map/filter returning FiniteMappedStream — silently DROPPING the Relation face, so r.filter(..) would stop composing as algebra the way r.where(..) does.\n\nTHE SHAPE: bound -> List -> fold. takeN is body-backed over splitFirst (eval-reachable, unlike rule-backed head/headOption) and returns a List, which DOES provide FiniteCollection (list.anthill:168) — so the fold happens where the finiteness is REAL: foldLeft(r.takeN(100000), empty(), add). NOT a concession: 052's own motivating consumer (WI-713's query_id_set) is ALREADY capped at 100000 (main.anthill:2725, self-described as 'a runaway guard'), so this is the SAME semantics it has today with the boilerplate deleted.\n\n- tests: wi714_drain_test (4, all EVAL) — the WI-713 query_id_set pattern end-to-end (1-arg rule cited by name -> Relation[String] -> folds into a Map set), set-dedup vs bag multiplicity (5 rows -> 3 distinct, OQ6), membership, and composition with the algebra (where + distribute-dot still drains+folds).\n- stdlib relation.anthill: the doc comment claimed head/headOption/map/find/toList. Replaced with the ops that ACTUALLY RUN — splitFirst/takeN/find/isEmpty/map/filter, VERIFIED EMPIRICALLY by probe (map -> [bob,alice], filter -> [alice], find -> some(alice)) — plus why there is no collect/toList and how to drain instead. LESSON: I nearly replaced the toList over-claim with an UNVERIFIED map/filter claim (same bug class); the review caught it, the probe settled it.\n- 052 + Rust comments (value.rs, typing.rs x2) corrected.\n- ADJACENT GAP FOUND: head/headOption/tail RESOLVE under SLD but do NOT EVALUATE (rule-backed; eval has no equational-rewrite fallback, eval.rs:1624-1634; the WI-625 eq bridge cannot rescue arity-1). Every WI-714 test hand-rolls headOption from splitFirst as a result. NOT WI-714's — filed as WI-733.\n- /code-review (8 angles): 2 real defects in my own diff, both fixed (a wrong row count in a comment; a citation overshooting its target).\n\nFull workspace green 2919/0. DEFERRED FOLLOW-UPS NOW FILED: WI-728 (negate load-time membership guard), WI-729 (F1: Queen.find.where method call), WI-730 (row-lambda &&/||/! nesting), WI-731 (join collision + 1-collapse operands), WI-732 (project computed receiver + Project[T,keep] ctor), WI-733 (Stream head/headOption eval gap), WI-734 (Concat abstract-operand + relation_ref_arg_type cleanup), WI-735 (fix non-literal constants). ALL 3 citation positions + ALL 6 algebra increments + consumption are DONE — WI-714 is ready to close.", at: "2026-07-17T05:59:29Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-736", author: "claude", content: "HELD (user, 2026-07-17) until the rule-body soundness floor lands. eight-queens — the example proposal 052 keeps citing — needs BOTH WI-738 (its diagonal test is arithmetic: neq(sub(?x,?y), ?d)) and WI-739 (queens(?board) is queried with the answer FREE, the (out) mode that currently flounders). Writing it today would ship an example that 'runs' and prints garbage rather than failing loudly. Depends now on WI-738 + WI-739. NOTE for when this resumes: an arithmetic-free example (ancestor/transitive closure; map colouring) IS expressible today PROVIDED every guard is spelled not(eq(..)) rather than neq(..) — verified: not(eq(?x,?y)) enumerates 6 ground rows where neq(?x,?y) flounders to 1 unbound row. That workaround should DISAPPEAR once WI-739 lands, so do not bake it into the examples.", at: "2026-07-17T06:18:27Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-740", description: some(value: "examples/classic-mini/eight-queens — the N-queens search in anthill, the example proposal 052 has been citing all along. CONTEXT: 052 uses 'queens' / 'Queen.find' as its RUNNING EXAMPLE throughout (052:18, :71, :91-95, :310-327) — 'a relation with only board free is Relation[Board], so queens.head : Board' — and NO implementation exists anywhere in the repo ('queens' occurs only in proposals, design docs and Rust comments). So the canonical illustration of the language's headline feature has never been written or run. Part of the WI-736 classic-mini collection, SPLIT OUT because it is the one classic example BLOCKED on unimplemented engine work, whereas the arithmetic-free examples are not. BLOCKED ON (both verified empirically 2026-07-17): (1) WI-738 — the diagonal test is ARITHMETIC in a rule body ('no queen attacks another' = abs(Qi - Qj) /= abs(i - j), i.e. neq(sub(?x,?y), ?d)), and a builtin nested as an OPERAND is today neither reduced nor delayed but compared STRUCTURALLY, so the guard is silently, unconditionally TRUE — probed: neq(sub(?x,?y),1) admits (2,1). Writing queens today would ship an example that 'runs' and prints garbage rather than failing loudly. (2) WI-739 — queens is queried with the ANSWER FREE ('queens(?board)', the (out) mode), and a rule carrying a comparison guard FLOUNDERS in that mode: probed, 'num(?x), num(?y), neq(?x,?y)' with both columns free yields ONE row with unbound vars instead of 6, while the same rule applied as distinct_pair(1) (mode (in,out)) yields the correct 2. Generate-and-test with the answer free is exactly queens' shape. NOTE the not(eq(..)) workaround (verified to enumerate where neq(..) flounders) covers the COLUMN-distinctness half but NOT the diagonal half, which is irreducibly arithmetic — queens stays blocked on WI-738 regardless. SCOPE when unblocked: the board encoding (a List of column positions, one per row — permutation-style, so column distinctness is structural), the safety rule, the finite domain via facts (fact col(1)..col(8); there is NO between/range in the stdlib and SLD enumerates facts fine), and consumption through the WI-714 Relation face — 'queens' cited BY NAME is Relation[Board] (1-collapse), drained via splitFirst/takeN (NOT head/headOption: rule-backed, do not evaluate — WI-733). Prefer the classic Prolog encoding once the engine supports it, rather than a table-driven arithmetic-as-facts workaround; if a workaround is ever taken, say so loudly in the README. ACCEPTANCE: examples/classic-mini/eight-queens loads and RUNS from anthill source (not a Rust harness), producing correct solutions — 8-queens has exactly 92, and the first is checkable; a test exercises it so it cannot rot (github_todo_test.rs precedent; mind WI-719's load-ORDER hazard — stdlib must be collected BEFORE an examples/ dir sorting lexicographically before it); a README states the problem, the encoding and how to run it; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-736", "WI-738", "WI-739"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Open)
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-736", author: "claude", content: "SCOPE CORRECTION (user, 2026-07-17): eight-queens is SPLIT OUT to its own ticket WI-740, which depends on the unimplemented engine work (WI-738 arithmetic-in-rule-body soundness, WI-739 the (out,out) generate-and-test flounder). The COLLECTION itself is NOT blocked by those — its dependencies on WI-738/WI-739 are removed. Only queens needs them. So classic-mini can proceed with arithmetic-free examples (ancestor/transitive closure; map colouring) whenever it is resumed; it is PreOpened now only because the user chose to do the soundness floor (WI-738) first, not because it is blocked.", at: "2026-07-17T06:20:25Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-738", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED (pending commit). The floor: an unevaluated CALL in OPERAND position now DELAYS instead of being decided by a structural comparison. FIX: new is_unreduced_builtin_call (a builtin Apply that reduce_op_value left unreduced) + operand_is_unevaluated_call = that OR the existing WI-483 is_unreduced_op_call (bodied); the 4 DELAY sites (eq_operands, unify, builtin_cmp, builtin_arith) now ask the combined predicate. is_unreduced_op_call is UNCHANGED and still gates op_call_as_occ, which UNFOLDS a callee body to case-split — a builtin has no body, so widening that would have been wrong; the split of the two predicates is deliberate and commented.\n\nBOTH CARRIERS, and this was VERIFIED not assumed: a rule-body operand arrives Value::Node, but a directly-built term goal (a resolver-level query, or an or/push_choice branch body) arrives Value::Term — mirroring op_call_as_occ's own Term arm. I first added the Term arm speculatively while chasing a failure, then REMOVED it to check: the rule-body tests all still passed, so it looked like dead generality. It is not — a dedicated test (wi738_term_carried_operand_also_delays) shows neq(1, sub(2,1)) is silently PROVED via the Term carrier without it. Leaving it out would have been a carrier ASYMMETRY (Node delays, Term lies) of exactly the kind WI-348/621/685 spent tickets eliminating.\n\nLESSON — my first test suite was measuring through a broken lens. I asserted at EVAL level via isEmpty; the key test failed and I twice 'fixed' the fix. Instrumentation showed the fix was firing all along (uneval(a)=true -> Delay): the delayed goal rotates, flounders, and WI-737 then materializes the residual as a definite row, so isEmpty reads false BEFORE and AFTER for different reasons. The two bugs COMPOSE. Rewrote the tests at RESOLVER level with ResolveConfig.definite_only — the WI-519 decision boundary — which is the only honest lens on the floor's contract (definiteness). Verified the regression bites: with the fix disabled, not_diag(2,1) yields '1 definite solution' (the silent lie).\n\nSCOPE HONESTY: this makes the operand DELAY, it does NOT make it COMPUTE. eq(?z, add(?x,?y)) still does not bind ?z ('=' never binds, kernel-language.md:1535). Evaluation = the separate flattening step (macro: eq(?z, add(?x,?y)) -> the graph form add(?x,?y,?z), which the 3-arg arithmetic builtin ALREADY binds — verified working from surface syntax and pinned by wi738_three_arg_graph_form_still_binds so the floor cannot break the route evaluation will take). Also note the eq DIRECTION (false negative) is NOT regression-guarded: definite_only cannot distinguish 'refuted' from 'undecided', so that test asserts only 'not proved' and passes both before and after. The unsound direction (false positive) IS guarded.\n\nKNOWN RESIDUAL RISK (no test exercises it, flagging rather than hiding): a Value::Term whose head IS a resolver builtin but which is genuine reflect DATA — e.g. a residual GOAL term, since Solution.residual carries goals as List[Term] — will now DELAY rather than compare structurally. The same ambiguity pre-exists in op_call_as_occ's Term arm for bodied ops (it treats such a term as a call), so this is parity, not a new class. Full workspace green 2925/0 including the reflect suites, so nothing currently reachable trips it.\n\nTests: wi738_operand_call_delay_test (6, resolver-level): false-positive not proved; false-negative not decided; Term-carried operand also delays; DATA constructors still compare structurally (the line the fix must not cross — anthill can draw it because operation and entity are distinct declarations, unlike Prolog where 1+2 is legitimately a term); plain comparison on bound vars unaffected; 3-arg graph form still binds. Full workspace green 2925/0.", at: "2026-07-17T06:34:42Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-738", description: some(value: "SOUNDNESS BUG: a BUILTIN call nested as an OPERAND in a rule body is neither reduced nor delayed — it falls through to a STRUCTURAL comparison, so an arithmetic guard is silently, unconditionally TRUE. REPRO (probed): given fact num(v: 1..3), 'rule not_diag(?x,?y) :- num(v:?x), num(v:?y), neq(sub(?x,?y), 1)' admits ALL 9 pairs, INCLUDING (2,1) and (3,2) which the guard must exclude — sub(2,1) = 1, so neq(1,1) is false. The guard does nothing. Dual symptom: 'rule doubled(?x,?y) :- num(v:?x), eq(?y, add(?x,?x))' — doubled(1,2) FAILS although 1+1=2. So the same root cause yields a false POSITIVE in the neq direction and a false NEGATIVE in the eq direction. ROOT CAUSE (traced): (1) reduce_op_value (kb/resolve.rs:5402) returns a builtin call UNREDUCED — its comment says 'A builtin (field_access, arith, eq, ...) is reduced by its own path, not folded. Only a CONCRETE operation (one with a stored body) folds' — but 'its own path' only exists when the builtin is the goal's OWN functor; nested as an operand, sub(?x,?y) is simply left as a term. (2) is_unreduced_op_call (kb/resolve.rs:5698) returns 'self.builtins.get(functor).is_none() && ...' — FALSE for sub, because sub IS a builtin — so eq_operands (kb/resolve.rs:4442) does NOT delay. (3) sem_eq_values (kb/resolve.rs:4061) then reaches the structural verdict: the term sub(2,1) differs structurally from 1, hence neq is TRUE. NOTE this contradicts what wi483_rule_body_method_eval_test.rs:10-14 claims the design does — 'a COMPLEX op-call (body needs the interpreter — arithmetic, match/if/let, recursion) is LEFT uninterpreted, treated as un-ground (it residualizes / delays), NOT a loud error'. It does NOT residualize; it silently decides. Residualizing would be honest (and, with WI-737, would raise); deciding structurally is a wrong answer. MINIMAL FIX (the soundness floor, independent of any evaluation feature): make is_unreduced_op_call recognize BUILTIN calls in operand position so a non-evaluated operand DELAYS instead of being compared structurally. That converts every case above from a silent wrong answer into an honest flounder — which WI-737 then turns into a raise. Making it actually COMPUTE is a separate, additive step (see the functional-flattening ticket). GUARD the fix: an operand that is a genuine DATA constructor (cons/some) must still compare structurally — only OPERATION/builtin calls delay; anthill can tell them apart (unlike Prolog, where 1+2 is legitimately a term). ACCEPTANCE: neq(sub(?x,?y),1) no longer admits (2,1); eq(?y, add(?x,?x)) no longer silently fails; both surface as an honest residual/raise rather than a decision; structural comparison on constructor terms is unaffected; a regression test pins both directions; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-17T06:34:43Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-739", author: "claude", content: "DATUM from WI-737 (probed empirically 2026-07-17, now pinned by wi737_floundered_relation_test): the flounder is a RULE-LEVEL CALLER-VAR pre-check, and the discriminator is whether the guard's vars are CALLER vars — confirming this ticket's 'guard POSITION irrelevant' reading. Evidence, same guard `neq`, three verdicts: (a) distinct_pair(?x,?y) :- num(v:?x), num(v:?y), neq(?x,?y) — BOTH guard vars are caller vars -> the WHOLE RULE delays -> 1 unbound row; (b) floundered_member(?x) :- num(v:?x), neq(?y,?x) — ?y is BODY-LOCAL so not all guard vars are caller vars -> NO wholesale delay, num binds ?x, the rule yields 3 rows, but neq stays pending -> 3 rows each with a non-empty residual; (c) distinct_local() :- num(v:?x), num(v:?y), neq(?x,?y) — ZERO head params so no caller vars -> no delay, num binds both, guard DECIDES -> 6 correct rows. So (c) is the working spelling of (a): the 6 rows this ticket wants ARE reachable today by making the vars body-local. NEW DATUM: .fix() does NOT rescue (a). distinct_pair.fix(x: 1) still flounders (2 residual goals), even though the ticket records that the direct SLD call distinct_pair(1) gives the correct 2 — because fix WRAPS the query as guarded(query, eq(col,1)) rather than substituting the constant into the head, so the pre-check still sees ?x as a free caller var. Any fix for this ticket should treat the fix/where-supplied binding as satisfying the pre-check, not just head-supplied args. NOTE: WI-737 now makes all of the above LOUD — these cases raise Error[RelationFloundered] on drain instead of silently returning unbound/spurious rows, so this ticket's symptom is now an error rather than a wrong answer.", at: "2026-07-17T07:36:50Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-737", description: some(value: "WI-714 BUG: the Relation drain presents a FLOUNDERED (residual) solution as a definite row. RAISE instead (user decision 2026-07-17). SYMPTOM, probed empirically: a rule whose body cannot be decided yields, through the Relation face, ONE ROW WHOSE COLUMNS ARE UNBOUND LOGIC VARIABLES. Concretely, rule distinct_pair(?x,?y) :- num(v:?x), num(v:?y), neq(?x,?y) is typed Relation[(x: Int64, y: Int64)] and materializes a row (x: Var(Global(1231)), y: Var(Global(1232))) — the schema promises Int64 and the value IS a logic variable, a type-level lie in the very face (proposal 052) whose purpose is to replace raw Substitution walking with TYPED rows. Second symptom, same root: an applied membership relation over an undecidable body reports NON-EMPTY, so a floundered residual reads as a positive membership answer. ROOT CAUSE: Interpreter::materialize_solution (eval/mod.rs:1183) never inspects sol.is_definite() — it reads each column through resolve_as_value and returns whatever it finds, bound or not. THE REST OF THE CODEBASE GETS THIS RIGHT: (a) make_solution_value (eval/mod.rs:1126-1140) reifies the reflect Solution as definite(subst) vs undecided(subst, residual) precisely so consumers can inspect which goals stayed pending; (b) relation_negate (eval/builtins.rs:1086-1093) rejects a free-column operand LOUDLY, reasoning 'would flounder under NAF ... reading a floundered residual as a spurious solution ... rather than return a silently-wrong result' — the SAME hazard, guarded only for negate; (c) anthill-todo's hand-written walk (collect_id_set, main.anthill:2691-2709) explicitly skips 'case undecided(_, _)'. So the typed face built to RETIRE that boilerplate silently lost the residual honesty (WI-519) the boilerplate had. Missed by the existing wi714 tests because they all query ground facts, which only ever yield definite solutions. FIX (user decision): RAISE. A Relation[T] promises rows of T and T has no room for a third outcome, so a floundered solution must surface on the E channel — E is ALREADY >= {Error} on every relation, so the raise is already in the type and no signature changes. Follows relation_negate's precedent and the 'loud error over silent skip' principle. REJECTED: skipping residuals via ResolveConfig.definite_only — trades one silent lie for another, since isEmpty would then report empty for a goal the search never decided (undecidedness masquerading as refutation). NOTE the split this settles: the RAW reflect/LogicalStream face keeps undecidedness as DATA (mod.rs:1132 says it is never raised on execute's E channel, for WI-010's self-hosted resolver); the TYPED Relation face raises. Document the contrast where both live. SCOPE: gate materialize_solution on sol.is_definite(); raise an Error naming the undischarged residual goals (already carried; make_solution_value already renders them as List[Term]); decide whether the WI-628 truncated flag deserves a distinct message (search truncated at max_depth vs goal floundered). ACCEPTANCE: a relation over an undecidable body RAISES on drain instead of yielding a var-bearing row or a spurious membership; the message names the residual goals; definite relations unaffected (all wi714/wi727 suites green); a regression test pins BOTH symptoms; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-714"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-17T07:36:52Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-739", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED (pending commit). THE FIX IS ABOUT BUILTINS, NOT BODIES. The ticket asked to settle WHY neq and not(eq(..)) diverge. Answer: Not was always exempt from the caller-var pre-check because NAF SUSPENDS and goal rotation re-asks it. That is true of neq too, and of every builtin that suspends on a flex operand rather than answering. So the pre-check must fire only for a builtin that must NOT be REORDERED past whatever binds its var — a property of the BUILTIN (new builtin_is_reorderable), never of the body. neq/lt/gt/add are reorderable => no wholesale delay => distinct_pair(?x,?y) enumerates its 6 ground rows, matching distinct_naf exactly.\n\nNOT reorderable, two families, two different reasons: (a) nonvar/ground — they DO suspend, but rotation then lets a generator bind the var and they VACUOUSLY SUCCEED, turning an honest residual into a proof; the question is about the CALLER's instantiation so only the caller may answer it. (b) ho_apply — it HARD-FAILS on an unbound predicate var (step_init pops the frame), so there is no suspension to re-ask: reordering does not defer the question, it destroys it.\n\nAlso: TYPE-position caller vars now propagate delay UNCONDITIONALLY (WI-322's invariant made explicit). Nothing ever binds a type arg — the discrim tree keys on functor/arity/value-args — so the reordering argument cannot apply there. It previously held only by fixture accident.\n\nA REJECTED DESIGN, recorded so it is not re-attempted. I first built exactly what this ticket's FIX DIRECTION proposed — qualify the pre-check with 'can one of the rule's own body generators bind this var?' It passed the full suite (2938/0) and was WRONG twice over, which /code-review caught: (a) UNNECESSARY — rotation already reaches the body's generators AND the caller's siblings, because an opened body is spliced into the caller's goal list; bypassing the entire ~50-line scan left every behavioural test green. (b) UNSOUND IN BOTH DIRECTIONS — it must GUESS which conjuncts bind. Excluding builtin binders left THIS TICKET'S OWN BUG alive for the arithmetic spelling: graph_guard(?z) :- add(1,2,?z), neq(?z,5) returned [] instead of [3] — the exact shape WI-740's queens diagonal needs, and the route WI-738 designates for flattening. Counting forall_impl/ho_apply bodies as binders broke every <Sort>.induction(?P): BigInt.induction(?P) with ?P free went from an honest undecided residual to 0 SOLUTIONS — a silent false refutation, reintroducing the very conjunct-order divergence this ticket exists to remove. 'Which conjunct might bind this var' is a question the gate cannot answer and does not need to ask.\n\nFIX() SETTLED (this ticket's own feedback item, VERIFIED not assumed): distinct_pair.fix(x: 1) now drains 2 rows. Keying on reorderability settles it without ever asking where a binding came from — head-supplied, fix-supplied and caller-sibling all work because the question stops being asked. wi739_fix_supplied_binding_drains.\n\nWI-737 FIXTURE SWAP (the two bugs composed, as WI-738 predicted): WI-737's SYMPTOM 1 used distinct_pair as its floundering fixture. It no longer flounders, so pairCount returned 6 instead of raising. Retargeted to unbound_pair(?x,?y) :- neq(?x,?y) — genuinely floundered because NOTHING binds it. Property pinned is unchanged; the stale distinct_pair narrative in that file's comments (head-param-vs-body-local as the discriminator) is corrected — that divergence WAS this bug.\n\nLESSON, same class as WI-738's and one level up. I wrote two test docstrings asserting the simpler design was RULED OUT — 'this rules out the simpler fix', 'would wrongly come back proved' — from reasoning, never tested. Both were false: definite_only cannot separate 'refuted' from 'undecided', and neq suspends at the BUILTIN level (eq_operands => Delay) whether or not the rule delays wholesale, so both tests passed with the gate deleted. A test whose doc claims to rule out a design it does not discriminate is worse than no test: it freezes the wrong design. Both are rewritten honestly and labelled CONTROLS; the two NEW tests (ho_apply, arith) are mutation-verified to BITE.\n\nTests: wi739_guard_generator_delay_test (9) — the 6 ground rows; neq/not(eq(..)) agreeing; 9-row unguarded baseline; (in,out) still 2; zero-head-param control; unbindable flounders (control, asserts on the RESIDUAL not definite_only); ho_apply/BigInt.induction not silently refuted; arith graph form enumerates; fix()-supplied binding drains. Full workspace green 2941/0 via scripts/test.sh.\n\nPROCESS HAZARD: a /code-review subagent EDITED resolve.rs live (injected std::env::var(\"PROBE_OLD_GATE\") into the delay gate) and dropped probe files into tests/. Removed and verified clean, but it corrupted one full-suite run mid-flight. [[workflow-git-clobbers-uncommitted]] applies to review agents, not just git operations.", at: "2026-07-17T08:31:34Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-739", description: some(value: "BUG: a comparison guard makes a rule FLOUNDER in the (out,out) mode instead of letting the body's own generators bind first — while the logically identical not(eq(..)) enumerates correctly. REPRO (probed, fact num(v: 1..3)): (a) 'rule any_pair(?x,?y) :- num(v:?x), num(v:?y)' queried with both columns free yields 9 GROUND rows — enumeration works. (b) 'rule distinct_pair(?x,?y) :- num(v:?x), num(v:?y), neq(?x,?y)' queried with both columns free yields ONE row with UNBOUND vars — the guard collapsed the whole enumeration into a floundered residual (expected 6). (c) The SAME rule applied as distinct_pair(1) — first column bound at the CALL — yields the correct 2 rows, so the rule is fine in mode (in,out) and broken in (out,out). (d) Guard POSITION is irrelevant: moving neq before the generators flounders identically, so this is not delay-and-rotate ordering but a rule-level caller-var pre-check (the one wi483_rule_body_method_eval_test.rs:49-51 names: 'a ground query skips the caller-var delay pre-check, so the rule body actually runs'). (e) DECISIVE: 'rule distinct_naf(?x,?y) :- num(v:?x), num(v:?y), not(eq(?x,?y))' — the SAME logical content via NAF — yields the correct 6 GROUND rows. So not(eq(..)) works exactly where neq(..) flounders. Consistent with kernel-language.md:1535/:1548 ('= never binds ... Because = never binds, not(eq(...)) is always safe') and with cut_test.rs:236-245, whose NAF generate-and-test yields its 1 expected solution with the head var free. WHY IT MATTERS: generate-and-test is THE logic-programming idiom, and its natural spelling (a comparison guard after the generators) silently degrades to a floundered residual whose columns are unbound — which today materializes as a bogus Relation row (WI-737) rather than an error. It blocks map colouring, distinctness constraints, and the eight-queens column test (WI-736) in the mode that matters (query with the answer free), while the workaround — spelling every guard as not(eq(..)) — is undiscoverable and inconsistent. FIX DIRECTION: the pre-check delays a rule on the CALLER's unbound vars without accounting for the body's own generators binding them first; delay-and-rotate should suffice (delay neq, rotate, let num bind, retry) and demonstrably does not. Either make the pre-check account for body generators, or route the comparison builtin through the same path NAF takes. Settle WHY neq and not(eq(..)) diverge — one of the two is wrong, and the divergence itself is the bug. ACCEPTANCE: distinct_pair with both columns free yields the 6 ground rows, matching distinct_naf; distinct_pair(1) still yields 2; guards on genuinely unbindable vars still flounder honestly (they must not be forced); cut_test/wi525 NAF suites green; a regression test pins neq and not(eq(..)) agreeing; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-17T08:31:43Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-736", description: some(value: "examples/classic-mini: a collection of small CLASSIC programs implemented in anthill, one per subdirectory (eight-queens, eight-philosophers, ...). MOTIVATION: the repo's two examples (github-todo, webots-modelling) are both DOMAIN MODELS — there is no small, self-contained showcase of anthill AS A LOGIC LANGUAGE (rules + SLD search + the relational face). Notably proposal 052 uses 'queens' / 'Queen.find' as its RUNNING EXAMPLE throughout (052:18, :71, :91-95, :310-327) and NO queens implementation exists anywhere in the repo — verified: 'queens' occurs only in proposals, design docs and Rust comments. So the canonical illustration of the language's headline feature has never actually been written or run. SCOPE: examples/classic-mini/<name>/ per example, each self-contained (.anthill sources + a README stating the problem, the encoding, and how to run it), plus a collection index README. FIRST: eight-queens — 052's own running example, and the natural END-TO-END validation of WI-714: a rule 'queens(?board)' cited BY NAME is a Relation[Board] (1-collapse), consumed through the inherited Stream API (splitFirst / takeN — NOT head/headOption, which are rule-backed and do not evaluate, WI-733) and composable via the algebra (where / project / fix). THEN: eight-philosophers (dining philosophers) — anthill has no concurrency (Branch is out of 052's scope; Suspension = WI-069, unimplemented), so the INTERESTING part is modelled declaratively: states, the deadlock condition as a rule, and safety as negation-as-failure (watch the WI-525 NAF allowedness / floundering rules and the WI-728 free-column hazard). Further candidates (the 'etc'): zebra puzzle, towers of hanoi, family/ancestor transitive closure, map colouring, send+more=money. Each example is a separate increment; the directory + index + eight-queens is the first. ACCEPTANCE: examples/classic-mini/eight-queens loads and RUNS, producing correct solutions (8-queens has 92; the first solution is checkable), driven from anthill source not a Rust harness; the collection index + a per-example README explain the encoding; the examples are EXERCISED BY A TEST so they cannot rot (the github_todo_test.rs precedent — note WI-719's load-ORDER hazard: stdlib must register before an examples/ dir that sorts lexicographically before it); cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Claimed(agent: "user", since: "2026-07-17T08:46:16Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-714", author: "user", content: "SCHEMA-TYPING GAP found by WI-736 (classic-mini), 2026-07-17, BISECTED not guessed: a RECURSIVE rule cannot be cited by name as a Relation[T] AT ALL. 'rule anc(?c,?e) :- parent(of:?c, is:?e)' + 'rule anc(?c,?e) :- parent(of:?c, is:?m), anc(?m,?e)' then 'anc.takeN(50)' fails to LOAD with: 'type mismatch in anc.rule (rule): expected a common column type across relation clauses, got disjoint types for column e'.\n\nBISECTION (each probed live via anthill run): single-clause rule cited by name -> WORKS (2 rows); MULTI-clause non-recursive -> WORKS (4 rows); recursive -> LOAD ERROR. So it is the recursion, not multi-clause and not the LUB machinery generally.\n\nMECHANISM (confirmed by a discriminating probe, not inferred): a column whose ONLY typing source in a clause is the rule's OWN recursive self-reference gets no type, and the cross-clause LUB then reports it disjoint. PROOF: add a non-recursive goal that independently types the column in the recursive clause — 'rule anc(?c,?e) :- parent(of:?c,is:?m), parent(of:?m,is:?e), anc(?m,?e)' — and it LOADS and RUNS (3 rows). The recursive occurrence is being typed as if anc's schema were already known; it is what is being computed. The synthesis has no FIXPOINT (no assume-then-check over the clause set).\n\nSCOPE: not a takeN/drain issue and not citation-position-specific — the GROUND citation 'anc(\"bart\",\"abe\").takeN(50)' fails identically, so schema synthesis runs regardless of how the relation is cited. This is WI-714 CORE piece (3) 'Schema typing ... each column typed at the LUB of that head param across clauses (WI-287 join; a disjoint pair with no lub is a LOAD error, never a silent widen to Term)' — the LUB is correct to be loud, but a self-reference must not be an ordinary LUB participant.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS: transitive closure (ancestor/reachable) is THE canonical recursive logic program, and proposal 052's own 'Future direction — associated (dispatched) relations' worked example is 'rule reachable(?a,?b) :- edge(?a,?b)' + 'rule reachable(?a,?b) :- edge(?a,?c), reachable(?c,?b)' — i.e. WI-721's headline acceptance is this exact shape and would hit this the moment reachable is cited as a value. Recursive rules resolve FINE as subgoals; it is only the Relation VALUE face that cannot type them.\n\nBLOCKS: examples/classic-mini/ancestor (WI-736) — written, verified blocked, then WITHDRAWN rather than shipped with a workaround; the collection README states the gap and why the example is absent. Likely a fixpoint / assume-then-check over the clause set, or: treat a self-reference as contributing NOTHING to the LUB and take the column's type from the other clauses (which is exactly what the working probe does by hand).", at: "2026-07-17T08:53:00Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-736", author: "user", content: "INCREMENT 1 DELIVERED (pending commit): the directory + collection index + the first example (map-colouring). HOLD RELEASED — this ticket was held on the rule-body soundness floor; WI-738 (7506bc38) and WI-739 (5c89bde7) are both delivered.\n\nexamples/classic-mini/{README.md, map-colouring/{colouring.anthill, README.md}} + rustland/anthill-core/tests/classic_mini_test.rs. Runs from anthill SOURCE, not a Rust harness: it provides anthill.cli.Main, so 'anthill run examples/classic-mini/map-colouring/' prints 6. The test pins the ANSWER (6 = 3! for the WA/NT/SA triangle, Q/NSW/V forced) and that EVERY row is DEFINITE — a count alone would have read the WI-739 flounder's single unbound row as '1 solution'. WI-719 load-order hazard handled: stdlib collected BEFORE the example dir (classic-mini sorts before both github-todo and stdlib).\n\nMAP-COLOURING IS THE END-TO-END VALIDATION OF THE FLOOR, and this was VERIFIED not assumed: I reverted resolve.rs to 797659cb (pre-WI-739), rebuilt, and re-ran the example — it does NOT print 6, it raises 'error: relation_floundered'. That is WI-737 failing loudly on WI-739's bug, the two composing exactly as WI-738's notes predicted. Post-fix it prints 6. The guards are spelled neq(..), the natural way — this ticket's own feedback said the not(eq(..)) workaround 'should DISAPPEAR once WI-739 lands, so do not bake it into the examples', and it has, so it is not.\n\nANCESTOR WRITTEN, VERIFIED BLOCKED, WITHDRAWN. Transitive closure was to be the second example (this ticket's feedback named it as expressible today). It is NOT: a RECURSIVE rule cannot be cited by name as a Relation[T] at all. Bisected live — single-clause cited by name WORKS, multi-clause non-recursive WORKS, recursive fails to LOAD ('expected a common column type across relation clauses, got disjoint types for column e'). Mechanism confirmed by a discriminating probe: a column whose only typing source in a clause is the rule's OWN recursive self-reference gets no type, so the cross-clause LUB calls it disjoint; adding a non-recursive goal that independently types the column makes it load and run. WI-714's schema synthesis has no fixpoint. Filed as WI-714 feedback (it is WI-714 CORE piece (3), not a new ticket). NOT shipped with a workaround — per this ticket's own scope note, a workaround that reads as idiomatic is worse than a missing example; the collection README states the gap, why the example is absent, and that the collection stays incomplete until it can be written honestly.\n\nACCEPTANCE NOTE — this ticket's ACCEPTANCE text is STALE: it still says 'examples/classic-mini/eight-queens loads and RUNS', but queens was SPLIT OUT to WI-740 (user, 2026-07-17) and is not part of this ticket. Read the acceptance as: the collection + index + at least one example that loads, runs and is test-exercised. Queens (WI-740) is now UNBLOCKED — both 738 and 739 are delivered.\n\nREMAINING for this ticket (each its own increment): ancestor (blocked on the WI-714 gap above), dining philosophers, and the further candidates (zebra, hanoi, send+more=money). cargo-test green 2942/0 via scripts/test.sh.", at: "2026-07-17T08:58:09Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-741", description: some(value: "WI-714 follow-up (relation schema typing): a relation column typed ONLY by a SPEC operation takes the spec's UNINSTANTIATED TYPE PARAMETER, so a multi-clause relation mixing it with a concretely-typed column is a spurious LOAD error. VERIFIED live (2026-07-17), realistic shape: 'rule named(?x) :- eq(?x, \"root\")' + 'rule named(?x) :- parent(of: ?x, is: ?y)' => 'type mismatch in named.rule (rule): expected a common column type across relation clauses, got disjoint types for column x' -- though BOTH clauses plainly mean String. MECHANISM (instrumented, not inferred): relation_clause_columns (kb/typing.rs) types a column from collect_rule_var_types' var_types map; for 'eq(?x, ...)' that records ?x at 'anthill.prelude.PartialEq.T' -- the spec op's own type PARAMETER, carried as a Term::Ref to the parameter symbol (probe: 'a=anthill.prelude.PartialEq.T | b=anthill.prelude.String'). The cross-clause lub then compares Ref(PartialEq.T) against Ref(String), finds them incomparable, and reports the column disjoint. This is a THIRD spelling of 'the type is unknown', alongside the TypeVar inference wildcard (which join_types already short-circuits) and the raw Var::Global (which WI-714's join_column_types now treats as no-information -- the recursive-relation fix). A Term::Ref to a type param is neither, so it still reaches join_types. FIX DIRECTION (a HYPOTHESIS to verify first, not a diagnosis): collect_rule_var_types builds a Substitution and DROPS it (returns only (var_types, contradiction)), so var_types holds the UNRESOLVED PartialEq.T rather than the String the subst very likely bound when it constrained the literal argument \"root\" against the same param. Walking var_types through that subst in relation_clause_columns would then yield String. VERIFY that the subst actually binds it before building on this. BLAST RADIUS -- this is why it was NOT folded into the recursion fix: resolving var_types through the subst changes column types for EVERY relation, not just multi-clause ones (a single-clause 'rule r(?x) :- eq(?x, \"a\")' types Relation[PartialEq.T] today and would become Relation[String]), so it needs its own tests + review. Alternative narrower framing: treat an uninstantiated spec type param as no-information in join_column_types (gated on is_type_param, cf. WI-708) -- but that LOSES information the producer could have had, so prefer the producer fix. NOT a soundness bug: it fails LOUDLY at load, never silently. Orthogonal to recursion (WI-714's recursive schema gap is fixed and green). ACCEPTANCE: the two-clause 'named' relation above loads and drains, its column typed String; a genuinely disjoint pair (String vs Int64) is STILL a loud load error; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-736", author: "user", content: "INCREMENT 2 DELIVERED: ancestor — the example this ticket wrote, verified blocked, and WITHDRAWN. The blocker (WI-714 recursive schema synthesis) is FIXED, so it is restored and runs honestly, with no workaround.\n\nexamples/classic-mini/ancestor/{ancestor.anthill, README.md} + a classic_mini_test case. `anthill run examples/classic-mini/ancestor/` prints 12 / 3 / 1.\n\nTHE EXAMPLE: transitive closure as two clauses — an ancestor is a parent, or a parent's ancestor. No traversal, no visited set, no queue. It also shows what a rule-as-relation buys that a function does not: ONE rule answers three modes — `ancestor` (both columns free, the whole closure), `ancestor(\"bart\")` (first column bound; the applied citation BINDS a column and narrows T per 052 §8.3 → Relation[String]), `ancestor(\"bart\",\"orville\")` (both bound → Relation[Unit] membership).\n\nWHY 12: 5 parent facts close into 12 pairs (3+3+3+2+1 over bart/lisa/maggie/homer/abe). The base clause ALONE yields exactly the 5 parent edges, so the count pins that the recursive clause actually ran — a build that dropped it would print 5. The test asserts all three modes plus a fourth: the CONVERSE `ancestor(\"orville\",\"bart\")` is empty, so the relation is not accidentally symmetric. Rows are asserted DEFINITE (the WI-737/739 precedent map-colouring set — a count alone would read a floundered unbound row as an answer).\n\nThe test also REFACTORS map-colouring's hand-built goal onto a shared `query(kb, qn, args)` helper (both tests built the same Term::Fn + ResolveConfig by hand).\n\nNOTE the ancestor test's real gate is the LOAD: the example cites `ancestor` BY NAME, so the typer synthesizes its schema while type-checking main's body, and `load_example` panics on failure. Recursive rules always RESOLVED fine as subgoals — the resolver queries would have passed even before the fix. Said so in the test doc so it is not mistaken for a resolver test. The relation VALUE face over a recursive rule is covered end-to-end in wi714_recursive_relation_test.\n\nFOOTGUN worth knowing (hit twice, cost a bogus bisection): a namespace whose LAST SEGMENT equals the rule name SHADOWS it — `namespace classic.ancestor` + `rule ancestor` makes `ancestor.takeN` resolve the NAMESPACE and fail with a misleading `unknown functor` rather than the real error. Hence `namespace classic.ancestry` with `rule ancestor`. It fails loudly, so it is not a soundness issue, but it is undiscoverable from the message.\n\nCollection README updated: ancestor moves from \"Not yet here\" to Runs; the withdrawal paragraph is replaced by one line of history (it was withheld, the gap is fixed, it is here). REMAINING for this ticket: dining philosophers + the further candidates (zebra, hanoi, send+more=money). Queens is WI-740.", at: "2026-07-17T09:45:48Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-714", author: "user", content: "RECURSIVE SCHEMA-TYPING GAP FIXED — the last WI-714 item (the one WI-736 found). A recursive rule can now be cited by name as a Relation[T]; transitive closure runs.\n\nMECHANISM (bisected + instrumented live, NOT inferred; the handoff note's direction was right but its framing was not). The ticket said \"the synthesis has no FIXPOINT (no assume-then-check over the clause set)\". The precise cause is narrower and needs no fixpoint machinery: it is a CARRIER MISMATCH between two spellings of \"unknown\". relation_clause_columns types a column from (1) an explicit type bound, (2) collect_rule_var_types' body constraints, else (3) a fresh raw Term::Var(Var::Global). A RULE subgoal constrains NOTHING (collect_term_type_constraints handles only operations + entity constructors), so a column whose only source is the self-reference lands in case (3). join_types ALREADY returns the other side for its own wildcard -- but it tests `type_dispatch_name_view == Some(\"type_var\")`, and a raw Var::Global's tag is None (probe: `col=e a_tag=Some(\"sort_ref\") b_tag=None`, `b_var=Some(VarId 1236)`). So it climbed the lattice, found String vs a raw var incomparable, returned None => \"disjoint\". The absence of information was being treated as a rival type.\n\nFIX = join_column_types (kb/typing.rs, 8 lines + rationale), called from relation_columns_across_clauses' lub fold: an unconstrained column (resolved_var == Some -- the SAME seam relation_reference_type_applied already uses to spot one, where it accepts the arg and narrows) contributes NOTHING; take the informative side. That IS the fixpoint answer, reached without assume-then-check precisely BECAUSE a self-reference contributes nothing to begin with -- and for the same reason it covers MUTUAL recursion and a plain rule subgoal, neither of which a self-reference-specific rule would catch (tested). Returns without binding, mirroring join_types' own wildcard arm. NOT a Rust fixpoint pass; NOT a widen to Term.\n\nTHE GUARD SURVIVES: two clauses that BOTH type a column concretely at disjoint types (String vs Int64) are still a loud LOAD error (tested). The relaxation applies only where a clause carries no information.\n\nSCHEMA VERIFIED, not assumed. A `where(eq(x.e,\"abe\"))` test does NOT prove the column is String -- an open var unifies with String happily. The discriminating probe: annotate the recursion-typed column as Int64 => REJECTED, and the error prints the synthesized schema `got List[T = (c: String, e: String)]`; annotate String => loads. An open var would have unified with Int64 and loaded. That is now a test.\n\nADJACENT GAP FOUND (pre-existing, NOT filed, NOT mine to fix here): there is a THIRD spelling of \"unknown\" my fix does NOT cover -- a column typed only by a SPEC op takes the spec's uninstantiated TYPE PARAMETER as a Term::Ref, not a var. `rule corr(?x,?y) :- eq(?x,?y)` types ?x at `anthill.prelude.PartialEq.T` (probe: `a=anthill.prelude.PartialEq.T | b=anthill.prelude.String`), so a multi-clause relation mixing an eq-typed column with a concrete one is still a loud disjoint error. Provably unaffected by my change (resolved_var returns None for both Refs => identical path). Root cause is at the PRODUCER: collect_rule_var_types builds a subst and DROPS it, so var_types holds the unresolved PartialEq.T instead of the String the subst bound. Orthogonal to recursion, fails loudly not silently; recorded in the join_column_types docstring. Fix would be to walk var_types through that subst.\n\nTESTS: wi714_recursive_relation_test (7, all EVAL) -- transitive closure asserted as exact PAIRS (5 rows; the base clause alone gives 3, so it pins the recursive clause ran), the discriminating schema test, where over the recursion-typed column (3 of 5 survive => not vacuous), ground applied citation (member=1 AND non-member=0), projection (bag, OQ6), mutual recursion, and the surviving disjoint guard. VERIFIED THE TESTS FAIL WITHOUT THE FIX (disabled join_column_types => 5 of 6 failed; the guard test correctly passes both ways).\n\nALSO DELIVERED WI-736's ancestor increment (see that ticket).\n\nWI-714 is now COMPLETE: all 3 citation positions + all 6 algebra increments + consumption + recursive schema synthesis.\n\n/code-review (8 angles, high): NO correctness bug in the diff. Verified by tracing: join_types is UNCHANGED so its other callers (LatticeDir::Lub, the fold at 24042, its unit tests) are untouched; join_column_types has exactly ONE caller; both citation paths share relation_columns_across_clauses so bare + applied get the fix consistently. FIXED one simplification (collect_pairs/collect_strings were the same cons-walk with a different head extraction -> one generic collect_list, 122ddd69). Two notes recorded, not acted on: (1) ALTITUDE -- this is a consumer-side ADAPTER for a representation problem; there are now THREE spellings of \"unknown\" (TypeVar wildcard / raw Var::Global / Ref to a spec type param) and no consumer handles all three. The deep fix is ONE spelling, or the WI-741 producer fix. The adapter was chosen deliberately: re-minting the unconstrained column as a canonical TypeVar would route the APPLIED path from bind_resolved (which pins via sigma, so rel(5) -> Relation[Int64]) to types_compatible, likely losing that narrowing. (2) the cons-list decoder is hand-rolled in ~11 test files with no shared helper -- pre-existing, repo-wide, out of scope.\n\nSOUNDNESS TRADE-OFF, verified rather than argued. Relaxing the lub means a column untyped ONLY because a rule subgoal constrains nothing now adopts the other clause's type, which a lying rule subgoal could contradict at runtime ('rule r(?x) :- parent(of:?x,is:?y)' + 'rule r(?x) :- someIntRule(?x)' types x as String though clause 2 yields Int64). This is NOT a regression I introduced -- the SINGLE-clause baseline already has the identical hole: probed live, 'rule r(?x) :- intish(?x)' over an Int64-yielding rule ACCEPTS 'let l: List[String] = r.takeN(5)' and prints 1 TODAY. So the multi-clause \"disjoint\" error was accidentally catching a symptom of rule subgoals not being typed (WI-079 full relational typing), not protecting an invariant; the change makes multi-clause consistent with the single-clause baseline. The real fix for that class is WI-079, not this lub.\n\nCommits 1e172192 (fix + tests + example) + 122ddd69 (review simplification). Full workspace green 2950/0.", at: "2026-07-17T09:46:08Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-714", author: "user", content: "CORRECTION to the previous feedback: it says the adjacent spec-type-param gap was 'NOT filed' — it IS filed, as WI-741 (that text was drafted before filing). WI-741 = a column typed only by a SPEC op takes the spec's uninstantiated type parameter (Term::Ref to PartialEq.T), so a multi-clause relation mixing it with a concretely-typed column is a spurious LOAD error; verified live on a realistic shape ('rule named(?x) :- eq(?x, \"root\")' + 'rule named(?x) :- parent(of: ?x, is: ?y)'), pre-existing, orthogonal to recursion, fails loudly.", at: "2026-07-17T09:46:26Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-714", description: some(value: "Proposal 052 CORE: relations as first-class values (sort Relation[T]). Land the typed, composable face of the 026.1 LogicalQuery engine so a rule cited BY NAME is a first-class value that provides a runnable stream -- retiring the hand-assembled make_fn/pattern_query/execute + raw-Substitution boilerplate. Ref: docs/proposals/052-rules-as-stream-valued-operations.md (Draft; all prerequisites now landed, so this is READY not blocked). PREREQUISITES (all Delivered): WI-638 (named-tuple field access t.x -- 052's one language prereq), WI-639 (distribute-dot projection x.(...) -- the projection surface), WI-642 (MissingRequiresForSpecOp on rule/relation clause bodies -- 052's static requirement-check face), WI-287 (match-join lub), WI-300 (rule-body requirement dictionaries / find_dictionary -- the runtime path for a clause's requires), WI-603 (rule-body atom typing), and the 026.1 execute(kb, LogicalQuery) -> Stream[Solution] engine with conjunction/disjunction/negation/guarded already wired in kb/execute.rs. NO interpreter/resolver change -- this is a surface + typing + provides layer only. CORE (3 pieces, per 052 Build path): (1) sort Relation[T] provides LogicalStream[T, E=Error]: the sort + the provision backed by execute (lazy splitFirst = advance the resolver one solution), and materialization of a Solution's substitution onto the relation's free VarIds into a named-tuple record in declaration order -- the one place a solution materializes, only if not bound-through by a pattern; 1-collapse (single free var -> the element value, e.g. Relation[Board]) and 0-free -> Unit (a boolean/membership relation). (2) Rule-reference resolution in BOTH citation positions (052 Naming): (a) APPLIED -- Sort.rule(args) / queens(board) / rule(x: 3) parses as qualified-name application (fn_term), resolved via rule_id_by_qn to the rule (label else head functor); works on existing machinery; named-arg application binds parameters and narrows T. (b) BARE-QUALIFIED (the one NEW name-resolution arm): a bare Queen.find parses as field_access(Queen, find) per 6.7; add a resolve-time arm -- when a field_access receiver is a SORT/namespace symbol (statically known, 6.7 mode-2 sort-component access) and the member names a rule in that scope, produce the Relation[T] value (parallel to how WI-638 added the named-tuple arm). Bare unqualified `queens` is already a plain name. A rule is NOT a member of a runtime value's sort (dot on a value stays operation dispatch); a value yields a relation only via an op/field that RETURNS Relation[T]. (3) Schema typing: synthesize T = the named tuple of the relation's free head parameters (un-supplied head params in declaration order; body-internal vars are existential, never columns -- forced by relations being multi-clause), subtracting those supplied at the site (partial-entity expansion, 8.3); collapse 1->element / 0->Unit; each column typed at the LUB of that head param across clauses (WI-287 join; a disjoint pair with no lub is a LOAD error, never a silent widen to Term); thread the SAME T and the access-effect row E (>= {Error}, from execute's effects Error) through the provides edge so every inherited Stream op (head/headOption/map/find/toList/...) types at T and E, NOT at {} -- LogicalStream's stdlib fact Stream[T] currently omits the row and must carry it (a pre-existing gap the finiteness cluster WI-357/365/368 machinery already closes at the consumption boundary). INCREMENTS (one wired LogicalQuery constructor + one schema rule each; per 052 table): join(r1,r2,(a,b)->cond) -> conjunction (T = both rows' columns); where(r,(a)->cond) -> guarded (T unchanged); union(r1,r2) / r1|r2 -> disjunction (same T; column-free infix); negate(r) / not r -> negation; project r.(f1,f2) -> projected (the distribute-dot from WI-639 lifted over a relation, schema-preserving via name-keying) -- NOTE projected currently lowers as a PASS-THROUGH in kb/execute.rs (flattens to inner goals), so 052 applies the column restriction at ITS OWN materialization step (project the answer named tuple onto the kept columns), the one algebra op whose backing is not already wired; fix(x:1) -> guarded+project sugar (restrict a column to a constant then drop it; named-arg key, no lambda). join/where take a ROW LAMBDA (columns via destructuring (a,b)-> or WI-638 dot-access c->c.x); union/negate keep infix; projection is the distribute-dot; fix is column-by-key. NotFound = the ordinary Stream partial/total split (.head errors, .headOption -> Option None, stream/.toList empty) -- no bespoke nil-arm. OPEN QUESTIONS to settle (052 sec): OQ2 bare Sort.rule value vs field projection (default: ADD the arm -- a sort-symbol receiver is statically distinguishable from a value receiver); OQ4 whether | reuses logical-or (WI-529) or a distinct glyph; OQ5 do the 1-field collapse once at the typer schema/projection boundary, keep the stored element as the 1-field record; OQ6 ordering/multiplicity = resolver stream as-is (bag), documented. AFFECTS (052 header): typer (Relation[T] schema typing + provides LogicalStream edge + free-var subtraction + 1-field collapse + access-effect row), loader (rule reference -> Relation[T] in both positions incl. the field_access(Sort, ruleName) arm; application binds parameters; algebra ops -> LogicalQuery constructors), stdlib (Relation sort + provides LogicalStream[T,E]). OUT OF SCOPE (-> 027.2/027.3): the Branch effect, reflect(stream), solvers-as-handlers, match-over-a-solver, the eval<->SLD runtime switch, direct-style search bodies that re-enter search, scored/best-first consumption; and any change to the LogicalQuery ADT or the resolver. CONSUMERS to migrate after landing: WI-713 (anthill-todo's query_id_set/collect_id_set/walk_solutions/extract_string_field reflect-query boilerplate) -- repoint it to depend on this. ACCEPTANCE: a rule cited by name (bare unqualified, bare-qualified Sort.rule, and applied Sort.rule(args)) resolves to a Relation[T] value; it provides LogicalStream[T,E] so .head/.headOption/.map/.find/.toList type and run (lazy, one search step per pull), typed at the schema T and effect row E>={Error}; schema synthesis correct across 1-collapse / 0-Unit / multi-clause lub (disjoint = load error); at least join/where/union/negate/project usable, each lowering to its LogicalQuery constructor; a relation folds/collects as a stream (the WI-713 query_id_set pattern expressible as dep_satisfied.toList / a set-collect); cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh with tests per citation position, per algebra op, and per schema-arity case. Rust-only (typer/loader/stdlib; scaland has no typer)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-638", "WI-639", "WI-642", "WI-722", "WI-723", "WI-724", "WI-725", "WI-727"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-17T09:46:29Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-742", description: some(value: "DESIGN + FEATURE: give `?x: T` on a RELATIONAL rule head a MEANING — lift the WI-582 `[simp]`/`[unfold]`-only restriction and compile the annotation to a mode-directed `domain(?x, T)` goal, which is 052's declared COLUMN TYPE. (Rewritten 2026-07-17: this ticket's ORIGINAL premise was REFUTED — see PREMISE CORRECTION. It now carries the second half of its own former FIX line, \"give it a meaning\"; the silent-skip it thought it found is real but lives elsewhere and is WI-744.)\n\nPREMISE CORRECTION (the original ticket was WRONG; verified live 2026-07-17). The original claimed the annotation \"is SILENTLY DROPPED ... never reaches head_type_bounds at all ... the else-branch that raises the loud LoadError is never reached and the annotation vanishes at parse/convert. LOCUS: parse/convert.rs\". ALL FALSE. Loading the ticket's own shape — `rule colouring(?wa: NoSuchSortAnywhere, ?nt: AlsoBogus) :- palette(c: ?wa), palette(c: ?nt), neq(?wa, ?nt)` — through load_all yields THREE LOUD ERRORS: `unresolved name 'NoSuchSortAnywhere'`, `unresolved name 'AlsoBogus'`, and the WI-582 message itself. The annotation DOES reach `rule_head_type_bounds` (convert.rs:720 `typed_var_arg` → the `typed_var` marker → load.rs:6919-6929 push → load.rs:11836 drain), because `typed_var_arg` is a plain `_fn_arg` (grammar.js:1188) and head conversion (load.rs:11824-11836) is relational/equational-blind. The guard at load.rs:11925 fires exactly as designed. WHY THE ORIGINAL SAW \"LOADS AND RUNS, printing 2\": `anthill run` DEMOTES every non-blocking LoadError to `warning:` and exits 0 (run.rs:182-195, gated on `is_load_blocking`, load.rs:679 — an ALLOWLIST from which BOTH `LoadError::Other` and `LoadError::UnresolvedName` are absent). Verified on the real example: 3 `warning:` lines, then `6`, EXIT=0. That demotion is a SEPARATE and BROADER bug → WI-744. LESSON (recorded): an exit code is not a loader verdict; read the diagnostics, and re-run the claim before building on it.\n\nWHY THE RESTRICTION IS NOT PRINCIPLED (the question this ticket answers). It is ENFORCEMENT COVERAGE, not semantics. Written in exactly two places: load.rs:11918-11924 (comment) + load.rs:11934-11941 (the error), and docs/design/constrained-term-substrate.md:389 \"*Enforcement scope (deliberate):* the bound is enforced **only** where it can be\". \"Deliberate\" is NARROW — it means \"we chose loud-error over silently-ignored\", NOT \"relational typed heads are meaningless\"; and \"where it CAN be\" means WHERE THE CODE EXISTS: `typed_pattern_bounds_hold` has exactly ONE call site, resolve.rs:2991, inside `apply_eq_rules`. THE SAME DOC SAYS THE OPPOSITE ABOUT MEANING (:310): \"A **non-operation head** (a bare relation with no executable twin) is typed the **same way** — same conventions, same owed-on-consumption, same carried-type firing. Op-head vs non-op-head is **not a typing distinction**.\" TWO CORROBORATIONS: (i) the TYPER cannot enforce the bound either (its match keys by fresh globals, not the synthetic-DeBruijn entries the check reads), so simp_rewrite.rs:499 SKIPS typed rules ENTIRELY — enforcement is PARTIAL even inside the blessed `[simp]` path, and the doc files that as a \"follow-on\", which a principle would not tolerate. (ii) The M1 steelman (\"SLD matching must stay type-blind — structural matching never branches on a type, else regress\") CUTS THE OTHER WAY: the doc's desugaring (:325) is `head(…, ?x, …) :- conforms(typeof(?x), T), body` — an ordinary SLD goal over the sort lattice + instance facts, so \"the engine sees one more goal, never a type\". The RELATIONAL case is the one that fits M1 MOST naturally (no fold-into-matching at all); the EQUATIONAL case is the one that NEEDED the clever constraint+wakeup machinery, because a rewrite fires by MATCHING, not by running a body. The restriction has the difficulty exactly BACKWARDS relative to meaning.\n\nWHY 052 WANTS IT (not merely permits). `relation_clause_columns` (typing.rs:3822) ALREADY reads `rule_type_bounds` FIRST (:3838) — explicit bound, else `collect_rule_var_types` inference, else a fresh var (an unconstrained column). The typing half is BUILT AND WIRED; it never receives a bound only because the loader rejects it two steps earlier. Today the SOLE way to type a 052 column is to launder it through an entity FIELD — which is why examples/classic-mini/map-colouring carries a `Palette` wrapper sort it should not need; its own comment admits it (colouring.anthill:29-31): \"an entity field (`c: Colour`) is what tells the typer a column is a Colour, which is why the palette is an entity + facts rather than a rule\". SOUNDNESS — the original ticket's \"NOT a soundness bug in the resolver\" is WRONG under 052: the annotation becomes LOAD-BEARING FOR THE CONSUMER'S TYPE. `Relation[(wa: Colour)]` is a claim the row-destructurer relies on; a declared-but-unenforced bound = the typer asserting what the resolver never checked, because a rule subgoal constrains nothing (WI-079). That is worse than a silent skip, and it is why enforcement (not merely recording) is in scope.\n\nHOW IT COMPILES.\n  rule f(?x: T, ?y) :- body\n    HEAD   f(?x, ?y)                            -- stripped; structurally bare so discrim indexes it identically. EXISTS (WI-582)\n    BOUND  install_rule_type_bounds(db(?x), T)  -- EXISTS (kb/mod.rs:3766)\n    BODY   domain(?x, T), body                  -- NEW: the only new machinery\n    SCHEMA relation_clause_columns → column T   -- EXISTS (typing.rs:3838)\n`domain(?x, T)` is MODE-DIRECTED: (in) `?x` BOUND → the three-valued conformance test on its CARRIED type (WI-578 value_type_term): holds → keep; refuted → fail this binding; under-determined → SUSPEND, never NAF-decide an undecided guard (WI-067). (out) `?x` UNBOUND → DELAY + rotate, re-asked once a later goal binds it; still-unbound at the end → flounder LOUDLY via the WI-737 route (`Error[RelationFloundered]`), never presented as a definite row. THE KEY CONSEQUENCE: because the goal actually RUNS, the guard reading and the declaration reading MEET — the declared schema is true BY CONSTRUCTION (the relation only ever yields conforming rows) rather than a typer assertion WI-079 cannot back. That is precisely a CLP(FD) domain, and it is what makes the 052 column type honest WITHOUT waiting on WI-079. Prepend (not append): rotation delays it to where it can decide in mode (out) anyway, while mode (in) prunes earliest.\n\nPROBLEMS / COSTS (honest).\n  (1) `conforms`/`typeof` ARE NOT GOALS TODAY — verified: no `conforms` / `subsort` / `provides` builtin in resolve.rs. The doc's desugaring is MEANING, not code. A NEW DELAYABLE BUILTIN is the actual work, and it must be classified against WI-739's REORDERABILITY rule (a builtin that must not be reordered may not delay on a caller var; `neq` is the precedent — it suspends and rotation re-asks it, so `domain` sits on the same side).\n  (2) TWO ROUTES, ONE SYNTAX: equational + `[simp]`/`[unfold]` KEEPS the match-time wakeup (`typed_pattern_bounds_hold` at apply_eq_rules); relational gets the goal. The doc already anticipates the split (:352-355 — the body-guard form \"is the fallback for a guard that spans several variables\"). An UNTAGGED equational head keeps today's loud rejection (no body to prepend to, no wakeup site).\n  (3) MULTI-CLAUSE LUB falls out of 052's existing rule, no new machinery: a clause declaring `?x: Colour` against a sibling inferring `Int64` is disjoint → LOAD ERROR, never a silent widening to Term (052 §\"The schema T\"; WI-287 join).\n  (4) A `[simp]`-tagged RELATIONAL head — decide and state it (probably: the tag is meaningless on a `:-` head; today it is what the guard demands, which is backwards).\n\nACCEPTANCE: `rule f(?x: T, ?y) :- body` on a RELATIONAL head LOADS with no WI-582 error and its column types as T (`Relation[(x: T, …)]`, via the already-wired relation_clause_columns path); a body goal binding `?x` to a non-T value FILTERS that solution rather than yielding it (the schema is true by construction); an UNBOUND `?x` DELAYS and is re-asked after a later goal binds it (`rule f(?x: String) :- eq(?x, \"abe\")` MUST work and yield one row); a still-unbound `?x` at the end FLOUNDERS loudly (WI-737 route), never a silent non-check; a nonexistent sort in that position stays a loud `unresolved name` (it already is); the existing WI-582 `[simp]`/`[unfold]` equational path is UNCHANGED (wi582_typed_rule_pattern_test green); cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh. NOTE the map-colouring `Palette` wrapper is NOT retired by this ticket — the facts still GENERATE; only the column TYPE stops depending on the entity field. Retiring the wrapper entirely is WI-743 (the mode-(out) generator)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-743", description: some(value: "DESIGN + FEATURE: a finite closed ADT should BE a generator — the mode-(out) COMPLETION of WI-742's `domain(?x, T)` goal: when `?x` is UNBOUND and T is a FINITE closed ADT, ENUMERATE T's inhabitants instead of delaying, so an enum needs no hand-written domain facts. (Revised 2026-07-17 after the WI-742 design settled: open question (3) is now ANSWERED, (4) is now ANSWERED, and the ACCEPTANCE's \"infinite T is a loud error\" clause was WRONG and is REPLACED — see CORRECTIONS.)\n\nMOTIVATION (user, Socratic, 2026-07-17, from the map-colouring example): a colour is one of exactly three things, and 'sort Colour { entity red, entity green, entity blue }' ALREADY says so — the spec calls a sort-with-constructors a closed ADT whose 'inhabitants [are] enumerated' and 'All constructors are enumerated' (kernel-language.md:620, :1483). Yet to actually enumerate them you must ALSO hand-write a wrapper sort + facts ('sort Palette { entity palette(c: Colour) }' + 'fact palette(c: red())' x3). The user's question: why? The information is already in the sort.\n\nTODAY (each verified live by deleting it): (a) THE SORT DOES NOT GENERATE — drop the facts, keep 'rule colouring(?wa: Colour, ?nt: Colour) :- neq(?wa, ?nt)', and it raises 'relation_floundered': nothing binds ?wa, so the neq TEST cannot decide. A sort declaration is not callable; SLD resolves goals against facts/rules. (b) A RULE-SHAPED generator ('rule palette(?c) :- eq(?c, red())' x3, no wrapper sort) enumerates BUT yields 'Relation[(wa: ?_, nt: ?_)]' — untyped columns, because a rule subgoal constrains nothing (the same root gap as WI-741 / the WI-714 recursion fix / WI-079). (c) So the entity field 'c: Colour' is the ONLY thing that types the column — which is what makes the wrapper sort load-bearing today. NOTE (b) and (c) are what WI-742 fixes: once `?x: T` on a relational head is honored, the column types from the ANNOTATION and the entity field stops being the only channel. This ticket removes the remaining half — the FACTS.\n\nTHE INSIGHT (user): 'this means typed rule heads'. WI-582 already gives '?x: T' a MEANING and a desugaring — 'keep(?x: Summable, ?y) = ?x' MEANS 'keep(?x, ?y) = ?x :- conforms(typeof(?x), Summable)' (wi582_typed_rule_pattern_test.rs:9-17): the loader strips the annotation from the head (discrim-tree indexes it identically — carrier-neutral) and installs a per-variable bound by DeBruijn index; at fire apply_eq_rules reads the matched value's CARRIED type (WI-578 value_type_term) and checks conformance, SUSPENDING where the type is under-determined. So '?x: T' is a TEST (mode in) — and an unbound ?x IS under-determined, which is exactly why it suspends rather than generating.\n\nTHE PROPOSAL = the DUAL reading, mode-directed. WI-742 establishes `domain(?x, T)` with two modes — (in) conformance test, (out) DELAY-and-rotate. This ticket adds the THIRD arm: (out) AND T is a FINITE closed ADT → ENUMERATE T's inhabitants as a choice point, rather than delaying. 'conforms(typeof(?x), T)' read in mode (out) IS a generator — precisely a CLP(FD) 'domain'. Payoff: 'rule colouring(?wa: Colour, ?nt: Colour, ...) :- neq(?wa,?nt), ...' with NO domain facts and NO Palette wrapper, and typed columns from WI-742.\n\nFINITENESS IS THE PRECISE LIMIT: only an ALL-NULLARY sort has a finite known inhabitant set. 'entity colour(name: String)' is infinite (String is); 'sort List { nil, cons(head, tail) }' is recursively infinite. So the rule is 'a closed ADT all of whose variants are nullary is a finite domain' — a compile-time-decidable property. This fits the EXISTING finiteness machinery (FiniteCollection / WI-589 / library-003 Phase C: 'providing collect IS the finiteness guarantee'); note a finite enum CAN honor collect, unlike Relation (maybe-infinite, WI-714). A wider rule (all variants nullary OR all fields finite — a finite PRODUCT is finite) is a possible later generalization; start with all-nullary.\n\nCORRECTIONS (2026-07-17, from the WI-742 design session):\n  (3) ANSWERED — \"interaction with WI-582's [simp]/[unfold]-only restriction and with WI-742 (relational typed heads are currently SILENTLY DROPPED)\". They are NOT silently dropped: that premise was refuted (WI-742 PREMISE CORRECTION — the loader IS loud; `anthill run` mutes it, which is WI-744). And the restriction is NOT principled — it is enforcement coverage (`typed_pattern_bounds_hold` has ONE call site, resolve.rs:2991), while docs/design/constrained-term-substrate.md:310 says op-head vs non-op-head is \"not a typing distinction\". WI-742 lifts it for relational heads.\n  (4) ANSWERED — \"does the generator belong in the resolver, or as a loader-derived clause set?\" THE RESOLVER, as the (out)+finite arm of WI-742's mode-directed `domain` builtin. A loader-derived clause set (auto-generating the palette facts) was the alternative and is REJECTED: it would materialize N facts per sort at load whether or not anything enumerates, it needs a synthetic predicate name nothing cites, and it splits one notion (`?x: T`) across two mechanisms (a body goal for the test, a clause set for the generator) — whereas one mode-directed goal covers both, which is exactly what makes it a CLP domain rather than a codegen trick.\n  ACCEPTANCE FIX — the original's \"an infinite/non-nullary sort in that position is a LOUD error, never a silent non-generation\" is WRONG and is REPLACED. `rule f(?x: String) :- eq(?x, \"abe\")` MUST work: `String` is infinite, so `domain` DELAYS, `eq` binds `?x`, rotation re-asks, the check passes, one row. Making infinite-T a LOAD error would reject that legitimate rule. Infinite T is a DELAY, not an error; only a STILL-UNBOUND-at-the-end `?x` flounders, and that already routes loudly through WI-737 (`Error[RelationFloundered]`). So the loud-over-silent property is preserved by the FLOUNDER, not by a load-time rejection.\n\nREMAINING OPEN QUESTIONS: (1) SURFACE — implicit (a typed head param of a finite sort just generates) or explicit (a derived 'Colour.values' relation, citable by name via WI-714's bare-qualified Sort.rule machinery, written ':- Colour.values(?wa)')? Implicit is terser but magic — a rule silently changes from test to generator with the finiteness of a sort it names, and adding one non-nullary variant to Colour would silently turn a working generator into a delay/flounder (arguably fine: that IS a loud flounder, not a wrong answer). Explicit is discoverable and reuses 052 wholesale, and post-WI-742 the \"a rule subgoal would not type ?wa\" objection DISSOLVES — the head annotation types it. NOTE the two are not exclusive: `Colour.values` could be the derived relation AND the implicit reading could be defined as sugar for it. (2) ORDERING / MULTIPLICITY of the generated inhabitants — declaration order (WI-720 ctor order exists) and once each, presumably; state it, since a bag vs set difference is observable in the solution count.\n\nDEPENDS ON WI-742 (which now delivers the `domain` goal this extends; the dependency is unchanged but its content is now the restriction-lift, not the mis-diagnosed silent skip).\n\nACCEPTANCE (if adopted): map-colouring's Colour needs no Palette wrapper and no domain facts — 'rule colouring(?wa: Colour, ...) :- neq(..)' enumerates 6 definite colourings and types as Relation[(wa: Colour, ...)]; a FINITE closed ADT in a typed head param generates in mode (out), in DECLARATION order, once per inhabitant; an INFINITE / non-nullary sort in that position DELAYS (not a load error) and yields correctly when a later goal binds it ('rule f(?x: String) :- eq(?x, \"abe\")' yields one row), flounding loudly (WI-737) only if still unbound at the end; the mode-(in) conformance test from WI-742 is unchanged; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-742"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Open)
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-744", author: "user", content: "SCOPE EXPANDED (user, 2026-07-17): 'can we make it errors and try to fix the underlying issues?' — the ad-hoc warn-and-proceed cluster is now fixed too, not just the LoadError demotion. These bypassed BOTH LoadError and LoadWarning (bare eprintln!(\"warning: ...\") + continue/return), so the original ticket's is_load_blocking fix did not reach them.\n\nROOT CAUSE (not a per-site symptom): collect_files_recursive returned () — NO ERROR CHANNEL — so every caller had to swallow or print. Fixed at the producer: it now takes &mut Vec<String> errors. anthill-cli's collect_anthill_files already HAD an errors vec and simply never received the dir-read failure; collect_data_files had no channel at all and now returns Result<Vec<PathBuf>, Vec<String>>. anthill-todo has its OWN duplicate copy of both (a separate finding — the two collectors are near-identical across crates); same fix applied there, collect_anthill_files -> Result.\n\nSITES CONVERTED warning -> error: anthill-cli/main.rs collect_files_recursive (unreadable dir was dropped silently -> KB missing files the user NAMED); collect_data_files (same, no channel); the .toml/.json read arm; the .toml/.json LOAD arm (the worst — genuine load errors demoted, so a query answered from a KB missing supplied data: VERIFIED a broken.toml gave 'warning: ...' + 'loaded: 2250 facts' + exit 0, now 'error: <file>: format error: TOML parse error at line 1, column 6' + exit 1). anthill-todo: collect_files_recursive + collect_anthill_files + the project-file read arm (a file we can SEE but not READ was skipped -> 'list' silently under-reported). Errors ACCUMULATE per run so one invocation reports every bad file.\n\nKEPT AS WARNINGS (deliberate, each defensible): anthill-todo main.rs:535 the PARSE arm (WI-505 — a stale domain.anthill predating a grammar change must not cascade; it is attributable and never enters the KB, and it is the ONLY site printing a proper file:line); runner.rs:77 + rust-gen bundle.rs:282 (out-of-range exit clamped — defined behavior, not a failure).\n\n-d WARNING REMOVED (user, 2026-07-17: '-d option absence when we can find anthill-todo dir is ok'): find_project_dir printed 'warning: no -d flag specified, using current directory: ...' on EVERY -d-less invocation where ./anthill-todo/ was found — i.e. on the documented normal discovery path. It guarded nothing: the message was identical whether discovery landed on the right project or the wrong one (the rustland/anthill-todo CRATE dir footgun), so it annotated everything and caught nothing. Now silent on that path; the genuinely-guessy arm (cwd itself holds .anthill files -> treat cwd AS the project) still warns, reworded to say what it is actually guessing.\n\nREVERTED (a fix that was worse than the bug): surfacing LoadResult.warnings in anthill-todo. A review agent correctly found anthill-todo DROPS merged.warnings, making WI-744's own doc ('an advisory rides LoadResult::warnings') false in that binary — same stdlib, 2 advisories under anthill run, 0 under anthill-todo. But anthill-todo embeds the stdlib AND the bundle, so those advisories concern files the user neither owns nor can fix: surfacing them put 2 requires-shadow warts on top of EVERY anthill-todo command. cmd_default_parity_test's golden transcript caught it. The drop is deliberate and now documented at the site; the residual cost (an advisory about the user's OWN project file is dropped too) needs per-file attribution -> WI-745.\n\nNEW TESTS: anthill-cli/tests/load_cmd_test.rs (+ fixtures/load/{broken-data,good-data}) — a broken .toml blocks with a loud error naming the file; the same source without it loads (the control, so the failure is attributable to the data file). NOT covered: the unreadable-DIRECTORY arm — provoking it needs a chmod-000 dir, a no-op under a root runner and flaky in CI; recorded in the test's header.", at: "2026-07-17T12:30:00Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-746", author: "user", content: "SNIFF REFINED during WI-744 verify (2026-07-17): is_anthill_data now keys on meta.entity being a STRING, not on a bare 'meta' key. Keying on the bare key still over-claimed: a foreign TOML with [meta]/[data] sections, and a JSON with a nested 'meta' object, have no entity string yet were claimed and errored — the SAME spurious-block class as Cargo.toml, in a rarer shape (VERIFIED live: both produced 'error: ...: missing meta: meta.entity must be a string', exit 1; now both load clean). meta.entity is what load_section actually requires and what every real data file carries, so tightening rejects no loadable file. Pinned by anthill-cli/tests/fixtures/load/foreign-files/{config.toml,manifest.json} + foreign_data_files_are_not_our_business.\n\nTHE RESIDUAL THIS TICKET STILL OWNS (now a false-NEGATIVE, not a false-positive): a file GENUINELY meant as data but missing a string meta.entity reads as not-ours and is skipped SILENTLY. Acceptable only because such a file would not load anyway (load_section errors on the identical condition), so nothing loadable is dropped — but it is still a silent skip, and it is the same shape as the parse-failure residual already recorded here. Both dissolve under opt-in: once data is DECLARED, 'the user said this is data' is the evidence that turns the skip back into a loud error. So this ticket's acceptance already covers it — no new work, just a second concrete instance of why sniff-by-shape cannot be the final answer.", at: "2026-07-17T15:02:32Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-744", author: "user", content: "VERIFY PASS COMPLETE (2026-07-17), delivering. The adversarial verify + gap sweep (agents died on 529 overload; both threads finished by hand) surfaced two more real issues, now fixed:\n\n1. SNIFFER FALSE-POSITIVE (same class as the Cargo.toml regression, rarer shape). is_anthill_data keyed on a bare 'meta' key, so a foreign TOML with [meta]/[data] sections and a JSON with a nested 'meta' object were claimed and errored (VERIFIED: both blocked the load, exit 1). Retightened to key on meta.entity being a STRING — exactly what load_section requires, and what every real data file carries — so no loadable file is rejected. Both now load clean. Fixtures foreign-files/{config.toml,manifest.json} pin it. Residual (now a false-NEGATIVE: a genuine-but-entity-less data file skipped silently) is inherent to sniff-by-shape and recorded on WI-746.\n\n2. FALSE COMMENT on anthill-todo collect_anthill_files: the '!path.exists() { continue; }' skip was justified by 'scan_dir points at a dir that does not exist yet' — fiction (scan_dir only ever returns an existing dir; find_project_dir proves it). Removed the skip; a non-directory is now a loud error. Also fixed the load-arm asymmetry comment, which implied the parse arm skips because it is ATTRIBUTABLE — it skips because of WI-505 REDUNDANCY (bundle supplies the stale file's defs). The read arm is attributable too and correctly BLOCKS.\n\nVERIFIED the challenged read-arm block is correct: traced by hand that the old warning+continue does NOT hit the record_source zip corruption (that was unique to the load_all_per_file empty-Vec arm); the block stands on its own ground (unreadable file = unknown contents = silent under-report if skipped).\n\nFINAL DELIVERED SCOPE (grew from the original 3-line CLI fix):\n  CORE: deleted is_load_blocking allowlist -> every LoadError blocks; advisory rides LoadResult.warnings. LoadError::Other Display drops its 'load error: ' prefix (was doubling). UnresolvedName/UnresolvedImport/AmbiguousSymbol got the blocking rationale the deleted doc held.\n  CLI (run/main/todo): all three Err arms print error: + exit. collect_queries scan_definitions demotion fixed (anthill query blocked on a typo'd import). The ad-hoc warn-and-continue CLUSTER converted to errors at the PRODUCER (collect_files_recursive gained an errors channel); collect_data_files -> Result; .flatten() drop fixed in both collectors (per-entry io errors were dropped). Data files sniffed for the meta.entity envelope so foreign .toml/.json (Cargo.toml, package.json, JSONC .vscode/settings.json) are ignored not fatal. anthill-todo discovery by MARKER (project.anthill/workitems.anthill) not by dir-name, closing the rustland/anthill-todo crate-dir footgun. -d-less discovery no longer warns on the normal path.\n  TESTS: run_cmd_test (4 new: 3 promoted variants block + advisory still runs), load_cmd_test (broken-data blocks, foreign-files ignored, control), query_cmd_test (3: bad import blocks, control, bare pattern), shared tests/common/mod.rs harness with line-wise has_diagnostic. wi440 test renamed. Full workspace 2960 passed / 0 failed via scripts/test.sh.\n  FILED: WI-745 (no file:line on load diagnostics), WI-746 (data files opt-in vs slurp), WI-747 (8 collector copies), WI-748 (todo init ignores -d). NOT committed.", at: "2026-07-17T15:07:18Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-744", description: some(value: "BUG (silent skip, at the CLI boundary): `anthill run` DEMOTES every non-blocking LoadError to `warning:` and RUNS THE PROGRAM ANYWAY, exit 0 — including `unresolved name`. This defeats the repo's \"prefer a loud error over a silent skip\" principle for EVERY guard that raises the catch-all `LoadError::Other`, which is most of them.\n\nVERIFIED LIVE (2026-07-17), on the real example: take examples/classic-mini/map-colouring and annotate the relational head with sorts that DO NOT EXIST ANYWHERE — `rule colouring(?wa: NoSuchSortAnywhere, ?nt: AlsoBogus, ?sa, ?q, ?nsw, ?v) :- ...` — then `anthill run <dir>`:\n    warning: unresolved name 'NoSuchSortAnywhere' in scope 'mapcolouring' at 2288..2306\n    warning: unresolved name 'AlsoBogus' in scope 'mapcolouring' at 2313..2322\n    warning: load error: WI-582: typed rule patterns (`?x: T`) are supported only on `[simp]`/`[unfold]` ...\n    6\n    EXIT=0\nThe SAME source through `load::load_all` directly returns `Err` with those three as LOAD ERRORS. So the loader is LOUD and the CLI MUTES it. (This is what made WI-742 look like a loader/parse-convert silent skip when the loader was in fact raising exactly the error it was written to raise — see WI-742's PREMISE CORRECTION. One mis-read exit code cost a whole ticket's diagnosis.)\n\nLOCUS: rustland/anthill-cli/src/run.rs:182-195 — the `Err(errs)` arm splits per error on `e.is_load_blocking()`: blocking → `error: {e}` + `return Err(runner::EXIT_COMPILE)`; everything else → `warning: {e}` and FALL THROUGH to `Ok(kb)`. Plus rustland/anthill-core/src/kb/load.rs:679 `is_load_blocking` — an ALLOWLIST of ~25 named variants. `LoadError::Other { .. }` (load.rs, the catch-all used by dozens of deliberately-loud guards — WI-582's typed-rule-pattern guard, the multi-head-label guard, the denial-head guard, the WI-618 rule-tvar guards, ...) is ABSENT. `LoadError::UnresolvedName { .. }` (load.rs:168) is ALSO ABSENT. Both therefore demote.\n\nSEVERITY: a program with unresolved names runs to completion and reports success. Any guard whose author wrote `LoadError::Other` believing \"loud error\" got \"warning, then run\" at the only surface users actually invoke. The load-blocking allowlist is 25 variants long and every future `LoadError::Other` silently joins the muted set — the polarity means the DEFAULT for a new guard is \"advisory\", which is the opposite of the repo principle.\n\nFIX DIRECTION (decide, then do): INVERT THE POLARITY. `Err` should MEAN blocking. A genuinely advisory diagnostic already has a home — `LoadResult.warnings`, printed by run.rs:177-180 on the Ok path (the WI-346 channel, e.g. requires-shadow). So the clean shape is: every `Err` variant blocks; anything advisory MOVES to `warnings` and stops being an `Err`. That deletes `is_load_blocking` entirely rather than inverting its list, and makes the type say the thing (`Err` = will not run). The cheaper alternative — keep the predicate but make it a DENYlist (block by default, name the advisory variants) — preserves the split but keeps a footgun-shaped default; prefer the first unless the sweep below turns up many genuine advisories.\n\nREQUIRED SWEEP (this is the real work, not the 3-line change): enumerate the `LoadError::Other` and `UnresolvedName` sites and classify each as BLOCKING (nearly all — they are guards) vs ADVISORY (move to `warnings`). Also check the other CLI entry points for the same demotion (main.rs:553/570/588/1260/1313, prove.rs) — run.rs is where it was verified but it is unlikely to be the only one. Beware: existing tests/examples may currently ride the demotion (something may load with a muted error today and stop loading after this); a full-workspace run is the check, not `-p anthill-core` (WI-569 lesson).\n\nACCEPTANCE: `anthill run` on a program with an UNRESOLVED NAME exits NONZERO and does not execute; likewise for the WI-582 typed-rule-pattern guard and the other `LoadError::Other` guards; genuinely advisory diagnostics still print and still allow the run, via the `LoadResult.warnings` channel (not via an `Err` that gets demoted); a regression test drives the CLI (or the same `Err`/exit path) over a source with an unresolved name and asserts NONZERO exit + no program output; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh (FULL workspace)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-17T15:07:20Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-748", description: some(value: "BUG (silent wrong-place write): `anthill-todo -d <dir> init` IGNORES -d and scaffolds into the CURRENT DIRECTORY, then reports success naming no path. Every other subcommand honors -d via find_project_dir; init alone hardcodes the cwd.\n\nVERIFIED LIVE (2026-07-17, found during WI-744's code review; run_init is NOT touched by that diff — this is pre-existing):\n    cd /tmp/elsewhere\n    anthill-todo -d /tmp/target-proj init\n      -> 'created anthill-todo/ with:'   exit 0\n    ls /tmp/target-proj   -> EMPTY          (the -d target got nothing)\n    ls /tmp/elsewhere     -> anthill-todo/  (the scaffold landed here)\nThe message says 'created anthill-todo/' with no absolute path, so nothing in the output reveals where it went. A following 'anthill-todo -d /tmp/target-proj list' then correctly honors -d, finds no project, and (post-WI-744) says so loudly — but the user has an anthill-todo/ sitting in whatever directory they happened to be in.\n\nLOCUS: rustland/anthill-todo/src/main.rs run_init — 'let dir = cwd.join(\"anthill-todo\")' (~line 354), reached from the dispatch at ~line 494 which returns BEFORE find_project_dir is ever consulted. Contrast every other command, which routes through find_project_dir(explicit_dir) and honors -d.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS BEYOND THE OBVIOUS: this is the same family as the -d footgun WI-744 just closed on the READ side (discovery accepted any directory NAMED anthill-todo, so 'anthill-todo list' from rustland/ silently resolved to the CLI's own crate). WI-744 fixed discovery by MARKER (is_project_dir: project.anthill / workitems.anthill must be present). init is the WRITE side of the same mistake: it decides where the project lives by position rather than by instruction. Note the two now interact usefully — after a misplaced init, discovery from the intended dir fails LOUDLY instead of reporting 'No work items found', which is how this was noticed at all.\n\nFIX: run_init must honor -d. Two sub-decisions: (1) with -d given, scaffold at <dir>/anthill-todo (or at <dir> itself if it is already the project dir? decide, and note find_project_dir treats '<cwd>/anthill-todo exists' and 'cwd IS the project' as distinct arms); (2) the success message must print the ABSOLUTE path it created, so a wrong-place write is visible even when the flag is right — the current message would not have revealed this bug even to someone looking straight at it. Consider also erroring rather than silently re-scaffolding if a project already exists at the target (untested today).\n\nACCEPTANCE: 'anthill-todo -d <dir> init' creates the project under <dir> and nothing under the cwd; the success message names the absolute path created; running init from a different cwd than -d is covered by a test (anthill-todo/tests/ — note EVERY existing test passes -d, so the discovery/init interaction has no coverage at all today, which is why this survived); 'anthill-todo -d <dir> list' immediately after 'init -d <dir>' finds the project; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-17T17:08:04Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-747", author: "user", content: "HOME = anthill-core, not anthill-stl (ticket's 'Decide'): anthill-core is the only crate all 8 sites reach (anthill-stl depends on core, not vice-versa, so core's own test copies 4+8 and core-dependent copies 5+6 could never call an anthill-stl helper -> would force a 2nd copy, violating 'one traversal impl'). SHAPE = fail-fast Result<(),String> (ticket's 'pick one'): matches the folded-in file_store copy 3, is #[must_use] (kills the WI-744 warn-and-continue regression risk an out-param invites), and per-binary policy wrappers still accumulate per-input-path faults into their Vec<String>. load_cmd_test.rs:14 confirms the 'report every unreadable subdir' behavior was untested, so the change is invisible to the suite. New anthill-core/src/fs_util.rs holds the one walk (collect_files_recursive + has_extension + collect_files); copies 1-8 all route to it. OUT OF SCOPE (ticket's optional items, caveats it flagged): report_errors helper (exit-code disagreement + WI-745 will change presentation) and cli/tests harness. list_world_files/list_cpp_sources are non-recursive non-.anthill scaffolding listings, correctly not folded.", at: "2026-07-17T17:30:36Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-747", description: some(value: "CLEANUP: EIGHT copies of \"recursively collect .anthill files from a directory\" exist across the workspace, with THREE incompatible error policies. Surfaced by WI-744, which had to make the SAME edit to two of them in two crates and thereby paid the cost in-flight.\n\nTHE COPIES (verified by grep, 2026-07-17):\n  PRODUCTION (3):\n    1. anthill-cli/src/main.rs:409 `collect_files_recursive` + :367 `collect_anthill_files` -> Result<Vec<PathBuf>, Vec<String>>\n    2. anthill-todo/src/main.rs:80 `collect_files_recursive` + :106 `collect_anthill_files` -> Result<Vec<PathBuf>, Vec<String>>\n    3. anthill-core/src/persistence/file_store.rs:99 `collect_anthill_files` + :109 `collect_recursive` -> Result<_, PersistenceError>\n  TEST-ONLY (5), all `dir: &Path -> Vec<PathBuf>`, errors panicked-or-ignored:\n    4. anthill-core/tests/common/mod.rs:18            (the `common::collect_anthill_files` the core tests use)\n    5. anthill-smt-gen/tests/common/mod.rs:12         (verbatim clone of 4)\n    6. anthill-cpp-gen/tests/common/mod.rs:17         (verbatim clone of 4)\n    7. anthill-stl/src/reflect/builtins.rs:1076       (test mod)\n    8. anthill-core/src/kb/op_requirements.rs:236     (test mod)\n\nCOPIES 1 AND 2 ARE BYTE-IDENTICAL. Verified by diffing the two 22-line bodies with comments stripped: exactly ONE line differs, and only cosmetically — `has_extension(&path, extensions)` vs its inlined `path.extension().and_then(|e| e.to_str()).map_or(false, |e| extensions.contains(&e))` equivalent. WI-744 added the same 4th parameter (`errors: &mut Vec<String>`), the same recursion threading, and the same user-facing error string (`format!(\"cannot read directory {}: {e}\", dir.display())`) to BOTH, by hand, in two crates. That is the cost, already paid once.\n\nTHE HOME ALREADY EXISTS AND IS ALREADY IMPORTED. `anthill-stl` (lib name `anthill`) is depended on by both binaries and both already `use anthill::{runner, stdlib}` (anthill-todo/src/main.rs:5, anthill-cli/src/run.rs:9) — so the move needs NO new dependency. anthill-stl/src/runner.rs's module doc states the charter verbatim: shared CLI mechanics live there \"so the two binaries stay byte-identical in their error formatting and exit codes\" — and a duplicated user-facing error string is exactly that. NOTE runner.rs is about RUNTIME bootstrap; a file-collection helper is arguably its own module (anthill-stl/src/files.rs) rather than a runner concern. Decide.\n\nSCOPE / SPLIT:\n  - Copies 1+2: move `collect_files_recursive` + `has_extension` to the shared home. The POLICY wrappers (`collect_anthill_files`) legitimately differ per binary — anthill-cli errors on a non-existent path, anthill-todo skips it — so they STAY per-binary and call the shared traversal. This is the increment that pays for itself immediately.\n  - Copies 4-6: three verbatim clones exist only because Rust integration tests cannot share a `tests/common/` module across crates. The fix is a shared helper in a crate they all already depend on (anthill-core), not more copying.\n  - Copy 3 (file_store.rs) is the interesting one: it already implements this traversal in the FAIL-FAST `Result<(), _>` shape with `?` propagation and no out-param. See the ALTITUDE note below — it may be the shape the others should adopt, in which case it is the base, not another copy.\n\nALTITUDE (a real question, settle it before moving code): WI-744 gave copies 1+2 an `errors: &mut Vec<String>` OUT-PARAM so a scan reports EVERY unreadable directory rather than just the first. A reviewer argued for the fail-fast `Result` shape instead, on the grounds that (a) all three callers discard the collected `files` the instant `errors` is non-empty, so continuing the scan is dead work whose only observable effect is printing N lines instead of 1; (b) an out-param is not `#[must_use]` — a caller that forgets `if !errors.is_empty()` silently restores the exact warn-and-continue bug WI-744 exists to kill, whereas a `Result` cannot be ignored; and (c) file_store.rs:109 already does it the other way. COUNTER-ARGUMENT, for the record: `collect_anthill_files` ALREADY accumulates other faults (path-does-not-exist, not-an-.anthill-file) across multiple paths into that same vec, so a dir-read error joining them is coherent, and reporting every bad path in one run is a genuine (if small) UX win. The two shapes cannot both be shared — pick one, and say why in the code.\n  Settled and NOT up for revisit: `Vec<String>` rather than a `LoadError` variant is CORRECT. These are filesystem faults, terminal (printed once, then exit; never matched on), and a `LoadError::Io` would directly dilute the invariant WI-744 established at kb/load.rs's `pub enum LoadError` doc (\"every variant BLOCKS the load ... callers print and exit, they do not triage\"), which is a statement about the LANGUAGE, not about the filesystem.\n\nRELATED DUPLICATION, same theme, fold in only if cheap:\n  - The `for e in &errs { eprintln!(\"error: {e}\"); } return Err(EXIT);` block now occurs ~8x across the two binaries (cli/main.rs x5-6, run.rs:182, todo/main.rs x2), five of them added or rewritten by WI-744. A `report_errors(errs) -> ()` in the same shared home collapses them. Held out of WI-744 deliberately: the loop is 3 trivial lines, the two binaries disagree on the exit code (run.rs returns EXIT_COMPILE=2, cli/main.rs returns a bare Err(1) consistent with its own ~10 error returns), and the nicer `LoadError::format_with_source` renderer is structurally unusable at these sites (see WI-745). Worth revisiting once WI-745 gives errors a file identity, since that is when the presentation actually changes.\n  - anthill-cli/tests/{run_cmd_test,load_cmd_test}.rs share ~25 lines of harness (`bin()`, `fixtures_dir()`, `struct Output`, the Command runner) by copy. anthill-cli/tests/ has no `common/` module, unlike anthill-core / anthill-cpp-gen / anthill-smt-gen, which all do. Two copies is where this pattern starts, not where it ends.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS (not just tidiness): the three error policies genuinely disagree TODAY. An unreadable stdlib directory makes copy 4 panic (`expect(\"read dir\")`), copy 8 return an empty Vec and fail the suite as a mysterious missing-symbol error, and copy 1 report it cleanly — same fault, three behaviors, no shared test. And the next behavior change (a symlink-loop guard, a max-depth cap, honoring .gitignore, skipping target/ and node_modules/ — the last of which WI-746 will want) costs the same doubled-or-octupled edit; the first time someone patches only the crate they were looking at, the two CLIs silently disagree about which files constitute a project. That divergence is invisible to tests: each binary's suite exercises only its own copy.\n\nACCEPTANCE: one traversal implementation, in a crate the consumers already depend on; copies 1+2 gone and both binaries calling it, with their differing POLICY wrappers retained; copies 4-6 gone (a shared helper the three test suites call); copies 7+8 gone or justified in a comment; ONE error policy, with the choice between out-param and fail-fast `Result` stated in the code; `anthill load`/`check`/`query` and every `anthill-todo` command behave identically before and after (the WI-744 tests — run_cmd_test.rs, load_cmd_test.rs, cmd_default_parity_test.rs — are the guard); cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh (FULL workspace, per WI-569: a core-type change needs the whole workspace, not -p anthill-core)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-17T17:30:46Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-745", description: some(value: "BUG (diagnostic quality, surfaced by WI-744): a load error names NO FILE and often NO LINE — it renders a raw byte offset into an unidentified file, and for 2 of 3 `UnresolvedName`/`AmbiguousSymbol` producers that offset is `0..0`. Harmless while these were demoted to `warning:`; now that WI-744 makes every `LoadError` blocking, they are errors the user MUST locate and sometimes CANNOT.\n\nVERIFIED LIVE (2026-07-17, during WI-744's code review):\n  - `anthill run <ambiguous-symbol fixture>` → `error: ambiguous symbol 'widget' in scope 'app' at 0..0: candidates [\"lib.one.Thing.widget\", \"lib.two.Gadget.widget\"]` — printed TWICE, span `0..0`. Locatable only because the candidate list names the two colliding symbols; the USE SITE is unlocated.\n  - `anthill-todo list` on a project with one stale file → `error: unresolved name 'RenamedAwaySort' in scope 'f' at 220..233` — byte 220 OF WHICH FILE? The todo CLI loads embedded stdlib + embedded bundle + N project files, so the offset identifies nothing.\n\nTHREE DISTINCT DEFECTS, one theme (a blocking diagnostic you cannot act on):\n  (1) NO FILE IDENTITY. `load_all` / `load_all_per_file` merge N `&ParsedFile`s into one KB and return a FLAT `Vec<LoadError>`. No `LoadError` variant carries a file id (`UnresolvedImport.path` is the IMPORT path, not the source file), and `ParsedFile` itself holds no path or source text (`items`/`symbols`/`terms` only). CONSEQUENCE beyond messages: `LoadError::format_with_source(source)` (load.rs:521) — which DOES render line:col — has ZERO production callers and is structurally unusable at the three CLI sites, because they have no way to pick the right source string per error. It is reachable only from single-file `load::load` tests. This is also why anthill-todo's load arm cannot \"skip the offending file\" the way its parse arm does (see the DELIBERATE ASYMMETRY comment at anthill-todo/src/main.rs:549).\n  (2) BYTE-0 SPANS. `remap_name_str_inner(&mut self, name: &str)` (load.rs:6428) and `remap_symbol_strict(&mut self, sym: Symbol)` (load.rs:6516) push `UnresolvedName`/`AmbiguousSymbol` with `span: Span::default()` — NEITHER TAKES A SPAN. Only `remap_name(&mut self, name: &Name)` (load.rs:6548) has `name.span` and passes it. Fixing means threading a `Span` through both fns and all their callers — a real refactor, which is why WI-744 scoped it out.\n  (3) DUPLICATE EMISSION. The ambiguous-symbol error prints twice for one `fact widget(n: 1)`; presumably the name is remapped on two passes. Dedup at the push site or at the report site.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS NOW: WI-744's whole thesis is loud-over-silent, but a loud error that cannot be located is only half-delivered — and the anthill-todo case is the sharp end, where the CLI is hard-dead until the user finds a file the diagnostic declines to name. WI-744 traded a silent write-corruption for a loud-but-vague failure; this ticket closes the gap.\n\nLIKELY SHAPE: give the load pipeline a per-file provenance channel — either a `file: FileId`/`Arc<Path>` on every `LoadError` (stamped where the loader already knows which `ParsedFile` it is walking), or a `LoadError`-to-file map returned alongside the flat vec. Then (a) the three CLI sites can call `format_with_source` and print `path:line:col`, retiring the raw-offset Display; (b) anthill-todo's load arm gains the option to skip-and-warn per file exactly as its parse arm does (WI-505 parity), which would ALSO settle the asymmetry WI-744 deliberately accepted; (c) `format_with_source` stops being dead code. Do (2) in the same pass — the threading is the same shape of work. NOTE the CLI sites currently print the bare `Display`; anthill-todo's PARSE arm already does the good thing (`eprintln!(\"warning: {}:{}\", file.display(), err.format_with_source(&source))`, main.rs:535) and is the model.\n\nRELATED (small, separate, pre-existing — fold in only if convenient): the two anthill-cli load paths disagree on exit code for the same failure class — `run.rs build_kb` returns `runner::EXIT_COMPILE` (=2) while `main.rs load_kb_with_stdlib` returns a bare `Err(1)` (= EXIT_RUNTIME's value), so `anthill check` and `anthill run` report a load error as different codes. `main.rs` uses no `runner::EXIT_*` constant at any of its ~10 error returns, so this is that file's own convention, not a typo; WI-744 preserved it deliberately (changing it is a behavior change to every `anthill check`/`query`/`bundle` caller).\n\nACCEPTANCE: a load error names the FILE and a line:col (`path:line:col: message`), not a raw byte offset; `UnresolvedName`/`AmbiguousSymbol` from every producer carry a real span (no `Span::default()` reaching a user-visible diagnostic); one error is printed once; `format_with_source` has production callers (or is deleted if the design lands elsewhere); the WI-744 regression tests (`unresolved_name_blocks_the_run`, `ambiguous_symbol_blocks_the_run`, `catch_all_load_error_blocks_the_run`) still pass with their message anchors updated; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh (FULL workspace)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-18T06:13:40Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-746", author: "user", content: "REVIEW PASS (xhigh, 8 findings) — one REAL BUG found in my own first cut, now fixed.\n\nTHE BUG (self-inflicted, and the ticket's own thesis violated): the collector probed candidates with `candidate.is_file()`. That answers false not only for 'absent' but for a DANGLING SYMLINK, a directory wearing the name, and any stat failure — so a DECLARED data file that could not be read vanished with no diagnostic, exit 0, facts missing. VERIFIED LIVE both ways: dangling symlink at anthill.toml gave 'loaded: 2246 facts', exit 0. It was also a REGRESSION — the extension-walk it replaced collected such a path and let read_to_string fail loudly. Note fs_util.rs:58 tolerates the identical is_dir() gap, but its stated reason ('a dangling symlink would become a hard error — over-claiming files that were never ours') INVERTS at a conventional name, where the file is ours by definition. FIX: only io::ErrorKind::NotFound counts as absent; anything else is collected and the load site's read_to_string reports what is actually wrong. symlink_metadata not metadata, since following the link turns a dangling symlink back into NotFound and reopens the hole. Now: 'error: .../anthill.toml: read error: No such file or directory', exit 1; genuinely-absent still silent.\n\nTEST GAP (also fixed): all 6 original tests pinned a REFUSAL (blocked, or not-read) — NONE asserted that a valid anthill.toml's facts actually reach the KB. A regression that found the file and dropped its facts would have passed the whole suite. Added declared_data_reaches_the_kb (queries them back, not just a count — a count also rises if facts land malformed), unreadable_data_file_blocks_the_load (directory-named fixture; git cannot portably store a dangling symlink, both ride the same two lines), both_conventional_formats_load.\n\nOPEN, DELIBERATELY NOT DONE (judgement calls, not oversights):\n 1. ORDERING DEPENDENCY: conventional_data_files is silent on a missing/unstattable path and is safe only because collect_anthill_files runs first and rejects those. Documented, not enforced by the signature. A reordering or a second caller reinstates the bug class.\n 2. REUSE: anthill-todo/src/main.rs:137 already does PROJECT_MARKERS.iter().any(|m| dir.join(m).is_file()) — structurally the same 'known names in a directory' probe, INCLUDING the same is_file() gap now fixed here. Second copy of the pattern WI-747 spent a ticket collapsing; the is_file() fix arguably belongs there too.\n 3. ALTITUDE: 'which files constitute a project' landed in anthill-cli, not the fs_util home WI-747 established for exactly that. Counter-precedent is real (WI-747 kept POLICY wrappers per-binary, and PROJECT_MARKERS is per-binary), so this is defensible — flagged so it is a choice, not a default.\n 4. BUNDLE ASYMMETRY (pre-existing, now sharper): run_codegen_bundle collects only .anthill and never reaches conventional_data_files, so a project's anthill.toml facts are present under load/check/query and ABSENT from the generated crate. Predates this change; invisible today because no project uses data files. Needs a decision, possibly its own ticket.\n\nFULL WORKSPACE: 83 suites, 2973 passed, 0 failed via scripts/test.sh. NOT committed.", at: "2026-07-18T06:58:21Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-746", description: some(value: "DESIGN: make anthill data files OPT-IN instead of discovered by directory slurp — the residual WI-744 could not close. TODAY collect_data_files (anthill-cli/src/main.rs) recursively claims EVERY .toml/.json under any path handed to load/check/query/prove/codegen, descending into target/, node_modules/, .git/ and hidden dirs, and treats each as anthill data. That conflates 'in the directory' with 'addressed to us'. There is also NO way to opt out or in: collect_anthill_files REJECTS an explicitly-named .toml ('not an .anthill file'), so directory recursion is the ONLY channel by which a data file can be supplied.\n\nWI-744 made the load arm loud and that immediately exposed the conflation: an ordinary Cargo.toml gave 'error: missing meta: section has no meta key' and killed the load (VERIFIED live). The stopgap shipped in WI-744 is a SNIFF — term_ser::is_anthill_data / load_data_file — testing for the meta+data envelope: no envelope means not ours, so it is neither loaded nor complained about. That fixes the reported regression and is right as far as it goes.\n\nTHE RESIDUAL IT CANNOT CLOSE: intent is unobservable when data is DISCOVERED rather than declared. Two consequences, both accepted deliberately in WI-744 and both recorded at term_ser::load_data_file:\n  (1) A file that will not PARSE yields no envelope, hence no evidence it was ever ours, so it must be skipped silently. We tried the alternative (parse failure = Err) and it is untenable: a .vscode/settings.json or tsconfig.json with JSONC comments is not valid strict JSON, so it killed 'anthill load' for every VS Code user over a file the tool merely FOUND (VERIFIED: 'error: .../settings.json: format error: key must be a string at line 2 column 3', exit 1). So a file genuinely INTENDED as data, with a syntax error bad enough to stop parsing, is now dropped without a word — a silent skip, in the ticket whose whole point was to kill silent skips.\n  (2) A data file whose author forgot the meta envelope entirely reads as foreign and is likewise ignored.\nBoth dissolve the moment data is OPT-IN: 'the user said this is data' is the evidence a parse failure can be reported against. Sniffing is a proxy for intent; a declaration IS intent.\n\nLIKELY SHAPE (decide): (a) name data files on the command line — needs collect_anthill_files to stop rejecting non-.anthill paths and to route them to the data channel instead, which is a small change and gives an exact answer; (b) a conventional location (<project>/data/*.toml) auto-loaded, nothing else scanned; (c) a manifest/import declaring the project's data files, which is the most anthill-ish (the program says what it depends on) and composes with 007-persistence-layer. (a) and (b) are compatible. Whatever lands, the recursive unfiltered slurp goes away, and with it the descent into target/ (1585 .json files in this repo's own target/ today).\n\nKEEP the envelope sniff regardless — it is a cheap correctness guard for the conventional-location case, where a foreign file can still turn up. But once intent is declared, a NAMED data file that fails to parse must be a LOUD error again.\n\nACCEPTANCE: a .toml/.json is loaded as anthill data only when the user has said it is (named, conventional location, or declared); a NAMED data file that fails to parse or lacks the envelope is a loud error (intent is known, so silence is wrong); an unrelated Cargo.toml / package.json / .vscode/settings.json / target/**.json is never read, regardless of where it sits; anthill-cli/tests/load_cmd_test.rs's foreign_data_files_are_not_our_business still passes, and broken_data_file_blocks_the_load is extended to cover an unparseable NAMED data file; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-18T07:02:39Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-746", author: "user", content: "FOLLOW-UP DELIVERED (same ticket, not spun off): `anthill run` now reads the project's conventional data files.\n\nThe review's finding #4 was THREE claims and only ONE held. Corrected by reading the code rather than assuming:\n - `codegen rust` — NOT AFFECTED. generate_rust(&ParsedFile) per file, never builds a KB; it emits structs/enums from sort/entity DECLARATIONS. Data files carry FACTS, which play no part. I was wrong to list it.\n - `codegen bundle` — SHOULD NOT read, and the original claim was wrong on the merits. It vendors the program's .anthill SOURCE TEXT into a standalone crate. Data is the program's INPUT, not its code; baking anthill.toml in at bundle time FREEZES it, inverting the point of the .toml/.json channel (021: 'written by Tools' — data that changes independently of the logic). A bundled binary needing data should read it at ITS runtime — a separate capability, not a bundling gap.\n - `anthill run` — THE REAL GAP, and I had told the user it was fine. run::build_kb assembles its own KB (parse_user_files -> load_all) instead of going through load_kb_with_stdlib, so it never reached the data path. Same directory answered differently under `anthill query` (facts present) and `anthill run` (absent) — and unlike the bundle, run re-reads every invocation so nothing freezes.\n\nFIX: extracted the data-load block out of load_kb_with_stdlib into `load_conventional_data(kb, paths, verbose) -> Result<(), Vec<String>>`; both it and run::build_kb call it. Returns faults rather than printing+exiting because the callers use different exit codes (1 vs runner::EXIT_COMPILE). Extracted rather than copied — a second copy is exactly the WI-747 divergence, and the ORIGIN of this bug is that the KB was assembled in two places.\n\nTESTS: run_cmd_test +2 (11 -> 13). broken_data_blocks_the_run probes the WIRING (if run stops reading data the fixture is ignored and the program exits 0); valid_data_does_not_disturb_the_run is the control. What the facts DO once loaded stays pinned once, against the shared helper, by load_cmd_test::declared_data_reaches_the_kb — no reason to rebuild pattern_query machinery to re-test the same function. VERIFIED LIVE both ways before/after.\n\n021 updated: command list corrected to load/check/query/run/codegen-cpp, plus an explicit note on why codegen rust and codegen bundle abstain.\n\nFull workspace: 83 suites, 2975 passed, 0 failed. Remaining open findings from the review pass are unchanged (ordering dependency; anthill-todo:137 is_file gap; fs_util placement).", at: "2026-07-18T07:16:05Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-729", description: some(value: "WI-714 follow-up (F1): a method call on a BARE-QUALIFIED rule-ref value ('Queen.find.where(lambda ...)') is not re-routed to a dot-call and fails loudly as UnresolvedName. CONTEXT: user-confirmed 2026-07-15 during the WI-723 review. WI-714 landed all three citation positions, including bare-qualified 'Queen.find' (d43eba80: the LOADER collapses the field_access Ident-chain to the same var_ref(Ref(rule)) the bare-unqualified name lowers to, reusing check_bare_ref + reduce_var). But a METHOD CALL whose receiver is that multi-segment rule ref -- 'Queen.find.where(l)' -- is not re-routed: load.rs try_identifier_dot_call only probes the SINGLE-segment identifier, so the multi-segment prefix never reaches resolve_qualified_rule_readonly. This is WI-714 surface (a method call on a bare-qualified Sort.rule relation value), NOT a WI-443 limitation (WI-443 = local-identifier-rooted dot calls; its deferred multi-segment case is the local value-CHAIN p.x.m()). NARROW + workaround-able: bare in-scope 'person_row.where(l)' works, and 'let r = Queen.find; r.where(l)' works (WI-723), so nothing is blocked -- only the inline spelling. FIX DIRECTION (known precisely): in load.rs try_identifier_dot_call, on a single-segment miss, split the callee on the LAST dot and try resolve_qualified_rule_readonly(prefix) on the multi-segment prefix; on a hit, build the DotApply with the collapsed var_ref receiver. Reuse the shared read-only resolve_dotted_by_head(name, scope) probe (added in bb3d2f8e) so the bare + applied + method-call halves of Sort.rule cannot drift. Keep the Goal/Rule kind gate and the qualified_visible gate. ACCEPTANCE: 'Queen.find.where(lambda c -> ...)' loads, types and EVALS equal to the let-bound spelling; a value-receiver dot call is still re-routed FIRST (no regression); an identifier resolving to neither a local binding nor a rule stays the loud unknown-functor error (no silent fallback); cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-714"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-18T07:42:20Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-749", description: some(value: "WI-714 follow-up (F2): a ZERO-ARG member access on a rule-reference value ('person_row.isEmpty', 'Person.rows.isEmpty') is not re-routed to a dot call and fails loudly as 'isEmpty.name: expected resolved name, got unresolved'. CONTEXT: found by the /code-review pass on WI-729 (2026-07-18) and confirmed empirically. This is the ZERO-ARG dual of the method-call re-route, and it is INDEPENDENT of qualification: the bare-UNQUALIFIED 'person_row.isEmpty' fails too, so it predates WI-729 and is a WI-723/WI-280 gap, not a WI-729 regression. ROOT CAUSE (known precisely): WI-723 widened the METHOD-CALL receiver probe to accept a RULE (a rule reference IS a Relation[T] value, WI-714) -- load.rs dot_call_receiver falls back to resolve_qualified_rule_readonly -- but the FIELD-ACCESS path never got that widening: try_identifier_dot_field gates on field_access_root_is_value, which walks the chain to its root Ident and calls dot_receiver_binder, accepting ONLY a local binder / op param. A rule root returns None, so the chain stays a field_access, then try_qualified_rule_ref tries to resolve the WHOLE dotted name ('person_row.isEmpty') as a rule, which it is not, and it falls through to qualified-name flattening. NARROW + workaround-able: 'let r = person_row; r.isEmpty' works, so nothing is blocked -- only the inline zero-arg spelling. FIX DIRECTION: in try_identifier_dot_field (or field_access_root_is_value), when the receiver SUBCHAIN (everything but the final member) spells a name resolving to a rule via the shared read-only resolve_qualified_rule_readonly probe, build the zero-arg DotApply with the collapsed var_ref(Ref(rule)) receiver -- the same receiver dot_call_receiver synthesizes. Keep the Goal/Rule kind gate and the qualified_visible gate, and keep the LOCAL-value re-route ordered FIRST so a binder still shadows a same-named rule. Mind the ordering against try_qualified_rule_ref, which must still collapse a WHOLE-chain rule name ('Queen.find') to a bare reference. ACCEPTANCE: 'person_row.isEmpty' and 'Person.rows.isEmpty' load, type and EVAL equal to the let-bound spelling; a genuine field access on a local value still wins (no regression); a chain resolving to neither a local nor a rule stays the loud unknown-name error (no silent fallback); cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-729"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-18T08:53:00Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-750", description: some(value: "Converter+loader: a METHOD CALL whose receiver is a rule-reference CHAIN ('person_row.negate.takeN(5)', 'Queen.find.negate.where(lambda ...)') is not re-routed and fails loudly as 'unknown functor'. CONTEXT: found by the /code-review pass on WI-749 (2026-07-18), reproduced by four independent reviewers. WI-749 widened the ZERO-ARG field path to admit a receiver whose name has a PREFIX resolving to a rule, so member chains on a relation now re-route level by level ('person_row.negate.isEmpty' LOADS and evals equal to its let-bound spelling). The METHOD-CALL path was NOT widened and still reaches a rule receiver only at DEPTH 1: dot_call_receiver probes the WHOLE receiver name only. So the same receiver is a VALUE for 'x.m' and a static NAME PATH for 'x.m(args)' -- 'person_row.negate.isEmpty' works but 'person_row.negate.takeN(5)' does not. NOT a WI-749 regression (both spellings failed before it); WI-749 moved the field boundary without moving the call side. WHY IT MATTERS: takeN is the standard way to DRAIN a relation, so it is the first thing tried after the inline projection works; and it breaks the flagship 052 algebra chain 'person_row.negate.where(lambda c -> eq(c.name, \\\"alice\\\")).isEmpty', which fails with the exact WI-723 symptom ('<unresolved receiver>.name' -- the row lambda gets no schema). ROOT CAUSE (known precisely): parse/convert.rs push_fn_term flattens a dotted call CALLEE into ONE dotted functor symbol, destroying the receiver's parse node, whereas the 'name' arm keeps a dotted NAME as a nested field_access chain. So load.rs dot_call_receiver must return ONE Symbol to synthesize mk_var_ref(receiver_sym) and has no parse node to Visit -- structurally unable to express a chained receiver. This is the SAME shape as WI-443's deferred chained-LOCAL receiver, still open on both paths ('p.x.isEmpty' works, 'p.x.startsWith(...)' fails). FIX DIRECTION (preferred = b): (a) synthesize a NESTED DotApply receiver on the call path, lifting WI-443's deferral; (b) DEEPER: stop the converter flattening a dotted call callee -- have push_fn_term build the same structured field_access receiver the 'name' arm already builds, so BOTH paths share ONE structural receiver classifier and the loader stops maintaining two that must be diffed by eye (this asymmetry is the only reason two exist); (c) narrow the WI-749 field widening to the whole receiver name -- REJECTED: regresses WI-749 depth-2 and makes rule receivers behave unlike local ones. Prefer (b): it also closes WI-443's local chain and removes the drift hazard that produced WI-729 and WI-749. ACCEPTANCE: 'person_row.negate.takeN(5)', 'person_row.negate.where(lambda c -> ...)' and the full chain 'person_row.negate.where(lambda ...).isEmpty' load, type and EVAL equal to their let-bound spellings; the row lambda binder types at the CONCRETE schema through a chained receiver (the WI-729 discriminator, not an absorbing type var); if fixed via (b), the local chain 'p.x.startsWith(...)' works too; a receiver naming neither a local nor a rule stays the loud unknown-functor error (no silent fallback); WI-714/WI-723/WI-729/WI-749 suites unaffected; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-749"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-18T11:01:24Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-751", author: "user", content: "CORRECTION -- THE TICKET'S REPRO DOES NOT REPRODUCE. `rule anthill(?x) :- q(row: ?x)` loads clean at HEAD (402034c5), with or without the prelude import; the \"REPRO (verified)\" and \"A/B-confirmed IDENTICAL at 140246e7 and f3a31617\" claims are both false. A HEAD-PREDICATE rule does not take the name slot: scan_rule_goal (load.rs ~1711) mints the head-functor Goal ONLY when resolve_in_scope(functor_name) is NotFound, so with the namespace visible no Goal is defined and the head keeps resolving to the namespace. The declarations that DO occupy the head slot, measured: a LABELLED rule (`rule myroot: r(?x)`), a `sort`, an `operation`. A `let` binder does not (the head rung reads resolve_in_scope, which is blind to binders). The defect, mechanism and root cause were otherwise exactly as described -- it was never rule-specific, and the headline should read \"a scope-local declaration named after a namespace root\". PRECEDENCE ANSWERED, AGAINST THE TICKET'S HYPOTHESIS. The ticket proposed consulting by_qualified_name \"ahead of (or alongside)\" resolve_dotted_by_head. Ahead is WRONG and was verified so: with the absolute rung first, `x.foo()` inside `outer.user` silently returns the TOP-LEVEL `x.foo` (99) instead of the sibling `outer.x.foo` (41) -- WI-751's own disease in the opposite direction, any top-level namespace capturing paths a nearer same-rooted namespace already answers. Head-qualification is SCOPE-RELATIVE and must outrank a bare global path. Pinned by wi751_scope_relative_root_beats_a_top_level_namespace, which fails if the rungs are swapped. THE TRAP THAT COST A ROUND: \"head-qualification MISSED\" is NOT \"the name resolves to nothing\", so the rung is not purely additive (the first cut shipped that claim in a code comment as its justification). On a PARTIAL miss -- head resolving correctly to a nearer namespace, only a later segment absent -- it silently re-rooted the whole path at a same-spelled TOP-LEVEL namespace, detectable only by a downstream return-type mismatch. Caught by the /code-review pass, reproduced independently, then guarded by head_owns_path: skip the absolute rung when the path's head resolves in scope to a NAMESPACE (it owns every path beneath it, so a missing member is a member miss, not a licence to re-root) or resolves AMBIGUOUS (a reportable finding, not something to resolve past). DELIVERED AS FOUR CHANGES, not the one sketched: (1) absolute_qualified -- dotted-only by_qualified_name rung ranked BELOW head-qualification; (2) head_owns_path -- the partial-miss/ambiguous guard; (3) a SymbolKind::Field refusal in resolve_dotted_by_head -- entity fields register as <entity_qn>.<field>, so `sort data { entity user(name: Int64) }` supplies a complete <ns>.data.user.name and head-qualification HITS, capturing `data.user.name()` from namespace data.user; that is the HIT half of the defect and the absolute rung alone cannot reach it; (4) the same rung in resolve_qualified_rule_readonly -- shipping it only in remap_name_str_inner fixed the applied CALL and left the BARE rule-reference citation (`myroot.inner.rel.isEmpty`, `.takeN(1)`) broken, splitting the WI-729/749/750 citation forms that function exists to keep identical. LOUD-AMBIGUITY ALTERNATIVE EVALUATED AND DECLINED, and the ticket's premise for it is inaccurate: resolve_in_scope_recursive returns a LOCAL immediately (intern.rs ~491) and never consults parents, so Ambiguous arises only AMONG PARENTS -- local-vs-namespace-root is structurally not an ambiguity in that resolver, and local_shadows_parent pins local-wins as deliberate. Making it one is a new check that would reject every `sort data` / `operation app` sharing a spelling with a visible namespace root, breaking broad legitimate shadowing for a collision that only matters when a path under that root is actually written. Ladder documented in docs/kernel-language.md 8.6 (spec was silent on the dotted rungs entirely). STILL OPEN, all verified, deliberately out of scope -- worth their own tickets: (a) remap_name (TYPE position) has the absolute rung but NO head-qualification, so `util.T` fails where `util.f()` resolves -- the opposite ladder one position over, and fixing it adds a rung ABOVE an existing one, which can change what existing type names mean; (b) resolve_name_in_kb_opt (query patterns) ranks absolute FIRST with no dotted gate, so a query and the program it queries can bind the same text to different symbols; (c) qualified_name_resolves ranks absolute FIRST while the resolver ranks it LAST, so a re-route can decline on a hit the resolver would rank below a local reading; (d) a head-qualified hit that fails the internal gate is terminal and never falls through to the absolute rung. Tests: 9 in wi751_namespace_root_shadow_test.rs; full workspace 3013 passed / 0 failed via rustland/scripts/test.sh.", at: "2026-07-18T11:59:05Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-751", description: some(value: "Loader/name-resolution: a RULE named after a NAMESPACE ROOT SEGMENT disables every qualified reference under that root in its scope. REPRO (verified): namespace test.x with `rule anthill(?x) :- q(row: ?x)` and `operation useIt() -> List[T = Int64] = anthill.prelude.List.nil()` fails with `anthill.prelude.List.nil.apply: expected known operation or arrow-typed variable, got unknown functor`; the byte-identical control with the rule renamed to `notanthill` loads clean. Any user rule (or, by the same mechanism, any other scope-local symbol) sharing a name with a root namespace segment silently disables the whole import surface under that root -- action at a distance from one unrelated declaration, and the diagnostic names the qualified call rather than the colliding rule, so the cause is not discoverable from the error. ROOT CAUSE (known precisely): remap_name_str_inner (load.rs ~6645) resolves a dotted name by falling through resolve_in_scope -> NotFound -> resolve_dotted_by_head(name, scope) (load.rs ~6708), which splits the name on the FIRST dot and resolves the HEAD SEGMENT IN SCOPE. With `rule anthill` in the enclosing namespace the head `anthill` resolves to that RULE (nearer scope wins over the top-level namespace), so the probe appends the tail to the RULE's qualified path and looks up `test.x.anthill.prelude.List.nil`, which does not exist. The remaining fallbacks do not rescue it: implicit_qualified only covers the kernel/prelude short-name vocab, forbid_if_internal only reclassifies internal-visibility misses, so the name is interned as a bare unknown symbol (WI-476 path) and the typer reports `unknown functor`. NOT A WI-750 REGRESSION -- A/B-confirmed IDENTICAL at 140246e7 (pre-WI-750) and at f3a31617: same fixture, same error. WI-750 is adjacent only in that its first cut ALSO mis-decomposed this shape via the re-route prefix scan (`Relation.prelude: no such member`); the delivered gate stops the mis-decomposition and hands the name back to this resolver, where the pre-existing miss remains. THE ASYMMETRY THAT POINTS AT THE FIX: WI-750's loader-side probe `qualified_name_resolves` (load.rs, dot_call_receiver_chain) checks `by_qualified_name.contains_key(name)` BEFORE head-qualification, for exactly this reason -- an ABSOLUTE name stays a symbol's own qualified name no matter what a same-spelled rule does to the head. The MAIN resolver has no such check, so the re-route probe currently knows something about absolute names that remap_name_str_inner does not. FIX DIRECTION (starting hypothesis, settle precedence deliberately): consult by_qualified_name for the WHOLE dotted name in remap_name_str_inner's NotFound branch, ahead of (or alongside) resolve_dotted_by_head, subject to the same qualified_visible gate the head-qualified hit already passes through. THE DESIGN QUESTION IS PRECEDENCE, not plumbing: does a name that IS some symbol's fully-qualified name outrank a scope-local head that merely shares its first segment? Argument for yes -- the collision is between a WHOLE name and a strict PREFIX of it, and shadowing compares whole names (the same reasoning that settled WI-750's `let app = 1; app.helpers.Helper.twice(2)` regression). Mind the WI-749 footgun in the neighbourhood: a rule LABEL may itself be DOTTED (`rule a.b:` defines the LITERAL key `a.b` in scope locals), so a whole-name lookup and a scope lookup can BOTH hit for the same text and the order decides the answer -- do not add this as an unordered `or_else`, place it in a stated ladder as WI-750 did. CONSIDER ALSO (may be the better answer, or a companion): whether a rule/sort name colliding with a visible namespace root should be a LOUD load-time ambiguity rather than silently winning the head slot -- resolve_in_scope already returns Ambiguous for several neighbouring collision shapes, and that guard is what incidentally shields other cases (noted during the WI-750 review); making this collision explicit would fit the repo principle of a loud error over a silent wrong answer. ACCEPTANCE: the repro above loads and evaluates with `rule anthill` present; a scope-local rule/sort sharing a name with a namespace root no longer suppresses qualified references under that root (or is rejected loudly at load with a diagnostic naming the COLLISION, if that route is chosen instead); the chosen precedence is documented as an ordered ladder, not an incidental `or_else`, and a test pins the dotted-rule-LABEL interaction (rules `a` and `a.b` in scope, both spellings) so the WI-749 ordering footgun cannot reappear; WI-714/WI-723/WI-729/WI-749/WI-750 suites unaffected; cargo-test green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. SCOPE: Rust-only (scaland has no operation loading and no dot-call/name re-route). Found during the WI-750 /code-review pass (2026-07-18), reproduced by two independent reviewers and confirmed pre-existing by an A/B build against HEAD."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-18T11:59:16Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-753", description: some(value: "Diagnostics: per-SUBTERM spans in materialize_from_handle / build_expr_leaf (node_occurrence.rs:3788 / :3680) still read kb.term_span(t), which keys on the hash-consed TermId — a shared leaf (a bare Ref(x), a Const) interns to ONE TermId across every file, and term_spans is first-write-wins, so the materialized NodeOccurrence gets whichever file registered it first. This is the 'shared subterm' half of WI-458's title; WI-458 delivered only the fact/rule HEAD half (RuleEntry.head_span, keyed by RuleId). Filed as WI-727 originally — renumbered to WI-752 after fetch showed WI-727 was already taken upstream (variadic argument capture), then to WI-753 after a second collision: WI-752 was concurrently filed locally (unify the dotted-name ladder across all resolvers, delivered 2026-07-18) and filed first. Commit f51a8bdf's message still says WI-752 filed; this entry is that ticket. Live surface: build_body_atom_occurrence_inner (load.rs:~8809) falls back to materialize_from_handle for entity-headed and reflect-form body atoms, so those atoms' spans can point into a foreign file. Fix = narrow that fallback so body atoms build natively from the parse IR (which already yields correct per-occurrence spans, load.rs:8755). Non-trivial and already flagged in-code as later work: a native entity rebuild must reuse convert_term's MEMOIZED expansion or it mints different fresh vars than the head/term side (breaking var identity), and reflect forms need structural pattern handling."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-458", author: "claude", content: "Scope split. Delivered here: the fact/rule HEAD half of the title — RuleEntry.head_span (Option<SourceSpan>), set by the loader right after assert (set_rule_head_span, mirroring set_rule_label), keyed by RuleId so it cannot cross-file-alias. The three typing.rs head-error paths read it; the term_span(head) fallback was DROPPED because create_occurrence only ever records op-body subterms and FACT heads — never a rule head — so a term_span(rule_head) hit could only be a foreign aliased span (None beats a confident wrong file:line). Regression test: tests/include/wi458_head_span_occurrence_test.rs (in the WI-244 wi_tests aggregator) — two facts in different namespaces whose heads intern to ONE TermId (so term_span returns one shared source for both) now resolve to their own files.\n\nREBASE NOTE (2026-07-18): rebased onto origin/main, which had advanced 30 commits. One conflict, in check_entity_facts: WI-745 had meanwhile started reading THAT SAME head-span lookup to attribute an error's FILE (its cur_src). Resolved by feeding WI-745's head_ss from rule_head_span(rid) — so the aliasing bug was corrupting not just the offsets but the file label WI-745 prints. The two fixes compose; neither is redundant.\n\nSplit out, NOT covered here: WI-753 = the shared-SUBTERM half (materialize_from_handle leaf spans still term_span-keyed). NOT filed: the LoadError-has-no-SourceId item (the real cause of the symptom THIS WI's title cited — an UnresolvedName never touches term_spans at all) turned out to be WI-745, already delivered upstream; its writeup cites the same 'byte 220 OF WHICH FILE?' symptom. So WI-458's cited evidence was a WI-745 bug, and its stated directive was this one.", at: "2026-07-18T11:30:00Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-458", description: some(value: "Diagnostics: error SPAN side-table keys on hash-consed TermId, so a shared subterm's span resolves to whichever file registered it FIRST — an error about a term in file A can print a span pointing into file B (observed in WI-445: an UnresolvedName for a test-source 'x' printed list.anthill 1945..1946). Span lookup should key on the OCCURRENCE (per-use), not the hash-consed TermId. Surfaced while debugging WI-445; not blocking (the WI-445 fix removed that particular error)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-18T12:52:49Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-752", description: some(value: "Loader/name-resolution: UNIFY THE DOTTED-NAME LADDER ACROSS ALL RESOLVERS. Six resolvers in kb/load.rs resolve a dotted name, and they use FIVE different ladders; the divergence looks like accident, not design, and it is the drift that produced WI-729, WI-749, WI-750 and WI-751 in sequence -- each of those fixed ONE resolver and left the others spelling the same question differently. VERIFIED SYMPTOM (the headline, user-visible): the same spelling in the same scope resolves in TERM position and fails in TYPE position. `namespace app.util { sort T { entity t(v: Int64) } ; operation f() -> Int64 = 1 }` plus `namespace app { operation callSite() -> Int64 effects Error = util.f() ; operation typeSite(x: util.T) -> Int64 = 2 }` -- `util.f()` resolves via head-qualification, `util.T` fails `unresolved type name 'util.T' in scope 'typeSite'`. Cause: remap_name (load.rs 6877), the resolver for structured multi-segment Names (types, sort refs, entity/op names, proof targets), has the ABSOLUTE by_qualified_name rung but NO head-qualification rung at all, so it is missing the very rung that makes the term path work. INVENTORY (ladder per resolver, after WI-751): remap_name_str_inner 6645 (term functors/identifiers) = scope -> head-qual -> absolute(guarded) -> implicit -> forbid -> intern, the reference ladder; resolve_qualified_rule_readonly 8586 (bare + prefix rule citations) = scope -> head-qual -> absolute, agrees; qualified_name_resolves 8286 (the dot-call re-route gate) = ABSOLUTE FIRST -> scope -> head-qual, so it ranks absolute first while the resolver it gates ranks it last; remap_name 6877 (types/structured names) = scope -> absolute, NO head-qual; resolve_name_in_kb_opt 5647 (query patterns, a free fn over &KnowledgeBase) = ABSOLUTE FIRST -> scope -> implicit, no dotted gate, and it is the only one that resolves SHORT names absolutely (the WI-476 global-scan hazard); remap_symbol_strict 6845 (strict refs) = scope only, neither dotted rung. FOURTH ITEM, same family: a head-qualified hit that fails the `internal` visibility gate is TERMINAL -- accept_qualified_hit 6832 pushes ForbiddenInternalAccess and returns, never falling through to the absolute rung -- so a shadowing declaration carrying an `internal` member of the right name still breaks the path AND names a symbol the user never wrote (`namespace lib { operation util() }` + `sort lib { internal operation util() }` in the citing scope: reports \"'lib.util' is internal to '<ns>.lib'\"; rename only the internal member and the identical file loads clean via the absolute rung). CONSEQUENCES BEYOND THE TYPE-POSITION BUG: the qualified_name_resolves priority inversion lets the re-route DECLINE on the strength of an absolute hit that the resolver would have ranked BELOW a local reading, so a local rule receiver can be captured by a same-spelled foreign namespace; and loader-vs-query divergence means `anthill query` can bind the same dotted text to a different symbol than the program it queries. WHY ONE TICKET, NOT FOUR: the fix is a single shared ladder, and settling precedence four times independently is how the current five-way split happened. WHY THIS WAS NOT FOLDED INTO WI-751 (deliberate, not deferral-by-default): WI-751's fix was safe precisely because its new rung sits BELOW an existing one and fires only where nothing resolved -- strictly additive, no existing program changes meaning. Adding head-qualification to remap_name puts a rung ABOVE the existing absolute one, so a type name that resolves absolutely TODAY could start resolving scope-relatively; that is a silent meaning change for existing code and needs its own decision plus regression sweep. Same for demoting the absolute rung in qualified_name_resolves / resolve_name_in_kb_opt. THE DESIGN QUESTION, to settle ONCE: is WI-751's term-position ladder (scope-relative head-qualification OUTRANKS the bare absolute path; the absolute rung stands down when the head resolves to an owning NAMESPACE or resolves AMBIGUOUS; dotted-only, never short names) correct for ALL positions -- or do types / query patterns / the re-route gate have a principled reason to differ? Prior: they do not, and any survivor should be an explicitly documented exception. Note ONE known-deliberate exception to preserve: qualified_name_resolves is intentionally NOT visibility-gated and counts Ambiguous as resolving, because it asks 'does the qualified path have an ANSWER' (a forbidden-internal reference IS an answer, with a precise diagnostic that peeling the name into a member chain would bury). ACCEPTANCE: the dotted ladder is expressed ONCE and consulted by every resolver that resolves a dotted name, with any deviation carrying a stated reason at the deviating site; `util.T` and `util.f()` in the repro above resolve identically; the loader and resolve_name_in_kb_opt bind the same dotted text to the same symbol; a head-qualified hit hidden by `internal` falls through to the absolute rung rather than terminating (or the termination is justified in place); a test fixture exercises EVERY position -- term functor, type reference, rule citation, re-route gate, query pattern -- against the same source and asserts they agree, so a future rung cannot be added to one and forgotten in the others; docs/kernel-language.md 8.6 updated to describe the ladder as position-independent; WI-714/WI-723/WI-729/WI-749/WI-750/WI-751 suites unaffected; cargo-test green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. SCOPE: Rust-only (scaland has no operation loading and no dot-call/name re-route). Found during the WI-751 /code-review pass (2026-07-18); every item above was reproduced against a built CLI, not inferred."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-751"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-18T13:05:47Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-752", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED 535595f4. The ladder is ONE free fn resolve_dotted_in_kb(kb, name, scope, vis) over &KnowledgeBase -- a free fn because resolve_name_in_kb_opt is one; the loader binds current_scope via a thin resolve_dotted() wrapper. All six resolvers read it. DESIGN MOVE not in the ticket: visibility belongs to the LADDER, not the rung. accept_qualified_hit gated each rung in place and RETURNED on a hidden hit; that is what made the internal case terminal. Replaced by a DottedVisibility param -- VisibleOnly skips a hidden hit and keeps descending, and a separate forbid_if_dotted_internal (an Any pass) reports it only when no rung had a visible answer, so the precise diagnostic still stands (pinned by wi752_internal_with_no_other_reading_still_reports). The ticket framed the fourth item as 'fall through to the absolute rung'; generalizing it to the whole ladder is what makes it hold for any future rung. TEST HONESTY: 4 of 7 tests fail against baseline (measured by reverting load.rs); the other 3 are labelled in-file as uniformity/loophole guards so they are not mistaken for bug detectors. One of those three initially carried a FALSE rationale -- I wrote 'verified by flipping it' from reasoning, and flipping Any->VisibleOnly did not fail the test, because the re-route gate only matters when the DECOMPOSITION would succeed; with nothing in the citing scope naming the root, dot_receiver_binder and rule_prefix_split fail anyway and the diagnostic comes from the resolver regardless. Fixture rebuilt with 'rule lib:' so the gate's verdict is the only thing deciding; then it discriminates (VisibleOnly yields Relation.hidden ... no such member). Lesson: an error-assertion test can pass for a different reason than you think. WHY THE QUERY DIVERGENCE HID FOR FOUR TICKETS: anthill query runs at _global, where a head resolves to a top-level namespace so head-qualification and the absolute reading COINCIDE. The discriminating caller is contract_proof_target_qn (load.rs ~13688), which calls resolve_name_in_kb_opt at a NAMESPACE scope for the <op> prefix of a contract-proof target -- that is what the regression test exercises. REVIEW FINDING FIXED: the ladder resolved the head segment twice per dotted miss (dotted_by_head, then head_owns_path), rebuilding the Ambiguous candidate list both times; now resolved once and shared. Full workspace green: 3021 passed on the merged tree, WI-714/723/729/749/750/751 suites unaffected. NOT FIXED, PRE-EXISTING, unrelated to this ticket but found while probing and reproduced on unmodified main -- may warrant their own tickets: (1) 'import' does not parse at TOP LEVEL, so the CLI's -i/--import flag emits unparseable source and every 'anthill query --import ...' fails at parse; (2) 'anthill query -p f.anthill \"app.util.q(row: ?x)\"' returns 0 results for a fact that exists.", at: "2026-07-18T13:06:14Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-752", author: "claude", content: "CORRECTION to the delivery feedback above. It listed TWO pre-existing defects found while probing. Only ONE is real, and it is now WI-754 (a query pattern cannot use imports at all: top-level `import` is not in the grammar's `_declaration` set, and the namespace-wrapper workaround is closed because collect_queries only collects TOP-LEVEL facts). The SECOND item was MY USAGE ERROR, not a defect: I reported that `anthill query -p f.anthill 'app.util.q(row: ?x)'` returns 0 results for a fact that exists. An entity declared inside `sort Q` registers under `<ns>.Q.q`, so the correct spelling is `app.util.Q.q(row: ?x)` -- which returns `q(row: 7) [?x = 7]` correctly. The sort segment is part of the entity's qualified name; `app.util.q` genuinely names nothing. Caught by checking before filing rather than after, which is the WI-751 lesson (that ticket shipped with a repro that did not reproduce). Recording the retraction here because the claim is in this ticket's history and would otherwise read as an open defect.", at: "2026-07-18T13:18:59Z")
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-755", name: "wi502")
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-756", name: "wi502")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-756", description: some(value: "Proof-path coverage for override-aware eq: verify (and test) that the direct prove_from_gamma sites decide eq over a CUSTOM-eq carrier semantically, not structurally. This is WI-573's recorded 'RESIDUAL / review Finding C', filed separately as WI-573 flagged it out of its own guarded-effect-discharge scope. BACKGROUND: WI-573's override gate lives in the typer's conjunct_refuted (typing.rs ~21503) and therefore covers ONLY the guard-refutation route (refute_guard, typing.rs:1156-1158). The other prove_from_gamma call sites do NOT pass through that gate: (1) typing.rs:5471 — WI-538 'proof .. by derivation' (Expr::Proof, tier-A inline proof over Gamma union KB); (2) typing.rs:21871 — WI-539 precondition_proved, the call-site 'requires' obligation; (3) proof_verify.rs:602 — WI-558 contract 'ensures' discharge. NOTE Finding C named only sites (1) and (3) using pre-drift line numbers ('typing.rs ~3669' is now 5471); site (2), precondition_proved, was MISSED and must be included — it is the exact positive dual of precondition_refuted (typing.rs:21893), which DOES route through the gated conjunct_refuted, so the two polarities are asymmetric today and that asymmetry is itself worth asserting on. POLARITY OF THE RISK, which differs from WI-573's: the guarded-effect hazard was an unsound REFUTATION dropping an effect that should stay; here the hazard is an unsound PROOF — a precondition deemed satisfied, or an 'ensures' conjunct deemed derivable, on a structural eq verdict the carrier's own eq would contradict. CURRENT BELIEF (must be confirmed, not assumed): the gap appears to have closed INDIRECTLY when WI-616 made eq/neq semantic (BuiltinTag::SemEq/SemNeq -> sem_eq_core, resolve.rs:4010), since all four sites resolve THROUGH the resolver and so inherit its dispatch/delay discipline rather than a typer gate. If that holds, this ticket is characterization-and-regression work, not a fix. If it does NOT hold on some path, it is a live soundness bug in the WI-538/WI-539/WI-558 proof paths and should be fixed here. THE REAL GAP TODAY IS TEST COVERAGE: nothing exercises a custom-eq carrier on ANY proof path — none of WI-538/WI-539/WI-558 carry acceptance criteria mentioning one, and wi573_eq_override_discharge_test covers only guarded effects. Note also that WI-621 refactored precondition_proved/precondition_refuted/conjunct_refuted/guarded_atom_refuted to thread Value end-to-end but changed no eq semantics. Acceptance: for EACH of the three sites, a test with a carrier whose eq override DISAGREES with structural equality (the CUSTOM_EQ_TRUE_PRELUDE / STRUCTURAL_EQ_PRELUDE fixture pattern in tests/include/wi573_eq_override_discharge_test.rs) asserts the override decides — a 'proof by derivation', a call-site requires, and a contract ensures each resolve per the carrier's eq, NOT structurally; a non-ground or buried-override operand stays UNPROVED (definite_only maps the resolver's Delay to unproven — the obligation default) rather than being proved structurally; any path found deciding structurally is FIXED, not documented; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-755", description: some(value: "Retire the now-STALE WI-573 typer pre-gate so guarded-effect discharge reaches semantic-eq dispatch (WI-573's deferred 'option (a)'). WI-573 shipped a SOUND FLOOR on 2026-06-29: guard_conjunct_overrides_structural_eq (typing.rs, called from the shared per-conjunct core conjunct_refuted ~21503) returns false — SUSPEND, keep the effect — whenever an eq/neq operand's carrier, at top level or at any nested element/field, has a custom eq/neq member. That was correct AT THE TIME because anthill.prelude.PartialEq.eq was a purely STRUCTURAL builtin that never dispatched an override, so any refutation it produced was unsound. THAT PREMISE HAS SINCE EXPIRED. WI-616 (delivered 2026-07-03, with WI-625/WI-628/WI-664) made eq/neq SEMANTIC: they register as BuiltinTag::SemEq/SemNeq (kb/mod.rs:5435,5437), dispatch at resolve.rs:3159 into sem_eq_core (resolve.rs:4010) -> sem_eq_values (4029), whose ordered outcomes (4059-4081) are exactly the discipline WI-573 wanted: (3) no override in the KB -> structural; (4) head-carrier override over DEEP-GROUND operands -> sem_eq_dispatch (resolve.rs:4168) proves <carrier>.eq(a,b) by bounded sub-resolution, routing a bodied override through the eval bridge and a rules-backed one through prove_rule_predicate; (5) BURIED override -> delay rather than mis-decide (its doc at resolve.rs:4002 cites 'the WI-573 reasoning' by name); (6) otherwise structural. The load-time dispatch index (load.rs:4680-4726) is keyed on every constructor of a carrier whose carrier_own_op(.., eq) exists — i.e. precisely the WI-573 case. SO THE TYPER PRE-GATE NOW SHADOWS THE VERY DISPATCH IT WAS WRITTEN TO WAIT FOR: it short-circuits before refute_guard -> prove_from_gamma -> resolver, so the guard path can never reach SemEq. The in-code deferral comments still say 'until runtime monomorphization can dispatch the override' (typing.rs:21500-21501) and 'the deferred half (the ticket's option (a))' (21663-21665). SCOPE: narrow or remove guard_conjunct_overrides_structural_eq and let SemEq/SemNeq decide. SOUNDNESS ARGUMENT TO PRESERVE: the resolver returns Delay for non-deep-ground operands and for a buried override, and prove_from_gamma's definite_only maps a delay to UNPROVEN — the conservative direction — so the floor WI-573 established is kept by the resolver rather than by the typer. Verify that mapping holds before deleting the gate; if any path turns a delay into a definite verdict, narrow the gate to just that path instead of removing it. WATCH: WI-648 (modular typeclasses, scoped/non-canonical instances) would change WHICH override is canonical and thus interacts with a dispatch-based decision. THIS IS THE DISCHARGE CAPABILITY WI-566 AND WI-567 ACTUALLY NEED — both currently list WI-573 as satisfied and so read as unblocked, but the capability they depend on is this ticket, not the delivered floor. Acceptance: the existing characterization test wi573_eq_override_discharge_test::custom_eq_override_suspends_rather_than_dispatches (tests/include/wi573_eq_override_discharge_test.rs:176) FLIPS to assert the DROP — its own comment already says 'If this ever starts dropping, the dispatch half (option (a)) has landed and this test should move to assert the drop'; a custom eq override returning true still KEEPS the effect (custom_eq_override_keeps_effect, :98) and the nested-carrier keeps (:228, :271) stay green; native/structural carriers still discharge (:124, :317); a non-ground or buried-override operand still SUSPENDS rather than deciding; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-756"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-566", description: some(value: "Proposal 048 Phase 4 — Generalize guarded Error-effects to the remaining partial stdlib operations (head, nth, last, tail, and any other op that can fail on a precondition), migrating each from an unconditional Error payload to a guarded one { Error[E] :- guard } the way WI-479 did for Int64.div/mod/rem/divExact + Field.div/recip (Phase 3). The 048 MECHANISM is fully delivered and reusable UNCHANGED: grammar+representation (WI-478 Phase 1), typer discharge over Gamma (WI-067 Phase 2), and the migration pattern itself (WI-479 Phase 3) — so Phase 4 is pure stdlib content plus tests, no new typer/loader code (cf. WI-479 which added zero engine code). DEMAND-DRIVEN per the proposal (048 §Phasing item 4: 'apply to other partial stdlib operations as their error payloads are introduced'): the trigger is an op acquiring an Error payload (e.g. head on an empty list -> Error[EmptyList] guarded by isEmpty(l)). NOT actionable until such payloads exist — today head/nth/etc. are total-by-type or panic, not Error-typed; introducing the payload is the prerequisite and is the natural place to also add the guard. Guard vocabulary is the decidable boolean fragment refutable under step_cap (048 OQ-A: isEmpty/outOfBounds/eq/neq). Acceptance (per migrated op, as it lands): the op declares { Error[E] :- guard }; a call with a statically-refuted guard (literal/flow-fact) type-checks pure; a call that can't refute keeps the effect; cargo-test green. See docs/proposals/048-conditional-effects.md §Phasing(4) + §Generalization. Builds on WI-479 (Phase 3 precedent) + WI-067 (discharge)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-479", "WI-573", "WI-755"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-567", description: some(value: "Refactor head into a total headOption + a guarded-Error head (a concrete proposal-048 Phase 4 subject). Provide BOTH: headOption(...) -> Option[T] (the safe/total form — absence is the value none) AND head(...) -> T @ { Error[EmptyList] :- isEmpty(...) } (the ergonomic form — returns the element directly; the 048 guard discharges to PURE where the typer can refute isEmpty, and stays a must-handle Error otherwise). Motivation: Option alone is sound but forces unwrapping even where non-emptiness is statically provable; the guarded head gives direct-T ergonomics that are statically safe-where-provable — strictly better than a throwing/panicking head. LOAD-BEARING SCOPING (derived in design discussion 2026-06-25): the guarded head belongs on the CONCRETE List, NOT the abstract/lazy Stream. Reason: the 048 discharge can only refute a guard via (1) literal abstract-interp on a visible constructor, (2) a verbatim-matching flow-fact in Gamma, or (3) KB resolution under step_cap. On a concrete List, isEmpty(cons(h,t)) folds to false and isEmpty(nil) to true (channel 1), and if not(isEmpty(l)) then head(l) discharges via channel 2 — so head types pure exactly where expected. On the abstract/lazy Stream, isEmpty is itself an EFFECTFUL observation (carries effect E, like splitFirst) with no static value, so the guard NEVER discharges and the Error would stick everywhere (worse than Option) — Stream must keep the Option/headOption form. So: Stream keeps headOption (rename/keep its current Option head); add a guarded head ONLY on concrete List (plus headOption on List for the branch-on-presence case). The current stdlib head lives on Stream (stream.anthill:24, returns Option, derived from splitFirst) and there is NO List.head today. NEEDS: an EmptyList error payload (sort/effect); verify isEmpty discharges on a cons literal and via an if not(isEmpty) flow-fact. Acceptance: head(cons(...)) and head inside if not(isEmpty(l)) type PURE (no residual Error); head on a possibly-empty l keeps Error[EmptyList] (must handle); headOption returns Option[T]; cargo-test green. Concrete instance/demonstrator of WI-566 (048 Phase 4); builds on WI-067 (discharge) + WI-479 (migration precedent)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-067", "WI-502", "WI-573", "WI-755"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Open)
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-573", author: "claude", content: "FOLLOW-ONS NOW FILED (2026-07-18). This ticket's two deferrals were prose-only and untracked; they are now tickets. (1) The DEFERRED 'option (a)' (runtime-monomorphization dispatch so a custom-override guard can DISCHARGE, not just suspend) = WI-755. IMPORTANT UPDATE TO THIS TICKET'S PREMISE: option (a) is no longer 'build the dispatch' — WI-616 (delivered 2026-07-03, with WI-625/628/664) already built it, making eq/neq semantic (BuiltinTag::SemEq/SemNeq -> sem_eq_core, resolve.rs:4010; sem_eq_dispatch at :4168 already implements dispatch-on-ground / delay-on-buried-override, and its doc cites 'the WI-573 reasoning' by name). So the sound floor delivered HERE — guard_conjunct_overrides_structural_eq, called from conjunct_refuted (typing.rs ~21503) — has become STALE: it short-circuits before refute_guard -> prove_from_gamma -> resolver, shadowing the very dispatch it was written to wait for. WI-755 is therefore 'retire/narrow the gate', not 'implement dispatch'. (2) The RESIDUAL 'review Finding C' (direct prove_from_gamma sites deciding eq structurally over a custom carrier) = WI-756. Finding C's line numbers have drifted ('typing.rs ~3669' is now 5471) and it MISSED a third site: precondition_proved (typing.rs:21871, WI-539), the positive dual of precondition_refuted (:21893) which DOES route through the gated conjunct_refuted — so the two polarities are asymmetric today. WI-756 covers all three sites (typing.rs:5471 WI-538 'by derivation', typing.rs:21871 WI-539 requires, proof_verify.rs:602 WI-558 ensures); the gap likely closed indirectly via WI-616, making it characterization-and-regression work unless a path is found deciding structurally. ORDERING: WI-756 comes FIRST and WI-755 depends on it — WI-756 confirms the exact premise WI-755 relies on (that SemEq decides correctly through prove_from_gamma), so verifying it de-risks removing the gate. WI-566 and WI-567 now also depend on WI-755: they listed only this delivered ticket and so read as UNBLOCKED, but the discharge capability they need is WI-755, not the suspend-only floor delivered here.", at: "2026-07-18T13:43:25Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-730", description: some(value: "WI-714 follow-up: row-lambda CONDITION compiler -- support boolean nesting (&&, ||, !) and multiple predicates, beyond the single atomic predicate the where/join first increments accept. CONTEXT: proposal 052 'Compiling a row lambda into a query'. where (681ee37a) and join (6185dbc2) both compile a ROW LAMBDA into a LogicalQuery goal at compile time via the WI-722 macro route (where <=> guarded_of [simp]; join <=> conjoin_of [simp]), each reading the typed lambda body and minting column HOLES that the runtime back-end (where_run / join_run) fills with the relation's real column vars. Both FIRST increments accept only a SINGLE ATOMIC PREDICATE -- 'eq(c.x, 1)' / 'pred(c.x, q.y)'. 052's tree->query mapping (the LINQ Expression->SQL analog) specifies the rest: &&/||/! -> conjunction/disjunction/negation of goals, composing the same atoms. GAP: 'r.where(lambda c -> and(eq(c.age, 30), eq(c.name, \"alice\")))' is not compiled. The compiler locus is compile_condition / compile_operand (eval/builtins.rs), which today recognize field_access(binder, Const(String(field))) operands + one predicate head. FIX DIRECTION: recurse in compile_condition over the boolean spine -- map the logical connectives to the LogicalQuery constructors already wired in kb/execute.rs (conjunction / disjunction / negation), with the atoms compiled exactly as now; anything outside the goal-expressible Bool subset stays a LOUD compile error (052: the analog of LINQ's 'cannot translate to SQL'), never a silent drop or a runtime lambda application. Watch: negation of a goal with a FREE column var flounders under NAF (the WI-728 negate hazard) -- a ! over a column-bearing atom needs the same care. ACCEPTANCE: where + join accept &&/||/! nesting and multiple atoms, each lowering to its LogicalQuery constructor and filtering correctly end-to-end (eval tests, keep + drop directions so the guard is proven to constrain rather than be vacuously true); a non-goal-expressible condition (an if, a non-predicate call) is a loud compile error; the existing wi714 where/join tests stay green; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-714"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-18T14:07:03Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-734", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. DECISION: residual-on-abstract, loud-on-concrete, settled once for the family. An operand that is NOT YET KNOWN leaves the ctor SYMBOLIC; an operand that IS known but unmergeable stays LOUD. \"Not yet known\" = a logic variable (ViewHead::Var -- MEASURED: a generic op's [S] reaches the reducer as Var::Rigid) OR an unreduced residual one level down. Deliberately NOT keyed on TypeExtractor::Error: that is a total-ness catch-all covering malformed types too, so keying on it would silently convert real errors into residuals. Locus: operand_not_yet_known + a guard arm in reduce_binary_type_ctor (kb/typing.rs). TWO TICKET PREMISES WERE FALSE (measured, not reasoned): (1) \"which would let generic wrappers over join type-check\" is WRONG -- a generic op calling join with abstract schemas fails EARLIER at row-lambda column resolution ('type mismatch in <unresolved receiver>.name ... no such member (dot dispatch)'), so Concat is never reached; and a wrapper that FORWARDS the lambda is blocked differently ('type mismatch in conjoin_of.cond (op-arg): expected NodeOccurrence') because the WI-722 macro needs the lambda syntactically. Generic join wrappers stay blocked either way -- this change does not buy them. The live case is `fix` (no lambda): `wrapFix[S](p: Relation[T = S]) -> Relation = p.fix(name: \"a\")`. (2) An abstract operand was never a DECLARATION-time problem: an op whose signature writes Concat[A = L, B = R] over its own type params ALREADY loaded clean, because reduction fires only at CALL sites. VERIFIED END-TO-END (this was the load-bearing claim, so it was measured, not argued): a residual CONFORMS in a generic wrapper, and REDUCES once its operands ground -- a concrete call to the wrapper resolves a column from EACH operand (m.(name, dept)), which only the merged schema admits. Negative control: an unknown column is still rejected after grounding. ESCAPE HAZARD CHECKED: widening a wrapper to a bare `-> Relation` lets a residual escape unreduced (the gate reads the DECLARED return). A/B-verified this is SAFE, not a silent wrong schema -- a column use on an escaped residual, dropped or kept alike, fails LOUDLY at dot dispatch, because an unreduced ctor offers no named-tuple schema. Widening costs usability, never correctness. PART 2 -- relation_ref_arg_type MUST STAY, and the reason is ORDERING, not oversight (the acceptance's \"or a comment records why it must stay\" branch). A/B-verified: dropping the rung from projection_receiver_type fails all five wi714_join tests with '<unresolved receiver>.name/.who/.dept'. Its consumer is the row-lambda's callback param HINT; at the DotApply frame only the RECEIVER is pre-typed (WI-443), so when join(r1, lambda (c,q) -> ..) hints q with r2's schema, r2 has not been typed yet and carries NO stamp. A stamp cannot satisfy a reader that runs before the producer; retiring it would require pre-typing sibling args, which is what WI-443 removed. Rationale recorded on the fn. EXTRA (from /code-review, serves WI-732 + WI-759 directly): the family was enumerated in TWO independent places (return_reducible_ctors' hardcoded (bool,bool), the reduction boundary's if-chain) and the first draft added a THIRD. Now ONE const BINARY_TYPE_CTORS drives both -- the gate returns a positional [bool; N] and the boundary FOLDS over the family -- so adding Project[T,keep] or FieldOf is one line instead of three edits where missing one silently disables reduction. Also hoisted the ctor-symbol resolution out of the per-operand path (try_resolve_symbol is string-keyed; the gate exists precisely to keep that off the common path). TESTS: 7 in wi734_abstract_operand_test.rs -- Concat abstract-symbolic, residual-reduces-when-grounded, unknown-column negative control, Concat concrete-collision-loud, NESTED residual (the second not-yet-known shape, which a bare-variable test cannot reach), DENOTED operand still loud (a value can never be a named tuple = cannot-reduce-EVER, pinning that the rule did not over-broaden), Without abstract-symbolic via the real fix path. Full workspace: 3038 passed, 0 failed. Docs updated on both stdlib ctors (sort.anthill) + reduce_binary_type_ctor. LEFT OPEN (judgment call, not in acceptance): the residual escape via a widened return is documented but not DIAGNOSED -- the user learns of it at a far-away use site rather than at the widening. A warning at the widening point would name the actual cause; CLAUDE.md's loud-over-silent principle arguably wants one.", at: "2026-07-18T15:15:28Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-734", description: some(value: "WI-714 follow-up (cleanups from the join review): (1) Concat abstract-operand NEUTRAL behavior. Concat[A, B] is a bare type CONSTRUCTOR (sort.anthill, no entities, inert like Type) reduced by the internal typer op reduce_concat_in_type at the return-type normalization boundary. Its behavior when an operand is ABSTRACT (a generic wrapper whose schema is not yet known -- e.g. a Relation param inside a generic op, not a concrete named-tuple) is undecided: today it is a loud error in non-arg position. Decide whether an unreduced Concat over an abstract operand should stay SYMBOLIC (reduce later, once the operand grounds -- the neutral/lazy reading, which would let generic wrappers over join type-check) or remain a loud error. The same question applies to Without[T, Drop] (WI-727, the dual) and to any Project[T, keep] (WI-732), so settle it ONCE for the family rather than per-constructor. (2) relation_ref_arg_type -> stamp cleanup. relation_ref_arg_type (kb/typing.rs) computes a bare RULE-ref argument's Relation[T] ON DEMAND so a sibling callback param can project it at hint time (join's r2: varref_arg_env_type only covered a stamped receiver). That is a recompute where a STAMP would do -- align it with how the receiver path already stamps inferred_type, so a bare rule-ref arg and a bare rule-ref receiver share ONE mechanism instead of two. Both are review follow-ups from the join increment (6185dbc2), neither a correctness bug: /code-review (5 agents) found no correctness defect in join. ACCEPTANCE: Concat/Without/(Project) share one settled, documented rule for abstract operands, with a test pinning it in each direction; relation_ref_arg_type's on-demand recompute is retired in favor of the stamp (or a comment records why it must stay); existing wi714_join / wi727_fix tests green; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-714"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-18T15:15:30Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-759", description: some(value: "Retire the LAST two op-name-keyed typer hatches (field_access_projection_type, field_access_owning_ctor) and the INC-1b/mode-3 STAMP by making field_access's return type SELF-CORRECTING via a reducible type constructor -- and supply the enabling primitive (a compile-time NAME in TYPE-ARGUMENT position) that WI-732's Project[T, keep] needs too. DISCIPLINE: the repo has an explicit \"no op-name-keyed typer code\" rule -- stdlib/anthill/prelude/relation.anthill:256-262 (\"NOTHING in the typer keyed on fix's identity ... the alternative was an op_sym == Relation.fix recognizer, which that discipline forbids\") and typing.rs:1741-1743 (\"keyed on the constructor's sort symbol ... never keyed on a domain operation's identity\"). check_apply_iter's ladder has exactly THREE name-keyed early returns: :7290 field_access_owning_ctor, :7298 field_access_projection_type, :7311 relation_projection_type -- verified EXHAUSTIVE (the only fn_sym ==/!= guards in typing.rs are :3737, :7019, :7081). relation_projection_type carries a self-disclaimer (:3719-3728, \"a guard, not a hot path\"); the two field_access hatches carry none. DIAGNOSIS: the desugared node is ill-typed against its OWN declared signature field_access(object: Term, field: Symbol) -> Term (reflect.anthill:158) in ALL THREE positions -- the receiver is an entity/named-tuple (and Term is NOT a top type), the selector is Const(Literal::String) not Symbol, the result is the FIELD's type not Term. It survives only because :7298 returns before ordinary op typing. That this is a genuine contradiction and not a harmless convention is demonstrated by a COMPETING implementation: anthill-stl/src/reflect/builtins.rs:883-905 registers anthill.reflect.field_access under the SAME QN implementing the DECLARED signature (expect_term + expect_symbol) -- it would reject every typer-synthesized node. Harmless today ONLY because register_reflect_builtins is called exclusively from its own unit test (:1102) while production (anthill-stl/src/runner.rs:43) registers only standard builtins; register_builtin is a plain HashMap insert (eval/mod.rs:408-417), LAST WINS, so wiring the reflect builtins into any real driver after the standard ones would silently shadow the working impl. That landmine should be closed (or pinned by an assertion) regardless of the rest. CRUX -- the field NAME is a VALUE that must reach TYPE position: there are NO singleton/literal types, a String const in value position types as plain String (typing.rs:5071-5073), so FieldOf[R, F] with F bound from the VALUE argument receives String and has LOST the name. The only existing route is the DENOTED type-arg channel: the grammar admits a literal in type position (grammar.js:156-166), parser -> TypeExpr::Denoted (convert.rs:558-562), loader -> make_denoted_occ (load.rs:10785-10796), typer TypeExtractor::Denoted (:25428) + unify_denoted_view (:19896). Exercised today ONLY for INTEGER denoteds (wi404_denoted_self_conformance_test.rs:61-72, Vec[T = Int64, N = 3]); there are ZERO string-valued type arguments anywhere in the repo, so this would be the FIRST. WI-708 (Delivered) made the eval type-arg channel actually carry its bindings, so a consumer that READS type args is now viable -- its own text flags it was \"a live trap for the next one that does\". TWO ENCODINGS, weigh before building: (a) CHEAP -- keep pos_args[1] = Const(String) EXACTLY as today (eval/resolve/codegen UNTOUCHED) and have the forward synthesis populate the node's RESOLVED TYPE ARGS (occ.set_resolved_type_args, the WI-708 channel) with name = denoted \"f\"; signature field_access[R, name](object: R, field: String) -> FieldOf[R, name]; the re-type reads the type args off the STORED node and reduces generically. Blast radius = typer only. (b) FULL -- move the selector INTO the type-arg channel: blast radius crosses 5 crates (eval/builtins.rs:213-291 accepts Value::Str ONLY and hard-errors otherwise; resolve.rs:5265-5271/:5288-5295/:5340-5378; body_specialize.rs:666-696; anthill-cpp-gen/src/lib.rs:2186-2199/:2458-2485; anthill-smt-gen/src/lib.rs:1452-1476) and would add a THIRD selector encoding to a contract that already carries TWO (P1 parse/loader form, Term::Ident at pos_args[1], from convert.rs; P2 typer form, Const(String)), whose lockstep is documented at body_specialize.rs:672-679. PREFER (a) unless it fails -- it leaves the P1/P2 contract alone. FieldOf REDUCTION must cover THREE receiver cases from ONE lookup SHARED with the forward direction (that sharing is the whole point -- it makes forward/reverse drift structurally impossible, which is the WI-758 defect): ENTITY/sort (field_constructors_of_sort + entity_field_types + resolve_field_type, what :7031-7039 does), NAMED TUPLE (TypeExtractor::NamedTuple(Vec<(Symbol, Value)>) at :25445, by-name lookup as at :6182-6194), and Term (the genuine reflect metaprogramming call -> Term). The same lookup must ALSO yield the OWNING CTOR so WI-369's internal-visibility check (internal_field_hidden_from, :7054-7071) rides the same resolution instead of a second traversal -- owner is None for a named tuple (components have no owning ctor and no internal marker), which is the CORRECT answer there, NOT a missing arm. SETTLE an inconsistency while here: :6182-6194 matches components by SHORT name, without_named_tuple_types (:1877) by full symbol identity -- pick one for a name-keyed ctor. MACHINERY: BinaryTypeCtor (typing.rs:1713) + reduce_binary_type_ctor (:1750), reduced at the ONE return-type normalization boundary in check_apply_iter (:7913-7928); registration = inert sort in stdlib/anthill/prelude/sort.anthill (Concat :60, Without :88) + reducer + const + widen return_reducible_ctors (:1672, TODAY A HARDCODED 2-TUPLE) + the if-chain at :7916-7925 + the signature. A third ctor forces widening both, so generalize the table to a slice while here rather than growing the if-chain again. DEPENDS ON WI-734 (do NOT decide it here): the family's ABSTRACT-OPERAND rule. Today an unresolved operand does NOT stay symbolic -- it falls to the _ arm (:1802), reaches the reducer, and errors \"must be a named-tuple type\" -> TypeError::Other -> LOAD-BLOCKING (:1618-1623), so a reducible ctor CANNOT sit in a signature checked before the receiver type is known. field_access forces this harder than Concat does, because the P1 user-written field_access(?t, f) in a rule body has NO static receiver at all. WI-734 says settle it ONCE for the family (Concat/Without/Project) -- FieldOf joins that family. Contrast precedent for the lazy reading: s.T DOES survive as a neutral residual (ExprCarried, :25429-25434). UNBLOCKS WI-732 (cross-reference, do NOT merge): its stated reason for deferring Project[T, keep] is that the current shape \"is legitimized by the field_access INC-1b stamp+re-derive precedent, and encoding a keep-spec in TYPE position is awkward\" -- this WI removes that precedent AND supplies the name-in-type-position primitive the keep-spec needs. NOT a constraint (checked): op/rule bodies are NEVER printed back to .anthill text -- persistence writes FACTS only (print_fact takes a TermId, print.rs:1095-1106; persistence/** has zero field_access hits) and print_occurrence's only non-test callers are CLI display (anthill-cli/src/main.rs:1524, :1561). So there is no text round-trip to preserve; the real idempotency constraint is the IN-MEMORY re-type (:7290, :7298) plus the WI-506 effect re-key in stable_receiver_path (:17747-17759), which must keep working -- it is what preserves the Modify[c] -> Modify[arg] head, and stdlib/anthill/prelude/mutable_stack.anthill:16 documents that as load-bearing. ACCEPTANCE: field_access's declared signature is self-correcting (its return type reduces to the field's type at the same boundary Concat/Without reduce at); field_access_projection_type and field_access_owning_ctor are GONE and check_apply_iter's ladder has at most ONE name-keyed early return left (relation_projection_type, WI-732's); the INC-1b and WI-638 mode-3 set_inferred_type stamps are GONE, both paths synthesizing and push_visit-ing like the INC-1a method fallback already does (:6077-6081), so forward and reverse are ONE decision procedure; the WI-506 effect re-key still preserves Modify heads; a named-tuple field access survives a re-type round-trip (WI-758's acceptance, satisfied structurally rather than by a second arm); the anthill-stl duplicate field_access is retired or pinned; existing wi638 / wi509 / wi364 / wi714 / wi727 tests green; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-734"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-18T16:11:05Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-759", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. FieldOf[T, Name] joins the Concat/Without binary-type-ctor family; field_access is now field_access[R, Name](object: R, field: String) -> FieldOf[T = R, Name = Name] and both name-keyed hatches are GONE. check_apply_iter's ladder has exactly ONE fn_sym guard left (typing.rs:4029, relation_projection_type / WI-732) -- verified by grep for fn_sym ==/!=. ENCODING: took the ticket's option (a), the CHEAP one -- pos_args[1] stays the String const, so eval / SLD / cpp-gen / smt-gen are untouched; the name ALSO rides the type-arg channel as a denoted. But NOT via set_resolved_type_args as the ticket proposed: that is the eval OUTPUT channel, rewritten by check_apply_iter itself at :8452, so a re-type would clobber it. The right channel is Expr::Apply.type_args, the INPUT seed_op_type_args reads -- the same channel a user's [Name = \"f\"] writes, which is what makes the node self-describing. MEASURED, not reasoned: the denoted must be GROUND (make_denoted over a hash-consed Const), not the occurrence-form make_denoted_occ. The return type is term-backed, so the type-param binding resolves through the TermId deep sigma-walk, which STOPS at a non-Term binding (WI-394) -- with a Value::Node denoted every projection reduced to a residual and 34 tests failed with 'FieldOf.<col>: no such member'. A field name is closed ground data anyway, exactly what hash-consing is for. SHARED LOOKUP: resolve_projected_member is the one resolution both directions use -- that sharing is the deliverable, not a detail: it is what makes WI-758's drift structurally impossible rather than fixed. WI-369 rides it via hidden_field_owner. The reduce contract gained a CtorReduceSite (sp, span, enclosing scope) because FieldOf is scope-SENSITIVE while Concat/Without are purely structural. THREE REVIEW FINDINGS FIXED (all real, two probed live): (1) putting the Term arm in the SHARED lookup silently widened FORWARD dot dispatch -- 't.definitely_not_a_field' on a Term receiver LOADED CLEAN (probe-verified in both states). A Term declares no members; 'any name projects' is right for the REDUCTION only. Moved into field_of_type; regression test pins it. (2) owner was picked by find_map over field_constructors_of_sort, whose order is HashMap iteration order -- load.rs:10154 already documents that hazard and its remedy. A multi-variant sort with one internal variant sharing a field name got a RUN-DEPENDENT visibility verdict. Now collects EVERY declaring ctor; verdict is any-hidden (deterministic + encapsulation-safe). Pre-existing in both retired hatches; consolidation made it a one-place fix. (3) the forward probe built and discarded a format! diagnostic on every miss -- i.e. on every relation column projection r.col. Split into MemberMiss::NoSuchMember (no message; the forward direction's legitimate fall-through) vs Unresolvable (a real diagnostic, now LOUD in both directions via a new TypeErrorContext::DotProjection -- previously the old INC-1b swallowed it with .ok()?). ACCEPTED COSTS, not oversights: field_access having type params turns on the previously-skipped set_resolved_type_args block per projection, and synthesize_field_access does one extra lookup_operation_info_full (an O(1) map hit that Path 1 repeats immediately after) to read the Name param symbol off the DECLARATION rather than kb.intern(\"Name\") -- WI-708's lesson that the two keyings differ. Suppressing either would need op-identity-keyed code, which is the thing this WI removes. A positional type arg would be free but binds by declaration ORDER, silently wrong on a reorder; a loud lookup beats a quiet mis-bind. STL LANDMINE CLOSED: the duplicate anthill.reflect.field_access in anthill-stl/src/reflect/builtins.rs is DELETED (it implemented the never-live expect_term+expect_symbol shape and would have shadowed the working core impl, register_builtin being last-wins). Its unit test is replaced by one that installs BOTH sets in the shadowing order and asserts the PRODUCTION contract still answers. Codegen fallout: reflect.anthill's new 'import anthill.prelude.{FieldOf}' emits a use line, so prelude/mod.rs gains an opaque FieldOf stub -- the same treatment TypeExtractor already has for the excluded free op 'extract'. SETTLED (the ticket's inconsistency item): a NAME-keyed ctor matches by SHORT name (the convention of named_tuple_compatible / thread_expected_tuple_field / forward dot dispatch, and forced anyway -- there is no symbol to compare); without_named_tuple_types keeps symbol identity, correctly, because both ITS operands are type-level tuples from one producer. Documented on resolve_projected_member. TESTS: 10 in wi759_field_of_type_test.rs. A/B-VERIFIED rather than asserted: wi759_named_tuple_projection_survives_retype FAILS on pre-change code with the ticket's verbatim 'type mismatch in field_access.field (op-arg): expected Symbol, got String' -- that is WI-758's acceptance, met structurally. Its entity sibling PASSES pre-change (the old hatch covered that arm) and is labelled a preservation control, not a second regression test. Full workspace: 3048 passed, 0 failed (baseline 3038). NOT DONE / follow-on: WI-758 is satisfied by this and should be closed rather than worked. WI-732 is now unblocked -- its stated blocker (the field_access stamp precedent, plus 'encoding a keep-spec in TYPE position is awkward') is removed, and Project[T, keep] can use the string-in-type-position primitive this adds; it is also the last name-keyed early return in the ladder.", at: "2026-07-18T16:11:45Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-758", description: some(value: "WI-509 gap found while delivering WI-730: field_access_projection_type (kb/typing.rs) -- the RE-TYPE of a desugared 'field_access(recv, \"f\")' back to the FIELD's type -- has no NAMED-TUPLE receiver arm. It resolves the field only through sort_functor_of_view + field_constructors_of_sort, i.e. an ENTITY/sort receiver. A NAMED TUPLE has functor 'named_tuple', not a sort, which is precisely the case WI-638's THIRD dot-dispatch mode exists for: that mode types 't.x' on a named-tuple receiver and REWRITES it to a synthesized 'Apply{field_access, [recv, Const::String(\"x\")]}' whose type it stamps directly. So the forward direction (DotApply -> field type) handles named tuples and the reverse (re-type of the rewritten node) does not -- and when the rewritten body is re-typed, that node is checked against the DECLARED reflect signature 'field_access(object: Term, field: Symbol)' and fails 'type mismatch in field_access.field (op-arg): expected Symbol, got String' -- a diagnostic naming an internal desugaring, not anything the user wrote. REPRO (today, via the only path that re-types such a body): 'person_row.where(lambda c -> not(eq(c.name, \"alice\")))' BEFORE WI-730 -- the [simp] rewrite where <=> guarded_of fires and REWRITES the body, guarded_of then declines (it could not compile the nesting), the kept template is re-typed, and the lambda body's already-synthesized field_access nodes surface this error instead of anything about the condition. WI-730 removed that particular trigger by making the nesting compile, but the gap is untouched and will resurface on any future re-type of a body carrying a mode-3 synth node. FIX DIRECTION: give field_access_projection_type the same named-tuple arm its forward twin has -- when the receiver type extracts as TypeExtractor::NamedTuple(fields), resolve the component by name and return its type -- so the forward and reverse directions agree on which receivers have fields; a receiver that is neither a sort nor a named tuple keeps falling through to ordinary op typing (the genuine field_access call). Check the WI-369 companion field_access_owning_ctor for the same asymmetry (internal-field visibility on a named-tuple projection). ACCEPTANCE: a named-tuple field access survives a re-type round-trip (a regression test that re-types a body containing one and asserts the field's type, not the reflect signature); the existing WI-638 / WI-509 tests stay green; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-730", "WI-759"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-18T16:21:48Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-758", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED by WI-759 (cd0d5d9e), and DELIBERATELY NOT BY THIS TICKET'S FIX DIRECTION. The ticket said: 'give field_access_projection_type the same named-tuple arm its forward twin has'. That was rejected as the wrong altitude -- adding the arm would have restored parity while PRESERVING the duplication that produced the gap, leaving the next divergence free to happen. WI-759 instead DELETED both field_access_projection_type and field_access_owning_ctor and gave field_access a self-correcting signature (-> FieldOf[T = R, Name = Name]), so forward and reverse are ONE decision procedure over ONE lookup (resolve_projected_member). The missing-arm defect class is now unrepresentable rather than fixed: there is no second traversal for an arm to be missing from. THE COMPANION CHECK THIS TICKET ASKED FOR ('check field_access_owning_ctor for the same asymmetry'): it is gone too, and the named-tuple case correctly yields NO owning constructor -- a tuple component has no declaring entity and therefore no  marker to test. That is the CORRECT answer for that receiver, not a missing arm, and it is recorded on ProjectedMember::owners so it cannot be re-read later as an oversight. ACCEPTANCE, item by item: (1) 'a regression test that re-types a body containing one and ASSERTS THE FIELD'S TYPE, not the reflect signature' -- wi759_named_tuple_projection_survives_retype. It does BOTH halves: the re-type is clean, AND it reads the inferred_type the Stamp frame pushed onto the stored rewritten node during the SECOND pass and asserts String / Int64 / Int64 for t.name / t.age / t._2 (two different component types plus a positional, so it cannot pass by everything collapsing to one type). A widening to the reflect signature would read back Term; an unreduced ctor would read back FieldOf. VERIFIED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS rather than asserted: the test FAILS on pre-WI-759 code with this ticket's verbatim 'type mismatch in field_access.field (op-arg): expected Symbol, got String', and the type assertion was probed non-vacuous by flipping the expected value (reads 'String'). (2) 'existing WI-638 / WI-509 tests stay green' -- green. (3) 'cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh' -- 3048 passed, 0 failed. REPRO NOTE: this ticket's stated repro ('person_row.where(lambda c -> not(eq(c.name, \"alice\")))' before WI-730) is no longer reachable -- WI-730 made the nesting compile, so guarded_of no longer declines and the kept template is not re-typed. The test therefore drives the gap through the OTHER re-type path the ticket names, the free-op sweep (type_check_sorts with no owning sort), which is the same path wi509_field_projection_body_roundtrips_retype uses and the same one an incremental load takes. So the gap is pinned by a trigger that does not depend on a [simp] rule declining.", at: "2026-07-18T16:22:09Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-758", author: "user", content: "CORRECTION to the previous note: one word was lost to shell backtick substitution when it was recorded. The sentence should read \"a tuple component has no declaring entity and therefore no INTERNAL marker to test\" -- i.e. the WI-369 internal-visibility marker. Nothing else in that note is affected.", at: "2026-07-18T16:22:32Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-089", author: "user", content: "CROSS-REF (2026-07-18, from WI-576): this ticket's ADDENDUM specified a fully-qualified anthill.realization.lower_type predicate resolved by SLD, with the overlay/priority logic IN the rule body. That did not ship and could not: cpp-gen holds a shared KB borrow and queries via kb.query_view, a discrimination-tree HEAD MATCH that never runs a rule body, while SLD needs a mutable KB (it allocs/interns). So the delivered form is the Rust query_type_mappings + select_keyed pair, and lower_type exists nowhere in stdlib/. WI-576's realizes_effect accessor hit the identical wall and made the identical choice. The enabling substrate change is now tracked as WI-760 (codegen gets a resolving KnowledgeBase); it also records the trap that writing lower_type as a BODIED rule TODAY would head-match with its body silently unevaluated.", at: "2026-07-18T14:58:17Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-576", description: some(value: "Codegen/realization effect-discharge check (the CAPABILITY GATE). An operation's RESIDUAL required effect row must be a SUBSET of the active profile's supported-effect set (the keys of its effect_map). An effect with no realization on the target profile is UNREALIZABLE there and must fail LOUDLY at load/codegen (repo principle: loud error over silent skip), never silently dropped. NEW and distinct from two neighbours: (1) WI-089 defines HOW cpp effects MAP (the effect_map; its deferred increment (b) migrates the still-hardcoded {Modify->lvalue ref, Error->tl::expected} to queryable keyed facts) -- this WI is the REJECTION of effects a profile can't realize, not the mapping; (2) WI-329 is HANDLER discharge (handle_K removes handled label K from the row) -- that only NARROWS the residual row and is an INPUT to this check, not this check. v1 operates on declared op effects (handlers unimplemented, so residual = declared); once WI-329 lands the residual narrows by handled labels. REPRESENTATION (corrected): rust_std/scala_std carry effect_map NESTED in a single LanguageMapping fact (List[EffectMapping]). cpp has NO effect_map facts yet -- still hardcoded in Rust (WI-089 deferred increment (b)); only cpp's TYPE renames went FLAT keyed TypeMapping facts, and that was a query-mechanism pivot (cpp-gen reads an IMMUTABLE &KB via kb.query_view, can't member-walk a nested list), NOT a rejection of LanguageMapping. So cpp's effect_map can simply live in a cpp LanguageMapping.effect_map like the others (then NO wrinkle), or go flat to match cpp's type renames -- that nested-vs-flat choice is WI-089's still-OPEN fork. Either way this check must read support through ONE uniform accessor the chosen representation projects to (a fully-qualified anthill.realization.realizes_effect(profile, effect, receiver) sibling to lower_type), never assuming a representation. Surfaces scala_std's 'async lands in cats_effect/zio' as a CHECKED error instead of silent absence; protects WI-570's cpp emitter from lowering ops whose effects the target can't realize; and is where the planned EMBEDDED cpp profile's small effect set (no Branch/Suspension under no-heap) is enforced. Acceptance: an op requiring an effect absent from the target profile's supported set fails codegen with a clear message naming the effect + profile; an op whose effects are all realized lowers fine; tested against at least one profile (rust_std now; cpp once WI-089(b) lands). cargo-test green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-089"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-18T16:38:54Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-576", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED 2026-07-18 (commit a8761403). Both remaining pieces landed: the uniform realizes_effect accessor and the loud residual-subset rejection. Nine tests in anthill-cpp-gen/tests/effect_gate_test.rs; full workspace suite 3040 passed / 0 failed; /code-review high run with four findings applied. DEVIATION FROM THE DESCRIPTION, deliberate: this ticket (and WI-089's addendum) specified the accessor as a fully-qualified anthill.realization.realizes_effect PREDICATE, sibling to lower_type. It shipped as a RUST function instead, because lower_type never became an anthill rule either and could not -- cpp-gen holds a shared KB borrow and queries via kb.query_view, a discrimination-tree HEAD MATCH that never executes a rule body, while SLD needs a mutable KB. Matching the delivered WI-089 precedent rather than the aspirational wording. The enabling substrate change is filed as WI-760 (codegen gets a resolving KnowledgeBase), which also records the trap that writing either predicate as a BODIED rule today would head-match with its body silently unevaluated. WHAT THE OLD CODE DID: rec.effects was folded with .any(... == Some(\"ResultWrap\")), so an effect with no EffectMapping, a label whose kind could not be read, and a Value::Node denoted label (dropped by a bare `_ => false`, while the sibling param/return paths raised for that same carrier) were all indistinguishable from \"does not wrap\" -- C++ was emitted for effects cpp cannot realize. FINDING NOT ANTICIPATED BY THE DESCRIPTION: an effect ROW PARAMETER. Monad.flatMap[A, B, EffP](...) effects {EffP} stores its effect as a plain Term::Var; the first cut rejected it and broke two stdlib higher_kinded_arrow tests. An effect-polymorphic op states no CONCRETE requirement at its declaration -- the row is whatever a call site instantiates -- so the gate passes it, but narrowly: the label must BE one of the op's own declared type params, compared by TermId identity (type params hash-cons to the same var term), so a stray free var still errors loudly. The instantiation-site check remains unbuilt and is the natural successor to this WI once handler discharge (WI-329) narrows residual rows. CONTRACT WORTH KNOWING: realizes_effect REQUIRES a profile for a nested-representation language. Only the flat form has a language base (key: none) -- every stdlib LanguageMapping declares a profile (std / caps / anthill) and none declares profile: none -- so realizes_effect(kb, \"rust\", None, \"Modify\") resolves nothing even though rust plainly realizes Modify. Documented and pinned by realizes_effect_without_a_profile_resolves_no_nested_entry rather than papered over with a sole-mapping-wins fallback that would guess at the caller's profile. cpp is unaffected (its facts carry key: none and cpp-gen always threads ctx.profile). ALSO FILED: WI-761 -- the CLI discards the gate's diagnostic for the optional anthill.geometry header, because CppCodegenError cannot distinguish \"namespace declares nothing\" from \"failed to lower what it declares\" (anthill-cli/src/main.rs:966 and :1021). SCOPE NOTE: the gate is cpp-only, per this ticket's own acceptance (\"tested against at least one profile\"). anthill-core/src/codegen/rust.rs::analyze_effects is still hardcoded with a silent `_ => {}` arm and does not consume rust_std's nested effect_map at all -- untouched here, and a candidate follow-on now that realizes_effect can serve it.", at: "2026-07-18T16:39:19Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-732", description: some(value: "WI-714 follow-up: project -- multi-column over a COMPUTED receiver, and the Project[T, keep] type constructor (retire the stamp + idempotency handler). CONTEXT: proposal 052 project increment (07e15fdb). r.(f1, f2) selects relation columns. project has NO lambda, so unlike where/join there is no compile-time macro: the typer synthesizes project_run(r, <spec>) DIRECTLY off the dot form and STAMPS the projected schema (a 4th DotApply dispatch mode for the single-column case; a check_tuple_literal_constructor pre-check for multi-column, recognizing a name-keyed tuple whose fields are ALL DotApply(rel, col) on the SAME leaf receiver via projection_receivers_same = Rc-ptr-eq else leaf-sym). TWO DEFERRED ITEMS: (1) MULTI-COLUMN over a COMPUTED (non-leaf) receiver -- projection_receivers_same identifies the shared receiver by Rc-ptr-eq else leaf symbol, which covers a let-bound computed receiver ('let f = r.where(..); f.(a,b)' WORKS, verified) but not every non-leaf shape; the inline chain 'r.where(..).(a,b)' is a LOUD error (the WI-443 chain limit), not a silent tuple-of-relations. (2) The DESIGN note (NOT a bug): a Project[T, keep] TYPE CONSTRUCTOR -- mirroring join's Concat and fix's Without -- would make project_run's signature SELF-CORRECTING (it currently declares the nominal -> Relation[T = r.T] and relies on the typer's stamp, plus a defensive relation_projection_type idempotency handler in check_apply_iter that is verified NOT currently reached). With Project[T, keep] the schema would reduce at the same return-type normalization boundary Concat/Without reduce at, retiring both the stamp and the idempotency handler and putting all three algebra schema ops on ONE mechanism. The current shape is legitimized by the field_access INC-1b stamp+re-derive precedent, and encoding a keep-spec in TYPE position is awkward -- hence deferred, not wrong. ALSO: duplicate result keys are pre-existing + surface-guarded at parse (convert.rs:1164 validate_projection_labels). ACCEPTANCE: multi-column projection over the deferred computed-receiver shapes either works or is a loud error naming the fix; if Project[T, keep] is taken, project_run's signature is self-correcting and the stamp + relation_projection_type handler are GONE, with Concat/Without/Project sharing one reduction boundary; nothing keyed on project's identity in the typer; existing wi714_project tests green; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-714"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-18T17:28:39Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-764", author: "user", content: "Description note: the shape that led here, elided by a shell quoting slip when this item was filed, is the call-result receiver `src()` followed by a distribute-dot projection of `name` and `age` — i.e. projecting directly off a call result with no intervening `let`. That is the shape WI-732`s hardening branch would need in order to be reachable and testable.", at: "2026-07-18T21:02:19Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-762", author: "user", content: "RENUMBERED WI-760 -> WI-762 on merge with origin/main. A different ticket was filed upstream as WI-760 at 16:49Z (codegen gets a resolving KnowledgeBase); this one was filed locally later the same day. Applied the WI-752/WI-753 precedent rule: the number stays with the side filed first and carrying references. Upstream WI-760 is cited by name in the feedback of BOTH WI-089 and WI-576, while this one was referenced only from its own unpushed commit message. NOTE commit 85db6f98 still reads \"WI-760 filed\" for this ticket.", at: "2026-07-18T21:30:14Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-763", author: "user", content: "RENUMBERED WI-761 -> WI-763 on merge with origin/main, same collision as WI-762. Upstream filed a different WI-761 at 16:49Z (CppCodegenError conflates nothing-to-emit with failed-to-emit); it is cited by name in WI-576 feedback, so it keeps the number. NOTE commit 85db6f98 still reads \"WI-761 filed\" for this ticket.", at: "2026-07-18T21:30:25Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-764", author: "user", content: "RENUMBERED WI-762 -> WI-764 on merge with origin/main. This ticket did not collide directly, but was shifted to keep the three locally-filed items contiguous after WI-760/WI-761 moved to WI-762/WI-763. NOTE commit 9be1f834 still reads \"filed as WI-762\" for this ticket.", at: "2026-07-18T21:30:26Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-763", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. Grammar: new `denoted_field_decl` (`person: \"name\"`) + hidden `_type_literal` shared with `_common_type_expr`; `tuple_type` gains a ONE-component denoted form. DEVIATION from the ticket's stated approach, deliberate: the denoted component rides in the SHARED `_tuple_type_arg`, not a tuple-scoped symbol. Measured why -- `arrow_params` and `tuple_type` share a prefix and are told apart only by the `->` after `)`, so two component symbols make that prefix ambiguous at every `,` (tree-sitter reported exactly this conflict), i.e. a GLR fork on EVERY named arrow param list. The ticket's actual requirement is met: `field_decl` is untouched, so `entity person(name: \"foo\")` stays a parse error. An arrow param with a literal is refused at CONVERSION instead (located message, better than a syntax error). The one-component form uses prec(1) not a declared conflict -- the arrow reading is wrong for every such input, so it is resolved statically with no runtime fork. ACCEPTANCE MET: `Keep = (person: \"name\")` parses and reduces; all three previously-unreachable diagnostics now fire from written source (verified by PRINTING them, not by contains()); 12 new tests; corpus 181/181; workspace 3076 passed / 0 failed. FOUND WHILE REVIEWING (all fixed, see tests): (1) comments are tree-sitter `extras` so they appear among named children -- the new loud arm would have rejected every commented tuple type, and the SIBLING `convert_arrow_type` already had this bug, failing a whole file over a comment in a param list; (2) `arrow_type` was missing from convert_tuple_type's positional list, so `((A) -> B, C)` SILENTLY became a 1-tuple; (3) a written `Keep` with a duplicate result key built a duplicate-keyed schema `(a: String, a: Int64)` with no diagnostic, contradicting the stdlib doc's claim that duplicate result keys are rejected (true only for the dot surface) -- now checked in keep_spec_projections. STILL OPEN, out of scope: a duplicate-key named tuple TYPE is accepted language-wide (`operation f() -> (a: Int64, a: String)` loads clean); rejecting that is a broader change. Also still unwritable: a general one-component named tuple `(a: A)`, for the arrow_params reason above.", at: "2026-07-18T23:09:09Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-763", description: some(value: "Grammar: admit a literal as a named-tuple TYPE component, so a keep-spec (and any denoted-bearing tuple type) is WRITABLE. CONTEXT: WI-732 added Project[T, Keep], where Keep is a named-tuple TYPE whose field name is each result key and whose component is a denoted carrying the source column's name -- Project[T = (name: String, age: Int64), Keep = (person: \"name\", years: \"age\")] reduces to (person: String, years: Int64). The typer SYNTHESIZES that Keep and it reduces correctly, but it CANNOT BE WRITTEN: type-ARGUMENT position admits a literal (_common_type_expr in tree-sitter-anthill/grammar.js:156-166, the channel WI-759's FieldOf[Name = \"age\"] uses), while a named-tuple type COMPONENT does not (_tuple_type_arg is choice($._type, $.field_decl), grammar.js:1481). So Project is today reducible-when-synthesized and writable-only-abstractly (over type params), unlike Concat/Without/FieldOf which are fully writable -- the family is inconsistent in surface syntax even though it shares one reduction boundary. CONSEQUENCE beyond ergonomics: project_schema_type's and keep_spec_projections' diagnostics (a Keep naming no column, a non-denoted component) are currently UNREACHABLE, because the only producer is the typer itself and the forward gate shares projection_columns with the reduction -- so they cannot be tested. NOTE the naive fix is wrong: widening field_decl's RHS would let a literal into ENTITY field declarations too (entity person(name: \"foo\")). Wants a component production scoped to tuple TYPES. GOTCHA: run cargo clean -p tree-sitter-anthill after editing grammar.js; watch for GLR ties (see the prec.dynamic comment-tie precedent). ACCEPTANCE: Keep = (person: \"name\") parses in a signature and reduces; the Project reduction's error paths become reachable and get tests; npx tree-sitter test corpus green; full workspace green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-18T23:09:13Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-764", author: "user", content: "ROOT CAUSE was NOT binding order — measured, not reasoned. Instrumenting parameterized_compatible_view showed the two sides key the SAME slot with DIFFERENT symbols: the rule citation uses the sort's canonical param symbols (anthill.prelude.Relation.T = 1352, .E = 1353), the written annotation uses BARE ones (T = 2337, E = 2331). The subtype relation matched bindings by raw symbol identity, so neither slot was ever found; same-base means no provider fallback, so it rejected. The render order differed for the same reason and is only a FINGERPRINT: make_entity_term's canonicalizer sorts named args by symbol INDEX, and the two symbol sets intern in opposite relative order. This is WI-726's bug exactly, in the sibling direction — WI-726 fixed the UNIFY twin and left the SUBTYPE twin on raw identity, despite commit 0f31beb2 having consolidated the pair specifically to keep them in lockstep. FIX: one shared binding_for_param / binding_index_for_param keyed by a BindingKeyMatch{Label,Identity} derived from the two bases (label-match within one sort, identity across), replacing the raw-identity lookup in BOTH directions. Identity is tried first across the whole slice, label only on miss, so an exact key always wins over a merely same-labelled one.", at: "2026-07-19T00:10:31Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-764", author: "user", content: "TWO FURTHER DEFECTS the fix exposed, both found by /code-review and both MEASURED before being fixed. (1) DUPLICATE PARAM KEY: with conformance fixed, 'Relation[T = <right>, T = <wrong>, E = {Error}]' loaded with ZERO errors while the same two bindings in the opposite order rejected — binding lookup is first-wins, so a stale correct binding above a new wrong one satisfied the check and the wrong one was never compared. check_sort_type_args admitted it because it only asked whether each key is DECLARED, never whether it appears once. Now a loud TypeArgProblem::DuplicateParam at that gate (scoped to a Sort head, like the rest of that check — NOT universal). Note this defeats a naive negative test: prefixing a correct duplicate hides a wrong schema. (2) PARTIAL ANNOTATION DUPLICATED A SLOT from ORDINARY source: unroll_annotation_with_inferred merged annotation and value bindings by raw identity, so 'let r : Relation[E = {Error}] = person_row' built Relation[T = .., E = .., E = ..] — one slot bound twice, the annotation's bare E and the value's canonical Relation.E. Now merges through the shared key rule. Both have regression tests.", at: "2026-07-19T00:10:49Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-764", author: "user", content: "FALSIFIED PREDICTION + WHAT WAS DELIBERATELY LEFT. WI-732's hardening commit (9be1f834) called its unresolvable-receiver branch 'unreachable by measurement, therefore untested' and named this ticket as the blocker, expecting a projection off a call result to reach it once the annotation conformed. It does NOT: 'src().(age, name)' now types CLEANLY, because that branch fires only when projection_receiver_type cannot resolve the receiver and a call result resolves fine. So unblocking WI-764 did not make it reachable; it stays untested. (Measured for THIS shape only.) The regression test asserts the diagnostic's absence as a tripwire. LEFT UN-ENROLLED, deliberately, each a same-base guard followed by a raw-identity binding lookup — same defect class, but each is a behavior change in a subsystem with no failing case here: (a) match_candidate_against_goal (typing.rs ~12639/12645) — dispatch specificity; now ASYMMETRIC with the typer (subtype accepts the canonical-vs-bare pair, dispatch still drops the candidate), and enrolling it changes overload ORDERING via specificity, so it is not a free follow-up; (b) combine_parameterized_same_base (typing.rs ~24634/24650) — the LUB/GLB lattice; on a key miss returns the bare sort / nothing, silently dropping a schema. A reviewer built five if-join probes and could NOT demonstrate it, and instrumentation shows its binding matcher is executed by ZERO existing tests. Both named in binding_for_param's doc.", at: "2026-07-19T00:11:04Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-764", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED: conformance for a parameterized sort compares bindings by the shared key rule (label within one sort, identity across sorts) instead of raw symbol identity, in BOTH the subtype and unify directions via one binding_for_param/binding_index_for_param. Repro loads in both binding orders; 7 regression tests incl. both orders, two non-vacuity controls, the duplicate-param guard in both orders, the partial-annotation slot, and the projection-off-a-call-result shape; full workspace 3083 passed / 0 failed via scripts/test.sh; /code-review run at max effort (10 finder angles), findings applied — the two extra defects above were found there.", at: "2026-07-19T00:11:40Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-764", description: some(value: "An operation annotating its return as an explicit Relation[T = .., E = ..] does not conform against a rule citation, though both render the SAME bindings. REPRO (loads clean minus this op): rule person_row(?name, ?age) :- person(name: ?name, age: ?age); operation src() -> Relation[E = {Error}, T = (name: String, age: Int64)] = person_row -- fails with 'type mismatch in src.return (op-return): expected Relation[E = {merge[left = present[label = Error], right = empty_row]}, T = (name: String, age: Int64)], got Relation[T = (name: String, age: Int64), E = {merge[left = present[label = Error], right = empty_row]}]'. The two sides differ ONLY in the ORDER the bindings render (E,T vs T,E); writing the annotation in either order gives the same failure, so it is not the surface order that matters -- the DECLARED side normalizes one way and the rule-citation side (relation_type_from_columns -> assemble_relation_type, which fills bindings in sort-param order) the other. PRE-EXISTING, measured not assumed: reproduced byte-identically on e86e4dda (pre-WI-732) in a clean worktree, with a source containing NO dot expression and NO projection, so none of WI-732's paths are involved. FOUND WHILE trying to construct a receiver shape for WI-732's new hardening diagnostic --  -- which this blocks, so the hardening branch is currently unreachable-by-measurement and untested (59 recognizer hits across anthill-core, 0 fall-throughs). ACCEPTANCE: the repro loads; conformance for a parameterized sort compares bindings BY NAME rather than depending on either producer's fill order (or the two producers are made to agree, with the reason recorded); a regression test pins an annotated Relation return in BOTH binding orders; full workspace green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-19T00:11:42Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-764", author: "user", content: "Un-enrolled siblings filed as WI-768 (match_candidate_against_goal, dispatch — now asymmetric with the typer; enrolling also moves overload ORDERING via specificity) and WI-769 (combine_parameterized_same_base, LUB/GLB lattice — measured INERT: five if-join probes could not demonstrate it and its binding matcher is executed by zero tests, so the open question there is reachability, not the patch). binding_for_param's doc now cites both by number.", at: "2026-07-19T04:59:02Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-760", description: some(value: "Give codegen a RESOLVING KnowledgeBase so realization predicates can be real anthill rules. TODAY codegen is a strictly READ-ONLY consumer: CodegenContext holds a shared kb borrow (anthill-cpp-gen/src/lib.rs:281) and every query goes through kb.query_view (kb/mod.rs:3204), which takes &self and is a DISCRIMINATION-TREE HEAD MATCH -- it returns (RuleId, Substitution) for rules whose HEAD structurally matches and NEVER executes a body. Real SLD (KnowledgeBase::resolve, kb/resolve.rs:2835) takes &mut self because it calls kb.alloc / kb.intern -- it opens rules with fresh vars and hash-conses built goal terms into the TermStore. CONSEQUENCE (the actual motivation): every fact-driven realization lookup must encode its overlay/priority logic CALLER-SIDE in Rust instead of in a rule body. WI-089's addendum specified a fully-qualified anthill.realization.lower_type predicate resolved by SLD -- \"the discrimination tree IS the table; the rule IS the overlay logic\" -- but what shipped is the Rust query_type_mappings + select_keyed pair, and lower_type does NOT exist as an anthill predicate anywhere in stdlib/. WI-576 hit the same wall and shipped realizes_effect as a Rust fn for the same reason. So the in-language form BOTH WIs specified is blocked purely by this borrow choice. NOT AN EXTERNAL CONSTRAINT -- the shared borrow is codegen's own API decision: run_codegen_cpp (anthill-cli/src/main.rs:919) binds the KB from load_kb_with_stdlib, owning it outright, and could pass a mutable borrow. What makes it awkward is that CodegenContext is threaded as a SHARED borrow through deeply nested emit functions and keeps its own mutable state (requested_includes, type_params, value_bindings, emitting_namespace) behind RefCells precisely so it can be shared; ~29 ctx.kb uses + 47 free fns taking a KnowledgeBase reference in cpp-gen alone, plus emit_traits_struct / emit_namespace_header_with_profile are public API used across ~37 test files, plus anthill-rust-gen and anthill-smt-gen have the same shape. SHAPE TO WEIGH (do not pre-decide): (a) CodegenContext holds the KB in a RefCell of a mutable borrow, with an accessor returning Ref and a resolve method taking RefMut -- mechanical but panic-prone, since any Ref held across a resolve call is a runtime BorrowMutError, and today's code freely binds a local kb from ctx.kb and holds it across calls; (b) a narrow Resolver seam the context owns, so only the ~5 realization-query call sites change and ordinary ctx.kb reads stay immutable; (c) a resolve PRE-PASS with a mutable KB before emission, materializing answers into tables -- but that re-introduces exactly the reader/table layer WI-089 deleted, so it is the fallback, not the goal. TRAP TO RECORD REGARDLESS OF SHAPE: if someone writes realizes_effect (or lower_type) as a BODIED rule in realization.anthill and reads it through the EXISTING query_view path, the head matches but the BODY IS NEVER EVALUATED -- the guard conditions are silently skipped. Today that mostly degrades to a miss (the field readers return None on a var-valued field rather than a wrong string), but it is a silent-wrong-answer waiting for a rule whose head is ground enough. Either make the realization-query call sites refuse bodied rules, or land this WI before any realization predicate is written as a rule. ACCEPTANCE: codegen can execute an SLD query against the KB; at least one realization lookup (lower_type or realizes_effect) is expressed as a fully-qualified anthill rule in anthill.realization and read by codegen through RESOLUTION rather than head-matching, with its overlay/priority logic in the rule body rather than in select_keyed; the existing cpp-gen suite stays green (it is the regression surface for the borrow refactor); cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-576"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-19T01:22:24Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-767", description: some(value: "anthill query (CLI) FLOUNDERS on a rule whose body must enumerate an unbound variable, while the SAME rule resolves definitively via KnowledgeBase::resolve. Found while validating WI-760. REPRO: a namespace with `entity Local(name: String, val: String)`, two `fact Local(...)`, and `rule loc(?n) :- Local(name: ?n, val: \"one\")`. Through `anthill query -p stdlib -p <file> 'ns.loc(?n)'` the answer is the residual `loc(?#0) [?n = ?#0]` -- 1 result, ?n UNBOUND. Through the Rust API (kb.resolve with a Value goal) the identical rule returns ONE DEFINITE solution (empty residual) binding ?n = \"a\". Confirmed on both a local entity with local facts AND an imported stdlib fact (EffectMapping -> ResultWrap). A rule with a fully GROUND body resolves fine through the CLI, so the trigger is specifically a body goal carrying an unbound variable to enumerate. WHY IT MATTERS: this is a user-facing tool and the failure mode is a silent wrong answer -- the CLI reports '1 result(s)' rather than an error, so a correct rule reads as broken and an author will 'fix' a rule that was never wrong. It also makes the CLI useless for exactly the debugging it exists for. I nearly abandoned a correct WI-760 rule design on the strength of this output; only re-testing through kb.resolve showed the rules were fine. SUSPECTS (not investigated): the CLI's goal construction/var linkage differs from the test-side pattern (query vars may not be linked into answer_links, cf. WI-624), or it resolves with a config that delays/rotates rather than enumerating. START: compare anthill-cli's query path against the kb.resolve call shape in rustland/anthill-cpp-gen/tests/wi760_realization_rule_test.rs, which is a known-good template. ACCEPTANCE: the CLI returns the same definite bindings as kb.resolve for the repro above; a regression test pins CLI and API agreement on an enumerating rule body."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-19T07:58:35Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-770", description: some(value: "cpp-gen reads realization facts through HEAD-MATCHING with no bodied-rule refusal: query_realization_facts (rustland/anthill-cpp-gen/src/lib.rs:3778) maps every kb.query_view hit straight to kb.rule_head(rid), so a BODIED realization rule (e.g. rule TypeMapping(...) :- <guard>) would be read with its guard SILENTLY SKIPPED -- the trap WI-760's description recorded ('the head matches but the BODY IS NEVER EVALUATED'), still live for TypeMapping/IncludeMapping/NamingConvention after WI-760 delivered the resolve seam for realizes_effect only. Latent today (stdlib realization entries are all facts) but it is a silent wrong answer waiting: an author following WI-089's addendum direction who writes a guarded overlay rule gets the wrong host type emitted into generated C++ with no diagnostic. Surfaced by the WI-767 review, which fixed the same class in the CLI (a bodied head match must not read as an answer; the CLI now renders such matches as head :- body and resolves by default). FIX DIRECTION: in query_realization_facts, refuse candidates whose body_nodes is non-empty with a loud diagnostic naming the rule and pointing at the WI-760 resolve seam (or route them through that seam). ACCEPTANCE: a bodied TypeMapping rule in a test KB is either resolved (guard honored) or refused loudly, never silently head-matched; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-19T10:29:40Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-768", author: "user", content: "CODE-REVIEW findings recorded, not fixed here (each is outside the demonstrated defect):\n\n(1) DUPLICATE KEY SPELLINGS CAN DOUBLE-MATCH. binding_for_param is first-wins, so with the Label fallback two distinct candidate keys naming ONE slot can both resolve to the SAME per-call binding, leaving another per-call binding never compared. In values_structurally_equal the ab.len() != bb.len() arity guard does NOT catch it (2 == 2); match_candidate_against_goal arm (2) has no length check at all. Not hypothetical: WI-764's DuplicateParam guard is scoped to a Sort head only, and WI-764 itself recorded unroll_annotation_with_inferred building Relation[T=.., E=.., E=..] from ordinary source. Fixing it means tracking consumed indices in the SHARED helper, which changes WI-726/WI-764 consumers too.\n\n(2) values_structurally_equal NEVER COMPARES THE UNWRAPPED BASES. Its guard is sort_sym_of_term(a) == sort_sym_of_term(b), which yields the SortView WRAPPER symbol for a SortView-encoded value, so SortView(List,[T=Int64]) and SortView(Stream,[T=Int64]) pass the guard; parametric_value_parts then unwraps to List vs Stream and for_bases correctly returns Identity, both key T identically, and the function returns TRUE for two values of DIFFERENT sorts. A one-line same_sort_canonical(ab_base, bb_base) closes it and the full suite stays green (3091/0) with it in — but green only proves it does not break anything, not that it ever fires, and reachability is unclear (impl_subst stores only per-call values). REVERTED rather than ship a second unmeasurable tightening in a function whose WI-768 enrollment is already undemonstrated.\n\n(3) goals_equal IS A FOURTH HAND-ROLLED SPELLING of the binding-key rule. It does NOT carry the WI-768 bug (already same_label, so it bridges the pair), but it is label-ONLY: it lacks the identity-first pass that makes an exact key beat a merely same-labelled one, and it puts same_label's debug_assert on the path unconditionally. Its spec_sort guard already establishes Label, so enrolling it is a one-liner — deferred because changing its tie-breaking is a behavior change outside this ticket. NOW DOCUMENTED in binding_for_param's un-enrolled list, which previously undercounted (said 'one site').", at: "2026-07-19T06:12:46Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-768", description: some(value: "Dispatch still matches parameterized binding keys by RAW SYMBOL IDENTITY, so it now disagrees with the typer. SITE: match_candidate_against_goal (rustland/anthill-core/src/kb/typing.rs, fn at 12591) -- raw base guard `if c_base != p_base` at 12643, then `p_bindings.iter().find(|(kk, _)| kk == k)` at 12649 with `None => return false`; a second raw key lookup at 12818. SAME DEFECT CLASS AS WI-764: a same-base guard followed by a raw-identity binding-key lookup, where the two producers spell one slot differently -- a relation VALUE from a rule citation keys T as the canonical `anthill.prelude.Relation.T`, a WRITTEN signature keys it as a bare `T` (loader `reintern(p.last())`). WHY THIS IS NOW WORSE THAN BEFORE: WI-764 fixed the SUBTYPE relation to accept that pair but deliberately left dispatch alone, so the two are ASYMMETRIC -- a call can type-check and then fail to dispatch. On a key miss this site does not degrade, it returns false and DROPS the candidate, surfacing as a spurious DotDispatchNoMatch / ambiguity naming an impl that visibly should have matched, with no hint that a key merely failed to line up. NOT A FREE FOLLOW-UP, which is why WI-764 did not do it: `*specificity = specificity.saturating_add(1)` at 12647 means enrolling changes overload ORDERING, not only acceptance. NOT DEMONSTRATED -- no repro was constructed; the asymmetry is read off the code plus WI-764s own measured producer split, so step one is to build one (a spec op over a `Relation[T = .., E = ..]` carrier, called with a relation cited from a rule). FIX SHAPE: route both lookups through the shared `binding_for_param` / `binding_index_for_param` with `BindingKeyMatch::for_bases`, and consider widening the base guard from raw `!=` to `same_sort_canonical` to match. ACCEPTANCE: a repro that type-checks but fails to dispatch, pinned as a regression test; both lookups enrolled through the shared helper; the overload-ORDERING effect of the specificity bump MEASURED (e.g. instrument candidate ranking across the corpus) rather than assumed harmless; full workspace green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-19T06:14:36Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-773", author: "user", content: "WI-772 review outcome + 2026-07-19 design discussion: (1) TermPrinter::print_rule now EXISTS in anthill-core (persistence/print.rs) and cpp-gen render_rule is deleted -- the renderer bullet is half done; remaining copies are the two anthill-cli solution printers, whose heads go through the cli-local render_value (Value renderer), so full promotion needs that renderer too. (2) Consider reframing the accessor as THE single-valued/table read primitive with a pluggable bodied-rule policy (facts-only-refuse today; WI-774 may graduate specific functors to resolve+ordering) rather than facts-only per se. (3) Closing phase should be a visibility RATCHET: after migration, demote raw head-as-answer enumeration (rules_by_functor + rule_head used as answers) to pub(crate) or a deliberately-named browse API so the WI-770 class becomes unrepresentable outside kb. Legitimate rule-aware consumers keep scoped access: CLI --match browser, prove lift (rule_ids_by_qn), smt-gen collect_rule, and write-side store addressing (WI-780). is_fact itself stays public -- hiding the predicate would push worse hacks.", at: "2026-07-19T12:10:24Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-774", author: "user", content: "2026-07-19 design discussion: candidate decided-policy for graduating a table functor to resolution: resolve (guards honored) + SEMANTIC solution ordering (most-specific-first via discrim specificity, the simp precedent -- NOT source/positional order, which the WI-760 landmine already rejected) + loud refusal on incomparable-specificity ties + WI-767-style loud depth-cap discipline for the search. The loud channel never disappears for single-valued reads -- shape refusal (WI-770/772) relocates to ambiguity refusal. Enumeration-shaped reads stay walkable; write-side reads need fact identity and stay store-addressed (WI-780). Engineering note: resolve needs &mut KnowledgeBase (WI-760 threading precedent).", at: "2026-07-19T12:10:31Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-772", description: some(value: "smt-gen mishandles bodied rules twice, surfaced by the WI-770 review. (a) lookup_explicit_policy (rustland/anthill-smt-gen/src/policy.rs:69) does `if !kb.is_fact(rid) { continue; }` -- a SILENT skip: a guarded `rule TranslationPolicy(...) :- <guard>` never applies even when its guard holds, and policy_for falls back to the Inline/LiftedAxiom defaults with no diagnostic -- the opposite polarity of WI-770's loud refusal for the identical authoring shape (repo rule: loud error over silent skip; the author gets no hint their rule shape is unsupported). (b) collect_facts_for_referenced_entities (rustland/anthill-smt-gen/src/lib.rs:1535) walks rules_by_functor with NO is_fact guard and accepts the first candidate whose head named_args carry concrete numeric literals: a bodied `rule LinkParameters(mass: 2.0, ...) :- heavy_variant()` feeds mass=2.0 into the SMT encoding whether or not the guard holds, so a formal safety proof can be discharged from a premise the source guarded -- an unsound 'proof passes' with no diagnostic, the highest-stakes instance of the class anywhere in the workspace. FIX DIRECTION: loud refusal (WI-770 shape) at both sites -- (a)'s message should name the rule and state that guarded policies are unsupported; (b) additionally keep refusing even when OTHER ground facts exist for the entity (the bodied rule may shadow or race the fact in enumeration order). Route through the WI-773 facts-only accessor if it lands first. ACCEPTANCE: a bodied TranslationPolicy rule and a bodied referenced-entity rule are each refused loudly in tests, never silently skipped or harvested; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-19T13:08:07Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-769", author: "user", content: "MEASUREMENT (corrects the ticket premise). The 'binding matcher executed by ZERO tests' claim was an artifact of instrumenting only the wi_tests binary. Probes over the FULL WORKSPACE suite: 21 function entries, 4 reaching the matcher with two same-base Parameterized sides -- all four from the WI-464 in-file unit tests (covariant_join_builds_parameterized_lub, contravariant_join_uses_meet, invariant_join_falls_back_to_bare_base, meet_glb_basics), which drive join_types/meet_types DIRECTLY; all four key both sides bare/bare (the module's param() helper interns short names), 0 key misses -- which is exactly why the defect stayed invisible: the only exercisers spell keys uniformly. The defect was then DEMONSTRATED red at that same unit level: cross-spelled keys (canonical anthill.prelude.Option.T vs bare T) -> KEYMISS fired -> the join collapsed Option[cat] join Option[dog] to bare Option, the meet to whole-type nothing. DISPLAY TRAP: both spellings PRINT as 'T' (resolve_sym short name), so a miss is invisible in any log -- only symbol identity differs. The ticket's prescribed end-to-end pin (join of a cited and an annotated relation keeps its schema) is NOT constructible as an observable: identical schemas are mutually types_compatible post-WI-764, take join_types' (true,true) arm, and never reach the matcher; differing named-tuple schemas do not lattice-join per-param, so pre- and post-fix both produce the conservative bare Relation. The observable delta needs joinable-but-unequal binding values (entity sorts under a covariant param: cat/dog -> Animal), pinned at the injection level per the wi617 precedent. Also MEASURED: extract_type presents a duplicate-keyed term BY KEY (first value repeated) -- a values-differing duplicate cannot reach the lattice as such.", at: "2026-07-19T10:14:11Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-769", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED (reachable branch + review hardening; /code-review max: 10 finder angles + verify + sweep). combine_parameterized_same_base: ONE BindingKeyMatch::for_bases call derives both the same-base gate (key_match != Label -> None; canonical sort identity -- a raw != read a WI-617 twin pair as different sorts, spurious no-join) and the loop's key mode (the dispatch arm-(2) idiom); the per-binding lookup is the shared binding_index_for_param. Construction canonicalizes at the producer (WI-581): base via canonical_sort_sym AND result keys re-keyed to the sort's declared canonical param symbols -- SIX review angles independently flagged that a-side key spellings made the result argument-order-dependent, breaking join_types' documented == commutativity (plus a discrim positional-match hazard via canonicalize_record_named_args ordering canonical keys by a different rule). The re-key is GATED: only an UNDOTTED (bare-written) key re-keys by label; a dotted foreign key (another sort's T / WI-708 op-scoped) keeps its spelling -- ungated, the declared scan put same_label's both-dotted debug_assert on a previously assert-free path (MEASURED: the foreign-key test panics at typing.rs:9819 without the gate) and silently hijacked foreign keys in release. Label-bridging is NOT injective: a consumed-index guard (used slots) plus a result-key collision check make 'the result never carries duplicate keys' unconditional -- a duplicate-keyed side (the WI-764-recorded producer class) previously hit the sound fallback only by ACCIDENT of the raw miss; bridged, it would mint a duplicate-keyed type dropping one of b's bindings (for the GLB not even a lower bound). Both guards' discrimination PROVEN by disabling each (red) and restoring (green). Consumer aligned: the WI-595 sharpening head-pin in value_type_term_d compares heads via same_sort_canonical -- raw == would silently de-fire on a twin-copy value head now that the meet constructs canonically. RECORDED, NOT FIXED: (a) WI-595 may now sharpen a cross-spelled incompatible bound to Base[T=nothing] instead of dropping it (designed meet behavior, pre-existing for same-spelled keys; the head-pin comment's 'incomparable meets to nothing, a different head' is stale for same-base parameterized pairs generally -- a WI-595-side diagnostics question); (b) a debug tripwire distinguishing legit partial-instantiation misses from a fifth spelling drift was considered and DEFERRED: legitimate key spellings (WI-708 op-scoped, provider views) cannot be bounded tightly enough for an assert; producer-side validation is the right home; (c) structural commutativity is SCOPED: constrained params (filtered from sort_type_params_as_pairs), foreign-spelled keys, and invariant-arm kept values still follow the a-side (doc says so). 7 unit regressions (cross-spelled join+meet in both orders with canonical-result-key asserts, twin-base both orders, duplicate-keyed side, symmetric mixed-spelling duplicates, foreign dotted key). binding_for_param ledger updated: WI-769 enrolled with the corrected reachability note; goals_equal remains the one un-enrolled site, plus an in-situ note for matches_variance_fact. Full workspace green via scripts/test.sh.", at: "2026-07-19T10:14:29Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-769", description: some(value: "The LUB/GLB type lattice matches parameterized binding keys by RAW SYMBOL IDENTITY and silently drops the schema on a miss -- and its matcher has ZERO test coverage. SITE: combine_parameterized_same_base (rustland/anthill-core/src/kb/typing.rs, fn at 24630) -- raw base guard `if a_base != b_base` at 24646, then `b_binds.iter().find(|(q, _)| q == param)` at 24662 whose else-arm is `return Some(fallback(kb))`. SAME DEFECT CLASS AS WI-764 (a same-base guard followed by a raw-identity binding-key lookup, where a rule citation keys T canonically as `anthill.prelude.Relation.T` and a written signature keys it bare). WORSE FAILURE MODE THAN THE TWO SITES WI-764 FIXED: unify width-IGNORES a miss (silently true) and subtype REJECTS it (loud), but this one returns the conservative bound -- the BARE sort `Ref(Relation)` for Lub, `nothing` for Glb -- so a join of two same-sort relations keyed differently would silently LOSE T and E. Because a bare `S` conforms to `S[anything]` in the (parameterized, sort_ref) arm, a downstream annotation check could then accept a wrong schema with no diagnostic. TWO MEASUREMENTS, both from the WI-764 code review, both of which argue this is currently INERT rather than live -- record them so they are not re-derived: (1) HYPOTHESIS FALSIFIED -- five source-level if-join probes (annotation+citation, annotation+annotation, citation+citation, each with right and wrong schemas) ALL behaved correctly, because branch typing checks each arm against the declared return rather than routing through the lattice; (2) instrumenting the function ENTRY across the whole wi_tests suite yields only 13 entries, and in every one at least one side is not Parameterized so it returns at the extract_type guards -- i.e. the binding matcher itself is executed by NO existing test, including if_branch_join_test. So the real question is REACHABILITY, not the patch. ACCEPTANCE: determine by measurement whether any caller reaches the binding matcher with two Parameterized sides (instrument and run the corpus; if none, say so explicitly); if reachable, enroll through the shared `binding_for_param` with `BindingKeyMatch::for_bases`, widen the base guard to `same_sort_canonical`, and pin a regression test that a join of a cited and an annotated relation KEEPS its schema; if unreachable, record that and address the silent `fallback` on its own terms -- CLAUDE.md requires a loud error over a silent skip, and a key miss returning the bare sort reads as handled when it is not. Full workspace green via scripts/test.sh either way."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-19T10:16:29Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-777", description: some(value: "scaland's parser cannot read a one-component tuple type, so Rust and Scala now accept different languages. SITE: AnthillParser.scala's tupleType (scaland/core/src/main/scala/anthill/parse/AnthillParser.scala, ~line 314) = ('(' ~ ')') | ('(' ~ tupleTypeArg ~ (',' ~/ tupleTypeArg).rep(1) ~ ')') -- the .rep(1) forces two or more components, so there is no one-component alternative at all. For '(a: Int64)': arrowType backtracks (no '->'), tupleType fails the .rep(1), and simpleType cannot start at '('. DIVERGENCE BEGAN AT WI-763 (the denoted keep spec '(who: \"name\")') and is widened by WI-766, which also RESTRUCTURED the Rust grammar: an arrow's parameter list IS a tuple_type there now, with no separate arrow_params production, and a bare '(A)' parses as a one-component tuple and is refused at CONVERSION rather than by the grammar. Porting the .rep(1) fix alone would leave the two implementations structurally different even where they agree on the accepted language. CONSEQUENCE: any stdlib/ or examples/ file adopting the newly-admitted form loads in rustland and fails to parse in scaland, and NO test catches the split -- the two parsers are never run over the same corpus. Per docs the Rust implementation is ground truth. WORTH DECIDING FIRST, and recording on this ticket: whether scaland is still intended to track the kernel language, since the answer changes this from a port to a deprecation. ACCEPTANCE if tracking: scaland parses '(a: A)', '(a: \"x\")' and '(A) -> B', rejects a bare '(A)' as a type, and a shared-corpus test asserts both parsers accept/reject the same files so the next divergence is caught; sbt test green."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-766", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED via a THIRD route the ticket did not list, chosen on measurement. The ticket offered (a) declare a conflict [$.arrow_params, $.tuple_type] or (b) leave the surface alone. (a) was built and measured FIRST -- it works, costs +4 states, and its real-source cost is below the noise floor -- and was then superseded: an arrow's parameter list and a tuple type are the SAME surface, and keeping them as two productions is precisely what made `( name : Type )` ambiguous, since the parser had to pick a production at the `)`, one token before the `->` that tells them apart. arrow_type's params IS a tuple_type now; arrow_params and the declared conflict are both deleted, and `->` is an ordinary shift after the reduce. MEASURED vs the conflict variant: 3514 states vs 3523 (and 5 FEWER than the pre-WI-766 baseline of 3519), parser.c 1884 bytes SMALLER than baseline, ZERO forks where the conflict added +24 forked steps corpus-wide.\n\nTICKET PREMISES CORRECTED. (1) The conflict tree-sitter actually reports is [tuple_type, _tuple_type_arg], not [arrow_params, tuple_type]. (2) The ticket's \"a spec decision is needed first, because admitting (a: A) makes it differ from (A), which the spec says is grouping\" is FALSE: in TYPE position `(A)` was a plain syntax error, never grouping. The spec's grouping sentences are about TERM position (kernel-language.md:385/402) and its \"a single parenthesized element is not a 1-tuple (WI-620)\" line is about PATTERN position. There was no existing type-position meaning to contradict, so the spec question was narrower than stated.\n\nBEHAVIOR MOVED, NOT LOST. `(A)` now parses, because it is a valid parameter list; convert_tuple_type rejects the TYPE reading with a located message, while convert_arrow_type -- walking the same node as a parameter list -- does not. Walking it in two places is what scopes the rule to type position, pinned by a control test asserting `(Int64) -> Int64` stays legal. This is the same parse-permissive / convert-strict split WI-763 argued for, so the one-parameter `(a: \"x\") -> B` now gets that explanatory diagnostic instead of a bare syntax error (wi763's test strengthened from is_err() to the message; its doc comment described the prec that no longer exists).\n\nFIXED FOR FREE. `(a: Int64,)` and `(Int64,)` were parsing CLEAN into a tuple carrying a PHANTOM zero-width component -- on the baseline AND on the conflict variant -- because tree-sitter recovers a missing token as a zero-width node that is neither ERROR nor MISSING, so has_error() is false and collect_syntax_errors prunes at the root. Both are loud now. Pinned as behavior rather than mechanism, since the mechanism is a recovery heuristic.\n\nTWO DOWNSTREAM DEFECTS the newly-writable type exposed, both SILENT, both fixed with tests verified RED without the fix. (1) Rust codegen rendered a 1-tuple as `(T)`, which Rust reads as plain `T`, so `-> (a: Int64)` and `-> Int64` emitted identical signatures; now `(T,)`, at both tuple arms. (2) The printer rendered an arrow over a 1-component tuple identically to one over a named scalar -- the loader reads a single parameter by TYPE and drops the label -- so the printed text re-parsed as a DIFFERENT type; arity 1 now keeps its parens, arity 2+ unchanged.\n\nMY OWN MEASUREMENT WAS WRONG, and is corrected in the grammar comment. Counting forking sites by REGEX scored ~400, by matching `lambda (x: T) ->` binders (which never reach this conflict) and grammar quoted inside string literals in workitems.anthill, which is 81% of the repo's .anthill bytes and contains no arrow types. Counted from parse trees it is 12, in ~600 KB of code. The \"+13.7%\" was likewise unreproducible: ~+3-4% against a +/-2-3% noise floor, and a non-interleaved A/B of the same inputs swings +41%/-10% -- interleave or the number is meaningless. Every correction made the change look CHEAPER, so the decision was never at risk, but the numbers would have been cited as a calibration point for the next \"is a GLR fork affordable?\" question.\n\nREVIEW: /code-review at max (10 finder angles + verify + sweep). It caught a BUILD-BREAKER -- a review agent's scratch probe wired into the tracked wi_tests.rs, so a fresh checkout would not compile -- plus four documentation inaccuracies (including spec text at the 1-collapse section instructing users to write `(a: x.f)`, a value form that does not exist) and a stale converter comment. Both spec grammar copies were unified: section 4.5 and the section 11 Collected Grammar, which had never listed TupleType at all and still carried WI-763 residue.\n\nFILED: WI-775 (typer accepts a name-keyed tuple where a _N-keyed one is required, then field access TRAPS at runtime -- pre-existing soundness hole, widened to arity 1 here), WI-776 (a written 1-tuple never collapses but every computed one-column result always does, so the natural spelling is unsatisfiable; Concat and Without stop being inverses at arity 1), WI-777 (scaland cannot parse the one-component form -- divergence began at WI-763 and this restructure widens it), WI-778 (entity and operation parameters still SILENTLY accept a missing name or type -- the surviving half of the zero-width recovery class, since the tuple-type half is fixed above).\n\nFull workspace 3115 passed / 0 failed; tree-sitter corpus 183/183.", at: "2026-07-19T12:04:01Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-766", description: some(value: "Grammar: make a GENERAL one-component named tuple type `(a: A)` writable. WI-763 admitted the ONE-component form only for the DENOTED shape (`(who: \"name\")`, a one-entry keep spec), leaving an ASYMMETRY: `(a: \"x\")` is a 1-tuple type but `(a: A)` is not a type at all. CAUSE, measured during WI-763: `(a: A)` is EXACTLY `arrow_params`' single form (`seq('(', $._tuple_type_arg, ')')`, grammar.js), and the two readings are told apart only by whether `->` follows the `)` -- lookahead PAST the closing paren, which is not LR(1). The denoted form needs no fork because a literal after `:` cannot begin a `_type`, so `field_decl` is already dead; that is why only it was admitted, via prec(1). ROUTES: (a) declare a conflict `[$.arrow_params, $.tuple_type]` and let GLR keep both readings alive to the `->` -- costs a stack fork on EVERY named single-param arrow type, the cost WI-763 deliberately refused; (b) leave the surface alone. DECIDE (a) vs (b) on measured parse cost, not on symmetry alone. NOTE this is NOT the same question as bare `(A)`: spec docs/kernel-language.md and WI-620 state a single parenthesized element is GROUPING and NOT a 1-tuple, so `(A)` must stay grouping -- only the NAMED single component is in scope here, and admitting it makes `(a: A)` and `(A)` mean different things, which needs a spec decision before any grammar edit. ACCEPTANCE if (a): `(a: A)` parses as a one-component tuple_type; `(a: A) -> B` still parses as an arrow; docs/kernel-language.md TupleType production updated; corpus green; workspace green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-763"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-19T12:04:03Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-775", description: some(value: "Typer accepts a name-keyed tuple where a POSITIONAL _N-keyed one is required, then field access TRAPS at runtime. SITE: align_named_tuple_fields (rustland/anthill-core/src/kb/typing.rs, search 'WI-442'), whose positional fallback admits a_fields.len() == b_fields.len() && (is_positional(a) || is_positional(b)) -- so a tuple keyed (a: T) satisfies a parameter declared (_1: T). Reading ._N then goes to Value::Tuple.pos, which is EMPTY for a name-keyed tuple, and evaluation raises Internal(\"field_access: tuple has no component '_1'\"). MEASURED end-to-end: 'operation get1(t: (_1: Int64)) -> Int64 = t._1' + 'operation one() -> (a: Int64) = (a: 5, b: 6)' + 'operation drive() -> Int64 = get1(one())' LOADS CLEAN and traps only when drive() runs. PRE-EXISTING, not introduced by WI-766: an arity-2 control ((_1: Int64, _2: Int64) fed a (a: Int64, b: Int64)) reproduces with syntax that was already legal. WI-766 widens it to arity 1 by making (_1: T) writable. This is a type-system soundness hole -- the typer's job is to make this unrepresentable, and it currently licenses it. ACCEPTANCE: either the positional fallback stops bridging name-keyed to _N-keyed tuples (preferred -- decide by measuring what legitimately depends on it), or field access resolves _N against a name-keyed tuple by position instead of trapping; a regression test drives the failing program end-to-end (load AND evaluate, since load alone is clean); full workspace green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-19T13:07:47Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-780", author: "user", content: "2026-07-19 design discussion, part 2 (million-fact scenario): the boundary-identity question has a sharper answer than seam cleanup -- RuleId must become the address of a RESIDENT entry, not the identity of a fact, because a custom fact-storage backend with ~10^6 rows must NOT materialize a RuleEntry per row (today every fact = kb.rules Vec slot + hash-consed head + rules_by_functor/by_sort/by_domain/discrim entries; retract only tombstones, slots are never reclaimed; load parses+interns the full extent before the first query). Target shape = the deductive-DB EDB/IDB split: intensional content (rules, program facts, realization tables) stays resident and RuleId-addressed; extensional bulk facts live in the store, addressed by store-native identity (primary/domain key or content), with NO resident RuleId. REQUIRES: (a) a fact-source seam in resolution -- candidate lookup for a store-backed functor delegates to the store, the goal pattern ground fields becoming the store query (canonical named-arg order maps directly to a SQL WHERE); the discrim tree indexes only resident content; solutions return VALUES not RuleIds (resolve answers questions, walks address facts); (b) writes keyed by store identity -- the FactId=Handle(RuleId) boundary is untenable under virtualization, not merely inelegant; (c) semantics decided deliberately: NAF/constraints over an external extent need snapshot/closed-world discipline; proposal 053 fact_monotonicity + WI-177 epochs become the cache-coherence story for hot cached rows. SCOPE NOTE: this widens WI-780 from retract-seam cleanup to virtualizable fact extents; if that proves too big for one item, split an EDB/IDB umbrella design item and fold WI-780 (store addressing + retract seam) under it.", at: "2026-07-19T16:49:31Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-773", author: "user", content: "2026-07-19 (WI-780 EDB/IDB follow-through): design the accessor VALUES-FIRST -- the public read shape must not return or require RuleId, or it bakes in full residence, exactly what WI-780 virtualized-extent design forbids (a store-backed functor has no resident RuleId to hand out). Fact-address stays a store-layer concern behind the seam. Reads answer with values/rows; the loud bodied-rule channel reports the offending rule by rendered text (print_rule), not by id. WI-780 design output should be consulted while shaping this API.", at: "2026-07-19T17:03:45Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-780", author: "user", content: "2026-07-19 dependency note: the WI-773 edge was DROPPED -- only the RuleId-PRIVATIZATION step truly sequences after WI-773 reader migration (~20 external sites hold RuleIds via the raw walk; cannot privatize a type external callers traffic in). The design itself is unblocked and should land EARLY enough to shape WI-773 accessor API (values-first, no RuleId in the public read shape).", at: "2026-07-19T17:03:48Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-783", description: some(value: "NAMED ARGUMENTS in a function-VALUE application are bound by WRITTEN ORDER, not by name -- a silently wrong answer with no trap and no diagnostic. MEASURED end-to-end (verified directly, not inferred): 'operation sub2(a: Int64, b: Int64) -> Int64 = a - b' + 'operation apply2(f: (acc: Int64, x: Int64) -> Int64) -> Int64 = f(x: 10, acc: 3)' + 'operation drive() -> Int64 = apply2(sub2)' loads clean and evaluates to 7 (i.e. 10 - 3: the labels are ignored and the operands bind positionally in the order WRITTEN); changing ONLY the argument order at the call site to 'f(acc: 3, x: 10)' evaluates to -7. The declared binder names acc/x are inert. This is strictly worse than the WI-775 failure mode it neighbours: WI-775 traps loudly at eval, this returns a plausible wrong number. Distinct from WI-775 (closed: DATA-tuple key bridging) and from WI-782 (parameter-list ORDER/ARITY/slot-collapse in the TYPE relation) -- this one is in EVALUATION of an application whose callee is a function value, and it reproduces with MATCHING keys on both sides, so no positional bridging is involved. A control shows the name-keyed path is not merely mis-bound but unimplemented for closures: a 2-binder 'lambda (acc, x) -> ...' applied as 'f(x: 10, acc: 3)' traps ArityMismatch{op: closure, expected: 1, got: 2}. ACCEPTANCE: either bind named arguments of a function-value application to the arrow's declared binder names (and raise on an unknown label), or REJECT named arguments in that position with a located diagnostic -- silently reordering is not an option; regression test drives both orderings end-to-end and asserts they agree; full workspace green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-19T20:11:23Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-785", description: some(value: "A destructuring LAMBDA cannot match a NAME-keyed tuple -- `match_tuple_pattern` read only `Value::Tuple.pos`, so a named tuple presented as ZERO components, failed the arity test, and raised at eval on a program that loaded clean. MEASURED (fresh Interpreter per case): with `operation apply_tuple(f: Function[A = (acc: Int64, x: Int64), B = Int64]) -> Int64 = f((acc: 3, x: 10))`, passing `lambda (acc, x) -> acc - x` RAISED, while all three neighbours worked -- the positional tuple type `Function[A = (Int64, Int64), B]` with the same lambda gave Ok(-7), an OPERATION taking one `(acc: Int64, x: Int64)` parameter gave Ok(-7), and a non-destructuring `lambda t -> t.acc - t.x` gave Ok(-7). So exactly one corner was broken: destructuring-binder over name-keyed tuple. ROOT CAUSE: eval/pattern.rs `match_tuple_pattern` matched `Value::Tuple { pos, .. }` and compared `pos.len()` against the binder count, discarding the value's `named` components entirely; a name-keyed tuple carries all of its components in `named` and none in `pos`. The sibling `match_constructor_pattern` in the same file already had the right shape -- `constructor_sub_values` presents positional-then-named so the sub-pattern loop is keying-agnostic, plus WI-445 label resolution for named sub-patterns -- so this was an asymmetry between two adjacent matchers, not a missing concept. SECOND SILENT GAP FIXED WITH IT: the dispatch arm read `Pattern::Tuple { positional, .. }` and dropped the PATTERN's own `named` sub-patterns on the floor, so a labelled tuple pattern bound nothing and still reported a match -- a silent skip against the repo's loud-error principle. FIX: present the scrutinee's components as positional-then-named and match arity-strictly, with labelled sub-patterns resolved by LABEL, mirroring `match_constructor_pattern`. Indexing components positionally is well-defined precisely BECAUSE a named tuple is an ORDERED PRODUCT -- `canonicalize_record_named_args` (kb/resolve.rs:3842) returns early for one, documented \"source order IS its identity\", so component i is the i-th component as spelled. VERIFIED it is source order and not alphabetical: a tuple written `(x: 3, acc: 10)` (whose alphabetical order is acc,x) destructured by `lambda (p, q) -> p - q` yields -7, i.e. p bound the FIRST WRITTEN component; alphabetical binding would have yielded +7. Arity strictness verified too: a 3-component tuple against a 2-binder pattern still does not match. This does NOT re-conflate WI-775's keying distinction: that governs a tuple TYPE's identity, whereas a destructuring binder list is not itself a tuple and claims only \"bind the i-th component\". DISTINCT from WI-784 (closure ARITY -- `enter_closure` is strictly unary and rejects an N-arg application outright); this one applies the closure with exactly ONE argument and fails later, inside pattern matching. Found while code-reviewing WI-783. ACCEPTANCE: a destructuring lambda over a name-keyed tuple evaluates, agreeing with the positional-tuple and named-operation spellings of the same call; binding is by SOURCE order, pinned by a case whose field names sort differently from their written order; arity strictness retained (an N-component tuple does not match an M-binder pattern for N != M); a labelled tuple sub-pattern binds the component carrying that label and fails to match on an unknown or already-covered label; full workspace green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-19T21:46:04Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-785", author: "user", content: "CORRECTION to this ticket's own description: the claimed 'SECOND SILENT GAP' -- that the dispatch arm dropped a tuple pattern's labelled sub-patterns, so '(x: a, y: b) bound nothing and still reported a match' -- is FALSE and was never a live bug. Verified four ways: the grammar has no labelled tuple-pattern element (`_tuple_pattern_elem` is `_pattern | typed_binder`, and grammar.js states `named_pattern_field` is 'used only inside constructor patterns'); the parser emits `pattern_tuple` with positional elements only (parse/convert.rs ~1894); the sole `Pattern::Tuple` producer hardcodes `named: Vec::new()` (kb/node_occurrence.rs ~2810); and the reflect encoding `tuple_pattern(elements: ...)` has no named channel. So `Pattern::Tuple.named` is ALWAYS EMPTY. Moreover '(x: a, y: b)' in tuple position parses as two WI-517 TYPED BINDERS (binders x,y at types a,b) which bound correctly before AND after -- measured. The delivered fix therefore no longer implements labelled sub-patterns; it refuses them with a debug_assert plus a documented no-match, since carrying untestable code for a shape no producer builds is worse than declining it. SECOND CORRECTION -- the first cut of this fix was UNSOUND and is now fixed: presenting components as `pos ++ named` is NOT source order, because `classify_ctor_arg` routes a component into `pos` iff its label starts with '_' (how the parser's synthetic _1/_2 return to pos), and a user label like '_b' takes the same branch. MEASURED on the first cut: `lambda (p, q) -> p - q` over `(a: 3, _b: 10)` returned 7 instead of -7; `(a:1,_b:2,c:3)` returned 213 instead of 123; and an operation declared '-> Int64' returned Str(\"ess\") on a clean load -- a type-soundness hole, converting a pre-fix LOUD raise into a silent wrong answer. The delivered fix now applies the widening ONLY where the split is unambiguous (one of pos/named empty) and refuses the mixed shape, whose interleaving is unrecoverable because `pos` keeps no labels. Two regression tests pin both (mixed_pos_and_named_tuple_refuses_rather_than_misbinding, mixed_tuple_cannot_smuggle_a_string_into_an_int_slot). Root cause filed as WI-786; when it lands, the mixed-shape refusal can be relaxed. Found by /code-review (4 of 10 angles independently built binaries and drove it); my original 5 tests were all all-positional or all-named and could not reach the shape.", at: "2026-07-20T05:42:59Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-786", description: some(value: "`classify_ctor_arg` splits a tuple literal's components into `Value::Tuple.pos` vs `.named` by a `_`-PREFIX TEST ON THE LABEL TEXT, so a USER-WRITTEN label beginning with `_` is silently reclassified as positional -- the pos/named split then no longer follows source order, and the label is discarded. eval/eval.rs ~2583 does `kb.resolve_sym(sym).starts_with('_')`; the test exists to unwrap the parser's SYNTHETIC `_1`/`_2` labels (positional surface syntax lowers to them, per kernel-language.md \"Positional syntax is sugar for auto-generated names `_1`, `_2`\"), but the identifier grammar is `/[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9_-]*/`, so `_id`, `_b`, `_total` take the same branch. MEASURED: `(a: 3, _b: 10)` builds `Value::Tuple { pos: [10], named: [(a, 3)] }` -- the SECOND written component first, the first one second. A three-component `(a: 1, _b: 2, c: 3)` builds `pos: [2], named: [(a,1),(c,3)]`. THIS IS A KNOWN HAZARD THAT WAS GUARDED IN EXACTLY ONE PLACE: `validate_projection_labels` (parse/convert.rs ~1139-1147, WI-639 distributive projection) rejects `_`-prefixed labels with the comment \"classify_ctor_arg re-slots ANY `_`-keyed tuple field positionally in argument order, discarding the label, so a reordered projection silently returns the wrong column\". The same guard was never applied to the PLAIN tuple-literal producer, which is the common path. CONSEQUENCES BEYOND THE PRODUCER: (a) any consumer that reconstructs source order as `pos ++ named` gets the wrong order -- measured while fixing WI-785, where binding a destructuring lambda across the split returned 7 instead of -7 and made an operation declared `-> Int64` return `Str(\"ess\")` on a clean load; WI-785 now REFUSES the mixed shape rather than guessing, which is a workaround, not a fix; (b) `t.x` field access (eval/builtins.rs ~272) looks the label up in `named` ONLY, so a component reclassified into `pos` becomes unreachable by name -- `t._b` would have to be spelled positionally, and `_b` is indistinguishable from the synthetic `_2`; (c) the label is DISCARDED on the move to `pos` (`pos` is a bare `Vec<Value>`), so the split is UNRECOVERABLE downstream -- no consumer can re-interleave. FIX DIRECTIONS (pick one): (1) narrow the test to a label that is exactly `_<index+1>` for its own position, so only genuinely synthetic labels are unwrapped and `_b`/`_id` stay named -- smallest, and makes `pos ++ named` source-order-faithful whenever the tuple came from a literal; (2) reject `_`-prefixed user labels at the tuple-literal producer the way `validate_projection_labels` already does for projections -- consistent with the existing guard but a surface-compatibility break; (3) stop encoding positionality in the label text -- carry an explicit arity/positional marker on the tuple literal so the classifier never has to guess from a name. Direction (1) is the least invasive and preserves the existing surface. NOTE this interacts with the repo's stated direction that a tuple is an ORDERED PRODUCT whose \"source order IS its identity\" (kb/resolve.rs `canonicalize_record_named_args` returns early for `TupleLiteral`): that exemption keeps `named` internally ordered but cannot help once components have been split across two vectors by label text. ACCEPTANCE: a tuple literal mixing plain and `_`-prefixed user labels preserves source order in whatever representation survives (either both stay in `named`, or the literal is rejected at load with a located diagnostic); `t._b` on such a tuple resolves the component the user wrote; the WI-785 mixed-shape refusal (`mixed_pos_and_named_tuple_refuses_rather_than_misbinding`) can be relaxed to a successful positional bind, or is restated as the permanent rule with its reason updated; a regression test drives a mixed-label tuple end-to-end through destructuring AND field access and asserts they agree; full workspace green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-20T06:29:38Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-785", author: "user", content: "WI-786 (delivered) removed this ticket's mixed-shape workaround. With classify_ctor_arg narrowed, `pos ++ named` is now guaranteed source order, so match_tuple_pattern no longer refuses a tuple split across both halves -- the refusal existed only because the split was unreliable. The two workaround tests moved to wi786_tuple_component_order_test.rs and now assert CORRECT binding instead of refusal. This file keeps the 5 tests about destructuring a name-keyed tuple.", at: "2026-07-20T06:29:39Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-786", author: "user", content: "REGRESSION introduced by this ticket, filed as WI-787: narrowing classify_ctor_arg moved user `_`-prefixed labels out of `pos` into `named`, and `spread_eta_args` (eval/eval.rs ~1701) reads `pos` alone -- so those components vanished from its view. Driven A/B with ONLY classify_ctor_arg reverted: `(_x: 3, _y: 10)`, `(_2: 3, _1: 10)` and `(_01: 3, _02: 10)` each returned Ok(Int(-7)) before 93b23188 and now raise ArityMismatch{expected:2, got:1} when applied to a 2-parameter op-ref. Non-contrived path: `pr.takeN(5).map(sub2)` over all-named relation rows traps while the same call with a lambda works. My delivery summary claimed WI-786 had no regressions; that was wrong -- I checked only that the suite stayed green, and no test covered a `_`-labelled tuple reaching an op-ref. WI-786's own fix remains correct and is not being reverted; WI-787 fixes the consumer that was not enrolled in the new invariant.", at: "2026-07-20T08:21:16Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-782", author: "user", content: "THIS TICKET NOW GATES TWO LIVE SILENT-WRONG-ANSWER BUGS ON MAIN, not just a type-relation tidy-up. Both are the same defect at different sites: `align_named_tuple_fields` tries its BY-NAME rung first, so a permuted set of names type-checks, while the RUNTIME binds by POSITION -- the typer and eval then disagree about which value a name denotes. (1) WI-783 (Delivered) has a confirmed permuted-ARROW regression: `impl_ba(b, a)` passed where `(a, b)` is declared, `f(b: 10, a: 3)` returns 7 while the positional spelling of the SAME written order returns -7 -- driven with three controls. (2) WI-788 (new) is the data-tuple twin: `f((b: \\\"ess\\\", a: 3))` against declared `(a: Int64, b: String)` loads clean and returns a String from an operation declared `-> Int64`. Whatever this ticket settles for parameter lists should settle both -- they are the same rung of the same function, so fixing them separately guarantees drift. The shape of the fix in both cases: a type used to align a POSITIONALLY-CONSUMED binder list (arrow parameters, destructuring binders) must align by position, or reject the by-name permutation; by-name alignment stays correct for NAME-consuming readers like `t.x` field access (WI-638 defines that as order-independent). The bug is applying one relation to both kinds of consumer.", at: "2026-07-20T08:26:25Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-783", author: "user", content: "The permuted-arrow regression recorded against this ticket has a TWIN, filed as WI-788: a destructuring binder over an out-of-order tuple LITERAL binds by source position while the typer typed the binders by the type's declared field order, so `f((b: \\\"ess\\\", a: 3))` against `(a: Int64, b: String)` loads clean and returns a String from an `-> Int64` operation. Same root cause as this ticket's own regression -- `align_named_tuple_fields`' by-name rung admitting a permutation the runtime does not perform -- one on the arrow-parameter-list side (here) and one on the data-tuple side (WI-788). Both gate on WI-782, which has been annotated accordingly. Recording the link so neither is fixed in isolation: they are the same rung of the same function, and a fix to one that does not generalise will leave the other live.", at: "2026-07-20T08:26:26Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-788", author: "claude", content: "WI-782 is DELIVERED, so this is unblocked; recording the measurement it produced so the ByName decision need not be re-derived. WI-782 fixed the ParamList rung ONLY: equal arity + positional correspondence (names may differ -- the _1.._n eta rung -- but may not be reordered or dropped), plus an arity-1 TUPLE-typed parameter list now keeps a one-field wrapper so a parameter list is distinguishable from a lone tuple parameter (arrow_param_slot_for_list in kb/typing.rs; surface producer in kb/load.rs; decoder in arrow_parts). The ByName rung is EXACTLY as it was, which is why this ticket is still live. INSTRUMENTED BLAST RADIUS of tightening ByName: I logged every align_named_tuple_fields call whose by-name rung succeeded but an equal-arity/same-order-names rung would have refused, across the WHOLE workspace suite. 7 firings, all ByName, in 3 places. (1) PERMUTED x2, both from wi488_producer_component_escape_test::named_tuple_reordered_component_abstracting_producer_flagged -- a DELIBERATE test, fixture 'operation mkBare(m: MemStore) -> (b: Bool, a: KVStore) = (a: m, b: true)', a permuted RETURN asserted to pass conformance and then be flagged by the WI-488 abstracting-producer gate. That fixture is itself an instance of THIS bug (carrier is source-order [a,b], declared type says [b,a]); refusing the permutation changes its premise, so it would need a non-permuted fixture plus a separate guard for its 'align by name, not a raw positional zip' intent. (2) WIDTH x5: typing_test::subtype_named_tuple_width (explicit unit test asserting (a: Int64, b: String) <: (a: Int64)), plus two load-ERROR paths that end in rejection anyway (wi766_one_component_tuple_type_test::load_errs, wi775_positional_tuple_bridge_test::assert_arg_mismatch). ZERO firings elsewhere. So this is a genuine LANGUAGE decision -- does a named tuple's field ORDER belong to its identity, and does width subtyping survive -- gated on one deliberate permutation test and one deliberate width test, NOT on broad breakage. The WI-782 feedback points at the narrower answer: make the rule depend on the CONSUMER's read discipline (positionally-consumed destructuring binder list -> positional; name-consuming t.x -> by name), which leaves both those tests intact. CORRECTION to this ticket's premise: the ARROW half did NOT reproduce. Driven directly, 'f(b: 10, a: 3)' returns -7 and 'f(10, 3)' returns 7 -- inverted from the numbers recorded here, and both CORRECT: they are different calls (the first says a=3, the second a=10), and impl_ba's own binder names never reach its eta arrow, which is keyed _1.._n. So there is ONE live silent-wrong-answer bug in this cluster (the data-tuple one, this ticket), not two.", at: "2026-07-20T09:47:02Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-782", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED as cases 1+2 ONLY; case 3 (SLOT COLLAPSE) is deferred to WI-791 with a measured reason, agreed with the user. SHIPPED: TupleAlign::ParamList now aligns POSITIONALLY with EQUAL ARITY and binder names IGNORED entirely (align_named_tuple_fields, kb/typing.rs). That kills case 1 (ORDER -- the permuted list is now compared slot-for-slot and fails on the TYPES, instead of being matched up by name; this was the ticket's only SILENT WRONG ANSWER, an op declared -> Int64 evaluating to Bool(true)) and case 2 (ARITY -- width subtyping is not available to a list eval applies positionally). The WI-442 _1.._n positional rung and its helper is_positional_tuple_names are RETIRED, not merely bypassed: with names ignored there is no name test to escape from. BLAST RADIUS MEASURED BEFORE CHANGING ANYTHING: instrumented every align_named_tuple_fields call across the whole workspace suite -- the positional rung fired 81 times, ALL the single legitimate (_1,_2) vs (acc,x) eta shape, and ZERO pre-existing permuted/width cases in ParamList mode. So the tightening rejects only the buggy programs. The 10 regression tests were verified in an isolated git worktree to FAIL pre-fix (the rejections) while the CONTROLS PASS pre-fix, so they discriminate rather than track. DEFERRED, and why: case 3 needs the param slot to record parameter-list ARITY. I implemented the ticket's suggested route -- a conditional wrapper at the mint (wrap the arity-1 list iff its sole parameter's type is a tuple) plus a decoder in arrow_parts -- at both producers, and the full workspace went green. Review + driving then found it LEAKS, because the wrap is decided from the SPELLING at mint time but read back AFTER substitution. MEASURED: 'apply1[T](f: (x: T) -> Int64, v: T)' called as 'apply1(get_a, (a: 1, b: 2))' bound T to the WRAPPER and the sibling parameter rejected the literal -- a program that works on main. Also measured: the wrapper keys its field by the written binder name (which an arity-1 slot previously dropped), so '(t: (a,b)) -> R' stopped conforming to '(u: (a,b)) -> R'; and with the wrapper the printer's output DIVERGED, gaining a layer per print/reparse round until the third generation no longer parsed. All reverted. The lesson worth keeping: an encoding that unification can introduce or erase is not an invariant -- the sigma-stable alternative is a UNIFORM parameter list at every arity, which is WI-791. Case 3 remains a LOUD TRAP, not a silent wrong answer, which is what made deferring it the right trade; it is pinned by 'known_gap_tuple_typed_parameter_is_not_distinguished_from_a_two_parameter_list' so the gap stays visible and that test fails when WI-791 lands. docs/kernel-language.md states the shipped conformance rule and carries the known gap. Full workspace green (3158 passed).", at: "2026-07-20T10:30:38Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-782", description: some(value: "Arrow PARAMETER LISTS are compared by NAME with width subtyping, which licenses three more load-clean-then-trap programs. All PRE-EXISTING (measured identical on WI-775's parent commit db69a842 and after WI-775; WI-775 neither introduced nor widened them) and all distinct from WI-775's DATA-tuple bridge, which is closed. SITE: align_named_tuple_fields (rustland/anthill-core/src/kb/typing.rs) tries the by-NAME rung FIRST in BOTH TupleAlign modes, and that rung is order-insensitive and width-subtyping -- neither of which is sound for a list that is APPLIED POSITIONALLY. (1) ORDER: 'operation take(f: (x: Int64, y: Bool) -> Int64) = f(7, true)' + 'operation pass(g: (y: Bool, x: Int64) -> Int64) = take(g)' + 'operation impl(y: Bool, x: Int64) -> Int64 = x' loads clean; drive() DECLARED -> Int64 evaluates to Bool(true) -- a wrong-typed value, no trap. (2) ARITY: take3 expecting a 3-param callback accepts a 2-param one (extra field width-ignored), then traps ArityMismatch{expected:2,got:3}. (3) SLOT COLLAPSE: operation_as_function_value puts the PARAM'S TYPE in the arrow param slot for an arity-1 op ('if op.params.len() == 1'), so a 1-param op whose param IS a tuple is indistinguishable from an n-param list; 'operation get_a(t: (a: Int64, b: Int64)) -> Int64 = t.a' with 'let f: (_1: Int64, _2: Int64) -> Int64 = get_a' then 'f((7, 8))' loads clean and traps Internal(field_access: tuple has no component 'a'), plus the dual. (3) is the deepest: it means the param slot does NOT faithfully carry parameter-list-ness, so WI-775's TupleAlign::ParamList mode positionally aligns a data tuple there. ACCEPTANCE: in ParamList mode require equal arity and same-ORDER names on the by-name rung; decide how the arity-1 tuple param is represented so a parameter list is distinguishable from a single tuple parameter (a wrapper/marker at the mint in operation_as_function_value is the obvious route); regression tests DRIVE each program end-to-end (load AND evaluate -- all three load clean today); full workspace green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-20T10:30:41Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-788", author: "claude", content: "CORRECTION to my earlier feedback on this ticket (2026-07-20): it described main as carrying an arity-1 tuple-param WRAPPER, naming arrow_param_slot_for_list / a kb/load.rs surface producer / a decoder in arrow_parts. That wrapper was REVERTED before WI-782 shipped and NONE of those symbols exist on main -- I wrote that note mid-session and did not amend it when the revert landed. Ignore that sentence. What is true on main: WI-782 shipped the ParamList rung only (equal arity + positional zip, admitted when names line up or one side is the synthetic _1.._n), and the param slot still COLLAPSES an arity-1 parameter list to its parameter's bare type, so a one-tuple-parameter op is still the same term as an n-parameter one -- that is WI-791, open. The ByName blast-radius measurement in that earlier note stands and was taken against a tree whose ByName arm is byte-identical to main: 7 firings, all ByName, in 3 places (2 PERMUTED from wi488_producer_component_escape_test::named_tuple_reordered_component_abstracting_producer_flagged; 5 WIDTH from typing_test::subtype_named_tuple_width plus two load-error paths). The correction to this ticket's arrow-side premise also stands: driven, f(b: 10, a: 3) = -7 and f(10, 3) = 7 are both CORRECT, so WI-783's recorded permuted-arrow regression does not reproduce and this cluster has ONE live silent-wrong-answer bug (this one), not two. NEW, relevant to whoever fixes this: a /code-review of the shipped WI-782 measured that the ParamList tightening FALSELY REJECTS correct data-tuple programs, because the arity-1 collapse routes a lone tuple-typed parameter into ParamList -- 'operation get_x(t: (x: Int64, y: Bool)) -> Int64 = t.x' passed to 'take(f: (u: (y: Bool, x: Int64)) -> Int64)' loaded and evaluated to 7 on the parent commit and is refused now. That is the SAME confusion as this ticket from the other side: a relation is being chosen by TYPE POSITION when it should be chosen by the CONSUMER'S READ DISCIPLINE. If this ticket is fixed by making the discipline the axis (positionally-consumed binder list -> positional; name-consuming t.x reader -> by name), it should also retire that false rejection, and TupleAlign should probably be renamed ByName/ByPosition so the enum names the discipline rather than the site.", at: "2026-07-20T10:50:43Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-792", author: "claude", content: "ROOT-CAUSE text repair: three quoted identifiers were eaten by shell quoting when this ticket was filed, leaving blanks in the description. The sentence should read: extract_function_type_parts reads ONLY the arrow term children named result and effects, and DISCARDS the child named param. And: Path 1 per-argument validation (validate_arg_against_param) is nested inside the lookup_operation_info_full(...) block in check_apply_expr and is NOT shared with Path 2. Nothing else in the description was affected.", at: "2026-07-20T10:52:12Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-791", author: "user", content: "MEASURED against the WI-782 follow-up commit: the admissibility gate restored there does NOT close this ticket, and what it leaves open is the IDIOMATIC spelling. Both cases driven end-to-end with a fresh interpreter per case. (1) POSITIONAL-SPELLING BYPASS: operation get_a(t: (a: Int64, b: Int64)) -> Int64 = t.a, passed to operation take2(f: (Int64, Int64) -> Int64) -> Int64 = f(1, 2), LOADS CLEAN and traps ArityMismatch{expected: 1, got: 2} at eval; the control with the callback spelled (p: Int64, q: Int64) is correctly REFUSED at load. Cause: a positionally-written tuple type mints its components as _1, _2 via intern_positional_label (parse/convert.rs), which is exactly the synthetic shape is_positional_tuple_names admits, so the gate disarms itself on the shorter and more common spelling. (2) LEADING-ZERO BYPASS: the same program with the tuple spelled (_01: Int64, _02: Int64) also LOADS CLEAN and traps identically, because parse::<usize>() accepts leading zeros. That also puts the typer at odds with eval, where is_synthetic_positional_label (eval/eval.rs) guards on the digits not starting with a zero and WI-786 shipped leading_zero_label_is_not_synthetic asserting that _01 is a USER label. So the typer calls _01 synthetic while eval calls it a user label: the concrete cross-boundary divergence WI-789 and WI-790 predict, now observed. BOTH BYPASSES ARE PRE-EXISTING, not regressions of WI-782: is_positional_tuple_names on the follow-up commit is BYTE-IDENTICAL to the pre-WI-782 one (verified by git show against 528f4a92), so both holes are equally present on WI-782 parent. What WI-782 and its follow-up actually settled for this ticket is only the NAME-DISJOINT case. A NOT-YET-TRIED FIX DIRECTION, worth weighing against the uniform-parameter-list route already recorded here: carry ARITY on the arrow node as its own child, a sibling of param, result and effects. Both producers know the true arity at mint (op.params.len() in operation_as_function_value, params.len() in the TypeExpr::Arrow arm of load.rs) and arrow_parts already reads named children, so it is a mechanical addition. Critically, the reason the earlier wrapper attempt failed does NOT apply here: that wrapper lived INSIDE the param slot, so a type variable could bind to it and the encoding leaked through substitution. An arity child sits OUTSIDE the type that substitution acts on, so sigma can neither introduce nor erase it, which is the very invariant this ticket records as its lesson. Cost is one extra child rather than changing the term for every arrow in the codebase.", at: "2026-07-20T11:25:07Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-791", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED. The arrow gained a fourth child, `arity` -- the parameter-list LENGTH as written, carried as a ground Const(Int) sibling of param/result/effects. That placement IS the fix: the reverted WI-782 wrapper lived INSIDE the param slot, so a type variable could bind to it and sigma both introduced and erased the encoding; a ground count is not a type sigma acts on. Cost was one child, not the uniform-parameter-list route (which would have changed the term for every arrow and still had to be reconciled with Function[A,B]).\n\nTWO HALVES. (1) agreed_arrow_arity: two arrows relate only at EQUAL ARITY -- one equality, replacing every name-shape heuristic. (2) the count also selects WHICH relation the param slot gets: arity != 1 => the slot IS the list (TupleAlign::ParamList, WI-782's rules unchanged); arity 1 => the slot is the sole parameter's TYPE, so a tuple there is DATA and relates BY NAME with width subtyping. Half (2) repays the cost WI-782 knowingly paid.\n\nMEASURED end-to-end with a fresh interpreter per case; the pre-fix side re-measured in an isolated git worktree at 20e4eb77. FIXED (all four loaded clean and trapped pre-fix, all four now REFUSED AT LOAD): both ticket repros; the POSITIONAL-SPELLING bypass take2(f: (Int64, Int64) -> Int64); the LEADING-ZERO bypass (_01/_02). None of the four is refused by a better name test -- each is refused because 1 != 2, which is why both bypasses close without touching the WI-789/WI-790 _N divergence. RESTORED (refused pre-fix, now load and evaluate to 7): permuted and narrower tuple parameter -- WI-782's two knowing false-rejects. UNCHANGED: apply1(get_a, (a: 1, b: 2)) (the program the wrapper broke), the alpha-rename case, foldLeft eta, the Function spelling, scalar arity-1.\n\nDIAGNOSTICS. The two spellings used to RENDER IDENTICALLY, so a rejection would have restated the confusion it reported. type_display_name / type_display_name_occ / TermPrinter now wrap an arity-1 tuple param: \"expected (p: Int64, q: Int64) -> Int64, got ((a: Int64, b: Int64)) -> Int64\". The print round trip is FAITHFUL and a FIXPOINT -- pinned by a test that reparses its own output and prints a second generation, because the reverted wrapper gained a layer per round until generation three no longer parsed.\n\nONE DELIBERATE BEHAVIOR CHANGE, pinned as two_parameter_operation_is_refused_for_a_tuple_argument_arrow: take(f: ((Int64, Int64)) -> Int64) given a 2-param op LOADED and evaluated to -1 pre-fix, because eval's spread_eta_args spreads a single POSITIONAL tuple argument across a multi-parameter op. Refused now. It is the SAME relation as repro 2, which the acceptance requires refusing; the two only looked different because repro 2's tuple is NAME-keyed and so has nothing in .pos for the spread to find. Letting the relation depend on how a caller happens to build its tuple is the incoherence this ticket removes. The spread convention is untouched and still reachable through Function[(A,B),R] (driven, = 7) and a direct positional call.\n\nWHY NO ARITY CHECK ON THE arrow-vs-Function ARM: Function states no arity and cannot -- its A is the ONE argument apply(f, x: A) passes, yet Function[(T,T),R] has ALSO denoted a 2-param op's eta arrow since WI-775. Driven both ways and pinned. The spec's Function note is updated: the permutation/width half of that disagreement is RESOLVED (both spellings now relate a lone tuple parameter by name); what remains is only that Function admits either arity.\n\nLAMBDA ARITY = the WRITTEN binder count (Pattern::Tuple component count, else 1), NOT the runtime's. enter_closure is strictly unary, so a multi-binder lambda into an n-parameter callback still traps (WI-784). Minting 1 would convert that trap into a LOAD rejection -- measured as the only alternative, and left to WI-784 rather than taken here. Note the surface has NO spelling for \"one tuple-shaped binder\" other than naming it: WI-620 made outer parens a grouping node, so `lambda ((a,b)) ->` and `lambda (a,b) ->` are the same lambda.\n\nKNOWN GAP LEFT OPEN AND PINNED (known_gap_generic_callback_arrow_is_not_conformance_checked): a callback argument to a TYPE-PARAMETERIZED op is not conformance-checked at all, so apply2[T](f: (x: T, y: T) -> Int64) given a 1-tuple-param op still loads clean and traps. MEASURED IDENTICAL pre-fix -- PRE-EXISTING, not a hole in the arity child (the same program written non-generically IS refused). ROOT CAUSE for whoever closes it: validate_arg_against_param gates on resolved_type_is_ground for BOTH sides, and a declared (x: T, y: T) -> Int64 is non-ground while T is free, so the full types_compatible -- where the arity check lives -- never runs; the non-ground path delegates to validate_arrow_param_result, which checks param/result component-wise and only where each component is itself ground. ARITY IS THE ONE COMPONENT THAT CHECK COULD ALWAYS DECIDE: it is a ground Const(Int) however polymorphic the types are, so the groundness discipline that defers the rest does not apply to it. Not done here because it also rejects programs that work today via eval's tuple spread -- WI-784 / WI-792 territory, same family as WI-792.\n\nFOOTGUN THE /code-review CAUGHT, worth keeping: adding a REQUIRED child SILENTLY EMPTIED TWO TESTS. types_compatible_bootstrap_safe_when_prelude_not_registered and subtype_rejects_malformed_multi_tail_row hand-build raw Term::Fn{Arrow, named} to control their effects child; with no arity child agreed_arrow_arity returned false FIRST, so neither test reached a single one of the effect-row arms it names -- and both stayed GREEN. Fixed by giving them the child (make_arity_term is now pub for exactly this), and made loud by a debug_assert in agreed_arrow_arity: both callers arrive from an (arrow, arrow) dispatch arm, so a side stating no arity is a MALFORMED term, not a legitimate absence. The full suite passing in debug with that assert live is the completeness check on the producer map.\n\nFull workspace green via scripts/test.sh: 3172 passed, 0 failed. New suite wi791_arrow_arity_test.rs (12 tests) verified in an isolated worktree to FAIL pre-fix for all 9 changed behaviours while EVERY control passes pre-fix, so they discriminate rather than track. wi782's three known_gap_* tests are retired: two became positive end-to-end assertions, the third became this ticket's headline rejection. docs/kernel-language.md states the shipped rule; docs/proposals/typing_pass_spec.anthill updated so its arrow patterns still match.", at: "2026-07-20T12:44:31Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-791", description: some(value: "The arrow PARAM SLOT does not record parameter-list ARITY, so a one-tuple-parameter operation is the same term as an n-parameter one. This is WI-782 case 3 (SLOT COLLAPSE), deferred from WI-782 with a MEASURED reason. REPRO (loads clean, traps at eval, unchanged on main): 'operation get_a(t: (a: Int64, b: Int64)) -> Int64 = t.a' with 'let f: (_1: Int64, _2: Int64) -> Int64 = get_a' then 'f((7, 8))' traps Internal(field_access: tuple has no component 'a'); the dual, a 2-param op into 'let f: (t: (a: Int64, b: Int64)) -> Int64 = two', traps ArityMismatch{expected:2,got:1}. Both are LOUD traps, not silent wrong answers -- which is why WI-782 shipped its ORDER/ARITY fixes without this one. ROOT CAUSE: two producers collapse an arity-1 parameter list to the bare parameter type -- 'operation_as_function_value' (kb/typing.rs, 'if op.params.len() == 1') and the TypeExpr::Arrow arm of kb/load.rs ('if params.len() == 1') -- so '(t: (a: A, b: B)) -> R' and '(a: A, b: B) -> R' build the identical arrow(param = named_tuple[A, B]). WHAT WAS TRIED AND WHY IT FAILED (do not repeat it): a CONDITIONAL wrapper at the mint -- wrap the arity-1 list iff its sole parameter's type is itself a tuple, giving 'named_tuple[(v, T)]', with a decoder in arrow_parts undoing it. It was implemented, and the full workspace went green, but review + driving found it LEAKS, because the wrap is decided from the SPELLING at mint time while the slot is read back AFTER substitution: (a) MEASURED -- 'operation apply1[T](f: (x: T) -> Int64, v: T) -> Int64 = f(v)' called as 'apply1(get_a, (a: 1, b: 2))' bound T to the WRAPPER, so the sibling parameter rejected the literal with 'expected (_1: (a: Int64, b: Int64)), got (a: Int64, b: Int64)' -- a program that works on main; (b) MEASURED -- the wrapper keys its field by the written binder name, which an arity-1 slot previously dropped, so '(t: (a,b)) -> R' stopped conforming to '(u: (a,b)) -> R' (fixed by making ParamList ignore names, which WI-782 shipped); (c) a param slot written '(x: T) -> R' or '(c: r.T) -> Bool' is NOT wrapped at mint (its TypeExpr is not a Tuple) but BECOMES a named_tuple after substitution/projection elimination, at which point it reads as an n-parameter list -- four independent review angles identified this path in stdlib mapElems/where/optionMap shapes; I could not build a working fixture for it, so it is UNCONFIRMED, but it is the same mechanism as (a), which IS confirmed. The lesson: an encoding that unification can introduce or erase is not an invariant. FIX DIRECTION (the sigma-stable alternative): make the param slot UNIFORMLY the parameter list at every arity -- '(v: Int64) -> R' carries 'named_tuple[(v, Int64)]' -- so arity is a structural property substitution can neither create nor destroy, and no conditional test is needed at any producer. Cost: it changes the term for EVERY arrow in the codebase and must be reconciled with 'Function[A, B]', whose A is ONE tuple-typed ARGUMENT and carries no list (WI-775); every reader must then be classified as asking for the parameter LIST or the single ARGUMENT type (a full map of both exists in the WI-782 session). Consider also whether the arrow node should carry arity as its own child instead. ACCEPTANCE: the two repro programs above are REJECTED AT LOAD with a located diagnostic (or otherwise made to work), driven end-to-end; 'apply1(get_a, (a: 1, b: 2))' and the alpha-rename case still load and evaluate; a generic callback whose param type is instantiated to a tuple ('(x: T) -> R' with T := a tuple) is driven end-to-end in BOTH directions; full workspace green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-20T12:44:32Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-792", author: "claude", content: "A SECOND LOCUS of this ticket's family, folded in here rather than filed separately, and it is NOT the Path 2 the description already names. Path 2 is the APPLICATION site (calling a function VALUE). This one is the ARGUMENT-PASSING site: handing a callback to a TYPE-PARAMETERIZED operation. Different code, same shape of defect -- a conformance check that simply does not run.\n\nMEASURED end-to-end on the WI-791 commit AND, identically, on its parent 20e4eb77, so it is PRE-EXISTING and is not a hole in WI-791's arity child:\n\n  (a) operation apply2[T](f: (x: T, y: T) -- Int64, v: T, w: T) -- Int64 = f(v, w)\n      with operation get_a(t: (a: Int64, b: Int64)) -- Int64 = t.a\n      and  operation drive() -- Int64 = apply2(get_a, 7, 8)\n      LOADS CLEAN and traps ArityMismatch{expected: 1, got: 2} at eval.\n      The SAME program written NON-generically -- take2(f: (p: Int64, q: Int64) -- Int64) -- is correctly REFUSED at load by WI-791. Genericity is the whole difference.\n\n  (b) the dual: operation apply1[T](f: (x: T) -- Int64, v: T) -- Int64 = f(v)\n      with a TWO-parameter op and apply1(two, (7, 8)) also loads clean, and here it EVALUATES (to -1) because eval's spread_eta_args spreads one positional tuple argument across a multi-parameter op.\n\nROOT CAUSE: validate_arg_against_param (kb/typing.rs) opens with a groundness gate -- if either resolved_type_is_ground(actual) or resolved_type_is_ground(declared) is false it does NOT run types_compatible at all. A declared callback type (x: T, y: T) -- Int64 is non-ground while T is free, so the full relation, which is where WI-791's arity equality lives, is never reached. The non-ground path delegates to validate_arrow_param_result, which checks param and result COMPONENT-WISE and only where each component is itself ground (WI-469's discipline: a genuinely polymorphic component must not be rejected early).\n\nWHY THIS IS CHEAPLY FIXABLE AND WHERE: ARITY IS THE ONE COMPONENT THAT CHECK COULD ALWAYS DECIDE. It is a ground Const(Int) child no matter how polymorphic the param and result types are, so the groundness discipline that justifies deferring everything else does not apply to it. validate_arrow_param_result already decomposes both arrows and is already the designated home for \"what can be decided about a non-ground callback\". Adding the arity equality there is a few lines and needs no new machinery. Note also that arrow_arity returns None for a Function[A, B, E] (which states no arity and cannot -- see WI-791), so the WI-775 caveat that function guards in its own comment is respected for free: a Function side simply yields no arity and the check skips.\n\nTHE DECISION WHOEVER CLOSES IT MUST MAKE, and the reason WI-791 stopped short: adding that check also refuses case (b) above, which WORKS today. It works only because eval spreads a single POSITIONAL tuple argument; the name-keyed spelling of the same program traps. WI-791 already took exactly this trade at the CONFORMANCE rung -- it refuses take(f: ((Int64, Int64)) -- Int64) given a 2-parameter op, pinned as two_parameter_operation_is_refused_for_a_tuple_argument_arrow, on the grounds that letting a type relation depend on how a caller happens to build its tuple is incoherent, and that the spread convention stays reachable through Function[(A,B),R]. Taking the same trade here is consistent; taking a different one is not. Either way it should be a stated decision, not a side effect.\n\nALSO, NOW RESOLVED: this ticket's ACCEPTANCE says \"the arity of a positional application is checked against the parameter list too (see WI-791 -- until the slot reports arity faithfully, only the equal-arity case may be checkable)\". WI-791 HAS LANDED (7d674df1): the arrow carries an arity child, agreed_arrow_arity compares it, and arity 1 versus arity n are now distinct types. That caveat no longer binds -- a positional application can be arity-checked outright.\n\nPINNED: known_gap_generic_callback_arrow_is_not_conformance_checked in rustland/anthill-core/tests/include/wi791_arrow_arity_test.rs asserts case (a)'s CURRENT wrong behaviour (loads, then traps) on purpose, so the gap stays visible in the suite. It fails when this is closed -- replace it with a load-rejection assertion.", at: "2026-07-20T13:06:27Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-791", author: "claude", content: "CORRECTION to my delivery feedback: the new suite wi791_arrow_arity_test.rs has ELEVEN tests, not twelve. Everything else in that note stands, including the pre-fix worktree measurement (nine changed behaviours FAIL pre-fix across wi791 + wi782 while every control passes) and the 3172-passed workspace figure.", at: "2026-07-20T13:09:48Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-784", author: "claude", content: "ACCEPTANCE ITEM PARTLY STALE, corrected here rather than silently dropped. This ticket's acceptance says \"a 2-binder lambda applied as f(3, 10) and as f((3, 10)) both evaluate\". The f((3, 10)) half no longer holds AS WRITTEN, and the reason is WI-791, which landed after this ticket was filed.\n\nMEASURED on e5706e61 (this ticket's own parent), fresh interpreter per case:\n  (i)  operation apply_tuple(f: ((Int64, Int64)) -> Int64) with a 2-binder lambda is now REFUSED AT LOAD:\n       \"type mismatch in apply_tuple.f (op-arg): expected ((_1: Int64, _2: Int64)) -> Int64, got (_1: Int64, _2: Int64) -> Int64\".\n       The name-keyed spelling (t: (acc: Int64, x: Int64)) gives the same rejection.\n  (ii) the Function spelling, f: Function[A = (Int64, Int64), B = Int64], LOADS and EVALUATES to -7.\n\nWHY: lambda_written_arity (kb/typing.rs:20302) mints a lambda's arrow arity as the WRITTEN binder count, so lambda (acc, x) is arity 2, and WI-791 made arity 2 and arity 1 DISTINCT TYPES. A one-tuple-parameter slot is arity 1. So the ARROW spelling of that acceptance clause is now a conformance rejection, not an eval question.\n\nDECISION TAKEN: satisfying the clause as written would mean REVERTING a WI-791 rejection -- the same relation WI-791 pinned deliberately as two_parameter_operation_is_refused_for_a_tuple_argument_arrow, on the stated grounds that letting a type relation depend on how a caller happens to build its tuple is incoherent. Not reverted. The clause is delivered against the Function spelling instead, which states no arity (WI-791: function_spelling_states_no_arity_and_still_bridges) and is therefore the spelling in which the program still loads. Pinned as caller_built_tuple_still_matches, with the reasoning in the test's doc comment.\n\nThe EVAL-side behaviour the clause was really about is intact and unchanged: gather_closure_arg passes a SINGLE argument through untouched, so wherever f((3, 10)) loads, the caller-built tuple destructures exactly as before. Only the N-argument spelling is new.", at: "2026-07-20T14:34:20Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-784", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED. A closure now accepts an N-argument application, so a lambda and a named operation are interchangeable as function values.\n\nROOT CAUSE AND SHAPE OF THE FIX. dispatch_call has two callable arms. The OpRef arm ADAPTS its arguments (spread_eta_args, eval.rs:1675: one tuple argument spreads across a multi-parameter op). The Closure arm did not adapt at all -- enter_closure opened with a flat args.len() != 1 rejection. The fix is that arm's missing DUAL, gather_closure_arg: since a multi-binder lambda is ONE tuple pattern, N arguments are GATHERED back into the tuple its binder list destructures. Where spread_eta_args spreads one into N, this gathers N into one. That is why no stdlib change was needed: the existing f(init, h) convention now lands correctly.\n\nThe 0-component case falls out of the same rule rather than needing its own arm -- lambda () -> a is a Pattern::Tuple of zero components, so a zero-argument call gathers to the empty tuple and matches. That closed the arity-zero twin for free.\n\nWHAT LANDED: eval/eval.rs gather_closure_arg + the enter_closure call site (the arity decision now needs the param pattern, so it moved after the closure-arena read); Pattern::binder_arity in kb/node_occurrence.rs; lambda_written_arity rewritten to call it; docs/kernel-language.md section 4.7 gained an \"Applying a lambda\" paragraph; new suite wi784_closure_arity_test.rs (10 tests); the two load-only wi605 cases now DRIVE.\n\nMEASURED end-to-end, fresh interpreter per case. FIXED (each loaded clean and trapped ArityMismatch{op: closure} before, each evaluates now): apply_arrow(lambda (acc, x) -> acc - x) = -7, AGREEING with the sub2 operation spelling; FiniteCollection.foldLeft([1,2,3], 0, lambda (acc, x) -> acc * 10 + x) = 123, agreeing with the named-op spelling; run(lambda () -> 5) = 5; the generic apply2[T](f: (x: T, y: T) -> Int64, ...) case; and delayForce(delayPure(5)) = 5. UNCHANGED (controls, already passing before): the unary lambda, and the caller-built tuple f((3, 10)).\n\nTHE STDLIB DELAY MONAD WAS UNEVALUATABLE. Worse than the ticket's \"twin\" framing: delayed(lambda () -> a) forced by case delayed(t) -> t() meant the SHIPPED Delay monad trapped on any use. It stayed hidden because its only suite (wi516_graded_effect_row_test.rs) is load-only AND re-declares a local copy of the monad rather than driving the stdlib's. Now driven directly.\n\nTESTS DISCRIMINATE, not track. The new suite was run against an isolated worktree at the parent commit e5706e61: 6 of 8 FAILED pre-fix (the five behaviours above plus the arity-message change) and BOTH controls PASSED pre-fix. A fix that merely stopped trapping would not satisfy them -- the headline cases assert the lambda and OPERATION spellings AGREE on a value, not just that the lambda returns something.\n\nTHE ONE ACCEPTANCE DEVIATION is recorded in this ticket's preceding feedback: the f((3, 10)) clause is stale post-WI-791 in the ARROW spelling and is delivered against the Function spelling instead. WI-791's rejection was NOT reverted.\n\nFROM THE /code-review (high), 5 findings, all addressed. The load-bearing one: my first cut DUPLICATED the definition of a lambda's binder arity -- the typer had lambda_written_arity (positional + named) and I added a separate closure_param_arity (positional only, None for a labelled tuple). Two definitions of the one notion that MUST agree, when a typer/runtime disagreement about exactly this is the whole bug being fixed. Collapsed into a single shared Pattern::binder_arity that both call, with the invariant stated in its doc. Also retired lambda_written_arity's doc comment, which asserted \"enter_closure is strictly unary ... WI-784, open\" and would have actively misled the next reader into re-minting arity 1 and re-breaking every call site. Also corrected an overstated sentence in the spec paragraph and pinned the corner it overstated (a nullary thunk called with one argument surfaces as a raised MatchFailed, not an ArityMismatch, because the single-argument pass-through reaches the matcher before the arity comparison).\n\nADJACENT DEFECT FOUND AND PINNED, not this ticket's and not fixed: known_gap_list_foldleft_lambda_over_a_literal_does_not_load. List.foldLeft's callback is typed with the path-dependent xs.T; over a LIST LITERAL with a LAMBDA it never resolves and the program does not load. My first characterisation of it was WRONG in both obvious directions and the test now pins the real boundary with two controls: literal + named operation LOADS, and a declared List[T = Int64] parameter + the SAME lambda LOADS. It takes the literal AND the lambda together. Worth its own ticket.\n\nFull workspace green via scripts/test.sh: 3182 passed, 0 failed, 84 binaries, 0 non-ok.", at: "2026-07-20T15:03:52Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-784", description: some(value: "Multi-binder LAMBDAS cannot be applied at all -- `enter_closure` is strictly UNARY, so a lambda passed as a function VALUE and applied with N arguments traps while the identical call with a named OPERATION succeeds. Lambdas and operations are therefore NOT interchangeable as function values, and the failure is invisible at load. MEASURED end-to-end (fresh Interpreter per case, positive control fires): `operation apply_arrow(f: (acc: Int64, x: Int64) -> Int64) -> Int64 = f(3, 10)` gives Ok(Int(-7)) for `apply_arrow(sub2)` but `ArityMismatch{op: \"closure\", expected: 1, got: 2}` for `apply_arrow(lambda (acc, x) -> acc - x)`. THE STDLIB IS ALREADY WRITTEN AGAINST THE BROKEN CONVENTION: prelude/list.anthill:83 is `foldLeft(t, f(init, h), f)` and :87 is `f(h, foldRight(t, init, f))` -- both apply the callback with TWO SEPARATE ARGUMENTS, and FiniteCollection.foldLeft/foldRight delegate to them (finite_collection.anthill:47,50). Measured, BOTH SPELLINGS LOADING CLEAN: `foldLeft([1, 2, 3], 0, shift)` = Ok(Int(123)) but `foldLeft([1, 2, 3], 0, lambda (acc, x) -> acc * 10 + x)` = ArityMismatch{expected: 1, got: 2}. So `xs.foldLeft(0, lambda (a, b) -> a + b)` -- the single most idiomatic higher-order call in the language -- typechecks and then traps at eval. ARITY-ZERO TWIN: `enter_closure`'s `args.len() != 1` rejects 0 as well as 2; prelude/delay.anthill:50 builds `delayed(lambda () -> a)` and :61 forces it with `case delayed(t) -> t()`, routing through dispatch_call with an empty arg vec into the same rejection -- hidden only because Delay's one test (wi516_graded_effect_row_test.rs) is load-only. ROOT CAUSE: `spread_eta_args` (eval.rs ~1675-1706) converts a single tuple argument into N positional args for `Value::OpRef` ONLY, matching the op's declared arity; there is NO closure-side counterpart, and `enter_closure` (eval.rs ~1849) rejects `args.len() != 1` outright because a multi-binder lambda is ONE TUPLE PATTERN -- see proposal 018-expressions-and-operation-implementation.md:114 (\"Lambda always takes _one_ argument. Multiple parameters use tuple destructuring\"), eval/closure.rs:31-34 and eval/eval.rs:768-771. Note 018 nonetheless shows `fold(lambda (acc, x) -> add(acc, x), zero, my_list)` and `zip_with(lambda (a, b) -> ...)` as INTENDED usage at :126-128,:194,:201, so the proposal contradicts itself and the stdlib followed the intended form. WHY IT NEVER SURFACED (all verified, no counterexample found): (a) every EVALUATED higher-order call site across stdlib/examples/tests passes a named OPERATION, never a lambda -- ~30 sites (addp, shift, count_l, noisy_add, add_name, ...), zero lambdas; (b) every multi-binder lambda that IS written is either applied with ONE tuple (eval_test.rs:722,726,730; wi620_paren_lambda_param_test.rs:58-59; wi517_typed_lambda_binder_test.rs:97) or never applied at all -- the where/join row lambdas are erased into a LogicalQuery as syntax at compile time (relation.anthill:133,178-179), which is why the repo's most common multi-binder lambda (join's `lambda (c, q) -> ...`, 9 occurrences) never reaches enter_closure; (c) every site that MIXES the two is LOAD-ONLY -- wi605_bare_arrow_lambda_test.rs:81 passes a multi-binder lambda to foldRight, and wi605_bare_arrow_lambda_test.rs:110-130 defines `operation apply2(arrow: (a: Int64, b: Int64) -> Int64, x, y) = arrow(x, y)` describing itself as \"the foldLeft `f(init, h)` pattern\" -- both asserted only via load_errors, each one `interp_for` away from failing. NOT TRACKED ANYWHERE UNTIL NOW: the failure is recorded only as a discarded \"control\" sentence inside WI-783's description, whose delivery commit (07ecdec5) explicitly REFUTED it as evidence for that ticket (the trap fires on plain POSITIONAL `f(3, 10)` too, so it never had anything to do with named arguments) and whose fix touched only kb/typing.rs -- eval.rs was never opened, so enter_closure was never in scope. WI-783's reorder makes this path MORE reachable: it now rewrites `f(x: 10, acc: 3)` into a positional `f(3, 10)`, landing directly on the broken call. FIX DIRECTION: mirror `spread_eta_args` on the closure side -- have `enter_closure` accept N arguments by tupling them when the binder is an N-element tuple pattern (and accept 0 for a nullary binder). That fixes the arity-0 twin at the same time and requires NO stdlib change, since the existing `f(init, h)` convention keeps working. ACCEPTANCE: `foldLeft([1, 2, 3], 0, lambda (acc, x) -> acc * 10 + x)` evaluates to 123, agreeing with the named-operation spelling; a 2-binder lambda applied as `f(3, 10)` and as `f((3, 10))` both evaluate; a nullary `lambda () -> 5` forced via `t()` evaluates; a regression test drives the LAMBDA and OPREF spellings of the SAME call and asserts they AGREE end-to-end -- using a FRESH Interpreter per case, because reusing one after a trapped call returns a bogus Internal(\"deliver: parent frame had no awaiting state\") on every later call and reads as a second independent bug; the two load-only wi605_bare_arrow_lambda_test.rs cases gain eval assertions; full workspace green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-20T15:03:53Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-792", author: "claude", content: "DESIGN DECISIONS SETTLED (user, this session). This ticket now has NO OPEN FORKS and is implementable as stated. Three questions were raised; two were decided, and the third was WITHDRAWN because it turned out not to be a question. Recorded here so a fresh session can execute without re-deriving any of it.\n\nDECISION 1 -- the one the description flags: REFUSE case (b). TAKEN.\nAdding arity equality to validate_arrow_param_result also refuses apply1[T](f: (x: T) -> Int64, v: T) given a TWO-parameter op, which loads and evaluates to -1 today. Refuse it. Rationale is WI-791's, unchanged: it took exactly this trade at the CONFORMANCE rung (two_parameter_operation_is_refused_for_a_tuple_argument_arrow) on the grounds that letting a type relation depend on how a caller happens to build its tuple is incoherent. NEW SUPPORTING FACT since the ticket was written: WI-784 has landed (eff8ed6a), so the tuple-spread convention is now reachable through Function[A, B] for BOTH operations and lambdas, in BOTH application forms -- pinned by operations_and_lambdas_are_interchangeable_in_both_application_forms. Nothing is lost by refusing case (b); the working spelling remains available.\n\nDECISION 2 -- NOT IN THE DESCRIPTION, and it CONSTRAINS A GREEN TEST ON MAIN. The arity check on a POSITIONAL APPLICATION must SKIP when the callee states no arity.\narrow_declared_param_list returns None for a Function[A, B, E] -- WI-775 settled that A is the ARGUMENT's data type, ONE tuple-typed argument, not a parameter list -- but arrow_parts still DECOMPOSES a Function, so its param reads as the single type A. A naive \"positional argument i against parameter slot i\" check therefore sees ONE parameter at a Function slot and would REFUSE:\n\n  operation spread(f: Function[A = (Int64, Int64), B = Int64]) -> Int64 = f(3, 10)\n\nThat is 2 arguments against 1 parameter. It is a PATH 2 SITE (the callee f is a variable), it is GREEN on main, and it is pinned TWICE: operations_and_lambdas_are_interchangeable_in_both_application_forms (wi784_closure_arity_test.rs) and WI-791's function_spelling_states_no_arity_and_still_bridges. Locus 2 gets this behaviour for free, because arrow_arity already returns None for a Function. LOCUS 1 MUST DO IT ON PURPOSE.\n\nDECISION 3 -- RAISED AND THEN WITHDRAWN. It is not a fork. I had asked \"does a function-VALUE call inherit Path 1's Option auto-coercion (WrapSome), or do the two call paths coerce differently?\" That framing was wrong, and the codebase has already answered it. validate_arg_against_param's own doc says it is \"the shared core of the operation-argument (check_apply_iter) and entity-field (check_constructor_iter) validation\", and that some-coercion (WI-408) applies at \"both positions\". So it is a GENERAL CALL-SIDE TRANSFORMATION already applied at every argument position that exists; a function-value application is simply a THIRD such position that was never wired up -- the same shape as this ticket's own defect. There is no principled reason for it to differ.\n\nSo Decision 3 reduces to an implementation obligation: Path 2 becomes the third consumer and must ACT on WrapSome -- rewrite the argument occurrence -- copying either check_apply_iter (kb/typing.rs:8364 and :8392) or check_constructor_iter (:18161 and :18177). synthesize_some_wrap already takes exactly what Path 2 has (the child occurrence plus the declared type). THE ONE GENUINELY WRONG OPTION, to be avoided explicitly: treating WrapSome as Ok WITHOUT wrapping. That leaves the value bare in memory while its type says Option[T] -- the doc names it as precisely the behaviour WI-408 REPLACED (WI-385's lenient-accept interim). It is a known-bad state with a name, not a defensible shortcut.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION DIRECTION -- SHARE THE CORE, DO NOT REIMPLEMENT. Path 2 must CALL validate_arg_against_param rather than grow its own positional type check. A bespoke check would get the TYPES right and then silently lose the WI-408 some-coercion, the is_reflect_term_type reflection bypass, and the WI-385/WI-469 groundness discipline -- a THIRD divergent definition of \"does this argument fit this parameter\". This is the WI-784 lesson repeating verbatim: its /code-review caught a first cut that had DUPLICATED the binder-arity notion (the typer's lambda_written_arity beside a new closure_param_arity) instead of sharing it, and the fix was one shared Pattern::binder_arity read by both sides. Note Path 1's call sits INSIDE the lookup_operation_info_full(...) block in check_apply_expr and needs hoisting before Path 2 can reach it.\n\nSCOPE CORRECTION. An earlier statement of mine -- that locus 1 (the Path 2 positional check) is \"decision-free\" and could be split off as a quick win -- was WRONG: decisions 2 and 3 BOTH live in locus 1. What is true is that the two loci are INDEPENDENT OF EACH OTHER, so either may land first; neither is free of design content. Locus 1 closes the headline soundness hole (a Bool flowing into an Int64 slot, drive() returning Bool(true) from an operation declared -> Int64); locus 2 closes the generic-callback gap.\n\nTESTS TO WATCH.\n  MUST FLIP (asserts current wrong behaviour on purpose):\n    known_gap_generic_callback_arrow_is_not_conformance_checked (wi791_arrow_arity_test.rs) -- replace with a load-rejection assertion when locus 2 closes.\n  MUST KEEP PASSING (they encode decisions 1 and 2):\n    operations_and_lambdas_are_interchangeable_in_both_application_forms (wi784_closure_arity_test.rs)\n    caller_built_tuple_still_matches (wi784_closure_arity_test.rs)\n    function_spelling_states_no_arity_and_still_bridges (wi791_arrow_arity_test.rs)\n    two_parameter_operation_is_refused_for_a_tuple_argument_arrow (wi791_arrow_arity_test.rs)\n\nNOT FIXED BY THIS TICKET, so do not expect it to move: a 2-binder LAMBDA into a generic arity-2 callback slot was never a conformance gap -- the arity-2 lambda genuinely MATCHES the arity-2 slot. That case was WI-784's (the closure arm), and it is already closed.", at: "2026-07-20T15:57:15Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-792", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. Both loci landed; full workspace green (3192 passed, 0 failed) and BLAST RADIUS WAS ZERO -- no pre-existing test changed except the one this ticket was told to flip. The arity tightening (Decision 1) and the argument type check refused only buggy programs, the same result WI-782's measurement method got.\n\nPRE-FIX MEASURED MYSELF on eff8ed6a in an isolated worktree, not taken from the ticket text. All four claims reproduce, and one is NEW:\n  * headline: LOADS CLEAN, drive() -> Ok(Bool(true)) from an op declared -> Int64. Confirmed verbatim.\n  * arity: BOTH f(1,2,3) and f(1) on a 2-parameter arrow LOADED CLEAN.\n  * NAMED channel had the IDENTICAL silent wrong answer: f(y: 7, x: true) also loaded and returned Bool(true). The ticket only described the positional channel; the labels were resolved by WI-783 and then what landed in the slot was never checked. Both channels are closed.\n  * NEW, and it sharpens Decision 3: the Option case was not merely unchecked, it was load-clean-then-RAISE. take(f) = f(7, 3) against (o: Option[T=Int64], d: Int64) loaded and drive() RAISED on the failed match, because the bare 7 was never wrapped. So the known-bad shortcut (treat WrapSome as Ok without wrapping) is indistinguishable from the fix at LOAD time -- only driving separates them. The test drives it.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION, as directed: locus 1 CALLS validate_arg_against_param rather than growing its own check, so the WI-408 some-coercion, the is_reflect_term_type bypass and the WI-385/WI-469 groundness discipline all come with it; Path 1's call did NOT need hoisting after all -- the function is a free fn already, only the callee's parameter list had to be read on the Path 2 side. Locus 2 is the predicted few lines in validate_arrow_param_result, ahead of the groundness-gated component checks.\n\nDECISION 2 needed one new reader, not a reuse of arrow_declared_param_list: that one returns None at ARITY 1 (the arrow drops a lone binder's name, so a LABEL has nothing to bind to), but a POSITIONAL argument needs no name and IS checkable there. New arrow_positional_param_slots differs from it in exactly that one arm and DELEGATES to it otherwise, so the two cannot drift. Function still yields None and skips, on purpose.\n\nMEASURED SIDE EFFECTS, both judged acceptable and neither breaking a test:\n  * an exact-arity check FORBIDS PARTIAL APPLICATION at a function-VALUE site. Checked before accepting: eval's spread_eta_args already refused any count but an exact one, so it was never executable -- this converts a run-time trap into a load error. WI-374 partial application is about NAMED-OPERATION calls (Path 1), untouched.\n  * the arity error now PRE-EMPTS WI-783's 'records no parameter names' message when a labelled call to a 1-parameter arrow ALSO has the wrong count. WI-783's message still fires when the count is right (verified with f(v: 1)).\n\n/code-review (high) found 6, 3 fixed: (1) arrow_positional_param_slots returned None for a MALFORMED arrow, conflating it with the legitimate Function absence -- that would SILENTLY DISABLE the whole check, the exact WI-791 footgun where a hand-built arrow missing a child left two tests green covering nothing; now a debug_assert, and arity 0 was measured to land on the empty-named_tuple arm first so it cannot false-fire. (2) a comment claimed a coverage-rejected label draws only one diagnostic -- FALSE for a DUPLICATE label, which does match a param and is type-checked too; measured and the comment corrected (behavior matches Path 1, left alone). (3) the parameter-list decomposition was duplicated across two readers; now delegated.", at: "2026-07-20T17:00:18Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-792", description: some(value: "POSITIONAL ARGUMENT TYPES ARE NEVER CHECKED at a function-VALUE application, so a wrong-typed value flows with no trap and no diagnostic. MEASURED end-to-end on main: 'operation take(f: (x: Int64, y: Bool) -> Int64) -> Int64 = f(true, 7)' with 'operation impl(x: Int64, y: Bool) -> Int64 = x' and 'operation drive() -> Int64 = take(impl)' LOADS CLEAN and drive() evaluates to Bool(true) -- a Bool returned from an operation declared -> Int64. Note there is NO permutation, NO subtyping and NO conformance question here: the call site simply hands Bool to the Int64 slot and Int64 to the Bool slot, and nothing objects. ROOT CAUSE: check_apply_expr Path 2 (kb/typing.rs ~9583, the callee-is-a-variable-of-arrow-type path) computes the result type and effects via extract_function_type_parts, which reads ONLY the arrow's  and  children and DISCARDS . It then merges argument effects and runs the WI-783 named-argument coverage + reorder -- which checks LABELS only, never the types behind them -- and returns. No positional argument is ever compared against the arrow's parameter list. Path 1's per-argument validation (validate_arg_against_param) is nested inside the  block and is NOT shared with Path 2. WHY THIS MATTERS BEYOND ITSELF: WI-782 tightened the arrow-vs-arrow CONFORMANCE relation (equal arity + positional slots) on the premise that a mis-aligned parameter list would then fail on the TYPES. That premise holds only where conformance is actually checked; this ticket shows the APPLICATION site enforces nothing, so any residual mis-alignment stays silent rather than trapping. It is also strictly more reachable than WI-782's own case 1, needing no permutation at all. Found by /code-review of the WI-782 commit; PRE-EXISTING (Path 2's param-discarding predates WI-782 and that commit did not touch it). ACCEPTANCE: a positional argument whose type does not conform to the corresponding parameter slot is REJECTED AT LOAD with a located diagnostic naming the argument position and both types; the repro above is driven end-to-end and refused; a matching call still loads and evaluates; the arity of a positional application is checked against the parameter list too (see WI-791 -- until the slot reports arity faithfully, only the equal-arity case may be checkable); full workspace green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-20T17:00:24Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-793", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED as an ORDERING fix. Full workspace green (3206 passed, 0 failed, 11 ignored) and BLAST RADIUS ZERO -- 3192 was WI-792's baseline and I added exactly 14 tests, so no pre-existing test changed except the one this ticket was told to flip.\n\nPRE-FIX MEASURED MYSELF on the WI-792 delivery commit, not taken from the ticket text. All four claims reproduce verbatim, including both exact diagnostics. Two of the ticket's statements are WRONG, both measured:\n\n  * THE PREMISE IS PARTLY WRONG. The ticket says the idiomatic `xs.foldLeft(...)` form is unavailable. It is NOT: the DOT-CALL spelling `[1, 2, 3].foldLeft(0, lambda ...)` LOADS AND EVALUATES on the unfixed tree. Only the QUALIFIED `List.foldLeft(literal, ...)` form was broken. This is not a footnote -- it is the whole diagnosis (below). Pinned by `qualified_and_dot_call_spellings_agree` so the two spellings cannot drift apart again.\n  * IT IS NOT ABOUT LITERALS. A `cons` spine and a CALL receiver (`mk()`) fail IDENTICALLY. The ticket's own title and its 'unpinned literal receiver' framing would send an implementer at the literal syntax; a fix that read element types off a collection node would pass the headline and fail both. `mapElems` is affected too, so three stdlib combinators, not two.\n  * NEW: an EMPTY literal and a String-element literal also failed, the latter with a misleading 'EffP unconstrained' -- a knock-on, since a lambda whose body fails to type leaves the callback row uninferable. Both load now.\n\nROOT CAUSE = AN ORDERING DEPENDENCY, which is why the dot form worked. One argument's HINT depends on another argument's TYPE, but the `Expr::Apply` arm built hints for EVERY argument and only then pushed the argument Visits -- so at hint time nothing had been typed, the receiver included. WI-485 worked around it with three readers that need no typing (env binding / rule schema / stamp); none covers an ordinary expression. The dot form escaped because the `DotApply` frame pushes only its receiver's Visit and reads that result before the args are typed (WI-443) -- the correct shape, already in the codebase.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION. I first built the shallow fix (a fourth 'synthesizing' rung calling `type_check_node` re-entrantly on the receiver). It worked and was fully green, but /code-review flagged it as a bandaid at the wrong altitude, and the user directed the ordering fix instead. THAT is what landed: a new `ApplyHints` build frame stages the projection-receiver arguments, visits them FIRST on the SAME work-stack, completes the param->arg-type map from their results, then builds the hints and visits the rest. The receiver is typed ONCE.\n\nThe ordering fix subsumed four review findings the probe had earned rather than merely relocating them: no double typing; no `[simp]` fuel reset (the probe restarted a fresh `SIMP_FUEL` budget); no re-derivation of the simp gate (the probe re-ran the allocating `simp_equation_rids` scan per probe); and -- the one that mattered -- NO SWALLOWED ERROR. The probe did `.ok()`, the exact shape CLAUDE.md's loud-over-silent rule names. Under staging a staged argument's `Err` rides into the `Apply` frame and `collect_arg_errors` reports it; `a_staged_receiver_that_fails_to_type_reports_its_own_error` drives that.\n\nSTAGING IS NARROW, each condition measured: a HOF arg present; the param actually PROJECTED by a sibling (via `collect_projection_receivers`); the no-typing readers missed; and the arg not itself hof-shaped -- that last is NOT hypothetical, `foo(g: (x: Int64) -> Int64, h: (y: g.T) -> Int64)` puts a CALLBACK in the projected set and staging it would visit the lambda with no hint, the very thing the machinery provides.\n\nINSTRUMENTED TO CONFIRM THE TESTS ARE NOT BLIND, because the permutation logic was initially untested -- every test I had put the receiver at positional 0, where the splice is the IDENTITY and a wrong one still passes. Added two tests and verified with a temporary eprintln that they fire the real path: `staged=[1] pos=3` (later positional slot) and `staged=[2] pos=0 named=3` (named, label order reversed from declaration order). The same instrumentation showed `Stream.find` and `List.filterElems` stage too, so the mechanism is exercised by pre-existing stdlib tests, not only mine.\n\nACCEPTANCE, line by line: foldLeft over a literal LOADS and EVALUATES to 123 agreeing with the named-operation spelling; foldRight to 321, likewise agreeing; element type pinned from the literal with no annotation; controls 1 and 3 keep loading (control 3 now DRIVEN, not just load-checked); the pinned known-gap test converted to a positive assertion. THE ONE ACCEPTANCE LINE NOT DELIVERED, and it describes behaviour that NEVER EXISTED: 'an annotated binder that CONTRADICTS the element type is still rejected'. Bisected -- it holds at ONE binder and fails at TWO, and the multi-binder case reproduces with NO projection, NO literal and NO path-dependent type anywhere, so no amount of element-type resolution could deliver it. The one-binder half IS delivered here (`a_contradicting_binder_annotation_is_rejected`); the rest is FILED AS WI-794 with the full bisection and a known-gap test pinning the current wrong behaviour.\n\n/code-review (high) run TWICE, once per implementation. On the ordering fix it found 6; I applied 4. The most important was NOT a bug: the restructure had DELETED WI-750's deliberate scope warning that the stamp reader has no receiver gate and 'the bound is EMPIRICAL, not structural, so do not rely on receiver only when editing here' -- a warning aimed at exactly the edit I was making, and newly misleading by its absence now that narrower staging gates sit beside that reader. Restored, with a note that the staging gates decide which args are TYPED, never which are READ. Also: hints were being computed for ALL args twice on the staged path (now only the staged ones in phase 1); the frame's 'op_params non-empty whenever staged is' invariant was a comment with nothing enforcing it, and an empty vector would silently give every argument a None hint and restore this very defect with no diagnostic -- now a debug_assert (the WI-791 footgun by name); and `arg_at`'s underflow safety rested on `or_else` laziness, now an explicit branch. Two findings NOT taken, both cost-only and judged not worth the churn: an `op_params` Vec clone into the frame per staged call, and a `staged.contains` linear scan in the push loops.", at: "2026-07-20T19:10:01Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-793", description: some(value: "List.foldLeft / foldRight WITH A LAMBDA CALLBACK OVER A LIST LITERAL DOES NOT LOAD -- the path-dependent element type xs.T never resolves for a lambda binder, so the callback body fails to typecheck on a program that is correct. MEASURED on the WI-784 delivery commit (all four cases, exact diagnostics):\n\n  (1) CONTROL, loads clean:\n      operation drive() -> Int64 = List.foldLeft([1, 2, 3], 0, shift)\n      with operation shift(acc: Int64, x: Int64) -> Int64 = acc * 10 + x\n\n  (2) THE DEFECT, 1 load error:\n      operation drive() -> Int64 =\n        List.foldLeft([1, 2, 3], 0, lambda (acc, x) -> acc * 10 + x)\n      \"5:52: type mismatch in add.b (op-arg): expected Int64, got xs.T\"\n\n  (3) CONTROL, loads clean -- the SAME lambda against a DECLARED parameter:\n      operation drive(xs: List[T = Int64]) -> Int64 =\n        List.foldLeft(xs, 0, lambda (acc, x) -> acc * 10 + x)\n\n  (4) foldRight is affected identically:\n      List.foldRight([1, 2, 3], 0, lambda (x, acc) -> acc * 10 + x)\n      \"5:53: type mismatch in add.b (op-arg): expected Int64, got xs.T\"\n\nTHE BOUNDARY IS NARROW AND BOTH OBVIOUS READINGS ARE WRONG. It is NOT \"lambdas break List.foldLeft\" -- case 3 uses the same lambda and loads. It is NOT \"list literals break List.foldLeft\" -- case 1 uses the same literal and loads. It takes the LITERAL receiver AND the LAMBDA callback TOGETHER. A declared List[T = Int64] parameter pins the element type and the same lambda then typechecks; a named operation carries its own declared Int64 parameter types and does not need xs.T resolved at all.\n\nA WI-517 BINDER ANNOTATION DOES NOT OVERRIDE IT -- verified, not assumed:\n  List.foldLeft([1, 2, 3], 0, lambda (acc: Int64, x: Int64) -> acc * 10 + x)\ngives the SAME error at 5:66. This matches the documented rule that the context type wins over the annotation (kernel-language.md section 4.7), which means the user has NO in-source workaround at the call site short of hoisting the list into a typed binding or naming an operation.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS: List is the concrete workhorse collection and a literal is the most natural receiver, so xs.foldLeft([...], 0, lambda ...) -- close to the most idiomatic higher-order call in the language -- is unavailable. FiniteCollection.foldLeft is NOT affected (its callback is typed with a plain Element type param rather than a path-dependent projection), which is the route WI-784's own fold test drives.\n\nRELATION TO WI-784: adjacent, NOT the same defect, and NOT fixed by it. WI-784 was closure ARITY at eval; this one blocks LOAD one rung earlier, so the closure is never reached. Found while verifying a claim in WI-784's test comments -- the first two characterisations written down were both wrong (see above), which is why the pinned test carries two controls rather than a single failing case.\n\nALREADY PINNED: known_gap_list_foldleft_lambda_over_a_literal_does_not_load in rustland/anthill-core/tests/include/wi784_closure_arity_test.rs asserts the CURRENT wrong behaviour (case 2 fails to load with an xs.T diagnostic) plus controls 1 and 3, so the boundary stays visible in the suite. It FAILS when this is closed -- replace case 2 with a positive load-and-evaluate assertion.\n\nACCEPTANCE: List.foldLeft([1, 2, 3], 0, lambda (acc, x) -> acc * 10 + x) LOADS and EVALUATES to 123, agreeing with the named-operation spelling; foldRight likewise; the element type is pinned from the literal receiver so no annotation is needed, and an annotated binder that CONTRADICTS the element type is still rejected; controls 1 and 3 keep loading; the pinned known-gap test is converted to a positive assertion; full workspace green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-20T19:10:09Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-794", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. Full workspace 3214 passed / 0 failed / 11 ignored; 3206 was WI-793's baseline and exactly 8 tests were added (3 known-gap assertions converted + 8 new = 11 present), so BLAST RADIUS ZERO.\n\nROOT CAUSE = ONE `or_else`. extend_env_from_pattern's Pattern::Var arm read `scrutinee_type.or_else(|| type_ann...)` — with BOTH present the annotation was discarded and nothing compared them. WI-517 chose context-wins DELIBERATELY (an earlier draft letting the annotation override was reverted as unsound) and reasoned a contradiction 'surfaces loudly through the body's own use of the binder'. THAT CLAIM IS FALSE when the body never uses the binder at a type-constraining position — `lambda (a: Int64, b: String) -> a` never reads `b`. Context still wins; the LOSING annotation is now reported.\n\nWHY ARITY WAS THE DISCRIMINATOR (the ticket bisected it but could not explain it): at arity 1 the annotation wins the priority ladder one altitude UP, at the lambda (param_type = ann_type), so the arrow carries it and argument-position subsumption refuses it. At arity 2+ param_type comes from the CONTEXT and Pattern::Tuple splits it per component, where the annotation was dropped. Two arities, two mechanisms — pinned by an arity-1 control asserting ABSENCE of the binder-annotation tag and PRESENCE of op-arg, so rerouting arity 1 through the new check FAILS rather than silently passing.\n\nFIX AT THE DROP SITE => NO NEW ALIGNMENT LOGIC. The alternative (a type twin of validate_callback_effect_row at the argument position) would re-derive the binder<->slot correspondence — a SECOND alignment reader free to drift from the first, the hazard WI-782/791 warn about. At the drop site Pattern::Tuple has already handed each binder its component. Covers every pattern position admitting a typed_binder — lambda, let destructure, match arm; VERIFIED against the grammar that there is no fourth (typed_binder occurs only in pattern_typed and _tuple_pattern_elem, both lowering to pattern_var.type_ann).\n\nTWO FOOTGUNS HIT AND FIXED DURING IMPLEMENTATION:\n 1. INDEX ZIP WITHOUT ARITY EQUALITY — paired binder i with component i unconditionally, so a wrong parameter COUNT was reported as a wrong ANNOTATION (`lambda (x: Int64, y: Int64)` at a 3-component slot said 'binder y: expected String', blaming an annotation that is fine).\n 2. THE SHARPER ONE — the first arity guard CHANGED THE BINDING. Filtering components to None left binders as fresh vars and BROKE A LEGITIMATE LOAD: WI-785's arity_mismatch_still_refuses_to_match needs `lambda (p, q)` against a 3-component tuple to load clean (arity is a RUNTIME pattern-match property there) and `p - q` went dispatch-ambiguous. Correct form: suppress ONLY the CHECK, leave the binding byte-identical. GENERALIZES: when adding a validation beside an existing binding, the guard must not travel through the binding path.\n\nREVIEW: /code-review is user-invocable only and could not be launched from here, so I ran the pass by hand on the final diff. Two checks worth recording, both CLEAN: (a) the new TypeErrorContext::BinderAnnotation variant does NOT land in a wildcard arm — all three matches are exhaustive and the compiler enforced the new arms (the WI-538 footgun class); (b) no call site silently discards the new error channel — all three entry points report, sub-pattern recursions propagate, and the single discard is the deliberate misaligned-arity case. ONE COST-ONLY NIT NOT TAKEN: the misaligned branch allocates a fresh Vec per loop iteration where one hoisted outside would do — semantics identical, not worth invalidating a green full-suite run.\n\nPRE-EXISTING, SURFACED NOT FIXED: an arity-ONLY arrow mismatch renders 'expected X, got X' — the same text on both sides, because WI-791's `arity` child is not part of the rendered type. That is now the diagnostic this ticket deliberately defers arity defects TO, so it is worth sharpening. Recorded here rather than spun off.", at: "2026-07-20T22:43:42Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-794", description: some(value: "A MULTI-BINDER LAMBDA'S PARAMETER ANNOTATIONS ARE NEVER CHECKED against the callback parameter slot, so a contradicting annotation loads clean. MEASURED on the WI-793 delivery branch, with the boundary bisected -- the discriminator is BINDER COUNT and nothing else:\n\n  (1) THE DEFECT, LOADS CLEAN:\n      operation apply2(f: (a: Int64, b: Int64) -> Int64) -> Int64 = f(1, 2)\n      operation drive() -> Int64 = apply2(lambda (a: Int64, b: String) -> a)\n      Annotating BOTH binders wrong (`lambda (a: String, b: String) -> 1`) also loads clean.\n\n  (2) THE ONE-BINDER CONTROL, REJECTED:\n      operation apply1(f: (a: Int64) -> Int64) -> Int64 = f(1)\n      operation drive() -> Int64 = apply1(lambda (a: String) -> 1)\n      \"5:32: type mismatch in apply1.f (op-arg): expected Int64 -> Int64, got String -> Int64\"\n\nNO OTHER FACTOR IS INVOLVED -- each of these was measured, not assumed. It is NOT about projections: (1) has none, and a projection-bearing ONE-binder callback (`f: (x: xs.T) -> Acc`) with a contradicting annotation IS rejected. It is NOT about a decoupled effect row: the same one-binder contradiction is rejected both with `@ {EffP}` and with a closed `@ {}` row. It is NOT about an unbound accumulator type param: a one-binder callback with `Acc` in the slot is rejected (`expected Int64 -> ?Acc, got String -> Int64`). Adding a SECOND binder to any of those refused shapes makes it load clean.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS: the annotation is the user's explicit statement of intent, and at arity 2+ it is inert -- silently accepted and then ignored, which is worse than being rejected. The multi-binder lambda is the common callback shape (every fold), so this is the arity most callbacks actually use. Note the VALUE is still typed correctly from the context (kernel-language.md 4.7: the context type wins over the annotation), so this is a missing DIAGNOSTIC on a contradiction, not a wrong runtime value -- which is exactly why it stayed invisible.\n\nLIKELY LOCUS, not verified: the whole-arrow conformance check at the argument position compares the lambda's synthesized arrow against the declared param type. At arity 1 the two arrows compare component-wise and the mismatch surfaces. At arity 2+ the written binder types apparently do not reach that comparison -- WI-791 made arity a ground sibling of the param slot and WI-782 aligned a ParamList positionally, so the reader that builds a MULTI-binder lambda's arrow from its ANNOTATIONS is the thing to look at first.\n\nRELATION TO WI-793: found while measuring WI-793's acceptance line 'an annotated binder that CONTRADICTS the element type is still rejected'. That line turned out to describe behaviour that never existed, and WI-793's mechanism (resolving a path-dependent element type) cannot deliver it -- the defect reproduces with no projection, no literal receiver and no path-dependent type anywhere. WI-793 delivered the one-binder half of that acceptance line (`a_contradicting_binder_annotation_is_rejected`) and this ticket owns the rest.\n\nACCEPTANCE: a multi-binder lambda whose binder annotation contradicts the corresponding callback parameter slot is REJECTED AT LOAD with a located diagnostic naming the binder and both types; case (1) above is refused and case (2) keeps its current diagnostic; a lambda whose annotations AGREE still loads and evaluates; an UNANNOTATED multi-binder lambda is unaffected (its binders are typed from the slot, which is the normal path); full workspace green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-20T22:43:44Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-794", author: "user", content: "REVIEW FOLLOW-UP (xhigh pass on the delivered diff). Full workspace 3215 passed / 0 failed / 11 ignored (+1 test). Three findings applied, one WITHDRAWN as invalid, one FILED as WI-795, one alarm RETRACTED after driving it.\n\nAPPLIED — the conformance DIRECTION had no regression guard. Every existing fixture pairs DISJOINT types (String at an Int64 slot), which fail conformance BOTH ways, so the entire suite would have stayed green if the comparison were reversed or replaced with plain equality. The hazard is concrete: the call reads types_compatible(kb, subst, context_ty, annotation) — parameters (actual, expected) — while the error it builds names the sides the OTHER way round on purpose (expected = the slot, so the message reads in the user's direction). Anyone tidying that apparent inconsistency by swapping the arguments silently flips the rule. Added a_wider_annotation_is_accepted_and_a_narrower_one_is_refused using the sort_provides_admissibly widening (Square provides Shape): a WIDER annotation is imprecise but TRUE and loads (evaluates to 2), a NARROWER one is FALSE and is refused, and the message order 'expected Shape, got Square' is asserted rather than just both names. VERIFIED NOT BLIND by swapping the two arguments in place: exactly ONE test failed — the new one — while the other 11 passed, which also empirically confirms they were blind to direction.\n\nAPPLIED — the groundness gate's doc described a NARROWER gate than the code implements. It justified standing down only for an honestly-polymorphic slot, but resolved_type_is_ground takes an immutable &KnowledgeBase and consults NO Substitution (the same reason the comparison needs a scratch one), so a type var the surrounding inference has ALREADY bound to a concrete type also reads as non-ground and its contradiction is passed over exactly as the original defect did. Documented, with the note that closing it needs the check to run where subst is in hand — i.e. the argument-position alternative this ticket rejected for an unrelated reason.\n\nWITHDRAWN — 'the annotation is interned eagerly where it was lazy' was WRONG as stated: every intern IS consumed (by the check when the context wins, by the binding when it does not), so nothing is wasted. I then tried the real improvement — compare on the occurrence CARRIER via Value::Node and never intern for the check — and it FAILS: node_type_is_ground walks a Type/EffectExpression spine and a pattern's type_ann child is not yet in that form, so resolved_type_is_ground answers false, the gate stands the check DOWN, and every contradiction loads clean again. MEASURED: 6 of 11 tests failed, precisely the rejection ones. That is a silent no-op of exactly the kind this ticket exists to remove, and it was caught ONLY because those tests assert the binder-annotation TAG rather than a bare is_err — the anti-blindness design paying for itself. Reverted, and the reason is now a comment at the site so the next person does not retry it.\n\nFILED AS WI-795 — the arity-only arrow mismatch renders 'expected X, got X', byte-identical on both sides. Pre-existing and cosmetic until this ticket made it LOAD-BEARING by deferring every misaligned-arity case to it.\n\nALARM RETRACTED, recorded because it cost real time. I first ranked as the top finding that the misaligned-arity suppression 'silently drops the contradiction in let and match, where nothing else reports it', and verified it three ways at LOAD time (clean load, zero diagnostics, aligned control correctly rejected). The conclusion did not follow: DRIVING it shows the binder never binds — a misaligned let-destructure fails loudly at runtime ('error: match_failed', exit 1) and a misaligned match arm is dead code that falls through to the next arm (prints, exits 7). No false claim is accepted about any actual value. I stopped at load time on a ticket whose entire subject is load-time diagnostics; the lesson is that 'loads clean' is evidence about loading, not about correctness.", at: "2026-07-21T06:50:56Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-795", description: some(value: "AN ARITY-ONLY ARROW MISMATCH RENDERS 'expected X, got X' — the SAME text on both sides, so the diagnostic that owns every callback arity defect cannot express the defect it owns. MEASURED live on the WI-794 delivery commit:\n\n  operation apply3(f: (a: Int64, b: Int64, c: Int64) -> Int64) -> Int64 = f(1, 2, 3)\n  operation drive() -> Int64 = apply3(lambda (x: String, y) -> 0)\n\n  'error: 4:32: type mismatch in apply3.f (op-arg): expected (a: Int64, b: Int64, c: Int64) -> Int64, got (a: Int64, b: Int64, c: Int64) -> Int64'\n\nBoth rendered arrows are byte-identical. The user is told two types do not match and shown the same type twice; nothing in the message says the ARITY is what differs, and the actual mistake (a 2-binder lambda at a 3-parameter slot) is nowhere in the text.\n\nROOT CAUSE, not verified beyond reading: WI-791 made an arrow's parameter-list ARITY a GROUND SIBLING of the param slot rather than part of the param type, and the type renderer walks the param spine only — so two arrows differing solely in the arity child render identically. The renderer is the likely locus (type_display_name_value and the arrow arm it dispatches to), not the check, which is correctly refusing the program.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS MORE NOW THAN IT DID: this was pre-existing and cosmetic. WI-794 made it LOAD-BEARING. The per-binder annotation check deliberately STANDS DOWN when binder count and component count disagree — because at unequal arity the index zip pairs binder i with an unrelated component, and blaming a specific annotation for a missing parameter is worse than saying nothing (measured on WI-794's first cut: 'binder y: expected String, got Int64' against an annotation that was fine). That suppression is justified in the code by 'leaving the arity defect to whichever check genuinely owns it' — and the check it hands off TO is this one. A deferral target that renders the mismatch as 'expected X, got X' makes the handoff a downgrade, not a delegation.\n\nSCOPE NOTE, measured while reviewing WI-794: the handoff only happens at all for a lambda in ARGUMENT position, where arrow subsumption fires. In a let-destructure or a match arm there is no arrow to subsume, so a misaligned pattern's annotation contradiction is reported by nobody at load time. That is NOT a defect to fix here and was deliberately NOT filed: the binder never binds in those positions — a misaligned let-destructure fails loudly at RUNTIME ('error: match_failed', exit 1, verified) and a misaligned match arm is dead code that falls through to the next arm (verified). Recorded so the next reader does not re-derive the same alarm.\n\nACCEPTANCE: an arity-only arrow mismatch produces a diagnostic in which the two rendered sides DIFFER, and which states the arity of each (e.g. 'expected a 3-parameter function, got 2 parameters'); the example above names the real fault; an arity-AND-type mismatch still reports the type difference it already reports; existing arrow diagnostics that differ in a param TYPE are unchanged; wi791/wi782/wi794 suites stay green; full workspace green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-21T08:02:23Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-790", description: some(value: "`term_ser` mints positional field labels ZERO-based (`_0`, `_1`, ...) while the language convention and all seven other minters are ONE-based (`_1`, `_2`, ...), so a serialized `Term::Fn`'s positional slots carry keys no reader in the codebase interprets correctly. persistence/term_ser.rs ~896 does `all_fields.insert(format!(\"_{i}\"), val)` over `pos_args.iter().enumerate()`. Every other producer emits `format!(\"_{}\", i + 1)`: parse/convert.rs `intern_positional_label` (~326, the canonical one, serving both tuple literals and tuple TYPES), kb/load.rs ~1628, kb/load.rs ~10688, and kb/typing.rs ~4638 / ~7432 / ~17603 / ~27179. The spec agrees with the 1-based form -- docs/kernel-language.md states positional syntax is sugar for auto-generated names `_1`, `_2`, `_3`. CONSEQUENCES for a mixed positional+named `Term::Fn` serialized to JSON: (a) the key `_0` is rejected by every reader -- `field_access` (eval/builtins.rs ~280) does `idx.checked_sub(1)` so `_0` underflows to not-found, and `field_step_in_value` (kb/load.rs ~10133) filters `n >= 1` and drops it; (b) worse, the keys that DO parse are off by one in meaning -- `_1` in that JSON denotes source index 1, while `_1` everywhere else in the system denotes source index 0 -- so a value that round-trips through this encoding shifts by a slot; (c) after WI-786, `is_synthetic_positional_label` classifies `_0` as a USER label (it rejects a zero digit outright), so a reloaded positional component would additionally be re-slotted as named. BLAST RADIUS IS LOW TODAY -- this is a one-way encode with no matching decoder in the same file, so nothing currently reads these keys back -- which is precisely why it has survived; it is a latent trap that fires the moment anyone writes the decoder or feeds this JSON to an existing reader. WHY IT MATTERS BEYOND THE OFF-BY-ONE: this is the clearest single piece of evidence that the `_N` convention has NO OWNER. Eight sites mint it and five parse it, with five different rules (see the sibling ticket on the printer for the enumeration), and one of the eight has silently disagreed with the other seven for as long as it has existed. WI-786 narrowed one of the five recognizers and thereby de-synchronised it from the rest; adding a ninth spelling rather than removing the shared namespace is what makes each of these tickets necessary. FIX DIRECTIONS: (a) minimal -- change term_ser.rs ~896 to `format!(\"_{}\", i + 1)`; (b) better and shared with the printer ticket -- extract `positional_label(index) -> String` and `positional_label_index(label) -> Option<usize>` into `intern.rs` (which sits below parse/, kb/, eval/ and persistence/ in the dependency order, so every producer and consumer can call it), then route all eight minters and five recognizers through that one pair, so the convention can only be changed in one place. Note that direction (b) would have made this bug a compile-time impossibility rather than a silent textual divergence. Found by /code-review on 93b23188. ACCEPTANCE: `term_ser` emits `_1`-based keys agreeing with `intern_positional_label`; a round-trip test serializes a `Term::Fn` carrying both positional and named args and asserts the positional slots reload to the SAME indices (not merely that serialization succeeds); if direction (b) is taken, no module spells the convention with its own `format!`; full workspace green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-21T10:34:23Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-789", author: "claude", content: "CLOSED BY WI-790 (commit 8c11c7ad), not by its own change. Both tickets named the same fix direction (b) -- one owner for the `_N` convention in intern.rs -- and this ticket's own text said (b) 'also closes the term_ser 0-based drift filed separately', so the two were always one fix. WI-790 took (b) and routed all nine producers and all five recognizers through positional_label / positional_label_index / is_positional_label_at. The printer half specifically: is_positional_name was first narrowed to the shared rule (fixing `_01`) and then given the component INDEX, threaded from write_named_tuple_type (which already had it) through write_named_tuple_element (fixing a `_2` written in slot 0). Narrowing the rule alone was tried first and rejected during review -- it fixes one of the two losing shapes while leaving the other, and it left the code claiming a consistency it did not have. ACCEPTANCE VERIFIED CLAUSE BY CLAUSE against tests/include/wi790_positional_label_convention_test.rs: (1) both `(_2: Int64, b: Int64)` and `(_01: Int64, b: Int64)` print verbatim and reparse -- printer_round_trips_labels_outside_the_convention; (2) a genuine positional tuple still prints `(Int64, Int64)` -- same test; (3) print-then-reparse is the IDENTITY on BOTH shapes -- printed_tuple_types_are_a_reparse_fixpoint, a two-generation fixpoint like WI-791's; (4) the five recognizers share one definition -- intern.rs; (5) full workspace green -- 84 binaries / 3234 tests. Both printer tests were positive-controlled: reverting print.rs to its pre-WI-790 form fails them, and MEASURED that both shapes previously printed as the same erased string `(Int64, b: Int64)`, which the parser rejects for mixing positional and named fields.", at: "2026-07-21T10:34:43Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-789", description: some(value: "`TermPrinter` ERASES a `_`-prefixed tuple field label at ANY index, so printing a tuple type emits text the parser rejects -- or, where it does reparse, silently RENAMES the field. `is_positional_name` (persistence/print.rs ~992) tests `n.strip_prefix('_').is_some_and(|d| !d.is_empty() && d.bytes().all(|b| b.is_ascii_digit()))` -- any underscore followed by digits, regardless of which index the component sits at -- and its sole caller `write_named_tuple_element` (~969) then drops the label entirely: `if !positional { write name; \": \" }`. MEASURED via `TermPrinter::print_term` on a declared parameter type: `(_01: Int64, b: Int64)` prints as `(Int64, b: Int64)` and `(_2: Int64, b: Int64)` prints as `(Int64, b: Int64)`. Both lose the label. That output is then REJECTED by the parser -- \"tuple type cannot mix positional and named fields\" -- so the printed form does not round-trip at all; and in the shapes where such text does reparse, the loader mints `_1` for slot 0, i.e. a silent field RENAME across a persistence round-trip. The control `(a: Int64, _b: Int64)` prints intact, so the loss is exactly the `_`+digits class. WHY NOW: these are precisely the two shapes WI-786 pinned as USER labels -- `leading_zero_label_is_not_synthetic` and `synthetic_name_for_the_wrong_index_stays_named` assert that `_01` and a `_2` written first are user labels that keep their position and stay reachable by name. Before WI-786 the classifier and the printer AGREED (both treated any `_`+digits as positional); WI-786 narrowed the classifier to the exact synthetic name for a component's OWN source index and did not narrow the printer, so the two now contradict each other and a shipped test asserts one side of the contradiction. This is PRE-EXISTING -- the printer behaved this way before WI-786 -- but WI-786 turned it from a shared (wrong) convention into an explicit disagreement, which is why it is worth closing now rather than later. BROADER CONTEXT: the `_N` positional-label convention has EIGHT independent minters (parse/convert.rs `intern_positional_label`, kb/load.rs x2, kb/typing.rs x4, persistence/term_ser.rs) and FIVE independent recognizers spelling FIVE different rules -- this printer (`_`+digits, any index), `is_synthetic_positional_label` (eval/eval.rs, exact synthetic name for its own index, rejects leading zeros), `is_positional_tuple_names` (kb/typing.rs ~23775, bare `parse::<usize>()`, so `_01` IS positional), `field_access` (eval/builtins.rs ~280, `strip_prefix` + parse, any index), and `validate_projection_labels` (parse/convert.rs, rejects ANY `_` prefix). No single owner. FIX DIRECTIONS: (a) narrow `is_positional_name` to match `is_synthetic_positional_label` -- the label must be the synthetic name for its own index -- which requires threading the element index into `write_named_tuple_element` (the caller already iterates, so the index is in hand); (b) better, extract the convention to ONE owner: a `positional_label(index) -> String` / `positional_label_index(label) -> Option<usize>` pair in `intern.rs`, which sits below parse/, kb/, eval/ and persistence/ in the dependency order and is therefore callable from every producer and consumer, then route all five recognizers through it so a future narrowing cannot land in one and miss four. (b) also closes the term_ser 0-based drift filed separately. Found by /code-review on 93b23188 (two review angles independently drove the print-then-reparse failure). ACCEPTANCE: printing a tuple type whose label is `_`-prefixed but NOT the synthetic name for its own index preserves the label, so `(_2: Int64, b: Int64)` and `(_01: Int64, b: Int64)` print in a form the parser accepts and that reloads to the same type; a genuine positional tuple type still prints without labels (`(Int64, Int64)`, not `(_1: Int64, _2: Int64)`); a regression test asserts print-then-reparse is the IDENTITY on both shapes, not merely that printing does not panic; if direction (b) is taken, the five recognizers share one definition; full workspace green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-21T10:34:51Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-773", author: "user", content: "057 (extent sources) landed the read-path design that SHAPES this accessor — build to that contract so cpp-gen/CLI migrate onto the final shape, not an interim one. (1) VALUES-FIRST: returns row Values, never RuleId (057 Read path); a RuleId in the public read shape bakes in full residence that 057 forbids (a store-mounted functor has no resident RuleId). (2) POLICY PARAMETER, not hardcoded refuse: read_facts(functor, selection, policy) where policy in {Refuse = facts-only, bodied candidate -> loud Err rendering the rule via TermPrinter::print_rule; Resolve = WI-774 per-functor graduation}. The WI-774 EffectMapping/TypeMapping resolve-vs-refuse decision plugs in HERE, per functor — that is why the accessor MUST carry a policy param. (3) SELECTION = 057 lookup-contract LookupQuery.bound: ground field bindings pushed down (query_type_mappings already grounds anthill_type — that IS the selection); empty selection = enumeration. (4) RESIDENT + MOUNTED, uniform: reads resident discrim AND (once 057 item-1 mounts land) store-owned extents. CRUCIAL SEQUENCING: the RESIDENT path is buildable NOW, so WI-773 + the cpp-gen migration (WI-771) land ahead of 057 items 0-6 on resident-only, and gain store-mounted extents later with ZERO caller change. Error is Result-over-panic so CLI/cpp-gen render through their channels (replaces WI-770 assert-abort). This is 057 retirement stage R1; keep the accessor RuleId-free so the R4 visibility ratchet can privatize the raw walk.", at: "2026-07-21T07:20:18Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-771", author: "user", content: "REBUILD on the WI-773 accessor + 057 Store API, not standalone is_fact asserts. ALL cpp-gen facts-only realization readers become accessor clients (values-first, Refuse policy): CarrierTable::from_kb (Implementation, lib.rs:129), OpImplTable::from_kb (OperationImpl, :254), generated_targets (Generated, :1542) — the three unguarded siblings — PLUS query_realization_facts (:3789, already is_fact-guarded WI-770) and the op_info readers (:1330/:1915) all collapse to kb.read_facts(functor, selection, Refuse): the placeholder-var pattern build, query_view, is_fact assert, and rule_head extraction ALL disappear into the accessor. Refusal moves from assert! (abort exit 101, no span) to Err rendered through CppCodegenError (error: {msg} exit 1). BOUNDARY vs WI-774: the EffectMapping/LanguageMapping candidate readers (supported_effects ~4100) want the RESOLVE policy (guards honored on the deciding path), NOT Refuse — those stay WI-774's call and pass policy=Resolve to the SAME accessor; WI-771 migrates only the facts-only readers. STORE-API PAYOFF (why route through the accessor, not a local guard): 057 reads over resident+MOUNTED extents, so a project that mounts anthill.realization.TypeMapping on a shared host-mapping store is read transparently — cpp-gen unchanged — and cpp-gen's ground selection (anthill_type) becomes the LookupQuery.bound pushdown so the store indexes instead of scans. DEPENDS WI-773 (resident path suffices; no need to wait on 057 items 0-6). Subsumes the original three-is_fact-guards direction.", at: "2026-07-21T07:21:55Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-773", author: "user", content: "CORRECTION to the prior feedback's sequencing (design review): NO partial interface — the accessor is adopted whole or not. DROP the 'resident-only accessor now, gain mounted extents later' idea: a caller (cpp-gen) must migrate onto the FINAL contract (values-first, resident AND mounted, full lookup-contract), never a resident-only stub whose read semantics change under it when mounts land. So WI-773 lands TOGETHER WITH the ExtentSource read seam + discrim mounts + the shipped in-memory reference source (057 item 1) — COMPLETE. 'Ready when used.' v1 stays small not by shipping a partial interface but by LOUD-REFUSING unsupported capability behind the complete one (volatile/oracle = loud registration error; external writes = NotWritable). See 057 Phasing note.", at: "2026-07-21T07:24:03Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-771", author: "user", content: "CORRECTION (design review): WI-771 depends on the COMPLETE WI-773 accessor — which lands with 057 item 1 (ExtentSource read seam + discrim mounts + in-memory reference source), NOT a resident-only interim. No partial interface: cpp-gen migrates ONCE, onto the final resident+mounted shape.", at: "2026-07-21T07:24:03Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-781", description: some(value: "Wire policy_for (proposal 030 phase delta) into the prove/emit path. Surfaced by the WI-772 review cross-file tracer: policy_for / lookup_explicit_policy (rustland/anthill-smt-gen/src/policy.rs) have NO production caller -- tests only -- so TranslationPolicy facts do not actually drive lowering yet and the WI-772(a) bodied-policy refusal is unreachable outside unit tests. Wiring sketch: the prove driver collects cited_predicates (union of every ProofRecord.using), consults policy_for per (predicate, backend), and dispatches the four PredicatePolicy arms -- today only Inline and LiftedAxiom behaviors exist end-to-end (lift_rule_to_implication_clause), so DefineFun / DeclareFun either get real emission or a LOUD not-yet-supported diagnostic (no silent fallback). ACCEPTANCE: an explicit TranslationPolicy fact observably changes emitted SMT in an integration test; a bodied TranslationPolicy rule is refused through the CLI path (rendered via the prove error channel, not only in unit tests); cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-772"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-21T11:06:21Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-787", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED, but three of this ticket's own premises were REFUTED by driving them at HEAD -- the ticket was filed against 93b23188 and WI-789/790/791/792/793/794/795 have landed since. (1) The ACCEPTANCE FIXTURES NO LONGER REACH EVAL. WI-791 made arity a child of the arrow, so a one-parameter slot whose parameter is a TUPLE is no longer satisfied by a genuinely two-parameter operation: 'operation ap(f: (a: Int64, b: Int64) -> Int64) = f(<lit>)' driven as 'ap(sub2)' now fails at LOAD with 'expected 2 arguments -- the parameter list this function value declares, got 1 argument', and the Function[A = (a,b), B = R] spelling fails with 'expected Function[...], got (_1: Int64, _2: Int64) -> Int64'. The eval bug is therefore NOT reachable by the spellings the ticket names. It IS still reachable through a POLYMORPHIC hop, which is what the delivered tests use: 'operation apT[T](f: Function[A = T, B = Int64], t: T) -> Int64 = f(t)' driven as 'apT(sub2, (acc: 3, x: 10))' puts a type VARIABLE in the callback's parameter position, so the tuple's keying is invisible to the conformance check -- that program LOADS CLEAN and raises ArityMismatch{expected: 2, got: 1}, while the byte-identical 'apT(sub2, (3, 10))' returns Int(-7). Named-vs-positional is the only delta. (2) The ticket's 'pr.takeN(5).map(sub2) vs the lambda spelling returns cons(-7,nil)' A/B NO LONGER HOLDS EITHER: at HEAD mapElems REJECTS BOTH spellings at load ('expected a 1-parameter function ((x: ?x, y: ?y)) -> ?Dst, got a 2-parameter function'), the lambda included. The relation-row acceptance was met by routing the row through the same polymorphic hop; the delivered test asserts the OPERATION and LAMBDA spellings AGREE end-to-end (both cons(-7,nil)) rather than pinning either to a literal. (3) 'a driven case shows rel.fix(_1: 3) silently loses its column filter entirely' DID NOT REPRODUCE: the typer's Without reduction rejects it at LOAD -- 'Without drops the field _1, which is not a column of the base schema (a captured argument names no column to restrict)'. It is loud, not silent. The runtime half-read was still closed, but as a guard for a programmatically-built spec, not a live bug. DIRECTION TAKEN: (b) scaled down. Added Value::tuple_components() -> Option<TupleComponents> (eval/value.rs), an O(1)-len handle owning the 'pos ++ named is source order' invariant; migrated spread_eta_args (THE fix), match_tuple_pattern, and detect_cycle onto it; relation_project_run/relation_fix now share spec_record_fields, which refuses a POSITIONAL component loudly instead of reading the empty record as an identity filter. (b) AS LITERALLY SPECIFIED IS REFUTED, not merely expensive: making the halves private cannot 'turn every remaining half-reader into a COMPILE ERROR' because LEGITIMATE half-readers exist -- TermView for Value (kb/term_view.rs) reads pos by INDEX in pos_arg and named by SYMBOL in named_arg/named_keys, which IS the trait's contract (it mirrors Term::Fn{pos_args, named_args}), and field_access (eval/builtins.rs) reads both halves through DIFFERENT access paths (named by short name, pos by positional_label_index). Privatizing would false-positive on four sanctioned readers; it is deliverable only with an escape hatch, a materially different proposal. That argument is now recorded IN the tuple_components doc so it is not re-litigated. Also recorded there: this does NOT generalize to Value::Entity despite the identical layout, because finish_constructor canonicalizes an entity's named into DECLARED field order while a tuple is exempted from that canonicalization precisely so SOURCE order survives -- one shared accessor would be a single iterator silently meaning two different things. TESTS: 6 in wi787_eta_spread_named_tuple_test.rs. Verified NOT BLIND by reverting only spread_eta_args: the 3 regression tests fail with exactly ArityMismatch{expected:2,got:1} while the 3 controls (positional spread, arity strictness, parser mixing-refusal) stay green. One further measured fact worth recording: a tuple literal MIXING positional and named components is refused by the PARSER, so the prefix-split shape is not constructible today -- pinned by a test rather than claimed in prose. Full workspace green: 3251 passed, 0 failed.", at: "2026-07-21T11:41:11Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-787", description: some(value: "`spread_eta_args` reads `Value::Tuple.pos` ALONE, so a name-keyed tuple presents as ZERO components and an eta'd multi-parameter OPERATION cannot be applied to it -- the same defect WI-785 fixed in `match_tuple_pattern`, left unfixed in the sibling reader ~900 lines up the same file, and turned into a live REGRESSION by WI-786. eval/eval.rs ~1701 does `if let Value::Tuple { pos, .. } = &arg_values[0] { if pos.len() == arity { ...spread... } }`; it never consults `named`. WI-786 narrowed `classify_ctor_arg` so that user labels beginning with `_` stay NAMED instead of being hoisted into `pos` -- correct in itself -- but every component that moved out of `pos` vanished from this reader's view. CONFIRMED REGRESSION, driven A/B against a tree with ONLY `classify_ctor_arg` reverted (so WI-785 is held constant and the delta is WI-786's alone): with `operation sub2(p: Int64, q: Int64) -> Int64 = p - q` and `operation ap(f: (a: Int64, b: Int64) -> Int64) -> Int64 = f(<lit>)` driven as `ap(sub2)`, the literals `(_x: 3, _y: 10)`, `(_2: 3, _1: 10)` and `(_01: 3, _02: 10)` EACH returned `Ok(Int(-7))` before commit 93b23188 and now raise `ArityMismatch { op: \"function-value application\", expected: 2, got: 1 }`. Three programs that answered correctly now trap. NON-CONTRIVED PATH (this is the one that matters): a relation row is built all-named -- the row builder in eval/mod.rs constructs `Value::Tuple { pos: [], named: [...] }` because a row's field order IS the relation schema -- so `rule pr(?x, ?y) :- Rec(a: ?x, b: ?y)` then `pr.takeN(5).map(sub2)` raises ArityMismatch, while the byte-identical `pr.takeN(5).map(lambda (p, q) -> p - q)` returns `cons(-7, nil)`. Mapping a named OPERATION over relation rows is ordinary usage and is broken. SEPARATE PRE-EXISTING FACET (not a WI-786 regression, same root cause): `operation ap(f: (acc: Int64, x: Int64) -> Int64) = f((acc: 3, x: 10))` applied to a two-parameter op-ref traps identically both before and after 93b23188, while the same shape applied to `lambda (acc, x) -> ...` now works -- so the SAME source program has two verdicts depending only on whether the callee is an operation or a lambda. That asymmetry is precisely WI-785's motivating fold-callback shape with the callee kind swapped. ROOT CAUSE / ALTITUDE: `Value::Tuple { pos, named }` (eval/value.rs) exposes both halves as public fields with no owning accessor, so the \"`pos ++ named` is source order\" invariant WI-786 established lives only in doc comments and each consumer re-derives the walk. There are currently FOUR independent walks: `match_tuple_pattern` (eval/pattern.rs) and `detect_cycle` (eval/effects.rs) chain both halves correctly; `spread_eta_args` reads `pos` only (this ticket); `relation_project_run` / `relation_fix` (eval/builtins.rs ~1605/~1667) read `named` only -- and a driven case shows `rel.fix(_1: 3)` silently loses its column filter entirely. FIX DIRECTIONS: (a) minimal and local -- mirror the matcher at eval/eval.rs ~1701: `if pos.len() + named.len() == arity { pos.iter().chain(named.iter().map(|(_, v)| v)).cloned().collect() }`, which also closes the lambda/op-ref asymmetry as a side effect; (b) structural -- add `Value::Tuple::components() -> impl Iterator<Item = &Value>` and make the two halves private, turning the invariant from prose into a signature so that every remaining half-reader becomes a COMPILE ERROR rather than a runtime surprise; this is what would have caught the present bug, since WI-786 documented the invariant in three doc blocks across two files and the prose demonstrably did not reach a reader 900 lines away in the same file. (b) subsumes the `relation_fix` / `relation_project_run` gap and is the recommended direction if the extra scope is acceptable; (a) is the right immediate stop-the-bleeding change. Found by /code-review on 93b23188 (four of ten review angles independently built binaries and drove it). ACCEPTANCE: `ap(sub2)` over each of `(_x: 3, _y: 10)`, `(_2: 3, _1: 10)`, `(_01: 3, _02: 10)` evaluates to -7 again; `pr.takeN(5).map(sub2)` over an all-named relation row evaluates, agreeing with the `lambda (p, q)` spelling of the same call; a regression test drives the OPERATION and LAMBDA spellings of one call and asserts they AGREE end-to-end (using a fresh Interpreter per case -- a reused one returns a bogus Internal(\"deliver: parent frame had no awaiting state\") after any trap); if direction (b) is taken, `relation_fix` with a `_`-prefixed key no longer drops its filter; full workspace green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-21T11:41:18Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-788", author: "user", content: "ROOT CAUSE AS RECORDED WAS INCOMPLETE, and the recorded FIX DIRECTION was refuted. Measured: the headline repro never reaches align_named_tuple_fields at all. arrow_positional_param_slots returns None for Function[A,B,E] BY DESIGN (it states no arity; WI-775 settled that f(3,10) and f((3,10)) are both legal there), and 'no count can be REQUIRED' had been taken to mean 'nothing can be checked' -- so a function-value application at a Function slot checked NOTHING. Proof it is not a permutation gap: the IN-ORDER but type-wrong f((a: \"ess\", b: 3)) also loaded clean and returned Str from -> Int64, and f(true) loaded clean too. So there were TWO independent defects; the ticket recorded one. FIX DIRECTION REFUTED: 'align by the CONSUMER's read discipline' is not decidable where the permutation is admitted -- the value flows through a Function[A,B] PARAMETER to a consumer chosen at a DIFFERENT call site (ap(lambda (p,q) -> p) vs ap(lambda t -> t.a)), so the site relating the literal to A cannot know which reader it will meet. DECIDED BY THE OWNER (language decision, not a bug-fix detail): component ORDER IS part of a named tuple's type identity. Every tuple has a CANONICAL POSITIONAL REPRESENTATION numbering components _1.._n by DEFINITION order, so (a: Int64, b: String) is (_1: Int64, _2: String) and (b: String, a: Int64) is (_1: String, _2: Int64) -- DIFFERENT TYPES. Permutation is refused at every position; names still participate, so (a: Int64, b: String) != (Int64, String) still holds (004 rule 4). WIDTH survives PREFIX-ONLY: dropping a TRAILING component preserves every retained component's canonical position, dropping a MIDDLE one renumbers what follows (c moving _3 -> _2), so (a,b,c) </: (a,c). NOTE this is STRICTER than the subsequence rule I first measured, which allowed the middle drop. DOC CONTRADICTION FOUND AND FIXED (the reason to check the spec BEFORE coding): kernel-language.md said permutation and width ARE subtyping rules for name-read data tuples and spelled out a permuted arrow as ACCEPTED, while CLAUDE.md said source order IS its identity, and proposal 004 was SILENT on order. I built and nearly shipped a change against CLAUDE.md's claim without checking the spec; the two tests it broke were SPEC'D BEHAVIOR, not collateral. All three documents now agree. COST, as measured: exactly 2 deliberate tests asserted the permutation is legal and both are now inverted -- wi782 permuted_tuple_typed_parameter_still_applies (-> _is_refused, plus a new in-order control) and wi488 named_tuple_reordered_component_abstracting_producer_flagged (split: an IN-ORDER named-tuple gate test, since every other fixture in that file is positional, plus a mismatch-before-the-gate test). A third, wi784 wrong_arity_application_is_still_refused_with_the_binder_count, MOVED FROM EVAL TO LOAD -- same invariant (the diagnostic names the binder-derived count, not a hardcoded 1), earlier stage.", at: "2026-07-21T12:55:06Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-788", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. Two independent defects fixed, both measured end-to-end before and after: (1) ORDER IS IDENTITY -- align_named_tuple_fields' ByName arm went from an order-insensitive by-name lookup to a slot-by-slot zip with names required to agree at each slot, prefix width only; (2) THE Function SLOT NOW CHECKS ITS ARGUMENTS -- positional_arg_expectations is one total reader over BOTH callable spellings, returning Slots/CountMismatch/NoVerdict, replacing two inline branches with a hand-rolled arity error each. NoVerdict names the one genuine absence so it cannot read as 'handled'. /code-review ON MY OWN CHANGE FOUND 11 ISSUES, 3 of them mine and fixed here: (a) the count check compared A's components against the POSITIONAL count while the caller rendered the TOTAL, so a fully-labelled call f(a: 1, b: 2) produced 'expected ... or 2 ..., got 2 arguments' -- the expected-X-got-X shape WI-795 already fixed once -- AND preempted the accurate 'no parameter names to bind a label to' error; now returns NoVerdict when labels are present. (b) prefix width with an EMPTY expected list zipped to nothing and vacuously agreed, so ANY tuple conformed to the unit type () and then trapped against a nullary pattern -- pre-existing, but newly load-bearing on the prefix-width rule this ticket wrote into the spec; unit now relates to unit only. (c) the rewritten wi784 assertion was contains(\"2\") && contains(\"3\"), satisfied incidentally by a span like 3:2; now asserts the phrases. Both new guards CONTROL-TESTED by disabling them (2 tests fail) rather than assumed. /simplify also caught a comment I shipped claiming the two TupleAlign modes differ in EXACTLY ONE way -- there are TWO, and granting ByName the missing one (the synthetic _1.._n escape) would relate (a: Int64, b: String) to (Int64, String), reintroducing a variant of this bug. DOCS: kernel-language.md sections 4.5 / Arrow types / Function-equivalence / 6.7, proposal 004 rule 4a, and CLAUDE.md all reconciled -- they had CONTRADICTED each other, which is what nearly made me ship a spec-violating change. A late catch: my first pass at the docs said every tuple has a canonical positional representation numbering components _1.._n, which is NOT the implemented rule and cannot be -- read literally it erases names and collapses (a: A) into (_1: A), which 004 rule 4 refuses and positional_tuple_does_not_conform_to_a_named_one pins. Corrected in all five places to 'a component is identified by its name AND its position'. TESTS: 14 in wi788_tuple_component_order_identity_test, 8 of which fail against the parent (verified by control run); 3 inverted/moved elsewhere (wi782, wi488, wi784). Full workspace 3267 passed / 0 failed via scripts/test.sh. FOLLOW-UPS FILED: WI-798 (double extract_type per Function application), WI-799 (TupleAlign should name the discipline as a policy struct; also unblocks unify_named_tuple's asymmetric prefix width on an EQUALITY relation), WI-800 (thread_expected_tuple_field still order-blind, mis-aims diagnostics), WI-801 (typer pivots on A's component count while eval pivots on the callee's binder arity -- not enrolled in the Pattern::binder_arity one-owner discipline), WI-802 (\"anthill.prelude.Function\" hand-compared at 3 sites, silent failure mode).", at: "2026-07-21T13:39:37Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-788", description: some(value: "A destructuring binder over an OUT-OF-ORDER tuple literal binds by SOURCE POSITION while the typer typed those binders by the type's DECLARED FIELD ORDER -- the two disagree silently, and an operation declared `-> Int64` returns a String on a clean load. MEASURED end-to-end (driven directly, and independently reproduced by two review agents building pre/post binaries): `operation ap(f: Function[A = (a: Int64, b: String), B = Int64]) -> Int64 = f((b: \"ess\", a: 3))` with `operation drive() -> Int64 = ap(lambda (p, q) -> p)` LOADS CLEAN and returns `Ok(Str(\"ess\"))`. The all-Int64 variant `f((b: 10, a: 3))` with `lambda (p, q) -> p - q` returns 7 where the declared order (a, b) implies -7; a three-field scramble returns 123 where the declared order implies 321; an all-Int64 two-field case returns 12 vs 21 with no type signal at all. Reachable through a lambda binder, a `match` case, and `let`-destructuring alike. ROOT CAUSE: `align_named_tuple_fields` (kb/typing.rs) relates a tuple LITERAL's type to a tuple TYPE **by name, order-insensitively** -- the by-name rung is tried first -- so a permuted literal type-checks against the declared type and the typer assigns binder *i* the type of DECLARED field *i*. Meanwhile `match_tuple_pattern` (eval/pattern.rs, WI-785) binds binder *i* to the value's SOURCE component *i*. Where written order differs from declared order, the type the typer gave a binder is not the type of the value it receives. NOT FIXABLE IN EVAL: the matcher never sees the type, and the value carrier is a faithful ordered product (WI-786 guarantees `pos ++ named` is source order) -- the defect is entirely in the TYPE RELATION admitting a permutation that the value representation does not perform. THIS IS A REGRESSION OF WI-785: before it, a name-keyed tuple presented as ZERO positional components, so the old `pos.len() != positional.len()` test refused the match and raised LOUDLY. WI-785 correctly taught the matcher to read name-keyed tuples and thereby converted this shape from a loud failure into a quiet wrong answer. Reverting WI-785 is not the answer -- it fixed a real bug and its own tests pin it -- the type relation must stop admitting the permutation for this consumer. SAME DEFECT AS WI-783 AT A DIFFERENT SITE: WI-783 (Delivered) has a live, confirmed permuted-ARROW regression -- `impl_ba(b, a)` passed where `(a, b)` is declared, with `f(b: 10, a: 3)` returning 7 while the positional spelling of the same written order returns -7 -- and its recorded root cause is the same one: `align_named_tuple_fields` aligns by name (there via `TupleAlign::ParamList`, whose by-name rung is also tried first) while the runtime binds by position. So there are now TWO live silent-wrong-answer bugs on main sharing one root cause in one function, one on the arrow-parameter-list side and one on the data-tuple side. That reframes WI-782 from a tidiness ticket into the gate on both. FIX DIRECTION: a tuple type used to type a DESTRUCTURING BINDER LIST (or an arrow parameter list) must align POSITIONALLY, or the by-name permutation must be REJECTED when the consumer destructures -- because the consumer reads by position and the carrier is an ordered product. By-name alignment remains correct for consumers that read by NAME (`t.x` field access, which WI-638 defines as order-independent); the bug is applying one relation to both kinds of consumer. Whatever WI-782 settles for parameter lists should settle this identically -- if the two are fixed separately they will drift, since they are the same rung of the same function. RELATED: WI-785 (the matcher that made this observable), WI-786 (the carrier's source-order invariant, which holds and is not at fault), WI-775 (established that a tuple's KEYING is part of its type). Found by /code-review on 93b23188. ACCEPTANCE: `f((b: \"ess\", a: 3))` against declared `(a: Int64, b: String)` either binds `p` to the Int64 component or is REJECTED AT LOAD with a located diagnostic -- it must not load clean and return a String from an `-> Int64` operation; the all-Int64 permuted cases agree with the declared order or are rejected; the fix is expressed in the SAME place as WI-782's parameter-list fix, not as a second independent rule; a regression test drives an out-of-order literal end-to-end and asserts the answer matches the DECLARED field order (or that the program is refused); WI-783's permuted-arrow repro is re-driven and agrees; full workspace green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-782"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-21T13:39:48Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-804", description: some(value: "Restore NAME-KEYED width subtyping for data tuples: (A: TA, B: TB, C: TC) <: (A: TA, C: TC) must hold, with a component dropped from ANYWHERE, not only the end. WI-788 refused the middle drop, and the argument it gave was that dropping B renumbers C from _3 to _2 and so changes the type. That is an argument about IDENTITY applied to <:, which is a DIFFERENT RELATION -- the consumer of an (a, c)-typed value asks for .a and .c, and an (a, b, c) value answers both wherever the components sit. Order belongs to identity; it has no part in <:. THE NARROWING ALSO DID NOT BUY SAFETY, measured both ways: the PREFIX drop WI-788 ALLOWED breaks a destructuring reader exactly as the middle drop would (both change the binder COUNT, so the match fails and raises), so the rule refused a correct program while admitting an equally broken one -- anti-correlated with what actually breaks. THE PRE-WI-788 SPEC HAD THIS RIGHT and WI-788 rewrote it: \"Permutation and width are subtyping rules for data tuples whose components are read by NAME ... (A data tuple read by DESTRUCTURING is also positional; aligning such a reader by name is a known open defect, tracked as WI-788).\" The relation was correct and the READER was named as the defect. DELIVERED HERE: align_named_tuple_fields' data arm is name-keyed and order-PRESERVING (a cursor scan, so a dropped component may come from anywhere but a permutation is still refused); the ORDER requirement is recorded in code and spec as an explicit INTERIM, not as the rule. WHY THE INTERIM IS SOUND TO HOLD: a destructuring binder list reads by SLOT and COUNT, so it is unsound under any <: step, but the failure modes differ -- WIDTH changes the count and fails LOUDLY, while PERMUTATION keeps the count and swaps the VALUES, silently binding a component the typer typed from a different field (an operation declared -> Int64 returning a String on a clean load). So width is admitted now and order is held until the reader binds by label, which is the real WI-788 and is tracked separately. Unit is excluded from width: () is not \"the record with no fields\" but the UNIT sort, which spec 4.5 gives exactly ONE value, so a wider tuple does not conform to it. DOCS: kernel-language.md 4.5 (width paragraph plus the interim note), the Arrow-types paragraph, proposal 004 rule 4a, and CLAUDE.md all state that identity and <: are different relations and that carrying the order rule across from one to the other is the mistake to avoid. ACCEPTANCE: width_middle_drop_conforms drives (a: 3, b: \"ess\", c: 10) into a (a: Int64, c: Int64) parameter and evaluates; the permutation tests stay refused; full workspace green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-788"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-21T14:18:41Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-796", description: some(value: "Implement the ExtentSource read-seam FOUNDATION per proposal 057 (docs/proposals/057-extent-read-seam.md), decomposition items 1-2 (trait + reference source + conformance), buildable and testable in ISOLATION before the resolver wiring (its sibling item). BUILD: (1) the ExtentSource trait READ HALF only -- owned() returning (fully-qualified functor name String, ExtentProfile) pairs, plus query(kb, &QueryPattern) -> Result<Box<dyn ExtentCursor>, ExtentError>; NO write/mirror/sync methods yet (the trait grows one method-set per slice, never ahead of the code). (2) the types: QueryPattern{mode: usize, bound: Vec<(ArgKey, Value)>}, ArgKey = Named(Symbol) | Pos(u32), QueryMode{required_ground: Vec<ArgKey>}, ExtentProfile{query_modes, enumerable, complete, stability} (read axes; writability deferred to the write seam WI-780), Stability = Stable | Volatile, ExtentCursor::next(kb) -> Option<Result<Value, ExtentError>>, ExtentError{NoSupportedMode, Backend(String)}. (3) ExtentRegistry as a KB field kb.extents, successor to RouteRegistry: sources in a SourceId-keyed slab, mount table Symbol -> SourceId, materialized per-functor ExtentProfiles (subsuming store_monotonicity); register_extent_owner(key, source) resolves owned() names to Symbols ONCE at registration (loud on an unresolvable name), refuses a functor already owned OR a volatile source declaring more than one query mode. (4) the shipped InMemoryExtentSource -- enumerable+complete+stable in-memory table, SEEDED at construction, read-only (implements owned + query), a by_id-style key so content-to-key mapping is exercised; the reference owner AND owner-swap fixture, NOT a test-only mock. (5) the trait-level CONFORMANCE SUITE (the 057 query contract), driven by calling query DIRECTLY: a declared mode answers and an undeclared pattern yields NoSupportedMode; ground-equality-only pushdown; SOUNDNESS -- a backend that under-returns (drops a row satisfying bound) FAILS, one that over-returns PASSES because the caller re-filters against the full goal; enumeration on a non-enumerable source refused. Stability::Volatile and a non-enumerable oracle mode are loud REGISTRATION errors in v1 (deferred archetypes, refused not stubbed). ACCEPTANCE: the trait, types, ExtentRegistry, and InMemoryExtentSource exist; the conformance suite passes against InMemoryExtentSource via direct query calls; register_extent_owner enforces single-owner and volatile-single-mode loudly; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-21T14:05:26Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-798", description: some(value: "positional_arg_expectations (kb/typing.rs) extracts the callee type TWICE for every Function-typed application: arrow_positional_param_slots runs a full extract_type, returns None because the head is Parameterized rather than Arrow, and function_spec_param_type immediately repeats it. Each extract_type on a Parameterized head does a type_head classify plus TWO Vec allocations (named_keys, then the bindings vec) and clones every bound type (A, B, E). This is the per-application hot path. FIX: one extract_type at the top of the reader, matched once and dispatched on the variant -- the two readers are already disjoint on TypeExtractor::Arrow vs Parameterized, so no dispatch is lost. Found by /code-review on WI-788. SMALL. ACCEPTANCE: one extraction per call measured (or the two readers restructured to share it); full workspace green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-788"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-21T15:02:18Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-799", description: some(value: "TupleAlign (kb/typing.rs) names the SITE, not the discipline, and its ByName variant no longer aligns by name -- WI-788 made it a positional zip, and its own doc concedes the name is now 'about names PARTICIPATING, not about the correspondence being built from them'. A reader reaching for 'align by name' will pick it and get positional alignment. EVIDENCE THE NAMING MISLEADS: WI-788 shipped a comment claiming the two modes differ in EXACTLY ONE way (width); /code-review caught that there are TWO (width, and the synthetic _1.._n escape that ByName must NOT have -- granting it would relate (a: Int64, b: String) to (Int64, String), which 004 rule 4 forbids). FIX: replace the enum with a policy struct naming both axes, e.g. { width: Prefix|Exact, names: Exact|ExactOrSynthetic }, with DATA = {Prefix, Exact} and PARAM_LIST = {Exact, ExactOrSynthetic}. THIS ALSO UNBLOCKS A REAL DEFECT the two-mode enum cannot express: unify_named_tuple is an EQUALITY relation but takes the PREFIX-width mode, so unification of two named tuples is ASYMMETRIC -- unify((x: A, y: B), (x: A)) succeeds while the arguments swapped fail, and unification is used bidirectionally by the resolver. That is PRE-EXISTING (the old by-name arm was width-subtyping in the same direction) and is documented in place on unify_named_tuple, not fixed. 'Exact width, names exact' is the third discipline it wants. Found by /code-review on WI-788. ACCEPTANCE: the two axes are data, not variant names; unify_named_tuple takes exact width and the asymmetry is gone (or a measured reason it must stay is recorded); full workspace green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-788"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-21T16:10:22Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-800", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. thread_expected_tuple_fields now threads through align_named_tuple_slots in TupleAlign::DATA mode -- the SAME walk, same argument order (actual, expected), that named_tuple_compatible runs over the literal moments later -- instead of its own order-blind first-match-by-SHORT-NAME lookup.\n\nTHE TICKET'S PRESCRIPTION 'thread POSITIONALLY with name agreement' IS NOT WHAT SHIPPED, and the difference is measured. A literal index-for-index zip breaks WIDTH: WI-804 made the drop name-keyed from ANYWHERE, so `(mid: 1, head: h, rest: t)` conforms to `(head: xs.T, rest: List[T = xs.T])`, and a zip compares slot 0 `mid` against `head`, gives up, and the program STOPS LOADING (h is a free ?_ that only the hint can pin). CONTROL-RUN: with a naive zip in place, width_threads_past_a_dropped_component and width_threads_around_a_dropped_component both FAIL. Sharing the relation's own cursor scan is the faithful reading of 'the same name agreement the relations now use'; a positional zip is not.\n\nTHE DIAGNOSTIC GOT LESS SPECIFIC, NOT MORE -- reported honestly because the ticket predicted the opposite. MEASURED before/after on the permuted free-var case: 'got Option[T = (rest: List, head: xs.T)]' became 'got Option[T = (rest: List, head: ?_)]'. The ticket expected the mis-aimed hint to produce a WORSE message; what it actually produced was a more CONFIDENT one -- `head: xs.T` is the expected component type threaded in by name at a slot the relation was about to refuse, i.e. the got-side asserting a type the literal was never given. `?_` is what the literal actually has. The permuted CONCRETE cases (WI-788's headline, and the op-return spelling) are byte-identical before and after: with no free var there is nothing for a hint to change. So the diagnostic case for this ticket is thinner than it was written; the case that survives is agreement.\n\nTHE DEFECT THAT IS REAL, AND IT IS THE SECOND ONE THE TICKET DID NOT NAME: the two walks pick DIFFERENT COMPONENTS whenever a first-match and a resume-after-the-previous-match disagree -- i.e. on a duplicate label. Probed rather than assumed, and it is reachable: `take((a: 1, b: 2, a: \"ess\"))` against `(b: Int64, a: String)` LOADS CLEAN because the cursor takes the SECOND `a`. That is the alignment the accepted program is built on, so it is the one the hint must use.\n\nFILED WI-805 off that probe: the same program returns Int(1) from an operation declared -> String, because field_access reads the FIRST `a` while the relation typed the second. A live wrong-typed-value bug of the WI-788 family, reached through LABELS rather than order. Pinned live (and marked RECORDED-not-endorsed) by duplicate_label_alignment_resumes_after_the_previous_match.\n\nSEAM: align_named_tuple_fields -> align_named_tuple_slots, returning the correspondence itself (one `a` index per `b` component) rather than the (a_ty, b_ty) pairs. The pairs discarded the one thing a non-relation caller needs -- WHICH slot -- which is why the threading had to grow its own walk. The pair VIEW is restored for the three field-wise relations as `aligned_pairs`, a borrowing iterator: one owner for the 'slot indexes a, position indexes b' invariant, which written out three times compiles fine as `b_fields[slot]` and silently misaligns. Net allocation is DOWN, not up: SmallVec<[usize; 8]> inline vs a heap Vec of cloned Value pairs, and the old per-component `short_name_of(...).to_owned()` String malloc (paid unconditionally, even with no expectation) is gone.\n\nKEYING CHANGED from short names to SYMBOL IDENTITY, which the old doc explicitly warned against ('regardless of symbol identity'). The warning does not survive the sharing, and the argument is not local: the threaded list IS the list the built tuple type carries, so the symbols conformance compares on the actual side are these and on the expected side are exp_fields' -- threading now agrees with the relation BY CONSTRUCTION, and where symbols fail to line up conformance fails on the same comparison, so the hint is only ever withheld from a program that is refused anyway. Recorded in place.\n\nTESTS: 5 integration (wi800_tuple_threading_alignment_test) + 1 unit (alignment_reports_the_slot_each_component_matched, pinning the index return incl. the duplicate-label cursor case). CONTROL-RUN BOTH WAYS rather than assumed: with the OLD by-name lookup restored, permuted_literal_is_not_threaded FAILS; with a naive positional zip, both width_* FAIL. duplicate_label_* discriminates NEITHER and says so in its own doc -- it is a witness for WI-805, not coverage for this ticket.\n\n/simplify (4 agents): applied -- aligned_pairs (all 3 non-altitude agents flagged the 3 copies independently); three parallel same-length Vecs collapsed to ONE threaded IN PLACE (the list built for the tuple type IS the list threaded); an avoidable Value clone per matched component; SmallVec; a Debug-string assert replaced by a Value match; two STALE docs the rename left behind (resolve_projected_member's, which claimed named-tuple lookup is short-name-keyed EVERYWHERE -- it never was for the relations -- and wi462's, which described the removed behavior). SKIPPED: consolidating load_errs/assert_refused into tests/common (6 sibling files, all outside this diff -- half-consolidating leaves two ways to do it); the double load_errs 'waste' (FALSE POSITIVE, assert! format args are lazy); moving the duplicate-label witness to WI-805's ticket (it is the only live witness today and its doc says what it does and does not pin).\n\nRESIDUAL, recorded in code: the threading pass exists at all only because types_compatible will not BIND a var on the actual side. A checking relation that bound would need no separate hint and no second correspondence to keep in step. This ticket removes the duplicate CORRESPONDENCE, not the duplicate PASS.\n\nFull workspace 3295 passed / 0 failed via scripts/test.sh.", at: "2026-07-21T16:47:09Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-800", description: some(value: "thread_expected_tuple_field (kb/typing.rs ~2311) still matches expected component types by SHORT NAME, order-blind, after WI-788 made every relation around it order-sensitive. For a permuted literal (b: \"ess\", a: 3) against an expected (a: Int64, b: String) it threads the expected type of 'a' into the component NAMED a -- the SECOND written component -- before the order mismatch is reported. NOT A SOUNDNESS BUG: the relation still refuses the program, and this function is a hint-threading no-op on a miss, not a type relation. It is a DIAGNOSTIC-QUALITY defect -- the inference hint lands in the wrong slot and can mis-aim the error that follows. FIX: thread positionally with the same name agreement the relations now use. Its doc comment was updated by WI-788 to say this explicitly rather than changing behavior mid-ticket. Found by /code-review on WI-788. SMALL. ACCEPTANCE: threading is positional with name agreement; a test drives a permuted literal and asserts the diagnostic names the right component; full workspace green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-788"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-21T16:47:17Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-800", author: "user", content: "/code-review (xhigh, 10 angles + sweep) ON MY OWN CHANGE: 8 findings, all applied, none of them a live bug -- which is itself the result worth recording, since the two the review ranked highest are both LATENT fail-open paths I introduced while removing one.\n\n(1) aligned_pairs zips slots against b_fields with NO length check. slots is always one-per-b-component today, but zip stops at the shorter side, so a slots that ever drifted short would relate only a PREFIX and every caller's .all(..) would pass VACUOUSLY on the components it never looked at -- a relation accepting a tuple whose last component was never checked. The helper's own doc argued it existed to protect the pairing invariant; the zip was the one line in it that could fail QUIETLY. Now debug_assert_eq!'d, matching the guard the synthetic-escape branch already carries. (The sibling misuse, a slot out of range for a, panics on the index and so cannot pass silently.)\n\n(2) The escape branch's (0..b_fields.len()) changed the RELEASE failure mode: the old pair-building zip truncated to the shorter list, these indices make aligned_pairs PANIC on a_fields[slot]. Unreachable (the mod tuple_align seal + the existing debug_assert), and a loud wrong beats a silent one, but a typer panic is not a diagnostic and the diff changed it without saying so. Recorded in place, WITH the instruction not to 'fix' it by clamping to the shorter length -- that restores exactly the silent truncation the seal exists to prevent.\n\n(3) DOC OVERCLAIM I shipped in the delivery above: 'the SAME walk that named_tuple_compatible will run over this literal moments later'. types_compatible -> named_tuple_compatible decides ACCEPTANCE, but it is not the only relation the literal's type meets -- check_apply_iter's INFERENCE unify reaches unify_named_tuple, whose EQUALITY discipline takes EXACT width and so refuses a width step this hint just took. Tolerated for the same reason that unify's failure is (it does not decide acceptance -- the wi799 module doc measured this), but the justification for hardcoding DATA rests on this claim, so it now reads 'the relation that DECIDES' and names the inference unify as the other one that runs.\n\n(4) I wrote that SmallVec<[usize; 8]> 'covers every tuple the corpus writes' -- an UNMEASURED quantitative claim, and I did not instrument tuple arities before writing it. Replaced with the mechanism (a wider tuple spills exactly as the old pair vector always did, so this only ever removes allocations) plus an explicit note that 8 is the usual small-buffer pick and no survey was taken.\n\n(5) Effects merging moved from interleaved-with-threading to a second pass, and nothing said the reordering had been considered. Safe -- tsubst is local and never reaches effect merging, and both only ADD to a kb whose terms are hash-consed by structure, so allocation order is unobservable -- but that reasoning is now recorded rather than left to be re-derived.\n\n(6) My permuted-literal test pinned the literal string '(rest: List, head: ?_)', coupling it to how an UNBOUND type var prints. The load-bearing claim is that the got-side is the literal's own type in SOURCE order, so it now asserts rest-before-head and deliberately does NOT pin the printer. RE-CONTROLLED after loosening (the discipline the loosening could have broken): with the old by-name lookup restored, permuted_literal_is_not_threaded still FAILS, so the negative half is still what discriminates.\n\n(7) The unit-test helper converted AlignedSlots -> Vec just to satisfy assert_eq!, allocating on the path whose point is that it no longer allocates; now compares as_deref() slices, so the inline capacity can change without touching the test.\n\n(8) My rewrite of resolve_projected_member's doc nested five lines about the RELATIONS' symbol-identity keying INSIDE the bullet that opens 'components matched by SHORT name' -- readable as describing that function, i.e. the opposite of what the bullet says. Lifted out into its own paragraph after both receiver bullets.\n\nNOT FLAGGED, both close calls, traced rather than assumed: the short-name -> SYMBOL-IDENTITY keying change (no acceptance regression is constructible -- the threaded list IS the list the built tuple type carries, so conformance compares those exact symbols, and where they fail to line up conformance fails on the same comparison); and the silent return on alignment failure, which looks like a CLAUDE.md 'loud error over silent skip' violation but is not one -- the error is surfaced by the relation, with a location, and the doc states the no-op must stay.\n\nFull workspace re-run after the fixes: 3295 passed / 0 failed.", at: "2026-07-21T17:11:24Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-797", author: "user", content: "Scope decision (session 2026-07-21): criterion-2's 'eager Store::retrieve deleted' clause is DEFERRED to WI-780 (dep edge added). Store::retrieve (persistence/mod.rs:161) backs the LIVE persistence_retrieve builtin realizing the still-declared QueryableStore.retrieve op (stdlib store.anthill:85) -- NOT the claimed 'no declared twin'. Clean reroute needs the store_registry->kb.extents move = write-seam. WI-797 delivered: resolver discrim mount (QueryPattern pushdown, lazy per-pump match), FULL RouteHandler deletion, loader FunctorOwnedByExtent + register_extent_owner ResidentCollision refusals.", at: "2026-07-21T18:45:24Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-780", author: "user", content: "Now ALSO carries the READ-side Store::retrieve retirement deferred from WI-797 (2026-07-21): delete Store::retrieve->Vec<TermId> + IndexedFileStore override + persistence_retrieve builtin + NotQueryable + wi202_retrieve_test, rerouting the declared QueryableStore.retrieve through ExtentSource::query as part of the store_registry->kb.extents move. Depends on WI-797.", at: "2026-07-21T18:45:27Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-797", description: some(value: "Wire the ExtentSource mount into resolution and load, per proposal 057 (docs/proposals/057-extent-read-seam.md) decomposition item 1 (the resolver/loader half; foundation = WI-796). BUILD: (1) DISCRIM MOUNT -- a store-owned functor (registered in kb.extents, WI-796) is mounted at its discrim functor node; resolver candidate-gathering (kb/resolve.rs step_init and the kb/mod.rs query_view path) reaching the mount delegates to the owning source query with the goal ground fields pushed down as a QueryPattern, yielding TAGGED candidates: Resident(RuleId) opened exactly as today, or Row(Value) entering the substitution via bind_value with no TermStore allocation. ONE candidate path -- no consumer branches on ownership (the WI-770 divergence lesson applied to retrieval). Lazy per-pump cursor advancement, NOT an eager drain. (2) RETIRE the two read-beside-discrim surfaces INTO query (057 stage R2): delete RouteHandler (kb/route.rs) and the eager Store::retrieve -> Vec<TermId>, both in-tree-only with no declared twin; the read registries fully merge into kb.extents. (3) LOADER read-side refusal -- a source-file fact (or a same-head bodied rule) whose functor is owned by a mounted source is a loud LoadError (owned by store X, seed through its own channel), the single-owner decision. ACCEPTANCE: a functor owned by a mounted, seeded InMemoryExtentSource RESOLVES end-to-end through SLD (a goal over it yields the seeded rows), interleaved correctly with resident rules for other functors; RouteHandler and the eager Store::retrieve are deleted with no caller remaining; a source-file fact for an owned functor is refused at load; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh. DEPENDS WI-796."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-796"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-21T18:45:30Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-801", description: some(value: "The typer and eval pivot the spread-vs-whole decision on DIFFERENT QUANTITIES at a Function[A,B,E] slot, and nothing checks the two against each other. WI-788's positional_arg_expectations (kb/typing.rs) reads A's COMPONENT COUNT to decide that N arguments are A's components spread across the call; eval's spread_eta_args and gather_closure_arg (eval/eval.rs) pivot on the CALLEE's OWN arity (params.len() / Pattern::binder_arity). They coincide exactly when the callback conforms to Function[A, ...], which a separate conformance check establishes. If that conformance ever admits a callback whose binder count differs from A's arity, the typer ACCEPTS the call and eval traps ArityMismatch -- the load-clean-then-trap class WI-782/791/792/788 exist to remove. The codebase already names this hazard and owns a mechanism for it: Pattern::binder_arity (kb/node_occurrence.rs ~859) is documented as 'ONE definition, deliberately shared by the two sides that must agree on it... a disagreement is not a cosmetic drift -- it is a program that typechecks and then traps at eval'. The new typer site is a THIRD party to that agreement and is not enrolled; WI-788 added a doc cross-link naming spread_eta_args as its dynamic twin, which is a marker, not a fix. FIX: a shared classify_function_application(param_ty, supplied) both sides call, or an equivalent single owner of the quantity. Found by /code-review on WI-788. ACCEPTANCE: one owner for the spread decision, called by typer and eval; a test constructs a callback whose binder count differs from A's arity and asserts a LOAD error rather than an eval trap; full workspace green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-788"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-21T20:00:56Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-801", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED with a NARROWER rule than this ticket's stated acceptance, deliberately. The ticket asked for a LOAD error when a callback's 'binder count differs from A's arity'. That is too broad: it would refuse arity ONE, which is the CANONICAL inhabitant of the declared type -- 'lambda t -> t._1' simply IS an '(Int64, Int64) -> Int64'. docs/kernel-language.md (the 'Function states no arity' note) says a 'Function[(A,B),R]' 'accepts either' a single-tuple-argument callback OR a two-parameter one, so the admitted set is exactly {1, |A|}. Implemented that. MEASUREMENT also found a SECOND defect the ticket does not name: the spread call form 'f(1,2)' trapped against an arity-1 callee ('ArityMismatch expected 1 got 2') even though the spec makes both call forms legal at the slot. Only the whole-tuple form worked in every cell. That one cannot be fixed in eval -- a gather needs A's component LABELS, erased at runtime -- so the TYPER now normalizes a spread call into its whole-A tuple, and eval's two existing adapters are unchanged. All 16 matrix cells (positional/named A x whole/spread callee x whole/spread call, lambda and operation) now load and evaluate. TWO EXISTING TESTS CHANGED STAGE, not invariant: wi784::nullary_thunk_called_with_an_argument and wi785::arity_mismatch_still_refuses_to_match both documented themselves as pinning a runtime-detection corner and now refuse at LOAD; wi784::a_callback_arity_the_typer_cannot_decide_still_fails_in_the_matcher was added so the eval-stage matcher guard keeps coverage via a rigid A. /code-review additionally found the new arity diagnostic was installed on 1 of 3 channels (op-arg only); let-annotation and op-return now route through the same renderer.", at: "2026-07-21T20:01:21Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-802", description: some(value: "The string literal \"anthill.prelude.Function\" is hand-compared at THREE independent sites in kb/typing.rs -- ~18226 (the cheap head gate before arrow_parts), ~19068 (inside arrow_parts itself), and ~19398 (function_spec_param_type, added by WI-788) -- with no shared predicate. A rename or namespace move of the stdlib sort fixes two and leaves the third matching nothing, and the failure mode of the third is SILENT: the Function-slot argument check simply stops firing, reverting to the no-check state WI-788 fixed, with no test failing on the recognizer itself. WI-790 set the precedent of giving the _N positional-label convention ONE owner (intern::positional_label / positional_label_index / is_positional_label_at) for exactly this reason. FIX: one is_function_spec(kb, base) -> bool predicate (or a cached Symbol / TypeHead predicate) that all three call. Found by /code-review on WI-788; both the reuse and altitude reviewers rated it low priority but flagged it independently. SMALL. ACCEPTANCE: one owner for the recognizer, all three sites call it; full workspace green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-788"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-21T21:27:56Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-773", description: some(value: "kb layer lacks a facts-only query accessor, so every facts-reader hand-rolls the bodied-candidate policy -- and three divergent policies now coexist in the workspace: silent `if !kb.is_fact(rid) { continue; }` (~20 sites: anthill-cli check.rs x4 / run.rs / prove.rs, anthill-smt-gen policy.rs:69, cache/key.rs, core op_info/typing/eq_derive), a compiled-out debug_assert (cpp-gen cpp_method_name, lib.rs ~4186), and WI-770's hard assert (query_realization_facts). kb exposes query_rules -- the BODIED-only filter (kb/mod.rs:3260) -- but no facts-only dual with a decided refusal policy. Surfaced by the WI-770 review's altitude angle; the concrete demonstration that helper-local refusal is too shallow: cpp-gen itself still has three unguarded realization readers (WI-771), and anthill-todo's collect_workitems (rustland/anthill-todo/src/main.rs:331) head-matches WorkItem rules unguarded, so a bodied WorkItem rule would list/claim/deliver as a phantom item. FIX DIRECTION: add a kb accessor with an explicit bodied-rule policy -- e.g. query_facts_view returning facts plus a LOUD channel (Err or diagnostic callback) for bodied candidates -- then migrate the hand-rolled sites (including anthill-todo:331 and the WI-770/WI-771/WI-772 sites) onto it; while designing, weigh Result-over-panic so CLI paths can render the refusal through their error channels (the WI-770 assert bypasses CppCodegenError: process abort exit 101 with no source span, vs run_codegen_cpp's rendered `error: {message}` + exit 1 at anthill-cli/src/main.rs:938-942). Also consider promoting the `head :- body` rule renderer into anthill-core (TermPrinter) -- the WI-770 review counted three hand-rolled copies (anthill-cli print_rule_results main.rs:1594 + print_query_results main.rs:1624, cpp-gen render_rule) that are divergent at birth on value-head handling. ACCEPTANCE: the accessor exists with a pinned bodied-rule-policy test; the enumerated hand-rolled sites read through it (or are individually justified in comments); cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-797"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-21T22:39:41Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-803", description: some(value: "Make destructuring bind by LABEL, then drop the ORDER interim from data-tuple subtyping. THIS IS THE REAL WI-788, which WI-788 itself did not fix. The pre-WI-788 spec had already located it: \"Permutation and width are subtyping rules for data tuples whose components are read by NAME ... (A data tuple read by DESTRUCTURING is also positional; aligning such a reader by name is a known open defect, tracked as WI-788).\" The RELATION was right; the READER is wrong. WI-788 changed the relation instead and rewrote that spec paragraph to match, which refused correct programs; WI-804 restored name-keyed width and recorded the remaining order requirement as an explicit INTERIM. WHY THE INTERIM HOLDS, measured both ways: a destructuring binder list reads by SLOT and COUNT, so it is unsound under ANY subtyping step, but the failure modes differ -- WIDTH changes the count so the match fails LOUDLY, while PERMUTATION keeps the count and swaps the VALUES, silently binding a component the typer typed from a different field (an operation declared -> Int64 returning a String on a clean load). So width is admitted and order is held. THE FIX: the typer already knows the expected named-tuple type where it types a tuple pattern, so it should record WHICH LABEL each binder takes, and the matcher should fetch by label instead of by slot. PLUMBING ALREADY EXISTS: Pattern::Tuple (kb/node_occurrence.rs ~853) already carries a \"named\" vector of (Symbol, Rc<NodeOccurrence>) pairs alongside \"positional\", and it is currently DEAD -- match_tuple_pattern (eval/pattern.rs ~147) debug_asserts it empty because no producer mints a labelled tuple sub-pattern. A typer that resolves labels would move the sub-patterns from \"positional\" into \"named\", and the matcher would gain a real by-label arm in place of that refusal. NOTE the existing warning at that site: a label rule must agree with field_access short-name lookup (eval/builtins.rs), not raw symbol identity. Untyped destructuring keeps the positional path. This also makes WIDTH safe for the destructuring reader (a by-label fetch does not care about the component count), retiring the loud-trap half too. ACCEPTANCE: the order-preserving scan in align_named_tuple_fields data arm becomes an unordered name-keyed lookup and the interim notes in kernel-language.md 4.5, proposal 004 rule 4a and CLAUDE.md are removed; the WI-788 repro -- operation ap taking f typed Function with A = (a: Int64, b: String) and B = Int64, body f applied to the permuted literal (b: \"ess\", a: 3), driven by ap applied to a two-binder lambda returning its first binder -- binds the first binder to the Int64 component and returns 3; the permuted_* tests in wi788_tuple_component_order_identity_test invert to expect success; the wi782 and wi488 tests WI-788 inverted are restored to their original assertions; full workspace green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-804"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-21T22:50:27Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-803", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED. Both halves control-tested independently: disabling the reader fails 8 tests, reverting the relation fails 9.\n\nRELATION: TupleAlign gained a third axis TupleOrder, exactly as the existing code comment prescribed (branch on mode.order(), never on mode == DATA). DATA=Free -- width from anywhere AND permutation; PARAM_LIST/EQUALITY stay Preserved. Free means FIRST match, which is field_access' own rule, so relation and reader stop disagreeing on a duplicate label.\n\nREADER: bind_and_label_pattern (ex extend_env_from_pattern) records each binder's component name into Pattern::Tuple.labels; match_tuple_pattern fetches via TupleComponents::by_label, one owner shared with field_access. Headline repro returns 3.\n\nDEVIATED FROM THE TICKET'S REPRESENTATION. It said move sub-patterns into the dead `named` half; that silently empties every positional-only reader (binder_arity, lambda_binder_slots, destructure_bindings, the walkers, the printer). Used a parallel `labels` sibling, STRUCTURAL rather than a RefCell annotation, so the compiler surfaced all six readers instead of letting them ignore it.\n\nMY 15 TARGETED TESTS PASSED WHILE THE CHANGE WAS BROKEN. A spread call (f(3,10) -- so foldLeft and every two-binder callback) gathers into a POSITIONAL carrier while the expected type is named, so a by-label read raised MatchFailed. Only the FULL suite caught it (wi784/wi793/wi794/wi605). Fixed with an is_name_keyed() gate: a positional carrier has no names, and reading it by slot is EXACT, not a fallback.\n\n/code-review FOUND 3 DEFECTS IN MY FIRST CUT, all fixed here: (a) by_label normalized the COMPONENT side but passed the RAW label to the _N reader -- asserting a premise on one branch and relying on its negation on the other; (b) NO double-cover guard, so duplicate component names served one component to two binders -- driven, an operation declared -> String returned Int(3); match_constructor_pattern has guarded exactly this since WI-445; (c) synthetic _1.._n labels routed into the by-label arm, which cannot resolve them against a name-keyed value. (a) and (c) are DEFENSIVE: the end-to-end fixtures I wrote for them were CONTROL-RUN and PASSED -- blind -- so they were deleted rather than kept for appearances, and the guards are pinned at the reader in wi803_by_label_reader, which does discriminate. (b)'s first fixture was also blind (it drove `-> p`, which the buggy matcher satisfies); only the SECOND binder observes the collision.\n\nKNOWN GAP, recorded at the causing site rather than spun off: pattern_to_term DROPS labels, because the reflect surface (entity tuple_pattern(elements: List[Pattern])) has nowhere to put them -- so a typed pattern round-tripped through a term silently reverts to slot reading, the WI-788 wrong answer. Instrumented: that arm is reached ZERO times across the workspace suite, for ALL tuple lowerings, so the hazard is real, has no live witness, an assert would never fire, and a reflect-surface change would ship with no coverage either.\n\nINVERTED, each kept beside the defect it came from: wi788's four permuted_* tests, wi782's permuted tuple parameter (restored to still_applies), wi488's reordered-component escape (restored to its pre-WI-788 assertion -- while it asserted the mismatch, the gate's by-name alignment had NO coverage at all), and wi800's two. A speculative test I added asserting unification still refuses a permutation was REMOVED, not weakened: List[T = ...] goes through subtyping, so it proved nothing; EQUALITY is pinned directly at the relation instead.\n\nWI-805's program is now REFUSED as a side effect -- recorded, not claimed. Its own fix (refuse the duplicate where the tuple is BUILT) is still open, and a same-typed duplicate still loads.\n\nDOCS reconciled: kernel-language.md 4.5 / Arrow types / \"Destructuring binds by LABEL\", proposal 004 rule 4a, CLAUDE.md. /simplify also caught that I added a third axis while leaving SIX comments saying \"two axes\" plus a grid test blind to the new axis -- the exact drift WI-799's own comment names.\n\nFull workspace 3341 passed / 0 failed via scripts/test.sh. Committed 5a0970ad.", at: "2026-07-21T22:50:53Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-805", description: some(value: "A DUPLICATE-labelled tuple literal loads clean and yields a WRONG-TYPED value. MEASURED end-to-end: `operation take(t: (b: Int64, a: String)) -> String = t.a` with `operation drive() -> String = take((a: 1, b: 2, a: \"ess\"))` loads with ZERO errors and drive() returns Int(1) from an operation declared -> String. (Pinned live by duplicate_label_alignment_resumes_after_the_previous_match in wi800_tuple_threading_alignment_test, which RECORDS the wrong answer rather than endorsing it.) TWO things meet. (1) NOTHING REFUSES THE DUPLICATE: a tuple literal may carry the same label twice, typing as (a: Int64, b: Int64, a: String). Spec 4.5 does not state that component names must be distinct -- but the spec's own projection rule already gives the reason it should: `x.(a, a)` and the rename collision `x.(k: f1, k: f2)` are load errors because 'a duplicate-key tuple would silently drop the later column' (kernel-language.md, Distinct keys), and a call's named arguments likewise reject a 'duplicated label'. The one construct that can MINT such a tuple is the one place the rule is missing. (2) THE RELATION AND THE READER THEN PICK DIFFERENT COMPONENTS: align_named_tuple_slots (kb/typing.rs) is name-keyed and order-PRESERVING -- the scan resumes AFTER the previous match -- so expected `a`, searched after `b` matched slot 1, takes slot 2 (the String) and the program conforms; field_access (eval/builtins.rs) reads by name and takes the FIRST `a` (the Int64). Same wrong-typed-value class as WI-788, reached through LABELS rather than through order. FIX DIRECTION: refuse a duplicate label where a named tuple is BUILT -- the literal, and the tuple TYPE -- which makes the disagreement UNREACHABLE rather than arbitrating it. Teaching both sides to pick the same component is NOT sufficient: a component reachable by neither its name nor its position is still a component nothing can read, which is precisely what the spec's Distinct-keys rule refuses elsewhere. Found while delivering WI-800, by probing whether its two candidate correspondences (first-match by name vs the cursor scan) could disagree on a real program -- they can, and the program that shows it is accepted. ACCEPTANCE: the program above is REFUSED at load with a located diagnostic naming the duplicated label; spec 4.5 states the distinctness rule alongside the existing projection/named-argument ones; the wi800 test is updated to assert refusal; full workspace green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-21T23:49:59Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-805", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED, commit 4771ac56. Full workspace 3355 passed / 0 failed.\n\nTHREE PRODUCERS, NOT TWO — the ticket's premise was incomplete. It said to refuse the duplicate at 'the literal, and the tuple TYPE'. Both are done, at parse (check_tuple_label_unique, one owner, located at the offending component). But a `...rest: R` VARIADIC CAPTURE folds a call's leftover NAMED ARGUMENTS into a named tuple, and its labels are written in source yet never as a tuple, so the parse guard cannot see them. Measured live WITH the parse guard in place: `operation cap[R](x: Int64, ...rest: R) -> R = rest` with `cap(1, a: 2, a: \\\"ess\\\").a` loaded CLEAN and returned Int(2), having built (a: Int64, a: String) — the exact type the parse guard forbids writing. `let (p, q) = cap(1, a: 2, a: 3)` loaded clean and raised MatchFailed at eval. Closed in normalize_variadic_capture (kb/typing.rs), which needed threading a TypeError out of an Option-returning normalizer.\n\nHOW IT WAS FOUND, since this is the reusable part: the /code-review altitude agent refused to accept 'literal + type' as exhaustive and enumerated named_tuple_value's 11 callers instead. Two take user-written labels unguarded — the capture (overlooked) and arrow params (deliberate). I had written 'the one place the rule was missing' in three docs before that census. A producer list that FEELS exhaustive is the thing to verify, not assert.\n\nWI-803'S AGREEMENT WAS NOT ENOUGH, which is the ticket's own point and worth restating: making both readers take the FIRST match made the differently-typed spelling a visible mismatch, so the ticket's headline program was already refused when I started. The SAME-TYPED spelling (both a's Int64) still loaded clean and returned Int(1). Agreeing which component to read leaves the unread one unread.\n\nARROW PARAMETER LISTS DELIBERATELY EXEMPT, and my first rationale was FALSE. I wrote 'a repeated binder name leaves no parameter unreachable' in three places; measured, `operation two(a: Int64, a: Int64) -> Int64 = a` loads and two(1,2) returns Int(2) — the body reads the SECOND, so the first IS unreachable, and note that is the OPPOSITE occurrence from the one a tuple reader takes. The exemption still holds, for a different reason: params are applied POSITIONALLY, so the shadowed one's declared type is still checked against an argument at every call. Nothing silently unchecked. Corrected in all three places. Entity fields have the identical wart (`entity mk(a: Int64, a: Int64)` loads) — same class, out of scope, recorded not spun off.\n\nCONTROL-TESTED EACH GUARD SEPARATELY: disabling the literal check fails 4 tests, the type check 3, the capture check 2. Reporting at the FIRST occurrence instead of the second fails only the location test, so the span assertion discriminates.\n\nTHE FULL SUITE CAUGHT 3 FIXTURES MY TARGETED TESTS COULD NOT — again. wi763's written keep spec IS a tuple type (via denoted_field_decl) so it is now refused a stage earlier; two wi803 fixtures became unspellable. Each retargeted or tombstoned with what it costs recorded AT the site: keep_spec_projections' duplicate check now has NO live witness (instrumented with a panic across the full suite — never fired) and is kept as a backstop for derived schemas; match_tuple_pattern's double-cover guard loses the end-to-end witness the capture route was silently giving it, and still answers for distinct QUALIFIED labels sharing a last segment. TWO COMMENTS I WROTE claiming 'no source-written type reaches here' were FALSIFIED by the review and corrected — left alone they would have justified deleting a live guard two tickets later.\n\nSIDE EFFECT WORTH KNOWING: a duplicate label was the ONLY way a source program could distinguish first-match from resume-after-the-previous-match, so WI-803's choice between them is no longer observable from source. Noted on TupleOrder::Free; still load-bearing for derived labels.\n\n/simplify: a parallel written_labels vector pushed in LOCKSTEP with the authoritative collection (the silent-skip shape CLAUDE.md names) became a `seen` iterator over what each caller already builds; parse_errs/parses_clean lifted into tests/common (5 copies); two subsumed tests tombstoned, keeping wi763's because it reaches the type guard through a different grammar production. Efficiency review measured the corpus (max tuple arity 9, ~3177 comparisons workspace-wide) and confirmed no change warranted.\n\nDocs reconciled: kernel-language.md 4.5 + the projection Distinct-keys cross-reference, proposal 004 rule 8, CLAUDE.md.", at: "2026-07-21T23:50:31Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-807", description: some(value: "Guard the prove.rs discharge-path ProofRecord readers -- the WI-806 review soundness angle found the \"bodied ProofRecord refused loudly\" property does NOT hold where proofs are actually discharged. collect_proof_records (prove.rs:334) and find_proof_body_term (prove.rs:1119) head-match EVERY rid via kb.rule_head(rid) with NO is_fact filter, so a bodied ProofRecord rule is head-matched as a phantom discharge target / body source (the WI-773 class) and a value-fact head would panic (unreachable today -- ProofRecord is invariantly Term-carried -- but non-uniform vs the WI-806 validation/cite readers). These ARE the real discharge worklist (run_prove -> discharge_loaded_kb, and the discharge half of anthill check). find_proof_body_term has NO RuleId dependency -> migrates cleanly to read_facts(functor, empty, Refuse) (narrow to Term, existing rule-qn match, None on Err). collect_proof_records is WI-780-adjacent: read_proof_record returns Some(ProofRec{rid: Some(rid), ..}) so ProofRec carries the RuleId (used for retract/addressing downstream), which the RuleId-free read_facts cannot supply -- add a local is_fact filter + carrier-safe head read (fact_head_term) now, or route through the WI-780 store-addressed reader when it lands. ACCEPTANCE: neither reader head-matches a bodied ProofRecord rule or panics on a value head; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-773"]), status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-806", description: some(value: "Migrate anthill-cli realization/reflect facts-only readers onto the WI-773 read_facts accessor, fixing a latent value-fact-head panic. Five check.rs/prove.rs sites read a reflect/realization fact head via kb.rule_head(rid), which PANICS on a Value::Node/Entity head (the class WI-366/WI-663 hardened elsewhere), behind a local is_fact skip (one of the three divergent bodied-rule policies WI-773 centralizes): check.rs:90 run_check_with (ProofRecord, Result<_,i32> channel), check.rs:479 proof_record_exists (ProofRecord, bool), check.rs:568 check_scope_axiom_witness induction branch (SortInfo, CheckStatus), prove.rs:1449 cite_status (ProofRecord, CiteStatus enum), prove.rs:1585 implicit_cites_for (ProofRecord, bare Vec). Migrate each to read_facts(functor, empty selection, Refuse): rows are carrier-neutral Values (narrow Value::Term to the head TermId the existing extraction consumes), and a bodied rule is refused through one channel (Err(1) for run_check_with -- a checker must not skip a record it cannot read into a green result; CheckStatus::Failed for the axiom witness; conservative degrade false/NotFound/empty for the bool/status/Vec returns). The already-carrier-safe fact_head_named_args sites (check.rs:601 requires-branch, run.rs:66 find_main_providers) STAY: they read SortRequiresInfo which GENUINELY carries value heads (WI-366), so read_facts Value::Term extraction would warn spuriously on every denoted-spec load. OUT OF SCOPE (see WI-807): the prove.rs discharge-path readers, and the core op_info/eq_derive/typing.rs readers (already carrier-safe, compiler-internal tables, no bodied rules, Result-friction on hot paths for ~zero value; two typing.rs index-builders store the RuleId as payload). ACCEPTANCE: the five sites read through read_facts; a bodied ProofRecord/SortInfo rule is refused (not silently skipped or panicked); cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-773"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-21T23:44:48Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-808", description: some(value: "An ENTITY's field names must be DISTINCT. MEASURED with WI-805's tuple guards in place: `sort S entity mk(a: Int64, a: Int64) end` with `operation drive() -> Int64 = mk(1, 2).a` loads with ZERO errors and returns Int(1), the second `a` permanently unaddressable by name. Recorded as out of scope while delivering WI-805 and fixed here on request. THE HARM IS NARROWER THAN THE TUPLE CASE and the fix must say so rather than overstate it: a tuple component under a repeated name is unreachable ENTIRELY (by name AND by position, the relation being name-keyed) so its declared type is never checked against anything, whereas an entity's second field is still BUILT and READ positionally -- measured, `mk(1, 2)` type-checks both slots against their declarations (`type mismatch in mk.b (entity-field)`) and `case mk(p, q) -> q` reads the second. What it loses is its ACCESS PATH: `x.f`, a named argument and a rule pattern all resolve a field name to the FIRST match. Refused anyway because a field name IS the field's public interface, so a name identifying two fields addresses neither -- the same principle the spec states for a projection's result keys and a call's named arguments. FIX: check at convert_entity through WI-805's owner, generalized to take a per-kind rationale (check_label_unique with what/why). Field names scope PER ENTITY -- sibling entities in one sort may each declare `a`, the ordinary variant shape. NOT extended to operation/arrow parameters, per WI-805's settled reasoning (applied positionally, names are local binders not a type's interface). ACCEPTANCE: the program above is refused at load with a located diagnostic naming the repeated field and pointing at the SECOND occurrence; sibling entities sharing a field name still load; spec 6.3 states the rule alongside 4.5's tuple one; full workspace green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-22T04:33:50Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-808", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. Full workspace 3361 passed / 0 failed.\n\nGuard at convert_entity, through WI-805's owner generalized to check_label_unique(what, why, seen, sym, at) — the tuple sites' message text is byte-preserved (old what=\"tuple literal\" + hardcoded \" component label\" == new what=\"tuple literal component label\"), which is why WI-805's 11 tests pass untouched. Two module consts carry the per-kind rationale.\n\nWHY A SEPARATE RATIONALE STRING RATHER THAN ONE MESSAGE — this is the part worth keeping. The two rules bite for DIFFERENT reasons and a reader who hits one must not be handed the other's. A tuple component under a repeated name is unreachable ENTIRELY, so its type is never checked. An entity's second field is still built and read POSITIONALLY. Driven, not inferred: `case mk(p, q) -> q` returns the SECOND field (Ok(Str(\"ess\")); eval/pattern.rs indexes positional sub-patterns by slot), and `mk(1, 2)` against `mk(a: Int64, b: String)` yields `type mismatch in mk.b (entity-field)`. So the message's claim 'remains reachable positionally, which is why this is not a silently unchecked value' is TRUE, and the rule is justified on the narrower ground that a field name is the field's public interface.\n\nCONTROL-TESTED: disabling the entity check fails 3 of the 6 tests; the other 3 are the must-not-catch controls (distinct fields still load AND read by name end-to-end, sibling entities in one sort may each declare `a`, a field named like its own entity). Reporting at the declaration instead of the second field's name node would fail only the span test.\n\nOVER-FIRE WAS THE REAL RISK AND IT IS DEFUSED, verified rather than assumed. grammar.js:1524 `_tuple_type_arg` admits `field_decl` INSIDE tuple types and arrow parameter lists, so a field whose type is `(x: Int64, y: Int64)` nests field_decls under the entity. It is safe only because children_by_kind uses node.named_children() — SHALLOW — and node-types.json limits entity_declaration's direct named children to field_decl/meta_block/visibility. Probed: `mk(a: (x: Int64, y: Int64), b: (x: Int64, y: Int64))`, `mk(f: (x: Int64) -> Int64, g: (x: Int64) -> Int64)` and `mk(x: (x: Int64, y: Int64))` all parse clean.\n\nPRODUCER COVERAGE ENUMERATED, applying WI-805's lesson rather than assuming: parse::ir::Entity is built at exactly ONE site (convert_entity) and FieldDecl at exactly one (convert_field_decl, one caller — the guarded loop). kb entity_fields is written only via register_entity_fields, whose callers are one user-source path (register_entity_field_names_scan, downstream of convert_entity) plus EIGHT synthetic kernel entities with hardcoded field vectors the parse guard cannot see. Those are distinct today; a note now sits on register_entity_fields saying the invariant is expected there and why no check lives at that layer (no span, no error channel). Reflect/persistence/codegen/body_specialize are readers only.\n\nDEAD GRAMMAR DECOY, recorded not fixed: grammar.js:393 defines a SECOND entity production named `constructor` with its own commaSep1(field_decl). It is unreferenced anywhere in grammar.js and absent from node-types.json, so it is not a bypass today — but it is exactly the shape that becomes a second unguarded producer if revived.\n\nADJACENT HOLES FOUND WHILE MEASURING, NOT FIXED (different defect class — entity constructor ARGUMENT checking, not field declaration well-formedness). All measured live: (1) a DUPLICATE named argument to an entity constructor is not rejected even when the fields are distinct — `entity mk(a: Int64, b: Int64)` with `mk(a: 1, a: 2)` loads CLEAN, `.a` = 1, and `.b` raises Internal(\"field_access: entity has no field 'b'\") at RUNTIME; the identical duplicate against an OPERATION is correctly refused ('binds a parameter already given'), so the check exists and entity construction does not use it. (2) an UNKNOWN named argument `mk(a: 1, b: 2, zzz: 3)` loads clean. (3) an omitted field `mk(a: 1)` loads clean — this one is BY DESIGN (WI-716 omitted-field semantics: value position denotes none(), pattern position matches anything), so it is not a defect. (1) and (2) look like real gaps and want their own ticket; they were left alone because fixing argument checking is a wider blast radius than the declaration rule asked for.\n\n/code-review found three COMMENT defects, all fixed: a stale claim in check_label_unique's own doc still saying entity duplicates 'load today' (falsified by this very change), a misplaced paragraph that triplicated the rationale, and the WI-805 'DELIBERATELY SEPARATE' note whose stated cost ('a second string knob') this change half-paid — now narrowed to the Span-vs-Node reason, which still holds.\n\nDocs: kernel-language.md 6.3 states the rule and the narrower-harm distinction alongside 4.5's tuple one; CLAUDE.md records the per-entity scoping.", at: "2026-07-22T04:34:22Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-809", description: some(value: "A named-argument list may not REPEAT A LABEL, at any callee. MEASURED on a WELL-FORMED entity (distinct fields, so WI-806's declaration rule does not apply): `entity mk(a: Int64, b: Int64)` with `mk(a: 1, a: 2).a` loads with ZERO errors and returns Int(1). The value built has TWO `a` fields and NO `b` -- `.a` reads the first, a positional pattern sees the second `a` occupying b's slot, and `.b` raises Internal(\"field_access: entity has no field 'b'\") at RUN TIME. So one label written twice silently fills an unrelated field and leaves the intended one unbound. THE CHECK ALREADY EXISTED AND ENTITIES DID NOT USE IT: the identical spelling against an OPERATION was refused by named_arg_coverage_errors ('binds a parameter already given'); entity construction, facts and rule-body atoms never route through it. FIX AT THE SYNTAX LAYER, not by teaching the typer a fourth callee shape: whether one list repeats a label needs NO type information, so one rule covers every callee at once (check_label_unique with a NAMED_ARGS_DISTINCT rationale). named_arg_coverage_errors KEEPS both of its own reasons, neither syntactic -- an UNKNOWN label, and one colliding with a parameter already filled POSITIONALLY (`f(3, acc: 10)`, WI-783). ACCEPTANCE: the program above is refused at parse with a located diagnostic naming the label; the same refusal for a fact, a rule-body atom, an operation call and a dot call; distinct labels still construct and read; spec states the rule alongside 4.5 and 6.3; full workspace green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-22T05:07:13Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-809", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. Full workspace 3372 passed / 0 failed.\n\nFOUR PRODUCTIONS, NOT TWO — and the /code-review pass is what found the other two, for the THIRD ticket running. My first cut patched push_fn_term and push_dot_method_call and I wrote that it 'covers every callee at once'. The `named_arg` grammar node actually reaches THREE productions (fn_term, tuple_literal, proof_strategy) plus the pattern twin `named_pattern_field`:\n\n(a) PROOF STRATEGY args were uncovered and the gap was self-undermining: `by z3(logic: \\\"LRA\\\", logic: \\\"QF_NRA\\\")` parsed CLEAN while the NESTED spelling `z3(tactic: smt(logic: .., logic: ..))` was refused — because the nested value routes through convert_term into push_fn_term. prove.rs reads these LAST-WINS and pushes BOTH into the proof-cache canon, so the duplicate silently changed what was proved AND what was cached. Driven both ways.\n\n(b) CONSTRUCTOR PATTERNS: I had excluded them claiming they were 'already loud at run time' via the WI-445 double-cover guard. I MEASURED THAT ON THE WRONG FIXTURE. It raises MatchFailed only with NO following case; with a fallback — the common shape — the malformed arm just never matches: `case mk(a: p, a: q) -> 111` under `case _ -> 999` loaded clean and returned 999. A SILENTLY DEAD ARM, the exact failure mode this codebase ranks below a loud error. My own test file asserted the false claim.\n\nI FABRICATED A JUSTIFICATION AND IT WAS CAUGHT. When WI-807 subsumed WI-805's variadic-capture guard, I kept the guard and justified it by saying a WI-722 compile-time macro could synthesize an Expr::Apply with named args and bypass the parser. FALSE, verified in code: reflect_make_apply and reflect_make_fn both hardcode an empty named-arg list, and try_expand_macro DECLINES any template carrying one; the only synthesizer that does carry them (substitute_to_occurrence) copies keys from a source-parsed Term::Fn. The guard is retained as defence-in-depth with NO KNOWN REACHABLE PATH, and now says exactly that. A rationale that sounds reachable is worse than none — it stops the next reader re-checking. Same class of error as the WI-806 arrow-parameter rationale I got wrong yesterday.\n\nALSO: I referenced a function `push_dot_apply` in FOUR places (CLAUDE.md, typing.rs, two test sites). It does not exist — the function is push_dot_method_call. Corrected.\n\nWHY THE SYNTAX LAYER, and the split that survives it: repetition WITHIN one list needs no type information, so one rule covers every callee. named_arg_coverage_errors keeps both of ITS reasons, neither decidable from the list alone — an UNKNOWN label, and one re-binding a parameter already filled POSITIONALLY. Verified the positional case is still reachable on the OPERATION path too (`take(3, a: 10)` -> 'binds a parameter already given'), not just WI-783's function-value twin, so the typer check is not orphaned.\n\nOVER-FIRE CHECKED HARDEST ON RULE BODIES, since `mk(a: ?x, a: ?y)` could plausibly have meant 'unify this field with two patterns' — a real logic-programming reading. It never did: BuildFrame::FnTerm pushes each named slot verbatim into Term::Fn.named_args, no conjunction anywhere, so such an atom could only ever match an equally malformed fact. The intended meaning stays expressible as two goals. Per-list scoping verified: separate calls and nested calls may reuse a label.\n\nCONTROL-TESTED per site: disabling both call-site guards fails 5 tests; disabling ONLY push_fn_term leaves the dot-call test passing (proving that second site is independently witnessed — it was the one that would have stayed silently open); disabling the two new guards fails exactly their 2 tests.\n\nDocs: kernel-language.md states the rule in the named-arguments section and narrows the function-value note (an unknown label is still a load error, a duplicate is refused earlier); CLAUDE.md records the syntax-vs-semantic split.", at: "2026-07-22T05:07:43Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-778", description: some(value: "An ENTITY field or OPERATION parameter with a missing name or type is SILENTLY accepted, interning the empty string as a field name. Tree-sitter recovers the absent token as a ZERO-WIDTH node which is neither ERROR nor MISSING, so Node::has_error() is false, collect_syntax_errors (rustland/anthill-core/src/parse/mod.rs, the '!has_error() && !is_missing()' prune) never descends, and the converter's 'self.intern(self.text(n))' runs on empty text with no guard. MEASURED SILENT (parses clean, zero diagnostics): 'entity e(: Int64)', 'entity e(a: )', 'operation f(: Int64) -> Int64'. The resulting field label is real, not dropped -- under the tuple-type instance of the same bug the empty label reached a type mismatch rendered as 'got (: Int64)'. This directly violates CLAUDE.md's 'prefer a loud error over a silent skip'. SCOPE, measured: the TUPLE TYPE instance of this class is already FIXED -- WI-766 unified the arrow parameter list and tuple_type into one production, and '(: Int64)', '(a: )', '(a: Int64,)' and '(Int64,)' are all loud parse errors now (the last two were silently recovering a PHANTOM zero-width component into a 2-tuple before, on both the pre-WI-766 grammar and the rejected conflict-based variant). What remains is the entity-constructor and operation-parameter walkers, which are separate code paths and were never touched. Same pathology WI-440 fixed for '@ {}' -- see the _effect_set comment in grammar.js describing 'error-recovered into a zero-width simple_type ... with the annotation silently dropped'. PREFER a producer-side guard (a loud check on empty field name/type text in parse/convert.rs) over per-consumer checks, so every walker inherits it; note there are at least three walkers (entity constructor, operation params, arrow params). ACCEPTANCE: each measured-silent case above is a loud located error naming the missing part; a regression test covers entity and operation sites and asserts the MESSAGE (is_err alone would pass on any syntax error); full workspace green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-22T06:42:33Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-778", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED at 971a5aed, with two corrections to this ticket. (1) THE DIAGNOSIS WAS WRONG: this ticket states Node::has_error() is false so the '!has_error() && !is_missing()' prune never descends. Measured, has_error() is TRUE on the zero-width node and on every ancestor -- the walk always reached it and dropped it at the BOTTOM, in the 'interior node that merely contains an error -- descend' arm, which iterated its zero children and vanished. The prune is innocent. (2) FIXED AT THE WALK, NOT the producer-side guard in parse/convert.rs this ticket prefers: one arm in collect_syntax_errors keyed on byte_range().is_empty() closes the hole before conversion, so all ~31 intern(text(n)) sites inherit it. Also: this ticket lists THREE producers, but only TWO are in this class -- an arrow param's hole builds a real ERROR node and was already loud via WI-766 -- and measurement found TEN silent spellings, not three. NOTE FOR FUTURE READERS: this ticket's phrase 'Same pathology WI-440 fixed for @ {}' is accurate but easy to misread. WI-440 fixed it by making '@ {}' LEGAL (commaSep, not commaSep1, so the empty row parses), NOT by making it loud. The first implementation misread it as 'still loud' and asserted 'operation f(a: Int64) -> Int64 @ {}' is_err -- which passes VACUOUSLY, because '@ ...' is ungrammatical after an operation return type at all ('@ {Error}' and '@ Error' fail identically, 'effects {}' is clean). Caught in review and replaced with a CLEAN assertion in arrow-type position.", at: "2026-07-22T06:42:36Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-776", description: some(value: "A WRITTEN one-component tuple type never 1-collapses, but every COMPUTED one-column result always does -- so the natural spelling of a one-column result is unsatisfiable. SITE: collapse_schema (rustland/anthill-core/src/kb/typing.rs, the arm '1 => columns[0].1.clone()', which drops the column NAME). Since WI-766 made '(a: A)' writable the two sides can now disagree, and every disagreement is a rejection. THREE MEASURED, all loud: (1) 'operation as_tuple() -> (a: Int64) = resid()' where resid() -> Without[T = (a: Int64, b: String), Drop = (b: String)] gives 'expected (a: Int64), got Int64'; (2) the same for a one-entry Project keep spec: 'expected (who: String), got String'; (3) 'operation who() -> Relation[T = (name: String)] = Person.adult' over a one-column rule gives 'expected Relation[T = (name: String)], got Relation[T = String, E = ...]'. Consequence beyond ergonomics: Concat and Without stop being inverses at arity 1 -- Concat[(a: Int64), (b: String)] builds the 2-tuple, but dropping b back off yields the bare element, not (a: Int64). NOT a soundness hole (every rejection is loud and located) but the surface now admits spellings that are guaranteed wrong at every use, with no diagnostic explaining WHY. DECIDE which way to make them agree: either a one-column computed schema keeps its column and stops collapsing (check what depends on the collapse first -- wi763_single_component_keep_spec_reduces_and_collapses asserts it, and relation drain/materialize may too), or the collapse stays and the typer explains it at the mismatch ('a one-column schema collapses to its element type; write A, not (a: A)'). docs/kernel-language.md now records the disagreement in the 1-collapse section; whichever way this lands, that text and the wi763 test must follow. ACCEPTANCE: the two sides agree, or the mismatch names the collapse; full workspace green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-22T07:41:36Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-776", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED at 3a5aabef, as the \"explain it\" branch: the collapse STAYS and the mismatch names it. THE DECISION RECORD MATTERS MORE THAN THE CODE HERE, because the first cut rejected the other branch for a FALSE reason and wrote it into docs/kernel-language.md. The claim was that a one-column schema keeping its column would be unconstructible. It would not: §4.5 says such a type's inhabitants arrive by WIDTH SUBTYPING from a wider tuple, and measured, 'narrow() -> (a: Int64) = wide()' over 'wide() -> (a: Int64, b: String)' loads CLEAN in a return position (negative control: '-> (a: String)' is correctly rejected). materialize_solution is equally capable -- it builds the row as (name, value) pairs and discards the name at arity one. So Option A was IMPLEMENTABLE; it was declined, not impossible. The real ground, now recorded in the spec: the collapse is a PAIRED type-and-value convention whose value half §6.8 fixes at the TERM level ('x.(f)' -> 'x.f'; a single RENAME collapses and drops the label, WI-639), so keeping the column in the SCHEMA alone desynchronizes type from term across projection, relation drain and Without/Project -- a breaking change to a specified rule. NOTE FOR WHOEVER REVISITS THIS: the ticket's framing invites the false reading. It says the natural spelling of a one-column result is 'unsatisfiable', which is true only of COMPUTED results; '(a: A)' itself is perfectly inhabited from a wider tuple, in any position. STILL OPEN as a separate concern, asserted in wi776_one_collapse_diagnostic_test so it is not believed fixed: Concat and Without are not inverses at arity one. The collapse DESTROYS the column name, so 'Concat[A = Without[T = (a: Int64, b: String), Drop = (b: String)], B = (c: Bool)]' stalls unreduced as 'Concat[A = Int64, B = (c: Bool)]' and nothing downstream can supply the lost 'a'. No diagnostic can fix that; closing it means moving the type and value halves of the collapse together.", at: "2026-07-22T07:41:38Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-765", description: some(value: "Reject a DUPLICATE-KEY named tuple TYPE language-wide. Today `operation f() -> (a: Int64, a: String)` LOADS CLEAN -- `convert_tuple_type` pushes both components into its `named` vec and `make_named_tuple_type` builds two fields with the same symbol, so the type carries two columns named `a` that no field lookup (WI-638 mode 3, dot dispatch, `projection_columns`) can answer unambiguously. MEASURED, not assumed: probed during WI-763 and it produced zero diagnostics. WI-763 closed the ONE surface it made newly reachable -- a written `Project` keep spec `Keep = (a: \"name\", a: \"age\")`, now loud in `keep_spec_projections` (typing.rs, search 'names the result key') -- but that is a keep-spec-local guard, not the general rule. The general rule belongs at the PRODUCER: `convert_tuple_type` (parse/convert.rs), where the all-or-nothing positional/named check already lives, so every consumer of a named-tuple type inherits it. NOTE the positional path desugars to `_1.._N` and cannot collide, so only the explicitly-named path needs the check. SCOPE RISK to measure FIRST: a duplicate-key tuple may already exist in stdlib/ or examples/ and would start failing -- run the full workspace before assuming this is additive. Consider whether the same check is owed to entity constructor field lists (`entity e(a: Int64, a: String)`) and arrow param lists, which are separate walkers. ACCEPTANCE: a duplicate-key named tuple type is a loud located error naming the repeated key; keep-spec duplicates keep their more specific message; full workspace green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-763"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-22T09:01:14Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-765", author: "user", content: "Already delivered by WI-805, which put the rule at exactly the producer this ticket named (convert_tuple_type -> check_label_unique, parse/convert.rs). Verified by driving the ticket's own headline case: 'operation take(t: (a: Int64, a: String)) -> String' is refused at PARSE with 'duplicate tuple type component label `a`', located at the second `a`. The two 'consider whether' extensions are also settled: entity field lists covered by WI-808, named-arg lists by WI-809, arrow parameter lists deliberately EXCLUDED with a measured rationale (params apply positionally, so a repeated binder leaves nothing unreachable and its type is still checked at every call). Keep-spec duplicates keep their more specific message (WI-763). SCOPE RISK the ticket flagged did not materialize: no duplicate-key tuple existed in stdlib/ or examples/ -- full workspace green, 3395 tests. GAP CLOSED HERE: acceptance said 'loud LOCATED error', but only the LITERAL side was span-pinned; the type side asserted the message alone, which a diagnostic reported at the tuple's '(' or at the FIRST label would satisfy just as well. Added a span-pinned test and verified it is load-bearing by a positive control -- reporting at the component instead of its name node makes it fail (99..108 vs 99..100).", at: "2026-07-22T09:01:28Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-771", description: some(value: "cpp-gen sibling realization readers head-match with no bodied-rule refusal: WI-770 closed the guard-skip trap for the five functors routed through query_realization_facts, but three sibling readers in the SAME crate read realization heads via kb.rules_by_functor + kb.rule_head with NO is_fact guard: CarrierTable::from_kb reading anthill.realization.Implementation (rustland/anthill-cpp-gen/src/lib.rs:129), OpImplTable::from_kb reading anthill.realization.OperationImpl (lib.rs:254), and generated_targets reading anthill.realization.Generated (lib.rs:1542). A BODIED rule for any of these is silently head-matched with its guard skipped: a guarded Implementation binds carrier/host_type/binding-key unconditionally (wrong C++ types AND includes emitted), a guarded OperationImpl enrolls the operation body for emission unconditionally, and a guarded Generated emits the artifact / selects the profile overlay unconditionally (generated_targets feeds CLI profile selection at anthill-cli/src/main.rs:917 and the target-declaration check at main.rs:1003). Surfaced by the WI-770 review -- three independent finder angles converged on the same three sites; all are the exact silent-wrong-answer class WI-770's assert now refuses one screen away, which users will read as 'guards work here'. FIX DIRECTION: same refusal as WI-770 (assert kb.is_fact naming the rule via render_rule), or route through the WI-773 facts-only accessor if that lands first. ACCEPTANCE: a bodied Implementation / OperationImpl / Generated rule in a test KB is refused loudly, never silently head-matched; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-773"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-22T07:53:07Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-813", description: some(value: "CLI anthill.geometry emit swallows real codegen errors via .ok()/if let Ok -- distinguish empty-namespace from real failure. run_codegen_cpp (anthill-cli/src/main.rs:985, 'if let Ok(geometry_header) = emit_namespace_header_with_profile(kb, anthill.geometry, profile)') and run_codegen_cpp_project (main.rs:1049, '.ok()') treat the OPTIONAL anthill.geometry header as best-effort and swallow ALL errors so an absent/empty geometry namespace is skipped. But emit_namespace_header_in OVERLOADS Err for TWO cases: (a) 'no entities/sums/traits/consts to emit under namespace' (the benign case .ok() intends) AND (b) a REAL codegen failure -- an unlowerable type, and post-WI-810 a bodied TypeMapping/IncludeMapping/NamingConvention refusal via CppCodegenError. The .ok() cannot distinguish, so a real geometry codegen error is silently dropped (missing anthill_geometry.hpp + exit 0), violating the repo 'loud error over silent skip' rule. PRE-EXISTING (already swallowed unlowerable-type errors); WI-810 WIDENS it by one kind (a bodied-rule refusal that used to be an assert-abort/panic -- unswallowable -- now a graceful Err). NARROW reachability: the main-namespace emit runs first and the blanket read_facts(Refuse) usually fails there first (IncludeMapping/TypeMapping read unconditionally), shadowing the geometry slip; the live window is a bodied NamingConvention reached ONLY via geometry body-synthesis. FIX: distinguish 'empty namespace' (benign skip) from 'real error' (surface loudly, exit 1) -- e.g. give emit_namespace_header an Ok(Option<String>) empty-vs-content signal, or a CppCodegenError kind/predicate the CLI matches; then the two geometry sites propagate real errors and swallow only the empty case. Found by WI-810 /code-review."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-810", description: some(value: "Migrate cpp-gen's query_realization_facts facts-only callers off the WI-770 assert onto read_facts(Refuse) -- completing the WI-771 migration for the type-lowering-path readers. query_realization_facts (rustland/anthill-cpp-gen/src/lib.rs) still head-matches via kb.query_view + kb.rule_head behind the WI-770 assert!(is_fact) (abort exit 101). WI-771 migrated the three siblings (Implementation/OperationImpl/Generated) + the two OperationInfo readers onto kb.read_facts(functor, selection, BodiedRulePolicy::Refuse) (values-first, CppCodegenError exit 1), but LEFT query_realization_facts because its facts-only callers ride a deep Option-returning type-lowering path: query_type_mappings (TypeMapping) -> resolve_type_mapping -> marshal_for_type / is_body_emittable / synthesise_body_for, plus cpp_host_type / cpp_base_host_type; query_include_mappings (IncludeMapping); cpp_method_name (NamingConvention). Routing read_facts's Result through them is a wider Option->Result refactor -- each 'no mapping found' None must stay distinct from a bodied-rule Err (Result<Option<..>>). PAYOFF: (1) the WI-770 assert-abort (exit 101, no span) becomes a graceful CppCodegenError (exit 1) for these readers too, ending the two-mechanism split WI-771 documented; (2) a project that mounts anthill.realization.TypeMapping on a shared host-mapping store is read transparently (057 resident+mounted), with cpp-gen's ground anthill_type selection becoming the read_facts LookupQuery.bound pushdown so the store indexes instead of scans. BOUNDARY vs WI-774: supported_effects's EffectMapping/LanguageMapping reads STAY on query_realization_facts -- they want WI-774's Resolve policy (bodied overlay guards HONORED on the deciding path), NOT Refuse; so query_realization_facts survives (shrunk to that one caller) until WI-774. FIX: give query_type_mappings/query_include_mappings/cpp_method_name a read_facts(Refuse)-based reader returning Result, narrow Value::Term->TermId (these are Term-carried facts), thread Result through the Option-returning type-lowering helpers. ACCEPTANCE: a bodied TypeMapping/IncludeMapping/NamingConvention rule in a test KB is refused via CppCodegenError (not assert-abort); cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-771"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-22T10:11:10Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-811", description: some(value: "Unify the mount drain; read_facts(Resolve) delegates to the resolver instead of a second mount path. Mounting (proposal 057) is integrated in TWO places, both draining the same ExtentSource::query cursor over the shared kb.extents registry: resolve.rs::drain_extent_rows (per-frame mounted-extent cursor feeding SLD candidates AFTER the resident ones, WI-797) and extent.rs::read_mounted_facts (drain-fully + re-filter by bound_matches/row_has_functor, WI-773). Same substrate, DUPLICATED drain-and-match logic -- an artifact of read_facts (WI-773) and resolver-mount (WI-797) landing as separate steps in the 057 cluster: the substrate got shared, the drain did not. FIX: (1) extract ONE shared mount-drain helper so exactly one place speaks ExtentSource::query; both call sites ride it. (2) When the read_facts Resolve policy lands (WI-774) it MUST delegate to the resolver -- which already mounts, evaluates bodies, and can aggregate most-specific-first -- NOT add a THIRD mount path: Resolve IS SLD. NOTE: read_facts(Refuse) is a GENUINELY non-resolving read (find candidates, reject any with a body, return fact heads) -- SLD is the wrong engine for it (SLD would evaluate the very bodies Refuse rejects), so Refuse stays a candidate-read sharing only the substrate. Net target: SLD-over-(resident UNION mounted) is one engine; read_facts(Resolve) is its facade; read_facts(Refuse) is the non-resolving table read over the same candidate-find. Relates WI-774, WI-780, WI-773, WI-812."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-22T11:24:17Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-762", description: some(value: "Distributive projection: mark the desugaring at the producer instead of reverse-engineering it in the typer. CONTEXT: WI-639 desugars r.(f1,f2) to a name-keyed tuple (f1: r.f1, ...) sharing ONE receiver TermId, discarding the fact that it WAS a projection; the typer then RECOVERS that fact heuristically. WI-732 had to add a THIRD heuristic to that stack, so it now reads: receiver IDENTITY by source span (projection_receivers_same rung 3 -- Rc::ptr_eq fails because the shared TermId does not survive as a shared Rc, and leaf_sym is None for a computed receiver), receiver TYPE by a stamp the DotApply frame now writes onto the raw receiver occurrence (projection_receiver_type rung 3, which had no producer before), and the LOWERED receiver by reading pos_args[0] of whatever the field typed into, cross-checked by type equality (lowered_projection_receiver -- needed because a computed receiver reaches eval only as the WI-722 macro's where_run rewrite; handing eval the raw node raised 'unhandled Expr variant in eval: DotApply'). Each is individually tested, but all three are inferences about a fact convert.rs KNEW and threw away. The pre-WI-732 consequence of the identity heuristic being incomplete was a SILENT mis-typing: r.where(..).(name,age) typed as (name: Relation[String], age: Relation[Int64]) -- two independent single-column relations, two where evaluations -- and was loud only by accident, when the caller went on to use the result as a relation. PROPOSAL: carry a provenance mark from convert.rs's DistributiveProjection build frame (the WI-618 minted-accessor precedent, mint_op_node) so the typer recognizes a projection because it IS one. ACCEPTANCE: the typer no longer infers projection-hood from span/stamp/position; the three heuristics are retired or reduced to one provenance read; existing wi714_project + wi732 suites green; a hand-written tuple of column accesses on one relation still reads as a projection (proposal 052) OR the change deliberately narrows that and says so."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-714"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-22T11:39:35Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-816", description: some(value: "DECIDE the fate of the requirement-carrying `*Within` IR variants that NO producer builds, with `Expr::LambdaWithin` as the worked case: DELETE as superseded, or IMPLEMENT. DEFINITION -- what lambda_within is FOR. `entity lambda_within(param: Pattern, body: NodeOccurrence, requirements: List[NodeOccurrence])` (stdlib/anthill/reflect/reflect.anthill) is the requirement-carrying twin of `lambda_expr`: the BINDER-shaped peer of `apply_within`. Under the WI-237 NAMES model a callee reads its dispatching dictionaries BY NAME out of `frame.requirements` via `var_ref(__req_*)`; `apply_within` carries the list holding that dictionary at a CALL site, and `lambda_within` was to carry it at a LAMBDA site -- a lambda whose body calls a spec op must have the dictionaries available in the frame its closure pushes, and `requirements` is where they would ride (docs/design/operation-call-model.md, \"The IR\"). PROBLEM -- it is built NOWHERE in EITHER carrier, and the problem it names is ALREADY SOLVED ANOTHER WAY. Measured across the workspace: nothing constructs `Expr::LambdaWithin`; nothing allocs a `Fn{anthill.reflect.Expr.lambda_within}` term; and `eval::ReflectSyms.lambda_within` (eval/mod.rs:132, :175) resolves the symbol and is then NEVER READ. What makes the variant unnecessary is WI-223 choosing a DIFFERENT mechanism: `reduce_lambda` (eval/eval.rs:773-798) SNAPSHOTS the enclosing `frame.requirements` (and `type_args`, WI-272) into `Closure.requirements` (eval/closure.rs) at lambda-CONSTRUCTION time and restores them on invocation, preserving the lexical scope where the lambda was CREATED rather than where it is INVOKED (operation-call-model.md, \"Closure invocation: the one runtime exception\"). So a spec-op call inside a lambda body ALREADY gets its dictionaries -- the CLOSURE VALUE carries them and the IR node is not needed. STATE THIS PLAINLY: this ticket does NOT report a broken program. Calling it \"unimplemented\" would predict a failure nobody can reproduce; it is SUPERSEDED, and the open question is whether that supersession should be finished or reversed. THE COST OF LEAVING IT is paid at every walker, each handling a variant no producer builds and therefore untestable against one: `for_each_child` / `drain_pattern_children` (node_occurrence.rs), the DeBruijn open/close pair, `substitute_occurrence`, `simp_rewrite::reassemble`, `persistence/print.rs`, anthill-smt-gen's cache key, anthill-cpp-gen's reject arm, the typer's `Lambda|LambdaWithin` param read (typing.rs), eval's reject arm, and load.rs's describe. WI-814 hit this directly: it gave `Lambda` the term-twin head its loader twin already had but left `LambdaWithin` `Opaque`, on the stated ground that a twin written against no producer could not be tested against one -- so the dead variant now also costs an asymmetry in `occ_head`. SIBLINGS, same status, decide together: `HoApplyWithin` and `ConstructorWithin` are likewise constructed nowhere and referenced only by the same walker / reject arms. `ApplyWithin` is DIFFERENT and must NOT be swept in: its TERM side is LIVE -- `req_insertion` (WI-231) does rewrite a classified call to `Fn{Expr.apply_within, ...}` into `kb.dispatch_rewrites` (`record_apply_within_concrete`, typing.rs) -- but the OCCURRENCE stays `Expr::Apply` carrying its `CallClass`, which is what the runtime reads post-WI-248 (\"the term-keyed redirect is now diagnostic-only\"), so `Expr::ApplyWithin` is reached only by reading such a term BACK through `visit_fn`. DECIDE, do not assume: (a) DELETE `LambdaWithin` / `HoApplyWithin` / `ConstructorWithin`, their reflect declarations, the dead `eval::ReflectSyms` field and every walker arm -- if the closure-snapshot mechanism is the settled design, in which case SAY SO at `reduce_lambda`, which is then the SOLE carrier of a lambda's requirement scope and should not be silently re-derivable; or (b) IMPLEMENT it -- `req_insertion` would have to visit LAMBDA sites and not only Apply sites, and the ticket must then say what `lambda_within` gives that `Closure.requirements` does not. The leading candidate for (b), and the only one found: a requirement scope decided at TYPE time and visible to REFLECTION / CODEGEN rather than one materialized only at eval time -- anthill-cpp-gen currently REJECTS the variant, so a codegen backend needing a lambda's dictionaries STATICALLY is the case that would justify keeping it. ACCEPTANCE: the decision is recorded WITH ITS REASON; if (a), the variants and every arm handling them are deleted and the suite is green with no `LambdaWithin` left in the workspace; if (b), `req_insertion` emits it, a test drives a spec-op call inside a lambda body through the new IR, and `occ_head` gains the twin head WI-814 declined to write. Either way `eval::ReflectSyms.lambda_within` stops being a resolved-but-unread field. Full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-816", author: "user", content: "BETTER STATEMENT OF THE PROBLEM (supersedes the framing in the description; user correction). The description leans on \"nothing constructs it\" and treats the question as delete-vs-implement. The sharper framing: requirements are SIGNATURE data analysed at COMPILE time -- spec-level `requires` per kernel-language.md:907, which is the sort-level SPEC DEPENDENCY and is DISTINCT from the operation-level `requires <RuleBody>` PRECONDITION (:1023, :1034; do not conflate them). For EVERY operation the typer derives the SET OF USED REQUIRES from the body, and a LAMBDA BODY IS NO DIFFERENT -- the same classification runs inside it (`for_each_child` descends `Expr::Lambda`, so `req_insertion::collect_classified` sees nested spec-op calls; MEASURED: an uncovered call inside a lambda raises MissingRequiresForSpecOp with the span on the NESTED call, two binders deep, and does not over-fire). So the analysis is UNIFORM and a requirement channel on the `Function` TYPE is NOT what is missing: the derived set attaches to the CODE, not the type. THE ASYMMETRY THAT IS REAL is the BINDING TIME of that set: an OPERATION has its requirements supplied BY THE CALLER, per call (the uniform rule, operation-call-model.md:805, `callee.frame.requirements = caller.apply_within.requirements`), so a recursive call with a DIFFERENT type argument supplies a DIFFERENT instance; a LAMBDA has them supplied by its CREATOR, ONCE (`reduce_lambda` snapshots `frame.requirements` into `Closure.requirements`; the doc calls this \"the only place the uniform rule is overridden\"). Same analysis, different binding time -- and NOTHING justifies the difference. CONSEQUENCE, the case that decides the design: MUTUAL RECURSION IN WHICH A REQUIREMENT CHANGES TO THE TYPE-ARGUMENT TYPE. Each step needs a different instance. Per-call resolution delivers that; resolve-once cannot. So the case is EXPRESSIBLE AS AN OPERATION AND INEXPRESSIBLE AS A LAMBDA -- lambdas are SECOND-CLASS with respect to requirements. It is also the case that cannot be closed by compile-time resolution alone: the set of needed instances is unbounded, so `CallClass::PinNow` is unreachable (there is no single instance to pin) and only `DeferToRequirement` can serve. THIS REFRAMES OPTION (b): `lambda_within` is not an exotic feature, it is the lambda's DERIVED REQUIREMENT SET MADE EXPLICIT IN THE IR, instead of recovered at run time by copying the whole enclosing frame. Note the snapshot is also IMPRECISE -- `frame.requirements` wholesale rather than the set the body uses. Harmless for execution (the body reads by name via `var_ref(__req_*)`), but it is why anthill-cpp-gen can do nothing with the variant and REJECTS it: there is no static record of which dictionaries a given closure needs. THREE OPTIONS, and the language must pick rather than land on one by accident: (1) REJECT -- keep requirements fully compile-time-resolvable and DIAGNOSE the varying-instance recursion as unresolvable (what a monomorphising compiler does); (2) DEFER -- accept `DeferToRequirement` as the general case, which requires the derived set to travel WITH the closure, which is `lambda_within`; (3) TODAY -- neither, decided by accident: capture works when the creating scope happens to know the instance, and the cases needing a varying dictionary are NOT EXPRESSIBLE RATHER THAN DIAGNOSED. Option (3) is the current state and is the actual defect. Whatever is chosen, the lexical-capture RULE ITSELF is correct and must stay for the monomorphic case -- the body is type-checked in the creation scope, so running it against a callee's dictionaries would execute code against dictionaries it was never checked against; MEASURED: a closure built under one spec evaluates correctly when invoked by a callee that cannot even NAME its spec op. STILL UNVERIFIED, do not treat as fact: an agent reported that `requires` on a sort makes its operations untrappable at eval (`DeferToRequirement: projection index 0 out of range`); a hand reproduction produced a DIFFERENT error (`UnknownOperation`) on a differently-shaped program, so the claim is NOT established and needs its own reproduction before it is filed or relied on here.", at: "2026-07-22T13:31:08Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-816", author: "user", content: "DEPENDS ON WI-817 for its evidence. WI-817 constructs the WITNESS this ticket's decision turns on -- a lambda application whose requirement cannot be fixed at construction (mutual recursion where a requirement changes to the type-argument type), plus the operation-only CONTROL. Do not settle delete-vs-implement here before that witness exists: if the case needs dictionary passing, option (b) wins and  is its IR; if it can be cleanly rejected, option (a) wins and the  variants go. Deciding this ticket first would be choosing the design from a prediction rather than a program.", at: "2026-07-22T13:33:48Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-816", author: "user", content: "CORRECTION to the preceding feedback, which lost two names to shell expansion and reads with gaps. It should say: \"if the case needs dictionary passing, option (b) wins and `lambda_within` is its IR; if it can be cleanly rejected, option (a) wins and the `*Within` variants go.\" Substance is otherwise unchanged: this ticket DEPENDS ON WI-817 for its evidence, and delete-vs-implement should not be settled here before WI-817's witness exists.", at: "2026-07-22T13:34:08Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-814", author: "user", content: "MEASUREMENT AND REPORT, the acceptance clause that asked for the discrim / GoalKey / seen_goals effect to be \"measured and reported\". (1) DISCRIM CANDIDATE SETS. Before, a lambda-bearing structure was NON-INDEXABLE: `Opaque` carries no discrimination key at all, `insert_walk` / `remove_walk` PANIC on it, and only the `view_is_indexable` pre-check kept them from firing; a query with an `Opaque` head followed universal var-edges only and did NOT descend. Now a lambda keys `Functor(lambda_expr)` -> `Arity(2)` -> per-child `NamedKey` edges and the walk descends into `param` and `body`. Pinned by `lambda_cross_carrier_discrim_match`: a fact indexed under the TERM carrier is found by an OCCURRENCE-carrier query (1 hit), a DIFFERENT binder does NOT match (0 hits, so the candidate set is genuinely keyed on the lambda's structure rather than merely widened), and a term-side pattern matches the occurrence target. (2) GoalKey / seen_goals. `StructToken::Opaque` is PAYLOAD-FREE, so two goals differing only inside a lambda used to produce the SAME key -- i.e. `seen_goals` OVER-DEDUPED and silently dropped the second answer -- while `is_cacheable` rejected the key outright. Both moved the SAFE way and both are pinned by `lambda_goal_keys_separate_and_become_cacheable`: identical goals STILL key identically (`k1 == k1_again`, so nothing that used to dedup stops dedupping -- the clause's explicit requirement), distinct lambdas now SEPARATE (`k1 != k2`, the over-dedup fixed), the key is now CACHEABLE, and the TERM and OCCURRENCE carriers produce the SAME key (so the change is carrier-neutral). (3) BINDER SOUNDNESS. Matching does NOT need to refuse to descend, so the \"name the mechanism that keeps it out\" branch does not apply; what makes the descent capture-free is named in the arm's comment: WI-550 mints every binder symbol through `intern_unique` memoized on the binder's PARSE-NODE id, so binder symbols are globally unique BY CONSTRUCTION and a `var_ref` names exactly one binder -- Barendregt's convention enforced at the producer. `Var::DeBruijn` remains the second line of defence (discrim keys it as an inert var-edge; `unify_match_values` still refuses to bind it). The observable consequence is pinned by `one_source_lambda_equal_two_distinct_sources_not`. (4) ALPHA-EQUIVALENCE, stated as the clause required: the view is SYNTACTIC. Two occurrences of ONE source lambda compare EQUAL (they share the parse node, hence the gensym -- what WI-762's guard needs); two independently written `lambda x -> x` / `lambda y -> y` compare UNEQUAL despite being alpha-equivalent, because the term twin they must agree with is compared structurally, not up to alpha. SCOPE DELIVERED BEYOND THE TICKET, recorded so the delivery is not read as narrower than it is: `If` / `Let` / `Match` / `Proof` and ALL FIVE `Pattern` variants also became structural, under one shape table that makes `head`'s arity and `named_keys` structurally unable to disagree; and the PROOF TERM gained its `using` premise list (loader + `visit_fn` reader + view), correcting a term that had been an INCOMPLETE representation of a proof -- `proof Y using X` and `proof Y using Z` had compared EQUAL. KNOWN GAPS LEFT OPEN, each with an owner: `Let.type_annotation` is still invisible to the view and now yields a CACHEABLE GoalKey, so `let x: Int64 = 1` and `let x: String = 1` conflate -> WI-819 (which removes the second annotation channel rather than restoring the slot); `Pattern::Tuple.labels` is dropped by `pattern_to_term` -> WI-803; the `*Within` IR stays `Opaque` -> WI-816.", at: "2026-07-22T14:34:46Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-814", description: some(value: "Expr::Lambda should NOT be Opaque in occ_head -- give it the head its term twin already has, and settle what opacity under a BINDER was protecting. CONTEXT: surfaced reviewing WI-762. occ_head (kb/term_view.rs) gives a real ViewHead to Apply, Constructor/Instantiation, Const, Spliced, Ref, Ident, VarRef, Var, Bottom, DotApply and ListLit, each justified by pointing at the term the loader emits for the same source -- e.g. DotApply reads \"STRUCTURALLY as its dot_apply(receiver, name, args: List[ApplyArg]) term twin ... so a fact embedding a DotApply indexes/matches identically to the term the loader emits\" (WI-278/397/425). Expr::Lambda has no arm and falls to `_ => ViewHead::Opaque`, whose comment scopes the intent narrowly: \"control-flow and post-elaboration forms ... are opaque to rule-LHS MATCHING\". THE TERM TWIN ALREADY EXISTS, so the head is not a new invention: LoadBuildFrame::Lambda (kb/load.rs:8860-8872) builds Term::Fn{functor: anthill.reflect.Expr.lambda_expr, named_args: [param, body]} -- 0 positional, 2 named -- so the mechanical shape is exactly ViewHead::Functor{Some(lambda_expr), 0, 2}, the same one-liner DotApply/VarRef/ListLit use. WHY IT MATTERS NOW: head() is no longer read only by rule-LHS matching. It also backs views_structurally_equal (an IDENTITY question) and goal_fingerprint/GoalKey (dedup keys). Opaque has no (Opaque, Opaque) equal arm, so as an identity test it returns FALSE NEGATIVES. MEASURED: using views_structurally_equal to compare two projection receivers of the form `person_row.where(lambda c -> eq(c.age, 30))` reported them UNEQUAL and rejected all ten wi714_project tests -- not because of the dot (DotApply heads fine) but because the LAMBDA nested in its args is Opaque. That blocked WI-762's receiver-divergence guard, which had to be abandoned. THE REAL QUESTION, and why this is not just \"fill the arm\": Lambda is the BINDER case, and the opacity may be load-bearing for MATCHING rather than merely unwritten. Matching into a binder's scope without capture-avoidance is unsound, so `Opaque` may be deliberately protecting rule-LHS matching from descending under a lambda -- unlike DotApply, which binds nothing. CLAUDE.md's representation note states the adjacent position: hash-consing is \"specifically inappropriate for binders (arrow / dependent types), whose scope and alpha-equivalence don't fit a global dedup store\". So the ticket must decide, not assume: (a) is a head wanted for IDENTITY/fingerprint while matching still refuses to descend (two different consumers of head(), possibly needing to be distinguished), or (b) is descending safe once capture-avoidance is handled where matching happens? ALPHA-EQUIVALENCE, stated because it decides the semantics: WI-550 mints binder symbols via intern_unique memoized on the pattern_var PARSE-NODE id (load.rs:6373-6378). So two occurrences derived from ONE source lambda -- e.g. the N copies convert.rs's distribute-dot makes of one receiver -- SHARE a gensym and would compare EQUAL (which is exactly what WI-762's guard needs), while two independently-written identical lambdas get DIFFERENT gensyms and compare UNEQUAL. That makes the proposed head SYNTACTIC, not alpha-aware. State whether views_structurally_equal is supposed to hold for two alpha-equivalent lambdas; if yes, the binder symbols must be normalized (de Bruijn-style) in the fingerprint rather than compared as symbols. BLAST RADIUS -- measure before changing, since Opaque->Functor changes three consumers at once: (1) the discrimination tree begins INDEXING lambda-bearing facts/goals instead of treating them as opaque, changing which candidates are returned; (2) goal_fingerprint/GoalKey values change, so the resolver's seen_goals answer-dedup and the per-query cache key differently (GoalKey::is_cacheable's Opaque exclusion may start admitting keys it used to reject); (3) rule-LHS matching may now descend under a binder -- the soundness question above. NOTE the occurrence->term direction is deliberately NOT part of this: try_occurrence_to_term has no Lambda arm, and per the standing reify audit (WI-348/482/621/678/692) the shim should be shrinking, not growing. This ticket fills the VIEW, which is the carrier-neutral interface consumers should read. ACCEPTANCE: Expr::Lambda reads ViewHead::Functor{Expr.lambda_expr, 0, 2} (or a documented alternative if (a) wins), with the alpha-equivalence semantics STATED in the arm's comment; a test pins that two occurrences of ONE source lambda compare structurally equal and two distinct source lambdas do not; the effect on discrim candidate sets and on GoalKey/seen_goals dedup is measured and reported, with a test pinning that no goal that used to dedup stops dedupping (or the change is stated as intended); if matching must still refuse to descend under a binder, the mechanism that keeps it out is named and tested; WI-762's receiver-divergence guard becomes writable on views_structurally_equal with no second comparator; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-762"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-22T14:45:27Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-819", description: some(value: "ONE type-annotation channel, on the PATTERN: lift `type_ann` from `Pattern::Var` to every `Pattern` variant and DELETE `Expr::Let.type_annotation`. PROBLEM -- a `let` annotation has TWO homes, and which one it lands in depends on the PATTERN'S SHAPE, which is not something the author chose. (1) INNER: `Pattern::Var { name, type_ann: Option<Rc<NodeOccurrence>> }` (node_occurrence.rs), the per-binder `: T` -- `let (a: Int, b) = pair`, `lambda (y: T) -> …` (WI-517). (2) OUTER: `Expr::Let { pattern, type_annotation: Option<Value>, value, body }`, whose own doc calls it \"the OUTER `: T` annotation from WI-185 (`let p: T = …`)\". The outer channel exists ONLY because `type_ann` is declared on `Pattern::Var` ALONE -- `Wildcard`, `Literal`, `Constructor` and `Tuple` have no such slot, so `let (a, b): (Int, String) = pair` has nowhere on the pattern to put it. So `let x: T` and `let (a, b): T` are the SAME SYNTAX landing in DIFFERENT SLOTS. WHY IT IS NOT MERELY UNTIDY -- the two channels are ALREADY OUT OF SYNC, and the drift is silent. The INNER annotation is lowered onto the pattern's TERM twin: `load_pattern_var` (load.rs) calls `node_occurrence::value_to_term` and wraps it `build_some`/`build_none` into `var_pattern(name, type_ann)`, with a comment stating the case -- \"`value_to_term` is the total Value→Term boundary (WI-390) … a `denoted`-bearing type lowers losslessly\". The OUTER annotation is on NEITHER carrier: WI-342 T8 dropped the term-side `type_name` slot as write-only, and WI-814 finished the deletion (reflect field + `visit_fn` reader). CONSEQUENCE, MEASURED as part of WI-814: with `Expr::Let` now reading as a structural `Functor{let_expr, 0, 3}`, the outer annotation is invisible to `views_structurally_equal` AND absent from `goal_fingerprint`, so `let x: Int64 = 1` and `let x: String = 1` compare EQUAL and produce the SAME `GoalKey` -- and because no `StructToken::Opaque` is emitted any more, that key is now CACHEABLE, so the resolver's per-query cache and `seen_goals` treat two different programs as one. The inner channel has none of these problems, because it rides the pattern twin. NOTE THE FALSE JUSTIFICATION, so it is not repeated: the reason recorded for making the outer annotation `Value`-only was that a denoted-bearing type (`: Modify[c]`) cannot be hash-consed. That is FALSE since WI-390 (`value_to_term` is total; `Value::Node` lowers via `occurrence_to_term`), and it is falsified IN PRACTICE three functions away by `load_pattern_var`, which has been lowering exactly such types all along. PROPOSAL -- delete the second channel rather than repair it. Move `type_ann: Option<…>` from `Pattern::Var` up to EVERY `Pattern` variant (or to a wrapper carrying pattern + optional annotation, whichever the implementer finds less invasive), and remove `Expr::Let.type_annotation` entirely. Then: `let`, `lambda` and match branches annotate UNIFORMLY; there is ONE reader instead of two; the annotation is already on the term (the pattern twin's existing `value_to_term` path, no new slot on `let_expr`); the view already exposes it (`var_pattern(name, type_ann)` is in WI-814's `pattern_shape`), so comparison and fingerprinting become correct with NO new key and NO new mechanism; and the WI-814 conflation above disappears without restoring anything. DECIDE, do not assume: whether EVERY variant gets the slot or only the ones that can bind. `let _: T = e` is meaningful (it asserts the value's type and binds nothing); `let 5: Int64 = e` is odd but harmless. State the rule chosen. BLAST RADIUS, to be measured before starting rather than discovered: the reflect surface grows a slot on `tuple_pattern` / `constructor_pattern` / `literal_pattern` / `wildcard` (`stdlib/anthill/reflect/reflect.anthill`), `pattern_to_term` and `term_to_pattern_occurrence` gain it on every arm, and `for_each_pattern_child` / `drain_pattern_children` / `reassemble_pattern` must visit it uniformly. On the deletion side, `Expr::Let.type_annotation` is walked by the DeBruijn open/close pair, `substitute_occurrence`, the `*_value_type` helpers and `simp_rewrite::reassemble` (node_occurrence.rs), and is READ by the typer in several places that must be repointed at the pattern: the `Expr::Let` arm's annotation handling (WI-399 projection elimination, WI-400 let-alias canonicalization), the value's expected type, the `LetAfterValue` conformance check (WI-379), and `unroll_annotation_with_inferred` (WI-374 / spec §8.1). The binder-side reader (`bind_and_label_pattern` / `extract_pattern_type_ann`, plus WI-794's contradicting-binder-annotation check) is the one that SURVIVES and absorbs the others. DO TOGETHER WITH WI-803's REMAINING GAP: `Pattern::Tuple.labels` is dropped by `pattern_to_term` (\"KNOWN LOSSY AND UNCOVERED\"), so `Pattern::Tuple` is touched by both concerns; doing them separately means editing the same arms and the same reflect entity twice. ACCEPTANCE: exactly one annotation channel remains and it is on the pattern; `Expr::Let.type_annotation` no longer exists; a test pins that `let x: Int64 = 1` and `let x: String = 1` are NOT structurally equal and do NOT share a `GoalKey`; a test pins that `let (a, b): (Int64, String) = p` annotates through the SAME channel as `let x: Int64 = 1` (the shape-dependence is gone); the existing let-annotation behaviours stay green -- WI-379 conformance rejection, WI-399 projection elimination, WI-374 partial-parametric unrolling, WI-400 alias identity, WI-517 typed lambda binders, WI-794 binder contradiction; the rule chosen for non-binding patterns is stated; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-22T16:07:32Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-819", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. ONE annotation channel, on the pattern occurrence; `Expr::Let.type_annotation` deleted. Full workspace green (3424 tests / 85 binaries / exit 0) via rustland/scripts/test.sh.\n\nREPRESENTATION CHOSEN, and the rule for non-binding patterns. `type_ann` hangs on `NodeKind::Pattern { pattern, type_ann }` -- the OCCURRENCE wrapper -- not on each of the five variants. 23 `NodeKind::Pattern` sites vs ~90 variant-construction sites, but the real reason is that an annotation is written ABOUT a pattern occurrence (`let p: T = e`), so no variant should own it. THE RULE: EVERY pattern may be annotated, including ones that bind nothing. Hanging the slot on the occurrence DISSOLVES the \"which variants get it\" question rather than answering it -- there is no variant to exclude. `let _: T = e` asserts the value's type and binds nothing (tested; a false assertion is rejected); `let 5: Int64 = e` is odd but harmless. Grammar already admitted both.\n\nTERM SURFACE. `type_ann` declared on ALL SIX reflect pattern entities (including `named_tuple_pattern`, which has no producer -- declared anyway so the enum has NO annotation-less variant, since a shape that cannot carry `: T` IS the WI-819 defect), emitted ONLY when present. Keeps `wildcard` NULLARY, which `KnowledgeBase::alloc`'s WI-511 collapse and WI-436's cross-carrier head identity rely on -- pinned by `an_unannotated_wildcard_stays_nullary`. TRADEOFF STATED IN THE FILE rather than papered over: a declared `Term` that is sometimes absent is less type-honest than an always-present `Option[Term]`; the latter was rejected because it makes `wildcard` unary. NOTE A FALSE PRECEDENT I WROTE AND THEN REMOVED: I cited `proof_stmt.strategy` as a surface precedent for conditional keys. `proof_stmt` has NO reflect entity declaration -- it appeared in that file exactly ONCE, in my own comment. Same failure mode as WI-776 (a claim the file itself refutes), caught by review, not by me.\n\nWI-803's REMAINING GAP CLOSED on the same arms: `tuple_pattern` gained `labels` (emitted when non-empty), so `pattern_to_term` no longer drops them and a round-tripped pattern stops reverting to reading components BY SLOT (the WI-788 wrong answer on a permuted value).\n\nTHE DEEP FIX, which was NOT where I first looked. A first cut passed `Value::Node` straight into the slot to keep a COMPOUND type projection (`s.cell.T`) on the carrier `eliminate_type_projections` understood. That was fixing the wrong end, and it introduced a REGRESSION: a `Type`-kind occurrence in an ordinary occurrence-child slot breaks collect/close LOCKSTEP -- `node_to_debruijn` closes it via `rewrite_type_occurrence` while `collect_occurrence_global_vars_ordered` asserts on it and collects NOTHING (stray `Global` in a stored rule body, undercounted arity). Pre-WI-819 that path worked via `collect_option_value` -> `collect_value_type`. MEASURED with a witness, not reasoned. Root cause was one altitude lower: `resolve_receiver_path_type` matched `Value::Node` + `Expr::DotApply` STRUCTURALLY, so a compound receiver resolved on ONE CARRIER ONLY -- and `eliminate_expr_carried_projection` rejected it on the other with \"receiver is not a simple value reference\". WI-814 had already given `Expr::DotApply` and its `dot_apply` term twin ONE view head with the same three keys, so the fix is to read the receiver through `TermView`. Carrier-neutral now; the verdict no longer depends on which carrier a type is written on (WI-425).\n\nWHAT THAT DELETED, all at once: the `Value -> Term -> Occurrence -> Term -> Value` roundtrip; `ann_override` / `term_to_param_occurrence_annotated` / `BuildFrame::Let.type_annotation`; the double lowering in `annotate_let_pattern`; and the let-vs-lambda fidelity asymmetry (both spellings now reach the slot identically). The TERM is the single source -- `term_to_param_occurrence` reads the annotation back out of it -- so the two carriers hold one value BY CONSTRUCTION rather than by two writes that must agree. Also retired: four `*_option_value` walker helpers, three explicit `Expr::Let` walker arms, `extract_named_arg` (became dead), and node_occurrence's private `build_some`/`build_none` (duplicates of load.rs's).\n\nA FALSE JUSTIFICATION I WROTE, recorded so it is not repeated. `for_each_pattern_child`'s doc claimed the split `(pat, type_ann, f)` signature \"is a compile error until every caller passes the annotation along\". That is INVERTED: `for_each_pattern_child(pat, None, f)` compiles, so the split PERMITTED the very silent skip it advertised against, while taxing all twelve call sites. It now takes the OCCURRENCE, which makes omission unrepresentable; `for_each_subpattern` is the named opt-out for the two callers that legitimately supply the annotation themselves.\n\nTESTS, and TWO VACUOUS DRAFTS the controls caught. `wi819_annotation_channel_test.rs` (10 tests). The identity claim is HAND-BUILT with one shared binder symbol, and the file records why: a loader-driven pair CANNOT isolate the annotation, because WI-550 mints every binder Symbol per SOURCE SITE, so two `let`s in two operations differ in their binder name regardless. Draft 1 also differed in its VALUE. BOTH DRAFTS STAYED GREEN with `pattern_shape` deliberately blinded to `type_ann` -- the control that caught them. Every claim is now control-verified: blinding the view, dropping the loader annotation, dropping the labels, and re-introducing the Node passthrough each turn the relevant test RED. What the loader actually does is pinned separately (`the_loader_attaches_distinct_annotations_to_distinct_lets`, `the_annotation_rides_the_pattern_term_not_the_let_term`).\n\nDIAGNOSTICS. The two new loader errors are SPANNED variants (`PatternAnnotatedTwice`, `LetAnnotationOnNonPattern`), not `LoadError::Other`, which carries no span and renders with no line:col -- WI-745 made file identity part of every load error's contract, and the double-annotation case is user-reachable. A test asserts the location is present, and fails when the error is downgraded to `Other`.\n\nSPEC. `docs/kernel-language.md` gained a `let`-annotation paragraph -- the form was NEVER documented despite shipping in WI-185. Every claim in it is backed by a passing test, including `let (a: A, b): T` (sub-binder and whole-pattern annotations coexist: different slots on different patterns). An illustrative `Map.empty()` example was dropped rather than ship one I had not verified exists.\n\nSTALE COMMENTS SWEPT: seven sites in node_occurrence.rs / resolve.rs still described `Let.type_annotation` as a live TermId-typed field the walkers descend; term_view.rs's \"two lets differing only in annotation compare EQUAL / the annotation CAN be restored to the let_expr term\" block (now false, and the restoration it invited is what `NodeKind::Pattern` forbids); and the typer's lambda arm claiming \"the grammar's `pattern_var` doesn't surface [an annotation] today\", false since WI-517.\n\nNOT RUN: the `/code-review` skill (bug-hunting). `/simplify` was run (4 agents: reuse / simplification / efficiency / altitude) and all findings are applied or recorded above. `/code-review` remains outstanding per the repo rule.", at: "2026-07-22T16:08:22Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-774", description: some(value: "Guarded realization selection: resolve seam vs WI-770's blanket refusal (design follow-up). WI-770's refusal is deliberately blanket, and the review recorded two over-refusal consequences to weigh, both latent (no bodied realization entries exist in-tree). (a) ENUMERATION: supported_effects' candidate gathering (rustland/anthill-cpp-gen/src/lib.rs ~4100; flat EffectMapping query with ground=None, plus the LanguageMapping nested walk) inherits the refusal, but its own doc says candidates are deliberately over-broad and the realizes_effect RESOLUTION filter makes the answer exact -- and the stdlib realizes_effect rules genuinely SLD-resolve EffectMapping subgoals with guards honored (stdlib/anthill/realization/realization.anthill:354-380), so a bodied EffectMapping is correct on the DECIDING path yet refused by the candidate path; worse, the abort fires inside describe_supported_effects (lib.rs ~4136) while RENDERING the WI-576 capability-gate error (call site ~1464), turning an intended diagnostic into a panic. WI-770's message therefore tells EffectMapping authors 'guarded selection is honored only where codegen RESOLVES' -- true, but the refusal itself is what stops them reaching that path via guarded DATA; the sanctioned shape is a fact plus a realizes_effect overlay arm. (b) POST-FILTER BLINDNESS: query_type_mappings grounds only anthill_type and post-filters lang AFTER the query (lib.rs ~3870), so a bodied TypeMapping for ANOTHER language panics every cpp codegen touching that anthill_type even though the lang filter would have discarded it. OPTIONS TO WEIGH: make enumeration resolve (supported_effects already holds &mut kb -- resolve EffectMapping(effect: ?e, ...) so guards gate candidate names; the WI-767 'resolve by default' shape); ground `lang` structurally in the pattern (option_value already builds some(...) entities for goals) so other-language candidates never surface; give TypeMapping/IncludeMapping/NamingConvention a real resolve seam (WI-089's addendum lower_type predicate, which still does not exist -- cpp-gen's sole kb.resolve consumer is realizes_effect at lib.rs:4011); or explicitly re-affirm blanket refusal and pin the enumeration abort as intended. Coordinate with WI-773's accessor if both land. ACCEPTANCE: a bodied EffectMapping that realizes_effect resolves correctly no longer aborts error-message rendering (resolved at enumeration or narrowed out), OR a design note + test pin blanket refusal as the accepted contract; a bodied other-language TypeMapping no longer panics cpp codegen, or same explicit pin; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-22T15:04:20Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-812", description: some(value: "read_facts resident selection pushdown via the discrim tree + O(1) bodied-rule gate. read_facts's RESIDENT arm is a LINEAR scan over the functor bucket (rules_by_functor) + per-row bound_matches, NOT a discrim-indexed lookup -- because the generic accessor lacks the functor's field SCHEMA to build a full-arity discrim query pattern (extent.rs comment). It is the ONLY read path in the KB that does not use the discrim tree (both query_view and SLD do). Consequences: (a) O(bucket) reads where a discrim index would be O(matching) -- the MOUNTED arm already pushes the selection down and indexes, the resident arm scans; (b) the BLANKET bodied-refusal (functor-wide, WI-770/772) is ENTANGLED with the full-bucket scan. FIX: (1) give read_facts the functor's field schema (the KB has it -- canonicalize_record_named_args keys on DECLARED field order) so it builds a discrim query and pushes the selection down (indexed + naturally scoped). (2) Factor the blanket bodied-refusal into an O(1) per-functor has_bodied_rule cached flag (cf. WI-635 head_has_vars, WI-646 O(1) gate) so 'find matching rows' (indexed) and 'is this functor a pure table' (O(1)) are SEPARATE concerns -- and blanket-vs-scoped becomes a DELIBERATE choice, not a scan artifact. NOTE vs WI-811: distinct -- WI-811's Resolve->SLD gives the Resolve path discrim indexing for free, but read_facts(Refuse) is a non-resolving read WI-811 leaves as a scan, so this WI (resident Refuse indexing + the O(1) gate) stands on its own. Relates WI-773, WI-774, WI-811."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-22T16:50:56Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-818", author: "user", content: "DECISION: option (2) — backed means EXECUTABLE (a runnable body or a builtin); a spec-level rule is a LAW, not backing. Reasons: the stdlib already treated laws as non-evaluable specification (stream.anthill 'resolvable but not evaluable'; WI-362 default-body pattern exists because of it); an SLD route for a value-returning op has no sound answer for multiplicity/floundering/no-solution (empty head would still fail at run time, so option (1) cannot restore the load-time guarantee); the WI-625 eq bridge stays the predicate-shaped exception. REVERSES WI-363's pin (provider_with_spec_default_rule_loads -> _is_rejected). Shipped with: Stream.headOption/head/tail default bodies over splitFirst (tail's row gains guarded Error[EmptyStream]); List.head/headOption own overrides (WI-444); prelude Nothing = bottom alias; body may raise a guarded-declared label; override-refinement effects leg param-aligns guards; eval dispatch fall-through reports OperationBodyMissing (qualified) when the op has a signature; unhandled surface Error.raise -> Raised{payload}. Spec updated (kernel-language.md section 8.7 area: 'Backing is executable'). Follow-on candidates: guard refutation via op fold (head(cons..) discharging to PURE is still aspirational); result-position op reduction in law answers (both law answers flounder today, pinned as count-only).", at: "2026-07-22T21:46:42Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-818", description: some(value: "A spec-level `rule` DEFAULT satisfies the load-time provider-backing check but CANNOT BE EXECUTED, so the program LOADS CLEAN and fails at RUN time -- and the diagnostic it fails with names the wrong problem. MEASURED, three variants that differ ONLY in how the spec operation is backed; carrier `Widget` provides spec `Nameable` (`operation tag(x: T) -> Int64`), and a third sort `Box` declares `requires Nameable[T = T]` with `operation same(x: T) -> Int64 = tag(x)`. (A) NO backing at all -- LOAD IS REJECTED, with a diagnostic that is exactly right: \"'probe.a.Widget' provides 'probe.a.Nameable' but backs no operation 'probe.a.Nameable.tag' (no default on 'probe.a.Nameable', no own 'tag' on 'probe.a.Widget')\". (B) carrier supplies a real implementation (`operation tag(x: Widget) -> Int64 = 111` in the Widget sort body) -- LOADS CLEAN and EVALUATES: `Widget.tag(widget(1))` = Ok(Int(111)) and, through the `requires` path, `Box.same(widget(1))` = Ok(Int(111)). (C) THE DEFECT -- the spec carries a `rule tag(?x) = 111` DEFAULT and the carrier supplies nothing: LOADS CLEAN, then `Box.same(widget(1))` = Err(UnknownOperation { name: \"tag\" }) and the direct call `Nameable.tag(widget(1))` = Err(OperationBodyMissing { name: \"probe.c.Nameable.tag\" }). THE DIAGNOSIS IS EXACT, because (A)'s own message names the two conditions the check tests -- \"no default on <spec>\" and \"no own <op> on <carrier>\". Adding the `rule` default satisfies the FIRST condition, so the load check passes; the evaluator then cannot execute a rule default, because a RULE IS NOT A BODY (rules are SLD clauses; `invoke_op_with_requirements` wants an operation body). So the backing check and the evaluator DISAGREE ABOUT WHAT COUNTS AS BACKING, and the load-time guarantee that (A) exists to provide is void for exactly the programs that take the default path. SECOND, SMALLER DEFECT visible in the same run: the SAME missing body reports as `OperationBodyMissing` when the op is called DIRECTLY but degrades to `UnknownOperation { name: \"tag\" }` when reached THROUGH the `requires` path. `tag` is not unknown -- it is declared on `Nameable`, and `requires Nameable[T]` is precisely what brings it into scope. The requires path loses the information the direct path has, and reports a name-resolution failure for what is a missing-implementation failure. WHAT THIS TICKET IS NOT: an agent reported that a sort-level `requires` makes ANY of that sort's operations untrappable at eval (\"DeferToRequirement: projection index 0 out of range (requirement `__req_eq` bundles 0 sub-requirement(s))\"). Variant (B) REFUTES the general claim -- the `requires` dispatch path works and returns the right value. That separate error was seen on a differently-shaped program (stdlib `Eq`/`PartialEq`) and is NOT reproduced here; if it is real it is its own ticket, and it should not be conflated with this one. DECIDE, do not assume, because the fix could go either way: (1) the rule default is MEANT to be executable and the evaluator should route a bodyless spec op to its default rule (via SLD, the WI-580 body-derived-rule machinery being the nearby precedent) -- then (C) should return 111; or (2) a `rule` default is NOT executable backing, and the load check must STOP counting it -- then (C) should be REJECTED AT LOAD with (A)'s message, and the check's \"no default on <spec>\" condition needs to mean \"no executable default\". Option (2) is the smaller change and restores the load-time guarantee; option (1) is the larger and gives spec defaults real meaning. Whichever is chosen, the `UnknownOperation`-vs-`OperationBodyMissing` degradation is a bug in EITHER world and should be fixed alongside. ACCEPTANCE: the three variants are committed as tests, with (A) pinning the load rejection, (B) pinning the working value through the `requires` path, and (C) pinning the CHOSEN behaviour; the decision between (1) and (2) is recorded with its reason; a spec op reached through `requires` with no executable backing reports the SAME error as the direct call, naming the missing implementation rather than an unknown name; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-22T21:46:44Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-818", author: "user", content: "/code-review (max): 15 findings, all fixed, full suite green. Top: op_backed's {ns}.{op_short} candidate certified namespace-level bodies dispatch can never reach (probe-confirmed loads-clean/dies-at-runtime survivor) -- leg retired, pinned by ns_level_body_is_not_backing. Entry path and dispatch fall-through now share one unrunnable-target classifier (declared op -> OperationBodyMissing qualified, else UnknownOperation) with a presence-only signature probe + env-gated backtrace. guarded_effect_label owns guarded-atom recognition, matching the QUALIFIED EffectExpression.guarded functor. New pins: inheriting-carrier eval witness (head over MappedStream = spec default), negative control for the guarded admission, tail/isEmpty multiplicity, Error-handler resume refusal. Latent bug found by the new witness test and fixed: try_occurrence_to_term's try-contract was not recursive -- a lambda-bearing argument to a guarded-effect call panicked in build_call_guard_sigma; occ_build_fn now threads Option so the guard sigma declines conservatively. Docs synced: check header, kernel-language.md (Eq mechanism corrected: Set/Map exempt via concrete-carrier gate; rules-define-operations doctrine qualified to the eq family), relation/logical_stream stdlib comments, Nothing alias comment.", at: "2026-07-23T08:03:45Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-817", author: "user", content: "DELIVERABLE 1, THE WITNESS — CONSTRUCTED, MEASURED, COMMITTED (tests/include/wi817_polyrec_requirement_test.rs, 10 tests, all pinning observed behaviour; positive controls prove both the load and eval harnesses report breakage). Program family: spec Desc { describe(x:T)->Int64 }, base instance fact Desc[T=Leaf] (describe=1), CONDITIONAL instance sort WrapDesc { sort E=?; requires Desc[T=E]; fact Desc[T=Wrap[A=E]] } (describe = 10*describe(inner)+2, so correct values are depth-coded: drive(n) = 1, 12, 122, 1222). Mutual recursion f<->g where g calls f at the CHANGED type Wrap[.]; control = direct call; witness = the g->f leg inside  passed to a requirement-free applier that cannot name Desc. VERDICT: THE PREDICTION IS NOT OBSERVABLE TODAY — THE OPERATION-ONLY CONTROL FAILS IN EVERY EXPRESSIBLE SPELLING, AND THE LAMBDA WITNESS NEVER FAILS DIFFERENTLY FROM ITS CONTROL. Measured matrix, outcomes verbatim: (ROW 1, op-scoped requires over the op's OWN [T] — proposal-042 explicit type args, requires Desc[FT]) outcome (b) LOAD ERROR for control AND witness at the SAME site (f's covered describe call, NOT the recursion): '31:24: type mismatch in wi817.control.Desc.describe.dispatch: expected matching impl for per-call bindings, got no impl matches'; both binding spellings Desc[PT] / Desc[T=PT] fail identically even with NO recursion — op_requires_covers_call does not see op-level params at all (spec §5.4 says they are legal in requires positions; GAP). (ROW 2, op-scoped over a SORT param, cross-sort mutual recursion, per-call binding via argument inference — wi418's channel) outcome (c) EVAL ERROR, control AND witness identical: drive(0)=Ok(Int(1)), drive(>=1)=Err(Internal(\"DeferToRequirement: requirement param `__req_desc` not bound in caller frame\")); the MINIMAL repro needs NO recursion and NO lambda — ONE conditional level probe(wrap(leaf())) already dies the same way. (ROW 3, SORT-level requires) the 1-conditional-level case WORKS CORRECTLY — Ok(Int(12)) — but the recursion is outcome (d) SILENTLY WRONG, control AND witness identical: drive(0..2) = Ok(Int(1)) at EVERY depth (correct: 1, 12, 122); the caller's Desc[GT] dictionary is forwarded UNCHANGED into f's Desc[FT:=Wrap[GT]] slot, the conditional instance is NEVER consulted, and the Leaf impl runs on a wrapped value. MECHANISM (verified in source, kb/typing.rs build_dep_projection): Strategy 1's entries_cover is WILDCARD-TOLERANT by design (WI-419 comment: 'a caller Eq[A] covers a dep Eq[B] whenever either element is a type param') and the WI-419 sigma-class check only DISAMBIGUATES 2+ covering entries — a SOLE covering wildcard entry blindly forwards var_ref(__req_desc) without consulting the call-site substitution (FT:=Wrap[GT]); Strategy 3 (SLD construction of the Conditional tree via resolve_inner + emit_tree_as_projection — the machinery wi224 tests at the resolver layer and V8 proves correct END-TO-END) is SHADOWED by that early return. Row 2's loud twin: an op-scoped chain has NO call-site supply channel at all (ConcreteApplyWithin gates on the callee's PARENT SORT chain) and value-directed dispatch pushes the impl frame WITHOUT the impl's own requires, so the read is unbound instead of wrong. THE REQUIRES-EVAL-PATH HAZARD: SETTLED. Sort-level requires does NOT make ops untrappable — V8 runs a conditional dictionary end-to-end correctly; NEITHER reported error spelling ('projection index 0 out of range' / 'UnknownOperation') reproduced anywhere in the family; the real failure modes are the two supply defects above, each now pinned minimally. BONUS HAZARD (pinned as unconditioned_parametric_fact_mispins_abstract_call): removing the conditional's requires leaves fact Desc[T=Wrap[A=E]] with FREE E, and an ABSTRACT covered call is then silently MIS-PINNED to WrapDesc.describe at load (var-var unification; WI-325's protection guards NoCandidates/NoMatch, not a bogus Unique) and dies at eval on field 'inner' — this is also why the first-cut positive control ('remove the requires, expect a load error') was itself refuted by measurement.", at: "2026-07-23T09:26:58Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-817", author: "user", content: "CORRECTION to the preceding feedback, which lost a phrase to shell expansion: the witness sentence should read \"witness = the g->f leg inside (lambda w -> FHolder.f(sub(n,1), w)) passed to a requirement-free applier that cannot name Desc\". Substance otherwise unchanged. DELIVERABLE 2, THE RULES, from the witness. RULE A (the rule the witness actually dictates, upstream of the lambda question): at a call site supplying a callee requirement, FORWARDING the caller dictionary is sound ONLY when the dep element sigma-resolves to the SAME unification class as the covering entry element; when it sigma-resolves to a COMPOUND OVER the caller element (FT := Wrap[GT]) the dictionary must be CONSTRUCTED per call as the Conditional tree with FromScope leaves — construct_requirement(WrapDesc, [var_ref(__req_desc)]) — machinery that already exists (resolve_inner + emit_tree_as_projection, wi224-tested at the resolver layer) and is proven correct end-to-end by the V8 pin. Concretely: extend the WI-419 sigma-class check in build_dep_projection from TIE-BREAKING (2+ covering entries) to GATING (reject a sole covering wildcard entry whose sigma-resolution disagrees, fall through to Strategy 3). With that, V9/V10 compute 12/122 — per-call dictionary passing serving the unbounded instance family, with each call site still emitting ONE finite compile-time construction; the unboundedness lives only in runtime tree depth. This is hypothesis (2) DEFER in the ticket taxonomy, and it needs NO new IR at the call site. RULE A-2 (op-scoped parity): the op-scoped channel needs (i) a call-site supply channel at all — ConcreteApplyWithin gates on the callee PARENT SORT chain today, so an op-scoped chain gets nothing and dies loud, (ii) impl-frame seeding on value-directed dispatch — the dispatcher must resolve and install the impl own requires at the value concrete type when pushing the impl frame (the V6 failing read), and (iii) op_requires_covers_call must see op-level type params (ROW 1 load rejection; spec par 5.4 declares them legal in requires positions). CANDIDATE (1) REJECT is REFUTED as the kernel rule: V8 proves per-call construction of a changed-type requirement is already wanted and working one level deep; rejecting varying-instance recursion would either also reject V8-shaped programs or draw an unprincipled line between them. A monomorphising BACKEND may still reject residual DeferToRequirement — a backend property, not the kernel rule (matches the anthill-cpp-gen stance recorded in WI-816). ON THE LAMBDA PREDICTION: the predicted operation-vs-lambda asymmetry DID NOT MATERIALIZE and cannot be measured until Rule A lands — both legs fail identically at the shared call-site defect, before any closure-snapshot semantics can bite. The witness also sharpens the prediction itself: the lambda that would NEED per-invocation instances (created ONCE, invoked at MANY types) is not WRITABLE — a closure arrow type is monomorphic, so every writable lambda is created at a FIXED instantiation, where creation-scope capture is semantically CORRECT; the honest lambda rendering (a FRESH closure per recursion step) failed only because its creation scope already held the wrong/missing dictionary — the control defect, not a closure defect. The closure channels themselves behaved correctly in every case that reached them (the V7 lambda leg executes and the failure surfaces later, in the impl frame). CONSEQUENCE FOR WI-816 (recorded as evidence, decision stays there): this witness produces NO case where lambda_within (per-invocation requirement supply to closures) is needed — Closure.requirements creation-scope capture plus a FIXED call-site supply plausibly serves the polymorphic recursion; REMEASURE the committed witness after Rule A lands (the d-row pins must flip to 12/122, the c-row pins to values), and only then settle delete-vs-implement; the one argument left for implement is codegen static visibility of the derived set, which this witness neither supports nor refutes. WHAT BECOMES OF Closure.requirements under Rule A: unchanged — it keeps snapshotting the creation frame; the fix makes the snapshot CORRECT by fixing what the creation frame holds. FOLLOW-UP WORK, recorded here per the no-ticket-spinoff feedback rule, for the user to file or fold: (F1) the Rule A sigma-gate in build_dep_projection Strategy 1; (F2) op-scoped supply channel + value-directed impl-frame seeding + op-param coverage, items i-iii above; (F3) the unconditioned-parametric-fact mis-pin (V5 pin) — an abstract covered call silently pinned via var-var unification, sound behaviour is a load rejection. ACCEPTANCE state: witness + control committed as 10 pinned tests (wi817_polyrec_requirement_test.rs) including positive controls for both harness verdicts; outcomes recorded verbatim; requires-eval-path question SETTLED (V8 correct end-to-end, neither reported spelling reproduces); rules stated with reasons above; full workspace suite run pending at the time of this note, result recorded in the delivery.", at: "2026-07-23T09:27:34Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-817", author: "user", content: "ADDENDUM, MULTI-HOP MEASUREMENT (user question: does a lambda passed operation-to-operation, each hop holding a DIFFERENT operation-level requires dictionary, survive?). New pinned test lambda_relay_chain_closure_correct_but_hop_dicts_forwarded: closure created under Desc[Leaf] (describe=1), relayed through Relay and Invoker which each hold their own CONCRETE Desc[Pebble] (describe=5), invoked two frames from creation. ALL bindings concrete — isolates dictionary FLOW from the changed-type recursion. Value coding: correct = 551 (fn(0)=1 creation dict + 10*5 + 100*5 hop dicts); hop-dict leak INTO the closure would read 555. MEASURED TODAY = 111 = 1 + 10*1 + 100*1: the CLOSURE HALF IS CORRECT — fn(0)=1, creation-scope capture holds across two foreign frames — but each hop OWN describe read 1 not 5: the Maker Leaf dictionary was FORWARDED into Relay and Invoker frames OVER each call site own concrete Pebble resolution. This BROADENS the Rule A finding: the Strategy-1 sole-wildcard-entry blind forward is wrong even for ALL-CONCRETE deps whenever the caller holds a same-spec wildcard requires — not only for changed-type recursion. The V8 pin works only because its driver holds NO requires (no covering entry, Strategy 3 reached). wi419 measured the 2-covering-entries disambiguation; the SOLE-entry different-instantiation forward was unmeasured and is wrong. Consequence: across EVERY measurement in this ticket the lambda machinery has not failed once; the operation-side call-site supply has failed everywhere it was asked to change instantiation. Suite: 11 tests, all green.", at: "2026-07-23T09:35:06Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-817", author: "user", content: "REOPENED behind WI-821 (user decision): the witness and its measurements stand delivered in substance (tests committed at 8d3ec77a, outcomes and rules recorded above), but the ticket's ORIGINAL question — is the case expressible as an operation and inexpressible as a lambda — is answerable only after the call-site supply defect is fixed. REMAINING DELIVERABLE, blocked on WI-821: REMEASURE the committed witness once the sigma-gate lands — the (d) pins flip to 1/12/122 (control AND lambda witness) and the relay pin to 551; then state the prediction's final verdict from the remeasured pair (if the lambda witness computes the same correct values as the control, the prediction is FALSE for every writable lambda and WI-816 option (a) gains its evidence; if the lambda leg diverges once the shared defect is gone, THAT divergence is the real witness). The op-scoped (c) and op-param (b) rows stay pinned and are NOT gated on WI-821 — they flip under their own future tickets.", at: "2026-07-23T09:55:03Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-822", description: some(value: "FIX the op-scoped requirement-supply gap (outcome c of WI-817's witness): an operation whose requires chain is OP-SCOPED (WI-448/WI-562 — requires written on the operation, member-style) never receives dictionaries at a call site, and the value-directed dispatch path pushes an impl frame WITHOUT the impl's own requires — the first dictionary read dies loud at eval. MEASURED, pinned in rustland/anthill-core/tests/include/wi817_polyrec_requirement_test.rs: op_scoped_single_conditional_level_dies_unbound — ONE conditional level (probe(wrap(leaf())) under op-scoped requires Desc[HT]) loads clean and dies Err(Internal(DeferToRequirement: requirement param __req_desc not bound in caller frame)); op_scoped_recursion_control_and_lambda_witness_fail_identically — the cross-sort mutual recursion (control AND lambda witness) works at depth 0 and dies the same way at depth 1+, so the failure needs NO recursion and NO lambda. MECHANISM, two legs — the first verified in source, the second CONSISTENT-WITH but the exact failing frame NOT directly observed (the error text does not name its frame; ESTABLISH it first with a probe): (LEG 1, call site) ConcreteApplyWithin classification gates on the CALLEE'S PARENT SORT having a requires chain (impl_parent_of_op / synth_req_names are sort-keyed), so a call to an op carrying only an op-scoped chain is never classified into the requirements channel and no dictionary rides the apply — the callee frame lacks __req_desc; the WI-562 licensing (op_requires_covers_call) legitimately leaves the SPEC-OP call for value-directed eval, but nothing supplies the op's own chain. (LEG 2, dispatch site) when value-directed dispatch selects an impl operation from the runtime value (wrap -> WrapDesc.describe), it pushes the impl frame without resolving the impl sort's own requires chain (WrapDesc requires Desc[T = E]) at the value's concrete type — even though at dispatch time that type IS concrete and resolution would succeed. FIX DIRECTION: (i) extend the call-site requirements channel to op-scoped chains — callee chain source = op-scoped chain if present, composed with the parent-sort chain, reusing build_dep_projection WITH the WI-821 sigma gate so per-dep sources are forwarded-or-constructed identically for both channel kinds and the pre-gate blind forward is never inherited; (ii) at value-directed dispatch, resolve the selected impl's requires chain at the VALUE'S concrete type and seed the impl frame; a requirement that fails to resolve there must be a LOUD dispatch error naming the requirement and the type, not an unbound-name Internal. Record at both sites why the two legs exist. DEPENDS WI-821 (the gate predicate is built there; building op-scoped supply on the ungated forward would bake the wildcard bug into a second channel). ACCEPTANCE: the two (c) pins FLIP and are updated to assert correct values (one-level: 12; recursion control AND lambda witness: 1 at depth 0, 12 at depth 1); op_scoped_sort_param_simple_concrete_works stays green; the member/List and WI-562-family suites stay green; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-821"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-823", description: some(value: "FIX the op-param requires coverage gap (outcome b of WI-817's witness): an op-scoped requires over the operation's OWN [T] type param (proposal 042) does not license the covered abstract spec-op call — the call is rejected at load with DispatchNoMatch — even with no recursion and no lambda. MEASURED, pinned in rustland/anthill-core/tests/include/wi817_polyrec_requirement_test.rs: op_param_requires_is_rejected_at_load — minimal form, operation probe[PT](x: PT) requires Desc[PT] = Desc.describe(x), rejected with (type mismatch in ...Desc.describe.dispatch: expected matching impl for per-call bindings, got no impl matches), BOTH binding spellings (Desc[PT] and Desc[T = PT]) identically; op_param_control_and_witness_rejected_identically — the full mutual-recursion control and lambda witness die at the SAME site (f's covered describe call, not the recursion). The kernel spec (docs/kernel-language.md par. 5.4) explicitly admits operation type parameters in requires positions, so this is a GAP, not a rule — and it blocks the PUREST rendering of the WI-817 question, the explicit-type-argument polymorphic recursion (f[Wrap[A = GT]]). SUSPECTED MECHANISM, NOT yet established — DISCRIMINATE FIRST with a probe before fixing: either (hypothesis 1) the op-scoped requires clause over an op param is never collected into TypingEnv.op_requires (set_op_requires source — does env.op_requires() contain the entry at classification time?), or (hypothesis 2) it is collected but op_requires_covers_call's sigma walk misses the identity because an op-level type param has TWO symbols (WI-708 dual-keying, bare vs op-scoped ns.op.T) and the requires entry's element and the call's carrier resolve to different keys. A one-probe discrimination decides which layer owns the fix. DEPENDS WI-822: without the op-scoped supply channel, fixing coverage alone merely converts the (b) load rejection into the (c) unbound-dict eval error — an intermediate state that would churn the pins twice; land after WI-822 so the (b) pins flip once, to values. ACCEPTANCE: the two (b) pins FLIP and are updated — the minimal probe loads and evaluates (drive = 1), the full op-param CONTROL and LAMBDA WITNESS load clean and evaluate 1/12/122 by depth (this is the ticket that finally makes the proposal-042 spelling of the witness RUN); WI-562/member coverage behaviour unchanged for sort-param op-scoped requires; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-822"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Open)
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-817", author: "user", content: "SHARPENED QUESTION (user framing, confirmed by a new measurement): the sort-based mechanism works by passing a dictionary AS IS; the searched cases are those where passing-as-is cannot be right because the OP-SCOPED requirement set CHANGES when passing to another operation. Accordingly the dependency chain now runs WI-821 (sigma-gate) -> WI-822 (op-scoped supply) -> WI-823 (op-param coverage) -> THIS ticket: the remeasure that answers the prediction runs on the OP-SCOPED channel, in both its sort-param and explicit op-param spellings; the sort-level remeasure alone is not sufficient. NEW MEASUREMENT completing the picture (pinned as op_scoped_relay_chain_correct_via_value_direction): the same relay chain that the SORT-level channel computes WRONG (111 — hops receive the maker dictionary as-is) computes the CORRECT 551 TODAY when its requirements are OP-SCOPED — not because op-scoped supply works (it supplies nothing) but because every describe resolves VALUE-DIRECTED: the runtime value itself selects Leaf/Pebble.describe and no dictionary is consulted, so the per-hop changed instantiation is served by the values. THE TWO CHANNELS FAIL ON COMPLEMENTARY SHAPES: dict-directed pass-as-is is wrong the moment the instantiation changes (sort-level 111, recursion 1/1/1); value-directed no-supply is right until a dictionary is semantically REQUIRED — a conditional impl whose body needs its own chain — where it dies unbound (op-scoped one-level and recursion pins). NEITHER channel serves the searched case (changed requirement AND dictionary genuinely needed), which is exactly what WI-821+WI-822 together must produce. Constraint recorded for WI-822: its supply channel must KEEP the value-directed 551 green — supplying real dictionaries may change the mechanism but not the value.", at: "2026-07-23T10:01:58Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-822", author: "user", content: "CONSTRAINT from a post-filing measurement (pinned as op_scoped_relay_chain_correct_via_value_direction in the WI-817 suite): an op-scoped relay chain whose requirement instantiation changes per hop ALREADY computes the correct 551 today, served entirely by VALUE-DIRECTED dispatch with no dictionaries. The supply channel this ticket adds must not regress that: after (i)+(ii) land, the same program must still compute 551 — whether the describes are then served by real per-hop dictionaries or still by value-direction is a design choice to record, but the value is fixed. This is the complement of the sort-level twin (111, wrong): dict-directed pass-as-is fails at changed instantiation; value-directed no-supply fails only where a dictionary is semantically required (a conditional impl's own chain — this ticket's unbound pins). The searched WI-817 case needs BOTH legs healthy.", at: "2026-07-23T10:01:58Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-817", author: "user", content: "TWO FURTHER MEASUREMENTS from user-directed probes, both pinned. (1) TWO DESCRIBERS FOR ONE CARRIER (the precondition for measuring provider SELECTION — with one describer the system just discovers the sole candidate and pins it, testing nothing): REJECTED AT LOAD — DispatchAmbiguous at BOTH describe sites (multiple impls match, coherence rule) plus the global check (ambiguous witness: 2 distinct witness sorts provide wi817.tds.Desc for carrier wi817.tds.Pebble, keep exactly one). This PINS A SPEC-VS-IMPLEMENTATION DIVERGENCE: kernel-language par. Instance-coherence specifies SCOPED selection (different scopes may resolve the same Spec[carrier] to different providers, the per-import choice); the implementation enforces GLOBAL one-provider-per-carrier. Consequence: the provider-selection dimension of this ticket's question (same carrier, requirement decides the impl) is UNCONSTRUCTIBLE today; dictionaries can vary only along the TYPE dimension (conditional instances — the committed recursion pins). The fork (implement scoped selection vs amend the spec to global coherence) is a language-design decision for the user; pinned as two_describers_for_one_carrier_rejected_globally, which flips to 75 if scoped selection is implemented. (2) DIFFERENT REQUIREMENT SETS PER OPERATION — the essence-of-bug measurement (user framing: all earlier fixtures gave every op the SAME singleton set, making as-is inheritance and correct per-callee supply indistinguishable; the essence is that the sets DIFFER and a hand-off must REBUILD the callee set): caller a requires (Desc[AT]) calls b requires (Desc[BT], Tagd[BT]) at concrete BT := Pebble while a holds AT := Leaf. MEASURED 31001 vs correct 35001, decomposing as: the OVERLAPPING dep is WRONG (a's Leaf dictionary wildcard-forwarded into b's Desc slot — describe(pebble) runs the Leaf impl) while the DISJOINT dep is CORRECT in the SAME call (no covering entry, Strategy 3 statically constructs the PebbleTag dictionary, tag = 3). The callee set is rebuilt exactly where the caller set does NOT overlap and inherited as-is exactly where it DOES — live proof that WI-821's fall-through construction already works and its gate only needs to stop the overlap-forward. Pinned as different_sets_overlapping_dep_forwarded_disjoint_dep_constructed; flips to 35001 under WI-821.", at: "2026-07-23T10:07:49Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-821", author: "user", content: "SHARPEST ACCEPTANCE TARGET, added after a user-directed probe (pinned as different_sets_overlapping_dep_forwarded_disjoint_dep_constructed in the WI-817 suite): a hand-off between operations with DIFFERENT requirement sets — caller (Desc[AT]) to callee (Desc[BT], Tagd[BT]) at concrete BT := Pebble — measures 31001 today vs 35001 correct: in ONE call the overlapping dep is wildcard-forwarded WRONG while the disjoint dep is Strategy-3-constructed RIGHT. This is live in-fixture proof that the fall-through this ticket gates to ALREADY WORKS for the very call whose sibling dep is being mis-forwarded; the sigma-gate only has to stop the overlap-forward. ACCEPTANCE addition: this pin flips to 35001.", at: "2026-07-23T10:07:49Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-648", author: "user", content: "FOLD-IN from WI-825 (filed 2026-07-23 in overlap with this ticket, now deleted in its favor) — new MEASUREMENTS and a DECIDING ARGUMENT from the WI-817 witness work, all relevant to this design. (1) MEASURED PIN of today's global rule (two_describers_for_one_carrier_rejected_globally, rustland/anthill-core/tests/include/wi817_polyrec_requirement_test.rs): two witness sorts providing Desc[T = Pebble] from two DIFFERENT namespaces are rejected at load — DispatchAmbiguous at every dispatch site (multiple impls match, coherence rule) plus the global check (ambiguous witness: 2 distinct witness sorts provide ... keep exactly one) — confirming this ticket's description of the current rule. The fixture is selection-discriminating by VALUE: a lambda created under the loud provider (describe=5) is invoked by an op under the quiet provider (describe=7) which also describes the same value itself; correct scoped/named selection = 75, both-loud = 55, both-quiet = 77 — ready as the acceptance program for the implement phase. (2) DECIDING ARGUMENT (user): an algebraic-specification language must express Int carrying BOTH the additive and multiplicative monoid — two instances of ONE spec on ONE carrier — and the VALUE cannot select between them (5 does not say which group it is in); only a requirement/scope/explicit argument can. Today the stdlib dodges Num-style: algebra.Ring BUNDLES both operation families into one spec. This settles the DIRECTION (selection must become expressible) while the mechanism (scoped vs named vs explicit-argument, and the coherence story) remains this ticket's design question. (3) TENSION TO RESOLVE IN THE PROPOSAL: kernel-language.md par. Instance coherence ALREADY promises per-scope selection (within one scope at most one provider; different scopes may resolve the same Spec[carrier] to different providers, the per-import choice) — while THIS ticket's framing keeps global coherence as the DEFAULT with named instances as the opt-in. The proposal must either implement the spec's scoped text, or amend the spec to global-default + named opt-in; today's implementation matches NEITHER text exactly. (4) CANDIDATE SYNTAX for the select leg (user suggestion: a scala-using analogue; sketch drafted in-session): reuse the call-site BRACKET binding channel with SPEC = WITNESS pairs — fold[Monoid = AddMonoid](xs) vs fold[Monoid = MulMonoid](xs) — since instances ALREADY have names (the witness sorts) and the runtime already IS parameter-insertion dictionary passing (operation-call-model.md names Scala using explicitly; apply_within.requirements + construct_requirement(witness, [...]) need NO new runtime). Typer validates the witness provides the spec at the call bindings and emits the construct_requirement tree; EXPLICIT beats implicit; unbound slots resolve as today; declaration-side twin names the requirement param (requires d: Desc[T]) so a body can FORWARD it. Guard: reject an op whose type-param name shadows a required spec short name. Explicit passing needs NO scope-aware candidate collection, sidesteps ambiguity per-site (the two-describer program becomes loadable with each site saying which), and is how the WI-817 provider-selection dimension becomes measurable without the scoped-resolution machinery. (5) RELATION: WI-817 (the requirement-witness remeasure) now DEPENDS ON THIS ticket for its provider-selection dimension; the sharpest related defect pins live in the same test file (the sigma-gate WI-821 family for the as-is-forward defect — independent of this ticket).", at: "2026-07-23T10:28:18Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-817", description: some(value: "CONSTRUCT the lambda-application case whose requirement CANNOT be fixed at construction, record what actually happens, THEN define the rules for handling it. CONSTRUCT FIRST -- the design decision is downstream of a witness, not the other way round. CONTEXT (from WI-816 feedback). Requirement analysis is UNIFORM: for every operation the typer derives the set of used requires from the body, and a LAMBDA BODY IS NO DIFFERENT (`for_each_child` descends `Expr::Lambda`, so `req_insertion::collect_classified` sees nested spec-op calls; MEASURED -- an uncovered call inside a lambda raises MissingRequiresForSpecOp with the span on the NESTED call, two binders deep, and does not over-fire). The asymmetry is BINDING TIME: an OPERATION has its requirements supplied BY THE CALLER PER CALL (operation-call-model.md:805, `callee.frame.requirements = caller.apply_within.requirements`), while a LAMBDA has them supplied BY ITS CREATOR ONCE (`reduce_lambda`, eval/eval.rs, snapshots `frame.requirements` into `Closure.requirements`; the doc calls this \"the only place the uniform rule is overridden\"). THE PREDICTION THIS TICKET MUST TEST, stated as a prediction because IT HAS NOT BEEN MEASURED: mutual recursion in which a requirement CHANGES TO THE TYPE-ARGUMENT TYPE needs a DIFFERENT instance at each step; per-call resolution can deliver that and resolve-once cannot; therefore the case should be EXPRESSIBLE AS AN OPERATION and INEXPRESSIBLE AS A LAMBDA. Do not build the fix on this sentence -- build the program and see. DELIVERABLE 1, THE WITNESS. Construct the minimal program: two mutually recursive operations where a call passes a DIFFERENT type argument so the required instance differs per step, with one leg routed THROUGH A LAMBDA so the requirement must ride the closure. Record VERBATIM which of these occurs: (a) clean load and correct evaluation -- the prediction is FALSE and the ticket says so and stops; (b) a LOAD error, with the exact variant and the span it lands on; (c) an EVAL error, with the exact variant; (d) a SILENTLY WRONG answer, which is the worst case and must be called out as such. COMMIT THE OPERATION-ONLY CONTROL BESIDE IT -- the same mutual recursion with no lambda. The whole claim is that the operation form works where the lambda form does not; IF THE CONTROL ALSO FAILS THE DIAGNOSIS IS WRONG and the ticket must report that instead of proceeding. HARNESS TRAPS, both already paid for once: `anthill run` MUTES load errors (exit code is not the loader's verdict) -- call `load_all` directly as the existing tests do; and after ANY trapped call every later `interp.call` on the SAME interpreter returns a bogus Internal error -- use a FRESH `interp_for` per evaluated case. A POSITIVE CONTROL is required: deliberately break something and confirm the harness actually reports it. PRE-EXISTING HAZARD THAT MAY MASK THE RESULT, unresolved: an agent reported that a sort-level `requires` makes ANY of that sort's operations untrappable at eval (`DeferToRequirement: projection index 0 out of range (requirement '__req_eq' bundles 0 sub-requirement(s))`) with no lambda in the program; a hand reproduction on a differently-shaped program produced a DIFFERENT error (`UnknownOperation`). NEITHER is established. If the witness trips either, ESTABLISH WHICH IS THE REAL BEHAVIOUR OF THE `requires` EVAL PATH FIRST -- a broken requires-eval path would make the lambda result uninterpretable, and would itself be the more serious bug. DELIVERABLE 2, THE RULES. With the witness in hand, state what SHOULD happen, with the reason. Candidates, none assumed: (1) REJECT -- keep requirements fully compile-time-resolvable and diagnose the varying-instance recursion as unresolvable, naming the requirement that cannot be pinned (what a monomorphising compiler does); (2) DEFER -- accept `DeferToRequirement` as the general case, the closure carrying its DERIVED requirement set and the dictionary being supplied per invocation, which is exactly what `lambda_within` would be the IR of; (3) some third rule the witness suggests. The USER'S LEADING HYPOTHESIS IS (2), DICTIONARY PASSING -- recorded as the hypothesis to beat, not as the answer. NOTE for (2): today's snapshot is also IMPRECISE, copying `frame.requirements` WHOLESALE rather than the set the body uses -- harmless for execution (the body reads by name via `var_ref(__req_*)`) but it is why anthill-cpp-gen REJECTS the variant, having no static record of which dictionaries a closure needs. WHATEVER IS CHOSEN, LEXICAL CAPTURE ITSELF MUST STAY for the monomorphic case: the body is type-checked in the CREATION scope, so running it against a callee's dictionaries would execute code against dictionaries it was never checked against (MEASURED -- a closure built under one spec evaluates correctly when invoked by a callee that cannot even NAME its spec op). RELATION TO WI-816: that ticket asks delete-vs-implement for the `*Within` IR variants; THIS ticket supplies the witness that decides it. If the constructed case needs dictionary passing, WI-816 option (b) wins and `lambda_within` is its IR; if the case can be cleanly rejected, option (a) wins and the variants go. ACCEPTANCE: the witness program AND its operation-only control are committed as tests pinning whatever behaviour is observed today (a test that pins a CURRENT DEFECT is fine and is the point -- name it as such so it is not mistaken for desired behaviour); the observed outcome is recorded verbatim in the ticket; the `requires`-eval-path question above is settled or explicitly excluded with a reason; the rules are stated with their reason; if (2) is chosen the design says WHERE the derived set is computed, HOW it rides the closure, and HOW it is supplied at invocation, and says what becomes of `Closure.requirements`. Full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-821", "WI-822", "WI-823", "WI-648"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-821", description: some(value: "FIX the call-site requirement-supply defect that WI-817's witness measured: a caller's dictionary is FORWARDED into a callee requirement slot whose element the call-site substitution maps to a DIFFERENT type. MECHANISM (verified in source): build_dep_projection (kb/typing.rs, ~11404) Strategy 1's entries_cover is WILDCARD-TOLERANT — a caller (requires Desc[GT]) covers a callee dep (Desc[FT]) whenever either element is a type param — and the WI-419 sigma-class check (disambiguate_covering_by_sigma) runs ONLY to break ties between 2+ covering entries, never to REJECT a sole one; so a single covering wildcard entry blindly emits var_ref(__req_desc), forwarding the caller's dictionary and SHADOWING Strategy 3, whose SLD construction of the Conditional tree (resolve_inner + emit_tree_as_projection) handles the changed type correctly when reached. MEASURED CONSEQUENCES, pinned in rustland/anthill-core/tests/include/wi817_polyrec_requirement_test.rs: (1) sort_level_recursion_silently_wrong_control_and_lambda_identical — polymorphic recursion (g re-enters f at Wrap[GT]) returns 1 at EVERY depth (correct: 1, 12, 122); the Leaf dictionary rides the whole recursion, the conditional WrapDesc instance is never consulted, and the Leaf impl runs on wrapped values — outcome (d), silent wrong answers on a clean load; (2) lambda_relay_chain_closure_correct_but_hop_dicts_forwarded — with ALL-CONCRETE bindings and no recursion, a relay chain measures 111 instead of 551: the closure half is CORRECT (the creation dictionary held across two foreign frames) while BOTH hops' own dictionaries arrive wrongly forwarded — so the defect is NOT confined to changed-type recursion; it hits ANY same-spec cross-instantiation call whenever the caller holds a covering wildcard entry. THE FIX (Rule A, WI-817 Deliverable 2): make sigma-class agreement a GATE on forwarding, in Strategy 1 AND Strategy 2's sub-chain matching (same predicate): a covering entry may be forwarded only when the dep's element sigma-resolves to the SAME unification class as the entry's element; a DISAGREEING sole entry is NO COVER and falls through to Strategy 3's construction — for the recursion that emits construct_requirement(WrapDesc, [var_ref(__req_desc)]), the Conditional-with-FromScope-leaf tree. The sigma machinery already computes exactly this agreement for the 2+ case — the change is consulting it for ONE covering entry and treating disagreement as no-cover rather than keeping first-match. WATCH: same-class forwarding must STAY — it is the dominant correct case (wi418 abstract forwarding, wi419 disambiguation, every delegating stdlib body) and must keep forwarding BY NAME, not re-constructing; and the sigma-less diagnostic path (req_insertion passes disambig = None) must keep today's behaviour — gate only when a call-site substitution is in hand, and say why at the site. ACCEPTANCE: the two (d) pins in wi817_polyrec_requirement_test.rs FLIP and are updated to assert their documented CORRECT values (recursion: 1/12/122 per depth, for the control AND the lambda witness; relay chain: 551); sort_level_single_conditional_level_is_correct stays green (12); wi418/wi419/wi613 forwarding suites stay green (no regression of same-class forwarding); full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. OUT OF SCOPE, deliberately (already recorded as siblings in WI-817 feedback, to be filed separately): the op-scoped channel parity (outcome-c pins — no call-site supply channel for op-scoped chains; value-directed dispatch pushes impl frames dict-less) and the op-param requires coverage gap (outcome-b pins, spec par. 5.4); those pins stay pinning under this ticket and flip only under their own tickets. RELATION: WI-817 is REOPENED and depends on THIS ticket — its remaining deliverable is the REMEASURE of the committed witness after this lands, which finally answers the original operation-vs-lambda prediction and supplies WI-816 its evidence; WI-816 stays behind WI-817."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-23T11:57:29Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-821", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. The ticket's stated fix (gate Strategies 1/2) was NECESSARY BUT NOT SUFFICIENT — the first gate attempt changed NOTHING observable (all three defect pins kept measuring 31001/111/1). The wildcard tolerance has FOUR sites, each re-creating the forward when the previous is gated: (1) Strategy 1 entries_cover — gated; (2) Strategy 2 sub-chains — gated on the sub-entry COMPOSED into caller scope (build_child_subst_map + substitute_in_spec; raw slot-space entries would sigma-disagree on CORRECT nested forwards); (3) resolve_inner's scope loop (requires_entry_covers_goal) — Strategy 3's FromScope short-circuits before provider candidates and emitted the IDENTICAL var_ref(__req_desc); sigma now rides ResolutionScope and gates it; (4) match_candidate_against_goal's WI-507 leniency left a Var::Rigid per-call element UNBOUND, so the conditional instance's sub-goal kept the raw impl param and died Cyclic — the rigid is now RECORDED (first-writer-wins; a stored rigid yields to an incoming concrete for order symmetry). The sigma-precise predicates are FOLDED into entries_cover/requires_entry_covers_goal as an Option<&SigmaCtx> mode (sigma-precise implies coarse; one walk decides) so the coarse and gating covers cannot drift. ACCEPTANCE DEVIATION, resolved in-ticket: the lambda WITNESS could not flip by supply-gating alone — inside the lambda the binder types from the declared hint with apply_fn's X unpinned (args typed before the per-call subst exists), so the inner f-call's dict was built against a wildcard. Fixed by generalizing WI-793 staging: a non-HOF arg pinning a callee type param (op-scoped AND parent-sort params, via elem_var_step) mentioned by a HOF param's type is typed first, and hint_instantiation_subst instantiates the lambda hint from the known sibling types (per-pair scratch-sigma probe so a failed pair's partial bindings cannot corrupt the hint). All three pins flipped and renamed: sort_level_recursion_correct_control_and_lambda_identical (1/12/122 both), lambda_relay_chain_closure_and_hop_dicts_correct (551), different_sets_overlapping_dep_and_disjoint_dep_both_constructed (35001); sort_level_single_conditional stays 12; op-scoped (b)/(c) pins and the value-directed 551 unchanged; wi418/wi419/wi613/wi227/wi224 green; full workspace 3464 passed 0 failed. SIBLING GAP recorded (no pin, no ticket): DeferToRequirement slot attribution (disambiguate_slot_by_sigma) keeps the soft pick_precise fall-back — a sole covering param-vs-DIFFERENT-param entry still attributes a body call and reads a sigma-disagreeing dict at runtime (WI-414 guards only concrete-vs-wildcard). Same silent-wrong class one site over; gating it changes classification outcomes (Deferred -> MissingRequires), so it needs its own measurement-first ticket.", at: "2026-07-23T11:57:30Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-821", author: "user", content: "/code-review (max): 15 findings reported (8 correctness CONFIRMED by live drives — two against a HEAD~1 worktree — 3 correctness PLAUSIBLE, 2 efficiency, 1 simplification, 1 test-coverage); 6 finder candidates REFUTED by driving (arrow-literal staging inertness, Node-carrier forward regression, σ-less-dispatch rigid mis-wiring, cross-namespace raw sort gate — that drive also re-validated the fix across 2- and 3-namespace splits — explicit-type-arg rigid spelling, positional-shorthand empty bindings). MECHANICALLY FIXED in the follow-up commit: the σ/coarse pair verdict folded into ONE binding_pair_covers (kills the drift channel between entries_cover and requires_entry_covers_goal, and gives WI-825 a single fix locus); hint_instantiation_subst probe now clones the ACCUMULATED σ and commits atomically (closes the cross-pair pollution, halves the unify work — Substitution::clone is O(1) imbl); op_tp_pinning_params kind-gates the parent lookup on SymbolKind::Sort and reads the memoized sort_type_params_as_pairs instead of the per-call full-symbol-table type_params_of_sort scan; Strategy 2's σ arm composes lazily and only for same-sort candidates (map at most once per slot, never for leaf-spec empty chains, no allocating compose for sort-mismatched entries); wi817 header pin inventory completed (two_describers) and the covers_goal doc's misattributed fold history corrected; NEW TEST sort_param_applier_witness_matches_control pins the parent-SORT-param staging half at 1/12/122 (was documented-but-unmeasured — it passes, the mechanism works). FILED for the non-trivial families: WI-825 (compound-element σ-blindness — the (d) defect one constructor level deeper, measured 12-vs-122), WI-826 (cover-walk orientation holes — bare bindingless requires bypass measured 1010001-vs-5010001, partial-binding asymmetry, empty-map Strategy-2 no-op), WI-827 (candidate-matching σ integration — Ref-spelled compound Cyclic, two-distinct-rigids half-wrong dict, head-only yield granularity, arm-2.5 symmetry), WI-828 (σ-refusal of unconstrained elements must be a load-time diagnostic — measured regressions Ok(11)→runtime-Internal and Ok(4)→unexplained WI-420). CONSCIOUSLY SKIPPED as minor (recorded here, no ticket): eagerly-formatted never-read NoMatch/Cyclic/Ambiguous diagnostic Strings (lazy formatting = ResolutionResult enum change); the wasted resolve_type_deep_value for VarRef-in-non-arrow slots (safe gate needs a σ-mention walk); narrowing the staging trigger to intersected tp sets (behavior-order change); ResolutionScope named constructors; threading the op symbol on OperationInfoFull instead of beside it.", at: "2026-07-23T13:21:29Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-825", author: "user", content: "NUMBER RECYCLED — disambiguation for readers: an earlier, UNRELATED WI-825 (scoped/named instance selection) was filed and then folded into WI-648 earlier the same day (see WI-648's feedback 'FOLD-IN from WI-825 ... now deleted in its favor' and commit 4cf167c8's subject); the CLI reissued the freed number to THIS ticket. Any WI-825 reference recorded before 2026-07-23T13:00Z means the folded instance-selection ticket (now WI-648); this ticket is the compound-element σ-gate fix from the WI-821 /code-review.", at: "2026-07-23T13:34:41Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-828", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. DECIDED: REJECT at load, no fallback semantics — any pick for a genuinely-unconstrained element is a guess (caller-param instantiation was exactly the pre-WI-821 unsound forward; an ambiguous-provider pick is the arbitrariness DispatchAmbiguous already refuses; deferring to eval is the anti-pattern this ticket kills). Both driven fixtures produce located load diagnostics via new TypeError::UnsatisfiableRequirement carrying a RequirementRefusal (dep, unconstrained elements, sigma-refused covers, construction outcome): (a) direct call — the WI-415 classify site surfaces Err instead of dispatch_dict: None; (b) eta — the WI-420 site raises the same variant (eta-flagged); the bare WI-420 message remains only for non-refusal classes. GATE per the CAUTION: refusal = sigma-refused coarse cover (Strategy 1 direct OR Strategy 2 slot-COMPOSED, mirroring S2's own one-level composition) OR S3 Ambiguous; bare NoMatch (WI-415/418 uncovered-abstract gap) and Cyclic (WI-827 sphere) keep the silent no-dict behavior; op-scoped chains never reach the build (parent-SORT chain gate) so the (c) pins are untouched by construction — wi817 15/15 green. MEASUREMENT DEVIATION: fixture (a)'s Strategy 3 does NOT go Ambiguous(2) on HEAD — the sigma-gated candidate match refuses an unbound element against ground heads outright, so construction terminates NoMatch, rendered via ResolutionResult::NoMatch's hint (its FIRST-EVER reader; the eagerly-formatted hint had no consumer since WI-821). The Ambiguous trigger leg is kept but unexercised by the driven fixtures. FIXTURE NOTES: (b) needs the sort-member-import spelling (import ns.Maker.{bump}) — bare/dotted cross-sort refs resolve nothing — and a caller carrying the covering requires (else HEAD~1 would also have refused). A nullary call's SPELLING decides constraint: '-> CT = Maker.make()' loads clean (return-type unification pins MT := CT into a sigma-AGREEING forward); the unconstrained shape is the call in spec-op-argument position. /code-review (4 agents) applied: kernel-language.md spec-sentence CLOBBER restored (my edit ate the Standalone-entity lead-in); explain walk extended to S2 slot-composed covers — the one spelling where load-clean-die-at-eval survived the first cut, now pinned by nested_cover_refusal_is_also_a_load_diagnostic; the duplicate S3 resolve on clean-load silent paths eliminated by threading build_dep_projection's own terminal ResolutionResult out (opt-in out-param; 2x cost gone, drift surface halved); refusal Boxed so the hot Result stays small; dep goal derived once via goal_from_requires_entry; diagnostic strings built only after the trigger decides. RESIDUAL MIRROR (doc-commented at explain_dep_refusal): the S1/S2 cover re-scan must track WI-826/827 walk changes or the diagnostic silently stops firing for that spelling. Spec updated (kernel-language.md requires-declaration section: requirement supply checked at load). 6 new tests; full workspace 3470 passed 0 failed.", at: "2026-07-23T16:23:00Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-828", description: some(value: "DECIDE + surface the σ-refusal of GENUINELY-UNCONSTRAINED requirement elements as a load-time diagnostic — today it lands as silent dict-None → load-clean-then-runtime crash (/code-review findings 4 and 6, both CONFIRMED by driving HEAD vs HEAD~1 in a worktree). MEASURED REGRESSIONS: (a) nullary/return-only element — 'operation make() -> MT' on Maker requires Desc[MT], called from Box requires Desc[CT]: HEAD~1 measured Ok(Int(11)) (the pre-WI-821 sole wildcard cover silently instantiated MT := CT — loose but ran); HEAD loads CLEAN then dies Err(Internal(DeferToRequirement: __req_desc not bound in caller frame)) — the σ-class of unbound MT can never equal the caller's rigid, Strategy 3 goes Ambiguous (2 instances), require_complete → None, ConcreteApplyWithin{dispatch_dict: None} → eval plain apply. The refusal itself is defensible (MT is truly unconstrained — the old forward guessed); the FAILURE MODE is the repo's named anti-pattern (loud-error principle: load-clean-then-eval-crash). (b) eta path — Applier.apply1(bump) with bump on a requires-carrying Maker, expected arrow Int64→Int64 pins nothing: HEAD~1 Ok(Int(4)); HEAD rejects the LOAD with the WI-420 'unsatisfiable requirement' TypeError — loud, but names neither the σ refusal nor the unconstrained element, so the migration story is a riddle. FIX SHAPE: in build_concrete_dispatch_dict, when require_complete yields None for a CROSS-SORT call with a non-empty chain AND a σ was in hand, emit a located load diagnostic naming the dep, the refused covering entry (or Ambiguous provider set), and the unconstrained element — instead of silently classifying dispatch_dict: None. CAUTION: the op-scoped (c)-row pins (op_scoped_single_conditional_level_dies_unbound, op_scoped_recursion_...) pin the SAME runtime-unbound outcome for dict-less flows that must NOT get a new load error under this ticket (their supply channel is the separate WI-822-sphere work) — gate the diagnostic on the σ-refusal path (sort-level chain, disambig was Some) so those pins stay green, and decide explicitly whether a program like (a) should be REJECTED (diagnostic) or given a defined fallback semantics (must NOT resurrect the blind wildcard forward). ACCEPTANCE: the two driven fixtures produce located load-time diagnostics (or the decided semantics) instead of runtime Internal / unexplained WI-420; op-scoped (c) pins unchanged; wi817 (15) + full workspace green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-23T16:23:00Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-825", description: some(value: "FIX the σ-gate's COMPOUND-element blindness (WI-821 /code-review finding 1, CONFIRMED by live drive): the cover pair verdict is HEAD-only — is_type_param_value tests only the head, so a compound element (Wrap[A = CT]) classifies concrete and two same-head compounds with different interiors compare via dispatch_values_match, whose fallback compares head sort symbols with args ignored (types_lesseq REJECTS the pair first, so the head fallback is the ACCEPTING leg; both legs never consult ctx.subst). MEASURED: caller requires Desc[T = Wrap[A = CT]] handing off to a callee dep σ-instantiated to Desc[T = Wrap[A = Wrap[A = CT]]] FORWARDS the shallower dict — single hand-off 12 where 122 is correct; compound-element recursion 12 at EVERY depth (correct 12/122/1222) — the exact pre-WI-821 (d) signature one constructor level deeper. Positive control (no cover in scope) constructs the depth-2 dict correctly (122), so Strategy 3 handles the shape; only the gate lets the forward through. FIX LOCUS is ONE place now: binding_pair_covers (kb/typing.rs — the folded per-pair verdict, whose doc carries a KNOWN LIMIT note pointing here). Rule: under σ, a (c,c) pair whose sides are COMPOUND must compare σ-STRUCTURALLY — same head AND each param-position pair recursively binding_pair_covers-σ (σ-classes at param leaves, dispatch_values_match at ground leaves); head-only acceptance is what re-admits the defect. WATCH: the coarse (None) mode must keep today's head tolerance (diagnostic path); concrete GROUND same-head pairs (Wrap[Int64] vs Wrap[Int64]) must still cover (types_lesseq already accepts identical grounds — verify before relying on the fallback deletion). ACCEPTANCE: the driven fixture (write it as a wi821 test: DeepHolder requires Desc[T = Wrap[A = DT]] hand-off shape from the /code-review verifier) measures 122 and 12/122/1222; wi817 suite (15 tests incl sort_param_applier_witness) + wi418/wi419/wi613 + full workspace green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-23T16:52:39Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-825", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED as specified — the ticket's prediction was exact: driven pre-fix measurements matched verbatim (single hand-off 12, recursion 12 at EVERY depth, positive control 122), and the fix locus was ONE place. sigma_pair_precise's (c,c) arm now compares a both-compound pair sigma-STRUCTURALLY: same base sort (same_sort_canonical) + per-argument recursion (sigma-classes at param leaves, dispatch_values_match at ground leaves); named args paired via the shared binding-key rule (binding_for_param + BindingKeyMatch::for_bases — enrolled per /simplify review, NOT a fifth hand-rolled find(same_label)); a==b fast path restores O(1) for identical hash-consed compounds (identical term = identical instantiation). The shared verdict had THREE consumers, not two: binding_pair_covers (entries_cover + requires_entry_covers_goal), entry_sigma_matches_subst (WI-613 tie-break), reached_carrier_matches_call (WI-653 transitive coverage) — all compound-aware now, header names all three. Coarse (None) mode untouched per WATCH; ground same-head pairs still cover (wi825 ground_compound_cover_still_serves). ACCEPTANCE MET: wi825_compound_element_test measures 122 + 12/122/1222 (4 tests, incl the no-cover positive control); wi817 15/15 incl sort_param_applier_witness; wi828 + wi418/wi419/wi613 green; full workspace 85/85 result lines 0 failed (x2 runs, jobs capped 6). NAMED RESIDUAL (no ticket): both-ARROW/both-NAMED-TUPLE element pairs still accept via dispatch_values_match's head fallback (same meta-ctor symbol, interiors ignored) — same defect class, unclosed because no sigma-gated path is known to carry those forms as elements; documented at parameterized_parts.", at: "2026-07-23T16:52:39Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-829", author: "user", content: "RELATED findings from the same WI-825 /code-review (σ-cover-verdict completeness), NOT fixed by the WI-825 follow-up cleanup commit — verification was cut short by a Fable-5 credit exhaustion, so most are PLAUSIBLE not driven. (1) CONFIRMED-trace: RequirementRefusal::render (typing.rs ~11424) emits the WI-821 'covers only as a wildcard / different type parameter (WI-821)' clause for the both-COMPOUND structural refusals explain_dep_refusal now collects, where neither side is a wildcard/param and the unconstrained scan (~11529, head-only is_type_param_value) names no element — misdiagnosis; NOTE wi828's assert_refusal_diagnostic requires the literal 'WI-821' + 'CT' pieces, so a render fix must update those asserts. (2) PLAUSIBLE: explain_dep_refusal mirrors only the DEP-driven entries_cover walk (~11476); an ENTRY-driven S3 FromScope σ-refusal (multi-param dep, caller binds fewer) leaves refused_entries empty → Ok(None) → silent load-clean-die-at-eval, the anti-pattern WI-828 removed, newly reachable via compound refusal. (3) PLAUSIBLE regression: the both-parameterized arm hard-refuses base/arity mismatch WITHOUT the dispatch_values_match fallthrough, dropping types_lesseq's provider-admissibility (List provides Stream) + width-tolerant acceptances — may refuse correct programs (suite green ⇒ latent). (4) PLAUSIBLE: interior param σ-bound-to-concrete hits the syntactic mixed rule → over-refusal vs the pre-fix head accept. (5) PLAUSIBLE residuals (same head-fallback class one form over, undocumented/mis-documented): bare-vs-applied same-sort (requires Desc[T=Wrap] covers any depth), and interior arrow/tuple leaves (Wrap[A=arrow1] vs Wrap[A=arrow2] via meta-ctor head match) — parameterized_parts' 'no σ-gated path carries those forms' doc claim is false for interiors (Relation[T=(tuple)] is live, WI-764). (6) PLAUSIBLE: named-args one-directional pairing is not a bijection under duplicate labels (sort_binding lists lack check_label_unique) or dual-spelled keys → a named_b component uncompared. (7) reached_carrier_matches_call (WI-653 op-requires license) + entry_sigma_matches_subst (WI-613 tie-break) both silently strictened for compounds with no test coverage.", at: "2026-07-24T05:44:26Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-779", author: "user", content: "2026-07-24 -- re-scoped by the 057 design merge (docs/proposals/057-extent-seam.md, decomposition item 4). The fix is CONTENT-KEYED, not RuleId-typed: the write peer of read_facts(Refuse) -- keyed on the head content, O(1)-gated by has_bodied_rule (WI-812), refusing loud (blanket, WI-772) and naming the rule via print_rule. It surfaces NO RuleId/FactId (the seam end-state invariant: no RuleId in any interface signature). Methods are pub(crate); RuleId stays internal to the resident path only, while the FactId two-call API survives until R3. The hazard is IDB-only BY THE MODEL -- an EDB functor cannot have a bodied head (LoadError) -- so this is an internal step of the RESIDENT write path; a mounted extent never reaches it. The RuleId-typed draft written 2026-07-22 (pub resolve_asserted_fact -> Option<RuleId> / check_retractable_fact(RuleId)) is SUPERSEDED -- re-derive content-keyed; that parked draft is kept as a reference in the session scratchpad. WI-780/R3 later absorbs this refusal into the write seam.", at: "2026-07-24T07:02:15Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-780", author: "user", content: "2026-07-24 -- refiled against the merged proposal 057 (docs/proposals/057-extent-seam.md); the extent seam is ONE proposal -- read half delivered, write half = WI-780 = decomposition items 5-7. (5) One write home (R2): store_registry/store_monotonicity off Interpreter onto kb.extents; register_store -> register_mirror/register_extent_owner. (6) The write seam + identity cutover (R3): assert/update/retract_persistent owning the store-kb ordering + the 053 guard; store-native identity via a digested RowKey -- the store declares its KEY (its notion of Id), the write analog of QueryPattern (shared bound carrier, DISTINCT type: superset vs unique-key/exactly-one), NO RuleId/FactId in any signature; NonMonotonicStore.update; the write overlay; FactId/find_fact/Handle deletion; the DECLARATIVE config binding (007 section 8 config-in-file, but single-owner mount not route rules: an extent_owner fact + a host factory + a bootstrap store, replacing hardcoded register_store -- writability materializes from fact_monotonicity, ExtentProfile.writability stays Option); every anthill consumer migrated (anthill-todo store.anthill: find_fact -> content-keyed forget, the two-flush replace -> update). (7) Ratchet (R4): kb.retract -> pub(crate); InMemoryExtentSource write half + write conformance suite. Subsumes WI-779 (item 4) at R3. Still depends WI-797 (delivered). The million-fact/EDB-IDB feedback below is now realized: RuleId is IDB-resident-only; boundary identity is store-native, surfaced as Term (a compound entity, never FactId); read searches by a Term pattern, write keys by a ground Term digested to RowKey.", at: "2026-07-24T07:02:25Z")
