fact WorkItem(id: "WI-437", description: some(value: "anthill-todo: PLUGGABLE BACKEND -- per-project work-item data source selection (examples/github-todo/docs/pluggable-backend.md; the multi-developer driver: git conflicts in workitems.anthill). Project config selects one of: local (files, today's IndexedFileStore via the WI-009 SingleFile convention), github (work items are GitHub issues), api (future standardized remote). Natural shape = the path-dependent-types design sec.5 factory: openStore(cfg) -> C ensures Store[C, ...] returning local/github/api behind one store interface -- THE first real consumer of WI-402's existential half (ensures Spec[C] + dict-out), which is parked on this ticket for exactly that reason. Builds on the delivered WI-009 cutover (backend selection lives in the bundle's store layer; WorkItemStore spec already separates spec from FileBasedWorkitemStore impl). NOTE from the 2026-06-11 WI-200 analysis: multi-instance Modify state (WI-200) is NOT needed for the CLI path -- one backend instance per invocation, distinct backend SORTS occupy distinct slots, and the bundle state rides Cell (per-allocation arena identity); WI-200 becomes relevant only for the api/server direction (many stores in one process)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-009", "WI-402", "WI-699", "WI-830"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-830", description: some(value: "Extent migration: make the resident FileStore an explicit mirror, not an extent owner. Move persistence registration into kb.extents with a mirror role; preserve resident WorkItem/Tag facts and bodied rules as the query source, pull persisted facts at load, and shadow resident writes to the mirror. Define declarative binding syntax with an explicit role so future SQL/GitHub sources can be owners without changing FileStore semantics. Prerequisite for WI-437 pluggable backend selection."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-780", author: "user", content: "DECISION (2026-07-24, supersedes the R3 line that said FactId is deleted in favor of content-only Term addressing): R3 replaces resident-only FactId = Handle(Fact, RuleId.raw) with source-neutral opaque FactRef, paired with visible content as library StoredRef[T] (docs/proposals/library/005-stored-references.md). The Rust seam returns StoredRow { row, reference: FactRef }; FactRef privately selects source + store-native RowKey, so an external entity does not know whether a row is resident, mirrored, or owner-backed. Persist/query-with-reference return StoredRef[Term]; retract/update accept FactRef; update returns a replacement StoredRef atomically. FactRef is KB-session-scoped/non-persistable unless the row itself has a durable domain key. WI-779 remains FactId-compatible interim safety only. R3 atomically migrates all in-tree clients then deletes FactId/find_fact/Literal::Handle(Fact, RuleId); do not keep a long-lived FactId alias.", at: "2026-07-24T17:05:00Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-829", description: some(value: "DeferToRequirement sole-cover arm does not σ-gate a compound element: the direct-dispatch defer path forwards the frame's shallower dictionary to a deeper in-body call. DRIVEN REPRO (WI-825 /code-review, max effort): sort DHolder2 / sort HT=? / requires Desc[T=Wrap[A=HT]] / operation d2(y:HT)->Int64 = Desc.describe(wrap(wrap(y))), called DHolder2.d2(leaf()), returns Ok(Int(12)) where 122 is correct (the wi825 positive_control pins 122 for the same depth-2 shape). ROOT CAUSE: WI-825 made binding_pair_covers/sigma_pair_precise compound-aware for the FORWARDING path (build_dep_projection), but the classification path find_requires_location/find_requires_slot returns a SOLE coarse cover without consulting σ — the sole-cover arms [(only,_)] => Some(only.clone()) (typing.rs ~13354) and [only] => Some(*only) (~13124); σ is tie-break-only (pick_precise) and the dispatch trigger dispatch_spec_op_cached passes sigma=None (~18080). entry_matches_subst coarse-accepts the shallow-vs-deep compound pair via dispatch_values_match's head fallback (Wrap==Wrap, interiors ignored), so DeferToRequirement reads the wrong-depth frame dict. This is the SOLE-ENTRY compound variant of the gap WI-821 delivery-feedback already flagged (disambiguate_slot_by_sigma soft pick_precise fall-back, one site over). FIX DIRECTION: σ-gate the sole-cover selection in find_requires_location/find_requires_slot (or make the DeferToRequirement classification construct/refuse when the sole cover σ-disagrees), sharing the WI-825 compound-aware verdict. ACCEPTANCE: the driven d2 fixture measures 122; wi825/wi817/wi828 + full workspace green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-825"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-24T19:42:44Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-827", description: some(value: "INTEGRATE σ into provider-candidate matching — match_candidate_against_goal is σ-blind and spelling/granularity-sensitive exactly where WI-821 taught it to record rigids (/code-review findings 3, 5, 8, 11 — three CONFIRMED by live drives, one PLAUSIBLE). MEASURED DEFECTS: (a) a requires-clause COMPOUND element (requires Desc[T = Wrap[A = HT]]) keeps Ref-SPELLED components — substitute_spec_via_subst deliberately preserves abstract elements as written Refs — and the record arm (matches! Var::Rigid only) SKIPS them, so the conditional sub-goal keeps the raw impl param, the σ-gated FromScope refuses it, resolution dies Cyclic → dict None → runtime '__req_desc not bound'; the semantically identical whole-param spelling (requires Desc[T = WT] at WT := Wrap[A = rigid]) works (measured Ok(52) vs Internal). (b) TWO DISTINCT rigids for one impl param (diagonal provider fact Desc[T = Pair[A = E, B = E]] at per-call Pair[A = rigidGT, B = rigidHT]) are accepted with only the FIRST recorded — measured Ok(11) = half-wrong dict (sound = refusal; both-correct = 15); the type-param early-return never compares against the occupied slot. (c) the stored-rigid-yields-to-concrete arm checks only the stored HEAD: a stored compound-containing-rigid (Wrap[A = rigid]) vs a structurally different incoming compound drops the candidate in BOTH orders (NoMatch → dict None → unbound), while the bare-rigid twins ACCEPT the same structural disagreement (measured Ok(9)/Ok(12)) — the verdict depends on which side of the fact the compound is written, not on the disagreement. (d) PLAUSIBLE: arm (2.5) (self-provides alignment) was not given the rigid-yield symmetry — values_structurally_equal(rigid, concrete) rejects the whole candidate when arm (1) recorded a rigid and (2.5) later threads a concrete into the same param. DEEPER FORM (altitude review): thread the σ ctx (ResolutionScope.sigma is already in resolve_inner's hand at the collect_provides_candidates call) into candidate matching and classify each per-call element ONCE via sigma_class — spelling-neutral: no σ-class → concrete arm; rigid-terminal class → record the canonical rigid (any spelling); global class → unconstraining skip — and define rigid-conflict semantics deliberately (two distinct rigids = refuse or unify-check, compound granularity = component-wise). σ-less consumers (resolve_at_goal etc.) keep today's behavior — verified no divergence exists there (two claims driven and REFUTED on both commits). ACCEPTANCE: the three driven fixtures flip to sound verdicts (compound-requires witness = depth-coded values, not Cyclic; diagonal-rigid = refusal or both-correct; compound-stored yield consistent across spellings); wi817 (15) + wi224/wi227 + full workspace green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-24T19:47:22Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-826", author: "user", content: "DISCOVERED WHILE BUILDING THE FINDING-10 FIXTURE, adjacent but OUT OF SCOPE (present on HEAD, not introduced by this ticket): Strategy 2's nested forward emits requirement_at_sort(slot, k) where k indexes the SPEC's static direct_requires_chain, but the RUNTIME bundle is built from the resolution tree's sub_resolutions, which come from the CARRIER's requires. So a carrier providing a spec UNCONDITIONALLY while the SPEC itself carries requires resolves to ResolvedRequiresNode::Leaf, whose emit_tree_as_projection builds construct_requirement(impl, NIL) - an EMPTY bundle the static index still points into. Repro: 'sort Mid { sort MT2 = ?; requires Desc[T = MT2]; operation midop(x: MT2) -> Int64 }' + 'sort Gem { entity gem; fact Desc[T = Gem]; fact Mid[MT2 = Gem]; operation describe(x: Gem) = 5; operation midop(x: Gem) = 50 }' + 'sort Outer { requires Mid[MT2 = Gem]; operation o(n) = BOps.b(gem()) }'. Loads CLEAN (Strategy 2 finds the composed cover Desc[T = Gem] and forwards), then panics at eval: 'requirement_at_sort: index 0 out of range (slot has 0 sub-requirements)' (eval/requirement_arena.rs project). Loud rather than silently wrong, but the load-time verdict and the runtime bundle disagree about a slot's shape - the Leaf arm should either discharge the SPEC's own requires into the bundle, or the load should refuse the forward. Consequence for this ticket: wi826's bound-slot fixture (bound_slot_nested_forward_is_found_at_load) is pinned at LOAD only, with the reason recorded in its doc comment.", at: "2026-07-24T20:14:40Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-826", author: "user", content: "FINDING 9 (partially-bound entry) REFUTED BY DRIVING - its premise is false. The ticket predicted a partially-bound 'requires Desc[T = X]' (U omitted) would be key-MISSING, refused by Strategy 1's dep-driven walk and accepted by Strategy 3's entry-driven one. It is never key-missing: a BRACKETED requires is stored as a SortView whose omitted parameters are FILLED with the spec's own param ref, so 'requires Pair[P = MT]' carries 'Q = Pair.Q'. Driven: the 3-sort fixture (Holder requires Pair[P=Leaf,Q=Leaf] -> Partial requires Pair[P=MT] -> Callee requires Pair[P=CP,Q=CQ]) does not even load on HEAD - WI-828 refuses it with 'element Q = Pair.Q is unconstrained at this call site'. BOTH walks see Q and both hand it to binding_pair_covers, so there is no orientation asymmetry to fix. Only the BRACKET-LESS form (no SortView at all, hence genuinely zero bindings) produces the missing key - i.e. finding 2 is the whole of the defect. Pinned as wi826's partially_bound_entry_fills_the_omitted_param_and_is_refused_at_load so the refutation stays refuted. FINDING 2 reproduced EXACTLY as measured (1010001 pre-fix, 5010001 post-fix). FINDING 10's sigma refusal was already the behavior on HEAD; the fix makes it principled (a bare-requires slot's composition map is empty, so its sub-entry cannot denote anything in caller scope) and it is now pinned.", at: "2026-07-24T20:14:56Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-826", author: "user", content: "/simplify (4 agents) APPLIED. Reuse+Simplification converged on the same locus: the hoisted key iteration was still the pre-existing hand-rolled find(same_label), which the binding_for_param doc explicitly forbids ('any bindings-list lookup by param key goes through binding_for_param', WI-726/764/768/769/825) - enrolled BOTH legs, so the identity-first pass is gained and one function can no longer decide 'entry names goal key' and 'goal names entry key' by two different rules; binding_for_param's 'keep this list honest' enrollment ledger updated. Altitude found the REAL doc defect: my leg-2 justification ('the demand is a DERIVED goal that sort_goal_from_subst drops keys from') is CALLER-SPECIFIC and false for the Strategy-3 caller, whose goal is goal_from_requires_entry(dep) - the very same WRITTEN dep Strategy 1 just handed entries_cover. Re-justified by the QUESTION each walk answers instead: entries_cover asks 'does this dictionary SATISFY that slot' (a slot naming nothing takes any dictionary - the callee said nothing to contradict it), requires_entry_covers_goal asks 'IS this THE dictionary for this goal' and a yes SHORT-CIRCUITS construction, so an unnamed element must answer 'cannot tell'. Altitude also named the resulting DUAL asymmetry (entry names a key, goal names none: the two walks give OPPOSITE verdicts; Strategy 1 returns first so entries_cover's is the reachable one) - documented as deliberate + currently unobservable, NOT driven. Orientation is now TYPE-ENFORCED (newtype Supply/Demand around the two same-typed slices) so a transposed call - literally this ticket's defect - is a compile error. Also applied: entries_cover's stale doc updated for the delegation (the WI-802 stale-neighbour failure mode); explain_dep_refusal's mirror note trimmed to the actionable half; spec now states the NON-symmetric converse (fewer elements weakens a SUPPLY and widens a DEMAND); one-line alloc win in type_param_sym_of_binding (resolve_sym borrows, no to_string needed - 15 call sites, and this diff doubled its frequency); test file: BOPS const hoisted (3 verbatim copies), the rot-prone WI-828 prose substring dropped from the refutation assert (only the rendered element witnesses the fill), the bound-slot doc trimmed to the one bit it actually asserts, sync note added to the shared Desc/Leaf block. SKIPPED with reasons: hoisting with_instances into tests/common (5th copy, but touches 4 sibling files - already tracked as a WI-829 residual); a debug_assert on sort_goal_from_subst's three STRUCTURAL drops (out of diff, and the re-justification no longer depends on that distinction); efficiency's bitmask/spec_qn-laziness (its own verdict: 'micro- to sub-microsecond against a 400ms load, nothing justifies restructuring'); sharing stdlib loads across the 5 tests (needs a cached-KB helper in common/, pays off suite-wide not here); a should_panic companion for the Leaf-empty-bundle gap (pins an incidental panic string; the feedback record is the right home).", at: "2026-07-24T20:28:33Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-826", author: "user", content: "FOLLOW-UP CANDIDATE (no ticket, recorded here): refuse a BRACKET-LESS 'requires Spec' at LOAD when Spec has type parameters. Corpus impact is ZERO - all 18 bracket-less requires in .anthill sources target parameter-less specs (anthill.cli.Main, Logic.Minimal, Logic.Constructive, and the test-only Base/Pingable) - and it turns a spelling that loads clean and silently means 'wildcard' into an early named error, per CLAUDE.md 'prefer a loud error over a silent skip'. It would also retire the reachable half of the dual asymmetry documented at requires_entry_covers_goal. NOT a replacement for the walk fix, and the altitude review gave the decisive reason: unwrap_spec_view_value returns (sym, EMPTY) for ANY spec value that is not a SortView application - the ViewHead::Ref/Ident arms and the non-SortView functor arm - so a denoted (Value::Node) spec for a parameterized spec arrives keyless no matter how it was written. A parser-level guard closes the syntactic door only; the cover walk is where the invariant is CONSUMED and is total over every producer. Also REJECTED at review: normalizing bracket-less into a filled SortView. The loader's fill loop (load.rs ~4067-4090) walks sort_base_subst, which carries OPERATION slots as well as type params and auto-binds them via find_operation_in_scope, so normalizing would change the op-binding shape of all 18 existing sites and the stored SortRequiresInfo fact shape that direct_requires/spec_base_functor, the flatten key, the resolve_cache key and the nominal-subtype chain all read - large blast radius, and it still would not cover the Value::Node producer.", at: "2026-07-24T20:28:49Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-826", description: some(value: "FIX the cover-walk ORIENTATION holes that let bindingless/partially-bound requires spellings bypass or over-refuse the WI-821 σ-gate (/code-review findings 2, 9, 10 — finding 2 CONFIRMED by live drive, 9/10 code-traced PLAUSIBLE). (1) MEASURED: a bare bindingless 'requires Desc' (no brackets — legal, loads clean, zero stdlib/test occurrences) scope entry covers ANY goal: requires_entry_covers_goal iterates only ENTRY bindings and returns true on empty BEFORE the σ mode, so Strategy 3's FromScope forwards the middle sort's dict over a CONCRETE downstream goal — 3-hop drive (AOps requires Desc[AT] → MOps bare requires Desc → BOps at concrete Pebble) measured 1010001 where 5010001 is correct. The empty early-return is literally redundant (the loop falls through to true) — the defect is ORIENTATION: the walk never examines goal keys the entry lacks. (2) The same orientation asymmetry means a PARTIALLY-bound entry (multi-param spec, requires Desc[T = X] with U omitted) is refused by Strategy 1 (dep-driven walk, missing key → false) yet accepted by Strategy 3's scope cover (entry-driven walk checks only T) — resurrecting the exact var_ref forward the gate refused, falsifying the ResolutionScope.sigma doc's guarantee; latent until a multi-param-spec fixture exists. (3) Strategy 2's composition is an EMPTY-MAP no-op for a bindingless slot entry (build_child_subst_map finds no bindings), leaving the sub-entry in slot-space where — per the hunk's own comment — a correct nested forward always σ-disagrees: nested forwarding under a bare-requires slot is refused where the coarse mode forwards requirement_at_sort correctly. DECIDE + FIX: under σ, a bindingless or partially-bound cover should be NO cover (fall through to construction) or a deliberate, documented wildcard — and whichever rule wins, BOTH walks must examine the UNION of binding keys so entry-vs-entry and entry-vs-goal agree (the pair verdict is already shared via binding_pair_covers; this ticket shares the KEY ITERATION). ACCEPTANCE: a wi821 fixture for (1) measures 5010001; a multi-param-spec fixture pins (2); a bindingless-slot nested-forward fixture pins (3); wi817 (15) + forwarding suites + full workspace green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-24T20:29:15Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-831", description: some(value: "anthill-todo: move persisted work-item relations to a durable extent owner. Replace the resident-mirror read path for WorkItem, Tag, and Feedback with an indexed file-backed owner that queries rows lazily (enumerable plus keyed modes), returns Value/StoredRow without creating RuleEntry or hash-consed heads per row, and uses store-native FactRef/RowKey rather than RuleId→source-span identity. Keep domain/workflow bodied rules resident; reject mixed resident facts for owned functors. Preserve existing CLI semantics for list, tagged/unblocked queries, add/claim/update/retract/feedback, and source-editable workitems.anthill. Depends on the extent write/FactRef cutover (WI-780) and follows WI-830's explicit mirror-role migration; do not treat the existing IndexedFileStore (whose retrieve reads kb.rules) as an owner without redesigning it."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-780", "WI-830"]), status: Open)
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-831", name: "extent-seam")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-779", description: some(value: "Write-side bodied-rule hazard in the retract path (the WI-772 class on the WRITE side), surfaced by the WI-772 review discussion. find_fact (rustland/anthill-core/src/eval/builtins.rs:2831-2838) mints a FactId handle for ANY rule whose head TermId equals the ground target -- no is_fact check -- and a bodied rule can carry a fully ground head (`rule TranslationPolicy(...) :- guard()`). persistence_retract (builtins.rs:3612) then checks is_rule_alive + the proposal-053 monotonicity guard but never fact-shape, so a bodied RULE is retracted through the fact API. Downstream desync: FileStore::retract prints the HEAD canonically (persistence/file_store.rs:131-136) and apply_retracts content-matches that text in the source file, but the source line is `rule H :- B` -- no match, so the file keeps the rule the KB just dropped and the next load resurrects it (silent KB/store desync). The IndexedFileStore span path does not cover bodied rules either: spans are recorded for fact_rule_ids only (rustland/anthill-todo/src/main.rs:733), so a bodied rule falls to the known-dangerous content fallback (see the corruption note at main.rs:648). SEMANTIC KICKER: even the pure KB-side retract of fact H is ineffective while a same-head `rule H :- B` remains derivable -- the statement the caller meant to remove is still provable, so a silent success is a lie. FIX DIRECTION: WI-772-shape loud refusal -- find_fact matches facts only and REFUSES (loud) when any same-head candidate is bodied (do not skip: hash-consing makes fact H and `rule H :- B` share the head TermId, and blanket refusal is the safe polarity per WI-772); persistence_retract asserts fact-shape before the store call as a second line. Regression test: ground-headed bodied rule + find_fact/retract flow -> loud error, file unchanged. ACCEPTANCE: cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "codex", at: "2026-07-25T14:08:55Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-780", description: some(value: "Retract/persistence identity and seam redesign -- rework the write-side boundary on top of the WI-773 accessor pattern (depends on it landing the decided bodied-rule policy and the raw-access scoping precedent). THREE identities coexist for a stored fact: domain key (IndexedFileStore.by_id String->RuleId, persistence/indexed_file_store.rs:54), content (hash-consed ground head TermId -- canonical identity for ground facts), and event (RuleId -- an ephemeral in-process index). The identity crossing the anthill boundary today is the EPHEMERAL one: FactId = Literal::Handle(HandleKind::Fact, RuleId.raw) (eval/builtins.rs:2842) -- meaningless across processes, guarded in-process only by is_rule_alive. The composed flow even maps content->RuleId (find_fact bucket scan) and then RuleId->content again (FileStore::retract canonical head print) -- the durable identity was the content all along. Second wart: a comment-enforced temporal protocol -- Store::retract must run BEFORE kb.retract or the store reads released TermIds (eval/builtins.rs:3609-3611, persistence/mod.rs:117). DIRECTION: (1) boundary retract keyed by content or domain key; RuleId demoted to the store/persistence layer private address -- the same visibility ratchet as the WI-773 read side; (2) ONE retract_persistent seam owning the store+kb ordering so the two-phase discipline is not caller-visible; (3) FactId handle becomes an internal optimization or is dropped; (4) decide where the proposal-053 monotonicity guard lives in the new seam. Related: WI-774 (resolve-vs-refuse) covers the READ semantics; this item is store addressing only. WI-779 is the interim loud guard this redesign subsumes. ACCEPTANCE: design note or implementation with the boundary API switched; ordering no longer caller-visible; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-797"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "codex", at: "2026-07-25T19:52:06Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-824", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. DECISION RECORDED: ticket option (i) — goal type params are RIGID in candidate matching — taken AT ITS WORD: with a call-site sigma in hand, EVERY bare type-param element is refused against a STRUCTURED candidate head, not merely those a sigma-class walk can PROVE rigid. Option (ii) Deferred rejected: a skolem is not provably Wrap[A=E] for any E, so there is nothing to defer TO; and Deferred with no supplier loads clean and dies at eval on an unbound __req_*, the failure mode WI-828 exists to convert INTO a load error. Refusing adds no new outcome — the call takes the existing ladder (MissingRequiresForSpecOp uncovered, WI-562 licensing covered). LOCUS: match_candidate_against_goal arm (2)'s non-parametric per-call branch (kb/typing.rs), the only place holding BOTH the sigma context and the (candidate-head, goal-element) PAIR; alternatives priced and rejected at review — goal construction cannot drop the element (resolve_inner needs it to find a FromScope cover), collect_provides_candidates would re-walk the same pairs, pick_most_specific sees only a u32, and resolve_at_goal IS option (ii) and would also refuse WI-507's legitimate Unique. ASYMMETRY ESTABLISHED FIRST as the ticket asked: the CONDITIONAL twin matched its head IDENTICALLY and diverged only AFTER, on its own unresolvable sub-goal — the head match was the defect, and the two now agree. SECOND WITNESS the ticket did not have, found by building the sigma-present path: cross-sort dictionary CONSTRUCTION (caller entry Desc[Wrap[A=AT]] sigma-disagrees with callee goal Desc[BT:=AT], so Strategy 3 constructs) loaded CLEAN and returned 99 from the mis-pinned provider — the ticket's hypothetical 'would compute WRONG VALUES silently', measured. It flips to the WI-828 refusal. MEASUREMENT THAT DECIDED THE SHAPE: the narrow sigma-CLASSIFIED form was written and measured first and is IDENTICAL on the corpus (17678 sigma-present arm-(2) firings, every one classifies rigid, ZERO unpinned); the wide form was chosen because the narrow one's retained leniency cites WI-507, whose unpinned-sibling case actually meets an impl-param REF and is decided by arm (1) — a rationale transplanted across candidate shapes — and because sigma-PRESENCE keying drops a dependency on the classifier, which cannot see an OPERATION's own type param (sort_param_rigids is built from parent-sort params only), returns None on chase-bound exhaustion, and chases a KB-wide canonical global. ACCEPTANCE MET: 3 WI-817 pins flip (mis-pin pin -> load rejection naming the spec op + the missing requires; the two op-param (b) pins keep pinning the SAME gap but reach it via the WI-325 ladder instead of DispatchNoMatch, there being no bogus candidate left for dispatch to fail on); 8 new tests in wi824_abstract_mispin_test.rs with both witnesses DRIVEN HEAD->fix and 6 controls green in BOTH directions; wi210/wi221/wi224/wi228 dispatch suites and stdlib provides green; full workspace 3498 tests, 0 failed.", at: "2026-07-25T05:53:13Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-824", author: "user", content: "REVIEW RESIDUALS (recorded here per the no-ticket-spinoff rule). /simplify (4 agents) APPLIED: flattened the guard; test driver() helper + one MISSING_REQUIRES const per file (the needle carries a U+2026, spelled 4x before); module registration appended in delivery order. Its altitude agent caught the WI-826 failure mode reproduced VERBATIM in my own comment — I attributed 'compat-only' to the sigma-less path after enumerating ONE of its FOUR entries; the omitted three include resolve_bridge_requirements, which builds REAL dictionaries at EVAL and is safe only because it resolves fully-ground goals (its own all_pinned guard, whose rationale I annotated as still-true-because-sigma-less). Comment now enumerates all four. /code-review (12 findings): APPLIED the retained-lenient-branch tightening (see the decision note above — two independent reviewers converged on it being corpus-dead, untested and transplanted), the sole_error_at test helper (every refusal pin asserted needles against the JOIN of all load errors, which stays green if the clauses drift into two diagnostics), and a doc precondition sentence naming the sigma gate + the four sigma-less entries (a port reading the table would otherwise take rows 2-3 as total). NOT REPRODUCED, both DRIVEN: (F1) 'the classifier cannot see an OPERATION's own type param, so the mis-pin reproduces with the element moved to operation a[AT]' — the mechanism claim is TRUE but the consequence is not: every faithful op-scoped spelling dies FIRST on the WI-822 no-supply error (DeferToRequirement: __req_desc not bound), so op-scoped chains never reach construction at all today. The blind spot becomes OBSERVABLE when WI-822 lands a supply channel — recorded there too; the tightening makes the guard spelling-independent, so it is closed in advance. (F2) 'spec_resolves_at_bindings accepts a declared field type on the strength of the lenient match' — probed with and without the parametric provider in scope: the verdict is IDENTICAL (both load clean), so that branch is not what decides it. SKIPPED with reasons: consolidating the rule's three implementations behind one predicate (the altitude review priced it — a blanket hoist above arms (2)/(2.5)/(3) would newly refuse dispatch_values_match's universally-quantified-candidate fast path, i.e. a legitimate 'fact Eq[T]' provider = doc row 4; documented at both emergent sites instead); hoisting the Desc/Leaf/Wrap fixture + load_errs + eval_fresh into tests/common (SEVEN sigma-cluster files carry copies now — doing it for two would create a split convention; same reason WI-826 skipped the with_instances hoist, and this is the second ticket to pay it); resolve_at_goal collecting the candidate set TWICE per goal (pre-existing; the tightening removed the sigma chase from this branch entirely, so the diff no longer adds cost to it).", at: "2026-07-25T05:53:37Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-823", author: "user", content: "PRE-FIX SIGNATURE IN THIS TICKET IS NOW STALE (WI-824, delivered 2026-07-25). This ticket quotes the (b) pins as failing with 'type mismatch in ...Desc.describe.dispatch: expected matching impl for per-call bindings, got no impl matches' — that is no longer what they report. WI-824 removed the bogus candidate the uncovered call used to match (an unconditioned parametric provider head matched var-to-structure), so with no candidates at all the call now lands on the WI-325 ladder: 'type mismatch in ...Desc.describe.requires: expected requires Desc[...] covering abstract type parameter, got missing requires Desc[T = ...] on enclosing sort'. Both pins were updated in place and still pin THIS gap at THIS site (f's covered describe call) — the acceptance is unchanged (they must flip to clean loads that evaluate 1/12/122), only the pre-fix text differs. Do not chase the old string.", at: "2026-07-25T05:53:52Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-822", author: "user", content: "CONSTRAINT FROM WI-824 (delivered 2026-07-25), measured while reviewing it. Op-scoped requires chains never reach dictionary CONSTRUCTION today — every faithful spelling (op-scoped requires over an op param, and over a sort param, both calling cross-sort) dies first on this ticket's own error, 'DeferToRequirement: requirement param __req_desc not bound in caller frame'. That is why a /code-review finding predicting a WI-824 mis-pin through the op-scoped channel did NOT reproduce. WHEN THIS TICKET LANDS A SUPPLY CHANNEL, that path becomes reachable, so: (i) the WI-824 refusal must still fire there. It does — the guard keys on sigma PRESENCE, not on a sigma-class walk, precisely because sigma_class_terminal cannot recognise an OPERATION's own type param (sort_param_rigids is built from parent_sort_params only, and the call-site subst is seeded with the enclosing SORT's rigids), so an op-scoped element spelled Ref(op.PT) classifies UNPINNED. Do not 'restore' the narrow sigma-classified form when adding the op-scoped channel — it would silently not fire for exactly the elements this ticket makes reachable. (ii) If you DO add op-scoped rigids to sort_param_rigids (or an equivalent op-level map), say so at the WI-824 guard: it would make the narrow form viable again, and the choice should be re-derived rather than inherited. (iii) An op-scoped construction path that meets an ABSTRACT element must land on the WI-828 refusal, not build a dictionary — the wrong-value witness in wi824_abstract_mispin_test.rs is the sort-level shape of the same hazard.", at: "2026-07-25T05:54:09Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-824", description: some(value: "FIX the abstract-call mis-pin (bonus hazard from WI-817; it refuted the witness's first-cut positive control): with an UNCONDITIONED parametric provider in scope — fact Desc[T = Wrap[A = E]] whose head carries a FREE type var E and NO requires precondition — an ABSTRACT spec-op call (Desc.describe(x) at abstract FT) is silently resolved to that provider at LOAD: var-var unification lets the parametric head match the abstract per-call binding, dispatch returns a bogus Unique, and the WI-325 protection never fires (it guards NoCandidates and NoMatch, not Unique). The program then dies at eval — Err(Internal(field_access: entity has no field 'inner')) on a leaf value — or, with a signature-compatible wrong impl, would compute WRONG VALUES silently. MEASURED, pinned: unconditioned_parametric_fact_mispins_abstract_call (wi817_polyrec_requirement_test.rs). NOTE the asymmetry worth establishing before fixing: in the same witness family the CONDITIONAL parametric candidate (same head WITH requires Desc[T = E]) did NOT mis-pin the abstract call — it produced NoMatch — so the defect is specific to how an UNCONDITIONED parametric head matches an abstract goal; find where the two diverge (collect_provides_candidates / pick_most_specific / the dispatch walk, kb/typing.rs) before choosing the fix shape. SOUND BEHAVIOUR, decide precisely: an abstract per-call binding must not be PINNED via var-var unification — either (i) goal type params are RIGID in candidate matching, so the abstract call flows to NoMatch and into the existing ladder (MissingRequiresForSpecOp when uncovered, WI-562 licensing when covered), or (ii) the match survives but classifies Deferred, never Unique. WATCH: do not break parametric providers at CONCRETE bindings (stdlib provides Stream/FiniteCollection/Iterable with type-param bindings; wi224 conditional resolution; the witness family itself). INDEPENDENT of WI-821/822/823 — this is dispatch candidate matching, not the supply channel. ACCEPTANCE: the mis-pin pin FLIPS to a load-time rejection naming the spec op and abstract param (wi325-style) or to the explicitly chosen Deferred semantics, with the choice and reason recorded; wi224/wi210 dispatch suites and stdlib provides stay green; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-25T05:54:17Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-822", author: "user", content: "LEG 2 DELIVERED (commit 3b40da8c); LEG 1 NOT delivered, residue newly MEASURED and pinned — ticket stays OPEN. THE TICKET'S OWN ATTRIBUTION WAS WRONG, established by the probe it asked for: the frame that died was the value-directed-dispatched IMPL's (WrapDesc.describe), NOT the op-scoped caller's (Holder.probe) that LEG 1 predicted. The message named no frame; it now names the running op + the requires-chain owner. LEG 2 ALONE flipped BOTH (c) pins (12; 1/12/122 control and lambda witness alike) — Holder.probe needs no dictionary at all, since WI-562 licensing leaves its call value-directed and it reads no slot. IMPLEMENTATION: requirements_for_value_directed_impl reuses WI-625's resolve_bridge_requirements verbatim (identical 'concrete op, real argument values, no caller dictionary' problem) — unify each arg's runtime type against the impl op's declared param types, substitute into the impl sort's chain, SLD-resolve each slot with an empty scope; applied at BOTH value-directed arms (WI-444 carrier override, WI-350 abstract receiver). DESIGN CHOICE RECORDED (the constraint asked for it): op-scoped requirements stay served BY VALUE-DIRECTION, not by dictionaries — an op-scoped chain has NO frame slots (synth_req_names is sort-keyed), and value-direction demonstrably serves it (the 551 relay chain is unchanged, still dictionary-free). The defect was never the channel; it was that the channel stopped at the impl's door. DEVIATION FROM THE TICKET, forced by measurement: the specified LOUD-at-dispatch error on an unresolvable chain broke 29 green stdlib tests (Stream/Iterable/FiniteCollection: wi435, wi439, wi492, wi588, wi614, …) — Map.iterator reached on a Value::Map HANDLE cannot pin Map's requires Eq[T = Map.K], and never reads it; 'has an unpinnable chain' and 'needs it' are different questions and only the body answers the second. So the frame is entered UNSUPPLIED and the failure stays loud AT THE READ (now frame-named), with ANTHILL_TRACE_REQ tracing what was not built. A WRONG dictionary stays impossible: resolve_bridge_requirements' fully-pinned gate refuses an abstract element before candidate matching — which is what discharges WI-824 feedback (iii) here, and matters because that resolve is sigma-LESS so WI-824's sigma-gated refusal is NOT in force on this path. WI-824 (ii) is moot: NO op-scoped rigids were added to sort_param_rigids, so the WI-824 guard's sigma-PRESENCE keying is untouched. LEG 1'S REAL MOTIVATION, measured for the first time (the ticket inferred LEG 1 from the (c) pins, which LEG 2 in fact fixed — so this is the evidence that actually justifies it): a spec op with NO receiver argument (operation zero() -> T) cannot be value-directed at all, and with TWO providers the op-scoped spelling is REJECTED AT LOAD ('multiple impls match (coherence rule)' at Zeroable.zero.dispatch) while the SORT-level twin of the same program loads and computes the correct 5. Two spellings of one program, one of which does not load. Pinned as a current defect in receiverless_spec_op_op_scoped_rejected_sort_level_correct (new file wi822_op_scoped_supply_test.rs, 4 tests) with the correct value stated. CLOSING IT needs frame slots keyed by the OPERATION (sort chain ++ op chain) — synth_req_names, expand_dispatching_dict, start_apply_deferred, call_with_requirements, seed_entry_requirements, classify_pin_or_apply_within and record_apply_within_concrete are all sort-keyed today — i.e. a change to the requirement ABI itself, with the whole stdlib and anthill-todo in blast radius. Full workspace 3502 passed 0 failed; /code-review not yet run (user-triggered).", at: "2026-07-25T06:55:38Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-832", description: some(value: "Eliminate lexical scope terms from public and private APIs: replace scope_raw TermId identity with an explicit symbol-based lexical scope identity (including global scope); retain requirement-instantiation data such as Eq[T]; migrate symbol table, loader, resolver, and reflection callers; remove scope-term reconstruction/exposure and cover nested sort/namespace/operation scopes."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-820", author: "user", content: "Classification after the CLI entry-discovery migration: anthill run uses read_facts_resolved for SortRequiresInfo, with regressions for a passing guard, a failing guard, and duplicate sorted providers. Remaining Refuse readers are deliberate: check/prove/todo read persisted source truth; stored-fact reads require stable FactRef; SMT must not admit guarded facts; C++ mapping needs explicit aggregation/select_keyed semantics; STL reflection cannot resolve until bootstrap paths provide declared field schemas and mutable KB access. No further safe Resolve migration remains in WI-820.", at: "2026-07-27T05:54:04Z")
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-833", name: "extent-seam")
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-834", name: "extent-seam")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-820", description: some(value: "Retire RuleId from the external read interface (057 R4). Goal: a fact read must not depend on whether the fact is memory-resident (has a RuleId) or extent-mounted (has none) — callers speak only values. read_facts / read_facts_resolved are already RuleId-free at the interface, and cpp-gen was fully migrated off RuleId-based reads (WI-771/810/774: no query_view/rule_head/rules_by_functor usage remains). REMAINING WORK: (1) shrink query_view to resolver-only — it returns (RuleId, Substitution) legitimately ONLY for SLD candidate selection (needs the rule BODY to open) and the CLI --match rule-browse; audit for any surviving READER (fact-shaped) caller and migrate it to read_facts/query_fact_heads, then ratchet query_view + head-as-answer enumeration to pub(crate) (057 R4). (2) Drive the Refuse->Resolve migration where a consumer can handle multi-valued/aggregated results, since Refuse is the TRANSITIONAL non-resolving candidate read and Resolve (read_facts_resolved) is the correct evaluate+aggregate read — the durable read primitive. cpp-gen's realization reads stay on Refuse until select_keyed / cpp_method_name can consume aggregated mappings (WI-774 deferred single-valued refinement). Relates 057, WI-773/806/810/771/774, WI-780 (write seam retires RuleId from resident storage, R3/R4)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-833", "WI-834"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Claimed(agent: "codex", since: "2026-07-26T08:02:53Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-834", author: "claude", content: "Delivered as the vocabulary-correction + capability-enablement it was scoped to be (proposal library/006). 3a85ac39 landed steps 1-4 (MemberKind/MemberInfo/DescriptionInfo declared; loader emission -> named facts; reader.rs+builtins.rs decode by name; old member/Description kernel functors removed; bootstrap test). THIS increment adds proposal step 5's missing empty-relation test (parse_test.rs reflection_inventory_schemas_survive_an_empty_relation): declared schemas present AND read_facts_resolved returns [] on a zero-row relation, never NoFieldSchema -- the exact schema-less-bootstrap failure, pinned where a recover-schema-from-a-row shortcut has no row. READER-POLICY REVIEW (proposal line 197): every current STL reflection reader is source-inventory / stored-fact / program-text (read_sort_infos, read_operations, members_of_kind, read_descriptions, rule_heads_for_sort, find_entity_schema, facts_of, stored_facts_of) -> all correctly RETAIN Refuse. Eligible-for-Resolve = EMPTY, exactly as 006 Non-goals dictate (source inventory stays Refuse; derived/effective views are separate FUTURE relations -- resolveMember/member-dispatch proposal, derivedDescription). read_facts_resolved capability now proven for the inventory functors (bootstrap + empty-relation). Refuse enforcement already pinned by bridge::facts_of_refuses_a_bodied_rule + core::read_facts_refuse_names_the_bodied_rule; the 'resolver enumeration test for each Resolve reader' clause is vacuous. Full workspace green (0 failures; parse_tests green in the full run). Does NOT unblock WI-820 alone: WI-833 (cpp-gen aggregation) remains its other blocker.", at: "2026-07-28T07:03:10Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-834", description: some(value: "STL reflection Resolve bootstrap: enable resolving reflection reads without schema-less bootstrap failures. Ensure reflected functors have declared field schemas on every bootstrap/test path, provide mutable KB access where resolution allocates, and migrate eligible STL reflection readers from Refuse to read_facts_resolved. Missing schema or unsupported carrier must remain loud; full cargo test."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-28T07:03:15Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-644", description: some(value: "Implement library proposal 004 (docs/proposals/library/004-partial-vs-total-equality-and-ordering.md): split the conflated Eq into PartialEq (partial -- holds the eq/neq ops; Float provides, IEEE) + Eq (requires PartialEq + a CHECKED reflexivity law eq_refl: eq(?a,?a)<=>true; Float does NOT provide -- the instance-law conformance checker rejects it automatically), and split Ordered into PartialOrd (partialCompare -> Option[Int64]) + Ord (requires Eq + PartialOrd, total).\n\n[EVOLUTION: this ticket started as \"proposal 051 Phase 2 -- drop the universal structural Eq default\"; investigation (WI-645 Float NaN soundness bug + Rust/Lean prior art) showed the principled resolution is the PartialEq/Eq SPLIT, not dropping a default -- so the design landed as library proposal 004 and THIS ticket is its implementation driver. See the feedback trail.]\n\nNO new language machinery -- reuses the existing spec-hierarchy (`requires Spec[T]`: Ordered-requires-Eq precedent), laws-as-labeled-<=>-rules (Ordered.compare_refl precedent), and instance-law CONFORMANCE CHECKING (load.rs Specialization / requires-law Discharged, ~3866-3972,12425). The one kernel-touching piece (the resolver reflexivity-shortcut fix) is an implementation consequence tracked here, like library/003's typer work.\n\nBUILD STEPS (proposal Build order): (1) stdlib specs+laws: new PartialEq/PartialOrd sorts; move eq/neq + neq<=>not(eq) to PartialEq; add eq_refl to Eq; re-parent `Eq requires PartialEq` / `Ord requires Eq, PartialOrd`; re-register builtins (PartialEq.eq->SemEq, ...); confirm the conformance checker REJECTS a synthetic non-reflexive `provides Eq`. (2) resolver soundness fix (CLOSES WI-645 direction B): gate sem_eq_core's `values_equal` reflexivity fast-path to reflexive-Eq carriers only; eval builtin_eq IEEE-for-Float path (numeric value_num compare; fall back to structural for non-numeric so Set/Map/entity eq unchanged, WI-616 override dispatch preserved); un-ignore wi645_float_nan_ieee_test.rs. (3) Float: provides PartialEq/PartialOrd (IEEE), drop provides Eq; add TotalFloat newtype providing Eq/Ord (total). (4) codegen: PartialEq.eq->C++ `==`, Eq/Ord/TotalFloat -> total operator==/<=> (incl. `= default` on entity structs, closing the WI-645 struct-operator== gap). (5) docs: kernel-language.md sec-equality three-notion table; sync 051.\n\nMIGRATION (intent audit, like 051's ===): Eq.eq->PartialEq.eq (mostly source-compatible via the requires-chain, WI-614); audit every `requires Eq` / `requires Ordered` -- keep Eq/Ord for reflexivity-dependent (Set, Map keys, dedup, sort), weaken to PartialEq/PartialOrd for compare-only.\n\nPRIOR ART: Rust (f64: PartialEq but !Eq) and, more precisely, Lean's 3-level =/BEq/LawfulBEq (Anthill ===/PartialEq.eq/Eq); Lean withholds LawfulBEq Float for BOTH non-reflexivity (NaN) AND non-congruence (-0.0==+0.0 but 1/0 != 1/-0). NB Anthill's Eq = EQUIVALENCE laws (reflexive/sym/trans), NOT Lean's coincide-with-= (since === is structural, not per-type logical =) -- else Set/Map (semantic eq != structural) would wrongly fail lawfulness.\n\nCLOSES WI-645 (direction B). Modular/named non-canonical instances (SortedSet custom Ord) = WI-648 (deferred sibling). WI-642's is_builtin exclusion stays correct.\n\nACCEPTANCE: PartialEq/Eq/PartialOrd/Ord load with eq_refl a checked law; a non-reflexive `provides Eq[T=Float]` FAILS to load; interpreter Float eq/neq/ordered are IEEE and AGREE with the C++ codegen (wi645_float_nan_ieee_test un-ignored + green); Map[K=Float] is a load error, Map[K=TotalFloat] loads; Set/Map/entity semantic eq + hash-consing + === unchanged; full workspace suite green. REF: docs/proposals/library/004, proposal 051, WI-616/645/642/300/614/648, stdlib/anthill/prelude/{eq,ordered,float,set,map}.anthill, kb/{resolve,load}.rs, anthill-cpp-gen/src/lib.rs:2889-2906, kernel-language.md. Rust + scaland."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-625", "WI-658", "WI-664", "WI-835"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Open)
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-644", author: "claude", content: "REOPENED 2026-07-28 (Delivered -> Open) because its last acceptance item is unmet, not because anything delivered regressed. Measured today with `anthill load` (embedded stdlib): `Map[K = Float, V = Int64]` loads CLEAN, and the `Map[K = TotalFloat, V = Int64]` control ALSO loads -- so 'Map[K=Float] is a load error, Map[K=TotalFloat] loads' passes only VACUOUSLY; nothing distinguishes the two spellings and TotalFloat is a documentation convention rather than an enforced boundary. That use-site enforcement is now OWNED by WI-835 (added as a dependency of this ticket). It had been ownerless since the 2026-07-08 deferral: WI-658's description points at 'sibling WI-649' for the use-site half, but WI-649 is the reify/reify_value cyclic-sigma occurs-check ticket -- a repurposed number, the same failure WI-658 itself was created to fix after the provision-site half had been mis-attributed to the repurposed WI-650. Everything else in this ticket stands as delivered (PartialEq/Eq/PartialOrd/Ord split, eq_refl law, IEEE Float eval/resolver path, NonEq, TotalFloat, codegen). Surfaced while reviewing proposal 058 §6, which asserted the embedded `requires Eq[T = K]` as an operative check; 058 §6 now records the gap and the measurement.", at: "2026-07-28T07:00:29Z")
