fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-648", author: "user", content: "DESIGN PHASE DELIVERED (2026-07-25, amended twice 2026-07-28): docs/proposals/058-modular-instances.md — declare / select / thread + the coherence story, SortedSet worked end to end, library/004 interaction spelled out, plus an amendment to kernel-language.md §Instance coherence (whose per-import promise cannot express either driver). RULING: explicit per-call selection first, implicit scoped selection deferred. IMPLEMENTATION IS NOW A TAGGED SEQUENCE, not this ticket — tag 'modinst', driven one per session: WI-839 (phase 0, close the silent bracket drops), WI-836/837/838 (three PRE-EXISTING defects the design assumed were already checked — argument-position type-var agreement; witness-supplied eq never keys the sem-eq index; a mixed instance-fact + witness pair loads clean), WI-840 (phase 1 declare: the named requires binder, the only grammar in the proposal), WI-841 (phase 2 select), WI-842 (phase 3a harden the bracket-less provider reads), WI-843 (phase 3b move coherence to the use site), WI-844 (phase 4 SortedSet), WI-845 (phase 5 spec). WI-648 stays OPEN as the umbrella and depends on all ten; close it when the sequence is green. Two reviews of the proposal are recorded in its Status line; the second one falsified two claims the first had let stand (§5.3's merge guarantee and §6's Eq enforcement), which is why three of the ten tickets are pre-existing-defect tickets rather than phases.", at: "2026-07-28T09:38:12Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-836", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED. ROOT CAUSE was a WALK DEPTH, one line: validate_arg_against_param (kb/typing.rs, the WI-385 conformance check shared by op-arg / entity-field / function-value sites) resolved both sides with walk_view, which follows only the HEAD var / sort-alias chain and stops at a Term::Fn. A type variable NESTED inside a sort application therefore read as non-ground even once sigma had bound it, and the groundness gate SKIPPED. Fix: retry with the DEEP pure-sigma walk (walk_type_deep_value) on the non-ground verdict. Both spellings closed with one change - op type param (List[T = X]) and the sort's OWN params written out (Box[T = T, O = O]). MEASURED, not predicted: the ticket's third probe (sort-level) is caught only in the WRITTEN spelling; the BARE spelling (union(a: Box, b: Box)) was ALREADY caught by WI-374's enforce_member_tie and still is - the written one reached this hole precisely because a NESTED conflict never attempts a rebind, so it records no contradiction_details for the member tie to read. That asymmetry is now pinned as a control. REMAINING GAP, recorded not papered over: two ARROW-typed arguments sharing one type variable (f: (X) -> Int64, g: (X) -> Int64) are STILL not checked - measured load-clean at delivery and pinned by an inverted known_gap test. The deep walk is deliberately NOT applied when either side is callable: making a callback read as ground routes it from the component-wise arrow checkers (validate_arrow_param_result WI-469 / validate_callback_effect_row WI-440) to the whole-type types_compatible, which REFUSES the Function[A = tuple]-vs-2-parameter-eta-arrow pairing WI-775/WI-792 settled must be ACCEPTED. Measured: without that gate, wi787 (3 cases), wi784's rigid-A case and wi424's row-gated callback all flipped from load-clean to refused, plus wi791/wi792 message drift. Closing the arrow spelling means teaching arrow_compatible_view the Function-states-no-arity rule - a change to who OWNS arrow conformance, not a walk depth, and its own ticket if wanted. WI-844 (058 phase 4 SortedSet driver) is served either way: union(a: SortedSet[T=T,O=O], b: SortedSet[T=T,O=O]) is a sort application, not an arrow.", at: "2026-07-28T10:08:41Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-836", description: some(value: "TYPER SOUNDNESS: a parameter whose declared type contains a type VARIABLE accepts ANY argument after the variable's first binding occurrence — two arguments sharing one type param are never checked against each other. MEASURED at HEAD (dcdaf0cd, `anthill load`, 2026-07-28): `operation same[X](a: List[T = X], b: List[T = X]) -> Int64 = 1` called as `same(li(), ls())` with `li() -> List[T = Int64]` and `ls() -> List[T = String]` LOADS CLEAN. Identical result with the 042 bracket seeded explicitly (`same[X = Int64](li(), ls())` — so this is not an inference-only gap), and identical for a SORT-level param: `sort Box { sort T = ?  sort O = ?  entity box(v: T)  operation union(a: Box[T = T, O = O], b: Box[T = T, O = O]) -> Box[T = T, O = O] = a }` with `Box.union(mkq(), mkr())`, `mkq() -> Box[T = Int64, O = Q]`, `mkr() -> Box[T = Int64, O = R]` — clean. CONTROLS, so this is the hole and not the harness: (a) a variable-FREE param mismatch IS caught — `takeR(b: Box[T = Int64, O = R])` applied to a `Box[T = Int64, O = Q]` gives `type mismatch in takeR.b (op-arg): expected Box[T = Int64, O = R], got Box[T = Int64, O = Q]`; (b) the variable DOES bind from argument 1 — `idbox[X](a: List[T = X]) -> List[T = X]` applied to a `List[Int64]` against a declared `List[String]` return errors at go.return with `got List[T = Int64]`; (c) an argument list that constrains X nowhere is loud — `expected a type for 'X', got unconstrained — use pick[X = …](…)`. So binding happens and conformance happens; what is missing is re-checking a LATER argument against the ALREADY-BOUND variable (equivalently: unifying the two arguments' bindings through σ instead of comparing each against the raw declared type). Impact: every generic multi-argument operation silently accepts heterogeneous calls. BLOCKS docs/proposals/058-modular-instances.md §5.3 (WI-648 phase 4): the SortedSet driver's entire safety claim — a `union` of a ByLength set and an Alphabetical one is a TYPE error — rests on this check, and as measured such a union loads clean and merges sets ordered by different comparators. 058 §4.7's evidence was read too broadly: its probe measured the RETURN position, which IS checked. SCOPE: Rust typer, the per-argument conformance path in kb/typing.rs (seed_op_type_args seeds σ; the argument walk must unify against σ and write back, rather than compare against the declared type with the variable left free). ACCEPTANCE: the three probe programs above each fail to load with a diagnostic naming the two conflicting argument bindings and the parameter they disagree on; controls (a)/(b)/(c) still behave exactly as measured; a regression test pins op-level AND sort-level parameters and both the inferred and the explicitly-seeded forms; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-28T10:29:08Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-833", description: some(value: "Cpp-gen Resolve aggregation: make realization mapping selection consume multi-valued resolved rows. Define deterministic aggregation/priority semantics for TypeMapping, IncludeMapping, and NamingConvention after read_facts_resolved; migrate select_keyed / cpp_method_name from Refuse once duplicate, guarded, and competing mappings can be selected or rejected loudly. Cover passing/failing guards, duplicate answers, overlay priority, and ambiguity; full cargo test."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-28T10:25:26Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-839", description: some(value: "Proposal 058 (WI-648) PHASE 0 — PREREQUISITE: close EVERY §2.2 silent drop of the call-site bracket (`type_args`) channel, at ALL THREE producers. Today `f[Bogus = Int64](args)` LOADS CLEAN whenever the callee declares no OP-LEVEL type params (`typing.rs:12925`: `if type_args.is_empty() || op.type_params.is_empty() { return Ok(()) }`), and a rule-/term-body bracket is parsed and then DISCARDED — the occurrence-body lowering skips ParseAux children (`load.rs:9679`) and hard-codes `type_args: Vec::new()` (`load.rs:9711`); the term/constraint path filters them at `load.rs:7574` / `:8155`. MEASURED (2026-07-25/28): `plain[Bogus = Int64](n)`, `go[Desc = QuietDesc](pebble())` and `Box.mk[Bogus = Int64](5)` all load clean and mean nothing; a committed corpus fixture already writes a rule-body bracket (`term_as_entity[E = WorkItem](?t)`, tree-sitter-anthill/test/corpus/op_type_params.txt:175). WHY THIS IS FIRST: proposal 058's entire SELECT surface reuses this channel, so the spelling is ALREADY ACCEPTED and ALREADY INERT — a program written against it loads silently wrong until phase 2 lands. It is also a \"loud error over silent skip\" violation in its own right, independent of 058. SCOPE: (a) an unmatched bracket key at a classified call is a loud error EVEN WHEN the callee declares no type params — reuse the existing `NoSuchTypeParam` diagnostic; (b) a rule-/term-body bracket on the ParseAux `type_args` channel is a LOUD REFUSAL with a not-supported-here diagnostic (selection inside rule bodies is DEFERRED, not ignored — §4.2; a rule body needing a chosen witness routes through an operation). CONTROL THAT MUST STILL PASS: the refusal must key on the ParseAux `type_args` CHANNEL, not on \"a bracket in a rule body\" — a rule-body TYPE APPLICATION is a different producer and is legitimately checked by WI-710 (`load.rs:9694-9709`), so `:- Modifiable[T = ?t]` and `is_modifiable(Cell[V = Int64])` must keep loading, and an undeclared param inside one must keep raising `InvalidTypeArgument`. ACCEPTANCE (§9 phase 0, verbatim): `plain[Bogus = Int64](n)` fails to load (the existing NoSuchTypeParam message is reused); a rule body containing `f[T = X](…)` fails to load with a not-supported-here diagnostic; plus the WI-710 control above pinned by a test. Full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. REFERENCE: docs/proposals/058-modular-instances.md §2.2, §4.2, §9 phase 0."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-28T11:44:42Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-839", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. Baseline MEASURED first: the drop was at FOUR loader/typer sites, not the three the ticket names — the fourth is an ENTITY-CONSTRUCTOR call in an operation body (`boxed[Bogus = Int64](n: 1)`), where `build_call_type_args` READ the channel and the `Expr::Constructor` build then threw the Vec away. A fifth turned up one classification step past the ticket's site (a): a call on a FUNCTION VALUE never reaches `seed_op_type_args` at all. Two more spellings of site (a) were silent for the same reason as the named one: a POSITIONAL binding past the last declared param (`plain[Int64](n)`, `idy[Int64, String](n)`) hit a `continue` in the same loop.\n\nTWO OWNERS, split by what each can decide. (1) LOADER — a whole-parse-store SWEEP (`check_unconsumed_call_type_args`, run per file after `load_items`) against a `consumed_call_type_args` set the channel's TWO honouring readers record: `build_call_type_args` (op-body Apply) and `collect_rule_tvar_names` (a rule HEAD's `[T]` introducer, WI-582 — which rides this same channel and had to be marked or every `keep[T](…)` rule broke; verified by deleting the mark, which took down wi582 + wi619 too). Deliberately NOT a check at each dropping lowering: enumerating droppers is the WI-805 mistake, and this ticket's own producer list was short by one. `build_call_type_args` is now called ONLY on the non-entity arm, so calling-then-discarding cannot mark a vanished bracket consumed. (2) TYPER — `resolve_call_type_arg_targets` over a `declared` SLICE is the single decider for 'does this binding land'; the op path passes `op.type_params`, the function-value path passes `&[]`, so a param-less operation and an arrow-typed variable are literally one case. `op.type_params.is_empty()` short-circuit deleted; positional overflow gets a new `ExcessCallTypeArgs` (an unmatched positional has no key to name).\n\nACCEPTANCE: `plain[Bogus = Int64](n)` refused reusing the existing NoSuchTypeParam message (pinned against the already-loud `idy[Bogus = …]` twin, so 'reuse' is asserted and not just claimed); a rule body's `f[T = X](…)` refused with the not-supported-here diagnostic. CONTROL held: the refusal keys on the ParseAux `type_args` CHANNEL — `:- Modifiable[T = ?t]` / `is_modifiable(Cell[V = Int64])` still load, and WI-710's own InvalidTypeArgument still fires on an undeclared param. TWO more controls that are not decorative: `fresh_var[Term](name)` (a MATCHING positional bracket, live in anthill-todo/anthill/main.anthill) still loads, and an entity constructor without a bracket still loads.\n\nONE existing test moved rather than broke: wi710's `a_call_site_type_argument_list_is_not_a_sort_application` wrote its call in a RULE BODY. Its claim ('type_args is not a type parameter of anything') is now demonstrated in an OPERATION BODY — the stronger form, since the bindings actually reach `seed_op_type_args` — and a new sibling pins that the rule-body spelling is refused by the WI-839 message and specifically NOT by 'no type parameter named type_args', which is the tempting wrong implementation a refusal-only assertion would pass.\n\nNON-VACUITY: each of the six checks was deliberately reverted in turn and the expected tests failed each time (param-less short-circuit → 3; positional continue → 1; function-value check → 1; loader sweep → 4 incl. the wi710 sibling; introducer mark → 4 incl. wi582/wi619; op-body mark → 8 incl. wi270/wi272).\n\nGrammar UNCHANGED — the corpus fixture at tree-sitter-anthill/test/corpus/op_type_params.txt:175 still parses; only the loader refuses it. Spec updated: kernel-language.md §Operation type parameters now states that every call-site binding must land and that the bracket is honoured only in an operation body and as a rule-head introducer. Full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh (3546 passed, 0 failed). scaland not mirrored — 058 §9 scopes it to phases 1-2 and it has no operation loading.", at: "2026-07-28T11:45:12Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-839", author: "user", content: "/code-review PASS — 11 findings, and I drove every measured one before acting: SIX reproduced exactly (nothing refuted this time). Fixed, in descending order of seriousness:\n\n(1) INCOMPLETE FIX, same class as the ticket itself. An APPLIED RULE CITATION in an op body (`p[Bogus = Int64](1)`, `p[Int64, Int64, Int64](1)`) still loaded clean: the loader marks it consumed on the non-entity Apply arm, and `check_apply_iter` returns early for the WI-714 citation — and for a constructor symbol — BEFORE either typer check. My first cut had guarded only the function-value path, i.e. I recommitted the enumerate-the-sites mistake on the typer side while explicitly avoiding it on the loader side. Now refused ABOVE every early return: `call_type_args_of(occ).is_some() && lookup_operation_info_full(...).is_none()` — the classes are disjoint (`kind_of` gives one kind), so 'not an operation' IS 'will not reach the seeding path'. The Path-2 guard was deleted as subsumed; one site, not three.\n\n(2) TWO SILENT DROPS INSIDE THE BRACKET, in the very loop I rewrote to guarantee none. A REPEATED key (`two[A = Int64, A = String]`) resolved both copies to one param and discarded the second — the guard WI-805/808/809 put on tuple labels, entity fields and named ARG lists, absent from the type-arg list. And a POSITIONAL after a NAMED binding (`idy[T = Int64, String]`) re-targeted the slot the named key held, because I counted positionals against `declared.len()` instead of the slots left FREE. Both now loud (`DuplicateCallTypeArg`; `ExcessCallTypeArgs` re-keyed `declared`→`free`).\n\n(3) A FALSE REJECTION I introduced. A callee with a parameter literally named `type_args` (`f[T = Int64](type_args: n)`) collides with the converter's own channel key — they share one named-arg namespace and the synthesized pair is appended LAST — so `read_parse_aux`'s first-match read returned None, the bracket went unconsumed, and the sweep refused a CORRECT program (pre-WI-839 it merely dropped it). `read_parse_aux` now scans for the entry whose VALUE is a matching ParseAux, which a user argument never is; this also covers `type_name`/`proof_meta`. WI-809 cannot catch it — it runs before the synthesized pair exists.\n\n(4) THE SWEEP FOUND A POSITION NOBODY LISTED, which is the argument for the sweep. An operation's `requires`/`ensures` CONTRACT expression is lowered by the term converter, so a bracket there was dropped and is now refused. Correct, but my single message told the author to 'route the call through an operation' — they ARE in one — and for a rule HEAD it named the head introducer as a place the bracket IS honoured, which is where they had written it. The error now carries a `CallTypeArgsPosition` (RuleHead | NoChannel); `collect_rule_tvar_names` records a head whose bracket is not all-introducers, and the head message says what a head actually admits.\n\n(5) MESSAGE HONESTY: a non-operation callee no longer reports 'unknown type-param X' (which implies some other key would work, and rendered a local binder via `qualified_name_of` as if it were an operation). New `TypeArgsOnNonOperation`. I had defended the shared message as 'one decider'; the decider is shared, the RENDERING should not have been.\n\nAlso: three test assertions weakened to the shared substring were strengthened to name the callee (the sweep derives the callee from whatever node it lands on, so a wrong-node bug would have kept them green); `consumed_call_type_args` re-keyed u32→TermId with the parse-store stability caveat from parse/ir.rs cross-referenced; the spec paragraph rewritten — its closed list of refused positions was already exceeded by the implementation, and it now states the rule as 'read or refused' with the enforcement mechanism, plus the distinct-slot and duplicate-key rules.\n\nSKIPPED ONE: making `unify_types`' discarded verdict loud in `seed_op_type_args` (finding 5). WI-367/WI-379 depend on a failed unify against an already-pinned slot being a no-op, so that is a separate change with its own blast radius. With (2) fixed, no binding can be contradicted by a SIBLING binding; contradiction by an ARGUMENT is WI-385/WI-836's check. The function doc now states that limit instead of claiming 'nothing is skipped'.\n\nFull workspace green after the fixes: 3552 passed, 0 failed.", at: "2026-07-28T12:36:31Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-761", description: some(value: "CppCodegenError conflates NOTHING-TO-EMIT with FAILED-TO-EMIT, so the CLI discards real codegen errors for the anthill.geometry header. emit_namespace_header_in (anthill-cpp-gen/src/lib.rs:1621) returns a plain CppCodegenError when a namespace declares no emittable entities/sums/traits/consts -- a benign \"nothing here\" signal. Two CLI sites treat that as the ONLY reason emission can fail and swallow every error: run_codegen_cpp (anthill-cli/src/main.rs:966) wraps the call in an `if let Ok(...)` and run_codegen_cpp_project (anthill-cli/src/main.rs:1021) ends it in `.ok()`, both with the standing comment \"anthill::geometry only emits if the namespace declared anything there; ignore the error when the namespace is empty (carrier-only / unrelated namespace)\". Their INTENT is sound and should be preserved -- the geometry header genuinely is optional. The defect is that the error type carries no way to tell the two apart, so ANY failure lowering anthill.geometry is silently dropped and the header is quietly absent from the output directory, with the user's only clue being a missing file at C++ compile time. FOUND WHILE DELIVERING WI-576 (the effect capability gate), which is what makes this newly load-bearing: before that gate, emitting a namespace essentially only failed for the empty reason, so the swallow was near-harmless in practice; WI-576 added a whole new class of hard failure (\"operation X requires effect E, which profile P cannot realize\"), and if anthill.geometry ever carries such an op the diagnostic naming the effect and profile is thrown away at exactly these two lines. Directly contradicts the repo rule in CLAUDE.md, \"prefer a loud error over a silent skip ... surface it as an explicit error or diagnostic rather than silently continue/dropping it\". FIX DIRECTION: make \"nothing to emit\" a DISTINCT outcome rather than an error string -- either a dedicated variant/flag on CppCodegenError (e.g. an is_empty_namespace predicate the callers match on) or, cleaner, have the optional-header path return a Result of an Option where Ok(None) means \"namespace declares nothing\" and Err stays a genuine failure. Then both CLI sites skip ONLY on the empty outcome and report everything else via the same eprintln + non-zero exit the primary header already uses at main.rs:927. Prefer not to add a second \"does this namespace have anything?\" pre-check helper -- that would duplicate the classify_namespace + consts_in_scope work the emitter already does, and the two could drift. WATCH: check whether any other caller of emit_namespace_header_with_profile / emit_namespace_header depends on the current error-for-empty behavior before changing the signature, and keep the primary-namespace path (main.rs:927) failing loudly on an empty namespace since a typo'd --namespace SHOULD be an error there, which is the reason the error-on-empty behavior exists at all. ACCEPTANCE: a genuine lowering failure in anthill.geometry (e.g. an op declaring an effect the profile cannot realize, per WI-576) makes the CLI report the underlying message and exit non-zero rather than silently omitting the header; a genuinely empty or carrier-only anthill.geometry still skips quietly with no error, as today; a typo'd primary --namespace still fails loudly; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-576"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-28T13:11:17Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-849", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED with TWO deviations from the ticket, both driven by measurement.\n\n(1) The ticket specified a LOUD PANIC for a non-var `type_params` entry, on the stated assumption that its reachability was 'unmeasured / defensive — the loader is the only in-tree producer'. MEASURED (2026-07-28): that is FALSE. A hand-written `fact OperationInfo(name: <any resolvable symbol>, return_type: Int64, ..., type_params: cons(head: 42, tail: nil()), meta: meta())` reaches the arm from ordinary user source and panicked the loader. Two earlier probe spellings did NOT reach it and would have 'confirmed' the ticket's assumption: `type_params: 42` (a non-list — `list_to_vec` yields no entries) and a well-formed cons list whose `name:` does not resolve to a `Term::Ref` (`head_name_ref` returns None, so `build_op_signatures` skips the fact). The decisive spelling needs BOTH a resolvable name AND a real cons list AND a `return_type`. So the fix is a TYPER DIAGNOSTIC, not a panic: `build_op_signatures` carries the offending entries out of the pass it already makes, and `type_check_sorts_collect` reports one `TypeError::Other` per entry. The decode cannot raise itself — it runs under `&KnowledgeBase` with no error channel, and pre-typer (const-purity gate, eq-dispatch build).\n\n(2) ROOT CAUSE of that reachability is NOT type-param-specific and is left OPEN. `fact mk(a: 1, undeclared_field: 2)` over `entity mk(a: Int64)` LOADS CLEAN — an UNDECLARED named arg on a fact head is accepted generally. `type_params` is not a declared field of the stdlib `OperationInfo` entity (stdlib/anthill/reflect/reflect.anthill:641 lists name/params/return_type/effects/requires/ensures/meta and no type_params — the loader appends it directly at load.rs:13655), so the label is only writable BECAUSE of that hole. Closing it is what would make the malformed entry genuinely unreachable, and it is the WI-805/808/809 family's missing member (those cover tuple labels, entity fields and named ARGUMENT lists; a fact head's unknown label is not covered). Worth its own work item — deliberately not spun off here.\n\nSCOPE NOTE vs the ticket's step (v): `sort_type_params_as_pairs` was NOT moved to `Var`, on measurement rather than the ticket's guess that moving both is cheaper. Its consumers want the param as a TERM (typing.rs:20514 feeds `Value::term`, 10271 walks it through a view) and several run under `&KnowledgeBase` where they could not re-alloc; its own `Var::Global` filter is also load-bearing and documented (a param may alias a CONCRETE sort, `sort T = Int64`, which is not skolemizable — so its skip is intentional and stays). The two tables meet at exactly one place, the `rigidify_op_type_params` concat, where the sort side converts with a loud `unreachable!` licensed by that filter.\n\nCOVERAGE: the cpp-gen effect-row-param identity port (TermId compare -> Var compare, lib.rs `classify_effect_label`) is pinned by the EXISTING `effect_gate_test::effect_row_parameter_is_not_gated` + `stdlib_monad_...`; verified by deliberately breaking the identity test and watching BOTH fire ('declares an effect whose kind C++ codegen cannot read'). No new cpp-gen test was needed. New tests: 4 in wi849_type_param_var_test.rs, including a CONTROL that a hand-written OperationInfo with a well-formed empty type_params list still loads (without it, the refusal test would pass on an implementation that rejected hand-written OperationInfo facts wholesale).\n\nA SEVENTH reader the ticket did not enumerate was found BY THE COMPILER once the type changed: `op_tp_pinning_params` (typing.rs:5687), a third silent `filter_map` drop. That is the argument for the type change over enumerating the sites.\n\nFull workspace: 2720 passed, 0 failed.", at: "2026-07-28T13:53:21Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-849", description: some(value: "Re-type the operation type-param table from `Vec<(Symbol, TermId)>` to `Vec<(Symbol, Var)>` — the entries ARE variables, and the TermId spelling forces a destructure-and-silently-drop at every reader.\n\nWHAT THE FIELD HOLDS (measured at HEAD b0411496, 2026-07-28). ONE producer: `load_operation` (kb/load.rs:13387-13401) mints each entry as `kb.alloc(Term::Var(Var::Global(vid)))` over `vid = kb.fresh_var(tp_sym)`, `build_list`s them into a ground cons-list and stores it as `Value::term(type_params_list)` (load.rs:13655). `type_params` is deliberately NOT part of the value-fact test (`all_ground`, load.rs:13644, is `params_all_ground && effects_all_ground && matches!(return_value, Value::Term{..})`), so even on a value-fact `OperationInfo` head the field rides as a ground `Value::Term`. `extract_type_params` (kb/op_info.rs:423) decodes it to `Vec<(Symbol, TermId)>` where the Symbol is `vid.name()` and the TermId is the `Term::Var(Global)` itself. So BOTH components of every pair are functions of one VarId, and the type says \"some term\" where the value is always a variable.\n\nWHY TermId IS THE WRONG TYPE — every reader immediately undoes the wrapping, and three of them SILENTLY DROP what doesn't fit:\n  * `extract_type_params` (op_info.rs:430-433) — a `filter_map` that DROPS any element that is not `Term::Var(Global)`.\n  * `rigidify_op_type_params` (kb/typing.rs:33038) — `if let Term::Var(Var::Global(vid)) = kb.get_term(*var_tid)`, then `bind_term(kb, vid, rigid)`; a non-Var entry is silently skipped.\n  * `sort_type_params_as_pairs` (typing.rs:23400-23405) — the same gate as a `.then_some(...)` filter (\"a param whose alias is not a plain `Global` var is skipped\", per its own doc).\n  * `check_unconstrained_type_params` (typing.rs:13182) and `check_signature_self_contradiction` (typing.rs:19819-19821) — re-wrap the TermId as `TermIdView(*var_term)` to get a carrier-neutral read back out.\n  * `walk_term_to_resolved` (typing.rs:24394-24397) — re-destructures `Term::Var(Global)` to reach `subst.resolve_as_value(vid)`, which is what the walk actually wanted.\nThe substrate ALREADY treats a term-var and a value-var as one thing: `resolved_var` (typing.rs:24480-24486) accepts BOTH `Value::Term{id}` → `Term::Var(Global)` AND `Value::Var(Var::Global(vid))`; `walk_value_to_resolved` has a `Value::Var(Global)` arm (typing.rs:24419-24428); `Value` implements `TermView` with a WI-109 Var arm (kb/term_view.rs:1629); typing.rs:26743 bridges `Value::Var(v) -> kb.alloc(Term::Var(*v))`. Nothing in the machinery needs the var to arrive as a TermId.\n\nWHY BARE `Var` AND NOT `Value` / `ViewItem`. `Value::Var(Var)` is a wrapper over the identical payload that re-admits carriers (Node/Entity/Term) which cannot occur here — it would buy a dead arm at every reader, and CLAUDE.md's \"loud error over silent skip\" then makes each of those an `unreachable!`. `ViewItem<'a>` is out on lifetimes regardless of design: it borrows `kb` (op_info.rs:314) and this record is cloned out of the `OpSignature` cache and then read while `kb` is mutably borrowed (`check_unconstrained_type_params(kb: &mut KnowledgeBase, ..., op: &OperationInfoFull, ...)`). `Var` is `Copy` with a manual `PartialEq`/`Eq` (kb/term.rs:82, 166, 177), so the pair stays as cheap as `(Symbol, TermId)` and identity tests still compile.\n\nKEEP THE PAIR — do NOT collapse to `Vec<VarId>`. On the OP side `Symbol == vid.name()` by construction, so the Symbol looks redundant; it is not, because `rigidify_op_type_params` consumes the op table CONCATENATED with `sort_type_params_as_pairs` (typing.rs:33204 `all_params.extend(parent_sort_params.iter().cloned())`), and on the SORT side the Symbol is the QUALIFIED param symbol while the var may be an ANONYMOUS `?` — the rigidify naming comment (typing.rs:33045) exists precisely because `vid.name()` there renders every skolemized param as `?_`. Use `Var` rather than `VarId` for the same reason: it keeps the sort side (which gates on `Var::Global`) expressible unchanged.\n\nKEEP THE KB FACT A HASH-CONSED TERM LIST. The `OperationInfo.type_params` field is persistent asserted structure — exactly CLAUDE.md's hash-consing sweet spot. This ticket changes the DECODED record type and its readers, not the fact representation. `extract_type_params` becomes a total map whose non-`Term::Var(Global)` case is a LOUD error instead of a filter_map drop.\n\nREACHABILITY OF THAT CASE IS UNMEASURED — say so, do not overclaim. The loader is the only in-tree producer and cannot emit a non-var, so the loud arm guards FUTURE producers (and a hand-written `fact anthill.reflect.OperationInfo(...)`, which I have not driven). What it would cost if ever reached is concrete, which is why loud is right: a dropped op type param is invisible in four ways at once — `check_unconstrained_type_params` (typing.rs:13170) never checks it; `resolve_call_type_arg_targets` (typing.rs:13060) cannot find its label, so `op[T = ...]` reports \"no such type parameter\" naming the USER's call for a malformed DECLARATION (the WI-839 failure shape); `rigidify_op_type_params` never skolemizes it, so the body sees a solvable flex var instead of a rigid (WI-392); and `seed_op_type_args` has nothing to match.\n\nONE READER HARD-DEPENDS ON THE HASH-CONSED TermId, and porting it is the change's main risk: `classify_effect_label` (anthill-cpp-gen/src/lib.rs:2979-2990) tests `type_params.iter().any(|(_, tid)| *tid == t)` to recognise an effect-row param (`EffP`), by TermId identity — its comment cites WI-632 for choosing reference identity over a name-string match. Under `Var` this becomes `matches!(kb.get_term(t), Term::Var(v2) if *v2 == v)`: the same judgement (a var's identity is its VarId — CLAUDE.md's representation note: hash-consing is storage, not identity), but if it is missed the row param silently reclassifies `RowParam` -> `Unreadable` and the C++ signature degrades with no error. It must be ported WITH a driven cpp-gen test over an op declaring an effect-row param.\n\nWORK. (i) Change the field on `OpInfoRecord` (op_info.rs:52), `OpSignature` (op_info.rs:89) and `OperationInfoFull` (typing.rs:12965) to `Vec<(Symbol, Var)>`. (ii) Make `extract_type_params` total: map every element, and raise loudly on a non-`Term::Var(Global)` rather than dropping it. (iii) Port every reader listed above — replace each `kb.get_term(tid)` destructure with a direct `Var` read and each `TermIdView(tid)` re-wrap with `Value::Var(v)`. (iv) `typing.rs:10115` calls `walk_type_deep(kb, &subst, *var_term)`, which takes a TermId: either alloc the var term at the call site or route through the value walk — pick one and state which in a comment (the surrounding WI-394 code already surfaces a `Value::Node` binding via `surface_node_binding_to_term`, so do not regress that). (v) Decide whether `sort_type_params_as_pairs` (typing.rs:23392, `Rc<Vec<(Symbol, TermId)>>`) moves in the same change — `rigidify_op_type_params` consumes the two concatenated, so leaving them in different types forces a conversion at typing.rs:33204; moving both is the cheaper end state. (vi) Fold in the corresponding loud-vs-silent decision for `sort_type_params_as_pairs`' skip, or record explicitly why it stays a filter.\n\nACCEPTANCE: no reader destructures a `type_params` entry out of a `TermId`; a non-var entry is a loud error rather than a silent drop, with the comment stating that its reachability is defensive and unmeasured; the cpp-gen effect-row-param classification is covered by a driven test that fails if the identity port is wrong; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh.\n\nSOURCE: design question raised 2026-07-28 — \"can `OperationInfoFull.type_params` be `Vec<(Symbol, Value)>` (or `TermView::Item`) and use a carrier-neutral interface for parsing?\". My first answer measured that the producer always writes `Value::Term` and concluded `Value` adds no inhabitant; that was true and beside the point — the field holds VARIABLES, and `Var` (not a carrier-neutral `Value`) is the honest type. This is NOT a carrier-neutrality change: a variable is not a carrier, and none is gained. What is gained is that the destructure-and-drop becomes unrepresentable. `extract_type_params`' use of the term-only `head_field_term` + `list_to_vec` (vs the carrier-neutral `head_field` + `value_list_to_vec` its siblings `effects_of_head` / `clause_list_field` / `extract_params` use) was measured to be a no-op difference today and is NOT in scope.\n\nSCOPE: rustland/anthill-core/src/kb/{op_info.rs, typing.rs}, rustland/anthill-cpp-gen/src/lib.rs."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-28T13:53:32Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-851", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. One check at the loader's term-conversion funnel (kb/load.rs convert_term constructor arm, beside the PositionalPlan::OverArity sibling) + one gate helper (kb/resolve.rs unknown_named_labels). All EIGHT measured positions now refused with '<ctor>' has no field '<label>' (declares: ...)'.\n\nTHE TICKET'S OPEN QUESTION IS ANSWERED: operation BODIES (cases b, i) come through the SAME loader arm. No second site in check_constructor_iter was needed, so there is no pair of messages that can drift.\n\nTHE EXEMPTION IS NOT THE ONE THE TICKET GUESSED, and the difference is load-bearing. The ticket proposed mirroring positional_to_named_plan's REFLECT-NAME exemption (is_reflect_form_functor + 'anthill.reflect.' prefix). MEASURED: with that exemption the suite is green BUT the WI-849 path stays open (OperationInfo is anthill.reflect.*, so a hand-written fact carrying the undeclared type_params label still loads) — i.e. the ticket's own stated purpose was unmet while every test passed. Dropping the reflect exemption and gating on entity_field_names(...).is_some() alone then broke ~1900 tests, EVERY failure reading 'has no field 'x' (declares: )' — an EMPTY declared list. Root cause: the schema-less functors (named tuples / ordered products, reflect meta-ctors) register Some([]) rather than None, so is_some() admitted them and every label looked unknown. The correct gate is NO SCHEMA = ABSENT OR EMPTY (.filter(|d| !d.is_empty())). That is both precise and honest — a declared entity always has >= 1 field (a zero-field  does not parse), so an empty list never means 'declares nothing'. A name-prefix exemption would additionally have exempted reflect entities that DO declare fields, letting an undeclared label back into exactly the metadata facts that most need to be well-formed.\n\nINTERACTION WITH WI-849, now pinned by tests: this CLOSES the source path that made WI-849's malformed-type_params arm reachable.  is now refused TWICE and wi849_type_param_var_test asserts BOTH messages. WI-849's control test had to change — it asserted that the same fact with a well-formed  still loads, which is no longer true BY DESIGN (the label is undeclared regardless of its value); it now uses only declared fields. The WI-849 typer report is KEPT and stays non-fatal: what closed is the TERM CONVERSION path, not the decode's contract — a producer asserting an OperationInfo fact directly (as the loader itself does, via kb.alloc) still bypasses this refusal, and a panic there would take down a load over metadata the user never wrote. Comment in op_info.rs updated to say exactly that.\n\nTypeError::UnknownField (defined at typing.rs:51 with a Display arm and a LoadError conversion, CONSTRUCTED NOWHERE since it was written) is DELETED per the acceptance. The check lives in the loader, so it uses LoadError::Other exactly like its positional sibling; keeping a typer variant nothing builds would have been the same dead-code shape the ticket was filed about.\n\nCOUNT CORRECTION for the record: earlier WI-849 runs were reported as '2720 passed'. That figure was an artifact of summing the log's ANSI colour codes as numbers. The true baseline was 3556; the final run here is 3567 = 3556 + the 11 new WI-851 tests, 0 failed. The pass/fail verdicts were never affected, only the totals.\n\nTESTS: 11 in wi851_unknown_field_label_test.rs — the 8 refusals, plus 4 controls chosen so the refusals cannot pass for the wrong reason: a well-formed constructor still loads, a PARTIAL named-arg list still loads (the WI-716 fill must not read as an unknown label), a NAMED TUPLE with arbitrary labels still loads (the case that breaks if the gate is 'has named args' instead of 'has a declared schema'), and the stdlib still loads.", at: "2026-07-28T14:28:57Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-851", description: some(value: "An entity constructor's NAMED ARGUMENT naming no declared field is accepted EVERYWHERE — `fact mk(a: 1, bogus: 2)` over `entity mk(a: Int64)` loads clean. Refuse it, at the loader's ONE term-conversion funnel, beside the POSITIONAL over-arity check that is already there.\n\nMEASURED MATRIX (HEAD b0411496 + WI-849 working tree, 2026-07-28). `sort Box entity mk(a: Int64) end`, then one `bogus:` label too many. EVERY constructor position LOADS CLEAN:\n  (a) fact head — `fact mk(a: 1, bogus: 2)`;\n  (h) fact head, positional + unknown named — `fact mk(1, bogus: 2)`;\n  (d) a DIFFERENT entity's fact — `fact outer(inner: 1, bogus: 2)`;\n  (b) operation body — `operation go() -> Box = mk(a: 1, bogus: 2)`;\n  (i) NESTED in an operation body — `outer(inner: mk(a: 1, bogus: 2))`;\n  (j) rule HEAD — `rule mk(a: ?x, bogus: 2) :- on(v: 1)`;\n  (c) rule BODY atom — `rule r(?x) :- mk(a: ?x, bogus: ?y)`;\n  (e) constraint body — `constraint c :- mk(a: ?x, bogus: ?y)`.\nCONTROLS: an OPERATION call with the same shape IS refused — `take(a: 1, bogus: 2)` gives \"named argument 'bogus' names no param\" (`named_arg_coverage_errors`, WI-426/WI-783) — and a well-formed `fact mk(a: 1)` still loads. So this is ONE missing check on the ENTITY-CONSTRUCTOR side, not eight independent holes.\n\nWHY IT IS SILENT, mechanically. Two readers, both keyed the same way and both blind in the same direction:\n  * `check_constructor_iter` (`kb/typing.rs`) iterates the DECLARED FIELDS and looks each one up in the supplied named args (`field_types.iter()` … `named_args.iter().find(|(s, _)| s == field_sym)`). A supplied label matching no declared field is never visited, so its value's type is NEVER CHECKED — the WI-805 shape exactly (\"reachable by neither its name nor its position, with its declared type never checked\").\n  * `canonicalize_record_named_args` (`kb/resolve.rs:4013`) sorts by declared field order via `order.get(s).copied().unwrap_or(usize::MAX)` — an unknown label sorts LAST and rides into the hash-consed term as a real named arg.\nSo the extra field is BUILT, STORED and INDEXED: it participates in discrim matching and in structural equality, it just corresponds to nothing declared. `fact mk(a: 1, bogus: 2)` and `fact mk(a: 1)` are DIFFERENT terms, so a query written the other way silently fails to match.\n\n`TypeError::UnknownField { span, entity_name, field }` ALREADY EXISTS for precisely this (`kb/typing.rs:51`), with a Display arm (:432, \"unknown field '{}' in entity {}\") and a `LoadError` conversion (:649) — and is CONSTRUCTED NOWHERE in the crate. The diagnostic was designed and never wired.\n\nWHERE THE CHECK GOES — the funnel, not the eight producers. `Loader::convert_term`'s constructor arm (`kb/load.rs` ~7743-7830, the `new_functor` / `new_named` / `new_pos` block) is the SINGLE path every one of (a)(c)(d)(e)(h)(j) reaches, and it ALREADY hosts the positional twin of this check: `PositionalPlan::OverArity` raises \"constructor '…' given N positional argument(s) but has M unfilled field(s)\" at :7753. An unknown NAMED label is that error's missing sibling and belongs on the same gate (`kb.entity_field_names(functor).is_some()` — a functor with no declared schema has nothing to check against). This is deliberately NOT eight per-producer checks: \"enumerate the droppers\" is the WI-805 / WI-839 mistake, and cases (i) and (e) were both found only by running the matrix.\n\nOPEN QUESTION THE IMPLEMENTATION MUST SETTLE BY MEASUREMENT, not by guessing: whether operation BODIES (cases b, i) come through the same loader arm or need the check in `check_constructor_iter` too. If a second site is genuinely needed, say so in a comment and give the two ONE shared helper so their messages cannot drift — the WI-839 rule.\n\nEXCLUSIONS to verify against the FULL SUITE rather than assume:\n  * REFLECT-FORM functors. `positional_to_named_plan` already skips `anthill.reflect.*` and the reflect meta-ctors for the POSITIONAL rule; whether the NAMED rule needs the same exemption is UNMEASURED. Note the loader's own `OperationInfo` fact carries a `type_params` named arg that is NOT in the declared `OperationInfo` entity (reflect.anthill:641 lists name/params/return_type/effects/requires/ensures/meta) — but it is built by `kb.alloc(Term::Fn{…})` directly (load.rs:13670), NOT through `convert_term`, so it should not reach this check. CONFIRM that by running the suite; do not assume it.\n  * ORDERED PRODUCTS (named tuples, `anthill.reflect.TupleLiteral`): a tuple's labels are the AUTHOR's, not a declared schema, so it must not be checked. Expected to fall out of the `entity_field_names(...).is_some()` gate — verify.\n  * SUGAR-GENERATED facts (the loader's own emissions that DO go through convert_term) are the real risk. The suite is the measurement.\n\nACCEPTANCE: every one of (a)(b)(c)(d)(e)(h)(i)(j) above is REFUSED with a diagnostic naming the entity AND the offending label; the two controls still behave (the operation-call message unchanged, `fact mk(a: 1)` still loads); a well-formed named tuple with arbitrary labels still loads; `TypeError::UnknownField` is either the variant used or deleted as dead — not left constructed-nowhere; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh.\n\nSOURCE: found 2026-07-28 while delivering WI-849. It is the ROOT of that ticket's residual: a hand-written `fact OperationInfo(…, type_params: cons(head: 42, …))` is only writable because of THIS hole, and it is what made WI-849's malformed-entry arm reachable from user source at all. Closing this makes that arm genuinely unreachable — WI-849's diagnostic then becomes defence in depth rather than a live path.\n\nRELATED: the WI-805 (tuple labels) / WI-808 (entity field names) / WI-809 (named-argument lists) family. Those made every label list DISTINCT; this one makes an entity's label list CLOSED. Same defect class, the member nobody wrote.\n\nSCOPE: rustland/anthill-core/src/kb/load.rs (primary), rustland/anthill-core/src/kb/typing.rs (only if the op-body measurement says a second site is needed)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-28T14:28:59Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-851", author: "user", content: "ADDENDUM — three code fragments were dropped from the previous feedback note (backticks in the shell argument ate them). Restoring them verbatim:\n\n1. \"a declared entity always has >= 1 field (a zero-field ... does not parse)\" — the elided spelling is: entity on()  — an entity declaration with an empty field list, which is a PARSE error, which is why an empty declared-field list never means \"an entity that declares nothing\" and can safely be read as \"no schema\".\n\n2. \"this CLOSES the source path that made WI-849's malformed-type_params arm reachable. ... is now refused TWICE\" — the elided program is the hand-written fact:\n     fact OperationInfo(name: Driver, params: nil(), return_type: Int64,\n                        effects: nil(), requires: nil(), ensures: nil(),\n                        type_params: cons(head: 42, tail: nil()), meta: meta())\n\n3. \"it asserted that the same fact with a well-formed ... still loads\" — the elided spelling is the same fact with type_params: nil() instead of the cons list. That control is now WRONG by design: the label type_params is undeclared on the OperationInfo entity regardless of what value it carries, so the fact is refused either way. The control was replaced with one that writes only declared fields (name/params/return_type/effects/requires/ensures/meta).", at: "2026-07-28T14:29:35Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-851", author: "user", content: "/code-review OUTCOME — 15 findings; 13 fixed, 2 recorded. Full workspace 3571 passed / 0 failed. The two that changed the DESIGN were driven before acting, not taken on trust.\n\nTHE ONE THAT MATTERED (finding 1) — CONFIRMED BY DRIVING, and it was a hole I shipped. The gate treated an EMPTY declared-field list as \"no schema\" and exempted it. Measured: `fact mk(xs: nil(bogus: 1))`, `fact mk(v: none(bogus: 1))` and a user `entity on` + `fact on(bogus: 1)` ALL LOADED CLEAN while the control `some(value: 1, bogus: 2)` was correctly refused. My justification in the doc — \"a declared entity always has >= 1 field (a zero-field entity does not parse)\" — was FALSE, generalised from ONE probe of the PARENTHESISED spelling. The paren-less form parses and is what the prelude uses throughout: entity nil, entity none, entity console, entity Nothing, entity empty_row, entity empty_stream. So the exemption covered the two most-written constructors in the language.\n  The gate is now: exempt an ORDERED PRODUCT (a named tuple — its labels are the author's, order IS identity) and a reflect FORM meta-ctor (is_reflect_form_functor, a structural test), then require a registered schema. An EMPTY schema is CLOSED AT ZERO — a zero-field entity accepts no labels at all. Deliberately NOT the `anthill.reflect.` NAME prefix, which hides the same cases but also exempts reflect entities that DO declare fields.\n  THE LESSON, and it is the same one twice in one session: a probe that fails to reach a case proves nothing about the case. I made this exact error in WI-849 (two probe spellings that missed the arm) and repeated it here on the parse question.\n\nDESIGN CHANGE THE USER DROVE, past what the review asked: the type_params decode is now CARRIER-NEUTRAL. Reading the field with the term-only `head_field_term` had a blind spot one level above the one being fixed — it returns None for a value-carried field, INDISTINGUISHABLE from \"this operation declares no type parameters\", and an `Err(TermId)` error channel structurally cannot report a non-term offender because there is no TermId to report. Now reads via `head_field` and yields `Result<(Symbol, Var), Value>`, mirroring effects_of_head / clause_list_field / extract_params. This also closed finding 6: a `type_params` field that is not a LIST at all (`type_params: 42`) previously decoded to zero entries and zero reports (list_to_vec breaks out of the walk and returns what it has) — the same silent-drop class one layer out, now reported via `list_items_strict`.\n\nALSO FIXED:\n  2. The report said \"operation 'X'\" for a symbol that, by the mechanism making it reachable, is never an operation: build_op_signatures keeps the FIRST fact per name and the loader emits one for every real op, so a hand-written fact naming a real operation is SHADOWED and never decoded. Now says \"the `OperationInfo` fact naming 'X'\". Same message-honesty defect WI-839's review fixed once already.\n  3. The offender rendered via `format!(\"{:?}\")` — `Fn { functor: Symbol(16), … TermId(3060) }`. Now carries the offender unrendered to the report site and prints it as source text (`42`, `[1]`). Note `type_display_name_value` was NOT enough — it is a TYPE renderer and still leaked `TermId(13506)` for an entry that is not a type; term-carried offenders go through TermPrinter.\n  4 + 10. The WI-851 error was a span-less LoadError::Other, so it rendered with no path:line:col AND dedup_load_errors (keyed on the rendered string) collapsed every occurrence of the same entity+label across the whole load — even across files — into ONE unlocatable message. Now a span-bearing LoadError::UnknownEntityField carrying entity/field/declared, added to user_span's arms. That also restores the structure lost by deleting TypeError::UnknownField.\n  7. My new doc on rigidify_op_type_params justified its Var::Global skip with \"a sort param may alias a CONCRETE sort\" — a case sort_type_params_as_pairs FILTERS OUT before the concat, so the skip guards nothing. That is the WI-809 \"fabricated a reachability justification for dead code\" pattern re-committed; the doc now states it is defensive and says why it is kept as a total match rather than an assertion.\n  8. build_op_signatures walked each type_params list TWICE (once to report, once for the signature). One decode, partitioned at the caller.\n  9. type_param_var_term used kb.alloc, which increments the refcount even on a hash-cons HIT — monotonic inflation on per-node typer paths with no matching release. Added KnowledgeBase::find_term / TermStore::find, a non-mutating lookup for a caller that only needs to NAME a term it already holds.\n  12. The unreachable! I put in op_tp_pinning_params panicked on data reachable through a `pub` field. That function computes a STAGING HINT, so an unusable entry costs a missed reordering, never a wrong judgement — back to a skip, with the reason stated.\n  13 + 14. Removed the duplicated declared-field rendering (now one `render_field_list`, shared with the positional OverArity sibling, which also spells the empty case \"none\" instead of the bare `(declares: )`), and dropped the `.unwrap_or_default()` that would have silently produced that exact bug-signature string.\n  15. The WI-849 assertion checked only a shared substring, so findings 2 and 3 were both invisible to it. It now asserts the SYMBOL named and the RENDERED entry.\n  11. PRE-EXISTING, fixed anyway since it was one line and adjacent: check_pattern_fragment pushes to `errors` without a paired `sources` push INSIDE the per-sort loop, so after one pattern-fragment error every later entity-fact / operation-body error paired with the previous error's SourceId and rendered path:line:col for the WRONG file. The trailing resize pads the tail but cannot re-align the middle.\n\nRECORDED, NOT FIXED:\n  5. extract_type_params still drops silently on lookup_operation_info's FALLBACK scan — the pre-typer path (const-purity gate, eq-dispatch build) and any KB that never type-checks (codegen-only, query-only, an embedder calling load_all without typechecking). The decode runs under &KnowledgeBase with no error channel, which is why the report lives on the build_op_signatures → typer path; giving it one means either an interior-mutable diagnostic channel on the KB (none exists) or making the decode fallible at ~every call site. Now much narrower than when WI-849 shipped: WI-851 makes a malformed OperationInfo fact unwritable from source, so the remaining exposure is a producer asserting such a fact directly.\n\nA TEST BUG THE FIX EXPOSED, worth recording because it would have passed as a refusal test for the wrong reason: my first `nil`/`none` cases used `namespace test.wi851.nil` — a namespace whose LAST SEGMENT equals the constructor's short name SHADOWS it (WI-714), so `nil` resolved to the namespace and the program loaded clean. Renamed to `…nil_ctor` / `…none_ctor` and the reason is in the test's doc.", at: "2026-07-28T15:16:03Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-848", description: some(value: "cpp-gen row_named_term: the `Value::Var` (\"field is unbound\") arm has an UNMEASURED reachability claim in its own doc comment, and FIVE natural spellings of that claim do not reach it — so either the arm is dead, or its real trigger is something nobody has identified. Decide which, and pin it.\n\nWHAT THE CODE CLAIMS (rustland/anthill-cpp-gen/src/lib.rs, row_named_term, added by WI-833 0f979eac). The doc names two triggers verbatim: \"the resolved fact/rule OMITTED a required field (the loader var-fills a missing required field; an omitted `Option` field is `none`-filled instead, so it stays a `Term`) or a rule head left it unconstrained\". On that reading it returns a loud CppCodegenError, which is the RIGHT direction (CLAUDE.md \"loud error over silent skip\") — this ticket does NOT propose making it silent again.\n\nWHAT I MEASURED (HEAD 2cdfde3e, driven through emit_namespace_header via the wi833 `emit_inc_header` harness, IncludeMapping over `int64_t`, 2026-07-28). All FIVE emit CLEANLY — no error, header produced, the malformed mapping simply contributing no probe:\n  (a) `rule IncludeMapping(lang: \"cpp\", host_type: \"int64_t\") :- Toggle(on: true)` — omitted required `include`;\n  (b) the same with `include: ?anything` in the head;\n  (c) `include: ?i` where `?i` is bound by a body goal that resolves to nothing (`Src(v: ?i)` with no Src facts);\n  (d) `include: ?i` where `?i` appears NOWHERE in the body;\n  (e) `include: ?` (anonymous head var);\n  plus (f) `fact IncludeMapping(lang: \"cpp\", host_type: \"int64_t\")` — a FACT omitting the required field.\nSo the doc's first trigger (\"a realization fact is missing this required field\") and its second (\"a rule head left it unconstrained\") are BOTH refuted as written, for this functor. This also refutes the /code-review finding that motivated the ticket, which predicted (a) would \"kill every namespace-header emit in the project\" — it does not.\n\nWHY THIS IS STILL WORTH A TICKET, i.e. what is actually wrong. (1) An arm whose stated trigger cannot be produced is either DEAD CODE carrying a false explanation, or LIVE with an unknown trigger — and both readings are bad in the same way: the next person to touch resolve_realization_rows will trust the comment. (2) If it IS live, the diagnostic is unusable: the message is a bare format! carrying only the field name — no rule identity, no span — so an author told \"field `include` is unbound\" has no way to find which of their rules is at fault. Contrast the ExtentReadError::BodiedRule refusal this reader replaced, which rendered `head :- body`. (3) The blast radius is the whole read: row_named_term's Err propagates out of Includes::from_kb / resolve_realization_rows and aborts the entire emit, so ONE malformed row takes down every header in the project — acceptable for a loud error only if the message can be acted on, which is (2).\n\nWORK: (i) Determine the arm's real reachability — inspect what read_facts_resolved actually hands back for an under-determined row (does the enumeration goal's var-fill survive to the reified Value, or does the solution flounder / get dropped first?), across BOTH a fact and a bodied rule, and for a field the functor declares REQUIRED vs one declared `Option`. Try functors other than IncludeMapping (TypeMapping, NamingConvention) before concluding — the measurement above is IncludeMapping-only. (ii) If reachable: a test that reaches it, and a diagnostic that names the offending rule (or at minimum its span / rendered head), so the error is actionable. (iii) If NOT reachable: say so — delete the arm, or keep it as an explicit unreachable/defensive case whose comment states it is defensive and NOT a described user-facing behaviour. Do not leave a comment asserting a trigger that has been measured absent. (iv) Whatever the verdict, record it in the doc comment, since the current text is the artifact that misled the review.\n\nACCEPTANCE: the arm's status is DECIDED and stated in code — either a driven test that reaches it plus a diagnostic naming the offending rule, or its removal/redesignation as defensive with the reachability finding written down; the six probe spellings above keep emitting cleanly (they are correct programs — a mapping missing a required field contributes no mapping); full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh.\n\nSOURCE: /code-review of the WI-833 merge, 2026-07-28 — finding recorded as \"aborts the ENTIRE realization read for one malformed row\"; its repro was driven and REFUTED, and what survives is the reachability/diagnostic question above. SCOPE: rustland/anthill-cpp-gen (and kb/extent.rs read_facts_resolved for the reachability determination only)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-28T19:05:59Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-850", description: some(value: "`operation foo[T = Int64](...)`: the type-param DEFAULT is parsed, recorded in the IR, and then SILENTLY DROPPED by the loader — decide whether it is a feature and honour it, or refuse it at load.\n\nWHAT I MEASURED (HEAD b0411496, 2026-07-28). The parse IR carries it: `parse/ir.rs:544-550`, `pub struct TypeParam { name: Symbol, default: Option<TypeExpr>, span: Span }`, whose doc reads \"Default value from `[T = Int]`; `None` for bare `[T]` (which the loader treats as `T = ?` — a fresh anonymous logical variable)\". The parser FILLS it — `anthill-core/tests/include/parse_test.rs:4797-4808` asserts `op.type_params[0].default` is `Some(...)` for a defaulted param, and :4783 asserts `None` for a bare one. The loader NEVER READS it: `load_operation` (kb/load.rs:13387-13401) mints a fresh var per param from the NAME alone (`kb.fresh_var(tp_sym)`), ignoring `tp.default`; `scan_operation_params` (kb/load.rs:1704-1709) reads only `tp.name`. A grep for `.default` across the whole of kb/load.rs finds no `TypeParam` read at all. So the field is write-only.\n\nCONSEQUENCE. `operation foo[T = Int64](x: T) -> T` loads EXACTLY as if written `operation foo[T](x: T) -> T`. The default is accepted by the grammar, stored in the IR, and has no effect anywhere in the KB, the typer, or codegen. The user-visible failure is worse than inert: a call that leaves `T` otherwise unconstrained raises `UnconstrainedTypeParam` (kb/typing.rs:13163-13195), whose message tells the author to pin it with `op[T = ...]` — when they already wrote that pin ON THE DECLARATION. Silent acceptance of a construct with no semantics is the exact shape CLAUDE.md's \"prefer a loud error over a silent skip\" rule exists for, and passing parse tests are what hid it (parse_test covers that the default PARSES; nothing covers that it DOES anything).\n\nDECIDE FIRST, DO NOT GUESS. Read docs/kernel-language.md and docs/proposals/ BEFORE coding — the sort side has both `sort T = ?` and `sort T = Concrete`, so there may already be an intended reading for the operation side that the loader simply never implemented. Two admissible outcomes:\n  (a) HONOUR IT. The default becomes the seed for `T` when nothing else constrains it — neither a call-site `[T = ...]` (`seed_op_type_args` / `resolve_call_type_arg_targets`), nor the expected type, nor arg unification — i.e. it is consulted at exactly the point `check_unconstrained_type_params` currently raises, turning that error into a fill. This requires carrying the default INTO the type-param table as an ADDED field beside the var (an `Option<Value>`, NOT a re-typing of the var itself), threading it through the `OperationInfo` fact, and a decision on whether a default may mention an earlier param (`[T, U = List[T]]`) — say yes or no explicitly. Coordinate with the sibling ticket that re-types that table to `Vec<(Symbol, Var)>`: this is the one place the table legitimately needs to carry a TYPE as well as a var, and the two changes touch the same three struct definitions.\n  (b) REFUSE IT AT LOAD, with a located error naming the operation and the parameter, until (a) is designed. Cheap, honest, and reversible.\nEither is acceptable. Silently dropping is not.\n\nWORK. Decide (a) or (b) with the spec in hand; implement it; test the defaulted declaration END TO END — a driven load + call, not another parse assertion. Whichever way it goes, replace the doc comment on `TypeParam.default` with the verdict and its reason, since the current text (\"Default value from `[T = Int]`\") is the artifact that reads as though the loader honours it.\n\nACCEPTANCE: `operation foo[T = Int64](x: T) -> T` either (a) type-checks with `T` filling to `Int64` at an otherwise-unconstrained call — pinned by a test that FAILS on today's HEAD — or (b) fails to load with an error naming the op and the param; `TypeParam.default`'s doc states the verdict; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh.\n\nSOURCE: found 2026-07-28 while auditing `OperationInfoFull.type_params` — while establishing that every entry in that table is a plain variable, `TypeParam.default` turned out to be the ONE place the table would legitimately need to carry a type instead.\n\nSCOPE: rustland/anthill-core/src/{parse/ir.rs, kb/load.rs, kb/typing.rs}, docs/kernel-language.md."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-28T20:20:26Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-847", description: some(value: "cpp-gen resolve_type_mapping: the QUALIFIED-over-SHORT anthill_type priority was lost in the WI-833 migration, turning a working override into an ambiguity ERROR — and the function's own doc still promises the priority it no longer honors. CODE-READ at HEAD 2cdfde3e (rustland/anthill-cpp-gen/src/lib.rs, resolve_type_mapping): the loop MERGES the hits of every candidate spelling into ONE hits vec (hits.extend(query_type_mappings(kb, \"cpp\", name)?)) and then hands the merged list to select_keyed_unique, whose per-key aggregation is unique_or_ambiguous — 0 distinct falls through, 1 selects, >1 is a LOUD CppCodegenError. So a stdlib mapping written under the SHORT name and a project override written under the QUALIFIED name, both at the same key, are now two competing mappings at one key and codegen ABORTS where it used to take the override. VERIFIED against the parent: 0f979eac~1:lib.rs:3861-3872's select_keyed selected by hits.iter().position(|h| key_of(h) == ak) — FIRST match in list order — and resolve_type_mapping pushes the qualified spelling's hits FIRST, so the qualified entry won. That first-wins was deliberate at the time; select_keyed_unique's own doc now says the ambiguity is \"never the silent first-wins the WI-089 select_keyed did\", i.e. the change was intended for a SAME-SPELLING conflict, but the CROSS-SPELLING case it also swept in is not discussed anywhere. THE DOC IS NOW FALSE either way: resolve_type_mapping still reads that its `names` are the candidate `anthill_type` spellings \"tried in order and merged (a sort's qualified name first, then its short name, so a fact written either way resolves)\" — \"tried in order\" does no work once the merged list is deduped-or-refused, so the text describes a priority the code cannot express. NOT YET DRIVEN, and the reason is the second half of the finding: the two-name call is reached from exactly ONE site, marshal_for_type (lib.rs:1261, passing &[&qualified, short]) — the value-level lift/lower reader at a carrier-dispatch boundary. Every other resolve_type_mapping caller passes a SINGLE name (lib.rs:1164, 1172, 3700, 3790, 4074), and every WI-833 TypeMapping test goes through money_base -> cpp_base_host_type, one spelling. So the merge path has NO coverage at all, which is why the behaviour change is invisible to the suite. DECIDE, do not guess: either (a) restore qualified-over-short as an EXPLICIT priority — select per spelling in order and take the first spelling that yields a selection, so a cross-spelling pair is an OVERRIDE and only a same-spelling pair is ambiguous; or (b) keep the merge and rule that a cross-spelling pair IS a conflict — in which case say so in the doc, and make the diagnostic name BOTH spellings so the author can see why two facts they believe unrelated collide. (a) preserves a working override idiom; (b) is defensible but is a breaking change to how a project overrides a stdlib mapping, and nothing records that decision today. ACCEPTANCE: (1) a driven test through marshal_for_type (emit with a carrier binding + lift/lower mappings) exercising a qualified/short pair at one key — the coverage that is missing whichever way the decision goes, and it must be shown to FAIL on the current behaviour if (a) is chosen; (2) the decided behaviour implemented and resolve_type_mapping's doc made true; (3) the existing WI-833 same-spelling ambiguity tests pass unchanged; (4) full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. SOURCE: /code-review of the WI-833 merge, 2026-07-28. SCOPE: rustland/anthill-cpp-gen."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-29T03:37:42Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-852", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED. Parse errors now render `path:line:col: message`, the rendering WI-745 gave load errors.\n\nTHE OWNER. `span::render_located(path, body, has_span)` owns the file-prefix rule; `LoadError::located_render` delegates to it and the new `ParseError::format_located(&Path, &str)` calls it.\n\nWHY NOT THE CARRIER THE TICKET PREFERRED — the reason is written at `format_located`, as the ticket required. The two families TRAVEL differently. A `LoadError` is raised deep in the loader with no `&ParsedFile` in hand and reaches `load_all`'s caller merged with N files' errors, so its file identity must RIDE with it — that is what WI-745's `Located` is for. A `ParseError` is returned by `parse()` straight to the caller that just read the text, and is rendered in that same expression, so a carrier would be constructed and consumed on one line. The one place that DID hold `ParseError`s across a boundary — `PersistenceError::Parse` — now renders at its raise site, so nothing in the tree stores an unrendered `ParseError`.\n\nThe cost of a rendering (a call-site obligation instead of a type invariant) is paid off differently: `ParseError`'s sourceless `Display` AND its `std::error::Error` impl are DELETED, not documented as discouraged. Measured before deleting — nothing else in the workspace referenced them; every remaining consumer uses `{e:?}` / `expect` (Debug). A printer that forgets to pass the source no longer compiles.\n\nNINE PRINTERS, not the four the ticket named — and enumerating was the risk it flagged (WI-805). anthill-cli `main.rs` (the shared file-parse loop behind load/check/query/codegen-cpp; `codegen rust`; both `collect_queries` arms), `run.rs`, anthill-stl `stdlib.rs`, anthill-todo bundle + project files, anthill-core persistence, and the anthill-rust-gen GENERATED bundle template — a printer living inside a string literal, verified by running the ignored `emitted_bundle_compiles` test.\n\nSYNTHESIZED SOURCES. `collect_queries` parses `--import` lines + the user's text, so a raw span indexes into text the author only partly wrote. Split at the prefix: a span past it is SHIFTED back into the user's text, so `--query-file` reports the same line whether or not `-i` was passed (unshifted it drifted one line per flag — a location that is worse than none); a span inside the prefix names `--import`; an inline `--pattern` names the flag, because a line:col there would point into the import lines and the synthesized `fact` keyword, i.e. at text the author never wrote.\n\nTESTS. `anthill-cli/tests/wi852_parse_error_location_test.rs`, 7 tests pinning RENDERED STRINGS, including `stage_does_not_change_the_rendering` (one fixture through check / load / run / codegen rust must yield the IDENTICAL location) and a whole-stderr negative control on the byte-offset format. Verified by stashing the source changes and re-running: 7/7 FAIL against pre-change code, 7/7 pass after. Full workspace green, 3596 passed / 0 failed.\n\nREVIEW (/simplify, 4 agents) — applied: dropped `Option<&Path>` from `format_located` (no caller passed None, and my doc had named two None users that do not exist — the WI-809 fabricated-justification shape again); collapsed `PersistenceError::Parse` to `Vec<String>` rendered at the raise site (kills a measured 32 -> 72 byte widening of `Result<(), PersistenceError>` on per-fact persist/retract paths, plus a whole-file copy); `render_located` takes `body` by value so the pathless arm moves; `format_with_source` now uses the previously-DEAD `Span::format_start`; `QueryOrigin` enum -> `Option<(&Path, &str)>`; the import prefix is built in one place so the two arms cannot disagree about the split point.\n\nNOT DONE, deliberately, all outside this ticket's scope:\n  - Four near-identical read -> parse -> locate loops (cli main.rs x2, run.rs, todo) and three embedded-source parse loops (stdlib, todo bundle, generated template) stay separate; consolidating them changes control flow in three commands.\n  - `Span::line_col` walks from byte 0 per error, so N errors on one file is O(N x len). A reviewer measured it: a corrupted 3.1 MB workitems.anthill with 688 errors spends ~1.3 s debug / ~0.10 s release in location rendering. It is an error path that then exits; a shared line-start index would also pay off for load.rs's ~20 hand-rolled line:col arms.\n  - The two `collect_queries` LOAD printers (scan_definitions errors) stay unlocated, so inside that one function the stage still decides. Stamping `.with_path()` there would be WRONG while the source is a concatenation: the line would be shifted and then falsely attributed to the file. It is blocked on the `--import` defect filed separately.", at: "2026-07-28T20:56:48Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-852", description: some(value: "CLI parse errors render a BYTE OFFSET, not `path:line:col` — the span-aware `format_with_source` twin exists and exactly one binary uses it.\n\nWHAT I MEASURED (2026-07-28, HEAD 47a0a730 + WI-850). Driving `anthill check` on a file whose only fault is a converter diagnostic prints, with exit 1:\n  error: /tmp/.../defaulted.anthill: parse error at 86..95: operation `foo`: type parameter `T` carries a default …\nThe byte pair is `ParseError`'s `Display` (parse/error.rs:28-36). The span-aware twin `ParseError::format_with_source` (parse/error.rs:22-25) renders `line:col: message` and IS in the tree — used by `anthill-todo` (main.rs:654) and two tests, and by nothing else. Every CLI parse-error site formats with `Display`: anthill-cli/src/main.rs:555-558 (load / check / query / codegen cpp), :853-856 (`codegen rust`), and :1417-1422 + :1474-1477, which print `parse error: {e}` without even naming the FILE.\n\nCONSEQUENCE. WI-745 gave LOAD errors a `path:line:col` rendering (`LoadError::Located`, whose whole point was that a raw byte offset \"named nothing once N files merged into one KB\"); PARSE errors were left behind. So which STAGE found the fault decides whether the author gets a clickable location or a byte offset they have to count to — a distinction the author cannot predict, and one that widens as converter-stage refusals multiply: WI-805, WI-808, WI-809 and WI-850 are all reported as parse errors.\n\nWORK. Give parse errors the same file + `line:col` rendering load errors have. The source text is in hand at every one of those call sites (it was just read from disk), so this is a RENDERING change, not a plumbing one. Decide where it belongs: a `Located`-style wrapper mirroring WI-745, so ONE owner renders both error families and they cannot drift, or a fix at each of the four call sites — prefer the wrapper, and state the reason if not. The two sites printing `parse error: {e}` must also name the file. Check `anthill run` / `anthill-todo` / the bundle path for further parse-error printers before assuming four is the whole list — enumerating printers is exactly where WI-805 went wrong.\n\nACCEPTANCE: `anthill check <file>` on a source carrying a converter diagnostic prints `path:line:col: message`; a test in anthill-cli pins the RENDERED STRING (a bad fixture plus an assertion on the message, not just on the exit code); full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh.\n\nSOURCE: found 2026-07-28 while delivering WI-850, driving that ticket's new refusal through the real CLI to confirm it was located. It named the file and the operation but pointed at bytes 86..95.\n\nSCOPE: rustland/anthill-cli/src/main.rs, rustland/anthill-core/src/parse/error.rs."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-28T20:56:54Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-852", author: "claude", content: "/code-review OUTCOME — 13 findings; 6 fixed, 7 recorded. Full workspace 3598 passed / 0 failed. The one that mattered was DRIVEN before I acted on it, and it was a live defect I had shipped.\n\nFINDING 1, CONFIRMED BY DRIVING — my own fix reintroduced the defect this ticket removes. `located_query_parse_error` split the synthesized query source on `span.start < prefix_len`. But when the user's text does not begin a valid top-level item, tree-sitter's recovery merges the `--import` prefix and that text into ONE `ERROR` node starting at byte 0. Measured, on a query file beginning `) oops`:\n  without -i:  error: <path>:1:1: syntax error near `) oops`\n  with -i:     error: --import: syntax error near `import wi852.good ) oops`\n— the file is never named, the location is gone, and the author is pointed at a flag that is not at fault. The existing test could not catch it: `bad-query.anthill` starts with `fact`, a valid top-level start, so recovery does not merge.\nFIX: attribute by OVERLAP, not by where the span STARTS. A span lying WHOLLY inside the prefix is an `--import` fault; a span reaching past it is located in the user's text with a start that began in the prefix CLAMPED to the user's first byte. New test `a_span_merged_across_the_prefix_is_located_in_the_file` with a `) oops` fixture; verified it FAILS against the `start`-keyed rule and passes after.\n\nFINDING 2 exposed the SAME false attribution one level down. The review proposed naming which `-i` flag by line index. Implemented, then driven — and the index was WRONG: with `-i wi852.good -i 'not a name!!'` the recovery node covers BOTH import lines, so the flag at its start is `wi852.good`, blaming the first flag for the second's fault. Naming a range instead: `--import (one of `a`, `b`): …` when a span covers several, `--import `a`: …` when it covers one. Never wrong, and strictly more than the bare `--import:` the second diagnostic (\"unexpected top-level node: ERROR\") otherwise carried alone. The lesson generalises: a synthesized-source span identifies a REGION, and every attribution from it must be a region too.\n\nFINDING 3 — I wrote an overclaiming doc again, the WI-809 shape. `render_located`'s note said each family's `line:col` half \"is spelled by `Span::format_start`\", and load.rs said the two families \"render identically, by construction\". Both false: `format_start` has ONE caller (`ParseError::format_with_source`) and `LoadError` hand-rolls `Span::line_col` + `format!` in ~20 arms. The docs now state exactly what is enforced — the PREFIX is shared; the position half is two implementations agreeing by CONVENTION and able to drift silently. Routing load.rs's 20 arms through `format_start` would make the stronger claim true and is the real fix; it is outside this ticket.\n\nFINDING 11 + 12, tests. `prepended_import_lines_do_not_shift_the_file_location` was the only one of the seven asserting neither an exit code nor a control — fixed. And `assert_no_byte_offsets` had become UNFALSIFIABLE: it anchored on the literal \"parse error at\", which no code path can emit any more since `impl Display for ParseError` was deleted, so it guarded only against re-adding that exact wording and would miss any other sourceless rendering. Replaced with a POSITIVE control — every `error:` line naming the fixture must carry `:<digits>:<digits>:` — which fails the moment a diagnostic names the file without saying where in it.\n\nRECORDED, NOT FIXED:\n  4. `render_located(path, body, has_span)` is boolean-blind — nothing checks that `has_span` agrees with how the caller built `body`. Two callers, both correct, and the /simplify reviewer judged the 3-arg shape right-depth; deriving the flag would mean restructuring both families' body construction.\n  5. The generated-bundle printer is compile-checked only by the `#[ignore]`d `emitted_bundle_compiles`. Run manually after every edit to it here (passes); un-ignoring it puts a nested `cargo check` of anthill-core in the default suite.\n  6. Deleting `Display` + `impl Error` on `ParseError`, and re-typing `PersistenceError::Parse` to `Vec<String>`, IS a breaking change for a downstream consumer doing `parse::parse(s)?` into `Box<dyn Error>` or inspecting a parse error's span programmatically. anthill-core is not published (the bundle generator still describes crates.io as future), and the enforcement — a printer that forgets the source does not compile — is what the carrier design would otherwise have had to buy. Deliberate, with that cost stated.\n  7. `Span::line_col` walks from byte 0 per error (O(N x len)). Measured by a reviewer at ~1.3 s debug / ~0.10 s release for 688 errors on a 3.1 MB file. Error path that then exits; the fix (one line-start index per source) belongs with load.rs's 20 arms.\n  8. The `--import`/shift mechanism remains a bandaid over the WI-853 root cause, which the WI-853 ticket says to DELETE once imports are supplied structurally. Kept because the flag is documented and the machinery is now correct; WI-853 carries the instruction to remove it.\n  9. The span shift clones the `ParseError` to overwrite two u32s. Kept: building the body at the call site instead would re-spell `format_with_source`'s format, which is the drift this ticket exists to prevent.\n  13. Seven near-identical read -> parse -> locate loops stay separate — as recorded in the delivery note.", at: "2026-07-29T05:47:19Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-854", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED. A/B measured on the same inputs, same binary path, stashing only the sources:\n\n  2100 parse diagnostics, 2.7 MB source:  50.3 s  ->  ~2.0 s\n  600 load diagnostics, 2.4 MB source:    23-26 s ->  ~0.7 s\n\nTHE OWNER. `span::LineIndex` — line starts in a `Vec<u32>` built once per source (via `match_indices('\\n')`, which searches through memchr and so stays fast in the DEBUG builds every measurement here comes from), then `partition_point` for the line and a walk of that ONE line for the column. The column walks rather than subtracts because columns count CHARACTERS, and that is also what makes an offset past the end or inside a multi-byte character resolve instead of panic — which a tree-sitter recovery span can hand it.\n\nBATCH AT THE PRINTER. `ParseError::all_located(errors, path, source)` and `LoadError::render_all(errors)`; every printer holds a whole `Vec` and now goes through one of them. `render_all` keys a per-file index on the source `Arc`'s ADDRESS — every error from one file shares that `Arc` (the loader and the typer's `SourceRegistry` are handed the same `parsed.source.clone()`), so it is exact, and a miss would cost a rebuilt index, never a wrong answer.\n\nTHE QUADRATIC CANNOT BE REINTRODUCED BY ACCIDENT: `Span::line_col` and `Span::format_start` are DELETED (both were dead once the arms moved), so `LineIndex` is the only way to resolve a position and a caller with N diagnostics has to build one. Same enforcement-by-deletion as WI-852's `Display`.\n\nONE OWNER FOR THE WHOLE LOCATION, finally. WI-852 could only claim the file PREFIX was shared, because `LoadError`'s ~20 arms each hand-rolled `Span::line_col` + `format!(\"{}:{}: …\")`; a /code-review finding called that doc out as overclaiming. All 20 now render `loc.format_start(*span)`, so prefix AND position are single-owner and the two families agree by construction. The stale comments in span.rs and load.rs that said otherwise are corrected.\n\nCORRECTNESS BAR WAS EQUIVALENCE, not plausibility. The replaced scan is kept verbatim as an ORACLE in `span::line_index_tests`, asserted equal at EVERY offset of 9 sources including empty, newline-only, no-trailing-newline, `\\r\\n`, and multi-byte (`é`, `…`). A review agent then differential-fuzzed 200k random strings over an alphabet including multi-byte and NUL, at every offset plus `u32::MAX`: zero mismatches. Also verified `partition_point` can never yield 0 (so `starts[line-1]` cannot panic) and that distinct live `Arc<str>`s cannot share an address.\n\nFOUND BY REVIEW, FIXED HERE (three paths I had missed, all the same shape):\n  - `QuerySource::located_error` rendered in a LOOP, rebuilding an index per error — now `located_errors` builds one and the path+index travel as ONE `Option<(&Path, &LineIndex)>`, which also deleted a silent `_ =>` arm that would have rendered a query-FILE error as a `--pattern` one.\n  - `collect_queries`' two `scan_definitions` printers used `{e}`, and `scan_definitions` stamps every error `Located` — so `Display` rebuilt an index per error. Now `render_all`.\n  - The GENERATED bundle's load-error loop (anthill-rust-gen template) was left on the per-error path — in code shipped to every bundled binary. Now `render_all`; the `#[ignore]`d `emitted_bundle_compiles` was run to prove the generated crate still builds.\n  - Plus the last two dev/test printers in anthill-stl's reflect helpers.\n\nALSO FROM REVIEW: `LineIndex::source()` had no caller (it was the accessor for the one-slot-cache design not taken) — deleted; the oracle test's `Span::line_col` assert had become tautological after the delegation — deleted; `format_at` on both families made private; `ParseError::format_located` deleted once its last caller took the indexed form. Added `render_all_matches_the_per_error_display`, which pins the batch against the per-error `Display` over a batch INTERLEAVING two files (so the cache is exercised out of order) plus a non-`Located` error — `render_all` is a second, structurally different rendering path and had no test.\n\nRECORDED, NOT FIXED: the column walk is O(column), so a SINGLE-LINE source keeps the old cost (measured at -O0: 2100 lookups over a 2.7 MB one-line source, 49.4 s; the same text at ~54-byte lines, 2.2 ms). Never slower than before, and hand-written sources have lines; a memo of the last lookup would close it if a generated one-line `.anthill` ever appears. Documented on `LineIndex`.\n\nFull workspace 3601 passed / 0 failed. No rendered diagnostic changed — verified by the pinned CLI tests, the oracle, and driving check / query / anthill-todo on multi-byte and multi-file sources.", at: "2026-07-29T06:39:44Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-854", description: some(value: "Rendering a diagnostic re-walks its whole source from byte 0, so N errors in one file cost O(N x len) — measured at 50 SECONDS for one `anthill check`.\n\nWHAT I MEASURED (2026-07-29, HEAD c6f6a7e7). `Span::line_col(source, offset)` walks `source.char_indices()` from byte 0 until it reaches the offset, and EVERY located diagnostic calls it — once per parse error (`ParseError::format_with_source`) and once per span-bearing load error (~20 hand-rolled arms in kb/load.rs). Printers loop over a whole `Vec` of errors from ONE file, so the source is re-walked per error. Driving the debug CLI:\n  - 2100 parse diagnostics in a 2.7 MB file: 50.3 s\n  - 600 load diagnostics in a 2.4 MB file: 23-26 s\nBoth are ~99% location rendering: the same input renders in 2.0 s / 0.93 s once the source is indexed once. This is not a synthetic shape — anthill-todo's own workitems.anthill is 3.1 MB, and a stale grammar against a large project produces exactly this: hundreds of errors in one big file.\n\nCONSEQUENCE. The cost is invisible at small scale (a 45 KB file with 3 errors is ~20 us) and quadratic past it, so it shows up as \"the compiler hangs\" precisely when the author has the most to fix. It also lands on the two paths that matter most: `anthill check` on a broken file, and `anthill-todo` on a big tracker.\n\nWORK. Resolve positions through a per-source LINE INDEX built once: line starts in a `Vec<u32>`, binary search for the line, walk ONE line for the column (columns count CHARACTERS, not bytes — that is why the column cannot be a subtraction). Give both error families a BATCH entry point (the printers always hold a whole `Vec`), and route every printer through it. `Span::line_col` stays as the one-shot wrapper so single-lookup callers are unaffected.\n\nTHE CORRECTNESS BAR IS EQUIVALENCE, not plausibility: this changes no output, so keep the old scan verbatim as an ORACLE in a test and assert the index agrees at EVERY offset of a set of sources — including the offsets no caller means to produce but a recovery span can: past the end, inside a multi-byte character, on a `\\r\\n` file, in a source that is only newlines.\n\nWATCH FOR: the ~20 load arms must keep their exact message strings (they are pinned by tests, and the rewrite is mechanical); `LoadError::Located` renders from its OWN source and must ignore a passed index; a batch renderer keyed on the source's identity must not confuse two files.\n\nACCEPTANCE: the two measured cases above drop from tens of seconds to ~1-2 s (state the measured before/after); an equivalence test pins the index against the scan it replaces at every offset; no rendered diagnostic changes; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh.\n\nSOURCE: recorded as out-of-scope in WI-852's delivery note (a /code-review finding measured it at ~1.3 s debug for 688 errors on 3.1 MB), then measured properly here at 50 s for the real end-to-end run.\n\nSCOPE: rustland/anthill-core/src/{span.rs, parse/error.rs, kb/load.rs}, plus the printers in anthill-cli, anthill-stl, anthill-todo, anthill-rust-gen."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-29T06:39:46Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-854", author: "claude", content: "/code-review OUTCOME — 14 findings; 11 fixed, 3 recorded. Full workspace 3603 passed / 0 failed. Two of the fixes were things I had asserted in writing and had NOT actually done.\n\nTHE ONE THAT MATTERED MOST — MY OWN PATCH SILENTLY NO-OPPED. `render_all`'s doc still claimed the non-`Located` arm sees only errors with \"no file, no span to resolve\". A previous reviewer had already flagged that as false and I had \"fixed\" it — but the fix was a `str.replace` in a patch script with NO assert on the match count, so it matched nothing and the stale text shipped. The behaviour is fine (a typer error whose provenance lookup came back empty is never stamped, carries a span, and renders its raw byte range — the same as the loop it replaced, and unfixable there since there is no text to resolve against); the DOC was a lie for two commits. Every replacement in this round asserts its count. The test now feeds a span-bearing non-`Located` error through that arm so the claim is checked, not just written.\n\nTHE COMMIT MESSAGE'S CENTRAL CLAIM WAS FALSE, and is now true. It said deleting `Span::line_col` / `Span::format_start` meant \"the quadratic cannot be reintroduced by accident\". It could not: `format_with_source` was still `pub` on BOTH families and built a fresh whole-source index per call, so `for e in &errs { e.format_with_source(&src) }` compiled and was exactly the 50-second shape. Worse, I had deleted `ParseError::format_located` for having no production caller while leaving `format_with_source`, which also had none — the same rule applied inconsistently. Both one-shots are now DELETED and `format_at(&LineIndex)` is the public form; the six test callers construct the index explicitly, which is the point (the cost is visible). `LoadError`'s `Display` still resolves per call and cannot be deleted (`impl Error` needs it), so the honest statement is the narrower one now in the docs.\n\n\"NEVER SLOWER THAN THE SCAN IT REPLACED\" WAS ALSO FALSE. The old scan stopped AT the offset; `LineIndex::new` reads to EOF, so ONE lookup at byte 100 of a 3 MB file went from ~100 steps to a full pass. The trade is still right — every caller in the tree renders a batch — but the ticket had specified keeping a one-shot \"so single-lookup callers are unaffected\", and they are affected. Both the deviation and its reason are now written where the claim used to be.\n\nTHE GENERATED BUNDLE BROKE, AND THE WORKSPACE STAYED GREEN — the exact gap the reviewer flagged for the second time. Switching `all_located` / `render_all` to return ITERATORS (restoring the streaming every per-error printer had, instead of materializing 2100 strings before the user sees a line) left the template calling `.join` on an iterator. `cargo build --workspace --all-targets` passed; `emitted_bundle_compiles` — the `#[ignore]`d nested `cargo check` — failed, and I only ran it because I had just added a NOTE at the template telling the next editor to. Fixed by collecting at those two sites. The note stays; un-ignoring the test is worth considering, since it has now caught a real break.\n\nALSO FIXED: a `debug_assert` on source length (a >= 4 GiB source would truncate `as u32` and leave `starts` unsorted — silently wrong for EVERY offset, not just far ones); the `Vec::with_capacity(len/32+1)` heuristic dropped (measured 24x too large on this repo's own 3.1 MB tracker at 767 B/line, and too small for ordinary sources — it mis-served both directions); the redundant `as *const u8` cast replaced with a comment naming WHICH `as_ptr` resolves; the oracle sweep extended to `u32::MAX` (the delivery note claimed those offsets were verified — a review agent's fuzz had covered them, the committed test had not); `render_all`'s test given a genuine cache MISS (a second `Arc` holding file a's text byte-for-byte, asserted to be a distinct allocation) so the address-keying's miss branch is exercised; statement terminators made consistent across the three parallel parse-error arms.\n\nRECORDED, NOT FIXED:\n  - `located_errors` builds the user-text index even when every error is prefix-local (`-i 'bad flag'` with a large query file). One wasted scan on a path that has already parsed the file; a lazy `OnceCell` would close it.\n  - `anthill-stl::stdlib::parse_embedded` and `anthill-todo::anthill_bundle::parse_embedded_bundle` are now byte-identical apart from a `\"stdlib \"` / `\"bundle \"` tag; this change made two of WI-852's \"seven near-identical loops\" converge exactly, so a shared `parse_embedded_table(sources, tag)` is now a mechanical extraction.\n  - The single-line-source case (column walk is O(column), so one line can be the whole file) stays as documented on `LineIndex`.", at: "2026-07-29T07:47:58Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-846", description: some(value: "SILENT SKIP (cpp-gen, introduced by WI-833 0f979eac): a ZERO-result cpp NamingConvention read now returns identity-cased method names with NO diagnostic, so an unloaded or misconfigured realization profile emits non-compilable C++ against the vendor header and cpp-gen says nothing. CODE-READ at HEAD 2cdfde3e (rustland/anthill-cpp-gen/src/lib.rs, cpp_method_name): after unique_or_ambiguous, `let Some((source_case, method_case)) = convention else { return Ok(source.to_string()) }` — zero conventions falls straight through to identity. Every carrier dispatch then renders `self->get_gps()` where the vendor header declares `getGps()`. VERIFIED REGRESSION, not a pre-existing gap: the parent commit (0f979eac~1:lib.rs:4357-4366) guarded BOTH ends with `debug_assert!(facts.len() == 1, \"expected exactly one cpp NamingConvention fact, found {}\", facts.len())`, whose own comment read \"Zero (misconfigured or unloaded profile) or >1 ... is a profile bug: fail loudly in dev/test per the repo's 'loud error over silent skip'\". WI-833 re-established only the >1 half (as a hard CppCodegenError via unique_or_ambiguous) and replaced the zero half with a NEW and CONTRADICTORY rationale in the comment — \"an absent one is an ordinary miss, not an error (a project may emit without loading a naming profile)\". Those two comments cannot both be right, and the ticket must DECIDE which, not split the difference: this is a policy question, so if 'a project may legitimately emit without a naming profile' is the intended rule, then the requirement is a DIAGNOSTIC (a warning naming the absent profile), not silence — the repo rule is 'prefer a loud error over a silent skip ... Silent skips hide bugs and read as handled when they arent' (CLAUDE.md, Development principles). NOT YET DRIVEN, and the reason is itself part of the work: the cpp-gen test harness (anthill-cpp-gen/tests/common/mod.rs, load_kb_with_extras) ALWAYS loads STDLIB_PARSED + CPP_BINDINGS_PARSED, and the stdlib always ships exactly one cpp NamingConvention (snake_case -> camelCase), so NO existing test can reach the zero branch at all — which is why the branch's behaviour change went unnoticed. ACCEPTANCE: (1) a driven repro that reaches the zero branch — a KB loaded WITHOUT anthill.realization.cpp_std — showing what is emitted today, so the decision is made against a measurement rather than a reading; (2) the decided policy implemented — loud error, or a diagnostic that names the absent profile — never silence; (3) a test that reaches the zero branch, which requires the harness to gain a no-realization-profile load path (state it in the test if the harness change is the bulk of the work); (4) full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. SOURCE: /code-review of the WI-833 merge, 2026-07-28. SCOPE: rustland/anthill-cpp-gen."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-29T08:20:59Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-853", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED by a THIRD option the ticket did not list, and it is the one that makes the ticket's (a) cheap: FIX THE GRAMMAR. `import_clause` is now an alternative of `_declaration` (grammar.js), so a file's TOP LEVEL admits an import the same way a namespace / sort body does.\n\nWHY THE GRAMMAR AND NOT THE CLI. A file's top level IS a scope — `_declaration` already admits `sort` / `fact` / `rule` / `operation` outside any namespace and the loader defines every one of them in `_global` — and `import` is how names enter a scope. Admitting the declarations while refusing the import that feeds them was an asymmetry with no rule behind it; anthill-todo's own `workitems.anthill` is a top-level-fact file that could not have imported anything. The `-i` flag is then not a special case at all: it is one ordinary top-level import, and `_global` is exactly the scope `convert_query_term` resolves the pattern in.\n\n(b) REJECTED, with the reason the ticket asked for. Wrapping the synthesized source in a namespace parses, but the pattern is converted at `_global` (`collect_queries` passes `global_raw` explicitly), so an import into `ns` would not reach it — (b) would additionally have to move the conversion scope, and then a query file's rule heads and fact functors would be DEFINED under a synthetic namespace whose name can collide with a real one. It changes name resolution to buy nothing.\n\n(a) DELIVERED on top of it: nothing is concatenated any more. Each `-i` flag is parsed as its OWN source (`import <flag>\\n`) and scanned into `_global`; the pattern / query file is parsed ALONE. Both fallouts follow:\n  1. `.with_path(query_file)` is now correct unconditionally — `parsed.source` IS the file — so a LOAD error from a query file renders `path:line:col`. MEASURED: a query file whose own top-level `import nosuch.ns` sits on line 3 reports `<path>:3:8: unresolved import 'nosuch.ns'`, identically with `-i` and without. Test `a_query_file_load_error_names_the_file` drives both.\n  2. WI-852's `QuerySource` is DELETED: prefix_len, the overlap split, the span shift and `imports_covered`'s flag-range naming. One source per flag makes the range unnecessary — a span covers exactly one flag — so `--import (one of `a`, `b`)` collapses to `--import `b``. `a_malformed_import_flag_names_which_flag` (wi852) is DELETED with that reason recorded at its site; its claim (the good flag is never blamed) is asserted more strictly by `a_malformed_flag_names_only_itself` (wi853). The other 8 WI-852 tests stay GREEN, two with rewritten rationales: `prepended_import_lines_do_not_shift_the_file_location` and `a_span_merged_across_the_prefix_is_located_in_the_file` now hold BY CONSTRUCTION (a flag and the file are different sources, so no recovery node can span both) — kept, because \"cannot happen by construction\" is a claim about the current construction.\n\nMEASURED, before touching the CLI: the grammar change ALONE made `anthill query --path kb.anthill -i wi853.demo \"mk(x: ?v)\"` return `?v = 1` where it had exited 1. The CLI rewrite is what removes the shift machinery and locates the file, not what makes the flag work.\n\nSCOPE SEMANTICS, measured and pinned because it is not the file-local import other languages have: a top-level import enters `_global`, so it is visible from every scope that chains to `_global` — including a namespace IN ANOTHER FILE of the same load (`a_top_level_import_enters_the_shared_global_scope` drives exactly that, with a control). That is the same rule top-level DEFINITIONS already follow. The language has no per-file scope to attach a narrower one to; `_global` is the top. Documented in kernel-language.md §5.1 plus a `File ::= NamespaceContent*` production in the full grammar.\n\nTWO SITES THE TICKET DID NOT NAME, both found by asking who reads a scope's imports:\n  - `codegen/rust.rs`: `generate_rust_with_config` walked `parsed.items` only, so a file's top-level imports would have emitted NO `use` lines while a namespace's did — silently generating a module whose names do not resolve. Now emitted at the same altitude as `emit_namespace`'s.\n  - `parse/convert.rs`: the import collection is now ONE helper (`collect_imports`) shared by the three things that own a scope (file, namespace, sort), rather than a third copy of the same four lines.\n\nALSO REFUSED (WI-767's rule, applied to `-i`): a listing mode (`--mode sort` / `functor` / `domain`) looks its argument up by qualified name, never through the query scope, so `-i` was INERT there and silently accepted. A flag that silently does nothing is this ticket's whole defect, so it now blocks with a message. Test: `an_import_flag_is_refused_where_it_cannot_apply`, with a control that the listing still runs without the flag.\n\nRECORDED, NOT FIXED. A LOAD error from a `-i` flag renders `error: --import `nosuch.ns`: 1:8: unresolved import 'nosuch.ns'` — the `1:8` is a position in the one-line source the CLI synthesized for that flag. It is not false and it cannot be misread as the query file (the flag prefix names the origin), but it is noise: the rule the PARSE half follows is \"one visible argument, so the flag IS the location, with no line:col\". Dropping it needs a message-WITHOUT-position rendering for `LoadError`, i.e. the same ~20-arm restructure WI-852's finding 3 recorded, so it is not done here.\n\nDIVERGENCE: scaland's `sourceFile` is still `declaration.rep` with no imports channel on its parse result, so the Scala port will refuse a top-level import. Not ported — scaland's newest referenced ticket is WI-671, ~180 behind, so a single-ticket sync there would be unrepresentative.\n\nVERIFICATION: tree-sitter corpus 184/184 (new `Top-level import (WI-853)` case covers all three forms plus a following declaration); full workspace via rustland/scripts/test.sh 3613 passed / 0 failed. New tests: `anthill-cli/tests/wi853_query_import_test.rs` (7, each with a control that fails without the flag) and `anthill-core/tests/include/wi853_top_level_import_test.rs` (4, including the not-an-`Item` pin, since an import smuggled in as an `Item` variant would be dropped by every items walk).", at: "2026-07-29T08:24:02Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-853", description: some(value: "`anthill query -i/--import <name>` is NON-FUNCTIONAL by construction — the prefix it synthesizes is a top-level `import`, which the grammar does not admit, so EVERY use of the flag fails the parse.\n\nWHAT I MEASURED (2026-07-28, HEAD ed990f3f + WI-852). Driving the built CLI on a KB that loads clean, with a VALID pattern and a VALID import:\n  $ anthill query --path good.anthill -i wi852.good \"mk(x: 1)\"\n  error: --import: syntax error near `import wi852.good`\n  error: --import: unexpected top-level node: ERROR\n  (exit 1)\nSame for `--query-file` with any `-i`. Without `-i` both work. The flag has never worked: before WI-852 the identical run printed `parse error: parse error at 0..11: unexpected top-level node: ERROR`, which named neither the file nor the flag and read as a fault in the user's pattern — which is why it went unnoticed.\n\nROOT CAUSE, in the grammar, not the CLI. `collect_queries` (anthill-cli/src/main.rs) builds the source it parses as `import {imp}\\n` per `-i` flag, then the user's text. But `import_clause` appears only in `_namespace_content` and `_sort_content` (tree-sitter-anthill/grammar.js:209, :237) — `_declaration`, the top level reached from `source_file` (grammar.js:62-93), has NO import alternative. So a bare top-level `import` is a syntax error BY DESIGN, and the synthesized prefix can never parse. Confirmed directly: a file whose first line is `import demo` fails `anthill check` with `1:1: syntax error near `import demo``.\n\nWORK. Decide which of the two is right and say why:\n  (a) SUPPLY THE IMPORTS STRUCTURALLY. Do not concatenate text — put the import names into the ParsedFile / scan_definitions the way a namespace body's imports arrive, so the parsed source is EXACTLY the user's text.\n  (b) WRAP THE SYNTHESIZED SOURCE IN A NAMESPACE, so the imports sit where the grammar admits them. Cheaper, but it changes the scope the query pattern is resolved in — state what that does to name resolution before choosing it.\nRefusing `-i` loudly is NOT an acceptable outcome on its own: the flag is documented in `--help` (\"Import names into query scope\") and the capability is wanted; if it must be disabled temporarily, say so explicitly and file the enabling work.\n\nTWO THINGS FALL OUT, both currently blocked on this:\n  1. WI-852 left the two LOAD printers in `collect_queries` (the `scan_definitions` error loops) UNLOCATED — they print `error: {e}` with no path. The fix is `.with_path(query_file)` on the synthesized ParsedFile, but that is WRONG while the source is a concatenation: `parsed.source` is prefix + file, so with any `-i` the reported line is shifted and would then be FALSELY attributed to the file. Under (a) the source IS the file and the stamp becomes correct unconditionally.\n  2. WI-852's span-shift machinery in `located_query_parse_error` (the `--import` / shift split) exists solely because the prefix can carry faults and displace the file's spans. Under (a) `prefix_len` is always 0 and the whole helper collapses to a plain `format_located(query_file, &file_source)` — delete it then, do not leave it as dead structure.\n\nACCEPTANCE: `anthill query --path <kb> -i <namespace> \"<pattern>\"` resolves the pattern with the imported names in scope and exits 0; a test in anthill-cli drives the flag END TO END (a query that only resolves BECAUSE of the import, not merely a non-zero solution count); a `--query-file` parse error still reports the file's own line:col (the WI-852 test `prepended_import_lines_do_not_shift_the_file_location` must stay green, or be deleted with its reason if the shift mechanism goes); full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh.\n\nSOURCE: found 2026-07-28 while delivering WI-852, driving `-i` to check that the new `--import` attribution branch reported the right origin. It did — and the run it reported on could not have succeeded.\n\nSCOPE: rustland/anthill-cli/src/main.rs (collect_queries), tree-sitter-anthill/grammar.js if (b), rustland/anthill-core/src/kb/load.rs (scan_definitions entry) if (a)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-29T08:24:08Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-838", description: some(value: "COHERENCE HOLE (pre-existing, measured 2026-07-28 at HEAD dcdaf0cd): a MIXED provider pair — one WI-431 instance fact AND one WI-450 witness sort for the SAME (spec, carrier) — loads clean, because the two ambiguity checks are each grouped by provider KIND and neither sees the cross-kind pair. REPRO (`anthill load`, loads with no diagnostic): `sort Combiner { sort T = ?  operation combine(x: T, y: T) -> T }`, `sort Tag { entity tag(n: Int64) }`, `operation tagCombine(x: Tag, y: Tag) -> Tag = tag(n: 99)`, `fact Combiner[T = Tag, combine = tagCombine]`, PLUS `sort WitCombiner { fact Combiner[T = Tag]  operation combine(x: Tag, y: Tag) -> Tag = tag(n: 5) }`. CONTROL (so the checks do fire on same-kind pairs): replacing the instance fact with a SECOND witness sort gives `ambiguous witness: 2 distinct witness sorts provide 'Combiner' for carrier 'Tag' (keep exactly one)`; two differing instance facts give `AmbiguousInstanceFact` (load.rs:283). So two distinct dictionaries for one (spec, carrier) coexist and dispatch silently picks one — the exact condition both checks exist to prevent. WHICH ONE WINS is UNMEASURED (the resolver leaves `eq(?r, combine(tag(n: 1), tag(n: 2)))` residual and `anthill query` does not reduce the op body); the eval-side chain is `own .or_else(instance_fact_op_binding) .or_else(witness_op_for_carrier)` (eval/eval.rs:934), which predicts the INSTANCE FACT wins there, while the typer-side witness path reads `witness_provision` (kb/typing.rs:16116) — so the two sides may not even agree. Measure both before fixing. GROUPINGS TO FIX: `AmbiguousInstanceFact` keys the full canonical application (load.rs:283); the witness check groups `(canonical spec, canonical carrier)` and explicitly SKIPS a provision whose provider IS the carrier (kb/typing.rs:~15980) — a namespace-level instance fact is not a witness by that test, so it is invisible to the witness grouping. The fix is one group keyed on (spec, carrier) over ALL provider kinds, with the existing exemptions kept (a spec that declares no ops; a CONCRETE provider with constructors, where value-directed dispatch answers — kb/typing.rs:15977). RELATED: docs/proposals/058-modular-instances.md §4.3 records this as the reason tier-3 coexistence must be gated on every candidate being NAMEABLE (an instance fact has no name, so a mixed pair could not be disambiguated at a use site even after WI-648 lands). ACCEPTANCE: the repro above fails to load with a diagnostic naming BOTH providers and their kinds; the two same-kind controls keep their current diagnostics; the concrete-provider and no-ops exemptions still load; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-29T09:13:04Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-841", author: "user", content: "PHASE 1 (WI-840) DELIVERED 2026-07-29 — two measurements phase 2 must not re-derive. (1) THE SLOT POSITION'S REFERENT IS NOT THE FACT ORDER. `kb.named_requirement_slots(owner)` gives (binder, position); the position indexes SOURCE order, which `SortInfo.requires` preserves and the `SortRequiresInfo` FACT order does NOT — `resolve_requires_bindings` (load.rs) RETRACTS and re-asserts every requirement whose op bindings it completes, moving it to the end of the functor index. Measured: a sort declaring `requires O: Ord[T = E]` then a parameterless `requires Marker` comes back `Marker, Ord` as facts while `SortInfo.requires` stays `Ord, Marker`, so a reader that took `direct_requires`' enumeration (facts) for the referent resolves `O` to the WRONG requirement. Pinned by `the_sort_level_slot_position_indexes_sort_info_requires`, which also asserts the divergence so the warning cannot go stale. At OP level the referent is the flattened GOAL order (`op_requires_entries`, value preconditions included), NOT `OperationInfo.requires`, which holds CLAUSES — `convert_clause_list_with_extra` collapses a multi-goal clause into one `conjunction`, so `requires plus: Monoid[T], times: Monoid[T]` indexes a list of length 1 there. IF the mapping turns out load-bearing rather than incidental, the coordinate-free fix is to put the binder ON the requirement (a third `SortRequiresInfo` field, which the retract/re-assert carries) — recorded in the `named_requirement_slots` doc. (2) THE BINDER IS AN ORDINARY TYPE PARAMETER, so rule (1) already reaches it: measured, `biFold[lo = ByValue](n)` loads on `operation biFold(n) requires lo: Ord[T = Int64]` while `biFold[Bogus = …]` is loud. What phase 2 still owes is the SORT-LEVEL seeding rung (§2.2 rows 5-6) and the key->slot resolution; the op-level binder needs no new seeding. (3) THE §4.2 GUARD IS ALREADY IN, at `check_op_type_param_shadowing` (load.rs), covering all four sources incl. the two-scope collision phase 2's ticket lists — so phase 2 inherits it rather than adding it. NOTE a fourth case the ticket does not list, found by driving: written BARE, `requires Ord[…]` beside a `[Ord]` param resolves `Ord` to the PARAMETER (the capture already happened), so there is no spec left to be ambiguous with; it has its own diagnosis.", at: "2026-07-29T10:39:12Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-840", description: some(value: "Proposal 058 (WI-648) PHASE 1 — DECLARE: grammar + loader for the NAMED requirement slot `requires name: Spec[…]`, on an OPERATION and on a SORT (§4.7), plus the §4.2 shadowing guards. Today `requires d: Desc[E]` does not parse (measured: `syntax error near ': Desc'`): the two existing productions carry no name slot — `requires_declaration: 'requires' <type>` (sort/namespace level, a bare TYPE) and `requires_clause: 'requires' <rule_body>` (op-scoped, WI-448) — and design/operation-call-model.md states the current property outright, sub-requirements are \"positional and nameless (impl-side `requires` clauses have no source-level names)\". WHY IT IS NOT OPTIONAL PLUMBING: a NAMED slot IS a type parameter, which is what lets a witness enter a TYPE (`SortedSet[T = String, O = ByLength]` vs `… O = Alphabetical]` are then different types by the ordinary checker); without a name the SortedSet merge hazard cannot be STATED at all. The SELECT surface needs no grammar change (§4.2 reuses 042's bracket); this DECLARE binder is the only new grammar in the proposal. THREE WRINKLES, all found by reading the productions rather than assumed (§4.7): (1) the op-scoped clause is OVERLOADED — one `rule_body` list holds both spec requirements (`operation member(x: T, l: List) -> Bool requires Eq[T]`, stdlib/anthill/prelude/list.anthill:58) and VALUE preconditions (`requires neq(b, 0), gt(b, 0)`, WI-539); a binder attaches only to the TYPE flavor and must coexist with predicates in the same comma list (the implementation already draws that line — `is_value_precondition_clause`); the sort-level form takes a type only, so it is clean there; (2) `d: Desc[E]` sits next to named-argument syntax inside a rule_body (`f(x: 1)`) — a bare `name: Type` is not valid at rule-body top level today so the extension is available, but `requires_clause` already needs `prec.dynamic(1)` to win a GLR tie against `requires_declaration`, so this wants a tree-sitter CORPUS TEST, not an assumption; (3) the binder names the TOP-LEVEL slot only — sub-nodes inside a ResolvedSortNode stay positional (`requirement_at_sort(node, k)`); phase 1 adds a name where one was missing, it does NOT re-key the projection path. GUARDS (§4.2 — refused AT THE DECLARATION, not at the call): an operation may not declare a type parameter whose name collides with (a) one of its own requirement SPEC SHORT NAMES, and for a spec's own operations with the SPEC's own short name (which would shadow the direct-dispatch key `Desc.describe[Desc = …]`); (b) one of its ENCLOSING SORT's type parameters, including a sort-level named slot — phase 2 makes bracket rule (1) span both scopes, and an ordered ladder there would be a silent capture the reader cannot see. Binder-vs-parameter collision needs no separate case: a named slot IS a parameter, so the ordinary duplicate-parameter rule covers it. ACCEPTANCE (§9 phase 1, verbatim): a two-slot op loads; a named sort-level slot is addressable in type position (`Box[E = …, O = …]`); a colliding type-param name is refused AT THE DECLARATION. PLUS: a tree-sitter corpus test pins the GLR tie (binder form vs `requires <type>` vs a value precondition in one clause list). SCOPE: tree-sitter-anthill grammar + Rust loader; scaland mirrors. Full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. REFERENCE: docs/proposals/058-modular-instances.md §4.7, §4.2, §9 phase 1."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-839"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-29T10:39:19Z"))
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-855", name: "modinst")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-837", description: some(value: "A WI-450 witness-supplied `eq` never keys the semantic-equality dispatch index, so a carrier whose PartialEq is provided by a WITNESS SORT silently answers STRUCTURAL equality. The index build (rustland/anthill-core/src/kb/load.rs:4805-4815, WI-616 pass 3) takes `carrier_own_op(kb, sort, PartialEq.eq, eq).or_else(|| instance_fact_eq_target(...))` — the carrier's own member, else a CARRIER-KEYED instance fact. A provision whose `sort_ref` is the WITNESS (`sort MyEq { fact PartialEq[T = Pebble]  operation eq(a: Pebble, b: Pebble) -> Bool = … }`) is keyed on the witness, not the carrier, so it is invisible to that walk. The asymmetry is already known and bridged on the OTHER dispatch path — `witness_provision` / `witness_op_for_carrier` (kb/typing.rs:16116, :16164) exist precisely for `sort_ref != carrier` — but semantic eq/neq does NOT go through them: resolver and interpreter both probe `sem_eq_dispatch_target` only (resolve/*.rs eq/neq builtin; eval/builtins.rs:549), which reads the index. TODAY this is a silent DEGRADATION (the witness's `eq` is ignored and structural equality answers). It becomes a SOUNDNESS GATE for docs/proposals/058-modular-instances.md phase 3b (WI-648): 3b deletes the load-time `AmbiguousWitness` refusal (kb/typing.rs:~15995, which is spec-generic and is currently the ONLY thing rejecting two witness-sort PartialEq providers for one carrier), and 058 §4.9's hardening refuses a second candidate AT THE INDEX BUILD — but no witness ever reaches the index, so two Eq witnesses would coexist with no diagnostic from either check and equality would silently go structural. 058 §6's claim that the Eq family keeps load-time coherence 'by mechanism, not accident' is FALSE for the witness form until this is closed; 058 §4.9 currently mis-files this as an independent candidate ticket rather than a phase-3a prerequisite. SIBLING BLIND SPOTS from the same audit (fix together or file separately): a first SortProvidesInfo fact that binds no `eq` hides a second that does (provider_spec_view_bindings early-returns at kb/typing.rs:18229), silently degrading to structural eq; the eval bridge's `BridgeRequirements::Unresolvable` outcome is trace-only (eval/eval.rs:1785). ACCEPTANCE: a carrier whose ONLY PartialEq provider is a witness sort dispatches `eq` to the witness's member — test with a witness `eq` whose answer is deliberately NON-structural, observed by BOTH the resolver and the interpreter; the index build refuses a second candidate for one carrier at LOAD, naming both providers; the existing carrier-own-op and instance-fact paths are unchanged (their tests stay green); full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-29T11:35:10Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-855", description: some(value: "The eval bridge silently DROPS an AMBIGUOUS requirement-dictionary verdict: `resolve_bridge_requirements` (rustland/anthill-core/src/kb/typing.rs, `BridgeRequirements`) folds \"no provider\", \"AMBIGUOUS provider\", \"cyclic provider\" and \"under-determined carrier\" into ONE `Unresolvable { detail }` bucket, and its eval consumer (rustland/anthill-core/src/eval/eval.rs:1785) converts that into a silently UNSUPPLIED frame — trace-only, printed just under `ANTHILL_TRACE_REQ`. So when two providers genuinely tie for one requirement, the ONE place that DETECTED the tie throws the detection away and the call proceeds with no dictionary; the eventual failure (an unbound requirement further in, or a wrong answer) names neither the tie nor the candidates. Recorded by docs/proposals/058-modular-instances.md §4.9's consumer table as a 'genuinely independent blind spot / candidate ticket', beside the first-fact-wins one WI-837 retired for the sem-eq index. NOT re-litigating WI-822: that ticket MEASURED that raising loudly at dispatch for EVERY `Unresolvable` breaks 29 tests, and deliberately chose trace + loud-at-the-read; the un-relitigated question is the AMBIGUOUS sub-case specifically, which is a coherence violation rather than an unpinnable-at-these-types situation and has no legitimate 'proceed unsupplied' reading. ACCEPTANCE: `BridgeRequirements` distinguishes an AMBIGUOUS verdict from the other unresolvable causes (a distinct variant or a tagged cause), carrying the tied candidates; the eval consumer surfaces it as a named error/diagnostic naming the requirement and both candidates rather than entering unsupplied; the other unresolvable causes keep WI-822's measured trace-and-continue behaviour (its 29 tests stay green); a test drives a genuine two-provider tie through value-directed dispatch and asserts the diagnostic identity; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-29T12:48:33Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-855", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED with two corrections to the ticket's own framing, both measured. (1) The ticket says the eventual failure 'names neither the tie nor the candidates' — MEASURED, it was Internal(DeferToRequirement: __req_desc not bound, running WrapDesc.describe): the failure was LOUD, just attributed to a MISSING dictionary rather than to the tie. The defect is mis-attribution, not silence. (2) OPEN, recorded not filed: the reason a tie reaches the runtime at all is that WI-838's per-(spec,carrier) coherence grouping admits a candidate only as an instance FACT (binds an op) or as a WITNESS (provider != carrier), so an ordinary SELF-PROVIDER (sort Leaf { fact Desc[T=Leaf]; operation describe }) is a candidate of NEITHER kind and can never be one half of a refused pair. Adding a self-provider candidate kind would move this class to load time; that is a design increment on 058's grouping, recorded in 058 section 4.9. My first explanation blamed the concrete-provider exemption and was REFUTED by driving an abstract-rival twin (still loads clean) — the test pins both spellings. Also NOT split: ResolutionResult::Cyclic is a program-level defect too and stays in the Unresolvable bucket, unmeasured; splitting it is the same edit one variant over.", at: "2026-07-29T12:48:50Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-856", description: some(value: "A NAMESPACE-LEVEL entity carrier never keys the semantic-equality dispatch index, so its `eq` supplier is silently ignored and equality answers STRUCTURALLY. The index build (`build_eq_dispatch_index`, rustland/anthill-core/src/kb/load.rs) derives its carrier list from `sorts_and_own_ops`, i.e. from `anthill.reflect.SortInfo` facts — and `emit_sort_info` has ONE caller, `load_sort_with_body`, so ONLY a `sort … end` block emits one. A free-standing `entity binding(n: Int64, note: Int64)` written at namespace level (as `stdlib/anthill/cli/parse.anthill` and `examples/github-todo/domain.anthill` do) is its own carrier with no `SortInfo`, so the walk never reaches it. MEASURED with a control pair (rustland/anthill-core/tests/include/wi837_witness_eq_dispatch_test.rs, `a_sort_wrapped_entity_carrier_keys_the_index` / `a_namespace_level_entity_carrier_does_not_key_the_index`): one otherwise-identical program with `fact PartialEq[T = C, eq = cEq]` where `cEq` answers true for ANY pair gives 1 solution when C is a `sort`-wrapped entity and 0 when C is a namespace-level entity. PRE-EXISTING and route-independent: the same walk has always fed the index, so the WI-625 gap-2 instance-fact route, the WI-616 own-member route, and WI-837's witness route all degrade identically for such a carrier. Two consequences worth naming: (a) the WI-664 lawful-Eq boundary classifier (`eq_derive::is_eq_boundary`) enumerates a DIFFERENT universe (`composite_sorts`, off the entity-field-schema registry), which DOES include the namespace-level entity — so after WI-837 the two share a PREDICATE but not a DOMAIN, and a namespace-level entity can be a lawful-Eq boundary whose `eq` never dispatches; (b) it is the safe direction (structural eq, no derived NonEq) but it is silent, which is what this repo refuses. ACCEPTANCE: the index build enumerates namespace-level entity carriers too — reusing whatever `eq_derive::composite_sorts` uses rather than a second hand-rolled walk, so the domain gets ONE owner like the predicate now has; the measured control pair above flips to 1/1 and its 'PINS A KNOWN GAP' premise is deleted; the WI-837 ambiguity refusal (`AmbiguousEqDispatch`) also fires for such a carrier — assert the diagnostic identity; blast radius checked, since every free-standing entity with an eq supplier becomes newly index-keyed (stdlib, examples/, anthill-todo/ all load clean and full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-29T13:35:05Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-856", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED with the ticket's own premise REFUTED by driving, plus two findings recorded not filed. (1) The ticket says the WI-616 own-member, WI-625 instance-fact and WI-837 witness routes 'all degrade identically for such a carrier' and are all restored by widening the index DOMAIN. MEASURED: after the domain fix the witness arm still gave 0 solutions. The witness route was broken a SECOND time, one owner earlier — provision_carrier_sort (kb/typing.rs) required SymbolKind::Sort, so a free-standing entity named as T = binding answered None and the provision was filed as a SELF-provider keyed on the witness sort; the widened domain then correctly enumerated the carrier and found nothing there. Two owners had to move. A probe of only the instance-fact route would have 'proved' the ticket complete. The own-member route is not a route at all for such a carrier: a namespace-level entity has no body in which to declare one. (2) The predicate was hand-rolled before review: KnowledgeBase::is_free_standing_entity already owns it, and its pairing with SymbolKind::Sort is already the idiom in check_bare_ref for 'a bare name denotes a TYPE'. Using it is not just reuse — it bottoms out in the SAME entity-field-type registry the widened domain walks, so the accepted carrier set equals the enumerable one by construction, and it fails SAFE on a divergent symbol copy where the kind-table spelling would admit a VARIANT as a carrier. (3) Blast radius MEASURED EMPTY on real code: no provision in stdlib/examples/anthill-todo binds a carrier param to an entity, so nothing became newly index-keyed there; the tests are the only coverage of the widening, hence the negative control a_variant_named_as_a_carrier_is_not_a_witness_carrier. Interleaved timing (7 runs each, old vs new binary) shows no load regression. OPEN, recorded not filed: (a) sorts_with_constructors feeding check_provider_operations's 'concrete' gate has the SAME SortInfo-only blind spot — MEASURED, a namespace-level entity carrier with an unbacked provided op loads CLEAN while its sort-wrapped twin errors; it is the one remaining SortInfo reader where 'missing key' is not equivalent to 'empty answer' (every other sorts_and_own_ops caller asks which ops a sort DECLARES, and an entity declares none). (b) KnowledgeBase::constructors_of_sort scans all of entity_parent although sort_children is the ready-made inverse index; via composite_field_sorts that makes eq_derive::run O(#composite sorts x #entities) — MEASURED 3 ms to 26-41 ms on a 4000-entity corpus. Pre-existing, one-line fix. NOT run: /code-review (user-triggered); /simplify was run and produced the is_free_standing_entity swap, the assert_loads_clean helper and the doc consolidation.", at: "2026-07-29T13:35:24Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-658", author: "user", content: "POINTER REPAIR (WI-835, 2026-07-29): this ticket's last acceptance bullet and its REF list name 'sibling WI-649 (use-site requires Eq[K] => Map[K=Float] load error)' -- that number is WRONG. WI-649 is the reify/reify_value cyclic-sigma occurs-check ticket (Delivered); the number was REPURPOSED -- the same failure mode this ticket was filed to fix on the provision-site half, which had been mis-attributed to the repurposed WI-650. The use-site half is WI-835 (Delivered 2026-07-29): every written parameterized type instantiation is recorded at the sole type lowering and refused post-eq_derive when a requires Eq[param] binding names a NonEq carrier. Read every 'WI-649' in this ticket as WI-835. The complementarity the bullet asserts is real and holds: WI-658 refuses an unlawful PROVISION (Eq exclusive-or NonEq at the carrier), WI-835 refuses an unlawful USE (a NonEq carrier bound where a sort requires Eq).", at: "2026-07-29T13:55:24Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-835", description: some(value: "Enforce a parametric sort's `requires Eq[param]` at TYPE-INSTANTIATION sites: `Map[K = Float]` / `Set[T = Float]` must FAIL to load, while `Map[K = TotalFloat]` keeps loading. This is WI-644's ONE outstanding acceptance item (library proposal 004).\n\nWHY THIS EXISTS -- ORPHANED ENFORCEMENT (the second time on this same feature). WI-644's ACCEPTANCE requires \"Map[K=Float] is a load error, Map[K=TotalFloat] loads\". Its feedback (2026-07-08) CONFIRMED that does not hold, gave the mechanism, and deferred it to a focused session with \"Not a spinoff -- this is WI-644's own acceptance\"; WI-644 was then marked Delivered, so the item appears in NO open-work list. WI-658's description points at \"sibling WI-649 (use-site requires Eq[K] => Map[K=Float] load error)\" -- but WI-649 is the reify/reify_value cyclic-sigma occurs-check ticket (Delivered), i.e. that number was REPURPOSED. This is exactly the failure WI-658 was itself created to fix, when the provision-site half had been mis-attributed to the repurposed WI-650. Net: the use-site half has had no owner since 2026-07-08. WI-658's stale \"sibling WI-649\" pointer should be repointed HERE.\n\nEMPIRICAL GAP (verified 2026-07-28, `anthill load` with the embedded stdlib): a program declaring `operation m() -> Map[K = Float, V = Int64] = Map.empty()` loads CLEAN; the `Map[K = TotalFloat, V = Int64]` control ALSO loads clean. So the acceptance pair currently passes only VACUOUSLY -- nothing distinguishes the two spellings, and `TotalFloat` is a documentation convention rather than an enforced boundary. ROOT CAUSE per WI-644's feedback: WI-616 made `requires Eq` a vacuous universal-structural default, so the sort-level `requires Eq[T = K]` (stdlib/anthill/prelude/map.anthill:7) and `requires Eq[T]` (set.anthill:10) constrain nothing at instantiation.\n\nMECHANISM (from WI-644's own feedback; NO new language surface): at a TYPE-INSTANTIATION site, for each binding that a parametric sort's `requires Spec[param]` ranges over, check that the bound carrier PROVIDES the spec (`sort_provides`). `Float` provides PartialEq / PartialOrd / NonEq but NOT `Eq` (float.anthill), so `Map[K = Float]` is refused with a diagnostic naming the sort, the parameter, the carrier, the required spec, and the lawful alternative (`TotalFloat`). `TotalFloat` provides `Eq` (totalfloat.anthill:33-34), so it is accepted. This COMPLEMENTS WI-658 (delivered): provision-site law refutation there, use-site provision check here -- the two halves of library/004's lawful-key guarantee.\n\nRISK / REQUIRED AUDIT (why WI-644 deferred it rather than tacking it on): the check is vacuous today, so giving it teeth can REJECT programs that load now -- every `Map` / `Set` / dedup / sorted-key instantiation over a carrier that does not provide lawful `Eq`, including entity carriers that WI-664's composite derivation classifies `NonEq` (a plain entity with a `Float` field). AUDIT stdlib + rustland tests + examples/ + anthill-todo/ before landing, and expect to weaken some `requires Eq` to `requires PartialEq` where only comparison is used (WI-644's MIGRATION note).\n\nACCEPTANCE:\n - `Map[K = Float, V = _]` and `Set[T = Float]` FAIL to load, with a diagnostic naming the sort, the parameter, the carrier and the required spec.\n - `Map[K = TotalFloat]`, `Map[K = Int64]`, `Map[K = String]`, `Set[T = Int64]` still load.\n - A `Map` keyed by an entity carrier that WI-664 classifies `NonEq` (an entity with a `Float` field) FAILS to load; a lawful entity key still loads.\n - The refusal is a LOAD error, never deferred to eval (loud-over-silent).\n - Full workspace `cargo-test` green AFTER the audit.\n - WI-644's last acceptance item is then met, and library/004's Status can move off Draft (this is its only unshipped bullet -- the PartialEq/Eq/PartialOrd/Ord split, NonEq, and TotalFloat all shipped).\n\nREF: docs/proposals/library/004-partial-vs-total-equality-and-ordering.md; WI-644 (parent -- this IS its outstanding acceptance, and the parent now depends on this); WI-658 (provision-site sibling, delivered -- its \"sibling WI-649\" pointer is stale and means THIS ticket); WI-664 (composite Eq/NonEq derivation, delivered); WI-616 (made `requires Eq` vacuous); WI-649 (NOT this -- repurposed to the reify cyclic-sigma occurs-check; do not rely on that number); stdlib/anthill/prelude/{map,set,eq,float,totalfloat}.anthill; rustland/anthill-core/src/kb/typing.rs (type-instantiation checking); docs/proposals/058-modular-instances.md §6 (records this gap and the measurement). Rust + scaland (scaland mirrors only if it gains provides-checking)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-29T14:35:59Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-835", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED 2026-07-29 (commit c1c80110). The ticket's premise was half wrong and driving it corrected two things. (1) The check was NOT missing: check_use_site_requires_eq shipped with WI-644 and was already NEGATIVE. The ticket's prescribed MECHANISM (a positive sort_provides(carrier, Eq) test) was NOT built -- the existing doc argues in detail why positive is both vacuous (WI-616's universal structural default) and over-rejecting (WI-664 derives NonEq for partial composites but no Eq for lawful ones), and driving confirmed it. The defect was purely the check's SCOPE: entity FIELD types only, so the ticket's own acceptance program (an operation RETURN type) was invisible. (2) The RISK/AUDIT paragraph predicted widening 'can REJECT programs that load now ... expect to weaken some requires Eq to requires PartialEq'. MEASURED: zero fallout. stdlib, examples/, anthill-todo/, anthill-testcases/, cpp-gen and cli fixtures all load clean, and the full suite was green (87 binaries, 3678 passed) with no stdlib weakening at all. FIX SHAPE: the position list is gone -- type_expr_to_child's Parameterized arm (the sole type lowering, already the home of WI-709's syntactic arg-fit check) records every written instantiation; the check walks that after eq_derive::run. Nine positions measured covered and pinned individually, three beyond the acceptance: a sort alias, a body let annotation, a typed lambda binder, and nesting inside a tuple / arrow / another instantiation (Map[K = Set[T = Float]] now refuses on the INNER Set at its own span -- WI-644's doc had listed that as deliberate non-scope). My own draft doc claimed body annotations were out of scope; a probe refuted it before it shipped. DIAGNOSTIC: names sort, the CONTAINER's parameter (K, not Eq's T -- recovered via a second empty-sigma requires walk, since reverse-lookup by carrier misnames it for Map[K = Float, V = Float]), the carrier and the required spec, and is LOCATED path:line:col. TWO SILENT DROPS found reviewing my own new code: the per-site dedup key carried byte offsets without their SourceId, so two byte-identical-layout files reported ONE refusal instead of two (measured 1 vs 2, now pinned); and Display omitted the span that dedup_key is built from. PERF: load time unchanged (0.62-0.67s vs 0.67-0.91s stashed baseline), so the per-site requires walk was left unmemoized despite being redundant across duplicate types. DOCS: library/004 Status moved off Draft (its two Open questions stay open, incl. TotalFloat not being Ordered); 058 section 6's 'not enforced today' updated; kernel-language.md section on equality gained the refusal rule and lost a false claim that TotalFloat provides Ordered; float.anthill's and WI-658's stale 'WI-649' pointers repointed here; the map.anthill:7 / set.anthill:10 line citations my stdlib comments would have invalidated were dropped. NOT MIRRORED to scaland -- it has no SortProvidesInfo/sort_provides at all and parameterized requires is a literal TODO in load/Loader.scala, which is the ticket's own stated condition. CAVEAT: /code-review was NOT run (not available as a skill in this session); I ran the four /simplify angles myself, which produced the type_expr_span reuse, the located_in_kb_source extraction, and both silent-drop findings above.", at: "2026-07-29T14:36:26Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-835", author: "user", content: "/code-review ROUND (commit 07a69e5f, after the c1c80110 delivery). The review returned 12 findings; I drove each rather than accepting them, and one of my OWN first probes was invalid (I used a nonexistent 'Vector' sort, so the file died on unresolved names and my grep read that as 'loads clean'). Two findings were REAL HOLES, both from ONE decision -- recording the assembled type TERM instead of the bindings the lowering had already resolved. (1) A DENOTED sibling binding switched the check OFF for the carriers beside it: Map[K = Float, V = Buf[T = Int64, N = 3]] loaded CLEAN while the identical type without the literal was refused (measured, plus a lawful-key control proving the literal itself was not the cause). The value-in-type poisons the whole instantiation to Value::Node, which has no ground term, so K = Float was dropped with it -- adding an unrelated argument disabled a soundness check. My comment had justified skipping the Node branch as 'those bind a VALUE, not a carrier', which is true of the denoted binding and false of its siblings; I never probed a MIXED instantiation. (2) 'The sole type lowering' is TWO: sort_binding_to_value has its own TypeExpr::Parameterized arm, so a container written in a requires/provides clause was unrecorded (requires Iterable[C = Map[K = Float]] loaded clean). I had READ that function and still wrote 'sole', quoting type_expr_to_value's own wrong doc. FIX: the registry now stores ParameterizedSite { base, bindings, span } -- a denoted binding drops ITSELF, never the site -- and both lowerings record. That also deleted a DEAD positional branch in the check (the lowering maps positionals to declared names before assembling, so a recorded site is always named) which a new test's docstring falsely claimed to pin. Further review fixes: the registry is DRAINED (push-only meant a second load_incremental re-walked and re-reported every earlier batch); an empty-bindings site is not recorded; the per-base Eq-clause walk is cached (direct_requires_chain clones its memoized vector per call, and the check now visits every written Option[..]/List[..]); the bare-name decode got one owner shared with the substitution that must agree with it; located_in_source's doc comment -- which c1c80110 accidentally reassigned to a new function -- is back; and the Display byte-range comment no longer claims a dedup path these errors do not take. RESTORED a gap my doc rewrite had DELETED: a key unlawful in its ARGUMENT (Map[K = List[T = Float]], Map[K = (a: Float)]) is still not caught, since the check reads the key's own provisions and neither carrier provides NonEq; both are genuinely unlawful keys, so it is a real gap owned by WI-664's propagation follow-up, now pinned by a test named known_gap_ so it cannot quietly stop being documented. Audit re-run clean; full suite 87 binaries / 3682 passed / 0 failed; load time unchanged (interleaved A/B against a pre-WI-835 binary, minimum 0.62s both, spread is machine noise). Findings NOT acted on: none material -- the remaining ones were the same items in different words.", at: "2026-07-29T15:20:58Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-754", description: some(value: "CLI/resolver: an UNRESOLVABLE functor in a query pattern returns `no solutions` (exit 0) SILENTLY instead of erroring, so it reads as \"no such fact\" when the truth is \"that name could not resolve to any known functor.\" Violates the repo's 'loud error over silent skip' rule.\n\nNARROWED 2026-07-29. This ticket originally reported TWO grammar/CLI causes by which a query pattern could not use imports AT ALL: (1) top-level `import` was ungrammatical (`_declaration` omitted `import_clause`), so anthill-cli's `collect_queries` synthesized `import <name>` prefix died at parse and `query -i/--import` could never succeed; (2) the namespace-wrap workaround was also closed because `collect_queries` only scanned TOP-LEVEL `Item::Fact`. BOTH are now RESOLVED by WI-853 (`7a32bc79`), which added `import_clause` to `_declaration` so a file's top level admits an import the same way a namespace/sort body does. `query -i <ns> \"<pattern>\"` now resolves and exits 0, and a plain top-level `import` in an ordinary .anthill file parses. Verified live on a clean rebuild (grammar edits need `cargo clean -p tree-sitter-anthill` first — `parser.c` is generated by build.rs from grammar.js and is NOT git-tracked, so an incremental build silently keeps the stale parser). WI-853 shipped 7 end-to-end `-i` CLI tests + 4 top-level-import loader tests + a corpus case, full workspace green.\n\nWHAT REMAINS (why this stays open): the import MECHANISM works, but an unresolvable name is still a silent zero. Measured on the fresh binary against a KB with `namespace app.util sort Q entity q(row: Int64) end fact q(row: 7) end`:\n  $ anthill query -p kb.anthill 'app.util.Q.q(row: 999)'   -> no solutions   (exit 0)   # KNOWN functor, no matching row — legitimately empty\n  $ anthill query -p kb.anthill 'nope(row: ?x)'            -> no solutions   (exit 0)   # UNKNOWN short name — SILENT, should be loud\n  $ anthill query -p kb.anthill 'app.util.Q.nope(row: ?x)' -> no solutions   (exit 0)   # UNKNOWN qualified name — SILENT, should be loud\n  $ anthill query -p kb.anthill -i 'app.util.Q' 'q(row: ?x)' -> ?x = 7        (exit 0)   # import path works (control)\nAll three unknowns are INDISTINGUISHABLE from the legitimately-empty case today. The distinction to draw: a name that RESOLVES to a known functor with no matching facts legitimately yields `no solutions` (exit 0); a name that does NOT resolve to any known functor should ERROR (non-zero) naming the functor, not be conflated with the empty-answer case.\n\nNOTE ON A NON-BUG (kept so nobody re-chases it): an entity declared inside `sort Q` registers under `<ns>.Q.q`, so the qualified spelling is `app.util.Q.q(row: ?x)`, NOT `app.util.q(...)`. The sort segment is part of the entity's qualified name; a wrong spelling there is user error, not this silent-zero defect.\n\nFIX DIRECTION (not settled): in the query path (anthill-cli `collect_queries` / `convert_query_term`, and/or the resolver entry), detect a query whose head functor resolves to NO known symbol/rule/fact and surface a loud diagnostic naming the functor, rather than handing an unresolvable pattern to SLD (which correctly finds nothing and is right to). Decide whether this belongs in the CLI (a pre-resolve symbol lookup) or in the resolver (an \"unknown predicate\" signal distinct from \"no solutions\"), and whether an anonymous/variable head is exempt.\n\nACCEPTANCE: `anthill query -p <kb> \"<unknown_functor>(...)\"` exits NON-ZERO with a message naming the unresolvable functor (not `no solutions`); a KNOWN functor with no matching facts still prints `no solutions` and exits 0 (both the short-name-via-import and fully-qualified spellings); an anthill-cli test drives both the unknown-functor and the known-but-empty case; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-30T06:49:00Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-754", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. An unresolvable functor in a query pattern is now reported LOUDLY (a message naming it, non-zero exit) instead of resolving to a silent empty set; a KNOWN functor with no matching row still prints `no solutions` (exit 0).\n\nDESIGN — resolve-first, not a pre-resolution head check. The CLI resolves (or, under `--match`, browses) the pattern FIRST and reports only a DEFINITE empty result whose head names no known functor. Refusing before resolving over-fired on everything that legitimately answers empty-or-true without sitting in a functor table. Parts:\n  1. `KnowledgeBase::undefined_query_functor(tid)` (anthill-core/src/kb/mod.rs) — `Some(sym)` iff the head is a concrete functor with no `kind_of` and no `rules_by_functor` entry. `None` for a non-functor head (`?x`, a literal), a defined functor, and a resolver SCOPING MARKER (`forall_in`/`some_in`/`forall_impl`/`__pop_assumption`), reusing `is_scoping_marker_name` (now `pub(crate)`) as the single authority so the set never drifts.\n  2. `report_if_unknown_functor` (anthill-cli/src/main.rs) — called AFTER an empty resolve/browse, in both the default and `--match` arms. Returns a bool (not `Err`) so the caller banks the failure and still attempts every remaining query.\n  3. A discrim-index BACKSTOP in that helper: `browse_program_clauses_matching` before reporting, because one declared thing escapes both enumeration tables — a TOP-LEVEL arity-0 rule head that evaluated to FALSE (it matches only through the discrim tree, under a symbol the bare query re-interns). So a false proposition reads `no solutions`, while a genuinely absent name (and a namespaced name queried out of scope) heads no clause and is reported.\n\nTWO ADVERSARIAL REVIEW ROUNDS (fresh finders built the CLI and drove crafted KBs; every finding reproduced then addressed):\n  ROUND 1 (over/under-fire):\n   - OVER-FIRE, bounded quantifier `(forall/some ?x in xs: P)` refused — FIXED by the scoping-marker whitelist; a FALSE quantifier now answers `no solutions`, not \"unknown functor\".\n   - OVER-FIRE, arity-0 proposition `prop_false` refused though declared — FIXED by the discrim backstop (a TRUE one was already fine via resolve-first; `has_bodied_rule` was tried and does NOT cover it — the bare query re-interns a different symbol — so it was not used).\n   - MESSAGE, a namespaced name queried by bare leaf claimed \"no declaration defines it\" though a qualified declaration exists — behaviour is correct (a bare name is out of the query's `_global` scope, the rule the WI-853 `mk`-without-import control follows, updated here), but the message now says \"does not resolve to a known functor ... in scope\" and guides to qualify or `-i`.\n  ROUND 2 (final diff):\n   - REGRESSION, an unknown functor MID `--query-file` aborted the loop (`?` returned out of `run_query`), silently dropping later patterns — FIXED: the helper returns a bool, the loop banks `any_unknown` and returns non-zero only AFTER every pattern has run. Pinned by `an_unknown_functor_mid_file_does_not_drop_later_queries`.\n   - Confirmed NON-ISSUES: `kind_of` is `Some` for every Resolved symbol (no declared const/sort/namespace/operation is ever wrongly refused); a truncated search can't hide an unknown functor (an undefined head gathers 0 candidates at the root, never recurses to the depth cap); `resolve_sym` on an Unresolved dotted name prints it verbatim, no panic; borrows are clean (`TermId`/`Symbol` are `Copy`).\n\nRESIDUALS — filed as WI-863, all boundaries of the head-of-top-level scope, recorded per 'loud over silent': (A) a NESTED unknown functor under `not(...)` silently succeeds (`not(nonexistent(42))` -> `true`) — the head-only check cannot see `pos_args`, and NAF-over-undefined is standard closed-world, so the fix is a query-mode diagnostic, not a resolution change; (B) a surface operator desugaring to an unloaded functor is reported naming the DESUGARED functor (`6 / 2` -> `div`), compounded by `query -p` not loading stdlib; (C) an undefined functor whose SHORT NAME collides with a scoping marker (`some_in`) escapes the report (exempted for consistency with the resolver, which reserves those names by short name too) — a distinct name would close it.\n\nTESTS: anthill-cli/tests/wi754_unknown_functor_test.rs (11, three fixtures: namespaced `kb.anthill` for the core + scope cases, top-level `props.anthill` for propositions/quantifiers, `multi-query.anthill` for the query-file case) — each pins a behaviour against its opposite. wi853's `an_import_flag_puts_the_name_in_query_scope` control updated: without the import `mk` is now reported (a stronger proof the import is load-bearing than the old \"no solutions\"). Full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh.", at: "2026-07-30T06:48:54Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-841", description: some(value: "Proposal 058 (WI-648) PHASE 2 — SELECT: make the call-site bracket bind a REQUIREMENT SLOT. FOUR pieces (§4.2, §4.4, §4.5). (1) RULE (1)'s SORT-LEVEL SEEDING LEG — a call bracket must bind the ENCLOSING SORT's type params, which today it cannot: measured, `Box.mk[T = String](5)` on `sort Box { sort T = ? }` errors at the RETURN (`expected Box[T = String], got Box[T = Int64]` — T was inferred from the ARGUMENT and the binding contributed nothing), and `Box.mk[Bogus = Int64](5)` loads clean because the guard swallows the whole list when the callee has no OP-level params. One more WI-708-style seeding rung; without it §5.3's construction-site selection means nothing. (2) BRACKET KEY -> SLOT resolution, in the §4.2 order: (1) a declared TYPE PARAMETER name — the operation's, or its enclosing sort's — matched by SYMBOL IDENTITY (`n == name_sym`, typing.rs:12931), because both sides are the bare spelling (`build_call_type_args` lowers the key to a bare interned Symbol, \"the param label (A) … NOT a caller-scope value\"; op.type_params holds `kb.intern(\"T\")`, WI-708); (2) a requirement's SPEC SHORT NAME when unambiguous among the callee's remaining ANONYMOUS slots — this one CANNOT match by identity (its candidate set is derived from the slots' canonical, qualified spec symbols), so it is a NAME LOOKUP: `same_label`'s sanctioned family 2 (typing.rs:11328), and THE \"UNAMBIGUOUS AMONG THE REMAINING SLOTS\" CLAUSE IS THE GATE THAT MAKES IT SOUND, not a courtesy — without a gate this is the short-name identity comparison WI-672 deleted (owners `same_sort_canonical` / `same_qname`, neither of which matches on last segment). Anything else is a LOUD error, including on a callee with no type parameters (the phase-0 drop). Rule (2) also admits the DISPATCHED SPEC's OWN short name, so a direct spec-op call selects the dictionary backing it: `Desc.describe[Desc = LoudDesc](w)` — the thing selected is `requirements[0]`. A QUALIFIED key is REFUSED, not resolved (`fold[algebra.Monoid = AddM]`): rule (1) already refuses one (measured — a qualified spelling of a REAL type param is `unknown type-param 'Id.T'`), a resolved key would be a second name law in one bracket list (rule (1)'s key is unresolvable IN PRINCIPLE — `T` is a binder of the callee, visible in no scope — so a ladder mixing them would rank a label against a reference, and resolve-then-fall-back-to-label is a fallback), it would make selection depend on the CALLER's imports (measured: the supply path takes no scope at all — `build_concrete_dispatch_dict`, typing.rs:11700, reads provider facts and resolved symbols only; a cross-namespace call into a `requires`-carrying callee from a namespace that never imports the spec loads clean), and it buys no expressiveness (every selection a short name cannot express is written with the §4.7 NAMED SLOT instead, which puts it under rule (1) — rule (1) is the COMPLETE mechanism, rule (2) a gated shorthand). Also mind `same_label`'s own debug_assert (typing.rs:11336), which fires on the partially-qualified pair. (3) WITNESS VALIDATION at the selection site (§4.4): W provides Spec at the call's bindings (a `SortProvidesInfo(sort_ref = W, spec = Spec[…])` whose spec view unifies with the goal) — else a loud error naming BOTH; the slot exists on the callee; and the selection is REFUSED on a call whose dispatch is already value-directed on a CONCRETE carrier (§1.1 — there the VALUE decides and an explicit witness would silently contradict it; refuse, do not prefer). (4) `resolve` STEP 0 (§4.5): if the call site explicitly bound this goal's spec, return `ResolvedTree::leaf { impl: W, type_args }` after check (1); steps 1-4 untouched — a conditional witness still recursively resolves its own `:-` subgoals and those always SEARCH (tier 2). A bracket key must NOT reach sub-resolutions: rule (2)'s candidate set is the CALLEE's slots, and extending it into the resolution tree would make key resolution depend on which witness was pinned (pinning changes which subgoals exist). Steering a sub-resolution is done by the WITNESS declaring a named slot and the caller binding it in the key's VALUE position (`fold[Monoid = ListM[O = MyEq]]`) — an ordinary type application, where selection happens at the witness's own boundary as a callee slot again. THREADING NEEDS NOTHING NEW (§2.3, §4.6): the resolved instance already rides as a `Value::Requirement` dictionary rebuilt from `sub_requires` at every `apply_within`; the whole change is WHERE the impl in `ResolvedTree::leaf` comes from. ACCEPTANCE (§9 phase 2, as amended by the second review): `Box.mk[T = String](5)` fails at the ARGUMENT, not the return (§2.2 row 5 flipped); with the carrier's SOLE provider, `fold[Monoid = AddM]([2,3,4])` computes 9 AND THE PIN IS OBSERVABLE — a `[Monoid = NotAProvider]` or wrong-spec witness at the same site is a loud §4.4 error, which is what distinguishes tier 1 from tier 2 silently finding the same provider; a QUALIFIED key, a short name ambiguous across two anonymous slots, and an op type param colliding with its enclosing sort's are each a LOUD error naming the slots. NOTE — the two-monoid VALUE test (9 beside 24) belongs to PHASE 3b, not here: AddM and MulM cannot coexist until tier 3 moves, so as ONE program it does not load at phase 2, and as TWO single-provider programs it measures nothing selection-specific. Full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. REFERENCE: docs/proposals/058-modular-instances.md §4.2, §4.4, §4.5, §2.3, §9 phase 2."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-839", "WI-840"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-29T16:49:14Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-841", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. All four §9 phase-2 pieces, and the acceptance UNDERSTATED what could be measured.\n\n(1) SORT-LEVEL SEEDING RUNG: the bracket's declared list is now the op's params ++ its enclosing sort's, CONCATENATED rather than laddered because phase 1's declaration-site collision guard makes them disjoint — `Box.mk[T = String](5)` now fails at the ARGUMENT (§2.2 row 5 flipped). It reuses the MEMOIZED `sort_type_params_as_pairs`, whose own doc calls it \"the same shape an operation's own type_params take\", and is kind-gated because `impl_parent_of_op` returns a NAMESPACE for a free op. (2) KEY -> SLOT in the §4.2 order; rung (2) spans the op's `requires`, its sort's, and — for a body-less spec op — the dispatched spec itself. A qualified key never enters rung (2), so `same_label`'s debug_assert stays out of reach. (3) §4.4 validation. (4) `resolve` STEP 0 via `ResolutionScope.selected`, gated on `stack.is_empty()` so it reaches the call's own goal and no sub-goal.\n\nTHE PIN IS OBSERVABLE IN A VALUE, which the ticket assigned to 3b. Two providers whose DISPATCH CARRIERS differ coexist today (a ground `fact Monoid[T = Int64]` beside a parametric `fact Monoid[T = E]`) and both answer the concrete goal, so ONE program computes 5 or 99 by its bracket key alone, with the bracket-less control measuring what `pick_most_specific` picks. AddM-beside-MulM on ONE carrier still belongs to 3b.\n\nFOUR SILENT DROPS FOUND BY DRIVING, all \"the author named a provider and nothing used it\". The decisive one: a witness providing the spec at OTHER bindings failed THREE DIFFERENT WAYS on the three routes a slot can be served by — loud (spec-op dispatch), LOADS CLEAN then `Internal(__req_monoid not bound)` at eval (dictionary build), and SILENTLY IGNORED (value-direction, which has no dictionary channel at all — WI-822 leg 1). Fixed at the CALL SITE, once, because only two of the three have a place to complain from; enumerating consumers is the mistake WI-839's own review had to undo. Also: a pinned dep that failed to project fell back to `Ok(None)` (\"no dict, carry on\"), and the WI-239 defer PRE-CHECK swallowed the pin before any resolution ran — TWO defer gates were needed, not one.\n\n/code-review (4 agents) then found the most valuable defect: MY NEW CHECK WAS VACUOUS ON THE STDLIB'S OWN SPELLING. `goal_from_op_requires_entry` read NAMED args only, but an op-scoped `requires` is a BARE APPLICATION and the stdlib writes it POSITIONALLY (`operation member(x: T, l: List) -> Bool requires Eq[T]`, prelude/list.anthill:58; `requires Ring[F]`). MEASURED: `requires Monoid[T = HT]` refused a wrong-bindings pin while `requires Monoid[HT]` loaded clean. Now binds positionals in declared param order. RECORDED not fixed: `RequiresEntry.spec` has TWO shapes and FOUR readers that disagree about the bare one — `unwrap_spec_view` drops its bindings, which also makes `entries_cover` vacuously cover any same-spec dep.\n\nTWO DOC CLAIMS I WROTE WERE FALSE, both disproved by review. (a) \"a per-sort predicate would give the §1.1 criterion a second implementation to drift from\" — `sort_has_constructors` already existed, and it is a DIFFERENT criterion (SortInfo.constructors vs Entity-kind children), so the fix was to say which one §1.1 means and to probe membership BOTH raw and canonical (the set is raw-keyed). (b) \"the position is still the slot's identity for the projection path\" — nothing on that path reads it; it is recorded-but-unread, and now says so.\n\nCOORDINATE-FREE, per WI-840's own feedback: `NamedRequirementSlot` records the SPEC BESIDE the position, so the typer matches by spec multiset and never maps a coordinate through a list whose order it does not hold. WHICH entry of a same-spec pair is tagged named is arbitrary — sound only because a selection is SPEC-KEYED so it is unobservable and only the COUNT is read; said out loud rather than left as accident.\n\nCLEANUPS APPLIED: one shared slot enumerator for both consumers (they were two walks that had already DRIFTED — only one filtered value preconditions, under a doc comment asserting they could not disagree); one `pinned_witness_for` owner for what was five copies; both loop-invariant guards hoisted to the iteration SOURCE; `selection_witness_sym` reduced to `sort_functor_of_view`; `same_sort_canonical` not raw `==` where two decoders meet; `sorts_with_constructors` hoisted out of the per-binding loop. DECLINED with reason: threading `selected` on `SigmaCtx` — the cache key needs `selected` by CONTENT and `disambig` only by PRESENCE, and it would make coarse-mode-with-selections inexpressible.\n\nNON-VACUITY: fifteen checks reverted in turn, each failing the expected tests (sort-level rung 6; rung 2 11; step-0 filter 2; site binding check 1; op-scoped goal reader 1; positional bindings 1; pinned-dep refusal 1; check 3 1; Strategy-1/2 skip 1; memo key 1 — and that one needed a NEW test, since no existing one made two differently-pinned calls in one program; both defer gates 1 each; both hoisted guards 2). 26 tests. Full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh (3708 passed, 0 failed); stdlib loads clean through the CLI. Spec amended (kernel-language.md §Operation type parameters: the two rungs, the three rung-(2) refusals, §4.4 validation) and 058 §2.2/§4.2/§4.5/§9 updated with what phase 2 measured. scaland NOT mirrored: it has no typer and no instance resolution (no SortProvidesInfo, no dispatch), so phase 2 has nothing to mirror — the same finding as WI-839.", at: "2026-07-29T16:49:50Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-841", author: "user", content: "CORRECTION to the delivery note above, found by driving a case I had asserted without running. That note says the pin is validated on all three routes and that the value-directed one diverges only after phase 3b. FALSE. With two providers that coexist today (their dispatch carriers differ), an OP-SCOPED `requires` computed the answer of the provider the author did NOT name: `Holder.probe[Monoid = AddM](2, 3)` = 99, which is AnyM's. An op-scoped chain has no dictionary channel at all (`synth_req_names` is keyed by the parent SORT), so the bracket was accepted, validated, and discarded — a silently wrong number in a loading program.\n\nAn op-scoped selection is now REFUSED whenever two or more providers answer the goal, which is exactly when the ignored selection could pick wrong. With the carrier's SOLE provider the pin necessarily names it, so that case still loads and computes — which is what §9 phase 2's acceptance asks for. The refusal lifts with WI-822 leg 1. The diagnostic does not pretend the workaround is free: moving the `requires` to the sort threads it but imposes the requirement on every dispatch through that sort, which is why `List.member`'s `Eq[T]` is written on the operation.\n\nTWO OF MY OWN TESTS WERE FALSE POSITIVES for the same reason: they pinned the witness the SEARCH already picks (`[Monoid = AnyM]` -> 99) and so passed without anything being selected. Pin the provider the search would NOT choose, or the assertion is vacuous. Both corrected.\n\nSO: PHASE 2 DELIVERS SELECTION ON TWO ROUTES, NOT THREE — the sort-level dictionary build and the spec-op dispatch. The proposal (058 §4.5) and the check's own doc now say so; the earlier claim that a bracket 'selects the provider' was too broad. Full workspace green (3717 passed, 0 failed).", at: "2026-07-29T18:42:29Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-842", description: some(value: "Proposal 058 (WI-648) PHASE 3a — HARDEN the bracket-less provider readers (§4.9). This is phase 3b's PREREQUISITE, not its cleanup. AUDITED 2026-07-28: only ONE consumer can raise a use-site ambiguity — `resolve_inner` -> `pick_most_specific` (typing.rs:15030), which returns Ambiguous on a tie and is reachable only from `check_apply` (op-body calls and rule-body DOT-calls, exactly the sites that have a bracket). EVERY other consumer of provider facts selects by FIRST MATCH with NO ambiguity arm, and the code names today's LOAD-TIME refusal as its license — `witness_provision`'s own doc, verbatim (typing.rs:16114): \"Coherence (two witnesses for one application) is rejected at load … so first-match is the sole instance\". Phase 3b DELETES that premise, so these reads must be hardened FIRST or coexistence lands on silent first-match. THE RULE: a read that asks EXISTENCE stays BOOLEAN — two providers satisfy a constraint as well as one (`sort_provides`, the WI-300 `find_dictionary` and `[simp]` guards, typing.rs:31372: two providers are MORE true, the guard fires and hands the real choice to a selecting read). A read that SELECTS — returns one provider's op binding — goes LOUD ON THE SECOND CANDIDATE, never first-match: `provider_spec_view_bindings` (typing.rs:18192, returns at :18229; ~15 call sites including type conformance and WI-664's NonEq classifier; the \"first-provider-wins contract\" is named at load.rs:283), `witness_provision` / `witness_op_for_carrier` (typing.rs:16116, :16164), and the value-directed `or_else` chain (eval.rs:934) — which is what rule-body / constraint-body / quantifier-goal / `proof … by derivation` spec-op atoms fall to, since they are never typer-classified (`type_rule_bodies`, typing.rs:33543, recurses through a plain Apply without check_apply). Diagnostics in the spirit of WI-737's `Error[RelationFloundered]`: named, at the read. THE SEM-EQ INDEX IS THE SPECIAL CASE and refuses at LOAD instead: it is built by a bare `HashMap::insert` in `sort_ops.keys()` walk order (load.rs:4790-4845, mod.rs:3200), so under coexistence the winner would be NON-DETERMINISTIC PER LOAD, and no call site exists ANYWHERE to bracket — the dispatch fires from unification (resolver eq/neq builtin; interpreter `sem_eq_dispatch_target`, eval/builtins.rs:549). An index has no later site to complain from. INCLUDES WI-837 as an integral part: a WI-450 witness-supplied `eq` never keys that index (load.rs:4811-4813 reads `carrier_own_op .or_else(instance_fact_eq_target)`), so without it the new index check has NOTHING TO FIRE ON and the witness pair sails through both checks once 3b removes `AmbiguousWitness`. CONSEQUENCE TO STATE HONESTLY (§6): hardening the index means two PartialEq/Eq providers for one carrier remain a LOAD error — for that family tier 3's coexistence simply never applies, because semantic equality dispatches from unification where no selection can ever be written. ACCEPTANCE (§9 phase 3a, as amended): a two-PartialEq-provider program is a load error RAISED BY THE INDEX BUILD — assert the DIAGNOSTIC IDENTITY, not merely that the load fails, because `AmbiguousWitness` (typing.rs:15995) already refuses that exact program today and a failure-only assertion would pass with nothing implemented; the WITNESS-supplied pair (WI-837) is refused by the same check; a two-provider rule-body dispatch is a NAMED diagnostic, never first-match; existence guards (`sort_provides`) still fire. Full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. REFERENCE: docs/proposals/058-modular-instances.md §4.9, §6, §9 phase 3a."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-837", "WI-838"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-29T21:47:50Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-843", description: some(value: "Proposal 058 (WI-648) PHASE 3b — COHERENCE: move the two-provider refusal from LOAD TIME to the UNSELECTED USE SITE (§4.1 tier 3). Admissible ONLY on top of phase 3a. TODAY two independent refusals fire on the §1 program: the per-call-site one (typing.rs:10731 — \"expected unique impl for per-call bindings, got multiple impls match (coherence rule)\") and the LOAD-TIME global one (`AmbiguousWitness`, typing.rs:15995; the instance-fact flavor `AmbiguousInstanceFact`, load.rs:283) — and the second does not depend on any call at all: delete the calling sort from the two-monoid program and the load still fails with \"keep exactly one\". What Anthill rejects today is the pair of DECLARATIONS; the question of which monoid a call wants is never reached. AFTER: two providers may COEXIST, and what is refused is an UNSELECTED dispatch against them — at that use site, naming the candidates and the syntax that picks one. NOTHING may become silently dispatched: an ambiguous site that says nothing is still an error, with a better message. The criterion is unchanged from the one already shipping (§1.1): the concrete-provider exemption (typing.rs:15977) asks whether the CALL has one answer — with a concrete carrier the VALUE answers, so two providers are no ambiguity — and tier 3 supplies an answer for the case where the value is silent. GATE — COEXISTENCE REQUIRES EVERY CANDIDATE TO BE NAMEABLE (§4.1, §4.3): an instance fact has no name, so two instance facts stay refused at load exactly as today, AND SO DOES A MIXED instance-fact + witness pair. WI-838 is the trap here: measured 2026-07-28, that mixed pair LOADS CLEAN TODAY because the two ambiguity checks are each grouped by provider KIND and neither sees the cross-kind pair — an implementation that merely DELETES the witness check inherits that hole as its coexistence rule. A tier-3 diagnostic listing a candidate the author cannot spell is a dead end, not a fix. ACCEPTANCE (§9 phase 3b, as amended): the §1 two-monoid program LOADS, and `fold[Monoid = AddM]([2,3,4])` / `fold[Monoid = MulM]([2,3,4])` compute 9 / 24 (the value test deferred here from phase 2, since it needs coexistence); the unselected `Use.go` becomes an error naming AddM/MulM and the syntax that picks one — the diagnostic MOVES from the declarations to the one call that is actually ambiguous. `two_describers_for_one_carrier_rejected_globally` (rustland/anthill-core/tests/include/wi817_polyrec_requirement_test.rs:662) flips to 75 with one selection written per site (`Desc.describe[Desc = LoudDesc](w)` in the loud scope, `[Desc = QuietDesc]` in the quiet one) — 55 or 77 is a FAILURE, not a variant: either betrays a pin ignored or cross-wired. TWO CHORES THAT GO WITH THE FLIP, or the tree keeps a false claim: (a) that fixture's doc comment currently reads \"lambda keeps its creation-scope provider (5), the quiet hop uses its own (7)\" — a SCOPE-CAPTURE reading the pinned program no longer demonstrates (a pin is a per-occurrence static constant emitted at classification; the flipped fixture measures PIN SURVIVAL through a closure hop, which is the right acceptance for this proposal since implicit scoped selection is deferred). Rewrite it; the VALUE 75 is unchanged, which is exactly why a stale comment here would go unnoticed. (b) KEEP AN UNPINNED TWIN as the tier-3 control — the current test's own name is that control, and it must keep failing, now at the USE SITE rather than at the declarations. Editing the only fixture into the pinned form would leave tier 3's refusal untested. ALSO: a mixed witness + instance-fact pair stays a LOAD error; every current single-provider program is unchanged — FULL SUITE GREEN via rustland/scripts/test.sh is the control. REFERENCE: docs/proposals/058-modular-instances.md §4.1, §4.3, §5.1, §5.2, §9 phase 3b."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-841", "WI-842", "WI-838"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-29T23:16:16Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-843", author: "user", content: "WIDENING FOUND BY REVIEW, PINNED NOT FIXED: a specificity-ordered pair never reaches tier 3. Two providers in ONE (spec, carrier) group whose heads differ in groundness are not a tie — pick_most_specific takes the more specific one and the call runs with NO diagnostic. MEASURED: ground `Monoid[T = List[T = Int64]]` beside parametric `Monoid[T = List[T = E]]` loads and answers 111 (the ground one); control — the mixed-kind twin is still refused 'for carrier anthill.prelude.List', proving both provisions are one group and so were refused before this ticket. So the ticket's 'NOTHING may become silently dispatched' is FALSE as written; what holds is 'nothing became silently FIRST-MATCHED' (WI-842's readers). Not fixed here because tier 2's specificity rule is '058 §4.1 today's rule, unchanged' and WI-841's committed acceptance (selection_overrides_the_search_and_the_value_shows_it) DEPENDS on the search taking the ground provider silently; making same-group pairs loud is a new coherence rule needing its own measurement. Pinned by a_specificity_ordered_pair_silently_takes_the_more_specific and recorded in 058 §4.1.", at: "2026-07-29T23:16:34Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-844", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED 2026-07-30. Four things the ticket asserted that DRIVING changed, each now pinned in wi844_sorted_set_driver_test.rs (18 tests) and recorded in 058 §5.3/§9. (1) 'THREAD: nothing new (§4.6)' was HALF true. The construction site worked, and the very next line did not: SortedSet.insert(a, \"zz\") after empty[T = String, O = ByLength]() REFUSED with 'constructing Ordered[T = String] is ambiguous among providers: String, ByLength, Alphabetical', and so did union(a, b) on two AGREEING sets — the program that must work. WI-841 built the WRITE half of §4.7 (a bracket binds the slot AND records a pin) and nothing read the choice back off a TYPE, so the driver was writable only by repeating [T = String, O = ByLength] at every call. What landed is the READ half: selections_from_slot_bindings looks a named slot's parameter up in σ once argument unification is complete and turns a witness found there into an ordinary InstanceSelection, judged by the same check_selection_bindings a written one is. An ABSTRACT binding derives nothing (is_type_param_value), which is what keeps §7.1's report[T, O] a FORWARD — measured, one polymorphic body answers zz for a ByLength argument and aaa for an Alphabetical one. (2) The two orderings CANNOT live in the stdlib, and that is measured rather than preferred: the Rust binding already declares fact Ordered[T = String], so ByLength + Alphabetical in the PRELUDE would make every bracket-less Ordered.compare(a: String, b: String) in every downstream program an ambiguous-dispatch load error naming three candidates. Tier 3's coexistence is a per-PROGRAM choice; a standard library may not impose it on its consumers. SortedSet is in stdlib/anthill/prelude/sortedset.anthill; the orderings are in the test. (3) The ticket's 'omitting the binding still works' holds exactly as written and no further: SortedSet.empty[T = Int64]() LOADS and tier 2 resolves it, then DIES at eval — dispatching dict has arity 0 but its requires chain has 2 entries. Pre-existing, needing none of this arc's vocabulary (sort H { sort T = ?  requires Eq[T]  operation same(a,b) = PartialEq.eq(a,b) } at Int64 fails identically, with PartialEq — empty own chain — as the passing control), so 'the same program you write today meaning the same thing' is literally true. Filed as WI-857 with that 058-free control pinned in this ticket's own test file. A WITNESS provider sidesteps it (dispatch lands on the witness's own member, whose parent chain is empty), which is why every NAMED spelling runs — driven at String (zz vs aaa) and at Int64 (Ascending 3 vs Descending 7). (4) REVIEW found two more, both measured. A THIRD σ producer: an ETA'd op reference pins its params from the EXPECTED ARROW, so let f: (SortedSet[T = String, O = ByLength], String) -> … = insert names the ordering with no call and no bracket; that site passed an empty selection list under a comment ('an eta carries no call-site bracket') that was true under WI-841 and had silently stopped being the whole story. And RANKING the producers re-opened the very first-match the conflict check exists to prevent: a first cut exempted a derived witness colliding with a bracket-written one, true for ONE slot and false across two same-spec slots — with requires A: Ord[T] beside requires B: Ord[T], adding [A = ByLength] to a call whose argument typed B = Alphabetical DELETED the refusal and left one spec-keyed pin serving both deps. A debug_assert written to DOCUMENT the exemption fired on the first program that exercised it. push_selection now compares unconditionally and owns the rule for all three producers; the diagnostic names both witnesses without attributing either, because a MIXED pair is reachable. ALSO: the stdlib file's two recursion carriers are internal (WI-369, list.anthill's precedent) so an outside call is refused rather than documented; union is a single-pass merge over the operands' shared invariant rather than a fold of insert (O(|a|+|b|), with a value test — {1,3,5} u {2,3,4} = {1,2,3,4,5}, both directions, both empty bases); and wi232_resolved_spec_carries_matched_entry_not_just_symbol had to be scoped to its own fixture, because SortedSet.insertSorted's Ordered.compare is the FIRST stdlib op body ever to classify as DeferToRequirement (Set/Map read their requires from RULE bodies, which are never typer-classified) and its whole-KB walk had been asserting a fixture claim over a KB-wide set. Full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh; /simplify review round applied (the bug-hunting /code-review was NOT run — it is user-triggered).", at: "2026-07-30T07:03:43Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-844", description: some(value: "Proposal 058 (WI-648) PHASE 4 — the `SortedSet` DRIVER, on phases 0-3b (§5.3). This is the phase that makes the arc worth having. BLOCKED ON WI-836: without argument-position type-variable agreement the driver's safety property DOES NOT EXIST — measured 2026-07-28, `union(a: Box[T = T, O = O], b: Box[T = T, O = O])` applied to a `Box[… O = Q]` and a `Box[… O = R]` LOADS CLEAN. DECLARE: the ordering is NOT incidental to a sorted set, so its slot is NAMED (§4.7) and thereby becomes a type parameter — `sort SortedSet { sort T = ?  requires O: Ord[T]  operation empty() -> SortedSet[T = T, O = O]  operation insert(s: SortedSet[T = T, O = O], x: T) -> SortedSet[T = T, O = O]  operation union(a: SortedSet[T = T, O = O], b: SortedSet[T = T, O = O]) -> SortedSet[T = T, O = O]  operation toList(s: SortedSet[T = T, O = O]) -> List[T = T] }` — with two providers of `Ord[String]` (`ByLength`, `Alphabetical`) that now COEXIST because tier 3 no longer refuses them at load. SELECT at the CONSTRUCTION site via the ordinary §4.2 bracket (no new grammar), riding rule (1)'s sort-level seeding leg from phase 2: `let a = SortedSet.empty[T = String, O = ByLength]()`, `let b = SortedSet.empty[T = String, O = Alphabetical]()`. THREAD: nothing new (§4.6) — `insert`'s body calls `Ord.compare(x, y)`, which resolves through slot O, which the caller filled with `construct_requirement(ByLength, [])`; `a` and `b` carry different dictionaries at run time because they were constructed with different ones. ACCEPTANCE (§9 phase 4, as amended): two orderings coexist; `union` of a ByLength set and an Alphabetical one is a TYPE error (`expected SortedSet[T = String, O = ByLength], got SortedSet[T = String, O = Alphabetical]`) — THIS IS WI-836's ACCEPTANCE, NOT a free consequence of the existing checker, and the test must fail before WI-836 and pass after; omitting the binding still works — `SortedSet.empty[T = Int64]()` leaves O to inference, which resolves it to the unique `Ord[Int64]` provider by §4.1 tier 2, the same program you write today meaning the same thing. STATE THE SCOPE OF THE GUARANTEE IN THE STDLIB DOC COMMENT rather than leaving it to be discovered (§4.7): omission in TYPE position means ANY — measured, `check_sort_type_args` (kb/mod.rs:1357) requires NO declared param to be bound (only undeclared / duplicate / excess-positional are errors) and `take(b: Box[T = Int64])` accepts a `Box[T = Int64, O = Q]` — so the merge safety is PER-SIGNATURE, holding exactly where O is written. That is deliberate: it is how \"works for any order\" is spelled, and it is what keeps `report[T, O]` (§7.1) and existing `SortedSet[T = Int64]` code working. NOTE what is NOT in scope: a comparator chosen at RUN TIME does not exist as a concept (every witness is a sort declared in the program text; \"runtime choice\" means which statically-pinned branch executed — ordinary universal polymorphism, §7.1), and the heterogeneous-collection existential (`List[SortedSet[T = String, O = ?]]`) is the general existential question (WI-402), neither needed nor foreclosed here. SCOPE: stdlib/anthill/prelude/. Full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. REFERENCE: docs/proposals/058-modular-instances.md §5.3, §4.7, §7.1, §9 phase 4."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-836", "WI-841", "WI-843"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-30T07:03:43Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-857", author: "user", content: "SCOPE EXTENDED by the 058 third review (2026-07-30; docs/proposals/058-modular-instances.md §4.5 correction, §5.4, §9 phase 6; brainstorm prelude-multiple-orderings.md addendum; probes q4a-q4g, anthill load/run at HEAD). Three additions, each measured. (1) The ticket's SECOND CONTROL ('a witness sort supplying the op runs — the parent's requires chain is empty') is the ONLY witness configuration that runs: a PARAMETRIC, chain-bearing witness dies identically. Minimal reproducer (q4e): enum Duo { sort A = ?, sort B = ?, entity duo(l: A, r: B) } providing PartialEq/Eq[T = Duo] componentwise, plus witness LexFst { sort A = ?, sort B = ?, requires Ordered[T = A], fact PartialOrd[T = Duo[A = A, B = B]], fact Ordered[T = Duo[A = A, B = B]], compare calling Ordered.compare(al, bl) } — LOADS clean (check_provider_requires discharges the Eq leg from the carrier's provides and the PartialOrd leg from the witness's own fact), then dies at eval on BOTH routes (σ-pinned O = LexFst through SortedSet's named slot, and direct searched Ordered.compare on two duos): 'dispatching dict for anthill.prelude.Ordered.compare has arity 0 but its requires chain has 2 entries'. The count STAYS 2 when the witness's own chain is cut to 1 (q4f), so the frame is named from the SPEC's chain even though the provider is a witness — plausibly the op-target lookup misses the PARAMETRIC member and dispatch falls to the spec builtin, whose parent is the spec; read the internals at fix time, the boundary is measured. CONTROLS: the ground, chain-free, slot-free-body bundle (q4g) RUNS (-1); and the SAME parametric+chain shape over a spec whose OWN chain is EMPTY (local Sized, q4a) threads the witness's conditional chain end to end (3+6=9) — so the trigger stays the spec's chain depth, now measured on the witness route too. ACCEPTANCE ADDITION: the q4e reproducer runs. (2) The settlement must include the LOCALITY rule: when constructing provider W's dictionary, a sub-goal that W itself provides resolves to W's OWN provision before global search. Reason: the lawful form of an alternative Ordered witness is a PartialOrd+Ordered BUNDLE (ordered.anthill:44-48 derives the inherited gt/lt from compare, so a lone witness's dictionary contradicts itself — 058 §5.4), and once the producer bundles the SPEC's chain, PartialOrd[carrier] has TWO candidates inside each of two coexisting witnesses' chains; the only right answer is witness-local. The rule depends on the SELECTED provider, never on caller scope, so 058 §4.2's import-coupling objection does not touch it. (3) IMPACT RE-SCOPED: this ticket gates the ENTIRE 058 shipped-library goal — §9 phase 7 (two orderings in the prelude, the WI-844 instruction) and phase 8 (rung 2a: an unselected dispatch routes into carrier-keyed dictionaries) — not merely the omitted-O spelling. 058 §4.5 now records the same finding from the pin side: step 0's ResolvedTree::leaf is right only for a CHAIN-FREE witness; the chain-bearing form must yield the CONDITIONAL node with sub-goals resolved under σ. Probes preserved in the session scratchpad (q4a-q4g); pin as tests at fix time. NOTE: anthill query -p diverges from anthill load (sort-level member imports refused; panics with the bindings dir) — drive reproducers via anthill run + a Main sort, not query.", at: "2026-07-30T10:32:35Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-861", description: some(value: "Proposal 058 PHASE 8c — RUNG 2a: an unselected dispatch takes the DEFAULT among the tied most-specific candidates (058 §3.2). WORK: the classifier's tie (pick_most_specific returning none / DispatchOutcome::Ambiguous) consults phase 8b's index — if exactly one tied candidate answers default_provider(spec, carrier, ?W), take it; otherwise the tier-3 refusal stands unchanged. SPECIFICITY RANKS FIRST: a strictly-more-specific unmarked candidate beats a less-specific default (the Rust default-impl reading; boundary 3's pinned silent-pick widening untouched). THE FLIP INVENTORY, written before implementation because a refusal that MOVES must enumerate its consumers — each asserted as a test: (1) two_string_orderings_make_a_bare_compare_ambiguous FLIPS into this rung's acceptance — bracket-less compare answers HOST order beside two loadable witnesses, each still opt-in by bracket; (2) WI-855's AmbiguousRequirement on the self-provider-plus-rival pair RESOLVES to the self-provider, while the witness-only tie KEEPS the diagnostic (control); (3) the value-directed AmbiguousSpecOpDispatch likewise takes the default where one candidate is the carrier's own, witness-only control stays loud; (4) the Eq family is UNTOUCHED — coherent stays load-refused, no rung consulted (control); (5) a DefaultProvider-driven flip: a marked witness for a carrier with NO self-provision answers bracket-less (the Money shape), and unmarking it restores the tier-3 refusal. GATED ON WI-857: the rung routes bracket-less dispatch INTO carrier-keyed dictionaries — today's measured eval death. ACCEPTANCE: the five flips above; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. REFERENCE: 058 §3.2, §3.6; design/058-implementation.md §3, §8 phase 8."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-860", "WI-857"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-862", description: some(value: "Proposal 058 SURFACE CONSOLIDATION (058 §4, proposed). TWO HALVES, one grammar session. (a) SUGAR: default provides X[…] — one leading modifier (the internal-modifier pattern), desugared by the converter to a DefaultProvider row with provider = the enclosing sort. A trailing [default] annotation is REJECTED — a bracket list after a bracketed type invites a GLR tie, and [simp]'s precedent follows a rule BODY, not a type. coherent on a PROVISION is REFUSED: the attachment principle — a modifier attaches where its relation's KEY lives — puts Coherent's sugar on the spec sort (coherent sort X, the enum-sort precedent), and that arrives with the DEFERRED Coherent re-homing, not here; a provision must not foreclose a spec/carrier it does not own. (b) RETIREMENT: the fact spelling of provisions retires — provides becomes the one provision spelling, fact returns to plain data assertions — removing the language's one container-sensitive construct (a fact inside a sort meant something different than outside). The provides/fact synonymy is MEASURED (probe q5, recorded in 058-implementation.md §6: a witness spelled provides loads and runs identically). Deprecation warning at the loader's provision-lowering site, which already distinguishes a provision fact from a plain one; then migrate: the stdlib binding fact-rows (rustland/anthill-stl/anthill/*.anthill), the wi84x fixtures, examples, and the 038 provides-block idiom. OUT OF SCOPE: namespace-level op-binding instance facts (outside sorts, quarantined by the nameability gate); the per-clause provides-with-body form (recorded, not required — lands with or after WI-857's one-chain settlement if wanted). ACCEPTANCE: default provides desugars to a row one_default sees — a collision with an explicit DefaultProvider fact is refused naming both; a provision-fact warns (with the flag-day refusal staged behind the migration); the full tree migrated; binding-free stdlib load clean; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. REFERENCE: 058 §3.6, §4; design/058-implementation.md §6."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-860"]), status: Open)
