fact WorkItem(id: "WI-845", description: some(value: "Proposal 058 (WI-648) PHASE 5 — SPEC: amend docs/kernel-language.md §Instance coherence to the ladder actually implemented, and document the named requirement slot. KEPT AS ITS OWN TICKET DELIBERATELY: folding a doc change into the phase that implements it is how spec drift happens, and §3 of this proposal exists BECAUSE the spec drifted — three texts describe three different rules and none matches the code (kernel-language.md:2109 / :2111 promise PER-SCOPE selection, \"the per-`import` choice\"; WI-648's own framing says global default + named opt-in; the implementation is GLOBAL, unconditional, refused at LOAD). The ruling (§3): the per-`import` text is not merely unimplemented, it CANNOT EXPRESS EITHER DRIVER — the deciding argument needs both monoids in ONE body (`fold[Monoid = AddM](xs)` beside `fold[Monoid = MulM](ys)`) and per-`import` selection admits at most one provider per scope; `SortedSet.new(cmp)` chooses per CONSTRUCTION SITE, and two differently-ordered sets routinely coexist in one function. So it is the TEXT that must change, and the amendment NARROWS a promise the code never kept, so no loading program changes meaning. WORK: (a) replace the §Instance coherence paragraph with §10's text — at most one DEFAULT provider per carrier; a second is permitted, but only when EVERY candidate can be NAMED, and every dispatch must then say WHICH by binding the requirement slot at the call; an unselected dispatch with two candidates is an error naming both; selection is EXPLICIT and PER-CALL, not per-scope; implicit scope-directed selection (a nearer provider silently winning) is deliberately NOT the rule. (b) Replace the sentence at :2109 (\"A consumer chooses which instantiation to use via `import`\") — an import governs VISIBILITY, and among visible providers the CALL selects. (c) DROP, do not reword, the retained clause \"resolved in that sort's scope and captured when its instance is constructed, so `Spec[carrier]` behaves consistently within any one instance\": under §4.7 an ANONYMOUS slot records nothing, and every embedded requirement in the stdlib today is anonymous (`requires Eq[T = K]`, map.anthill:7; `requires Eq[T]`, set.anthill:10) — keeping it would repeat in the REPLACEMENT exactly what §3 charges the current text with. State instead the named-vs-anonymous rule: a NAMED slot is a type parameter, so the chosen provider is part of the sort's type identity and every value of that type carries it; an ANONYMOUS slot is a constraint — solved, not recorded — so it fixes nothing about the type and is re-answered at each dispatch, which is why two `Map[K = Int64]`s cannot differ by which `Eq` satisfied them. (d) Document §5.4 named requirement slots and the §4.7 named-vs-anonymous rule. (e) Split kernel-language.md:909's description of operation-level `requires` in TWO — a VALUE precondition (`requires neq(b, 0)`, WI-539) and a TYPE precondition (`requires Ord[T]`, WI-448); the implementation already draws that line, the call-site contract check filtering to the value goals via `is_value_precondition_clause` and leaving the type ones to dispatch. ACCEPTANCE (§9 phase 5): spec and implementation AGREE, which is what §3 says they do not today; every claim in the new text is either measured or cites the code that enforces it; the proposal's Status line is updated to Accepted/Landed. REFERENCE: docs/proposals/058-modular-instances.md §3, §10, §4.7, §9 phase 5."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-843", "WI-861"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Open)
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-845", author: "user", content: "RE-SCOPED by the 2026-07-30 third review, two ways. (1) The proposal was RESTRUCTURED (rules+syntax only; implementation details in docs/design/058-implementation.md, which carries a reference map for this ticket's now-stale section numbers): old §10 → proposal §8; old §4.7 → §3.4; old §5.4 (named slots doc item) → §3.4/§4; the WORK items (a)–(e) stand, but read the amendment text from proposal §8 AS IT NOW IS. (2) NEW DEPENDENCY WI-861 (rung 2a), added deliberately: proposal §8's amendment text now contains the DefaultProvider sentence — the default is the carrier's own provision or the one a DefaultProvider fact names, at most one per carrier, and an unselected dispatch TAKES the default among tied most-specific candidates. Writing that into kernel-language.md before rung 2a exists would repeat the exact sin this ticket's own §3 indicts the per-import text for: a spec promising what the implementation does not do. So this ticket lands LAST in the modinst sequence — after WI-857 → WI-858 (library) and WI-859 → WI-860 → WI-861 (defaults) — when every sentence of §8 is either delivered or cites the check that enforces it. WI-862 (surface consolidation) is NOT a dependency: the amendment text does not mention the sugar or the fact-retirement; if WI-862 lands first, add its one sentence, else leave it to a later amendment.", at: "2026-07-30T11:38:24Z")
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-857", name: "modinst")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-857", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED 2026-07-30. SETTLEMENT: one layout, one owner — `dict_layout` (kb/typing.rs), stated in design/operation-call-model.md §'Dispatch rule' and CLAUDE.md. A dictionary for spec S supplied by provider P bundles S's own direct requires chain THEN P's; P == S is ONE list (which is also the parent-bundle dict, whose functor IS the frame owner, so `build_dispatching_dict_from_chain` is unchanged). The SPEC half is the PREFIX so `requirement_at_sort`'s projection path — whose k indexes the required spec's own chain — needs no offset and no knowledge of the provider. `expand_dispatching_dict` takes a new `dispatched_from` (the classification's pre-existing `spec_op_sym`) and hands the frame exactly the half `impl_parent_of_op(target)` owns; when that is a THIRD sort (an inherited same-short-name default, a WI-431 binding elsewhere) it gets no slots, loud if it declares any. THREE THINGS THE TICKET DID NOT ANTICIPATE, each forced by measurement. (1) The spec half CANNOT be a hard demand: requiring it to resolve broke 33 TESTS, because a spec-level requires is routinely satisfied only via `check_provider_requires`' abstract fallback (base-level existence) — `FiniteCollection requires Iterable[C = C]` holds for a List only through `List provides Stream provides Iterable`, which no `Iterable[C = List[…]]` provision matches. So an unresolved spec-half goal is RECORDED (`ResolvedRequiresNode::Unavailable` -> an empty bundle over an `anthill.reflect.NoProvider` marker), never dropped (would shift the provider half) and never refused; dispatching through the marker is refused at ONE owner (`resolve_op_target_checked`). Consequence for a fixture: a GROUND unprovided spec half is unreachable — the load check refuses it — so the `Unavailable` pin needs the parametric shape where the abstract fallback fires. (2) EVERY producer must build a layout-valid dict, and the ticket's SCOPE named neither of the two that were not: the host-entry STAND-INS (`seed_entry_requirements`, `call_with_requirements`' `__req_self`) were `cr(functor, [])`, which the layout reads as 'claims functor's whole chain, bundles none'. Found by wi818's value-directed rescue FAILING, not by reading. Now `stand_in_requirement`. (3) The `EffectsRuntime` kind-anchor was SKIPPED by the provider half while `build_dep_projection` kept it and the layout counts it — MEASURED 144 dictionaries across the suite disagreeing (Iterable-over-Stream alone = 140), invisible because none reached a frame push. Both halves now keep the slot as a structural leaf; `effects_runtime_sym` is its owner. A `debug_assert_eq!` at the producer now pins producer == layout; it is what caught this. LOCALITY delivered as review required (`resolve_inner`'s `local_provider`, immediately-enclosing provider only). ACCEPTANCE: all met — both Holder reproducers run (Eq->PartialEq transitively = true/false decided; Ordered.compare at Int64 = 1); the PartialEq and chain-free-witness controls still run; q4e runs on both routes (-1, and a tie-break arm = 1 that a mis-sliced half would silently pass); `SortedSet.empty[T = Int64]()` sorts genuinely (wi844 test flipped to == 3); full workspace green 3777/0. Pinned in `wi857_dictionary_layout_test.rs` (10 tests). REVIEW FOUND, all fixed: `is_effects_runtime` narrowed the qualified-NAME compare it replaced to a raw == (would re-open the same arity gap); the reflect `Dictionary.resolveOp`/`ops` faces bypassed the marker refusal, drifting from `resolve_op_target` in the exact direction its own doc forbids; the spec half still dropped an unreadable-head slot the provider half kept. OPEN / DEFERRED, in priority order: (a) `type_params_of_sort` (kb/mod.rs) is an O(|symbols|) scan called per resolved goal, and this ticket multiplies goals ~5x (the Ordered->Eq/PartialOrd->PartialEq diamond resolves PartialEq twice) — MEASURED suite 219s -> 235s; memoize it on the `sort_param_pairs_cache` pattern, and consider a per-resolve memo keyed on (SortGoal, local_provider) — local_provider MUST be in the key. (b) `Unavailable` discards the failure KIND, so a TIE reports as 'no provider'; carrying it needs the reason in the handle, i.e. an arena-slot variant (`SlotFunctor::NoProvider { spec }`), which would also give the diagnostic the bindings it claims to name. (c) the layout is COUNTED independently of what the producer produces (bridged only by the debug_assert); have `dict_sub_goals` return the layout it built. (d) `alloc_requirement` is the public host constructor and is layout-blind — `call_with_requirements` now validates each supplied dict, but a layout-aware constructor should be exposed and the doc repointed. (e) the σ short-name re-key allocates Strings per resolved goal, now on a per-dispatch path. (f) 058 §7's unmeasured composition leg (`O = ListOrd[OE = LexFst]`) is now drivable — phase 7 should drive it.", at: "2026-07-30T13:36:32Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-857", description: some(value: "A requirement DICTIONARY built from a CARRIER-KEYED provider bundles NO sub-requirements, so any spec whose OWN `requires` chain is non-empty dies at eval with an arity mismatch. MEASURED 2026-07-30 (`anthill run`, HEAD + WI-844), and reproducible with ZERO proposal-058 vocabulary — no named slot, no second provider, no bracket: `sort Holder { sort T = ?  requires Eq[T]  operation same(a: T, b: T) -> Bool = PartialEq.eq(a, b) }` called as `Holder.same(7, 7)` loads clean and dies `Internal(DeferToRequirement: projection index 0 out of range (requirement `__req_eq` bundles 0 sub-requirement(s)))`. The `Ordered` twin — `requires Ordered[T]`, body `Ordered.compare(a, b)`, called at Int64 — dies `Internal(deferred dispatch frame-push: dispatching dict for anthill.prelude.Ordered.compare has arity 0 but its requires chain has 2 entries)`. CONTROL, so the trigger is the SPEC's chain depth and not the shape: the same Holder over `PartialEq` (whose own requires chain is EMPTY) RUNS, printing `PE-false`. SECOND CONTROL, so it is the PROVIDER KIND and not the spec: with a WITNESS sort supplying the op (`sort Ascending { fact Ordered[T = Int64]  operation compare(a, b) = sub(a, b) }`) the identical program runs — because dispatch then lands on `Ascending.compare`, whose parent's requires chain is empty, so `expand_dispatching_dict` asks for 0 names and gets the 0-arity dict. ROOT CAUSE (read, not inferred): `candidate_sub_goals_owned` (kb/typing.rs) builds a resolved node's sub-goals from `direct_requires_chain(impl_sort)` — the PROVIDER's own chain. For a carrier-keyed `fact Ordered[T = Int64]` the provider is `anthill.prelude.Int64`, whose chain is empty, so `resolve` returns a `Leaf` and `emit_tree_as_projection` emits `construct_requirement(Int64, [])`. But the CONSUMER, `expand_dispatching_dict` (eval/eval.rs), names the frame slots from `synth_req_names(impl_parent_of_op(target))`, and when the target is the spec's OWN op (the builtin `Ordered.compare`, no impl member) that parent is the SPEC — whose chain is `Eq[T], PartialOrd[T]`. Producer walks the provider's chain, consumer walks the spec's; they agree only when the provider IS a witness sort. The information the producer needs already exists and is already checked: `check_provider_requires` (WI-343/WI-356) verifies that every SPEC-level `requires`, instantiated at the provision's bindings, resolves — it just is not put in the dictionary. FIX DIRECTION (settle at pickup, both faces touch the WI-239 'direct requires' arity invariant): either the producer bundles the SPEC's chain at the goal's bindings when the provider contributes none, or the consumer names from the PROVIDER when there is one. Whichever is chosen, the two owners must be made to read ONE chain — that they read two is the defect. IMPACT: today this is why `SortedSet.empty[T = Int64]()` (proposal 058 §5.3's 'omitting the binding still works' spelling) loads, resolves by tier 2, and then dies at eval — pinned in `wi844_sorted_set_driver_test::omitting_the_ordering_is_resolved_but_dies_at_eval`, which asserts the CURRENT failure and its 058-free control so this ticket has a live reproducer. Not caused by 058: it predates the whole arc and needs none of it. SCOPE: Rust — kb/typing.rs `candidate_sub_goals_owned` / `emit_tree_as_projection`, eval/eval.rs `expand_dispatching_dict` / `start_apply_deferred`. ACCEPTANCE: the two Holder reproducers above RUN; the PartialEq and witness-sort controls still run; `SortedSet.empty[T = Int64]()` with no `O` written sorts genuinely (the WI-844 test flips from asserting the failure to asserting the value 3); full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-30T13:36:42Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-864", description: some(value: "WI-857 FOLLOW-UP (perf, independent) — the instance-resolution path got ~5x hotter and one of its primitives is an O(|symbols|) SCAN. WI-857 made a dictionary bundle the SPEC's requires chain as well as the provider's, so resolve_inner now walks the spec chain too: resolving Ordered[T = Int64] went from 1 node to 5, and the prelude diamond (Ordered requires Eq + PartialOrd, both requiring PartialEq) resolves PartialEq TWICE. MEASURED end-to-end: rustland/scripts/test.sh 219s -> 235s (+7%). TWO costs, one path. (1) collect_provides_candidates calls kb.type_params_of_sort(goal.spec_sort) per goal, and that (kb/mod.rs) does a format! plus a FULL SCAN of by_qualified_name with a starts_with per entry, then clones a Vec<String> — so per-goal cost is O(|symbols|) and the goal count just went up ~5x. Memoize it on a RefCell<HashMap<Symbol, Rc<Vec<String>>>>: the sort_param_pairs_cache (kb/mod.rs, used by typing.rs) is the SAME walk already cached this way, so the pattern and its invalidation are established. This is the only cost here with a superlinear factor and it is independent of WI-857 — it was always O(|symbols|), WI-857 only amplified it. (2) dict_sub_goals builds a SmallVec<[(String, TermId); 2]> per resolved goal (resolve_sym(k).to_string()) to feed provider_requires_subgoals' short-name-keyed sigma; its only consumer, map_requires_name, compares n == kb.resolve_sym(s), so keying by Symbol and comparing at the point of use preserves the documented contract with ZERO allocation. Touches check_provider_requires' call site, so it is a signature change, not a local edit. NOTE the sigma-by-short-name keying is pre-existing documented debt (the loader does not record the cross-param binding when the names differ) — WI-857 moved it from once-per-load onto a per-dispatch path, so the debt is now hotter but not new. OPTIONAL THIRD: a per-resolve memo threaded alongside resolve_inner's stack, keyed on (SortGoal, local_provider), kills the diamond duplication exactly — local_provider MUST be in the key, because WI-857's locality rule made a sub-goal's outcome depend on it. resolve_bridge_requirements calls the UNCACHED resolve per requires slot on the per-dispatch value-directed path with a constant empty scope, so that regime is soundly cacheable. ACCEPTANCE: the memo(s) in place; suite wall-clock measured BEFORE and AFTER on the same machine and reported (the 219/235 numbers above are the baseline pair); full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-865", description: some(value: "WI-857 FOLLOW-UP — a recorded-absent dictionary slot DISCARDS THE FAILURE KIND, so a provider TIE is reported as 'no provider'. WI-857 made an unresolvable SPEC-half slot carry an empty bundle over the anthill.reflect.NoProvider marker (ResolvedRequiresNode::Unavailable), refused at any USE by resolve_op_target_checked. The variant is built for NoMatch, Ambiguous and Cyclic ALIKE — deliberately, so the placement rule has no cases to get wrong — but the marker then carries nothing to runtime, so the refusal message can only say 'nothing provides it there, or more than one does' and can never say WHICH, nor name the bindings the message claims. That is a REGRESSION IN ATTRIBUTION relative to WI-843, which went to specific trouble to FORWARD a sub-goal tie verbatim rather than restamp it (resolve_at_goal: 'the tie is FORWARDED, not restamped'): the same tie is a precise coherence diagnostic at the call's own goal and an anonymous one inside a spec half. FIX DIRECTION (settle at pickup): carry the reason to runtime. The typer side is cheap — Unavailable { spec_sort, why } holding the discriminant or the InstanceTie. The RUNTIME side is the real work and the reason this is its own ticket: RequirementArena's Slot carries only functor: Option<Symbol>, so a marker can hold no payload. An explicit enum SlotFunctor { Provider(Symbol), NoProvider { spec: Symbol, why } } would (a) make 'get the provider' FALLIBLE AT ONE ACCESSOR, which forces every read site to handle it — today that discipline is held by resolve_op_target_checked plus a hand-written check in start_apply_deferred's proj_path loop; (b) let the slot carry the spec the diagnostic needs; (c) retire the sentinel-functor encoding, so 'unnameable marker' stops depending on SymbolTable::intern and define keeping disjoint maps. NOTE the typer IR needs a functor symbol in a construct_requirement TERM regardless, so the slot enum covers only the runtime half — the term half stays a sentinel unless Expr::ConstructRequirement grows a field. ACCEPTANCE: a two-provider tie inside a spec half reports as a TIE naming both candidates, not as 'no provider'; the existing wi857_dictionary_layout_test 'reading_an_unprovided_spec_half_slot_is_loud' still passes with its no-provider wording; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-866", description: some(value: "WI-857 FOLLOW-UP — the dictionary layout is COUNTED independently of what the producer PRODUCES, and one of its inputs is derived with a silent fallback. TWO gaps in the invariant WI-857 established. (1) dict_layout(spec, provider).arity() = |direct_requires_chain(spec)| + |direct_requires_chain(provider)|, while the list itself is built by dict_sub_goals (two different functions: provider_requires_subgoals for the spec half, candidate_provider_sub_goals for the provider half). The two are related only by a 1:1 slot discipline neither signature states, bridged by a debug_assert_eq! at the producer — which is RELEASE-OFF, for an invariant whose violation is a wrong-slot read rather than a crash. It is not theoretical: that assert is exactly what caught the EffectsRuntime anchor being dropped from the provider half while the layout counted it (144 dictionaries measured). FIX: have dict_sub_goals RETURN the layout it actually built (from_halves(spec, provider, spec_goals.len(), provider_goals.len())), keeping dict_layout as the 'predict without a goal' face for expand_dispatching_dict and stand_in_requirement, documented as asserted-equal. Then the NUMBER has one source of truth, not just the ORDER. CONSIDER ALSO a producer-side check at build_construct_requirement (kb/typing.rs), through which all four term-side producers funnel — it would need the spec passed alongside the functor, and it would move enforcement off the last consumer in the chain, which is where WI-857's failure lived. (2) expand_dispatching_dict derives the layout's SPEC from impl_parent_of_op(dispatched_from).unwrap_or(provider), commented as covering a namespace-level dispatched_from. impl_parent_of_op is qn.rsplit_once('.') + try_resolve_symbol(parent), so it ALSO returns None for a dot-less top-level op QN (which _global declarations produce) and for any dotted op whose parent segment is not a registered symbol — and in those cases the layout silently degrades to the one-list reading, which is the PRE-WI-857 BUG SHAPE, rather than complaining. FIX: pass Option<Symbol> and make 'no spec' an explicitly asserted case (the caller knows whether it holds a parent-bundle dict), or debug_assert that an unresolvable parent implies dict.functor() is the frame owner. ACCEPTANCE: the layout's count comes from the producer; a synthetic divergence is caught at the producer, not at a frame push; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-867", description: some(value: "WI-857 FOLLOW-UP — the PUBLIC host-facing dictionary constructor is layout-blind, and 058 phase 7 is what turns that from latent into live. Interpreter::alloc_requirement (eval/mod.rs) is what call_with_requirements' own doc points hosts at ('Use alloc_requirement to build each handle'), and it takes (functor, subs) with no knowledge of the WI-857 layout — so a host can build a dictionary claiming a provider and bundling nothing, which is precisely the shape that dies later at a frame push, attributed to the CALLEE rather than to the caller that built it. WI-857 added a per-dict layout check inside call_with_requirements (each supplied chain dict is validated against dict_layout(chain[i].required_sort, dict.functor())), so the BOUNDARY is guarded; the CONSTRUCTOR is not, and neither is any other host path. MEASURED: the one in-tree host (rustland/anthill-todo/src/main.rs) builds alloc_requirement(impl_sym, SmallVec::new()) for each of Main's chain slots — arity-0 dictionaries for spec WorkItemStore supplied by FileBasedWorkitemStore — and is layout-valid ONLY because both chains are empty. That is the same chain-free-provider accident WI-857 documents as the reason the split went unnoticed for so long. WI-858 (058 phase 7) gives Pair/SortedSet specs real chains, so this is a near-term trap, not hypothetical. FIX DIRECTION: expose the layout-aware constructor — either make stand_in_requirement pub, or add alloc_dictionary(spec, provider, subs) that ERRORS unless subs.len() == dict_layout(spec, provider).arity() — repoint call_with_requirements' doc at it, and convert anthill-todo. ACCEPTANCE: a host that hand-builds a short dictionary is refused AT CONSTRUCTION with a message naming the spec, the provider and both halves; anthill-todo converted and its CLI still runs (anthill-todo list / show); the doc in eval/mod.rs points at the layout-aware constructor; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-868", description: some(value: "WI-857 FOLLOW-UP (design question, depends on the runtime marker payload) — TWO coexisting representations of 'this slot has no evidence', and the refusal ordering is what keeps them apart. WI-857 left two: (a) ResolvedRequiresNode::Unavailable -> an empty bundle over the anthill.reflect.NoProvider marker, which resolve_op_target_checked REFUSES to dispatch through; (b) stand_in_requirement (eval/mod.rs), the host-entry stand-in, whose sub-slots are markers but whose OWN functor is a claim that e.g. Main provides WorkItemStore — a claim call_with_requirements' own doc already calls 'wrong impl, runtime mis-dispatch'. They cannot be unified TODAY because the marker refusal sits at resolve_op_target_checked, i.e. BEFORE the WI-350/WI-822 value-directed rescue that is exactly what makes the stand-in work: a stand-in dispatches onto the spec's own op, which for a bodyless spec op resolves the impl from the RUNTIME VALUE and gets the right answer (pinned by wi818_executable_backing_test::variant_b_carrier_impl_loads_and_evaluates, which MEASURED this — it failed when the stand-in was not layout-valid). If the refusal moved to AFTER the rescue fails, ONE representation would serve both: a marker functor would mean 'this dictionary pins nothing; let the value decide, and be loud if it cannot'. THE OPEN QUESTION is whether that is sound: the rescue is sound when it finds a carrier member, but when the spec op is a BUILTIN (PartialEq.eq, Ordered.compare) falling through runs the HOST default and decides structurally — which is the silent-wrong-answer case the refusal exists to prevent. So the move probably needs to distinguish 'the spec op is builtin-backed' from 'the spec op is bodyless and value-directable', and that distinction should be measured, not assumed. DEPENDS on carrying the marker's payload to runtime (the arena-slot variant), since a merged representation must still say WHY it pinned nothing. ACCEPTANCE: either one representation with a measured account of the builtin case, or a written decision that the two stay separate, recorded where stand_in_requirement is defined."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-865"]), status: Open)
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-858", author: "user", content: "UNBLOCKED — WI-857 delivered 2026-07-30; the bundled parametric witness form now LOADS AND RUNS on both routes (its q4e reproducer is pinned in wi857_dictionary_layout_test::a_parametric_chain_bearing_witness_threads_both_halves, -1 plus a tie-break arm = 1). TWO THINGS THIS PHASE INHERITS. (1) DRIVE THE UNMEASURED COMPOSITION LEG: 058 §7 records that binding a witness's own NAMED slot inside a bracket value (O = ListOrd[OE = LexFst], proposal §5) was never measured — the pre-restructure §4.5 called it 'the composition of two measured legs' and the q4 series drove only the INFERRED form (O = LexFst, params bound by unification). Eval is restored, so it is drivable now; this phase is the natural place, since PairByFst/PairBySnd over a parameterized element is exactly that shape. (2) THE LOCALITY RULE IS WHAT MAKES TWO COEXISTING BUNDLES WORK, and this phase is its first REAL exercise: once the producer bundles the spec's chain, PartialOrd[Pair] has one candidate inside EACH of the two witnesses' chains and a global search TIES — WI-857 resolves it witness-locally (resolve_inner's local_provider, immediately-enclosing provider only). Its pin today is a synthetic two-witness fixture; the prelude Pair pair is the shipped one. If a bracket-less compare on Pair does NOT reach the tier-3 diagnostic naming both witnesses, suspect locality having been applied too widely (it must key on the SELECTED provider, never on caller scope) before suspecting the classifier.", at: "2026-07-30T14:11:43Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-857", author: "user", content: "CODE REVIEW (/code-review, post-delivery) FOUND ONE REAL REGRESSION AND FOUR REAL GAPS — all fixed, suite 3778/0. (1) REGRESSION, the one that mattered: an OpRef minted by Dictionary.resolveOp carries op = the RESOLVED impl member while its dict witnesses the SPEC, so the OpRef apply path (which passed (op, op) to expand_dispatching_dict) measured a spec-instance dictionary against the PROVIDER's chain — for a chain-free witness that is 0, so a valid arity-2 Ordered dict was rejected 'wants 0 slot(s)'. Pre-WI-857 it matched by accident (dicts bundled only the provider's chain). FIX: Value::OpRef gained a  field — the op the call NAMED, None for an eta (whose captured dict is its own parent's bundle, so spec == provider). The compiler found all four construction/match sites. Pinned by wi577_dictionary_test::resolve_op_remembers_the_named_spec_op. LESSON: I checked the reflect faces for the marker guard and NOT for the layout — the same file, one line apart. (2) Dictionary.ops' per-element marker refusal could never fire, because a marker's sort_ops_for_impl is EMPTY: a no-provider dictionary answered  = 'this dictionary has no operations'. MY OWN COMMENT SAID SO ('the same silence, one level quieter') and I shipped it anyway — refusal moved up front. (3) reduce_requirement_at_sort_node / eval_requirement_chain_node called RequirementHandle::project with NO bounds check, so projecting into a marker (arity 0) hit the arena's panic! instead of an EvalError; only start_apply_deferred's proj_path loop had the guard, and markers made it newly reachable. FIX: one owner, project_requirement, used by all three. (4) All three refusals were EvalError::Internal, which the SLD bridge and simp_rewrite debug_assert AGAINST — resolve.rs says so explicitly and records that AmbiguousRequirement (WI-855) and AmbiguousSpecOpDispatch (WI-842) got their own variants for exactly this. A bridged rule dispatching through a marker would have ABORTED the test binary instead of delaying. FIX: EvalError::UnpinnedRequirement, the third member of that family. (5) The bindings-free SortGoal I pushed for an unreadable  head is RESOLVABLE, so it could raise a NEW load error in check_provider_requires (which shares the walk) and hard-fail a dispatch in the provider half — my comment justified it as 'harmless, that check does not index', which is the wrong reason (resolving, not indexing). MEASURED the branch ZERO times across the suite; kept the slot for positional exactness and made the guess a debug_assert!(false) so it is loud if it ever becomes reachable. PLUS six comments of mine that had gone FALSE and were corrected — the worst: inserting resolve_op_target_checked between resolve_op_target's doc and its body silently TRANSFERRED the whole doc block, leaving the pub API undocumented and the new fn carrying two unrelated contracts. Also: resolve_bridge_requirements' doc still asserted the EffectsRuntime skip this ticket removed; seed_entry_requirements' doc still described the empty stand-in the same commit made invalid; the FieldSymbols doc claimed ONE identity channel for the marker while the hot path still compares by name; the marker's 'nothing can declare it' claim is true for the wrong reason (intern vs define maps are disjoint; the name is NOT reserved); and the apply_within comment stated a -slot convention that has TWO producers with opposite conventions (safe only because that arm is rebuild-only today — now stated conditionally). Review findings already covered by filed follow-ups: the discarded failure kind (WI-865), the String allocs (WI-864), and the debug-only producer/layout assert (WI-866).", at: "2026-07-30T14:43:31Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-857", author: "user", content: "CORRECTION to the code-review entry above: three backtick-quoted fragments were eaten by shell command substitution when it was written (a footgun: pass feedback text in SINGLE quotes, or the shell evaluates anything in backticks). The three: (1) \"Value::OpRef gained a  field\" should read \"gained a named: Option<Symbol> field\"; (2) \"a no-provider dictionary answered  = this dictionary has no operations\" should read \"answered nil =\"; (3) \"the apply_within comment stated a -slot convention\" should read \"stated a fn-slot convention\". Nothing else was lost.", at: "2026-07-30T14:44:04Z")
