fact WorkItem(id: "WI-648", description: some(value: "Modular typeclasses -- scoped / named (NON-CANONICAL) instances. Today Anthill enforces GLOBAL instance coherence: exactly one provider per (spec, carrier); load.rs rejects an \"ambiguous witness\" (two providers). Some use cases need a NON-CANONICAL instance chosen AT A USE SITE rather than the carrier's default -- the standing example is SortedSet / sorted Map ordered by a CHOSEN comparator rather than the carrier's default Ord (Scala's `SortedSet(...)(Ordering)`, Haskell's newtype-per-order). (Proposal library-004's TotalFloat newtype is the coherence-preserving workaround for the Float case; a scoped-instance mechanism would let you pick an ordering/equality without a wrapper.)\n\nSCOPE (design first, then implement): a mechanism to (a) DECLARE named / local instances (a named `provides`, or a first-class witness value -- WI-431 instance facts are the substrate), (b) SELECT one at a use site (`SortedSet.new(cmp)` / a `using`/`given`-scoped instance in a lexical region) while KEEPING global coherence as the DEFAULT, and (c) THREAD the selected witness through dispatch via the requirement-dictionary path (WI-300 / design/requirement-dictionaries.md) rather than the canonical provides-lookup. The hard part is the COHERENCE STORY: does a local instance override the global one? how is ambiguity ruled out? (Scala `given` priority / Haskell orphan-instance hazards are the cautionary prior art.)\n\nDRIVER: SortedSet (needs a chosen Ord to be useful). RELATED: library proposal 004 (the canonical PartialEq/Eq/PartialOrd/Ord split -- this is its deliberately-DEFERRED modular extension; library-004 is built on canonical instances so it neither needs nor forecloses this), WI-300 (requirement dictionaries -- the witness-threading substrate), WI-431 (instance facts -- op-valued provisions, a candidate for first-class witnesses), WI-616 (semantic-eq dispatch).\n\nNON-TRIVIAL -- start with a DESIGN PROPOSAL (a new docs/proposals/ entry), not code. ACCEPTANCE (design phase): a proposal covering declare / select / thread + the coherence story, with SortedSet as the worked example and the interaction with library-004's canonical instances spelled out. Rust + scaland."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-836", "WI-837", "WI-838", "WI-839", "WI-840", "WI-841", "WI-842", "WI-843", "WI-844", "WI-845", "WI-858", "WI-859", "WI-860", "WI-861", "WI-862", "WI-869", "WI-870", "WI-871"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-872", description: some(value: "A PROVISION'S CARRIER BINDING IS MATCHED BY SHORT NAME AT DISPATCH, so a LOCAL sort that merely SHARES a provider's short name is offered that provider's impl and then refused. Against the repo's standing direction (WI-672: same_symbol DELETED; 'identity is by resolved symbol, never by last segment', spec §8.6) and against CLAUDE.md's no-short-name-comparison rule. PRE-EXISTING, MEASURED at WI-858's HEAD in two independent instances, neither involving the other: (1) `namespace p; sort Set { entity mkset(a: Int64, b: Int64) } operation f() -> Bool = eq(mkset(a:1,b:2), mkset(a:1,b:2))` — error 'type mismatch in anthill.prelude.PartialEq.eq.dispatch: expected matching impl for per-call bindings, got no impl matches', the prelude `Set`'s `provides PartialEq[T = Set]` having been offered for a carrier that is a DIFFERENT sort with the same last segment; (2) the identical program with `sort Pair` after WI-858 gave `anthill.prelude.Pair` a `PartialEq` provision. CONTROL, same file, same load: an identically-shaped `sort Duple` — a short name nothing provides for — loads and evaluates clean, so the refusal is attributable to the NAME and not to the shape or to composite equality. HOW IT SURFACED: WI-858 broke wi664_composite_eq_test, whose fixture happened to name a two-Int64 composite `Pair`; the fixture was renamed to `Duple` with the reason recorded in place, which is a workaround and not the fix. IMPACT: every short name the prelude provides an instance for (`Set`, `Map`, `List`, and now `Pair`) is effectively RESERVED against a user sort with an entity — a name-shadowing rule nobody declared, reported as an impl mismatch rather than as a collision. TWO THINGS TO SETTLE, in order: (a) WHERE the conflation is — a carrier compared by last segment somewhere on the provides-candidate path rather than by canonical symbol (`same_sort_canonical` is the correct comparator and has one owner; find the site that does not use it, by DRIVING the two reproducers above under a probe rather than by reading); (b) the DIAGNOSTIC — 'no impl matches' names neither the candidate that was offered nor the carrier it was offered for, which is why this read as a composite-equality failure for as long as it did. ACCEPTANCE: both reproducers load and evaluate; the `Duple` rename in wi664_composite_eq_test is REVERTED to `Pair` with its note deleted (that reversion IS the acceptance); a test pins a local sort per colliding prelude short name; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. REFERENCE: CLAUDE.md 'no short-name comparison'; WI-672; docs/kernel-language.md §8.6."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-873", description: some(value: "`dispatch_origin` KEEPS ONLY ONE DEFER REWRITE PER SPEC OP FOR THE WHOLE IMAGE — a second sort whose `requires` chain names that spec EVICTS the first, silently. MEASURED at WI-858's HEAD, by loading the same stdlib beside two different fixtures and listing `kb.dispatch_origin_iter()` in full: (a) with fixture `sort Wi227Flat { sort T = ?  requires PartialEq[T]  operation use_eq(a: T, b: T) -> Bool = eq(a, b) }` the surviving `anthill.prelude.PartialEq.eq` entry is `var_ref(name = __req_partialeq_14325)` — `anthill.prelude.Pair`'s, whose chain names `PartialEq` twice — and the FIXTURE'S OWN rewrite is ABSENT; (b) with the near-identical `Wi222Box` fixture the surviving entry is the fixture's `__req_partialeq` and Pair's is absent, while in that same load the `Ordered.compare` entry is a `Pair` ordering's `__req_ordered_14331`. TOTAL entries were 11 in every configuration — the same count with and without a fixture that indisputably adds a rewrite, which is the measurement that says entries are LOST rather than merely ordered. WHY IT WAS INVISIBLE UNTIL NOW: the recorded terms carry `args = nil()` — the argument list is EMPTY in the stored rewrite — so the only distinguishing content is the requirement-param name, and before WI-858 no sort in the tree repeated a spec in its chain, so `synth_req_names` never produced a disambiguated `__req_<spec>_<n>` and every sort's rewrite for one spec was the SAME term. Two sorts sharing one entry was indistinguishable from two sorts sharing one shape. THE MECHANISM IS NOT YET IDENTIFIED and must be DRIVEN, not read: the map is `HashMap<TermId, Symbol>` (`kb/mod.rs`, `dispatch_origin.insert(rewritten_apply, spec_op_sym)`) and two rewrites differing in the var_ref name are structurally different, hence hash-cons to different TermIds, hence should BOTH be present — so either the insert happens before the requirements sub-term is filled in (one placeholder TermId reused, last writer wins) or a later pass re-allocates over the entry. Find which by instrumenting the insert site, not by inspection. CONSUMER IMPACT: three tests (wi222_defer_rewrite_test x2, wi227_projection_search_test x1) select 'the rewrite for this spec' from this map and assert the requirement-param name, so they were asserting whichever sort's rewrite happened to survive; they now assert the SPEC of the name via common::assert_req_param_spec, which tolerates the disambiguating suffix, and that helper's doc carries this measurement. Whether any NON-test consumer reads the map for anything but diagnostics is part (a) of the work — if one does, a lost entry is a lost rewrite. ACCEPTANCE: every defer rewrite in a load is present in the map (assert the count grows when a fixture adds a sort with a fresh chain); the three tests above go back to asserting the EXACT synthesized name and assert_req_param_spec is deleted (that reversion IS the acceptance); full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. REFERENCE: docs/design/operation-call-model.md 'Names model'; rustland/anthill-core/src/kb/mod.rs dispatch_origin; rustland/anthill-core/tests/common/mod.rs assert_req_param_spec."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-873", author: "claude", content: "ACCEPTANCE NEEDS ONE MORE CLAUSE, found in review of WI-858. 'The three tests go back to asserting the EXACT synthesized name' is not sufficient and would be FLAKY: `dispatch_origin` is a HashMap, so the moment it correctly holds more than one rewrite per spec op, the tests' current selection — 'take the first (or last) entry for this spec' — becomes nondeterministic and an exact-name assertion fails at random. Fixing the eviction therefore BREAKS those tests unless their SELECTION is fixed in the same change. So the acceptance must also require each test to scope its selection to its OWN fixture's rewrite. NOTE, measured: no per-fixture handle exists today — the stored rewrite carries `args = nil()`, an EMPTY argument list, so nothing in the term names the fixture's binders (a marker search over the term was tried at WI-858 and found nothing). Whatever makes the entries distinguishable is part of this ticket: either the rewrite must retain its args, or `dispatch_origin` must be re-keyed by something that identifies the enclosing operation. Also note there are FOUR call sites, not three: wi222_defer_rewrite_test x3 (deferred_call_rewrites_to_apply_within_with_spec_op_fn, requirement_name_tracks_requires_chain_entry, dispatching_dict_is_caller_direct_requirement_var_ref) and wi227_projection_search_test x1. The fourth was found only by the FULL-workspace run — a `--test wi_tests` run had it passing, because which entry survives varies with interning order, so a per-binary green is not evidence for this class.", at: "2026-07-30T16:06:59Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-858", description: some(value: "Proposal 058 (WI-648) PHASE 7 — the SHIPPED-LIBRARY driver: two lawful orderings of one carrier in the prelude (the WI-844 instruction phase 4 measured impossible for String: the Rust binding's own fact Ordered[T = String] makes any third String provider break every downstream bracket-less compare — brainstorm prelude-multiple-orderings.md). CARRIER: Pair. WORK: (a) Pair gains componentwise provides PartialEq[T = Pair] / provides Eq[T = Pair] in stdlib/anthill/prelude/pair.anthill, conditional on Eq[A]/Eq[B] (the Set precedent — sort-level requires honored via the provides fact; the q4c probe measured this shape loading, on a local Duo stand-in). (b) Two BUNDLED witnesses — PairByFst (lex fst-then-snd) and PairBySnd (lex snd-then-fst) — each providing its OWN PartialOrd[T = Pair[A, B]] AND Ordered[T = Pair[A, B]] with requires Ordered[T = A], Ordered[T = B]: the 058 §3.8 bundle rule (ordered.anthill:44-48 couples a lone witness's compare to the carrier's inherited PartialOrd, so ANY single-floor alternative is unlawful; a compare-fst-only witness is additionally a PREORDER that drops elements — do NOT resurrect it). NO DefaultProvider row for (Ordered, Pair): no canonical pair order exists, silence stays loud (tier 3), correctly. (c) SortedSet-over-Pair driver value tests: construction-site bracket; bracket-less thread; the union-across-orderings type-error twin; the two first-elements swap when the two brackets do. ACCEPTANCE: two orderings in the shipped prelude; bracket-less Ordered.compare on Pair is the tier-3 diagnostic naming both witnesses; String behavior unchanged (control); the binding-free stdlib load stays clean (obstacle B control — the witnesses' Eq/PartialOrd legs discharge from Pair's own provisions, q4c shape). GATED ON WI-857: the bundled parametric witness form LOADS today and DIES at eval on the dictionary-chain split, both routes (probes q4d/q4e, recorded in WI-857's feedback). Full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. REFERENCE: 058 §3.8, §5; design/058-implementation.md §7, §8 phase 7."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-857"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-30T16:11:59Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-858", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED 2026-07-30. Shipped: prelude/pair.anthill carries PairByFst/PairBySnd (bundled PartialOrd+Ordered, NAMED element slots OA/OB) and Pair provides PartialEq/Eq componentwise; acceptance in wi858_pair_orderings_test (15 tests), full workspace green (3803 passed). Positional type-arg spelling throughout (Ordered[A], Pair[A, B]) — measured identical to the [T = …] form and far more readable.\n\nTHE TICKET'S PRESCRIBED CHAIN WAS WRONG AND DRIVING SHOWED IT. (a) said 'conditional on Eq[A]/Eq[B]'. Measured: with an Eq chain, Pair[A = Float, B = Int64] becomes a LOAD ERROR — Float provides NonEq and WI-835's check_use_site_requires_eq refuses a NonEq carrier at a parameter whose sort requires Eq — i.e. Pair stops being a general PRODUCT. The Set precedent the ticket cites does NOT transfer: Set/Map genuinely need Eq on keys, a pair of anything is a pair. Shipped with requires PartialEq[A]/[B] — the weakest chain the componentwise body needs, conditioning PartialEq[Pair] exactly — on the USER's call (they chose the ticket's shape first, then refined to 'unconditional PartialEq, conditional Eq to a follow-up'). Residue recorded not hidden: a sort's requires chain is SHARED by every provision, so Eq[Pair] rides the same chain and OVER-CLAIMS. That is WI-869, now in the plan and in proposal 058 §3.8/§4 as a stated rule with its own production.\n\nTHE COMPOSITION LEG (feedback item 1) IS DRIVEN AND UNWIRED — WI-870. Two arms: the value's bracket IS validated against the witness (an unknown slot name is refused, naming A, B, OA, OB) and then DISCARDED — [Ordered = PairByFst[OA = PairBySnd]], whose value provides no Ordered[Int64] at all, loads clean and computes the unbound answer (-1). A silent drop, and a defect rather than a gap because TieRepair::SubGoal PRINTS that exact spelling as the repair.\n\nTHE LOCALITY RULE (feedback item 2) HELD, first try, on the shipped pair: a bracket-less Ordered.compare on a Pair reaches the tier-3 diagnostic naming both witnesses — so PartialOrd[Pair], which has one candidate inside EACH bundle, resolved witness-locally rather than tying.\n\nTHREE PRE-EXISTING DEFECTS FOUND BY DRIVING, all invisible until a PRELUDE sort repeated a spec in its chain: WI-871 (componentwise provider's second requirement slot read from the first; four-cell matrix, pinned with a failing arm that panics WITH INSTRUCTIONS when fixed), WI-872 (a provision's carrier matched by SHORT NAME at dispatch — a local sort Set collides with the prelude's identically, so never about Pair; broke wi664_composite_eq_test, whose fixture was renamed Pair->Duple, and is now pinned in this phase's suite since the change WIDENS the reserved-name set), WI-873 (dispatch_origin keeps ONE rewrite per spec op for the whole image; four order-dependent call sites now go through common::assert_req_param_spec).\n\nTWO PROCESS LESSONS. A per-binary green is NOT evidence for order-dependent tests: --test wi_tests passed while the full-workspace run failed a fourth wi222 site, because which dispatch_origin entry survives varies with interning order. And a review subagent ran a git checkout mid-review and REVERTED pair.anthill to HEAD — the whole delivery's central file — recovered by rewriting it and confirming by measurement (2312 facts, matching exactly).", at: "2026-07-30T16:12:22Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-858", author: "claude", content: "REVISED 2026-07-30, SAME DAY, ON REVIEW — the shipped shape is ONE canonical ordering, not two witnesses. The delivered form (PairByFst + PairBySnd in the prelude) satisfied this ticket's LETTER and worked against its PURPOSE. Two things drove the revision, both raised by the user:\n\n(1) OBSTACLE A BITES Pair TOO, which the brainstorm's Options table missed. It reasoned that a Pair witness pair costs nothing because Pair self-provides nothing — true when written, but obstacle B REQUIRES Pair to gain Eq before any prelude ordering of it is admissible, and once Pair is a provider, shipping two rivals hands every downstream bracket-less pair compare the same tier-3 error that rules String out. A standard library may not impose that on consumers who never opted in.\n\n(2) A PAIR HAS A CANONICAL ORDER. Lexicographic fst-then-snd is what Haskell (Ord (a,b)), Rust (Ord for (A,B)), Python tuples and std::pair all give it. 'No canonical pair order exists' — which the delivered pair.anthill asserted — is false against that precedent, and it was the load-bearing premise for shipping two co-equal witnesses and for declining a DefaultProvider row.\n\nSHIPPED NOW: Pair provides PartialOrd/Ordered ITSELF, lexicographic fst-then-snd, beside componentwise PartialEq/Eq. A bracket-less Ordered.compare on a Pair ANSWERS (tier 2) and SortedSet.empty[T = Pair[Int64, Int64]]() needs no ordering bracket — MEASURED: -1 / 1 on tie / 0, and (1,9)(2,1) through a bracket-free SortedSet pipeline. Nothing ordered a Pair before, so this is a capability ADDED, not a choice imposed. An ALTERNATIVE ordering is the PROGRAM's to declare; wi858_pair_orderings_test declares BySnd and ByFstDesc locally and selects them by name, which is 058's coexistence exercised the way a real consumer meets it.\n\nNEW MEASURED LIMIT, pinned: while a rival IS declared, the canonical order is UNREACHABLE through a requires slot. The bare goal is ambiguous, and the repair that error suggests is itself refused — Pair is a CONCRETE provider, so §3.5 check 3 rejects an explicit [Ordered = Pair] (the value decides). That pincer is exactly what rung 2a removes via §3.6's inference rule (the carrier's own provision IS the default), with NO edit to pair.anthill. WI-861 now gates the Pair story as well as the String one; the test's first arm becomes a value assertion when 8c lands.\n\nALSO NEW: requires Ordered[A], Ordered[B] joins the shared chain, so a SECOND over-claim rides it (every Pair nominally demands ordered components — false for Pair[Term, Term], unenforced since no use-site check exists for Ordered). Same fix as the Eq over-claim: WI-869.\n\nTHE LESSON: 'the shipped library must carry two orderings' was a PROXY for 'the mechanism must be exercised outside the suite'. Proxy and goal came apart at the one carrier that has a canonical order — where carrying two imposes on every consumer what one consumer should opt into. Read a phase's instruction for its purpose before satisfying its letter.", at: "2026-07-30T16:38:36Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-861", author: "claude", content: "NOW GATES `Pair` AS WELL AS `String` — WI-858's revision made rung 2a load-bearing for a SHIPPED library type, not only for the String scenario the brainstorm framed it around. (This entry replaces an earlier one whose backtick-quoted identifiers were eaten by shell interpolation.) MEASURED at WI-858's HEAD: `anthill.prelude.Pair` provides its own canonical `Ordered` (lexicographic fst-then-snd), and a program that declares an ALTERNATIVE pair ordering can no longer reach the canonical one through a `requires` slot. Two errors form a pincer: (a) the bracket-less goal is ambiguous — 'constructing Ordered[T = Pair[A = Int64, B = Int64]] is ambiguous among providers: anthill.prelude.Pair, <the rival>'; and (b) the repair (a) suggests is REFUSED, because `Pair` is a CONCRETE provider and §3.5 check 3 rejects an explicit `[Ordered = Pair]` ('its values carry their own sort, so the dispatch is already directed by the value'). So there is no spelling that gets the canonical order back. §3.6's INFERENCE rule closes it exactly — default_provider(?S, ?C, ?C) :- self_provides(?C, ?S) makes the carrier's own provision the default, so silence takes `Pair` and the rival stays opt-in — WITH NO EDIT TO pair.anthill, which is the property worth preserving when 8c is implemented. ADD TO THE FLIP INVENTORY: wi858_pair_orderings_test::the_canonical_order_is_unreachable_beside_a_rival — its first arm asserts the ambiguity TODAY and its message says so; when 2a lands it becomes a VALUE assertion expecting the canonical (1,9)(2,1). Its second arm (the concrete-provider refusal) should NOT flip: naming a concrete provider stays refused, and 2a is what makes naming it unnecessary.", at: "2026-07-30T16:38:52Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-874", description: some(value: "Declaration-driven guarded-effect evaluation: extract a partial operations guard from its declared CONDITIONAL EFFECT and use it as the runtime guard, instead of hard-coding. A conditional effect E :- G (e.g. Int64.div ... effects { Error[DivisionByZero] :- eq(b, 0) }, int64.anthill:25) is TODAY type-level only (effect inference/refinement, WI-478); its runtime counterpart is hard-coded per-op in Rust (int_div/int_mod/int_rem check == 0 and call raise_division_by_zero — eval/builtins.rs:393/402/415; WI-066/WI-467). PROPOSAL: give a guarded effect OPERATIONAL force. At a partial-op call, read the ops signature effect row (OpSignature.effects, cached, op_info.rs), find the guarded Error effect (EffectExprNode::Guarded { label, guard }, node_occurrence.rs:1159 — guard is a resolvable Value like eq(b,0); label names the effect), substitute params->args, resolve the guard; if it holds -> EVAL raises Error[label] via raise_error_payload (label FROM the declaration, not hard-coded), RESOLVER yields no solution (SLD has no effect machinery). Both the CONDITION and the EFFECT-TO-RAISE come from the declaration; a new partial op (sqrt(x) :- lt(x,0)) inherits the behavior with no evaluator code, retiring the hard-code/declaration divergence risk. FIRST CONSUMER: div/mod/rem/divExact — migrate the eval hard-codes AND the WI-863 case-B resolver div/mod onto this path. EDGES: (1) type-level->runtime is a real semantic addition, commit deliberately; (2) AUDIT every op with a guarded Error effect before enabling; (3) multi-guard ops / guards calling user ops (arithmetic guards are simple eq/neq/lt/gt = easy first case); (4) the SAME partiality is ALSO a constraint (div_nonzero_primary, int64.anthill:39) — use the EFFECT guard as canonical since it names the label; note mod effect-guard eq(b,0) differs from constraint gt(b,0); (5) per-call guard-resolution cost (cheap, guarded ops only). Machinery present: guarded-effect repr (WI-478), raise helpers (raise_error_payload/raise_division_by_zero, eval/effects.rs), op-signature accessor (op_info.rs). SCOPE: Rust — eval/builtins.rs, kb/resolve.rs, a shared guard-extract-and-resolve helper. Soft-follows WI-863 case B. ACCEPTANCE: div/mod/rem by zero raise the declared Error[DivisionByZero] (eval) / yield no solution (resolver) with NO Rust-side == 0 literal in the op builtins; a synthetic partial op with a declared guarded effect and no bespoke Rust guard exhibits the same behavior; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-875", description: some(value: "Unchecked arithmetic overflow PANICS in both the resolver and the eval interpreter; unify to a consistent non-panic policy. FOUR-WAY inconsistency, all measured (WI-863 review, 2026-07-30). RESOLVER (kb/resolve.rs builtin_arith dispatch): add/sub/mul closures are |a,b| Some(a+b) (UNCHECKED) — anthill query add(9223372036854775807, 1, ?r) PANICS in debug (attempt to add with overflow), WRAPS in release (silent-wrong); but div/mod use checked_* -> clean Failure. EVAL (eval/builtins.rs): int_mod (x.rem_euclid(*y), :394) and int_rem (x % y, :403) are UNCHECKED -> PANIC on (i64::MIN, -1) in BOTH debug AND release (integer remainder overflow traps unconditionally); but int_div (:416) uses checked_div -> clean Err(Overflow). numeric_add/sub/mul (:320/332/344) use checked_* -> Err(Overflow). So: eval add is safe but eval mod/rem are NOT; resolver add is NOT but resolver div/mod ARE. Reachable: eval mod/rem panic via anthill run on any program evaluating mod/%/rem at (i64::MIN, -1); resolver add/sub/mul panic (debug) / wrap (release) on any overflowing query. NOTE the correct answer for mod/rem at (MIN, -1) is 0 (x mod -1 = x rem -1 = 0 for every x) — representable, so it can be RETURNED. DESIGN (settle at pickup): pick ONE overflow policy across resolver + eval so NO path panics. Options: (a) checked everywhere -> a loud non-panic result (eval has EvalError::Overflow; the resolver has only Success/Delay/Failure so overflow is a Failure/no-solution or a new diagnostic); (b) for mod/rem, guard y == -1 -> 0 (the representable answer), leaving only genuine div-quotient overflow (MIN/-1) to error. WI-863 deliberately left resolver mod(MIN,-1) as no-solution and did NOT touch the eval panic — this WI owns both, plus resolver add/sub/mul overflow. SCOPE: Rust — resolve.rs builtin_arith add/sub/mul closures, eval/builtins.rs int_mod/int_rem. Distinct from WI-874 (div-by-ZERO, which has a declared Error[DivisionByZero] effect; overflow has NO declared effect). ACCEPTANCE: no arithmetic input panics the resolver or the eval interpreter — add/sub/mul overflow, and mod/rem at (i64::MIN,-1); the two engines handle the same overflow input consistently; the eval int_mod/int_rem panic reproducer no longer panics; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-876", author: "claude", content: "FIX SPACE NARROWED TO ONE OPTION, by driving each candidate. (1) GIVE THE SPEC DEFAULT BODIES — refuted. A builtin registered on a spec op beats that spec's own default body ALWAYS, not just for host scalars: MEASURED, with Ordered.max carrying a real body, the call on a Pair still died in value_compare, because the body's own gte call hit the PartialOrd.gte builtin. So the in-language derivation cannot be reached while the builtins are registered. (2) DROP THE BUILTIN REGISTRATIONS and rely on default bodies — refuted for a different reason: PartialOrd.gt/gte/lt/lte on Float are IEEE (a NaN operand answers false) and Float does NOT provide Ordered, so there is no compare for an in-language derivation to bottom out in. Removing the registrations would break the partial carrier the surface exists to serve. (3) MAKE THE BUILTINS FALL BACK TO DISPATCH when the operands are not host scalars — the remaining option, and the one with a working precedent IN THIS FILE: PartialEq.eq is already builtin-backed AND carrier-dispatching. builtin_eq calls semantic_equal, whose step 4 finds a head-carrier override (sem_eq_dispatch_target) and proves <carrier>.eq via prove_rule_predicate, with a separate branch through bridge_eq_op_to_eval for an override that has a RUNNABLE BODY, plus bridge-mode Suspend handling so an applicable-but-undecidable override never masquerades as a structural answer. THAT is exactly why eq works on a Pair today and compare does not: ordered_compare goes straight to value_compare with no dispatch step. WHAT THE WORK IS: a semantic_compare mirroring semantic_equal — same head-carrier lookup, same runnable-body vs rule-backed split, same bridge-mode discipline — with ordered_compare/gt/gte/lt/lte routed through it and the scalar path kept as the fast path. NOT a small edit, and it lands in a hot shared path (gt is called everywhere), which is why WI-858 shipped the per-carrier members instead. NOTE the asymmetry this explains: the reason eq needed no member on Pair while compare/gt/max all did is not a property of Pair — it is that ONE of the two builtin families learned to dispatch and the other did not.", at: "2026-07-30T16:59:53Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-876", author: "claude", content: "CORRECTION — the previous entry's fix space was wrong twice, and the right answer is the DICTIONARY. (a) 'Dropping the builtin registrations is refuted because Float needs IEEE gt' CONFLATED two different things: the registrations are on the SPEC OP (anthill.prelude.PartialOrd.gt), which is exactly what makes them shadow every carrier. Registering per SCALAR CARRIER instead — or bypassing them via the dictionary — keeps Float's IEEE gt and stops a structural carrier being intercepted. 'Drop the spec-op registration' is not 'have no builtin'. (b) 'Mirror semantic_equal' is the WRONG ALTITUDE. PartialEq.eq uses a value-directed head-carrier heuristic BECAUSE equality is coherent and fires from UNIFICATION — there is no call site to carry a dictionary (058 §3.7). Ordered is SELECTABLE: every dispatch has a call site and the typer already threaded the answer. Re-deriving the provider from the value's shape would recompute what the dictionary states, and would get it wrong wherever the value's head is not the selected provider (a witness ordering over a foreign carrier is exactly that case — ByLength over String). THE FIX: give builtins the frame's requirement dictionary. Frame.requirements (eval/frame.rs:180) already carries (__req_<spec>, RequirementHandle) pairs, and eval.rs:322 already reads one by name via find_requirement for every body that dispatches; the builtin ABI (fn(&mut Interpreter, &[Value])) is the only thing that does not see it. So: ordered_compare/gt/gte/lt/lte try the host-scalar path first (unchanged, still the fast path) and otherwise resolve their spec's handle from the current frame and invoke the provider's op through it. NOTE this generalizes past Ordered — any builtin registered on a SPEC op has the same hole, since a spec op is exactly the thing that can have per-carrier providers. ACCEPTANCE, restated: pair.anthill declares NO comparison operations at all (not gt/gte/lt/lte, not max/min, and not compare) and wi858_pair_orderings_test::the_whole_comparison_surface_works_on_a_pair still passes; Float's IEEE gt/lt/gte/lte are unchanged; the scalar fast path is preserved; full workspace green.", at: "2026-07-30T17:03:53Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-876", author: "claude", content: "FIX DIRECTION SUPERSEDED — the host implementation belongs in the LANGUAGE BINDING, keyed to the carrier, not registered on the spec op and not reached by teaching builtins to dispatch. The user's framing, and it is right: 'it should have defined compare in mapping'. WHY THE BUILTIN IS ON THE SPEC OP AT ALL — measured, and it is a grammar gap, not a choice: a 'provides X language rust' block admits artifact_clause, carrier_clause, namespace_map_clause, fact/rule declarations and proofs (tree-sitter-anthill/grammar.js, _provides_content) and NOTHING that maps an OPERATION to a host function. So rustland/anthill-stl/anthill/int64.anthill can assert 'fact Ordered[T = Int64]' and 'carrier { Int64: \"i64\" }' but has no way to say which host function is Int64's compare — and the registration had to go somewhere, so it went on anthill.prelude.Ordered.compare. That single registration is then the implementation for EVERY carrier that never wrote its own, which is exactly why it intercepts Pair. NOTE the check is not at fault: check_provider_operations deliberately SKIPS a host carrier (an anthill.realization.Implementation fact targeting X), on the correct grounds that the host artifact backs its ops. Int64 providing Ordered with no anthill-level compare is therefore legitimate — the implementation exists, it is in Rust. The defect is only WHERE it is keyed. WORK: add an operation-mapping clause to the binding block (the carrier_clause/namespace_map_clause pattern — one more _provides_content alternative), map compare/gt/gte/lt/lte/max/min for Int64/Float/String/BigInt there, and DELETE the spec-op registrations. Then give Ordered/PartialOrd the default BODIES that ordered.anthill today states only as laws — with the spec-op builtin gone they are no longer shadowed, and they terminate on the per-carrier builtin (gt(a,b) = gt(compare(a,b), 0), whose inner gt is on Int64). Float keeps its IEEE gt/lt/gte/lte as a per-carrier mapping, which is also more honest than a spec-op builtin that silently means IEEE for one carrier and total order for the rest. RESULT: a structural carrier needs ONLY compare — no max/min, no comparisons — and Pair gains 'provides PartialOrd/Ordered' plus that one operation. SCOPE NOTE: this is a language-surface increment (new binding clause) plus a stdlib migration, larger than the dictionary hack it replaces but correctly placed; the dictionary idea is recorded in the previous entry as the alternative that needs no grammar.", at: "2026-07-30T17:10:05Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-876", author: "claude", content: "CORRECTION TO THE PREVIOUS ENTRY — calling this a GRAMMAR gap was too narrow. The realization layer already has the vocabulary; what it lacks is an operation-to-host mapping, and the primitives' declared realization is EMPTY. MEASURED: (1) stdlib/anthill/realization/realization.anthill declares Implementation, CarrierBinding, NamespaceMapping, TypeMapping, LanguageMapping, EffectMapping, ReceiverRule — a full realization vocabulary — and OperationImpl, which despite the name marks an ANTHILL operation that has an expression body, NOT a host mapping. There is no entity mapping an operation to a host function. (2) Every primitive binding DECLARES an artifact: rustland/anthill-stl/anthill/{int64,bool,float,string,bigint}.anthill each name 'artifact \"rustland/anthill-stl/src/prelude/<x>.rs\"'. NONE OF THOSE FIVE FILES EXIST — rustland/anthill-stl/src/prelude/ contains only logical_stream.rs, meta.rs, mod.rs, stream.rs. (3) The loader reads the artifact path only to store it in the Implementation fact (kb/load.rs:15436-15515); nothing loads code from it. So the realization for the primitives is declared and empty. The REAL implementation is the hardcoded register_if_present list in eval/builtins.rs, keyed by SPEC-OP qualified name ('anthill.prelude.Ordered.compare'), which is how it ended up with no carrier key and therefore intercepting every carrier. THE WORK IS THEREFORE LARGER AND BETTER-DEFINED than 'add a clause': give the realization layer an operation-to-host mapping entity (the TypeMapping/NamespaceMapping pattern, keyed by lang + carrier + operation), a binding-block clause that produces it (the carrier_clause/namespace_map_clause pattern in _provides_content), and make the builtin registry read THOSE FACTS instead of a hardcoded spec-op list. That also removes the standing oddity that a binding's artifact path names a file that does not exist. NOTE the scale check before starting: the same hardcoded-by-spec-op-name registration serves every primitive operation (Numeric.add, String.concat, Bool.not, …), not just the ordering family, so migrating all of it at once is a large change — the ordering family is the one with a demonstrated defect and can go first, with the mechanism proved on it.", at: "2026-07-30T17:17:01Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-863", description: some(value: "CLI/resolver: the WI-754 unknown-functor guard is HEAD-ONLY and covers only the TOP-LEVEL query pattern's functor, so two adjacent silent-skip cases survive. Both measured 2026-07-29 on the WI-754 build.\n\n(A) NESTED unknown functor inside a goal-consuming builtin is NOT flagged, and `not(...)` over one silently SUCCEEDS. `anthill query -p kb 'not(nonexistent(42))'` prints `true` / `1 solution(s)` (exit 0). The head is `not` (a resolved builtin, so the WI-754 guard passes it); the unknown `nonexistent` sits at `pos_args[0]`, which the head-only `undefined_query_functor` never inspects. `step_naf` resolves the inner goal, an undefined functor yields a complete-empty search (not truncated, not floundered), and NAF reads that as \"the negand is false, so `not` holds\" — a confident `true` for a predicate that does not exist. This is WORSE than the silent-empty WI-754 fixes: it asserts a negation. (An unbound inner `not(nonexistent(?y))` correctly flounders; only the GROUND inner goal hits this.) The same blindness applies to any functor nested below the head — a conjunct of a bounded quantifier body, an argument that is itself a goal. NOTE the tension: NAF-over-undefined = false is standard closed-world Datalog, so \"fix\" here means a QUERY-MODE diagnostic (warn/refuse when a nested goal names a functor with no clause), not a change to resolution semantics — decide the altitude. FIX DIRECTION (unsettled): walk the query term's goal positions (not just the head) collecting undefined functors, gated to the CLI query path so NAF elsewhere is untouched.\n\n(B) A SURFACE OPERATOR that desugars to an unloaded functor is refused with the DESUGARED name, which misattributes the cause. `anthill query -p kb '6 / 2'` -> `error: 'div' ... does not resolve to a known functor` (exit 1); likewise `7 mod 3` -> `mod`, `2 ^ 3` -> `pow`, `a and b` -> `and`. Two causes compound: (b1) `div`/`mod`/`pow` are defined in NO loaded scope, and `and`/`or`/`neg` are stdlib-only while `anthill query -p` loads with `include_stdlib=false` (main.rs), so the operator's target functor genuinely is not present; (b2) the pratt table (parse/pratt.rs) desugars `/` to `div` BEFORE the query is checked, so the diagnostic names `div`, not the `/` the user wrote. The WI-754 refusal is not wrong (the functor really is absent, and loud beats the old silent `no solutions`), but the message sends the user to \"qualify or import\" a name they never typed. FIX DIRECTIONS (pick, unsettled): (i) load the stdlib into `anthill query` so arithmetic/boolean operators resolve — the larger, likely-correct fix, but it changes query's default KB; (ii) map a desugared operator functor back to its surface spelling in the diagnostic; (iii) leave arithmetic unloaded but special-case the message. `or` is currently accepted where `and` is refused (kernel.or is registered, Bool.and is stdlib-only) — an inconsistency (i) would also erase.\n\n(C) An undefined functor whose SHORT NAME collides with a resolver scoping marker escapes the report. `anthill query -p kb 'some_in(x)'` with `some_in` declared nowhere prints `no solutions` (exit 0), because `undefined_query_functor` exempts `forall_in`/`some_in`/`forall_impl`/`__pop_assumption` via `is_scoping_marker_name(resolve_sym(sym))` — a SHORT-name check. The exemption is deliberate and consistent: the resolver ALSO keys these forms on the short name (`bounded_quant_kind` → `resolve_sym`), so the resolver already reserves the whole `some_in` name (even a qualified `myns.some_in` is intercepted as a malformed quantifier), and reporting it while the resolver hijacks it would be incoherent. The residual is only that a user who genuinely typos one of these four names gets silent `no solutions` instead of a loud report. FIX DIRECTION (unsettled, low value): if the resolver's marker recognition were tightened (e.g. arity-checked, or the markers moved to a reserved namespace), the CLI check could tighten in lockstep — do NOT diverge the two independently.\n\nALL THREE are BOUNDARIES of the WI-754 guard, deliberately left out of its head-of-top-level-pattern scope and recorded so the silent cases are tracked, per 'loud error over silent skip'. Found by the WI-754 adversarial review rounds (2026-07-29/30). None blocks WI-754's acceptance (unknown TOP-LEVEL functor -> loud; known-empty -> no solutions), which is DELIVERED.\n\nACCEPTANCE: `anthill query -p <kb> 'not(<absent>(42))'` does not silently return `true` — it either refuses naming the nested functor or carries an explicit undecided/undefined caveat; `anthill query -p <kb> '6 / 2'` either computes (stdlib loaded) or refuses with a message naming the OPERATOR, not `div`; a known functor with no match still prints `no solutions`; anthill-cli tests drive each; full workspace green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-30T19:35:13Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-883", description: some(value: "GIVE `Ordered`/`PartialOrd` A USE-SITE PROVISION CHECK — an operation of a spec the carrier does not provide must be refused at LOAD, naming the carrier and the missing provision, not left to fail at eval as an Internal. FOUND DELIVERING WI-876, which made the case REACHABLE: before it, `Ordered.compare`/`max`/`min` and `PartialOrd.gt`/`gte`/`lt`/`lte` were eval builtins on the SPEC op, so they answered for ANY operand pair whether or not its carrier provided the spec — `Ordered.max(1.5, 2.75)` returned 2.75 even though `Float` deliberately provides `PartialOrd` and NOT `Ordered` (WI-644: an IEEE order is partial), and it answered using `total_cmp`, under which NaN ranks LARGEST — the 'same program, opposite answers by backend' `wi645_float_nan_ieee_test` exists to forbid. With the host implementations keyed per carrier, that call correctly no longer resolves. MEASURED, and the defect is the DIAGNOSTIC: it dies at eval with `Internal(\"var_ref(__req_eq) unbound in requirement position\")` — the typer could not resolve `Ordered`'s own `requires Eq[T]` at `T = Float` (`Float` provides `NonEq`) and emitted a var_ref forward rather than an error, so the failure surfaces as an interpreter-internal message naming a synthetic slot. Pinned as `eval_test::m3_float_comparison_and_max`'s second arm, deliberately asserting only `is_err()` and NOT the message, with a comment saying pinning the message would pin the defect. THE SHAPE TO COPY IS WI-835's, which built exactly this for `Eq`: a use-site check refusing a `NonEq` carrier at a parameter whose sort `requires Eq`, fired wherever the type is WRITTEN. Ordering needs the dual and does not have one — WI-877's own note records it ('no use-site check exists for Ordered, unlike Eq's') as the reason `Pair`'s `requires Ordered[A], Ordered[B]` over-claim is unenforced and therefore measured harmless. NOTE the two halves are different questions and both are open: (a) a CALL to a spec op whose receiver carrier provides no such spec (this ticket's `max(1.5, 2.75)`); (b) a TYPE written at a parameter whose sort `requires Ordered` (WI-835's shape, the `SortedSet[T = X]` case). Decide whether one check covers both. SCOPE NOTE: the general form is 'any spec', not 'Ordered' — keying a check on op identity is against the repo's typer rule (feedback-typer-no-nongeneric-code); `Ordered` is only the family with a demonstrated bad diagnostic. ACCEPTANCE: `Ordered.max(1.5, 2.75)` is a LOAD error naming `Float`, `anthill.prelude.Ordered` and the unprovided requirement; `m3_float_comparison_and_max`'s second arm is upgraded from `is_err()` to an assertion on that message; the `Float` `PartialOrd` surface (`gt`/`gte`/`lt`/`lte`, IEEE) is unchanged and still loads; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. REFERENCE: WI-835 (the `Eq` twin), WI-876 (what made this reachable), docs/design/058-implementation.md §10."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-879", description: some(value: "MAKE THE SLD BUILTIN REGISTRY FACT-DRIVEN TOO, and stop `builtin_cmp` failing SILENTLY on operands it cannot compare. The half of WI-876 that could not move. WHAT MOVED: the EVALUATOR's registry now reads `anthill.realization.OperationMapping` facts (`register_operation_mappings`, eval/builtins.rs), so a host implementation is keyed to the CARRIER that declared it. WHAT DID NOT: `KnowledgeBase::register_standard_builtins` (kb/mod.rs) is still a hardcoded `(qualified name -> BuiltinTag)` list, because it runs BEFORE `load_all` — there are no facts to read yet. WI-876 did the one thing it could without a new pass: the four comparison tags are no longer SPEC-OP-KEYED — a loop registers `{Int64,BigInt,String,Float}.{gt,lt,gte,lte}` beside `PartialOrd.*`, because those carriers now declare their own comparison members and a bare `gt(?a, 0)` in a rule inside `sort Int64` resolves to `Int64.gt`, which would otherwise have lost the resolver builtin it always had. WORK: a post-load pass that registers resolver tags from the same `operation_mappings` reader the evaluator uses (one owner for the fact shape already exists — `load::operation_mappings`), so the hardcoded loop and its carrier list go away and a NEW carrier's binding block gets the resolver behaviour without a Rust edit. Needs a decision on WHERE it runs (`resolve_builtins` already re-resolves the pre-load registrations after `scan_definitions`, which is the natural neighbour) and on what a mapping means for the RESOLVER, which has `BuiltinTag`s, not host functions — probably a second, resolver-side key column rather than reusing `host_fn`. SECOND, SEPARABLE DEFECT in the same function, and it violates the repo's no-silent-skip rule: `builtin_cmp` (kb/resolve.rs) reads NUMERIC operands only (`value_num`: Int/Big/Float) and returns `BuiltinResult::Failure` on anything else. So an SLD goal `lt(\"a\", \"b\")` does not compare strings and does not complain — it FAILS, indistinguishably from \"a >= b\". That is pre-existing and unchanged by WI-876 (it was equally true when the goal resolved to `PartialOrd.lt`), but registering `String.lt` makes the claim more visible: the tag says the resolver can compare strings and it cannot. Either teach `builtin_cmp` the carrier's comparison (the eval side already has `value_compare`, which handles `Str` and `Bool`) or make a non-comparable operand pair a LOUD error rather than a silent failure. ACCEPTANCE: the resolver's comparison tags come from the binding blocks, not a Rust list — adding `operation_map { lt: ... }` to a new carrier's binding is enough; the hardcoded carrier loop in `register_standard_builtins` is DELETED; an SLD `lt(\"a\", \"b\")` either answers correctly or raises, never silently fails; `wi876_operation_mapping_test` and the WI-863 operator-desugaring tests stay green; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. REFERENCE: docs/design/058-implementation.md §10; kb/mod.rs `register_standard_builtins`; kb/resolve.rs `builtin_cmp`; eval/builtins.rs `register_operation_mappings`."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-876"]), status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-880", description: some(value: "MIGRATE THE REMAINING SPEC-OP-KEYED HOST REGISTRATIONS to per-carrier `operation_map` bindings — the generalization WI-876 proved on the ordering family and deliberately did not attempt in one go. THE HOLE IS THE SAME AND IT IS LATENT EVERYWHERE: `eval/builtins.rs` registers `anthill.prelude.Numeric.add`/`sub`/`mul`/`neg`, `anthill.prelude.PartialEq.eq`/`neq` and friends by SPEC-OP qualified name, so ONE host implementation is the backing for every carrier that never wrote its own — including carriers whose values it cannot handle. `op_backed`'s `kb.is_builtin` leg then certifies the operation as backed for ALL of them, which is WI-876's defect A stated for a different family. It has not bitten yet only because no structural carrier provides `Numeric` — exactly the position `Ordered` was in until `anthill.prelude.Pair` became its first structural provider (WI-858), which is how the ordering defect was found. NOTE `PartialEq.eq` is the ONE that is already safe, and for an instructive reason: `builtin_eq` -> `semantic_equal` DISPATCHES to a carrier's own `eq` before deciding structurally. It should still migrate — a dispatching builtin is a workaround for the missing key, not a design — but it is not urgent, and unlike the others it cannot simply be re-keyed: equality fires from UNIFICATION, where there is no call site and no dictionary (058 §3.7), so the value-directed step has to stay. WORK, per family: declare the operations on each host carrier (`Int64.add` etc., body-less), add the `operation_map` entries to the binding blocks, add the host functions to `host_fn_by_key`'s closed registry, delete the spec-op `register_if_present` lines, and give the spec real default bodies wherever a derivation exists. SIZE CHECK BEFORE STARTING: `Numeric` is the big one — `add`/`sub`/`mul`/`neg` across `Int64`/`Float`/`BigInt`, with the overflow behaviour differing per carrier (`Int64.add` raises `Overflow`, `Float` saturates, `BigInt` cannot), which is itself an argument FOR per-carrier keying: today one function branches on the operand carrier to decide which arithmetic it is implementing. Watch the same two traps WI-876 hit: a carrier's own member must be EXECUTABLE for `carrier_override_op` (`op_is_executable` now covers host mappings, so this is already paid for), and a spec default body may only call operations the spec's own `requires` chain covers — otherwise an OP-SCOPED `requires` on the default is the only place it can live. ALSO EXPECT the WI-883 class of fallout: a spec-op call on a carrier that does not provide the spec stops resolving, which is correct and may look like a regression in tests that relied on the builtin ignoring provisions. ACCEPTANCE: no `register_if_present` in `eval/builtins.rs` names an operation of a spec that has per-carrier providers; a structural carrier providing `Numeric` gets a working surface from its own primitives with no per-carrier boilerplate beyond what the spec cannot derive; a test drives that carrier the way `wi876_operation_mapping_test` drives the ordering one; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. REFERENCE: WI-876 and docs/design/058-implementation.md §10 (the mechanism and its two surprises); CLAUDE.md's 'A HOST IMPLEMENTATION IS KEYED PER CARRIER' entry."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-876"]), status: Open)
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-876", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. The fix is the one the last correction named — an operation-to-host mapping in the binding block — and it landed as: grammar `operation_map { compare: \"ordered_compare\" }` in `_provides_content`; a flat `anthill.realization.OperationMapping(carrier, operation, host_fn, lang)` fact emitted per entry; `register_operation_mappings` (eval/builtins.rs) reading those facts instead of the hardcoded spec-op names; the four spec-op ordering registrations DELETED; `Int64`/`String`/`BigInt` declaring and mapping `compare`+`gt`/`gte`/`lt`/`lte`, `Float` declaring and mapping the four IEEE ones and NO `compare`; and `PartialOrd.gt`/`gte`/`lt`/`lte` + `Ordered.max`/`min` gaining real DEFAULT BODIES. `host_fn` is a key into a CLOSED Rust registry, not a reflected symbol, and both failure modes are loud (unknown key; `<carrier>.<operation>` undeclared). PRE-FIX MEASURED FIRST, both halves exactly as the ticket said: the load refused the structural carrier for `max`/`min`, and with those hand-written `gt(pt(2,1), pt(1,9))` died 'expected Ordered scalars of matching type, got Entity and Entity'. THREE THINGS THE TICKET DID NOT PREDICT. (1) 'EXECUTABLE' HAD TWO DEFINITIONS AND ONE OF THEM WAS A BODY. `carrier_override_op` (eval's WI-444 override resolution) filtered on `op_has_runnable_body`, so a member whose implementation is the HOST's read as ABSENT and the SPEC DEFAULT ran instead of the carrier's own host code. Not hypothetical — it broke 7 tests: `gt(nan, 1.5)` fell into `PartialOrd`'s compare-based default and died `OperationBodyMissing {Ordered.compare}` (`Float` has no `compare`), and a `String` comparison inside a witness ordering fell through into `AmbiguousSpecOpDispatch` between `String.compare` and the program's two witnesses — SIX `wi844_sorted_set_driver_test` arms, which looked like a coexistence regression and was not. Both now share ONE predicate with the load check: `op_is_executable` = body | builtin | host mapping. Verified by INVERTING it — re-narrowing the filter re-breaks exactly two of this ticket's tests. (2) THE DERIVATION'S `requires` HAD EXACTLY ONE PLACE IT COULD LIVE, and I found it by hitting the other two. `PartialOrd.gt`'s default body reads `Ordered.compare`, which `PartialOrd` does not require — `MissingRequiresForSpecOp` at load. It cannot move to `Ordered`: `sort_ops` has ONE slot per (carrier, short name) and `build_sort_ops_table` pass 2 iterates a HashMap, so a carrier providing both specs would take whichever came first — a coin flip, not a rule. And `PartialOrd` cannot `requires Ordered[T]` at SORT level: `Float` provides one and not the other. What is left is the OP-SCOPED `requires Ordered[T]` (`list.anthill`'s `member` precedent), which says the right thing — this DERIVATION needs a total order — and demands nothing of a carrier supplying its own comparisons. (3) DECLARING CARRIER OPS MOVED SLD NAME RESOLUTION. A bare `gt(?a, 0)` in a rule inside `sort Int64` now resolves to `Int64.gt`, not the spec op, so it would have lost the resolver builtin it always had; `register_standard_builtins` gained carrier-keyed tags for `{Int64,BigInt,String,Float}.{gt,lt,gte,lte}`. Still a hardcoded list — that registry runs BEFORE `load_all` and has no facts to read — but no longer spec-op-keyed. `String.padRight`'s `gt(d, 0)` (on an `Int64`) had to be qualified for the same reason. ONE DELIBERATE BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: `Ordered.max(1.5, 2.75)` no longer resolves — `Float` provides `PartialOrd`, not `Ordered` (WI-644), and the old answer came from a spec-op builtin ignoring provisions AND using `total_cmp`, under which NaN ranks LARGEST. Correct refusal, poor diagnostic (an `Internal` from the unfilled requirement slot) — `eval_test::m3_float_comparison_and_max` pins `is_err()` and NOT the message, so the defect is not pinned: WI-883. PROCESS NOTE, a footgun: a `git stash` used to run a pre-change CONTROL silently left the generated tree-sitter parser stale (build.rs regenerated it from the reverted `grammar.js`, and `src/` is gitignored so the pop did not restore it). The suite still passed on the stale parser for unrelated reasons; caught by grepping `src/grammar.json` for the new rule. Regenerate after any stash that touches `grammar.js`. TESTS: `wi876_operation_mapping_test` (9) — the one-operation acceptance, the mapping facts, per-carrier `is_host_mapped_op`, the scalar controls on all three total carriers, Float IEEE incl. four NaN arms, the own-member-beats-default arm beside a rival ordering, and both loud failure modes. Full workspace 3821 passed / 0 failed via rustland/scripts/test.sh. FOLLOW-UPS FILED: WI-883 (use-site provision check / the diagnostic), WI-879 (fact-drive the SLD registry; `builtin_cmp`'s silent failure on non-numeric operands), WI-880 (migrate `Numeric`/`PartialEq`/the rest). WI-877 is UNBLOCKED and now costs one operation.", at: "2026-07-30T18:10:51Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-877", author: "user", content: "UNBLOCKED — WI-876 delivered. The seven-or-nothing cost this ticket was created to avoid is GONE: a structural `Ordered` carrier that declares `compare` ALONE now gets `gt`/`gte`/`lt`/`lte` from `PartialOrd`'s default bodies and `max`/`min` from `Ordered`'s, and `wi876_operation_mapping_test::the_whole_comparison_surface_works_from_one_operation` drives exactly that on a local two-field carrier (all nine arms, both directions, ties included). So the WORK section stands unchanged and is now literally one operation. THREE THINGS TO RE-CHECK when picking this up, none of which existed when the ticket was written. (1) THE ELEMENT COMPARISONS INSIDE `Pair.compare` now resolve to the ELEMENT CARRIER's own member (`Int64.compare`, `String.compare`) rather than to the spec-op builtin. That is what `wi858_pair_orderings_test`'s two witnesses already exercise and they pass, but `Pair` self-providing changes who the suppliers are for the PAIR carrier — assert the interaction the ticket already flags (a rival pair ordering cannot reach the canonical one; 058 §3.5 check 3) rather than assuming it. (2) THE `requires Ordered[A]`, `requires Ordered[B]` OVER-CLAIM the ticket notes is still unenforced and still WI-869's, but WI-883 now exists to build the missing use-site check for `Ordered` — so 'measured harmless' may stop being true once it lands. Re-measure `Pair[Term, Term]` rather than trusting the earlier measurement. (3) `pair.anthill`'s HEADER has been rewritten: it no longer describes the withdrawn boilerplate as a live cost, and points here. Replace that section with what actually ships rather than editing around it. REFERENCE: docs/design/058-implementation.md §10 (what WI-876 changed and its two surprises).", at: "2026-07-30T18:11:09Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-879", author: "user", content: "SCOPE SHARPENED BY WI-876's CODE REVIEW, with three things measured that this ticket must not walk into blind. (1) THE SPEC-OP REGISTRATIONS ARE STILL LIVE, and WI-876's first draft of its own documentation got this wrong — it claimed the resolver registry was 'no longer SPEC-OP-KEYED'. FALSE, and now corrected in `kb/mod.rs` and CLAUDE.md: WI-876 ADDED the carrier entries BESIDE `PartialOrd.gt`/`lt`/`gte`/`lte` and deleted nothing, because a bare `gt(?x, 5)` written in any other namespace still resolves to the spec op. So at SLD the WI-876 defect stands unfixed — MEASURED, the rule-body goal `PartialOrd.gt(\"b\", \"a\")` yields NO SOLUTIONS while the same comparison in eval answers `true`; control `PartialOrd.gt(3, 1)` is true. That divergence between the two engines on ONE program is the sharpest statement of what this ticket is for. (2) THE 'IT RUNS BEFORE load_all' REASON IS NOT A REASON FOR THE LIST BEING HARDCODED, only for that function not reading facts. `load::build_host_op_mappings` (kb/load.rs) is a POST-LOAD pass holding `&mut KnowledgeBase`, called from `load_all`, and `KnowledgeBase::register_builtin` is a plain `&mut self` method — so the derivation site already exists and WI-876 built it. Deriving each carrier tag by MIRRORING the BuiltinTag already registered for the spec op the mapped operation overrides yields EXACTLY today's hand-written array (`Ordered.compare` has no tag, so `compare` correctly gets none) and is self-maintaining; the array is currently hand-synced with four `.anthill` files in another crate. (3) THE TRAP, and it is the reason this is not a pure deletion: WI-876 put an OP-SCOPED `requires Ordered[T]` on `PartialOrd.gt`/`gte`/`lt`/`lte` (the derivation needs a total order, and it is the only place the requirement can live — see WI-876's feedback). Its CALL-SITE half is currently MASKED by `check_one_spec_op_requirement`'s early return for a BUILTIN functor — and `PartialOrd.gt` is a builtin only because of the very registration this ticket deletes. MEASURED at HEAD: a generic operation with `requires PartialOrd[T]`, and even one with NO `requires` at all, calls `PartialOrd.gt(a, b)` and loads clean. Delete the spec-op registration and that mask goes with it, and every `PartialOrd`-only generic caller starts being asked for an `Ordered[T]` it has no reason to have. Decide what to do about that BEFORE deleting — the note is now in `ordered.anthill` beside the clause, as cost (2). ALSO CONFIRMED as this ticket's second half: `builtin_cmp`'s silent `Failure` on non-numeric operands is what makes (1) a WRONG ANSWER rather than an error.", at: "2026-07-30T18:40:06Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-880", author: "user", content: "TWO ADDITIONS FROM WI-876's CODE REVIEW, both about readers that did NOT move when the mechanism did. (a) THE LOAD CHECK'S EXEMPTION IS COARSER THAN THE MAPPING, and it is the per-carrier version of the very bug WI-876 fixed per-spec-op. `check_provider_operations` skips ANY carrier that is an `Implementation.target` WHOLESALE — i.e. 'it is a host carrier, so assume every operation is backed' — and since `emit_operation_mapping_facts` only ever runs alongside `emit_implementation_fact`, every carrier that CAN carry an `operation_map` is already skipped before the op walk. CONSEQUENCE, measured by the reviewer: a `provides Widget3 language rust` carrier with body-less, UNMAPPED, unimplemented `compare`/`eq` plus `fact Ordered[T = Widget3]` LOADS CLEAN. So `op_backed`'s new `op_is_executable` host-mapping leg is correct-by-construction but UNREACHED today, and `docs/kernel-language.md`'s sentence about it has been softened to say so rather than describe a path nothing takes. Now that backing is knowable PER OPERATION, the skip is the thing to retire: keep it only for a carrier with an `Implementation` and NO `operation_map`, or drop it once the migration is complete. (b) THREE SIBLING READERS STILL ASK THE NARROW QUESTION, and two of them degrade SILENTLY once `eq` is host-mapped — which this ticket plans. `resolve.rs`'s `sem_eq_dispatch` and `eval/builtins.rs`'s `semantic_equal` branch on `op_has_runnable_body` to choose the eval bridge over `prove_rule_predicate`; a host-mapped `eq` would take the SLD branch, find no clauses, and return Refuted — EQUAL VALUES REPORTED UNEQUAL, precisely what both functions' own comments say they exist to prevent. And the typer's three static-pin gates (`typing.rs`, the WI-237/WI-444 sites) gate on a runnable body too, so a host-mapped member is never statically pinned and always takes eval's value-directed path — harmless today because WI-876 widened `carrier_override_op`, but the static and dynamic paths now DISAGREE about what 'runnable impl' means, and one of those comments still justifies itself with 'lets the runtime resolve it via its registered builtin', a rationale WI-876 removed for this family. THE DEEPER FIX, and it should land with or before the `eq` migration: name the two questions apart — `op_is_executable` (can the interpreter INVOKE it) versus an `op_has_anthill_body` (is there a BODY to inline / unfold / eta-lift) — and make each of the ~10 readers pick one EXPLICITLY. `body_specialize.rs`'s four sites and `eq_override_backed` are correctly the body question and their comments already say so; the five above are annotated neither way.", at: "2026-07-30T18:40:26Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-876", author: "user", content: "CODE-REVIEW PASS (4 agents: reuse / simplification / efficiency / altitude). FOUR THINGS I HAD WRONG, all found by review rather than by the suite, which was green throughout. (1) A SILENT SKIP IN THE CODE THAT EXISTS TO REMOVE SILENT SKIPS. `operation_map { squish: no_such_host_function }` written UNQUOTED — which `bindings` accepts, and which `carrier { T: i64 }` in the grammar's own doc comment TEACHES — produced a fact whose `host_fn` was not a string literal, the shape reader dropped it on a `continue`, and the mapping VANISHED: loaded clean, ran clean, registered nothing, no layer ever mentioning it. REPRODUCED myself before fixing. Now refused at the PRODUCER (`emit_operation_mapping_facts`), which makes the reader's skip unreachable by construction — the WI-805/808/809 'guard at each producer' discipline. (2) THE WRONG OWNER FOR THE OTHER REFUSAL. A mapping naming an operation the carrier does not DECLARE was caught only by the runtime's registration pass, so `anthill load` and `anthill check` — the tools whose job it is — both passed it silently. Split by who can answer: 'this operation does not exist' is now a LOAD error from `build_host_op_mappings`; 'this RUNTIME has no such host_fn' stays with the runtime, which is the only thing that can answer it and which must not judge a cpp-only mapping. (3) A FALSE CLAIM I WROTE TWICE. I said the resolver registry was 'no longer SPEC-OP-KEYED'. It is not: WI-876 ADDED the carrier entries BESIDE the spec-op ones and deleted nothing (a bare `gt(?x, 5)` in another namespace still resolves to the spec op). MEASURED by the reviewer: `PartialOrd.gt(\"b\", \"a\")` as a rule-body goal yields NO SOLUTIONS while eval answers `true` — the ticket's own defect, alive in the SLD engine. Corrected in `kb/mod.rs` and CLAUDE.md and pushed into WI-879 with the measurement. Same for kernel-language.md's 'a mapping makes the operation executable for the backing check' — true by construction, UNREACHED in fact, because `check_provider_operations` skips a host carrier wholesale (WI-880). (4) MAX/MIN LEFT INCONSISTENT WITH MY OWN RATIONALE. I host-mapped `gt`/`gte`/`lt`/`lte` for the scalar fast path and then let `max`/`min` fall to the spec default — an interpreter frame plus a dictionary dispatch where the host answers in one call, four of six keyed to the host and two not. Now mapped on all three total carriers. ALSO APPLIED: the mappings are CACHED on the KB (`register_standard_builtins` re-runs for every fresh interpreter, and `run_in_bridge_interp` builds one per bridged evaluation — a fact walk there cost a named-args clone and four linear scans per mapping, per crossing); `op_is_executable` asks its two O(1) host legs before `op_has_runnable_body`, which clones a whole `OpInfoRecord` to read one `is_some()`; `is_host_mapped_op` fast-paths the empty set and the raw symbol before hashing a qualified name; `read_string_field` (duplicated in `proof_verify.rs`) became the shared `get_named_string_arg`; the two fact emitters now derive the block's carrier QN and language through ONE helper instead of maintaining agreement by comment; a doc comment I had orphaned (my items were spliced INTO `build_sort_ops_table`'s doc block) was moved back; the stale `float_pair` pointer in `resolve.rs` and the spec-op names in the moved builtins' `expect_args` labels were corrected. HOT PATH, the acceptance's 'scalar fast path preserved', now traced rather than assumed: `gt(1, 2)` is statically PinNow'd to `Int64.gt` (`PartialOrd.gt` having a body makes `lookup_spec_op_dispatch` return None, opening the WI-444 block) and hits the builtin in ONE step; the dictionary route reaches `Int64.compare` in one step likewise. Timed control on the heaviest binary: 74.6s/1890 tests at baseline vs 80.2s/1899 after — the +9 are this ticket's own tests, each a full stdlib load (~0.5s), so the residual is inside noise. FINAL: full workspace 3823 passed / 0 failed; scaland 238/239, its one failure PRE-EXISTING (`relation.anthill`, untouched here, last modified by WI-818) — scaland needed only an operation-COUNT update for Float (28 -> 32). PROCESS: two `git stash` round-trips for pre-change controls each left the generated tree-sitter parser stale (build.rs regenerates from the reverted `grammar.js`; `src/` is gitignored so the pop does not restore it). Regenerate and VERIFY by grepping `src/grammar.json` for the new rule after any stash touching `grammar.js`.", at: "2026-07-30T18:41:01Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-876", description: some(value: "THE ORDERED/PARTIALORD COMPARISON SURFACE IS SCALAR-ONLY AT EVAL, AND THE LOAD CHECK TRUSTS IT ANYWAY — two independent defects that meet on the first STRUCTURAL Ordered carrier. Found driving WI-858, which made anthill.prelude.Pair that carrier (before it, every Ordered provider was a host primitive or a witness over one, so nothing exercised this). DEFECT A — a builtin backs the load check without being able to handle the carrier. eval/builtins.rs registers PartialOrd.gt/gte/lt/lte and Ordered.compare on the SPEC ops, all routing through value_compare, which handles host SCALARS only ('expected Ordered scalars of matching type'). kb::typing::op_backed accepts kb.is_builtin(c) as backing with no check that the builtin applies to THIS carrier's values, so a concrete structural carrier providing PartialOrd passes check_provider_operations on the strength of an implementation that dies on its values. MEASURED on Pair: Ordered.compare(pair(2,1), pair(1,9)) answers 1 — because Pair.compare is a MEMBER and a carrier's own member overrides the builtin (WI-350/444) — while gt(pair(2,1), pair(1,9)), which ordered.anthill DERIVES from compare by rule and which Pair had no member for, died 'expected Ordered scalars of matching type, got Entity and Entity'. So the load was clean and the surface was half-broken. DEFECT B — the builtin SHADOWS the in-language derivation, so the derivation cannot be repaired in the language. ordered.anthill states the surface as laws (gt(a,b) <=> gt(compare(a,b),0), max(a,b) <=> ite(gte(a,b),a,b)) but a rule is not BACKING (WI-818), and MEASURED: giving Ordered.max/min real DEFAULT BODIES satisfies the load check and still fails at eval, because the body's own gte call hits the same scalar-only builtin. Removing just the Ordered.max/min registrations does not help either — the failure moves to PartialOrd.gte. INCONSISTENCY WORTH NOTING, since it is what makes the shape confusing: max/min are NOT resolver builtins (kb.is_builtin is false) so the load check DEMANDS members for them, while gt/lt/gte/lte ARE, so it demands nothing and they break silently — two halves of one surface treated oppositely. CURRENT WORKAROUND, shipped: pair.anthill spells all six members (compare + max/min + gt/gte/lt/lte), each a one-liner over compare. FIX DIRECTIONS, not yet chosen: make the ordered builtins FALL BACK to ordinary dispatch when the operands are not host scalars (most likely right — it preserves the scalar fast path); or register them per scalar carrier rather than on the spec op; or make op_backed verify a builtin's applicability to the carrier. ACCEPTANCE: a structural Ordered carrier gets a working compare/gt/gte/lt/lte/max/min with NO per-carrier members; pair.anthill's six members are DELETED and its Ordered surface still passes wi858_pair_orderings_test's full-surface arm (that deletion IS the acceptance); a load-time claim that an op is backed implies it runs for that carrier; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. REFERENCE: rustland/anthill-core/src/eval/builtins.rs (value_compare and its callers), kb/typing.rs op_backed + check_provider_operations, stdlib/anthill/prelude/ordered.anthill's derivation block, WI-818 (a rule is not backing)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-30T18:41:14Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-876", author: "user", content: "CODE-REVIEW FINDINGS WORKED (15 raised; each VERIFIED by probe before fixing, since a review is a claim like any other). FOUR PRODUCER HOLES, all reproduced first and all now refused. (a) THE TARGET WAS ONLY CHECKED TO RESOLVE, NEVER TO BE AN OPERATION — an entity constructor is a qualified `<ns>.<Sort>.<entity>` name, so `operation_map { pt: \"ordered_compare\" }` over `entity pt(x, y)` resolved, registered, and would have made the CONSTRUCTOR run a comparison, every builtin lookup consulting the builtin map FIRST. A `const` (`Float.nan`) resolves the same way. Now gated on `kind_of == SymbolKind::Operation`, LOAD error. (b) NO ARITY AGREEMENT between the declared operation and the host function — and MY OWN CONTROL TEST enshrined it, mapping a 1-argument `squish` to the 2-argument `ordered_compare` and asserting the program LOADS CLEAN; it then died `ArityMismatch` on the call, i.e. this ticket's own defect class one level down. `host_fn_by_key` now returns a `HostFn { arity, f }` and registration refuses a mismatch, naming both arities. The check is at REGISTRATION, not load, because that is the earliest point that knows both: the loader knows the operation's arity but not another language's function set. (c) `operation_map` UNDER `language anthill` was silently dropped — loads clean, runs clean, registers nothing, which is verbatim the shape this ticket removes. Now refused, naming the block. (d) A REPEATED KEY silently last-won: both facts were emitted and the runtime's HashMap insert let the second overwrite the first, so `gt` could quietly acquire IEEE semantics. Refused as SYNTAX via the existing `check_label_unique`, per WI-805/808/809's discipline — and applied to `carrier` and `namespace_map` too, which had the identical hole. ONE PREDICATE SPLIT IN TWO. `op_is_executable`'s `kb.is_builtin` leg reads the RESOLVER's BuiltinTag map, and this ticket made the two registries maximally divergent — `PartialOrd.gt` is in the resolver's and NOT the interpreter's, its eval registration having moved per carrier. So an eval-side gate trusting it would select a carrier member the interpreter cannot call and skip the spec default that would have worked. Now `op_is_interpretable` (body | host mapping) for `carrier_override_op`, `op_is_executable` (+ resolver builtin) for `op_backed`. Unreachable today — every carrier-keyed resolver tag is also mapped — but WI-880 is exactly where that coincidence ends. ALSO: the host-mapping index canonicalized while the interpreter's builtin map is a RAW lookup, so the predicate could promise a spelling the map lacked; both now key BOTH spellings, the eq-index precedent. A SECOND SILENT SKIP FOUND AND CLOSED: a MALFORMED hand-asserted `OperationMapping` fact hit a bare `continue` — the producer guard cannot see a hand-written fact, so my 'unreachable by construction' comment was false. Now a load error. NOTE the wider consequence, documented but NOT closed: a WELL-FORMED hand-asserted fact is indistinguishable from a loader-emitted one, so a program can rebind a carrier's operation without a binding block. That is a property of the whole realization vocabulary (`Implementation` is equally hand-assertable) and wants a provenance rule. TWO CLAIMS CORRECTED rather than fixed, because the code was right and the comment was not: `value_compare`'s `total_cmp` Float arm STAYS — `ordered_*` is the TOTAL family and a float-valued TOTAL carrier (the `TotalFloat` shape) needs exactly that, transitivity being unavailable from `partial_cmp`; the carrier now picks its family in a visible `operation_map` line instead of a branch buried in shared code. And the unknown-`host_fn` refusal keeps its full blast radius (it stops every interpreter, including the resolver's per-bridge one) because a binding naming a function the runtime lacks is broken for the whole program — the MESSAGE now says so, since it will often surface at a call unrelated to the mapping. SCALAND: the `operation_map` clause was missing from the Scala parser, so scaland could not read any shipped binding file. Added (grammar + IR + loader arm) with its own parse test. TEST COST: the suite did ~25 full stdlib loads where 4 do — the fresh-interpreter-per-call rule guards a TRAPPED call, and these all succeed. wi876 went 4.31s -> 1.21s. NOT DONE, and flagged: `float.anthill` still carries `import Ordered.{max}` and `rule abs(?a) <=> max(?a, neg(?a))`, both now DEAD for `Float` since `Ordered.max` is unreachable there — restating the law over `PartialOrd` is a stdlib semantics decision, not a cleanup, and is left to the user (the `int64.anthill` half, a shadowed import, was unambiguous and is gone). And the two new load errors carry no SPAN: `pb.span` is in hand, but 25 sibling `LoadError::Other` uses in the same file are equally unspanned, so a purpose-built spanned variant is a file-wide cleanup rather than this ticket's — the messages name the carrier and the block instead. FINAL: rustland 3827 passed / 0 failed; scaland 239/240, the one failure PRE-EXISTING (`relation.anthill`'s `fix[R](p:` form, untouched here).", at: "2026-07-30T19:47:12Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-881", author: "user", content: "THE SHAPE OF THIS TICKET CHANGES — the user's question ('traditionally inlined? but where is [simp]? or I forgot?') is right, and MEASURED: the marker was FORGOTTEN, not the mechanism missing. `float.anthill`'s four laws sit under a bare `-- Laws` heading with NO attribute, while `set.anthill`, `map.anthill` and `relation.anthill` all tag theirs `[simp]` with explicit looping analysis beside each one (`insert(insert(?s,?x),?y) <=> insert(insert(?s,?y),?x)` is deliberately NOT [simp] — 'would loop'). CONTROL, on a local sort so nothing in the stdlib moved: `operation twice(a: Int64) -> Int64` with `rule twice(?a) <=> add(?a, ?a)` dies `OperationBodyMissing`; the SAME rule with `[simp]` answers 42. So [simp] DOES make a body-less operation evaluable by inlining its law, and that is plainly what these four rules were written to be. THEN MEASURED PER LAW, by adding [simp] to all four in `float.anthill` and reverting immediately: (1) `neg(?a) <=> sub(0.0, ?a)` -> -1.5 WORKS (bottoms out in the `Numeric.sub` builtin); (2) `recip(?a) <=> div(1.0, ?a)` -> 0.25 WORKS (`Float.div` is registered); (3) `abs(?a) <=> max(?a, neg(?a))` -> STILL FAILS, `Internal(var_ref(__req_eq) unbound)`, because it bottoms out in `Ordered.max` which is unreachable for `Float`; (4) `tau <=> mul(2.0, pi)` -> STILL FAILS `OperationBodyMissing{Float.tau}` — [simp] did not fire AT ALL for a NULLARY head, which is a separate finding worth its own probe (`pi` is unbacked too, so this one is doubly blocked). SO THE 24 SPLIT FOUR WAYS, and only the last group needs host functions: (a) TWO — `neg`, `recip` — need ONLY the forgotten `[simp]`; (b) ONE — `abs` — needs `[simp]` AND its law restated in the surface `Float` actually has (`ite(lt(?a, 0.0), neg(?a), ?a)`, since `Float` provides `PartialOrd` and not `Ordered`); (c) ONE — `tau` — needs `pi` backed AND the nullary-[simp] question answered; (d) the remaining ~20 (sqrt, floor, ceil, round, hypot, fmod, pow, the six trig, the three log, exp, pi, e, atan2) have NO law at all and genuinely need `operation_map` entries. ACCEPTANCE AMENDED: settle each of the four laws explicitly ([simp] added, restated, or deleted) rather than blanket-mapping everything to the host — a law that is meant to be the DEFINITION should say so with the marker, and one that is meant to be a specification should keep a host implementation beside it. The `-- Laws` heading in that file is doing real damage: it reads as 'these are specifications' when at least two of them are definitions.", at: "2026-07-30T19:55:09Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-882", description: some(value: "THE STDLIB'S 11 DENIAL CONSTRAINTS ARE INERT, AND `kernel-language.md` §8.4 PROMISES THEY ARE NOT — decide which way the divergence resolves, then make the stdlib say it once in a channel that fires. FOUND by a user question: 'I see constraint statement in Float. Where is constraint described in kernel-language.md?', followed by 'we have precondition in operations and can use preconditions directly. Or error effect if x < 0 (as div)'. Both halves of that turned out to be exactly right. WHAT THE SPEC SAYS. `constraint` is documented in three places and they agree with each other: §5.3 (`constraint I :- G` = integrity constraint, invariant I must hold when guard G holds — listed under Rule because it is sugar over one), §6.2 with the grammar and the desugaring (`constraint non_negative: gte(balance(?a), 0) :- balance(?a, ?b)` -> `rule non_negative: ⊥ :- balance(?a, ?b), lt(balance(?a), 0)`), and §8.4 'Constraint Enforcement': 'Constraints (denials) are checked whenever a new fact is asserted. … If the body is fully satisfied, the assertion is REJECTED.' WHAT THE KERNEL DOES. Nothing. `load_constraint` (kb/load.rs) says so in its own comment: 'Historical behavior: store an inert Constraint(head:, guard:) fact. Denial/invariant constraints are NOT registered as guards (WI-023 wires only the quantified forms; enforcing the existing denial constraints is a separate concern with its own regression surface — THE STDLIB RELIES ON THEM BEING INERT TODAY).' Only the QUANTIFIED forms (no/lone/one/some/forall + aggregations) reach `check_all_guards`. MEASURED, three ways, each loading CLEAN: (a) `constraint always_false: gt(0, 1)` — guardless and plainly false; (b) a fact-guard denial in §6.2's own documented shape with a VIOLATING fact (`constraint non_neg: gte(?v, 0) :- bal(?v)` + `fact bal(v: -5)`); (c) the stdlib's own `constraint mod_positive: gt(?b, 0) :- mod(?_, ?b)` — `mod(5, -3)` evaluates to 2 with no complaint. THE 11: int64 (in_bounds, div_nonzero_primary, div_nonzero, rem_nonzero, mod_positive), float (recip_nonzero, div_float_nonzero, sqrt_nonneg, log_positive, log10_positive, log2_positive). Every one of them is a PRECONDITION written in a channel that does not fire. THE TWO LIVE CHANNELS ALREADY EXIST and are enforced at LOAD — MEASURED: an operation-level `requires` (`operation root(a: Int64) -> Int64 requires gte(a, 0)`) refuses the call site: 'expected precondition gte provable at the call site, got unsatisfied precondition'; and a GUARDED ERROR EFFECT (`div(a, b) -> Int64 effects { Error[DivisionByZero] :- eq(b, 0) }`) propagates to the caller: `div(1, 0)` refuses with 'undeclared effect: Error[T = DivisionByZero]'. NOTE `Int64.div`/`rem`/`mod` ALREADY DECLARE BOTH — the live effect row AND the dead constraint — so the stdlib states the same precondition twice, once in a channel that works and once in one that does not, which is precisely how a reader is misled into writing the dead one (as `float.anthill` did: its six constraints have NO effect-row twin, so `sqrt(-1.0)` is unguarded by anything). THE DECISION THIS TICKET OWNS, and it is a decision, not a cleanup: EITHER (1) implement §8.4 — register denial constraints as guards and enforce them, which the loader comment warns has 'its own regression surface' and would need the 11 re-checked (`in_bounds` over every Int64 is not obviously a decidable post-load check); OR (2) accept that a denial constraint over an OPERATION APPLICATION is not the mechanism, delete the 11, express each as a `requires` precondition or a guarded `Error` effect (whichever the operation's totality calls for — `div` already shows the pattern), and CORRECT §8.4, which currently describes behaviour the kernel does not have. Option 2 is the smaller change and matches what the working code already does; option 1 is what the spec says. Either way §8.4 and the loader must stop disagreeing. NOTE a THIRD reading the spec does not document at all, and which the 11 rely on: §6.2's guard is a FACT PATTERN ('when this fact exists'), while these guards are OPERATION APPLICATIONS ('wherever this call is formed'). If option 1 is taken, that reading has to be specified — it is a different quantification over a different domain. ACCEPTANCE: the 11 are each resolved explicitly (enforced, or replaced by requires/effects and deleted); `sqrt(-1.0)`, `log(0.0)` and `recip(0.0)` are guarded by something that FIRES; §8.4 matches the implementation; a test drives one guarded operation per mechanism; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. REFERENCE: kb/load.rs `load_constraint`'s ConstraintBody::Denial arm and its comment; WI-023 (which wired the quantified forms only); WI-881 (`Float`'s unbacked operations — the same file, the same 'declared but not real' shape)."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-882", author: "user", content: "DECIDED BY THE USER: option 2 — DELETE. 'solution is delete - we don't want duplication.' So this ticket is no longer a choice between implementing §8.4 and removing the constraints; it removes them, and §8.4 is corrected to match the kernel rather than the kernel changed to match §8.4. TWO GROUPS, and they are not the same job. (a) THE FIVE IN `int64.anthill` ARE PURE DUPLICATION and delete outright: `div_nonzero_primary`, `div_nonzero`, `rem_nonzero` and `mod_positive` each restate a precondition the operation ALREADY declares as a live guarded `Error` effect (`div`/`rem`/`mod`/`divExact` all carry `effects { Error[DivisionByZero] :- eq(b, 0) }`), so deleting them loses nothing — the working channel stays. `in_bounds` (`gte(?n, minValue), lte(?n, maxValue)`) is the odd one: it is guardless, it is not a precondition of any operation, and it is not obviously checkable at all (it quantifies over every Int64 value); it should go too, but say WHY in the commit rather than lumping it with the four. (b) THE SIX IN `float.anthill` ARE NOT DUPLICATES — they are the ONLY guard those operations have, and deleting them bare would leave `sqrt(-1.0)`, `log(0.0)`, `log10`, `log2`, `recip(0.0)` and float `div` guarded by nothing. Each needs its live replacement written FIRST, in whichever channel fits: a guarded `Error` effect for the partial ones, following `Int64.div`'s exact pattern, is the closer match to what the constraint was trying to say ('this call is an error when the guard holds') than an operation-level `requires` ('the caller must prove this'), and it is what the user's own message named ('Or error effect if x < 0 (as div)'). NOTE the ordering dependency with WI-881: five of those six operations (`sqrt`, `log`, `log10`, `log2`, `recip`) have NO IMPLEMENTATION AT ALL today, so 'guard them' and 'back them' are one piece of work on the same lines of the same file — do them together or WI-881 will re-touch every one. `div` is the exception: it IS backed, so its float constraint can be replaced with an effect row immediately. AND the doc half is not optional: §8.4 currently tells a reader that denial constraints are enforced on every fact assertion. Once the stdlib stops using them, that paragraph is the only thing still claiming they work — it must say what `load_constraint` says, namely that denial/invariant constraints are stored inert and only the QUANTIFIED forms (`no`/`lone`/`one`/`some`/`forall` + aggregations) are enforced by `check_all_guards`. Whether the surface should keep admitting a form that does nothing is a separate question this ticket may raise but need not answer.", at: "2026-07-30T20:10:58Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-885", description: some(value: "`[simp]`-DEFINEDNESS IS A BACKING KIND NO PREDICATE CAN SEE — give `op_is_executable` / `op_is_interpretable` a leg for it, or mark a definitional equation apart from a normalization hint. FOUND during WI-881's review, on the change that created the first stdlib case. `op_is_executable` (kb/typing.rs) is `builtin │ host mapping │ runnable body`; `op_is_interpretable` is that minus the builtin leg. NEITHER knows about a `[simp]` equation — and MEASURED (WI-881's `a_bare_nullary_simp_head_never_fires` control), a `[simp]` equation MAKES A BODY-LESS OPERATION RUN by inlining LHS->RHS at the typer, while the same equation untagged dies `OperationBodyMissing`. So there is a fourth way an operation runs and the two predicates that answer 'can this run' both say no. `anthill.prelude.Float.recip` is the first shipped operation backed that way (WI-881: its equation `recip(?a) <=> div(1.0, ?a)` IS the definition — `f64::recip` is literally `1.0/self` — so it carries `[simp]` and has no `operation_map` entry). THE LOAD CHECK IS NOT THE EXPOSURE, and that was checked rather than assumed: `check_provider_operations` enumerates `own_ops.get(&p.spec)`, `Float` provides PartialEq/PartialOrd/NonEq/Numeric/Ring, and `recip` is declared only by `anthill.prelude.Field` — which `Float` does not provide — so `op_backed` is never asked about it, however far WI-880 narrows the host-carrier skip. THE EXPOSURE IS `carrier_override_op` (kb/typing.rs), whose doc states the exclusion as 'a member backed only by rules, WHICH THE INTERPRETER CANNOT INVOKE'. That sentence is now false for a `[simp]` rule, and the failure shape it produces is verbatim WI-876's: a `[simp]`-backed carrier member reads as ABSENT, so the spec default runs on the value-directed route while the typer's inlining answers on the static route — one call, two answers by route. Unreached today only because no spec `Float` provides names `recip`; WI-876's own review insisted the gate must not depend on that coincidence. A SECOND, DESIGN-LEVEL QUESTION THE TICKET SHOULD ANSWER: `[simp]` is proposal 043's DIRECTIONAL-REWRITING attribute ('commutative laws must stay bare or the phase loops'). Making it double as the definitional-backing switch couples two intents — an operation whose defining equation is not orientation-terminating can never be backed this way, and tagging `recip` to make it run also forces every `recip(x)` in every body to normalize to `div(1.0, x)`, which is worse symbolic output for the proof and codegen layers. Options: (a) add the leg, keyed off `simp_equation_rids`, so one predicate still answers the question; (b) a distinct marker for 'this equation is the definition'; (c) decide `[simp]` is NOT backing and host-map `Float.recip` like its 25 siblings. NOTE WI-881 ALREADY MEASURED ONE REACH LIMIT that (c) would also fix and (a) would not: a `[simp]` head is an APPLICATION, so it rewrites `tau()` and NOT a BARE `tau` call site (a `var_ref`) — which is exactly why `tau`'s equally-exact equation was NOT tagged and `tau` is host-backed instead. The reaches that were never enumerated are value-directed dispatch, `ho_apply`/`OpRef`, and any second backend. WI-884 plans the same `[simp]`-instead-of-host move for `String.isEmpty`, so this decides that one too. ACCEPTANCE: 'can this operation run' has ONE answer per route, and the route that cannot run a `[simp]`-defined operation says so rather than reading it as absent; `carrier_override_op`'s doc matches what its predicate does; a test drives a `[simp]`-defined member of a carrier that PROVIDES the spec declaring it, on both the static and the value-directed route, and gets the same answer; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. REFERENCE: WI-881 (which created the case and measured the head/call-site reach limits), WI-876 (the same absent-member defect, for host mappings), WI-818 (a rule is not backing — the claim this refines), kernel-language.md 'Equational rules'."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-880", author: "user", content: "PERF NOTE FROM WI-881'S REVIEW, on the exact registration path this ticket reworks — worth folding in rather than filing separately. `register_operation_mappings` recomputes a KB-STATIC result for EVERY fresh interpreter, including the short-lived one `run_in_bridge_interp` builds per bridged evaluation (`bridge_op_to_eval` once per SLD goal whose op must be evaluated, `try_expand_macro` once per macro fire) — where `register_standard_builtins` IS the entire startup cost, since the KB is `mem::take`n and there is no parse or load. Per crossing it does, per mapping: 3 `String` clones in the `.cloned()` snapshot (`op_qn`/`host_fn`/`lang`, of which `op_qn` and `host_fn` are read ONLY inside the two `format!` error arms), a `host_fn_by_key` str match, an arity lookup, a `canonical_sym` qualified-name hash, and 1-2 `Arc::new` in `register_builtin_sym`. WI-881 DOUBLED THE MULTIPLIER: the stdlib's mapping count went 25 -> 50 (float alone 4 -> 29), so this is now ~150 String allocations and ~50 symbol+arity lookups per bridged evaluation. WI-881 already took the cheapest piece — the arity read no longer builds a whole `OpInfoRecord` (which cloned five per-field `Vec`s to be dropped after one `.len()`); it reads the cached WI-656 signature, with the fact-scan kept as the fallback. THE REST IS STRUCTURAL AND BELONGS WITH THIS TICKET, because migrating `Numeric`/`String`/`Bool` will double the count again: memoize the VALIDATED `Vec<(Symbol, fn(&mut Interpreter, &[Value]) -> ...)>` once (a `OnceCell` beside `host_op_mappings`, invalidated by `set_host_op_mappings`), leaving per-interpreter work as N hashmap inserts. That collapses the snapshot clones, the key match and the arity check in one move. CALIBRATION, so it is not overstated: for the TEST harness (`common::interp_for`) this is noise — measured 0.46s per `interp_for`, entirely stdlib parse+load. The exposure is the bridge interpreter and it scales with bridged-call volume.", at: "2026-07-30T22:05:47Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-881", description: some(value: "BACK `anthill.prelude.Float`'s ARITHMETIC — 24 of its 32 declared operations have NO implementation and die `OperationBodyMissing` at eval, on a program that loads clean. MEASURED (each run individually): `abs(-1.5)`, `neg(1.5)`, `sqrt(4.0)`, `floor(1.7)`, `pi()` all die `OperationBodyMissing { anthill.prelude.Float.<op> }`. THE FULL UNBACKED LIST: abs, neg, recip, floor, ceil, round, sqrt, hypot, fmod, pow, sin, cos, tan, asin, acos, atan, atan2, exp, log, log10, log2, pi, e, tau. WHAT IS BACKED, and it is only 8: div, isNaN, isInfinite, isFinite (registered pre-WI-876) plus gt, gte, lt, lte (WI-876's per-carrier `operation_map`), and the three `const`s infinity/nan/negativeInfinity. So the standard library's float sort is mostly a declaration of intent. PRE-EXISTING, NOT INTRODUCED BY WI-876 — verified against the committed tree: `git show HEAD:rustland/anthill-core/src/eval/builtins.rs` registers exactly seven `anthill.prelude.Float.*` names and never `abs`/`neg`/`sqrt`/…. WI-876 is only what made it easy to SEE and easy to FIX. WHY NOBODY NOTICED — two reasons, both worth stating because each is its own defect. (1) THE LOAD CHECK EXEMPTS A HOST CARRIER WHOLESALE: `check_provider_operations` skips any carrier that is an `Implementation.target`, on the reasoning that 'the host artifact backs its ops'. `Float` is one, so a binding claiming 32 operations with 8 implementations passes. That is the per-carrier form of the very bug WI-876 fixed per-spec-op, and narrowing it is already WI-880's. (2) THE LAWS READ LIKE DEFINITIONS AND ARE NOT: `rule abs(?a) <=> max(?a, neg(?a))` and `rule neg(?a) <=> sub(0.0, ?a)` look like they define the operations, but a rule is SPECIFICATION, not backing (WI-818) — the evaluator cannot run one. This is exactly the question a reader asks ('why is abs a rule and not an operation?'): it IS an operation, declared at float.anthill:31; the rule beside it is a law, and the operation has no implementation. NOTE `abs`'s law is doubly dead now: it is stated over `max`, and `Ordered.max` is unreachable for `Float` (`Float` provides `PartialOrd`, not `Ordered` — WI-644), so `float.anthill`'s `import anthill.prelude.Ordered.{max}` names something no `Float` value can use. Either the law is restated over `PartialOrd` (`ite(lt(?a, 0.0), neg(?a), ?a)`) or it goes; that is a stdlib semantics decision this ticket should settle explicitly. THE WORK IS NOW MECHANICAL, because WI-876 built the channel: add the host functions to `host_fn_by_key`'s closed registry (`f64::abs`, `f64::sqrt`, `f64::sin`, … — Rust's `f64` has every one of them, and `f64::max`/`min` are IEEE maxNum/minNum) and name them in `rustland/anthill-stl/anthill/float.anthill`'s `operation_map`. The arity check added in WI-876's review pass will catch a mis-pairing at registration. DECIDE AS PART OF THIS: whether `Float` should also declare and map `max`/`min` — TODAY THERE IS NO WAY TO TAKE THE MAX OF TWO FLOATS AT ALL (`Ordered.max` is unreachable for `Float`; `max` is not a `PartialOrd` operation). The user's argument for the IEEE pair specifically: `f64::max`/`min` ABSORB NaN and are commutative, whereas a `gte`-derived `max` is not (MEASURED: it answers 1.0 on (nan, 1.0) and nan on (1.0, nan)) — so if the derived one would differ from IEEE `minNum`/`maxNum`, better to expose only the IEEE pair than to offer a float `min` that quietly is not the one a float programmer means. CHECK THE SIBLINGS TOO: `Int64`, `BigInt` and `String` should be audited the same way (declared vs registered) — this ticket found `Float` because a question happened to land on `abs`. ACCEPTANCE: every operation `float.anthill` declares either has a host mapping or is deleted from the sort; `abs`/`sqrt`/`floor`/`pi` and the trig/log family evaluate; the `abs` and `neg` laws are consistent with what the operations do; a test drives one operation from each family; the sibling primitive sorts are audited and any gap is filed or fixed; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. REFERENCE: WI-876 (the `operation_map` channel and its arity check), WI-880 (narrowing the host-carrier exemption that hid this), WI-818 (a rule is not backing)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-30T22:10:13Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-888", description: some(value: "THE SPEC AND THE LOADER DISAGREE ON WHETHER `=` IS AN EQUATIONAL-RULE HEAD — the spec says only `<=>` is, the loader accepts BOTH, and the stdlib is written both ways. kernel-language.md §5.3 ('Equational rules') states: 'The head connective is `<=>`, **not** `=`: `=` (`Eq.eq`) is a semantic equality *test* that never binds, whereas an equational rule head *unifies* the redex with the rule's LHS and derives the RHS.' THE IMPLEMENTATION DOES NOT MAKE THAT DISTINCTION: `is_equational_head` (kb/load.rs) classifies through `is_equality_connective_functor` (kb/mod.rs), which matches the `eq` symbol OR the `unify` symbol — i.e. both spellings, deliberately, with a WI-526 comment saying a `<=>`-spelled equation 'stays cite-required-by-default exactly like its `=`/`eq` predecessor'. MEASURED by driving all four (connective × attribute) combinations on one shape (`the_head_connective_is_not_what_enables_an_equation`, wi884_sibling_backing_test): with `[simp]`, `=` and `<=>` BOTH fire and answer 10; without it, BOTH die `OperationBodyMissing`. THE ANSWER TRACKS THE ATTRIBUTE ALONE. THE COST IS NOT HYPOTHETICAL — it already produced a wrong diagnosis in a shipped comment. WI-884 read §5.3, concluded that `Bool.ite`'s dead rule-level reduction was CAUSED by `bool.anthill` spelling its case laws with `=`, and wrote that into two files before driving refuted it; the real cause is that those laws are UNTAGGED (WI-881: `[simp]` is the enablement). The error runs the other way too: a reader who trusts §5.3 will believe an `=`-spelled `[simp]` rule is inert when it fires. THE STDLIB IS WRITTEN BOTH WAYS, so this is not dormant: `=` heads appear in bool.anthill (13, including the two `ite` cases), algebra.anthill (the Ring and VectorSpace law blocks), map.anthill, logical_stream.anthill (two `interleave` rules that read as a DEFINITION rather than a law), and reflect.anthill:89-90, which carries `[simp]` ON AN `=` HEAD — the exact combination §5.3 says cannot exist. DECIDE ONE WAY. (a) The spec is right and the loader should REFUSE (or warn on) an `=`-spelled equational head — a breaking change to every file above, needing a migration. (b) The loader is right and §5.3 should say the two connectives are INTERCHANGEABLE in a rule HEAD, with `<=>` the preferred spelling, and should state the test-vs-bind distinction only where it actually holds (guards, contracts, `ensures` — which §5.3 already lists separately). (c) They should genuinely differ, which requires naming what an `=`-headed bodyless rule IS if not an equation, and what becomes of the ones in the stdlib today. (b) is the cheap one and is probably right, but settle it by reading what `is_equality_connective_functor`'s CALLERS depend on — the resolver's eq/non-eq candidate triage, `simp_equation_rids`, and WI-139's cite-required unindexing all read it — not by preferring the cheap option. ACCEPTANCE: kernel-language.md §5.3 and the loader agree; a test pins the decision across all four (connective × attribute) combinations — WI-884's exists and asserts TODAY's behaviour, so it either stands or is inverted deliberately; if (a), the stdlib is migrated and the refusal names the file and line; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. REFERENCE: WI-884 (which was misled by §5.3 and drove the refutation), WI-881 ('[simp] is the enablement, not the direction'), WI-887 (`Bool.ite`, the operation the wrong diagnosis was about), kb/load.rs `is_equational_head`, kb/mod.rs `is_equality_connective_functor`, kernel-language.md §5.3."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-880", author: "user", content: "WI-884: YOUR ACCEPTANCE IS WORDED FOR SPEC OPS AND SO CLAIMS NEITHER HALF OF THE SPLIT THIS TICKET WIDENED. It says no `register_if_present` may name 'an operation of a SPEC that has per-carrier providers' — but `anthill.prelude.String.concat` and `anthill.prelude.Int64.abs` are CARRIER-OWNED operations of a sort, so the hardcoded qualified name already keys them per carrier and they answer correctly. No open ticket claims them, and after WI-884 one carrier's host surface is enumerated at TWO altitudes: `String` has 8 operations registered by hardcoded name (`concat`/`length`/`startsWith`/`endsWith`/`substring`/`toUpper`/`toLower`/`repeat`) and 5 through `operation_map` (`contains`/`indexOf`/`replace`/`trim`/`split`); `Int64` has 9 and 2. THE TWO HALVES ARE NOT EQUIVALENT TO THEIR READERS, which is the cost: (i) `op_is_interpretable` (kb/typing.rs) counts a host MAPPING and not a hardcoded registration, so `String.contains` reads as backed and `String.concat` does not, though one interpreter runs both — WI-876's own defect shape (a host-backed member reading as ABSENT to `carrier_override_op`), unreached today only because no spec declares `concat`, which is exactly the coincidence WI-876's review refused to rely on; (ii) `kb.host_op_mappings()` — the reader WI-886 wants a second backend to consume — sees 5 of String's 13 host operations, so 'write the binding block, get the backend' is true for the new five and false for the old eight; (iii) only the mapped half has its arity checked against the anthill declaration at registration. WI-884 deliberately did not migrate the other seventeen (out of its scope, and it would have doubled the mapping list this ticket's own feedback already asks to memoize), and left a pointer comment at the `register_if_present` block naming this ticket. SUGGESTED: widen the acceptance with one clause covering CARRIER-OWNED registrations, so the split has an owner. Also note WI-884 added 7 mappings (57 total across the four carriers), so the per-interpreter cost this ticket's feedback flags has grown again.", at: "2026-07-30T22:57:23Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-884", description: some(value: "BACK THE SIBLING PRIMITIVE SORTS' DECLARED-BUT-DEAD OPERATIONS — WI-881's sibling audit, MEASURED by driving each one from an operation body on a program that LOADS CLEAN. EIGHT operations across two sorts die `OperationBodyMissing`, in exactly the shape WI-881 found on `Float`: declared on the sort, exempted from the load-time backing check because the carrier is a host `Implementation.target` (`check_provider_operations` skips it wholesale — WI-880), and backed by nothing. THE LIST. `anthill.prelude.Int64`: `minValue()`, `maxValue()` — both unbacked, and `int64.anthill`'s own `constraint in_bounds: gte(?n, minValue), lte(?n, maxValue)` is stated OVER THEM (that constraint is separately inert and WI-882 deletes it, so the two questions are independent). `anthill.prelude.String`: `isEmpty`, `contains`, `indexOf`, `replace`, `trim`, `split` — six unbacked out of the sort's fourteen non-ordering operations; `concat`/`length`/`startsWith`/`endsWith`/`substring`/`toUpper`/`toLower`/`repeat` are registered and `padRight` has a real body. CLEAN: `anthill.prelude.BigInt` — every declared operation runs (`compare`/`gt`/`gte`/`lt`/`lte`/`max`/`min` via WI-876's `operation_map`, `to_bigint`/`to_int`/`to_float` as builtins). `Bool` was not in WI-881's audit scope and should be checked here too. THE WORK IS THE SAME MECHANICAL SHAPE WI-881 USED — add the host function to `host_fn_by_key`'s closed registry and name it in the carrier's `operation_map` — but each one carries a SEMANTICS DECISION that `Float`'s did not, so it is not a copy-paste: `indexOf` returns `-1` when absent (BYTE offset or CHAR offset? the declaration says `Int64` and nothing more, and Rust's `str::find` gives BYTES while a user reading `substring(s, start, end)` will assume the same unit as `length` — so `length`'s existing unit has to be measured first and the three made to agree); `split` must BUILD a `List[T = String]` (a host function returning a constructed list value, which none of the current `host_fn_by_key` entries do — `bigint_to_int` builds an `Option` and is the pattern to follow); `trim` must say WHICH whitespace (Rust's `str::trim` is Unicode `White_Space`, not ASCII). TWO OF THE EIGHT MAY NOT NEED HOST CODE AT ALL, and WI-881 settled the same question for `Float.recip`/`tau`: `string.anthill` already carries `rule isEmpty(?s) <=> eq(length(?s), 0)` untagged, and that IS the definition — `[simp]` makes it run with no host function (a `[simp]` equation is executable by INLINING; an untagged one is inert, see kernel-language.md 'Equational rules'). `contains` has only the partial law `contains(?s, \"\") <=> true`, which is not a definition, so it needs the host. Settle each of the six `String` laws the way WI-881 settled `Float`'s four, rather than blanket-mapping. `Int64.minValue`/`maxValue` are unambiguous (`i64::MIN`/`i64::MAX`) and are the two to do first. ACCEPTANCE: every operation `int64.anthill`, `string.anthill` and `bool.anthill` declare either runs or is deleted from the sort; a test drives each newly-backed operation; `indexOf`/`substring`/`length` agree on their index unit and the spec says which; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. REFERENCE: WI-881 (the same defect on `Float`, and the per-law settlement method), WI-876 (the `operation_map` channel), WI-880 (the host-carrier exemption that hides all of these at load)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-30T23:02:23Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-880", author: "user", content: "WI-886 FOUND A SECOND, WIDER FACE OF THE HOST-CARRIER EXEMPTION, and shipping cpp binding blocks is what made it bite: `check_provider_operations` (kb/typing.rs) builds its skip set from `anthill.realization.Implementation` facts with NO LANGUAGE FILTER, while `emit_implementation_fact` (kb/load.rs) emits one for EVERY `provides X language <L>` block. So a `provides Box language cpp` block alone puts `Box` in `host_targets` and exempts its provisions from the backing check — for the RUST build too.\n\nBefore WI-886 this was invisible because every binding block in the tree said `language rust`, so 'the carrier is host-realized' and 'THIS host realizes it' were the same sentence. They are not any more, and the ticket's own test program (`wi886_cpp_mapping_language_test.rs`, `CPP_ONLY_MEMBER`) is the first thing in the tree shaped to notice: it declares a body-less `Box.max` realized ONLY in C++ and loads clean — because of this skip, NOT because `op_is_executable`'s language-agnostic leg counted the cpp mapping. That distinction was asserted wrongly in the test's first draft and is now stated correctly there.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR THIS TICKET'S SHAPE: narrowing the exemption 'now that backing is knowable per operation' is not only about per-operation granularity — it must also decide the LANGUAGE question. A cpp mapping is a real implementation of the operation (which is why `is_host_mapped_op` is language-agnostic and `op_is_executable` reads it) and is NOT one the rust build can run (which is why WI-886 split `is_interpreter_mapped_op` out for eval, `kb/mod.rs`). Whichever way `op_backed` should answer, `check_provider_operations`' `host_targets` must agree with it, or the two legs of one check disagree about what 'host-realized' means.\n\nALSO RECORDED HERE because this ticket's acceptance is spec-op-worded and does not currently claim them: WI-886 added `anthill_core::kb::op_info::declared_arity` (pub, two tiers, the `operation_is_declared` shape) so a backend crate can ask an operation's arity without building a whole `OpInfoRecord` — `op_record` is `pub(crate)`, which is why the rust registry's documented-cheap path was unreachable from cpp-gen. Any further host-surface migration should use it.", at: "2026-07-31T05:49:07Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-891", author: "user", content: "WIDEN THE FIX: the DEFAULT is what is wrong, not just the payload. WI-886's /simplify review looked at what `synthesise_body_for` actually swallows and found that most of it is not a cpp-gen capability gap at all — these arms reach the degrade today and every one becomes `// TODO … return {};`:\n  * 'DeBruijn var in expression body — should have been opened by the loader'\n  * 'logic variable ?x appeared in an expression body'\n  * 'unresolved method call .foo reached cpp-gen'\n  * 'constructor X is neither a known entity nor a recognised collection literal'\nThose are program / invariant faults, and degrading them produces a header that compiles and answers zero. Only three arms are honestly 'not emitted yet' (HoApply / post-elaboration, `Expr::Spliced`, `Bottom`).\n\nSO THE SHAPE TO CONSIDER IS AN INVERSION: FATAL by default, degradation OPT-IN at the producers that mean it — rather than 'degrade everything, and make the payload non-compiling'. The two are not exclusive (a static_assert payload is still right for the opt-in arms), but the inversion is the one that stops the next unclassified error from silently returning zero.\n\nMECHANISM NOTE, measured against the alternative WI-886 rejected: making `lower_node` return a two-variant error is LOSSY as a drop-in. Its recursive helpers (`lower_let_chain_node`, `lower_match_branches_node`, `lower_stdlib_wrapper_node`, `lower_constructor_literal_node`) return `CppCodegenError` and re-enter `lower_node` with `?`, so a classification raised inside a `let` or a `match` arm is silently downgraded at that conversion unless every one of them changes too. WI-886 used a `Cell` on `CodegenContext` (`unrealized_refusal`, clear-then-read by the single degrading caller) precisely because it cannot lose the classification; an inversion that keeps a typed error must convert all the recursive helpers in the same commit, or it reintroduces the silent-downgrade one level down.\n\nALSO: WI-886 added the FIRST caller that must not degrade, so the `Cell`'s clear-then-read contract now has one reader and would need re-stating if a second degrading caller appears (a per-operation 'keep going' mode in project codegen is the obvious one). Whatever this ticket lands should subsume that flag rather than sit beside it.", at: "2026-07-31T05:49:31Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-886", description: some(value: "THE CODEGEN BACKENDS ENUMERATE THE PER-CARRIER HOST SURFACE IN HAND-WRITTEN RUST TABLES, one commit after WI-876 built the declarative channel for it — and the three enumerations MEASURABLY DISAGREE. `operation_map` facts already carry a `lang` column (`HostOperationMapping.lang`; `build_host_op_mappings` is deliberately language-agnostic so 'a cpp-only mapping still means the author supplied an implementation'), and `rustland/anthill-cpp-gen/anthill/int64.anthill` is a real `language cpp` binding block that could carry the clause. NO CODEGEN BACKEND READS `OperationMapping` TODAY. THE DIVERGENCE, counted on `Float` after WI-881: the INTERPRETER (`host_fn_by_key`) has 29 host functions plus `div`/`isNaN`/`isInfinite`/`isFinite` and `recip` by `[simp]` inlining. CPP-GEN (`render_as_math_call` / `render_as_math_constant` / `render_as_float_special`) has 21 math calls, `neg`/`div` as operators, the three constants and the IEEE specials — MISSING `isNaN`/`isInfinite`/`isFinite` and `recip` ENTIRELY, and missing `Int64.max`/`min`/`compare` which WI-876 declared. Worse, cpp-gen's comparison entries are still keyed on `PartialOrd.gt` while WI-876 moved `Float`'s comparisons to `Float.gt`, so the table is now inconsistent WITHIN ONE BACKEND: WI-881 added a CARRIER-keyed `Float.max`/`min` pair beside SPEC-keyed comparisons. AND THE FALL-THROUGH IS SILENT: an unmatched apply becomes `Ok(format!(\"{fn_short}(...)\"))`, i.e. an unresolvable C++ call that the anthill layer never complains about — a generated program that does not compile, reported nowhere. SMT-GEN is also partial (only `div`, `cos`, `sin`, `abs`, `neg` plus spec-op comparisons) but is NOT part of this ticket's defect: an unmapped op there is `'v0: unhandled arithmetic op'`, so it errors rather than mis-lowers, which is what the repo's loud-over-silent rule asks. Only the SILENT one needs fixing, and it needs fixing at the source rather than by topping up a table. THE WORK: feed `render_as_math_call` (and its constant/special siblings) from `kb.host_op_mappings()` filtered to `lang == \\\"cpp\\\"`, move the pairs into `provides Float language cpp` / `provides Int64 language cpp` blocks, and make the fall-through LOUD so an operation with no cpp mapping is a codegen error naming the operation and the carrier — not an emitted call to a function that does not exist. That is one pass, it deletes the table, and it is what makes 'add a carrier's binding, get the backend' true. WI-879 argues the identical case for the SLD registry; this is the same argument for codegen, and the two could share the reader. WATCH: a spec-op-keyed entry and a carrier-keyed one may both match (cpp-gen's `PartialOrd.gt` vs `Float.gt`) — decide the precedence once, the way `register_operation_mappings` did (mappings registered AFTER the hardcoded list, so a mapping wins). ACCEPTANCE: cpp-gen lowers every operation the rust runtime backs for the primitive carriers, or names the gap loudly at codegen time; the op->host-function pairing for a carrier is maintained in ONE place per language rather than four; a test generates C++ for a program using `Float.isNaN` and `Float.max` and the output compiles; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. REFERENCE: WI-876 (the channel and its `lang` column), WI-881 (which added `Float.max`/`min` to the table and measured the divergence above), WI-879 (the same argument for the SLD registry)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-31T05:58:06Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-878", description: some(value: "CLI/resolver: an undefined functor whose SHORT NAME collides with a resolver scoping marker escapes the WI-754/WI-863 undefined-functor report — silent `no solutions` instead of a loud refusal (Case C of WI-863, measured 2026-07-30). The four reserved marker short names — `forall_in` / `some_in` (WI-027 bounded quantification), `forall_impl` (WI-108 hereditary-Harrop), `__pop_assumption` (scope discharge) — are exempted by `undefined_query_functor` (kb/mod.rs) via the shared authority `is_scoping_marker_name` (kb/resolve.rs), because the RESOLVER also keys these forms on the SHORT name (`bounded_quant_kind` -> `resolve_sym`) and reserves the whole name: even a qualified `myns.some_in` is intercepted as a malformed quantifier. So reporting one as 'undefined' while the resolver hijacks it would be INCOHERENT — the exemption is deliberate and consistent, not a CLI bug. RESIDUAL (the only harm): a user who genuinely typos one of these four names gets silent `no solutions` instead of the loud undefined-functor error every other typo gets. Measured: `anthill query -p kb 'some_in(x)'` with `some_in` declared nowhere -> `no solutions` (exit 0); a real `some_in` needs arity 3 (binder, collection, body), so `some_in/1` is a malformed quantifier the resolver treats as failure. LOW VALUE: 4 unusual names, `__pop_assumption` a double-underscore internal one never types by accident. Recorded here (per loud-error-over-silent-skip) so the boundary stays tracked after WI-863 is delivered; explicitly OUT of WI-863's acceptance. FIX DIRECTION (unsettled): the fix belongs in the RESOLVER, not the CLI — tighten marker recognition so a user name cannot collide: (a) ARITY-CHECK (a real forall_in/some_in/forall_impl is arity 3, __pop_assumption arity 1, so an off-arity call is NOT a marker and CAN be reported undefined), or (b) move the markers to a RESERVED NAMESPACE. LOAD-BEARING CONSTRAINT: the CLI exemption (`is_scoping_marker_name`) and the resolver's marker recognition are ONE shared authority and MUST be tightened IN LOCKSTEP — do NOT diverge the two independently, or a CLI that reports `some_in/1` while the resolver still hijacks the name would disagree with resolution. Soft-follows WI-863 (case C); no technical blocker (independently workable). Rust-only (resolver marker recognition + the shared name check). ACCEPTANCE: a genuine typo of a marker short name at a non-marker arity is either reported undefined (loud) or made impossible by arity/namespace tightening; a well-formed bounded-quantifier / forall_impl / __pop_assumption query still resolves unchanged; the CLI undefined-functor check and the resolver marker recognition remain a single shared authority (no divergence); a test drives the typo case and a well-formed-marker regression; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-31T07:18:35Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-891", description: some(value: "cpp-gen's UNLOWERABLE-BODY DEGRADE EMITS `return {};`, which COMPILES and answers a zero-initialized value — so a diagnosed gap becomes a silently wrong PROGRAM, and the diagnosis is a comment nobody has to read.\n\nWHERE. `synthesise_body_for` (rustland/anthill-cpp-gen/src/lib.rs): when `lower_node` fails on an operation's expression body, it returns\n\n    // TODO: cannot lower expression body — <message>\n    return {};\n\nThe comment's stated intent is right — 'the user sees \"this op has a body but cpp-gen can't lower it yet\" immediately at codegen time, not as a compile error about a missing body later' — and for a CAPABILITY GAP in the generator that is the correct shape: the fix is in cpp-gen, the author can see which method is affected, and the rest of the header still emits. What is wrong is the PAYLOAD. `return {};` is valid C++ for every type that is value-initializable, so `int64_t` becomes 0, `bool` becomes false, `double` becomes 0.0, `std::string` becomes empty. The generated project BUILDS, and the operation answers a plausible constant.\n\nMEASURED during WI-886: an unmapped `Int64.compare` produced exactly this, and it was strictly LESS loud than the defect WI-886 was removing — the bogus `compare(a, b)` it replaced at least failed the C++ build. WI-886 handled its own class by refusing outright (`CodegenContext::unrealized_refusal`, and `synthesise_body_for` re-raises rather than degrading), which leaves the degrade in place for the two classes it was built for: a self-referential anonymous lambda (`unsupported_test::recursive_anonymous_lambda_rejected`) and a marshalled parameter with no `lower` adapter (`conversion_test`, WI-088). Both still emit a compiling zero.\n\nTHE FIX DIRECTION, small: make the degraded body FAIL THE C++ BUILD while keeping the diagnostic at the exact method — `static_assert(false, \"<message>\")` is ill-formed in a non-template function and reports the message verbatim, so the author gets the codegen-time comment AND cannot ship it. Alternatives worth a moment: `#error` (kills the whole translation unit, losing the per-method locality), `throw std::logic_error(...)` (compiles and defers to run time — the same defect one level down), or promoting every degrade to a hard `CppCodegenError` (loses the 'emit the rest of the header' property the degrade exists for, and would need the two existing tests re-pointed).\n\nWATCH: a method emitted inside a TEMPLATE struct is a different case — `static_assert(false, ...)` in an uninstantiated template is ill-formed-no-diagnostic-required before C++23 and some compilers fire it eagerly; the dependent-false idiom (`static_assert(dependent_false_v<T>, ...)`) is what works there, and `anthill_runtime.hpp` is the natural home for `dependent_false_v`. Check both shapes — cpp-gen emits template traits classes routinely.\n\nACCEPTANCE: a body cpp-gen cannot lower produces output that DOES NOT COMPILE, carrying the codegen-time message; the two existing degrade tests (`unsupported_test::recursive_anonymous_lambda_rejected`, `conversion_test`'s missing-`lower` case) are updated to assert that and still see their diagnostics; the template-struct shape is covered by a driven test, not by assumption; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh.\n\nSOURCE: found while delivering WI-886."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-31T13:09:50Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-885", author: "user", content: "SCOPE NARROWED TO ONE OPERATION, and the option list was missing the option WI-580 had already decided. `String.isEmpty` is now HOST-BACKED (`string_is_empty`, O(1)) instead of `[simp]`-backed, so `Float.recip` is the stdlib's SOLE `[simp]`-defined operation — this ticket's entire exposure.\n\nTHE MISSING OPTION IS (d) A BODY. WI-580 settled that an operation's definition is its BODY, from which the equational / relational / proof views are DERIVED (WI-669, delivered) rather than hand-written beside it; `[simp]` is proposal 043's DIRECTIONAL-REWRITE attribute and was never a backing channel. So the question is not 'which predicate should learn to see a [simp] definition' but 'why is a definition written as a tagged rule at all'. MEASURED, all three converted with the full workspace green at each: `String.isEmpty` as `operation isEmpty(s: String) -> Bool = eq(length(s), 0)` answers all three call forms; `Float.recip` as `operation recip(a: Float) -> Float = div(1.0, a)` is green including `the_simp_definition_fires_away_from_its_sort`; and `Float.tau` as `operation tau() -> Float = Numeric.mul(2.0, pi())` WITH ITS HOST MAPPING DELETED answers BARE `tau` AND `tau()`. That last one is decisive: this ticket's own note says a [simp] head is an APPLICATION so it cannot reach a bare var_ref call site, and that (c) would fix that while (a) would not. A BODY fixes it too, because a body is DISPATCHED rather than structurally matched — `tau` never needed a host function at all.\n\nA BODY AND A HOST MAPPING COEXIST, so (c)-vs-(d) is not a fork. `dispatch_resolved_operation` reads the builtin registry at STEP 2 and the anthill body at STEP 3, and `register_operation_mappings` writes into that same registry — so the mapping WINS at eval while the body remains the portable definition (visible to op_is_executable / carrier_override_op, extractable by WI-669, and what a second backend emits when it has no primitive). MEASURED: `tau` carrying BOTH loads clean and passes.\n\nWHY `isEmpty` WENT HOST-ONLY RATHER THAN BODY+MAPPING: `length` is declared in UNICODE SCALARS, so `eq(length(s), 0)` is O(n) — structurally, needing no benchmark, since Rust's str::len and C++'s std::string::size are O(1) BYTE counts and neither language has an O(1) character count over UTF-8. Emptiness is the one question that does not need the count. WI-884 had recorded this cost as UNMEASURED after an interpreter benchmark was swamped by the per-call argument copy; that benchmark was answering the wrong question. The equation SURVIVES, untagged, as the total specification of the operation — which also stops it inlining eq(length(s), 0) into every proof and codegen output where the author wrote isEmpty(s), the symbolic-quality cost this ticket raises.\n\nREMAINING WORK IS SMALLER; THE ACCEPTANCE STILL STANDS. Decide `Float.recip`: a BODY (the WI-580-consistent answer — `div` is already host-mapped, so the body costs one extra dispatch and nothing else) or a host mapping. Either way [simp] STOPS BEING A BACKING KIND, so op_is_executable / op_is_interpretable need NO fourth leg — instead carrier_override_op's doc becomes true again as written, and the load-time backing check should REFUSE a [simp]-only operation rather than let it look backed. Options (a) and (b) are then dead rather than chosen against. CHANGED HERE: rustland/anthill-core/src/eval/builtins.rs (string_is_empty + HOST_FNS), rustland/anthill-stl/anthill/string.anthill (operation_map), stdlib/anthill/prelude/string.anthill (declaration restored, law untagged), and the wi884 test renamed `is_empty_reaches_every_call_form`, kept as the guard that no call form lost its answer.", at: "2026-07-31T06:37:10Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-885", author: "user", content: "CORRECTIONS TO THE PRECEDING FEEDBACK — three claims in it are FALSE, found by review. Read this entry, not that one, for the counts and the cpp consequence.\n\n1. ROOT CAUSE (user, and it settles the option list). A [simp] equation CANNOT BACK A DISPATCHED CALL, because NO REQUIREMENT DICTIONARY CAN BE BUILT FROM ONE. Confirmed at both halves. (i) The dictionary slot holds an operation SYMBOL and the symbol gets INVOKED: dispatch_via_sort_ops_table (eval/eval.rs:932) resolves a Symbol out of the dict's (impl_sort, op_short) table and eval.rs:1352-1356 hands it to dispatch_apply_with_requirements -> dispatch_resolved_operation, whose steps are builtin (2), body (3), value-directed impl (3b). A rewrite rule is in none of them; there is no slot it could occupy. (ii) [simp] needs a STATICALLY KNOWN CARRIER, which is exactly what a dictionary abstracts away — set.anthill:55-57 says so in the stdlib's own words ('tagged [simp] so the type-directed rewriter fires them once the redex's carrier is KNOWN to provide Set'). So [simp] requires a syntactic REDEX and a known carrier; dictionary-passing polymorphism has neither.\n   CONSEQUENCE FOR THIS TICKET'S OPTIONS: (a) and (b) are WRONG, not merely worse. A predicate leg — or a distinct definitional marker — would make op_is_executable promise an implementation for which no dictionary entry can be constructed, i.e. lie at the one site that matters. It also RETROACTIVELY JUSTIFIES carrier_override_op: reading a [simp]-backed member as ABSENT is the CORRECT answer to 'can the interpreter invoke this', not the defect this ticket's description calls it. kernel-language.md's ''[simp] is what makes an equation run'' paragraph said [simp] was 'a third way beside a body and a host mapping'; that sentence is now corrected in place with this argument.\n   LEFT OPEN DELIBERATELY (user): building a dictionary entry FROM a [simp] equation — synthesizing a callable out of the rewrite — may well be possible. Not attempted; recorded as the door this ticket may choose to open rather than a settled no.\n\n2. 'Float.recip is the stdlib's ONLY remaining [simp]-backed operation' IS FALSE. It was inherited from this ticket's own description and never measured. Measured now, there are at least three more, in two clusters:\n   * relation.anthill:154/:156 and :203/:206 — Relation.where and Relation.join are declared BODY-LESS and backed by nothing but a total single definitional [simp] equation ('where(?r, ?cond) <=> guarded_of(?r, ?cond) [simp]'). Neither is registered at eval (only guarded_of/where_run/conjoin_of/join_run are, builtins.rs:138-141) and there is no 'provides Relation language ...' block anywhere, so no host mapping. recip's shape exactly, and identically invisible to all three predicates.\n   * set.anthill — empty/insert/eq/member/subset/union/intersection/difference are ALL declared body-less; none appears in the builtin registry; and there is no concrete Set carrier (sortedset.anthill:72 states it deliberately does NOT 'provides Set'). member/insert/subset/union carry [simp] laws, eq/subset/member carry ':-' relational rules, and intersection/difference carry NOTHING AT ALL — a WI-884-shape audit hole that no ticket owns yet.\n   * NOT affected: Map. All nine of its body-less operations ARE registered builtins, so op_is_executable's builtin leg covers them.\n   COST TO THIS TICKET: its narrowed scope was built on a count of one when it is at least three plus a cluster, and the remedy the preceding feedback recommended — 'the load-time backing check should REFUSE a [simp]-only operation' — WOULD REFUSE THE RELATION ALGEBRA'S TWO-STAGE MACRO LOWERING. where/join use [simp] as WI-722/043.1's deliberate COMPILE-TIME MACRO trigger, which is a different intent from 'definition' — and no predicate distinguishes them, which IS this ticket's complaint. Item 1 supplies the criterion that would: refuse a [simp]-only operation reached through DISPATCH (a dictionary, a spec op); permit one that is a compile-time macro at a statically known carrier. That distinction has to be made explicit before any refusal lands.\n\n3. 'A body is what a second backend emits when it has no primitive' IS MEASURED FALSE for cpp-gen today, and it INVERTS this ticket's remaining recommendation. String.padRight HAS a body, so unlowerable_operation (anthill-cpp-gen/src/lib.rs:3407-3435) returns None and codegen 'succeeds', emitting 'return padRight(s, w);' — a call to a C++ function that does not exist. That is the hole WI-886 explicitly left open. Meanwhile Float.recip, body-less and [simp]-only, codegens CORRECTLY to 'return (1.0 / a);' — [simp] DOES reach cpp-gen. So converting recip to a BODY (the preceding feedback's recommended (d)) would REGRESS cpp-gen from a working expression to a silently broken call. Decide recip with that in front of you: a HOST MAPPING is the option that is right on both routes. It is also the unrecorded justification for what shipped here — going host-only on isEmpty kept it a LOUD cpp refusal instead of moving it into the silent hole, which is a stronger argument than the O(1) one that was written down.\n\n4. 'WI-884's benchmark was answering the wrong question' IS FALSE, and the O(1) claim shipped false too. expect_args (builtins.rs:517-523) is std::array::from_fn(|i| args[i].clone()) and Value::Str owns its String, so the first cut of string_is_empty deep-copied the whole subject before the O(1) test — Theta(|s|), measured ~4000x off O(1) at 64 KB. The per-call argument copy WI-884 hit is real and was present on BOTH routes; that benchmark was honest and genuinely inconclusive. FIXED HERE: string_is_empty now borrows via error_raise's arity-check shape. Corrected per-call picture — [simp] route: 2 dispatches (length's clone + chars().count(), then eq's semantic_equal with two value walks); host route as now shipped: 1 dispatch, O(1). The win was always real (one dispatch, one char-decode and semantic_equal removed); the O(1) headline only became true with the borrow.\n\nALSO FIXED IN THIS PASS: builtins.rs:79-97's split comment under-reported the mapped half (five of thirteen -> six of fourteen, isEmpty missing from its enumeration) — the same staleness this ticket is about, one commit later.", at: "2026-07-31T07:04:22Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-887", author: "user", content: "MEASURED, and it CORRECTS the SLD half of this ticket's own description (which quotes WI-884's measurement). Probe: a driver sort with a  under BOTH attributes, driven at SLD under BOTH `ResolveConfig.simplify` settings, beside a control. THREE findings. (1) THE SLD ROUTE NEEDS TWO THINGS, NOT ONE. `[simp]` on the case laws is necessary but NOT sufficient: `ResolveConfig::default()` has `simplify: false`, and the resolver's equational rewrite (`resolve.rs`, the `if self.config.simplify` block feeding `apply_eq_rules`) is the ONLY SLD firing site. Tagged + `simplify: true` -> 1 solution; tagged + default config -> 0; untagged + either -> 0. So the ticket's 'the cause is that the two case laws are UNTAGGED' names half the cause. (2) WI-884'S SLD MEASUREMENT WAS NOT A MEASUREMENT OF `ite`. Its goal shape is `eq(Bool.ite(true,10,20), 10)`, and the `eq` builtin short-circuits AHEAD of the rule-candidate path that hosts the simp rewrite — so that shape yields 0 for a `[simp]`-tagged USER operation too, under `simplify: true`, with a literal condition. A/B on one KB: `holds(myIte(true,10,20))` against `fact holds(10)` -> 1 solution; `eq(myIte(true,10,20), 10)` -> 0. No repair to `ite` could have moved WI-884's number. The 'redex nested in a compound goal' shape is the one `apply_eq_rules`'s own WI-634 comment calls the intended use. (3) THE LITERAL-ONLY LIMIT HOLDS ON BOTH ROUTES, as the ticket says for eval: `myIte(Int64.gte(2,1), 10, 20)` -> 0 at SLD even tagged + `simplify: true`. The reason is not `ite`-specific — the simp walk is bottom-up but does not EVALUATE a builtin-headed ground subterm, so `gte(2,1)` never becomes `true` and the `ite` head never has a redex. CONSEQUENCE FOR THE OPTIONS: option (a) buys LESS than the ticket credits it with (eval: literal conditions only; SLD: literal conditions AND a non-default resolve config), and option (b)'s cost is smaller than it looks on the eval route — at RUN TIME the condition is always an evaluated literal, so a registered `ite` has no literal-only limit; strictness over the BRANCHES is its whole cost. AND THE ROUTE THAT ALREADY WORKS IS UNMENTIONED BY THE TICKET: anthill-smt-gen lowers the `Bool.ite` FUNCTOR to SMT-LIB `(ite c t e)` today (`is_ite_op`, lib.rs; WI-680, delivered), so the design-doc use the description calls a plan is shipped. Which means `ite`'s one working consumer needs the FUNCTOR and no value-level implementation at all — evidence for the reading that its value-level absence is a category fact rather than a gap.", at: "2026-07-31T07:37:57Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-887", author: "user", content: "ERRATUM to the probe feedback above: one word was elided by shell backtick substitution when it was recorded. \"a driver sort with a  under BOTH attributes\" should read \"a driver sort with a `myIte` operation under BOTH attributes\" — the probe declared its own two-law operation rather than touching the prelude, and drove `Bool.ite` beside it as a control.", at: "2026-07-31T07:38:19Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-892", description: some(value: "ALLOW `if`/`then`/`else` IN A RULE BODY — the grammar gate is the ONLY reason `anthill.prelude.Bool.ite` exists, and WI-887 settles `ite` as a `[simp]`-rule-only functor rather than removing the need for it. THE GATE, exactly: `if_expr` is a member of `_expr_body` (tree-sitter-anthill/grammar.js:1354, under the \"Expressions (operation bodies)\" heading) while `_goal` is `choice($.cut, $.let_binding, $._term)` (:459) and `rule_body` is `commaSep1($._goal)` (:451) — so a rule body reaches `_term` and never `if_expr`. THE EVIDENCE THAT THIS IS THE WHOLE REASON: `rustland/anthill-smt-gen/tests/wi680_ite_lowering_test.rs`:10 states it in its own words — \"a rule-body twin must spell it `ite(...)`\" — and its demonstrator rule is `clamp(?x, ?r) :- ?r = ite(gte(?x, 0), ?x, 0)`, which is `if gte(?x,0) then ?x else 0` with no conditional surface to write it in. WI-680's ticket says the same (\"if/then/else does NOT parse in rule-body position (only operation bodies), so the hand-written demonstrator uses the ite(...) functor form\"). The three stdlib laws that spell `ite` — `ordered.anthill`:108-109 (`max`/`min`) and `int64.anthill`:73 (`sign`) — are the same case. WHAT IT WOULD BUY. (1) One conditional surface instead of two. Today the SAME conditional is written `if` in an operation body and `ite` in a rule body, and the two lower differently — smt-gen has a path for the `Expr::If` occurrence (lib.rs:1152) AND a separate one for the `Bool.ite` functor (:1182, `is_ite_op`, which also matches the BARE short name \"ite\" and so can reinterpret an unresolved same-named user op — WI-680's own review recorded that hazard). Allowing `if` in a rule body would let the functor path be retired and the bare-name match with it. (2) It removes the LAZINESS question that WI-887 had to settle. `Expr::If` is lazy by construction; `ite` as an operation could not be (an operation's arguments are evaluated before the call), which is exactly why WI-887 took it OUT of the operation block. With `if` available in a rule body, an author who wants a conditional writes the lazy one everywhere. (3) The refold in `kb/body_specialize.rs` (WI-669) already produces `Expr::If`; a rule-body `if` would be the same shape the synth seam emits, so one representation serves the hand-written and the derived rule alike. WHAT TO WATCH. (a) GRAMMAR AMBIGUITY: `_goal` is comma-separated, and `if_expr`'s branches are `_expr_body` which is not comma-delimited — an `else` arm followed by `, next_goal` needs a precedence decision, and tree-sitter is GLR so a wrong one shows up as a conflict rather than an error. `if_expr` is already `prec.right`. (b) `occurrence_to_term` ASSERTS/⊥s on `If` (recorded in docs/design/abstract-interpreter-and-rules.md §3.4.1: control flow \"cannot be represented\" in a `TermId`, called \"an intrinsic fact of goal position\"). So an `If` reaching a hash-consed-term boundary must be refused LOUDLY at that boundary, and the consumers that convert a rule body to terms have to be enumerated before this lands — this is the substantive risk, not the grammar. (c) SLD: a rule-body `if` still has to mean something during resolution, or be refused there. WI-887's probe measured that a rule-body `ite` reduces at SLD only via the `apply_eq_rules` path, only under `ResolveConfig.simplify = true` (NOT the default), and only where the condition is already a literal — so \"what a conditional means in a goal\" is an open question this ticket inherits rather than creates. ACCEPTANCE: `rule f(?x, ?r) :- ?r = if gte(?x, 0) then ?x else 0` parses, loads and lowers to SMT-LIB `(ite ...)` through the EXISTING `Expr::If` path; the tree-sitter corpus covers a rule-body `if` followed by a further comma-separated goal; every rule-body→`TermId` boundary either handles `If` or refuses it loudly (enumerated, not assumed); `ordered.anthill`/`int64.anthill`/`bool.anthill` and `wi680_ite_lowering_test` are considered for restatement in the `if` surface, and whether `Bool.ite` (and `is_ite_op`, and its bare-name match) can then be RETIRED is answered explicitly; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. REFERENCE: WI-887 (`ite` is `[simp]`-rule-only; its feedback carries the SLD probe), WI-680 (the functor lowering and the \"must spell it ite\" note), WI-669 (the `Expr::If` refold and the `occurrence_to_term` limitation)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: PreOpened)
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-887", author: "user", content: "DECISION (user): `ite` IS A `[simp]` RULE ONLY — it comes OUT of `Bool`'s operation block and survives as a term-level functor introduced by its two case laws. This is option (c), and the reasoning is one line: an operation's arguments are evaluated before the call, so an `ite` that IS an operation cannot be a conditional; a `[simp]` REWRITE discards the untaken branch STRUCTURALLY, never evaluating it, so laziness is free. The user's two governing statements: \"if it operation, than it should be implemented\" (which rules out leaving a declared operation unbacked — my recommended option 1 — and rules out `[simp]`-as-backing, for which WI-885's feedback gives the decisive reason: no requirement dictionary can be built from a rewrite) and \"simp rule can coexist with operation\" (so keeping the laws was never in tension with anything).\nTHE LITERAL-ONLY LIMIT IS NOT A COST HERE, which inverts the ticket's own objection to tagging. Every actual use of `ite` is SYMBOLIC and none of them wants reduction: smt-gen lowers the functor to SMT-LIB `(ite c t e)` with the condition left symbolic, and the three stdlib spellings (`ordered.max`/`min`, `int64.sign`) are specification. The two case laws are then ordinary algebra with the same status as `and(true, ?a) = ?a` sitting four lines above them. \"Half-backed looks backed\" was an objection to a HALF-IMPLEMENTED OPERATION; once it is not an operation there is nothing to look backed.\nMEASURED, so the implementer knows the deletion is clean. Removing the `ite(cond: Bool, then: ?A, else: ?A) -> ?A` line from `bool.anthill` and tagging both case laws `[simp]`: (1) the full stdlib LOADS CLEAN (`anthill check`, 2514 facts / 117 rules, no new diagnostic) — the `[simp]` rule heads alone introduce the functor; (2) `import anthill.prelude.Bool.{ite}` STILL RESOLVES at both stdlib consumers (`ordered.anthill`:68, `int64.anthill`:7), so an import of a rule-introduced functor is legal; (3) all EIGHT `wi680_ite_lowering_test` cases still pass, so the SMT lowering — `ite`'s one real consumer — is untouched. Not yet run: the full workspace suite.\nSTILL TO DO for delivery, beyond the two-line edit: `bool.anthill`'s 25-line comment block still argues the OLD open state and must be replaced by the decision and its one-line reason; `eval/builtins.rs`'s header paragraph likewise; `wi884_sibling_backing_test`'s three `ite` doc comments and the `bool_is_audited_and_ite_reduces_nowhere` assertion (it asserts `OperationBodyMissing { anthill.prelude.Bool.ite }`, which will no longer be reachable — the test should assert the new state instead of being deleted); `a_simp_ite_reaches_only_a_literal_condition` stays, but as the record of WHY a rewrite is the right carrier rather than why tagging was refused; and the DIAGNOSTIC half of the acceptance (\"an unbacked-BY-DESIGN operation and an unwritten one both die `OperationBodyMissing`\") is now MOOT for `ite` specifically but still true in general — decide whether it stays in this ticket or goes to WI-880.\nBETTER DIRECTION, FILED AS WI-892 (PreOpened, user): allow `if`/`then`/`else` in a rule body. The grammar gate (`if_expr` under `_expr_body`, `_goal` reaching only `_term`) is the ONLY reason `ite` exists at all — `wi680_ite_lowering_test`:10 says so in its own words. With `if` available in a goal, `Bool.ite`, smt-gen's `is_ite_op` functor path and its bare-short-name match could all be retired, leaving one conditional surface with one lowering. WI-887 does not block on it and should not wait for it.", at: "2026-07-31T08:07:53Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-892", author: "user", content: "THE `occurrence_to_term` BOUNDARY IS THE SUBSTANTIVE WORK, and it will NOT have been eliminated by then (user). Two things pin it. (1) THE CONVERSION STAYS BY DESIGN: WI-348's goal is that logic/matching/identity never reify, NOT that the conversion is deleted — it is kept at genuine term boundaries (hash-consing, codegen, persistence, TermId-keyed indexes). So this ticket cannot wait for it to go away. (2) `Term` HAS NO CONTROL-FLOW VARIANT AT ALL: the enum is `Const | Var | Fn | Ref | Bottom | Ident | ParseAux` (kb/term.rs:213-239). An `If` is not merely unhandled, it is UNREPRESENTABLE, so \"extend `occurrence_to_term`\" means either adding a control-flow representation to `Term` (with everything that implies for hash-consing and the discrimination tree) or keeping the boundary TOTAL AND LOUD.\nTHE INVARIANT THIS TICKET FALSIFIES IS STATED IN THE FUNCTION'S OWN DOC, twice, and both statements become false the moment `if` parses in a goal: \"Recursion is bounded by the goal-atom structure (predicate applications over leaves); CONTROL-FLOW FORMS CAN'T APPEAR AT A GOAL POSITION and fall to ⊥\" and, on the `None` arm, \"A child-bearing control-flow / post-elaboration form CAN'T OCCUR AT A GOAL POSITION\" (kb/node_occurrence.rs:2466-2489).\nAND THE GUARD IS A `debug_assert!`. In a RELEASE build the `None` arm falls through to `kb.alloc(Term::Bottom)` — a rule-body `if` would SILENTLY REIFY TO ⊥ rather than fail, which is precisely the silent-skip this repo's principles forbid, and it would be invisible to a debug-mode test suite. Whatever else this ticket does, that arm must become a real error before `if` can reach it.\nTHE TWO NAMED CONSUMERS, from the same doc comment: the resolver's dedup `seen_goals` key and `Solution.residual`. Both are goal-position boundaries, so both are reached by exactly the shape this ticket introduces. `try_occurrence_to_term` (the total variant, WI-297) already returns `None` for `If`/`Let`/`Match`/`Lambda`/`HoApply` and is the right shape to build on. There are ~76 `occurrence_to_term` references in anthill-core and a prior audit of ~20 real call sites exists — enumerate against it rather than from scratch, and note its lesson: reading a site is NOT enough to classify it, RUN the live tests (a \"removable\" verdict on the lambda-annotation sites was wrong because the conversion there INTERPRETS rather than round-trips).", at: "2026-07-31T08:12:49Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-887", author: "user", content: "DELIVERY ATTEMPTED AND BACKED OUT — the decision (\"`ite` is a `[simp]` rule only\") is NOT IMPLEMENTABLE AS STATED, and driving it also REFUTED TWO CLAIMS I recorded in the probe feedback above and ONE the ticket description makes. Working tree restored; the attempt is kept as a diff, nothing committed.\nFIRST, MY OWN TWO CLAIMS WERE WRONG. (1) \"The full stdlib LOADS CLEAN\" is FALSE. `anthill check` on ONE file reported clean (2514 facts / 117 rules, only the two pre-existing `map`/`filter` shadow warnings) and MISSED it; the test harness's `load_kb_with` reports THREE `unresolved import 'anthill.prelude.Bool.ite'`. (2) \"`import anthill.prelude.Bool.{ite}` STILL RESOLVES\" is FALSE and is the same error — I read the single-file check as the whole answer. THE IMPORT SITES ARE THREE, not the two the description implies: `list.anthill`:9 as well as `ordered.anthill`:68 and `int64.anthill`:7.\nWHY \"SIMP RULE ONLY\" IS NOT EXPRESSIBLE TODAY. An `import` names an operation or a sort; it CANNOT name an equation's LHS head, so with the declaration gone the three importers fail to load. Removing their imports makes the stdlib load again — but then their bare `ite` no longer names `Bool.ite` at all, which SILENTLY DECOUPLES the three stdlib spellings from the two laws: a load-clean program in which the laws and their uses are different symbols. And the typer refuses an unknown functor in an OPERATION BODY in BOTH spellings (`type mismatch in ite.apply: expected known operation or arrow-typed variable, got unknown functor`), so a rewrite-only `ite` would be rule-body-only — which is coherent and arguably right, but it is a LANGUAGE CHANGE (a declaration form for a term-level functor that is not an operation), not a stdlib edit.\nTHE DECISIVE MEASUREMENT, and it retires option (a) outright. With the declaration KEPT and BOTH case laws tagged `[simp]`, `ite` still runs NOWHERE: `Bool.ite(true, 1, 2)` in an operation body dies `OperationBodyMissing { anthill.prelude.Bool.ite }`, and `holds(ite(true, 10, 20))` against `fact holds(10)` yields 0 solutions at SLD under `simplify: true`. So THE DESCRIPTION'S \"tagging them `[simp]` DOES make the operation run — but ONLY where the condition is already a LITERAL\" IS FALSE FOR `Bool.ite`. It was generalized from `a_simp_ite_reaches_only_a_literal_condition`, whose `myIte` is declared in the SAME sort as its laws AND called from that sort; my own probe reproduced that (1 solution) and it does NOT carry to the prelude. Option (a) buys nothing at all, not \"a little\".\nPROBABLE CAUSE, NOT YET DRIVEN — the next session should start here. The type-directed guard (`simp_fire_guard_holds`) places a SORT-SCOPED equation by the redex's carrier. `ite`'s laws are scoped to sort `Bool`, but `ite`'s RESULT type is `?A` — `Int64` at both call sites above — so a `Bool`-scoped rule cannot be placed on an `Int64` redex. `equation_is_requires_guarded` is NOT the cause (`bool.anthill` declares no `requires`, so `requires_chain` is empty). If that is right it is a GENERAL fact about any equation whose head is scoped to one sort and whose redex carrier is another, not an `ite` quirk, and it would deserve its own ticket.\nWHERE THAT LEAVES THE DECISION: the user's reasoning stands (an operation cannot be a lazy conditional; a rewrite discards the untaken branch structurally) but neither shipped mechanism can carry it — a rewrite cannot be placed, and a declaration cannot be avoided. The realistic route is WI-892 (allow `if` in a rule body), after which `ite` has no reason to exist in any form; until then `ite` stays exactly as it is, dead and documented. Recommend re-scoping this ticket to \"document `ite` as inert pending WI-892\" and moving the executable-conditional question to WI-892 — but that is a re-scope, not something to decide inside a delivery.", at: "2026-07-31T08:24:00Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-887", author: "user", content: "ROOT CAUSE FOUND (user: \"[simp] should work inside operation body\" — it does, and this is why it does not for `ite`). It is NOT that `[simp]` is inert in an operation body, and NOT any of the three things I suspected. THE A/B MATRIX, five controls varying ONE property at a time, all on one KB, `[simp]` tags added to `bool.anthill`'s two case laws for the run: laws-and-caller in the SAME sort -> 10; caller in a DIFFERENT sort, QUALIFIED (`C.myIte(...)`) -> 10; cross-sort BARE (via `import C.{myIte}`) -> 10; the `=` connective instead of `<=>` -> 10; a POLYMORPHIC result (`polyIte(cond: Bool, then: ?A, else: ?A) -> ?A`, `ite`'s exact signature shape) -> 10. Every control FIRES. Only `Bool.ite(true, 10, 20)` does not, and it dies `OperationBodyMissing` with the laws TAGGED. So cross-sort, the connective and the type parameter are all REFUTED as causes.\nTHE CAUSE IS THAT `Bool.ite` IS A SPEC OP AND THE CONTROLS ARE NOT. `simp_fire_guard_holds` (kb/typing.rs:34312) splits on `lookup_spec_op_dispatch`: a NON-spec-op functor is \"a concrete monomorphic identity whose functor already pins the sort\" and fires GUARD-FREE, while a spec op must pass `simp_guard_holds_core`, which fires only when the op has >=1 CARRIER ARGUMENT and EVERY carrier's sort head PROVIDES the spec. `lookup_spec_op_dispatch` returns `Some` for a BODY-LESS operation whose parent sort is provided — and `rustland/anthill-stl/anthill/bool.anthill`:3 says `provides Bool language rust`, so `anthill.prelude.Bool` IS provided and `ite` IS body-less. `ite`'s only `Bool`-typed parameter is `cond`, whose inferred sort head is `Bool` itself, and there is no `fact Bool[...]` — a sort does not self-provide (the binding file asserts `PartialEq[T = Bool]` and `Eq[T = Bool]`, not `Bool`). So the carrier check cannot be satisfied and the rewrite is declined. My local sorts C/D/F/G are provided by nobody, so they take the guard-free branch — which is why every control fired and the real case did not.\nCONSEQUENCE, AND IT IS GENERAL — NOT AN `ite` QUIRK. Any body-less operation on a HOST-PROVIDED sort is treated as a call-site dispatch placeholder, so its `[simp]` definition is gated behind a provision witness for its own sort. `and`/`or`/`not` never exposed this because they are host-BACKED, so nothing needed to fire. This is the same shape as WI-880's host-carrier exemption seen from the other side: the load check trusts a host carrier wholesale, and the simp guard distrusts it wholesale.\nWHAT THIS MEANS FOR THE DECISION. \"`[simp]` rule only\" is the right shape and is NOT reachable by editing `bool.anthill` alone — it needs the guard to stop treating a body-less op on a provided sort as a dispatch placeholder when the op has a `[simp]` DEFINITION. That is a change to `simp_fire_guard_holds`, which is SHARED with the resolver through `simp_guard_holds_core` (the doc says the two must not disagree on which argument carries the spec), so it is a real change with a blast radius, not a stdlib edit — and it should be measured against WI-292's dormant-equation list (~14 Set/Map rules skipped by the sibling `equation_is_requires_guarded` path), which may be the same defect wearing a different guard.\nSTATE: nothing committed, working tree clean, full workspace suite GREEN at the committed baseline (re-run to verify the back-out). The attempt's diff is saved outside the repo. WI-887 remains Open and unclaimed.", at: "2026-07-31T08:35:47Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-887", author: "user", content: "RETRACTION — THE ROOT CAUSE I RECORDED TWO ENTRIES ABOVE IS FALSE, and so is the measurement it rested on. Read this entry, not that one. Found by a `/code-review` altitude agent; both halves re-verified by hand before accepting.\nFALSE CLAIM 1: \"`lookup_spec_op_dispatch` classifies any body-less operation on a PROVIDED sort as a spec op, and `provides Bool language rust` makes `Bool` one, so `ite` was gated behind a provision witness for its own sort.\" `spec_op_parent_sort` (kb/typing.rs:14753) NEVER READS A PROVISION. Its gate is `if kb.type_params_of_sort(parent_sym).is_empty() { return None }` — a TYPE PARAMETER, i.e. a `sort T = ?`. `anthill.prelude.Bool` declares none, so `Bool.ite` was never a spec op and `simp_fire_guard_holds` never touched it. The agent drove the axis directly: a non-parametric UNPROVIDED sort fires; a non-parametric sort WITH `provides ... language rust` + `carrier` ALSO fires; only a PARAMETRIC sort is gated. Shipped counterexample one file over: `Float.recip` is body-less, `[simp]`-defined with no host mapping, on a host-provided sort — and it runs (WI-881).\nFALSE CLAIM 2, the measurement: \"with the declaration kept and BOTH case laws tagged `[simp]`, `ite` still runs nowhere.\" Only ONE law was tagged in the program I measured. A GLR tie in the grammar SILENTLY ATE the `[simp]` on `ite_true`, because a `-- if-then-else` comment line sat immediately above it — the attribute re-parsed as a separate junk rule headed by the list `[simp]`. I then probed exactly `Bool.ite(true, 1, 2)`, the one branch whose tag was gone. That defect is now WI-893, filed with the bisection and the sweep (exactly one dropped attribute repo-wide — the one my own edit introduced).\nWHAT IS ACTUALLY TRUE, re-measured with the comment removed so both tags parse AND THE OPERATION STILL DECLARED: `Bool.ite(true, 10, 20)` -> 10, `Bool.ite(false, 10, 20)` -> 20. OPTION (a) WORKS. The declaration is not the problem and never was; the laws were simply UNTAGGED, which is WI-881's headline unchanged and exactly what this ticket's description said in the first place. The user said `[simp]` should work inside an operation body and that a `[simp]` rule can coexist with an operation — both correct, and my measurement was what appeared to contradict them.\nCONSEQUENTLY THE DELETION IS WITHDRAWN and the working tree is back at the last commit. Two further costs of the deletion the review found, which no longer apply but are worth keeping as evidence it was the wrong shape: it left `wi680_ite_lowering_test.rs`:31's `import anthill.prelude.Bool.{ite}` a LIVE load error hidden by that harness' `let _ = load::load_all(...)`, so the eight SMT cases passed through the bare-name fallback rather than the import they document; and it made smt-gen's `is_ite_op` arms 1-2 dead, leaving the bare `\"ite\"` short-name match as the only path and so making its known user-op-collision hazard strictly worse.\nALSO WITHDRAWN: the eagerness argument as a reason to DELETE. It is true of a CALL, but a `[simp]` definition is INLINED in operation bodies before dispatch, so a declared body-less op with `[simp]` laws is never called — the probe above evaluates neither branch WITH the declaration present. The argument argues against registering a builtin or host mapping for `ite`, which nobody proposed and `eval/builtins.rs` already declined. It does not argue against declaring it.\nTHE REMAINING WORK IS SMALL AND BLOCKED ON WI-893: tag `bool.anthill`'s two `ite` case laws `[simp]`, keep the declaration, and drive both branches. It must NOT land before WI-893, or the comment above the laws silently disables half of it again — which is the whole reason that ticket exists. NOTE FOR WHOEVER TAKES IT: the earlier \"two claims of mine were wrong\" entry (the `anthill check` one) still stands; this retraction is additional, not a replacement.\nCOMMIT 0baed15c IS WRONG IN ITS MESSAGE — it states the spec-op/provision story as the finding. It changed no code, only ticket text. It should be corrected by a follow-up commit rather than trusted.", at: "2026-07-31T09:27:50Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-894", author: "user", content: "DECISION (user): \"rule should not travel to the global namespace from its places.\" So the answer to this ticket's open question is SCOPED, not global — a rule-introduced functor stays where it is declared, and the alternative this ticket floated (leave it global and merely refuse a duplicate loudly) is REJECTED. A duplicate is not the disease; the travel is.\nWHAT THAT MAKES MANDATORY, and it is the half this ticket already flagged as the reason it is not a one-liner: there is TODAY NO WAY TO NAME A RULE-INTRODUCED FUNCTOR FROM ELSEWHERE. `import anthill.prelude.Bool.{ite}` is `unresolved import` — an import names an operation or a sort, never an equation's LHS head (MEASURED under WI-887). So scoping without an import path does not tighten the language, it breaks it. Both halves land together or neither does.\nTHE STDLIB IS ALREADY A CONSUMER OF THE BEHAVIOUR BEING REMOVED, which sets this ticket's real scope: `ordered.anthill` (`max`/`min` laws) and `int64.anthill` (`sign` law) write BARE `ite(...)` and reach `bool.anthill`'s `[simp]` rules ONLY because the name is global today. Under the decision they must reach it by an explicit path instead. Those two files are the acceptance case — if they still load and their laws still connect after scoping, the import path works.\nAND IT BITES WHAT WI-887 JUST SHIPPED, recorded here rather than discovered later: WI-887 deleted the `ite` OPERATION, so `ite` is now exactly a rule-introduced functor, and the arrangement RESTS ON THE TRAVEL. `bool.anthill` defines it, three other files use it, and nothing but globality connects them. That is not an argument to undo WI-887 — its own reason stands and is independent (an operation at `ite`'s signature evaluates both branches, so no operation is the only correct form) — but it does mean this ticket must carry `ite` as its worked example, and that WI-887's state is provisional until this lands. Cross-referenced on WI-887.", at: "2026-07-31T09:47:02Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-887", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED (this session). `ite` is no longer an operation. THE REASON IS THE USER'S AND IT IS THE SIGNATURE ITSELF: `ite(cond: Bool, then: ?A, else: ?A) -> ?A` types the branches as VALUES, and a call evaluates its arguments, so both branches are computed — an `ite` operation is simply incorrect at that signature. One taking THUNKS (`then: () -> A, else: () -> A`) would be a DIFFERENT operation with different call sites, not this one. So no operation is the only correct form; as a rewrite the untaken branch is discarded structurally and never evaluated. That argument is independent of every measurement in this ticket, which is why it survived two of my diagnoses being wrong.\nWHAT SHIPPED: the declaration deleted from `bool.anthill`; the two case laws moved to their OWN rule block and tagged `[simp]` (they are now `ite`'s whole definition), placed clear of a preceding comment line because that position silently eats the attribute (WI-893); the three dead `Bool.{ite}` imports removed from `list`/`ordered`/`int64`; and a FOURTH copy removed from `wi680_ite_lowering_test.rs`, where it had become a live load error hidden by that harness' `let _ = load::load_all(...)` — so the eight SMT cases had been passing through the bare-name fallback rather than the import they document. `is_ite_op` (smt-gen) loses its two now-unreachable qualified arms. Stale \"ite is an operation / is dead\" prose corrected in cpp-gen's `bool.anthill` (which still said this ticket was open and invited a `?:` mapping), `ordered.anthill` x2, `kernel-vocab-provenance.md`, `smtlib-forward-mapping.md`, `abstract-interpreter-and-rules.md`. CLAUDE.md deliberately NOT touched (user).\nBEHAVIOUR, DRIVEN: bare `ite(true, 1, 2)` in an operation body REDUCES to 1 — the typer's `[simp]` pass inlines it before dispatch, so the user was right that `[simp]` works in an operation body and right that it coexists with (or here, replaces) a declaration. The MEMBER spelling `Bool.ite(...)` is refused as an unknown functor, since a rule head introduces a functor and not a qualified operation symbol. At SLD a redex nested in a compound goal reduces under `ResolveConfig.simplify`; under the `eq` builtin it never does, because `eq` short-circuits ahead of the rewrite — the shape WI-884 measured, which was never a measurement of `ite`. Test `ite_reduces_bare_and_is_refused_as_a_member` pins both spellings; `bool_is_audited_and_ite_is_not_one_of_its_operations` pins the SLD arm under BOTH `simplify` settings so neither cause can masquerade as the other. Full workspace suite GREEN.\nPROVISIONAL, and the ticket should be read with this attached: the arrangement RESTS ON A BEHAVIOUR THE USER HAS SINCE REJECTED. A rule-introduced functor is not sort-scoped — it is one bare global name (MEASURED: two sorts with inverted `[simp]` definitions of `pick` both answer the FIRST sort's, loading clean) — and `bool.anthill`'s `ite` reaches `ordered.anthill` and `int64.anthill` only through that globality. The user's rule is \"a rule should not travel to the global namespace from its places\", which is WI-894; when it lands, `ite` needs an explicit naming path, and today an import cannot name a rule head. WI-894 carries `ite` as its worked example.\nREVIEW: `/code-review` (4 agents). The altitude agent is what caught the false diagnosis — worth recording that the two cheap agents found only cleanups while the one asked to judge DEPTH found the substantive error.", at: "2026-07-31T09:47:32Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-887", description: some(value: "DECIDE WHAT `anthill.prelude.Bool.ite` IS — it runs on NEITHER route, and WI-884 measured that the recorded justification covers only one of them. THE MEASUREMENT (`wi884_sibling_backing_test.rs`): `Bool.ite(true, 1, 2)` in an operation body dies `OperationBodyMissing`, and the same call as a rule-body goal (`eq(Bool.ite(true, 10, 20), 10)`) yields ZERO solutions in BOTH orientations, beside a control goal that yields one. So `ite` reduces NOWHERE on a program that loads clean — WI-881's defect shape, on the one operation WI-884's audit could not close. THE VALUE-LEVEL HALF IS DELIBERATE and stands: an operation's arguments are evaluated BEFORE the call, so a registered `ite` evaluates BOTH branches, which is not what a conditional means; `if`/`then`/`else` is the lazy value-level form. `eval/builtins.rs`'s header says so, and `ordered.anthill` records the same decision from the other side (it writes `max`'s LAW with `ite` and `max`'s BODY with `if`, and says why). THE RULE-LEVEL HALF WAS FALSE: that same header claimed 'rule-level uses of `ite` are handled by the prelude's rewrite rules during SLD resolution'. The cause is that `bool.anthill`'s two case laws (`ite_true`, `ite_false`) are UNTAGGED, and an untagged equation is INERT — WI-881's headline recurring one file over. NOT the `=` spelling, which was WI-884's first diagnosis and was REFUTED by driving (that divergence is WI-888). WHY TAGGING THEM IS NOT THE FIX, MEASURED: a `[simp]` head matches STRUCTURALLY, so `ite(true, ?t, ?_)` has a redex only where the CONDITION IS ALREADY A LITERAL. Driven on a control (`a_simp_ite_reaches_only_a_literal_condition`), `myIte(true, 10, 20)` inlines to 10 while `myIte(Int64.gte(a, b), 10, 20)` — the shape every real use has, and the shape `ordered.anthill`'s own `max` law writes — still dies. Half-backed is WORSE than dead: it looks backed. THE OPTIONS, each with its cost. (a) Tag the two laws anyway and document the literal-only limit — cheapest, and it leaves `ordered.anthill`'s `max` law no more executable than today. (b) Give `ite` a value-level body (`= if cond then then else else`) and accept STRICTNESS: it then runs everywhere, and `ite(neq(b, 0), div(a, b), 0)` RAISES where today it dies loudly — a changed meaning that wants a name saying it is strict. (c) DELETE `ite` from `Bool` in favour of a term-level conditional that is not an operation; the users to carry are `ordered.anthill` (`max`/`min`), `int64.anthill` (`sign`), and `docs/design/abstract-interpreter-and-rules.md`:272, which plans to translate the `Bool.ite` FUNCTOR to SMT `ite` — that SMT use is the reason `ite` probably has to survive in some form. ALSO SETTLE THE DIAGNOSTIC: today an unbacked-BY-DESIGN operation and an unwritten one both die `OperationBodyMissing`, which reads as 'not implemented yet' and sent WI-884 looking for a missing implementation that was never meant to exist. ACCEPTANCE: every operation `bool.anthill` declares either runs or is deleted (WI-884's own acceptance, for the one it left open); if `ite` survives with no value-level implementation, the failure NAMES why instead of reporting a missing body; a test drives whichever route the decision keeps; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. REFERENCE: WI-884 (the three measurements and their controls), WI-881 ([simp] is the enablement), WI-888 (the head-connective divergence that made the wrong diagnosis plausible), `ordered.anthill`'s note above its `max`/`min` laws."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-893"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-31T09:47:53Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-893", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. The GLR tie is broken by `prec.dynamic(1, $.meta_block)` on `rule_entry`'s trailing meta_block — the WI-448 remedy in the second production pair that has this hazard. MEASURED both ways, and the pre-fix measurement is the acceptance: with the original grammar restored, `(entry, --comment, entry [simp], entry [simp])` yields ONE meta_block + one collection_literal; with the fix, TWO. Line comment and block comment trip it alike, and in EITHER position of a tagged pair (a comment above the SECOND entry eats that one's tag) — the ticket's bisection only covered the first.\n\nWHY `prec.dynamic` AND NOT A GRAMMAR RESTRICTION, since the ticket did not choose: the alternatives were driven and are worse. Restricting what a head may START with needs a `_head_goal` forking `_atom_term`, which the grammar's own WI-312 comment argues against (\"keeping it as a shared rule guarantees the receiver set never drifts\"), and it would refuse `rule [?a] = ?b`, which the load rule below PERMITS — a stricter rule than the one stated, and a disagreement to maintain. A separator between entries is source-breaking and needs an external scanner this grammar does not have. Decisive: the defect is a tree-sitter GLR ARTIFACT, not a language defect — `scaland`'s fastparse port has the same juxtaposed `ruleEntry.rep` and never had the bug (PEG, ordered choice), so the other two options would change the LANGUAGE and force a matching change in a port that was never broken. The bias also loses nothing expressible: DRIVEN, `rule { p(?x) [?a] = ?b }` still parses as two entries, because `[?a]` is not meta-block-shaped (`meta_entry` needs a `name` key).\n\nNO SIBLING PRODUCTION HAS THIS TIE — checked, not assumed. The hazard needs the NEXT member of a `repeat` to be able to begin with `[`; all 15 `optional($.meta_block)` sites and all 11 repeats were enumerated, and every other member is keyword-led or name-led (`operation_entry` starts `optional(visibility) name`). `rule_entry` is the only `[`-startable sibling, which is why `[$.rule_entry]` is the only such declared conflict.\n\nTHE ANSWER TO THE \"CONSIDER\": a bare `collection_literal` head is NEVER legal, and neither is any other bare literal. MEASURED first: `rule { [simp] }` LOADED CLEAN before this change, which is what kept the defect silent end to end. Refused now at `convert_rule_heads`, the function that already owns this class (`let_binding`, `cut`), for a reason independent of the accident — a head is a CONCLUSION, a literal denotes a VALUE, and a value is not a proposition. It is not merely a second line of defence: it is what makes the `prec.dynamic` bias EXACT rather than arbitrary tie-breaking, since the reading the bias discards is one nothing may write. Scope is the whole literal family (scalars + `[…]`/`{…}`/`(…)`), because the argument does not distinguish `[simp]` from `42`; enumerating only the accident's shape would be a gap list wearing a rule's clothes.\n\nTHE REFUSAL WAS A HALF-FIX AT FIRST, found by the review's altitude agent: `fact` is the OTHER producer of a conclusion, and CLAUDE.md's own \"facts are rules — a fact is a rule with empty body\" makes the rule apply there. DRIVEN: `fact 42`, `fact [simp]`, `fact \"s\"` all parsed and reached `assert_fact` — the identical junk-entry outcome, on the surface a user is likelier to hand-write. Both producers now go through one owner, `reject_literal_conclusion`.\n\nTESTS. Corpus (`test/corpus/rule_entry_meta.txt`): four cases — no-comment control, line comment above the first tagged entry, above the second, and a block comment — sharing one body so each differs from the control by exactly one comment line. Rust (`wi893_comment_eats_simp_test.rs`), because NOTHING in the repo runs `npx tree-sitter test` under `cargo test`: two body-less operations whose `[simp]` laws are their whole definition, one with a comment above its THEN law and one above its ELSE law, all four branches driven on one interpreter. That asymmetry is the point — pre-fix exactly two of the four died `OperationBodyMissing` while their siblings answered, so asserting one branch per operation would have passed against the defect half the time, which is precisely how WI-887 was misled. Plus the differential fact/rule count (the ticket's own instrument, on a small program rather than the stdlib, since the stdlib has no such comment to add or remove today; the control half is DERIVED by stripping comment lines so the two cannot drift), and the refusal + its atom-heads control. VERIFIED PRE-FIX: with the original grammar restored, the two load-level tests FAIL, and they fail LOUDLY through the new refusal rather than silently — which is the second line of defence doing exactly what it was added for.\n\nTHE ACCEPTANCE ITEM THAT DOES NOT APPLY: \"the parser is regenerated and the generated file committed\" — `tree-sitter-anthill/src/` is GITIGNORED in this repo, and `build.rs` regenerates `parser.c` whenever `grammar.js` is newer. Nothing to commit; regenerated and verified live (the built artifact is byte-identical to a fresh regeneration of the post-change grammar).\n\nMEASURED COST OF THE FIX: table-neutral. `parser.c` is 2,926,093 bytes both ways; STATE_COUNT / SYMBOL_COUNT / PRODUCTION_ID_COUNT / TOKEN_COUNT / FIELD_COUNT all identical; the entire delta is 13 action entries changing `REDUCE(sym_rule_entry, N, 0, P)` to `REDUCE(sym_rule_entry, N, 1, P)`. No new conflict is introduced — `[$.rule_entry]` already existed, and dynamic precedence is consulted only when versions are already being compared. Stdlib parse time indistinguishable across 3 runs each.\n\nALSO: `bool.anthill`'s \"Keep the tags clear of a preceding comment line — see WI-893\" is DELETED — the constraint no longer exists, and a library must not carry a workaround for a fixed defect. The rule went to `docs/kernel-language.md` §\"A head is an atom\" (and says convert-time, not load-time — the WI-850 distinction); CLAUDE.md deliberately untouched.\n\nADJACENT, NOT FILED, because it is documented design and not a defect: an operation's TRAILING `[...]` is absorbed into the return type as an application — DRIVEN, `operation get_values(self: GPS) -> Vec3 [Vec3FromConstDoublePtr3]` silently becomes `Vec3[Vec3FromConstDoublePtr3]`. `grammar.js`'s `meta_clause` comment states this explicitly and the `meta` keyword is the disambiguating vehicle provided for it. Different mechanism (static `prec(1)`, no comment, no GLR tie), and no repo file uses the absorbed spelling.\n\nREVIEW: `/code-review` (4 agents). The altitude agent found the `fact` half-fix and that the refusal is the bias's justification rather than mere defence; the reuse agent found that `parse_rule_body_goals_ir` already was the unconstrained-term harness the parse tests needed (so `parse_rule_head_ir` became a thin `parse_term_ir` over it, rather than a new probe-wrapper twin) and that `is_data_literal_kind` should be folded into `is_term_kind` so the two lists cannot drift; the efficiency agent caught that the file's \"STDLIB LOADS: TWO\" header counted THREE. Full workspace suite GREEN via `rustland/scripts/test.sh`; tree-sitter corpus 190/190.", at: "2026-07-31T10:25:33Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-893", description: some(value: "A `[simp]` ATTRIBUTE IS SILENTLY EATEN WHEN A COMMENT LINE PRECEDES THE RULE ENTRY — a GLR tie in `tree-sitter-anthill/grammar.js` drops the attribute and turns it into a SEPARATE JUNK RULE, with no diagnostic. Found by a review agent while driving WI-887, and it is what made that ticket's central measurement an ARTIFACT.\nMEASURED, by `npx tree-sitter parse` against the checked-in `src/parser.c`. Inside a `rule { }` block, an entry carrying `[simp]` parses correctly — UNLESS a comment line immediately precedes it, in which case the parse yields `rule_entry [n,4]-[n,38]` with NO `meta_block`, followed by a SECOND `rule_entry [n,39]-[n,45]` whose head is a `collection_literal` — the `[simp]` text re-parsed as a new rule headed by the list `[simp]`. Bisected to exactly that trigger: `(entry, entry [simp], entry [simp])` yields TWO meta_blocks and the rules fire; `(entry, --comment, entry [simp], entry [simp])` yields ONE meta_block plus one collection_literal. Confirmed on the real file both ways: `bool.anthill` with a `-- if-then-else` line above `ite_true` parses ONE meta_block; delete that one comment line and it parses TWO.\nTHE CONFLICT IS DECLARED: `tree-sitter-anthill/grammar.js`:52-53 names exactly this pair. This is the WI-448 hazard in a new production pair, and the remedy there was `prec.dynamic` (recorded as the `tree-sitter-glr-comment-tie` lesson: tree-sitter `extras` — comments — are NAMED, so they participate in the tie).\nWHY IT IS WORSE THAN A PARSE QUIRK: it is SILENT and it produces a WRONG PROGRAM rather than an error. The attribute vanishes, the equation becomes inert (WI-881: `[simp]` is the ENABLEMENT), and a junk fact is asserted — stdlib load goes 2362 facts with the comments, 2361 without. Nothing warns. The repo's standing principle is a loud error over a silent skip, and this is a silent skip in the PARSER, upstream of every check.\nTHE DAMAGE IT ALREADY DID, and why this is filed rather than fixed inline: driving WI-887 I tagged `bool.anthill`'s two `ite` case laws `[simp]`, and a `-- if-then-else` comment sat immediately above the FIRST one. So `ite_true`'s tag was eaten and `ite_false`'s survived. I then probed exactly `Bool.ite(true, ...)` — the one branch whose tag was gone — measured `OperationBodyMissing`, and concluded that tagging buys nothing. It buys everything: with the comment removed so both tags parse, and the operation DECLARED, `Bool.ite(true, 10, 20)` -> 10 and `Bool.ite(false, 10, 20)` -> 20. A half-eaten pair of case laws is the \"half-backed looks backed\" failure WI-887's own prose warns about, arriving from the parser.\nSWEEP: a review agent parsed EVERY `.anthill` file in the repo (stdlib, anthill-stl, cpp-gen, examples, anthill-todo) and found exactly ONE dropped attribute — the one my WI-887 edit introduced, now reverted. So the shipped tree is CLEAN today and this is a latent trap, not an outstanding corruption. That is precisely why it needs a test: the next author to comment a `[simp]` law will hit it, and nothing will tell them.\nACCEPTANCE: a comment line immediately preceding a `[simp]`-tagged `rule { }` entry parses with the `meta_block` attached (tree-sitter corpus case, BOTH orders, plus the no-comment control); a RUST load-level test asserts the rule is indexed as a `[simp]` equation and FIRES, since corpus tests do not run under `cargo test` and this defect is invisible to a green suite; the junk `collection_literal`-headed rule no longer appears (assert the stdlib fact count is unchanged by adding or removing a comment above a tagged law); the parser is regenerated (`npx tree-sitter generate`) and the generated file committed; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. CONSIDER, and answer explicitly: whether a rule head that is a bare `collection_literal` is EVER legal — if not, refusing it at load is a cheap second line of defence that would have made this loud. REFERENCE: WI-448 / the `prec.dynamic` remedy, WI-881 (`[simp]` is the enablement, so a dropped tag is a dropped definition), WI-887 (the ticket whose measurement this invalidated)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-31T10:25:35Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-897", description: some(value: "SMT-GEN MATCHES OPERATIONS BY NAME STRING, AND WI-894 UNBLOCKED THE PRINCIPLED FORM. Every `map_*_op` table in `rustland/anthill-smt-gen/src/lib.rs` (`map_arith_op`, `map_trig_op`, `map_unary_op`, `map_bool_connective`, `map_inequality_op`, `is_ite_op`) decides what an operation MEANS by comparing `qualified_name_of(functor)` against literal strings, each spelling a fully-qualified / sort-qualified / bare triple. WI-680 recorded the hazard the bare arm carries: a user's own same-named op is indistinguishable from the prelude's and is silently reinterpreted as the SMT builtin.\nWHAT CHANGED: the call site already has `functor: Symbol` and `&self.kb` in hand (`lib.rs`, `translate_expr`'s `Expr::Apply` arm), so the test can be a SYMBOL-IDENTITY compare — `kb.try_resolve_symbol(\"anthill.prelude.Bool.ite\") == Some(functor)` — which no user name can collide with. `is_eq_functor` in the same file already does exactly this, so the pattern is in-repo and proven; it was simply unavailable to the rest because a rule-introduced functor had NO qualified identity until WI-894 scoped it. WI-887's comment on `is_ite_op` states the opposite ('nothing more specific in front of it') and is now out of date.\nSCOPE AND WHY IT IS ITS OWN TICKET: this is table-wide (6 predicates, ~40 name arms), it changes signatures from `&str` to `Symbol` + `&KnowledgeBase`, and each arm needs a decision about whether the bare fallback stays for un-imported rule-body uses (which still intern bare — `wi680_ite_lowering_test`'s own fixture relies on it). Doing it inside WI-894 would have been an unmeasured sweep. ACCEPTANCE: a user-defined op sharing a short name with an SMT builtin is NOT reinterpreted (a test drives the collision WI-680 described); the existing smt-gen suites stay green; full workspace green. REFERENCE: WI-680 (the recorded hazard), WI-894 (which made a rule functor nameable), WI-887 (whose `is_ite_op` comment this supersedes)."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-894", description: some(value: "A RULE-INTRODUCED FUNCTOR IS NOT SORT-SCOPED — it is one BARE GLOBAL NAME, so two sorts defining the same short name silently share one definition and the loser's own laws are ignored INSIDE ITS OWN SORT. Silent wrong answer on a program that LOADS CLEAN, which is the worst category. Found by the user while reviewing WI-887.\nMEASURED, one file, two sorts, the second deliberately INVERTED so a collision is visible rather than inferred:\n  sort A  rule pick(true, ?t, ?_) = ?t [simp]  /  rule pick(false, ?_, ?e) = ?e [simp]\n          operation drive(n: Int64) -> Int64 = pick(true, 10, 20)   -> 10  (correct)\n  sort B  rule pick(true, ?_, ?e) = ?e [simp]  /  rule pick(false, ?t, ?_) = ?t [simp]\n          operation drive(n: Int64) -> Int64 = pick(true, 10, 20)   -> 10  (WRONG; B's own laws say 20)\nLoads clean, no diagnostic. A's definition wins for BOTH sorts. Sort B cannot use its own rules.\nTHE MECHANISM: a `[simp]` rule head introduces a FUNCTOR, not a namespace-qualified operation symbol. The symbol stays `SymbolDef::Unresolved`, and `qualified_name_of` (kb/mod.rs:1444) returns the RAW SHORT NAME for an unresolved symbol — so `pick` in sort A and `pick` in sort B are literally the same interned name. Contrast a DECLARED operation, which is qualified: `anthill.prelude.Bool.and`. So the two kinds of member a sort can introduce are scoped OPPOSITELY, and only one of them says so.\nTHE SECOND HALF, AND WHY THIS IS NOT A ONE-LINE FIX: there is today NO WAY TO IMPORT A RULE-INTRODUCED FUNCTOR. WI-887 measured that `import anthill.prelude.Bool.{ite}` is `unresolved import` once `ite` stops being a declared operation — an import names an operation or a sort, never an equation's LHS head. The stdlib RELIES on the current bare-global behaviour: `ordered.anthill` and `int64.anthill` write bare `ite(...)` in laws and it reaches `bool.anthill`'s `[simp]` rules only because the name is global. So SCOPING ALONE WOULD BREAK THE STDLIB. Both halves are needed together: a scope for a rule-introduced functor, and a way to name one from another sort or file.\nWHY IT MATTERS BEYOND THE TOY: WI-887 makes `anthill.prelude.Bool.ite` exactly such a functor, so the PRELUDE now claims the unqualified global name `ite`, and any user writing their own `ite` rules collides with it silently. It is also why `anthill-smt-gen`'s `is_ite_op` (lib.rs) can only match the bare `\"ite\"` — there is nothing more specific to match, and WI-680 already recorded that short-name matching can reinterpret a same-named user op. That hazard is now unavoidable rather than incidental.\nACCEPTANCE: the two-sort program above is either CORRECT (each sort uses its own rules) or REFUSED at load naming both definitions — not silently merged; whichever is chosen, a test drives the INVERTED pair so a merge cannot pass; the stdlib's existing cross-file bare `ite` uses keep working, with the naming path they use stated explicitly (if scoping lands, say how `ordered.anthill` reaches `bool.anthill`'s rules); the answer says whether a rule-introduced functor is scoped to its SORT, its NAMESPACE, or its FILE, and why; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. CONSIDER: whether the two kinds of sort member should be scoped alike at all, or whether a rule functor is deliberately global and the real fix is refusing a DUPLICATE definition loudly. REFERENCE: WI-887 (which made `ite` such a functor, and measured that the member import does not resolve), WI-680 (the short-name matching hazard in smt-gen), WI-893 (the other silent `[simp]` defect found in the same review)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-31T13:19:58Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-899", description: some(value: "A RULE CLAUSE ON A BUILTIN-BACKED NAME IS INERT AT SLD, AND NOTHING SAYS SO. Once WI-896 made a rule head functor resolve rather than declare, a head naming an already-resolving name contributes a clause to that symbol — and if the symbol carries a resolver BuiltinTag, the builtin decides the goal before any clause is consulted. The clause loads clean and is never reached.\nMEASURED (`wi896_labeled_predicate_head_test::a_clause_on_a_builtin_backed_name_is_inert_at_sld`, which pins today's behaviour and will FAIL when this lands): with `rule or(?a) :- p896(?a)` and `rule or(?a, ?b) :- p896(?a), p896(?b)` in one namespace, the 1-ary goal yields the user's clause (1 solution) while the 2-ary goal yields the kernel disjunction's choice points and never reaches the 2-ary clause. So ARITY decides, silently: the clause is dead exactly at the builtin's own arity.\nNOT INTRODUCED BY WI-896, WHICH IS WHY IT IS ITS OWN TICKET. The smt-gen `rule bound: gte(?x, 3.0) :- gte(?x, 5.0)` lemma has ALWAYS been a clause on the builtin-backed `anthill.prelude.PartialOrd.gte` — inert at SLD, cited only by the SMT backend, and that is the intended use. So the refusal cannot be 'head resolves to a builtin': that criterion refuses the 9 smt-gen/prove targets. WI-896 only widened WHICH names reach the state (a bare `or`/`not` head now joins the labeled ones), while REMOVING a worse silence in the same breath — the old behaviour minted `<ns>.or` and re-pointed every bare `or` in the scope, measured at 0 solutions for a disjunction two lines down.\nWHAT THIS TICKET MUST DECIDE is which of the two uses is which, since the source looks identical: a citable LEMMA about a builtin-backed operation (keep, silent inertness is correct) versus an attempted DEFINITION the resolver will never run (refuse, or diagnose). Candidate discriminators, none settled: the target's SymbolKind (`Operation` for `gte`, and `anthill.kernel.or`/`push_choice`/`cut`/`find_dictionary` are control primitives that are not operations at all); whether the clause's arity equals the builtin's; whether the rule is cited by any `using`. Note `eq`/`unify`/`struct_eq` must stay permitted in head position regardless — `field.anthill` writes three bodied equation-shaped rules whose head functor IS the connective.\nACCEPTANCE: the two uses are told apart by a stated rule; a clause the resolver can never reach is refused or diagnosed rather than silently loaded; the smt-gen policy renders and the `prove_*` family still pass; full workspace green. REFERENCE: WI-896 (which made the head resolve), WI-523/WI-526 (the original 'a user's own `or` must not merge with the kernel primitive' concern, now measured), WI-895 (the sibling silent-inertness gap in a rule BODY)."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-896", description: some(value: "A LABELED PREDICATE RULE'S HEAD FUNCTOR STILL TRAVELS TO THE GLOBAL NAMESPACE — the one shape WI-894 left unscoped, and the spec says so explicitly (`docs/kernel-language.md` §'The label rule is a frozen boundary, not a semantics').\nTHE ASYMMETRY, MEASURED: `rule gte(?x, 3.0) :- gte(?x, 5.0)` (unlabeled) mints a scope-local `<ns>.gte`; the LABELED twin `rule bound: gte(...) :- gte(...)` mints nothing and rides the bare global name. Same head, same meaning, opposite scoping — decided by whether a label is present.\nWHY WI-894 FROZE IT RATHER THAN FIXING IT: removing the label carve-out was tried and BROKE 9 TARGETS (every smt-gen policy render, the whole `prove_*` family). Those fixtures write `rule bound: gte(?x, 3.0) :- gte(?x, 5.0)` as a LEMMA ABOUT the prelude's `gte`, and minting a local `<ns>.gte` silently changes what the lemma is about — so the carve-out is load-bearing, not cosmetic.\nWHY THE OBVIOUS UNIFICATION DOES NOT WORK, checked before filing: gating the predicate path on `implicit_qualified` (the discriminator WI-894 uses for the EQUATION path, where it is correct) re-triggers WI-523 — a user's own `or`/`not` predicate head must keep its own Goal symbol rather than rerouting to the kernel primitive — and `gte` is a prelude name too, so it lands on the same side as `or`. The available refinement is a TWO-LEVEL split, `kernel_vocab_qualified` (never introducible) vs `prelude_qualified` (introducible), which does not by itself settle `gte`.\nSO THE REAL QUESTION IS A LANGUAGE ONE, and it is what this ticket must answer: when a predicate rule head names something no scope defines, is it DEFINING a new predicate or CONCLUDING about an existing one — and what in the source says which? Today the answer is 'the presence of a label', which is not a reason. ACCEPTANCE: the four cases (labeled/unlabeled x new-name/prelude-name) are each decided by a stated rule rather than by the label; the 9 targets above still pass; full workspace green. REFERENCE: WI-894 (which scoped the equation half and froze this one), WI-530 / WI-523 (the implicit-prelude decision and the regression a broad skip caused)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-31T14:37:30Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-757", description: some(value: "WI-730 follow-up (WI-722 macro contract): a compile-time MACRO that FAILS has no way to report WHY. try_expand_macro (kb/simp_rewrite.rs) maps a macro's Err to None -- the macro 'declines', its template call is kept, and the user sees whatever downstream type error that residual template produces. That was a reasonable contract while a decline meant 'not applicable yet', but WI-730 made 'the row lambda cannot be translated to a query' a DEFINITIVE, user-caused macro failure, and it is now the primary failure mode of where/join: 'person_row.where(lambda c -> ite(true, true, false))' correctly fails to LOAD, but reports 'type mismatch in guarded_of.r (op-arg): expected NodeOccurrence, got Relation[...]' -- naming neither the offending condition nor the reason, while compile_condition's own 'a goal-expressible predicate ... it has no meaning as a query goal' message is discarded. The typer must NOT special-case this (repo rule: the typer stays universal, never keyed on op identity), so the fix belongs at the macro seam: give a macro a DIAGNOSTIC channel -- a way to distinguish 'decline, keep the template' (the current contract, for a macro that is merely not ready) from 'REJECT with this message', and have the loader surface the latter as a LoadError at the redex span. Watch: a macro may be attempted more than once (re-entry cap, repeated simp attempts), so a rejection must be keyed/deduped so one genuine failure is not reported N times, and an early attempt that later succeeds must not leave a stale error. ACCEPTANCE: an untranslatable where/join condition reports the compiler's own 'cannot translate' text at the condition's span; the 'decline quietly' path stays available and unchanged for macros that are not applicable; wi730_untranslatable_condition_is_a_load_error and wi730_bare_column_projection_is_not_a_condition assert the real message rather than the residual 'guarded_of' one; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-730"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-31T15:30:53Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-901", description: some(value: "WI-757 follow-up (proposal 043.1 §7): an anthill-written macro's rejection has no SPAN. WI-757 (and its follow-on) gave a macro two negative outcomes -- DECLINE (keep the template) and REJECT (a load error carrying the macro's own words) -- and an anthill-written macro reaches the reject channel by RAISING: 'operation m(x: NodeOccurrence) -> NodeOccurrence effects Error[E] = Error.raise(payload)', with the payload rendered by eval::render_raised_payload. That works (wi757_macro_diagnostic_test::an_anthill_macro_rejects_by_raising) because WI-702/054's effectful-rewrite gate now EXEMPTS a macro at the [simp] RHS head -- the macro is the rewriter, evaluated away at compile time, so its call never reaches runtime and cannot be duplicated/reordered/dropped there. What is still missing is PRECISION: 'raise' carries a payload and no occurrence, so a raised rejection is located at the REDEX, while a HOST macro (EvalError::MacroRejected) additionally names the offending sub-expression -- 'where(lambda c -> Person.alwaysTrue())' points at the condition, not at the whole call. The shape that closes it is an effect-free reflect op, e.g. 'operation reject(message: String, at: NodeOccurrence) -> Nothing', whose eval builtin returns EvalError::MacroRejected{detail, span: Some(at.span)}. NO CLIENT TODAY: both macros in the tree (Relation.guarded_of / conjoin_of) are host builtins that already use the precise channel, so this is unbuilt on purpose. Build it when the first anthill-written macro needs to point at a sub-expression. ACCEPTANCE: an anthill-written macro can locate its rejection at an occurrence it names, asserted by span (not just by message); raising without a span keeps working and stays at the redex; the decline path stays unchanged; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-757"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-904", description: some(value: "`typing::is_macro` materializes a full OpInfoRecord to answer a two-field boolean, on a path that runs per fired [simp] rewrite. Surfaced by WI-902's review; PRE-EXISTING since WI-722, which is why it is its own ticket rather than folded into that delivery.\n\nis_macro (kb/typing.rs) -> lookup_operation_info_full -> op_info::lookup_operation_info -> op_info_from_signature (kb/op_info.rs), which CLONES all six per-field signature vectors -- params, return_type, effects, type_params, requires, ensures -- of which is_macro reads exactly `params` and `return_type` (and lookup_operation_info_full drops `ensures`/`meta`/`body_node` immediately). VERIFIED by reading the chain.\n\nTWO COSTS. (a) Operation head: 1-5 Vec alloc/free pairs plus N Value clones per call, to decide a boolean. (b) Non-operation head: `kb.op_record` misses, so lookup_operation_info falls to the linear scan over every anthill.reflect.OperationInfo fact (271 `operation` declarations in stdlib alone, before prelude/builtins) and returns None -- reachable because substitute_to_occurrence builds EVERY functor RHS as an Expr::Apply, so a [simp] rule whose RHS head is an ENTITY CONSTRUCTOR (`rule f(?x) = some(?x) [simp]`) takes the O(N_ops) branch on every fire. Review measured/derived (b); confirm the constructor case by driving before fixing.\n\nSecond-order: try_expand_macro's own doc (kb/simp_rewrite.rs) claims it gates on is_macro \"BEFORE building the argument vector ... so the common path allocates nothing\". The first two conjuncts (as_expr, named/type-args empty) are free; is_macro is not, so the sentence is FALSE as written. Fix the comment with the code.\n\nTHE FIX HAS TWO ESTABLISHED PRECEDENTS in the same module, both introduced for this exact reason: op_info::operation_is_declared (WI-818) and op_info::declared_arity (WI-886), whose doc says \"reading one number should not build an OpInfoRecord, whose construction clones every per-field Vec to be dropped immediately\". Add the third sibling -- e.g. op_info::is_occurrence_signature(kb, op) -> bool -- reading kb.op_record(op).and_then(|r| r.signature.as_ref()) and testing is_occurrence_type over return_type/params BY REFERENCE. Zero-alloc, and it also kills cost (b): a symbol with no record is not an operation, hence not a macro. MIRROR THE TWO TIERS of lookup_operation_info exactly (WI-656 cached record, then the pre-build_op_signatures fact scan) so the classifier's verdict cannot change -- a semantic change here silently turns macros on or off.\n\nSTRONGER OPTION, if measurement justifies it: macro classification is load-stable (OpSignature's own doc: \"the typer rewrites bodies, never signatures, so a cached copy never goes stale within a load\"), so the macro set could be precomputed in build_op_signatures and is_macro become an O(1) set hit at all four call sites, with a KB containing no macros paying one emptiness check.\n\nACCEPTANCE: is_macro answers without constructing an OpInfoRecord and without the O(N_ops) scan for a non-operation head, with the two-tier verdict preserved (a test pinning is_macro's answer for an operation, a constructor, and an unknown symbol, before and after); try_expand_macro's \"allocates nothing\" comment states what the code does; wi722/wi757/wi902 macro suites stay green; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-902", description: some(value: "WI-722 gap found by WI-757's review: the DOT-RULE firing site never macro-expands. There are TWO typer-side [simp] firing sites. simp_rewrite::try_fire builds the RHS template and then calls try_expand_macro (so 'rule where(?r,?c) <=> guarded_of(?r,?c) [simp]' runs the macro at compile time); typing::try_fire_dot_rule -- the WI-279 INC2 sort-scoped dot-rule path, 'rule dr: dot_apply(?e, member, ?x) = rhs(...) [simp]' -- ends at substitute_to_occurrence and returns the template with NO expansion. So a macro-headed dot rule silently declines. MEASURED: a sort with 'operation wrap(r: NodeOccurrence, x: NodeOccurrence) -> NodeOccurrence = make_apply(...)' plus 'rule dr: dot_apply(?e, bump, ?x) = wrap(?e, ?x) [simp]' reports 'type mismatch in wrap.r (op-arg): expected NodeOccurrence, got Holder' -- the pattern vars arrive as their VALUE types because the macro never ran. Consequences: (a) a macro is unusable from a dot rule; (b) WI-757's rejection channel is unreachable there, so an untranslatable input falls back to exactly the residual-template message WI-757 exists to eliminate. Pre-existing since WI-722 (WI-757 only made the asymmetry visible). Note also the ADJACENT gap the same review surfaced: an anthill-WRITTEN macro cannot DECLINE at all -- its only non-Node exits are a raise (now a REJECTION, WI-757) and a non-occurrence return (impossible, it is typed) -- so 043.1 §3.6's 'decline' half is host-only. Decide whether that needs a spelling (e.g. returning the input occurrence unchanged is already a no-op rewrite) or whether it is fine as is. ACCEPTANCE: a macro-headed [simp] DOT rule expands at compile time (a test where the macro's output functor differs from the macro name, so expansion is observable, as wi722_compile_time_macro_test does); a macro that REJECTS from a dot rule reports through WI-757's channel at the redex; the decline path is unchanged at both sites; the two sites share the expansion step rather than each having a copy; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-757"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-31T17:11:53Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-903", description: some(value: "WI-582 x WI-279 gap surfaced while delivering WI-902: a TYPED PATTERN BOUND on a DOT rule is SILENTLY IGNORED. load.rs installs `?x: T` bounds (`install_rule_type_bounds`) on any `[simp]`/`[unfold]` EQUATIONAL head, and refuses every other rule shape loudly precisely because \"a non-rewrite rule would silently ignore it\". A dot rule -- `rule dr: dot_apply(?e, m, ?x: T) = rhs(...) [simp]` -- passes that check, but the site the loader names as the enforcer (the resolver's `apply_eq_rules` / `typed_pattern_bounds_hold`) never sees a `dot_apply` head on the path the rule is written for: a dot rule is matched by `typing::try_fire_dot_rule` against a surface `Expr::DotApply` occurrence, and that function never reads `kb.rule_type_bounds`. The other typer site, `simp_rewrite::try_fire`, SKIPS typed-bound rules outright (WI-582), so the two firing sites disagree about which rules they fire.\n\nMEASURED (2026-07-31, delivering WI-902): inside `sort Holder`, `rule dr: dot_apply(?e, bump, ?x: String) = wrap(?e, ?x) [simp]` with `operation consumer(h: Holder) -> Int64 = ?h.bump(5)` -- an UNSATISFIABLE bound (`5` is not a String) -- loads CLEAN and FIRES: the stored consumer body is the rewrite's output (`wrapped(...)`), not a `bump` no-match. The bound is consulted nowhere.\n\nSECOND CONSEQUENCE, recorded at `simp_rewrite::macro_expanded_rhs_head`: the WI-702/054 effectful-rewrite gate's macro exemption is keyed on the conditions `try_fire` applies, one of which is \"no typed-pattern bounds\". Since WI-902 the dot site DOES macro-expand, so an `Error`-declaring macro (WI-757's rejection channel) under a typed-bound dot rule is refused at load with the \"a directional rewrite DUPLICATES, REORDERS, or DROPS the matched call\" message even though its call is expanded away and never reaches runtime. MEASURED: the rejection AND that refusal are both reported for one such rule. Deliberately left conservative in WI-902 (loud, and refused before WI-902 too) rather than widening a safety gate around a rule shape that is itself broken.\n\nDECIDE AND FIX one of: (a) ENFORCE the bound in `try_fire_dot_rule` -- the typer holds each matched occurrence's stamped `inferred_type`, so the check is the occurrence-side twin of `typed_pattern_bounds_hold`; this is the honest reading of what the author wrote and makes `macro_expanded_rhs_head`'s exemption correct by construction. (b) REFUSE a typed-bound dot rule at LOAD, extending load.rs's existing loud check to a `dot_apply` LHS -- cheapest, makes the illegal state unrepresentable, and consistent with the rule the same check already states. Prefer (b) unless a client actually needs a bounded dot rule; note (b) is a behaviour change for any program relying on today's ignored bound (none in stdlib/examples -- verify).\n\nACCEPTANCE: a typed-bound dot rule either ENFORCES its bound (a receiver/arg outside it does not fire, asserted by a test whose bound is unsatisfiable, as the measurement above) or is REFUSED at load with a message naming the rule; the divergence note in `macro_expanded_rhs_head`'s doc comment is retired together with the case it describes; WI-582 and WI-279 dot-dispatch tests stay green; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-31T17:58:33Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-905", description: some(value: "WI-702/054 effectful-rewrite gate has a hole for a METHOD-SYNTAX RHS. typing::check_simp_effectful_ops collects the operation functors a [simp]/[unfold] rule mentions via collect_op_functors_in_value over the head equation, and its comment justifies not collecting from a dot_apply position with 'a DotApply redex never fires under simp (WI-279 try_fire returns None)'. That justification is STALE: since WI-279 INC2 the typer's try_fire_dot_rule DOES fire sort-scoped dot rules, and since WI-902 it instantiates and macro-expands their RHS. So a rule whose RHS is written in method syntax -- rule dr: dot_apply(?e, m, ?x) = ?e.effectful_method(?x) [simp] -- keeps its RHS as a dot_apply TERM, effectful_method is never collected, effect_row_blocking_equations is never consulted, and the rewrite splices an External/Modify call into every matching call site with no diagnostic. That is exactly the duplicate/reorder/drop hazard WI-702 exists to refuse. NOT introduced by WI-903 (which only made macro_expanded_rhs_head's typed-bounds condition exact) and NOT driven -- raised by /code-review on the WI-903 diff, code-read only. FIRST STEP: drive it (assert the source above loads clean today and that the effectful call survives into the stored body); if confirmed, either collect functors through a dot_apply RHS or refuse the shape. ACCEPTANCE: a [simp] rule with an effectful method-syntax RHS is refused (or its call collected) with a test that fails before the fix; the stale comment at check_simp_effectful_ops is corrected; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-906", description: some(value: "WI-903 follow-up: the typed-bound DOT-rule refusal is keyed on [simp] alone, which is wider than 'the typer can fire it', and the [unfold] carve-out rests on an UNMEASURED premise. WI-903 refuses a typed pattern bound on any rule matching simp_rewrite::is_typer_fired_dot_rule = is_simp_equation && stored LHS is Expr.dot_apply. Two residuals, both raised by /code-review on the WI-903 diff, both code-read not driven. (a) OVER-BROAD: typing::try_fire_dot_rule additionally applies a per-receiver ENCLOSING-SORT guard (same_sort_canonical(recv_sort, rule_domain(rid)) || sort_provides(...)). A dot_apply-headed [simp] rule whose rule_domain no receiver can conform to -- e.g. one written at NAMESPACE level, whose domain is not a sort at all -- can therefore never fire at the typer, so only the resolver fires it, and the resolver DOES enforce the bound (fire_simp_equation gates on is_directional_equation, which accepts [simp] as well as [unfold], then calls typed_pattern_bounds_hold). Such a rule is refused today with a message that says the bound would be silently ignored, which would be false for it, and no alternative spelling preserves its firing sites. (b) UNMEASURED PREMISE: WI-903 deliberately KEEPS the bound on an [unfold] dot rule on the grounds that the resolver enforces it there, but nothing drives a resolver fire against a dot_apply TERM redex -- wi903_typed_bound_dot_rule_test::typed_bound_on_an_unfold_dot_rule_is_kept asserts only that rule_type_bounds is installed (storage, not enforcement). If the resolver in fact never reaches such a redex, the [simp]/[unfold] asymmetry is unjustified and BOTH should be refused. FIRST STEP: drive (b) -- construct a goal carrying an Expr.dot_apply term and an [unfold] dot equation over it, and assert the bound decides firing. (b)'s answer settles (a): if the resolver never fires these, refuse both and delete the carve-out; if it does, narrow the refusal to rules whose rule_domain is a sort. ACCEPTANCE: the [simp]/[unfold] split is either justified by a driven enforcement test or removed; the doc comments at simp_rewrite::is_typer_fired_dot_rule and docs/kernel-language.md section 5.3 state whichever is true; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-742"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-889", description: some(value: "A HOST-SUPPLIED `const` HAS NO DECLARATIVE CHANNEL IN ANY LANGUAGE, so `anthill.prelude.Float.{infinity, negativeInfinity, nan}` are keyed by HARDCODED QUALIFIED NAME in every backend that realizes them — and cpp-gen's `render_as_float_special` is the LAST of the four hand-written per-carrier tables WI-886 set out to delete.\n\nWHAT WI-886 DID AND WHY THIS SURVIVED IT. WI-886 fed cpp-gen's per-carrier host surface from `operation_map` data (`kb.host_op_mappings()` filtered to `lang == \"cpp\"`), deleting `render_as_math_call` (21 entries), `render_as_math_constant` (3), and the per-carrier half of `render_as_operator` (5). `render_as_float_special` (rustland/anthill-cpp-gen/src/lib.rs) could not follow: `infinity`/`negativeInfinity`/`nan` are `SymbolKind::Const` (stdlib/anthill/prelude/float.anthill declares them as `const infinity: Float`, WI-084/proposal 039), and `build_host_op_mappings` (kb/load.rs) REFUSES a mapping whose name is not an OPERATION — deliberately, because WI-876 MEASURED that admitting other kinds made `operation_map { pt: \"ordered_compare\" }` over `entity pt(...)` register a comparison as the CONSTRUCTOR.\n\nSO THE GAP IS SYMMETRIC ACROSS HOSTS, not a cpp-gen wart. The rust runtime keys the same three by hardcoded qualified name too (`register_if_present(interp, \"anthill.prelude.Float.infinity\", float_infinity)`, eval/builtins.rs ~line 120, whose own comment notes a const's value source IS the builtin map because `force_const` reads `self.builtins`). Two backends, two hardcoded lists, one absent channel — which is exactly the shape WI-876 removed for operations and WI-886 for cpp codegen.\n\nWHAT A FIX LOOKS LIKE, none assumed. (a) A `const_map { infinity: \"...\" }` clause in a binding block, peer of `operation_map`, landing as an `anthill.realization.ConstMapping` fact — symmetric, and it costs a grammar rule plus a loader arm plus two readers. (b) WIDEN `operation_map` to accept a const and record the KIND in the fact, so one clause covers both; cheaper, but it re-opens exactly the hole WI-876's kind check closed unless the widening is by an explicit opt-in rather than by dropping the check. (c) Declare that a host-supplied const IS a nullary operation and delete the `const` spelling for these three — note `Float.pi`/`e`/`tau` ARE nullary operations already and reach both backends through `operation_map`, so the three IEEE specials are the ODD ONES OUT within their own sort; but WI-884 recorded that a nullary operation and a bare const differ at the CALL SITE (a `[simp]` head is an application, `in_bounds` writes the bare `minValue`), so this is not a rename.\n\nSCOPE NOTE: whatever is chosen must keep the WI-876 kind check's guarantee — a mapping must not be able to attach a host implementation to a symbol that is neither an operation nor a const (an entity constructor, a sort).\n\nACCEPTANCE: the three Float IEEE specials are realized from DATA in at least the rust and cpp backends, or the decision to keep them hardcoded is written down where `render_as_float_special` and `register_standard_builtins` define them, with the reason; `render_as_float_special` is deleted or its survival is justified in place; the WI-876 kind check still refuses a non-operation, non-const target (a test); full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh.\n\nSOURCE: WI-886, which references this ticket number from `render_as_float_special`'s doc comment."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-31T21:22:54Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-909", description: some(value: "THE IMPLICIT TIER IS ABSENT FROM TYPE POSITION: `Loader::remap_symbol_strict` and `Loader::remap_name` (rustland/anthill-core/src/kb/load.rs) run scope resolution, the dotted ladder and the `internal` diagnostics, then intern + `UnresolvedName`/`TypeName` — they never call `resolve_implicit`. So an implicit-tier SORT (`SortInfo`, `OperationInfo`, `FieldInfo`, `SortView`, the literal carriers) resolves bare in TERM and QUERY position but requires an explicit import in TYPE position. That is the term-vs-type asymmetry WI-752's headline symptom describes, one tier down. Currently UNEXERCISED: stdlib/anthill/reflect/typing.anthill imports all of them explicitly, which is why nothing fails. NOT DRIVEN: raised by /code-review's altitude pass on the WI-900 diff (which unified the tier's other four readers behind `resolve_implicit`), code-read only — stated as an asymmetry read, not a defect measured. FIRST STEP: drive it — a user file naming `SortInfo` bare in a type annotation with no import, against the same name bare in term position, and record which resolves. Then decide: extend the tier to type position (the tier's own doc says these names 'always denote their target'), or state that the type namespace is deliberately import-only and say so at both sites. ACCEPTANCE: the two positions agree, or the divergence is documented at `remap_symbol_strict` and tested; the reflect typing.anthill import stays valid either way; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-910", description: some(value: "NOTHING ENFORCES THAT THE IMPLICIT VOCAB'S SHORT NAMES ARE DISTINCT, so a duplicate would make one entry DEAD SILENTLY. `implicit_qualified` (rustland/anthill-core/src/kb/load.rs) resolves by LAST DOT-SEGMENT — `kernel_vocab_qualified(name).or_else(prelude_qualified(name))`, each a linear `find` over its `&[&str]` — so if two entries ever share a short name (within a list, or across the two), the first wins and the second can never be reached by any of the four consumers. Checked at filing: all 61 entries (28 KERNEL_VOCAB_QUALIFIED + 33 PRELUDE_QUALIFIED) have distinct short names today, so this is a guard against rot, not a live bug. It is the second way these tables can go quietly wrong; WI-900 closed the first (an entry whose TARGET is not declared, now pinned by `implicit_target_orphans` + `wi900_implicit_tier_agreement_test::every_implicit_target_is_declared_by_the_standard_load`). Deliberately NOT folded into WI-900: it is a different invariant about the same table, and the ticket-spinoff rule says a surviving follow-up becomes its own ticket. ACCEPTANCE: a test (or a const-time check) fails when two implicit entries share a short name, naming both; the check lives beside the tables like `implicit_target_orphans` does; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-900", description: some(value: "AN IMPLICIT NAME WHOSE TARGET IS NOT LOADED FALLS TO A BARE GLOBAL, AND A RULE HEAD THEN COLLAPSES ACROSS SCOPES — WI-894's defect class surviving inside its own fix. Two spellings of the name ladder's fallback tier disagree: `scan_rule_goal` / `rule_introduced_functor_name` (rustland/anthill-core/src/kb/load.rs) skip minting when `implicit_qualified(name).is_some()` — a STATIC const lookup — while `remap_name_str_inner` (same file) resolves the tier only if the target is actually present, `implicit_qualified(name).and_then(|qn| by_qualified_name.get(qn))`, and otherwise CONTINUES to the bare `intern(name)`. When the target is missing the head is neither captured nor resolved, so it interns bare: one global name.\nMEASURED. In a KB built with `register_prelude` + `register_standard_builtins` and NO stdlib files (the configuration several test harnesses and any embedder use), this file loads CLEAN with zero errors, mints neither `probe896.A.and` nor `probe896.B.and`, and leaves both sorts' clauses on one bare global `and`:\n  namespace probe896\n    sort A  fact pa896(1)  rule and(?x) :- pa896(?x)  end\n    sort B  fact pb896(2)  rule and(?x) :- pb896(?x)  end\n  end\nWith the full stdlib loaded the divergence does not bite — all 18 sampled implicit targets (`Bool.and`, `Numeric.neg`, `SourceSpan.source_span`, `reflect.as_term`, `kernel.or`, `reflect.not`, `PartialOrd.gte`, `List.cons`, `kernel.cut`, `push_choice`, `find_dictionary`, `struct_eq`, `Int64.div`, `BigInt.to_bigint`, `SortView`, `Expr.dot_apply`, `field_access`, `ListLiteral`) are present, so the static and loaded readings agree. The exposure is embedders and stdlib-less harnesses.\nNOT A NEW HOLE, WIDENED: WI-894 introduced the static check on the EQUATION path; WI-896 extended the same check to the PREDICATE path and so extended the hole with it. It is recorded at the guard rather than left to be rediscovered.\nWHY THE OBVIOUS FIX IS NOT OBVIOUS, and why this is its own ticket: `resolve_name_in_kb_opt` (load.rs) already spells the whole ladder read-only — scope, `resolve_dotted_in_kb`, then the implicit tier GATED on `by_qualified_name` — and routing `scan_rule_goal` through it would close the gap in ~4 lines AND give the ladder one owner instead of six partial spellings. But that is exactly the `by_qualified_name`-at-scan-time check WI-530's feedback records as MEASURED FLAKY ('passed in isolation, failed full-binary intermittently; root cause undiagnosed'), which is why WI-530 chose the static const. So this ticket must first REPRODUCE OR REFUTE that flakiness — pass 3 now runs after passes 1-2 across every file, so the population order may no longer be the one that broke, and the ticket should say which.\nALSO IN SCOPE, both found alongside: `scan_rule_goal`'s `!matches!(..., NotFound)` treats an AMBIGUOUS resolution as resolved (no mint) while `remap_name_str_inner` pushes the error and falls to a bare intern — benign today since the load blocks, but a third disagreement that should be decided explicitly rather than left silent. And the `name.contains('.')` guard is a SPEC rule ('a qualified subject references'), deliberately stricter than `resolve_dotted_in_kb`, not a ladder tier — it should be commented as such so it is not mistaken for a fourth spelling.\nACCEPTANCE: the guard and the ladder agree on which names fall through, or the divergence is a stated and tested decision; the WI-530 flakiness is reproduced or refuted by name; a stdlib-less KB no longer collapses two sorts' same-named rule heads onto one global; full workspace green. REFERENCE: WI-896 (which widened it), WI-894 (which introduced it), WI-530/WI-523 (the static-vs-loaded decision and the flakiness), WI-752 (`resolve_dotted_in_kb`, the precedent for giving the ladder one owner)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-31T21:42:10Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-900", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED 2026-07-31 (commit 0cad10bd, branch wi900-implicit-tier-agreement). Full workspace 3914/0 green.\n\nWI-530 FLAKINESS: REFUTED, and the ticket's stated reason for expecting it was wrong. The ticket guessed 'pass 3 now runs after passes 1-2 across every file, so the population order may no longer be the one that broke' — checked: git show of load.rs at the commit BEFORE WI-530 (78918b77, 2026-06-19) has the identical all-pass-1-then-all-pass-2-then-all-pass-3 structure, so that ordering was ALREADY in place when WI-523's attempt was measured flaky. Whatever broke then, it was not that. Re-measured instead: three full-workspace runs and five full anthill-core runs with the loaded gate in place, identical counts, zero intermittency. The stronger argument is that the suite is its own detector — the stdlib load runs this rung on every implicit-named law (eq/cons/or/gte/...), so a spurious 'not loaded' immediately re-fires WI-530's own regression rather than flaking.\n\nMEASURED BLAST RADIUS: zero in any stdlib-bearing KB. implicit_target_orphans over both const tables returns EMPTY under the standard load, so the static and the loaded readings differ on nothing there; the change is confined to KBs that do not load the declaring file. Ten targets are absent from a bare register_prelude KB (as_term, source_span, occurrence_owner/span/term, sub_occurrences, neg, div, mod, and) — those are the names whose rule-head treatment changes.\n\nTHE AMBIGUOUS DISAGREEMENT, decided: an Ambiguous name DENOTES (several things is still something), so the head references it and the ambiguity is the reference's diagnostic. Not a divergence from the codebase after all — Loader::qualified_name_resolves already had this exact policy with the reason stated ('an ambiguity is a real finding with a real error to emit'), which /code-review caught me framing as novel. Minting would be the silent outcome: a scope-local Goal outranks the ambiguous candidates. Pinned by an_ambiguous_head_is_a_reference_so_the_load_is_refused.\n\nTHE contains('.') GUARD is commented as the SPEC rule it is, and the coupling it creates is now CHECKED: name_denotes_for_rule_head omits the dotted rung purely because that refusal lives 4k lines upstream, so it carries a debug_assert instead of a paragraph.\n\nSHAPE CHANGE FROM REVIEW (the biggest win): my first cut wrote the tier a FOURTH time and explained the agreement in a 46-line rung-by-rung doc comment — the ticket's own 'six partial spellings' complaint, committed again. All four reviewers converged on it. Now resolve_implicit(kb, name) owns implicit_qualified + the by_qualified_name gate and all four consumers call it; implicit_qualified is private; the doc shrank by ~35 lines. I did NOT route the guard through resolve_name_in_kb_opt (two reviewers' suggestion): after the rung extraction there is no duplication left to remove, it would double the resolve_in_scope walk, and it would couple the guard to that function's Ambiguous-falls-through policy — which the altitude reviewer independently identified as a silent skip (now WI-907). Also skipped: deleting kernel_vocab_qualified/prelude_qualified in favour of one chained find (the two names document the precedence, and WI-896 records them as the axis of a possible future two-level split); merging tests 2/3 to save a stdlib load (per-claim naming, as wi894's header records).\n\nFOLLOW-UPS FILED, all code-read not driven, all said so in their text: WI-907 (resolve_name_in_kb_opt resolves past an Ambiguous silently — the third of three policies, and the silent one), WI-908 (KnowledgeBase::resolve_name_in_global still ranks the absolute rung first, pre-WI-752), WI-909 (the implicit tier is absent from TYPE position — remap_symbol_strict/remap_name — so an implicit sort is bare-resolvable in term position and import-only in type position), WI-910 (nothing checks the implicit tables' short names are distinct; a duplicate silently kills the later entry).\n\nALSO: lifted the verbatim stdlib-less KB builder to common::load_kb_bare (wi720_ctor_order_test and wi458_head_span_occurrence_test held byte-identical copies); ~270 other test-local KB builders were NOT swept — different shapes, out of scope.", at: "2026-07-31T21:42:10Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-911", description: some(value: "`register_extent_owner` refuses an `internal` functor with `UnresolvableName` — the mount takes only the ladder's VISIBLE half. WI-908 routed `KnowledgeBase::resolve_name_in_global` through the shared ladder (`resolve_name_in_kb_opt`), which runs `resolve_dotted_in_kb` with `DottedVisibility::VisibleOnly`; the ladder's DIAGNOSTIC half (`Loader::forbid_if_dotted_internal`, load.rs ~8216 — an `Any` pass run after `VisibleOnly` comes up empty, documented as 'for the positions that have an error channel') is a `Loader` method and unreachable from kb/extent.rs. So a host name that EXISTS and is HIDDEN is reported as one that does not exist — the misleading-diagnostic case WI-752 split the ladder to avoid. The mount HAS an error channel (`ExtentRegError`). DRIVEN: wi908_global_name_ladder_test::an_internal_member_is_not_mountable_from_global pins today's `UnresolvableName` and says at the site that it is loud but imprecise. FIRST STEP: lift the `Any` pass off `Loader` so a free `&KnowledgeBase` caller can run it. ACCEPTANCE: mounting an `internal` functor reports a distinct `ExtentRegError::ForbiddenInternal { functor }` naming the owning scope; the WI-908 test flips to it; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-912", description: some(value: "THE SAME DEFECT CLASS WI-908 FIXED, still live in persistence: `persistence/term_ser.rs::resolve_entity_functor` (~289) reads a functor name from a PERSISTED file (human-authored, KB-external — structurally identical to an extent-owner name) with a hand-rolled ladder: absolute `by_qualified_name` FIRST, then a BARE SHORT-NAME GLOBAL FALLBACK via `name.rsplit('.')` — the WI-476 global scan the shared ladder deliberately deleted. No scope, no head-qualification rung, no visibility gate. Two more spellings in the same file: `resolve_variant_sym` (~553/561 and ~636/641) builds `format!(\"{}.{}\", kb.qualified_name_of(sort), key)` — `dotted_by_head` re-spelled locally — then an absolute rung, then `intern`. That final `intern` is WI-894's hazard: two scopes' same-spelled names collapse onto one bare global. NOT DRIVEN: code-read, raised by /code-review's altitude pass on the WI-908 diff. FIRST STEP: drive it — persist a fact whose functor short name is ambiguous across two namespaces, reload, assert which symbol it lands on. ACCEPTANCE: each of the three reads `KnowledgeBase::resolve_name_in_global` (or states why a persisted name is a different question, at the site); the short-name fallback is gone or justified; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-913", description: some(value: "FOUR MORE host-supplied-name positions resolve absolute-only, each a WI-908 twin (code-read, NOT driven; from /code-review's altitude pass on the WI-908 diff). (1) `anthill-stl/src/reflect/builtins.rs::lookup_symbol_op` (~750): the anthill-visible `lookup_symbol(\"Foo\")` errors with \"'{name}' not in scope\" while `try_resolve_symbol` consults NO scope — MESSAGE AND CODE DISAGREE (the WI-886 shape). It has `kb_mut`, so routing to `resolve_name_in_global` is a one-liner. (2)+(3) `eval/builtins.rs` `make_fn` (~3302) / `make_apply` (~3347): a functor name written as a `Value::Str` INSIDE an anthill program, resolved absolute-only — and `make_fn`'s own doc (~3290) says these are rule-head functors NOT scanned as definitions, i.e. exactly the names that will have no `by_qualified_name` entry under the spelling the program used. (4) `eval/builtins.rs::persistence_monotonicity` (~4252): 'a functor passed as its raw name string', absolute-only. Also `kb/mod.rs::make_sort_ref_by_name` (~5529) is absolute-or-`intern`, whose bare-intern branch is WI-894's collapse hazard. FIRST STEP: drive (1) — its message already claims the behaviour the code lacks, so a two-namespace fixture settles it. ACCEPTANCE: each position either reads the shared ladder or carries a stated reason at the site for asking a different question; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-915", description: some(value: "THE `_global` SCOPE IS A MAGIC STRING RE-DERIVED AT ~12 SITES. `kb.make_name_term(\"_global\")` (intern + hash-cons alloc) is spelled as a scope TermId at kb/mod.rs ~5155, kb/load.rs ~1859/3309/3370/4585/12780, kb/body_specialize.rs ~1151; and as a domain Symbol at kb/execute.rs ~444, kb/load.rs ~5096/5241/5449, kb/flow_derive.rs ~82. Nothing caches it. Two costs: (a) `KnowledgeBase::resolve_name_in_global` takes `&mut self` ONLY to re-derive the scope, so a read-only caller cannot ask what a host name denotes without a mutable borrow — WI-908 made this the named entry point for host names, which sharpens the constraint; (b) 'the global scope, spelled differently at position N' is prevented by convention rather than made unrepresentable (CLAUDE.md: prefer make illegal state unrepresentable over check logic). PROPOSAL: establish `global_scope: TermId` (+ `global_domain: Symbol`) once at KB construction, read via `pub fn global_scope(&self)`; `resolve_name_in_global` then becomes `&self`. NOT DRIVEN — a refactor, not a defect; the sites were enumerated by grep, not measured. ACCEPTANCE: one owner for the global scope term and domain symbol, every site reading it; `resolve_name_in_global` takes `&self`; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-908", description: some(value: "`KnowledgeBase::resolve_name_in_global` (rustland/anthill-core/src/kb/mod.rs) IS STILL PRE-WI-752: it ranks the ABSOLUTE `by_qualified_name` lookup FIRST, ahead of scope resolution, and carries no head-qualification rung at all — the exact inversion WI-752 removed from `resolve_name_in_kb_opt`, whose ticket recorded it as the reason `anthill query` could bind the same dotted text to a DIFFERENT symbol than the program it queries. It is `pub`; live callers are kb/extent.rs (three sites) and the anthill-stl reflect bridge's SortQuery. WI-900 routed its IMPLICIT tier through the shared `resolve_implicit`, so that rung now agrees with the other three — the surrounding ladder does not. NOT DRIVEN: raised by /code-review's altitude pass on the WI-900 diff, code-read only; WI-752's own suite (wi752_dotted_ladder_test) covers the loader and query positions, not this one. FIRST STEP: drive the divergence — a name whose absolute reading and whose scope reading differ, resolved through an extent/reflect-bridge path, asserting which symbol it binds. ACCEPTANCE: this position reads `resolve_dotted_in_kb` in the same order as every other, or its deviation is a stated and tested decision naming why the reflect bridge needs a different one; wi752 tests stay green; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-31T23:54:19Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-908", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED 2026-07-31 (commit 2d17e0b9, branch wi908-global-name-ladder). Full workspace 3929/0 green; wi752_dotted_ladder_test and both extent suites green.\n\nTHE FIX: resolve_name_in_global delegates to load::resolve_name_in_kb_opt at `_global` (that fn went pub(crate)). 9 lines -> 2.\n\nTHE TICKET WAS WRONG TWICE, both checked not assumed. (1) Its caller list names the anthill-stl reflect bridge's SortQuery; the bridge has not called this since WI-632 (a10105ec, 2026-07-09), which moved sort_query to carry the sort BY REFERENCE with 'zero name-string resolution'. So the acceptance clause 'or its deviation is a stated and tested decision naming why the reflect bridge needs a different one' has no subject — three callers remain, all kb/extent.rs. (2) It frames the defect as the order inversion alone; there were THREE.\n\nDRIVEN through register_extent_owner (the live caller), pre-fix measured by restoring the old body:\n- ORDER: with a top-level `import wi908.lib`, mounting `Member` bound the loader's QUALIFIED-ONLY kernel meta-sort, not `wi908.lib.Member`. A DIFFERENT SYMBOL — and exactly WI-423's delocalized phantom, resurrected by the absolute rung.\n- MISSING RUNG: `Box.thing` (reachable only by qualifying an imported head) resolved to NOTHING.\n- VISIBILITY: `wi908.priv.Vault.secret` mounted despite `internal`.\nFour tests fail pre-fix, each at its own claim line; the fifth passes BOTH sides — the control that the alignment costs the implicit tier nothing.\n\nWHY THE PURELY-SOURCE ORDER INVERSION IS UNREACHABLE, since the fixture looks contrived and is not: at `_global` a resolvable head's qualified name EQUALS its own spelling for locals, and locals beat imports, so head-qualification and absolute coincide. The only way the two readings differ is a name that is a _global local/import AND some symbol's whole qualified name — which needs a qualified-only registration, the loader's own idiom for KERNEL_META_SORTS/KERNEL_FUNCTORS. That is why `Member` is the subject: it is the mechanism in use, not an invention.\n\nONE CAPABILITY DROPPED, pinned as the decision the acceptance asked for: the absolute rung is dotted-only (WI-476), so a short host name must be in scope. `Sort`/`Fact`/`Member` (qualified-only kernel registrations) no longer mount bare.\n\nREVIEW CAUGHT A FALSE CLAIM OF MINE IN THREE PLACES AT ONCE — the biggest win. I wrote, in the doc comment, in kernel-language.md and in a test NAME, that a short name 'now resolves only if it is in scope'. FALSE: the ladder ends in `.or_else(resolve_implicit)`, which is SHORT-NAME KEYED, so `SortInfo`/`OperationInfo`/`and`/`not`/`cons` still resolve bare with no scope presence (measured with the stdlib loaded; the pre-fix comment I deleted had stated this correctly). Corrected in all three, and pinned by a fifth test. Its subject is `SortView` not `SortInfo` because SortInfo carries 119 resident facts and the mount is then refused as ResidentCollision — which is the single-owner rule, not the ladder; noted at the site.\n\nALSO FROM REVIEW: ~20 lines of doc prose retelling this ticket at three sites, deleted (the WI-900 lesson, committed again and caught again); `resolve_implicit`'s pub(crate) had become vestigial and is private; the local `fn sym` was the 6th copy of `kb.resolve_symbol`; an implied assertion dropped; an unused fixture entity dropped. Efficiency review: neutral, measured — all three callers are cold (registration / per-mutation write; the READ path already takes a Symbol).\n\nFOLLOW-UPS FILED, all code-read not driven, all saying so: WI-911 (the mount takes the ladder's VisibleOnly half but NOT its diagnostic half, so a hidden name reports as absent — the misleading-diagnostic case WI-752 split the ladder to avoid), WI-912 (persistence/term_ser.rs, the CLOSEST twin — still carries the WI-476 bare short-name global fallback, plus two local re-spellings of dotted_by_head), WI-913 (four more absolute-only host-name positions; reflect/builtins.rs lookup_symbol_op's MESSAGE and CODE disagree), WI-914 (anthill query --mode listing modes disagree with --mode pattern — a stated decision to re-examine, not an assumed bug), WI-915 (`_global` re-derived at ~12 sites; caching it makes resolve_name_in_global `&self`), WI-916 (one question, three failure shapes, and External FactRefs re-resolving a name the mount already resolved).", at: "2026-07-31T23:54:50Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-890", description: some(value: "`String` AND `BigInt` HAVE NO C++ BINDING BLOCK AT ALL, so every operation of theirs is now a LOUD cpp codegen refusal — which is right, and is also a gap somebody should close now that it is visible.\n\nSTATE AFTER WI-886. `rustland/anthill-cpp-gen/anthill/` carries `float.anthill` and `int64.anthill`; there is no `string.anthill` and no `bigint.anthill`, though `rustland/anthill-stl/anthill/` has both on the rust side. Before WI-886 a call to `String.length(s)` in a cpp-targeted spec emitted `length(s)` — a C++ function that does not exist — into a header codegen reported no problem with. It now refuses at codegen time naming the operation, the carrier, and the clause that would fix it (`unlowerable_operation`, rustland/anthill-cpp-gen/src/lib.rs), and `wi886_host_operation_mapping_test::an_operation_with_no_body_and_no_cpp_realization_is_a_codegen_error` uses `String.length` as its subject precisely because the gap is real.\n\nSO THIS TICKET IS THE FILL, NOT A BUG. `String` maps to `std::string` already (a `TypeMapping` in stdlib/anthill/realization/cpp_std.anthill), so its VALUES lower; only its OPERATIONS have no realization.\n\nTHE SURFACE, from stdlib/anthill/prelude/string.anthill: `concat`, `length`, `substring`, `contains`, `indexOf`, `startsWith`, `endsWith`, `toUpper`, `toLower`, `trim`, `replace`, `split`, `repeat`, `isEmpty`, plus the `Ordered` family WI-876 declared (`compare`/`gt`/`gte`/`lt`/`lte`/`max`/`min`).\n\nTHE ONE THING THAT MUST NOT BE SKIPPED: WI-884 settled that `String`'s INDEX UNIT IS THE UNICODE SCALAR, not the byte, for `length`/`substring`/`indexOf` alike — it MEASURED that the host `str::find` answers in BYTES (`\"éb\".find(\"b\")` = 2 where the character index is 1) and that a byte answer makes `substring(s, indexOf(s, sub), ...)` cut the wrong span. `std::string` is BYTES throughout, so the naive `s.find(sub)` / `s.substr(i, n)` mapping REPRODUCES exactly the defect WI-884 refused, silently, for any non-ASCII input. A correct cpp realization needs scalar-indexed helpers, which is more than an expression template can carry — expect this to want either a small `anthill_runtime.hpp` helper set (the header is already copied beside every generated one) or a documented ASCII-only restriction stated in the binding and enforced somewhere. WI-884 also settled that the EMPTY PATTERN occurs at every boundary and that `split` keeps its empty pieces; both must hold in C++ too, and `std::string::find(\"\")` already answers 0, which is compatible.\n\n`BigInt` is a different question and may belong in its own ticket: `cpp_std.anthill` maps no `BigInt` type at all, so there is no host type to realize operations against — C++17 has no arbitrary-precision integer in the standard library. Decide whether the cpp profile supports `BigInt` (naming a vendored type through a `carrier` clause and an `artifact`) or declares it out of scope, and write that down; the current state — a type mapping failure rather than an operation gap — is at least loud.\n\nREFERENCE: WI-886 (which made the gap loud and left the fill), WI-884 (the index unit and the empty-pattern semantics, both MEASURED), `rustland/anthill-cpp-gen/anthill/float.anthill` for what a cpp binding block looks like and what `host_fn` means under `language cpp`.\n\nACCEPTANCE: `String`'s operations lower to C++ that COMPILES AND COMPUTES THE SAME ANSWERS AS THE RUST HOST on a non-ASCII input (the WI-884 round trip `substring(s, indexOf(s, sub), +length(sub)) = sub` driven through generated C++), or the restriction is stated in the binding block and the operations it cannot realize stay a loud refusal; `wi886_host_operation_mapping_test::every_rust_realized_primitive_operation_is_realized_in_cpp` covers `String` once it has a block; the `BigInt` question is answered in writing either way; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-01T05:02:33Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-916", author: "user", content: "WI-907 added ONE MORE instance of the shape this ticket owns, deliberately and with the reason recorded here rather than fixing it in passing: `retract_persistent`/`update_persistent` now share one `external_owner_functor(op, name)` helper (so the name is read once, not twice), and that helper's AMBIGUOUS arm is a fourth `ExtentError::Backend(format!(\"{op}: ambiguous owner ...\"))` string, beside its pre-existing `unknown owner` twin — while the MOUNT got a typed `ExtentRegError::AmbiguousName { functor, candidates }`. Making only the ambiguous half typed would have split a pair that reads as one; the unification is this ticket's. So the count is now: mount = 2 typed variants (UnresolvableName, AmbiguousName), retract/update = 2 strings, one helper. Note also that `external_owner_functor` is the single point this ticket's PROPOSAL (let `FactRefInner::External` carry the resolved `Symbol` so the mount is the sole `resolve_name_in_global` caller) would DELETE outright — it is the whole re-resolution, now in one place.", at: "2026-08-01T00:44:52Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-907", description: some(value: "`resolve_name_in_kb_opt` (rustland/anthill-core/src/kb/load.rs) SILENTLY RESOLVES PAST AN AMBIGUITY, and it is the third of three policies for one question. Its match is `Found(sym) => Some(sym), _ => resolve_dotted_in_kb(..).or_else(resolve_implicit)` — the `_` arm covers Ambiguous, so an ambiguous short name falls through to the lower rungs and the QUERY-PATTERN position binds it to the implicit-prelude target with NO diagnostic. The same name in a program is refused (`remap_name_str_inner` pushes AmbiguousSymbol, which blocks the load), and since WI-900 the rule-head mint guard counts it as denoting (`name_denotes_for_rule_head`, which cites `Loader::qualified_name_resolves`'s stated reason: an ambiguity is a real finding with a real error to emit). So three sites answer 'what does this ambiguous name mean here?' three ways, and the query one is the silent one — exactly the silent-skip the repo forbids. NOT DRIVEN: raised by /code-review's altitude pass on the WI-900 diff, code-read only. FIRST STEP: drive it — an `anthill query` whose pattern names a short name that is ambiguous at the query scope, and assert what it binds today. Then decide: report (and return None), or state why a query pattern is the one position that may guess. Note the risk named at filing: a query that answers today would start erroring. ACCEPTANCE: the Ambiguous arm of `resolve_name_in_kb_opt` is a stated decision with a test, not a wildcard; the three sites' policies are each documented at their site; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-08-01T00:54:51Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-907", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED (commit 2e18efc6). DRIVEN FIRST, as the ticket asked, and the ticket's own risk note was RIGHT: a query that answered today does start erroring. Fixture — two wildcard-imported user sorts named SortInfo at _global. anthill query 'SortInfo(name: ?n)' answered FOUR rows out of anthill.reflect.SortInfo; with ONE import it was correctly empty; with NONE the tier answers legitimately. So the second import — which makes the name strictly MORE contested — is what brought back the reading the user's own declarations had shadowed. The three-way control (NotFound / Found / Ambiguous) is what proved the Ambiguous arm was doing it rather than a plain miss.\n\nTHE TICKET'S FRAMING ('three policies for one question') RESOLVED TO TWO, not to three documented sites: name_denotes_for_rule_head is now literally !matches!(resolve_name_in_kb(..), NotFound), so the mint guard and the ladder cannot disagree, and its WI-900 debug_assert about the omitted dotted rung went with it (the rung is no longer omitted, so the coupling dissolved rather than needing narration). What remains is the ladder (no channel: Ambiguous is returned, each caller states its arm) and remap_name_str_inner (has a channel: reports and refuses). Applying WI-900's own lesson one level up — the fix for N agreeing comments is one function.\n\nSCOPE THAT GREW BY NECESSITY: returning ResolveResult forced resolve_name_in_global's callers to decide, so the MOUNT got a typed ExtentRegError::AmbiguousName. Reporting an ambiguous host name as UnresolvableName would have been loud-and-wrong, and it is my change that created that arm.\n\nFOUR FALSE OR ROTTEN CLAIMS OF MINE, all caught by /code-review, two of them prose I wrote to explain my own fix (the recurring failure): (1) 'None sends the whole target to remap_name, whose own AMBIGUITY REPORT refuses the load' — DRIVEN: it refuses with 'unresolved name f.requires', never an ambiguity, because remap_name re-asks about the whole dotted string. (2) The spec paragraph I added said the ambiguity is 'reported wherever there is a channel' — DRIVEN FALSE: 'rule r(?x) :- Widget907.w907a(v: ?x)' with two ambiguous Widget907s LOADS CLEAN. (3) 'lifted at the THIRD copy' — wi752's copy was already DEAD (the lift is what surfaced it), so it was the second LIVE copy. (4) The rename left six 'resolve_name_in_kb_opt' references, one of them INSIDE the very doc comment that lists resolve_implicit's consumers — that list rotted in the same commit that wrote it, which is the argument for deleting such lists rather than maintaining them.\n\nREVIEW'S BIGGEST WIN WAS DELETING PROSE, for the third ticket running: the SortInfo measurement had been retold at six sites. It survives in ONE place (the core test header, which is what it justifies) plus here.\n\nEFFICIENCY: neutral, measured — resolve_in_scope already returned ResolveResult by value, the guard still makes exactly one of those calls, and the new dotted rung is one split_once('.') that returns immediately for a short name. In debug builds the guard now scans LESS, since the deleted debug_assert ran a full contains() on every call.\n\nFOLLOW-UPS: WI-917 (the two gaps, both driven — an ambiguous dotted HEAD is reported nowhere and reported FALSELY at a pattern; and the WI-863-tolerated query positions). WI-916 got feedback: this diff added one more instance of the shape it owns, deliberately, and external_owner_functor is the single point its proposal would delete.", at: "2026-08-01T00:55:18Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-918", description: some(value: "A DOTTED PATH'S AMBIGUITY MESSAGE NAMES THE PATH BUT LISTS THE HEAD'S CANDIDATES, and the two do not visibly line up. WI-917 made a contested head reportable and reused `LoadError::AmbiguousSymbol` / the CLI's ambiguity arm unchanged, so `Widget917.w917a` is reported as `ambiguous symbol 'Widget917.w917a' … candidates [\"wi917.alpha.Widget917\", \"wi917.beta.Widget917\"]` — the candidates are the HEAD's readings (the only segment the ladder resolves), which are strictly SHORTER than the name they are candidates for. Not false, and deliberately shipped that way: the alternative readings the author could write are `wi917.alpha.Widget917.w917a` / `wi917.beta.Widget917.w917a`, and only ONE of those exists (`w917a` is alpha's), so listing both as candidates would name a path that resolves to nothing — worse than the imprecision. DRIVEN that the advice is at least true: `anthill query 'wi917.alpha.Widget917.w917a(v: ?x)'` answers, so 'Qualify the name' has somewhere to go. DECIDE whether the message should say which SEGMENT is contested (e.g. 'the head segment X of the path Y'), which needs the head string at the report site — one `split_once('.')` outside `resolve_dotted_in_kb`, i.e. a second spelling of the ladder's own dotted split, unless the ladder carries the segment in its answer. Both reporters would change together: `AmbiguousSymbol`'s two renderers in load.rs and `report_unknown_functor_name` in anthill-cli. ACCEPTANCE: either the message names the contested segment with the split spelled ONCE, or the decision to keep the current wording is recorded with its reason at the `AmbiguousSymbol` variant."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-917", description: some(value: "AN AMBIGUOUS HEAD SEGMENT OF A DOTTED PATH IS REPORTED NOWHERE, and at a query pattern it is reported FALSELY. WI-907 made the whole-name ambiguity terminal and reported (load error at a reference, candidate-naming refusal at a query head / negation-committed goal). The DOTTED half is untouched: for `Sort.member` with two `Sort`s in scope, resolve_in_scope on the whole dotted string is NotFound, then resolve_dotted_in_kb stands BOTH rungs down under an ambiguous head (dotted_by_head needs Found; dotted_absolute is blocked by head_owns_path, whose doc calls the ambiguity 'a real finding with a real error to emit' — but nothing emits it), so the path falls to the WI-476 bare intern. DRIVEN, both halves: (1) REFERENCE — `rule r907(?x) :- Widget907.w907a(v: ?x)` with two wildcard-imported `Widget907`s LOADS CLEAN, no diagnostic, on a position that HAS an error channel. (2) QUERY — `anthill query 'SortInfo.si907a(v: ?x)'` under the same imports reports \"does not resolve to a known functor — no rule, fact, or declaration is in scope for it\", the exact false message WI-907 removed for the short-name case, one dot away. SECOND, SEPARATE GAP, also driven: a short ambiguous name in a query position that TOLERATES unresolvable names is silent — `push_choice(w907a(v: ?x), SortInfo(name: ?n))` answers 1 solution, exit 0, while pre-WI-907 that branch bound anthill.reflect.SortInfo and answered 4 rows; a data slot `w907a(v: SortInfo(name: ?n))` likewise. That silence is INHERITED from WI-863's deliberate non-descent into a bare disjunction / quantifier body / data slot, whose stated reason ('an undefined name there does not corrupt the correct answer') was written for an ABSENT name and has not been re-argued for an ambiguous one — decide whether it transfers. Both gaps raised by /code-review's altitude pass on the WI-907 diff and then DRIVEN with the built CLI. FIRST STEP: decide where the dotted-head ambiguity is REPORTED — head_owns_path is read by resolvers with a channel (remap_name_str_inner) and by ones without (resolve_name_in_kb), so the report cannot live in the rung; the ladder's existing split (VisibleOnly resolve, then an 'Any' diagnostic pass for positions with a channel — Loader::forbid_if_dotted_internal) is the shape that already solves this for 'internal', and WI-911 is lifting that pass off Loader. ACCEPTANCE: an ambiguous dotted head is reported as an ambiguity naming its candidates at a reference and at a query pattern; the WI-863 tolerance question is answered with a stated reason at undefined_query_goal_functors; docs/kernel-language.md 8.6's 'what is not yet reported' paragraph (added by WI-907) is narrowed to whatever remains; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-01T05:57:45Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-917", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED (commit 55fb7989). BOTH GAPS DRIVEN before and after, through the shipped CLI, as the ticket asked.\n\nTHE FIRST STEP THE TICKET ASKED FOR ('decide where the dotted-head ambiguity is REPORTED') RESOLVED TO 'NOWHERE NEW'. The ticket's own framing — head_owns_path is read by resolvers with a channel and without, so the report cannot live in the rung — was right about the rung and wrong about needing a new site: the answer is that the LADDER stops carrying Option<Symbol>. resolve_dotted_in_kb now returns ResolveResult and the contested head is returned BEFORE either rung is consulted, so every existing reporter — the three loader positions, the CLI's report_unknown_functor_name, the mount's ExtentRegError — got the ambiguity through the arm it ALREADY had for a contested whole name. No new diagnostic site, no lift off Loader, so WI-911 is untouched rather than needed first. The ticket named Loader::forbid_if_dotted_internal's split (VisibleOnly resolve, then an 'Any' diagnostic pass) as 'the shape that already solves this'; it is the shape for the INTERNAL half only, because a hidden hit is information the first read genuinely lacked, whereas vis never reaches the head.\n\nMADE ILLEGAL STATE UNREPRESENTABLE RATHER THAN CHECKED: the early return killed head_owns_path's Ambiguous arm, so the rungs now take Option<Symbol> and the state they used to guard against cannot reach them. wi751_ambiguous_head_is_not_resolved_past still passes and now ALSO reports — its doc named removed code and was corrected.\n\nGAP 2 ANSWERED 'DOES NOT TRANSFER', by WI-863's OWN reason: the tolerance holds because an ABSENT name's branch has no solutions to lose. A contested one's branch answers under either reading, so tolerating it DROPS solutions — the corruption the tolerance exists to avoid. Implemented as a SEPARATE walk (ambiguous_query_names, every node — goal or data, negated or not), not a flag on the existing one: the two questions have opposite descent rules for one reason, that walk asks what the SEARCH commits to and an ambiguity is a defect of the TEXT.\n\nTHREE FALSE OR WORTHLESS THINGS OF MINE, all caught by my own review pass rather than shipped:\n(1) A test I wrote as a DEFECT PIN — an_ambiguous_dotted_head_does_not_fall_through_to_the_implicit_tier — was green on BOTH sides. The tier is keyed on a name's LAST SEGMENT (kernel_vocab_qualified's rsplit), so it can never answer a dotted name at all. Deleted, and the same false claim removed from the comment in resolve_name_in_kb that had motivated it.\n(2) My first '4 rows -> 0 rows' measurement for gap 2 conflated the IMPLICIT TIER answering (WI-907's fall-through, no imports) with a CANDIDATE READING answering. On WI-907's fixture the contested SortInfo is a sort with no clauses, so nothing was actually dropped and the fixture could not show the defect. Rebuilt a fixture where every contested name answers under either reading (a rule in both namespaces; a fact whose DATA slot names a contested constructor), re-measured — one row under either import alone, 'no solutions' exit 0 under both — and corrected the prose at three sites.\n(3) A test-header claim quoted a query string I had never run. Measured the exact string against a temporarily reverted ladder before keeping it.\n\nPRE-FIX VERIFICATION IS THE CONTROL: 3 of the 6 core tests were confirmed to FAIL against a one-line revert of the ladder's Ambiguous arm, with the 2 both-sides controls still green — which is what proves the refusals are the ambiguity being caught and not the head-qualification rung having stopped working.\n\nREVIEW'S WIN WAS CONSOLIDATION, not deletion this time: after my change 'an answer ends the ladder' had FOUR spellings (two !matches!(.., NotFound), one Found|Ambiguous => true arm, one nested match). It is now ResolveResult::or_else / denotes, one home for the WI-907 rule that this ticket had to restate at every site. Three loader positions each spelled the dotted step out; that is resolve_dotted_reported once, because the ORDER is the policy.\n\nEFFICIENCY: neutral. The ladder returns 32 bytes instead of 8 on a path resolve_in_scope already pays for, and the Ambiguous Vec is now MOVED rather than built-and-dropped. The query-side walk is new but runs only over query patterns, orders the cheap ladder read ahead of the discrimination-tree backstop, and fires per undefined node only.\n\n/code-review NOT RUN — it is user-invocable only. The four /simplify angles were applied inline; that is not a substitute.\n\nFOLLOW-UP: WI-918 (the dotted message names the path but lists the HEAD's candidates — deliberate, since the full paths would name one that resolves to nothing, but the wording decision is unrecorded). WI-911 remains as it was: the mount still takes only the ladder's VisibleOnly half, and wi908's test doc was updated to say why its AMBIGUITY half no longer needs that lift.", at: "2026-08-01T05:57:47Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-916", description: some(value: "ONE QUESTION ('which functor does this host name denote?'), THREE FAILURE SHAPES, and one name resolved TWICE. In kb/extent.rs: `register_extent_owner` (~596) returns the typed `ExtentRegError::UnresolvableName(name)`, while `retract_persistent` (~524) and `update_persistent` (~562) return `ExtentError::Backend(format!(\"persistent retract/update: unknown owner \\`{name}\\`\"))` — a NAME-RESOLUTION failure classified as a BACKEND failure, stringly. WI-908 made the resolution one function; the failure should be one error too. Deeper: `FactRefInner::External { functor: String }` (~69) carries an UNRESOLVED NAME where `Resident` carries a resolved `RuleId`, so every retract/update re-runs the ladder on a string `register_extent_owner` ALREADY resolved and stored in `extents.mounts` — all three `FactRef::external` sites (~1329/1343/1370) pass `self.functor_name.clone()`. Two independent resolutions of one name, and a load between mount and retract can make them disagree. PROPOSAL: hand the resolved `Symbol` back to the source at registration and let `External` carry it; that leaves the MOUNT as the sole `resolve_name_in_global` caller and makes the three-shapes problem mostly vanish. NOT DRIVEN (code-read, /code-review altitude pass on WI-908). FIRST STEP: drive the disagreement — mount, load a shadowing declaration, then retract. ACCEPTANCE: one resolution per name and one error type for its failure; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-01T06:37:44Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-916", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED (commit 72ce1b99, branch wi916-external-ref-symbol). Full workspace 3956/0 green.\n\nDRIVEN FIRST, as the ticket's FIRST STEP asked, and the disagreement it predicted is real. Fixture: mount `Widget916.w916a` under ONE wildcard import, insert a row through the mount, then load a second top-level import contesting the head. Pre-fix, measured through the shipped seam:\n\n  read    = Ok(1)   <- Symbol-keyed off the mount\n  insert  = Ok      <- routed by the ROW's head symbol\n  retract = Err(Backend) `persistent retract: ambiguous owner Widget916.w916a: candidates [wi916.alpha.Widget916, wi916.beta.Widget916]`\n  update  = Err(Backend) `persistent update: ambiguous owner Widget916.w916a: ...`\n\nTHAT ASYMMETRY IS THE FINDING, and it is sharper than the ticket's framing: the extent stayed mounted, readable and writable, and its rows became permanently unretractable — because the only two operations that re-asked the NAME question got a different answer than the mount had. The measurement is the shipped test's header; the two defect tests were confirmed to FAIL against the pre-fix tree at their own claim lines, with the third (read + insert) green on BOTH sides, which is what proves the refusals were the re-resolution and not the mount having died.\n\nI DID NOT IMPLEMENT THE TICKET'S PROPOSAL, and the deviation is the substance. It proposed handing the resolved Symbol back to the source at registration. That leaves the source holding an Option<Symbol> — `InMemoryExtentSource` is constructible and usable UNMOUNTED, so 'not yet stamped' becomes a state to check — and a source owning several functors would still have to decide which of its symbols a given row belongs to. Instead the source never learns the owner at all: `persist`/`update`/`ExtentCursor::next` return a new `SourceRow` (content + its own RowKey, NO owner), and the KB attaches the functor it ROUTED the call through, at the three places a row leaves a source (the two write seams and `drain_extent_query`). Illegal state unrepresentable rather than checked: the type gives a source nowhere to name an owner, so it cannot name a premature or wrong one. `FactRef::external` is now private and reached only through `SourceRow::attach`.\n\nTHE 'THREE FAILURE SHAPES' RESOLVED TO ONE, BY DELETION. `external_owner_functor` — which WI-907 recorded here as the single point this ticket's proposal would delete — is gone with both of its strings. What survives is the mount's two typed variants; a question asked once has one place to fail. The seam has one failure left and it is NOT about names: `ExtentError::UnmountedOwner`, a reference used in a KB that did not produce it (within one KB it is unreachable — the stamp is the routed owner and a mount is never removed).\n\nMY OWN DEFECT, caught before it ran: my first `UnmountedOwner` rendered the owner with `self.resolve_sym(owner)` — which indexes `SymbolTable::defs` and PANICS on an out-of-range index, i.e. exactly the foreign-KB case the error exists for, and NAMES A DIFFERENT FUNCTOR when the index happens to be in range. Found by reading `SymbolTable::name` while writing the cross-KB test. The variant now carries the `Symbol` and renders it as one, with the reason at the variant: this KB's table is the wrong book to look a foreign symbol up in.\n\nTWO FALSE CLAIMS OF MINE IN PROSE EXPLAINING MY OWN FIX, the recurring failure, both caught in my own review pass: (1) the test header said 'STDLIB LOADS: three ... the fourth is bare' — the cross-KB test loads one for the PRODUCING KB and only its second KB is bare, so four. (2) I wrote that the shared mount idiom keys on `id` 'which is why the fixture entity has that field' — not why: the seed is empty so nothing validates the key, and the reads take the enumeration mode. Restated as coordination, not necessity.\n\nA THIRD COPY OF THE MOUNT IDIOM, caught in the reuse pass: my fixture hand-rolled `InMemoryExtentSource::new` + `register_extent_owner`, which is `common::mount_extent` verbatim (WI-907 lifted that helper at the second copy). Now it calls it.\n\nDOC ROT FIXED IN THE BLAST RADIUS: docs/design/kernel-vocab-provenance.md listed `resolve_implicit`'s four consumers, and the list had rotted on both counts — it named `resolve_name_in_kb_opt` (renamed by WI-907) and attributed `resolve_name_in_global` to the reflect bridge (WI-908 measured it had not called since WI-632); two of the four had also come to reach `resolve_implicit` through the ladder rather than directly, leaving `remap_name_str_inner` and the ladder as the only direct callers. The enumeration is deleted, the claim kept — the WI-907 lesson that such a list rots inside the commit that writes it, applied to the list that proves it. Proposal 057's seam signatures carry `SourceRow` and the one-resolution rule.\n\nEFFICIENCY: better, not benchmarked. The mounted READ path loses one String allocation PER ROW — `InMemoryExtentSource::query` cloned `self.functor_name` into every returned reference; the KB now stamps one Copy `Symbol`. Two double-HashMap-lookups (`owner(..).is_some()` then `owner_mut(..).expect(..)`) collapsed to one `if let` / `let else` each, which the typed error made possible: its error path touches no `self`.\n\n/code-review NOT RUN — it is user-invocable only; reminded. The four /simplify angles were applied inline (agents not spawned, per session instruction); that is not a substitute.\n\nFOLLOW-UPS FILED, both code-read not driven and saying so: WI-919 (the CLOSEST remaining twin — `resolve_fact_monotonicity` renders a resolved Symbol back to a qualified NAME to look up `mirror_monotonicity`'s String-keyed table, and a miss silently defaults to Monotone, which surfaces as a 'not non_monotone' RETRACT REFUSAL: a name-keying miss reported as a policy verdict), WI-920 (`assert_persistent` / `persist_mirrored` stamp every resident persisted fact with a hardcoded sort `Fact` and domain `anthill.todo` — one tool's domain baked into the core write seam).", at: "2026-08-01T06:38:20Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-919", description: some(value: "A RESOLVED SYMBOL IS RENDERED BACK TO A NAME TO ASK A POLICY QUESTION, AND THE MISS IS SILENT — the closest remaining twin of WI-916's shape, one seam over. `Interpreter::resolve_fact_monotonicity` (rustland/anthill-core/src/eval/builtins.rs ~4183) takes a `Symbol`, and when no reflect rule answers it does `let qname = self.kb.qualified_name_of(functor).to_string(); self.kb.mirror_monotonicity(&qname).unwrap_or(Monotonicity::Monotone)` — a Symbol turned back into a String to key `ExtentRegistry.mirror_monotonicity: HashMap<String, Monotonicity>` (kb/extent.rs ~406), which a mirror populated from `Store::owned_monotonicity() -> Vec<(String, Monotonicity)>` at registration. TWO THINGS. (1) The round trip: a resolved identity is re-derived as text to look up a table the registration could have keyed on the Symbol it was about to resolve anyway — the WI-916 defect with the arrow reversed (there a name was re-resolved; here an identity is re-named). The field carries a STATED reason — \"Names remain strings because a backend may name a functor that is only interned after bootstrap\" — so this is a decision to re-examine, not an assumed bug: check whether that ordering is still real now that `register_extent_owner` resolves owner names at registration, and if it is, whether the mirror can resolve lazily-but-once rather than per call. (2) The silent default is worse than the round trip: `.unwrap_or(Monotonicity::Monotone)` means a key MISS is indistinguishable from a mirror that declared Monotone, and `Monotone` is what the proposal-053 guard refuses to retract — so a name that does not match its table's spelling surfaces to the author as \"retract refused: functor `X` is not non_monotone (proposal 053)\", a policy verdict, when nothing declared any policy at all. That is the repo's silent-skip rule broken in the direction that looks like a decision. NOT DRIVEN — code-read, raised while delivering WI-916. FIRST STEP: drive the miss — register a mirror whose `owned_monotonicity` names a functor by one spelling and retract through a reference whose `qualified_name_of` gives another, and assert what the author is told. ACCEPTANCE: a mirror policy lookup that misses is distinguishable from one that answered Monotone (either a loud refusal naming the unmatched spelling, or the lookup is Symbol-keyed and the miss cannot arise); the `Monotone` default is a stated decision at its site either way; cargo-test green via rustland/scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-01T07:09:39Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-919", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED (commit 89247a23, branch wi919-mirror-policy-keying). Full workspace 3959/0 green.\n\nDRIVEN FIRST, as the ticket's FIRST STEP asked, and both halves of its claim held. A PolicyStore declaring the SHORT name `Ghost` for a KB whose functor is `test.syn.Ghost`:\n\n  registration = accepted, silently\n  monotonicity = anthill.reflect.Monotonicity.monotone   <- rung 3, i.e. the miss\n  retract      = Err(Raised) `retract refused: functor test.syn.Ghost is not non_monotone (proposal 053)`\n\nThe control run (same store, qualified spelling) retracts Ok(true). So the author declared exactly that policy and is told their functor does not have it, in a message naming neither the store, nor the declaration, nor the spelling.\n\nWHERE THE FIX WENT, and it is NOT where the ticket's part (1) suggested. The ticket framed the round trip (Symbol rendered back to text) as one thing to fix and the silent default as another. They are one thing: the round trip is only harmful because the miss it can produce is unreportable at the READ. A miss at the read is legitimate and common — no store speaks for most functors, and that is precedence rung 3 — so no refusal can live there. The name question therefore moves to REGISTRATION, where \"the name you declared denotes nothing\" is both knowable and actionable: `register_mirror` resolves each declared name once and keys the table on `Symbol`. Rung 3's `monotone` now means only what rung 3 says.\n\nTHE `.unwrap_or(Monotone)` SURVIVES UNCHANGED, and that is the stated decision the acceptance asked for: it is the documented default, not a fallback for a lookup that failed. Its site now says so, and says why a miss can no longer mean anything else.\n\nTHE REFUSAL IS A REFUSAL, not a dropped entry, for the reason the driving exposed: the reader cannot tell a dropped entry from a functor no store spoke for, so a silent drop IS this ticket's defect, deferred to the next retract. The cost is stated at the site: declaring a policy for a functor this KB does not have is no longer expressible. Checked rather than assumed — no in-tree backend declares an intrinsic policy at all (the filesystem stores return []), and the field's old reason (\"a backend may name a functor that is only interned after bootstrap\") describes NO LIVE CALLER: the single production registration (anthill-todo/src/main.rs) runs after the project is loaded. If a schema-reading backend ever needs it, that is a decision to take deliberately.\n\nREVIEW'S WIN WAS DELETING MY OWN SECOND COPY. My first cut spelled the resolve-and-refuse match a SECOND time, verbatim beside `register_extent_owner`'s — the exact shape this ticket line keeps deleting, reintroduced inside the fix for it. Both registration paths now share one `registration_symbol`: a source's `owned()` functor and a mirror's `owned_monotonicity()` functor are the same question asked by the same kind of author, so the identical answer is now structural rather than asserted. The AMBIGUOUS arm came free with it, and is pinned by a test (a contested name at this site was also accepted in silence pre-fix, measured).\n\nAN UNVERIFIED VALUE OF MINE, caught before shipping: my first driver printed `monotonicity = Ok(Entity { functor: Symbol(2121) })` and I wrote 'monotone' into the test header from inference — the guard's message implies it — without checking. Re-measured through `Monotonicity::reflect_variant_qname`, which exists precisely to name a variant: it is `anthill.reflect.Monotonicity.monotone`. The header now carries the name, not my reading of a symbol index.\n\nA SILENT TOLERANCE I NEARLY LEFT: making `register_mirror` fallible turned 8 call sites into unused-`Result` WARNINGS rather than errors, which compile and run. Each is now handled — the anthill-todo caller reports and exits EXIT_RUNTIME, and each test `.expect`s with the reason its store cannot fail (the filesystem stores declare no policy).\n\nCONTROL: both refusals were measured accepted-in-silence against the pre-fix tree (the source files temporarily reverted, the driver adapted to the old `()` signature); the third test — the matching spelling registering AND its policy still reaching the guard — is green on both sides, as is all of wi667_store_monotonicity_test, every case of which registers a mirror.\n\nEFFICIENCY: better, not benchmarked. The guard path loses one String allocation per policy query (`qualified_name_of(functor).to_string()`) in favour of a `HashMap<Symbol, _>` lookup; it runs per mutation. Registration pays one ladder read per declared name, once.\n\n/code-review NOT RUN — user-invocable only; reminded. The four /simplify angles were applied inline (agents not spawned, per session instruction); that is not a substitute.\n\nNO NEW FOLLOW-UPS. WI-920 (the hardcoded `Fact` / `anthill.todo` sort+domain in the resident write seam), filed alongside this one, remains the open neighbour.", at: "2026-08-01T07:09:40Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-920", description: some(value: "ONE TOOL'S DOMAIN IS BAKED INTO THE CORE WRITE SEAM. `KnowledgeBase::assert_persistent` and `persist_mirrored` (rustland/anthill-core/src/kb/extent.rs ~534 and ~553) both stamp every resident persisted fact with `let sort = self.intern(\"Fact\"); let domain = self.intern(\"anthill.todo\");` — a literal, unconditional pair. So a row written through proposal 057's general write seam by ANY embedder lands in anthill-todo's domain, and its sort is the kernel `Fact` regardless of what the row's own head declares. Note the contrast INSIDE THE SAME FILE: `assert_checked_persistent` (~435) derives the sort from the row (`self.fact_trigger_sort(&row).or(sort_hint)`) and refuses loud when it can find neither — \"row has no trigger sort and no sort hint\" — so the seam already knows how to ask; two of its three writers just do not. `update_persistent`'s resident arm is the third behaviour again: it INHERITS `rule_sort`/`rule_domain` from the row it replaces, which is right and is also why the discrepancy is invisible in tests (a fact asserted with the hardcoded pair keeps it across updates, self-consistently). WHAT IS AT STAKE is not cosmetic: `domain` is what the loader and the reflect queries partition a KB by, so a mis-domained fact is one a domain-scoped query does not see. NOT DRIVEN — code-read, noticed while delivering WI-916 (which changed the mounted arm of `assert_persistent` and left the resident arm untouched). FIRST STEP: establish who actually reaches the resident arm today (the mounted arm returns before it; `persist_mirrored` is the mirror path) and whether any non-anthill-todo caller does, before deciding whether this is live or latent. ACCEPTANCE: the resident write seam derives its sort and domain from the row (as `assert_checked_persistent` does) and refuses loud when it cannot, or the constants are a stated decision at their site naming why this seam is anthill-todo's alone; cargo-test green via rustland/scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-01T07:37:04Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-920", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED (commit 67186943, branch wi920-resident-write-domain). Full workspace 3963/0 green.\n\nDRIVEN FIRST, as the ticket asked, and it is LIVE, not latent. A source `fact Widget920()` inside `namespace test.alpha`, then the SAME fact persisted at runtime, beside one from `test.beta`, through a ByDomain store (whose output FILE is the domain, so the domain is observable from outside the KB):\n\n  source fact (sort, domain) = [(Fact, test.alpha)]\n  after the runtime persist  = [(Fact, test.alpha), (Fact, anthill.todo)]\n  files written              = [anthill/todo.anthill]\n\n`assert_fact` dedups on (term, sort, domain), so the constant domain makes ONE fact TWO, and the ByDomain store files both namespaces together under a name neither program mentions. Post-fix the same run gives one entry and [alpha.anthill, beta.anthill].\n\nHALF THIS TICKET'S PREMISE WAS WRONG, and I wrote it. It says the sort is `Fact` \"regardless of what the row's own head declares\", implying the sort was part of the defect. `load_fact` settles it: `f.sort.as_deref().unwrap_or(\"Fact\")` — `Fact` IS the loader's default sort for a `fact` with no `sort:` annotation, so the sort constant already agreed with the loader and only the DOMAIN was ever wrong. The fix is domain-only.\n\nTHE FIRST STEP ANSWERED: `assert_persistent`'s resident arm has NO caller at all — its only two callers (extent.rs's own unit test and wi916's) both mount first and return through the mounted arm. The live writer is `persist_mirrored`, reached by every `Store.persist`. Both are fixed; only one was reachable.\n\nTHE DOMAIN IS A DECISION, stated at its site, because the acceptance's first branch (\"derives its sort and domain from the row\") is not achievable as stated: a source `fact` takes the scope it is WRITTEN in, which for one functor may be any scope that can see it, so nothing DERIVES it. Of the scopes available, the head's DECLARING scope is the only one about this functor at all — and where the two coincide, the runtime write is not merely similar to the source fact but the SAME fact (it dedups). That is the first test.\n\nI ALSO NOTED, but did NOT adopt, the third convention already in the file: `assert_checked_persistent` passes (trigger_sort, trigger_sort) — the sort as its own domain. It is neither the loader's convention nor this one, and it is the reflect `KB.assert` contract, so I left it alone rather than unify by assertion. Flagging it as the remaining disagreement rather than filing it, since nothing measured says it is wrong.\n\nMY FIRST CUT WAS THE WRONG KIND OF LOUD, and the suite caught it: I refused a head with no declaring scope, and two existing `persistence_builtins_test` cases broke — they persist a bare-interned `Foo` that no fixture declares. An undeclared name HAS a right answer, so a refusal was wrong: it takes `_global`, which I then MEASURED is exactly the domain the loader gives a TOP-LEVEL source `fact` (probe written, run, removed). The rule now reads the same either way — a fact is filed under the scope its head belongs to — and a fourth test pins the undeclared case.\n\nA PROBE OF MINE READ A FALSE ZERO first: `kb.intern(\"test.alpha\")` mints a symbol from the RAW STRING, while the namespace's symbol is keyed by qualified name, so my first `by_domain` reading said 0 for both the real domain and the constant. Corrected to `try_resolve_symbol`, which is what made the (sort, domain) pairs above legible; the reason is noted at the test helper so the next reader does not repeat it.\n\nRIDING ALONG, stated because it is not the domain: `persist_mirrored` now refuses a non-functor-headed row, as `assert_persistent` already did — it needs the head to ask the domain question at all, and the two writers of one seam disagreeing about what a row must be was its own small defect.\n\nCONTROL: tests 1 and 2 were measured against the pre-fix tree (extent.rs reverted, same fixture) and both fail there with the values above; test 3 (content still reaches disk) is green on BOTH sides. Test 4's pre-fix value is read from the reverted source — `let domain = self.intern(\"anthill.todo\")` is unconditional — not separately re-run.\n\nEFFICIENCY: neutral. Two interns per resident write before, one intern plus a scope lookup (two map reads) now.\n\n/code-review NOT RUN — user-invocable only; reminded. The four /simplify angles were applied inline (agents not spawned, per session instruction); review's win was DELETING a measurement narrative I had written at both the code site and the test header — the site now states the decision and points at the test for the numbers.\n\nNO NEW FOLLOW-UPS FILED.", at: "2026-08-01T07:37:06Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-923", description: some(value: "`anthill query --mode domain _global` names the top-level domain by a KERNEL TAG the name ladder can never return, so the CLI matches that one spelling by hand (anthill-cli/src/main.rs, the `--mode domain` arm, WI-914). Measured: 139 of 140 distinct `by_domain` keys in a stdlib load are DECLARED symbols (a namespace or a sort), which is why WI-914 could put that mode on `resolve_name_in_global`; the 140th is `_global`, a raw `intern(\"_global\")` from `make_name_term` that `define` never registers, holding 242 clauses on the stdlib alone. The hand-match keeps those listable without re-admitting a silent fallback for every unresolvable name, but it is a SECOND SPELLING of a scope identity the loader already owns, and WI-908's lesson is that a separately-spelled rung is how a mount comes to take a functor its author never named. NOT DRIVEN. FIRST STEP: check whether `_global` can simply BE a declared symbol (a `define_qualified_only` Namespace at bootstrap, beside KERNEL_META_SORTS) — if it can, the ladder answers it and the CLI arm deletes; if it cannot, find out what that would make resolvable (`_global.foo` as a path) and whether the loader's scope-term identity survives it. ACCEPTANCE: either the CLI's `_global` arm is gone because the ladder answers, or the reason it cannot is stated where the arm is; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-914", description: some(value: "`anthill query --mode sort|functor|domain` (anthill-cli/src/main.rs ~1370/1380/1389) looks its USER-SUPPLIED argument up by absolute name only, while `--mode pattern` resolves the same text through the shared ladder at `_global`. So `--mode functor Map.empty` cannot name what `--mode pattern 'Map.empty()'` names — the WI-752 headline divergence, one layer up, on the same CLI. This is a DECISION TO RE-EXAMINE, not an assumed bug: the comment at ~1345 states the current reading deliberately ('a listing mode looks its argument up by name, not in the query scope'). WI-908 makes the counter-argument concrete — every other position that reads KB-EXTERNAL text now runs one ladder, and a CLI argument is the most external text there is; a user who can write `-i` (WI-853, a top-level import into `_global`) has said which names they mean. NOT DRIVEN. FIRST STEP: drive the divergence — with `-i`, compare `--mode functor <short>` against `--mode pattern` on the same name. ACCEPTANCE: the two modes agree on what a name denotes, or the comment at ~1345 states why a listing mode is a different QUESTION (as `DottedVisibility::Any`'s documented exception does) rather than merely a different lookup; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-01T08:13:15Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-924", description: some(value: "`SortInfo(name: <sort>, ...)` matches ONLY as a short name under `-i`; the qualified spelling matches nothing, silently. MEASURED in WI-921 on a one-namespace KB (sort Shape with constructors Circle/Rect), `anthill query --match 'SortInfo(name: X, constructors: ?c)'`, full 2x2: short+no-`-i` -> 0; short+`-i wi921.demo.*` -> 1; qualified `wi921.demo.Shape`+no-`-i` -> 0; qualified+`-i` -> 0. So the ONE cell that matches is short-under-import, and `wi921.demo.Shape` — which resolves absolutely via the dotted rung (WI-476) and denotes the SAME sort — matches nothing and reports `0 result(s)` with no diagnostic. This is the class kernel-language.md 8.6 declares closed ('a name that denotes something in one position denotes the same thing in every other'), one layer in: a DATA SLOT of a reflect fact. WI-914 closed the same divergence for a listing mode's argument; WI-917 refuses a CONTESTED name in a data slot, so the position already has a diagnostic channel — an unresolvable-or-differently-resolving one is what falls through. NOT DIAGNOSED: it is not established whether the short spelling matches because it RESOLVES to the sort symbol or because it stays an unresolved Ident that happens to be structurally equal to the stored name term; those have opposite fixes, and the reflect fact's name field is built by the loader (reflect emission), so the answer is there. FIRST STEP: print the stored `name:` term's carrier and symbol for one sort, then the converted pattern's, and compare — do not infer it from the 2x2. ACCEPTANCE: the two spellings agree, or the position states why a reflect name field is a different QUESTION (as 8.6's dot-call exception does) rather than merely a different lookup; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-923", author: "user", content: "WI-921 SHARPENED THIS, and left a false claim of mine as the reason to. Deleting `--mode sort` removed the one MODE whose argument was not a name, and I wrote that up in six places as 'the last position in `query` where `-i` is inert'. That is FALSE, and the counterexample is this ticket's own subject: `--mode domain _global` (main.rs, the Domain arm) still takes `kb.intern(name)` by hand instead of `resolve_listing_name`, so `-i` cannot bear on that one spelling. Caught by review, corrected at all six sites (the enum doc, `resolve_listing_name`'s doc, kernel-language.md 8.6, and three test files), each now naming `_global` as the surviving exception and pointing here.\n\nWHAT CHANGED FOR WI-923: the asymmetry is now the whole of it, and it is WORSE than the deleted mode's. `--mode sort` was inert for `-i` and REFUSED it loudly (WI-853's rule). `--mode domain _global` is inert for `-i` and SILENTLY ACCEPTS it. So the one instance of the inert-flag rule that survives in `query` is the one instance that does not obey it — and WI-921 deleted the test that pinned the rule (`wi853_query_import_test::an_import_flag_is_refused_where_it_cannot_apply`) because its subject was the mode, recording exactly this at the deletion site. Either `_global` becomes declared (this ticket's proposal, after which the arm and the exception both delete) or the arm must refuse `-i` the way the deleted mode did; doing neither leaves the rule with a live counterexample and no pin.", at: "2026-08-01T20:52:07Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-922", author: "user", content: "WI-921 REMOVED THIS TICKET'S CONSUMER, corrected its census, and left the index question untouched.\n\nTHE CLI HALF IS DONE, not by fixing the two lookups but by deleting the mode that carried them. This ticket's acceptance says 'the second lookup in `--mode sort` deleted if it becomes dead' — the whole arm is gone, both lookups with it, so that clause is discharged. Nothing else here is.\n\nTHE CENSUS IN THIS DESCRIPTION IS TOO NARROW, re-measured in WI-921 over a stdlib-only load (14 distinct `by_sort` keys; this ticket's 17 was stdlib + rust-bindings, so the counts are compatible — the SHAPE is not):\n  - 9 clause-kind tags that ARE declared: Sort 473, Member 842, Operation 364, Rule 229, Fact 115, Requirement 101, Namespace 28, Description 12, Constraint 11. All are in `load.rs` KERNEL_META_SORTS, registered `define_qualified_only(name, name, SymbolKind::Sort, _global)`. So 'nothing declares a tag' — which I also wrote into kernel-language.md 8.6 in WI-914 — is FALSE. They are declared, qualified-only, deliberately out of every scope, which is why the ladder still cannot reach them and why the conclusion held anyway.\n  - 2 tags NOT declared: Flow 16 (`flow_derive.rs:81`), OperationImpl 90 (`load.rs:14939`). Absent from KERNEL_META_SORTS. Any 'make the tag space declared' option must decide about these two.\n  - 3 USER SORTS, not tags: `anthill.prelude.PartialEq` 90 (`emit_operation_equation`, load.rs:15017), `anthill.realization.ProofRecord` 109 (load.rs:5093/5238/5446/15897), `anthill.prelude.EffectsRuntime` 1 (`emit_effects_runtime_bridge_fact`). This ticket names only the third and calls it 'every loader site but ONE'. It is THREE sites, and the first two are raw interns of a sort's QN — the same intent as the third (this clause is ABOUT sort S) with the opposite spelling.\n\nSO THE FIRST STEP AS WRITTEN IS UNDER-SPECIFIED: 'decide which way the one site moves' has three sites, and they do not move as one — EffectsRuntime is a declared symbol, PartialEq/ProofRecord are raw interns of declared sorts, and the 11 tags are a different question from all three. The dichotomy that fits the measurement is: is `sort` ONE field holding two classifications, or two fields? CLAUDE.md's 'make illegal state unrepresentable' argues for two — a closed `ClauseKind` enum (total, typed, checkable) beside an `Option<Symbol>` sort — after which the 3 user-sort keys stop being exceptions and become the sort field's only inhabitants.\n\nALSO NOTE, from WI-921's review: `by_sort()` (kb/mod.rs) unions `by_sort[S]` with `by_sort[child]` for each entity child of S. That walk is meaningful ONLY for a declared sort and is dead for every tag — it is the surviving evidence that this index was designed for the sort question and captured by the kind one. `docs/cli-design.md`'s `members` row (`kb.by_sort(member_sort)`) is a live INTERNAL reader keyed on the Member tag, so the two key spaces both have consumers today.", at: "2026-08-01T20:52:35Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-921", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED BY DELETING THE MODE, and the ticket's own premise ('the CAPABILITY missing') did not survive measurement.\n\nTHE DECISION. The ticket offered (a) rename the tag mode to `--mode kind`, freeing `--mode sort`, or (b) add `--mode entities-of`. Neither. `--mode sort` is DELETED with no replacement, because the key it selected over is not a classification fit to publish and the capability it was accused of missing is not a dimension. Measured over a stdlib load, `by_sort` has 14 distinct keys in THREE spaces: 9 declared clause-kind tags (KERNEL_META_SORTS, `define_qualified_only`), 2 undeclared tags (Flow, OperationImpl), and 3 USER SORTS (PartialEq 90, ProofRecord 109, EffectsRuntime 1) — see WI-922's feedback for the full census, which corrects that ticket's 'every loader site but ONE' to three. `--mode sort` now fails at ARGUMENT PARSING (exit 2, `invalid value 'sort' … [possible values: pattern, functor, domain]`), so a script passing it stops rather than listing something else.\n\nTHE PREMISE WAS FALSE, and this is the finding. The ticket says browsing a sort's facts requires knowing its constructors and writing one `--mode pattern` per constructor. Measured across stdlib/ + anthill-todo/ + examples/ + anthill-stl/, that shape HAS NO INSTANCE: the functors carrying facts are free-standing entities used as predicates (WorkItem 925, Feedback 891, Tag 239, IncludeMapping 38, TypeMapping 12, Implementation 9, Project 4), each its OWN single constructor, so `--mode functor WorkItem` is already the complete answer and the 'union over constructors' is a union of one. The multi-constructor sorts are data ENUMS whose constructors never head a fact — 0 facts each for Main/Test/Draft/Open/Claimed/ToolPasses/FileRef/ExitZero; they occur as FIELD VALUES inside `WorkItem(… status: Open …)`. The only sorts carrying facts do so under the SORT'S OWN name (`fact Effect[T = Clock]`, `fact Store`), which `--mode functor Effect` finds directly. So sorts with many constructors have zero facts, and functors with many facts have one constructor. 'Facts of a sort' is not a primitive notion at all: it is well-defined only where a sort's constructors are used as PREDICATES, which is the `entity`-as-constructor / `fact`-as-clause overlap, not a query dimension.\n\nI WROTE A FALSE CLAIM INTO SIX PLACES AND REVIEW CAUGHT IT. Having deleted the one MODE whose argument was not a name, I wrote up 'the last position in `query` where `-i` is inert'. FALSE: `--mode domain _global` still takes `kb.intern(name)` by hand, so `-i` is inert for that spelling — and unlike the deleted mode, it is SILENTLY ACCEPTED rather than refused. Corrected at all six sites (enum doc, `resolve_listing_name` doc, kernel-language.md 8.6, three test files); WI-923 has the handoff. The gate was stated in the deleter's vocabulary and was wider than the code — same shape as WI-903.\n\nDOCS. kernel-language.md 8.6's deviation list drops from two entries to one and the ladder rule strengthens; the retrospective paragraph I first wrote there was CUT — history in a spec is the correction I have been given twice (rule->spec, evidence->ticket). `docs/cli-design.md` turned out to be where the conflation STARTED: it used `by_sort` for both readings in adjacent examples (`by_sort WorkItem` a declared sort, `by_sort Operation` a kind tag), so the implementation could only be one of them. `docs/proposals/010-query-system.md` R1 still DECIDED 'yes' on query-by-sort with a `by_sort(Color)` built-in and carried it into the query algebra — amended in place (answers its own OQ4.1 against that option), because a decision recorded only where it was implemented leaves the design corpus arguing the other way. 007's two `query by_sort X` spellings re-worded.\n\nTESTS. wi914 pins the deletion (exit 2 + the surviving modes enumerated) and that the replacement route composes; wi853's `-i`-inert test DELETED with the reason at the site (its subject was the mode; an assertion that cannot fail is not a pin) plus the `_global` counterexample recorded there; wi767 moved to `--mode functor` since its claim is about the FLAG's position. Full workspace via scripts/test.sh: 3971 passed, 0 failed. Net -66 lines.\n\nREVIEW (4 agents) also found: a stderr assertion bypassing `Output::has_diagnostic` (fixed — the harness documents why line-wise matters), a strictly weaker duplicate spawn (deleted), and the rationale written at full length in 4-5 places with counts already drifting against WI-922's (deduplicated to one self-contained site; the code comment's pointer INTO cli-design.md went stale within minutes when that paragraph was edited away, which is why it is now self-contained rather than a cross-file reference). SKIPPED: extracting an `assert_no_listing` helper (4 copies exist repo-wide — wider than this diff); keeping the two-step route test despite its second half duplicating the headline test (it is the only end-to-end pin of the replacement, which is this ticket's acceptance); putting a migration hint in clap `after_help` (there is no replacement for the tag listing, and delete-only was the decision).\n\nSPUN OFF: WI-924 (a reflect `name:` field matches a short name under `-i` but not the qualified spelling that denotes the same sort — silent `0 result(s)`, full 2x2 measured).", at: "2026-08-01T20:58:25Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-921", description: some(value: "`anthill query` has no way to list the facts of a DECLARED SORT. `--mode sort` does not do it — measured in WI-914: a clause's `sort` is the kernel's CLAUSE-KIND TAG (`Sort`/`Fact`/`Rule`/`Operation`/`Namespace`/`Constraint`/…), interned as RAW TEXT at the loader site that files the clause, so `--mode sort wi853.kb.S` answered `0 result(s)` about a sort that has a fact. WI-914 made that a loud refusal naming what the mode does list, which closes the LIE but leaves the CAPABILITY missing: to browse one sort's facts you must know its constructors and write a `--mode pattern` per constructor. NOT DRIVEN as a design. FIRST STEP: decide whether this is a new mode (`--mode kind` for the tag, freeing `--mode sort` for the sort) or a new one (`--mode entities-of`) — the rename is user-visible and breaks `--mode sort Fact`, so it is a DECISION, not an implementation. Whichever wins, the sort's constructors are reachable through `kb.sort_entities` / `constructor_parent_sort`, and the listing is a union over them. ACCEPTANCE: one documented way to list a declared sort's facts, its argument read by the SAME `_global` ladder WI-914 put on `--mode functor`/`--mode domain`; kernel-language.md §8.6's deviation list updated if the tag mode survives under a different name; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-01T20:58:33Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-925", author: "user", content: "WI-926 DELIVERED, and it dissolved this ticket's stated blocker rather than answering it. This ticket said \"decide the desugaring's SYMBOL SHAPE before writing it, because a same-named sort and constructor collide\". There is now no collision: §6.3 was reformed so an EPONYMOUS constructor IS its sort (`sort Project { entity Project(...) }` defines ONE symbol; no nested `Project.Project`), which is exactly the shape the desugaring produces. So `entity Standalone(a)` -> `sort Standalone { entity Standalone(a) }` is identity-preserving on the symbol: `test.Standalone` keeps its symbol, its field schema, and its fact-head functor. `wi926_eponymous_entity_test::the_sugar_and_the_desugaring_agree` already pins that the two forms present identically (schema present, `constructor_parent_sort` none, no nested twin, `is_entity_constructor` true) -- it is the acceptance test for this ticket's core claim, written from the other side. WHAT ACTUALLY REMAINS for WI-925 is narrower than filed: (1) `sort_kind` and `kind_of` still DIFFER between the two forms -- measured `test.Standalone` = kind Entity / sort_kind None vs `test.Project` = kind Sort / sort_kind Some(Sort) -- and the desugaring is what would make them agree; (2) the `is_entity_of` guard comment at load.rs (the `emit_entity_info` `is_sort_scope` arm) still says \"A namespace-level entity has no parent sort\", which stays TRUE under WI-926 (an eponymous sort is its own type and deliberately gets NO self `entity_of` edge) -- so revisit whether the trailing `is_entity_of(?x, ?sort)` guard in typing.anthill is still needed at all, rather than assuming the desugaring removes it. ALSO CORRECTED: this ticket's premise that WI-922's `constructor_parent_sort(functor).unwrap_or_else(|| kb.intern(\"Fact\"))` was the origin case is now moot -- that fallback is already gone (WI-922 replaced it with an unconditional `ClauseKind::Fact`), and WI-926 replaced the remaining `unwrap_or_default()` schema fallback with a loud `SerError::NoFieldSchema`.", at: "2026-08-01T23:27:03Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-926", description: some(value: "A single-constructor sort's NAME resolves to the SORT, so the persistence loader deserializes every row of such a section against an EMPTY field schema and files the fact under the sort symbol. MEASURED with a probe over `sort Project { entity Project(name: String, language: String) }`: `test.Project` -> Symbol(172), sort_kind=Some(Sort), constructor_parent_sort=None, entity_field_names=None; `test.Project.Project` -> Symbol(173), sort_kind=None, constructor_parent_sort=Some(172), entity_field_names=Some(2). The sort takes the plain qualified name and the constructor takes the nested one. `term_ser.rs::resolve_entity_functor` resolves a section's `meta.entity` with `try_resolve_symbol` and therefore lands on 172; the caller then does `kb.entity_field_names(functor).map(..).unwrap_or_default()`, so the `None` becomes an EMPTY field list and every row in the section is reconstructed with no schema -- WI-501's whole point is that reload MUST consult each field's declared type. The rows are additionally asserted with the SORT as their head functor, which is why `toml_ser_test` then finds them via `rules_by_functor(try_resolve_symbol(\"test.Project\"))`: the test and the bug agree with each other. Driving the fix confirms the shape -- resolving through to the single constructor makes the section load with the real 2-field schema, and `toml_ser_test::load_toml_primitives` then fails at its own `rules_by_functor` query because the stored head functor legitimately moves from 172 to 173. So the fix is a persistence BEHAVIOUR change with round-trip tests encoding the current shape, which is why WI-922 did not smuggle it in (the site carries the measurement as a comment). NOT DRIVEN beyond that. FIRST STEP: decide whether `meta.entity` names the SORT or the CONSTRUCTOR, since the two have different qualified names and only one has a schema; a data file naming `test.Project` today gets neither reliably. THIS TICKET GATES WI-925: implementing 6.3's desugaring would give EVERY standalone entity this same sort/constructor name collision, generalizing the defect from single-constructor sorts to all entities. ACCEPTANCE: a persisted section resolves to something that HAS a field schema or fails loudly -- no `unwrap_or_default()` standing in for a schema; round-trip tests updated to the decided head functor; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-01T23:32:45Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-922", author: "user", content: "Dependency on WI-925 REMOVED as satisfied differently, not as ignored. It existed because WI-925 was named \"the ORIGIN of the parentless-entity case\" that `term_ser.rs::entity_sort`'s `constructor_parent_sort(functor).unwrap_or_else(|| kb.intern(\"Fact\"))` papered over. WI-926 settled that case without the desugaring: a persisted section's schema lookup is now a loud `SerError::NoFieldSchema` instead of `unwrap_or_default()`, so a functor with no field schema is refused rather than reconstructed against an empty one -- whether or not it has a parent sort. Nothing in this ticket's acceptance needs the standalone-entity desugaring. DELIVERED SHAPE, wider than the acceptance asked: rather than unifying the two `by_sort` key spaces, the index was DELETED. Its name was the original defect -- stage0 design specified \"all facts of a given sort (including its entities)\" and proposal 010 planned a user-facing `by_sort(Color)` on it, but the loader (its only producer) never filed a clause under a sort; it files the clause KIND, so the index answered \"which clauses are of kind K\" from the first commit while its name promised something else. Naming it honestly showed it should not exist: a kind is a FILTER, not a selector. `rule_heads_for_sort` selects on the far more selective `by_domain` and merely filters on kind; the proof tests want `rules_by_functor`; `hint_cites_for` runs once per Z3 dispatch and discriminates on a `hint` meta flag anyway. One cold caller does not pay for a map pushed at every assert and retain-scanned at every retract. `clause_kind` is now a `Copy` enum on `RuleEntry`, filtered on a live-clause walk. The sort question keeps its three correctly-keyed homes (`is_entity_of`, `guards_by_sort`, `sort_info_index`), none of which was ever this index. THIRD ACCEPTANCE CLAUSE MOOT: \"the second lookup in `--mode sort` deleted if it becomes dead\" -- WI-921 (commit c51cde25) deleted `--mode sort` entirely, so there is no lookup left to retire. The bridge site `emit_effects_runtime_bridge_fact` now files `ClauseKind::Fact` with `EffectsRuntime` as its domain and head functor, and its comment retracts the \"same convention as the stdlib's `fact Effect[T = Modify[?]]`\" justification as a convention that never existed (`Fact.sort` was `None` at the only site that built one, and no such stdlib fact remains).", at: "2026-08-02T05:27:18Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-922", description: some(value: "A clause's `sort` key is a RAW-INTERNED clause-kind tag at every loader site but ONE. Measured in WI-914 over a full stdlib + rust-bindings load: 16 of the 17 distinct `by_sort` keys are raw interns (`intern(\"Fact\")`, `intern(\"Rule\")`, `intern(\"Sort\")`, `intern(\"Namespace\")`, `intern(\"Constraint\")`, and dotted spellings like `intern(\"anthill.prelude.PartialEq\")` from `emit_operation_equation`); the 17th is `emit_effects_runtime_bridge_fact` (load.rs ~3495), which files its bridge fact under the RESOLVED `anthill.prelude.EffectsRuntime` symbol. Those two key spaces are DISJOINT — `intern(qn)` and `by_qualified_name[qn]` are different symbols (`define` never touches `intern_map`) — so the one declared key is reachable only by a second, differently-spelled lookup, which is why `anthill query --mode sort` carries two. The site's own comment justifies it as 'the same convention as the stdlib's `fact Effect[T = Modify[?]]` (its fact sort is `Effect`)'; that convention is DEAD — `Fact.sort` is `None` at every parser site (`parse/convert.rs` builds `Fact { sort: None }` and nothing else constructs one), so a source `fact` is filed under the tag `Fact`, and no `fact Effect[…]` survives in the stdlib. NOT DRIVEN beyond the measurement. FIRST STEP: decide which way the one site moves — file the bridge fact under a raw tag like every sibling, or make the tag space declared. ACCEPTANCE: one key space for `by_sort`, the stale comment at the bridge site corrected or removed, the second lookup in `--mode sort` deleted if it becomes dead; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-921", "WI-926"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-02T05:27:20Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-927", author: "user", content: "Resolved by the FIRST option (give the bracket surface its own reading), not the second (refuse the combination at declaration). Refusing would have banned a generic record type -- `sort Box { sort T = ?; entity Box(value: T) }` is exactly the shape anthill-cpp-gen's `generic_entity_emits_template_prefix` emits a `template<typename T>` for -- so the \"unrepresentable combination\" reading would have removed a working capability to avoid a reading bug. WIDER THAN THE TICKET MEASURED. The ticket recorded the NAMED form (`Box[T = Int64]` refused as `unknown field 'T'`). Driving found the POSITIONAL form was worse and SILENT until the very end: `Box[Int64]` reached the WI-433 positional->named desugar, which filled the entity's FIRST FIELD with the type argument, so `check_sort_type_args` then reported \"`Box` has no type parameter named 'value'\" -- a label the author never wrote, describing a rename the loader had just done. Also found: the stray case DOUBLE-REPORTED (`Box[W = Int64]` produced both `UnknownEntityField{W}` and `InvalidTypeArgument{W}`), so one written node yielded two errors under two readings. FIX SITES, both producers, both using provenance parse ALREADY records (`mark_type_application`) -- no new mechanism: (1) `convert_term`'s `Term::Fn` arm binds `is_type_app` ONCE and skips the entity-field reading (the positional->named desugar and the unknown-label check); the sibling `check_sort_type_args` gate now reads that same binding instead of re-asking parse, so the gate and the reading it excludes cannot disagree. (2) The `ApplyOrConstructor` occurrence drain (operation bodies) makes `is_entity` false for a bracketed node. The generalization is not eponymous-specific: a BRACKET IS NEVER A CONSTRUCTION, for any functor. What the ticket called \"a missing CHANNEL, not a wrong choice\" was right -- but the channel already existed at parse; it simply was not consulted at these two sites, because the functor's KIND stood in for the surface, which worked only while a sort and an entity were always distinct symbols (pre-WI-926). MEASURED MATRIX after the fix, each eponymous case paired with a non-eponymous control of the same shape: `Box[T = Int64]` clean (was `unknown field 'T'`); `Box[Int64]` clean (was \"no type parameter named 'value'\"); `Box[W = Int64]` reports ONLY `no type parameter named 'W'` (was a double report); `Box(value: 1)` constructs; `Box(valu: 1)` still reports `'Box' has no field 'valu'`; `b: Box[T = Int64]` in type position unchanged. Control run with the fix disabled: 3 of the 5 new tests fail; the other 2 are must-not-change controls that hold both ways by design. kernel-language.md 6.3's bullet, which recorded the pre-fix behaviour, is replaced by the surface rule.", at: "2026-08-02T05:56:53Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-927", description: some(value: "A sort that is BOTH eponymous (§6.3: `sort Box { entity Box(value: T) }`) and PARAMETERIZED (`sort T = ?`) cannot be written as a bracketed type application in VALUE position. MEASURED at WI-926's HEAD, control vs subject in one probe: a NON-eponymous `sort Holder { sort T = ?; entity mk(value: T) }` loads `rule ok(?x) :- eq(?x, is_modifiable(Holder[T = Int64]))` clean, while the same rule over an eponymous `Box` is REFUSED with `UnknownEntityField { entity: \"Box\", field: \"T\", declared: \"value\" }`. TYPE position is unaffected -- `entity use(b: Box[T = Int64])` loads CLEAN, because an annotation lowers through `TypeExpr::Parameterized` and never reaches the Apply/Constructor decision. ROOT CAUSE: the bracketed `X[..]` and parenthesized `X(..)` surfaces reach `LoadBuildFrame::ApplyOrConstructor` as ONE node -- `is_type_application` is parse-time provenance that load.rs:9052 documents as the only thing that can tell the two apart, and it IS consulted at the three `check_sort_type_args` sites, but it is NOT carried onto the built `Expr` (every `Expr::Apply` is built with an empty `type_args` at the ApplyOrConstructor drain). Once §6.3 makes one symbol both the sort and the entity, the `is_entity` decision at that drain can only pick one reading, and it picks CONSTRUCTION -- correct for the common case, and LOUD (not silent) for this one. The defect is therefore a missing CHANNEL, not a wrong choice. NOT A REGRESSION IN REACH: before WI-926 the shape was unreachable for a different reason (the eponymous entity had a separate nested symbol), and no source in the repo exercises it -- the stdlib's three eponymous sorts (TotalFloat, Duration, Timestamp) are all non-parameterized. FIRST STEP: decide whether the bracket surface gets its own occurrence channel (carry `is_type_application` onto the Expr, the load.rs:9052 reading) or whether an eponymous parameterized sort is refused AT DECLARATION as an unrepresentable combination (the make-illegal-states-unrepresentable reading). ACCEPTANCE: either a bracketed value-position type application over an eponymous parameterized sort loads and denotes the type, or the combination is refused at its declaration with a located diagnostic; the kernel-language.md §6.3 bullet recording today's behaviour updated to match; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-926"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-02T05:56:56Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-929", description: some(value: "HARDEN the symbol category SET against name-collision merges: several has_kind readers were written when a symbol carried exactly ONE category, and WI-925 made define/define_qualified_only ACCUMULATE.\n\nALL FINDINGS BELOW ARE LATENT. The WI-925 /code-review agent grepped every .anthill in the repo (409 sort/namespace declarations) and found NO live instance of any of them; the full workspace is green at 3992/0. Filed so they are not lost, not because anything is broken today.\n\nPROVENANCE: from the WI-925 /code-review correctness agent. I independently verified only the two it found that WERE live -- the by-qualified-name arm dropping SymbolKind::Entity, and symbol_is_value_place losing exclusivity -- and both are already FIXED in WI-925. The items below are REPORTED, NOT independently re-verified; DRIVE each before fixing it.\n\nROOT CAUSE (common to most): define reuses an existing symbol on a repeated SHORT NAME in one scope, and now records the second declaration's category too. So two genuinely different declarations sharing a name merge their categories, and any has_kind reader that meant the EXCLUSIVE question (\"was declared as X\") silently widens.\n\n(1) MOST SEVERE -- SILENT TRIGGER-SORT HOLE. Item::SortWithBody (load.rs ~2618) reuses an existing by_qualified_name symbol WITHOUT calling define. If that qualified name was already claimed by a non-Sort declaration -- an explicit namespace, an implicit namespace minted by ensure_intermediate_namespaces for a dotted name, or an operation, scanned earlier in the same file OR in ANY earlier file since pass 1 is global -- then the sort's symbol never gets SymbolKind::Sort while register_sort still runs. sort_of_head(X) then returns None where it used to return Some(X), and collect_trigger_sorts DROPS the trigger sort of a constraint whose pattern head is X: the guard is registered under no sort and never re-fires on an assert. A silent hole on a clean load -- the failure mode this repo's development principles single out.\n\n(2) is_sort_scope WIDENING via a merged Namespace/Sort. Source shape:\n    namespace Foo ... end\n    sort Foo = Int64\nyields kinds {Namespace, Sort}, so is_sort_scope answers TRUE for the namespace's scope term.\n\n(3) PASS-1 vs LOAD DISAGREEMENT. Item::AbstractSort ALWAYS defines, so a later \"sort Foo = Int64\" merges Sort into a namespace Foo that pass 1 already used as a non-sort scope. An \"entity Bar\" inside \"namespace Foo\" then takes add_kind(Sort) in pass 1 but is treated as sort-body at load (the !is_sort_scope block is skipped, so NO EntityInfo and NO register_self_sort). One declaration, two answers.\n\n(4) ensure_intermediate_namespaces (load.rs ~2166): for a dotted name whose PREFIX is a free-standing entity, the has_kind(Namespace) filter fails, define merges Namespace into the ENTITY symbol and returns it, and the resulting ns_term is Fn{E} -- which is_sort_scope now answers true for. So \"entity E.Bar(...)\" would become a constructor OF THE ENTITY.\n\n(5) load.rs ~11964, the guard \"if !has_kind(head_resolved, Sort) { return None }\", is a live WIDENING: every free-standing entity now carries Sort, so a two-segment \"E.Member\" in type position no longer bails. It falls into the else arm, which looks up by_qualified_name of \"<E_qn>.<Member>\" -- and emit_entity_info registers entity FIELDS under exactly that key. So \"entity Foo(Bar: Int64)\" plus a type annotation \"x: Foo.Bar\" resolves to the FIELD symbol as a sort ref. Latent only because uppercase field names are unusual; the arm's own doc (\"Enum.Entity\") no longer describes what reaches it.\n\n(6) logical_sort_qn (load.rs ~11897) states the EXCLUSIVE reading in its own doc (\"app.Config is a Namespace\", and therefore not stripped). has_kind(Sort) no longer excludes a symbol that is both, so that doc is false as written.\n\n(7) DOWNWARD ASYMMETRY. register_self_sort pushes into sort_entities, so sort_children(E) == [E] and constructors_of_sort(E) == [E] for a wrapped entity, while constructor_parent_sort cuts the fixpoint on the UPWARD side only. Current readers are harmless (sort_constructor_syms feeds match candidates; the exhaustiveness reader is gated on sort_kind == Enum, unreachable for a free-standing entity), but the asymmetry needs either a strict downward view or a stated reason none is needed, recorded next to constructor_parent_sort's doc.\n\n(8) PRE-EXISTING, now inherited by add_kind (WI-720): for a DOTTED entity name both is_sort_scope guards read the OUTER scope while the symbol is defined in actual_scope, so \"sort S { entity a.B(...) }\" marks B a constructor of S although B lives in namespace a.\n\nAPPROACH NOTE: do NOT sweep the ~58 remaining \"kind_of == Some(K)\" predicate sites mechanically. The two readings coincide for every symbol carrying ONE category, so only a site-by-site judgement can tell which was meant -- a blind sweep is exactly how (5) and the already-fixed symbol_is_value_place bug arise.\n\nACCEPTANCE: each item above either driven to a failing case and fixed, or closed with a recorded measurement showing it unreachable; a name-collision fixture (namespace + same-named sort; free-standing entity + dotted child) added as a regression test; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-925"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-925", description: some(value: "Spec 6.3's standalone-entity desugaring is NOT IMPLEMENTED, and it is the ORIGIN of the parentless-entity case that WI-922 found being papered over. kernel-language.md 6.3 (and the 5.x summary) state that `entity Account(id: AccountId)` desugars to `sort Account { entity Account(id: AccountId) }`, and `entity Marker` to `sort Marker { entity Marker }`. The loader does not do this. MEASURED with a probe over `namespace test / sort Project { entity Project(name: String, language: String) } / entity Standalone(a: String) end`, reading the KB after load_all: `test.Standalone` -> Symbol(176), sort_kind=None, constructor_parent_sort=None, entity_field_names=Some(1). So a standalone entity gets its field schema registered but NO enclosing sort and NO EntityOf link. Confirmed structurally: `register_sort` is called only from the sort-body path (load.rs:12945 and 13004); `load_entity` (load.rs:13531, the namespace-level arm reached from load.rs:12834) calls neither `register_sort` nor `register_entity_of`. The loader KNOWS and documents the divergence in place -- load.rs:13578 reads \"A namespace-level entity has no parent sort, so the `entity_of` typing rule is guarded with a trailing `is_entity_of(?x, ?sort)`\". WHY THIS IS AN ORIGIN, not a cosmetic gap: because an entity may legitimately have no parent sort, `constructor_parent_sort` can miss for a perfectly good entity, and callers invent something for the miss. WI-922 found `term_ser.rs::entity_sort` doing exactly that -- `constructor_parent_sort(functor).unwrap_or_else(|| kb.intern(\"Fact\"))` -- returning a real declared SORT on a hit and a clause-KIND tag on a miss, one Symbol holding answers to two different questions, selected by whether registration had happened yet. FIRST STEP: decide the desugaring's SYMBOL SHAPE before writing it, because a same-named sort and constructor collide -- see WI-926, which measures that collision on today's sort-body form and is the reason this ticket must not land first. ACCEPTANCE: a standalone `entity E` produces a sort and an EntityOf link per 6.3, or the spec is amended to match the loader; the `is_entity_of` guard at load.rs:13578 revisited with its comment; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-926"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-08-02T08:31:14Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-925", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED, but the acceptance was met by a DIFFERENT reading than filed, and that difference is the ticket's real content -- flagged here so a reader does not think the original plan was executed.\n\nFILED ACCEPTANCE: \"a standalone entity E produces a sort AND AN ENTITYOF LINK per 6.3, or the spec is amended to match the loader.\" The sort half is done. The EntityOf half is satisfied REFLEXIVELY, not by giving the entity a distinct parent: a wrapped entity's sort is ITSELF (WI-926 -- one symbol), so the belongs-to relation is TOTAL rather than partial-plus-an-exception. This came from the user's questions, not from the ticket: \"wrapping was created for satisfying EntityOf (which should be total)\" and \"why do you think that parent should not be cyclic?\".\n\nTWO OF MY OWN CLAIMS WERE REFUTED IN THE COURSE OF IT, both by driving:\n - I first fixed this by calling register_sort for a free-standing entity. That made ONE symbol carry SymbolKind::Entity in one index and SortKind::Sort in another, with nothing keeping them in step -- the duplication the user objected to. Deleted; the categories answer instead.\n - I then declared the self-edge \"refuted, measured\" on the strength of a stack overflow I had NOT diagnosed. Wrong: it was evidence about three pre-WI-926 WALKERS that climb parent(parent(..)) under an invariant one states outright (\"a parent is a sort, never a constructor\"), which WI-926 had already made stale. The fixpoint is now cut ONCE in the strict view constructor_parent_sort -- guarding two walkers individually still overflowed on the third.\n - Second obstacle, also measured: register_entity_of ALSO sets constructor_symbols, which drives the WI-511 nullary alloc canon and re-spells a sort's own scope key. Routing the self edge through it produced 24 failures (\"expected Type, got WorkItem\") -- exactly what WI-926's comment predicted when it declined to mark the eponymous constructor. register_self_sort records belongs-to without that flag.\n\nTHE CATEGORY SET was the user's diagnosis too (\"we have symbol and have category of it -- now we should have list of categories for the same symbol?\"). A symbol now carries an ORDERED SET; both §6.3 spellings record {Sort, Entity}, differing only in which keyword heads the list. Done in two phases so the storage change was provably behaviour-preserving (baseline 3988/0 -> phase A 3988/0 identical -> final 3992/0).\n\n/code-review CAUGHT A REAL BUG IN THE FIX: my comment claimed it recorded \"every role this name plays\" and it did not -- two of the three arms producing an entity's symbol never call define, so with `sort Colour {..}` written before `entity Colour(x)`, has_kind(Colour, Entity) was FALSE while the field schema WAS registered, and flipped to true if the declarations were swapped. The exact source-order dependence a category set exists to remove, surviving inside the change meant to remove it. Fixed at the declaration; pinned by membership_is_independent_of_declaration_order. Review also caught a probe I had wired into the suite that asserted nothing, a control that passed both ways, a doc contradicting the code, and symbol_is_value_place where my migration to membership silently broke an EXCLUSIVE discriminator (now reads primary_kind, which reproduces the old field exactly).\n\nREVISITED AS THE FEEDBACK ASKED: the trailing is_entity_of(?x, ?sort) guard in typing.anthill STAYS -- measured by deleting it, entity_of(bank.Account, ?p) yields 1 instead of 0, binding the enclosing NAMESPACE as a bogus parent. register_self_sort does not obviate it; the two answer different questions.\n\nConsequence worth recording: sort_kind now has ONE reader and it asks == Enum, so sort_info no longer answers \"is a sort\" anywhere -- that duplication went by disuse. constructor_symbols is NOT redundant with has_kind(Entity); it gates the alloc canon.\n\nFOLLOW-UPS FILED: WI-928 (§6.3's load-time half -- an ill-typed fact over a free-standing entity still loads CLEAN, verified both arms myself, because such an entity reaches neither defined_sorts nor emit_sort_info so type_check_sorts skips it) and WI-929 (latent name-collision hazards from define's new accumulation; the reviewer grepped all 409 sort/namespace declarations and found no live instance).\n\nCommitted 14983b08, pushed to main. Full workspace 3992 passed / 0 failed.", at: "2026-08-02T08:32:12Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-930", description: some(value: "A loader DECLARATION RECORD does not conform to the field types reflect declares for it -- 685 measured mismatches, in 7 classes, and the whole family is one question: what does a reflect record's slot actually hold?\n\nMEASURED under WI-928 (stdlib + Rust host bindings), by letting typing.rs's check_entity_facts run over them:\n  EntityInfo.name              180x  expected Symbol, got <the named sort> (Expr, Pattern, MemberKind, ...)\n  MemberInfo.name              180x  expected Symbol, same shape\n  ProofRecord.witness          128x  expected Term, got ProofWitness\n  ProofRecord.parametric_context 128x expected Term, got List\n  SortInfo.constructors elem    50x  expected Symbol, got <the named sort>   (reported as 'SortInfo.head' -- the cons cell's field, outer entity name)\n  OperationInfo.params elem     17x  expected Term, got TypeExtractor / EffectExpression\n  OperationInfo.return_type      2x  expected Term\nZERO of the 685 is a user fact. Every one is a record the loader wrote about a declaration.\n\nWHY IT IS NOT SIMPLY A BUG IN EITHER SIDE. The loader writes a reflect HANDLE into these slots -- a name term for a name, a KB term handle for a term, a structured witness for a witness -- and check_value_against_type asks a SUBTYPE question. Reaching a handle from a value is a CONVERSION (as_term / term_as_entity, WI-406) and deliberately NOT subsumption: 'Term' is not a top type (user decision, 2026-06-06). So the check as it stands reports a mismatch for every well-formed record, and 'make Term a top type' is the one fix that is ruled out in advance.\n\nCURRENT STATE (WI-928): check_entity_facts SKIPS declaration records, keyed on KnowledgeBase::is_metadata_functor -- the same predicate as WI-630's write-side tripwire, so the set the loader may write and the set the check skips cannot drift. That restores exactly the status quo (these facts were unreachable before free-standing entities got a SortInfo) and is documented in place. It is a boundary, not a repair.\n\nTHE QUESTION THIS TICKET OWNS: can the reflect schema and the loader's writes be brought into agreement, so declaration records are checked like any other fact? Directions, in rough order of promise:\n (a) DECLARE WHAT THE SLOT HOLDS. EntityInfo.name : Symbol may simply be wrong if what is stored is a NAME REFERENCE whose type is the referenced sort; likewise ProofRecord.witness : Term for a ProofWitness value. Cheapest to try, and it may absorb 5 of the 7 classes. Note the corpus evidence that a declared type nobody checks drifts from the data: WI-928 found WorkItem.description : Option[T = Term] and Feedback.content : Term holding 921 + 894 prose strings, and corrected both to String.\n (b) TYPE THE HANDLE RELATION. Give Symbol / Term a checking rule that recognizes a name reference / a term handle without making Term a top type -- the WI-385 arg/field-validation family's unfinished half.\n (c) CONVERT AT THE WRITE. Have the loader write through the explicit conversion, so the slots hold what they declare.\nCONSTRAINT: whatever lands must keep 'Term is not a top type' true. Verify against the user's WI-406 decision before touching types_compatible.\n\nACCEPTANCE: either declaration records type-check (and the WI-928 skip in check_entity_facts is deleted), or the reason they cannot is written down in the spec next to the reflect schema and the skip cites it. cargo-test green via rustland/scripts/test.sh.\n\nSOURCE: measured while delivering WI-928 (2026-08-02). The 685 were invisible until then because reflect's records are declared as free-standing entities and a free-standing entity emitted no SortInfo, so no constructor list ever named one."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-928", description: some(value: "§6.3's standalone-entity desugaring is still HALF-implemented: a free-standing entity is absent from defined_sorts and emits no SortInfo, so its facts are NEVER type-checked at load. WI-925 made the two §6.3 spellings agree on sort_kind (the runtime/trigger-sort half). The LOAD-TIME half is still divergent, and it is the same defect class -- two spellings of one declaration disagreeing on whether a write is accepted. MEASURED (probe over namespace test.wi925tc, identical fact under each spelling): `entity Thing(count: Int64)` + `fact Thing(count: \"hello\")` -> loads CLEAN, 0 errors; `sort Thing { entity Thing(count: Int64) }` + the SAME fact -> LoadError '9:8: type mismatch in Thing.count (entity-field): expected Int64, got String'. MECHANISM: load.rs runs type_check_sorts over LoadResult.defined_sorts; load_sort_with_body pushes to defined_sorts and calls emit_sort_info, load_entity does NEITHER. typing.rs's per-sort loop then gates twice -- the free-standing entity is absent from sort_names AND has no SortInfo, so `None => continue` skips it and check_entity_facts never runs over its facts. That `continue` is also a SILENT SKIP of the kind the repo's development principles forbid. SCALE: 68 free-standing entities in stdlib + rust bindings alone (measured), including anthill.reflect.{EntityInfo, SortInfo, OperationInfo, FieldInfo, MemberInfo} and anthill.realization.{Implementation, OperationImpl, ProofRecord} -- every fact over any of them is currently unchecked at load, in stdlib and user code alike. NOTE WI-925 SHARPENED one inconsistency rather than closing it: sort_kind(E) now says 'E is a sort' while KB.sorts (which reads SortInfo FACTS via reflect/reader.rs, not the index) still says it is not -- two indexes now disagree, where before both were consistently wrong. ALSO IN SCOPE: emit_induction_rule is not run for the sugar (a single-constructor sort's induction principle is well-defined, so this is a straight omission); and emit_member_facts_for_items emits a MemberInfo SELF EDGE (name: E, kind: Constructor, parent: E) for the long form that register_entity_of refuses two blocks earlier as 'a containment that does not exist' (WI-926 residue). BEWARE: turning load-time type-checking ON for 68 previously-unchecked entities may surface existing violations in stdlib -- measure the blast radius before assuming it is a small change. ACCEPTANCE: the two §6.3 spellings agree on whether an ill-typed fact loads; typing.rs's None => continue is loud or justified in place; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-925"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-02T09:50:37Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-928", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED, with the enablement's blast radius MEASURED before committing to it -- which the ticket asked for, and which changed what the ticket could contain.\n\nTHE FIX. load_entity now pushes to defined_sorts and emits the same SortInfo the long form emits (one constructor, ITSELF -- WI-926), plus the single-constructor induction principle. Verified on 6 shapes (Int64, Term, Option[Term], Symbol slots): sugar and long form give the identical verdict and the identical message, differing only in the line the fact sits on. The MemberInfo SELF EDGE is gone too (the containment register_entity_of refuses two blocks earlier).\n\nTICKET CORRECTION. The NOTE says 'sort_kind(E) now says E is a sort'. MEASURED: sort_kind(E) is None for the sugar -- WI-925 wrote no register_sort, and its own test says so. The accessor that says 'E is a sort' is has_kind(E, SymbolKind::Sort), the WI-925 category set. The disagreement the NOTE describes was real (category set vs SortInfo facts) and emitting SortInfo is what closes it; sort_kind stays None, and its one consumer (Enum match exhaustiveness) is unaffected either way.\n\nWHAT THE ENABLEMENT ACTUALLY TURNS ON -- not one check, but EVERY SortInfo-driven load-time pass, for all 68 entities. Full measured inventory:\n (1) 685 reports over the loader's OWN declaration records: EntityInfo.name 180, MemberInfo.name 180, ProofRecord.witness 128, ProofRecord.parametric_context 128, SortInfo.constructors 50, OperationInfo.params 17, OperationInfo.return_type 2. ZERO are user facts. This is a CATEGORY ERROR, not 685 defects: those slots hold reflect HANDLES, check_value_against_type asks a SUBTYPE question, and reaching a handle is a CONVERSION (as_term, WI-406 -- 'Term is not a top type' is your standing decision, so the one fix that would absorb them is ruled out in advance). check_entity_facts now skips declaration records, keyed on the same predicate as WI-630's write-side tripwire. That RESTORES the status quo (they were unreachable before, reflect's records being themselves free-standing entities) rather than adding an exemption. -> WI-930 filed with the full inventory.\n (2) 12 unbacked provider operations, TRUE by check_provider_operations' own criterion: Vec3 (vec_add/sub/scale/zero backed only by RULES -- WI-818 settled that a rule is not backing) and FileStore / IndexedFileStore / SqlStore (host-implemented, with no Implementation fact recording it). Invisible until now because sorts_with_constructors reads SortInfo -- the check's own doc named Vec3 as a value-less 'namespace-level provision head', which it never was (Vec3(x: 1.0, y: 2.0, z: 3.0) is a value). Doc corrected. Per your call: STAGED behind load::free_standing_entity_sorts with the inventory in place, and WI-931 owns closing it (host bindings for the stores; bodies-or-drop for Vec3's provision). Both the skip and the set are marked for deletion with that ticket.\n (3) WorkItem.description : Option[T = Term] and Feedback.content : Term hold PROSE -- 921 + 894 rows, not one of them a Term. Corrected to String in rustland/anthill-todo/anthill/domain.anthill and its examples/github-todo twin. Same defect one layer up: a declared type nobody ever checked had drifted from its data. The tracker CLI was broken by the enablement until this landed, and loads clean now.\n\ntyping.rs's None => continue is JUSTIFIED in place rather than made loud: both producers of defined_sorts now emit a SortInfo, so None no longer means 'this declaration has no record'; the only remaining reach is a name no load defined. Measured, not argued -- two full-workspace runs with a panic! in that arm never reached it.\n\nDECISION A READER SHOULD KNOW: SortInfo.kind gains a fourth value, 'entity', alongside sort/enum/abstract. It records the KEYWORD WRITTEN, which is the rule WI-925 already set for the category set's head, and it also gives the WI-931 staging a key read from the same facts as the classifier (no symbol-identity hazard). No reader may branch on it to decide what a declaration IS -- name, definition and constructors agree.\n\nTests: 9 in wi928_entity_load_record_test.rs. Control discipline measured by disabling the emission: 6 SUBJECT tests fail without it, 3 control_ tests pass either way BY DESIGN (a well-typed fact still loads; a differently-named sort-body variant is untouched; the stdlib still loads clean) -- each says so at its site. Full workspace: 4001 passed, 0 failed. Spec 6.3 updated with the load-time half; reflect.anthill's kind comment updated; the eq_dispatch_carrier_domain doc asserting 'emit_sort_info has exactly one caller' corrected in the same commit that falsified it.", at: "2026-08-02T09:51:07Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-932", description: some(value: "Expose BulkStore.pull to the evaluator, and restore the file backends' BulkStore provision.\n\nSTATE AFTER WI-931. Neither FileStore nor IndexedFileStore provides BulkStore. The provision was removed (stdlib/anthill/persistence/filesystem.anthill) because BulkStore declares exactly one member -- pull(store: BulkStore) -> List[T = Term] -- and NO anthill-callable implementation of it exists. MEASURED: the six other storage operations (persist/flush/monotonicity/retract/update/retrieve) are registered eval builtins; pull is not, in either registry. A fact BulkStore[X] therefore certified an operation that dies at the call site, which is the unsoundness check_provider_operations exists to refuse (WI-363 / WI-818).\n\nWHAT EXISTS. The Rust trait method BulkStore::pull(&self) -> Result<Vec<ParsedFile>, PersistenceError> is implemented by both file backends (rustland/anthill-core/src/persistence/{file_store,indexed_file_store}.rs) and driven FROM RUST -- the CLI's cold start reads the .anthill tree through it. So the capability is real; only the anthill-side bridge is missing.\n\nWHY IT IS NOT A ONE-LINER, and this is the actual work. The host side returns parsed FILES; the anthill signature says List[Term]. There is no ParsedFile -> Vec<TermId> conversion in the tree -- kb::load::load asserts a ParsedFile INTO the KB rather than yielding its items as terms -- and what pull should do with a file's non-fact items (rules, namespaces, imports) is a design question, not a mechanical one. Note the sibling that already solved the easier half: persistence_retrieve wraps store.retrieve(...) -> Vec<TermId> as a Stream[Term, Error], because retrieve queries rows that are already terms.\n\nSHAPE. Add a store_pull host function to HOST_FNS (rustland/anthill-core/src/eval/builtins.rs), map it in the BulkStore binding block (rustland/anthill-stl/anthill/persistence.anthill, which already carries Store/NonMonotonicStore/QueryableStore), and restore fact BulkStore[FileStore] + fact BulkStore[IndexedFileStore] THERE -- in the host closure, not in the stdlib, per the WI-931 rule that a satisfaction fact may only stand where the spec's operations are backed.\n\nACCEPTANCE: pull is callable from an anthill program against a registered file-store mirror and returns the persisted facts; both file backends provide BulkStore again; the stdlib loads clean with check_provider_operations covering them (no staging); cargo-test green via rustland/scripts/test.sh.\n\nSOURCE: measured while delivering WI-931 (2026-08-02)."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-933", description: some(value: "A namespace-level bracket-less 'fact Spec' is SILENTLY DROPPED, while the docs say it means 'the sort declared in this namespace provides Spec'.\n\nMEASURED (WI-931, by dumping every anthill.reflect.SortProvidesInfo fact of a full stdlib + host-bindings load): two such lines existed in the tree and NEITHER produced a provision.\n  stdlib/anthill/persistence/filesystem.anthill  'fact BulkStore'     after entity FileStore(...)  -> no FileStore -> BulkStore edge\n  stdlib/anthill/persistence/sql.anthill         'fact QueryableStore' after entity SqlStore(...)  -> no SqlStore -> QueryableStore edge\nTheir bracketed neighbours on the very next line (fact NonMonotonicStore[FileStore], fact NonMonotonicStore[SqlStore]) DID produce one, so this is the spelling and not the position.\n\nWHY IT IS NOT AN AUTHORING SLIP. docs/rust-forward-mapping.md documents exactly this spelling, twice, as a FEATURE:\n  'fact QueryableStore in the namespace where SqlStore is defined means \"SqlStore is-a QueryableStore\", which maps to implementing the trait'\n  'fact QueryableStore in the SqlStore namespace becomes impl QueryableStore for SqlStore'\nSo a reader following the docs writes a line that reads as a declaration and does nothing. The same spelling INSIDE a sort body ('sort NonMonotonicStore { fact Store }') works and is what the store hierarchy is built from -- so the two positions disagree about what a carrier-less fact means.\n\nWHAT MUST BE DECIDED, and it is one of two:\n (a) IMPLEMENT the documented reading -- derive the carrier from the enclosing namespace's sole/most-recent entity declaration. Note this is a guess by proximity, which is why option (b) may be better.\n (b) REFUSE it loudly -- a namespace-level 'fact Spec' over a spec with type params or operations has no carrier and cannot be a provision; report it at load and correct the doc. This is the project's stated 'prefer a loud error over a silent skip' direction, and it is what would have caught both stdlib lines the day they were written.\nWhichever is chosen, docs/rust-forward-mapping.md must be corrected or confirmed IN THE SAME COMMIT -- it is currently the only written statement of the rule and it disagrees with the loader.\n\nBLAST RADIUS TO MEASURE FIRST: how many bracket-less namespace-level 'fact X' lines exist across stdlib/, examples/, anthill-todo/ and the test corpus, and how many of those X are SPECS (rather than data sorts, where 'fact Color[Holder]' is a data fact and correctly not an edge -- see wi407_provider_edges_test's data_sort_fact_does_not_widen_* pins). Only the spec ones are in scope.\n\nACCEPTANCE: the carrier-less namespace-level spelling either produces the documented provision or is refused with a located diagnostic naming the spec and saying what to write instead; docs/rust-forward-mapping.md agrees with the implementation; a test drives both the namespace-level and the sort-body positions so they cannot silently diverge again; cargo-test green via rustland/scripts/test.sh.\n\nSOURCE: measured while delivering WI-931 (2026-08-02)."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-931", author: "claude", content: "CLI WRITE-PATH SMOKE (WI-931): written by the freshly-built binary, i.e. through the operation_map registration that replaced the six hardcoded eval registrations. persist + flush dispatch unchanged.", at: "2026-08-02T10:41:39Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-931", author: "claude", content: "POST-REVIEW CLI SMOKE: written by the binary built after the /code-review fixes (per-carrier realization + op_backed spec-op narrowing + Vec3 withdrawal). persist/flush still dispatch through the operation_map registration.", at: "2026-08-02T11:35:17Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-931", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED. The 12 reports are closed at their source, the staging (load::free_standing_entity_sorts and its skip) is DELETED, and the check now runs over §6.3's free-standing carriers with nothing keyed on which spelling was written. Full workspace: 4016 passed, 0 failed.\n\nMEASUREMENT LADDER, staged one family at a time against the 12: Vec3 -> 8, the persistence binding -> 1, withdrawing BulkStore -> 0.\n\n(a) THE THREE STORES. The backing was REAL all along and only its DECLARATION was missing: the six storage operations were registered by hardcoded qualified name in eval/builtins.rs, a registry no load-time reader can see (op_is_executable's builtin leg reads the RESOLVER's kb.builtins; these sat in the interpreter's). They moved to operation_map clauses in a new rustland/anthill-stl/anthill/persistence.anthill, plus store_* entries in HOST_FNS. The store satisfaction facts moved OUT of stdlib into that host closure -- geometry.anthill's own precedent and proposal 038's rule: a fact Spec[Carrier] may only stand where the spec's operations are backed, and in a stdlib-only load NOTHING backs retract. That move is what fixed the ~200 stdlib-only test failures the first cut produced.\n\nTHE FIX I SHIPPED FIRST WAS WRONG AND /code-review CAUGHT IT, confirmed by my own probe. Keying the operation_map on the SPEC (Store / NonMonotonicStore / QueryableStore) DISABLED the very check this ticket restores: is_host_mapped_op is a flat set with no carrier dimension and op_backed tried the spec op FIRST, so ONE mapping backed the member for EVERY carrier of that spec. MEASURED: an arbitrary 'entity ZzNotAStore(v: Int64)' with 'fact NonMonotonicStore[ZzNotAStore]' LOADED CLEAN, and so did a reinstated SqlStore. That is WI-876's defect A restated -- being genuinely polymorphic answers defect B (a wrong answer at run time) and says nothing about A (a load-time claim that does not imply the operation runs).\n\nUSER'S CALL settled the shape ('we can introduce common abstract operations over state'): the operations ARE common -- one host function over the store's state, resolving the store VALUE to its registered mirror -- so they stay declared and mapped ONCE, and what is per-backend is only WHICH BACKENDS THE HOST REALIZES (a one-line 'provides FileStore language rust' block emitting the Implementation fact). op_backed now refuses a host mapping on the SPEC op as backing for a CARRIER. My FIRST attempt at that narrowing was position-based (drop spec_op from the candidate list) and CHANGED NOTHING -- sort_ops_lookup's inherited-default leg returns the spec's own member for a carrier that overrides nothing, so the spec op reaches the carrier slot too. Keyed on the SYMBOL instead. Both probes now refuse; pinned by a_spec_level_host_mapping_backs_no_carrier and reinstating_the_sql_store_provision_is_refused.\n\nSqlStore: provisions DELETED. No host implements a SQL backend in any form (rustland/anthill-core/src/persistence/ has the two file stores and nothing else), so there is no closure they could honestly move to. Per the user, relocating the whole sketch out of stdlib is WI-934.\n\nBulkStore: provisions WITHDRAWN from both file backends. pull has no anthill-callable implementation in EITHER registry; the Rust BulkStore::pull trait method returns parsed FILES and is driven from Rust. WI-932 restores it.\n\n(b) VEC3 -- OPTION (b), NOT (a), AND THE REASON IS A MEASURED REGRESSION I ALMOST SHIPPED. Giving Vec3 the four functional bodies WORKS: they evaluate, the check is satisfied, and the FULL WORKSPACE STAYED GREEN AT 4010. It also silently re-targets the imported short name. Driven, on a rule body doing 'import anthill.geometry.{vec_add}' and calling the relational form: 1 solution before, 0 after, no diagnostic -- the bare name binds to the new Vec3.vec_add/2 instead of the namespace rule vec_add/3. The fully qualified name still resolved, so the rules were intact; only the import moved. NOTHING CAUGHT IT because vec3_ops_test::vec_ops_callable_from_user_rule imports exactly that name and asserts only that the rule LOADS. I found it only because /code-review asked for a test that the two spellings AGREE -- the agents did not find this one. So 'fact VectorSpace[Vec3, Float]' is withdrawn, algebra_spec_test now asserts the ABSENCE and names WI-935, and WI-935 owns restoring it once the naming is settled.\n\nBLAST RADIUS BEYOND THE TICKET, both found by /code-review and both real defects the ticket exposed rather than created:\n * cpp-gen emitted TWO 'struct Vec3' for an eponymous sort with fields AND operations (§6.3/WI-926 make it ONE symbol) -- a C++ redefinition error on a header this generator produced. Fixed at classify_namespace (the three bands are now DISJOINT by contract) and emit_entity_struct_by_symbol (one symbol, one struct), NOT at the namespace walk: that walk is one of three callers, and the other two are the public by-name API plus traits_classes_in_namespace, which the CLI's codegen cpp-project uses to scaffold 'one controller per traits class' -- it was offering Vec3, a data type, as a controller target (MEASURED). New suite wi931_eponymous_struct_test with a LOCAL fixture, since withdrawing the Vec3 provision leaves the tree with no sort of that shape. My own control there caught me turning emit_traits_struct's empty-traits-class error into a silent struct; narrowed.\n * A namespace-level bracket-less 'fact Spec' is SILENTLY DROPPED (measured by dumping every SortProvidesInfo: 'fact BulkStore' in filesystem.anthill and 'fact QueryableStore' in sql.anthill produced NO provision, while their bracketed neighbours did), while docs/rust-forward-mapping.md documents that spelling twice as a FEATURE. -> WI-933.\n\nTESTS: 11 in wi931_free_standing_provider_backing_test.rs + 5 in wi931_eponymous_struct_test.rs. CONTROL DISCIPLINE MEASURED, not asserted: re-introducing pre-WI-928 blindness (dropping every SortInfo.kind=entity carrier out of the concrete scope) fails control_an_unbacked_free_standing_carrier_is_still_refused and NOTHING ELSE; control_a_backed_free_standing_carrier_loads passes either way BY DESIGN and says so, so the refusal is about backing and not about the free-standing spelling.\n\nREUSE (/code-review): three hand-rolled load sequences replaced by existing helpers (load_stdlib_kb, try_load_kb_with_files), and the SortProvidesInfo walk lifted to common::sort_provisions -- it was about to be a FOURTH copy. Two comments corrected that were false as written: stdlib.rs's entry contradicted the file it annotated, and sql.anthill claimed a guarantee the first cut had disabled (now enforced and pinned). Spec 6.3 gained the provider-check bullet, the long-form/short-name caveat, and the satisfaction-fact placement rule, which had no canonical home and was restated in four places.\n\nSKIPPED, with reason: consolidating the suite's per-test stdlib loads (~2.8s). wi896's header explicitly accounts for its five loads -- per-claim naming is what the repo pays for, and these claims are distinct.", at: "2026-08-02T11:42:21Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-931", description: some(value: "Four free-standing carriers provide specs whose operations nothing can dispatch to: 12 measured UnbackedProviderOperation reports, true by check_provider_operations' own criterion, currently STAGED OUT of that check.\n\nMEASURED under WI-928 (any stdlib load):\n  anthill.geometry.Vec3                        VectorSpace.vec_add / vec_sub / vec_scale / vec_zero   (4)\n  anthill.persistence.filesystem.FileStore     NonMonotonicStore.retract / update                     (2)\n  anthill.persistence.filesystem.IndexedFileStore  NonMonotonicStore.retract / update, BulkStore.pull, QueryableStore.retrieve (4)\n  anthill.persistence.sql.SqlStore             NonMonotonicStore.retract / update                     (2)\n\nWHY THEY WERE INVISIBLE. check_provider_operations scopes itself to CONCRETE carriers via sorts_with_constructors, which reads SortInfo.constructors. All four are declared with the free-standing 'entity' keyword (spec 6.3), and until WI-928 that spelling emitted no SortInfo at all -- so they read as abstract/value-less and were skipped. The check's own doc used to name Vec3 as an intended skip 'since no runtime value can dispatch through it', which is false: Vec3(x: 1.0, y: 2.0, z: 3.0) is a value. WI-928 corrected the classification and the doc.\n\nWHY THEY ARE TRUE. Vec3's four operations are backed by RULES (rule vec_add(?a, ?b, ?c) :- ...) and WI-818 settled that a rule is not backing -- it is specification the SLD world resolves against, not something the evaluator can run. The three stores are implemented by the Rust host (rustland/anthill-core/src/persistence/*), but no anthill.realization.Implementation fact records that, so the host-carrier skip at the top of check_provider_operations cannot see them either. Either way the provision certifies an operation that dies at run time with UnknownOperation -- exactly the unsoundness WI-363/WI-818 exist to refuse.\n\nCURRENT STATE (WI-928): check_provider_operations skips carriers in load::free_standing_entity_sorts (SortInfo.kind = entity) and cites this ticket. That set exists ONLY for staging -- 6.3 makes the two spellings one declaration, so keying anything semantic on it re-introduces the divergence WI-928 removed. An eponymous LONG-form 'sort P { entity P }' is checked, as it always was.\n\nWHAT THIS TICKET DECIDES, per carrier -- these are stdlib semantics questions, not kernel ones:\n (a) THE THREE STORES: declare the host backing. The shape is a 'provides X language rust ... end' block with artifact + carrier mapping (see rustland/anthill-stl/anthill/bool.anthill) or an Implementation fact naming the target; this is the WI-876 / WI-886 host-operation-mapping family, and the store ops (retract/update/pull/retrieve) are effectful, so check what proposal 054's effect rows require of the declaration.\n (b) VEC3: either give vec_add/vec_sub/vec_scale/vec_zero runnable operation bodies on Vec3 (the rules stay, as the SMT-facing specification), or drop 'fact VectorSpace[Vec3, Float]' from rustland/anthill-stl/anthill/geometry.anthill. Note the rules are deliberately relational for Z3 (per-component Float arithmetic), so adding bodies must not disturb that.\n\nACCEPTANCE: the staged skip in check_provider_operations and load::free_standing_entity_sorts are BOTH deleted, the check runs over free-standing carriers, and the stdlib loads clean; cargo-test green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. A carrier that legitimately cannot back an op must say so in its declaration, not by being invisible to the check.\n\nSOURCE: measured while delivering WI-928 (2026-08-02)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-08-02T11:42:31Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-934", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED. stdlib/anthill/persistence/sql.anthill -> examples/sql-store/ (sql.anthill + a new demo.anthill + README.md), namespace anthill.persistence.sql -> anthill.examples.persistence.sql (the anthill.examples.* convention webots/lf1 already uses). Full workspace: 4023 passed, 0 failed, 95 binaries. scaland core compiles.\n\nTHE CONSUMER LIST IN THIS TICKET WAS INCOMPLETE — two more, one of which would have broken a build:\n * scaland/core/src/main/scala/anthill/load/EmbeddedStdlib.scala listed 'anthill.persistence.sql'. Its resolver returns Left on a missing file and ParserIntegrationTest asserts errs.isEmpty, so leaving it would have failed the Scala suite. (That suite has a PRE-EXISTING failure — 7 tests, all 'stdlib anthill.realization.cpp_std: Parse error at 9975', a scaland parser gap in a file this ticket does not touch. Confirmed pre-existing by running the same suite with HEAD's EmbeddedStdlib restored: same failure, and zero occurrences of 'sql' anywhere in the log.)\n * docs/cpp-forward-mapping.md section 7.1 and the comment above the unsupported_runtime_profile call in anthill-cpp-gen/src/lib.rs both named SqlStore as a member of the cpp17-stl refusal set. That set is keyed on the string prefix 'anthill.persistence.', so the rename removed SqlStore from it and both sites became false. Corrected to IndexedFileStore. The gate's behaviour change is benign — SqlStore/SqlDialect/ColumnDef are plain scalars and are lowerable; QueryBinding's three Term fields are refused anyway via the anthill.reflect. arm — and coverage was NOT reduced: anthill-cpp-gen/tests/unsupported_test.rs drives both arms, the persistence one on anthill.persistence.Store, which stayed.\n\nAlso updated: docs/kernel-language.md 8.8's routing example (it illustrated routing with SqlStore, a sort the stdlib no longer ships — now IndexedFileStore/FileStore), and docs/proposals/007 par.99, which taught 'fact NonMonotonicStore[SqlStore]' as THE example of a provision — the exact spelling WI-931 refuses.\n\n(1) THE anthill-stl BUILD ENTRY: DROPPED, not re-pointed, and the ticket asked for this to be measured first. Measured: 'grep -rn persistence::sql' over the workspace matches NOTHING outside the include itself, so the four generated structs had no Rust consumer. build.rs entry and src/persistence/sql.rs both deleted. collect_trait_sorts is unaffected — sql.anthill declared only entities and an enum, so it contributed zero trait names.\n(2) gen_store.rs: entry dropped.\n(3) rust-forward-mapping.md par.2.13 / par.6.1: namespaces updated; the WI-933 bracket-less question is left to WI-933 and cited, not pre-empted.\n\nTHE RULE NOW HAS ONE OWNER. 'A shape no host realizes is an example, not a standard library' was, in my first cut, stated in full at ~14 sites — CLAUDE.md's own 'Localized invariants — not restated here' failure mode, and /code-review caught it from three angles at once. It is stated once in docs/proposals/038-builtin-sorts.md, 'What the stdlib carries', beside the backing rule it follows from; the other sites are one-line citations.\n\nTESTS. sql_store_example_test.rs (6, top-level binary like classic_mini_test/github_todo_test since it drives an example): the shape left the stdlib, it resolves at its new home, and four the_demo_* tests DRIVE each field kind to an answer — a String, the Postgresql variant (asserted by QUALIFIED name; resolve_sym returns the SHORT name, so the .rsplit('.') I first wrote was a no-op), a Quoted SQL fragment, and the List[T = ColumnDef]. wi931's two SqlStore tests now load the example explicitly and keep their claims. stdlib_drift_test gained an assertion over the DIRECTORY (persistence/ holds exactly store.anthill + filesystem.anthill) rather than a name filter — re-adding the sketch as persistence/database.anthill would walk past a name filter.\n\nCONTROL MEASURED, NOT ASSERTED, AND IT CAUGHT THE TEST BEING WEAKER THAN IT READ. Copying sql.anthill back into stdlib/anthill/persistence/ PASSED the_sql_store_shape_left_the_stdlib on the first run: I had asserted only the OLD name absent, and the move renamed the namespace too, so the old name stays absent however the file is filed. Both spellings are named now and the backout fails as it should — it fails that test plus the two stdlib_drift_test tests, and nothing else.\n\nTWO OF MY OWN CLAIMS WERE FALSE AND DRIVING KILLED THEM. (a) I wrote that QueryBinding could not be instantiated because 'quoted terms have no surface syntax' — I had grepped grammar.js for 'quoted' and found nothing. Quoted(\"sql\", \"...\") is an ordinary application and LOADS CLEAN; the demo now builds a real QueryBinding with five Quoted fragments, which is a materially better example. (b) 'anthill-core/src/persistence/ holds the two file stores and nothing else' — it also holds mod.rs, print.rs, term_ser.rs. Written twice before /code-review caught it.\n\nA REAL LOADER BUG, FOUND BY MY OWN CLEANUP -> WI-936. /code-review asked load_example to WALK examples/sql-store/ rather than name its two files, so a third file could not be silently skipped. That is right in general and it BROKE THE EXAMPLE: the walk is alphabetical, so demo.anthill loaded before sql.anthill, and that order LOADS CLEAN while silently costing the columns destructuring. Measured both ways, same helper, only the order swapped: sql,demo -> account_column_type answers 1; demo,sql -> 0, no diagnostic. QueryBinding.columns' declared List[T = ColumnDef] is what desugars the literal to a cons spine, and sibling rules reading columns as a bare variable are UNAFFECTED, which is what makes the loss quiet. Local fix: an EXAMPLE_FILES constant in dependency order, reconciled against the directory (the SOURCES + stdlib_drift_test shape). The loader half is WI-936.\n\nFOLLOW-UPS I FILED AND THEN WITHDREW, on the user's rule that a follow-up is for a BUG:\n * cpp-gen's refusal keyed on a namespace prefix — I claimed 'no test on this gate at all'. FALSE: unsupported_test.rs drives both arms. Deleted.\n * rustland/anthill-core/examples/gen_store.rs has no consumer (content grep for 'gen_store' matches nothing repo-wide), writes into a gitignored dir (.gitignore:3), and has no completeness or removal guard — the only one of the four parallel stdlib file lists with neither. Had this ticket missed it, nothing would have failed. That is dead code, not a bug; recorded here, delete/keep is the user's call.", at: "2026-08-02T14:01:43Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-936", author: "claude", content: "MECHANISM ESTABLISHED — the description's 'something reads the declared field type from state only populated once the declaring file has been loaded' was a hypothesis; it is now traced, and it is exact.\n\nTHE CHOICE. A list literal has two KB representations: 'anthill.reflect.ListLiteral(a, b, c)', a flat n-ary node, or a 'cons(head:, tail:)' spine. rustland/anthill-core/src/kb/load.rs picks between them in convert_term_with_expected (line ~8816, tagged 'WI-007 context-aware ListLiteral desugaring'): it computes 'elem_hint = expected.and_then(find_list_element_type)' and rewrites ListLiteral -> cons/nil ONLY when that is Some. Otherwise the ListLiteral stays, by design — the doc comment says so ('otherwise it stays in the KB as ListLiteral for downstream consumers').\n\nWHERE 'expected' COMES FROM. self.kb.entity_field_types(new_functor) (load.rs ~8874 / ~8911 / ~9068). That registry is written by register_entity_field_types (load.rs ~13727) while LOADING the file that declares the entity.\n\nSO THE ORDER DEPENDENCE IS DIRECT: converting demo.anthill's fact argument reads entity_field_types(QueryBinding). If sql.anthill was loaded first the entry is there, expected is List-shaped, and the literal becomes a spine. If demo.anthill is converted first the entry is absent, expected is None, and the literal stays a ListLiteral — which is the SAME representation an untyped argument gets, and is exactly what was measured independently.\n\nWHY THIS IS A GAP AND NOT THE DOCUMENTED ORDER-SENSITIVITY. scan_definitions is 4-pass precisely so cross-file order does not matter (WI-321, wi321_cross_file_mutual_recursion_test): pass 1 defines every NAME across every file before any pass 2 runs. But entity_field_types is a LOAD-time registry, not a scan-time one, so this type-directed decision sits outside that guarantee. The separate documented order-sensitivity in anthill-stl/src/stdlib.rs is about 'provides' dispatch hijacking — a different mechanism, and not this.\n\nTHAT NARROWS OPTION (a) IN THE DESCRIPTION to something concrete: make the field schema available at SCAN time (or have convert_term_with_expected consult the pass-1 definition table rather than entity_field_types) so both orders produce the spine. Option (b) — refuse loudly — would fire where expected is None AND the functor's schema is not yet known, which must be distinguished from the legitimately-untyped case the WI-007 doc comment protects, since a ListLiteral in a genuinely untyped position is CORRECT and must keep working.\n\nSTILL TO MEASURE, unchanged: how many .anthill sites put a list literal in a List-typed field of an entity declared in a DIFFERENT file, and how many of those are destructured by a cons pattern. Only the destructured ones can be silently wrong today.", at: "2026-08-02T14:03:02Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-934", description: some(value: "Relocate the SQL store sketch out of the standard library — it declares a backend no realization implements.\n\nWHAT IT IS. stdlib/anthill/persistence/sql.anthill declares SqlStore(connection, schema, dialect), the SqlDialect enum (Postgresql/Mysql/Sqlite/Duckdb), QueryBinding and ColumnDef. MEASURED (WI-931): NO host implements a SQL backend in any form. rustland/anthill-core/src/persistence/ holds file_store.rs and indexed_file_store.rs and nothing else, so retract / update / retrieve against a SqlStore value reach no registered mirror. WI-931 removed the file's two provision facts for exactly that reason; what remains is a SHAPE with no realization, which is an example, not a standard library.\n\nUSER CALL (2026-08-02): move it to examples/.\n\nWHY IT IS NOT A FILE MOVE — three live consumers point at the stdlib path, and each needs a decision:\n (1) rustland/anthill-stl/build.rs maps ('persistence/sql.anthill', 'sql.rs') and rustland/anthill-stl/src/persistence/sql.rs include!s the generated output, so the generated SqlStore / SqlDialect / QueryBinding / ColumnDef structs are COMPILED INTO the anthill-stl crate today. Decide whether they are still wanted: if yes the build script must read from the new location (a build script reaching into examples/ is itself a smell — consider whether these structs have any Rust consumer at all, which should be measured before either answer); if no, drop the entry and the include, and the crate loses four unused types.\n (2) rustland/anthill-core/examples/gen_store.rs names the same path.\n (3) docs/rust-forward-mapping.md uses SqlStore as THE worked example, in two sections. Whatever moves, the doc's paths must move with it. Note WI-933 also touches this doc — the bracket-less 'fact QueryableStore' it documents is one of the SqlStore lines — so sequence the two or make both edits in one pass.\nAlso: rustland/anthill-stl/src/stdlib.rs embeds the file in SOURCES (the CLI's bundled stdlib) and tests/stdlib_drift_test reconciles that list against the on-disk tree, so both must be updated or the drift test fires.\n\nSCOPE NOTE. The abstract store algebra (stdlib/anthill/persistence/store.anthill: Store / NonMonotonicStore / QueryableStore / BulkStore) STAYS in the stdlib — it is the language-agnostic spec every backend is written against, and the filesystem backends realize it. Only the SQL sketch moves.\n\nACCEPTANCE: anthill.persistence.sql no longer ships in the standard library or its embedded bundle; every consumer above is updated or deliberately removed with the reason recorded; the drift test passes; docs/rust-forward-mapping.md's paths are correct; cargo-test green via rustland/scripts/test.sh.\n\nSOURCE: measured while delivering WI-931 (2026-08-02)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-02T14:03:25Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-936", author: "claude", content: "PROVENANCE: WI-007, 'remove default desugaring of ListLiteral' (Delivered). Its feedback is the design record and it makes this ticket sharper, not weaker.\n\nWI-007 replaced an UNCONDITIONAL 'ListLiteral -> cons/nil' rewrite with one 'keyed on the surrounding entity field type', adding convert_term_with_expected, the Option<TermId> hint propagation, and find_list_element_type — the exact machinery this ticket is about — and it names entity_field_types as the source of the hint. So the load-time registry was the design from the start.\n\nTHE DECISIVE LINE, from WI-007's own feedback: 'Set/Vec/non-list contexts leave ListLiteral as-is for the eval path'. Leaving the literal alone is INTENDED behaviour for a genuinely non-List context. The defect is that a List context whose schema has not been registered yet is INDISTINGUISHABLE from a non-List one — both give expected = None — so the unloaded-declaration case takes the deliberate non-list branch and looks like a correct decision. WI-007 considered which CONTEXTS should desugar; it did not consider a context whose type is not yet knowable. That is why the failure is silent rather than merely order-sensitive.\n\nThis also rules out the cheap reading of option (b): a diagnostic cannot fire on 'expected is None' alone, because that is the normal Set/Vec case WI-007 protects. It must fire only when the functor's field schema is UNKNOWN — i.e. the fix needs to tell 'this field is not a List' apart from 'I cannot yet say what this field is', which today's Option<TermId> hint cannot express. That distinction is the actual work.\n\nWI-007's tests were github_todo_test (cons/nil over depends_on: Option[List]) and eval_m2_test::m2_list_literal_builds_cons_chain — both single-file or same-load-unit, which is why the cross-file order case was never exercised.", at: "2026-08-02T14:09:33Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-936", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED as option (a), and it is one PHASE, not one special case. A DECLARATION PASS (Loader::declare_field_types, run per file over every file before any file's terms are converted) lowers and registers every entity's field types; load_entity and load_sort_with_body's EntityInfo loop now READ that lowering back (declared_field_values) instead of producing it. The ticket's traced mechanism was exact and nothing in it had to be revised.\n\nTHE TICKET NAMED ONE DECISION. THERE ARE THREE, AND THE ONE IT DID NOT NAME IS THE WORST. entity_field_types is the expected-type hint the WHOLE conversion reads. Driven on two files, same helper, only the order swapped (wi936_field_type_load_order_test):\n\n  probe                                                   decl,use   use,decl\n  list literal -> cons spine (WI-007)                        1          0\n  bare value in an Option field wrapped in some() (WI-408)    1          0\n  OMITTED optional field, queried `note: some(?)` (WI-716)    0          1\n\nThe third is not a missing answer, it is a WRONG one. With the field types unknown an absent optional is var-filled, so the produced entity is `forall v. E(note: v)` and a `some(?)` query matches a fact that never mentions the field. WI-716's own comment states that unsoundness; load order alone reaches it. Zero errors in every order, before and after.\n\nWHY (b) IS NOT THE FIX, MEASURED — AND MY FIRST REASON FOR IT WAS FALSE. (b) needs a discriminator for \"cannot yet tell\". The candidate is \"field NAMES registered (WI-499, scan-time) but no TYPES\". I was about to write that it is unsound because the synthetic kernel entities carry names without types; I probed instead, and that is FALSE: MemberInfo, DescriptionInfo, SortInfo, EntityInfo, FieldInfo, SortProvidesInfo, SortRequiresInfo and List.cons all answer names=true types=true, because their reflect.anthill declarations register types under the same resolved symbol. The real counterexample is the KEY ASYMMETRY: register_entity_field_names_scan registers names under BOTH the resolved symbol AND the bare short one, while types are registered under the resolved symbol only — measured, for an entity whose types ARE registered: `bare Holder: names=true types=false`. So the check would fire on the short-name twin of every declared entity. (I did NOT find a live conversion path keyed on a short symbol — convert_term_inner resolves its functor through remap_symbol — so this is an asymmetry, not a second live defect; recorded because it is what rules the cheap diagnostic out.)\n\nWith (a) in place the state is unreachable for source-declared entities, and the loud guard sits on the INVARIANT instead: declared_field_values refuses (unreachable!) if the load walk reaches an entity the declaration walk did not, with NO lowering fallback — a fallback there would silently reinstate exactly this ticket.\n\nBLAST RADIUS, MEASURED as the ticket asked, over stdlib/ + examples/ + anthill-todo/ + the Rust host bindings (paren-stack attribution of every `field: [` site to its enclosing constructor, cross-referenced against the entity declarations):\n * 229 entity declarations, 53 with at least one List-shaped field.\n * 1630 list-literal sites; 1334 in a List-shaped declared field; 1330 of those CROSS-FILE (declaring file != using file). The at-risk population was almost the whole of it. By site count: anthill-todo/workitems.anthill 928 WorkItem.acceptance + 348 WorkItem.depends_on (declared in examples/github-todo/domain.anthill), then LanguageMapping.effect_map/receiver_map/type_map and execution_platform.properties across the four realization files, Implementation.carrier/namespace_map (webots lf1), Project.tools, ToolDef.args, WorkItem.context, QueryBinding.columns. Only 4 sites are same-file (RobotKind.devices, WorldRobot.extra_devices). A further 296 sites are `depends_on: some(value: [...])` in this project's OWN tracker — an Option-wrapped List, which reaches the same hint through the WI-408 peel, and `depends_on` is one of the two cons-destructured fields.\n * DESTRUCTURED BY A cons PATTERN — exactly two readers, and BOTH are over cross-file fields: stdlib/anthill/realization/realization.anthill:447 walking LanguageMapping.effect_map (whose literals are written in rust_std / scala_std / scala_caps / rust_anthill), and examples/github-todo/rules.anthill:28 walking WorkItem.depends_on (1276 fact sites between the example and this project's own tracker). Those two were the silently-breakable ones today; the rest were latent — one cons rule away.\n * What kept the corpus working was that anthill::stdlib::SOURCES is hand-maintained in dependency order and the test walks happened to agree. That is the same accident WI-934 hit from the other side.\n\nTHE WI-934 WORKAROUND IS GONE, and that is the acceptance on real files. sql_store_example_test's EXAMPLE_FILES pin is replaced by the directory WALK /code-review originally asked for (a file added later cannot be silently skipped) — and collect_files SORTS, so the walk hands the loader demo.anthill before sql.anthill: the order that used to lose is now the suite's default. examples/sql-store/README.md's \"Load sql.anthill before demo.anthill\" section was false after this and now states the rule instead.\n\nTHE LOWERING NOW HAPPENS EXACTLY ONCE, retiring a pre-existing double-lower: a sort-body entity's field types were lowered twice — once for the EntityInfo/FieldInfo fact, once for the registry — so the fact and the registry were two lowerings of one written type, with two chances to fire emit_desc_fact and the WI-835 parameterized-site record. WI-630's comment called it out and left it (\"out of scope for WI-630\"); it is gone.\n\nCONTROL, MEASURED not asserted. Declaration pass made a no-op + the load-time lowering restored: exactly SIX tests fail across the workspace — the four WI-936 subjects (each on its `use, decl` half; the forward-declaration one outright) and sql_store_example_test's both_load_orders_of_the_example_answer_the_same + the_demo_column_list_destructures_through_its_declared_type. The WI-936 CONTROL — a list literal in a position with NO declared type stays a flat n-ary node, WI-007's deliberate Set/Vec case — passes BOTH ways by design, and is what stops the fix from being \"desugar everywhere\". The other 4023 pass either way.\n\nTESTS: 4029 passed, 0 failed, 95 binaries (4023 before + 5 new + 1 new).\n\nTHREE THINGS THIS PASS HAD TO GET RIGHT, for whoever touches it next:\n (1) It runs load_sort_with_body's type-param SortAlias PRE-LOAD (lifted to the shared preload_type_param_aliases), or a field type naming `sort T = ?` mints a fresh divergent Var instead of the alias's backing var — the exact failure that comment warns about, one phase earlier.\n (2) It must NOT mirror the WI-201 carrier bindings: scan_sort_carrier_bindings CONVERTS terms, which is what this pass exists to precede. Safe because the narrowing it feeds is gated on bare_spec_sugar, which only an operation signature sets, so no entity field type can reach it.\n (3) Loader::new REGISTERS the file in kb.sources, so the second loader over a file must reuse the first's SourceId (new signature takes Option<SourceId>) — otherwise one file gets two ids and two spans into the same text render as different sources.\n\nCOST: measured with ANTHILL_LOAD_TIMING=1 on a 78-file load (debug): declare_field_types 7-13ms against scan_definitions 10-16ms and load_with_visited 223-314ms — ~3-4%, part of it repaid by the retired double-lower.\n\nSCALAND: no peer defect. The Scala port has no type-directed literal desugaring at all — ListLiteral is parsed and kept, and there is no entity_field_types registry — so there is nothing there to make order-independent. Recorded so the next person does not go looking.\n\nSPEC: kernel-language.md §4.6 (Collection literals) now carries the rule — a declared type is in force for the whole load, whichever file it is written in and wherever in that file it stands, and the same holds for the some() wrap and the none() fill — plus why it has to be written down at all: its violation is SILENT, because a not-yet-known field type is indistinguishable from a field that is legitimately not a collection.\n\nRECORDED, NOT FILED (dead code, not a bug — the WI-934 rule): Loader stores `resolver: &dyn SourceResolver` and `loaded_paths: &mut HashSet<String>` and reads NEITHER (self.resolver / self.loaded_paths match nothing). That no file is ever pulled in during the load pass is what lets this ticket's pass assume a fixed file set. Removing them cascades into load_with_visited's and load_all's public signatures, so it is the user's call.", at: "2026-08-02T14:52:45Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-936", description: some(value: "A type-directed list-literal desugaring is LOAD-ORDER SENSITIVE and fails SILENTLY.\n\nMEASURED (WI-934, examples/sql-store/). A list literal in a field whose declared type is List[T = X] desugars to a cons(head:, tail:) spine, so a rule can destructure it. Whether that desugaring happens depends on the order the files were handed to the loader, and the losing order LOADS CLEAN.\n\nDriven, same two files, same KB helper (try_load_kb_with_files), only the order swapped:\n  sql.anthill then demo.anthill -> account_column_type answers 1 solution (\"text\")\n  demo.anthill then sql.anthill -> account_column_type answers 0 solutions\nNeither order produces a parse error, a load error, or any diagnostic. sql.anthill declares 'entity QueryBinding(... columns: List[T = ColumnDef] ...)'; demo.anthill asserts a QueryBinding fact whose columns argument is a '[a, b, c]' literal and a rule matching 'cons(head: ColumnDef(...), tail: ?)' against it.\n\nWHY IT IS SURPRISING. scan_definitions is documented as 4-pass precisely so cross-file order does not matter -- pass 1 defines every name across every file before any pass 2 runs, which is what makes cross-file mutual recursion work (WI-321, wi321_cross_file_mutual_recursion_test). Field TYPES ought to be knowable regardless of order by the time a fact body is converted. Something in the literal-desugaring path reads the declared field type from state that is only populated once the DECLARING file has been loaded, not merely scanned.\n\nWHY IT IS QUIET, and this is the part that makes it dangerous. Sibling rules that bind the same field to a bare variable ('columns: ?cols') are UNAFFECTED and keep answering, so a suite that reads the list as a whole stays green while the rule that walks the spine goes to zero. In the losing order the literal stays a flat n-ary node (measured separately: the same literal in an UNTYPED fact argument is always a flat node, which is the same representation), so the cons pattern simply matches nothing.\n\nWHAT TO DECIDE:\n (a) Make the desugaring order-independent -- read the declared field type from the pass-1 definition table rather than from loaded state, so both orders produce the spine. This is the fix that matches WI-321's contract.\n (b) If (a) is not reachable, make the loss LOUD: a list literal in a position whose declared type is a List[T = X] that is not yet known must be an error naming the file that declares the field, not a silent flat node. The project's stated direction is a loud error over a silent skip.\n\nBLAST RADIUS TO MEASURE FIRST: how many .anthill sites across stdlib/, examples/, anthill-todo/ and the test corpus put a list literal in a List-typed field of an entity declared in a DIFFERENT file, and how many of those are destructured by a cons pattern anywhere. Only the destructured ones can be silently wrong today; the rest are latent.\n\nWORKAROUND IN PLACE: rustland/anthill-core/tests/sql_store_example_test.rs pins the order with an EXAMPLE_FILES constant reconciled against the directory (the anthill::stdlib::SOURCES + stdlib_drift_test shape). That is a local fix for one example, not a fix for the language.\n\nACCEPTANCE: both load orders of the two examples/sql-store/ files produce the same answers, DRIVEN (resolve the goal, assert the solution), or the losing order is refused with a located diagnostic naming the field and its declaring file; a test drives BOTH orders so they cannot silently diverge again; cargo-test green via rustland/scripts/test.sh.\n\nSOURCE: measured while delivering WI-934 (2026-08-02), by an alphabetical directory walk that broke the example and loaded clean."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-02T14:52:48Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-939", description: some(value: "May a namespace-level rule and a sort member share one short name? WI-935's handoff -- the question it removed the instance of rather than answered.\n\nWI-935 asked it as its option (a) and then made it moot: 'anthill.geometry.vec_add/3' (a namespace rule) and 'anthill.geometry.Vec3.vec_add/2' (a sort member) were about to coexist, and finishing the WI-138 lift deleted the former instead of adjudicating the pair. The language question survives, unanswered and unowned.\n\nTHE MEASUREMENT THAT RAISED IT (WI-931, re-confirmed by WI-935's control perturbation). With both present, a rule body doing 'import anthill.geometry.{vec_add}' and calling the relational form went from ONE solution to ZERO, silently -- the bare name bound to the arity-2 sort member instead of the arity-3 namespace rule. The FULLY QUALIFIED name kept resolving both ways, so nothing was broken except which target the bare name reached, and no diagnostic was emitted. WI-935's control perturbation (re-adding the namespace rule beside the member) reproduces the coexistence and loads clean.\n\nTHE THREE OPTIONS, none of them today's behaviour:\n (a) RESOLVE BY ARITY -- the import binds per call site by argument count. Note this cannot be decided at the import: 'import ...{vec_add}' names a symbol, not an arity.\n (b) AMBIGUITY ERROR at the import or at the call -- the WI-907 shape ('an ambiguous dotted head ends the ladder'), applied to a bare imported name whose namespace and sort tiers both answer.\n (c) FORBID the coexistence at DECLARATION -- refuse a namespace-level name that a sort in the same namespace also declares. Loudest and earliest; would have refused the pre-WI-935 geometry outright.\nToday it is none of these: it silently picks one.\n\nRELATED, NOT THE SAME. A distinct duplication exists under ONE functor: a hand-written clause plus a body on the same operation. There the precedence IS defined (rules win -- design/abstract-interpreter-and-rules.md 3.3, and WI-935 drove it: adding a clause under Vec3.vec_add's functor stops WI-669's derived relational view). That doc already names its own follow-up -- \"'both exist' should eventually be a loader warning (it is exactly the duplication WI-580 outlaws)\". Whoever takes this ticket should decide whether the two warnings are one pass or two; they key on different things (short-name collision across tiers vs. same-functor rule-and-body).\n\nFAMILY: WI-907 (ambiguity ends the ladder), WI-933 (a declaration that silently does nothing), WI-908/WI-914 (name ladders).\n\nACCEPTANCE: the rule is stated in docs/kernel-language.md and ENFORCED at one site, driven by a test that constructs the collision and asserts the chosen behaviour, plus a CONTROL that a non-colliding pair is unaffected. If the answer is (c), the refusal must name both declarations. cargo-test green.\n\nSOURCE: WI-935 option (a), handed off on delivery."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-937", author: "user", content: "REPRODUCES ON STDLIB, not just the synthetic fixture. Re-measured after WI-935 landed, via common::load_kb_with + query_unary (stdlib + rust bindings from disk):\n\n  rule val(?c) :- ?c = Vec3.vec_add(Vec3(x: 1.0, y: 2.0, z: 3.0),\n                                    Vec3(x: 10.0, y: 20.0, z: 30.0))\n\npanics the same way — anthill.geometry.Vec3.vec_add's body reads a.x / b.x, so the debug_assert at resolve.rs:5870 fires. This is now a defect reachable from a plain rule body against a STDLIB type, which is what raises its priority: WI-935 gave Vec3 four such members and '?c = vec_add(?a, ?b)' is the natural way to call them.\n\nThe same probe measured the sibling gap in the other position — the arity+1 goal 'Vec3.vec_add(a, b, ?c)' loads clean and answers 0 with no diagnostic. That half is WI-938, not this ticket: value position PANICS, goal position is SILENT, and they have different causes.", at: "2026-08-02T15:38:58Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-937", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED with WI-935 (commit 1a8435dd). ROOT CAUSE was NOT what this ticket guessed. The ticket said the comment at the site names the intended behaviour and pointed at materialize_value -> term_to_value, with the explicit caveat 'ROOT CAUSE NOT ESTABLISHED -- term_to_value was not read'. Reading it settled it, and the defect is one predicate call:\n\n  term_to_value asked kb.constructor_parent_sort(functor), whose body is\n    self.entity_parent.get(&functor).copied().filter(|&p| p != functor)\n\nThe .filter is the STRICT (irreflexive) view -- correct for a walker CLIMBING to a parent, and the wrong question here. term_to_value is asking 'is this an entity constructor'. For an EPONYMOUS constructor entity_parent[Vec3] == Vec3, so the filter drops it, the answer is None, and the value stays Value::Term -- whereupon a body reading a.x reports 'field_access: receiver is not an entity (got Term)'.\n\nFIX: use sort_of_constructor, the TOTAL (reflexive) view. Its own doc already says which to use -- 'Prefer this wherever the question is \"which sort does this belong to\". constructor_parent_sort is the STRICT (irreflexive) view, for walkers that climb the chain.' One line, two call sites (TryFn and TryRef).\n\nBLAST RADIUS WIDER THAN THE TICKET: this affected EVERY 6.3 free-standing / eponymous entity reaching the SLD->eval bridge, not just Vec3 -- reproduced with a free-standing entity AND a sort-declared eponymous one, entity-returning AND scalar-returning bodies, inline-constructor AND fact-bound args. Same WI-926 family as WI-928 / WI-931 / WI-940: a predicate that silently reads an eponymous name as 'not the thing'.\n\nDRIVEN: vec3_ops_test::the_imported_short_name_answers_through_the_derived_view and every_member_answers_relationally resolve goals whose bodies read a.x / b.x and assert the BOUND VALUE. CONTROL: the_four_members_evaluate calls the interpreter directly and passes either way BY DESIGN, which is why it is kept separate -- it isolates the bridge from the bodies. Full workspace green.", at: "2026-08-02T18:58:26Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-938", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED with WI-935 (commit 1a8435dd), option (a) -- IMPLEMENT it. USER'S CALL on the one decision the ticket left open, and it went against the spec's own wording: route to unify, not eq.\n\nWHY THE SPEC WAS WRONG. The ticket quoted 'f(args, result) = eq(f(args), result)' as a stated equivalence. It describes a form that can NEVER answer: eq is a semantic equality TEST that never binds (8.3), so builtin_eq's value_is_flex check delays on an unbound ?r. MEASURED both ways on the real stdlib Vec3 -- eq gives 1 INDEFINITE solution with ?r free; unify gives 1 DEFINITE solution carrying Vec3(11.0, 22.0, 33.0). The spec paragraph is rewritten.\n\nIMPL: kb.functional_relation_arity(f) (resolve.rs) mirrors bare_bodied_bool_relation's gate -- non-builtin, runnable body, effect-free, RULE-LESS -- and returns the declared param count; step_init routes a goal at arity+1 to unify(f(a1..an), ?r), same in-place goal[0] rewrite and delay_mode threading as the Bool hook.\n\nTWO THINGS THE ROUTING MUST GET RIGHT, both found by DRIVING, neither anticipated by the ticket:\n (1) The call must be rebuilt in the OCCURRENCE carrier. reduce_op_value opens with 'let occ = match &v { Value::Node(o) => .., _ => return v }', so a make_goal_value Entity-carried call is returned UNTOUCHED -- it would never reduce and the bug would look like the routing not firing. Args are reused from the goal's own occurrence (keeps occurrence identity, WI-621).\n (2) Route ONLY once the call reduces. My first cut handed unify the raw call and got 1 DEFINITE solution binding ?c to the CALL TERM -- proposal 049's invariant is that unify is structural and never dispatches, which I had read and still walked into. An unreduced call now falls through to ordinary candidate selection (no answer, the pre-WI-938 behaviour) rather than a confidently wrong one.\n\nNEEDED WI-937 TO BE VISIBLE AT ALL: with the routing in place but the bridge still failing to materialize an eponymous entity, every geometry goal aborted instead of answering. The two are one delivery.\n\nSURVIVING FOLLOW-UP, not done: a call whose args are NOT ground falls through silently (0 solutions) rather than DELAYING. Delay is the right answer there -- 'cannot decide yet' -- and it is the same silent-skip shape this ticket was filed about, one case over. Also unmeasured: whether stdlib needs_rebuild (realization/platform.anthill:147, status(?fs, ?p, FileStatus(...))) now answers; it is still driven by no test.\n\nDRIVEN: vec3_ops_test::the_imported_short_name_answers_through_the_derived_view asserts the VALUE, not the count -- len()==1 passed for the old residual too. every_member_answers_relationally covers sub / scale / zero. Full workspace green.", at: "2026-08-02T18:58:57Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-941", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED with WI-935 (commit 1a8435dd). The ticket's read of the cause was CORRECT and its quoted line was the whole defect:\n\n  let params = self.symbols.arg_places(op);\n  if params.is_empty() { return v; }   // nullary / unscanned op -- nothing to fold\n\nThe comment conflates two situations and the early return is right for only one. UNSCANNED (signature declares params, no arg places stamped): folding with an empty substitution would leave the body's param vars unbound and reduce to garbage -- bail, as before. GENUINELY NULLARY: nothing to bind, but there is still a BODY to reduce. FIX reads the declared signature to tell them apart:  bails,  falls through with an empty fold. The conflated comment is split so the remaining early return says which case it is.\n\nFOUND BY DRIVING, not by the ticket: after WI-937 + WI-938 landed, vec_add / vec_sub / vec_scale all answered and zero_r ALONE still failed. That isolation is the control -- the other three members exercise the same routing and the same bridge, so a failure confined to the nullary member can only be the nullary path.\n\nDRIVEN: vec3_ops_test::every_member_answers_relationally asserts vec_zero(?c) yields 1 DEFINITE solution binding Vec3(0.0, 0.0, 0.0). Backing this fix out fails that one case and nothing else. Full workspace green.", at: "2026-08-02T18:58:59Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-941", author: "user", content: "CORRECTION to the previous entry: two backticked fragments were eaten by shell expansion and read as blanks. The sentence should be: \"FIX reads the declared signature to tell them apart: a declared param count other than Some(0) bails (unscanned, or no signature at all), while Some(0) falls through with an empty fold.\"", at: "2026-08-02T18:59:27Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-935", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED (commit 1a8435dd) as option (c) -- a THIRD option the ticket did not list, and the two it did list both rested on a false premise.\n\nTHE PREMISE. The ticket framed this as \"may vec_add/3 and Vec3.vec_add/2 coexist under one short name\" and offered (a) adjudicate the collision or (b) rename. Both assume the two spellings must coexist. They must not: a rule-less bodied operation IS its own relation, and a hand-written clause of the same functor SUPPRESSES the derived view (design 3.3 precedence, stated in three places in the code). The relational clauses were a hand-rolled instance of what the machinery generates -- written in April by WI-137, before WI-138 lifted the shape to a spec and before WI-669 derived the reading. So the fix was to finish the WI-138 lift, not to adjudicate anything. Option (a) is handed off as WI-939, unanswered but no longer blocking.\n\nWHAT THE TICKET GOT WRONG ABOUT ITS OWN EVIDENCE. It called the relational clauses \"a working relational surface\". MEASURED: they answered 1 CONDITIONAL solution with ?c UNBOUND and the whole body residual. They computed nothing -- the residual was the point (Z3 consumed it). Both tests asserting them checked len()==1, which the residual satisfied. So the thing this ticket was protecting was never what it said it was, and the post-lift behaviour (1 DEFINITE solution, Vec3(11,22,33)) is strictly better than anything that ever existed here.\n\nWHAT IT COST, which the ticket did not anticipate: THREE resolver fixes, because deleting the clauses exposed that the derived reading did not work outside a prove run -- WI-937 (eponymous entities failed to materialize; the bridge aborted), WI-938 (no arity+1 view at resolve time), WI-941 (nullary ops never folded). Each was found only by DRIVING the goal; each loaded clean.\n\n/code-review CAUGHT THE ONE THAT MATTERED. My first cut shipped the change with the clauses deleted and no arity+1 view, and I reported the collision as \"gone\". It was -- and the pre-existing user spelling (import anthill.geometry.{vec_add}) went 1 -> 0 silently, reproducing by another route the exact regression WI-931 refused to ship. My control test asserted try_resolve_symbol(\"anthill.geometry.vec_add\").is_none() -- the FULLY QUALIFIED name -- while the hazard lives on the IMPORT path, and no test in the file imported a geometry name at all. Same class of error as the one WI-931 caught: checking a name instead of driving it.\n\nALSO FROM THE REVIEW, all real, all fixed: a spec headline of mine that contradicted the paragraph two lines below it (backing IS reachable via an inherited spec default); an algebra_spec_test assertion that was carrier-AGNOSTIC under a carrier-specific name (!rules_by_functor(spec).is_empty() passes for any carrier -- now uses common::sort_provisions); a \"provision is WITHDRAWN\" banner sitting directly above the test asserting it is present; the Ring-qualification inventory missing the two names my own new law added; 6.3s and scala-forward-mapping.md worked examples still naming Vec3 as free-standing; and a comment of mine claiming realization.anthill carries multi-line -: rules when it has ZERO.\n\nMEASURED SCOPE NOTE FROM THE TICKET WAS RIGHT AND INCOMPLETE: nothing outside geometry.anthill consumes the vec names, and the 8 laws are cited by no proof. But the CONSUMERS of the file shape were missed -- scaland asserts geometry entity/rule COUNTS (3 tests re-pointed) and cpp-gen pins the emitted struct (Vec3 is now the first stdlib type of the eponymous-with-operations shape WI-931 fixed cpp-gen for). scaland Bootstrap emits enum Vec3: case Vec3(...) plus a separate Ops trait for it and DROPS the bodies -> WI-940.\n\nSPEC: the functional-relation paragraph documented f(args, result) = eq(f(args), result), a form that can never answer. Rewritten around unify. Added: a NAMESPACE-level operation backs nothing (measured), and backing conformance is checked by SHORT NAME ONLY -- wrong arity, wrong return type and swapped params all load clean (measured, unenforced).\n\nSTILL OPEN, none of it done here: the generic route (requires VectorSpace[V, F]) reportedly loads clean then dies OperationBodyMissing -- reported by review, NOT verified by me, and it would be the first generic consumer of this lift; vec_sub_def has no body verification (an inert law with invented names loads clean); cpp-gens collect_entity_deps ignores operation signature types; lf1s realization.anthill points lift/lower at a C++ namespace and header that do not exist in the tree.\n\nTESTS: 8 in vec3_ops_test (4 driving values, 2 controls, 2 name checks), algebra_spec_test flipped and made carrier-aware, wi931 suite Vec3 family inverted. Controls MEASURED not asserted: restoring the namespace clause fails exactly one test; adding it inside sort Vec3 fails exactly one other; backing out WI-941 fails only zero_r. Full workspace exit 0. scaland core at its pre-existing baseline (8 failed / 232 passed, all downstream of an unrelated cpp_std parse break that predates this work).", at: "2026-08-02T19:00:03Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-937", description: some(value: "A bodied operation reading an ENTITY FIELD panics the SLD->eval bridge when called in VALUE position from a rule body.\n\nMEASURED (2026-08-02, while delivering WI-935), reproduced four ways -- with a free-standing entity AND a sort-declared one, with an entity-returning AND a scalar-returning body, with an inline-constructor argument AND a fact-bound one:\n\n  sort Geo2\n    operation padd(a: P, b: P) -> P = P(x: a.x + b.x, y: a.y + b.y)\n  end\n  rule val_form(?c) :- ?c = Geo2.padd(P(x: 1.0, y: 2.0), P(x: 10.0, y: 20.0))\n\n  thread 'main' panicked at anthill-core/src/kb/resolve.rs:5870:\n  bridge_op_to_eval: internal evaluator error bridging 'test.funrel2.Geo2.padd':\n  internal evaluator error: field_access: receiver is not an entity (got Term)\n\nWHERE. resolve.rs's bridge_op_to_eval carries a debug_assert!(!matches!(e, EvalError::Internal(_))) -- so a DEBUG/test build aborts and a RELEASE build residualizes SILENTLY (the arm returns None). Both halves are wrong: the assert says this is an evaluator-INVARIANT bug, and it is being reached from ordinary user source.\n\nTHE COMMENT AT THE SITE NAMES THE INTENDED BEHAVIOUR AND IT IS NOT HAPPENING: 'materializing each term/occurrence operand into the interpreter's native form (Value::Term(box(...)) / Value::Node -> Value::Entity), else a body that reads a field errors with \"receiver is not an entity\"'. materialize_value (eval/mod.rs:729) delegates Value::Term -> builtins::term_to_value, which is evidently NOT reconstructing a Value::Entity for an entity-constructor term. ROOT CAUSE NOT ESTABLISHED -- term_to_value was not read; do not take the preceding sentence as a diagnosis.\n\nWHAT WORKS, for contrast (so this is a bridge defect, not a body defect): the SAME operations called through the interpreter with a Value::Entity argument evaluate correctly -- vec3_ops_test::the_four_members_evaluate drives anthill.geometry.Vec3.vec_add/vec_sub/vec_scale/vec_zero and asserts (11,22,33) / (-9,-18,-27) / (2,4,6) / (0,0,0). So the bodies are fine; what fails is the SLD-side entry.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS MORE AFTER WI-935. Vec3 now carries four such operations, and '?c = vec_add(?a, ?b)' from a rule body is the natural way to use them. That call is this bug. WI-935 shipped anyway because its own surface (interpreter calls + the WI-669 derived relational view for the SMT tier) does not go through this path, but the gap is real and user-visible.\n\nACCEPTANCE: a rule body calling a bodied op whose body reads an entity field either EVALUATES (preferred) or reports a located, user-facing diagnostic -- never a debug_assert abort and never a silent release-mode residual. Driven by a test that resolves the goal and asserts the bound value; a CONTROL that the same op still answers via the interpreter. cargo-test green.\n\nSOURCE: measured while delivering WI-935."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-02T19:00:13Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-938", description: some(value: "The FUNCTIONAL-RELATION form 'f(args, ?result)' answers NOTHING at ordinary resolve time, while the spec documents it as an equivalence and the typer deliberately exempts it.\n\nTHE SPEC CLAIM. docs/kernel-language.md (the WI-583 paragraph, 'A Bool-returning operation may be used directly as a rule-body goal'): '...the functional-relation form f(args, result) (one extra argument, the result column -- e.g. status(?fs, ?p, FileStatus(...)) = eq(status(?fs, ?p), result)) is unaffected'. It is written as a stated equivalence.\n\nMEASURED (2026-08-02, delivering WI-935), via common::load_kb_with + query_unary -- stdlib + rust bindings from disk, not the CLI:\n\n  rule rel(?c) :- Vec3.vec_add(Vec3(x: 1.0, y: 2.0, z: 3.0),\n                               Vec3(x: 10.0, y: 20.0, z: 30.0), ?c)\n  -> LOADS CLEAN, 0 solutions, no diagnostic.\n\nanthill.geometry.Vec3.vec_add is a 2-param bodied operation; the goal is at arity+1. Nothing implements the equivalence at resolve time: bare_bodied_bool_relation (resolve.rs:6147) grants a relational view only to BOOL-returning ops (routing f(args) to eq(f(args), true)), and there is no arity+1 sibling. check_goal_atom_op (typing.rs:37318) SKIPS any call whose arity != the op's param count, so WI-583's loud NonBoolOpInGoalPosition never fires either. The result is precisely the silent failed relation lookup WI-583 exists to eliminate, one arity over.\n\nWHERE IT IS ACTUALLY IMPLEMENTED, and where it is not: the PROVE tier builds it. anthill-cli/src/prove.rs:1931 calls synthesize_body_derived_defrules, which synthesizes 'op(?0..?n-1, ?result) :- ?result = <body>' under the op's own functor (WI-669) for the obligation's transitive body. That is run-scoped and prove-only. lf1 depends on it in production (safety_gps.anthill:153, FollowerController.desired_position(?leader_real, ?off, ?target)). SLD resolution outside a prove run has no such path.\n\nSTDLIB IS WRITTEN IN THIS FORM AND NEVER DRIVEN. stdlib/anthill/realization/platform.anthill:147 'rule needs_rebuild(?fs, ?input, ?output) :- exists(?fs, ?input), status(?fs, ?input, FileStatus(...)), ...' calls Filesystem.status/2 at arity 3, with the file comment 'needs_rebuild below uses the Filesystem operations as relations'. No test resolves needs_rebuild -- grep found only typer-pass tests naming it. So whether it answers is UNMEASURED, and on this ticket's evidence it probably does not.\n\nWHAT MUST BE DECIDED (the ticket does not prescribe):\n (a) IMPLEMENT it -- give a non-Bool bodied op the arity+1 relational view at resolve time, the same way WI-580 gives a Bool op the arity+0 one. The prove tier already shows the shape. Note the precedence rule must carry over: a hand-written clause of the same functor WINS (design abstract-interpreter-and-rules.md 3.3), which WI-935 measured -- adding a clause under Vec3.vec_add's functor stops the derivation.\n (b) REFUSE it -- widen check_goal_atom_op's arity gate so an arity+1 call on a rule-less bodied op is a located load error, and rewrite stdlib needs_rebuild. Cheaper, but it deletes a form the spec, lf1 and stdlib all use.\nEither way the spec paragraph must stop stating an equivalence the resolver does not provide.\n\nACCEPTANCE: driven either way -- (a) a resolve that BINDS the result var, plus a control that a hand-written clause still wins; or (b) a located diagnostic plus a rewritten needs_rebuild that is itself driven. In both cases needs_rebuild gains a test that RESOLVES it. Spec updated. cargo-test green.\n\nSOURCE: measured while delivering WI-935."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-02T19:00:16Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-941", description: some(value: "A NULLARY bodied operation in value position answers 1 INDEFINITE solution with an unbound result -- silent in debug AND release.\n\nMEASURED (2026-08-02, via common::load_kb_with + query_unary against the shipped stdlib):\n\n  rule z_out(?z) :- ?z = Vec3.vec_zero()\n  -> 1 solution(s); definite = false; value = Term { id: TermId(...) }  (unbound residual)\n\nNo panic, no diagnostic, no zero. A caller that counts solutions -- 'query_unary(...).len()', the dominant pattern in this repo's suites -- sees SUCCESS and consumes a residual as if it were a value. That is worse than either sibling defect: WI-937 aborts loudly in debug, WI-938 answers 0. This one answers 1 and is wrong.\n\nCAUSE (read, not inferred): rustland/anthill-core/src/kb/resolve.rs:5726-5728 --\n\n  let params = self.symbols.arg_places(op);\n  if params.is_empty() { return v; }   // nullary / unscanned op -- nothing to fold\n\n-- so a nullary bodied op never folds and never reaches bridge_op_to_eval at all. The comment conflates two cases ('nullary' and 'unscanned'), and for the nullary one the early return is not a decline but a silent wrong answer.\n\nNOT COVERED BY THE SIBLINGS. WI-937 is the SLD->eval bridge aborting on an entity-field read; WI-938 is the arity+1 goal form answering nothing. Neither names nullary ops, and neither fix would reach this line.\n\nCONTROL that separates it from WI-937: the SAME operation called through the interpreter works -- vec3_ops_test::the_four_members_evaluate drives anthill.geometry.Vec3.vec_zero and asserts (0,0,0). So the body is fine; the resolve-time fold is the defect.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS NOW: WI-935 gave anthill.geometry.Vec3 a nullary member (vec_zero), so this is reachable against a stdlib type from a plain rule body. Found by /code-review on that ticket and re-measured independently.\n\nACCEPTANCE: '?z = <nullary bodied op>()' either EVALUATES (preferred -- it is the same fold the n-ary case gets) or DELAYS/errors visibly; it must not answer a definite-looking solution carrying an unbound residual. Driven by a test that resolves the goal and asserts BOTH the bound value AND is_definite(). CONTROL: an n-ary op is unaffected, and the interpreter path still answers. Split the conflated 'nullary / unscanned' comment so the remaining early return says which case it is. cargo-test green.\n\nSOURCE: /code-review on WI-935."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-02T19:00:19Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-935", description: some(value: "Make Vec3 provide VectorSpace for real — blocked on a MEASURED short-name collision, not on the arithmetic.\n\nSTATE AFTER WI-931. 'fact VectorSpace[Vec3, Float]' is GONE from rustland/anthill-stl/anthill/geometry.anthill. It was never backed: VectorSpace declares FUNCTIONAL members (vec_add(a, b) -> V, vec_sub, vec_scale, vec_zero) while anthill.geometry implements the vector operations RELATIONALLY (rule vec_add(?a, ?b, ?c) :- ...), and WI-818 settled that a rule is not backing. It read as satisfied only because Vec3 is a 6.3 free-standing entity, which emitted no SortInfo and so was skipped by check_provider_operations as if abstract (WI-928). anthill-core/tests/include/algebra_spec_test.rs now asserts the ABSENCE and names this ticket, so restoring the fact flips that assertion back.\n\nTHE OBVIOUS FIX WAS TRIED AND MEASURED, AND IT REGRESSES. Writing 'sort Vec3 { entity Vec3(x: Float, y: Float, z: Float); operation vec_add(a: Vec3, b: Vec3) -> Vec3 = Vec3(x: a.x + b.x, ...) ... }' works on its own terms: the four bodies evaluate (11.0 / 33.0 / -18.0 / 6.0 / 0.0 on the obvious inputs), check_provider_operations is satisfied, and the whole workspace suite stayed GREEN -- 4010 passed. It also SILENTLY BREAKS THE RELATIONAL SURFACE. Driven, on a rule body doing 'import anthill.geometry.{vec_add}' and calling vec_add(a, b, ?c):\n  before the operations exist: 1 solution\n  after:                       0 solutions, no diagnostic\nThe FULLY QUALIFIED anthill.geometry.vec_add still resolves to 1 solution either way, so the rules themselves are intact -- what moves is the IMPORTED SHORT NAME, which binds to the new Vec3.vec_add/2 (arity 2) instead of the namespace rule vec_add/3. No existing test caught it: vec3_ops_test::vec_ops_callable_from_user_rule imports exactly this name and asserts only that the rule LOADS, so it passed throughout. That rule now has a driven peer in wi931_free_standing_provider_backing_test::the_relational_vec_rules_keep_their_short_name, which asserts the 1 solution.\n\nWHAT MUST BE DECIDED FIRST -- the naming, and it is a language question before it is a geometry one:\n (a) Is a namespace-level rule 'vec_add/3' and a sort member 'Vec3.vec_add/2' allowed to coexist under one short name at all? If yes, the import must either RESOLVE BY ARITY or be an AMBIGUITY ERROR -- what it must not do is silently pick one, which is what it does today. Note this is the same family as WI-907 (an ambiguous dotted head ends the ladder) and WI-933 (a declaration that silently does nothing), and the fix probably belongs with them rather than here.\n (b) Or do the two spellings get distinct names? e.g. move the relational clauses to their own namespace, or rename them -- but they are the SMT-facing surface (result as last positional per the smt-gen convention) and the algebraic laws in geometry.anthill (vec_add_comm, vec_add_assoc, vec_add_identity, vec_sub_def, vec_scale_distrib_v, vec_scale_distrib_s, vec_scale_assoc, vec_scale_identity) are all written against the 3-arity form, so a rename touches every law.\nMEASURED SCOPE NOTE, which makes (b) cheaper than it looks: NOTHING outside stdlib/anthill/geometry.anthill consumes these names. Grepped across examples/, anthill-todo/, anthill-smt-gen/ and the .anthill tree -- the only readers are the laws in that same file and vec3_ops_test.\n\nACCEPTANCE: Vec3 provides VectorSpace and the four members evaluate through the spec; the relational clauses still answer through whatever name they end up with, DRIVEN (a resolve, not a load); the ambiguity question in (a) is answered explicitly in docs/kernel-language.md or handed to the ticket that owns it; algebra_spec_test's assertion is flipped back with its comment updated; cargo-test green via rustland/scripts/test.sh.\n\nSOURCE: measured while delivering WI-931 (2026-08-02), during its /code-review."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-02T19:00:21Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-944", description: some(value: "`fact Ring[Float]` is an UNBACKED provision: nothing implements Ring's members at Float, on any route.\n\nMEASURED 2026-08-02 during WI-942, and pinned by vec3_ops_test::control_ring_now_loads_and_its_residual_failure_is_the_unbacked_provision, which asserts the CURRENT (defective) behaviour so it cannot be lost.\n\nREPRO, with NO generics anywhere -- this is the control that attributes it:\n  sort CR\n    operation dbl(a: Float) -> Float = Ring.add(a, a)\n  end\nloads clean and dies at the call:\n  operation has no body: anthill.prelude.algebra.Ring.add -- this is a\n  typer-guaranteed invariant violation (should be unreachable).\nThe generic route (`dbl[T](a: T) -> T requires Ring[T]`) fails IDENTICALLY, which is how WI-942 established the cause is the provision and not generic consumption.\n\nWHY. `rustland/anthill-stl/anthill/float.anthill:79` asserts `fact Ring[Float]`, naming Float as the provider of Ring[T = Float]. But `anthill.prelude.Float` declares no `add`/`sub`/`mul`/`zero`/`one` of its own -- its arithmetic comes from `Numeric`, a DIFFERENT spec -- so `spec_op_suppliers_for_carrier(Ring, Float, Ring.add, add)` finds nothing runnable. Five of Ring's five members are in this state.\n\nTHIS IS EXACTLY §8.7's WI-818 CLASS ('a rule is not backing') AT A HOST CARRIER: Ring's members are defined only by its law rules, which are not backing, and Float's host operation_map names no Ring member. WI-880 narrows the host-carrier exemption from the load-time backing check; whatever that check covers today, it did not cover this.\n\nSECOND FINDING, and it may be the cheaper half: the error text claims 'this is a typer-guaranteed invariant violation (should be unreachable)' (rustland/anthill-core/src/eval/error.rs:167). It is plainly REACHABLE from four lines of ordinary user source. Either the invariant holds and this KB violates it (then the LOADER must refuse `fact Ring[Float]`), or it does not hold and the message is lying to the reader. Decide which; do not leave a message that tells a user their program is impossible.\n\nOPTIONS, not prescribed: (a) make Float supply Ring's members (delegate to the Numeric/host ops, or a per-language operation_map entry); (b) delete `fact Ring[Float]` and re-express `VectorSpace requires Ring[F]` against a spec Float does provide; (c) leave the fact and make the LOAD refuse it. Note VectorSpace requires Ring[F] and `fact VectorSpace[Vec3, Float]` discharges that requirement TODAY -- so (b)/(c) must keep `anthill.geometry.Vec3`'s provision loading, which WI-935 delivered and vec3_ops_test drives.\n\nACCEPTANCE: `Ring.add(2.5, 2.5)` at Float either answers 5.0 or is REFUSED AT LOAD naming the unbacked member -- not certified-then-dead. Driven both ways; the WI-942 control test flipped to assert the new behaviour rather than deleted; `anthill.geometry.Vec3`'s VectorSpace provision still loads and `the_four_members_evaluate` still green. cargo-test green.\n\nSOURCE: WI-942 (it was that ticket's CONTROL B; re-measuring it is what showed the Ring construct's residual failure is the provision, not the consumer)."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-945", description: some(value: "A SORT-level requirement element nothing pins dies at EVAL, where §5.2 says it is a LOAD error -- and the OP-level twin of the same shape IS refused at load.\n\nMEASURED 2026-08-02 during WI-942 (probe S / probe T on the post-WI-942 tree).\n\nDIES AT EVAL (sort-level):\n  sort HolderVS\n    sort V = ?\n    sort F = ?\n    requires VectorSpace[V, F]\n    operation twice(a: V) -> V = VectorSpace.vec_add(a, a)\n  end\n  sort Driver\n    operation drive(a: Vec3) -> Vec3 = HolderVS.twice(a)\n  end\nLOADS CLEAN, then:\n  internal evaluator error: DeferToRequirement: requirement param `__req_vectorspace`\n  not bound in caller frame (running `HolderVS.twice`, requires-chain owner `HolderVS`)\nThe call pins V = Vec3 from its argument; NOTHING pins F, so `build_concrete_dispatch_dict` returns None and the callee frame is entered with no dictionary.\n\nREFUSED AT LOAD (op-level, the same missing element):\n  operation twice[V, F](a: V) -> V requires VectorSpace[V, F] = VectorSpace.vec_add(a, a)\n  operation drive(a: Vec3) -> Vec3 = twice(a)\n  -> type mismatch in ....twice.type_arg: expected a type for 'F',\n     got unconstrained -- use `twice[F = ...](...)`\nwhich is the RIGHT behaviour and names the fix. Writing `twice[F = Float](a)` loads and answers Vec3(2.0, 4.0, 6.0) (vec3_ops_test::a_generic_consumer_of_vector_space_loads_and_dispatches drives it).\n\nTHE DISAGREEMENT IS THE FINDING. docs/kernel-language.md §'Requirement supply at a call site is checked at load' (WI-828) states the rule for BOTH: 'A requirement element left genuinely unconstrained at the call ... is a load-time error naming the requirement, the unconstrained element, any σ-refused covering entry, and the construction outcome. There is no fallback semantics: ... deferring the failure to evaluation would be a clean load that crashes at run time.' The sort-level route does exactly the thing that sentence forbids.\n\nCONTROL that isolates it to the UNPINNED ELEMENT and not to the sort-level route: pin F concretely in the holder's own clause --\n  sort HolderVS\n    sort V = ?\n    requires VectorSpace[V = V, F = Float]\n    operation twice(a: V) -> V = VectorSpace.vec_add(a, a)\n  end\n-- and the identical Driver call answers Vec3(2.0, 4.0, 6.0). So the machinery works; what is missing is the refusal when an element is left open.\n\nNOT ROOT-CAUSED. `build_concrete_dispatch_dict` is documented to return Ok(None) for a same-sort call OR when a requirement fails to project outside the WI-828 refusal signature, and WI-828 already turned the σ-refused case into a load diagnostic. The unconstrained-element case appears to fall in the Ok(None) hole rather than the Err(RequirementRefusal) one. Lead, not a diagnosis -- read `build_concrete_dispatch_dict` / `RequirementRefusal` before assuming.\n\nPRE-EXISTING, not created by WI-942 (which touched the op-scoped σ bridge and the runtime carrier reader, neither on this path).\n\nACCEPTANCE: the two routes AGREE -- the sort-level shape is refused at LOAD with a located diagnostic naming the requirement and the unconstrained element, in the same words the op-level one already uses. Driven: the refusal asserted, AND the F-pinned control still answering Vec3(2.0, 4.0, 6.0) so the fix is a refusal and not a regression. cargo-test green.\n\nSOURCE: WI-942."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-942", description: some(value: "A GENERIC consumer of a spec certifies at load and dies at the call -- and two specs disagree about the identical construct.\n\nMEASURED 2026-08-02 on the post-WI-935/937/938/941 tree, via common::try_load_kb_with + interp_for. Pinned by vec3_ops_test::a_generic_consumer_of_vector_space_loads_clean_and_fails_at_the_call, which asserts the CURRENT (defective) behaviour so it cannot be lost.\n\nSUBJECT -- two type params, VectorSpace:\n  sort G\n    operation twice[V, F](a: V) -> V requires VectorSpace[V, F] =\n      VectorSpace.vec_add(a, a)\n  end\nLOADS CLEAN. At the call: Err(OperationBodyMissing { name: \"anthill.prelude.algebra.VectorSpace.vec_add\" }). The requires-dictionary route does not reach anthill.geometry.Vec3.vec_add.\n\nCONTROL A -- the SAME call at a concrete carrier WORKS:\n  operation twice_vec3(a: Vec3) -> Vec3 = VectorSpace.vec_add(a, a)   -> Vec3(2.0, 4.0, 6.0)\nSo the provision is sound and the member is backed. What fails is abstract dispatch, not the carrier.\n\nCONTROL B -- THE SAME SHAPE OVER A ONE-PARAM SPEC IS REFUSED AT LOAD:\n  sort H\n    operation dbl[T](a: T) -> T requires Ring[T] = Ring.add(a, a)\n  end\n  -> type mismatch in anthill.prelude.algebra.Ring.add.requires: expected `requires Ring[...]`\n     covering abstract type parameter, got missing `requires Ring[T = ...]` on enclosing sort\n\nTHAT DISAGREEMENT IS THE FINDING, not the failure alone. One spec is caught loudly at load, the other is certified and dies at run time, for the identical construct. At least one of the two is wrong and the ticket must decide WHICH -- do NOT assume the Ring refusal is the correct behaviour and the VectorSpace pass is the bug; it may be the other way round (stdlib list.anthill:57 `operation member(x: T, l: List) -> Bool requires Eq[T]` is the same op-level-requires shape and loads, so the refusal may be over-firing on an OP-DECLARED type param where the working case inherits its param from the enclosing sort).\n\nWHY IT MATTERS. Writing code against the SPEC rather than the carrier is the entire point of the WI-138 lift that WI-935 completed. This is the first generic consumer of it, and it does not work. docs/kernel-language.md 8.7 (WI-818) says the loader rejects a provision \"rather than certify a program whose call fails only at run time\" -- that check guards the PROVIDER; nothing guards the generic CONSUMER, which is the same failure one level up.\n\nPRE-EXISTING, not created by WI-935: this is a generic-dispatch gap. WI-935 is what makes it reachable against a STDLIB type, and what makes it matter -- before it, nothing provided VectorSpace at all.\n\nNOT ROOT-CAUSED. I did not read the dictionary-construction path. Candidates, unverified: WI-357 records that a shorthand `requires Ring[F]` where Ring names its param T loads as `Ring[T = Ring.T]` -- the cross-param link dropped -- and that `Eq[T]` only APPEARS to work because the names coincide; that is the right neighbourhood but it is a lead, not a diagnosis.\n\nACCEPTANCE: the two constructs AGREE -- either both load and both dispatch (preferred: `twice` returns Vec3(2.0,4.0,6.0)), or both are refused at load with a located diagnostic naming the spec and the op. Driven both ways, with the existing test flipped to assert the new behaviour rather than deleted, plus a CONTROL that the concrete-carrier route is unaffected and that stdlib `List.member` (op-level `requires Eq[T]` over a SORT-declared param) still loads. cargo-test green.\n\nSOURCE: reported by /code-review on WI-935, reproduced and controlled after delivery."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-02T21:31:00Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-942", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED 2026-08-02 (commit 596f0c74; workspace suite 4039 passed / 0 failed). TWO independent causes, both driven end-to-end.\n\nDECIDED, as the ticket required: THE PASS WAS THE ACCIDENT AND THE REFUSAL WAS THE OVER-FIRING -- not the other way round. Two pieces of evidence, neither assumed: (1) WI-562's own delivery note states the semantics -- an op-scoped requires LICENSES a spec-op call for VALUE-DIRECTED eval and threads NO dictionary (\"no requirement dict is threaded\"); so licensing is the intended behaviour and the refusal contradicted it. (2) The refusal's own text demanded a requires \"on enclosing sort\" when the author had written one on the OPERATION, which is exactly where WI-448/WI-562 put it.\n\nCAUSE 1 (run time), eval/eval.rs runtime_carrier_sort: it asked constructor_parent_sort, the STRICT/irreflexive view. Since WI-926 a sort Vec3 with an eponymous entity Vec3 is ONE symbol, so entity_parent[Vec3] == Vec3 and the strict view's reflexive-cut filter answered None -- every Vec3 value reached dispatch with NO CARRIER, so resolve_spec_op_target_by_value returned before it ever looked for a supplier. Now sort_of_constructor (TOTAL, WI-925), whose own doc says to prefer it for the \"which sort does this belong to\" question. Identical to the correction WI-937 made at the field-access reader.\n\nCAUSE 2 (load time), kb/typing.rs check_operation_bodies: it rigidifies the op's OWN type params together with its sort's in ONE rigidify_op_type_params call, but recorded only the SORT half in the sigma bridge. Compounding it, an op type param has TWO canonical vars -- the OperationInfo.type_params one (what rigidify binds; measured VarId 1325) and the SortAlias one (what a WRITTEN occurrence resolves through, and so what sigma_class sees; measured VarId 1). For a SORT param the two COINCIDE, which is why the asymmetry was invisible. Both identities now bridge to the same rigid.\n\nTHE TICKET'S OWN UNVERIFIED LEAD WAS RIGHT, and is now measured. WI-357's \"Eq[T] only APPEARS to work because the names coincide\" is exactly it: the subject twice[V, F] requiring VectorSpace[V, F] names its op params EXACTLY as VectorSpace names its own, so the written param resolved to the SPEC's var which the call had already bound, and sigma agreed for free. RENAME them to [A, B] -- positionally or by named binding -- and the identical source was refused. renamed_op_type_params_are_covered_by_the_operations_own_requires pins that, and it is the test that fails under EITHER revert while the subject survives the sigma revert. The subject ALONE could not have witnessed the load-side fix.\n\nCONTROL DISCIPLINE, measured by backing each change out (and re-measured AFTER the review refactor):\n - revert cause 1 -> the subject and renamed_... fail at the CALL; one_parameter_spec_... is unmoved, because Int64 is a SCALAR whose carrier comes from the fixed prelude map, never from a constructor. My first written table was WRONG about this (it predicted renamed_... would survive) and was corrected to the measurement.\n - revert cause 2 -> one_parameter_spec_..., renamed_... and control_ring_... fail AT LOAD; the subject PASSES.\n - control_the_concrete_carrier_route_is_unaffected passes under BOTH by design -- the provision and the member were sound throughout; only the abstract route was broken.\n - stdlib List.member stays driven to BOTH verdicts by wi431 instance_fact_eq_powers_list_member (not merely loaded).\n\nCORRECTION TO THIS TICKET'S FRAMING. CONTROL B assumed the Ring and VectorSpace constructs differed only in LOAD behaviour. They do not. Ring now LOADS (the agreement asked for), but its CALL still fails -- AND the concrete route with no generics anywhere (an operation taking a Float and calling Ring.add on it) fails IDENTICALLY. So the Ring[Float] satisfaction fact is an UNBACKED provision: Float declares no add/sub/mul/zero/one of its own, its arithmetic being Numeric, a different spec. That is a WI-818-class stdlib gap, filed WI-944 and pinned by control_ring_now_loads_and_its_residual_failure_is_the_unbacked_provision, which asserts the CURRENT behaviour and names WI-944 so it flips rather than rots.\n\nSPEC. docs/kernel-language.md described an operation-level requires ONLY as \"(preconditions on individual operations)\" -- the reading this whole ticket turns on was unstated. Added the op-scoped-requirement rule (licenses, does not thread a dictionary; may name the op's own params as well as its sort's; renaming them does not change the meaning) and made the older sentence point forward instead of asserting the superseded reading. One clause was CUT before shipping because it was unverified (that a carrier-less spec op such as a nullary Monoid.unit is not licensed) -- I did not drive it.\n\nREVIEW (/simplify, 4 agents) found my own first cut reproduced the defect it fixes: I stored TWO rigid lists with the sort half duplicated, agreeing wherever the op half is empty. Collapsed to ONE list + sort_rigid_len prefix + two NAMED views; the alias lookup I hand-rolled already existed as type_param_vid_in_sort; the two extraction loops became one walk of all_params (the list the rigidifier is actually given). It also caught THREE doc claims my own diff falsified -- WI-824's justification at typing.rs (\"it cannot see an OPERATION's own type param\"), carrier_provision_short_bindings' stated reason, and my own field doc's consumer count (said one, there are three). All corrected. NOT run: /code-review (this was /simplify, which is quality-only and does not hunt for bugs).\n\nFOLLOW-UPS: WI-943 (the two-var identity -- this bridges it, does not remove it), WI-944 (unbacked Ring[Float] provision), WI-945 (a sort-level unconstrained requirement element dies at EVAL where 5.2 says LOAD, while the op-level twin is correctly refused at load), WI-946 (constructor_parent_sort is the STRICT view wearing the GENERAL name; a review agent classified all 16 callers and reports ~7 more belongs-to readers with the same latent bug, one allegedly a silent skip of a field-value type check -- recorded as UNVERIFIED leads requiring a driving test per site, since agent reports have been refuted here before).", at: "2026-08-02T21:32:28Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-898", description: some(value: "AN EQUATION-INTRODUCED FUNCTOR BORROWS `SymbolKind::Goal`, AND A NON-REDEX USE THEN GETS A MISLEADING DIAGNOSTIC. WI-894 registers a rule-introduced functor as `SymbolKind::Goal` — the kind WI-714 reserves for a RELATION REFERENCE. An equation's clauses are indexed under the `eq`/`unify` connective, not under the functor, so when the relation machinery looks the functor up it finds zero clauses and reports something that is not true.\nMEASURED, the same program in both spellings, with an operation body whose condition is NOT a literal (so there is no `[simp]` redex to inline):\n  operation pickMax(a: Int64, b: Int64) -> Int64 = ite(Int64.gte(a, b), a, b)\n  - bare, no import (the pre-WI-894 spelling): 'type mismatch in ite.apply: expected known operation or arrow-typed variable, got unknown functor' — ACCURATE.\n  - with an `import anthill.prelude.Bool.{ite}` (the spelling WI-894 tells users to write): 'type mismatch in ite.name: expected resolved name, got unresolved' — MISLEADING. The name IS resolved; it is a `Goal` routed into `relation_reference_type_applied`, which finds no clauses because they live under `eq`.\nNOT A NEW FAILURE — both spellings fail to load, and this shape has never worked (WI-884's `a_simp_ite_reaches_only_a_literal_condition` pins that a computed condition has no redex; that test uses a DECLARED operation, which is why it dies at eval rather than at load). What regressed is the MESSAGE, on the recommended spelling. `wi894_rule_functor_scope_test` pins that both spellings are refused, deliberately without pinning the string, so this can be fixed without editing a test.\nTWO DIRECTIONS, and the ticket should pick one. (a) Give an equation-introduced functor its OWN SymbolKind rather than borrowing `Goal`, so it never enters the WI-714 relation path — the honest fix, but `Goal` is read at ~8 sites (`typing.rs` x4, `eval` x2, `resolve.rs`, the reflect bridge) and each needs a decision. (b) Make the relation path FALL THROUGH when a `Goal` functor has zero clauses, which lands on the same `unknown functor` message the bare spelling gives — small, but it changes a WI-714 site on one measured shape. ALSO IN SCOPE: WI-565's remedy-naming refinement (`member_owning_sorts_for_bare`) filters on `SymbolKind::Operation`, so it cannot see a `Goal` and the user never gets the '`ite` is a member of sort Bool — call it `Bool.ite(...)`' hint that exists for exactly this confusion. REFERENCE: WI-894, WI-714 (the relation-reference reading of `Goal`), WI-565 (the remedy naming), WI-884 (the literal-condition limit)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-02T20:05:38Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-946", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED 2026-08-02 (workspace suite 4065 passed / 0 failed). The rename shipped; SEVEN sites converted, TWO recorded as not-misbehaving, and the ticket's own classification of its candidates was wrong in BOTH directions.\n\nRENAME (scope a). `constructor_parent_sort` -> `strict_parent_sort`, repo-wide (src, tests, docs/design). Its doc now says what a caller must be asking to use it, and points anything else at `sort_of_constructor`.\n\nDRIVEN, NOT ASSUMED (scope b). Every candidate got a CONTROL writing the same declaration the sort-NESTED way; a subject was converted only where the two spellings measurably disagreed. Each subject was then re-measured by reverting THAT SITE ALONE, not the batch.\n\nCONVERTED, with the failure each one was:\n - check_value_sort_membership (via check_value_against_sort_ref) -- SILENT ACCEPT, the ticket's lead, confirmed. `fact Holder(c: Vec3(x: 1.0))` on a field declared `Colour` loaded clean; the nested carrier was refused `expected Colour, actual Shape`. The `let parent = parent?` was the skip.\n - check_value_against_parameterized -- SILENT ACCEPT, same shape at `List[T = Int64]`. The strict `None` skipped the belongs-to guard entirely and fell through to the per-field walk, which the foreign carrier's own fields satisfy.\n - Pattern::Constructor pattern subst -- FALSE REJECTION, not a skip. For an eponymous PARAMETRIC sort `build_pattern_subst` never ran, so `case Box(v) -> v` returning Int64 out of a `Box[T = Int64]` was reported as a type mismatch while `Crate`/`Boxed` type-checked.\n - head_result_carrier (WI-652 eq-override probe) -- UNDER-REPORT, confirmed. Its own comment claimed it \"answers that uniformly\"; it did not, and that claim was FALSE for one 6.3 spelling from the day it was written. Corrected in place.\n - ctor_sort_sym / same_ctor_sort (body_specialize.rs) -- a LOUD DECLINE downstream, and the one candidate whose observable was not where I first looked. `same_ctor_sort(Mix, Other)` answered false though both are variants of `sort Mix`, so a definite non-match read as undecidable, the residual stayed a `match`, and `flatten_arms` refused the whole WI-687 per-call-site synth: `synthesize_op_defining_rule_at` returned None where the all-nested spelling returned a RuleId. My first three probe attempts (folded_call_match, op_defining_equations, the goal-closure sweep) all returned None on BOTH arms and measured nothing.\n\nTHE TICKET WAS WRONG IN BOTH DIRECTIONS, which is the finding worth carrying:\n - It listed check_constructor (kb/typing.rs ~23475) and constructor_value_type (~34202) as \"behaviourally RIGHT but duplicate the relation\" -- a pure collapse. They are NOT. Both feed `finish_constructor_type`, which SHORT-CIRCUITS on a `None` parent, so the collapse changes what the build produces. Measured: `operation mk(x: Int64) -> Box[T = String] = Box(x)` loaded CLEAN on the eponymous sort, while the nested spelling was refused `expected Crate[T = String], got Crate[T = Int64]`. A silent accept, found only because I probed a site the ticket said needed no probe.\n - Two of its seven candidates do NOT misbehave, and are recorded at their sites with the probe rather than dropped:\n   * tuple_field_expected_from_ctor -- both the direct build and the WI-462-shaped path-dependent `Box((h, t))` load clean either way; the components type bottom-up to what the hint would have pushed. Kept strict; the site says why, and says converting is right the moment a failing case exists.\n   * pattern_var_ctor_sym -- the disjunct is UNREACHABLE, so the ticket's \"wrong question\" framing is right but has no consequence. `name` is load_pattern_var's FRESH BINDER symbol. Measured, not reasoned: `case Blue` where `Blue` is a genuine `is_constructor_symbol` nullary ctor of another enum is treated as a catch-all BINDER exactly as a free-standing `entity Bare` is -- that the two AGREE is what shows the fallback is dead rather than strict-biased.\n\nONE SITE CONVERTED WITHOUT ITS OWN SUBJECT, and the note says so rather than implying otherwise: constructor_value_type. Reverting it alone fails nothing -- through `value_type_term` a constructed parametric value types as the BARE sort ref for BOTH spellings, so the strict/total difference is invisible from there. It is converted for the WI-578 no-drift tie (leaving one half of `finish_constructor_type`'s two producers on the strict view is exactly the drift the shared tail exists to prevent), and `the_value_typer_answers_both_spellings_alike` pins the parity that claim rests on.\n\nA REGRESSION THE CONVERSION EXPOSED, and the rule it was standing in for. Conversion 1 broke wi716's `reflect_term_field_keeps_var_for_omitted_optional`: `entity Holder(pat: Term)` + `fact Holder(pat: Thing(id: \"z\"))` is a QUOTED pattern, and it had been accepted only because the check was SKIPPED for the free-standing carrier -- a sort-NESTED carrier in the same field was already REFUSED. So the accident stood in for a rule that was never reached. Fixed at the right depth by routing the field check through the existing owner `is_reflect_term_type` (WI-385: reflection value->Term is TOTAL, so any value conforms; reification stays partial, so Term is NOT a top type -- and that doc explicitly forbids putting this in `types_compatible`, so the field-check dispatcher is where it belongs). Both spellings now accept, for the stated reason. Pinned by `a_quoted_term_field_accepts_either_spelling_of_carrier`.\n\nCOLLAPSED (scope c), 4/4. eq_derive `composite_sorts` and cpp-gen `parent_sym` (WI-926's `.or_else(is_entity_constructor)` per-site patch) now read the total accessor; check_constructor / constructor_value_type collapsed as part of their conversion. Evidence the fallbacks were the reflexive case and not a wider set: over the loaded stdlib, 244/244 `entity_field_type_functors` answer `Some` to `sort_of_constructor` -- every write to the field-schema registry is paired with a `register_entity_of`/`register_self_sort`.\n\nCONTROL DISCIPLINE. 20 tests in wi946_belongs_to_readers_test.rs. Reverting all conversions: exactly 7 subjects FAIL, 13 pass. Reverting each site alone maps to exactly one subject (measured per site, one at a time). Passing either way BY DESIGN: every nested-spelling control, the two over-fire guards (an eponymous carrier in its OWN field; the RIGHT parametric binding), the two recorded probes, the value-typer parity test, and the Term-field test. `strict_view_is_blind_to_the_reflexive_belongs_to_edge` asserts the premise the file rests on instead of assuming it.\n\nSPEC. docs/kernel-language.md 6.3 gains two bullets: that every READER of the belongs-to relation answers for both spellings (WI-928 made the record reachable; this is the same equivalence for the checks that read it), each named with the disagreement it removes; and the reflect-`Term` quoted-field rule, which was in no spec section. 6.3's existing \"strict step\" sentence was already correct and is left alone.\n\nSCALAND. Nothing to mirror: it has `entityParentSort` but no `registerSelfSort` -- the reflexive edge is not implemented there, so there is no strict/total split to diverge.\n\nREVIEW. /simplify run and applied: my test file had re-implemented `common::load_stdlib_kb` + `load_stdlib_kb_with_source` verbatim -- the exact duplication that shared helper's doc says it exists to prevent -- and the value-typer helper returned an `Option<Term>` that was never `None` to answer a yes/no question. Both fixed. NOT run: /code-review (it is user-triggered and billed; I cannot launch it).\n\nFOLLOW-UPS: none blocking. `tuple_field_expected_from_ctor` and `pattern_var_ctor_sym` stay on the strict view with their measurements recorded in-place; neither is a ticket, because neither has a failing case to fix.", at: "2026-08-02T22:58:54Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-946", description: some(value: "`constructor_parent_sort` is the STRICT view wearing the GENERAL name, and ~7 belongs-to readers still pick it by accident.\n\nWI-942 fixed the THIRD per-site instance of one structural gap. `KnowledgeBase::constructor_parent_sort` (kb/mod.rs:3341) and `sort_of_constructor` (kb/mod.rs:3324) differ only by `.filter(|&p| p != functor)`; the strict one's own doc calls it 'the accessor a chain-CLIMBING walker wants' -- a RESTRICTION -- but nothing in the NAME says so, so every 'which sort does this belong to' reader reaches for it. Strict has ~16 callers in src/, total has ~3.\n\nTHE PREMISE IS LIVE IN THE SHIPPED STDLIB, on BOTH §6.3 shapes: stdlib/anthill/geometry.anthill has `sort Vec3 { entity Vec3(...) }` (eponymous, WI-926) and a free-standing `entity EulerAngles(...)`. Both go through `register_self_sort` (kb/load.rs ~13418 / ~13944), so `entity_parent[E] == E` and the strict view answers None for either.\n\nALREADY FIXED ONE SITE AT A TIME, three times: WI-926 (anthill-cpp-gen/src/lib.rs, an `.or_else` fallback), WI-937 (eval/builtins.rs entity materializer -- `a.x` died 'field_access: receiver is not an entity'), WI-942 (eval/eval.rs `runtime_carrier_sort` -- every Vec3 value reached dispatch with NO carrier, so a generic VectorSpace consumer died OperationBodyMissing). Three tickets, one cause.\n\nCANDIDATE SITES -- REPORTED BY A REVIEW AGENT DURING WI-942 AND *NOT* INDEPENDENTLY VERIFIED. Treat as leads; DRIVE each before believing it (this repo has refuted confident agent reports before):\n  * kb/typing.rs ~35621/35627 check_value_against_sort_ref -> check_value_sort_membership: `let parent = parent?;` -- claimed SILENT SKIP of a field-value type check for such a value. If true this is the WI-928 class (a write accepted that the long form rejects) and is the most serious of the list.\n  * kb/typing.rs ~35713 check_value_against_parameterized -- WI-274 binding-precision check skipped.\n  * kb/typing.rs ~26357 Pattern::Constructor -- for an eponymous PARAMETRIC sort, `case Box(v)` builds no pattern subst.\n  * kb/typing.rs ~6382 tuple_field_expected_from_ctor -- tuple-literal expected-type hint lost.\n  * kb/typing.rs ~38072 head_result_carrier -- WI-652 eq-override-backing probe under-reports (its own comment claims it answers 'uniformly').\n  * kb/body_specialize.rs ~789 ctor_sort_sym -- `same_ctor_sort` misses a [simp] specialization prune.\n  * kb/typing.rs ~1736 pattern_var_ctor_sym -- WRONG QUESTION: 'is this name a constructor', a weaker hand-rolled copy of `is_entity_constructor` (kb/mod.rs ~6423), which already owns it AND has the WI-926 arm.\nPlus 4 sites that open-code `sort_of_constructor(c).unwrap_or(c)` and are behaviourally RIGHT but duplicate the relation (kb/eq_derive.rs ~161, kb/typing.rs ~23475, kb/typing.rs ~34202, anthill-cpp-gen/src/lib.rs ~1131).\nAnd 4 genuine chain-climbers that must KEEP the strict view (kb/typing.rs ~31652 widen_to_parent_sort, ~32213 sort_sym_compatible, ~32259 sort_provides_admissibly, ~32308 bare_provider_binding_precise).\n\nSCOPE: (a) RENAME `constructor_parent_sort` -> `strict_parent_sort` (or similar) so the restriction is in the name and cannot be picked by accident; (b) per candidate above, DRIVE the eponymous/free-standing case, and convert only those that actually misbehave; (c) collapse the 4 open-coded `unwrap_or(c)` fallbacks onto the total accessor.\n\nACCEPTANCE: each converted site has a test that DRIVES the eponymous (or free-standing) carrier to a VALUE or to a LOUD refusal and FAILS before the conversion -- 'it loads clean' is not evidence. Any candidate that turns out NOT to misbehave is recorded as such with the probe that showed it, rather than silently dropped. cargo-test green.\n\nSOURCE: WI-942 review."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-02T22:58:57Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-948", description: some(value: "RUSTLAND'S `parse_equation_lhs` DECIDES 'IS THIS HEAD AN EQUATION?' FROM THE FUNCTOR NAME, not from the WI-618 `minted` provenance that exists to replace exactly that kind of re-derivation. FOUND 2026-08-03 porting the pass-3 rule-introduced-functor scan to scaland, whose twin was fixed in the port; the rust side is untouched and is the reason this is a ticket. TODAY (rustland/anthill-core/src/parse/convert.rs, `parse_equation_lhs`): a body-less single-head rule is read as an EQUATION when the head is a 2-ary `Term::Fn` whose functor name satisfies `pratt::is_equation_functor` (eq / unify / struct_eq). Nothing checks that the node was BUILT by the infix desugar. CONSEQUENCE, two directions, both silent: (1) `rule eq(?a, ?b)` — a legitimate 2-ary PREDICATE head written as an ordinary call — is misread as an equation, its 'LHS' is the variable `?a`, `rule_introduced_functor_name` falls through its `Term::Fn` match to `None`, and NO symbol is introduced for `eq/2`; a later `import ns::{eq}` of it then fails and every reference falls back to a bare interned name. (2) `rule struct_eq(f(?x), g(?x))` written as a call introduces `f` stamped `SymbolKind::EquationFunctor` instead of `Goal` — WI-898's distinction inverted for that rule. FIX: guard the equation reading on `parse_terms.is_minted(head)` — the store already carries the mark (pratt.rs:176 marks every infix desugar) and the same function calls `is_minted` two lines later on the SUBJECT, so this is one condition, not new machinery. `SimpleTermStore::minted`'s own doc comment states the principle being violated: provenance is carried, not re-derived from a name. Scaland's `Loader.parseEquationLhs` already has the guard and a test (`WI-618: a written eq(?a, ?b) head is a predicate, not an equation`) — port both. ACCEPTANCE: the two directions above as tests that FAIL on today's rustland."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-950", author: "user", content: "Soft ordering (not a dependency): option (a) — dropping errors whose position the successful parse did not consume — needs a usable position, which is WI-947's work. Options (b) and (c) do not, so this is not blocked.", at: "2026-08-03T05:26:19Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-951", description: some(value: "RUSTLAND'S WI-850 TYPE-PARAM-DEFAULT REFUSAL QUOTES RAW CST TEXT, so a default written with an interior comment or line break renders that trivia into the diagnostic. The scaland twin was fixed under WI-950 (2026-08-03); the rust side is untouched and is the reason this is a ticket. MECHANISM: `convert_operation_type_param` (rustland/anthill-core/src/parse/convert.rs, ~3485) takes `let written = self.text(default)`, and `text` is a raw byte slice over `source[node.start_byte()..node.end_byte()]`. Comments are tree-sitter `extras` (grammar.js lists whitespace, `line_comment`, `block_comment`), so anything written INSIDE the type node sits inside that byte range. MEASURED 2026-08-03 by a scratch test on `wi_tests`: an `operation foo[T = List[ {- note -} Int64` continued on the next line as `]](x: T) -> T` produces a message quoting the default as `List[ {- note -} Int64` plus the line break and indentation before `]` — and the same blob a SECOND time, in the suggested call-site spelling `foo[T = …](…)`. What is already CORRECT and needs no work: a TRAILING comment (`[T = Int64 -- why` then a newline then `]`) renders clean, because it falls outside the type node's byte range; only trivia INTERIOR to the type is affected. FIX, with scaland's shape for reference: WI-950 added `normalizeCapture` — every run of trivia collapses to one space, and a string literal is copied verbatim so a comment opener inside one (`Tagged[\"a -- b\"]`) is not eaten as trivia — sharing one trivia definition with the whitespace instance, so the parser cannot skip a comment form that the diagnostic then renders. rustland can do better than a slice scan: walk the `default` node's children and skip `line_comment` / `block_comment` nodes, or render the converted `TypeExpr`. NOTE this is a DIAGNOSTIC-TEXT defect only — the refusal itself fires correctly and WI-850's acceptance tests pass; the cost is that the author reads their own comment back twice inside a message about a dropped default. ACCEPTANCE: a test asserting the quoted default for a type written with an interior block comment and an interior line break (the measured repro above), plus one whose default contains a string literal holding a comment opener, so the normalisation cannot eat it. Both must FAIL on today's rustland."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-951", author: "user", content: "CORRECTION to the \"FIX, with scaland's shape for reference\" paragraph — do NOT copy what that paragraph describes. The first cut of the WI-950 fix normalised the `.!` source capture by re-lexing it for trivia, and /code-review measured that this TRUNCATES a `-`-bearing identifier: an anthill identifier is `[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_-]*`, so `my--type` is ONE token whose `--` is not a line comment, and the refusal quoted (and then RECOMMENDED) `T = my`. Stripping trivia from a capture needs the whole token model, not just the comment forms. The delivered scaland fix instead renders the parsed `TypeExpr` (`renderTypeExpr` / `renderTerm` / `renderLiteral` in AnthillParser.scala), whose matches are exhaustive over sealed types — a new IR case is a compile error rather than a silently mis-rendered diagnostic. So for rustland, take the route this ticket already lists second: render the converted `TypeExpr`, or walk the `default` node's children skipping `line_comment` / `block_comment` nodes. ADD to the acceptance criteria: a default written as a hyphenated identifier (`[T = my--type]`) must be quoted back in full — that case passes on today's rustland (it quotes raw text) and is the one a re-lexing fix would break, so it belongs in the suite as a control.", at: "2026-08-03T07:21:36Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-950", description: some(value: "A SCALAND PARSE ERROR CAN BE RECORDED FROM A PRODUCTION THE PARSE LATER DISCARDS, so a failing parse may carry a message about text that is not in the final tree. FOUND by /code-review on the parser resync (2026-08-03). MECHANISM: `AnthillParserImpl` threads a shared `errors: ArrayBuffer[ParseError]` and several productions push into it from inside a fastparse `.map` — `validateProjectionLabels` (WI-639), the braced-operation-block visibility refusal, and now `refuseTypeParamDefaults` (WI-850). A `.map` runs when ITS production succeeds, which is not the same as the enclosing parse accepting that text: fastparse may backtrack out of the alternative afterwards. `AnthillParser.parse` then returns `Left` if `errors.nonEmpty` EVEN ON `Parsed.Success`, so a stale entry is not merely noise on a failure — it can refuse an otherwise-good parse. REPRO SHAPE (noise direction, the reachable one today): `operation { g[T = Int](x: A) -> B  <garbage> }` — the entry parses and records the WI-850 refusal, then the closing brace fails and the `~/` cut fails the whole parse; the returned errors carry both the genuine 'Parse error at N' and the stale refusal. The poisoning direction is not reachable today (`singleOperation` cannot reach `operationTypeParamList` on a path that later succeeds as a braced block) but nothing PREVENTS it — the next backtrackable alternative over one of these productions makes it live, silently. OPTIONS: (a) record errors with the input position and drop any whose position the successful parse did not consume; (b) return refusals in the production's VALUE (an `Either`/accumulator that rides the IR) instead of a side buffer, so a discarded parse discards them with it; (c) keep the buffer but only surface it on `Parsed.Success`, accepting that a refusal is lost when the parse fails for another reason. SECOND, SMALLER DEFECT in the same area, fix alongside: `refuseTypeParamDefaults` quotes the default via `typeExpr.!` (RAW SOURCE), so `[T = List[ /* note */ Int ]]` renders the comment and interior whitespace into the message — `.trim` only strips the ends. Rendering a structured `TypeExpr` (which is what the pre-WI-850 code held) or normalising the capture would fix it. ACCEPTANCE: a test that a discarded production contributes no error, plus one on the quoted-default text."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "scaland-sbt-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-03T07:25:54Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-952", description: some(value: "SCALAND'S TRIVIA SKIPPER SWALLOWS AN UNTERMINATED `{- -}` / `{< >}` BLOCK SILENTLY, and then reports a parse error pointing INSIDE the comment. FOUND by /code-review on the WI-950 delivery (2026-08-03); pre-existing, untouched by that change, which is why it is its own ticket. MECHANISM: the `given ws: Whitespace` instance in `AnthillParserImpl` (core/src/main/scala/anthill/parse/AnthillParser.scala) scans a block comment with the guard `index + 1 < length`. On a file that ends mid-comment the inner loop exits with `depth > 0` at `index == length-1`; `continue` is still true, so the OUTER loop reads that last character, finds it is not trivia, and stops. The parser then tries to begin a declaration on the final character of an unterminated comment and reports `Parse error at <length-1>` — a position inside text the author intended as a comment, with no hint that a `-}` is missing. The `{< >}` doc-block arm has the same shape, where `if index + 1 < length then index += 2` silently no-ops on the unterminated case. CONSEQUENCE is a confusing diagnostic, not a wrong parse: the file does not load either way. But CLAUDE.md asks for a loud error over a silent mis-handle, and \"unterminated block comment\" is decidable exactly where the skip gives up. WORK: on exhausting the input with `depth > 0` (or with no `>}` found), report an unterminated-comment error naming the OPENING position, not the position where the scan stopped — the opener is what the author has to fix. The whitespace instance has no error channel today, so this needs a decision on how it reports: `AnthillParserImpl` holds the `errors` buffer, so the instance can push into it directly, but note WI-950 — the buffer is refusal-SCOPED, and a trivia error recorded inside a production that later backtracks would be dropped with it, so this one probably belongs on the `Parsed.Failure` path in `AnthillParser.parse` instead. NOTE for whoever takes it: rustland does not share this defect — tree-sitter handles comments as `extras` and reports its own unterminated-token error. ACCEPTANCE: a file ending inside `{- ... ` and one ending inside `{< ... ` each produce an error that says the comment is unterminated and points at the opener; plus a control that a well-formed nested `{- {- -} -}` still skips exactly as today."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "scaland-sbt-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-03T07:39:03Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-949", description: some(value: "SCALAND'S THREE SCAN PASSES EACH RE-SPELL THE SAME NAMESPACE/SORT RECURSION, and the pass-3 port made it three copies. `Loader.scanItemsPass1` / `scanItemsPass2` / `scanItemsPass3` each derive the child scope the same way — joinSegments -> makeQualified(prefix, shortName) -> look the scope symbol up -> recurse with the new (scopeTerm, prefix) — and differ ONLY in what they do at a rule / operation / import / requires item. FOUND by /code-review on the pass-3 port (2026-08-03). WHY IT MATTERS rather than being tidiness: the WI-853 top-level-import arm had to be threaded through two of them in lockstep, the `pending` buffer through two, and the three disagree TODAY on the miss case — pass 1 cannot miss (it defines), pass 2 uses `sortSym.foreach` (silent skip), pass 3 now reports (`descend`). A single `forEachScope(items, scopeTerm, prefix)(body)` walker parameterised by the per-item action would make the three passes differ only where they actually disagree, and give the miss ONE answer. NOT urgent and deliberately not folded into the pass-3 delivery: it touches all three passes at once, which is a different risk profile from adding one. ACCEPTANCE: the walker extracted, the three passes expressed through it, suite green — and the miss behaviour stated once."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "scaland-sbt-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-03T08:00:18Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-949", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED 2026-08-03 (58152bb7). ONE walker: `walkScopes(items, scopeTerm, prefix, pass)` owns the descent (joinSegments -> makeQualified -> obtain child scope -> recurse); a pass supplies `enterScope` (what it does AT a scope, yielding the scope to recurse into) and `atItem` (every other item). Four passes now: DefinePass / ImportPass / RuleHeadPass / LoadPass — the phase-2 `loadItems` was a FOURTH copy of the same recursion, so it joined rather than being left as the odd one out. `ScopeDecl` (a parameterized enum over Namespace | SortWithBody, forwarding name/imports/items) is what lets the DEFINING pass tell the two apart without an unreachable third arm — passing the bare `Item` would have needed a silent-skip or fabricated-error catch-all. THE MISS ANSWER, stated once, in `lookupDefined`: report. `lookupScope` is its scope-shaped use. FOLDED IN from /code-review: LoadPass's `EntityItem` arm was the SAME invariant (DefinePass defines every entity) and still dropped its `entity_of` fact on a `.foreach` miss — it now goes through the same helper. Pass 1 also lost a `fileTerms` parameter it threaded but never used. DRIVE + CONTROL: new LoaderTest case calls `Loader.load` WITHOUT `scanDefinitions` — the only reachable miss, since `loadAll` runs the defining pass over every file first — and asserts the diagnostic names the scope; VERIFIED discriminating by backing the report out (fails with an empty ArrayBuffer, and only that test fails). Control = `load namespace with scoping`, which takes the SAME hoisted fixture (`buildNamespacedParsedFile`) through the full pipeline and passes either way. Full scaland suite green, 307 tests. /code-review run (4 agents: reuse / simplification / efficiency / altitude). Efficiency clean (ScopeDecl+Some allocate only on the ~196 scope openers of the stdlib, x4 passes; the pass classes capture fewer slots per scope than the closures they replaced). Applied: parameterized-enum ScopeDecl (15 lines -> 3), test-fixture hoist, stale `scanItemsPass1` doc reference in parse/IR.scala, the EntityItem arm. SKIPPED, with reasons: (a) `kb.tryResolveSymbol` instead of the raw `byQualifiedName.get` — 8 other pre-existing sites in Loader.scala use the raw form, so changing only the new one makes the file LESS uniform; take it as a sweep or not at all. (b) making `lookupScope` the trait's DEFAULT `enterScope` so a fifth pass cannot re-open the divergence by returning None for its own reason — real hardening, but it needs `kb`/`errors` as ScopePass members and hands DefinePass an errors buffer it never writes; over-abstraction for two identical implementors. (c) NARROWING THE API so the miss is unrepresentable (make `load` private to `loadAll`, or have `scanDefinitions` return a token `load` requires) — this IS the deeper fix for the only reachable miss, and reporting is the floor rather than the ceiling; not taken because it is outside WI-949's scope and would delete the only drive of the loud miss. Worth its own ticket if the two-caller `load`/`scanDefinitions` split is ever revisited. FILED WI-953: the rustland twin — same four-way duplication (scan_items_pass1/2/3 + load_items over find_scope_by_name), and its pass 3 CANNOT report a miss at all, taking no errors vec.", at: "2026-08-03T08:00:21Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-954", description: some(value: "A type parameter's canonical var is reconstructed by the READER; the LOADER already computes it uniformly and throws it away.\n\nWI-943 gave `typing::type_param_global_var` a two-channel ladder — exact `SortAlias` for an alias-form declaration, `OperationInfo.type_params` for a bracket parameter, then the legacy short-name guess. That satisfied WI-943 (one owner, no reader has to know), but it RECONSTRUCTS at every read a fact the loader already had.\n\nMEASURED (WI-943 /simplify altitude pass): `LoaderState.type_param_vars: HashMap<(u32 scope_raw, String short), TermId>` (rustland/anthill-core/src/kb/load.rs, field decl ~7536) IS the unified map, filled by BOTH declarers — `type_param_var` (~11653, alias-form) and `load_operation` (~15116, bracket). Its key is literally the key `SymbolTable::is_type_param` uses. It is dropped at end of load and never published. The four `add_type_param` sites (load.rs ~2318 op bracket, ~2759 WI-452 marked, ~2772 `sort T = ?`, ~14945 WI-402 existential carrier) are the same uniform choke point.\n\nCOST OF NOT PUBLISHING IT: (a) two scan tiers survive that a loader-filled index would delete — `op_info::declared_type_param_var`'s pre-`build_op_signatures` `lookup_operation_info` fallback, and `scan_sort_aliases`; (b) the identity round-trips THROUGH STRINGS in both directions (`op_declared_type_param_var` goes symbol -> scope -> op -> short name -> pick; WI-708's `op_scoped_type_param_symbol` does the inverse); (c) the ladder's RUNG ORDER is load-bearing with nothing enforcing it — WI-402's existential carrier is an OP-SCOPED parameter that publishes via `SortAlias`, so it is only correct because the alias rung runs first.\n\nSHAPE: `named_requirement_slots` (kb/mod.rs ~809) is the precedent — loader-produced, typer-consumed, no runtime or persistence surface, therefore a side-table rather than a fact field. A `type_param_canonical_var: HashMap<Symbol, VarId>` is its sibling. `type_param_global_var` then becomes one map read: no ladder, no channel dispatch, no order to get wrong.\n\nNOT THE ALTERNATIVE WI-943 REJECTED: asserting a `SortAlias` fact per bracket param was evaluated and REFUSED — see the sibling ticket on `reconstruct_sort_params`' two keyings, plus it pollutes `by_name` and redefines a user-queryable relation.\n\nACCEPTANCE: driven — `wi943_type_param_identity_test` still green with the ladder REPLACED by the single map read, plus a test that an op-scoped WI-402 existential carrier resolves correctly WITHOUT depending on rung order (today's only protection). cargo-test green.\n\nSOURCE: WI-943 /simplify altitude review."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-943", description: some(value: "An OPERATION type parameter has TWO canonical unification vars, and every reader must know which one it is holding.\n\nMEASURED 2026-08-02 while fixing WI-942 (see rustland/anthill-core/src/kb/typing.rs, the `op_param_rigids` loop in `check_operation_bodies`). For `operation cmp[T](a: T, b: T) -> Int64 requires Ordered[T]`:\n  * `OperationInfo.type_params` holds `Var::Global(1325)` -- minted at load, and what `rigidify_op_type_params` binds when it skolemizes the body;\n  * the param SYMBOL `...cmp.T` aliases (SortAlias) to `Var::Global(1)` -- what a WRITTEN occurrence of `T` resolves through (`type_param_global_var`), hence what `sigma_class` sees inside the `requires Ordered[T]` clause.\nFor a SORT parameter the two COINCIDE (`sort_type_params_as_pairs` IS the alias table), which is why the split stayed invisible: every sort-param reader is right by accident of the two being one var.\n\nWHAT IT COST. WI-942: the σ bridge (`canonical_global_var`) was built from one identity and consulted with the other, so `op_requires_covers` could not see that an operation's own `requires Ordered[T]` covers its own `Ordered.compare(a, b)`. It refused the program at load, demanding a `requires` `on enclosing sort` that the author had already written on the OPERATION. WI-942 fixed it by recording BOTH identities against the same rigid -- correct, but it is a bridge over a split that should not exist.\n\nWHY THIS IS NOT MERELY COSMETIC: the two-identity split is exactly the shape of defect this repo keeps re-finding (one question, two answers, agreeing only where the names coincide). Any future reader of an op type param picks an identity and is silently right or silently wrong depending on which. `declared_type_param_vid` / `type_param_vid_in_sort` / `sigma_class` take the alias one; `rigidify_op_type_params` / `check_unconstrained_type_params` take the record one.\n\nSCOPE: decide whether the two can be made ONE var (make the illegal state unrepresentable), or -- if they genuinely must differ -- give the pairing ONE owner with a doc-commented invariant, so no reader has to know. NOT root-caused: I did not read where the SortAlias for an op type param is asserted versus where OperationInfo.type_params is minted; that is the first question.\n\nACCEPTANCE: driven, not just loaded -- a test that resolves an op type param through BOTH routes and asserts they agree, plus the WI-942 tests (renamed_op_type_params_are_covered_by_the_operations_own_requires in particular) still green with the WI-942 double-recording REMOVED if the identities are unified. cargo-test green.\n\nSOURCE: found by WI-942, which works around it."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-03T10:55:34Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-947", description: some(value: "SCALAND DIAGNOSTICS REPORT A BYTE OFFSET, NEVER `file:line:col` — port rustland's WI-745/852/854 to scaland. MEASURED (2026-08-03, during the parser resync): every scaland span is built by `AnthillParser.mkSpan(s, e) = Span(fileName, s, e, 0, 0, 0, 0)` — row and col are hard-coded 0 at EVERY construction site, and 27 of the parse-error sites additionally pass `mkSpan(0, 0)`, so they carry no offset either. What a user sees today: `Parse error at 9975: Position 128:91` (fastparse's own rendering, only on the whole-parse failure path) or, for a collected `ParseError`, nothing locational at all. A LoadError is worse: `LoadError.Other(message: String)` — the variant the WI-727 variadic-capture refusals and several others use — has NO span field, so those diagnostics cannot point anywhere even in principle. rustland fixed the same three gaps: WI-745 (blocking load errors get a file identity), WI-852 (a parse error names its file and position, like a load error does), WI-854 (resolve line:col through a PER-SOURCE INDEX, not a rescan of the file per error). WORK: (a) a per-source line index built once per file, byte offset -> (row, col); (b) `mkSpan` populates row/col from it, and the ~27 `mkSpan(0, 0)` sites get real indices (most already have an `Index` available or can take one cheaply); (c) `LoadError.Other` gains a span, or is split into located variants — the WI-727 refusals are the driving case, and `Param` carries no span today so the parser must capture one; (d) one renderer that prints `file:line:col: message` for both error families. ACCEPTANCE beyond the tool passing: a test that parses a fixture with a known-bad line and asserts the reported row/col, and a load-error twin — 'it produces an error' is not evidence the position is right. NOTE what is already correct and needs no work: the WI-850 type-param-default refusal's offset was MEASURED exact across three whitespace shapes (offset lands on the identifier, not the preceding space), so the `Index`-before-whitespace hazard a review predicted does not occur here."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "scaland-sbt-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-03T11:48:50Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-955", description: some(value: "reconstruct_sort_params' two paths disagree on a NESTED SortAlias, and WI-402 already produces one.\n\n`reconstruct_sort_params` (rustland/anthill-core/src/kb/typing.rs) recovers a sort's type params from its SortAlias facts by TWO routes that are supposed to agree:\n  * INDEXED (`SortAliasIndex.by_parent`, built by `build_sort_alias_index`): keys the alias's qualified name with `rsplit_once('.')` — LAST segment is the param, the rest is the parent.\n  * PRE-INDEX SCAN (load-time value typing, when `kb.sort_alias_index` is None): keeps every alias whose name has the parent's as a `.`-boundary PREFIX, then slices EVERYTHING after it as the param name.\n\nThey agree only for a one-segment param. `build_sort_alias_index`'s own comment states the premise: 'sorts don't nest, so no `Sort.Inner.T` alias exists to make the last-segment split and the prefix test disagree.' THAT PREMISE IS FALSE TODAY.\n\nDRIVEN (WI-943 /simplify altitude pass, probe run against current main):\n\n  sort Holder\n    sort T = ?\n    entity holder(item: T)\n    operation mk() -> C ensures KVStore[C, K = String, V = Int64]\n  end\n\n  SortAlias sources under `<ns>.Holder.` = [\"T\", \"mk.C\"]\n  type_params_of_sort(Holder)            = [\"T\"]\n\nWI-402's existential carrier (`build_existential_return`, kb/load.rs ~14935) defines `<op_qualified>.<carrier>` and calls `emit_type_param_backing_var` on it — a SortAlias named `<ns>.<Sort>.<op>.C`. The pre-index scan slices `mk.C` as a PARAM NAME of `Holder` and (per its WI-384 tail) injects it as a `mk.C = ?_` binding into every constructor type it reconstructs for `Holder`; the indexed path files the same alias under the OPERATION and a lookup for the SORT never sees it. Silent drop on one path, garbage param on the other.\n\nNOT DRIVEN, and the ticket should start here: I measured that the SHAPE reaches the slicing rule and that the two keying rules disagree on it. I did NOT drive a user-visible wrong outcome — the pre-index path runs only in the load-time value-typing window, and no test moved. FIRST QUESTION: is there a program where a constructor of a sort with an existential-returning member gets the spurious binding into a type that is then compared? If not, this is a latent trap to close, not a live bug — say which, in the ticket, before fixing.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS BEYOND ITSELF: this is the exact hazard that made WI-943 REFUSE the obvious unification (assert a `SortAlias` per operation bracket parameter, so both kinds publish through one channel). That alternative would take this rare shape and make it universal — 45 `operation X[…]` brackets in stdlib alone. Closing this reopens that design option; see the loader-published-index sibling ticket.\n\nSCOPE: make the two keyings ONE. The prefix test cannot distinguish `Sort.param` from `Sort.op.param` from name alone — the symbol table can (`declaring_scope_symbol`), and `type_params_of_sort` already gives the declared list the reconstruction should be driven BY rather than re-derived from names.\n\nACCEPTANCE: driven — a test that the two paths return the SAME params for a sort with an existential-returning member (force each path: run the reconstruction before and after `build_sort_alias_index`), and that neither reports a param `type_params_of_sort` does not list. cargo-test green.\n\nSOURCE: WI-943 /simplify altitude review."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-03T11:52:42Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-955", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. Answer to the ticket's FIRST QUESTION: LATENT TRAP, not a live bug.\n\nMEASURED (the decisive control): with the pre-index branch of reconstruct_sort_params replaced by a panic!, the ENTIRE workspace suite passed - 4069 tests, 0 failures, 0 runtime panics. Nothing in the tested surface reaches that path. It is reachable in principle - value_type_term's callers are resolve_bridge_requirements, value_carrier_overrides_eq_family_deep, simp_requires_guard_holds, find_dictionary_guard and typed_pattern_bounds_hold, any of which can run during load (a [simp] requires guard, a typed pattern bound, a dictionary guard under a compile-time macro / const) before type_check_sorts_collect builds the index - but no program in the repo drives it.\n\nTHE DISAGREEMENT ITSELF, driven before the fix:\n  reconstruct_sort_params(Holder)  indexed=[\"T\"]  scanned=[\"T\",\"mk.C\"]  type_params_of_sort=[\"T\"]\n  value_type_term(holder(item: 42))  indexed Holder[T = Int64]  scanned Holder[T = Int64, mk.C = ?_]\n\nCORRECTION to the ticket's own framing: it says 'Silent drop on one path, garbage param on the other.' The INDEX was not dropping anything - filing <ns>.Holder.mk.C under mk IS the right answer, because Holder must not see it. Only the scan was wrong. Verified: for all 106 var-target SortAlias facts in a stdlib load the last-segment split and the symbol-table scope link agree exactly (0 disagreements, 0 with no readable scope), so the index half of this fix is behaviour-identical by construction.\n\nFIX - one rule, and one gate. sort_param_alias_entry(kb, alias_functor, target) is now the sole answer to 'is this alias a sort's declared type parameter, whose, and under what name'; build_sort_alias_index's by_parent and reconstruct_sort_params' pre-index scan are the same two calls in the same order. by_parent re-keyed String -> Symbol. reconstruct_sort_params split into sort_param_alias_vars (index-or-scan) + ONE shared WI-384/WI-516 tail (it was duplicated once per branch).\n\nWENT DEEPER THAN THE TICKET ASKED, on evidence: the gate is is_sort_param_symbol, which already existed and already documented the exact distinction ('sort T = ? inside sort Stream { … } IS a type-param; sort Term = ? at namespace level is a top-level abstract sort, not a type parameter'). Without it the reader answers 'declared in the scope of', not 'a declared type parameter of', and by_parent files 9 top-level abstract sorts under NAMESPACE parents (reflect's Term/Symbol/FactRef/SourceId/NodeOccurrence/ConstraintId, prelude.Type/Unit, kernel.T) - inert only because no caller passes a namespace symbol, a caller accident rather than a property. MEASURED with the gate: 55 parents kept, exactly those 9 dropped, and the entries recovered for a parent equal type_params_of_sort for EVERY parent - 0 mismatches. The ticket's acceptance ('neither reports a param type_params_of_sort does not list') is now true by construction, not by caller accident.\n\nDEVIATION from SCOPE, stated. The ticket said the reconstruction 'should be driven BY' type_params_of_sort. It is not, and the real competitor is not type_params_of_sort but sort_type_params_as_pairs (typing.rs), which IS driven by the declared list and IS memoized. Three verified blockers, now written at sort_param_alias_vars: (a) its sort_param_pairs_cache has a get and an insert and NO invalidator, so populating it from the load-time window would let a half-loaded sort's param list poison every type-check-time reader; (b) it resolves each param through resolve_sort_alias, whose last rung is the name-directed sort_alias_by_name guess this reader just stopped making; (c) it keys pairs by the QUALIFIED param symbol, not the bare one make_parameterized_type needs. type_params_of_sort alone also cannot serve: it returns NAMES with no backing VarId, and it scans symbols.by_qualified_name in full on a per-constructor-node path. It is the tests' ORACLE instead.\n\nALSO, at the user's direction during review: all three readers of the SortAlias relation - build_sort_alias_index, scan_sort_aliases and sort_param_alias_vars - now decode a fact through one carrier-neutral sort_alias_head_slots (rule_head_value + TermView, the surface load's sort_alias_exists already uses), returning (source functor Symbol, target TermId) - the shapes the consumers actually use - with the is_fact test folded in so a caller cannot forget it. fact_head_term answers None for a Value::Node-carried head and skipped the fact WHOLE on a property no consumer depends on. Not a behaviour change: measured 0 value-carried heads among 107 SortAlias facts, and for a loader-produced alias a value-carried head and a non-term target are the same condition. Routing scan_sort_aliases was found by BOTH the /simplify reuse and altitude passes - my first cut had converted only two of the three, which would have introduced this ticket's own defect class one map over (index vs its own pre-index fallback disagreeing about which facts exist).\n\nTESTS - wi955_one_alias_keying_tests (typing.rs):\n  both_paths_report_the_declared_params_and_nothing_else\n  the_constructor_type_is_identical_before_and_after_the_index\nCONTROL, measured on the final code by restoring the prefix slice: they fail with [\"T\",\"mk.C\"] vs [\"T\"], and Holder[T = Int64] vs Holder[T = Int64, mk.C = ?_]. The INDEXED arm passes either way BY DESIGN (the split already reached the right answer) and is asserted so that a re-keying which merely LOSES params fails loudly instead of letting both paths agree at zero. The type assertion renders BINDINGS, not just keys - a keys-only check would pass on Holder[T] carrying no Int64 at all.\n\ncargo-test: 4071 passed, 0 failed (baseline 4069 + these 2).\n\nREOPENS the WI-943 alternative's keying hazard only: a SortAlias per operation bracket param is declared in the OPERATION's scope, so it files under the operation and can never be read as a param of an enclosing sort. WI-954 still refuses that alternative for its other reasons (pollutes by_name, redefines a user-queryable relation).\n\nFOLLOW-UPS FILED: WI-960 (make_type_var per-call symbol resolution on the constructor-typing hot path), WI-958 (three spellings of 'operation -> its parent sort'), WI-959 (two remaining hand-rolled stdlib fixtures in typing.rs test modules). Feedback added to WI-954 and WI-956.", at: "2026-08-03T11:53:20Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-954", author: "user", content: "From WI-955 (delivered). Two things this ticket should absorb.\n\n1. IT COMPOSES, it does not conflict. WI-954's type_param_canonical_var is param-symbol -> var (the VALUE half). WI-955's rule is alias -> (parent, param) (the KEY half). Neither answers the other's question - but WI-954's stated key, (u32 scope_raw, String short), is literally what KnowledgeBase::declaring_scope_symbol recovers, which is what WI-955 moved the SortAlias keying onto. The two converge on one key from opposite ends.\n\n2. WI-954 SUBSUMES WI-955's structure - take it with you. With the map published, reconstruct_sort_params needs no alias walk at all: type_params_of_sort(parent) -> resolve each name -> one map read. That deletes SortAliasIndex.by_parent, sort_param_alias_entry, and sort_param_alias_vars' entire two-source (index-or-scan) shape, and the pre-index window disappears with them. WI-955 was worth doing because the two sources disagreed TODAY, but its structure is transitional; do not preserve it out of caution.\n\n3. RUNG ORDER, now settled at one site. This ticket notes the ladder's rung order is load-bearing with nothing enforcing it, because WI-402's existential carrier is an OP-SCOPED parameter that publishes via SortAlias and is only correct because the alias rung runs first. WI-955 measured that carrier directly: <ns>.<Sort>.<op>.C, declaring scope = the OPERATION. The keying now reads the scope link, so at THAT site the answer no longer depends on rung order. The ladder in type_param_global_var is untouched and still does.\n\n4. Datum for the 'four uniform add_type_param sites' claim, verified: the sort T = ? site (load.rs, Item::AbstractSort arm of scan_items_pass1) is gated on matches!(s.definition, TypeExpr::Variable { .. }), so an in-body 'sort T = Int64' is NOT registered as a type param. WI-955 relies on this - it is why every declared param that carries a SortAlias has a Var::Global target.", at: "2026-08-03T11:58:38Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-956", author: "user", content: "From WI-955 (delivered). Three corrections to this ticket's own text, and one blocker that is now gone.\n\n1. ITEM 2 IS STALE where it describes reconstruct_sort_params. It says the pre-index tail 'collects EVERY alias under a parent prefix'. It no longer does: it keeps every alias whose DECLARING SCOPE is the parent and which is_sort_param_symbol accepts. The prefix slice is gone.\n\n2. ITEM 2's VERDICT STANDS and was respected. WI-955 did NOT consolidate the WALKS with scan_sort_aliases - first-match vs collect-all really are different loops. It DID share the per-fact HEAD READ across all three (build_sort_alias_index, scan_sort_aliases, sort_param_alias_vars) via a new sort_alias_head_slots(kb, rid) -> Option<(source functor Symbol, target TermId)>. That is a different thing from the consolidation this item closed; do not read item 2 as forbidding it. Note scan_sort_aliases keeps its rules_by_functor_iter and its first-match return - only the 6-line decode changed.\n\n3. ITEM 1's STATED BLOCKER NOW EXISTS AS A HELPER. This ticket says reuse by load.rs::find_sort_alias_var 'needs the predicate widened to (functor, target)' because its Term::Var(_) filter is on the TARGET and sits inside the loop. sort_alias_head_slots returns exactly (functor, target), and reads the head through rule_head_value + TermView - the same carrier-neutral surface load.rs::sort_alias_exists already uses, so find_sort_alias_var would not have to change carrier posture to adopt it. It is pub(crate)-adjacent in typing.rs; reaching it from load.rs still needs a visibility bump.\n\n4. ITEM 3 IS PARTLY DONE, differently. WI-955 did not touch the five test-side copies of 'SortAlias source -> target Var::Global'. It DID lift a different test duplicate in typing.rs - #[cfg(test)] mod stdlib_fixture, the stdlib-loading sequence - because it was about to add a fourth copy. Two hand-rolled copies remain there and are now WI-959. Item 3 (tests/common/mod.rs helper for the five files) is untouched and still open.\n\n5. ITEM 4 (kind_of vs has_kind at ground_rigid_projection_if_concrete) untouched. Related: WI-958 files the three qualified-name spellings of 'operation -> its parent sort', two of which carry a type_params_of_sort gate that has the same 'is this gate doing more than it says' question item 4 raises.", at: "2026-08-03T11:58:57Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-955", author: "user", content: "ADDENDUM (user review, after delivery). USER ASKED: 'why do you think that type-param aliases of parent can be only var?'\n\nMy comment justified the Var::Global gate with 'a sort T = Int64 alias resolves to a sort ref and pins nothing' - an argument about the TARGET, and the weak version. The real guarantee is upstream in the REGISTRATION: add_type_param is gated on matches!(s.definition, TypeExpr::Variable { .. }) (load.rs, Item::AbstractSort arm of scan_items_pass1), so an in-body 'sort T = Int64' or 'sort U = List[T]' is never a declared type parameter at all - it is not in type_params_of_sort, and with is_sort_param_symbol as the membership gate it never reaches the target check. Every form that IS a param and carries a SortAlias gets it from emit_type_param_backing_var (WI-452 marked, WI-402 carrier) or from load_abstract_sort's Variable arm - a Var::Global in each case. An operation's BRACKET params publish through OperationInfo.type_params and produce no SortAlias.\n\nCONSEQUENCE, fixed: Var::Global is not a filter here, it is the EXTRACTION of the var the reconstruction reads out of the substitution, and it cannot fail for a declared param. Silently returning None on it would make the sort quietly lose a param - the arity-shrinking WI-384 exists to prevent - so it is now a debug_assert! naming the alias and the target found. Measured vacuous: across 55 parents the recovered entries equal type_params_of_sort exactly, which is only possible if no declared param was dropped there; the full suite runs the assert live and it never fires.\n\nThis is the second time in this ticket that a justification I wrote was weaker than the checkable fact behind it (the first: calling the index's behaviour 'the silent drop half' when the index was right). Both are now stated as the thing that can be checked.", at: "2026-08-03T11:59:15Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-957", description: some(value: "SCALAND: LOCATE THE `AmbiguousSymbol` LOAD ERROR — the one diagnostic WI-947 could not reach. `Loader.resolveName` raises `LoadError.AmbiguousSymbol(name, candidates, Span.empty, \"\")`, and after WI-947 that is the ONLY load diagnostic in the tree that renders with no position — and with an empty scope name, so it reads `ambiguous symbol 'x' in scope ''`. WHY IT WAS OUT OF SCOPE: `resolveName` is handed a NAME STRING lifted out of an already-built term by `reallocTerm`, and scaland's parse-time `SimpleTermStore` records no span per `TermId` — every other WI-947 site had an IR node with a span in reach, this one has nothing to point at. WORK: give `SimpleTermStore` a TermId -> Span side table (it already carries a `descriptions` HashMap keyed the same way, so the shape is established), populated where the parser allocates a source-derived `Term.Ident` / `Term.Fn`, and thread it to `reallocTerm`'s name-resolution rung; fill the `scopeName` field from the enclosing scope's qualified name while there (it is passed `\"\"` today). ACCEPTANCE beyond the tool passing: a fixture with a genuinely ambiguous short name asserts the reported row/col lands on the OCCURRENCE that is ambiguous, and that the scope name is non-empty — \"an error is produced\" is not evidence either field is right. NOTE the enforcement site already names this WI in a comment (`Loader.resolveName`). Rustland's twin (`resolve_name_in_kb`) has a span because tree-sitter nodes carry one; this is a scaland-only gap."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "ToolPasses:scaland-sbt-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-03T12:36:30Z"))
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-961", name: "scaland")
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-962", name: "scaland")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-960", author: "user", content: "MEASURED BEFORE IMPLEMENTING - THIS TICKET'S PREMISE IS REFUTED. Status left Open pending a decision; nothing was changed in the tree.\n\nInstrumented make_type_var with a call counter, full stdlib + anthill-stl host-bindings load and type-check:\n  bare stdlib load                          6 calls   (3x \"?_\", 2x \"?param\", 1x \"?logical_var\")\n  same load + a 200x driver on the UNBOUND-param path   6 calls  (UNCHANGED)\n  load wall time                            ~440,000 us\n\nThe driver was built specifically to force reconstruct_sort_params' WI-384 unbound branch - 'sort Pair2 { sort A = ?; sort B = ?; entity onlyA(fst: A) }' applied 200 times, so B is left unbound by the field unification - and it added ZERO calls. The three \"?_\" calls come from fresh_type_var, not from the reconstruction tail this ticket names.\n\nSo the ticket is RIGHT about where the code sits (the tail is per constructor node) and WRONG about how often it runs. Caching the TypeExtractor.TypeVar symbol would save ~6 hash probes inside a 440ms load - about one part in a million - in exchange for a KnowledgeBase field that has to be kept correct. Not worth doing as specified. This is the same trap WI-881 recorded: a code path being on a hot line is not evidence that it executes.\n\nTWO THINGS FOUND WHILE MEASURING, both worth more than the optimization:\n\n1. THE by-name MEMO ALTERNATIVE IS UNSAFE, and this ticket should not have offered it. TermStore is refcounted with a free_list, and alloc() increments the refcount even on a hash-cons HIT (term.rs, the doc on alloc says so explicitly). So a memoized TermId can dangle after a free, and returning a cached id WITHOUT an incref would over-decrement every caller that later drops it. Only the symbol-cache half was ever safe - and that half is the one the measurement says is pointless.\n\n2. THE PREMISE THIS TICKET SHOULD HAVE QUESTIONED IS THE RETURN TYPE. make_type_var returns a TermId, so every transient inference wildcard is hash-consed AND refcounted into the PERSISTENT term store. CLAUDE.md's representation note reserves hash-consing for persistent, heavily-shared structure and says transient terms (query patterns, occurrence twins) are deliberately NOT interned - they ride as Value::Node/Entity and match structurally. A \"?_\" standing for an under-determined type is transient by definition. MEASURED at the call sites: 8 of 11 production callers immediately write Value::term(kb.make_type_var(fresh)) - the TermId is incidental there. Only three want a term: TypeChild::Ground (typing.rs:2059) and the two in reconstruct_sort_params, which feed make_parameterized_type building a hash-consed type spine. That is a REPRESENTATION question, not a performance one, and it is what made the memo unsafe in the first place.\n\nSOURCE of the correction: user asked 'why we use TermId in WI-960?' - the same question that dissolved this ticket's framing.", at: "2026-08-03T12:23:34Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-960", description: some(value: "make_type_var re-resolves a 41-char qualified name per unbound type param, per constructor node.\n\nreconstruct_sort_params (rustland/anthill-core/src/kb/typing.rs) mints a fresh `?_` wildcard for every param the field unification left UNBOUND (WI-384 - keep the sort's full param arity, never drop): `kb.intern(\"?_\")` then `kb.make_type_var(name)`. Both constructor-typing paths reach it - the occurrence typer (check_constructor_iter) and the value typer (constructor_value_type) - so it is per constructor node. That is the same path WI-657(7) turned into an O(1) index read precisely because per-node work there is measurable.\n\nVERIFIED, make_type_var (kb/mod.rs:6200):\n  resolve_symbol(\"anthill.prelude.TypeExtractor.TypeVar\")   <- hash probe over a 41-char qualified name\n  intern(\"name\") + alloc(Term::Ref(name)) + make_entity_term  <- and a hash-cons\n\nIts result is fully determined by `name`, and every call from this site passes the same `\"?_\"`, so the whole call is one constant TermId there. Nothing about it is per-node.\n\nPRE-EXISTING, not introduced by WI-955 - both of the pre-WI-955 branches did it identically and the fix only moved it into the shared tail. Surfaced by WI-955's /simplify efficiency pass, which measured it as materially more per-call work than the by_parent lookup that same change optimized.\n\nSCOPE: cache the TypeExtractor.TypeVar symbol on the KB the way op_records / sort_alias_index / sort_info_index already cache their lookups, or memoize make_type_var by name. Check the OTHER make_type_var callers first - if they mostly pass a varying name, the symbol cache is the win and the by-name memo is not.\n\nACCEPTANCE: driven - assert the returned TermId is unchanged (it is hash-consed, so a wrong cache shows up as a different term), plus a stated before/after measurement of a stdlib type-check. Say at the test site which assertion fails when the cache is backed out. cargo-test green.\n\nSOURCE: WI-955 /simplify efficiency review."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Rejected(reason: "Premise refuted by measurement, before implementing. make_type_var is called 6 times in a full stdlib + host-bindings load and type-check (3x \"?_\", 2x \"?param\", 1x \"?logical_var\"), and a 200x driver built specifically to force reconstruct_sort_params' unbound-param branch added ZERO further calls. Caching the TypeExtractor.TypeVar symbol would save ~6 hash probes inside a ~440ms load - one part in a million - for a KnowledgeBase field that must be kept correct. The ticket is right about where the code sits (per constructor node) and wrong about how often it runs. The by-name memo it also offered was never safe: TermStore is refcounted with a free_list and alloc increments even on a hash-cons HIT, so a cached TermId can dangle and returning one without an incref over-decrements every caller. See the feedback entry for the full numbers and for the representation question this should have asked instead (make_type_var returns a TermId, so transient inference wildcards are hash-consed into the PERSISTENT store; 8 of 11 production callers immediately wrap it in Value::term).", at: "2026-08-03T12:27:13Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-963", author: "user", content: "ANSWERED BEFORE STARTING - and the answer TILTS AGAINST this ticket's own premise. USER asked: 'why make_type_var returns TermId and why it create term, why Var is not enough?'\n\nA BARE Var IS NOT AN UNKNOWN TYPE, IT IS A MALFORMED ONE. type_head (typing.rs) reads head.functor_sym(); ViewHead::Var(_) returns None (term_view.rs:72), so a bare Var in type position classifies as TypeHead::Error. The type language is a DECLARED vocabulary - stdlib/anthill/prelude/sort.anthill:309 declares 'entity TypeVar(name: Symbol)' beside SortRef / Parameterized / the arrow - and type_head dispatches on the functor's QUALIFIED NAME. Var is simply not in that vocabulary. Types are terms in it so anthill code can read them through reflect; that is the design, not an accident of the API.\n\nTHE SEMANTICS DIFFER, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS LOAD-BEARING. Var::Global is a LOGIC variable: unification BINDS it, and the discrim tree treats a flex Global as a wildcard var-edge matching any subterm. type_var(name: \"?_\") is INERT - compatible-with-anything in the unify/subtype dispatch WITHOUT committing. That is fresh_type_var's documented M6 flounder posture. With a logic var the first unification turns 'I do not know this type' into 'it is now Int64' and propagates that commitment to every occurrence of the same VarId. WI-384's comment already says it at the site: 'a type_var WILDCARD (not a bare logic Var)'.\n\nIDENTITY, the point this ticket got backwards: type_var hash-conses per NAME, so every \"?_\" is ONE shared term - ~7 in the whole store across all 7 literal names. A Var::Global carries a unique VarId, so two 'unknown's would be structurally UNEQUAL and every site would need a fresh_var allocation. Per-name interning of a declared vocabulary member is NOMINAL IDENTITY, which CLAUDE.md's representation note lists as exactly what SHOULD be hash-consed - not the 'transient query pattern' this ticket compared it to. The 8 Value::term(...) call sites are not evidence of a wrong carrier; they are callers of a term-producing builder whose product legitimately IS a term.\n\nCONSEQUENCE: option (b) (a Value-returning twin) is NOT indicated - it would hand callers a carrier that type_head cannot classify unless it re-wraps the same entity term, i.e. the same term by another road. Option (a) is the answer: leave the representation, and WRITE THE REASON AT make_type_var so the next reader stops re-deriving it. Narrowing this ticket to that.", at: "2026-08-03T12:29:51Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-963", description: some(value: "make_type_var interns a TRANSIENT inference wildcard into the PERSISTENT term store - a representation question, not a performance one.\n\nNOT A PERFORMANCE TICKET. Its predecessor WI-960 was, and was REJECTED on measurement: make_type_var runs 6 times in a full stdlib + host-bindings load (see WI-960's feedback). Do not reopen that framing; the question here is where these terms should LIVE, and it is worth answering at zero calls per second.\n\nTHE SHAPE. `make_type_var(name) -> TermId` (kb/mod.rs) builds `type_var(name: <sym>)` through make_entity_term, so it is HASH-CONSED and REFCOUNTED into TermStore. CLAUDE.md's representation note reserves hash-consing for PERSISTENT, HEAVILY-SHARED structure - asserted facts, rule heads, nominal sort identities - and states that transient terms (query patterns, occurrence-derived twins) are deliberately NOT interned: they ride as Value::Node/Entity and match structurally, since the discrimination tree keys on DiscrimKey, never on TermId identity. A `?_` standing for an under-determined type is transient by definition - WI-578's own doc calls it 'the TOTAL fallback for an under-determined type'.\n\nMEASURED at the call sites (11 production, 2 test):\n  8 of 11 immediately write `Value::term(kb.make_type_var(fresh))` - the TermId is incidental, an artifact of the return type.\n  3 genuinely want a term: TypeChild::Ground (typing.rs:2059), and the two in reconstruct_sort_params (typing.rs), whose results feed make_parameterized_type building a hash-consed type SPINE.\n  Every caller passes a CONSTANT literal name - \"?_\", \"?T\", \"?param\", \"?result\", \"?pat\", \"?logical_var\", \"?ungrounded\" - so there are ~7 distinct wildcard terms in the store, not one per inference site. That bounds the leak: this is not unbounded growth, which is exactly why it is a design question and not a bug.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS BEYOND TIDINESS: the return type is what made WI-960's memo alternative UNSAFE. TermStore is refcounted with a free_list and alloc() increments even on a hash-cons HIT, so a cached TermId can dangle and one returned without an incref over-decrements every caller. Any future caching, sharing, or cross-phase reuse of these wildcards inherits that hazard purely from the carrier choice.\n\nTHE DECISION TO MAKE, not a prescription: (a) leave it - the ~7 interned wildcards are cheap and the 3 term-wanting callers need the TermId anyway, in which case WRITE THAT REASON at make_type_var so the next reader stops re-deriving it; or (b) give the 8 Value-wrapping callers a Value-returning twin and keep the TermId form for the 3 spine builders, which needs a check that a Value-carried type var unifies and subtypes identically to the interned one (WI-342/348 carrier-neutrality); or (c) something else the reader finds. Option (b) is only worth it if the carrier-neutral path is already total for type vars - CHECK before choosing, do not assume.\n\nACCEPTANCE: driven, whichever option wins. If (a): the reason is at the site and a test pins that the 3 term-wanting callers actually need a TermId (make_parameterized_type's spine), so a later reader cannot delete the justification as speculation. If (b): a test that the SAME under-determined type unifies/subtypes identically through both carriers - assert the verdict, not that it loads - plus the control naming which assertion fails if the Value form is routed through a path that re-interns. cargo-test green.\n\nSOURCE: user question during WI-960 ('why we use TermId in WI-960?')."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-03T12:43:45Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-963", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED as option (a) - the representation STAYS, the reason is at the site, and three tests drive it so it cannot be deleted as speculation.\n\nWHY (a) AND NOT (b): established by driving, not argued. A bare Var in type position has no functor, so type_head reads ViewHead::functor_sym() = None and classifies it TypeHead::Error - it is not an UNKNOWN type, it is a MALFORMED one. A Value-returning twin would therefore have to re-wrap the same TypeExtractor.TypeVar entity to be classifiable at all: the same term by another road. This ticket's own framing (a transient wildcard mis-filed in the persistent store) was wrong in both directions - see the earlier feedback entry.\n\nDOC at KnowledgeBase::make_type_var (kb/mod.rs) records four claims and points at the tests.\n\nTESTS - typing::wi961_type_var_representation_tests, 3 tests, all measured before being written:\n  a_bare_logic_var_in_type_position_is_not_a_type_variable\n      type_head(type_var) = TypeVar(\"?_\");  type_head(Var) = Error.\n      Opposite verdicts on two terms built in the SAME test, so neither is vacuous.\n  a_type_var_never_commits_where_a_logic_var_does   <- the load-bearing one\n      type_var vs Int64 -> true, 0 bindings; then vs String in the SAME subst -> true, 0 bindings.\n      logic Var vs Int64 -> true, 1 binding;  then vs String in the SAME subst -> FALSE.\n      That is the M6 flounder posture driven: compatible-with-anything WITHOUT committing.\n      The logic var has silently turned 'undetermined' into 'it is Int64' and now refuses String.\n  a_type_vars_identity_is_its_name\n      same name -> same TermId; different name -> different; two same-named logic vars -> DISTINCT.\n      Per-name interning is NOMINAL IDENTITY (what CLAUDE.md reserves hash-consing for), not a per-site transient.\n\nCONTROL, and it came out STRONGER than I first wrote - the comment was corrected to the measured result rather than the predicted one. I had claimed a backout would flip the assertions' values. It does not get that far: making make_type_var return a bare Var::Global STOPS THE STDLIB FROM LOADING, with exactly the commitment the test describes -\n  62:39: type mismatch in match.rule: expected Option[T = Pair[A = ?T, B = MappedStream]], got Option[T = Pair[A = ??_, B = MappedStream]]\n  211:26: same shape in list.anthill\nSo the representation is load-bearing for real stdlib code, not only for the synthetic unifications here; under that backout these tests fail at their FIXTURE, not their assertions, and the test doc says so - the assertions exist to name WHY, which a load error does not.\n\ncargo-test: 4074 passed, 0 failed (4071 + these 3).\n\nNOT DONE, deliberately: no change to the 8 Value::term(make_type_var(..)) call sites. Wrapping is what those callers want and the term is what type_head needs; there is nothing to fix there, which is the answer this ticket was filed to find.", at: "2026-08-03T12:43:48Z")
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-964", name: "scaland")
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-965", name: "scaland")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-961", description: some(value: "SCALAND: A MISSED `allocAt` SILENTLY REINTRODUCES A LOCATIONLESS DIAGNOSTIC, and nothing fails when it does. WI-957 gave `SimpleTermStore` a TermId->Span table and located every name-bearing allocation site that a resolution path reaches. The COVERAGE IS CONVENTIONAL, NOT ENFORCED: `alloc` and `allocAt` are both public and equally easy to call, so the next parser production that mints a resolvable functor — a new desugar, a new literal form — compiles clean with `alloc`, and the only symptom is an `AmbiguousSymbol` (or a future located diagnostic) that renders with no position. That is exactly the WI-947/957 bug, silently restored. WHY THE LINE IS EASY TO GET WRONG, from WI-957's own review: 'the functor looks synthetic' is NOT the criterion. `Loader.reallocTerm` resolves the functor of EVERY `Term.Fn` it walks, so `unify`, `ho_apply`, `ListLiteral`, `SetLiteral` and `TupleLiteral` — all pure desugar markers — reached `resolveName` and reported locationless; WI-957's first draft cited `ListLiteral` as an example of a SAFE marker and was wrong. Only the markers `convertExprTerm` dispatches on BY NAME (`if_expr`, `pattern_var`, ...) and the `TypeExtractor.*` type lowerings are genuinely shielded, and that shielding is an emergent consequence of two unrelated pattern-matches, not a stated invariant. OPTIONS, roughly increasing depth: (a) a test-only assertion that no `LoadError` carrying a resolvable NAME is ever raised with `Span.empty`, run over the whole embedded stdlib load — cheap, catches a regression the moment any stdlib construct trips it, but blind to surface forms the stdlib does not use; (b) make the distinction structural — a distinct constructor (`allocSynthetic`) whose name states the claim, plus a narrowed `alloc` that REFUSES a `Term.Fn`/`Ref`/`Ident` whose functor is not in an explicit shielded set, so a new name-bearing marker cannot reach the store without a decision; (c) carry the shielded set in ONE place that both `convertExprTerm`'s dispatch and the store consult, so 'this marker never reaches resolution' stops being two facts that happen to agree. ACCEPTANCE: introduce a NEW parser production that mints a resolvable functor via `alloc`, and show the chosen mechanism fails — a green suite with that production present is the thing this ticket exists to prevent. NOTE the enforcement site comments in `IR.scala` (`spans`) and `Loader.resolveName` already state the invariant this would enforce."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "scaland-sbt-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-03T13:21:37Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-959", description: some(value: "Two typing.rs test modules still hand-roll the stdlib-loading fixture, and their walk is the one WI-747 fixed.\n\nWI-955 lifted `#[cfg(test)] mod stdlib_fixture { load_stdlib(extra: Option<&str>) }` in rustland/anthill-core/src/kb/typing.rs at the second caller, and routed wi802_function_spec_owner_tests plus the new wi955_one_alias_keying_tests through it. Two older copies remain, each with its own directory walk:\n  wi341_alpha_tests::load_ops    typing.rs ~40000\n  wi464_variance_join_meet_tests::load_kb  typing.rs ~40115\n\nBoth hand-roll a recursive `collect()` instead of crate::fs_util::collect_files. That is the exact pattern WI-747 replaced elsewhere, and the reason is written on the lifted fixture: a hand-rolled walk returns an EMPTY list on an unreadable/missing dir, so the module's headline test then fails as 'the stdlib declares no <X>' - blaming the subject for a bad path. The lifted copy fails LOUD with the path instead.\n\nSCOPE: route both through stdlib_fixture::load_stdlib and delete the local walks. Check the shapes first - load_ops may want something load_stdlib does not return (it is named for ops, not a KB), so the fixture may need one more accessor rather than a swap.\n\nACCEPTANCE: no directory walk left in typing.rs's #[cfg(test)] modules outside stdlib_fixture (grep is the check). cargo-test green.\n\nSOURCE: WI-955 /simplify reuse review."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-03T14:50:13Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-959", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED, with scope expanded on user direction mid-session.\n\nWHAT THE TICKET ASKED: route wi341_alpha_tests::load_ops and wi464_variance_join_meet_tests::load_kb through typing.rs's stdlib_fixture and delete their hand-rolled walks. Done, then the USER asked 'why it in typing, not in test?!' and directed the full lift.\n\nTHE ANSWER TO THAT QUESTION, both halves. It CANNOT live in anthill-core/tests/common: that is a separate crate and sees only pub items, while these tests drive crate-private ones (typing::join_types and ::meet_types are bare fn, ::is_function_spec likewise, and region is a pub(crate) mod). But 'in src/' is not 'in typing.rs' - THREE kb/ modules hand-rolled the same sequence. New crate-internal kb/test_support.rs (#[cfg(test)] pub(crate) mod) with load_stdlib / load_stdlib_and_stl; typing.rs's mod stdlib_fixture deleted (61 lines, 5 modules repointed), region.rs and op_requirements.rs converted. op_requirements' workspace_root() cwd-walk replaced by CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR - a compile-time constant, cwd-independent.\n\nTHE REAL FINDING, and it is bigger than the ticket's. The ticket framed the hand-rolled walks as a MISDIAGNOSIS risk (empty list on a bad dir -> 'the stdlib declares no X'). Driving it found something worse: load_ops also ran `let _ = load::load_all(..)`. MEASURED, same broken fixture (an unresolved import), two code versions - old: the test PASSES; new: fails with '3:39: unresolved import'. Not a worse message - NO GUARD AT ALL. wi341's two tests would have stayed green over a half-loaded KB.\n\nUSER'S THREE QUESTIONS, each found a defect I had shipped or was about to:\n1. 'Why we receive and silently throw-out Err?' -> the survey. 30 matches, of which one legitimate use (a fixture DELIBERATELY carrying an unresolvable shape, documented at anthill-smt-gen/tests/common/mod.rs:34, handled there by a two-function split), and a NAME TRAP: common::load_kb_with PANICS in anthill-core/tests and DISCARDS in anthill-smt-gen/tests. -> WI-966.\n2. 'Why we need a load a broken stdlib?!' -> I had added a should_panic test that loaded the entire stdlib to assert a panic about a FIXTURE. Removed. It also exposed that my panic message said 'stdlib load errors' when the fixture is the likely culprit - a false diagnostic, the exact misdiagnosis the WI-747 comment three lines above claims to prevent. Now 'load errors (library + fixture)', with the reason at the site.\n3. 'Why we use invalid fixtures at all?!' -> the decisive one. Flipping BOTH remaining `let _` to strict: op_requirements' fixture loads CLEAN (6 passed - the discard bought nothing). region.rs's failed on ONE thing: its own fixture spelled the unit value `unit`.\n\nTHE unit SPELLING DOES NOT EXIST. stdlib/anthill/prelude/unit.anthill said '`unit` is a built-in literal constant' and 'Requires: grammar keyword `unit`, Rust `Literal::Unit` variant'. VERIFIED: grammar.js has no unit keyword (the only spelling is `()`, an empty tuple_literal, line 1321) and there is no Literal enum in parse/ir.rs at all. The SPEC already settles it - kernel-language.md section 4.5, `TupleLiteral ::= '(' ')'  -- unit value`. So this was not unimplemented work, it was a comment CONTRADICTING the spec, and region's fixture was written against it. Fixture fixed (`unit` -> `()`), comment corrected to state the `()` spelling and that no bare keyword exists. BLAST RADIUS CHECKED: no other .anthill or inline .rs fixture uses it (the `unit(42)` hits in wi453_hk_concrete_fill_test are calls to the monadic unit OPERATION, unrelated).\n\nCONSEQUENCE: there is NO lenient entry point in the new module, because measurement showed nothing needs one. `let _ = .. load_all(..)` is now absent from all of anthill-core/src/ (verified 0).\n\n/simplify (four angles run inline, not via agents):\n- EFFICIENCY, fixed: 37 fixture calls each re-walked and re-parsed the whole stdlib. Now LazyLock<Vec<ParsedFile>> per library, the shape anthill-smt-gen/tests/common:22 already uses. MEASURED over the 29 tests in the fixture-bearing modules: 22.0s -> 19.1s user CPU (~13%). Smaller than expected - the KB build dominates, not the parse - and the doc comment SAYS SO rather than overclaiming (my first draft claimed ~0.75s/test, which the measurement refuted).\n- REUSE, none available: verified no stdlib-path helper exists in src/, and tests/common is unreachable. Stated in the module header so the next reader does not re-derive it.\n- SIMPLIFICATION / ALTITUDE: clean.\n- SKIPPED, filed as WI-967: every fixture calls register_prelude + register_standard_builtins, and load_all calls register_prelude itself (4196), which calls register_standard_builtins itself (3547) - both done twice, at every fixture in the workspace. Changing one fixture while the rest keep the pattern is how the next reader concludes the fixture is the odd one out.\n\nTESTS: no #[test] added or removed (verified on the diff), so the count is the same set as HEAD. Full workspace 4074 passed, 0 failed. Net -173 lines across 4 files, +97 in the new module.\n\nCONTROL, stated honestly: this is a refactor, so the existing tests pass both before and after BY DESIGN - they are not the evidence. The evidence is the two flip-to-strict measurements above, each of which changed a result: wi341 green->red on a broken fixture, and region 4-passed->4-failed revealing the `unit` defect.\n\nNOT COMMITTED and /code-review NOT RUN (it is user-triggered; CLAUDE.md gates commit on it).\n\nFOLLOW-UPS FILED: WI-966 (24 remaining `let _ = load_all` sites + the load_kb_with name collision), WI-967 (double registration of prelude/builtins).\n\nNOTE ON NUMBERING: filed first as WI-964/965, which COLLIDED with two scaland tickets pushed from WI-961's session while this work was uncommitted. Re-filed as WI-966/967 against the updated tracker; if any text elsewhere cites WI-964/965 for these, it means this.", at: "2026-08-03T14:50:14Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-967", description: some(value: "Every KB-building fixture calls register_prelude and register_standard_builtins, and load_all does both again.\n\nVERIFIED in load.rs:\n  load_all (4191)          -> calls register_prelude(kb) itself, line 4196\n  register_prelude (3531)  -> calls kb.register_standard_builtins() itself, line 3547\n\nSo the house sequence\n\n    let mut kb = KnowledgeBase::new();\n    load::register_prelude(&mut kb);\n    kb.register_standard_builtins();\n    load::load_all(&mut kb, &refs, &resolver)\n\ndoes register_prelude twice and register_standard_builtins twice. It is written that way at every fixture in the workspace - anthill-core/tests/common, anthill-smt-gen/tests/common, anthill-cpp-gen/tests/common, the new anthill-core/src/kb/test_support.rs, and the ~24 sites WI-964 covers.\n\nNOT A BUG TODAY. register_prelude documents itself idempotent ('Idempotent: skipped on re-entry or when stdlib has already been scanned', via register_stdlib_scopes), and the suite is green, so nothing observable depends on the second pass. WI-959 deliberately did NOT change it: dropping two lines from one fixture while every other fixture keeps them is how the next reader concludes the fixture is the odd one out.\n\nTHE QUESTION IS WHICH DIRECTION IS RIGHT, and it is a real fork:\n  (a) The caller's two lines are redundant - delete them everywhere and let load_all own the setup. Cheapest, but only sound if NO caller depends on prelude symbols existing BEFORE load_all runs (e.g. a fixture that interns or resolves a name between construction and load).\n  (b) load_all should NOT silently re-register - a function that mutates setup its caller already performed is the thing that made (a) hard to see. Then load_all takes an already-prepared KB and the callers keep their lines.\nAnswer it by MEASUREMENT: delete the caller lines in one fixture, run; then make load_all's internal register_prelude a panic-if-already-registered, run. Each tells you something different about who actually depends on what.\n\nWATCH FOR: load_incremental (load.rs:4216) already skips register_prelude deliberately, so whatever rule is chosen must keep the incremental path coherent with it - that asymmetry is the existing evidence about intended ownership and should be read before deciding.\n\nACCEPTANCE: one stated owner of prelude/builtin registration, applied uniformly, with the reason written where the ownership lives; load_incremental's asymmetry explained or removed; cargo-test green.\n\nSOURCE: WI-959 /simplify review (skipped there as out of diff scope)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-03T15:37:38Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-967", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. The ticket's fork was answered (a) BY MEASUREMENT, and both prescribed probes were run.\n\nPROBE 1 (the ticket's 'make load_all's register_prelude a panic'): inverted to the informative direction - assert at load/load_all/load_all_per_file that the KB was ALREADY bootstrapped, then count instead of panic. Full-suite tally over 3970 arrivals: 3476 already bootstrapped, 418 BARE.\n\nTHAT REFUTES OPTION (b). 'load_all should not silently re-register; callers keep their lines' would require ADDING registration to ~418 call paths that today rely on load_all to bootstrap (anthill-cli, anthill-todo, generated bundles). The ticket framed (b) as the principled option; the measurement makes it the one that multiplies the duplication.\n\nPROBE 2 (the ticket's 'delete the caller lines in one fixture'): generalized - classified all 271 registering functions by (calls register_prelude, calls the KB's register_standard_builtins, calls a load entry point):\n  (rp, rsb, load) 190 - both caller lines redundant\n  (rp,  - , load)  22 - register_prelude redundant\n  (rp, rsb,  -  )  28 - rsb redundant, register_prelude correct (hand-built KB)\n  (rp,  - ,  -  )  31 - correct as written\n  ZERO functions call the KB's register_standard_builtins without also calling register_prelude or loading. So it has no legitimate caller-side use AT ALL - a stronger result than the ticket assumed, and the one that made the rule clean.\n\nTHE RULE, stated at register_prelude: it IS the bootstrap; every load entry point calls it; a caller that loads must NOT pre-register; a caller that never loads calls it itself. Deleted 218 register_standard_builtins + 222 register_prelude lines across 172 files. 60 register_prelude calls remain: 3 load entry points + 57 hand-built KBs.\n\nA THIRD OPTION THE TICKET DID NOT LIST, measured and REJECTED: bootstrap in KnowledgeBase::new(). It makes every arrival pre-bootstrapped (3911/3911) and would delete all 60 remaining calls, but it fails 25 tests - 22 because eq_functor/unify_functor fall back to a bare intern(\"eq\") for 'kernel-less unit KBs' and those tests build their [simp] equations on that fallback, 2 on whole-KB counters, 1 (WI-337) existing to pin the bare configuration itself. So a bare KB is a SUPPORTED configuration, not an illegal state to design out. Written down at register_prelude so the next reader does not re-run this. Deferred as WI-969.\n\nload_incremental's ASYMMETRY: REMOVED, not explained. It was load_phase(..) where load_all is register_prelude(..) + load_phase(..) - identical but for that one line. Skipping was never protective (register_prelude is idempotent; measured 3476 re-entries green) and the cost was real: nothing enforced 'second phase only', and reaching it FIRST did not degrade, it PANICKED mid-load - 'resolve_symbol: anthill.reflect.Expr.match_expr is not a resolved symbol'. Now an alias of load_all, like load_stdlib already was.\n\nCONTROL, honestly stated. This is a refactor over an IDEMPOTENT function, so the 4074 existing tests pass both before and after BY DESIGN - they are not the evidence, and the new test's doc-comment says so at its site. The evidence is: (1) the two probe tallies above, each of which changed a result; (2) ONE new test, incremental_load_test::load_incremental_bootstraps_a_fresh_kb, which drives load_incremental as the FIRST call into a fresh KB and asserts both halves of bootstrap (Int64 resolves; PartialEq.eq carries a builtin tag) plus the loaded fact being indexed. VERIFIED to fail when load_incremental is reverted to load_phase - the other 4 tests in that file pass either way, because they all call load_stdlib first.\n\n/simplify (four angles inline, not via agents - Agent calls are disabled this session; WI-959 set the precedent):\n- ALTITUDE, applied, and the best finding: after the deletions KnowledgeBase::register_standard_builtins had exactly ONE caller, in-crate. Narrowed pub -> pub(crate). Being pub is what let the redundant line reach 172 files; this makes the whole class unrepresentable outside the crate instead of merely documented.\n- SIMPLIFICATION, applied to MY OWN prose: the pre-existing lead-in on register_prelude said 'Call this before scan_definitions / load', which my new rule 1 directly CONTRADICTS. Rewritten. Also said 'in two ways' over three bullets.\n- DOC ROT caught inside this commit: rustland/CLAUDE.md's 'Test Patterns' section PRESCRIBED the deleted house sequence ('Parse + register_prelude + register_standard_builtins + load_all') - it would have re-seeded the defect. Fixed, plus examples/github-todo/building.md (two places) and emit_effects_runtime_bridge_fact's idempotency note, which justified the guard by citing the very pattern this ticket deleted. The three remaining .md hits (proposals 033/028, design/058) were CHECKED and are accurate - two of them describe the interpreter's same-named function, not the KB's.\n- REUSE / EFFICIENCY: nothing. No perf claim is made: the double registration was ~110 hash lookups per load, and I did not measure a controlled before/after, so I am not claiming the deletion made anything faster.\n\nTESTS: 4075 passed, 0 failed, 0 target failures (baseline 4074 + the 1 new test; verified on the diff that exactly one #[test] was added and none removed). Net -300 lines across 176 files. Build clean - the only new warning was one unused 'register_prelude' import, fixed.\n\nFOLLOW-UPS FILED: WI-968 (two unrelated functions both named register_standard_builtins, one imported BARE in type_extract_test.rs; plus a stale load.rs comment that names neither), WI-969 (the deferred bare-KB decision and the eq_functor/unify_functor fallbacks it keeps alive).\n\nNOT COMMITTED, and /code-review NOT RUN - it is user-triggered, and CLAUDE.md gates commit on it.", at: "2026-08-03T15:38:13Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-969", description: some(value: "Decide whether a NEVER-BOOTSTRAPPED KnowledgeBase is a supported configuration, and retire the fallbacks it exists to feed.\n\nWI-967 established that register_prelude is the one owner of bootstrap and that every load entry point calls it. It deliberately did NOT put bootstrap in KnowledgeBase::new(), because a bare KB turned out to be a real, load-bearing configuration. This ticket is that deferred decision.\n\nMEASURED under WI-967 (probe: register_prelude called from KnowledgeBase::new(), full workspace run):\n  - every load-entry arrival becomes pre-bootstrapped: 3911 of 3911.\n  - exactly 25 tests fail, in three groups:\n      22 in kb/resolve.rs + kb/simp_rewrite.rs - the decisive ones. KnowledgeBase::eq_functor / unify_functor resolve the qualified name and FALL BACK to a bare intern(\"eq\") / intern(\"unify\"), documented as 'only for kernel-less unit KBs'. Those tests build their [simp] equations on that fallback (see the comment at resolve.rs, `let eq_sym = kb.intern(\"eq\"); // matches eq_functor()'s bare fallback`). On a bootstrapped KB their rules stop matching, so they stop firing - the failure is a rewrite that silently does not happen, not an error.\n      2 in kb/mod.rs - assert whole-KB counters (fact_count() == 0) that the WI-320 bridge fact breaks. Shallow; these tests should count what they asserted, not the whole KB.\n      1, typing_test::types_compatible_bootstrap_safe_when_prelude_not_registered - WI-337 made the typer's arrow comparison return false instead of panicking on a KB that was never register_prelude'd.\n\nTHE TENSION, and it is a CLAUDE.md one: 'avoid fallbacks, better know about errors early' vs a bare-name fallback that exists solely so unit tests can skip bootstrap. The fallback is not reachable from any real program - every production path loads, and every load bootstraps. So today the kernel carries two spellings of `eq` where only one can occur in production, and the test suite is the only thing that exercises the second.\n\nIF the bare KB is declared unsupported, the following all become dead and removable, which is the actual prize:\n  - eq_functor / unify_functor's unwrap_or_else(|| self.intern(..)) fallbacks\n  - WI-337's try_make_empty_effects_rows bootstrap-safe path (verify: check whether it has any other caller first)\n  - register_prelude's idempotency guards could be reconsidered (NOT removable on this ticket's evidence alone - a KB loaded into twice still re-enters; see the emit_effects_runtime_bridge_fact doc)\nand KnowledgeBase::new() can bootstrap, making 'un-bootstrapped KB' unrepresentable per 'prefer make illegal state unrepresentable'.\n\nCOST: the 22 tests must be rewritten to build equations on the QUALIFIED eq/unify symbol. That is arguably a fix in itself - they currently test a symbol resolution that production never takes.\n\nWATCH FOR: this is NOT a pure refactor. Rewriting a [simp] test onto the qualified functor changes what the test drives. Each of the 22 needs its assertion re-checked against what it was pinning, not just made green.\n\nACCEPTANCE: a stated decision on whether a bare KB is supported, written where the answer lives (KnowledgeBase::new and/or register_prelude, which currently states the WI-967 position); if unsupported, the fallbacks named above are GONE, not merely unused, and each rewritten test says at its site what it now drives; cargo-test green.\n\nSOURCE: WI-967 - measured and deferred there as its own decision, not a refactor detail."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-03T16:24:32Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-969", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. The decision came from the USER, mid-session: 'why we use fallback for bare kb? We should remove fallback, and have one or two smoke tests for kb without prelude.' That reframed the ticket - I had been heading for bootstrap-in-KnowledgeBase::new(), which would have DELETED the bare KB rather than DEFINING it, leaving the smoke tests nothing to pin.\n\nTHE DECISION, written at register_prelude: a bare KB stays constructible and is a MINIMAL configuration, not a second supported mode. Nothing in production holds one (measured under WI-967: KnowledgeBase::new() appears in non-cfg(test) code at only 5 sites - the two CLI entry points, the generated bundle template, and two test-support helpers; there is no KB-per-call path). It exists so crate-internal unit tests can skip the stdlib. Asking it for kernel vocabulary is an ERROR.\n\nTHE LINE THAT DECIDES SUCH CASES, and it is the reusable part: a bare KB may give a DEFINED conservative answer, but it may not INVENT a symbol that silently differs from the canonical one.\n  - eq_functor/unify_functor invented one -> REMOVED, now panic naming register_prelude.\n  - WI-337's try_make_empty_effects_rows returns None and the caller REJECTS - it never claims compatibility it cannot check. KEPT, and it is one of the smoke tests.\nThe ticket had listed try_make_empty_effects_rows for removal. That was wrong on this line, and the user's framing is what exposed it.\n\nWHY THE FALLBACK WAS INDEFENSIBLE: it was unreachable from any real program (every path loads, every load bootstraps), so the kernel carried TWO spellings of the canonical equation head where production can only produce one. Its failure mode was the worst available - a [simp] rule built on the bare spelling simply never matched, so the rewrite silently did not happen and nothing reported why. WI-283 is that bug.\n\nMY OWN TICKET'S COUNT WAS WRONG: it said 22 tests. The real number is 26 (18 resolve.rs, 8 simp_rewrite.rs), plus 2 integration tests in wi523_unify_test.rs the ticket did not mention at all. The 22 came from the constructor-bootstrap probe - a DIFFERENT measurement. Removing the fallback and removing the bare KB do not break the same set, and I had conflated them.\n\nTHE 26 + 2 were not merely greened. Each now calls register_prelude and kb.eq_functor()/unify_functor(), so they drive the head production actually uses instead of one only they could produce. The ticket's WATCH FOR asked for each assertion to be re-checked; the check that makes that honest: the POSITIVE tests (simplify_constant_equation, apply_eq_rules_returns_changes) assert a rewrite HAPPENS and pass, so [simp] firing demonstrably works in the new setup - which is what makes the NEGATIVE tests (simplify_no_match_passthrough and friends, asserting no rewrite) a real non-match rather than a dead machine.\n\nSMOKE TESTS for a KB without prelude, as asked - two, plus a control:\n  kb::tests::bare_kb_eq_functor_is_loud        (#[should_panic])\n  kb::tests::bare_kb_unify_functor_is_loud     (#[should_panic])\n  kb::tests::bootstrapped_kb_resolves_the_canonical_equality_head - the CONTROL, without which the two above would also pass if the accessors panicked unconditionally. It also asserts eq_functor() != intern(\"eq\"), the distinction the fallback erased.\nPlus the pre-existing typing_test::types_compatible_bootstrap_safe_when_prelude_not_registered, kept.\n\nCONTROL, verified by backing the change out: restoring the unwrap_or_else makes bare_kb_eq_functor_is_loud FAIL with 'test did not panic as expected'. The other two pass either way by design, and the test-site comment says so.\n\nONE FALLBACK DELIBERATELY LEFT, and it is the more dangerous one: is_equality_connective_functor's SHORT-NAME arm, reached whenever the connective cache is empty. It is the exact Map.eq/Set.eq mis-classification WI-627 fixed, and cache_connective_syms' own comment already says so. MEASURED by panicking the arm over the full workspace: 61 distinct tests reach it, ALL crate-internal bare-KB unit tests, zero production reach. Left because 61 tests is a change of its own, not because it is defensible - filed as WI-970 with the measurement and the likely answer (return false). Documented at the site so it is not mistaken for settled.\n\n/simplify (four angles inline; Agent calls disabled this session):\n- REUSE, applied: I had repeated a 2-line bootstrap pattern 26 times. typing.rs already establishes a kb_with_prelude() convention 4x, so I added one per test module and collapsed 32 sites. It also makes each test's CONFIGURATION visible in its first line - kb_with_prelude() vs a deliberate bare KnowledgeBase::new().\n- SIMPLIFICATION: removed two now-false doc claims of the pre-existing kind - is_equality_connective_functor's 'which mint a bare fallback', and simp_equation_rids' 'deduped when the two functors coincide, e.g. a prelude-less unit KB' (they never coincide; distinct qualified names are distinct symbols, so that was already false before this ticket).\n- Also fixed the WI-967 doc block I wrote in the PREVIOUS commit, which stated the opposite decision ('a bare KB is a SUPPORTED configuration') and justified it BY the fallback this ticket deletes, and a stale simp_rewrite comment.\n- EFFICIENCY / ALTITUDE: clean. The fix is at the accessor (one source) rather than at call sites, and no perf claim is made.\n\nTESTS: 4078 passed, 0 failed, 0 target failures (4075 after WI-967, + 3 smoke tests). No new build warnings (51 -> 50).\n\nNOT COMMITTED, and /code-review NOT RUN - user-triggered; CLAUDE.md gates commit on it.", at: "2026-08-03T16:25:09Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-969", author: "user", content: "SCOPE CORRECTION, on USER challenge: 'why we need follow-up ticket?'\n\nThe feedback above described leaving is_equality_connective_functor's SHORT-NAME arm in place and filing WI-970 for it. That was WRONG on two counts, and the user's one-line question found both.\n\n1. IT WAS THIS TICKET'S OWN SCOPE. WI-969's title is 'retire the fallbacks it exists to feed' - plural. The short-name arm is exactly such a fallback. Spinning it out is the anti-pattern: an OPEN ticket's own scope belongs to that ticket, and splitting would have shipped a half-state with the MORE dangerous fallback still in and a doc explaining why.\n\n2. MY JUSTIFICATION WAS A NUMBER THAT MEASURED THE WRONG THING. I wrote '61 tests is a change of its own'. 61 was the count of tests that REACH the arm (from the panic-the-branch liveness probe). The blast radius of actually changing it is a different number, and I never measured it. When I did - arm returns false, full workspace - exactly ONE test failed: kb::resolve::tests::is_equation_true. Reach is not blast radius. This is the SECOND time in this ticket I quoted a count from the wrong probe (the description's '22' was from the constructor-bootstrap probe; the real fallback-removal count was 26+2).\n\nDONE INSTEAD: the arm is gone. An uncached connective answers false - a KB with no canonical equality connective has none. WI-970 deleted.\n\nis_equation_true fixed to use kb_with_prelude() + kb.eq_functor(). Its NEGATIVE peer is_equation_false_for_rule was fixed WITH it, though it passed either way: it pins 'an eq-headed rule WITH A BODY is not an equation', and on a bare KB with a short-named head it would have passed because the head was not the connective at all - measuring nothing about the body. The pair is only a contrast if both carry the canonical head.\n\nCONTROL for this half, which did not exist until I looked for it: after the fix, NO test failed when the arm was restored - is_equation_true now uses the canonical head and passes either way. Added kb::tests::bare_kb_does_not_classify_a_short_named_eq_as_the_connective: a bare KB, an -NAMED head, assert NOT an equation. VERIFIED to fail ('a bare -NAMED head is not the canonical equality connective') when the arm is put back. That is the WI-627 Map.eq/Set.eq mis-classification, now pinned.\n\nFOLLOW-ON SIMPLIFICATIONS the removal enabled:\n- is_equality_connective_functor collapsed from a closure over a short name to two Symbol comparisons; nothing reads a name any more.\n- resolve.rs's kb_with_builtins() had degenerated (WI-967 removed its register_standard_builtins call, this ticket's helper removed the rest) into an alias of kb_with_prelude(), and its name was misleading post-WI-967 since builtins come FROM the prelude. Deleted; 30 call sites repointed.\n\nFINAL: 4079 passed, 0 failed, 0 target failures. Warnings back to the 50-line baseline (the helper collapse had introduced one). No follow-up tickets - nothing survives.", at: "2026-08-03T16:52:24Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-969", author: "user", content: "CORRECTION to the paragraph above (shell mangled it): the added control test is\nkb::tests::bare_kb_does_not_classify_a_short_named_eq_as_the_connective. It builds a bare KB,\nheads a fact with a symbol NAMED \"eq\" (kb.intern(\"eq\"), not the canonical\nanthill.prelude.PartialEq.eq), and asserts is_equation is FALSE. VERIFIED to fail with\n'a bare `eq`-NAMED head is not the canonical equality connective' when the short-name arm is\nrestored. That mis-classification is the WI-627 bug: a carrier's own `Map.eq` / `Set.eq`\nmerely SHARES the short name and is a normal relational op, so matching it as an equational\nlaw would unindex it (WI-139) and drop it from SLD candidates.", at: "2026-08-03T16:52:48Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-968", description: some(value: "Two different functions are both named `register_standard_builtins`, and one is imported BARE.\n\nMEASURED (WI-967):\n  KnowledgeBase::register_standard_builtins  (kb/mod.rs)          - registers builtin TAGS on the KB. 1 caller: register_prelude. Now pub(crate).\n  eval::builtins::register_standard_builtins (eval/builtins.rs)   - registers host fns on an Interpreter, returns Result. 29 call sites, legitimately re-run per fresh interpreter.\n\nThey do unrelated things to unrelated receivers on unrelated schedules, and nothing in either name says so. WI-967 could only survey the KB one by matching the METHOD-call form `.register_standard_builtins` to separate them mechanically.\n\nWORST CASE, and it is live: anthill-core/tests/include/type_extract_test.rs:5 does `use anthill_core::eval::builtins::register_standard_builtins;` and then calls a BARE `register_standard_builtins(&mut interp)` five times. A reader who knows the KB method reads those as KB bootstrap. eval_test.rs and several others spell it fully qualified, so the codebase is inconsistent about which one a bare name means.\n\nALSO STALE: kb/load.rs's OperationMapping doc-comment says '`register_standard_builtins` is re-run for every FRESH interpreter'. True of the eval one, false of the KB one, and the sentence does not say which - it sits in load.rs, where the KB one lives.\n\nTHE QUESTION: rename one of them. The eval one is arguably the odd one out (it registers host FUNCTIONS, not 'builtins' in the KB's tag sense) - e.g. `register_host_fns` / `register_interpreter_builtins`. Alternatively rename the KB one to `register_builtin_tags`, which is what it actually does and would pair with its `register_builtin` element function.\n\nACCEPTANCE: one name per concept; no bare import of either that could be read as the other; the load.rs OperationMapping comment names which function it means; cargo-test green.\n\nSOURCE: WI-967 (found while establishing the single owner of prelude/builtin registration)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-08-03T17:39:18Z"))
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-970", name: "scaland")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-965", description: some(value: "SCALAND: THE `Index ~ x ~~ Index` + `mkSpan` SPAN-CAPTURE IDIOM IS OPEN-CODED AT ~50 PARSER SITES. FOUND by /simplify on WI-961 (2026-08-03); the WI-947 convention put it at ~35 sites and WI-957/961 added ~15 more. Two combinators would collapse most of them: `private def spanOfToken[$: P](p: => P[Unit]): P[Span] = P(Index ~ p ~~ Index).map { case (s, e) => mkSpan(s, e) }` covers the keyword-led forms (`ifExpr`, `letExpr`, `lambdaExpr`, `matchExpr`, `matchBranch`, `proofStatement`, `effectPresence`, `effectAbsence`, `patternTuple`'s empty case), and `private def located[A, $: P](p: => P[A]): P[(A, Span)] = P(Index ~ p ~~ Index).map { case (s, a, e) => (a, mkSpan(s, e)) }` covers the value-carrying ones (`boundedQuantification`, `typedVarArg`, `typedBinder`, `patternVar`, `patternLiteral`). THE FLATTENING OBJECTION DOES NOT APPLY, and this was CHECKED rather than assumed: the reviewer ran fastparse 3.1.1 (the version scaland depends on) and confirmed that `~`-flattening decomposes only literal `scala.TupleN`s, so a nominal case-class-valued slot — `Span`, like the existing `Name` / `OpToken` / `ProjectionMember` — rides through further `~` composition as ONE opaque element. WHY IT WAS NOT DONE IN WI-961: converting only the new sites would leave two idioms side by side, a net readability LOSS, and converting all ~50 is well outside that WI's scope. So this is all-or-nothing and wants its own change. ACCEPTANCE: every site converted (grep for `Index ~` should leave only genuine multi-token spans), suite green, and — because this is a pure refactor with no behavioural delta — say at the site which EXISTING span tests (`DiagnosticLocationTest`, `DeclarationSpanTest`, `ParseSpanCoverageTest`) are the ones that would catch a mis-conversion, since none of them will change."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "scaland-sbt-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-03T19:13:31Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-966", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. Acceptance met on all three counts; NOT COMMITTED.\n\nTHE TICKET'S COUNTS WERE BOTH OFF, and the difference mattered. \"24 code sites\" is 22 FILES; the site count is 28 (25 in anthill-core/tests/include across 19 files - not all `load_all`: 3 are `load_incremental`, 1 is `load` - plus 2 smt-gen, 1 cpp-gen). Two more `let _ = load::scan_definitions(..)` sites (a different return type, `Vec<LoadError>`, so no grep for `load_all` finds them) were also discarding; MEASURED empty at both, now asserted.\n\nMEASUREMENT FIRST, exactly as the ticket asked: all 28 flipped to panic at once, full workspace run, triage the failures. 22 of 28 loaded clean - the discard bought nothing. The 6 that failed named THREE genuinely broken fixtures plus one deliberate one, all silent for as long as the discard was there:\n- toml_ser_test: `17:72: unresolved name 'List'` carried through all 18 of its tests.\n- specialization_witness_test (2 tests): `B provides A, which requires Eq, but B does not provide Eq`.\n- wi224_sld_resolution_test (2 tests): the same coherence complaint - and here it is DELIBERATE (the file's own comment says so: an unsatisfiable `PartialEq` leg is the subject, recorded as `Unavailable`).\n\nTHE TICKET'S \"ONE LEGITIMATE USE\" WAS IN THE WRONG CRATE. It pointed at anthill-smt-gen/tests/common/mod.rs:34, whose comment states the discard is \"load-bearing for the fixtures that deliberately carry an unresolvable shape\". REFUTED by flipping it: all 100 smt-gen tests pass strict. No such fixture exists there. So the name trap DISSOLVED instead of being renamed around - the discarding `load_kb_with` is deleted and `load_kb_strict` takes the name. `load_kb_with` now means panic-on-load-error in all three test crates, and `load_kb_strict` no longer exists to choose wrongly between. (WI-959's memory recorded the same shape: a comment justifying a silence is evidence about the author, not the code.)\n\nTHE REAL LEGITIMATE USE IS cpp-gen's `load_kb_with_lenient`, which the ticket listed as a site to convert. It is already a NAMED lenient helper, so it meets acceptance as-is - but its 57 callers had never been measured. Flipping the whole crate strict: 53 load clean and now go through `load_kb_with`; 4 stay, each with the measured error at the call site (three `unsupported_test` degrade-to-`static_assert` fixtures and `higher_kinded_arrow_test`'s applied first-order param - all write the error they test). Of the 6 original failures, only 4 were real: TWO WERE PLAIN FIXTURE BUGS whose call-site comments blamed the typer.\n- expr_body_f: comment said \"the typer doesn't yet model raise's `Nothing` return as compatible with `Int64`\". The loader was complaining about something else entirely - `expected declared: [Error], got undeclared effect: Error[T = String]`. A missing type argument.\n- expr_body_c: `operation lam(n: Int64) -> Int64 = lambda x -> add(x, n)`. Declaring the arrow return type it actually has loads clean and lowers identically.\n\nA FOURTH DISCARD CHANNEL THE TICKET DID NOT LIST: cpp-gen's strict `load_kb_with` skipped its own panic when `ANTHILL_TEST_IGNORE_LOAD_ERRORS` was set - an env var nothing in the workspace sets, that would have silently downgraded EVERY test in the crate. Removed; the opt-in is the named helper.\n\ntoml_ser's RESOLVER WAS FICTION TOO. Fixing the missing import surfaced `List` having no type parameter `T`. `FileSourceResolver` maps `anthill.prelude.List` to `anthill/prelude/List.anthill` and the file is `list.anthill`, so every lookup missed and `List` was only ever the bare name `register_prelude` registers. CONTROL: swapping that resolver for `NullResolver` changed nothing - 20 passed / 1 failed either way, same error. Both helpers now load the real stdlib via `common::load_kb_with`, which is what their names always claimed. (The case convention is a live resolver question, not touched here - no ticket filed, since no caller in the workspace depends on it and one would be inventing a requirement.)\n\nWHAT LANDED\n- `common::expect_loaded` - one owner of \"a load error fails the test\", for the suites that build their own file set (custom resolver, incremental second phase, stdlib-only) and so cannot go through `load_kb_with`. All 25 core sites route through it.\n- `common::expect_load_errors` - the deliberate-dirty twin. wi224's two fixtures now PIN their coherence complaint instead of discarding it; a fixture that starts loading CLEAN fails just as loudly as one acquiring a second error.\n- specialization_witness's two fixtures made coherent (they never meant to be incoherent - nothing in the file measures that).\n- smt-gen: one loader. cpp-gen: `load_kb_with_lenient` documents its 4 survivors and why.\n\nCONTROL, stated honestly. This is a strictness change, so the ~4080 existing tests pass with the discards restored BY DESIGN - that property is exactly what let three broken fixtures live this long, and it is why the delivery includes `wi966_loader_verdict_test`. MEASURED: restoring ONE discard (logic_sorts_test) makes it fail and NAME the file and line, while logic_sorts_test's own two tests keep passing. It carries its own recogniser control, since a typo in the matcher would make it pass vacuously over a tree full of discards, plus a floor on the file count for the same reason. The other evidence is the flip-to-strict run itself: 28 sites, 6 result changes, 3 broken fixtures.\n\n/simplify (four angles inline, no agents):\n- EFFICIENCY, fixed: `try_load_kb_with_files` has 683 call sites in anthill-core's suites and re-walked, re-read and re-parsed all 76 stdlib files at every one. Now `LazyLock<Vec<ParsedFile>>`, the shape smt-gen/cpp-gen already use and WI-959 put on the src/ side. MEASURED on wi_tests: 86.1s -> 79.3s wall, 32m33s -> 29m53s user CPU (~8%). Modest, and the doc comment says so rather than overclaiming - the KB build dominates, not the parse, which is the same thing WI-959 measured.\n- REUSE, fixed: `load_kb_with` had its own `unwrap_or_else` spelling of the panic policy `expect_loaded` owns. `expect_loaded`/`expect_load_errors` are generic over `E: Display`, so the `Vec<String>` twin shares them.\n- SIMPLIFICATION, fixed: toml_ser's `load_kb_with_stdlib` had become a one-line alias for `common::load_kb_with` while `build_test_kb` in the same file called the shared helper directly - two names for one thing. Deleted.\n- ALTITUDE: clean. The one judgement recorded: `wi966_loader_verdict_test`'s ALLOWED list is keyed by PATH, which a file move breaks - accepted, because the failure mode is a loud test failure and the alternative (allow any file containing a `_lenient` fn) would let a real discard hide beside a legitimate one.\n\nTESTS: 4080 -> 4082 (the two new guard tests; verified on the diff that no other #[test] was added or removed). Full workspace green, 0 failed. Full-run wall time 245-252s -> 234s.\n\nNOT COMMITTED, and /code-review NOT RUN - it is user-triggered and CLAUDE.md gates commit on it.\n\nNO FOLLOW-UP TICKETS FILED. The two candidates were considered and rejected: the `FileSourceResolver` case convention (no caller depends on it; a ticket would be inventing a requirement) and the 19 remaining hand-rolled stdlib walks in tests/include (each keeps its own file set and resolver deliberately, so consolidating them would change what fixtures load - the copy-propagated PANIC POLICY, which was the actual defect, now has one owner).", at: "2026-08-03T19:06:46Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-966", description: some(value: "Twenty-four test fixtures still discard the loader's Err, and the same helper NAME means opposite things in two crates.\n\nWI-959 removed the last `let _ = load::load_all(..)` from anthill-core/src/ (see kb/test_support.rs). 24 code sites remain, all under tests/: 19 anthill-core/tests/include/*, 2 anthill-smt-gen/tests, 1 anthill-cpp-gen/tests/common.\n\nWHY THIS MATTERS - MEASURED, not theoretical. With `let _`, region.rs's wi353 fixture carried an unresolved name for its whole life and all 4 of its tests passed GREEN over a half-loaded KB. Flipping it to strict was the only thing that surfaced it. WI-887 recorded the same failure mode in anthill-smt-gen (wi680_ite_lowering_test). A discarded Err does not merely degrade a diagnostic - it lets a test assert over a KB that never finished loading.\n\nTHERE IS ONE LEGITIMATE USE, already documented at anthill-smt-gen/tests/common/mod.rs:34: a fixture that DELIBERATELY carries an unresolvable shape. That crate handles it correctly with a two-function split. The 24 sites are not that - they are copy-propagation of a house pattern (the one WI-959 removed arrived with commit 892a5ddd as a copy, and its fixture loaded clean).\n\nTHE NAME TRAP, verify before touching anything:\n  anthill-core/tests/common::load_kb_with   PANICS on load errors (try_load_kb_with is the Result twin)\n  anthill-smt-gen/tests/common::load_kb_with  DISCARDS them (load_kb_strict is the panicking twin)\nSame name, opposite guarantee, same workspace. Renaming one is probably the real fix; decide before converting call sites.\n\nSCOPE: for each of the 24, determine by MEASUREMENT (flip to strict, run) whether its fixture loads clean. Convert those that do. For any that genuinely need leniency, route through a NAMED lenient entry point, never a bare `let _`. Resolve the load_kb_with name collision.\n\nACCEPTANCE: no bare `let _ = .. load_all(..)` outside a named lenient helper; the two load_kb_with spellings no longer disagree; cargo-test green.\n\nSOURCE: WI-959."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-08-03T19:06:49Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-958", description: some(value: "Three spellings of 'operation -> its parent sort', all by qualified-name slicing.\n\nMEASURED (WI-955 /simplify altitude pass, verified against the source):\n  impl_parent_of_op        rustland/anthill-core/src/kb/typing.rs ~11890\n  spec_op_parent_sort      typing.rs ~15162\n  self_receiver_spec_sort  typing.rs ~20729\n\nEach answers the same question - given an operation Symbol, which sort declares it - by stripping the LAST segment of the qualified name and calling try_resolve_symbol. Two of the three additionally gate on type_params_of_sort(parent).is_empty(). KnowledgeBase::declaring_scope_symbol (kb/mod.rs) answers it from the symbol table, and op_declared_type_param_var (typing.rs ~13513) already uses it with a doc that makes exactly this argument ('a qualified-name split would have to decide where the owner's name ends - <ns>.<Sort>.<op>.<T> has TWO plausible owners').\n\nNOT A BUG, and do not open it as one: all three are correct BY CONSTRUCTION today, because their subject is a DIRECT child of the scope they want. WI-955's failure needed a depth-2 subject read by a depth-1 rule, which is not this shape. This is a one-owner cleanup - the value is that three copies of one query cannot drift, not that any is currently wrong.\n\nDECIDE, do not assume: check each site's gate BEFORE merging. The two type_params_of_sort gates may be doing more than 'the owner is an operation' (compare WI-956 item 4, which flags the sibling kind_of-vs-has_kind divergence at ground_rigid_projection_if_concrete for the same reason).\n\nALSO in the neighbourhood, the INVERSE round-trip: sort_type_params_as_pairs (typing.rs ~26453) and type_param_vid_in_sort (~26474) rebuild '<parent_qn>.<param>' with format! + try_resolve_symbol. WI-954 flags this as 'the identity round-trips THROUGH STRINGS in both directions'; if WI-954 lands first, these go with it.\n\nACCEPTANCE: one owner, or a stated per-site reason why it must stay separate - written at the site, not just in this ticket. cargo-test green; say which tests fail when the merge is backed out.\n\nSOURCE: WI-955 /simplify altitude review."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-08-03T20:48:33Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-958", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. One owner, plus a second one layered on it - and the answers to the ticket's two \"DECIDE, do not assume\" questions.\n\nSTRUCTURE. impl_parent_of_op is now the sole reader of \"which symbol declares this operation\". spec_op_parent_sort calls it and states the two gates; self_receiver_spec_sort calls spec_op_parent_sort and adds ONLY the self-receiver gate. Three qualified-name splits became one. The two callers that consult both spec readers in one pass (check_apply's WI-357 and WI-444 blocks) now agree on the spec sort by construction.\n\nANSWER to \"the two type_params_of_sort gates may be doing more than the ticket says\". They are not - but they were not the interesting divergence either. The real one was that spec_op_parent_sort had a has_kind(Sort) gate and self_receiver_spec_sort DID NOT. Merging adds it. MEASURED behaviour-identical: 0 of 373 operations have a non-Sort parent with a non-empty param list. It is also UNREACHABLE, and worth writing down why, because \"inert\" and \"unreachable\" are different claims: add_type_param's sites are sort scopes and op scopes, so the only non-Sort scope that can carry params is an OPERATION - and measured, all 373 operations have a Namespace (70) or Sort (303) parent, never an operation. Kept anyway, at the site: a reader should not owe its correctness to the NEXT gate catching what its own is missing. NOT subsumed, either - operation outer[U](...) really does give type_params_of_sort(outer) == [\"U\"], so the parametric gate alone means \"some scope with brackets\".\n\nREFUSED the ticket's own proposal, on measurement. impl_parent_of_op does NOT become declaring_scope_symbol. The ticket's argument (op_declared_type_param_var's doc: \"a qualified-name split would have to decide where the owner's name ends\") is sound for ITS subject - a type param at depth 2 - and does not transfer: this reader's subject is always a direct child. MEASURED over stdlib + anthill-stl, 2598 distinct symbols: the two AGREE for all 373 operations and all 246 entity constructors, and DISAGREE for 293 others - 227 entity fields, 53 callback param/result slots, and the 13 DOT-LESS names (kernel vocab Fact/Sort/Rule/meta/..., and the anthill namespace root) where the split correctly finds NO parent and the scope link answers _global. That last group decides it: own_eq_op_carrier does impl_parent_of_op(functor)? to reach a CARRIER SORT, so the scope link turns a \"?\" meaning \"no owner, give up\" into a wrong answer that reads as a real one.\n\nSay its strength honestly: LATENT TRAP, not a live bug. THE DECISIVE MEASUREMENT - with the body replaced by declaring_scope_symbol, the FULL workspace ran 4084 tests with exactly ONE failure, the new WI-958 test written against that paragraph. 4083 green. Nothing else in the tested surface can tell the two spellings apart. So this is a judgement between two things that both work today, settled on failure mode and written at the site with the numbers.\n\nMETHOD NOTE, because it changed an answer: my first measurement walked symbols.by_qualified_name's KEYS and reported 298 disagreements including operations. Wrong - one symbol is registered under several keys (BigInt and anthill.prelude.BigInt are one Symbol), and impl_parent_of_op splits qualified_name_of, not the key. Re-measured by SYMBOL: 293, and 0 among operations. The test does it the right way and says why.\n\nINVERSE ROUND-TRIP, done (the ticket's \"ALSO in the neighbourhood\"). WI-954 has NOT landed, so these did not go with it: sort_type_params_as_pairs and type_param_vid_in_sort each spelled format!(\"{parent_qn}.{param}\") + try_resolve_symbol. Now one qualified_type_param_sym, which also drops a needless String allocation (qualified_name_of is an O(1) &str read, so the hoisted qn.to_string() bought nothing). Still a string round-trip - the doc says so and points at WI-954 as where it goes away rather than gets shortened.\n\nTESTS - wi958_one_op_parent_tests (typing.rs), 2 tests, and the CONTROLS were measured by making each edit, not predicted:\n  each_reader_narrows_the_one_before_it - four shapes in one fixture (parametric sort + self-receiver; parametric, no self-receiver; plain sort; free op).\n    * collapse self_receiver_spec_sort into spec_op_parent_sort -> BOTH tests fail, and NOT on an assertion: THE STDLIB STOPS LOADING (collect.effects undeclared, FiniteCollection.collect.requires missing, Stream vs MappedStream rule mismatch). That gate holds up the WI-357 carrier bind and effect-close. Any stdlib-fixture test catches it; this one says WHICH gate.\n    * drop the parametric gate -> row 3 fails (Plain reported as a spec sort).\n    * drop the has_kind(Sort) gate -> NOTHING fails, here or in the workspace. Expected, and the comment says so rather than pretending a test justifies it.\n  the_split_and_the_scope_link_agree_on_operations_only - the evidence above, re-run rather than re-argued.\n    * CONTROL: point impl_parent_of_op at declaring_scope_symbol and it fails (\"Rule has no parent to strip; declaring_scope_symbol answers Some(_global)\"). It is the ONLY test in the workspace that does. That is the point of writing it - without it the next reader makes the swap, sees green, and lands it.\n\nCORRECTED TWICE DURING REVIEW, both self-inflicted: (a) the module-level test doc PREDICTED the three controls and TWO of the three predictions were wrong once measured - a doc rotted inside its own commit, now deleted in favour of the per-test measured ones; (b) \"0 of 29 namespaces\" was miscounted from an AGREE table that excluded the one disagreeing namespace - it is 30.\n\ncargo-test: 4084 passed, 0 failed, 95 binaries (baseline 4082 + these 2). /simplify run - performed inline rather than by subagents, per session policy.\n\nNOT DONE, deliberately: the kind_of-vs-has_kind asymmetry between the caller-side gates (call_bracket_scopes, rigidify_op_type_params, the WI-374 member tie) and spec_op_parent_sort's has_kind. MEASURED inert - all 52 parametric sorts have kind_of == Some(Sort). Left to WI-956 item 4, which owns that class; feedback added there.", at: "2026-08-03T20:49:30Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-956", author: "user", content: "From WI-958 (delivered). Datum for ITEM 4 (kind_of vs has_kind), measured rather than argued.\n\nMEASURED over stdlib + anthill-stl: of the 52 parametric sorts (has_kind(Sort) and a non-empty type_params_of_sort), ZERO have kind_of != Some(Sort). So the divergence is INERT on today's surface - which is the same shape WI-955 found for the by_parent keying: correct by caller accident, not by property.\n\nWHERE IT STILL DIVERGES IN THE SOURCE, after WI-958: spec_op_parent_sort now asks kb.has_kind(parent, Sort) (WI-958 switched it from the equivalent kb.symbols.get(parent).has_kind(..) spelling). Its three SIBLING caller-side gates still ask kind_of(parent) == Some(SymbolKind::Sort) and all three ask it for the same reason - 'is this parent a sort, so it can have type params':\n  rigidify_op_type_params   typing.rs ~6119\n  the WI-374 member tie     typing.rs ~10210\n  call_bracket_scopes       typing.rs ~14439\nEach sits directly on an impl_parent_of_op call, so they are one merge candidate, not three. Item 4's ground_rigid_projection_if_concrete is a fourth of the same class.\n\nNOT touched by WI-958, deliberately - this ticket owns the class, and the measurement says it is a hardening rather than a fix. Note the trigger if you want a live case: kind_of returns primary_kind (first-declared), so it takes a parametric sort whose ENTITY role registered before its sort role (a section 6.3 eponymous constructor) to make any of the four skip a real parametric sort's params.", at: "2026-08-03T20:50:00Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-954", author: "user", content: "From WI-958 (delivered). The inverse round-trip this ticket flags now has ONE spelling - and it is still yours to delete, not to keep.\n\nWI-958 said 'if WI-954 lands first, these go with it'. It has not, so WI-958 took them: sort_type_params_as_pairs and type_param_vid_in_sort each built '<parent_qn>.<param>' with its own format! + try_resolve_symbol. Both now call\n\n  qualified_type_param_sym(kb, sort_sym, short) -> Option<Symbol>    typing.rs, next to sort_type_params_as_pairs\n\nwhose doc says outright that it is a string round-trip for an identity the LOADER already computes and drops, names this ticket as where it goes away rather than gets shortened, and should therefore be DELETED with its two callers when the loader-side map lands - not preserved. Same instruction WI-955's feedback gave about its own transitional shape.\n\nIncidental: the merge also removed a qn.to_string() that sort_type_params_as_pairs hoisted out of its loop. qualified_name_of is an O(1) &str field read, so the hoist was buying an allocation, not saving one.\n\nALSO relevant to this ticket's key. WI-958 REFUSED to route impl_parent_of_op through declaring_scope_symbol, which is the same key this ticket converges on - and the reason is worth carrying, because it is about the READER's domain, not the key's correctness. MEASURED over 2598 distinct symbols: split and scope link agree for all 373 operations and all 246 entity constructors. They part on 293 others, and the deciding 13 are the DOT-LESS kernel names (Fact, Sort, Rule, meta, ..., and the anthill namespace root): the split answers None, declaring_scope_symbol answers Some(_global). A caller doing impl_parent_of_op(functor)? to reach a carrier sort would take the pseudo-scope for a real one. Decisive control: with the swap made, the full workspace ran 4084 tests with exactly ONE failure - the new WI-958 test written against that argument. So if this ticket's published map is keyed on (scope_raw, short), a reader that means 'a DECLARATION owns this' must still refuse the _global scope explicitly; the key alone will not.", at: "2026-08-03T20:50:03Z")
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-971", name: "scaland")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-970", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. THE DECISION: the widened end is a DEFECT, not an upper bound by design. Measured, then fixed, then pinned.\n\nWHAT SETTLED IT was not the argument but the probe. Span doc called end an upper bound, which costs nothing while only formatStart renders. But: name over `namespace demo.sub` reported width 11 for 8 characters, ending 1:11..2:3; importPath over `anthill.prelude.List` followed by a blank line reported 24 for 20, ending at 4:3 - TWO LINES down; proofStrategy over `auto` reported 17, with a whole `{- t -}` block comment inside the span. An end that lands past a comment on a later line is not a loose bound on the construct, it is a position in unrelated source, and adjacent spans OVERLAP under it. The first containment or caret reader would be wrong, not coarse.\n\nEIGHT SITES, not the six the ticket named, in three shapes:\n  FIVE trailing plain-sequence Index captures (name, simpleName, importPath, distributiveProjectionSeg, proofStrategy) -> no-whitespace captures.\n  TWO zero-width: operationTypeParam (mkSpan(idx,idx)) -> located; and a SEVENTH the ticket did not list, the braced-block visibility refusal at mkSpan(s,s), which had NO test at all. Fixed inline, 4 lines, rather than filed - it is the same class and the refusal is now driven.\n  ONE that no grep could find: ruleArrowChoice used fastparse flatMap, which runs the whitespace skipper before the continuation - and the continuation for a rule WITH a body is Pass, so EVERY such rule declaration span ran to the start of whatever followed. Not a trailing Index at all. Three greps had already declared the sweep complete.\n\nHOW THE EIGHTH WAS FOUND, which is the part worth keeping: the altitude reviewer pointed out that the parser doc directs a new declaration production author to DeclarationSpanTest, which asserted STARTS only - so a new production would get a start case and never an end one. Adding the end assertion there (one-line declarations span exactly their line) failed immediately on `rule single(?x) :- Point(x: ?x)`. The test written to prevent FUTURE drift found a PRESENT bug on its first run.\n\nCONTROLS, both measured against the delivered code (321 tests):\n  revert the whole parser change -> 9 fail: the eight per-site tests plus the DeclarationSpanTest end assertion. Nothing else moves.\n  break located alone -> 8 fail. Before this WI the same break failed NOTHING (WI-965 measured that and left it as a documented hole); it failed 3 mid-way, and 8 once the four multi-token productions were routed through located too.\n  drop the explicit whitespace request in the reversed-arrow branch -> 4 fail, including a PRE-EXISTING proposal-032 integration test, so that half is protected by more than my own test.\n\n/simplify (4 agents). APPLIED:\n  - located is generic in its payload, so MULTI-TOKEN was never the reason a production stayed hand-written. WI-965 said it was and I repeated it. The reviewer proved otherwise by converting all four and running the suite green. Converted; the comment now states the real line - the six that structurally cannot are the ones whose start is not an Index at their own entry (four sortDecl branches take it handed down, sortTypeParam reads it off nameSpan.start, proofStep takes two ends off one start), and the remaining ~20 are a BACKLOG, not a design line.\n  - Span doc claimed nothing else in the suite reads end. FALSE on day one: DeclarationSpanTest:172 reads it. Also trimmed the fastparse how-to out of Span (a file with no fastparse dependency) and the duplicated evidence, leaving the contract sentence plus pointers.\n  - stale pointer in the combinator doc to the Span sentence this change deleted.\n  - the hot-path paragraph rejecting located for spanOfToken now sits above a paragraph announcing two productions routed through located. Distinguished with the counts the reviewer measured over the 105-file corpus: ~46000 bracket tokens vs ~2500 declarations and 83 type-param lists. Two orders of magnitude.\n  - SpanFixture extracted: parse + allItems were a verbatim second copy from DeclarationSpanTest, and allItems is the one that matters - it enumerates scope-opening Item shapes, so a missed copy silently stops descending and reports as fixture drift.\n  - termSpan simplified from collectFirst-with-a-match-in-the-guard to find + map.\nSKIPPED, with reasons: merging fixtures (each sits next to the comment naming ITS trivia, which is the point); the ~20-site sweep and the Index-shadowing lint (far larger than this WI - filed as WI-971, and the parser comment cites it).\n\nONE SITE DELIBERATELY LEFT ZERO-WIDTH, now documented rather than silently exempt: the top-level parse-failure diagnostic. A parse failure has no construct to bracket, and formatTrailing is a display string whose length is not a source length. Span states that a point diagnostic is the invariant, not an exception to it.\n\nTESTS: 311 -> 321 (SpanEndTest 9, DeclarationSpanTest +1). No test removed. Full scaland suite green.\n\nNOT COMMITTED and /code-review NOT RUN (user-triggered; CLAUDE.md gates commit on it). /simplify was run with 4 fresh general-purpose agents.\n\nFOLLOW-UP: WI-971.", at: "2026-08-03T20:31:23Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-970", description: some(value: "SCALAND: SIX PARSER SPANS HAVE AN `end` THAT IS NOT THE TOKEN'S END — five stretch over trailing trivia, one is zero-width. FOUND while converting the span-capture idiom in WI-965 (2026-08-03), and deliberately LEFT ALONE there because WI-965 was a pure refactor and each of these is a behavioural change.\n\nMEASURED, not inferred. `name`, `simpleName`, `importPath`, `distributiveProjectionSeg` and `proofStrategy` end on a plain `~ Index` rather than the `~~ Index` every other production uses. fastparse's `~` skips whitespace BEFORE its right operand, so the captured end sits past the construct, inside the trivia that follows it. Probe: `simpleName` over the label in `rule lbl   :   p(?x)` reports width 6 for a 3-character `lbl` — the three spaces before the `:` are inside the name's span. `Span`'s own doc already names this shape ('`end` IS AN UPPER BOUND … `~~ Index` is the spelling that avoids it, and is what the item productions use') — these five are the productions that do not. THREE of the five are multi-token anyway, so no combinator fits them regardless; `simpleName` is the one that WI-965's `located(ident)` would have absorbed exactly.\n\nSEPARATELY, `operationTypeParam` builds `mkSpan(idx, idx)` — a ZERO-WIDTH span at the parameter's first character, not the identifier token. It is what the WI-850 type-param-default refusal points at (driven: `operation f[T   = S](x: S) -> S` reports at 3:17, exactly T's first character), so a caret renderer would have nothing to underline.\n\nWHY NOTHING CAUGHT EITHER: no reader consumes `end` today — `Span.render` prints `formatStart` only. Measured control from WI-965: swap the two new span combinators' `~~` for a whitespace-skipping `~`, i.e. inflict this same defect on ~30 sites at once, and the whole 311-test suite stays GREEN. The one width assertion that exists (`DeclarationSpanTest`, the `F` of `sort Parameterized[A, F[T]]`) sits where no trivia follows the token, so it cannot see it either.\n\nDECIDE, then do: is the widened end a defect to fix, or is `end` an upper bound BY DESIGN (as `Span`'s doc half-says) and these five are within spec? If a defect: the five take `~~ Index`, `simpleName` collapses into WI-965's `located(ident)`, and `operationTypeParam` takes `located` too. ACCEPTANCE: whichever way it goes, the answer becomes a TEST — a width assertion over a construct with trivia after it, which no test has today, so the next `~`-vs-`~~` slip fails something. Say which test fails when the change is backed out; today the honest answer is 'none', which is why this ticket exists."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "scaland-sbt-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-03T20:31:26Z"))
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-972", name: "scaland")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-971", description: some(value: "SCALAND: ~20 declaration productions still hand-write the span capture WI-970 just repaired at eight sites, and the strongest fix would make the class unrepresentable.\n\nWI-970 fixed eight span-end defects and routed six productions through the `located` combinator. What remains: ~26 productions spell `Index ... ~~ Index` by hand, ~20 of which are MECHANICALLY convertible. That is verified, not assumed - four of them (name, importPath, distributiveProjectionSeg, proofStrategy) were converted during WI-970 review with the suite green, and those four are now delivered as part of it.\n\nSIX genuinely cannot, and the reason is structural rather than stylistic - their start does not come from an `Index` at their own entry:\n  the four sortDecl branches take theirs handed DOWN as an Int (the WI-947 dispatcher rule documented at namespaceDecl)\n  sortTypeParam reads its back off nameSpan.start\n  proofStep takes two different ends off one start\n\nTHE STRONGEST FIX IS REACHABLE BUT NOT FREE: shadow `Index` inside AnthillParserImpl so a hand-written capture is a COMPILE error - the same trick this file already plays on fastparse.P for WI-950 refusal scoping - with a greppable escape hatch (rawIndex) for the six. That cuts the hand-written surface 26 -> 6 and makes the WI-970 defect class unrepresentable rather than merely fixed at the sites someone happened to grep. COST: a ~20-production mechanical diff, each site gaining one nesting level in its destructuring (case ((vis, n, fields, meta), span)).\n\nREJECTED ALTERNATIVE, recorded so it is not re-invented: the parser owns its whitespace function (given ws) and ParsingRun exposes a misc map, so the skipper could record the pre-trivia index and a tightIndex could hand it back whenever ctx.index equals the post-skip index - making plain and no-whitespace captures agree everywhere including the six. It works, but swaps a visible local syntax rule for hidden parser state, and would silently pull a START index back over leading trivia if one were ever placed after a plain sequence.\n\nWATCH FOR - THE GREP DOES NOT FIND EVERYTHING: WI-970 found that ruleArrowChoice leaked trivia into EVERY `:-` rule span through fastparse flatMap (which runs the whitespace skipper before the continuation, and the continuation there is Pass), not through a trailing Index at all. Three greps missed it; the declaration-end assertion added to DeclarationSpanTest is what caught it. An Index-shadowing lint would not have caught it either. Exactly one flatMap exists in the parser today - a new one is a new instance of the same class.\n\nACCEPTANCE: the hand-written Index captures are gone except the named six, or the WI records why shadowing was rejected AFTER trying it; SpanEndTest plus the DeclarationSpanTest end assertion stay green; scaland-sbt-test green.\n\nSOURCE: WI-970 /simplify altitude + reuse review."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "scaland-sbt-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-04T03:34:02Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-974", description: some(value: "WI-956 item 4 fixed the gates it named and PROVED the class is live. The rest of typing.rs still asks `kind_of`, and this ticket carries the proof so the next reader does not re-derive it.\n\nTHE DATUM, measured over stdlib + anthill-stl (2598 distinct symbols):\n  - 68 symbols carry more than one category. 64 are [Entity, Sort] (the top-level `entity X(...)` sugar of §6.3), 4 are [Sort, Entity].\n  - So for 64 sorts, `kind_of(s) == Some(Sort)` is FALSE while `has_kind(s, Sort)` is TRUE.\n  - A name can also be [Entity, Sort, Operation] (`entity foo(k)` beside `operation foo(...)`), so the Operation direction diverges too.\n  - `kind_of`'s own doc (kb/mod.rs) already says it is for DISPLAY and that `has_kind` is the membership question.\n\nDRIVEN, not argued (wi956_kind_gate_tests, typing.rs):\n    entity Rec(n: Int64)\n    sort Rec\n      sort T = ?\n      operation peek(x: T) -> T\n    end\n  loads clean, and under `kind_of` `call_bracket_scopes(Rec.peek)` returned [] — the sort's own T missing from the bracket scopes. `has_kind` returns [\"T\"]. The same misread emptied the WI-565 bare-member hint.\n\nWHAT WI-956 FIXED (do not redo): the five `impl_parent_of_op` + kind gates, now one `impl_parent_sort_of_op`; `member_owning_sorts_for_bare`; `genuine_concrete_sort` (the shared filled_carrier_sort / ground_rigid_projection_if_concrete test); ground_rigid's Operation gate.\n\nWHAT IS LEFT — every remaining `kind_of(..) == Some(SymbolKind::..)` in typing.rs used as a TEST rather than for display. At the time of writing: ~4547, 6420, 7291, 8166, 9828, 10830, 14402(recheck), 18954, 18992, 22111, 26196, 26218, 27722, 28196, 29625. Grep rather than trust the line numbers.\n\nDECIDE PER SITE, do not sweep blindly. Some may genuinely mean \"was it WRITTEN with this keyword\" (a diagnostic that echoes the source, reflect's `kind` string). Those must stay `kind_of` and say so at the site. The rest are membership questions and should switch.\n\nACCEPTANCE: each site either switched or annotated with why it is a display question; cargo-test green; name which tests fail when the change is backed out (WI-956's experience: for most of these, NOTHING fails without a purpose-built fixture — say so rather than implying a test justifies it).\n\nSOURCE: WI-956 item 4."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-956", description: some(value: "Three SortAlias walks survive outside typing.rs's consolidated one, and one test-side reader has five copies.\n\nWI-943 collapsed typing.rs's SortAlias readers into `sort_alias_exact` / `sort_alias_by_name` / `scan_sort_aliases` (and DELETED `sort_alias_target_exact`, a third exact-match copy whose caller was doing a full linear scan on a post-index path). The /simplify reuse pass found what it did NOT reach.\n\n1. `find_sort_alias_var` (rustland/anthill-core/src/kb/load.rs ~11580, 2 callers) is a near-verbatim twin of `scan_sort_aliases`: same two-pass exact-then-by-name shape, same `rules_by_functor(SortAlias)` -> `fact_head_term` -> `pos_args[0]` functor -> `pos_args[1]` walk, same value-fact-skip caveat spelled out again in its own comment. NOT a mechanical swap, and this is why WI-943 left it: its `Term::Var(_)` filter is on the TARGET and sits INSIDE the loop (a non-Var target is skipped and the scan CONTINUES), so it cannot be a post-filter. Reuse needs the predicate widened to `(functor, target)` and `scan_sort_aliases` raised to `pub(crate)`.\n\n2. Cannot and should not be consolidated — recorded so the next reader does not retry it: `build_sort_alias_index` (one pass building THREE maps over ALL facts; `scan_sort_aliases` returns on first match); `reconstruct_sort_params`' pre-index tail (collects EVERY alias under a parent prefix — see the sibling ticket on its keying); `sort_alias_exists` (load.rs ~13359, keys on pos-0 TermId equality and reads the head via `rule_head_value`/`pos_arg` PRECISELY so a `Value::Node` head still dedups — `scan_sort_aliases` skips exactly those heads via `fact_head_term`, so reuse would silently break the WI-390 dedup).\n\n3. TEST SIDE: 'SortAlias source symbol -> target Var::Global id' is hand-rolled in five files — wi210_dispatch_test.rs (`subst_with_param`), wi221_defer_to_requirement_test.rs (`subst_with_param`), wi224_sld_resolution_test.rs (`goal_for`), wi452_marked_param_backing_var_test.rs (`has_backing_var`), wi943_type_param_identity_test.rs. `tests/common/mod.rs` has no such helper. Lift one there and route all five through it (`has_backing_var` becomes `.is_some()`).\n\n4. `ground_rigid_projection_if_concrete` (typing.rs) gates its owner check with `kind_of(sort) != Some(SymbolKind::Operation)` while `op_declared_type_param_var` uses `has_kind`. `kind_of` is documented (intern.rs) as 'the keyword the declaration opened with — for DISPLAY only … Not a test for what the name can be used as: see `has_kind`.' Pre-existing; WI-943 is what made the two siblings by pointing both at `op_info::declared_type_param_var`. DECIDE, do not assume: the `kind_of` gate there may be doing MORE than 'owner is an operation' (it is the WI-383 op-vs-sort projection split), so check whether the two questions are actually the same before merging them.\n\nACCEPTANCE: cargo-test green; each item either done or CLOSED IN THE TICKET with the reason (item 2 is already closed above — do not reopen it).\n\nSOURCE: WI-943 /simplify reuse review."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-08-04T03:20:57Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-956", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. Four items, three done and one already closed — plus item 4 turned out to be a LIVE reachable defect, not the hardening the ticket and WI-958's feedback both expected.\n\nITEM 1 — find_sort_alias_var (load.rs) now IS typing.rs's scan.\nscan_sort_aliases' predicate widened to (functor, target) and raised to pub(crate), exactly as the ticket prescribed; the 30-line duplicate walk in load.rs is gone. Its two passes stay spelled at the site because they must: the Var test is a filter INSIDE each pass, and resolve_sort_alias' index path cannot serve a load-time reader anyway.\n\nThat in-loop-vs-post-filter property is NOT theoretical. MEASURED — with find_sort_alias_var's body replaced by the post-filter spelling anyone consolidating these would reach for first (resolve_sort_alias(sym).filter(is_var)), `-p anthill-core` ran 3456 tests with two failures: wi946_belongs_to_readers_test's an_eponymous_parametric_destructure_resolves_its_type_param and its control_ sibling. So the property was already guarded end-to-end; what those two could not say is WHY. New test wi956_alias_scan_tests::the_var_filter_must_run_per_fact_not_on_the_answer says it in one pair of calls (a concrete alias earlier in rules_by_functor order, a param alias later, same short name) and carries the post-filter answer — None — as its own control.\n\nThe head-read change (fact_head_term -> sort_alias_head_slots) is not a behaviour change, and the reason is structural, not just the 0-of-107 count WI-955 measured: load_abstract_sort runs every alias target through lower_value_or_gate (WI-390) precisely so the SortAlias head stays a hash-consed Term::Fn. A value-carried head and a non-Var target are the same condition, and the Var predicate rejects either.\n\nITEM 2 — closed by the ticket, not reopened. Confirmed still true.\n\nITEM 3 — done. tests/common::sort_alias_backing_var(kb, sym) -> Option<VarId>; all five suites routed (wi210/wi221 subst_with_param, wi224 goal_for, wi452 has_backing_var -> .is_some(), wi943). It is deliberately a HAND read of the relation, not a call into resolve_sort_alias: every one of those suites exists to assert a production reader agrees with the DECLARATION, and routing the expected side through the production reader would only assert it agrees with itself. Noted at the site: three of the five took the LAST match, two the FIRST; that never differed because sort_alias_exists dedup-guards emission — the shared reader takes FIRST and says so rather than depending on an unstated property.\n\nITEM 4 — the ticket asked \"is the kind_of gate doing MORE than 'the owner is an operation'?\" It was doing LESS, and the class is LIVE.\n\nDRIVEN, and this is the headline:\n    entity Rec(n: Int64)\n    sort Rec\n      sort T = ?\n      operation peek(x: T) -> T\n    end\nloads CLEAN. `Rec` registers [Entity, Sort], so kind_of(Rec) == Some(Entity) while has_kind(Rec, Sort) is true, and type_params_of_sort(Rec) == [\"T\"]. Under kind_of, call_bracket_scopes(Rec.peek) returned [] — the sort's own T missing from the bracket scopes, so `peek[T = ...]` had no T to name. Write the two declarations the other way round and everything works: exactly the source-order sensitivity kind_of's own doc warns against.\n\nREFUTES the inertness both prior tickets recorded — and neither was wrong, they measured a narrower set. WI-958: \"0 of 52 parametric sorts have kind_of != Some(Sort)\". WI-956's own feedback repeated it. TRUE of the LIBRARIES, and now measured wider: of 2598 distinct symbols, 68 carry more than one category — 64 [Entity, Sort] and 4 [Sort, Entity] — so 64 sorts are invisible to kind_of. 0 of those 64 carries a type param, a named requirement slot or a requires chain, and 0 of 373 operations has such a parent, because the only library route to one is the top-level `entity X(...)` sugar whose desugared body is that one entity. It takes the re-declaration above to build a kind-hidden sort that carries anything.\n\nSTRUCTURE. impl_parent_sort_of_op(kb, op) = impl_parent_of_op + has_kind(Sort) is the ONE owner of \"the SORT that declares this operation, None for a free op\". Five sites that each spelled it themselves now call it: rigidify_op_type_params, the WI-374 member tie, names_any_requirement_slot, call_bracket_scopes, callee_requirement_slots. spec_op_parent_sort layers only its parametric gate on top (WI-958 had already given that one has_kind; this moves the gate down so its four siblings inherit it rather than each getting a copy). NOTE: WI-958's feedback named THREE such sites; there are five — names_any_requirement_slot and callee_requirement_slots were missed.\n\nAlso switched, and their strength stated honestly at each site because it differs:\n  - member_owning_sorts_for_bare — DRIVEN. A kind-hidden sort that declares a member was dropped from the WI-565 bare-member hint, so the author got the terse \"unknown functor\" instead of the sort name. CONTROL measured: restoring kind_of, the message is `type mismatch in wi956Peek.apply: ... unknown functor`, with no Wi956Owner.\n  - genuine_concrete_sort — NEW, one owner for a two-line gate that filled_carrier_sort and ground_rigid_projection_if_concrete each spelled. HARDENING, not a fix, and the site says so: no program can observe it, because a kind-hidden sort cannot carry the `provides` this grounding reads (MEASURED — `provides Resource[...]` inside a re-declared body reports \"unresolved name 'Resource'\").\n  - ground_rigid_projection_if_concrete's Operation gate — the one the ticket literally named. HARDENING. `entity foo(k)` beside `operation foo(...)` really does register [Entity, Sort, Operation] (MEASURED), but that collision is not a usable program: the ENTITY wins the call site and the load dies in the entity's own field check long before any projection grounds. Switched because a membership gate should ask the membership question, not because a program noticed. It does not blur the WI-383 split — declared_type_param_var two lines down still decides, so the split turns on where the param was declared.\n\nTESTS — 7 new, 4091 passed / 0 failed (baseline 4084).\n  typing.rs wi956_kind_gate_tests (4): a_sort_can_be_registered_under_another_kind_first (the premise — if the loader ever refuses the re-declaration, THIS names it); an_operation_sees_its_sorts_type_params_whichever_way_the_sort_was_declared; the_parent_sort_gate_admits_a_sort_and_refuses_a_namespace; on_the_libraries_the_two_spellings_still_agree.\n  typing.rs wi956_alias_scan_tests (1) — item 1, above.\n  tests/include/wi956_kind_gate_test.rs (2) — the diagnostic half, because message content belongs where the loader's own words are read.\nCONTROLS, measured by making each edit and re-running, not predicted:\n  * restore kind_of in impl_parent_sort_of_op -> the two middle tests fail ([] vs [\"T\"]; (Some(\"Rec\"), None) vs (Some(\"Rec\"), Some(\"Rec\"))). FULL WORKSPACE under that edit: 4086 passed, 2 failed, and the 2 are exactly these. Nothing else in the workspace can tell the spellings apart — which is why the divergence survived WI-943, WI-955 and WI-958, all three of which looked straight at it.\n  * restore kind_of in member_owning_sorts_for_bare -> the entity-first row fails on the terse message; the sort-first row passes either way BY DESIGN and is there so a regression that merely breaks the hint cannot let the pair agree at \"no owner named\".\n  * on_the_libraries_the_two_spellings_still_agree and a_sort_can_be_registered... pass either way by design; each says so.\n\nANSWERING THE USER'S QUESTION asked during the work — \"why run scan_sort_aliases at all when we have an index?\" MEASURED rather than argued. build_sort_alias_index has ONE call site, at type-check start, so nothing that resolves an alias during LOAD has an index to read. Panicking in each fallback and running -p anthill-core: the exact pass is reached by essentially the whole suite (all 20 test targets fail, 2635 hits); the by-name pass separately by 51 tests. Both are live, not vestigial. The COST, though, is small: instrumented, one full stdlib + anthill-stl load makes 130 scan calls over 103 SortAlias facts — 10086 fact-visits total. WI-659 already moved the hot per-node traffic onto the maps. The scan could still be deleted outright by maintaining the index at its single producer (assert_sort_alias, one emission point behind a dedup guard), which would turn \"the index is complete once built\" into \"complete from the first assert\"; retraction has no hook today. Not filed as a ticket — it is a clarity cleanup on a cold path, worth doing only alongside whoever next touches SortAliasIndex.\n\n/simplify run inline (per session policy, as WI-958). One fix applied: the lifted test helper now uses rules_by_functor_iter rather than allocating a Vec snapshot per call, matching the production scan and its stated reason.\n\nFOLLOW-UPS FILED, both necessary because item 4 proved a class live rather than inert:\n  WI-974 — the remaining ~15 kind_of gates in typing.rs, carrying the measurement so the next reader does not re-derive it. Says DECIDE PER SITE: some genuinely mean \"was it WRITTEN with this keyword\" and must stay.\n  WI-975 — the re-declaration itself. `entity Rec(...)` followed by `sort Rec { ... }` is ACCEPTED with no diagnostic and is half-wired: the second body resolves no name from its namespace (measured on three shapes, including an imported Int64). A section 6.3 spec decision — refuse, or define as extension and fix the scope wiring — deliberately not made here. wi956_kind_gate_tests points at it, and notes that if WI-975 refuses the shape the FIXTURE needs replacing, not the fix.", at: "2026-08-04T03:21:47Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-973", description: some(value: "USER-DIRECTED, done inline after WI-956. Two renames that turned into a defect.\n\n1. `KnowledgeBase::resolve_sym(Symbol) -> &str` sat one truncation from `resolve_symbol(&str) -> Symbol` and ran the OPPOSITE way. Renamed `local_name_of`.\n\n2. NOT `short_name_of`, which is where the interesting part is. MEASURED over stdlib + anthill-stl: 53 of 2598 symbols answer with a DOTTED name. All are WI-341 callback places — `register_callback_places` (load.rs) defines every place in the OPERATION's scope keyed by its path relative to that op, so `anthill.prelude.Monad.flatMap.f._1` is filed under `f._1`. The dot is load-bearing: it keeps `f._1` distinct from a sibling callback's `g._1` in one flat `scope.locals` map. So the stored name is the name WITHIN THE DECLARING SCOPE, not a single segment.\n   Renamed with it: `SymbolDef.short_name` -> `local_name`, `SymbolTable::name` -> `local_name`, and its \"backward compatibility\" alias `SymbolTable::resolve` DELETED (2 callers). `codegen/rust.rs`'s own `resolve_sym` helper (same meaning, String) went too.\n   NOT renamed, deliberately: `typing::short_name_of(&str)`, `anthill-cpp-gen`'s copy, `anthill-smt-gen::short_name`, and the LANGUAGE-level `anthill.reflect.short_name`. Those really do slice the last segment, and they exist BECAUSE the stored name is not one — `builtin_short_name`'s `rsplit` is what turns `f._1` into `_1`. Composing them (`short_name_of(kb.local_name_of(sym))`) is a real idiom; they are not interchangeable, and both docs now say so.\n\n3. THE DEFECT the rename exposed, spotted by the user: `SortAliasIndex.by_name` was keyed on that name ALONE. A local name identifies nothing outside its scope — 37 SortAlias sources are named `T` after a stdlib load — so `sort_alias_by_name` answered with whichever came first, i.e. an unrelated declaration's variable. It was documented as \"a NAME-DIRECTED GUESS\" and had already caused one measured bug: WI-943 found a bracket parameter reaching it and being answered `anthill.kernel.T`, so `cmp[T]` and `cmp2[T]` shared one var. WI-943 fixed that caller and LEFT THE GUESS.\n\nDECISION: removed, not re-keyed on `(parent, local_name)`. THE MEASUREMENT that settled it — with `sort_alias_by_name` forced to `None`, the FULL WORKSPACE ran 4091 tests, 0 failures; with `find_sort_alias_var`'s matching second pass ALSO deleted, again 4091/0. Nothing wanted it. Re-keying would have preserved a rung no caller needs and left every caller owing a parent it does not always have.\n\nREMOVED: `SortAliasIndex.by_name`; `sort_alias_by_name`; `type_param_global_var`'s third rung (the very one WI-943 measured going wrong); `find_sort_alias_var`'s second pass. `resolve_sort_alias` and `sort_alias_exact` MERGED — WI-943 split them only so an identity-wanting reader could dodge the guess, and with the guess gone they were the same function.\n\nRETRACTS PART OF WI-956 ITEM 1. That item widened `scan_sort_aliases`' predicate to `(functor, target)` so `find_sort_alias_var` could reject a non-Var target PER FACT and let the scan continue past it. That was correct while a by-name pass could see several same-named candidates; with only an exact match there is at most one candidate (`sort_alias_exists` dedup-guards emission; MEASURED, 103 facts over 103 distinct sources), so the predicate is back to `Fn(Symbol)` and the caller post-filters. WI-956's test for that property is retired with it — a test whose subject no longer exists.\n\nTESTS — `wi956_alias_identity_tests` (typing.rs), 2, replacing the retired one:\n  an_alias_is_found_only_by_its_own_symbol\n  a_type_param_reader_answers_nothing_rather_than_someone_elses_var\nFixture: three declarations sharing one local name `Wi956T` in three scopes — a type param, an alias to a concrete sort, and an ENTITY that is no alias at all. CONTROL, measured by restoring the by-name pass: BOTH fail, the entity being answered with `Wi956Param`'s var.\nCORRECTED DURING THE WORK: the first draft declared the concrete alias first, so the borrowed target was a `sort_ref`, `as_global` rejected it, and the second test passed under the control for the right result by the wrong route. The doc had already claimed it failed. Reordered the fixture and re-measured; the fixture doc now says why the order matters.\n\ncargo-test: 4092 passed, 0 failed (4091 + 2 new - 1 retired).\n\nNOT DONE: the flattening itself. Giving each callback param its own scope (`<op>.f`) would make every local name a single segment and delete the `rsplit`s. That is a design change to WI-341, not a rename — file it separately if wanted."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-08-04T04:05:57Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-956", author: "user", content: "PARTIAL RETRACTION of item 1, from WI-973 (user-directed, delivered).\n\nItem 1's widened `(functor, target)` predicate on `scan_sort_aliases` is REVERTED to `Fn(Symbol)`. Its justification was that `find_sort_alias_var` must reject a non-Var target PER FACT so the scan continues past it — true only while the BY-NAME pass could put several same-named candidates in front of it. WI-973 deleted that pass everywhere (MEASURED: forced to `None`, the full workspace ran 4091 tests, 0 failures), leaving an EXACT symbol match with at most one candidate (MEASURED: 103 SortAlias facts over 103 distinct sources; `sort_alias_exists` dedup-guards emission). So the caller post-filters, and `wi956_alias_scan_tests::the_var_filter_must_run_per_fact_not_on_the_answer` is retired — its subject no longer exists.\n\nSTANDS unchanged: the consolidation itself (load.rs carries no copy of the walk), items 2 and 3, and all of item 4.\n\nAlso note the WI-946 control measurement recorded in this ticket's delivery — 'the post-filter spelling fails two wi946 tests' — was true of the code AS IT WAS, and was driven by the by-name pass, not by the exact one. It no longer reproduces.", at: "2026-08-04T04:06:00Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-972", description: some(value: "SCALAND: the file's LAST top-level declaration spans to end-of-input, swallowing every trailing newline, blank line and comment after it.\n\nA ninth site of the class WI-970 repaired, and the first one no reading of the productions could have found. WI-971's corpus span-tightness audit (ParseSpanCoverageTest, `looseEnds`) is what surfaced it, on its first run, and that audit EXCLUDES this one case today - the exclusion is documented at its site and points here.\n\nMEASURED, with a probe over the delivered parser (probe deleted; reproduce with any 3-line fixture):\n  `fact p(x: 1)`            -> 0..12  tight\n  `fact p(x: 1)\\n`          -> 0..13  spans the newline\n  `fact p(x: 1)\\n\\n\\n`      -> 0..15  spans both blank lines\n  `fact p(x: 1)\\n-- done\\n` -> 0..21  SPANS THE COMMENT\n  `fact p(x: 1)\\nfact q(x: 2)` -> 0..12 tight; the second is tight too\n\nNOT A WI-971 REGRESSION: `git checkout HEAD -- AnthillParser.scala` and the probe prints byte-identical numbers. Present before the conversion.\n\nNOT A `~` vs `~~` DECISION ANYWHERE, which is why the WI-970/971 sweeps could not see it. Every capture is at the correct offset; only END OF INPUT behaves differently. The trigger is a production whose LAST element is an optional or a `rep` (`metaBlock.?`, `typeExpr`'s trailing `rep`): mid-file its whitespace skip resets over the trivia, at EOF it does not. A production ending in a fixed token is unaffected - `namespace d ... end\\n` is tight, which is why most stdlib files pass and `primitives.anthill` (top-level braced `sort`s) is the one that fails.\n\nWHY IT IS FILED RATHER THAN PATCHED: the fix is not local to a production. Trimming trailing whitespace in `mkSpan` is the obvious move and is INSUFFICIENT - it does not cover the trailing-comment case above, since a comment is trivia the skipper consumed but is not whitespace. A correct fix either (a) finds why fastparse's optional/rep tail reset differs at EOF and stops it, or (b) gives `mkSpan` a real trivia-aware backward scan (the `ws` skipper already knows the comment forms; running it backwards is not free). Decide between them with a measurement, not by taste.\n\nACCEPTANCE: the exclusion in `ParseSpanCoverageTest.looseEnds` is DELETED and both audit cases stay green; a case in SpanEndTest pins a declaration at end-of-input followed by a comment; scaland-sbt-test green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "scaland-sbt-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-04T04:17:52Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-964", description: some(value: "SCALAND: `typeExprSpan` RE-DERIVES ITS ANSWER AT EVERY RECURSION LEVEL — O(n^2) in nested type depth. FOUND by /simplify on WI-961 (2026-08-03). MECHANISM: `typeExprToRef`'s `Arrow` arm computes `val span = typeExprSpan(arrow)`, which walks the whole remaining chain to find the first located leaf — O(n). It then recurses into `typeExprToRef(ret)`, which independently recomputes `typeExprSpan` over the SAME subtree it just walked. Total O(n) + O(n-1) + ... = O(n^2) for a right-nested chain like a curried `(A) -> (B) -> (C) -> D`. `namedTupleTypeTerm` compounds it with a further ~2x constant: for a tuple field whose type is an arrow, `typeExprSpan(ty)` runs once for `fieldSpan` and again inside the recursive `typeExprToRef(ty)`. CURRENTLY INERT, and the reasons are worth keeping because they are what makes this safe to defer: (a) an operation PARAMETER type is kept as IR and never lowered by the parser, so `typeExprToRef` is only entered from TERM position — typed vars, instantiation arguments, `let` annotations, bounded quantification; (b) hand-written .anthill nests arrow/tuple/effect types 2-3 deep at most, and nothing in the stdlib or in `ParseSpanCoverageTest`'s corpus nests an arrow inside an arrow. At depth <= 5 the difference is invisible. FIX DIRECTION: stop re-deriving from the raw `TypeExpr` — the children are BUILT either way, so each level can read its children's spans back in O(1) via `terms.spanOf(childId)`, or the once-computed span can be threaded down as a parameter to the recursive `typeExprToRef` calls. ACCEPTANCE: a parse of a type nested ~50 deep in term position, with the term count or a timing assertion showing linear rather than quadratic growth; state which test fails when the change is backed out. WATCH: whichever fix is chosen must not weaken WI-961's invariant — `ParseSpanCoverageTest` must stay green, and blanking the derived span must still fail exactly the corpus case."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "scaland-sbt-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-04T04:47:20Z"))
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-976", name: "scaland")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-977", description: some(value: "RUSTLAND: `scope_display_name` IS DUPLICATED, AND IT DISAGREES WITH SCALAND ABOUT WHAT A SCOPE IS CALLED. Found while driving WI-962 (the scaland twin), verified in the source, NOT fixed there. Two defects, one file: (1) DUPLICATION — `load.rs` has the helper at ~:8320 and a byte-identical open-coded copy of the same unwrap at ~:3299 (the `ForbiddenInternalAccess` raise, which builds `scope_name` inline off `kb.get_term(scope)`). That is exactly the two-copies state scaland just retired: WI-962 promoted its helper to `KnowledgeBase` and routed both unwraps through one `scopeFunctor`, so 'what do we call this scope' has ONE answer. (2) DIVERGENCE — rustland's helper returns `local_name` (the SHORT name: `C`), scaland's returns the QUALIFIED name (`demo.C`); rustland degrades a non-`Fn` scope term to the string `\"_unknown\"`, scaland REFUSES with an IllegalStateException on the grounds that every scope term is a nullary name term by construction so a degraded string can only hide a producer bug. So the same source file loaded by the two implementations yields two different scope names in the same diagnostic, and two different not-a-scope policies. WORK: pick ONE of each and state it in `docs/kernel-language.md` — this is a user-visible diagnostic, so the choice belongs in the spec, not in whichever implementation was edited last. Qualified is the argument-carrying one (a short `C` does not say WHICH `C` among sibling scopes); the refusal is the one this repo's 'prefer a loud error over a silent skip' principle points at, and `_unknown` is precisely the placeholder-that-reads-as-handled that principle names. Note rustland has NO drifting raise site of the kind scaland's pass-4 import retry was — both its `UnresolvedName` sites (~:8605, ~:8686) already go through the helper. ACCEPTANCE: a test that DRIVES it — provoke the diagnostic and assert the rendered scope name, not that an error came back; say at the site which test fails when the change is backed out. Related: WI-962, WI-957."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-977", name: "rustland")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-962", description: some(value: "SCALAND: `UnresolvedName.scopeName` IS FILLED THREE WAYS, AND ONE OF THEM IS THE LITERAL STRING \"requires\". The field renders as `unresolved name 'x' in scope '<scopeName>'`, so it is a claim about WHICH SCOPE WAS SEARCHED. Its call sites disagree about what it means. (1) `Loader.load`'s pending-import retry passes `p.scopeName` = the IMPORTED PATH (`PendingImport.scopeName`) — a different but defensible meaning, 'the scope searched INTO'. (2) `processRequires` hardcodes `\"requires\"`, which names no scope at all: the reader is told the error happened 'in scope requires'. It is the WRONG one, and cheaply so — `processRequires` ALREADY has `scopeTerm: TermId` in hand, which is exactly what WI-957's `Loader.scopeDisplayName` turns into a real qualified name (`demo.C`). (3) WI-957's own `AmbiguousSymbol` reads it off `scopeTerm` via that helper. WORK: give the requires site the real scope name, and decide whether the pending-import meaning stays distinct (if so, SAY so at the field, because two meanings on one field with no note is how (2) got written). WHILE THERE: `scopeDisplayName` is currently `Loader`-private with ONE caller, which is why WI-957 left it there — a second caller is what justifies promoting it next to `KnowledgeBase.qualifiedNameOf`, so that 'what do we call this scope in a diagnostic' has ONE answer rather than a helper each. `isSortScope` already open-codes the same 'unwrap a scope term to its nullary Fn functor' step, making it a third instance of the unwrap. ACCEPTANCE: the existing `DiagnosticLocationTest` case 'an unresolved name is located at the name' asserts only the SPAN today — extend it (or add a sibling) to assert the scope NAME is the enclosing sort's qualified name, and say at the site which test fails when the change is backed out. NOTE this is pre-existing — WI-957 did not introduce it, it made the fix available. Related: WI-961."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "scaland-sbt-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-04T05:10:27Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-980", description: some(value: "A RULE HEAD's binding depends on DECLARATION ORDER. It must not.\n\nMUST AGREE WITH docs/proposals/059-secondary-entries.md R6, which defines the\ncorrect behaviour; this ticket implements it. Do not re-decide the policy here --\nif the implementation cannot meet R6, amend 059 rather than diverging from it.\n\nTHE POLICY IS NOT IN QUESTION. kernel-language.md §\"A rule head functor is\nresolved, not declared\" (WI-896) already says it: the functor runs the ordinary\nladder and the rule contributes a clause to whatever it lands on; only when the\nladder finds NOTHING does the rule introduce the name, scoped where written. The\ndefect is WHEN \"the ladder finds nothing\" is evaluated -- measured, against a\nHALF-BUILT name table, so textual order decides the answer.\n\nMEASURED (resolving the goal after load; counts are answers):\n\n  namespace s2                      namespace s1\n    rule p(1)                         sort Rec\n    sort Rec                            entity Rec(n: Int64)\n      entity Rec(n: Int64)              rule p(2)\n      rule p(2)                       end\n    end                               rule p(1)\n  end                               end\n\n  s2.p(2)=1   s2.Rec.p=<no symbol>    s1.p(2)=0   s1.Rec.p(2)=1\n\nSame pair, opposite order, two different programs: one predicate with two\nclauses, versus two predicates with one clause each.\n\nNOT A COMPANION QUESTION -- the identical split occurs with `namespace Rec ...\nend` (a proposal-059 secondary entry) in place of the sort body: enclosing rule\nwritten before => joins; written after => mints `Rec.p`. Both spellings agree\nwith each other and disagree with themselves across orders.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS: this is the source-order dependence the WI-925/926 category-set\nwork exists to remove, in the one name kind that still has it. It also silently\ndecides whether a rule EXTENDS someone else's predicate -- and extension is\nnon-monotone: MEASURED, `rule q(0) :- not p(2)` has one answer, and adding a\nsingle `rule p(2)` from an inner scope takes it to zero. A true statement becomes\nfalse, and proofs discharged from it (WI-537/538/539/558) were verified against a\nknowledge base that no longer exists. Which of the two readings you get should not\nturn on where in the file someone put a line.\n\nREQUIRED BEHAVIOUR (059 R6): the binding is computed against the FINISHED\nprogram, never the scanned prefix -- scopes outermost-first, all of a scope's\nheads together. The two programs above must both give the FIRST row's answer\n(`p(2)=1`, no `Rec.p`), since in the finished program `p` does resolve from\ninside `Rec`. A scope wanting its own name where an enclosing one resolves\nDECLARES it, the remedy §WI-896 already prescribes.\n\nLIKELY MECHANISM, to confirm rather than assume: the mint decision is taken in\n`scan_rule` during scan_definitions sub-pass 1, i.e. while the table that\ndecides it is still being built. Every other name kind is order-free because\npass 1 defines all of them before any pass 2 runs (the WI-321 cross-file\nmutual-recursion invariant); a rule head escapes it by being introduced during\nthat pass. Note sub-pass 3 also registers unlabeled rule head-functor Goals --\nestablish which pass actually takes the decision before changing either.\n\nWATCH FOR: a circular case the fix must define rather than stumble into -- an\ninner and an outer head that would each introduce, with nothing else declaring\nthe name. Outermost-first makes the inner one see the outer and join; confirm\nthat is what lands.\n\nMIGRATION RISK, to MEASURE FIRST: today's join-vs-mint split is load-bearing\nsomewhere or it is not, and the answer decides the size of this ticket. Before\nchanging behaviour, count the places in stdlib / anthill-stl / examples /\nanthill-todo / fixtures where an inner rule head currently MINTS only because\nits enclosing predicate is declared later in the file -- each becomes a join and\nchanges meaning. If that count is not zero, the fix needs a migration, not just\na reorder of passes.\n\nACCEPTANCE: the two programs above load to the same KB. A test asserts, for BOTH\norders and BOTH spellings (sort body and secondary entry), the same predicate\nidentity and the same answer counts -- four rows, since a pair that agrees in\nonly one order measures nothing. Say at the test site which rows fail when the\nfix is backed out (the enclosing-rule-written-after rows do; the others pass\neither way, by design, as the control). cargo-test green via\nrustland/scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Open)
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-981", author: "user", content: "MEASURED (2026-08-04), the count this ticket exists for. Probe: for every\ndeclared symbol, ask what its short name would resolve to in its declaring scope\nwith step 1 (locals) skipped -- the same ladder `resolve_in_scope_recursive`\nruns, steps 1b and 2 only -- and record BY WHICH ROUTE the name was already\nreachable. Corpus: stdlib/anthill + rustland/anthill-stl/anthill + examples/ +\nanthill-todo, loaded as one program.\n\n  63 capture sites total   =   import 4  +  enclosing-scope 40  +  requires/provides 19\n  (stdlib alone: 56 = 4 + 33 + 19; examples/anthill-todo add 7, all enclosing)\n\nSO THE CLAUSE CANNOT LAND AS WRITTEN -- 63 refusals, including the stdlib's\ncentral pattern. Nor does the narrowing this ticket anticipated rescue it:\n\"import route only\" is 4 sites, and all four are `FiniteFilteredStream.collect`\n/ `FiniteMappedStream.iterator` and kin -- a sort declaring the operation of a\nspec whose name it imported. Legitimate overrides, every one.\n\nMOST OF THE 63 ARE NOT MEMBER DECLARATIONS AT ALL, and should never have been in\nscope:\n  - PARAMS. `Float.pow.exp`, `String.contains.sub`, `KB.assert.kb` and many more\n    are an operation's PARAMETER shadowing an operation name. A binder is not a\n    declaration.\n  - TYPE NAMES. `IndexedSeq.Effect` over `prelude.Effect`, `TypeExtractor.Error`\n    over `prelude.Error`, `logic.Classical.Classical` over its own namespace.\n    Dot dispatch never routes through these.\n\nRESTRICTED TO THE R4-RELEVANT SHAPE -- a declared OPERATION capturing an\nOPERATION:\n\n  25 total  =  import 4  +  enclosing-scope 2  +  requires/provides 19\n\n  23 of the 25 are the OVERRIDE pattern: a member implementing an operation of a\n  spec the sort requires/provides (`List.length` over `IndexedSeq.length`,\n  `List.head` over `Stream.head`, ...), reached either through the requires\n  parent link or through an import of the spec's member. Refusing these would\n  refuse the way the standard library is written.\n\n  EXACTLY 2 have no override relationship, and both are the P1a shape -- a sort's\n  operation shadowing a namespace-level operation of the same name:\n\n      anthill.reflect.KB.nonvar   captures  anthill.reflect.nonvar\n      anthill.reflect.KB.ground   captures  anthill.reflect.ground\n\nVERDICT. The only formulation with a defensible blast radius is: refuse a MEMBER\nOPERATION that captures an OPERATION with NO OVERRIDE RELATIONSHIP -- the\ncaptured name is not a member of a spec the declaring sort requires or provides,\nnor imported from one. That is 2 sites, both in anthill.reflect, and both would\nneed a decision (rename, or exempt as intentional) before it lands.\n\nWORTH WEIGHING BEFORE IMPLEMENTING: at 2 sites, a WARNING may be the right\ninstrument rather than a refusal -- WI-346 already warns for the neighbouring\nrequires-shadow case, and this would be its sibling. That is a call for the\nproposal (059 R4), not for this ticket; 059 has been updated with the numbers.\n\nNOT MEASURED: inline test fixtures. The corpus above is what is on disk; a\nfixture that captures would be found by the guard test when the rule lands, not\nby this count.", at: "2026-08-04T07:56:06Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-981", author: "user", content: "CLAUSES 1 AND 2 MEASURED TOO (2026-08-04) -- the earlier feedback covered only\nclause 3. Asked because \"is R4 implementable\" cannot be answered from clause 3\nalone.\n\nDETECTION PROBLEM FIRST: clauses 1 and 2 CANNOT be checked by walking symbols.\n`SymbolTable::define` MERGES two same-named declarations in one scope into one\nsymbol, so by the time symbols exist the duplication is gone -- the 63-site\nprobe walked symbols and therefore could not have seen a duplicate at all. They\nneed each DECLARATION recorded as pass 1 makes it, keyed on (scope_raw, local\nname). That is the same ledger R1 needs for duplicate TYPE declarations, so it\nis one piece of machinery serving both rules.\n\nMEASURED that way (recorders on the Item::Operation, Item::Const and\nItem::OperationBlock arms of scan_items_pass1), over stdlib + anthill-stl +\nexamples + anthill-todo:\n\n  427 operation/const declarations recorded\n  NAMES DECLARED MORE THAN ONCE IN ONE SCOPE: 0\n  (stdlib alone: 371 declarations, 0 duplicates)\n\nSo clauses 1 and 2 land with NO MIGRATION.\n\nHONEST CAVEAT: the zero is easy. The corpus contains no secondary entries at all\n(WI-975 measured that -- the only twice-declared names in the workspace are three\nhand-written test fixtures), so clause 2 has nothing to bite on yet. Clause 1\nalso covers two same-named operations in one MAIN entry, and that is genuinely\nzero.\n\nNOT COUNTED: operations declared inside `provides ... language ... end` blocks\nreach the KB by another path and were not recorded. If the ledger becomes the\nreal implementation, cover that arm too.\n\nCLAUSE 3, RESTATED FROM THE FIRST MEASUREMENT: it must key on the OVERRIDE\nRELATION -- is the captured operation a member of a spec the declaring sort\nrequires or provides -- and NOT on the route the name was reached by. 4 of the\nlegitimate overrides arrive through an `import` of the spec's member rather than\nthrough the `requires` link, so a route-based rule refuses exactly the wrong\nfour. The relation is known only after pass 2, so that check runs there, not in\npass 1 beside the ledger.\n\nSUMMARY FOR THE IMPLEMENTER:\n  clause 1  0 sites   pass-1 declaration ledger (shared with R1)\n  clause 2  0 sites   same ledger\n  clause 3  2 sites   override relation, post-pass-2; both sites in\n                      anthill.reflect need a verdict first", at: "2026-08-04T08:05:57Z")
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-983", name: "scaland")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-975", author: "user", content: "CLOSED 2026-08-08. Resolved by WI-997: the pass-1 declaration ledger now refuses two written type declarations sharing one scope and local name, with diagnostics and controls for the legal eponymous-constructor shape. Proposal 059 keeps that decision as R1.", at: "2026-08-08T13:27:08Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-981", author: "user", content: "CLOSED 2026-08-08. The migration measurement is complete. Implementation is split among WI-1049 for duplicate operation declarations, WI-1000 for the secondary-entry body requirement, and WI-999 for capture under WI-1048 refinement semantics.", at: "2026-08-08T13:27:08Z")
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-984", name: "rustland")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-984", author: "user", content: "FROM WI-983 (delivered): DO NOT RESTATE THIS ACCEPTANCE AS A CALLER COUNT. WI-983's original acceptance was \"`scopeTerm` is left with exactly ONE caller in the tree\" and it was unreachable, contradicting that same ticket's own DO-NOT-CONVERT sentence. The miscount came from reading SIX loader assert sites as six pure conversions: at `entity_of` and at the provides-clause the ONE computed scope term fills a fact ARGUMENT as well as the domain, so typing the domain deletes four of the six calls, not six — and `provides_clause`'s `sort_ref`, a third term-level reader, had gone unlisted entirely. A site can serve two roles at once, so \"how many callers remain\" is the wrong shape for a criterion; \"no caller is in role X, and the rest are all role Y\" is the right one, and it is checkable. WI-984's own acceptance is already the better shape — a scope cannot be built from a raw integer or a term, said as a COMPILE ERROR — so keep it that way and resist tightening it into a count. Rustland is less exposed here regardless: `KnowledgeBase` already stores `domain: Symbol`, so it has no domain slot to convert; the term-level readers are the sites to leave alone.", at: "2026-08-04T08:28:17Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-976", description: some(value: "SCALAND: SCOPE-HOOD IS UNTYPED, SO 'THIS TERMID IS A SCOPE' IS A CLAIM EVERY CALLER RE-MAKES AT RUNTIME. WI-962 gave 'what do we call this scope in a diagnostic' one answer (`KnowledgeBase.scopeDisplayName`, over `scopeFunctor`), but both still take a bare `TermId` and both still have a not-a-scope arm: `scopeFunctor` answers `None` and `scopeDisplayName` THROWS. That arm is unreachable in fact — `makeNameTermFromSym` is the sole producer of a scope term and builds only a nullary `Term.Fn` (WI-962 funnelled the other two producers through it) — so it is a runtime check standing in for a type. The same untyped-ness runs deeper: `SymbolTable` keys NINE methods on a raw `scopeRaw: Int` (`define`, `addExposed`, `addTypeParam`, `addImport`, `addParent`, `scope`, `scopeMut`, `resolveInScope`, `resolveRecursive`), `SymbolDef.Resolved` carries `scopeRaw: Int`, and `ScopeInclusion` carries `parentScopeRaw: Int` — so a scope, an arbitrary term, and an arbitrary integer are the same type today. WORK: an opaque `ScopeId` minted ONLY by the producer, threaded through the `SymbolTable` API; then `scopeFunctor` needs no `Option` and `scopeDisplayName` needs no throw, by construction rather than by comment. CONSIDERED AND REJECTED as the primary fix: an opaque `ScopeName = String` on `LoadError`, which would make the literal `\"requires\"` WI-962 removed a compile error — cheap (~10 lines) but it types the SYMPTOM: it equally accepts the right type of name computed from the wrong scope, which is the likelier next drift. Do it only as a rider if `ScopeId` proves too big. ACCEPTANCE: `scopeDisplayName` has no failure arm, and the deleted `IllegalStateException` has no replacement; say at the site which test fails when the change is backed out. Related: WI-962, WI-957."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "scaland-sbt-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-08-04T08:28:28Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-983", description: some(value: "SCALAND: A RULE'S DOMAIN IS A SCOPE, SPELLED AS AN UNTYPED TERM. WI-976 typed scope-hood (`ScopeId`, minted from a symbol) through `SymbolTable`, `SymbolDef.Resolved`, `ScopeInclusion` and the whole loader spine — but stopped at the KB's write boundary. `assertRule`/`assertFact` still take `domain: TermId`, so every production caller converts on the spot: `Loader` calls `kb.scopeTerm(scope)` at SIX assert sites (fact, entity_of, rule, proof, provides-clause, provides-block fact). FOUR of the six compute that term for the domain slot ALONE — fact, rule, proof, provides-block fact — and lose the call along with it; at `entity_of` and at provides-clause the SAME term also fills a fact ARGUMENT, so those two calls SURVIVE the conversion: the slot changes, the call does not. Three facts say the slot should be a scope: rustland already stores it as a symbol (`kb/mod.rs`, `domain: Symbol`), so scaland's `TermId` is the divergence and not the parity; `byDomain`/`ruleDomain`/`factDomain` have ZERO callers anywhere in scaland, so nothing reads the term form; and the domain is only ever a `byDomain_` index key plus one equality test in `assertFact`'s dedup. WORK: `domain: ScopeId` on `assertRule`/`assertFact`/`RuleEntry`, and key `byDomain_` on the scope ITSELF (`HashMap[ScopeId, …]` — the shape `SymbolTable.scopes` already uses and rustland's `HashMap<Symbol, Vec<RuleId>>` mirrors), not on an unwrapped raw. Two efficiency findings fall out for free rather than needing a hoist: `loadRuleHeads` recomputes `scopeTerm` once per rule in a rule BLOCK, and `loadProvidesBlock`'s FactI arm recomputes it per item, both for a scope constant across the loop. DO NOT convert the TERM-LEVEL readers — each needs a term because a term is what goes in that position — and there are THREE of them, not the two first written here: `registerEntityOf`'s parent sort, the `entity_of` fact's embedded argument, and `provides_clause`'s `sort_ref` argument. Cost is concentrated in tests (~24 sites, mechanically `kb.makeNameTerm(\"test\")` -> `ScopeId.of(kb.intern(\"test\"))`). ACCEPTANCE (amended — the original was unreachable; the miscount behind it is recorded as feedback on WI-984, which inherits this ticket's reasoning): ZERO of `scopeTerm`'s callers is a domain slot, and the FOUR that remain are all term-level — `registerEntityOf`'s parent sort, the `entity_of` argument, `provides_clause`'s `sort_ref`, and `Builtins.executeScope`'s bound answer. Say at the site which test fails when the change is backed out. Related: WI-976, WI-984."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "scaland-sbt-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-08-04T08:28:40Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-982", description: some(value: "`reflect.KB.nonvar` / `reflect.KB.ground` RE-DERIVE the resolver's answer on a\nraw TermId. They must be CARRIER-NEUTRAL -- one owner per question.\n\nFOUND WHILE DRIVING WI-981 (proposal 059 R4). The capture clause flagged\n`anthill.reflect.KB.nonvar` as shadowing `anthill.reflect.nonvar`, and the\nshadow turned out to be the smaller half of the finding: the QUESTION is\nimplemented twice.\n\n  \"is this a variable?\"\n    resolver  resolve.rs:3474 builtin_nonvar -> walk_arg (chases σ) +\n              value_is_unbound_var; carrier-neutral via TermView;\n              THREE-VALUED (Failure / delay / Success)\n    host      anthill-stl/src/reflect/builtins.rs:804 kb_nonvar ->\n              !matches!(interp.kb().get_term(tid), CoreTerm::Var(_));\n              raw TermId; TWO-VALUED Bool\n\n  \"is this ground?\"\n    resolver  resolve.rs:3483 builtin_ground -> value_is_ground, documented as\n              \"the shared core of the ground(?x) builtin and the NAF groundness\n              gate, so neither materializes the goal to a TermId\"; handles\n              Value::Term (chasing σ into subterms), Value::Node occurrences,\n              and value-level logic vars\n    host      builtins.rs:812 kb_ground ->\n              interp.kb().collect_vars(tid).is_empty()\n\nTHREE DIFFERENCES, not one:\n  1. σ-AWARENESS. The resolver chases the substitution; the host copy never\n     consults σ. A term whose vars are all BOUND by σ is ground to one and not\n     ground to the other.\n  2. CARRIER. `expect_term` (builtins.rs:792) accepts ONLY Value::Term -- every\n     other carrier is a hard TypeMismatch. So the host copy cannot see the\n     Value::Node occurrence carrier that value_is_ground handles explicitly.\n  3. VALUEDNESS. delay vs plain Bool.\n\nTHE JUSTIFICATION IS PHASE-SCOPED, AND THE LANGUAGE HAS OTHER PHASES. kb_nonvar's\ndoc says \"Eval-time runs after SLD, so arguments are already grounded where they\nwill be; no DELAY semantics needed here.\" That is true of the post-SLD path and\nfalse of at least two others that are DELIVERED, not planned:\n\n  - COMPILE-TIME MACRO EXPANSION (WI-722 / proposal 043.1). simp_rewrite.rs:788+,\n    in its own words: \"We bind the macro's params to those occurrences as\n    Value::Node -- NOT materialized: the flatten in bridge_op_to_eval is\n    deliberately skipped ... so occurrence structure survives -- and run the body\n    through the WI-625 scratch interpreter.\" Arguments are occurrences carrying\n    pattern variables. Both halves of the assumption fail: not Terms, not ground.\n  - ABSTRACT INTERPRETATION ON SUSPEND (WI-580, design/abstract-interpreter-and-\n    rules.md §3.3, delivered 2026-07-09). `unfold_eq_operand` /\n    body_specialize.rs::folded_call_match case-splits a bodied op-call's body\n    PRECISELY BECAUSE the call is unground.\n\nSo the comment records an assumption about one caller, not an equivalence.\n\nMEASURED, not inferred. Driver in anthill-stl's own `reflect::builtins::tests`\nmodule, where both owners are reachable (anthill-stl depends on anthill-core, so\na test there can build a KB, resolve a goal, AND call the registered host fns --\nan earlier attempt from anthill-core failed only because register_reflect_builtins\nlives in anthill-stl):\n\n  [resolver] ground(5)                 => 1 solution     (\"yes, ground\")\n  [host]     KB.ground(kb, Int(5))     => ERROR: type mismatch: expected Term,\n                                                 got Int64 for KB.ground\n  [host]     KB.ground(kb, Str(\"hi\"))  => ERROR: ... got String\n  [host]     KB.ground(kb, Bool(true)) => ERROR: ... got Bool\n  [host]     CONTROL  Term(5)          => Bool(true)\n\nThe same question about the same value gets \"ground\" from one owner and a hard\ntype error from the other. The CONTROL is the point: they agree exactly when the\ncarrier happens to be Value::Term, i.e. the host answers BY CARRIER, not by\ncontent.\n\nSTATUS TODAY: the carrier divergence is driven (above). What is still LATENT is\nreaching it from the phases named above -- no .anthill file calls `.nonvar(` or\n`.ground(` on a receiver (measured across stdlib, anthill-stl, examples,\nanthill-todo), so no macro body or unfolded body hits it yet.\n\nWHAT TO DECIDE WHILE FIXING: value_is_ground takes a &Substitution and eval has\nnone. Delegation therefore means using the carrier-neutral VALUE-level check\nwhere σ is not needed (occurrence_has_unbound_var already needs none), not\nthreading a σ that does not exist. Whether the reflect API stays two-valued is a\nseparate call: Bool is defensible for an API that can SEE every carrier -- the\ndelay is a resolver concern -- but it must not be Bool BECAUSE it cannot see.\n\nRELATED: WI-535 migrated this bridge off bare TermId for other operations and\nnamed `nonvar/ground` in its scope; this is the part that did not move.\n\nTHREE FURTHER FINDINGS (2026-08-04), each measured, which change the shape of\nthe fix.\n\n1. THE `kb` PARAMETER IS DISPATCH CEREMONY, NOT A DEPENDENCY. Every reflect host\nfn that takes a KB receiver IGNORES it:\n\n     `let [_kb, ...]` (ignored):  11        `let [kb, ...]` (used):  0\n     uses of `interp.kb()` instead:  64\n\nThe KB comes from the interpreter's ambient context. The parameter exists only so\nthe operation DOT-DISPATCHES -- the receiver of a dot call is the first param, so\n`operation nonvar(kb: KB, x: Term)` is what makes `kb.nonvar(x)` a member call.\nConsequence for this ticket: delegating to the carrier-neutral predicate threads\nNOTHING; the member stays a receiver-shaped wrapper and `kb` stays ceremony. Note\nalso that the parameter which creates the 059 name collision is the one the\nimplementation discards.\n\n2. THE COLLISION IS NOT LATENT -- IT BITES IN VALUE POSITION. Measured:\n\n     import anthill.reflect.nonvar ... operation driver() -> Bool = nonvar(1)\n       => REFUSED: \"`nonvar` is a member of sort KB, not in scope as a bare name\n          here; call it qualified as `KB.nonvar(...)` or via a receiver\"\n     CONTROL, the same builtin in GOAL position (a rule body)   => works\n     fully qualified `anthill.reflect.nonvar(1)` in a body      => SAME REFUSAL\n\nSo the 1-ary resolver builtin is reachable as a GOAL and unreachable from an\nOPERATION BODY, and the diagnostic sends the author to the 2-ary member, which is\nnot what was asked for.\n\nNOT ESTABLISHED, and worth pinning first: the fully-qualified row. The name was\nwritten `anthill.reflect.nonvar` and still drew the member's diagnostic. Either\nthe qualified path genuinely resolves onto `anthill.reflect.KB.nonvar`, or the\nWI-565 hint is misattributed from the SHORT name. Those are different bugs --\none is resolution, one is a diagnostic -- and the fix differs.\n\n3. CONVERGENCE: PASS KB IN NEITHER, NOT IN BOTH (user's framing). Passing it in\nboth is out -- the param is ignored, and adding it to the resolver form would put\nceremony in rule bodies, the one place it is pure noise. Passing it in neither\nconverges the two onto ONE NAME and ONE ARITY, which is what \"one owner\" wants\nanyway. THE OBSTACLE, measured: `bare_bodied_bool_relation` (resolve.rs:6234)\nroutes a bare bodied Bool op used as a goal to `eq(goal, true)` -- but BAILS for a\nBUILTIN or a BODY-LESS op, and both of these are exactly that. So convergence is\nnot free: it needs a verdict on whether one name may be both a goal-level builtin\nand a value-returning operation.\n\nCORRECTION to this ticket's own framing, from the user: CARRIER-NEUTRAL DOES NOT\nMEAN STORE-FREE. A neutral `Value` still has a `Value::Term { id }` variant, so a\nreader accepting every carrier still receives TermIds and still needs the store\nfor those; only `Value::Node` is self-describing. The rule is therefore not \"drop\nthe KB\" but \"DO NOT FORCE THE TermId CARRIER -- use the store only for the carrier\nthat needs it.\"\n\nACCEPTANCE: one owner per question -- KB.nonvar/KB.ground answer by the same\ncarrier-neutral predicate the resolver uses, not by a second TermId-only\nderivation. A test in anthill-stl drives each on a Value::Node carrier and on a\nnon-Term scalar, asserting an ANSWER rather than a TypeMismatch; and a control\nasserts the two owners agree on a plain Term, which they do today (so the\ncontrol passes before and after -- say so at the site). cargo-test green via\nrustland/scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-04T09:06:59Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-984", author: "user", content: "CODE REVIEW OF WI-976/WI-983 (2026-08-04) FOUND A TRAP THIS TICKET SETS FOR ITSELF. The RIDER above says `instantiation_term_raw` is \"written by ~18 sites and read by NONE\" and invites deleting it, as scaland did. The READ claim checks out. The WRITE claim is the trap: rustland's producer is `build_instantiation_term(kb, …)` (`anthill-core/src/kb/load.rs:2998`), which takes `&mut KnowledgeBase` and has SIDE EFFECTS the field's deadness says nothing about — `kb.symbols.intern(&sort_name)` (:3164), `kb.alloc(Term::Ref/Fn)` (:3155, :3179), and `find_scope_by_name(..).unwrap_or_else(|| kb.make_name_term(&n))` (:3159). Delete the field, find the call dead, delete the call — and symbols plus name terms a `requires` declaration used to intern into the KB stop existing, changing `by_qualified_name` / `try_resolve_symbol` / `resolve_in_scope` answers SILENTLY. The field is dead; the CALL is not. Do the rider as two steps with a measurement between them: drop the field and keep the call, confirm `by_qualified_name`'s contents are unchanged over the stdlib load, and only then decide whether the interning is load-bearing or itself vestigial.", at: "2026-08-04T09:04:44Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-984", author: "user", content: "A DIVERGENCE TO RECORD BEFORE STARTING, not to undo (found by the same review). WI-976 moved SCALAND's scope key from a TermId raw to a SYMBOL raw. Rustland's is still a TermId raw: `intern.rs:198` (`SymbolDef::Resolved.scope_raw: u32`), `:255` (`ScopeInclusion.parent_scope_raw`) and `:397` (`define(…, scope_raw)`) are all fed TERM ids — see `kb/load.rs:2226` (`parent_scope_raw: current_scope.raw()`) and `:2747` (`parent_scope_raw: sort_term.raw()`). The two numbers index DIFFERENT arrays (Symbol -> `defs`, TermId -> the term store), so `resolve_recursive` / `internal_visible_from` / `add_parent` are no longer transcribable in either direction: a fix ported literally between the two trees keys the wrong table and yields a scope that resolves nothing — silently, with no type error, because both are `u32`. Note rustland is already internally split on this (scope keys are term raws while `by_domain` keys are `Symbol`s), so the symbol form this ticket asks for is the more coherent end state; the point is that DURING the port neither implementation's line numbers can be trusted as a guide to the other's.", at: "2026-08-04T09:04:48Z")
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-985", name: "scaland")
