fact WorkItem(id: "WI-987", description: some(value: "BOTH IMPLEMENTATIONS: `_global` IS A LEGAL IDENTIFIER, SO THE SYNTHETIC GLOBAL SCOPE CAN BE SHADOWED BY A DECLARED ONE THAT RENDERS IDENTICALLY. The file-level scope is named by interning the spelling `_global` — scaland `KnowledgeBase.globalScope`, rustland `kb/mod.rs:5506` plus four more sites (`flow_derive.rs:83`, `execute.rs:445`, `extent.rs:621`, `body_specialize.rs:1162`). That spelling matches both identifier grammars (`tree-sitter-anthill/grammar.js`; scaland `Tokens.identToken`, whose first char class includes `_`). So a file containing `namespace _global` makes `define` register `byQualifiedName(\"_global\")` -> a symbol DIFFERENT from `intern(\"_global\")`, because `define` never consults the intern map. Two live scopes, and `scopeDisplayName` prints `_global` for both, so a WI-962-style diagnostic cannot say which one it means; worse, names the user expects at file level resolve against one of them and not the other. Nothing today rejects the declaration. WORK: pick a sentinel the identifier grammar CANNOT produce (e.g. one containing a character no token admits) so the collision is unrepresentable rather than merely unlikely, and change BOTH trees together — a one-sided change silently diverges the two diagnostics, and rustland reads the spelling in five places. Alternatively refuse `namespace _global` with a load diagnostic, which is cheaper but leaves the sentinel expressible. NOTE the related mint hazard is separate and filed as its own ticket: `scopeByQualifiedName` cannot name this scope at all, because `_global` is interned and never defined. ACCEPTANCE: a test that DECLARES `namespace _global` and asserts the chosen answer — a diagnostic, or a resolution that proves the two scopes are one. Say at the site which test fails when the change is backed out. Found by code review of WI-976/WI-983 (2026-08-04). Related: WI-983, WI-984."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "scaland-sbt-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-987", name: "scaland")
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-988", name: "scaland")
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-989", name: "scaland")
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-990", name: "scaland")
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-991", name: "scaland")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-985", description: some(value: "SCALAND: AN ENTITY'S \"PARENT SORT\" IS ITS ENCLOSING SCOPE, SO AN ENTITY UNDER A NAMESPACE IS AN ENTITY OF A NAMESPACE. `Loader.DefinePass`'s `EntityItem` arm calls `kb.registerEntityOf(entityTerm, kb.scopeTerm(scope))` where `scope` is whatever scope encloses the declaration. Inside `sort S ... end` that IS the parent sort and everything is right. Written directly under `namespace demo`, it registers `entityParent_(Foo) = Fn(demo)` — so `KnowledgeBase.isEntityOf(Foo, demo)` answers true, `bySort(demo)` sweeps in the entity's facts, and the `IsEntityOf` builtin reports an entity \"of\" a namespace. The `entity_of` FACT asserted in `LoadPass` (Loader.scala, the EntityItem arm) carries the same wrong second argument, so a reflect query sees it too. `stdlib/anthill/reflect/typing.anthill` already documents and guards this exact hazard for the RULE form; the KB-index form has no guard. WORK: decide what an entity outside a sort body means — parent `none`, a load diagnostic, or a distinguished marker — and make the registration say it rather than defaulting to the enclosing scope. A load diagnostic is the likely answer if the kernel language does not admit the form at all, in which case say so in `docs/kernel-language.md` too. ACCEPTANCE: a test that DECLARES an entity under a bare namespace and asserts the chosen answer by resolving `is_entity_of` — not by checking that the file loads. Say at the site which test fails when the change is backed out. Found by code review of WI-976/WI-983 (2026-08-04). Related: WI-983."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "scaland-sbt-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-08-04T11:10:43Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-986", description: some(value: "SCALAND: `requires`'s UNRESOLVED-NAME DIAGNOSTIC NAMES A SCOPE THE NAME WAS NEVER SEARCHED IN, AND CAN ASSERT SOMETHING FALSE. WI-962 replaced the literal string `\"requires\"` in `Loader.processRequires` with `kb.scopeDisplayName(scope)`. But the lookup two lines above is `kb.symbols.byQualifiedName.get(nameStr)` — a GLOBAL, fully-qualified lookup that walks no scope chain at all. So the message reports a scope that had nothing to do with the resolution. MEASURED: `namespace demo / sort Widget end / sort S { import demo.Widget; requires Widget } end` renders `unresolved name 'Widget' in scope 'demo.S'` — and `Widget` IS resolvable in `demo.S`, it is imported there. The reader is sent to inspect a scope in which the name is present, and the actual rule (a `requires` must be written fully qualified) is never stated. A TOP-LEVEL `requires nosuchspec` — grammatical, since `requiresDeclItem` is an alternative of `declaration` — renders `in scope '_global'`, trading a literal that named no scope for a synthetic name the reader cannot open. `LoadError`'s own field doc says `scopeName` is \"the scope the name was RESOLVED AGAINST — what the reader must inspect\"; this filler contradicts it. WORK: either make the lookup match the claim (resolve through the scope chain, so the name really was searched in the named scope) or make the claim match the lookup (say the requirement must be fully qualified, and name the requirement, not a scope). The first is the better language answer if a `requires` may name an imported spec; settle that against docs/kernel-language.md first. `DiagnosticLocationTest` covers only the inside-a-sort form with a name absent everywhere, which is why this passed. ACCEPTANCE: a test whose subject name IS resolvable in the named scope — the measured case above — asserting the rendered diagnostic is true of the tree it describes. Say at the site which test fails when the change is backed out. Found by code review (2026-08-04). Related: WI-962, WI-957."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "scaland-sbt-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-08-04T11:36:56Z"))
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-992", name: "scaland")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-993", description: some(value: "RUSTLAND: `find_scope_by_name` FINDS ANY SYMBOL, NOT A SCOPE — SO A WILDCARD IMPORT OF A NON-SCOPE IS A SILENT NO-OP. The twin of WI-988's first case, and here the misnomer is doing the hiding. `kb/load.rs:3087` `fn find_scope_by_name(kb, qualified) -> Option<TermId>` is a bare `by_qualified_name.get(qualified)` followed by a nullary `Term::Fn` build: it asks nothing about scope-hood and nothing about the symbol's KIND. Its one caller is the `ImportKind::Wildcard` arm (`:3434`), which on `Some(target_scope)` does `add_parent(scope.raw(), ScopeInclusion { parent_scope_raw: target_scope.raw(), .. })` and on `None` raises `UnresolvedImport`. So `import demo.Host.op1.*` where `op1` is an OPERATION takes the success path: a parent is linked to a scope no `define` ever filled, `add_parent` creates the importing side's record and never the parent's, and resolution through that link answers nothing. The import contributes nothing and says nothing — the shape CLAUDE.md's development principles forbid (\"prefer a loud error over a silent skip\"). Scaland's fix is WI-988: a `parentScopeOf` gate that admits only a namespace or a sort (§5.1 names both as having contents) and otherwise raises a `LoadError` naming the symbol AND the kind it actually got, because \"did nothing\" is not a diagnosis a reader can act on. WORK: mirror it, and rename the helper to what it does — either make `find_scope_by_name` genuinely check scope-hood, or call it `find_symbol_by_name` and put the check at the call site. NOTE the `requires` half of WI-988 has NO twin here: rustland's `load_requires_decl` emits a `SortRequiresInfo` fact rather than linking a parent scope, so there is no parent-link hole on that side — confirm before mirroring. ACCEPTANCE: a test that wildcard-imports an operation and asserts the DIAGNOSTIC, plus a probe that the imported name resolves to nothing (the probe is what makes the diagnostic worth raising). Say at the site which test fails when the change is backed out. Related: WI-988."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-993", name: "rustland")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-988", description: some(value: "SCALAND: THE `requires`/`import` PATH DROPS THREE CASES SILENTLY, WHICH THE PROJECT FORBIDS. All three are in `Loader`, all three load with ZERO diagnostics, and all three leave a name the user wrote doing nothing. (1) A WILDCARD IMPORT FROM A NAME THAT IS NOT A SCOPE. `processImports`'s `Wildcard` arm does `addParent(scope, ScopeInclusion(ScopeId.of(sym), …))` on a symbol of ANY kind. MEASURED: `sort User { import demo.Host.op1.* }` where `op1` is an OPERATION loads clean, and `resolveInScope(\"op1\", demo.User)` answers `NotFound` — `addParent` creates the IMPORTING scope's record but never the parent's, and `resolveRecursive` then treats the missing parent as eligible and finds nothing. (2) The same shape in `processRequires`, where a `requires` naming a non-sort symbol links a parent that resolves nothing. (3) `case _ => // Parameterized requires — TODO` in `processRequires` drops `requires Ord[T]` entirely: no scope-parent link, no message, and every name the requirement was meant to bring into scope then fails to resolve later — where `resolveName`'s `NotFound` arm answers `kb.intern(name)`, also with no diagnostic. OUTCOME: (1) and (2) DELIVERED — a `parentScopeOf` gate admits only a namespace or a sort for a wildcard import and only a sort for a `requires` (§5.1/§5.2), and otherwise raises naming the symbol AND the kind it actually got, because \"did nothing\" is not a diagnosis a reader can act on. (3) MOVED TO WI-992, measured rather than assumed: routing the parameterized form through ordinary resolution fails EIGHT tests, and not one of them is a binding — every failure is a base name that does not resolve, e.g. `requires PartialEq[T]` inside `sort anthill.prelude.Eq`. The cause is not in this clause: a top-level DOTTED declaration is defined in `_global` under its whole dotted spelling, so a sibling's SHORT name is unreachable from it, which is also why the stdlib's only two bare `requires` are its only two fully-qualified ones. Deferring it kept a name-resolution change out of a stop-dropping-things-silently ticket; the arm now carries that reasoning and the WI-992 pointer instead of a bare TODO. The rustland twin of (1) is WI-993 — `find_scope_by_name` there checks nothing about scope-hood; (2) has no twin, rustland's `requires` emits a fact rather than linking a parent. ACCEPTANCE MET: one test per delivered case asserting the diagnostic, plus the probe that the import contributes nothing; both fail with the gate deleted and no other test moves. Related: WI-992, WI-993, WI-986."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "scaland-sbt-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-08-04T11:57:41Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-982", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED (2026-08-04). One question, one owner; one name, one arity, both phases.\n\nTHERE WERE THREE OWNERS, NOT TWO. The ticket named the resolver and\nreflect/builtins.rs. reflect/bridge.rs had a third -- KbBridge::nonvar / ground,\nwhose comment read \"Mirrors the core resolver's `value_is_ground`\" and did not.\nBoth holes MEASURED, by putting each deleted body back under the new test:\n\n  KbBridge::ground   Value::Entity{ red, [unbound] }  => true    (resolver: false)\n  KbBridge::nonvar   Value::Node(Expr::Var(_))        => true    (resolver: false)\n\nNeither is a TypeMismatch. A WRONG ANSWER, silently. `ground` had no\nEntity/Tuple arm, so a compound with unbound children fell to `_ => true`;\n`nonvar` had no Value::Node arm and did the same thing one variant over.\n\nFINDING 2's UNESTABLISHED ROW -- BOTH OPTIONS REFUTED. The ticket offered a\ndichotomy: \"either the qualified path genuinely resolves onto\nanthill.reflect.KB.nonvar, or the WI-565 hint is misattributed from the SHORT\nname.\" It is a third thing, and a sibling builtin was the discriminator:\n\n  `anthill.reflect.qualified_name(1)` in an operation body\n    => \"type mismatch in qualified_name.s (op-arg): expected Symbol, got Int64\"\n\nThat call TYPED. So the qualified path resolves correctly and the typer has a\ndeclaration to type it against. `nonvar` differed because `anthill.reflect.nonvar`\nWAS DECLARED NOWHERE -- the symbol existed only because register_builtin_tag mints\none for an undeclared name. A tag dispatches a GOAL; it does not type a CALL. So\nthe value-position call fell to the typer's path-3 unknown-functor edge, and the\nWI-565 refinement -- which keys on the SHORT name -- found KB.nonvar and named it.\nResolution was never wrong; the misattributed hint was the second-order symptom\nof a missing declaration.\n\nCensus, because the class matters more than the instance: of the 25 registered\nbuiltin tags, `nonvar` and `ground` were the ONLY two that were an operation in\nintent and declared nowhere. Every other reflect tag has its namespace-level\n`operation`; is_entity_of is a rule, ho_apply an entity, find_dictionary the\n`requires` desugaring target, neq/lt/gt/... spec members. The class is closed --\nno follow-up ticket.\n\nFINDING 3's VERDICT: YES, AND THE STDLIB ALREADY ANSWERS IT EIGHT TIMES. \"whether\none name may be both a goal-level builtin and a value-returning operation\" --\nqualified_name, short_name, lookup_symbol, scope, kind, not, field_access and\nresolve_sort_instantiation_param are each DECLARED and TAGGED today.\n`not(query: Term) -> Bool` is the exact shape: 1-ary, -> Bool, body-less, tagged.\nThe obstacle the ticket named is not one: bare_bodied_bool_relation bails for a\nbuiltin precisely because a builtin goal never needs it -- it dispatches on its\ntag. Convergence was free, so I took the framing recorded in the ticket: PASS KB\nIN NEITHER. KB.nonvar / KB.ground are gone.\n\nWHAT LANDED\n  stdlib  `operation nonvar(x: Term) -> Bool` / `ground(x: Term) -> Bool` at\n          namespace level; both members deleted from `sort KB`.\n  core    value_is_unbound_var / value_is_ground are `pub` -- THE one owner --\n          plus value_is_ground_no_subst, which is where the sigma question lands:\n          eval has no ambient sigma (Interpreter's SubstArena holds first-class\n          Value::Substitution DATA, not a pending resolution state), so the EMPTY\n          sigma is the truth, not a stand-in for a missing one. ONE named entry\n          point rather than an empty sigma conjured at each call site -- a second\n          predicate is exactly how these owners drifted apart.\n  stl     nonvar_op / ground_op, 1-ary, delegating; KbBridge's two deleted.\n\nTHE COMPILER NOW ENFORCES IT. The `KB` trait is GENERATED from reflect.anthill\n(anthill-stl/build.rs, emit_only) and KbBridge implements it, so a fourth copy\ncannot be added to bridge.rs without the trait declaring it, and the trait cannot\ndeclare it without the sort. Deleting the two members deleted the two trait\nmethods; the build proved it.\n\n059 R4 GOES TO ZERO. WI-981 measured exactly 2 no-override captures, and they were\nthese two members. Clause 3 now bites nothing in the corpus and lands with no\nmigration; proposal updated (61/38 in the class table, 2 -> 0 in both tables). The\nverdict was neither of the two the count anticipated (rename / exempt as\nintentional): the CAPTURED names were the ones worth keeping, so the members went.\n\nTESTS, and what each measures\n  nonvar_and_ground_answer_by_content_not_carrier -- 9 carriers x 2 questions x 2\n  doors. MUTATION-VERIFIED three ways: with the old expect_term body every\n  non-control row is a TypeMismatch; with the deleted KbBridge::ground body only\n  the Entity row fails; with the deleted KbBridge::nonvar body only the Node row\n  fails. The two Value::Term rows are labelled CONTROL at the site -- they pass\n  before and after, and that IS the finding: the owners agreed exactly on the one\n  carrier the host could see.\n    Also stated at the site so the resolver rows are not over-read: after the\n    change both doors reach ONE predicate, so they are no longer an independent\n    second opinion -- they are the assertion that the doors stay wired to the same\n    owner, and they fail the moment either grows its own derivation again. Before\n    the change they WERE independent, which is how the divergence was found.\n  nonvar_and_ground_are_callable_from_an_operation_body -- the row the ticket found\n  unreachable, DRIVEN rather than loaded: both spellings it measured (bare via\n  import, fully qualified) are CALLED and their values asserted, so a name\n  resolving to nothing fails here instead of passing quietly.\n  kb_nonvar_and_ground_classify_terms is deleted but not lost -- it is the two\n  CONTROL rows above; a comment at its old site says so.\n  Goal position is unchanged and has real controls: the six resolve.rs\n  nonvar_*/ground_* tests, wi670_floundered_rule_test, and the stdlib's own\n  typing.anthill:48 `nonvar(?x)` guard all stay green.\n\nFull workspace green via scripts/test.sh. scaland unaffected -- it registers the\nsame 1-ary tags and has no eval-time reflect host fns. docs/status/wi-047.md still\nnames KB.nonvar/KB.ground; left alone deliberately, it is a dated delivery record.\n\nNOT DONE, DELIBERATELY, NO TICKET FILED. expect_term still forces the Value::Term\ncarrier for sort_as_term / can_be_sort / term_as_sort. Same CLASS, different\nquestion, and I have no measurement that it bites -- nothing routes a Value::Node\nto them today. The 9 other reflect host fns keep their ignored `_kb`: their\nquestion IS about the KB, so the receiver is the right API shape whatever the\nimplementation happens to read.", at: "2026-08-04T09:07:01Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-975", author: "user", content: "059 FINAL REVIEW (2026-08-04) — four independent reviewers: internal consistency,\nclaims-vs-implementation, claims-vs-cited-sources, completeness-vs-grammar.\n\nVERDICT: 059 IS NOT YET DEFINED. An implementer cannot build it without asking\nquestions the document does not answer. Seven blocking design calls, five\nmisrepresentations of cited sources, and R3's exhaustiveness claim is false.\n\nDeduped and ranked below. Three factual errors were fixed in place immediately\n(listed last); everything else needs a human verdict and is left standing.\n\n=== BLOCKING — the document does not decide these ===\n\nB1. THE RULE BAN HAS A HOLE THROUGH A CONSTRUCT R3 ALLOWS. `_provides_content`\n(grammar.js:961) admits rule_declaration, rule_block, fact_declaration and\nproof_declaration, and load.rs:16554 loads them through the ordinary `load_rule`\npath. So `provides Show[T=Rec] language anthill rule show(?r) = \"x\" end` inside a\nsecondary entry reaches EVERY hazard R3's ban is justified by -- non-monotone\nsearch, and the `[simp]`-rewrites-in-the-typer one, since `[simp]` is read off\nkb.rule_meta and a rule is exactly its carrier. R3's \"exhaustive over an entry's\nDIRECT content\" puts the block's interior out of the lists' reach and gives it no\nverdict -- unlike the nested namespace and the nested sort, which each get an\nexplicit non-recursion verdict with a principled reason. DECIDE: do R3's refusals\nrecurse into a provides block? If not, R3's central refusal is decorative.\n\nB2. THE CLASSIFICATION PREDICATE IS ORDER-DEPENDENT, AND DISAGREES WITH THE\nDEFINITIONS. Two defects in one line (059:60, `has_kind(X, Sort)`).\n  (a) MEASURED: `namespace Rec {…}` written BEFORE `sort Rec {…}` leaves kinds\n      [\"Namespace\"] -- load.rs:2700 reuses a symbol found by qualified name and\n      never calls add_kind(Sort) on the reuse arm. Contrast Item::Entity\n      (load.rs:2864), which records roles FROM THE DECLARATION, which is why the\n      free-standing-entity spelling is order-insensitive and this one is not. So\n      the predicate 059 offers as \"the question an implementation asks\" answers\n      FALSE for a genuine secondary entry in one of the two orders, and any\n      R3/R4 gate keyed on it fails OPEN precisely where WI-979 already breaks.\n      059 flags the symptom at line 64 but states the predicate unconditionally;\n      the two belong in one sentence.\n  (b) `enum X`, `sort X = T` and `sort X = ?` are all Sort-kind, so has_kind\n      classifies a namespace beside any of them as a secondary entry -- while the\n      Definitions admit only \"`sort X … end` or `entity X(…)`\" as a main entry.\n      The verdict decides whether R3 refuses that namespace's rules and facts.\n\nB3. `import_clause` IS UNCLASSIFIED AND CONTRADICTORY, AND AN IMPORT CAPTURE\nREACHES NO R4 CLAUSE. It is in _namespace_content and in neither R3 list, so\nline 72's \"anything unlisted is refused pending classification\" refuses it --\ndirectly against line 90, which measures imports working and calls the sharing\n\"deliberate rather than tolerated\". Worse, line 90 states \"an import is a CAPTURE\nROUTE exactly as a declaration is\" and then draws the wrong conclusion: all three\nR4 clauses key on the CAPTURING side being a member declaration, and an import\ndeclares no member. `namespace Rec { import other.f }` shadowing a bare `f` that\nan existing main-entry body reads is line 162's identical measured hazard,\nadmitted by every clause.\n\nB4. \"ENTRY\" IS NEVER INDIVIDUATED, AND THREE RULES KEY ON IT. R3's proof clause\n(\"declared in another entry\"), the narrow rule's condition (2) (\"every clause of\nthat head is written in that same entry\"), and R4 clause 2 all turn on entry\nidentity. Line 123 says (2) is decidable \"by grouping a predicate's clauses by\nthe entry they are written in\" and never gives the key. One `namespace X … end`\nblock? All text at that address in one file? A file? Line 117's \"two secondary\nentries that each introduce freshp\" implies per-block; nothing states it.\n\nB5. \"PROVISION\" IS NEVER DEFINED, BUT R3'S FACT RULE IS A PARTITION OF FACTS.\n\"allow `provides`/`fact Spec[X]`, refuse every other fact\" needs a loader\npredicate `is_provision(fact)`. There is no `Spec` symbol kind (intern.rs:30) and\nno `spec` keyword -- a spec is an ordinary sort -- so the discriminator has to be\ninvented. Scope is open too: `fact Spec[Y]` for a FOREIGN carrier Y in a\nsecondary entry to X. R3's allow-list is spelled `fact Spec[X]` (about X) while\nthe module section (line 212) treats orphan provisions for foreign carriers as in\nplay. Allowed or refused? Unstated.\n\nB6. R1's KEY AND R4's LEDGER KEY ARE DECLARED ONE MECHANISM AND DISAGREE ON THE\nCORPUS'S COMMONEST SHAPE. R1 refuses two type declarations \"at one address\"; line\n191 says the ledger is \"keyed on (scope, local name)\" and that \"it is one piece\nof machinery for both\". On the §6.3 eponymous constructor `sort Rec { entity\nRec(…) }` (WI-926; load.rs:2830 reuses the sort's symbol) the two disagree:\naddress-keyed makes it `entity X` + `sort X`, which R1's own list names as an\nerror, so the ledger fails the stdlib on day one; (scope,local-name)-keyed passes.\n\nB7. R2 OVERTURNS 001's \"NAME UNIQUENESS\" -- AN IMPLEMENTED PROPOSAL -- WITHOUT\nSAYING SO. 001:139: \"A name may be declared as either a `sort` or a `domain` in\nthe same scope, not both. … Rationale: … Reopening would introduce complexity\n(what can be added? can constructors be added, breaking closed ADT?) without\nsufficient benefit.\" (`domain` is today's `namespace`.) R2 blesses exactly that\npair as the mechanism. 059 cites the rule only as background and never proposes\nan amendment. Mitigating: the rule was never carried into the spec, so the\nconflict is proposal-to-proposal; and R1/R3 do answer 001's two questions. It\nstill needs saying explicitly.\n\n=== SERIOUS — cited sources misrepresented, or the document contradicts itself ===\n\nM1. 058 §3.6 IS *PROPOSED*, NOT DELIVERED. 058's status line: \"defaults (§3.6)\nand per-provision conditions (§3.8) are proposed\". `one_default`,\nDefaultProvider, no-displacement and \"fill silence, never overwrite speech\" are\nall §3.6. 059 states them as in force -- \"one_default REFUSES a second default\",\n\"the orphan case is ALREADY refused\", \"Within one KB, 058 ALREADY decides it\" --\nand R4 clause 2 is \"058 §3.6's no-displacement, in its own words\". That sits\nbadly against 059's own promise that every claim is measured against the loader.\n(§3.1's nameability gate, which clause 1 uses, IS delivered -- that half is fine.)\n\nM2. SPEC §8.7 \"INSTANCE COHERENCE\" CONTRADICTS 059's LOAD-BEARING WHOLE-PROGRAM\nPREMISE, UNACKNOWLEDGED. §8.7:2326: \"Instance selection is SCOPED, NOT GLOBAL …\ndifferent scopes may resolve the same Spec[carrier] to different providers, the\nper-`import` choice noted above\"; :2324 \"A consumer chooses which instantiation\nto use via `import`.\" 059:207: \"there is no boundary for anything here to cross.\"\n058 §8 proposes replacing that paragraph -- but the amendment is unapplied, so\nthe spec still says the opposite. 059 never cites §8.7.\n\nM3. SPEC §8.7:2357 SAYS A NAMESPACE-LEVEL OPERATION \"BACKS NOTHING\". \"Backing\nmust be reachable THROUGH THE CARRIER … A free operation declared at NAMESPACE\nlevel, with the same name and signature and sitting beside the carrier, is none\nof those and BACKS NOTHING … The implementation therefore goes in the carrier's\nsort body.\" R2 claims and measures the opposite for a namespace AT the carrier's\naddress. The reconciliation is 059's own line-36 measurement (different address\n=> not attached), but §8.7's sentence carries no such qualification, and a reader\nof the spec concludes R2 is impossible. Live, not academic: WI-978 records that\nthe backing check never runs for that placement.\n\nM4. A DISCLAIMED MEASUREMENT IS ASSERTED AS FACT FOUR LINES LATER, AND IT IS THE\nREASON FOR R3's HARDEST REFUSAL. Line 152: \"whether a secondary entry's\n`requires` also changes runtime dictionary layout WAS NOT ESTABLISHED\". Line 156:\n\"Adding a requirement to a type one does not own RE-LAYS-OUT ITS DISPATCH for\nevery one of its users, and that is the single thing in this proposal a module\nboundary would certainly forbid.\" It also collides with line 138 (\"it is not\nabout dictionary layout … there is no cost specific to a secondary entry to price\nthere\") and 207 (\"there is none to charge\"), while 211 restores the layout harm.\nFIX DIRECTION: rewrite 156 in the vocabulary the section already chose -- the harm\nis WHO THE DECLARATION BINDS (an obligation added to every existing caller), not\nre-layout -- and let 211 carry the layout claim scoped to separate compilation.\n\nM5. BOTH \"COMPANION\" CITATIONS ARE MISREAD AS R2's SHAPE. 058's canonical\ncompanion is a SEPARATE WITNESS SORT AT ITS OWN ADDRESS (058:217 \"in lib_b …\nsort MoneyByAmount -- glue: a witness beside a foreign carrier\"); 001's deferred\n`companion` block \"desugars to a separate domain with an implicit import\". Both\nare a different address reached by import -- which 059's own line 9 and line 36\nsay is NOT R2's shape (\"attachment is by address, never by import\"). The one\nproperty R2 exists to supply is MEMBERSHIP, which neither has. So the difference\nis not \"the keyword\" (line 5) and 058 does not \"already contemplate this\nmechanism\" (line 199).\n\n=== COMPLETENESS ===\n\nC1. R3's \"EXHAUSTIVE OVER AN ENTRY'S DIRECT CONTENT\" IS FALSE. Of the 19\n_namespace_content productions, 4 fall through: `describe_declaration` (and it is\nNOT inert -- load_describe asserts DescriptionInfo FACTS, which R3's own \"refuse\nevery other fact\" reaches, and it takes a TARGET, so it writes about a\ndeclaration in another entry -- the identical objection R3 raises against a\nforeign-target proof); bodyless `abstract_sort` (`sort Alias = Int64` -- the\nallow-list is qualified \"with a body\", the refused list covers only `= ?`); and\nthe two standalone type-param binders `sort ?T` / `sort [T]`, where the braced\nform `sort [F] { sort [T] }` HAS a body so the allow-list admits what the\nrefused-list means to ban. Separately, R3 classifies a production that cannot\nappear: a bare `{< … >}` description block is a PARSE ERROR in a namespace body.\n\nC2. LINE 199 PRESCRIBES A SPELLING THAT DOES NOT PARSE. `namespace Rec { provides\nShow[T = Rec] }` is a parse error -- `provides_clause` is admitted only by\n_sort_content/_enum_content; a namespace body admits `provides_block` (with a\nmandatory `language L`) and `fact_declaration`. R3's allow-list is accidentally\nright; line 199 contradicts it. And 058 §4's proposed retirement of the `fact`\nspelling would leave a secondary entry with NO provision spelling at all. The two\nforms are not interchangeable anyway: load_provides_clause takes its carrier from\nthe enclosing domain implicitly, where `fact Spec[X]` writes it explicitly.\n\nC3. THE CENSUS INFERENCE USES THE WRONG POPULATION. \"Counting every DECLARATION\nwhose short name already resolves … gives 61 sites … so the unqualified clause is\nunimplementable.\" 38 of the 61 are parameters and type names, which the clause --\nstated over \"a member OPERATION … capture an OPERATION\" -- never reaches, and\nwhose stated exclusion reasons are definitional (\"a binder is not a declaration\"\n-- contradicting the word \"declaration\" in the count itself). The number for that\nargument is 23, every one an override (19 requires/provides + 4 import). The\nfigures themselves are sound: 38 + 19 + 4 + 0 = 61 = 63 - 2, and the pre-WI-982\ntable was the broken one (40 was the enclosing-ROUTE count, not the class count;\n40 + 19 + 4 + 2 = 65 != 63). Independently reproduced: 427 declarations, 427\ndistinct (scope,name), 0 duplicates -- and 0 addresses carrying both a namespace\nand a type, i.e. the corpus contains no secondary entries at all.\n\nC4. THE 427-DECLARATION LEDGER MEASUREMENT DROPS ITS OWN EXCLUSION, AND THE\nEXCLUDED ARM IS ONE R3 ALLOWS. WI-981: \"NOT COUNTED: operations declared inside\n`provides … language … end` blocks reach the KB by another path.\" R3 admits\nexactly those blocks in a secondary entry.\n\nC5. OTHER UNCLASSIFIED / UNSTATED CASES.\n  - A nested `sort T { … }` in a secondary entry where T is one of X's TYPE\n    PARAMETERS. R3's allow-list admits it verbatim; `define` merges same-named\n    declarations in one scope into one symbol, so the type parameter is silently\n    displaced -- IDENTITY, the one thing R3's title forbids, caught by nothing.\n  - R4 clause 3 is keyed on operations, but its motivating measurement is BARE-NAME\n    resolution in an unedited body. A const is a member and is reachable bare\n    (measured, line 37), so a secondary entry's const capturing a bare name is the\n    same silent meaning-change with no clause over it. Same for a nested sort/enum\n    name capturing an imported type name.\n  - R2's CONVERSE is never stated: a provision declared in a secondary entry,\n    BACKED BY A MEMBER OF THE MAIN ENTRY. Line 80's \"a satisfaction fact belongs\n    in the closure where its backing exists\" reads as a per-entry closure, which\n    would refuse it and contradict the one-scope model. WI-978 has to implement\n    the check and is not told which closure.\n  - PROOF TARGETS ARE NOT ONLY RULES. load_proof resolves contract_proof_target_qn\n    first -- a proof may target an operation contract <op>.<clause> (WI-539/025),\n    which has no rule symbol. R3's proof clause is argued entirely from\n    set_proof_result writing back onto the target RULE.\n  - TWO HOST `provides … language rust … end` BLOCKS for one (spec, carrier,\n    language) across a main and a secondary entry. 058's one_default arbitrates\n    PROVIDERS, not host carrier/operation_map realization tables (WI-876's\n    per-carrier host-op keying). Which mapping wins is unstated.\n  - `internal` VISIBILITY IS NEVER MENTIONED. It is real and enforced (WI-369,\n    §8.6). Because attachment is by address and the scope is shared, anyone may\n    write a secondary entry at a foreign sort's address and read every `internal`\n    member of it. That is a fourth item for \"Where this leads\", which names three.\n  - R1's pair-list omits `enum X` + `sort X`, `entity X` + `entity X`, and every\n    pair involving `sort X = T`. The general clause covers them; the colon reads\n    as the definition.\n\nC6. SMALLER, EACH MECHANICAL.\n  - Line 152/156 aside, the \"one question\" count is three in three places (205,\n    209, 217) and FOUR in a fourth (215). The classification flip was appended as\n    a tail clause to bullet 3 (commit 8df4fb93) and only 215 was recounted.\n  - R5 (line 201) covers only OPERATIONS; line 154 cites it for \"reaching\n    REQUIREMENTS too\", which R5 does not say.\n  - Line 181 asserts all three R4 clauses are stated \"over the scope\"; clause 2 is\n    stated over secondary entries in its own text, and \"the scope\" read literally\n    includes an ordinary namespace, which line 56 promises R3/R4 never reach. Say\n    \"over the sort's scope -- main entry and secondary entries alike\".\n  - Line 188 concedes clause 2 is \"a sub-case of 1\" and never says what it refuses\n    that 1 does not. (It is the asymmetric main-vs-secondary case, justified by\n    no-displacement rather than nameability -- say that, or fold it in.)\n  - Line 81 \"Refused, each naming the sort:\" has no blank line before it, so it\n    renders INSIDE the last allowed bullet -- the allowed list's last entry heads\n    the refused list.\n  - Line 86 promises \"the two conditions below\", 26 lines away; line 87 then says\n    \"the same condition\" singular, meaning condition (2).\n  - Line 123 reads as a self-contradiction (\"undecidable\" then \"decidable at scan\n    time\" two sentences later); it is right, but needs \"once both close\".\n  - Line 39 \"the three questions this proposal was asked\" -- never stated in the\n    document; they are WI-975's.\n  - Line 16 undersells its own finding: the `entity X` + `sort X { … }` fixture is\n    REFUSED (\"unresolved name 'Int64' in scope\"), not \"loads\".\n  - R6's placement splits R3 in half (R3's requires-material runs 138-156, after\n    R6 at 127-136), and R6 is forward-cited at 105 and 123 anyway, so the stated\n    reason for the position does not hold. Reading order is R1,R2,R3,R6,R4,R5.\n  - Line 103 \"a a secondary entry\"; line 136 \"§WI-896\" (a § on a ticket id); line\n    144's costs table has an empty header row.\n  - Status line: \"Every claim below is measured against the Rust loader\" is\n    contradicted by the document at 87 and 152 and by the whole prescriptive R6\n    and module sections. Scope it to the measurement tables.\n  - Spec §11 `NamespaceContent` (kernel-language.md:2692) is itself stale w.r.t.\n    proposals 039 and 025/031 -- it omits `const` and `proof`, which R3\n    classifies. Reconcile before \"anything unlisted is refused\" becomes code.\n\n=== FIXED IN PLACE (factual errors, no design judgment) ===\n\nF1. \"the sort's OWN member already outranks other supply routes at dispatch\n(WI-842 refuses a tie …)\" -- self-contradictory and false. WI-842's rule is \"LOUD\nON THE SECOND CANDIDATE, never first-match\"; it DELETED the carrier_own_op\nranking. Rewritten: a member does not outrank, a tie is refused, and that is the\nstandard R4 restores.\n\nF2. The WI-346 hand-off over-claimed. Driven with a both-sides control against\ncheck_requires_shadows (load.rs:6412-6446): fires on requires+own-op, absent with\n`fact Show[T=Rec]` added, absent with no requires. The derived conclusion (the\ntwo never co-fire) holds, but WI-346 covers only the requires-WITHOUT-provides\nsubset. Narrowed, and a note added that this clause's exclusion is wider than the\nspec's `override` (§8.7:2363 says a requires-user's same-named op \"is NOT\noverriding it: that operation is unrelated\"), so the clause needs its own term.\n\nF3. The builtin-tag caveat had ROTTED INSIDE ITS OWN COMMIT: it cited the two\nWI-982 sites as the example, and WI-982 declared them, so a reader checking\n`nonvar` today finds a declaration and concludes the paragraph is wrong. Measured\nover all 54 registered tags: 51 declared, 3 not -- `anthill.kernel.find_dictionary`\n(kinds [\"Operation\"], in no .anthill file) is now the named example.\n`anthill.reflect.typing.is_entity_of` is declared only as a RULE that joins the\ntag-minted symbol. And `anthill.reflect.Expr.ho_apply` rides an ENTITY symbol, so\n\"an operation by kind\" is not even universally true -- a check keyed on\n`kind == Operation` misses it. Both corrections are in the text.\n\n=== VERIFIED, NO ACTION ===\n\nAll five 058 verbatim quotes exist exactly once and mean what 059 uses them for\n(except M1's status and M5's companion). §6.3's \"operations move a free-standing\nentity to the long form\", §\"A rule head functor is resolved, not declared\",\n§\"A rule-introduced functor is scoped where it is written\", and the `[simp]`\ntyper-rewrite claim are all verbatim-accurate. No cited work-item status is\nmisstated. Dot dispatch requires membership with no free-function fallback --\ndriven both ways (`f(Rec(n:7))` => 7; `Rec(n:7).f()` => refused\n\"no such member (dot dispatch)\", typing.rs:384). A const is a member and not on\nthe dispatch surface -- driven both ways. The six measured symmetry directions\nmatch the six table rows line 90 counts. WI-857's dictionary-bundling claim is\nits delivered settlement. line 18's \"pass 1 gates the parent link on the\ndeclaration being new\" is verbatim at load.rs:2711.\n\nOne citation is misattributed rather than wrong: §6.7 is \"Field Access (Dot\nProjection)\" and says nothing about method dot dispatch or a free-function\nfallback. The claim is supported by the other half of the citation, WI-279.", at: "2026-08-04T11:27:15Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-979", description: some(value: "FOUND WHILE DRIVING WI-975 (see docs/proposals/059-secondary-entries.md).\n`sort X ... end` that REUSES an existing symbol never records the Sort category,\nso a `namespace X` written BEFORE the sort silently breaks the sort.\n\nMEASURED, one pair, the two declarations in both orders:\n\n  namespace q4a                     namespace q4b\n    namespace Rec                     sort Rec\n      operation helper(...)             entity Rec(n: Int64)\n    end                               end\n    sort Rec                          namespace Rec\n      entity Rec(n: Int64)              operation helper(...)\n    end                               end\n  end                               end\n\n  ns-first:   kind_of=Some(Namespace)  has_kind(Sort)=FALSE\n              has_kind(Entity)=FALSE   nested `q4a.Rec.Rec` EXISTS\n  sort-first: kind_of=Some(Sort)       has_kind(Sort)=true\n              has_kind(Entity)=true    nested `q4b.Rec.Rec` absent\n\nSo in ns-first order the name is not a Sort, `is_sort_scope` is therefore false,\nthe EPONYMOUS CONSTRUCTOR never collapses (§6.3 / WI-926), and a phantom nested\n`X.X` is defined. Downstream, a member call on such a receiver reports the sort\nas `q3b.Rec.Rec` and dot dispatch misses.\n\nCAUSE, in `scan_items_pass1`'s `Item::SortWithBody` arm (kb/load.rs ~2700):\n\n    let (sym, is_new) = if let Some(&existing) = kb.symbols.by_qualified_name.get(&qualified) {\n        (existing, false)                      // <-- no add_kind(Sort) here\n    } else {\n        (kb.symbols.define(&short, &qualified, SymbolKind::Sort, ...), true)\n    };\n\nThis is EXACTLY the defect WI-926 fixed one arm below. The `Item::Entity` arm\nrecords its category explicitly, with a comment saying why: \"Recorded from the\nDECLARATION, not from which arm above produced the symbol -- two of the three\nnever call `define`, so relying on `define` to carry the category silently loses\nit.\" `SortWithBody` has the same two-arm shape and was never given the same fix.\n\nNOTE the reuse branch is also the PRELUDE-bootstrap path (`register_prelude`\ndefines `anthill.prelude.List` before stdlib's `sort List ... end` reuses it) --\nthat one is unaffected because `register_prelude` already defines with Sort, so\nthe fix is additive there.\n\nRELATED, decide together: `SortWithBody`'s reuse branch also skips the\n`add_parent` that wires the sort's scope to its enclosing scope, which is why\n`entity X(...)` followed by `sort X ... end` produces a body that resolves\nNOTHING from outside itself. Proposal 059 R1 refuses that shape outright, so\nthat half may become unreachable rather than fixed -- but the category loss\nabove is reachable via `namespace X` + `sort X`, which R2 explicitly BLESSES.\n\nACCEPTANCE: both declaration orders give the same categories and the same\neponymous collapse. A test asserts, for the pair above, `has_kind(Sort)` and\n`has_kind(Entity)` true in BOTH orders and no nested `X.X` in either, and drives\na member call on the receiver in BOTH orders. Say at the test site which\nassertions fail when the fix is backed out (the ns-first row does; sort-first\npasses either way, by design, as the control). cargo-test green via\nrustland/scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-04T13:42:46Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-979", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED (2026-08-04). ONE LINE SHIPPED, AND ONE REVERTED — the revert is the\nmore useful half of this ticket.\n\nWHAT LANDED. `add_kind(sym, SymbolKind::Sort)` unconditionally after the two-arm\nlookup in scan_items_pass1's Item::SortWithBody arm. `define` accumulates\ncategories (WI-926) but the reuse arm never calls it, so the `sort` keyword's own\ncategory was lost whenever the name already existed. Exactly the fix the\nItem::Entity arm already carries, for the same two-arm shape; idempotent, so the\nprelude-bootstrap path is unaffected.\n\nWHAT I BUILT, MEASURED GREEN, AND THEN REVERTED. Driving found a second\n`is_new` gate on the same arm — the variant-exposure link — and I un-gated it,\nadded an idempotent `add_parent_once`, and ran the full workspace suite: 0\nfailures. /code-review then refuted it three ways, each of which I re-drove myself\nbefore acting:\n\n  1. `is_new` is false for TWO populations, and un-gating serves the wrong one.\n     register_prelude pre-registers anthill.prelude.{List, Option, TypeExtractor,\n     EffectExpression} and anthill.reflect.{MemberKind, Expr, Pattern}, all\n     re-declared with variants in stdlib source, so all seven gained an exposure\n     link they never had — ~55 short names newly bare-resolvable. DRIVEN\n     consequence: bare `guarded` under wildcard imports goes from resolving\n     uniquely to LogicalQuery.guarded to AMBIGUOUS against\n     EffectExpression.guarded. Both directions measured by flipping the one line.\n  2. It silently half-wired the shape this ticket deliberately left refused.\n     `entity Rec(n)` + `sort Rec { entity Inner }` started loading clean with bare\n     `Inner` resolving OUT, while the body still could not see IN (the enclosing\n     link stays gated). And MY OWN PINNING TEST FOR THAT SHAPE COULD NOT SEE IT —\n     its fixture had no variant, so `has_variant` was false and the new path was\n     never entered. A pinning test blind to the change it pins is not a pin; the\n     fixture now carries a variant.\n  3. My justification for `add_parent_once` was FALSE. I wrote that a duplicate\n     parent link 'can turn a unique resolution into Ambiguous'. It cannot:\n     resolve_in_scope_recursive has a `visited` guard (intern.rs:672) and dedups\n     matches (723-725), and a genuinely duplicated identical link (via a plain\n     `import <ns>.C` beside `sort C { entity Red }`) loads clean. The helper was\n     harmless and existed to prevent a harm that does not occur.\n\nTHE LESSON, and it is the one this ticket should be remembered for: A GREEN FULL\nSUITE WAS NOT EVIDENCE. There is exactly one wildcard import in the whole repo,\ninside a parse test, so nothing in-tree could trip the ambiguity. I had flagged\nthis exact risk to myself before making the change ('could ADD parent links for\nevery prelude sort ... changing resolution globally') and then accepted 2900+\npassing tests in place of measuring it. The corpus does not exercise a resolution\nroute that no file uses.\n\nThe reverted half is WI-985, with both driven consequences, the reason `if\nhas_variant` alone is wrong, and an acceptance clause requiring its own\nambiguity guard — because the suite will be green for the naive fix again.\n\nALSO NOTED, not fixed: `logical_sort_qn`'s guard doc says '`app.Config` is a\nNamespace' as if exclusive. Since this ticket a symbol can carry BOTH Namespace\nand Sort, so the guard passes where it used to fail. It takes three same-named\ndeclarations to reach and no such shape is in the corpus, so it is a noted premise\nrather than a measured wrong answer — recorded at the site.\n\nTESTS: wi979_declaration_order_test (5). Categories in both orders; no phantom\n`X.X`; a DRIVEN member call on a receiver built from the main entry's constructor\n(asserting the value, not that the file loaded); the variant-exposure defect\npinned in both directions with expect_load_errors, so WI-985 cannot land silently;\nand the `entity X` + `sort X` shape pinned as still failing loudly, its fixture\nnow carrying a variant. CONTROLS said at each site: every sort-first row passes\nwith the fix backed out. Backing out `add_kind` fails 4 of 5.\n\nA MISREAD WORTH RECORDING. On the first run all three tests failed and I read the\nmessage as the CONTROL failing, because both fixtures put `drive()` on line 10 and\nthe error was `10:32` either way. I chased it as a fixture problem through seven\nprobe variants — every one loading clean — before noticing the coincidence. It was\nthe ns-first row all along. Separately I nearly pinned an incomplete error list\nbecause my probe printed only the first two of three errors.\n\nFull workspace green via scripts/test.sh, EXIT=0, zero failures.\n\n059 UPDATED: its 'Today that is not quite true — a secondary entry read before the\nmain one breaks the sort (WI-979)' caveat is now true for the CLASSIFICATION and\nthe eponymous collapse (variant visibility is WI-985, and the note says so); and\nits problem table said `entity X` then `sort X { … }` 'loads', where it is\nrefused with three unresolved-name errors — driven.", at: "2026-08-04T13:42:50Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-995", description: some(value: "An import is scoped to the ADDRESS, not to the file it is written in — so one file changes how names resolve in another.\n\nTHE RULE (user decision, 2026-08-04): an import resolves ONLY in the file where it\nis listed, and it is SPENT BEFORE any merging. An import maps a local name to a\nSymbol; once names are Symbols it has no further job.\n\nThat second half is already true of the implementation and worth recording: the\nscope import table (`Scope.imports`, intern.rs:302) has EXACTLY ONE reader --\n`resolve_in_scope_recursive` step 1b (intern.rs:661) -- and `typing.rs` never\nconsults it. The only other consumer of import DATA is codegen/rust.rs, which\nreads the PARSE IR's import list to emit `use` lines in generated Rust, not the\nscope table. So nothing after resolution needs an import.\n\nTHE DIVERGENCE. `process_imports` writes into the table keyed by SCOPE, and a\nscope is the address: `namespace X` and `sort X` both alloc `Fn{X,[],[]}`, which\nhash-conses to one TermId, so every text writing into one address shares one\nimport table. MEASURED, four fixtures, identical but for the last block:\n\n  A CONTROL  main entry alone, body reads bare `f` via its own `import lib.f`\n             => drive() = 1\n  B  a SEPARATE FILE adds `namespace demo { namespace Rec { import other.f } }`\n             => drive() = 2      -- an unedited body in ANOTHER FILE changed\n  C  the import sits in a NESTED `namespace Inner`, read from the enclosing one\n             => drive() = 1      -- so it does NOT leak outward\n  D  the import sits in a SIBLING `namespace demo` block, NO SORT ANYWHERE\n             => drive() = 2\n\nC is the one that names the reach: the address, not the text. D is the one that\nplaces the ticket: there is no sort at that address, no main entry and no\nsecondary entry, so this is NOT about proposal 059's secondary entries -- it is\nplain namespace-level import scoping, and 059 says so and defers to this.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS. A file can silently change the meaning of a bare name in a file it\nhas never seen, by opening a namespace block at the same address. That is the\nwhole-program non-locality the module question (059 \"Where this leads\") is about,\nexcept it needs no new mechanism to reach -- two ordinary namespaces do it today.\n\nNOT ESTABLISHED, and worth pinning before implementing:\n  - WHAT BREAKS in the corpus. The stdlib writes many namespaces across files at\n    shared addresses; how much of it relies on an import written in one file\n    resolving a name in another is UNMEASURED, and it is the whole cost of this\n    change. Measure it first -- the count decides whether this lands as a refusal,\n    a warning, or a migration.\n  - WHETHER `_global` is exempt. load.rs:3495 folds imports into the global\n    scope's imports; a file-local rule has to say what that means.\n  - THE SORT BODY / SECONDARY ENTRY CASE. 059 R2 has members shared across\n    entries; under a file-local rule a secondary entry in another file must\n    re-import what its own signatures name. That is ordinary (every language with\n    file-local imports requires it) but it is a migration for anyone already\n    relying on the sharing.\n\nACCEPTANCE: an import written in one file does not affect resolution in another,\ndriven with fixtures A-D above (B and D flip to 1, C stays 1, A stays 1); the\ncorpus cost is MEASURED and reported before the behaviour changes; cargo-test\ngreen via rustland/scripts/test.sh. Rust-only unless the count says otherwise;\nscaland has the same shape and should follow."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-992", author: "user", content: "SETTLED, and the question was already answered twice: docs/kernel-language.md 5.1 states the desugaring verbatim (dotted names -> nested namespaces, siblings share a scope), and rustland's scan_items_pass1 has called ensure_intermediate_namespaces all along. So neither spec change nor stdlib rewrite: scaland alone had not implemented the rule. The ticket's 'rustland shows the same flat treatment' reads a LOOKUP helper (find_scope_by_name); rustland's DEFINITION side was never flat. Delivered as Loader.ensureNamespacePath + the parameterized requires arm; the fully-qualified rung in resolveSelectiveImport is retired (measured dead), findInNestedScope stays (removing it fails the WI-295 nested-scope test, and rustland still fills that gap too).", at: "2026-08-04T12:56:40Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-992", description: some(value: "SCALAND: A TOP-LEVEL DOTTED DECLARATION IS NOT ATTACHED TO THE NAMESPACE IT NAMES, SO A SIBLING'S SHORT NAME IS UNREACHABLE. `sort anthill.prelude.Eq` written at file top level is defined by `Loader.DefinePass` in `_global`, keyed by its WHOLE dotted spelling (\"anthill.prelude.Eq\" is both the short name and the qualified name), and its scope's only enclosing parent is `_global`. Nothing ever links it to a scope called `anthill.prelude`. So from inside `anthill.prelude.Eq` the name `PartialEq` resolves to nothing, even though `sort anthill.prelude.PartialEq` sits eleven lines above it in the same file. MEASURED, and this is how it surfaced: WI-988 routed the parameterized `requires` through ordinary resolution and EIGHT tests failed, every one a short spec name — `anthill/prelude/eq.anthill:35` `requires PartialEq[T]`, `ordered.anthill:77-78` `requires Eq` / `requires PartialOrd`, `numeric.anthill:8`, `lattice.anthill:5,30`. Corroborating evidence that this is the rule and not an accident: of the stdlib's 26 `requires`, the only TWO written without bindings are also the only two written FULLY QUALIFIED (`anthill/logic/classical.anthill:13`, `constructive.anthill:10`), which are exactly the ones the dotted `byQualifiedName` rung answers. `Loader.findInNestedScope` is a second workaround for the same gap on the import side, and says so in its own doc (\"registered at global with the dotted name and never gets attached to the `anthill.prelude` namespace's exports\"). CONSEQUENCE BEYOND DIAGNOSTICS: scaland's `requires` LINKS A PARENT SCOPE (it has no typer, so that is the whole of what a requirement does here), so the requires-chain inheritance the stdlib documents — `Eq` inherits `eq`/`neq` from `PartialEq`, WI-614 — has never worked in scaland for any stdlib spec. WORK: decide where a dotted top-level declaration lives. Either the loader synthesizes/enters the `anthill.prelude` scope and defines the SHORT name there (making the scope graph match what the reader sees, and retiring `findInNestedScope`), or the language requires such declarations to be qualified at every use — in which case say so in `docs/kernel-language.md` and fix the stdlib's 24 sites. Settle it against rustland, whose `find_scope_by_name` shows the same flat treatment. DO NOT fix it inside the `requires` clause: a local \"also try my own namespace prefix\" rung there is the second ladder WI-986 removed. ACCEPTANCE: `requires PartialEq[T]` in `sort anthill.prelude.Eq` links a parent, DRIVEN by resolving an inherited name (`eq`) from inside `Eq` — not by a clean load. Then WI-988's parameterized arm can stop deferring. Say at the site which test fails when the change is backed out. Related: WI-988, WI-986."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "scaland-sbt-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-08-04T12:56:43Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-991", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. The counter is off SimpleTermStore entirely: `spanOf` is a plain accessor (the class is now `open` for that ONE override, allocators `final`), and a count is taken PER PARSE by a counting subclass in the test tree — `SpanFixture.parseCountingSpanReads` hands it back with the file, so no later reader and no statement order can move it. `ParseSpanGrowthTest` now walks BEFORE it asserts (the ticket's acceptance) and reads 700/1400, unchanged; it also pins those counts exactly, which is the control for THIS ticket — the backed-out shape reads 800 there and stays green on every other assertion. LANDMINE, found by code review after a first WRONG shape: the seam must NOT be a public `terms` parameter guarded by `require(terms.size == 0)`. Size is a proxy for freshness, not freshness — MEASURED, `namespace d end` allocates no terms, so a store that had already backed a declaration-only parse passed the guard and two files aliased one store, silently. The shape that works is `private[anthill] parseInto(source, fileName, terms)` with the public `parse` minting its own store: freshness by construction on every production path, and no guard that can be wrong. Two corrections to this ticket's own text: the reorder ALONE does not let the quadratic back in (10600 against 41000 is ratio 3.87, still over the 2.5 ceiling) — what it breaks is what the number MEANS; and the '912' figure is a whole-corpus total over 58 stdlib files (largest single file 97), not per file, and the loader never touched this test's stores at all. Only readers of the SAME store instance can contaminate a count.")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-991", description: some(value: "SCALAND: `SimpleTermStore.spanOf` IS A MUTATOR WEARING AN ACCESSOR'S NAME, AND THE TEST IT EXISTS FOR IS HELD UP BY STATEMENT ORDER. WI-964 made `spanOf` increment a `spanReads` counter so `ParseSpanGrowthTest` can measure that type lowering reads children's spans back instead of recomputing them. The counter is the only observable the test has, and `spanOf` is on the loader's per-term path, so every reader anywhere moves it. Concretely: `ParseSpanGrowthTest` snapshots `spanReads` and then calls `SpanFixture.fnSpans`, which reads spans through the SAME counting accessor. Hoisting that `val` above the snapshot — an ordinary readability edit — leaves both the floor assertion and the ratio bound passing while they now measure the WALK rather than the parse, so the quadratic the test exists to catch could be restored with the suite green. The same erosion follows from routing `nameBearingWithoutSpan` / `spansEndingInWhitespace` through the accessor (they index `spans` raw today) or from adding a growth assertion to `ParseSpanCoverageTest` — measured: a stdlib parse is 0 span reads, but `Loader.loadAll` is 912, so loader reads would swamp the signal. WORK: make the observable insensitive to who else reads. A `protected def onRead()` overridden by a counting test subclass, or a store factory parameter on `Parser.parse`, keeps the measurement while taking the write off the production path. ACCEPTANCE: reorder the statements in `ParseSpanGrowthTest` so the walk precedes the snapshot and show the test still measures the parse — that reordering is the regression this ticket exists to make impossible. Say at the site which test fails when the change is backed out. Found by code review (2026-08-04). Related: WI-964, WI-965."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "scaland-sbt-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-08-04T19:22:34Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1001", description: some(value: "Replace 059 R3's BLANKET rule ban in a secondary entry with the NARROW rule the\nproposal actually intends. Spec: docs/proposals/059-secondary-entries.md, \"The rule a\nsecondary entry MAY declare, and the two conditions that make it sound\".\n\nA rule is a definition, and a definition of something NEW displaces nothing. So a\nsecondary entry may declare a rule exactly when BOTH hold:\n  (1) ITS HEAD INTRODUCES — the head resolves to nothing in the FINISHED program, so no\n      existing goal can be about it; and\n  (2) ONE ENTRY OWNS THE PREDICATE — every clause of that head is written in that same\n      entry, keyed as 059's Definitions individuate one: the main entry, or one file's\n      text at that address.\n\nEACH CONDITION ANSWERS A MEASURED FAILURE of the naive \"a fresh head is harmless\" reading:\n  * WITHOUT (2), two entries silently compose one predicate. Two secondary entries IN\n    DIFFERENT FILES that each introduce `freshp` do not collide — both clauses join it,\n    `freshp(1)` and `freshp(2)` both answering. Neither captured a pre-existing name, so\n    R4 is silent, and the predicate ends up assembled by two parties that never agreed on\n    it. R4 CANNOT express this, because a predicate legitimately has many clauses; it is\n    a rule about where they may be WRITTEN. Two blocks in ONE file are one entry and\n    compose freely — deliberately: that is one author writing two paragraphs.\n  * WITHOUT (1), the head is not really new. A main entry carrying\n    `rule q(0) :- not freshp(1)` answers q(0) once; add `namespace Rec { rule freshp(1) }`\n    and it answers ZERO times, though the head was as fresh as heads get — the symbol\n    lands at X.freshp, exactly where the main entry would have put it. The REFERENCE\n    existed before the DEFINITION did.\n\nWHY IT CANNOT LAND YET — the two gates, both already ticketed:\n  * WI-980 (R6). Condition (1) is UNDECIDABLE while text order decides whether a head\n    introduces. Measured today, `rule p(1)` then `sort Rec { rule p(2) }` joins both\n    clauses to one `p`, while the same two swapped make TWO predicates. R6 says the\n    binding is computed against the FINISHED program; until it is, (1) has no stable\n    answer to compute.\n  * WI-895. Condition (1) is UNSOUND while a body may reference what resolves to nothing.\n    Measured, a rule body and a constraint both LOAD while naming a predicate that\n    resolves to nothing (an operation body naming one is already refused, `unknown\n    functor`). A name that resolves to nothing must stop being referenceable before\n    \"resolves to nothing\" can mean \"no one refers to it\".\n\nNO FURTHER DESIGN IS NEEDED. Both conditions are decidable at scan time once the gates\nclose — (2) by grouping a predicate's clauses on (address, SourceId) with the main entry\nas its own group, which is the key the declaration ledger already carries.\n\nTWO SHAPES ARE EXCLUDED BY (1) RATHER THAN BY A CLAUSE OF THEIR OWN, which is the sign\nthe condition is the right one: a DOT RULE and an OPERATOR RULE carry the desugar's\nfunctor (dot_apply, add), and a desugared conclusion introduces nothing — so neither is\never fresh, and the `[simp]`-fires-in-the-typer hazard cannot arise through them.\n\nONE HONEST LIMIT, do not let this ticket be read as closing it: this does NOT close the\nunderlying hazard. A rule extending a predicate declared elsewhere is NON-MONOTONE\nwherever it is written — an ordinary nested namespace does the same, and so does the\nsort's own body. R3 closes the route 059 opens; the general case belongs with the module\nquestion."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-1000", "WI-980", "WI-895"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-996", description: some(value: "UMBRELLA for docs/proposals/059-secondary-entries.md — secondary entries: one scope,\na main entry and secondary ones. 059 is the spec for this work; do NOT re-decide its\nrules in a child ticket. If an implementation cannot meet a rule, AMEND 059 rather\nthan diverging from it.\n\nWHAT 059 SETTLES. A `namespace X` at an address that already has a main entry\n(`sort X` / `enum X` / `entity X`) is a SECONDARY ENTRY to that type's scope — not a\nmodule attached to it. Measured, the visibility is already symmetric in every\ndirection: a secondary entry reads the sort's type parameters, calls its members\nbare, names its variants in a match; the main entry sees the secondary entry's\nmembers and dot-dispatches them on its own receiver. NOTHING IS MISSING IN THE\nWIRING. What is missing is BOUNDS — today two secondary entries declaring one member\nname is silent last-wins, a secondary entry redeclaring a main-entry member silently\nwins, and a second `sort C { … }` body silently reopens a closed ADT.\n\nTHE RULES, and where each lands:\n  R1 a type is defined once ........................ ledger ticket\n  R2 a secondary entry declares members of X ....... NO WORK — measured, already true\n  R3 members and spec claims, never identity ....... classification ticket\n  R4 FILL, never DISPLACE (3 clauses) .............. three tickets\n  R5 §6.3's remedy restated ........................ doc only, spec-sync ticket\n  R6 head binding independent of order ............. WI-980 (exists)\n\nR2 IS DELIVERED BY MEASUREMENT, NOT BY CODE. It is listed so nobody builds it: the\nscope sharing it describes is what the loader already does. Its only follow-up is\nWI-978 — the backing obligation for a `provides` does not reach one declared in a\nsecondary entry, so R3's allowance of spec claims there is unguarded until that lands.\n\nSTILL OPEN AND NOT OWNED HERE: WI-994 (variant exposure is order-dependent),\nWI-995 (an import is scoped to the address, not the file — 059 states the rule,\nthat ticket implements it), WI-895 + WI-980 (both gate 059's NARROW rule for rules\nin a secondary entry; until they close the enforced rule is the blanket ban).\n\nONE DEPENDENCY OUTSIDE THIS REPO'S DELIVERED STATE: 059's R4 clause 2 and its\nspec-claim arbitration both rest on 058 §3.6 (`one_default`, no-displacement), which\nis PROPOSED, not delivered. 059's banner promises every claim is measured against the\nRust loader; that one is not. Resolve before R4 clause 2 lands."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-997", "WI-998", "WI-999", "WI-1000", "WI-1001", "WI-1002", "WI-1003", "WI-978"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-997", description: some(value: "Implements 059 R1 (a type is defined once) and builds the pass-1 DECLARATION LEDGER\nthat R4 clauses 1 and 2 also consume. Spec: docs/proposals/059-secondary-entries.md.\n\nWHY A LEDGER AND NOT A SYMBOL WALK. `SymbolTable::define` MERGES two same-named\ndeclarations in one scope into ONE symbol, so by the time symbols exist the\nduplication is gone — no post-hoc check can find it. Pass 1 must record each\nDECLARATION as it makes it.\n\nTHE KEY IS (scope, local name) — THE DECLARATION AS WRITTEN, NOT THE ADDRESS IT ENDS\nAT, and §6.3's eponymous constructor is why. `sort Vec3 { entity Vec3(…) }` collapses\nthe constructor onto the sort's own symbol (WI-926), so both declarations end at\naddress `ns.Vec3`; address-keyed that is indistinguishable from the SIBLING pair\n`entity Vec3` + `sort Vec3` that R1 refuses, and a legal shape gets rejected.\nWritten-keyed they differ — the sort is (ns,\"Vec3\"), the nested entity\n(ns.Vec3,\"Vec3\") — while the sibling pair is (ns,\"Vec3\") twice and stays caught.\n\nTHE RECORD MUST CARRY THE SourceId. 059 individuates an entry BY FILE: two\n`namespace X … end` blocks in one file are ONE secondary entry, the same text in a\nsecond file is a second one. Nothing else can express that — two blocks at one\naddress compute the same qualified name, reuse the same symbol and alloc the same\nhash-consed scope term, so no per-block identity exists to group by. `file_idx` is\nalready threaded through both scan passes.\n\nR1. Two TYPE declarations sharing a (scope, local name) are a load error naming BOTH\nspans: `sort X`+`sort X`, `enum X`+`enum X`, and — as SIBLINGS — `entity X`+`sort X`\n(§6.3 makes that pair two spellings of one declaration, so it is the same error).\nThe harm is reopening a closed ADT: measured, a second `sort C { … }` body beside\n`sort C { entity Red }` SILENTLY reopens it — both variants construct and the second\nbody's members dispatch.\n\nMEASURED, so the tests have their population: 4 eponymous sites (Vec3, TotalFloat,\nDuration, Timestamp) among 140 sort/enum headers across stdlib, anthill-stl, examples\nand anthill-todo — every one must stay legal. Over 427 operation/const declarations,\nno name is declared twice in one scope, so the ledger lands with no migration.\n\nCONTROL, stated at the test site per CLAUDE.md: the 4 eponymous sites pass WITH AND\nWITHOUT this change — they are the guard against address-keying, not a measure of the\nrule. The sibling pair and the second-body reopen fail ONLY with it.\n\nDO NOT \"FIX\" THE LOUD SIBLING. `entity X(…)` then `sort X { … }` fails today with\nunresolved-name errors (pass 1 gates the enclosing-scope parent link on the\ndeclaration being NEW, and a free-standing entity created no scope for the later body\nto inherit). WI-979 deliberately left it loud. Supplying the missing link would build\nexactly the capability R1 removes."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-04T20:13:26Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-997", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. The pass-1 declaration ledger (kb/load.rs) keyed on (scope_raw, local name), carrying keyword + span + file_idx, and 059 R1 built on it. Full workspace 4105 green.\n\nTHE KEY, MEASURED BY MUTATION rather than argued. TWO candidate keys both work, because both are 'as written': (scope_raw, short) — what ships — and the nested qualified name pass 1 computes, which for the eponymous constructor is '...Vec3.Vec3' since the collapse has not happened at that point. My first mutation tested that second one and the eponymous control PASSED, so the doc-comment's claim as originally written was not the one that bites. The key that FAILS is the address the declaration ENDS AT (the QN of the symbol pass 1 produces, after §6.3 collapses the constructor onto its sort, WI-926): keyed that way THE STDLIB ITSELF STOPS LOADING, at exactly the 4 predicted sites — anthill.geometry.Vec3 (sort 45:3 / entity 46:5), anthill.prelude.Duration (2:1 / 3:3), Timestamp (7:1 / 8:3), TotalFloat (27:1 / 30:3). Recorded in the test header so eponymous_sort_still_loads reads as the row that fails first if anyone re-keys on a symbol.\n\nERROR SHAPE: LoadError::DuplicateTypeDeclaration carries no span and is NOT wrapped in Located — the two declarations may sit in different files and one file prefix cannot name both, so sites are pre-rendered where the &ParsedFiles are still in scope. One message owner (duplicate_type_message) because LoadError has two rendering paths and only one is under test. Reported before pass 2 so the duplicate is named as itself, not through its cascade; loading continues, matching every other pass here.\n\nTHREE EXISTING FIXTURES WROTE THE REFUSED SHAPE — each a real update, none silenced:\n * wi979::entity_then_sort_body_still_fails_loudly_pending_059_r1 — named for this moment; now pins BOTH the R1 refusal AND the three pre-existing unresolved-name errors, which R1 does not repair. Un-gate that enclosing link later and those three vanish and it fails.\n * wi956_kind_gate_tests — its own comment said 'if WI-975 refuses the shape, this fixture needs replacing, not the fix'. Replaced with 059 R2's blessed pair (namespace Rec before sort Rec): head kind Namespace, has_kind(Sort) true, peek still reads Rec's type param from inside the secondary entry. The identical question in the shape the language blesses.\n * wi925::membership_is_independent_of_declaration_order — COULD NOT BE PRESERVED. Its property needed two ORDERS of a name carrying both Sort and Entity, and R1 leaves no legal pair that does. Converted to pin the refusal, with the coverage rerouted in the comment: order-independence to wi979::both_orders_record_the_same_categories, the eponymous-reuse arm to sugar_and_desugaring_agree_on_categories (its DESUGARED spelling takes exactly that arm).\n\nTWO LIMITS STATED IN CODE, not left silent. Item::AbstractSort is NOT recorded: 'sort T = ?' is a type parameter, and 'sort Alias = X' is one of 059's three unclassified productions (measured: 'sort Path = String' beside 'namespace Path { operation len }' loads and Path.len(\"x\") answers, the loader calling len 'a member of sort Path'), so 'sort Alias = X' beside 'sort Alias { ... }' is NOT caught — WI-1000's call. And the ledger records TYPES only: R4's member table has no reader yet, so WI-998 should add the table and its first consumer together rather than land untested state. WI-998 will also need it to outlive scan_definitions, since clause 2's 'already implemented' half is a post-pass-2 read.\n\nNOT RUN: /code-review (user-triggered only; flagged before commit and the user chose to proceed).", at: "2026-08-04T20:13:28Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-990", description: some(value: "SCALAND: `ScopeId`'s MINT SURFACE HAS TWO HOLES — A SYMBOL FROM THE WRONG TABLE, AND A NAME UNIVERSE NEITHER MINT COVERS. WI-976 made scope-hood a type; these are what the type does not yet state. (1) CROSS-TABLE SYMBOLS. `ScopeId.of` is total over `TermSymbol`, but a `TermSymbol` only means anything relative to ONE `SymbolTable`, and the loader threads two side by side — `kb.symbols` and the per-file `fileSym` — in nearly every signature. MEASURED: `kb.scopeDisplayName(ScopeId.of(foreignSym))` throws `IndexOutOfBoundsException`, and `kb.scopeTerm(ScopeId.of(foreignSym))` degrades SILENTLY to a name term for a different symbol. `ScopeId.of(imp.path.last)` type-checks today; all 12 current main-tree mints happen to use `kb.symbols`, so this is latent, not live — but it is the same promise-carried-by-hand the ticket removed, relocated from Int-vs-TermId to which-table. Minting THROUGH the table (`symbols.scopeOf(sym)`) closes it. (2) `scopeByQualifiedName` THROWS on a miss with no error channel. All four callers are private `Prelude` methods returning `Unit`, so a miss aborts KB bootstrap with a stack trace rather than a diagnostic; its unreachability rests on nothing but the statement ORDER inside `Prelude.register`, with no test naming that order load-bearing. Eight lines away, `registerBuiltinTags` handles the identical \"stdlib namespace not registered\" condition by DEGRADING — two opposite policies for one condition in one file. And `scopeByQualifiedName(\"_global\")` throws, because `_global` is interned and never defined: the KB exposes two scope mints over two DISJOINT name universes, and neither one can name every scope. WORK: (1) move the mint onto `SymbolTable`; (2) pick one policy for a missing stdlib scope and make `Prelude`'s ordering dependence either impossible or asserted. ACCEPTANCE: for (1), that `ScopeId.of(fileSym-symbol)` does not compile, asserted with `typeCheckErrors` as `ScopeIdentityTest` already does for the Int and TermId cases; for (2), a test that reorders `Prelude.register` (or asserts the invariant it needs). Say at the site which test fails when the change is backed out. Found by code review of WI-976/WI-983 (2026-08-04). Related: WI-976, WI-987."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "scaland-sbt-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-04T20:15:31Z"))
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-1004", name: "scaland")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-984", author: "user", content: "FROM WI-990 (delivered, scaland): DO NOT PORT SCALAND'S MINT SHAPE WITHOUT READING WI-1004. Scaland's `ScopeId` mint now lives on `SymbolTable` (`scopeOf`) rather than on the companion, because a `TermSymbol` indexes ONE table's `defs` and the loader threads two. That move is the RIGHT layer and worth mirroring, but it does NOT close the cross-table hazard: the check it carries is a range refusal, which catches only the direction where the foreign table is the LARGER one — and in the loader the foreign table (`fileSym`) is always the smaller. The close that is a type is a path-dependent `opaque type ScopeId` as a member of the table (verified in Scala 3), filed as WI-1004; Rust has no path-dependent types, so the equivalent there is a distinct newtype per table or a table-id tag on `Symbol`, and it is a DIFFERENT design question from this ticket. Keep WI-984 to what it says — an opaque `ScopeId` minted from a `Symbol`, threaded through `SymbolTable` and `SymbolDef::Resolved` — and do not inherit scaland's range check as if it settled provenance.", at: "2026-08-04T20:15:52Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-989", description: some(value: "SCALAND: `firstLocated` STILL FALLS BACK TO `Span.empty`, SO WI-964's \"NO PARSER-BUILT TERM IS SPANLESS\" IS FALSE ON SOURCE A USER CAN WRITE. `AnthillParser.firstLocated` degrades to `Span.empty` when it finds no located child, and `typeExprToRef` then mints name-bearing terms with no location — while `IR.scala`'s doc on `nameBearingWithoutSpan` asserts the set is \"EMPTY for every parser-built store\". MEASURED on the WI-976/WI-983 HEAD through that very probe: `rule e(?x) :- p(Foo[F = (?a) -> ?b])` yields 3 locationless name-bearing terms (`TypeExtractor.Arrow`, `TypeExtractor.EffectsRows`, `EffectExpression.empty_row`); `p(Foo[E = {}])` yields 1 (`effects_rows`); `rule a(?f) :- p(?f: (?a, ?b) -> ?c)` yields 11, including `Ref(_0)`, `NamedTupleElement` and `List.cons`/`nil`. Control: `p(Foo[F = (S) -> S])` yields 0. These are `Foo[...]` InstArg children, so they reach `Loader.reallocTerm`'s `Fn` arm and `resolveName(kb, name, scope, errors, Span.empty)` — a locationless load diagnostic, which is precisely the defect WI-957 exists to have closed. THE COVERAGE GAP IS THE INTERESTING HALF: `ParseSpanCoverageTest`'s grammar tour writes `S` at every type leaf, and `S` is exactly the passing control above, so that suite CANNOT catch this and did not. WORK: give the fallback a real span — the enclosing construct's is available at every call site — and delete `Span.empty` from the production path so the doc's claim is structural. Then extend the coverage fixture with `?var` type leaves, since a corpus of ground leaves measures nothing here. ACCEPTANCE: `nameBearingWithoutSpan` is empty for the three measured sources above, asserted as a test rather than as a doc sentence. Say at the site which test fails when the change is backed out. Found by code review (2026-08-04). Related: WI-964, WI-957, WI-961."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "scaland-sbt-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-04T21:34:44Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1005", description: some(value: "One AMBIGUITY, reported TWICE — an occurrence remapped at two scopes, and WI-745's dedup cannot see it.\n\nFOUND WHILE DRIVING WI-994, which made the shape reachable and PINNED the\ndouble rather than fixing it (wi994_variant_exposure_test::\none_variant_name_exposed_by_two_namespaces_is_ambiguous expects 5 errors, of\nwhich 2 are the same finding).\n\nMEASURED. One occurrence at 6:7, two errors:\n  6:7: ambiguous symbol 'guarded' in scope 'p':   candidates [...]\n  6:7: ambiguous symbol 'guarded' in scope 'amb': candidates [...]\n'p' is the operation, 'amb' the enclosing namespace — the same written name\nremapped once per scope. Only the AMBIGUITY rows double; the field-label and\ntype-mismatch rows for the same occurrence appear once, so this is not a\ngeneral 2x on name remapping and the cause is specific to the ambiguity path.\n\nWHY THE EXISTING DEDUP MISSES IT. LoadError::dedup_key is deliberately\nINJECTIVE — the full rendering, span included — and its doc says so on purpose:\ntwo references to one bad name in same-short-named but DISTINCT scopes must\nboth survive, since a short scope name is not a unique identity\n(no-short-name-comparison). Here the two renderings differ ONLY in the scope\nname, so nothing collapses them. WI-745 met the same class of duplicate (a head\nfunctor resolved for owner-tracking AND for the term build) and eliminated it\nAT THE PRODUCER for exactly this reason; this is a second producer pair doing\nthe same. WI-745's own CLI test asserts the property this violates —\nrun_cmd_test.rs asserts the ambiguous-symbol message appears exactly once.\n\nEASY TO MISS, which is why it went unnoticed: name the namespace and the\noperation alike and the two renderings COINCIDE and dedup. The probe WI-994 was\nwritten from did exactly that (namespace wi994.p, operation p) and reported\n4 errors; the test fixture renamed the namespace and reported 5.\n\nWORK: find the two producers (there are 5 push_ambiguous_symbol call sites in\nkb/load.rs, one shared spelling) and establish which two both remap the SAME\noccurrence at different current_scope values — then eliminate one, as WI-745\ndid, rather than widening dedup_key. Widening it is refused for the reason its\ndoc already gives.\n\nACCEPTANCE: the wi994 fixture reports the ambiguity ONCE (the pinned list drops\nto 4, and that edit is the test the fix must come with); the WI-745 CLI\ncount-is-1 assertion still holds; a CONTROL showing two genuinely distinct\nreferences to one bad name in two scopes still report twice. cargo-test green\nvia rustland/scripts/test.sh."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-994", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED as a PLAIN UN-GATE (`if has_variant`), NOT the declaration-vs-bootstrap\npredicate this ticket prescribed. User decision, 2026-08-04, after the measurement\nbelow. Two of this ticket's own claims were wrong.\n\nTHE CONTROL THIS TICKET LACKED, and it inverts population (b). Under `import\nanthill.prelude.*`:\n    bare `pair`      (enum anthill.prelude.Pair, NOT pre-registered)  => RESOLVES\n    bare `merge`     (enum anthill.prelude.EffectExpression, pre-reg) => unknown functor\n    bare `empty_row` (same enum)                                      => unknown functor\nSame namespace, same wildcard, same `enum ... entity ...` shape. So the 7 bootstrap\nsorts are not a population that \"never had the link\" — they are 7 sorts whose §8.6\nexposure the bootstrap silently DELETES. A pre-registration is the loader's own\nstand-in for the SAME declaration, so gating on it made the exposure read off which\nREGISTRATION came first — the very class of bug this ticket is about, one level down.\n\nACCEPTANCE CRITERION 3 WAS INVERTED for the same reason. It asked for a guard that\nthe double-wildcard `guarded` \"still resolves uniquely\". Measured, that unique\nresolution was the loader binding a name the author never chose: the fixture spells\n`guarded(label:, guard:)` — unmistakably EffectExpression.guarded — and the loader\nbound LogicalQuery.guarded, then reported `'guarded' has no field 'label' (declares:\nquery, condition)`. §8.6: two sorts exposing one variant name make the bare name\nAMBIGUOUS \"rather than letting one silently win\" — the verdict anthill-cli's\nambiguous-symbol.anthill already pins for two wildcard-imported SORTS. The guard test\nnow asserts the ambiguity; a single-wildcard CONTROL pins that `guarded` still\nresolves uniquely and passes either way.\n\nThe `define` vs `define_qualified_only` candidate this ticket suggested is also\nrefuted: register_stdlib_scopes uses `define` (scope-local) for all 7, so that\npredicate cannot tell the populations apart either.\n\nSECOND CONSEQUENCE DISSOLVED, not handled. `entity X` + `sort X` is refused outright\nby 059 R1 / WI-997, so the \"half-wired\" shape is a load error and no program reaches\na KB built from it. The ENCLOSING link stays `is_new`-gated — and the reason is\nstronger than symmetry: for both reuse populations that link is ALREADY on the scope\nwhen this arm runs (Item::Namespace adds it on the same hash-consed nullary term;\nregister_stdlib_scopes adds one per pre-registered sort), so un-gating it would\nre-offer a present link rather than supply a missing one. The one shape it genuinely\ngates out is `entity X` + `sort X`, where a free-standing entity built no scope.\n\nA REGRESSION THE UN-GATE INTRODUCED, found by review and FIXED here. `is_new` had\nbeen capping the exposure link at one push per name as a side effect. load_incremental\nre-scans files already in the KB — three idempotence tests do exactly that — so\nwithout the cap every reload pushed another copy: anthill.prelude 28 -> 56 exposure\nparents on the second load, unbounded in the number of loads, and silent (the WI-997\nledger is per scan_definitions call, so it reports no duplicate declaration).\nSymbolTable::add_parent is now IDEMPOTENT per distinct ScopeInclusion — the set\nsemantics resolution already assumes (visited-guarded walk, matches deduped by\nsymbol). Dedup is on the WHOLE inclusion, so two links differing only in\ninstantiation_term_raw (one spec required at two instantiations) both stand.\n\nTESTS. wi994_variant_exposure_test (4, new) + intern::tests::\nadd_parent_is_idempotent_per_distinct_inclusion (new) + wi979_declaration_order_test::\nvariant_exposure_is_order_independent_wi994 (flipped from expect_load_errors).\nBacking each change out was RUN, not predicted: restoring `is_new &&` fails 3 of the\nnew/flipped tests and leaves both controls green; deleting add_parent's guard makes\nthe idempotence assertion read 3.\n\nFULL WORKSPACE: 4110 passed, 0 failed (baseline 4105 + 5 new).\n\nFOUND, PINNED, NOT FIXED -> WI-1005. The ambiguity is reported TWICE for one\noccurrence, once per scope it is remapped in (the operation's, the enclosing\nnamespace's). dedup_key is deliberately injective and the two renderings differ only\nin the scope name. Easy to miss: name the namespace and the operation alike and they\ncoincide and dedup — which is what hid it from the probe this ticket's test was\nwritten from (4 errors) until the fixture renamed the namespace (5).\n\nNOT DONE, recorded rather than fired: the \"drive an op, assert the built entity's\nfunctor QN\" shape is hand-rolled at 9 sites across 5 test files (eval_test,\nwi818_executable_backing_test, wi204_sort_param_test, wi858_pair_orderings_test) and\nthis ticket adds 2 more; tests/common/mod.rs documents lifting such a shape at the\nSECOND live copy. A local helper covers this file; the lift is a migration of its own.", at: "2026-08-04T21:36:51Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-994", description: some(value: "Variant-exposure link is order-dependent: `namespace X` before `sort X { entity V }` leaves bare `V` unresolvable from the enclosing scope.\n\nFOUND WHILE DRIVING WI-979, and the obvious fix was TRIED AND REVERTED there --\nthe revert is the finding, so read it before re-attempting.\n\nTHE DEFECT. scan_items_pass1's Item::SortWithBody arm gates the variant-exposure\nlink on `is_new && has_variant` (kb/load.rs ~2771). Measured, on a NON-eponymous\nsort (the eponymous shape cannot see this -- with `entity Rec` inside `sort Rec`\nthe bare name resolves as the sort itself, so the link is never consulted):\n\n  namespace vb\n    namespace Colour  operation twice(c: Colour) -> Int64 = 2  end\n    sort Colour  entity Red(v: Int64)  end\n    operation drive() -> Int64 = Red(v: 5).twice()\n  end\n    => 10:32: type mismatch in Red.apply: expected known operation or\n              arrow-typed variable, got unknown functor\n  CONTROL, the same two declarations swapped  => loads clean, drive() = 2\n\nSame class as WI-979's category loss -- a fact read off WHICH DECLARATION CAME\nFIRST rather than off the declarations -- and reachable through exactly the pair\nproposal 059 R2 blesses. Pinned in both directions by\nwi979_declaration_order_test::variant_exposure_is_still_order_dependent_wi984,\nwhich uses expect_load_errors, so when this lands that test FAILS and the fix\ncannot arrive silently.\n\nWHY `if has_variant` ALONE IS WRONG -- MEASURED, this is the whole ticket.\n`is_new` is false for TWO unrelated populations, and un-gating serves the wrong\none:\n  (a) declaration-reuse -- `namespace X` (or `entity X`) already defined the\n      name. This is the case that wants the link.\n  (b) BOOTSTRAP-reuse -- register_prelude pre-registered the symbol. Measured:\n      anthill.prelude.{List, Option, TypeExtractor, EffectExpression} and\n      anthill.reflect.{MemberKind, Expr, Pattern} are all pre-registered and then\n      re-declared with variants in stdlib source. Un-gating hands all seven an\n      exposure link they never had -- ~55 short names newly bare-resolvable from\n      anthill.prelude / anthill.reflect.\n\nDRIVEN CONSEQUENCE of (b), the reason for the revert:\n\n  namespace p\n    import anthill.prelude.*\n    import anthill.reflect.*\n    operation p(t: Type, g: List[T = Term]) -> EffectExpression\n      = guarded(label: t, guard: g)\n  end\n\n  with `has_variant` alone => ambiguous symbol 'guarded': candidates\n      [\"anthill.prelude.EffectExpression.guarded\",\n       \"anthill.reflect.LogicalQuery.guarded\"]\n  gated (today)              => resolves uniquely to LogicalQuery.guarded\n\nTHE FULL WORKSPACE SUITE STAYED GREEN THROUGH THIS. There is exactly one wildcard\nimport in the repo (inside a parse test), so nothing in-tree trips it. A green\nsuite was not evidence, and will not be for the real fix either -- this ticket\nneeds its own ambiguity guard.\n\nSECOND CONSEQUENCE, also driven: un-gating half-wires the shape 059 R1 refuses.\n`entity Rec(n)` + `sort Rec { entity Inner }` starts loading clean with bare\n`Inner` resolving OUT of the sort, while the body still cannot see IN, because\nthe ENCLOSING link (load.rs ~2725) stays `is_new`-gated. Variants leak out of a\nscope whose body resolves nothing. WI-979's pinning test for that shape was blind\nto it (its fixture had no variant); the fixture now carries one.\n\nWHAT THE FIX NEEDS: a predicate that tells declaration-reuse from bootstrap-reuse.\n`is_new` cannot -- it is false for both. Candidates worth measuring: whether the\nexisting symbol was defined by `define` (scope-local) vs `define_qualified_only`\n(which is what register_prelude uses, and which deliberately does NOT insert into\nany scope's locals); or recording the reuse REASON at the site.\n\nALSO CONSIDER, same arm, same class: the ENCLOSING link at load.rs ~2725 is\n`is_new`-gated too. Left refused deliberately under WI-979 because 059 R1 refuses\n`entity X` + `sort X` outright -- do not un-gate it without that verdict.\n\nACCEPTANCE: both declaration orders resolve a bare variant from the enclosing\nscope; the sort-first control still passes; a GUARD test asserts the wildcard-import\n`guarded` case still resolves uniquely (it is the regression the naive fix causes,\nand no existing test covers it); the `entity X` + `sort X` shape still fails\nloudly; variant_exposure_is_still_order_dependent_wi984 is flipped to assert\nsuccess rather than pinned failure. cargo-test green via rustland/scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-04T21:36:54Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-877", author: "user", content: "RE-BLOCKED ON WI-869, MEASURED — the WORK section as written REGRESSES EQUALITY and must not ship before per-provision conditions exist. The prior feedback's 'the WORK section stands unchanged and is now literally one operation' is HALF right: the one-operation part holds (see below, it was built and driven), but re-check item (2) — 'measured harmless may stop being true' — came due. It is no longer harmless.\n\nTHE REGRESSION, with its control. Applied the WORK verbatim (requires Ordered[A], requires Ordered[B], provides PartialOrd[Pair], provides Ordered[Pair], one compare body): PartialEq.eq(pair(fst: 1.5, snd: 1), pair(fst: 1.5, snd: 1)) becomes the LOAD ERROR '7:10: type mismatch in anthill.prelude.PartialEq.eq.dispatch: expected matching impl for per-call bindings, got no impl matches'. CONTROL measured on baseline pair.anthill: the same program LOADS CLEAN and EVALS Int(1). Cause: Float provides PartialOrd and NOT Ordered, and a sort's requires chain is threaded WHOLESALE at every dispatch through the carrier, so the ordering requirement makes eq demand a total order too. That is Pair ceasing to be a general PRODUCT — the exact outcome pair.anthill's header records refusing an Eq chain to avoid, and WI-562's List.member lesson ('a blanket sort requires made List[NonEq] ill-formed for EVERY spec-op dispatch through it') met a second time. Naming the slots (requires OA: Ordered[A]) changes NOTHING — measured, same error.\n\nWHY WI-869 IS THE PREREQUISITE AND NOT A SOFT ORDERING: the only two other spellings are REFUSED BY THE IMPLEMENTATION, measured, so there is no way to express the ordering today without the sort-level chain. (a) OP-SCOPED on compare — the List.member fix — is refused: \"'anthill.prelude.Pair' overrides 'anthill.prelude.Ordered.compare' but does not refine it: it strengthens the precondition — the override requires a condition the spec operation does not\". An override cannot add a requirement the spec op lacks; this is why ordered.anthill could put op-scoped requires on PartialOrd.gt (a DECLARATION) and Pair cannot on compare (an OVERRIDE). (b) NO requirement at all is refused: 'type mismatch in anthill.prelude.Ordered.compare.requires: expected requires Ordered[...] covering abstract type parameter, got missing requires Ordered[T = ...] on enclosing sort'. So: sort-level chain or nothing, and sort-level chain costs equality. WI-869's 'provides Ordered[Pair] :- Ordered[A], Ordered[B]' beside an unconditioned PartialEq is exactly and only what unblocks this.\n\nWHAT IS ALREADY PROVED, so the pickup does not re-litigate it — the ORDERING HALF IS SOUND AND ONE OPERATION. With the WORK applied, all of this MEASURED WORKING: bracket-less Ordered.compare on pairs answers 1 / -1 / 0, with snd breaking a fst tie (lexicographic, not fst-only) and fst winning when snd runs the other way; a HETEROGENEOUS Pair[Int64, String] orders through two element orderings at once; bracket-less PartialOrd.gt/gte/lt/lte and Ordered.max/min all answer, with max/min read back through the winner's fst; SortedSet.empty[T = Pair[Int64, Int64]]() with NO ordering bracket sorts to (1,9)(2,1) from both insertion directions. And the one-ness is assertable STRUCTURALLY, which is the technique worth keeping: kb.sort_ops_lookup(Pair, kb.intern(short)) answers anthill.prelude.Pair.compare for 'compare' and the SPEC ops (anthill.prelude.PartialOrd.gt, anthill.prelude.Ordered.max, ...) for the other six — so a later 'fix' that writes the six one-liners back into pair.anthill is caught by the test rather than passing it. All 14 wi858_pair_orderings_test arms stayed green under the WORK, including the two program-declared rivals and their loud bracket-less tie.\n\nTHE 058 §3.5 CHECK-3 INTERACTION, measured as the ticket asked rather than assumed: with a rival declared, the bracket-less compare tie NAMES BOTH the program's rival and anthill.prelude.Pair, and [Ordered = Pair] IS refused (Pair is a CONCRETE provider). Confirmed. NOTE FOR WHOEVER PICKS THIS UP: a canonical WITNESS SORT in the prelude (its own requires OA/OB chain, provides PartialOrd/Ordered[Pair[A,B]], one compare) was measured as a full alternative — bracket-less compare, bracket-less gt, and bracket-less SortedSet ALL work, eq on a pair of floats still answers Int(1), and because a witness IS nameable at [Ordered = ...] it also dissolves the unreachability this ticket flags. It was NOT taken: the decision is to block on WI-869 and keep the order as Pair's own identity. Recorded so the option is not rediscovered as new.\n\nA TEST-SHAPE DEFECT FOUND ON THE WAY, worth fixing whenever wi858 is next touched: wi858_pair_orderings_test::a_pair_of_floats_loads_and_that_has_a_recorded_cost asserts only that a Pair[Float, Int64] DECLARATION loads, so it stayed fully green through this regression. It needs an arm that EVALUATES PartialEq.eq on a pair of floats — that arm is what turns red the moment the chain gains an ordering requirement.", at: "2026-08-05T02:31:02Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-869", author: "user", content: "A SECOND DRIVER, AND IT IS NOW A HARD BLOCKER: WI-877 depends on this ticket (edge added). WI-877 gives Pair its canonical lexicographic ordering, and MEASURED, it cannot be expressed at all without per-provision conditions — the ordering half works perfectly, but the requirement it needs poisons equality.\n\nWHAT WI-877 MEASURED, which extends this ticket's own measurement from Eq to Ordered. Adding 'requires Ordered[A], requires Ordered[B]' to Pair's chain (the only spelling the implementation accepts — see below) turns PartialEq.eq(pair(fst: 1.5, snd: 1), pair(fst: 1.5, snd: 1)) into the LOAD ERROR 'type mismatch in anthill.prelude.PartialEq.eq.dispatch: expected matching impl for per-call bindings, got no impl matches'. CONTROL: the same program on today's pair.anthill loads clean and evals Int(1). So the shared chain does not merely OVER-CLAIM here as it does for Eq[Pair] — it actively BREAKS a working operation, because Float provides PartialOrd and not Ordered.\n\nTHE OTHER TWO SPELLINGS ARE REFUSED, which is what makes this a technical prerequisite rather than a preference. Op-scoped 'requires Ordered[A], Ordered[B]' on Pair.compare: refused, \"overrides 'anthill.prelude.Ordered.compare' but does not refine it: it strengthens the precondition\" — an OVERRIDE cannot add a requirement its spec op lacks (which is why ordered.anthill's op-scoped requires on PartialOrd.gt is fine: that is a DECLARATION). No requirement at all: refused, 'expected requires Ordered[...] covering abstract type parameter, got missing requires Ordered[T = ...] on enclosing sort'. Naming the slots changes nothing.\n\nTWO THINGS THIS ADDS TO THE WORK/ACCEPTANCE HERE. (1) The failing path is the DISPATCH one — 'per-call bindings' in PartialEq.eq.dispatch — not only check_provider_requires. Whatever reads the sort's chain when threading a dispatch through the carrier has to read the OWNING PROVISION's chain too, or Pair will still demand Ordered at every eq. (2) The wanted end state for pair.anthill is FOUR conditioned provisions, not two: 'provides PartialEq[Pair] :- PartialEq[A], PartialEq[B]', 'provides Eq[Pair] :- Eq[A], Eq[B]', 'provides PartialOrd[Pair] :- PartialOrd[A], PartialOrd[B]', 'provides Ordered[Pair] :- Ordered[A], Ordered[B]' — and the acceptance arm that discriminates is that a Pair[Float, Int64] still COMPARES FOR EQUALITY (evaluates, not merely loads) while a total comparison of it is refused naming the unmet Ordered[Float]. REFERENCE: WI-877's feedback carries the full measurement including what the ordering half already does correctly.", at: "2026-08-05T02:31:29Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1006", description: some(value: "`anthill-todo list` orders ids LEXICOGRAPHICALLY, so at id 1000 the newest items stop appearing last.\n\nREPORTED BY USER, 2026-08-05, on the real project: \"list does not show\nWI-XXXX where XXXX > 1000\". They were shown — buried mid-list.\n\nMEASURED, before the fix, on the project's own data:\n  WI-093 WI-094 WI-1000 WI-1002 WI-1003 WI-1004 WI-1005 WI-102 WI-128 ...\n\"WI-1000\" compares below \"WI-102\" at the fourth character, so every\nfour-digit item wedges between WI-094 and WI-102.\n\nCAUSE. main.anthill's `merge_by_id` compared whole id STRINGS with\n`Ordered.lt`. Its caller `chrono_topo` documents the top-level cursor as\nadvancing CHRONOLOGICALLY, and ids are allocated monotonically (`max + 1`,\nanthill-todo/src/main.rs), so the number IS the age — which is what makes\nnumeric order the chronological one and the string compare simply wrong.\n\nFIX. `id_before` compares the trailing DIGIT RUN, leading zeros dropped, by\n(length, then code units) — numeric order once no leading zero survives, and\nneeding no string->Int64 parse, which `anthill.prelude.String` does not\ndeclare. Ties fall back to the whole id so the order stays TOTAL and the\nmerge sort stays stable and deterministic; an id with no digit run gives \"\"\nand sorts first, a defined place rather than a trap.\n\nTHE ZERO-STRIP IS LOAD-BEARING and has its own test, because the tempting\nsimplification (compare the digit run AS WRITTEN by length) gets the reported\ncase right and \"WI-0094\" vs \"WI-999\" backwards.\n\nTESTS: cmd_list_id_order_test (3). Both backouts were RUN, and the two\nfailing rows are not interchangeable — restoring `lt` fails only\nfour_digit_ids_sort_after_three_digit; dropping `drop_leading_zeros` fails\nonly zero_padding_does_not_decide_the_order. ids_below_the_boundary_are_\nunchanged passes in all three configurations, by design."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-05T02:35:57Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-984", author: "user", content: "FROM WI-1004 (DELIVERED, scaland) — WHAT THE PATH-DEPENDENT CLOSE COST, AND WHY IT DOES NOT MAKE THIS TICKET A PARITY ITEM. Scaland now has `class SymbolTable: opaque type ScopeId = TermSymbol` as a MEMBER, so `a.ScopeId` and `b.ScopeId` are distinct types and a scope minted by one table is a compile error at another's `resolveInScope` or at the KB's clause `domain` (measured: `Found: a.ScopeId  Required: b.ScopeId`, Scala 3.6.3). Rust has no path-dependent types, so this exact shape does NOT port — do not hold WI-984 for it, and do not widen WI-984's scope to chase it. THREE FACTS WORTH CARRYING ACROSS ANYWAY. (1) The cost was NOT a tag on the symbol: it was one type PARAMETER each on the three records that hold a scope with no table in scope — `SymbolDef` (covariant, so `Unresolved` stays a member of every table's), `ScopeInclusion`, and `KnowledgeBase`'s `RuleEntry` — plus dependent method types (`fn(kb, scope: kb.ScopeId)`) at ~30 loader signatures. The KB PINS ONE TABLE (`type ScopeId = symbols.ScopeId`) rather than each clause carrying a table of its own; that was the question to settle first and it sized everything else. (2) The close is over the USE, not the MINT, and that gap is REAL and stays open in scaland: `scopeOf` still takes a bare symbol, so `kb.symbols.scopeOf(fileSymSymbol)` type-checks, is in range, and silently names whatever KB symbol shares that index. Closing THAT needs the SYMBOL tagged with its table, which in both trees reaches the term store and the discrimination tree (they key on the symbol and have no table to speak of). Scaland asserts the hole rather than implying it is closed (`ScopeIdentityTest`, 'the mint refuses a symbol its table never issued'); a rust newtype-per-table would inherit exactly the same split. (3) The range check is therefore LOAD-BEARING, not vestigial — it is the only thing between a foreign symbol and an index panic in a display path — so if WI-984 mirrors `scope_of`, mirror the bound check with it. Delivered 2026-08-05.", at: "2026-08-05T03:02:45Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1004", description: some(value: "SCALAND: A SCOPE STILL CARRIES NO TABLE, SO A CROSS-TABLE MINT THAT IS IN RANGE IS SILENT. WI-990 moved the mint onto the table (`SymbolTable.scopeOf`) so every site WRITES which table it means, and made it refuse an out-of-range symbol. That refusal is the wrong direction and its own doc says so: `fileSym` is small and `kb.symbols` large, so the loader's own hazard — a parse-time symbol minted as a KB scope, `ScopeId.of(imp.path.last)` in its old spelling — is always IN range and always passes. MEASURED and asserted as a limit in `ScopeIdentityTest` (`WI-990: the mint refuses a symbol its table never issued`): a second table's symbol #0 mints a scope that `scopeDisplayName` calls `_global`, while its own table calls it `a`. The check catches only the reverse direction, which is the one the WI-990 ticket happened to measure. WORK: make the cross-table hand-off a COMPILE error by making `ScopeId` a path-dependent member of `SymbolTable` — `class SymbolTable: opaque type ScopeId = TermSymbol`. Then `st1.ScopeId` and `st2.ScopeId` are distinct types and `st2.resolveInScope(n, aScopeOfSt1)` does not compile. VERIFIED against Scala 3.7.4 on a two-class scratch: it reports `Found: (s : a.ScopeId) Required: b.ScopeId`. NOTE this needs NO tag on `TermSymbol` — the first guess, recorded in WI-990's own `scopeOf` comment before the check, was that closing this meant tagging the symbol and so reaching the term store and the discrimination tree. It does not. WHAT MAKES IT INVASIVE, and it is the whole cost: three types hold a `ScopeId` with no table in scope — `SymbolDef.Resolved.scope` and `ScopeInclusion.parent` (both in `intern/Symbol.scala`, OUTSIDE the class) and `KnowledgeBase`'s `RuleEntry.domain` plus `byDomain_`'s key (WI-983). Each needs a type parameter or a move inside `SymbolTable`, and every loader signature threading a scope becomes a dependent method type (`def f(kb: KnowledgeBase, scope: kb.symbols.ScopeId)` — legal, parameters are stable identifiers). Settle FIRST whether `RuleEntry` gets a type param or the KB pins one table, because that decision sizes the rest. ACCEPTANCE: a `typeCheckErrors` case asserting that a scope minted by one `SymbolTable` is rejected at another's `resolveInScope`, with a positive control on the same-table call, as `ScopeIdentityTest` already does for the Int/TermId/companion-mint cases; the `WI-990: the mint refuses...` case's third assertion (the in-range survivor) must then be INVERTED, since it exists only to record this hole. TRAP, measured during WI-990: zinc does NOT recompile a `typeCheckErrors` test when the code it type-checks changes, so backing the change out and running `testOnly` reports GREEN off a stale class file — every control here must be measured after `core/clean`. Say at the site which test fails when the change is backed out. RUSTLAND has no twin yet: WI-984 (open) has not brought `ScopeId` there at all, and rustland's scope keys are still TermId raws, so do not treat this as a parity item — note it in WI-984 instead. Found by code review of WI-990 (2026-08-04). Related: WI-990, WI-976, WI-983, WI-984."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "scaland-sbt-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-05T03:24:31Z"))
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-1007", name: "scaland")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-978", description: some(value: "FOUND WHILE DRIVING WI-975 (see docs/proposals/059-secondary-entries.md). The\nload-time BACKING OBLIGATION for a provision does not reach a provision declared\ninside a NAMESPACE AT A SORT'S ADDRESS (059 calls that a \"secondary entry\" to the\nsort's scope; `entity Rec(...)` beside `namespace Rec ... end`).\n\nMEASURED, with the detector \"declare the provision with NO backing and see\nwhether the load is refused\":\n\n  namespace y                              -- fires\n    sort Show { sort T = ?; operation show(x: T) -> Int64 }\n    entity Rec(n: Int64)\n    fact Show[T = Rec]\n  end\n  => REFUSED: 'y.Rec' provides 'y.Show' but backs no operation 'y.Show.show'\n     (no default on 'y.Show', no own 'show' on 'y.Rec')\n\n  ...the SAME fact moved inside `namespace Rec ... end`  => LOADS CLEAN.\n  ...and it still loads clean with the backing member sitting right beside it.\n\nSo the obligation is skipped for that placement, both when it would have fired\nand when it would have passed. The check is not reporting a different answer --\nit never runs.\n\nLIKELY MECHANISM (not confirmed): the provision scan walks declared sort BODIES,\ni.e. `Item::SortWithBody` items; a secondary entry's items arrive under an\n`Item::Namespace`, so they are never visited.\n\nNOT ESTABLISHED, and the FIRST thing to pin: whether a provision declared there\nparticipates in INSTANCE DISPATCH at all, or only misses the check. The\ngeneric-caller fixture built to test it (`operation useIt[T](x: T) -> Int64\nrequires Show[T] = Show.show(x)`) failed identically for BOTH placements\n(\"operation has no body: z.Show.show\"), so it measured nothing. Answer that\nbefore choosing the fix: if dispatch also misses the provision, the defect is\nlarger than a missing check.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS: proposal 059 R3 explicitly ALLOWS `fact Spec[X]` and host\n`provides ... language ... end` blocks in a secondary entry -- that placement is\nmost of the point of the mechanism (a secondary entry supplying members for a foreign\ncarrier must be able to carry the provision for them). It is allowed into a\nplacement the obligation scan skips, so a carrier can claim a spec with nothing\nbacking it and load clean.\n\nACCEPTANCE: the obligation fires for a provision declared in a secondary entry,\nwith the same message it gives one level out; a test drives BOTH placements with\nbacking absent (both refused) and present (both accepted) -- the both-sides pair\nis the control, since a test that only asserts the refusal passes on an\nimplementation that refuses every provision. State at the test site which half\nfails when the fix is backed out. cargo-test green via rustland/scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-05T03:58:27Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-978", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. The ticket's LIKELY MECHANISM was refuted; the real one is narrower\nand order-dependent.\n\nNOT the obligation scan. It does not walk `Item::SortWithBody` items — it walks\nthe `SortProvidesInfo` FACTS in the loaded KB (`check_provider_operations`,\ntyping.rs). It was never skipped for a secondary entry: measured, it already\nfired for a `sort X` main entry AND for an `enum X` one, and for a free-standing\n`entity X` when the `namespace X` block was written FIRST.\n\nThe EMITTER dropped it, keyed on DECLARATION ORDER. `maybe_emit_fact_provides_info`\n(load.rs) branched on `kind_of(domain)` = `primary_kind()` = the FIRST category\nadded to the symbol, while a secondary entry's symbol carries a SET of them\n(WI-956/WI-926). Measured, beside a `namespace X` block:\n\n    sort X … end     primary = Sort       has_kind(Sort) = true\n    enum X … end     primary = Sort       has_kind(Sort) = true\n    entity X(…)      primary = ENTITY     has_kind(Sort) = true\n\nSo a claim in a secondary entry to a §6.3 free-standing entity matched NEITHER\narm and fell out of the `_ => return`: no `SortProvidesInfo` was emitted at all.\nThat answers the ticket's \"FIRST thing to pin\" — the provision did not merely\nmiss the check, IT DID NOT EXIST, so every reader missed it, dispatch included.\nThe defect was larger than a missing check, but in the other direction: one\nemitter, not a scan that needed teaching about a new item shape.\n\nFIX: `has_kind(domain, Sort)` — \"does this scope name a type\" — the reading 059\nitself prescribes. One branch, one site (the only `kind_of(domain)` in load.rs).\n\nA SECOND POPULATION, found by making the fallthrough LOUD rather than by\nreasoning about it: a `fact Spec[X]` at a FILE'S TOP LEVEL, whose domain is the\nsynthetic `_global` root scope — no declared category at all, so `Namespace` was\nas false for it as `Sort` was. It emitted no provision either, while the\nidentical text one `namespace` in did. Hence the else is \"everything else\", not\n`has_kind(…, Namespace)`: the classification is binary, and there is no third\ncase left to fall out of. `parse_test::load_polynom_with_ring_requirement` is\nthat fixture; it fails loudly against the three-arm spelling.\n\nTESTS: `rustland/anthill-core/tests/wi978_secondary_entry_provision_test.rs`,\n5 tests. The 2x2x2 acceptance matrix (entity/sort carrier x claim in-entry/one-\nlevel-out x backing absent/present) is split into two tests that report EVERY\nbad row rather than panicking on the first, so backing the fix out names the\ndiscriminating row exactly. MEASURED by reverting the branch: in each half\nexactly ONE of the four rows fails — `entity` carrier, claim IN THE SECONDARY\nENTRY. The unbacked half loads clean; the BACKED half also loads clean and\nrecords `[]`, which is why the test counts provisions instead of reading the\nverdict. The other three cells pass either way, by design, and are the control.\n`plain_namespace_provision_still_derives_its_carrier` pins the arm the fix must\nnot have widened; it too passes either way.\n\nFull workspace: 4118 passed, 0 failed (rustland/scripts/test.sh).\n\nDOCS: the rule is in `docs/kernel-language.md` §6.3, on the WI-931 bullet it\ncompletes (\"the obligation follows the claim, not where it is written or which\ndeclaration came first\"). 059's table row and R3 paragraph corrected — they said\nthe check does not run; it runs, the emitter did not feed it.\n\nSPUN OFF as WI-1008, measured here and deliberately not folded in: with the\nprovision now recorded, a generic caller reaching the carrier through\n`requires Show[T = T]` STILL dies `OperationBodyMissing` when the backing\noperation is declared in the secondary entry — and identically whether the claim\nsits in the entry or one level out, while the same caller answers 111 with the\noperation in the SORT BODY. The OP's placement is that discriminator, not the\nclaim's. It is dispatch-side, in another subsystem, and needs a decision\n(widen dispatch vs. narrow `op_backed`, the latter contradicting 059 R2).", at: "2026-08-05T03:58:29Z")
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-1009", name: "scaland")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1007", description: some(value: "SCALAND: THE LOADER'S WHOLE EXPRESSION-CONVERSION CLUSTER IS UNREACHABLE — ~250 LINES, NO CALLER. `Loader.convertExprTerm` (load/Loader.scala) has exactly ONE reference in the tree besides its own definition: `exprRec`, five lines below it. `exprRec`'s callers are the twelve `load*Expr`/`load*Pattern` helpers, and every one of THOSE is called only from `convertExprTerm`'s own by-name dispatch. It is a closed loop with no external entry — as are `buildList`/`buildSome`/`buildNone`, whose only callers are inside it. MEASURED: deleting the block leaves `core/compile` clean, and `sbt test` is unaffected. The live path is `reallocTerm`, which `LoadPass` calls for facts and rule heads and which never dispatches into this cluster; `ExprLoaderTest` says so in a comment ('facts use reallocTerm, not convertExprTerm'). WHY IT MATTERS beyond line count: it is not inert. WI-1004 spent its most intricate new construct on it (the `Ctx` class, threading `kb.ScopeId` through 14 signatures) before the deadness was noticed, and WI-957/WI-989's span work threads through it too — every future change to the loader's types pays a tax here for code nothing runs. WORK: settle what it IS first, because that decides delete-vs-keep and the answer is not in the code. Two candidates: (a) a port-ahead seam mirroring rustland's `convert_expr_term`, written before scaland had anything to consume Expr terms — scaland has no typer and no evaluator, and `Item.OperationItem` deliberately loads no body ('the declared type + optional body are not loaded', DefinePass's `ConstItem` arm says the same); or (b) a caller that was removed and took the entry point with it — check the history of `LoadPass.atItem` and of `reallocTerm`'s `Term.Fn` arm. If (a), the honest form is a LOUD refusal at the one place a body would enter, not 250 lines of unexercised machinery kept warm; if (b), restoring the caller is the fix and the cluster needs tests that DRIVE it. Either way do not leave it as is. ACCEPTANCE: scaland-sbt-test green, and either the cluster is gone or a test resolves a goal that reaches `convertExprTerm` and asserts the Expr term it built — 'it compiles' is not evidence. Found by code review of WI-1004 (2026-08-05). Related: WI-1004, WI-957."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "scaland-sbt-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-05T04:35:29Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-953", description: some(value: "RUSTLAND'S FOUR SCAN/LOAD WALKS EACH RE-SPELL THE SAME NAMESPACE/SORT RECURSION, and a missing scope is a SILENT SKIP in three of them. The scaland twin was fixed under WI-949 (2026-08-03); the rust side is untouched and is the reason this is a ticket. TODAY (rustland/anthill-core/src/kb/load.rs): `scan_items_pass1` (~2685), `scan_items_pass2` (~2948), `scan_items_pass3` (~3016) and the phase-2 `load_items` (~13261) each derive the child scope the same way — join_segments -> make_qualified(prefix, name) -> `find_scope_by_name` (~3087) -> recurse with the new (scope, prefix) — and differ ONLY in what they do at a rule / operation / import / requires item. THE MISS HAS NO SINGLE ANSWER: pass 2 and pass 3 both write `if let Some(scope) = find_scope_by_name(..)` and drop the whole subtree with NO diagnostic (pass 3 does not even take an `errors: &mut Vec<LoadError>`, so it CANNOT report); pass 1 cannot miss because it defines. That is the silent skip CLAUDE.md forbids: a namespace whose symbol is absent has its imports unwired and its rule heads unregistered, and the load reports success. Note phase 2 dodges the question by a DIFFERENT route — `load_items` builds the scope term from the name (`name_to_sort_term`, ~11614) instead of looking it up, so it cannot miss but also cannot notice a name pass 1 never defined. WORK, mirroring the delivered scaland shape: extract ONE walker parameterised by the per-item action (scaland spells it `walkScopes` + a `ScopePass` trait with `enter_scope`/`at_item`, plus a `ScopeDecl` view over the two scope-opening items so the defining pass can still tell a namespace from a sort without an unreachable third arm); express all four walks through it; give the miss ONE answer — report it, from one helper (scaland: `lookupDefined`), which means threading an `errors` vec into pass 3. SAME-CLASS SITE to fold in, as scaland did: any phase-2 `by_qualified_name.get(..)`-then-skip over a pass-1-defined NON-scope name (scaland's was the `EntityItem` arm dropping its `entity_of` fact). ACCEPTANCE: the walker extracted and all four walks expressed through it; a test that DRIVES the loud miss and FAILS when the report is backed out (scaland's drives `load` without `scan_definitions`, the only reachable miss there — find rustland's equivalent rather than asserting the message alone); the existing suite green via rustland/scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-05T04:42:05Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-953", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED 2026-08-05. ONE walker: `walk_scopes(pass, items, root)` owns the descent (join_segments -> make_qualified -> obtain child scope -> recurse with the new (scope, prefix)); a pass supplies `enter_scope` (yields the scope to descend into), `at_item`, and optionally `exit_scope`. `ScopeDecl` (Namespace | SortWithBody, forwarding name/imports/items) is what lets the DEFINING pass tell the two apart without an unreachable third arm. FIVE passes, not the four the ticket counted: DefinePass / ImportPass / RuleHeadPass / DeclarePass / LoadPass — `declare_field_types` (the WI-936 declaration pass) was a fifth copy of the same recursion, so it joined. THE MISS ANSWER, stated once, in `lookup_defined`: report, via the new span-bearing `LoadError::UndefinedAfterDefinePass`. `lookup_scope` is its scope-shaped use. Sub-pass 3 gained the `errors` vec it never had (its silent skip was not a choice the code could have made differently), file-stamped like sub-pass 2's.\n\nTWO OF THE TICKET'S PREMISES DID NOT SURVIVE. (a) `exit_scope` HAD to exist. scaland's LoadPass only descends at a scope; rustland's `load_sort_with_body` does substantial work AFTER the body (the SortInfo roll-up, the induction rule) that reads names the body has just resolved. A pure enter->recurse shape cannot express that, so the trait carries an `exit_scope` hook and `load_sort_with_body` split into `enter_sort_with_body` / `exit_sort_with_body`; the carrier bindings each sort displaces ride a stack on the pass, since sorts nest. `current_scope` needs no stack — `site.enclosing` is the answer the walker already holds. (b) THE SAME-CLASS SITE HAS NO RUSTLAND TWIN. MEASURED: all nine phase-2 `by_qualified_name.get` sites — none is scaland's EntityItem shape. rustland's entity handling goes through `name_to_sort_term` (resolution), and the rest are speculative classification probes (`logical_sort_qn`, the rigid-projection parent gate, the sort-child ref) whose misses are legitimate and documented.\n\nTHE LOAD PHASE JOINED, and the ticket's 'it dodges the question' is now answered: it still DERIVES the scope by resolving the written name (`resolve_declared_scope` -> `name_to_sort_term`), because that is how it resolves every other name — imports and aliases included — and a scope reached differently from the members inside it would be a second answer to one question. What it gained is the CHECK: `lookup_defined` against the key sub-pass 1 writes, so a name the defining pass never defined is reported and its subtree abandoned instead of bare-interned into a phantom scope (`remap_name` cannot fail — that fallback is why the phase could not notice). Suite green with the check in place across ~2900 tests, so the qualified-lookup gate and `remap_name` agree on every scope in the corpus.\n\nDRIVE + CONTROL: new in-file `wi953_scope_spine_tests`. Within one `scan_definitions` run the miss is unreachable BY CONSTRUCTION — sub-pass 1 defines a scope under exactly the key the others look up — so the drive is the loader's two-phase CONTRACT, the same one scaland's uses: run a later walk over a file the defining pass never saw. Three tests do that (ImportPass and RuleHeadPass directly; the declaration pass and load phase through `declare_file_field_types` / `load_with_visited`) and assert the diagnostic names the scope. VERIFIED discriminating by backing the report out of `lookup_defined`: all three fail (left: [], right: [\"demo\"]), and only they. Control = `the_same_file_loads_clean_through_the_whole_pipeline`, the SAME fixture through `load`, which passes either way.\n\nFull workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh — 100 test binaries, 0 failures. Review pass run inline over the diff (reuse / simplification / efficiency / altitude), not via subagents. Applied: `lookup_scope` uses the existing `make_name_term_from_sym` instead of re-spelling the nullary-Fn alloc; `LoadPass::at_item` lost an inconsistent `this` alias; `sort_decl_kinds` extracted so the two halves of the sort load cannot disagree on kind/keyword; the `ANTHILL_ITEM_TIMING` env read moved from once-per-`load_items` to once-per-walk. SKIPPED, with reasons: (a) the walker allocates `written` for every pass though only DefinePass reads it — ~200 scopes/file, the same trade scaland took; (b) the five one-line `parse_symbols()` impls stay rather than becoming a `walk_scopes` parameter, which would let a pass be handed a different symbol table than it uses internally — the divergence the trait method exists to prevent. NOT filed as a ticket, noted here: ImportPass's `RequiresDecl` arm still skips silently when the required name resolves to no scope, but that is a USER name (not the pass-1 invariant) and phase 2's `load_requires_decl` reports it through `remap_name`, so it is not silent overall.", at: "2026-08-05T04:42:36Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1009", description: some(value: "SCALAND: A PARSE-TIME EXPRESSION MARKER IS NAME-RESOLVED AS IF IT WERE THE REFLECT ENTITY IT SHOULD HAVE BEEN TRANSLATED INTO — capture or leak, decided by a spelling coincidence.\n\nTWO LAYERS, DELIBERATELY NAMED DIFFERENTLY. The parser mints PARSE-TIME MARKERS (`lambda_expr`, `match_expr`, `if_expr`, `let_expr`, `match_branch`, `pattern_var`, `pattern_wildcard`, `pattern_literal`, `pattern_constructor`, `pattern_tuple`). The REFLECT ENTITIES are a different vocabulary — `stdlib/anthill/reflect/reflect.anthill` declares `var_pattern`, `wildcard`, `MatchBranch` — and rustland's `convert_expr_term` is the translation between them (rustland's own parser mints `pattern_var` 9x, `pattern_constructor` 8x, `match_branch` 5x, so the split is by design, NOT a scaland typo). `Prelude.registerExprSorts` registers the ENTITY side. Scaland's copy of the translation was dead and WI-1007 deleted it, so the entity vocabulary now has NO producer at all.\n\nTHE BUG IS NOT THE ORPHANS, IT IS THE FOUR SPELLINGS THE TWO LAYERS SHARE. A marker written in a rule body reaches `reallocTerm`, which resolves EVERY `Term.Fn` functor by name — so where a marker and an entity are spelled alike, the marker CAPTURES the entity symbol. MEASURED (probe written, run, deleted), loading `namespace demo / rule r(?x) :- p(lambda s -> s) / end`, load errors EMPTY:\n    Fn p                                   [bare intern]\n      Fn anthill.reflect.Expr.lambda_expr  [RESOLVED  <- the MARKER, captured]\n        Fn pattern_var                     [bare intern — no entity of that spelling]\n          Ident(69)\n        Ident(69)\nTwo distinct defects in one term. (1) CAPTURE: the KB now holds an `Entity` symbol applied POSITIONALLY to (pattern, body), which is not that entity's declared shape — reflect.anthill gives `lambda_expr` NAMED fields, as `entity match_expr(scrutinee:, branches:)` shows. Anything that later trusts `is_entity_of`/`entityFields` for this symbol reads a term that never went through the translation. (2) LEAK: `pattern_var` one line down has no entity to capture, so it lands as an undeclared predicate name with NO diagnostic — the silent skip the repo forbids. Which of the two a marker gets is decided by nothing but whether Prelude happens to register the same string.\n\nSCOPE, so this is not mistaken for a rename: 4 of 12 `Expr` entities are capturable (`match_expr`/`if_expr`/`let_expr`/`lambda_expr`); the other 8 (`apply`, `constructor`, `var_ref`, the 5 `*_lit`) have no producer; ALL 6 `Pattern` entities and `MatchBranch`/`ApplyArg` have neither producer nor colliding marker. Reachable from any rule body — `fnArg` accepts a full `exprBody`, so `lambda`/`match`/`if`/`let` are legal call arguments (the stdlib's cli/parse.anthill uses that form).\n\nWORK. Settle FIRST which of three scaland actually wants, because they differ in cost and none is obviously right: (a) REFUSE a marker in a position scaland cannot translate — loud, small, honest while there is no consumer, and it turns both defects into one diagnostic; (b) TRANSLATE — restore the WI-1007 cluster from 03415ce1 and give it a caller, which needs somewhere to PUT the result (scaland's KB has no op-body/occurrence slot; rustland uses `set_op_body_node`), so it is the big one; (c) RETIRE the entity registrations and let markers ride as bare interns — cheapest, but then the capture must still be broken or the four keep resolving. NOTE (a) and (c) are not exclusive.\n\nACCEPTANCE: scaland-sbt-test green, plus a test that DRIVES the loaded shape — load a rule body containing `lambda`, and assert what the functor IS (refused / translated to the entity WITH its named fields / a bare intern that is provably not the entity symbol). `hasQualifiedName` is not evidence: `ExprLoaderTest`'s four surviving registration tests pass today and this defect is why they mean nothing. Whatever is chosen, `pattern_var` and `lambda_expr` must stop getting DIFFERENT treatment for no reason.\n\nFound while delivering WI-1007 (2026-08-05); the registration block arrived in the same commit (03415ce1) as the cluster WI-1007 deleted, so this is the remaining third of that port-ahead. Related: WI-1007, WI-957."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "scaland-sbt-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-05T05:12:53Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-859", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. `Provider::SelfProvider` is the third candidate kind, keyed at `canonical_sort_sym(provider)` — the carrier `provision_supplier` (dispatch) already keys it at, so the load check and the dispatch reader cannot answer different carriers. Criterion is `witness_dispatch_carrier == None`, one owner, which also folds in the bare / own-param-bound provision (`provides Spec`, `provides Spec[T = OwnParam]`) since dispatch keys those at the provider too. `is_nameable` = TRUE: the carrier's own name is the bracket that selects it. The op-less-spec exemption is shared with the witness leg; the CONCRETE-provider exemption is deliberately NOT applied — a self-provider is not a rival backend but the carrier the group is keyed ON, and applying it would leave the kind empty (WI-855's `Leaf` is concrete).\n\nVERDICT MATRIX — measured cell by cell over the stdlib and the WHOLE corpus in one instrumented suite run, BEFORE any arm was written (group observations): (0,>=1,0) ADMIT 37; (0,>=1,1) ADMIT 40; (1,0,1) ADMIT 2; (>=2,*,*) AmbiguousInstanceFact 4; (1,>=1,0) MixedProviderKinds 4; (1,>=1,1) absent from the corpus. NO VERDICT CHANGED — 40 groups that were groups of ONE now hold two, and 2 more reach the new admission arm.\n\nTHE CELL THAT HAD TO BE DECIDED was (1 fact, 0 witnesses, 1 self), and the measurement decided it AGAINST the naive nameability reading (which says: a group holding an unnameable candidate is refused). The corpus's two instances are the two OPPOSITE shapes. `test.wi837.hidden.Pebble` is a type-only self-provision COMPLETED by a retroactive `fact PartialEq[T = Pebble, eq = pebbleEq]` — ONE dictionary written in two places, the WI-431 shape, which loads clean and whose `eq` ANSWERS. `test.wi837.ownplusfact.Pebble` is the carrier's OWN `eq` member RIVALLED by a fact binding another — already refused, by `AmbiguousEqDispatch`. A group cannot tell them apart, because the question is per-OP and a group is per-SPEC. Refusing the composition would have taken the first with the second. So the cell is ADMITTED (`fact_beside_self_provider_is_one_carrier`) and the RIVAL half stays with the readers that can count per op (058 3.7) — EqDispatchIndex at load for the Eq family, `spec_op_suppliers_for_carrier` at the value-directed read, `UnselectedInstance` at a classified call. Each is DRIVEN in the test, so \"someone else refuses it\" is measured, not assumed (the rival fixture's actual refusal was measured too: tier 3's `UnselectedInstance`, not eval).\n\nTHE COMPOSITION SPACE IS TILED AND ENFORCED, not argued: a `debug_assert` fires when a group is admitted by neither arm and named by neither diagnostic. DRIVEN — deleting the second admission arm makes it fire on both wi837 `Pebble` fixtures, which is what keeps that arm from being decorative rather than a comment with an `if` around it.\n\nACCEPTANCE. WI-855's pin still loads and still ties at dispatch — but its test was RENAMED (`the_tie_is_invisible_to_load_coherence_either_way` -> `load_coherence_admits_the_tie_either_way`): \"invisible\" became FALSE for the abstract arm, which now forms a group of two admitted by nameability, while the concrete arm still forms a group of one via the witness exemption. Two reasons, one verdict. Three docs that asserted the retired claim were corrected in the same commit (wi855's fixture docs, wi842's RIVAL doc, typing.rs's unit-test SRC doc) — the claim was load-bearing prose in each. The probe is `provider_coherence_candidates`, rendered `kind:qualified-name`, and it shows the (Desc, Leaf) group counting 2 for self+rival with the sole-provider control at 1. Matrix documented at the grouping and in design/058-implementation.md 11. Full workspace green.\n\nBOUNDARY RECORDED, NOT TAKEN (review finding). Kind 3's criterion is one notch coarser than its dispatch twin: `SelfProvider` is recorded from the PROVISION, while `SupplyRoute::Own` is derived from `carrier_own_op` — \"the carrier actually has a member\". Gating kind 3 on the carrier owning at least one of the spec's ops would split the (1,0,1) cell BY CONSTRUCTION (completion -> group of one; rival -> group of two, seen) and delete the admission arm — but the rival half would then need a VERDICT of its own, a NEW LOAD REFUSAL, which is the one thing this phase scoped itself out of. Phase 8b meets the same fork when `self_provides` has to say whether a carrier owning no member self-provides at all.\n\nFOR 8b (WI-860), stated so it is not inherited by accident: do NOT read this grouping for `self_provides`. It carries coherence POLICY — the op-less-spec exemption drops a self-provision whose spec declares no ops, and the concrete exemption reshapes the witness leg — and a defaults relation must inherit none of it. The policy-free classifier is `witness_dispatch_carrier` over `provisions_of_spec`.\n\nSPUN OFF: WI-1010 — a spec op's DEFAULT BODY silently shadows a retroactive instance fact's op binding, so a WI-431 instance of a carrier whose spec op defaults is INERT. Measured three ways while looking for a non-eq driver for the completion cell (answers 1 with a self-provision AND without one; the carrier's own member correctly answers 7). WI-444's \"defaults fill gaps, they do not shadow\" is wired to route 1 and never to route 2, and WI-876's default bodies on the ordering family gave that collision real carriers.", at: "2026-08-05T05:44:49Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-859", description: some(value: "Proposal 058 PHASE 8a — the coherence grouping's MISSING CANDIDATE KIND: a SELF-PROVIDER is a candidate of neither kind (measured at WI-855's delivery: WI-838's one-grouping-per-(spec, dispatch carrier) admits a candidate only as an instance FACT — provision_binds_any_op — or as a WITNESS — witness_dispatch_carrier, which returns None exactly when the provider IS the carrier). So sort Leaf { provides Desc[T = Leaf] } plus an abstract rival forms a group of ONE and the pair loads, tying only at dispatch (the_tie_is_invisible_to_load_coherence_either_way pins it). WORK: add the third candidate kind — a provision of an op-bearing spec whose carrier IS the provider, its ops the provider's own members — to the WI-838 grouping, with is_nameable answered (a self-provider is nameable by the carrier's own name). WHY: it is the PREREQUISITE of phase 8b's one_default load check (the inferred default row is keyed on exactly this candidate), and it independently closes the recorded load-time blind spot. GUARDRAIL — enumerate the verdict matrix and MEASURE each cell before believing it (the WI-805 lesson): self+witness must KEEP loading (tier-3 coexistence with a nameable rival is the point; the runtime tie is phase 8c's to retire via the default rung); self+instance-fact — decide by measurement, minding that both have sort_ref = carrier so WI-842's ConflictingProvisionBindings bucketing may already own the within-carrier conflict; witness+fact stays MixedProviderKinds. This phase changes VISIBILITY, not verdicts, unless a measured cell says otherwise — record any verdict change explicitly. ACCEPTANCE: the WI-855 pin still loads and still ties at dispatch (unchanged behavior, new visibility); a probe shows the group counting 2 candidates for self+rival; the verdict matrix documented at the grouping; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. REFERENCE: 058 §3.6; design/058-implementation.md §3, §6; the pre-restructure §4.9's recorded-not-filed increment."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-05T05:44:52Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1011", description: some(value: "Memoize the spec-op supplier lookup WI-1010 put on two hot paths. WI-1010 pointed both WI-444 sites (typer 'check_apply_iter', eval 'resolve_carrier_override_by_value') at 'carrier_override_suppliers' -> 'spec_op_suppliers_for_carrier', replacing a ~3-hash-probe route-1 read ('carrier_own_op') with a WALK over the spec's provision bucket plus a per-provision decode ('fact_head_named_args', two 'get_named_arg', 'sort_ref_functor', 'unwrap_spec_view' with a named-args clone, 'witness_dispatch_carrier' -> 'provision_carrier_sort'). The walk IS bounded by 'provides_rids_by_spec' (3-6 provisions per spec across the corpus, NOT a KB scan), but the FULL cost is paid by the calls producing NO candidate, which is the common case. BOUNDED SO FAR: the TYPER site is per apply-site-per-load, estimated well under 1ms against a 0.12s release / 0.77s debug stdlib load - negligible. The EVAL site is per INVOCATION of an unpinned DEFAULTED spec op, which includes Stream.head/tail/takeN/find/isEmpty/headOption/iterator - the interpreter's collection inner loop - and is UNMEASURED. WI-842's '63.2s -> 63.7s' does NOT transfer and must not be cited for these callers: it sampled 592 BODY-LESS value-directed dispatches, a population disjoint from the DEFAULTED ops these sites serve (058 impl doc S12 records this). WORK: (a) MEASURE FIRST - a thread-local counter in 'spec_op_suppliers_for_carrier' printed at exit gives the real invocation count on a stream-heavy program under 'anthill run'; wi_tests is a poor instrument (wall-clock dominated by 1833 stdlib loads, which will bury an eval regression). (b) If it matters, add a '(spec_op, canonical carrier) -> SmallVec<[SpecOpSupplier;2]>' memo storing the LIST, so multi-candidate visibility is preserved bit-for-bit unlike any short-circuit. Precedent 'sort_param_pairs_cache' (kb/mod.rs), whose doc names these same two hot paths; both readers take '&KnowledgeBase' so a RefCell<HashMap> needs no signature change. INVALIDATION IS REQUIRED and is what the precedent does not need: 'eq_derive::run' asserts provides facts MID-LOAD, so clear at both 'build_provides_index' sites and wherever 'sort_ops' is rebuilt. TRAP, verified: do NOT filter the walk through 'ProvidesIndex.by_carrier' - it keys on the provision's 'sort_ref', which for a WITNESS is the witness sort and not the dispatch carrier, so that bucket silently drops every route-3 supplier. NO SOUND SHORT-CIRCUIT EXISTS: skipping the provision walk on an own-member hit is exactly the first-match blindness WI-1010 removed. SEPARATELY, cheap and semantics-preserving, worth doing regardless of the memo: 'provision_carrier_sort' re-decodes a spec view that 'provisions_of_spec' already decoded and even hands to 'provision_supplier' (passing the bindings down removes half the decode and one heap alloc at all three call sites plus the load-time eq index), and 'provision_supplier' asks the expensive 'witness_dispatch_carrier' BEFORE the cheap 'does this provision supply the op at all' question. ACCEPTANCE: an invocation-count measurement recorded before any optimization; if a memo lands, a test proving a SECOND candidate is still seen through it, and the invalidation driven by a mid-load provides assertion. REFERENCE: WI-1010; WI-842; docs/design/058-implementation.md S12."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-1010"]), status: Open)
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1010", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. Both WI-444 sites (typer 'check_apply_iter', eval 'resolve_carrier_override_by_value') now read a new 'carrier_override_suppliers' = the EXISTING multi-route owner 'spec_op_suppliers_for_carrier' (WI-842 / 058 S4.9) narrowed by 'op_is_interpretable'. Chose the shared reader over 'carrier_override_op(..).or_else(fact)': the chain would have been a FOURTH enumeration of the three routes and first-match-blind, the shape WI-842 deleted. Two consequences, both wanted: route 3 (witness) closes as the same defect one route over, and a SECOND supplier is 'AmbiguousSpecOpDispatch' rather than a silent win for whichever route is read first. Acceptance met: (B) answers 7, (C) still 7, full workspace green 4137/0.\n\nTICKET CORRECTION: the loader accepts (A)/(B) via 'op_backed''s SPEC-OP candidate (the default body), NOT via 'op_bound_in_instance_fact' - that leg short-circuits first and is never reached in these programs. The defect is unchanged; what genuinely certifies the binding is 'check_instance_fact_op_signatures' (WI-431 inc 5), so that is what the docs cite.\n\nGUARDRAIL (the ticket's precondition): stdlib+anthill-stl have ZERO fact-route provisions of a defaulted spec op; the 3 witness rows (Stream.head/headOption, FiniteStream.collect) file under the TYPE PARAM 'List.T', which no runtime carrier equals. Both sites were then instrumented to log every old-vs-new disagreement over a full workspace run of the corpus AS IT STOOD: four, all four the ticket's own programs (A)/(B). Corpus-inert.\n\nCONTROL MATRIX MEASURED, NOT PREDICTED - and the prediction was WRONG: for a call the typer can PIN the two halves are REDUNDANT, so the three headline answers need BOTH backed out before they move. That gap is why two more tests exist: 'a_fact_bound_impls_effects_reach_the_call_site' (only the typer's static pin surfaces a bound impl's EFFECTS - without it the program loads CLEAN and then runs an effectful op, a soundness hole the signature validator cannot catch since WI-431 inc 5 checks arity/params/return but NOT effects) and 'an_abstract_receiver_reaches_the_fact_bound_impl' (only the eval read sees a carrier the typer could not pin). Two tests pass EITHER WAY by design: route 1 and the gap case.\n\n/code-review (4 agents) found and I fixed: (a) the new eval reader was a ~24-line verbatim twin of 'resolve_spec_op_target_by_value' and the tree's only two constructions of 'AmbiguousSpecOpDispatch' - collapsed into one 'sole_supplier_by_value' taking the supplier reader as a fn pointer; (b) MY OWN new comment on 'concrete_self_receiver_override' claimed both its callers are body-less-gated - FALSE, verified: the 'concrete_override_threaded' caller sits in the projection-elimination fallback gated only on the BODY-AGNOSTIC 'self_receiver_spec_sort', so a defaulted op does reach it. The exemption stands on real reasons instead (declining there RE-RAISES the projection error, so no shadow is possible; and for a self-receiver spec no route-2/3 supplier reaches the carrier at all); (c) the interpretability filter - the wrapper's whole reason to exist - had NO CONTROL: added 'an_unrunnable_own_member_is_not_a_supplier', measured both ways (7 with the filter, 'AmbiguousSpecOpDispatch' without). Its stated limit: this does NOT pin the filter's PLACEMENT - pushing it into the shared owner keeps the suite green because the tree has no cpp-mapped rival; the real guard is that 'op_is_interpretable' is BACKEND-RELATIVE (WI-886, rust-only) and the owner's other consumer counts COHERENCE, which must not vary by host backend. That argument is now written at the reader.\n\nDOCS: rule -> docs/kernel-language.md S'Operation override' (a default fills a gap and never shadows a written implementation, whichever of the three routes writes it); mechanism + measurements -> docs/design/058-implementation.md S12, continuing S11's record that filed this ticket.\n\nDEFERRED, both filed with the evidence: WI-1011 (the reader is strictly more work than the ~3 hash probes it replaces; the TYPER site is bounded and negligible, the EVAL site is per-invocation of unpinned defaulted ops incl. Stream.head/tail/isEmpty and is UNMEASURED - and WI-842's 63.2->63.7s does NOT transfer, it sampled a disjoint BODY-LESS population); WI-1012 (the tie is refused at the CALL and on the SLD path that DEGRADES TO SILENCE - resolve.rs residualizes the error to None, so a rule body calling a 2-supplier defaulted op now answers nothing where it previously answered the default; inherited family policy and corpus-unreachable, but the typer holds span+candidates and should refuse a concrete carrier at LOAD).\n\nRECORDED LIMIT, pre-existing and not closed: for a SELF-RECEIVER spec, 'provision_carrier_sort' files a provision under the spec's FIRST TYPE PARAM, so no fact/witness route reaches such a carrier - WI-450's carrier-as-artifact problem, which scoped self-receiver specs out. This ticket changes which routes are CONSULTED, not which carrier a provision is filed under; the eval half is driven through an abstract-spec carrier instead.", at: "2026-08-05T06:45:30Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1010", description: some(value: "A spec op's DEFAULT BODY silently shadows a RETROACTIVE INSTANCE FACT's op binding, so a WI-431 instance of a carrier whose spec op defaults is INERT. MEASURED at WI-859's delivery, three programs differing in one thing each: (A) 'sort Desc { sort T = ?; operation describe(x: T) -> Int64 = 1 }' + 'sort Leaf { entity leaf; provides Desc[T = Leaf] }' + namespace-level 'fact Desc[T = Leaf, describe = leafDescribe]' (leafDescribe = 7) loads CLEAN and 'describe(leaf())' answers 1; (B) the SAME without Leaf's own provision — the fact alone — also answers 1, so the carrier's provision is NOT the cause and this is not WI-859's grouping; (C) the same default beside Leaf's OWN 'describe' member correctly answers 7. So WI-444's rule — defaults fill GAPS, they do not SHADOW — is implemented for route 1 only ('carrier_override_op', filtered 'impl_parent_of_op(o) == carrier', plus eval's 'resolve_carrier_override_by_value'); WI-431's op-valued fact binding never reaches that gate, although the LOADER accepts it as backing ('op_bound_in_instance_fact'). The load check certifies the binding backs the op and dispatch then ignores it — a silent wrong answer, not a missing feature. WORK: give the fact binding the same standing as a carrier's own member at BOTH WI-444 sites (typer 'carrier_override_op', eval 'resolve_carrier_override_by_value'), or REFUSE the combination at load if the precedence is meant to run the other way; either answer must be written into the op-model doc, since 'a retroactive instance backs the op' is what WI-431 promises and what the loader already enforces. GUARDRAIL: this precedence now has real carriers — WI-876 gave 'PartialOrd.gt/gte/lt/lte' and 'Ordered.max/min' DEFAULT BODIES — so enumerate every instance fact in the stdlib and corpus binding a defaulted spec op BEFORE changing which wins; and mind that route 1's own fix needed the parent-sort filter to avoid matching a sibling spec's same-short-name default (WI-444's review gotcha). ACCEPTANCE: program (B) answers 7 (or is refused at load by a diagnostic naming the default and the binding); program (C) still answers 7; a control asserting which tests fail when the change is backed out; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. REFERENCE: WI-444; WI-431; docs/design/058-implementation.md §11."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-05T06:45:32Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-473", author: "user", content: "DISSOLVED by WI-815 (2026-08-05), not implemented — there is nothing left to reclaim. This ticket's entire subject was the `+1` that `NodeOccurrence::term_cache` owned and could never release. WI-815 deleted `cached_term`, the `term_cache` field on every occurrence, and the `KbId` stamp that existed only so a queued `TermId` could be routed back to the store that minted it (`KnowledgeBase::id` and the `KbId` type are gone with it — grep confirms no other reader existed in any crate, test or doc). The one non-test caller, `value_fact_dedup_key`, now keys on a carrier-agnostic `GoalKey` structural fingerprint walked through `TermView`, which allocates nothing in the term store and needs only `&kb`. So: no cached term, no pinned refcount, no queue to drain, no `KnowledgeBase::Drop` purge, and no per-cell-vs-per-TermId release-counting hazard (this ticket's and WI-472's note 1 — the subtlest part of the design, now moot). The WI-246 invariant this was filed to reconcile — \"body/head occurrences hold no term-store refcount\" — is true again with no exception. Closing rather than deleting so the mechanism and the reason it became unnecessary stay findable from WI-471/WI-472.", at: "2026-08-05T08:05:14Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-473", description: some(value: "Deferred-release queue: reclaim WI-471's pinned cache terms when their NodeOccurrence drops, instead of pin-for-lifetime. WI-471's term_cache owns a +1 it never releases (Drop cannot reach the store), so cached terms live until KB teardown -- a bounded but real leak under retract/re-assert churn or heavy transient-type inference. Mechanism (KB-DECOUPLED, no node->KB reference): NodeOccurrence::Drop, if term_cache is Some((KbId, TermId)), pushes that Copy pair into a process-wide thread_local release queue (Drop stays store-free); the KB force-drains its OWN KbId entries at safe op boundaries (top of assert/retract/query), calling terms.release once PER CELL (matching the per-cell +1; NOT per-unique-TermId -- see WI-472 note 1); KnowledgeBase::Drop purges its own orphan KbId entries (no release -- its store is dying). Drop fires only at last-Rc-drop, so sharing is handled automatically (no unique-ownership gating needed). Reconciles the WI-246 invariant exception WI-471 introduced (a cached occurrence now owns a term-store refcount). OPTIONAL, NOT a gate: WI-470 and WI-472 work with pin-for-lifetime and do NOT depend on this; it only trims the leak once they populate the cache in production. Depends only on WI-471; buildable + testable against WI-471's mechanism alone (cache occurrences, drop them, drain, assert refcounts released and the term freed)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-471"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Rejected(reason: "Dissolved by WI-815: cached_term / term_cache / KbId are deleted, so the pinned term-store +1 this queue existed to drain no longer exists. See the feedback entry for the full rationale.", at: "2026-08-05T08:05:42Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-815", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED 2026-08-05. value_fact_dedup_key now returns a GoalKey from goal_fingerprint, takes &self, and materializes nothing; cached_term, NodeOccurrence::term_cache, the KbId type and KnowledgeBase::id are deleted (no reader remained in any crate, test or doc); WI-473 -- not WI-472, which was already Delivered -- is the reclamation ticket, and it is closed as DISSOLVED. Workspace green 4140. /code-review run (4 angles); its findings changed three decisions, recorded below.\n\nSIX PLACES THIS TICKET'S OWN TEXT WAS WRONG OR INCOMPLETE.\n\n(1) THE ACCEPTANCE CLAUSE DESCRIBES AN UNREACHABLE STATE. \"a test asserting two structurally-distinct Opaque-bearing heads stay separate facts\" cannot be written: such heads cannot be facts. push_value_head_entry's discrim insert walks every positional and named child (insert_walk_args) and PANICS on an Opaque head -- a deliberate loud refusal predating this ticket. The guard is therefore driven where it lives, at the key (wi815_a_lossy_key_degrades_to_no_dedup), and the reachability claim is PINNED by a #[should_panic] twin that starts failing the moment such a head becomes storable. The guard still earns its place: the discrim panic is loud, a lossy dedup key silently DROPS a fact.\n\n(2) \"GoalKey::is_cacheable already performs the analogous Opaque/flex-var exclusion ... so the predicate is shared rather than restated\" -- sharing it WHOLE would have been a REGRESSION. The flex-var half is the query cache's own condition, not an injectivity one: two heads holding DIFFERENT Global vars already get different Var tokens and stay distinct. Adopting it would turn every var-bearing head (the loader's omitted-field fresh fills) into a dedup MISS the TermId key did not have. Split instead: one owner for the Opaque predicate (GoalKey::token_bears_payload), two callers, each a SINGLE pass -- an earlier draft had is_cacheable delegate to the new predicate, which doubled the scan on the resolver's per-goal path.\n\n(3) \"INJECTIVITY IS PRESERVED: GoalKey is an EXACT structural token sequence, not a lossy digest -- so distinct heads stay distinct\" IS FALSE IN ONE KNOWN WAY, and the ticket lost the record of it. occ_head's Expr::Apply arm drops type_args, so two Applies differing only there fingerprint identically with NO Opaque token. WI-472's doc had recorded exactly this; my first rewrite dropped it while asserting the stronger claim on a SHARED type, where any future consumer would inherit a promise the implementation does not keep. A review angle caught it. The predicate is consequently named is_opaque_free (what it CHECKS) rather than is_injective (what it is FOR), the caveat is restored at value_fact_dedup_key, and the gap is filed as WI-1013 -- which also names the two OTHER consumers that inherit it (seen_goals answer-dedup and views_structurally_equal) and identifies WI-839 as what would make it reachable.\n\n(4) THE TICKET DID NOT SEE THE KEY-SPACE QUESTION, which is the only behavioural one. fact_dedup was SHARED with Term heads, because the value key was materialized into it -- so pre-change a Value::Term head and a structurally-identical Node/Entity head could dedup against EACH OTHER, and a GoalKey-keyed map cannot. MEASURED rather than assumed, by instrumenting both hit paths to record the carrier of the entry already at the key over a full workspace run: 103451 value-head keys (103449 Entity, 2 Node), 57 dedup hits -- every one value-against-value -- and ZERO in either cross direction. Split kept (rekeying Term heads would trade an O(1) interned id for a walk on the load path, and a hash-consed TermId already IS a Term's structural identity). Because a measurement of one moment is not a guard, the disjointness is now a RULE with a test: wi815_the_two_key_spaces_are_disjoint.\n\n(5) \"fact-dedup behaviour is otherwise unchanged\" is now an exact number, not a claim: the same instrumentation on the NEW key gives 103455 / 58 / 3, and subtracting this ticket's own new tests (exactly +4 keys, +1 hit, +3 rejections) leaves 103451 / 57 / 0 -- identical key for key.\n\n(6) RuleEntry.dedup_key IS GONE TOO, which the ticket did not scope. WI-472 stashed the derived key only because cached_term needed &mut kb, which retract could not give it; value_fact_dedup_key is &self, so the obstacle vanished with the materialization. I nevertheless KEPT the field at first, on the argument that recomputation's correctness rests on view stability nothing pins. Three review angles pushed back and were right, for two reasons I had not weighed: remove_ground, ten lines below in the SAME function, already re-derives the discrim path from the same head through TermView, so retract's dependence on view stability is pre-existing and load-bearing either way; and the path is COLD -- instrumented, the entire suite performs FIVE value-fact retracts against ~103k asserts, all five agreeing with a recompute. A stash would have paid a GoalKey deep clone per ASSERT (plus 16 bytes on every RuleEntry, nearly all None) to save five walks.\n\nMY OWN INSTRUMENT WAS WRONG THE FIRST TIME, which is worth recording because the failure looked like data. v1 of that experiment compared stash against recompute on EVERY retract, including rules and Term-headed facts where the stash is None BY DESIGN and a recompute answers Some. It reported ~77k \"disagreements\" that measured nothing but the instrument. Restricted to the population the stash was for: 5/5 agree.\n\nAND A PREMISE I WROTE INTO MY OWN FIX'S COMMENT. The first draft justified the stash by claiming goal_fingerprint reads the typer's annotation channel and could answer differently later. FALSE -- occ_head reads pattern_type_ann, an immutable NodeKind::Pattern field, never the RefCell channels (classification / inferred_type / lowered_receiver). I corrected the premise before shipping; the conclusion it propped up did not survive the measurement either. Same pattern as WI-1010, WI-908, WI-934.\n\nCONTROLS, both measured over the full workspace rather than predicted. Deleting the injectivity filter from value_fact_dedup_key fails EXACTLY ONE of 4140 tests -- wi815_a_lossy_key_degrades_to_no_dedup, the one written for it; nothing else drove the filter. Stated at that test's site along with what passes EITHER way by design (an Opaque head got no key before this change either, by the weaker route of value_to_term erroring), so it is a control for the GUARD, not for the port; the port's controls are wi472_node_head_fact_dedup and the compile itself.\n\nPERFORMANCE, measured rather than argued, since the path runs ~56 times per KB load. Controlled A/B of the load-heavy wi_tests binary (1833 stdlib loads), same machine, three runs each: baseline 82.45 / 80.84 / 81.81 s, new 81.67 / 81.11 / 81.09 s -- fully overlapping. Expected: the same traversal minus a hash-cons store probe and refcount per node. Deleting term_cache also takes 24 bytes off every NodeOccurrence. goal_fingerprint now pre-sizes its output Vec (a StructToken is 32 bytes, so an un-sized one regrew 3-4 times per key), helping the resolver's per-goal path too. One efficiency premise was REFUTED by review and is recorded so it is not re-raised: Substitution::new() does NOT allocate (imbl HashMap::default is a null root), so the per-call empty substitution is ~104 bytes of stack init, not a heap allocation.\n\nREUSE OUTCOMES: live_dedup_hit and remove_dedup_entry now own the two halves of the dedup protocol (the indexes have different key types, so assert_fact / assert_fact_value / retract would otherwise spell the rid guard and the stale-retracted-entry rule twice apiece). The \"DO NOT finish try_occurrence_to_term's None arms\" instruction moved from this ticket into that function's OWN doc, where a widener will see it. Stale docs swept: resolve.rs's reify_goal_value no longer lists \"dedup key\" among its uses (WI-348 moved answer-dedup, WI-815 moved fact-head dedup), and typing.rs's SpecEqKey note no longer rests on a term_cache field that no longer exists.\n\nREFERENCE: WI-1013 (filed); WI-473 (closed dissolved); WI-472; WI-471; WI-348; WI-839; docs/design/value-facts-carrier-agnostic-resolver.md §Delivered.", at: "2026-08-05T08:08:22Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-815", description: some(value: "Rebuild value_fact_dedup_key CARRIER-NEUTRAL on GoalKey, retiring cached_term / term_cache / the pin-for-lifetime leak that WI-472 exists to reclaim. CONTEXT: surfaced while reviewing WI-762 (a projection guard reached for cached_term, which was the wrong tool). value_fact_dedup_key (kb/mod.rs:2176) needs an IDENTITY for fact-head dedup, and obtains it by MATERIALIZING the occurrence into a hash-consed TermId via node_occurrence::cached_term (kb/mod.rs:2186). It pays three costs for a question that is pure: (1) it needs &mut kb to answer a comparison; (2) cached_term's doc states it \"owns the single `+1` the final `alloc` returns and never releases it (pin-for-lifetime; Drop cannot reach the store)\", so every deduped fact head pins a term for the KB's life; (3) it inherits occurrence_to_term's GOAL-POSITION partiality -- a non-goal shape reifies to Term::Bottom, which is why the function already hand-codes the guard \"a lossy key must degrade to no-dedup, never collapse distinct heads\". THE MECHANISM ALREADY EXISTS AND THIS EXACT MOVE HAS BEEN MADE ONCE: GoalKey (kb/term_view.rs:887) is a \"carrier-agnostic structural fingerprint\" whose own doc says it \"keys the resolver's answer-dedup set directly with no materialization to a TermId and no kb in Hash/Eq, replacing the former materialized HashSet<TermId> key\" -- i.e. the resolver's seen_goals already migrated off exactly this pattern (WI-348). goal_fingerprint(kb, view, subst) -> GoalKey walks any carrier through TermView. INJECTIVITY (the fact-dedup crux, WI-472) IS PRESERVED: GoalKey is a Vec<StructToken> with derived Eq/Hash, an EXACT structural token sequence, not a lossy digest -- so distinct heads stay distinct. The Bottom rejection becomes a StructToken::Opaque rejection, the same rule made TOTAL; GoalKey::is_cacheable already performs the analogous Opaque/flex-var exclusion for the query cache, so the predicate is shared rather than restated. MEASURED BLAST RADIUS -- the chain is a straight line and collapses entirely: cached_term has EXACTLY ONE non-test caller in the workspace (kb/mod.rs:2186; every other hit is its own unit tests at node_occurrence.rs:5113-5132); term_cache is read/written ONLY by cached_term (node_occurrence.rs:2425 / :2431, the rest being the 6 Cell::new(None) inits); and KbId is stamped on term_cache expressly \"so a future (WI-472) deferred term-release queue can reclaim it\" (kb/mod.rs:392, :410). So converting this ONE function retires cached_term, the term_cache field on every NodeOccurrence, the KbId stamping rationale, and DISSOLVES WI-472's reclamation problem rather than solving it -- there is no allocation left to release. DIRECTION: this is one more step of the standing reify audit (WI-348 / WI-482 / WI-621 / WI-678 / WI-692), each of which removed occurrence_to_term calls by making the CONSUMER carrier-neutral rather than by widening the reifier. NOTE explicitly for the next reader: do NOT \"fix\" this by filling in try_occurrence_to_term's unimplemented arms (the args-bearing DotApply arm, Match/If/Let/Lambda). That makes the shim more capable, removes the pressure each None creates, and entrenches the conversion being retired. ACCEPTANCE: value_fact_dedup_key keys on GoalKey with no TermId materialization and no &mut kb for the key itself; a lossy key (Opaque) degrades to NO-dedup, never collapsing distinct fact heads, with a test asserting two structurally-distinct Opaque-bearing heads stay separate facts; cached_term, NodeOccurrence::term_cache and the WI-472 reclamation TODO are deleted (or WI-472 is closed as dissolved, stating so); fact-dedup behaviour is otherwise unchanged -- an existing test pins that re-asserting a structurally identical value-fact head still dedups; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-05T08:08:25Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1014", author: "user", content: "SCOPE CORRECTION + PART B'S SORT HALF ALREADY DELIVERED (WI-815 follow-up review, 2026-08-05). This ticket's Part B described the sorted-key / ordered-product hazard as something to DECIDE, and the code note that pointed here framed it as gated behind occ_head's Opaque arm for Expr::TupleLit. BOTH WERE WRONG, and a driven review found it: the hazard never went through occ_head at all -- a Value::Entity whose functor is anthill.reflect.TupleLiteral heads via functor_view_head and reached value_fact_dedup_key unchanged, so it was LIVE, not gated. MEASURED (heads (xcomp:1, ycomp:2) and (ycomp:2, xcomp:1)): identical GoalKey, both is_opaque_free, assert_fact_value -> RuleId(1) and RuleId(1) -- the second FACT DISCARDED; pre-WI-815 control, value_to_term -> TermId(39) vs TermId(40), distinct, both stored. So WI-815 had shipped a fact-dropping over-dedup. FIXED IMMEDIATELY rather than left to this ticket, because over-dedup drops a fact: fingerprint_into now honours canonicalize_record_named_args's ordered-product exemption (is_ordered_product_functor) instead of sorting unconditionally, and its 'a Term::Fn is sorted-by-name' premise is corrected at the site. A SECOND, INDEPENDENT fact-dropping mechanism on the same walk was found and fixed with it: fingerprint_into emitted NamedKey(k) then re-looked-up the child with named_arg, which resolves by SYMBOL and returns the FIRST match, so (d:1, d:2) and (d:1, d:3) fingerprinted identically -- driven to RuleId(2)/RuleId(2). Reading a duplicate correctly needs POSITIONAL named access TermView does not offer, so the key now DEGRADES (emits Opaque) rather than conflating. Third, same class: a promised child that views as None contributed ZERO tokens, so a key could fail OPEN with no marker -- now emits Opaque, which also closes the Expr::Spliced and type_node_keys variants of the same hole at the fingerprint level. All three pinned by wi815_a_named_tuples_component_order_is_its_identity, with both guards' controls MEASURED by backing each out. WHAT REMAINS FOR THIS TICKET: Part A (SetLit view arm) and the HEAD half of Part B (TupleLit view arm) -- now safer to write, since the sort no longer erases tuple order -- plus Part C (the reflect-gated Opaque fallback) and re-pointing WI-815's two lossy-key tests at a subject that is opaque BY NATURE. NOTE for Part B's remaining half: the duplicate-label degrade above applies to ANY functor with a repeated label, not just ordered products, so it is not tuple-specific. ALSO RECORDED, because it is the lesson: WI-815 claimed 'dedup behaviour unchanged, key for key' on an instrument that COUNTED keys and hits. No TupleLiteral-headed value fact exists in the tree (0 of 103451), and a hit count cannot detect a WRONG key in a population of size zero -- only driving the shape can.", at: "2026-08-05T09:13:27Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-815", author: "user", content: "POST-DELIVERY REGRESSION FOUND AND FIXED (/code-review, driven with controls). WI-815 AS DELIVERED SHIPPED A FACT-DROPPING OVER-DEDUP, and the delivery's own 'dedup behaviour unchanged, key for key' claim was made on an instrument that could not see it. THREE holes, all in fingerprint_into, all now closed and pinned by wi815_a_named_tuples_component_order_is_its_identity with each guard's control MEASURED by backing it out. (1) ORDERED PRODUCTS. The old key ran value_to_term -> canonicalize_record_named_args, which EXEMPTS is_ordered_product_functor (anthill.reflect.TupleLiteral) because WI-788 makes a tuple's component source order its IDENTITY. fingerprint_into sorted unconditionally, so (xcomp:1, ycomp:2) and (ycomp:2, xcomp:1) produced ONE GoalKey, both is_opaque_free, and assert_fact_value returned RuleId(1) for both -- the second FACT DISCARDED. Pre-change control: TermId(39) vs TermId(40), distinct, both stored. Verified independently by driving before acting on the report. Fixed: the walk now honours the exemption, and fingerprint_into's stated premise 'a Term::Fn is sorted-by-name' is corrected at the site -- TermStore::alloc hash-conses as given and occ_build_fn never canonicalizes, so it is false for exactly this functor. (2) DUPLICATE LABELS, an independent mechanism on the same walk: it emitted NamedKey(k) and then RE-LOOKED-UP the child via named_arg, which resolves by SYMBOL and returns the FIRST match, so (d:1, d:2) and (d:1, d:3) fingerprinted identically -- driven to RuleId(2)/RuleId(2). Reading a duplicate correctly needs POSITIONAL named access TermView does not offer, so the key now DEGRADES (emits Opaque) rather than conflating. Applies to any functor with a repeated label, not just tuples; producers refuse duplicates (WI-805/808/809), which is what kept it unreached. (3) A MISSING CHILD FAILED OPEN: Open(f, pos_arity, named_arity) promises N children, but a None child contributed ZERO tokens, so f(<missing>, X) and f(X, <missing>) shared one key with no marker. Now emits Opaque -- which also closes the Expr::Spliced and type_node_keys variants of the same hole at the fingerprint level, and matches how discrim::view_is_indexable answers the same question (map_or(false, ..)). THE LESSON, recorded because it is the reusable part: the delivery instrumented the dedup path and compared COUNTS (103451 keys / 57 hits / 0 rejections, identical before and after). A count cannot detect a WRONG key in a population of size ZERO -- no TupleLiteral-headed value fact exists in the tree -- so 'corpus-inert' was measured and 'correct' was assumed. Driving the SHAPE is what found it. FALSE STATEMENTS IN MY OWN DELIVERED COMMENTS, all corrected: (a) two doc blocks claimed the discrim panic fires BEFORE the dedup guard -- it is the reverse (assert_fact_value calls value_fact_dedup_key first), and the inverted version is precisely the reasoning that would justify deleting is_opaque_free as unreachable, i.e. the deletion its own control test exists to catch; (b) the 'DO NOT FINISH try_occurrence_to_term's None arms' block listed SetLit/TupleLit as None arms -- WI-559 gave both REAL reifier arms ~110 lines below, the list having been copied from occ_head's Opaque table, a different question; (c) the guard test's control rationale said value_to_term 'returned Err on an opaque child' -- its Value::Node arm is Ok(occurrence_to_term(..)) and cannot return Err, so that fixture had a VALID injective key pre-change and deduped correctly, meaning 'unchanged key for key' has a second exception (a MISS, safe direction); (d) retract's 'SYMMETRIC BY CONSTRUCTION' overclaimed -- the key is a function of (head, MUTABLE KB state) via is_constructor_symbol / try_resolve_symbol / lookup_symbol, so safety rests on the RID GUARD, not on stability, and the worst case is an orphan entry rather than an impossibility; (e) a test message asserted 'injective' on an Expr::Apply fixture, the one shape documented as NOT injective (WI-1013). ALSO: live_dedup_hit had turned an out-of-bounds RuleId from a PANIC into a silent 'store it' -- restored to an indexed read, since a bogus RuleId in a dedup index is corruption; the Vec::with_capacity(16) added on an unmeasured suggestion is REVERTED (driven: real head keys run 25-93 tokens, so 16 helps neither caller and over-allocates the resolver's retained short keys); the assert_rule_nodes or_insert / assert_fact_value insert asymmetry is now documented rather than implied; and docs/proposals/048 stopped prescribing WI-471's deleted cache.", at: "2026-08-05T09:14:50Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1014", author: "user", content: "WI-1015 turned Parts A/B from a latent gap into a LIVE REGRESSION on the dot path. `occ_head` answers Opaque for Expr::TupleLit / Expr::SetLit while try_occurrence_to_term has real WI-559 arms for both. `project_field` used to reify its receiver, so a Value::Node(Expr::TupleLit) receiver reified to Fn{TupleLiteral,...}, entity_fields contains TupleLiteral (reflect.anthill declares it), and Dispatch 1 found the named component. project_field now reads the head, so functor_sym() is None and field_access returns Failure. field_access is the desugaring of every ?x.y. NOT fixed inline on purpose: adding the two occ_head arms without the matching occ_view_pos_arg / named_arg / named_keys children reintroduces the WI-815 class (a head promising N children and supplying none), which is Parts A/B in full. The tuple field-access tests (wi638/wi786/wi803) all drive the interpreter twin reflect_field_access, so none of them catches this.", at: "2026-08-05T12:13:44Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1015", author: "user", content: "CORRECTIONS to this item's own description, from /code-review. (1) FIVE tests, not four. (2) builtin_operation_body is at resolve.rs:4071 — it is NOT one of the 'six by-carrier symbol reads outside resolve.rs'; the five outside are value_functor, term_functor_name, dict_resolve_op, view_child_sym, list_items_strict. (3) The producer flip is REVERTED and moved to WI-1016: minting Value::SymbolRef from symbol_value needs a workspace-wide audit and this item's grep covered only anthill-core/src, so anthill-stl's expect_symbol (5 host ops), MapKey, scalar_eq, detect_cycle, scalar_value_expr, constructor_sub_values, fn_value, is_duplicate_projection and anthill-cli render_value were never scanned. The forced-answer ones are fixed here; the representation decisions are WI-1016. (4) The migration to value_symbol is WIDER than the match it replaced, and a doc comment claimed the opposite: resolve_qualified_name_term deliberately bypasses alloc's nullary-constructor canon, so non-canonicalized Fn{c,[],[]} exist and read as ViewHead::Ref. Doc corrected; the widening is undriven at all five sites. (5) builtin_operation_body and term_functor_name lost their Term::Ident arm to functor_sym, which has none — fixed via a new value_head_symbol, the widest of the three symbol readers. (6) lower_ho_apply hand-rolled a chain walk that this file already owned (SearchStream::walk_value_chain), missing the Value::Var arm and carrying only a 1-cycle guard where a 2-cycle across carriers would HANG; now delegates. (7) ViewItem::to_value bypassed Value::node, the normalizing constructor, so project_field could bind a doubly-wrapped Spliced(Var) that reads as BOUND; fixed at the owner. (8) A regression this item caused on the dot path is recorded as feedback on WI-1014 (occ_head is Opaque for TupleLit/SetLit where the retired reify had real arms). STILL OPEN, not fixed: finish_result_value makes field_access and resolve_sort_instantiation_param into the non-Term extra-binding producers that the WI-649 note at resolve.rs:1102 says do not exist ('safe today only by ABSENCE OF A PRODUCER'), so a projected child sharing a var with the goal binds un-occurs-checked.", at: "2026-08-05T12:15:21Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1015", description: some(value: "Retire the resolver's Value -> TermId reify; add Value::SymbolRef. `carrier_term` / `reify_goal_value` were the shared 'structural carrier' readers behind the symbol-reflection builtins and field projection. Every remaining use was a READ that TermView answers without allocating, and the reify was not a narrow cost but a WRONG ANSWER on the carriers it declined: `None` for everything but Term/Node (a Value::Entity that plainly has fields; a Value::SymbolRef that IS a symbol), and try_occurrence_to_term's debug_assert!(false) -> Bottom for an Expr::Spliced leaf. MIGRATED: qualified_name, short_name, lookup_symbol, field_name_from_value, resolve_sort_instantiation_param (param symbol AND SortView instance), project_field (now &Value -> Option<Value>, so a Value::Entity receiver projects and the field child keeps its carrier), lower_ho_apply's predicate read plus its sigma chase (walk_view followed a chain only while every link was a Value::Term), and six by-carrier symbol reads outside resolve.rs: value_functor, term_functor_name, dict_resolve_op, view_child_sym, builtin_operation_body, list_items_strict. NEW SEAMS: KnowledgeBase::value_symbol as the one public by-content symbol reader (value_is_unbound_var precedent); finish_result_value so a builtin result binds in the carrier it arrived on; TermPrinter::write_symbol_ref as one owner for the bare-Ref rendering, so two carriers of one symbol cannot key differently in a store key. Value::SymbolRef(Symbol) is the value-level analog of Term::Ref (Value::Var / WI-109 precedent), views as ViewHead::Ref and lowers to Term::Ref; symbol_value in eval/builtins mints it so reflect ops stop interning transients. carrier_term and reify_goal_value are DELETED - the resolver has no Value->TermId reify left. Four tests, each with a MEASURED control. Not driven: the ho_apply sigma-chase widening (needs an initial sigma, which no resolve entry point accepts) - stated at the test site."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-05T12:26:42Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1017", author: "user", content: "CORRECTION to this item's own premise, then FIXED. This item said 'WI-1015 created exactly that producer'. It did not. MEASURED by probe on the PRE-WI-1015 path — a hash-consed Term receiver producing a Value::Term binding, no new carrier involved: field_access(ProbePoint(x: probe_g(?r)), 'x', ?r) returned ONE solution, bound ?r to TermId(probe_g(?r)) with is_contradiction()==false, and kb.reify of that answer OVERFLOWED THE STACK and aborted the process. So the hole was reachable from ordinary dot projection before WI-1015; the non-Term producers it added only widened the carrier set. The WI-649 note's framing is what hid it: it reasoned about 'non-Term extra bindings' and concluded absence-of-a-producer, but the merge loop never distinguished carriers, and field_access — the desugaring of every ?x.y — has had a live caller all along. FIX: the occurs_in_value guard the note itself prescribed, in the SuccessWithBindings merge loop, checked against new_subst as it accumulates so a cycle formed across two bindings of one extra is caught too; a positive check drops the branch exactly as BuiltinResult::Failure does. Pinned by a_projection_binding_its_own_receiver_var_is_refused, whose control is the stack-overflow abort above and which also asserts an ACYCLIC projection still binds, so the guard refuses cycles and not projections. Full workspace green, 4149 passed; suite runtime unchanged (259s vs 260s), so the per-binding check costs nothing measurable.", at: "2026-08-05T12:46:31Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1017", description: some(value: "Occurs-check the non-Term result bindings that finish_result_value now produces. The WI-649 note at resolve.rs:1102-1112 says the non-Term bind_waking path is 'NOT occurs-checked ... safe today only by ABSENCE OF A PRODUCER: every builtin that writes a non-Term extra binding is either occurs-checked already (builtin_unify) or has no live anthill caller ... If a reflect op that quotes an open term over a shared var ever gains a caller, this becomes the same cyclic-sigma route WI-649 closed at the fact fast-path.' WI-1015 created exactly that producer: finish_result_value binds a builtin result as a Value, and field_access (the desugaring of every ?x.y) plus resolve_sort_instantiation_param now hand a Value::Node / Value::Entity CHILD OF THE GOAL back through extra.bind_value. Concretely field_access(?obj, 'f', ?r) with ?obj sigma-bound to an occurrence Point(f: g(?r)) binds ?r -> Value::Node(g(?r)) with no occurs-check, and reify_value / substitute_occurrence then have no terminating walk over it. Found by /code-review on WI-1015; NOT fixed there because occurs-checking the value-binding path is a design change in WI-649's territory, not an arm. Scope: decide whether finish_result_value occurs-checks (bind_value_unifying already routes through the occurs-checked unify_match_values, so the machinery exists), or whether the projection path must refuse a result that shares a var with its receiver; then update the WI-649 note, which currently states the producer does not exist. Drive it with a rule that projects a field mentioning the result var - the pre-fix behaviour should be a hang or a cyclic binding, not a wrong answer."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-05T12:46:35Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1014", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. Part A (SetLit) and the head half of Part B (TupleLit) mirror their WI-559 twins in occ_head, with occ_pos_child / occ_named_child / occ_named_keys supplying children off the SAME slice the head counted, so arity and named_keys cannot disagree (the WI-814 shape-table discipline). ONE FINDING while writing them: the WI-559 comment at try_occurrence_to_term's tuple arm says its elements 'ride in named_args (positional -> _1/_2 labels)' — that describes the LOADER's build, not occ_build_fn, which passes positionals through AS positionals. A view mirroring the COMMENT instead of the CODE would have disagreed with the twin on arity; recorded at set_literal_functor. Part C: wrapped_expr_head / wrapped_expr_keys are now LOUD (reflect_ctor_sym) instead of falling through to Opaque, matching reflect_field_key one layer down which asserts the same invariant and panics. Chose panic over pre-interning precisely to avoid this ticket's own documented trap (pre-interning would make implicit_target_orphans' by_qualified_name.contains_key see every kernel-vocab name as non-orphaned and defang wi900's guard). Also noted at wrapped_expr_keys: its early 'return Vec::new()' was not even self-consistent with the head it claimed consistency with — the head announced named_arity: keys.len() while the keys came back empty, which is the WI-815 head-promises-N-children defect. WI-815's two lossy-key tests re-pointed: wi815_head's opaque subject is now Value::OpRef (a NATIVE CARRIER, opaque BY NATURE) instead of Expr::SetLit (opaque BY OMISSION); both tests still pass, including the should_panic one. BLAST RADIUS, MEASURED not argued: 0 stored clauses are SetLiteral- or TupleLiteral-headed in a full stdlib load, and could not have been — an Opaque head carries no discrim key and insert_walk PANICS on one — so no candidate set can SHRINK; pinned by no_stored_clause_was_ever_literal_headed. What moves is the QUERY side and is_cacheable, which now admits these goals. SIX tests, each control MEASURED: with both view arms backed out all four core tests fail at their own claim (0 vs 1 candidates; 'the occurrence must read structurally, got Opaque'; and 'a set literal equals itself' — the (Opaque, Opaque) hole). ALSO CLOSED: the dot-path regression recorded here from WI-1015 — a Value::Node(Expr::TupleLit) receiver projects again, pinned by a_tuple_literal_occurrence_receiver_projects_its_component, which drives the REAL declared TupleLiteral (no register_entity_fields overwrite, so it cannot mask whether the declared entity reaches the projection). Full workspace green: 4155 passed, 0 failed.", at: "2026-08-05T13:11:46Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1014", description: some(value: "FINISH occ_head: every form whose TERM TWIN EXISTS must have a structural view. Two forms are still ViewHead::Opaque with a twin sitting right there (SetLit / TupleLit), and one MECHANISM turns a structural head into Opaque on a symbol lookup. CONTEXT: surfaced reviewing WI-815, which made GoalKey the fact-dedup key and so made occ_head's under-determination a soundness surface rather than a precision one. THE RULE THIS RESTORES IS THE FILE'S OWN, stated in term_view.rs's WI-814 note: \"the view's whole contract is that it reads what the term twin reads ... a view that DISAGREES with its own twin is a cross-carrier miss, which is a WRONG ANSWER, not a precision loss\" (WI-425). Today one source `{1}` gives Fn{anthill.reflect.SetLiteral, pos_args: [1]} on the term carrier and Opaque on the occurrence carrier -- the disagreement the rule forbids -- and because views_structurally_equal has NO (Opaque, Opaque) arm, a set literal is not even equal to ITSELF through the occurrence carrier. That is precisely the defect WI-814 fixed for Lambda.\n\nWHY IT HAS NO OWNER: it is a SEAM BETWEEN TWO DELIVERED TICKETS, not a regression. WI-027 gave ListLiteral its term twin and occurrence-build and \"deliberately left the siblings to keep scope tight\". WI-559 (Delivered) closed that remainder -- SetLit/TupleLit got term twins (node_occurrence.rs try_occurrence_to_term, the WI-559 arms) and correct occurrence-builds. WI-814 (Delivered) built the VIEW side, for Lambda / If / Let / Match / Proof / all five Pattern variants, and its delivery note lists THREE known gaps EACH WITH AN OWNER (Let.type_annotation -> WI-819, Pattern::Tuple.labels -> WI-803, the *Within family -> WI-816). SetLit/TupleLit are in NEITHER list. Each ticket was complete against its own acceptance; nobody owns the intersection. ListLit is complete on BOTH sides today while its two siblings are complete on the TERM side only -- the same asymmetry WI-027 opened and WI-559 closed, still open one layer up. VERIFIED: WI-816 is strictly LambdaWithin/HoApplyWithin/ConstructorWithin; no other ticket names SetLit/TupleLit/SetLiteral for the view.\n\nWHAT KEPT IT INVISIBLE, worth naming because it is the reusable lesson: the other three gaps got TICKET NUMBERS; this one got a JUSTIFICATION in the `_ => ViewHead::Opaque` comment -- \"SetLit / TupleLit: path-B siblings of ListLit. TupleLit especially is entangled with tuple IDENTITY (WI-788 order, WI-803 labels, WI-805 distinctness), where a wrong key set is a wrong answer about type identity -- not a place to guess.\" That reads as a PRINCIPLED exclusion and is not one: WI-559 already fixed the functor, the child carrier and the labels ON THE TERM SIDE, so mirroring the twin is exactly what REMOVES the guessing. Inventing a key set would be guessing; nobody is proposing that. A reason in place of an owner is what kept it off every list.\n\nPART A -- SetLit. Mechanical, no open question. The twin is occ_build_fn(kb, SetLiteral, elems, &[]) -- elements in pos_args, byte-identical in shape to ListLiteral. The view arm is a copy of the ListLit arm: functor_view_head(kb, f, es.len(), 0), with children from the same slice. fingerprint_into walks positional children in index order, so no ordering question arises at all.\n\nPART B -- TupleLit, and THE ONE PLACE THE OLD COMMENT HAD A REAL POINT, misfiled. The head itself is equally mechanical -- functor_view_head(kb, f, positional.len(), named.len()), the twin being occ_build_fn(kb, TupleLiteral, positional, named). THE HAZARD IS NOT IN THE HEAD, IT IS IN fingerprint_into's SORT: it emits named args in `keys.sort_by_key(|s| s.index())` order, and justifies that with \"a Term::Fn is sorted-by-name, an occurrence is a fixed slice, so sorting is what makes a Term and a Node agree\". THAT PREMISE IS FALSE FOR THIS FUNCTOR, and that is the finding: TermStore::alloc does NOT canonicalize (it hash-conses the Term as given), and occ_build_fn does NOT call canonicalize_record_named_args -- so a TupleLiteral TERM PRESERVES SOURCE ORDER, while a sorted GoalKey does not. Since WI-788 makes tuple order IDENTITY (an ORDERED PRODUCT is the documented exemption from canonical named-arg sorting, rustland/CLAUDE.md), `(a: 1, b: 2)` and `(b: 2, a: 1)` are DIFFERENT tuples with DIFFERENT TermIds that would share ONE GoalKey. A naive mirror therefore trades \"every tuple collapses\" (today, Opaque is payload-free) for \"order-variant tuples collapse\" -- better, but still an over-dedup, and over-dedup DROPS an answer in seen_goals and DROPS A FACT in value_fact_dedup (WI-815). DECIDE, do not assume: either fingerprint_into stops sorting for ORDERED-PRODUCT functors (which needs a predicate naming them -- the same exemption canonicalize_record_named_args already encodes), or the tuple's order is carried some other way. CHECK WHETHER THE SAME HOLE IS ALREADY LIVE for Value::Tuple, which views as Functor{functor: None, ..} and whose named keys go through the same sort -- if so this is pre-existing and wider than TupleLit, and should be stated as such rather than introduced-and-blamed on this ticket.\n\nPART C -- a structural head must not be CONTINGENT ON A SYMBOL LOOKUP. wrapped_expr_head / wrapped_expr_keys resolve the reflect qname with `kb.try_resolve_symbol(qname)?` and, on None, fall through to `_ => ViewHead::Opaque` -- documented as \"fail-soft, as such a KB holds no loader-built occurrence of it\". ONE LAYER DOWN, reflect_field_key (term_view.rs:184) asserts the SAME invariant and takes the OPPOSITE policy: `lookup_symbol(k).unwrap_or_else(panic!)`, \"KB holds a {form} occurrence but never built a {form} term twin\". Same invariant, opposite policy, and they cannot both be right: if the invariant holds the `?` arm is dead code that DEGRADES instead of erroring; if it does not, the panic fires one layer down anyway. Degrading is the worse half -- Opaque is PAYLOAD-FREE, so it does not lose precision, it makes two structurally DIFFERENT ifs compare EQUAL and share a GoalKey, converting \"reflect not loaded\" into wrong answers rather than an error. That is the silent skip CLAUDE.md forbids. Note these are KERNEL_VOCAB_QUALIFIED reserved names declared by the standard load, and wi900_implicit_tier_agreement_test already asserts every one of them resolves after it, so the fallback is reachable only on a hand-built KB -- the state reflect_field_key declares impossible. FIX: make resolution infallible (pre-intern the reserved kernel-vocab names at KB construction) and delete the `?` arm as provably dead, or make it panic to match its neighbour. TRAP, verified, if pre-interning is chosen: implicit_target_orphans detects orphans with by_qualified_name.contains_key, so pre-interning would make EVERY kernel-vocab name look non-orphaned and silently defang wi900's guard -- that check must move to \"is it DECLARED\", not \"is it interned\".\n\nBLAST RADIUS -- MEASURE BEFORE LANDING, the same three consumers WI-814 measured, because Opaque -> Functor moves all of them at once. (1) DISCRIM: an Opaque head carries NO discrimination key and insert_walk/remove_walk PANIC on it, so a set/tuple-literal-headed clause cannot be stored at all today; giving it a head makes such clauses INDEXABLE and changes candidate sets. Take candidate-set counts over the full corpus before and after -- wi814_lambda_view_test's lambda_cross_carrier_discrim_match is the instrument, and its shape (a fact indexed under the TERM carrier found by an OCCURRENCE-carrier query, plus a negative control that a DIFFERENT literal does NOT match) is the one to copy. (2) GoalKey / seen_goals / query_cache: keys change; pin that nothing which used to dedup stops dedupping, and that distinct literals now SEPARATE. is_cacheable's Opaque exclusion will start ADMITTING keys it used to reject, so the per-query cache begins caching these goals. (3) views_structurally_equal starts answering TRUE for two identical set literals, where it answered FALSE even for a literal against itself.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR WI-815, do this as part of the change: wi815_a_lossy_key_degrades_to_no_dedup (kb/mod.rs) uses Expr::SetLit as its GENUINELY-OPAQUE subject, via the wi815_head(functor, n, opaque) builder. Closing Part A makes that test FAIL LOUDLY at its `assert_eq!(k1, k2, \"an Opaque child erases the difference -- this is the hazard\")` -- the right failure mode, but the subject was chosen wrong: SetLit is opaque BY OMISSION, not BY NATURE. Re-point the builder at something with no structural content in any future -- Value::OpRef{op, dict: none, named: none}, or a Closure / Map / Cell handle. wi815_an_opaque_bearing_head_cannot_be_stored_at_all shares that builder and must move with it.\n\nACCEPTANCE: SetLit and TupleLit read ViewHead::Functor mirroring their WI-559 term twins, with named_keys and children supplied so head arity and named_keys cannot disagree (the WI-814 shape-table discipline); a test pins that a TERM-carrier-indexed set literal is found by an OCCURRENCE-carrier query and that a DIFFERENT one is not; two identical set literals compare views_structurally_equal EQUAL and two distinct ones UNEQUAL; the TupleLit ordering question is DECIDED and its decision tested -- two order-variant tuples either key distinctly or the reason they may not is written at fingerprint_into, whose \"a Term::Fn is sorted-by-name\" premise is corrected either way; the reflect-gated Opaque fallback is gone (infallible or loud), with wi900's orphan check still guarding what it guarded; discrim candidate-set counts recorded before and after; WI-815's two lossy-key tests re-pointed at a subject that is opaque by nature; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. REFERENCE: WI-814 (the view, and its three owned gaps); WI-559 + WI-027 (the term twins, and the seam); WI-425 (a view must agree with its twin); WI-788 / WI-803 / WI-805 (tuple identity); WI-815 (why this became a soundness surface); WI-1013 (the OTHER occ_head under-determination -- Apply drops type_args -- deliberately kept separate: a missing CHILD on the most common form has a different blast radius from two forms that cannot be indexed at all today)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-05T13:11:48Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1018", author: "user", content: "SUPERSEDED BY WI-1019, which is the MECHANISM this ticket only described an instance of. This item framed the question as 'what head should an OpRef get' and listed a new ViewHead variant / a wrapper twin / a payload-bearing opaque as options. The better answer is none of those three as posed: GENERATE an entity representation for the native carrier, reusing the wrapped_expr_head shape table, so equality and keying and indexing all come from the structural machinery instead of a per-carrier decision. ALSO CORRECTING THIS ITEM'S OWN CONSEQUENCE NOTE: it said wi815_head's subject 'is now Value::FactRef, whose OWN DOC says it never exposes a resident RuleId, so a structural view would contradict the type. That subject is unaffected by this ticket.' MEASURED, both halves are wrong. FactRef behaves identically to OpRef — head Opaque, views_structurally_equal(a, a) = FALSE, two distinct rows sharing one goal_fingerprint. The doc's 'opaque' means ENCAPSULATED (do not hand a RuleId to client code), not STRUCTURE-FREE, and ViewHead is internal machinery — keying on identity exposes nothing to anthill. Two subject choices in a row (OpRef, then FactRef) were made by reading docs instead of running the two probes, and both were wrong the same way. The subject IS affected, and WI-1019 must decide it.", at: "2026-08-05T13:26:23Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1019", author: "user", content: "SCOPE DECIDED (user): add REFLECT ENTITIES for the operation reference and for FactRef, rather than a new ViewHead variant or a payload-bearing opaque. reflect declarations live in stdlib/anthill/reflect/reflect.anthill. TWO THINGS THE IMPLEMENTER MUST NOT MISS. (1) THE dict TRAP for OperationRef. The entity must NOT present only op and named. dict is the dispatch environment captured at MINT in the eta-site frame (WI-420); two OpRefs with the SAME op and named but DIFFERENT dicts dispatch under different requirement environments and are not the same value. An entity view that drops dict makes them structurally EQUAL and share a key — a FALSE POSITIVE, the dangerous direction, because this feeds value_fact_dedup where merging DROPS A FACT (WI-815). Note the current stopgap native_carrier_eq is STRICTER than that: it compares dict by slot (RequirementHandle's doc: 'Identity is (arena, raw)'). So a naive entity representation would be a REGRESSION against the thing it replaces. Options: (a) present the Value::Requirement as the dict child and leave it Opaque for now — the key then degrades via is_opaque_free, which is safe, but equality needs a Requirement arm by slot identity or an OpRef WITH a dict stops being equal to itself again; (b) give Requirement its own representation too — it is genuinely structural, (functor, [sub-dicts]) readable via handle.functor() / arity() / project(k), and the runtime already names it anthill.realization.runtime.Dictionary. (b) removes the Opaque entirely and is the recursive, complete answer. Do NOT encode dict as a slot-id scalar: that leaks an arena index into a structural view and two arenas can collide. (2) FactRef IS ALREADY DECLARED, as 'sort FactRef = ?' (reflect.anthill:35) — an ABSTRACT, CONSTRUCTOR-LESS sort, deliberately: 'application code can pass it to persistence mutation operations but cannot construct, print, or persist it as a durable identifier.' So this must be a VIEW-level representation, NOT an anthill-surface constructor; adding a surface constructor would contradict the declared contract. That is precisely the distinction I got wrong twice when picking wi815_head's subject: ViewHead is internal machinery, so keying on a value's identity exposes nothing to anthill, while the sort's 'opaque' is a statement about the SURFACE. Both can hold at once. FactRef's payload is a two-variant enum (Resident{rule, mirror} / External{owner, key}) — decide whether the view keys on the whole payload or on a session-scoped identity. The functor of any new form must be declared by the standard load, since WI-1014 made wrapped_expr_head PANIC on an unresolved reflect qname rather than degrade.", at: "2026-08-05T13:35:06Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1014", author: "user", content: "DEFECT IN WHAT THIS TICKET SHIPPED — Part B's view arm is wrong for POSITIONAL tuple literals, and my own note is where the wrong turn is recorded. Two term-producing paths disagree about TupleLiteral's shape. The PARSER (parse/convert.rs BuildFrame::TupleLiteral, ~line 1874) builds Fn{TupleLiteral, pos_args: EMPTY, named_args: [_1: x, _2: y]} — it converts every positional to a _N label via intern_positional_label and asserts all-or-nothing (mixing positional and named is an error). reflect.anthill documents that AS the representation: '(x, y) is represented as TupleLiteral(_1: x, _2: y)'. The OCCURRENCE REIFIER (occ_build_fn, WI-559 arm of try_occurrence_to_term) passes positionals through AS positionals. My Part B view arm mirrors the REIFIER, so an Expr::TupleLit occurrence with positional components views as Functor{TupleLiteral, pos_arity: N, named_arity: M} while the parser's term twin for the same source is Functor{TupleLiteral, 0, N+M}. That is the WI-425 cross-carrier disagreement this ticket exists to remove, reintroduced in the fix for it. HOW I MISSED IT, which is the reusable part: I NOTICED the discrepancy — the WI-559 comment says elements 'ride in named_args (positional -> _1/_2 labels)' while occ_build_fn does not — and recorded it at set_literal_functor as 'that describes the LOADER's build, not this one', then mirrored the code. The right conclusion from the same observation was that the two twins DISAGREE and one of them is wrong; instead I picked the one in front of me and wrote the discrepancy down as if it were a naming quibble. My test could not catch it because tuple_occ builds positional: Vec::new() — only NAMED components — so the positional case was never driven. SetLit is unaffected: its parser and reifier shapes agree (elements positional in both). SCOPE TO FIX: decide the canonical shape (the spec and the parser both say _N named, and WI-790 owns the _1/_2 convention — mint via intern::positional_label, never a local format!), then align occ_build_fn's TupleLit arm AND the view arm to it, and DRIVE the positional case — a positional tuple literal occurrence must key and compare identically to the parser's term for the same source. Also re-check whether occ_pos_child / occ_named_child should expose positionals at all under that shape.", at: "2026-08-05T13:35:28Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1014", author: "user", content: "FIXED. The canonical TupleLiteral shape is the PARSER's and the SPEC's — ALL-NAMED, zero pos_args, positionals carrying one-based _N labels minted through intern::positional_label (WI-790 owns that convention; never a local format!). Three sites moved to it: try_occurrence_to_term's WI-559 arm (which was the one out of step — its own comment had described the right shape while the code did another), occ_head's Part B arm, and the child readers. occ_pos_child no longer exposes TupleLit positionals at all, because under the canonical shape there are none — and children that agree with a WRONG head are still wrong, which is what made the first version look self-consistent. occ_named_child resolves an _N key through positional_label_index, positional_label's EXACT INVERSE, so a user label that merely starts with an underscore (_b, _0, _01) cannot be re-slotted positionally. occ_named_keys builds declared labels ++ _N in the same order the reifier does, so head arity, keys and children all read the two slices identically. SetLit untouched — parser and reifier already agreed. NEW TEST a_positional_tuple_literal_agrees_with_the_parsers_term drives the case the first version could not reach: it asserts the head is (0, 2), that the keys are _1/_2 with each component behind its own key, and — the real claim — that the occurrence and a hand-built PARSER-SHAPED term for the same source are views_structurally_equal AND share a goal_fingerprint. CONTROL MEASURED by reverting the head to the positional shape: (2, 0) vs (0, 2). Full workspace green: 4157 passed, 0 failed.", at: "2026-08-05T13:43:43Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1014", author: "user", content: "TUPLES FINISHED, and the closing check is the one that should have come first. All SIX TupleLiteral sites now agree on the spec's shape — all-named, zero pos_args, one-based _N: parse/convert.rs BuildFrame::TupleLiteral, convert.rs's projection desugar (x.(a, b)), load.rs's TypeExpr::Tuple instantiation build, node_occurrence.rs's term-to-occurrence reader (which already documented the rule correctly at its site), try_occurrence_to_term's reifier (FIXED), and occ_head plus the child readers (FIXED). The four that were already right had been right all along; only the reifier and the view I wrote from it were wrong. THE RULE WAS IN THE SPEC THE WHOLE TIME — docs/kernel-language.md: 'Positional syntax is sugar for auto-generated names _1, _2, _3, ...', 'one-based and canonical: a component at source index i is named exactly _<i+1>', and 'Tuple literals are represented as TupleLiteral(...) terms with named args'. I derived the shape from reading occ_build_fn, shipped it wrong, then re-derived it from parse/convert.rs — twice going to CODE for something the SPEC states plainly, when the standing rule is to check the spec before coding. The comment at the reifier now cites kernel-language.md rather than the stdlib file, so the next reader is pointed at the authority and not at a sibling implementation. Full workspace green: 4157 passed, 0 failed.", at: "2026-08-05T14:08:02Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1019", author: "user", content: "WHERE THE RULE GOES, decided (user): NOT a new proposal — both types already have a document that owns them. OperationRef extends docs/proposals/library/006-reflection-record-schemas.md (a reflection record schema is exactly what an operation-reference representation is). FactRef extends docs/proposals/library/005-stored-references.md, which already defines FactRef, its opacity contract, and why it is not reconstructible from a visible Term — so its view representation is a rule about that same type and must be written consistent with what 005 already says, not invented beside it. Correcting my own suggestion in passing: I proposed writing 'proposal 005' as a NEW document without looking, and 005 was already taken by the very type under discussion. READ 005 AND 006 IN FULL FIRST, then write the rule, then implement against it — the standing rule this whole ticket keeps re-learning (WI-1014 shipped a wrong tuple shape derived from sibling code while docs/kernel-language.md stated it three times). The design content to carry in: the dict trap (an OperationRef record dropping dict is LOOSER than the native_carrier_eq stopgap it replaces, in the direction that merges facts), and the view-level vs surface-level opacity distinction (ViewHead is internal machinery, so keying on identity exposes nothing to anthill, while 'sort FactRef = ?' is a statement about the SURFACE — both hold at once). SEPARATE FINDING, not this ticket: enum StoredRef is declared in anthill.reflect but is a PERSISTENCE pair, consumed only by stdlib/anthill/persistence/store.anthill, and the reflect header names only Term and FactRef as the abstract types it means to define. Namespace placement, worth its own item.", at: "2026-08-05T15:04:24Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1019", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED, and the ticket's own grouping was backwards — which the USER caught, not me. THE PLACEMENT DECISION WAS BUILT ON A MISSING FACT. Both prior scope notes assumed the operation reference had no declared home and needed a new reflect record under 006. It already had one: sort OpRef (accessors op, dict) and sort Dictionary (impl, arity, sub, resolveOp, ops) in stdlib/anthill/realization/runtime.anthill, both constructor-less, builtins registered since WI-577, owned by docs/design/requirement-dictionaries.md §2. That is the SAME mistake the previous note corrected in itself ('I proposed writing proposal 005 as a NEW document without looking, and 005 was already taken'), recurring one step later: I read 005 and 006 in full as instructed and still did not look for a THIRD document, because the two named ones felt like the whole question. The rule that would have caught it is not 'read the named docs' but 'find the doc that owns the TYPE'. 006 is about loader-emitted rows with declared field schemas; an OpRef is not a row.\n\nTHE GROUPING WAS INVERTED, BOTH WAYS. The ticket said OpRef and FactRef 'carry pure structural content' and put Requirement with the arena handles 'whose content mutates behind a slot'. Measured: a requirement slot is {functor, requirements, refcount} and is NEVER mutated after alloc — only refcount moves — and requirement-dictionaries.md §2.1 already said so ('immutable resolved values — no identity/mutation hazard (unlike Cell), so a structural reflect view is a pure view'). Meanwhile FactRef belongs with the handles. The USER supplied the discriminator after I had written a worse one: I framed it as 'reference into mutable storage vs resolved value', and the reply was that an OpRef is a reference too — it holds the operation's NAME. The real line is what the reference is SPELLED IN. An OpRef names its target with a Symbol, which has a Value carrier and denotes the same op to every reader; a FactRef locates its row by a private slot index (RuleId/RowKey) with NO Value carrier. One has a shape to present, the other only an identity to hold. Same word, opposite sides.\n\nI ALSO GOT THE STABILITY ARGUMENT BACKWARDS AND CAUGHT IT ONLY BY MEASURING. I was about to claim FactRef could not be content-keyed because RuleIds are reused after retract. They are not: retract sets a tombstone and entries are only appended (kb/mod.rs:428 states it outright). The arena is the one that reuses slots — RequirementArena has a free_list. So the WI-1014 stopgap compared dicts by the UNSTABLE half. Two facts, both opposite to my assumption, one command apart.\n\nA REAL DEFECT IN THE RETIRED STOPGAP, found by reading its own doc against its code: native_carrier_eq compared dictionaries with ha.raw() == hb.raw() while RequirementHandle's doc says 'Identity is (arena, raw)'. It dropped the arena half, so two dicts from different interpreter arenas sharing a raw compared EQUAL — a false positive, the fact-merging direction (WI-815), and reachable because a fresh interpreter per call is the normal pattern. Content comparison has no arena half to drop. Pinned by dictionaries_from_two_arenas_sharing_a_raw_are_not_equal.\n\nSHIPPED. Value::OpRef views as Functor{runtime.OpRef, 0, N} with named op (always) / dict / named (conditional on Some — the Expr::Proof precedent, so no Option wrapper is synthesized); Value::Requirement as Functor{runtime.Dictionary, arity, 1} with named impl and the sub-dicts positional, which is exactly its impl/arity/sub accessor set. Field keys resolve through DECLARED ACCESSOR qnames, not bare short names: reflect_field_key's lookup_symbol finds a short name only if something happened to intern it, and 'named' is spelled nowhere in the stdlib — it would have panicked on the first OpRef carrying one. New ViewItem::Owned(Value) for children the parent COMPUTES rather than stores; every reader pairs it with the borrowed arm so nothing downstream can tell them apart. The (Opaque, Opaque) arm is RE-FOUNDED, not retired as the ticket asked: with OpRef and Requirement now shaped, what remains is the law for carriers that genuinely have no shape — identity for equality, a deliberately coarse key that is_opaque_free already guards. native_carrier_eq -> opaque_carrier_eq, answering only for FactRef via FactRef::locates_same_row (a named method, not a PartialEq derive — Eq-ness is a claim 005 makes about the SURFACE).\n\nTWO GAPS CLOSED IN PASSING. runtime.anthill's header claimed 'the accessor ops expose everything the value holds' and had no accessor for named — declared it plus a builtin, driven through Interpreter::call on a real resolveOp-minted ref. And 005 said FactRef is 'not an Eq value'; the user had that clause removed, since it was the surface statement that made 'not equal to itself' look like policy.\n\nCONTROLS MEASURED, not predicted — and two of my predictions were wrong, so the comments now state the measurements. Five back-outs, each biting: OpRef head->Opaque fails 4 tests; Requirement head->Opaque fails 3; drop dict from the shape fails exactly two_oprefs_differing_only_in_their_dictionary_are_distinct; drop named fails exactly the_named_spec_op_half_is_part_of_the_identity; delete the (Opaque, Opaque) arm fails ONLY the FactRef unit test — which is the proof the two halves are independent. Dropping the OpRef.named registration fails the new accessor test while resolve_op_remembers_the_named_spec_op stays green, which is why the value-level test could never have caught that gap. wi815_head keeps FactRef as its opaque subject and its doc comment turns out to have been right all along. Full workspace: 4164 passed, 0 failed.", at: "2026-08-05T16:00:44Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1019", description: some(value: "GENERATE an entity representation in ViewHead for the native-carrier values, instead of ViewHead::Opaque plus a side-channel equality. A Value::OpRef is {op: Symbol, dict: Option<RequirementHandle>, named: Option<Symbol>} — a PURE VALUE whose op and named halves are plain symbols. It has structure; the view simply does not present it. The same holds for Value::FactRef. Presenting that structure AS AN ENTITY (a reflect-form head with named children) makes equality, goal_fingerprint keying, discrim indexing and unification all fall out of the machinery that already exists for every other structural form — nothing special-cased. WHY THE CURRENT STOPGAP IS THE WRONG SHAPE, and it is mine: WI-1014 follow-up added Value::native_carrier_eq plus a (ViewHead::Opaque, ViewHead::Opaque) arm in views_structurally_equal, because an OpRef MEASURED as not equal to ITSELF. That fixes equality and NOTHING ELSE — two distinct OpRefs still share one goal_fingerprint, because the head stays payload-free — so eq and the key now DISAGREE, which is a worse state to reason about than both being wrong together. It is also a SECOND equality path that has to be kept in sync with the structural one by hand, the kind of duplication WI-486 collapsed when it made views_structurally_equal the single structural compare. THE SHAPE TO REUSE is already in term_view.rs: wrapped_expr_head / wrapped_expr_keys / wrapped_expr_child present an Expr variant as Fn{<reserved reflect qname>, named: [...]} off ONE shape table, so head arity and named_keys cannot drift apart (the WI-814 discipline). An OpRef would read as Fn{anthill.prelude.Function.op_ref, named: [op: Ref(op), named: Option<Ref>]} or similar. DECIDE: (1) the functor must be a RESERVED kernel-vocab name so a generated head cannot collide with a user entity of the same name — and note WI-1014 just made wrapped_expr_head LOUD on an unresolved reflect qname, so a new form needs its symbol declared by the standard load or it will panic rather than degrade; (2) does the reflect surface need a matching TERM twin, or is the view representation enough? WI-425 says a view must agree with its twin; if no twin exists there is nothing to disagree with, but then a value_to_term of an OpRef has no target either; (3) SCOPE — which carriers get one. OpRef and FactRef carry pure structural content. Closure / Map / Cell / Stream / Substitution / Requirement are ARENA HANDLES whose content mutates behind a slot, so a content entity would be unstable; for those the representation would have to be the slot IDENTITY, or they stay Opaque with a documented by-nature reason (which the Value::Relation doc already states: a native carrier is not structural data). Do NOT assume the whole group answers the same way — MEASURE each with the two probes that found this: is it equal to itself, and do two distinct ones share a key? Both answered wrong for OpRef AND FactRef. ON DELIVERY retire native_carrier_eq and the (Opaque, Opaque) arm so there is ONE equality again, and MEASURE the blast radius the way WI-1014 did: is_cacheable's Opaque exclusion starts admitting these goals, discrim starts indexing heads that previously could not be stored at all (insert_walk PANICS on an Opaque head), and value_fact_dedup_key stops degrading for them — which is the point, but it means facts that were never deduped start being deduped. SUPERSEDES the specific WI-1018 (Value::OpRef must not be Opaque), which this is the mechanism for."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-05T16:00:50Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1018", author: "user", content: "RESOLVED BY WI-1019 (delivered). Value::OpRef is no longer Opaque: it views as Functor{anthill.realization.runtime.OpRef, 0, N} with named op/dict/named, so equality, goal_fingerprint keying, discrim indexing and unification all come from the structural machinery.\n\nTHE THREE OPTIONS THIS TICKET POSED WERE ALL WRONG, AND SO WAS THE SUPERSEDING NOTE'S FOURTH. (a) a new ViewHead variant, (b) a wrapper twin Fn{Function.op_ref, ...}, (c) a payload-bearing opaque — and the follow-up proposed generating an entity via the wrapped_expr_head shape table. The actual answer needed no new form at all: anthill.realization.runtime already declares sort OpRef and sort Dictionary, constructor-less, with accessor builtins registered since WI-577, owned by docs/design/requirement-dictionaries.md §2. The view heads under the sort that was already there. Both this ticket and its follow-up asked 'what head should we invent' when the question was 'what is this type already called'.\n\nTHIS TICKET'S 'CHECK THE SIBLINGS' INSTRUCTION IS WHAT FOUND THE REST, and it pointed the opposite way from its own prediction. It grouped Requirement with the arena handles and asked for it to be re-examined; re-examined, a requirement slot is {functor, requirements, refcount}, never mutated after alloc, and requirement-dictionaries.md §2.1 already called both OpRef and Dictionary 'immutable resolved values — no identity/mutation hazard (unlike Cell)'. Requirement is now structural too, which is what removes the dict child's Opaque and with it the last reason to keep a second equality path.\n\nAND THE CONSEQUENCE NOTE'S CORRECTION WAS ITSELF HALF WRONG. It said 'MEASURED, both halves are wrong. FactRef behaves identically to OpRef.' The SYMPTOMS were identical — Opaque head, not equal to itself, two rows sharing a key — but identical symptoms are not an identical cause, and that inference is what the correction got wrong. FactRef stays Opaque, by nature. The discriminator (the user's) is what the reference is spelled IN: an OpRef names its target with a Symbol, which has a Value carrier and denotes the same op to every reader; a FactRef locates its row by a private slot index (RuleId / RowKey) with no Value carrier at all. A shape to present vs an identity to hold. So wi815_head's subject IS unaffected after all, exactly as this ticket originally said and as its correction denied — and its doc comment was right the whole time.\n\nThe ViewHead-vs-surface half of the correction DOES hold and is now written into 005: view-level identity exposes nothing to anthill, while 'sort FactRef = ?' is a statement about the surface. Both hold at once; they just do not add up to a structural head here.", at: "2026-08-05T16:01:17Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1018", description: some(value: "Value::OpRef must not be ViewHead::Opaque. MEASURED (probe, prelude KB, OpRef{op, dict: none, named: none}): head = Opaque; views_structurally_equal(a, a) = FALSE — an OpRef is not equal to ITSELF; two OpRefs naming DIFFERENT ops share one goal_fingerprint and the key is not opaque_free. That is the exact symptom pair that made Expr::SetLit the wrong thing to leave Opaque (WI-1014), and WI-815 made it a soundness surface: an over-dedup DROPS an answer in seen_goals and DROPS A FACT in value_fact_dedup. WHY THE EXISTING JUSTIFICATION DOES NOT APPLY: OpRef sits in the same Opaque group as Stream / Map / Cell / Substitution / Requirement / Closure, whose stated reason (Value::Relation's doc) is 'a native carrier is not structural data' — an arena handle whose identity is a slot and whose content mutates. An OpRef is NOT a handle: it is {op: Symbol, dict: Option<RequirementHandle>, named: Option<Symbol>}, a pure value whose op and named halves are structural. Only dict is a runtime environment. THE DESIGN QUESTION, which is why this is a ticket and not an arm: what head? It must NOT be ViewHead::Ref(op) or a nullary Functor{op} — WI-436's functor_view_head note relies on 'an op-as-value is a Value::OpRef, never a Term::Ref, so no op/eta case depends on the Ref-vs-Fn shape', and conflating them would make an eta'd op unify with a bare constructor reference of the same name. Options: (a) a new ViewHead::OpRef(Symbol) variant — no conflation, gives discrim a concrete key, but every ViewHead matcher must decide about it (DiscrimKey, fingerprint_into, views_structurally_equal, unify); (b) a reflect-style wrapper twin Fn{Function.op_ref, named:[op, named]} — reuses the existing machinery but needs a declared reflect form and a term twin that nothing builds today; (c) keep Opaque but give ViewHead a payload-BEARING opaque so distinct handles at least key distinctly. DECIDE (b)/(a) against whether a term twin exists at all: if nothing lowers an OpRef to a term, WI-425's 'agree with your twin' rule dictates no shape, and the argument rests only on self-equality and key distinctness. CHECK THE SIBLINGS in the same group while here — Closure / Map / Cell / Stream ARE arena handles and their Opaque is sound, but Substitution and Requirement should be re-examined on the same test (is it equal to itself? do two distinct ones share a key?). CONSEQUENCE: wi815_head's opaque subject was briefly pointed at OpRef under WI-1014 on the false premise that it is opaque by nature; it is now Value::FactRef, whose OWN DOC says it 'never exposes a resident RuleId', so a structural view would contradict the type. That subject is unaffected by this ticket."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-05T16:01:33Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1021", description: some(value: "scaland TypeGen rewrites a sort's OWN name through the prelude type map, so a sort's members reach Scala typed by a DIFFERENT type -- measured, `PairOps` declares `def fst(p: Tuple2): A` and does not compile.\\n\\nMEASURED (2026-08-05, delivering WI-940). Bootstrap.generate on stdlib/anthill/prelude/pair.anthill emits into Pair.scala:\\n\\n  trait PairOps[A, B]:\\n    def fst(p: Tuple2): A\\n    def snd(p: Tuple2): B\\n    def eq(a: Tuple2, b: Tuple2): Boolean\\n\\nThe anthill declarations are `operation fst(p: Pair) -> A`, `snd(p: Pair) -> B`, `eq(a: Pair, b: Pair) -> Bool` -- every receiver is written `Pair`, the sort the file declares. `scala-cli compile --scala 3.6.3` on that file reports FOUR \"Missing type parameter for [T1, T2] =>> (T1, T2)\", one per `Tuple2` occurrence: Scala 3's `Tuple2` is a type constructor and a bare mention of it is an error.\\n\\nMECHANISM, and it is one line. `TypeGen.mapPrelude` (scaland/core/src/main/scala/anthill/codegen/scala/TypeGen.scala:45-62) maps prelude type NAMES to Scala stdlib counterparts by SHORT NAME, unconditionally: `case \"Pair\" => \"Tuple2\"`. That table exists for CONSUMERS -- a project sort with a `Pair[A, B]` field should reach Scala as `Tuple2[A, B]` -- but it fires just as readily inside pair.anthill itself, where `Pair` denotes the very type being emitted three lines up. The generator therefore declares `case Pair(fst: A, snd: B)` and then types its own members by something else.\\n\\nNote the entry is doubly wrong here even for a consumer: it drops the type ARGUMENTS. `TypeExpr.Simple(Pair)` -- an unparameterized written occurrence, which is what an operation's `p: Pair` is inside its own sort body -- renders through the `Simple` arm (TypeGen.scala:12-13), which has no bindings to pass on. A consumer writing `Pair[A = X, B = Y]` goes through the `Parameterized` arm and does get `Tuple2[X, Y]`; the receiver-position occurrence has nowhere to get them from, because in anthill it does not need them -- the sort's own parameters are in scope.\\n\\nTWO CANDIDATE FIXES, and the choice is the ticket:\\n (a) SUPPRESS the mapping for a name the file itself declares -- TypeGen would have to be told the enclosing declaration's name, which it is not today (`render(sym, te)` takes no context). Smallest change, and it leaves the `Pair -> Tuple2` mapping intact for real consumers.\\n (b) DROP `case \"Pair\" => \"Tuple2\"` from mapPrelude. Anthill's `Pair` is a nominal sort with named fields `fst`/`snd` and its own `eq`; Scala's `Tuple2` is structural. They are not the same type, and a `provides Eq[Pair]` cannot attach to `Tuple2`. Check the other suspect entries at the same time -- `List -> List`, `Set -> Set`, `Map -> Map`, `Stream -> LazyList` have the same nominal-vs-host question, and `Option -> Option` collides with anthill's own `none`/`some` constructors.\\nWhichever is chosen, say WHY the other was refused.\\n\\nACCEPTANCE: driven by WI-1020's compile harness -- the emitted prelude closure compiles, and a test names `Pair.scala` as the file that did not. A CONTROL that a genuine consumer's use of the prelude type still maps as intended (or, if (b) is chosen, that the fact it no longer maps is asserted and not incidental). Say at the test site which assertion fails when the fix is backed out. sbt test green.\\n\\nSOURCE: measured while delivering WI-940."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-1020"]), status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1022", description: some(value: "scaland Bootstrap maps a sort's `requires` onto the DATA declaration as `extends`, so two requirements of one spec at different type arguments emit Scala that is refused outright -- measured, \"trait PartialEq is extended twice\".\\n\\nMEASURED (2026-08-05, delivering WI-940). stdlib/anthill/prelude/pair.anthill declares\\n\\n  requires PartialEq[A]\\n  requires PartialEq[B]\\n\\nand Bootstrap emits (renderMainSort, scaland/core/src/main/scala/anthill/codegen/scala/Bootstrap.scala):\\n\\n  enum Pair[A, B] extends PartialEq[A], PartialEq[B]:\\n    case Pair(fst: A, snd: B)\\n\\n`scala-cli compile --scala 3.6.3`, with `PartialEq` as eq.anthill's own emission defines it, reports:\\n\\n  trait PartialEq is extended twice\\n  class Pair cannot be instantiated since it has conflicting base types\\n    anthill.prelude.PartialEq[A] and anthill.prelude.PartialEq[B]\\n\\nThis is not \"an abstract member was left unimplemented\" -- that would at least be a coherent shape. Scala forbids one trait in a class's linearization twice at ALL, so no implementation could rescue it.\\n\\nWHY IT IS THE MAPPING AND NOT THE STDLIB. docs/scala-forward-mapping.md §2.7 already says a `requires` \"can be either a type-class supertrait or a `using` context parameter, DEPENDING ON THE CALL SHAPE\", and the summary table's row reads \"upper bound or `using Eq[T]`\". Bootstrap implements only the first half: `renderMainSort` builds one `extends` clause from every `Item.RequiresDeclItem` and hangs it on whichever declaration the sort maps to. The `using` half is unwritten, and `PartialEq[A]` / `PartialEq[B]` on `Pair` is exactly the shape it exists for -- a requirement PER TYPE PARAMETER is evidence, not a supertype. `Pair` is not a `PartialEq`; it HAS componentwise equality GIVEN one for each component. pair.anthill's own header says so (\"The `requires` clauses do double duty: they CONDITION the provisions and they are the EVIDENCE the `eq` body dispatches through\").\\n\\nSCOPE. Decide, per §2.7's \"depending on the call shape\", when a `requires` is a supertrait and when it is `using` evidence, and implement the second. The obvious discriminator is whether the requirement's argument is one of the sort's OWN type parameters (`requires PartialEq[A]` on `sort Pair[A, B]` -- evidence) versus the sort itself or a ground type (`requires PartialEq[T]` on `sort Eq[T]`, which §2.7's own example maps to `trait Eq[T] extends PartialEq[T]` and which the existing BootstrapTest pins) -- but verify that against eq.anthill, ordered.anthill and set.anthill before committing to it, and note that the DATA shapes (`case class` / `enum`) may want a different answer from the ALGEBRA shape (`trait`), since only the last can carry a supertrait cheaply.\\n\\nRELATED, DO NOT CONFLATE: WI-1021 is the other mechanism making this same file uncompilable (a sort's own name rewritten through the prelude type map). Independent causes, independent fixes; both surface on Pair.scala.\\n\\nNOT INTRODUCED BY WI-940. `requires -> extends` predates it; WI-940 moved the clause into a shared `ext` string and left the placement exactly where the enum branch had it, and said so at the site.\\n\\nACCEPTANCE: driven by WI-1020's compile harness -- the emitted prelude closure compiles. A CONTROL that §2.7's supertrait case is UNCHANGED: `trait Eq[T] extends PartialEq[T]` is what the existing BootstrapTest \"WI-170/WI-644\" pins, and it must still hold, so the discriminator is asserted in both directions rather than the `extends` path simply being deleted. Say at the test site which assertion fails when the fix is backed out. sbt test green.\\n\\nSOURCE: measured while delivering WI-940."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-1020"]), status: Open)
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-940", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED. Bootstrap classifies a sort body into three DISJOINT shapes -- Record (eponymous single constructor) / Sum / Algebra -- and renders each once. `sort Vec3 { entity Vec3(x,y,z); operation vec_add(...) = ... }` now emits `case class Vec3(x: Double, y: Double, z: Double)` where it emitted `enum Vec3: case Vec3(...)`, the nested Vec3.Vec3 that §6.3/WI-926 rule out. Same for TotalFloat, stdlib's other eponymous sort. scaland: 366 passed / 0 failed after `core/clean` (baseline 360 + 6 new). Full rust workspace re-run on the consolidated test layout: 4128 passed, 0 failed.\n\nTHE TICKET'S RECORDED BASELINE WAS STALE: it says 8 failed / 232 passed (ParseTest:931 plus seven downstream of a cpp_std parse error). Measured at claim time: 360 passed, 0 failed. Those were fixed between WI-935 and now.\n\nEPONYMY IS KEYED ON THE ANTHILL NAME, and that is load-bearing rather than pedantic. `Names.scalaTypeName` is MANY-TO-ONE, and stdlib already contains the near-miss: `enum anthill.prelude.Pair { entity pair(fst: A, snd: B) }` -- the constructor is `pair`, the sort `Pair`, and both PascalCase to `Pair`. Comparing the EMITTED names would have collapsed Pair into a case class and broken a delivered test; comparing the written names keeps it a Sum, which is what §6.3 says (\"keyed on the name matching\"). The classifier checks \"any eponymous constructor\" BEFORE \"exactly one constructor\" for the same reason -- reversing those two lines is what makes Pair the wrong shape.\n\nDEFECT (2) OF THE TICKET IS ANSWERED BY A SCALA LIMIT, NOT IMPLEMENTED. The ticket asks for the operations \"on it\", citing cpp-gen putting an eponymous sort's fields AND operations in one `struct` (WI-931). That collapse has NO Scala analogue: a C++ member function DECLARATION needs no definition, while Scala has no abstract member in an instantiable `case class`, and bootstrap emits signatures only (bodies are the KB-driven gen's, proposal 034). So the members stay on the `<Sort>Ops` contract §2.3 already uses for EVERY sort with constructors -- a uniform mapping rule, not a carve-out for eponymous sorts. What §6.3 buys in Scala is that the TYPE is one declaration. docs/kernel-language.md §6.3 said \"the fields and operations belong to one type in every backend\", which over-claimed; it now states that what every backend owes is one declaration OF THE SORT'S OWN NAME, and that a differently-named companion is not the forbidden shape.\n\nBEYOND THE TICKET -- THE INVARIANT IS NOW TOTAL. §6.3 also admits an eponymous variant ALONGSIDE siblings (WI-946), and Scala has no spelling for that at all: the sum and one of its cases would have to be one name in one scope. That is REFUSED (`BootstrapError`, located) rather than emitted as `enum S: case S`, which would be the nested S.S in a second guise. So after this change no Bootstrap path can emit a nested X.X -- a stronger claim than \"the single-constructor case is fixed\". No stdlib file has that shape (measured), which is exactly why it needed a local fixture.\n\nCONTROL DISCIPLINE, MEASURED NOT ASSERTED, both probes run and reverted:\n  * `shapeOf` returning Sum for the eponymous single-ctor case (the old behaviour) fails EXACTLY FOUR tests: the Vec3 one, TotalFloat, the sugar/long-form equivalence, and the parametric `case class Box[T]`.\n  * replacing the sibling refusal with `Sum(ctors)` fails EXACTLY ONE: the REFUSED test. The record probe leaves it green, so the two claims are pinned separately.\n  * `WI-940 CONTROL: a non-eponymous multi-constructor sort still emits an enum` and `WI-170: standalone entity -> case class` pass BOTH ways BY DESIGN, and say so at their sites.\n\nTHE STRONGEST NEW TEST is the §6.3 EQUIVALENCE: `entity Acct(...)` and `sort Acct { entity Acct(...) }` are asserted BYTE-EQUAL as whole files, not by two independent expectations. That is what the spec actually claims (\"a codegen backend naming Acct gets the same answer either way\"), and it holds by CONSTRUCTION now -- `emitStandaloneEntity` and the Record branch call one `renderCaseClass`, where they previously had two independently-written templates that had drifted.\n\nREVIEW: /code-review is user-triggered and cannot be model-invoked, so four finder agents were run instead (reuse, simplification, altitude, test-quality) and all three actionable findings applied: BootstrapError now carries `span: Span` and renders through Span.render, the ONE located renderer ParseError/LoadError already share (it was pre-flattening the location into a bare string); the equivalence test asserts both sides emit one file rather than relying on `.head` happening to throw; `count` -> `exists`. The test-quality agent independently re-derived both control counts by branch-tracing and got the same numbers. THE USER MUST STILL RUN /code-review -- it is the repo's stated pre-commit gate and I cannot invoke it.\n\nFOUND WHILE HERE, FILED NOT FIXED: dumping the real emitted stdlib output for the first time showed Pair.scala does not compile, from two independent mechanisms, both PRE-EXISTING and neither touched by this change. Verified with `scala-cli compile --scala 3.6.3`, not asserted. WI-1021 (a sort's own name rewritten through the prelude type map -> `def fst(p: Tuple2): A`, four \"Missing type parameter\") and WI-1022 (`requires` -> `extends` on the data declaration -> \"trait PartialEq is extended twice\", \"conflicting base types\"). Both hang off WI-1020: §2.3 promises \"The generated file compiles as-is\" and NOTHING verifies it -- every Bootstrap assertion is a substring match, which is precisely how both survived.\n\nNOTE ON NUMBERING: those three were first filed as WI-1011/1012/1013 and renumbered to 1015/1016/1017 on rebase -- upstream had taken 1011-1014 in the interim.", at: "2026-08-05T12:32:56Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-940", description: some(value: "scaland Bootstrap emits an eponymous single-constructor sort as 'enum X: case X(...)' + a separate 'XOps' trait -- the nested X.X that WI-926 forbids.\n\nMEASURED (2026-08-02, delivering WI-935). scaland Bootstrap.generate on the new stdlib/anthill/geometry.anthill emits, for 'sort Vec3 { entity Vec3(x,y,z); operation vec_add(...) = ... ... }':\n\n  package anthill.geometry\n\n  enum Vec3:\n    case Vec3(x: Double, y: Double, z: Double)\n\n  trait Vec3Ops:\n    def vecAdd(a: Vec3, b: Vec3): Vec3\n    def vecSub(a: Vec3, b: Vec3): Vec3\n    def vecScale(c: Double, v: Vec3): Vec3\n    def vecZero(): Vec3\n\nTwo defects in one output. (1) 'enum Vec3: case Vec3(...)' is exactly the nested 'Vec3.Vec3' that docs/kernel-language.md 6.3 / WI-926 rule out -- an eponymous constructor IS its sort, ONE symbol, and the correct emission is a single 'case class Vec3(x: Double, y: Double, z: Double)'. (2) the members are split into a SEPARATE 'Vec3Ops' trait, so the type a consumer holds and the operations on it are two declarations rather than one.\n\nTHIS IS THE SAME DEFECT WI-931 FIXED ON THE RUST SIDE, in the other backend. cpp-gen emitted 'struct Vec3' TWICE for this shape (a C++ redefinition error) because classify_namespace built a data band from the entity-field registry and a traits band from OperationInfo parents, and one symbol satisfied both. The fix was at classify_namespace (the three bands are DISJOINT by contract) plus emit_entity_struct_by_symbol (one symbol, one struct). cpp-gen now emits fields THEN operations in one struct -- pinned by rustland/anthill-cpp-gen/tests/wi931_eponymous_struct_test.rs and, since WI-935, by lf1_test::lf1_vec3_and_euler_emit_correctly against the real stdlib Vec3. scaland's Bootstrap has the same band split and has NOT had that fix.\n\nPRE-EXISTING, SURFACED NOW. It is not caused by WI-935. It went unexercised because the tree shipped no stdlib sort of this shape -- WI-931's own fixture comment says so ('the tree currently ships no sort of this shape, and a rule with no example is exactly the one that rots'). WI-935 made Vec3 the first one, which is what exposed it.\n\nWHAT WI-935 DID INSTEAD, so this is not silently encoded as correct: BootstrapTest 'WI-170: standalone entity -> case class' was RE-POINTED from Vec3 to EulerAngles (still a standalone entity, so the test still measures the claim in its name). Vec3's current output is deliberately NOT asserted anywhere -- pinning it would make the bug the contract. scaland core is at its pre-WI-935 baseline (8 failed / 232 passed, all 8 pre-existing: ParseTest:931 plus seven downstream of the cpp_std parse error).\n\nACCEPTANCE: Bootstrap emits ONE declaration for an eponymous single-constructor sort -- a 'case class' carrying the fields, with the operations on it (companion or trait-on-the-same-name, whichever matches scala-forward-mapping) -- driven by a test that generates from the real stdlib geometry and asserts the absence of 'enum Vec3' AND the presence of the case class. A CONTROL that a NON-eponymous multi-constructor sort still emits an enum, and that a plain standalone entity is unchanged. sbt test at or below the current baseline.\n\nSOURCE: measured while delivering WI-935."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-05T12:32:57Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1016", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. The flip plus the four representation decisions, each with a control MEASURED by backing it out (and RE-MEASURED after the /code-review refactors, because restructuring can silently break a control). (1) anthill-stl expect_symbol reads through KnowledgeBase::value_symbol. (2) MapKey gained Ref(Symbol); try_from_value takes &KnowledgeBase and canonicalizes the Term::Ref twin into it, so one symbol is one map slot -- narrower than value_symbol ON PURPOSE, since for a store key a widening MERGES keys and one put would overwrite another. (3) fn_value test became \"is a leaf\" rather than \"is a Value::Term\". (4) is_duplicate_projection admits SymbolRef. CONTROLS: revert symbol_value -> 3 tests fail on the carrier premise; revert expect_symbol -> all five host symbol ops TypeMismatch while their SLD twins keep answering; drop MapKey::Ref -> Map.put hard-errors; drop its Term::Ref arm -> cross-carrier Map.get answers none; revert fn_value -> RuleId(0) vs RuleId(1) for ONE logical fact; revert is_duplicate_projection -> 2 solutions where 1 is right. 4170 passed. THE FLIP IS DELIBERATELY PARTIAL: lookup_symbol / scope / builtin_lookup_symbol still mint Term::Ref, so BOTH carriers stay in circulation. Flipping them all would have made the cross-carrier seams untestable by removing the second carrier -- the MapKey hole is only observable because both spellings exist. TWO SITES THE TICKET OWN LIST MISSED, both found by review, both fixed here: anthill-stl ref_repr_symbol (the same by-carrier match as expect_symbol, 60 lines up in the same file, reading a TermRepr name field -- DECLARED Symbol, so RefRepr(name: Dictionary.impl(d)) is well-typed and was rejected); and op_info head_name_ref, whose None means \"skip this fact\", so an OperationInfo would have gone silently invisible. THREE OF MY OWN CLAIMS WERE WRONG AND ARE CORRECTED AT THEIR SITES. (a) The dedup test first shape measured nothing: I put the multiplicity in the rule BODY, so the nearest ancestor ChoicePoint was the body disjunction, whose two answers genuinely differ -- the two routes must be alternatives for ONE goal. (b) Its premise assert read resolve_as_value, which stops at Term::Var; the rule route binds ?q -> Var(?v) with ?v -> SymbolRef, so it needs a full reify walk. (c) MapKey doc said \"a widening MERGES KEYS\" of all three carriers value_symbol adds -- true only of Term::Ident; declining a non-canonicalized nullary Fn{c,[],[]} accepts a key SPLIT, and Value::Node is a REFUSAL. FILED WI-1023 (renumbered from a local WI-1020 that collided with the scaland Bootstrap ticket pushed to main meanwhile) rather than fixed inline, because all three parts move in the answer-DROPPING direction and need their own controls: is_duplicate_projection allow-list was ALREADY stale before this ticket (Requirement and OpRef became structural in WI-1019, the commit immediately before; Value::Var fingerprints as a Var token -- all three still switch dedup off); fn_value leaf set is narrower than what alloc_from_value lowers, so a SCALAR child has the same disjoint-key-space split (measured unreachable: all four assert_fact_carrier callers pass Value::term); and MapKey nullary-Fn split. Each site now carries the TICKET NUMBER, not a justification. Also from review: leaf_term_of restated alloc_from_value two arms verbatim -- deleted, since that would be a second untied statement of the variant whole contract; symbol_value dropped its unused &mut KnowledgeBase (a function demanding a store write advertises the write this change removes); the four Map builtins share one map_key helper; the two symbol-ops tests now drive ONE assert_symbol_ops block, so \"both carriers answer alike\" is the assertion rather than two hand-copied lists; and anthill-stl load_stdlib_and_source got the LazyLock STDLIB_PARSED that anthill-core/tests/common already had (23 callers were each re-parsing 76 files). Docs: proposal 035 MapKey domain and the value-facts design doc split-key-space section now state the canonicalization rule.", at: "2026-08-05T21:41:25Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1016", description: some(value: "Mint Value::SymbolRef in production: complete the workspace carrier audit, then flip `symbol_value`. WI-1015 added the Value::SymbolRef carrier and made every path it already audited correct, but deliberately left `eval/builtins.rs::symbol_value` minting the interned Value::term(Term::Ref) carrier. Flipping that one line makes the variant live across the whole reflect surface at once (Dictionary.impl, OpRef.op), and /code-review found the audit it requires is WORKSPACE-wide while WI-1015's grep covered only anthill-core/src. Sites with a FORCED answer (mirror the Term::Ref twin) are already fixed under WI-1015: scalar_eq (two identical symbols compared UNEQUAL), scalar_value_expr (subst_var_leaf panicked; simp_rewrite silently rewrote a redex to bottom), detect_cycle (Modify.set's self-reference guard reported no cycle), constructor_sub_values (MatchDispatch's pre-filter promised an arm the destructure declined), anthill-cli render_value (a symbol answer binding printed the Rust Debug form), op_info list_items_strict. REMAINING, each needing a REPRESENTATION DECISION rather than an arm: (1) anthill-stl expect_symbol reads by carrier and backs qualified_name / short_name / scope / kind / resolve_sort_instantiation_param — the eval twins of ops whose SLD twins WI-1015 widened, so one op would answer two ways; KnowledgeBase::value_symbol is pub for exactly this. (2) MapKey has no Ref variant and try_from_value has no &mut kb to intern one, so a Symbol-keyed Map hard-errors — a representation gap, not a missing arm. (3) fn_value's all-Term test would push a SymbolRef-bearing head onto the Value::Entity path and into the DISJOINT value_fact_dedup key space (WI-815), storing two facts for one logical fact. (4) is_duplicate_projection's negative carrier test drops a SymbolRef binding into the opaque bucket and disables answer dedup (fail-open, extra answers). Deliver with a test that FAILS when symbol_value is reverted — WI-1015's did not, because sym_qn reads by content and accepts both carriers."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-05T21:41:26Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1013", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. Surfaced Expr::Apply.type_args through TermView as ONE named child holding the List[type_arg] the loader's own type_args_term_handle encodes, declared only when the bracket channel is non-empty; try_occurrence_to_term builds the identical slot, so the carriers stay isomorphic (WI-425). All three consumers repaired at once: goal_fingerprint/GoalKey (seen_goals + query_cache), GoalKey::is_opaque_free (value_fact_dedup_key), views_structurally_equal.\n\nTWO OF THIS TICKET'S OWN PREMISES WERE FALSE, both about REACHABILITY. (1) 'UNREACHABLE TODAY' (inherited from WI-472's 'no producer mints a type_args-bearing Apply'): the TYPER mints one on every field projection -- WI-759's synthesize_field_access rewrites ?x.y into field_access[Name = denoted(\"y\")](?x, \"y\"), 23 occurrences in the stdlib alone, before any user writes a bracket. (2) 'WHAT MAKES IT REACHABLE is WI-839': WI-839 delivered a REFUSAL, not a population -- a written bracket is now a load error everywhere except an operation-body call and a rule head's bare [T] introducer, so it NARROWED the surface. What actually kept a value-fact head safe was neither claim: such a head is built from a fact/rule position, and a bracket there does not load. The gap was unreachable by accident, not for the reason recorded twice.\n\nTHE KEY IS THE RESOLVED anthill.reflect.type_arg SYMBOL, NOT THE INTERNED NAME type_args, and that choice is load-bearing. type_args is converter-synthesized into the SAME namespace as a call's argument LABELS, and WI-839 deliberately pinned f[T = Int64](type_args: n) as legal, discriminating the channel 'without a reserved-name rule' (wi839 .. a_parameter_named_type_args_does_not_shadow_the_channel). A spellable key would put two same-keyed children on one head -- every reader takes the FIRST match (WI-805/808/809), so views_structurally_equal would report two DIFFERENT calls equal. The first draft instead REFUSED the collision at convert.rs; the full suite caught it as that WI-839 test going red, i.e. this ticket would have reversed a delivered decision as a side effect. A define()d qualified symbol never enters intern_map, so no argument label can be it -- premise pinned by the_slot_key_is_not_spellable_as_a_label rather than assumed.\n\nMEASURED BLAST RADIUS (stdlib corpus, instrument run on the parent commit and on the change): stored clause heads 169 -> 169 candidates, 0 -> 0 self-misses, 169 -> 169 distinct GoalKeys, 1214 -> 1214 key tokens; rule-body atoms 8 -> 8, candidates 0 -> 0, 46 -> 46 tokens; operation-body applies 205 -> 205, 23 -> 23 bracket-bearing, 198 -> 198 distinct keys. The ONLY delta is 1695 -> 2353 key tokens across the 23 field_access calls, which carry their bracket now. Nothing in the corpus SEPARATES as a result, because field_access supplies the projected name twice (WI-759) and the positional copy already distinguished them -- so the fix is precision restored, not a corpus behaviour change.\n\nWI-678's control moved rather than broke. synth_head_ignores_type_position_only_var asserted ?b (a var living only in [T = ?b]) was absent from BOTH collect_goal_view_vars and append_synth_key; both read through TermView, so both now SEE it and the head is arity-2. The invariant WI-678 actually protects -- head arity and key positions in LOCKSTEP -- holds at the wider arity, and the two view-side collectors have converged with the type-arg-aware collect_value_head_vars instead of diverging from it.\n\nTESTS: tests/include/wi1013_call_type_args_view_test.rs, 10 tests. Back-out MEASURED against the parent commit, not predicted: 6 fail (bracket_distinct_calls_key_apart, bracket_distinct_calls_are_unequal, bracket_distinct_fact_heads_do_not_over_dedup, a_bracket_survives_the_term_round_trip, a_written_bracket_reaches_the_view, a_type_args_argument_beside_a_bracket_still_loads), 4 pass either way by design (the two bracket-free carrier controls, the one-child control, and the slot-key premise pin). Full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh: 4180 passed, 0 failed.\n\nDOCS DE-ROTTED at every site that recorded the hole as open: value_fact_dedup_key (kb/mod.rs), GoalKey::is_opaque_free (which KEEPS its name -- injectivity is a joint property of the check and every view arm, and a promising name is exactly what would have hidden this), incref_value_ground's 'known gap' list, the WI-815 unit-test comment, and docs/design/value-facts-carrier-agnostic-resolver.md.\n\nADJACENT ITEM, ALREADY OWNED, NOT FOLDED IN: the ticket flags is_duplicate_projection's carrier allowlist (resolve.rs) as over-rejecting. WI-1016 already rewrote that doc and WI-1023 owns it BY NUMBER at the site, with the reason widening needs its own measured change (it moves in the answer-DROPPING direction and needs a control per carrier). Nothing to add here.", at: "2026-08-05T22:38:02Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1013", description: some(value: "TermView DROPS an Expr::Apply's type_args, so a GoalKey is not injective and THREE consumers can silently collapse two distinct goals. occ_head's Expr::Apply arm (term_view.rs ~863) destructures { functor, pos_args, named_args, .. } -- the `..` is type_args -- and they never reach pos_arg / named_arg / named_keys. So f[T = Int64](x) and f[T = String](x) present IDENTICAL views. THREE CONSUMERS INHERIT IT, each with a different failure mode: (1) goal_fingerprint/GoalKey -- the resolver's seen_goals answer-dedup (WI-348) DROPS the second of two answers differing only in a bracket, and query_cache, keyed on the same GoalKey, can serve one goal's candidate list for another; (2) GoalKey::is_opaque_free, the fact-dedup injectivity guard WI-815 introduced, answers TRUE on such a key, so value_fact_dedup_key would OVER-dedup -- and over-dedup DROPS A FACT, not merely precision; (3) views_structurally_equal reports two bracket-distinct occurrences EQUAL, which is what WI-762's receiver-divergence guard reads. UNREACHABLE TODAY and known to be: WI-472 recorded it as \"no producer mints a type_args-bearing Apply inside a value-fact head\", and WI-815 re-states it at value_fact_dedup_key as the one hole its guard cannot see -- which is why that predicate is named is_opaque_free (what it CHECKS) rather than is_injective (what it is FOR). WHAT MAKES IT REACHABLE is WI-839, whose whole purpose is to close every silent drop of the call-site bracket channel at all three producers; once brackets are populated, a type_args-bearing Apply at a goal or head position stops being hypothetical. NOT a duplicate of WI-322 (Delivered), which fixed collect_unbound_vars_node's VAR walk over these same fields -- the var walkers already chase type_args via collect_expr_termid_field_vars; it is the VIEW that does not, so this is the same gap one layer over. WORK: surface type_args through TermView for Expr::Apply, and through its TERM twin in the same shape so the two carriers stay isomorphic (WI-425 -- a view that disagrees with its twin is a wrong answer, not a precision loss, per the WI-814 note in term_view.rs). MEASURE THE DISCRIM BLAST RADIUS BEFORE LANDING: insert_walk_args walks pos_arity then named_keys, so adding children changes stored-pattern keying for every Apply-headed clause and every query -- take candidate-set counts over the full corpus before and after, the instrument wi814_lambda_view_test already uses for exactly this. Related but separate, worth a look while in the area: is_duplicate_projection (resolve.rs ~2781) guards the same answer-dedup question with a CARRIER allowlist over sigma bindings (`!matches!(v, Value::Term | Value::Node)`), which over-rejects -- Value::Str/Int/Bool view as payload-bearing Const and Value::Entity as Functor, none of them Opaque, yet each disables dedup wholesale; its doc still calls them \"opaque\". GoalKey::is_opaque_free is the precise predicate it wanted. ACCEPTANCE: two goals differing only in type_args produce DIFFERENT GoalKeys and both survive seen_goals; views_structurally_equal reports them unequal; occurrence and term carriers still agree (a term/occurrence GoalKey equality assertion, as wi814/wi819 already do); discrim candidate-set counts recorded before and after; full workspace green. REFERENCE: WI-815; WI-472; WI-839; WI-762; WI-425; WI-322 (adjacent, not this)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-05T22:38:12Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1013", author: "user", content: "REVIEW FOLLOW-UP, implemented inline (not spun off): the cons-list term spine had FOUR constructors, found while merging the two the WI-1013 review named. Merged to ONE. Deleted: load::build_list and node_occurrence::build_list_termid (byte-identical to each other, the latter's own doc calling itself a 'mirror of load.rs::build_list') and load::build_cons_list (parameterized on nil/cons/head/tail, but all six callers passed the prelude List symbols -- unused generality). KnowledgeBase::build_list is the single owner of the STRICT policy; the spine is KnowledgeBase::build_list_with, which persistence::term_ser::build_cons_list also calls, keeping only its own symbol policy (resolve-or-intern fallback for a schema-less deserialize, where build_list resolves-or-panics).\n\nTHE MERGE WAS NOT OBVIOUSLY BYTE-PRESERVING AND WAS MEASURED, NOT ASSERTED. The three deleted copies alloc'd Fn{cons, [(head, x), (tail, acc)]} RAW; the survivor goes through make_entity_term, which canonicalizes named args -- by DECLARED field order when the functor has a schema, else by INTERNING order. cons's declared order IS head, tail, so it is a no-op WHEN THE SCHEMA IS REGISTERED, and register_prelude registers cons as a CONSTRUCTOR without its FIELDS, so a window where entity_field_names(cons) is None genuinely exists. Added a debug_assert in build_list comparing the raw pair against the canonicalized one: silent across the full suite, and VERIFIED ABLE TO FIRE by inverting it and watching a test go red (a probe that cannot fire proves nothing). It stays in as a permanent guard, so a future reorder is loud rather than a silent byte change under every stored list.\n\nONE GUARD NEARLY LOST IN THE CLEANUP. load.rs's Specialization-witness emitter resolved cons only to pass it to build_cons_list, so deleting the call left the binding unused -- but the match's None => return was a graceful skip for a KB with no prelude List, and build_list resolves-or-PANICS. The binding is gone; the check stays, as an explicit try_resolve_symbol(..).is_none() with the reason written at the site. Also dropped a now-dead &ProjectionSyms parameter from wrap_dispatch_channel.\n\nTHREE cons/nil SPINES REMAIN AND SHOULD: build_occurrence_cons_list (OCCURRENCE carrier -- deliberately emits nil as a bare Expr::Ref per the bare-pattern convention, the divergence WI-436/WI-425 depend on), build_value_list (VALUE carrier, WI-348), and one test fixture. Different carriers, each documented as separate on purpose.\n\nFull workspace green: 4180 passed, 0 failed.", at: "2026-08-06T03:08:00Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1023", description: some(value: "\"Which carriers are structurally equivalent to their term twin\" is answered by four hand-written per-site lists, and they have drifted. Found by /code-review on WI-1016, which added a fifth arm to one of them. Each site asks the same question and answers it with its own `matches!`, so a new structural carrier needs N edits and gets fewer. THREE MEASURED-OR-NAMED GAPS, all fail-open today (nothing goes red), each with the site that owns it. (A) is_duplicate_projection (kb/resolve.rs) allows Value::Term | Node | SymbolRef. Value::Requirement and Value::OpRef became STRUCTURAL in WI-1019 -- the commit immediately before WI-1016 -- and Value::Var fingerprints as a Var token, yet all three still set has_value_binding and switch answer dedup OFF. Reachable by exactly the route wi1016_symbol_carrier_test drives: finish_result_value binds a projected child in its own carrier, and Dictionary.sub(d, i) yields a Value::Requirement. Effect: duplicate answers survive. CAUTION -- fixing it widens in the answer-DROPPING direction, which is why it is a ticket and not an arm: it needs a control per added carrier, and the domain argument at the site (this scan is over ALL of sigma; the fingerprint is over goal-reachable vars only) must survive the change. (B) fn_value (kb/mod.rs) accepts Value::Term | SymbolRef as leaves. alloc_from_value ALSO lowers Int/Str/Bool/Var/Entity losslessly, so the WI-815 disjoint-key-space argument stated at that site applies verbatim to a scalar child: f(Value::Int(1)) takes the Value::Entity path and keys value_fact_dedup, while f(1) built as a term keys fact_dedup -- two facts for one logical fact. MEASURED as unreachable rather than assumed harmless: all four assert_fact_carrier callers (eq_derive.rs:281, load.rs:14991, typing.rs:40273, assert_metadata_fact_carrier) pass Value::term(..) children. Widening RE-ROUTES existing heads between the two key spaces, so it needs the same instrumentation WI-815 used -- and note WI-815's lesson, that a count over a population of zero cannot see a wrong key. (C) MapKey::try_from_value (eval/map_arena.rs) canonicalizes Value::SymbolRef and Term::Ref into MapKey::Ref, deliberately declining Term::Ident (declining is CORRECT -- widening would merge two distinct keys and let one put overwrite another) but ALSO declining a non-canonicalized nullary constructor Fn{c,[],[]}, which resolve_qualified_name_term mints by bypassing alloc's canon. That one IS the same name as Ref(c), so declining it accepts a key SPLIT rather than avoiding a merge. Narrow today (that mint has few callers, none feeding a Map). WORK: decide whether one predicate can serve all four -- the honest answer may be NO, since (A)'s domain is all of sigma while (B) additionally needs 'lowers WITHOUT losing identity' (a Value::Node must never lower) and (C) needs 'is the same NAME' -- in which case deliver named predicates with the distinction written down, not one blurred one. Whatever the shape, each carrier admitted needs its own control, and (A) and (B) both move in a direction where being wrong LOSES data."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-08-06T04:17:45Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1024", description: some(value: "eval::value_functor is the fifth by-carrier list: Entity | Term{Fn|Ref} | SymbolRef answers 'what functor does this value name', which is exactly ViewHead::functor_sym(). Found by /code-review on WI-1023, which converted the other four. SAME DRIFT CLASS: Value::OpRef and Value::Requirement gained structural heads in WI-1019 and Value::Node has had one since WI-276, so all three name a functor the view reads and this list answers None for. NOT converted inline with WI-1023 because the direction is OPPOSITE to that ticket's three: those all fail OPEN (a duplicate answer survives, one logical fact stores twice), while widening this one makes values START naming a carrier sort that previously named none -- eval/eval.rs's carrier_sort route and dynamic spec-op dispatch both read it, and anthill-stl's reflect bridge (bridge.rs:205/269/740) reads it across the crate boundary. So each carrier admitted needs a control asserting WHERE it now dispatches, not merely that nothing broke; a green suite is not evidence (WI-979). WORK: decide per carrier whether functor_sym's answer is the wanted one -- note Value::Tuple heads Functor{None,..} and must stay None, which functor_sym already gives -- then convert and drive. Sites: eval/eval.rs value_functor; callers kb/execute.rs:862, anthill-stl bridge.rs:205/269/740, eval/pattern.rs:328."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-08-06T05:19:46Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1025", description: some(value: "A bare Expr::Ref occurrence is the THIRD carrier of a name, but eval::value_functor answers None for it -- so facts_of / stored_facts_of / is_modifiable / sort_query lowering / the reflect bridge all say 'not a sort reference' for a Value::Node that names one, while the Term::Ref and Value::SymbolRef spellings answer the symbol. Split off from WI-1024, which decided the other four carriers and found this one is NOT a line in that match. TWO THINGS MAKE IT ITS OWN TICKET. (1) NO SINGLE HEAD TEST SEPARATES REFERENT FROM ENCODING ON THIS CARRIER: occ_head answers Functor{Some(reflect ctor)} for a Lambda/If/Let/pattern/arrow occurrence -- an ENCODING, exactly like Dictionary, and reading it as a referent turns a loud TypeMismatch into a silently empty facts_of; Expr::Spliced(v) reads THROUGH to v.head(kb), so Node(Spliced(Requirement)) launders the carrier WI-1024 deliberately excluded straight back in; and restricting to ViewHead::Ref answers None for an applied sort occurrence whose TERM twin answers Some, which is the exact cross-carrier disagreement the ticket would exist to fix -- facts_of(kb, List[T = Int64]) (WI-707) is a live consumer of that shape. So the work is to state what makes an occurrence a REFERENT rather than to widen a match arm. (2) IT OBLIGES THE PAIRED SITES. eval/pattern.rs:326-333 (WI-1016) states the rule: a carrier value_functor accepts must be destructurable by match_constructor_pattern, or MatchDispatch's pre-filter promises an arm that then declines. match_constructor_pattern has no Node arm, so a nullary-constructor pattern arm does not match Node(Expr::Ref(nil)) though it matches both other spellings; effects::detect_cycle has the same hole for a Node resource key. MEASURED: the corpus population is ZERO -- instrumented over all four crates, 41631 value_functor calls, carriers Entity/Term/Int only, no Node ever -- so every arm needs a DRIVEN control, a count proves nothing (WI-815). Sites: eval/eval.rs value_functor; eval/pattern.rs match_constructor_pattern; eval/effects.rs detect_cycle."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-08-06T06:07:38Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1008", description: some(value: "FOUND WHILE DRIVING WI-978 (see docs/proposals/059-secondary-entries.md R2). An\noperation declared in a SECONDARY ENTRY backs a spec claim at LOAD TIME but is\nUNREACHABLE by the requires-dictionary DISPATCH route at run time: the program\nloads clean and dies. That is the WI-818 failure mode, in the placement 059 R2\nmakes the whole point of the mechanism.\n\nMEASURED, three fixtures differing only in where the backing op sits. Spec:\n`sort Show { sort T = ?; operation show(x: T) -> Int64 }`. Caller:\n`sort Caller { sort T = ?; requires Show[T = T]; operation useIt(x: T) -> Int64 = show(x) }`.\n\n  (1) op in the SORT BODY, provision in the sort body\n      => loads clean, 1 provision, `Rec.show` = 111, `Caller.useIt` = 111\n      <- CONTROL, works\n\n  (2) op in a SECONDARY ENTRY (`namespace Rec { operation show(x: Rec) = 111 }`),\n      provision ONE LEVEL OUT\n      => loads clean, 1 provision, `Rec.show` = 111,\n         `Caller.useIt` = EvalError::OperationBodyMissing { name: \"…Show.show\" }\n\n  (3) op AND provision both in the secondary entry\n      => identical to (2)\n\nSo the PROVISION placement is not the discriminator -- (2) and (3) fail alike\nwhile (1) passes. The OP placement is. `op_backed` (typing.rs,\n`check_provider_operations`) finds the secondary entry's `show` and accepts the\nclaim; the runtime dictionary route does not, and reports the SPEC op as missing\na body.\n\nNOT ESTABLISHED, and the decision this ticket has to make: which side is wrong.\n\n  (a) WIDEN dispatch -- the requires-dictionary route should see a secondary\n      entry's members, since 059 R2 says what a secondary entry declares IS a\n      member of the sort and may back its claims. Note the DIRECT call already\n      dispatches (`Rec.show` = 111 in (2)), so membership is partly wired\n      already; find which reader the dictionary route consults instead.\n\n  (b) NARROW `op_backed` -- refuse a secondary entry's op as backing, so the\n      LOAD is refused rather than the run. This CONTRADICTS 059 R2 and would\n      make the mechanism unable to supply members for a foreign carrier, which\n      is most of why one writes a secondary entry. Named for completeness, not\n      recommended.\n\nWI-978 fixed the neighbouring EMITTER defect (a claim in a secondary entry to a\nfree-standing `entity` recorded no provision at all, so no reader saw it) and\ndeliberately did not reach this: different subsystem, and (a)/(b) is a design\ncall. WI-978's own test file states the split at its head.\n\nACCEPTANCE: a test DRIVES the generic caller through the requires path with the\nbacking op declared in a secondary entry and asserts the VALUE -- not that it\nloads. Fixture (1) is included unchanged as the both-sides control, since it\npasses either way and is what attributes a failure to the placement rather than\nto the fixture. State at the test site which of the three fixtures fails when\nthe fix is backed out. cargo-test green via rustland/scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-06T07:12:02Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1012", author: "user", content: "IMPLEMENTED, workspace green 4212/0 (56 binaries); /code-review NOT YET RUN - it is user-invocable only. /simplify run instead (4 agents), findings applied.\n\nWHAT LANDED: new 'TypeError::AmbiguousSpecOpDispatch' + 'LoadError::AmbiguousSpecOpDispatch' (same name as the eval variant - one refusal, three faces, the 'MacroRejected' precedent), raised from the typer's WI-444 block when 'carrier_override_suppliers' returns >=2 on a statically concrete carrier. One message owner 'ambiguous_spec_op_dispatch_message' read by TypeError::format, both LoadError renderings and EvalError::Display. Ticket's acceptance met: WI-1010's tie test is now a LOAD refusal, the abstract-carrier twin still refuses at eval, and both render ONE body (asserted by byte-equality after stripping the load face's line:col).\n\nTHE REVIEW OVERTURNED MY MESSAGE, and it was the one thing I had already doubted. My shared sentence said 'No bracket at this site can choose between them' for EVERY tie. FALSE for the only tie shape the corpus drives: 'callee_requirement_slots' pushes a Dispatch slot only when 'lookup_spec_op_dispatch' is Some, so a BODY-LESS op's witness rival IS nameable via '[Spec = W]' - which is exactly WI-842's fixture (own member vs witness Rival). I had deleted WI-842's correct advice ('route the call through an operation that can write one'). The tree had said so twice: TieRepair's doc ('advertising a repair that the next compile rejects is the failure mode this enum exists to make impossible to reintroduce') and LoadError::CallTypeArgsNotSupportedHere's 'position' field, added because one wording told a rule head to route through an operation. I made the mirror-image error. FIX: 'SupplierTieRepair' {KeepOne, NameableWitness}, one owner 'supplier_tie_repair' asked by all three sites. Both arms driven - and WI-842's tie test had NO assertion on its message at all (it checked three names, all of which survived the wrong wording), so it now pins the repair and the sentence.\n\nTWO LIMITS, MEASURED, both filed rather than claimed closed:\n- WI-1026: a rule body naming the spec op DIRECTLY reaches none of this. Measured, 3 spellings x 2 supply shapes: with ONE fact-route supplier 'Desc.describe(leaf(), ?r)'=1 (the DEFAULT - so WI-1010's rule does not hold there either), 'describe(leaf(), ?r)'=[], 'leaf().describe(?r)'=1; with TWO: 1, [], 7. Three silent answers for one ambiguous program. CONTROL: the same tie through an operation body answers 7 / is refused at load, so the defect is the PATH. Pre-existing - neither WI-444 site is on the SLD goal path.\n- WI-1027: the BODY-LESS half has no load face; three declining arms of check_apply_iter hand a concrete carrier to eval unrefused. Structural reading from a review agent, NOT driven, so filed fixture-first. docs/kernel-language.md narrowed accordingly - the load refusal covers DEFAULTED operations, not the family. I had over-claimed it for both halves.\nAlso recorded AT the arm: it cannot fire for a SELF-RECEIVER spec (provision_carrier_sort files under the spec's FIRST TYPE PARAM), so the stdlib's largest defaulted-op family is out of reach - WI-450's limit, so closing it there stays a decision.\n\nBACK-OUT MATRICES RE-MEASURED, not carried over. New file: 5 of 6 rows FAIL when the '[_, _, ..]' arm falls through; the abstract-carrier row passes by design. WI-1010's tie row MOVED - was 'typer FAILS | eval FAILS | both FAILS', now 'typer FAILS | eval ok | both FAILS', because reverting the eval half alone leaves the typer to refuse. Measured over all four runs.\n\nALSO FROM REVIEW: 'render_suppliers' extracted - the typer made a THIRD copy of the candidate-rendering expression, which eval's 'sole_supplier_by_value' doc claims to exist to prevent; that doc was stale and is now corrected rather than left. wi1010's 'program' is 'pub(crate)' with a 'tail' param and shared (not copied), and the two duplicate control tests were deleted in favour of wi1010's, which fail identically if this arm over-fires. The rule test was renamed to 'a_rule_reaching_the_tie_through_an_operation_is_refused' and says at its site what it does NOT drive - review was right that it would pass with rule-body typing deleted.", at: "2026-08-06T09:18:57Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1027", author: "user", content: "CHECKED AGAINST proposal 058 - no contradiction, and one clause sharpened.\n\nWHY 'at LOAD' does not contradict S3.2 tier 3's 'loud error at that use site': the axis tier 3 legislates is BLAME, not phase. S1's complaint is that the OLD refusal hit the declarations ('Anthill refused two providers of one spec for one carrier at load'), and WI-843's note records the symptom - delete every call and the load still failed. A load error raised from 'check_apply_iter' carries the CALL's span and names the call, so it IS a use-site error that happens to be reported while loading. S3.7 already licenses load-time refusal outright where no call site can select. The proposal now says this explicitly (S3.2 amended: '\"At that use site\" fixes the blame, not the phase').\n\nS3.7 IS THE RULE THIS TICKET SERVES: 'a read that asks existence of a provider stays boolean; a read that SELECTS one goes loud on the second candidate - never first-match.' The three declining arms hand a selecting read to eval; making it loud earlier is the same rule, not a new one.\n\nTHE 'ALSO IN SCOPE' DECLARATION-LEVEL CLAUSE NEEDS ONE CORRECTION, now that the code was read rather than recalled. The pair WI-1012 refuses is cell (1 fact, 0 witnesses, 1 self-provider) of WI-859's verdict matrix ('check_provider_kind_coherence' in typing.rs), which is ADMITTED at load ON PURPOSE, measured, because it MAY be one dictionary written twice; the admission's own doc says it 'admits a group whose conflict, if it has one, is refused by a check that can see it' and names the typer-classified call as one of those checks. So a declaration-level refusal here would have to refuse inside a cell 058 S3.1's nameability gate deliberately admits - it is NOT simply the AmbiguousEqDispatch pattern applied one spec over. Note also that the same doc records THE DEEPER ALTERNATIVE (gate kind 3 on the carrier owning a member, splitting the cell by construction) and rejects it because the rival half 'would then need a VERDICT of its own - a new load refusal, which is the one thing WI-859 scoped itself out of'. WI-1012 has now built exactly that refusal, so the fork WI-859 declined is reachable in a way it was not. Weigh it there, with those two records in hand.", at: "2026-08-06T09:35:14Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1026", author: "user", content: "CHECKED AGAINST proposal 058 - no contradiction; the proposal REQUIRES this and does not currently reach it.\n\nS3.7 states the general rule verbatim: 'a read that asks EXISTENCE of a provider stays boolean; a read that SELECTS one goes loud on the second candidate - NEVER FIRST-MATCH.' The measured 'rule answer(?r) :- leaf().describe(?r)' = 7 with two suppliers IS first-match (route 1 wins), so today's rule-body path violates S3.7 directly.\n\nS3.1 states the model for the other rows: concrete providers 'pay in VALUES: their values carry their own sort, DISPATCH ANSWERS BY THE VALUE'. With a single fact-route supplier the rule body answers 1 (the spec DEFAULT), i.e. does not answer by the value. So the acceptance ('every row answers 7 or refuses') is S3.1 + S3.7 restated, not a new policy.\n\nWHAT 058 DOES NOT SAY, worth knowing before designing: it never legislates rule bodies. S2 models instance resolution AS the SLD goal 'provides(?W, Spec[..])' - but immediately adds that the checker implements it 'inside the typer rather than by literally querying the reflect KB', and S3.9 says every dictionary in flight 'was built at a site where the TYPER resolved the witness'. A rule body has no typer-resolved witness, so this is a GAP in the proposal, not a rule it grants an exemption to. Do not read S3.9 as licensing the present silence; if a decision is taken to leave a spelling alone, it needs writing INTO 058, not inferring from it.\n\nORDERING NOTE: the bare-goal '[]' row answers nothing even with ONE unambiguous supplier, so it is a resolution failure prior to any 058 question and should be diagnosed before the dispatch policy is touched.", at: "2026-08-06T09:35:33Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1012", description: some(value: "Refuse a supplier TIE at LOAD when the carrier is statically concrete, instead of only at the call. WI-1010 made a SECOND supplier of a DEFAULTED spec op loud (058 S4.9) rather than letting route order decide silently, and raised it at eval as 'EvalError::AmbiguousSpecOpDispatch' - the only wording that can name a route with no name of its own (an instance fact, S4.3). But the TYPER already holds everything the diagnostic needs at the moment it declines to pin: 'check_apply_iter' has the span, the carrier symbol and the rendered candidate list from 'SpecOpSupplier::render'. Deferring costs three things. (1) 'anthill check' passes on a program the interpreter will refuse - the repo principle is 'avoid fallbacks, know about errors early'. (2) A tie in a branch that never executes never reports. (3) WORST, and the reason this is filed rather than left: on the SLD path the refusal DEGRADES TO SILENCE - resolve.rs residualizes 'AmbiguousSpecOpDispatch' to None, so a rule body calling a 2-supplier defaulted op now answers NOTHING where pre-WI-1010 it answered the default. That is inherited family policy, documented in docs/kernel-language.md S'Where the ambiguity error is raised', and unreachable in the corpus today (WI-1010 measured the tie corpus-inert) - but it is exactly the 'prefer a loud error over a silent skip' case. WHY IT WAS NOT DONE INLINE: it needs a NEW 'TypeError' variant carrying (carrier: Symbol, candidates: Vec<String>, span). 'DispatchAmbiguous' cannot be reused - VERIFIED: its 'InstanceTie' carries PROVIDER symbols, and for a route-1-vs-route-2 tie both canonicalize to the SAME carrier, so 'render_instance_tie' would print 'Leaf, Leaf' and, both being concrete, pick 'TieRepair::ValueDirected', whose message ('each is a CONCRETE provider') does not describe this tie at all. WORK: add the variant + its 'format' / 'to_load_error' / 'span' arms, raise it from the typer's else-arm, and SHARE ONE MESSAGE BODY with the eval error (precedent: 'unselected_instance_message' already shares one sentence across 'TypeError::format' and two 'LoadError' renderings). The eval site STAYS - the typer's WI-444 block only fires on a statically concrete carrier, so an un-pinnable one still needs the late refusal; that is an argument for sharing the message, not for having only the late one. ACCEPTANCE: WI-1010's 'an_own_member_rivalled_by_a_fact_binding_is_refused_at_the_call' becomes a LOAD refusal (its concrete-'Leaf' program) while an abstract-carrier twin still refuses at eval, both quoting the same sentence; a rule-body case that today answers nothing reports something; full workspace green. REFERENCE: WI-1010; WI-842; WI-843; docs/design/058-implementation.md S12."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-1010"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-08-06T09:37:33Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-984", author: "user", content: "IMPLEMENTED, workspace green 4223/0 plus 4 doctests. /code-review NOT YET RUN — it is user-invocable only; /simplify run instead (4 agents), findings applied.\n\nWHAT LANDED: opaque `intern::ScopeId` wrapping a Symbol, minted only by `SymbolTable::scope_id`. `SymbolDef::Resolved.scope`, `ScopeInclusion.parent_scope` and every scope-keyed SymbolTable method take it; `KnowledgeBase::scope_id_of(name_term)` bridges where the loader holds the scope TERM, and `Loader::current_scope_id()` names the loader's own derivation. The acceptance is three `compile_fail` doctests on `ScopeId` plus one positive — they run under `cargo test --doc`, which a doc comment in tests/ would not.\n\nTHE MEASUREMENT THAT JUSTIFIED THE SYMBOL FORM, and it was not theoretical: 227 of 2602 resolved stdlib symbols answered `None` from `declaring_scope_symbol`, and `scope(?s, ?r)` failed on each. They are one population — an entity's FIELDS are declared in the CONSTRUCTOR's scope, and `alloc` stores a constructor's nullary Fn as `Term::Ref` (WI-511), which the Fn-only match could not read. Same defect at `builtin_scope`, `scope_qualified_name`, `scope_display_name`, `internal_field_hidden_from` (an `internal` field of a constructor-owned scope read as VISIBLE) and the op-scope check in `emit_sort_info`.\n\nMY FIRST CONTROL WAS WRONG and driving it said so. I predicted the EPONYMOUS sort (`sort P { entity P(...) }`) would be the failing case; measured, that symbol is NOT marked a constructor, so its members answered before the change too. The test file now says so explicitly and drives the real population (a variant's field), with a non-constructor scope beside it as the pass-either-way row.\n\nA DEFECT I INTRODUCED, found by a review agent and driven with a control: `provides Stack[T = Int64] language anthill ... end` ABORTED the loader. `load_provides_block` assigned the APPLIED spec term as `current_scope`, documented as a per-instantiation scope IDENTITY. That identity was VACUOUS — nothing is ever defined into an applied term's scope and no add_parent links one, so BOTH instantiations resolved against an empty scope with no parents while the bare `provides Stack` beside them used Stack's real scope. Fixed to the base sort; the two spellings now agree, matching the domain, which already collapsed both. The existing `parameterized_provides_block_test` missed it because its blocks carry only language/artifact clauses, so no name is ever resolved inside — rows added there that put a body in and resolve a Stack-scoped name through it. That file's module doc PINNED the retired rule and is corrected.\n\nA DEFECT AT THE SEAM: `anthill.reflect.scope` has TWO backings and only one was fixed. Its stdlib signature says 'Symbol -> enclosing scope symbol (None for top-level)'; the SLD builtin now implements that, but the eval host op called `KnowledgeBase::scope_of`, a scan for a SIBLING sort/namespace/operation. MEASURED on one fixture the two shared NO answer: eval gave none/none/Tank.fill/none where SLD gave Full/Tank/wi984s/Tank. Both now read the declaring scope. `scope_of` had no other caller and is DELETED — a function named 'the scope of' that answered a sibling is a trap. WI-1016's test pinned only the some/none shape (its comment explained why); it now pins the symbol.\n\nTHE RIDER, done as the two steps the feedback prescribed. `instantiation_term_raw` is gone; the `build_instantiation_term` CALL stays and is documented at the function rather than the call site. MEASURED with the call deleted: `by_qualified_name`'s NAME SET is identical (2602 both ways, so no resolution answer moves) but 10 symbols stop existing — the short names of `requires`d specs — and 202 of 3336 survivors change RANK. Interning order is the fallback key for named-arg canonicalization on a schema-less functor, and the term ids advanced name the requirement slots (`__req_partialeq_14996` -> `__req_partialeq_14975`). So the interning is NOT vestigial; retiring the walk is its own measurable change. Side effect of dropping the field: two `requires` of one spec at different instantiations were two links and are now one — unobservable (resolve_in_scope and internal_visible_from read only parent_scope + is_enclosing), asserted rather than left silent.\n\nALSO FROM REVIEW: a THIRD `_unknown` scope-name fallback survived in `forbid_internal_import` — same query, converted; `_unknown` now has no producer. Two loader maps still keyed scopes on a raw u32 term (`type_param_vars`, `requires_seen`) — the ticket's own defect surviving inside the loader, retyped. `KnowledgeBase::global_scope()` replaces 19 hand-spelled incantations in two forms under four names, and `builtin_scope`'s `local_name(owner) == \"_global\"` short-name comparison became a ScopeId compare. ~110 loop-invariant derivations hoisted. `existing_namespace_in_scope` shares a lookup both scan arms spelled inline.\n\nWHAT THE ScopeId DOES NOT CLOSE, stated at the type rather than implied: (1) WHICH TABLE issued the symbol — the range check catches only the larger-foreign-table direction, asserted by `scope_id_refuses_a_symbol_this_table_never_issued`; closing it needs the symbol tagged, which WI-1004's feedback says is a different ticket. (2) `Symbol::from_raw` is pub const, so `scope_id(Symbol::from_raw(3))` compiles and, in range, succeeds — the compile errors say a scope cannot be built from an integer IN A SCOPE POSITION, not that an integer can never reach one, and that hop is one call long. I had over-claimed this before review.\n\nDELIBERATELY NOT DONE: threading ScopeId through the ScopePass/ScopeSite spine so `scope_id_of` could be deleted (filed as WI-1028 — the loader holds 89 derivations and the terms it unwraps were built from symbols it already had); the term-level readers `is_sort_scope`/`eponymous_sort_symbol` (WI-983's feedback rules them out of scope — but WI-926's comment gave TWO reasons for not marking an eponymous constructor and this change made one of them FALSE, so the comment is corrected to rest on the one that holds).", at: "2026-08-06T11:19:31Z")
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-1028", name: "rustland")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-984", description: some(value: "RUSTLAND: THE SCOPEID TWIN — SCOPE-HOOD IS STILL UNTYPED HERE. Rustland's `intern.rs` has exactly the defect WI-976 removed from scaland: `ScopeInclusion.parent_scope_raw: u32` and `SymbolDef::Resolved`'s scope are bare integers, and every `SymbolTable` scope-keyed method takes one — so a scope, an arbitrary term and an arbitrary index are one type, and 'this raw is a scope' is a promise each caller carries. WORK: mirror WI-976 — an opaque `ScopeId` minted from a `Symbol`, threaded through the `SymbolTable` API and `SymbolDef::Resolved`. Note the shape scaland settled on and WHY, because the obvious alternative is worse: `ScopeId` wraps the SYMBOL, not the TermId. Wrapping the TermId keeps the functor projection a QUERY (fetch the term, match a nullary `Fn`, answer `Option`) — the runtime check the ticket exists to delete — while the symbol form makes it a total projection. Rustland is already halfway there: `KnowledgeBase` stores a rule's `domain: Symbol`. RIDER, and it is the cheap half: `instantiation_term_raw` is written by ~18 sites and read by NONE (`kb/load.rs` says so at its own writers, calling it vestigial); scaland dropped its copy in WI-976, so the two records have already diverged and keeping it buys no parity. ACCEPTANCE: cargo-test green; a scope cannot be built from a raw integer or a term, said as a compile error rather than a comment — say at the site which test fails when the change is backed out. Related: WI-976, WI-962, WI-983."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-06T11:20:08Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-984", author: "user", content: "CORRECTION, driven after delivery — a premise I wrote into the delivery record was FALSE, and the user's question 'why do we need persistent term ids?' is what refuted it.\n\nI kept the `build_instantiation_term` call and argued the interning was load-bearing because deleting it re-ranks 202 of 3336 symbols and renames requirement slots (`__req_partialeq_14996` -> `__req_partialeq_14975`). I MEASURED THAT THE IDS CHANGE and treated it as evidence that something DEPENDS on them. It is not the same claim. Checked: `persistence/term_ser.rs` writes NO numeric id — every reference is name-keyed — so no id crosses a process boundary; and the interning-order fallback in `canonicalize_record_named_args` needs all producers to agree WITHIN one KB, which they do whatever the order is, not to be stable ACROSS loads. The decisive experiment: delete the call, run the full workspace — 4223/0, including the golden CLI transcript and every persistence round trip. The walk was then DEAD (141 lines, no caller) and is deleted, with the same evidence applied to the namespace-term `alloc` in `ensure_intermediate_namespaces`.\n\nWI-1028 WAS MISFRAMED, and by exactly the defect this ticket's own feedback quotes. Its acceptance was '`scope_id_of` is deleted' — a caller count, which WI-983's feedback calls the wrong shape ('no caller is in role X, and the rest are all role Y' is the right one). I filed it one commit after delivering the ticket carrying that warning. Re-scoped, and the role split done inline instead: every PURE-SCOPE-ROLE site now takes a `ScopeId` (`scan_rule`, `scan_rule_goal`, `scan_operation_params`, `process_imports`, `ensure_intermediate_namespaces` including its return type, and `register_stdlib_scopes`, which minted 21 nullary terms whose only consumer was the projection back to their own functor). 89 derivations -> 29, and each of the 29 hands the same value on AS A TERM (`is_sort_scope`, `walk_scopes`, `register_prelude_constructor`, `enter_sort_with_body`, `assert_fact`). The criterion is written at `scope_id_of` itself so the next reader is not tempted back into counting callers. Workspace green after each step.", at: "2026-08-06T11:52:37Z")
