fact Tag(workitem: "WI-1031", name: "rustland")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1028", author: "user", content: "IMPLEMENTED, workspace green 4228/0 plus 4 doctests. /code-review NOT RUN — user-invocable only; /simplify run instead (4 agents, ~30 findings, 11 applied).\n\nWHAT LANDED: every scope-carrying value in the loader is a `ScopeId`. `ScopePass::{enter_scope,exit_scope,at_item}`, `ScopeSite.enclosing`, `walk_scopes`, `lookup_scope`, `resolve_declared_scope`, `find_scope_by_name`, `Loader::current_scope`, `current_domain`, `enter_namespace`, `enter/exit_sort_with_body`, `load_operation`'s `op_scope`, `check_op_type_param_shadowing`'s `enclosing`, `scan_operation_params`, and the prelude bootstrap's last two term round trips. `KnowledgeBase::scope_id_of` has no caller in any role and is DELETED. `current_scope_id()` became the identity across 21 reads and is deleted too.\n\nTHREE SITES DERIVE A TERM, all genuine term positions: the namespace fact's subject, the sort's description-fact subject, and `MemberInfo.parent`. (A fourth, `is_sort_scope`, went away with WI-1029.)\n\nTHE BEHAVIOUR QUESTION THE TICKET FLAGGED, MEASURED BEFORE CONVERTING, NOT ASSUMED. (1) `held == make_name_term_from_sym(name_term_sym(held))` asserted at every scope site the walk reaches: 4223 tests, never fired; inverted to `assert_ne!` it fires on the first file, so the probe was REACHED. Re-deriving the term is TermId-identical. (2) `resolve_declared_scope`'s two derivations (`lookup_defined` as guard, `remap_name` as answer) agree over the whole corpus — that sentence is now a `debug_assert_eq!` rather than prose, because a sentence is not a guard: `remap_name` cannot fail (bare-interns) and `scope_id` only range-checks, so a divergence would mint a scope over a symbol owning none.\n\nTHE CONTROL, SAID PLAINLY: this is a REPRESENTATION change with no intended behaviour change, so 4225 of the tests pass either way BY DESIGN — they are the control for \"nothing moved\", not evidence anything is new. Three new tests in `wi1028_scope_id_spine_tests`; two fail by NOT COMPILING against a `TermId` spine. `a_clause_is_filed_under_the_owner_of_the_scope_it_is_written_in` is the discriminating one — MEASURED: file every clause under the walk root instead and it fails while the walk test still passes, which is why both exist.\n\nA TEST OF MINE THAT MEASURED NOTHING, caught by driving its control: the first resolve assertion was one-sided (`Red` resolves from Colour's scope) and PASSES with the descent crossed, because `Red` answers `Found` from `_global` too. It is two-sided now on a sort-private OPERATION, and the NEGATIVE half is what fires.\n\nFROM /simplify, applied: reverted my own `emit_member_fact` change (it put a caller-side pair invariant on 10 sites AND made both callers re-derive a term they held — flagged by all four angles); `resolve_declared_scope` now shares `lookup_scope`; `process_imports` had become literally `A && A` / `A || A` and is one `has_qualified_name`; the Entity arm decided \"is this a sort body\" three times in two spellings, now once; `lookup_scope` lost a `&mut` it no longer needs; two dead `_global` term round trips removed from `anthill-cli/src/prove.rs` and `kb/body_specialize.rs` (the shape WI-984 deleted 21 of).\n\nTWO CLAIMS OF MY OWN THAT WERE WRONG. I listed \"a `SortAlias` subject\" among the term-needing sites in two doc comments — there is no such site; I fabricated it. And I wrote that `&mut` on the `is_sort_scope_id` predicate was \"the honest price\": `TermStore::find` exists for callers that only want to NAME a term, and `alloc` bumps a refcount on a hash-cons hit. Verified in `kb/term.rs`. That fed WI-1029.\n\nALSO: a comment cited `wi984_param_provides_test`, a test name that does not exist; corrected to `parameterized_provides_body_resolves_a_spec_scoped_name`.", at: "2026-08-06T14:02:15Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1028", description: some(value: "RUSTLAND: CONVERT THE SCOPE-FINDER RETURN TYPES AND THE ScopePass SPINE, so `KnowledgeBase::scope_id_of(TermId)` has no caller left. NOTE THE ACCEPTANCE BELOW IS NOT A CALLER COUNT — the first version of this ticket said 'scope_id_of is deleted', which is the shape WI-983's feedback warns against ('a site can serve two roles at once, so how many callers remain is the wrong shape for a criterion; no caller is in role X, and the rest are all role Y is the right one'). WI-984 quoted that warning and then wrote the defect into this ticket one commit later. WHAT WI-984 ALREADY DID, inline, after that was noticed: every PURE-SCOPE-ROLE site converted — `scan_rule`, `scan_rule_goal`, `scan_operation_params`, `process_imports`, `ensure_intermediate_namespaces` (return type too) and the whole `register_stdlib_scopes` bootstrap, which minted 21 nullary terms whose only consumer was the projection back to their own functor. 89 derivations -> 29, all 29 at genuine two-role sites, and the criterion is written at `scope_id_of` itself. WHAT IS LEFT, and why it is not a sweep: the remaining 29 hand the SAME value to `is_sort_scope`, `walk_scopes`, `register_prelude_constructor`, `enter_sort_with_body` or `assert_fact` AS A TERM. Removing them means changing `resolve_name_to_scope` / `find_scope_by_name` / `lookup_scope` / `resolve_declared_scope` to return `ScopeId`, and `ScopePass::{enter_scope,exit_scope,at_item}` + `ScopeSite.enclosing` (3 methods, 5 impls) plus `Loader.current_scope`; the term-consumers then re-derive with `make_name_term_from_sym(scope.owner())`. THE REASON TO DO IT is not throughput — measured at ~0.5% of a debug load, immaterial. It is that the derivation HID A DECISION: `load_provides_block` assigned an APPLIED spec term as the scope and the question 'what scope does that name?' only surfaced when `name_term_sym` aborted, behind 22 reads instead of at the one write. TERM-ID STABILITY IS NOT A CONSTRAINT — WI-984 measured this rather than assuming it: persistence writes no numeric ids (all name-keyed), and deleting two allocation sites plus a 141-line walk shifted symbol ranks with the full suite green, including the golden CLI transcript. So an `alloc` may be dropped when its term has no consumer; do not repeat the 'term ids are observable' reasoning WI-984 first wrote and then refuted. DO NOT FOLD IN the term-level readers `is_sort_scope` / `eponymous_sort_symbol` (WI-983's feedback rules those out) — but note their caller may hand them a term re-derived from a ScopeId, which is a behaviour question to answer, not assume. ACCEPTANCE: no caller of `scope_id_of` remains in a TWO-ROLE position either — i.e. every scope-carrying value in the loader is a `ScopeId` and the few sites needing a term derive it explicitly; cargo-test green; say at the site which test fails when the change is backed out. REFERENCE: WI-984; WI-983; WI-1004."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-06T14:02:16Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1029", author: "user", content: "IMPLEMENTED, same session as WI-1028, workspace green 4228/0.\n\n`is_sort_scope` now reads `!kb.is_constructor_symbol(scope.owner()) && kb.has_kind(scope.owner(), Sort)` and takes a `ScopeId` and `&KnowledgeBase`. `eponymous_sort_symbol` takes a `ScopeId` and returns `scope.owner()`; its second `Term::Fn` match was dead (the `is_sort_scope` guard above it had already established `Fn`) and is gone. The `is_sort_scope_id` adapter WI-1028 introduced is gone with them, and with it the `&mut` on a predicate and the per-call `alloc` refcount bump. The `DefinePass::at_item` Entity arm no longer derives a term at all.\n\nTHE PROHIBITION DID NOT HOLD, and the ticket said why: WI-983's criterion is \"a term is what goes in that position\" and neither of these puts a term anywhere. Verified rather than repeated.\n\nNO DISCRIMINATING TEST EXISTS, BY CONSTRUCTION — the two readings are equal. The acceptance was written as a shape and is met that way. `a_sort_scope_is_one_whose_owner_is_a_sort_and_not_a_constructor` pins the RULE on both shapes (constructor-owned and not) and carries the old term reading beside it as an oracle, asserting they agree on all three rows; it passes either way BY DESIGN and says so. A future change to the WI-511 canon breaks it loudly.\n\nMY FIRST FIXTURE WAS WRONG and the test said so: I built the symbols with `intern`, but a kind lives on `SymbolDef::Resolved`, so every row read false for the same uninteresting reason. `define`d now, with the reason at the site.\n\nDOC ROT FIXED IN THE SAME COMMIT: WI-926's comment said marking an eponymous constructor \"would flip the sort's own nullary term through the WI-511 canon, which `is_sort_scope` matches only in its `Fn` spelling\" — true of the old encoding, meaningless now. It states the dependency as the plain sentence it always was: a constructor-owned scope is not a sort body, so marking it would stop `sort P { entity P(…) }` reading as one from the inside. `rustland/CLAUDE.md`, the `ScopeId` doc and the `ScopePass` doc all cited `is_sort_scope` as the live example of the trap; each now says it WAS one until this ticket.", at: "2026-08-06T14:02:49Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1029", description: some(value: "RUSTLAND: THE SCOPE PREDICATES READ THE CONSTRUCTOR FLAG THROUGH A TERM ROUND TRIP. `is_sort_scope(kb, term)` matches `Term::Fn{f,[],[]}` + `has_kind(f, Sort)`; `eponymous_sort_symbol` sits on top of it and returns `Option<Symbol>`. After WI-1028 every caller holds a `ScopeId` and derives the term to feed them (`is_sort_scope_id`, and `DefinePass::at_item`'s `scope_term`), so the composition is provable by reading `KnowledgeBase::alloc`: it rewrites a nullary `Fn` to `Ref` iff `is_constructor_symbol(functor)`, and the reader matches only `Fn`. Therefore `is_sort_scope_id(scope)` IS `!is_constructor_symbol(scope.owner()) && has_kind(scope.owner(), Sort)` — the term is an ENCODING of the constructor flag, and the readers pattern-match the encoding instead of naming the referent (the WI-1024 shape). WORK: make the symbol form primary; keep `is_sort_scope`/`eponymous_sort_symbol` only if something still needs the term spelling, and delete them otherwise. WHY THE PROHIBITION DOES NOT APPLY, because it will be quoted at whoever picks this up: WI-1028 says \"DO NOT FOLD IN the term-level readers (WI-983's feedback rules those out)\". CHECKED — it does not. WI-983's criterion is \"each needs a term BECAUSE A TERM IS WHAT GOES IN THAT POSITION\" and it names four sites, all of which PUT a term somewhere (`registerEntityOf`'s parent sort, the `entity_of` argument, `provides_clause`'s `sort_ref`, `Builtins.executeScope`'s answer). These two put no term anywhere — one returns `bool`, the other `Option<Symbol>`. The generalization entered at WI-984's DELIVERY RECORD (\"the term-level readers ... WI-983's feedback rules them out of scope\") and WI-1028 restated it as an instruction; neither re-checked it against WI-983's own criterion. THE TRAP, and it is the whole reason this needs care: THERE IS NO DISCRIMINATING TEST, BY CONSTRUCTION. The two readings are equivalent, so no input separates them — do not invent a test that appears to. MEASURED over the whole corpus during WI-1028: the term reading and `has_kind(Sort)` on the owner NEVER disagree, i.e. no scope owner reachable today is a marked constructor at all. So state the acceptance as a SHAPE, not as a behaviour: (1) no predicate about a scope takes a `TermId`; (2) `is_sort_scope_id` takes `&KnowledgeBase`, not `&mut` — today it must be `&mut` because `make_name_term_from_sym` routes through `TermStore::alloc`, which bumps a refcount even on a hash-cons HIT (`kb/term.rs`, and `TermStore::find` exists for exactly this caller but answers `None` for a never-allocated term, which is why the two questions are one ticket); (3) the WI-926 dependency is spelled `is_constructor_symbol` at its site rather than implied by a `Term::Fn` match. PIN THE EQUIVALENCE rather than a behaviour change: a test that builds both shapes — a scope whose owner is `mark_constructor_symbol`'d and one that is not — and asserts the answer, so a future change to the WI-511 canon breaks it loudly. Say at the site which test fails when the change is backed out, and say plainly that the corpus passes either way BY DESIGN. REFERENCE: WI-1028; WI-984; WI-983; WI-926; WI-511; WI-1024."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-06T14:02:49Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1030", author: "user", content: "IMPLEMENTED, same session, workspace green 4228/0.\n\nThe `requires` ladder routes through `ResolveResult::or_else`, so an ambiguity ends it. `resolve_name_to_scope` had exactly this one caller and is deleted.\n\nBOTH CONTROLS MEASURED, and the first attempt caught a test of mine that measured NOTHING. I moved the top-level `sort Rival` into a namespace when turning the probe into a test — but the absolute rung is `by_qualified_name[<base>]`, which can only hit an undotted name, so the namespaced fixture wires no parent either way and the test passed with the fix backed out. Restored to the reproducing shape, with the reason written at the fixture so nobody tidies it back.\n\nWith the fixture right: (1) restore the old ladder → `an_ambiguous_requires_wires_no_parent` FAILS, control passes; (2) apply the cheapest WRONG fix, deleting the absolute rung outright → `an_unambiguous_requires_still_wires` FAILS, the first passes. Neither row pins the fix alone.\n\nTHE TICKET'S FRAMING NEEDED ONE CORRECTION, made before the fix: this was never silent end to end — the load reports `ambiguous symbol 'Rival' in scope 'User'`. What is silent is the ORDER. The wiring is sub-pass 2; the reporter is `Loader::push_ambiguous_symbol` in the LOAD phase; the loader accumulates errors and continues by design. So sub-pass 3 and the whole load phase resolved that scope's names against a spliced-in stranger — one bad declaration corrupting the scope used to diagnose the rest of itself. That is the defect, and it is second-order, not a missing diagnostic.\n\nNOT DONE: `ResolveResult` still has no `found() -> Option<Symbol>`, and `match { Found(s) => Some(s), _ => None }` is hand-spelled at three remaining sites in `load.rs`. That re-derivation is what let this ladder drift. Left alone deliberately — each site needs its own answer to \"what should an ambiguity do here\", which is the question this ticket answered for exactly one of them.", at: "2026-08-06T14:02:50Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1030", description: some(value: "RUSTLAND: AN AMBIGUOUS `requires` BASE IS READ AS A MISS, AND THE ABSOLUTE RUNG THEN WIRES A SORT NOBODY SELECTED. `ImportPass::at_item`'s `RequiresDecl` arm does `resolve_name_to_scope(kb, name, scope).or_else(|| find_scope_by_name(kb, name))`, and `resolve_name_to_scope` collapses `ResolveResult::Ambiguous` to `None` (`_ => None`). So an ambiguity falls through to the absolute rung instead of ending the ladder — §8.6 says an ambiguity ENDS it, and `ResolveResult::or_else` (`intern.rs`) exists to spell exactly that; its own doc names a site that \"re-derived it WRONG (silently standing an ambiguity down as if it were a miss)\" as the whole point of WI-917. This site re-derives it, in that direction. DRIVEN, not inferred — fixture: a top-level `sort Rival { operation zap }`, plus `pa.Rival { operation aaa }` and `pb.Rival { operation bbb }`, plus `namespace pc { import pa.*; import pb.*; sort User { requires Rival } }`. Measured from `User`'s scope: `resolve_in_scope(\"Rival\")` = `Ambiguous([\"Rival\", \"pa.Rival\", \"pb.Rival\"])`; `resolve_in_scope(\"zap\")` = `Found(Rival.zap)`; `resolve_in_scope(\"aaa\")` = `NotFound`. The top-level homonym's member is reachable and neither actual candidate's is — the absolute rung wired the one sort nobody named. CORRECTION TO THE OBVIOUS FRAMING, and this is why the ticket is not \"a silent phantom parent\": the load DOES fail, with `18:14: ambiguous symbol 'Rival' in scope 'User'`. What is silent is the WIRING and its ORDERING. The reporter is `Loader::push_ambiguous_symbol` — the LOAD phase. The wiring is `scan_definitions` sub-pass 2, which runs first, and the loader accumulates errors and continues by design (\"one bad declaration must not hide the rest of the file's diagnostics\"). So between the two, sub-pass 3's rule-head registration and the entire load phase resolve every name in `User` against a scope with an unrelated sort spliced in: one bad declaration corrupts the scope used to diagnose everything else in it. The parent link also persists in the symbol table across `load_incremental` re-scans. WORK: route this ladder through `ResolveResult::or_else` so the ambiguity stands the absolute rung down, as every other ladder in the tree does. ACCEPTANCE — TWO ROWS, and the second is load-bearing: (1) the fixture above, asserting that with the ambiguity present NO parent is wired (`zap` is `NotFound` from `User`); (2) THE CONTROL — an UNAMBIGUOUS `requires` whose base resolves only by qualified/absolute name must still wire its parent. Without row 2 a \"fix\" that merely deletes the `.or_else(find_scope_by_name)` fallback passes row 1 and silently kills every absolute-rung `requires`. Say at the site which test fails when the change is backed out. Note the sibling spelling `match { Found(s) => Some(s), _ => None }` is hand-written at four sites in `load.rs`; `ResolveResult` has `denotes()` and `or_else()` but no `found() -> Option<Symbol>`, which is why the collapse keeps being re-derived. Found by code review of WI-1028. REFERENCE: WI-917; WI-1028; kernel-language.md §8.6."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-06T14:02:51Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1031", author: "user", content: "CLOSED BY MEASUREMENT — THE HOIST WAS WRITTEN AND REVERTED. No perf change landed; what landed is a doc comment at `emit_member_fact` recording the numbers.\n\nMEASURED, release, stdlib load, n=25 (best-of-5 first, then quartiles when the first pair looked like a regression):\n  without the hoist  min 0.0397s\n  with    the hoist  min 0.0380-0.0392s, median 0.0411-0.0415s over two runs\nIndistinguishable. The arithmetic agrees and should have been done first: ~750 `format!`s at ~100ns are ~75µs of a 40ms load — 0.4%, well under this measurement's ~10% spread.\n\nTHE REVIEW NOTE THAT MOTIVATED THE TICKET WAS TRUE AND IRRELEVANT. \"Dwarfs every scope-term derivation in the diff combined\" is correct — and both are negligible. A relative comparison between two negligible costs reads as significance; \"dwarfs X\" is only a finding once X is known to matter. Recorded in docs/process/converging.md as the general lesson.\n\nA SECOND REASON NOT TO WANT IT, found by measuring rather than reasoning: the natural hoist is per-SCOPE, and the `MemberKind` map costs 7 `resolve_symbol`s per scope. At ~147 scopes and ~750 members that is MORE string lookups than it removes. A real hoist would have to cache per Loader, which is more machinery for a 0.4% ceiling.\n\nONE THING THE ATTEMPT DID EARN: the exhaustive `KINDS` list panicked on the first stdlib load with \"MemberKind `Enum` is emitted but absent\" — the computed `SortWithBody` arm yields `Enum`, which my list missed. Loud beat silent exactly as intended. Nothing to fix in the reverted code (the `format!` path handled `Enum` fine); the episode is noted because it is the argument for the panic over a fallback, should anyone build this later.\n\nTHE DOC COMMENT IS THE DELIVERABLE: it states the numbers, the 0.4% arithmetic, and the per-scope trap, so the next reader does not re-derive this from an operation count. Filed and closed the same day; had it sat Open it would have read as pending work indefinitely, because the operation count is persuasive and nobody re-measures a backlog item.", at: "2026-08-06T14:03:16Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1031", description: some(value: "RUSTLAND: `emit_member_fact` REDERIVES ITS WHOLE SYMBOL SET PER MEMBER. Per `MemberInfo` fact it does `resolve_symbol(\"anthill.reflect.MemberInfo\")`, three `intern`s (`name`/`kind`/`parent`), a `format!(\"anthill.reflect.MemberKind.{kind_name}\")` HEAP ALLOCATION, and a second `resolve_symbol` on that freshly-built string. Every one of those is loop-invariant across `emit_member_facts_for_items`, which is the sole caller (9 call sites inside it, one per member kind). At ~750 members per stdlib load that is ~750 `format!` allocations and ~1500 string-hash lookups. WORK: hoist into the caller — the `MemberInfo` symbol, the three field symbols, and a `kind_name -> Symbol` table built once (there are ~7 kinds: Constructor, Sort, Operation, Rule, Namespace, Const, plus the `kind` variable arm). The existing `EntityInfoSyms` struct is the shape this codebase already uses for exactly this (`Loader::entity_info_syms`), so mirror it rather than inventing one. WHY IT IS FILED SEPARATELY AND NOT AS PART OF WI-1028: it is entirely outside that ticket's diff, and it is a PERF change, which in this repo means it needs a measurement rather than a green suite — the suite is only the control for \"the facts are identical\", which is the easy half. SIZE IT FIRST: this was found while measuring WI-1028's scope-term derivations, and the reviewer's count says this one call dwarfs every derivation in that diff combined — so take a before/after (`ANTHILL_ITEM_TIMING=1`, or a load-time measurement on the stdlib corpus) and record BOTH numbers, not just the after. If the measured win is immaterial, say so and close it rather than landing a hoist on the strength of an operation count. ACCEPTANCE: the emitted `MemberInfo` facts are byte-identical over the stdlib corpus (assert the fact SET, not just that the load is green — a hoist that dropped a kind would still load); a before/after load-time measurement recorded in the delivery; say at the site which test fails when the change is backed out, and say plainly which pass either way by design. Found by code review of WI-1028. REFERENCE: WI-1028."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-06T14:03:17Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1027", description: some(value: "Refuse a supplier tie at LOAD on the BODY-LESS spec-op path too, or record why its declining arms may not. WI-1012 gave the refusal a load face for the DEFAULTED half of a two-half mechanism: its arm sits inside 'check_apply_iter's 'lookup_spec_op_dispatch(kb, fn_sym).is_none()' block, so it covers exactly the ops eval's 'resolve_carrier_override_by_value' serves. The BODY-LESS half - eval's 'resolve_spec_op_target_by_value', the reader WI-842 built - has no load face, and three arms of the same 'check_apply_iter' hand a STATICALLY CONCRETE carrier to it without refusing: (a) the 'DispatchOutcome::Unique' arm's 'if impl_op_sym != fn_sym && op_has_runnable_body(..)' has NO else, so a host-mapped or builtin-backed impl (WI-876) leaves the call as the spec op; (b) the concrete 'NoCandidates' arm deliberately leaves the call untagged; (c) 'Deferred' with no matching slot. 'DispatchOutcome::Ambiguous' does NOT cover these: it ties PROVISIONS matching the goal, whereas route 1 (the carrier's OWN member) is registered by 'build_sort_ops_table' pass 1 regardless of any provision, so route 1 is invisible to it and 'spec_op_suppliers_for_carrier' can hold two where the provision walk holds one. NOT DRIVEN - this is a structural reading from a WI-1012 review agent, and reaching >=2 there needs route 1 to disagree with the provision count. FIRST WORK IS THEREFORE A FIXTURE, not a fix: build a program that makes one of (a)/(b)/(c) reach two suppliers and confirm it currently answers 'AmbiguousSpecOpDispatch' at the call; if none can, the answer is a comment at each arm saying so, and this ticket closes as recorded rather than fixed. IF IT REPRODUCES: the shared refusal already exists - 'render_suppliers' + 'supplier_tie_repair' + 'TypeError::AmbiguousSpecOpDispatch' - so the fix is one helper called from the WI-444 arm and the reproducing arm(s), all of which already hold the span, carrier and op. Note the repair differs by half and is already typed for it: a body-less op HAS a dispatch slot, so a witness rival is 'SupplierTieRepair::NameableWitness' there, which the defaulted half can never be. ALSO IN SCOPE, from the same review: WI-1012's refusal is keyed per CALL SITE (the span is in 'dedup_key'), so N call sites give N errors; 'AmbiguousEqDispatch' does the declaration-level thing once at the index build. A declaration-level check is NOT simply better here - WI-1010 rejected refusing the declaration pair because 'check_instance_fact_op_signatures' has just certified the binding and WI-859 admitted the same pair as one dictionary written twice - but the reasoning should be written down once rather than re-derived. ACCEPTANCE: either a driven fixture plus the refusal extended to it, or a comment at each of the three arms recording that no tie reaches them and why. REFERENCE: WI-1012; WI-842; WI-843; WI-876; docs/design/058-implementation.md S13."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-08-06T15:54:57Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1032", author: "user", content: "REVIEW CORRECTED TWO CLAIMS IN THIS TICKET'S OWN DESCRIPTION, both measured rather than argued.\n\n(1) The headline shape is NOT WI-859's cell (1 fact, 0 witnesses, 1 self-provider). 'Provider::Fact' is recorded only when a provision BINDS an op ('binding_op_symbol' demands a SymbolKind::Operation value), and a bare 'fact Desc[T = Leaf]' binds a SORT — so both provisions classify as SelfProvider(Leaf), 'record' collapses them, and the group holds ONE: cell (0, 0, 1), which the size gate skips and 'fact_beside_self_provider_is_one_carrier' never sees. The claim that this falsified WI-859's licence is therefore WITHDRAWN; the shape that IS in (1,0,1) is the op-binding pair, which genuinely has a conflict — exactly what the licence covers. The correct framing is sharper: 'Provider::SelfProvider' ALREADY states the missing rule ('Two self-provisions of one spec by one carrier are therefore ONE candidate ... they name one member set'), so the LOAD grouping had it and the dispatch collector did not.\n\n(2) 'a defensive dedup in render_instance_tie has no driver' was FALSE. The shape I tried (one provision binding a second type param, the other omitting it) does not tie — tier 2 picks the ground one. But two provisions binding DIFFERENT extra params ('provides Desc[T = Leaf, A = Int64]' beside 'fact Desc[T = Leaf, B = Int64]') both score +1, tie at equal specificity, and differ in resolved_head_bindings, so the collector cannot collapse them — reproducing '(Leaf, Leaf)' + ValueDirected verbatim WITH the collector dedup in place. So the renderer now dedups names by CANONICAL PROVIDER and a new 'TieRepair::OneProviderTwoProvisions' carries the repair that applies (write the provisions as ONE — not 'keep one', since they may agree and be merged into one view).\n\nALSO ADDED FROM REVIEW: the cross-namespace shape (byte-identical facts hash-cons to one fact only WITHIN a namespace; asserted from a second namespace they are two facts and the call was refused — the retroactive-instance shape WI-431 exists to support) now has its own test; 'check_selection_bindings' reads the SET SIZE and so is a second consumer the soundness argument did not cover; 'wi859_self_provider_candidate_test::a_fact_rivalling_the_carriers_own_member_is_still_refused' passed on BOTH builds because its assertions were substrings common to both messages, and is now tightened to the route rendering. Recorded but not closed: the collector compares the carrier canonically and the binding KEYS raw, where siblings compare by short name; failure direction is safe and no driver exists.", at: "2026-08-06T16:46:42Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1032", description: some(value: "Two provisions that say the same thing are ONE dispatch candidate. MEASURED, two defects from one cause: (a) 'sort Leaf { provides Desc[T = Leaf]; operation describe(x: Leaf) = 7 }' beside a namespace-level 'fact Desc[T = Leaf]' is ONE dictionary written in two places - exactly WI-859's admitted cell (1 fact, 0 witnesses, 1 self-provider) - yet 'collect_provides_candidates' produced TWO structurally identical candidates, they tied at equal specificity in 'pick_most_specific', and the CALL was refused 'ambiguous dispatch ... 2 instances provide Desc (Leaf, Leaf)'. A program with a SINGLE implementation, refused, naming one carrier twice, advising 'pin the carrier through the call's receiver' - a repair that changes nothing since the carrier IS pinned. WI-859's admission rests on the cell 'admitting a group whose conflict, IF IT HAS ONE, is refused by a check that can see it'; a check that refuses the no-conflict half is not what it licensed. (b) the same pair where the fact BINDS an op ('fact Desc[T = Leaf, describe = otherDescribe]') is a GENUINE conflict, and it reached the same wrong rendering - 'Leaf, Leaf' with 'TieRepair::ValueDirected' - which is the exact rendering WI-1012 gave the supplier tie its own variant to AVOID. FIX: dedup structurally-equal candidates in 'collect_provides_candidates'. Sound by construction - a Candidate is (impl_sort, resolved_head_bindings, impl_subst, head_specificity) and every downstream product (the resolved tree node, the subgoals 'requires_chain(impl_sort)' instantiates through 'impl_subst') is a function of exactly those, so two equal candidates resolve identically. NOT first-match: candidates differing in ANY field are all kept and 'pick_most_specific' still refuses among them. The op-binding leg is why (b) collapses too - the candidate loop 'continue's on any binding that is not a spec TYPE param, so a fact's 'describe = otherDescribe' differs in nothing Candidate records; deduping lets it reach the reader that CAN see the conflict, WI-1027's supplier tie, which names both texts by supply route. Corpus-inert: full suite green apart from the two fixed shapes. NOT DONE and deliberately not: a defensive dedup in 'render_instance_tie'. A second same-impl_sort tie shape was attempted (two provisions differing in whether they bind a second TYPE param) and it does NOT tie - tier-2 specificity picks the ground one - so the duplicate-name rendering has no remaining driver and an undriven dedup there would be untested code. Recorded at that site instead. REFERENCE: WI-1027; WI-859; WI-1012; WI-843; docs/design/058-implementation.md S15."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-08-06T17:43:46Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-869", description: some(value: "PER-PROVISION CONDITIONAL PROVISIONS — the per-clause form 058 §3.8 records and §4 folds into the provides-consolidation: `provides X[…] :- goals`, conditions scoped to ONE provision instead of the whole sort. DRIVEN BY A SHIPPED SHAPE (WI-858): `anthill.prelude.Pair` provides PartialEq AND Eq componentwise, and a sort has ONE `requires` chain shared by every provision it makes, so the two cannot be conditioned separately. MEASURED both ways at WI-858's HEAD: with the chain spelled `requires Eq[A], requires Eq[B]` (the shape WI-858 originally prescribed) `Pair[A = Float, B = Int64]` became a LOAD ERROR — Float provides NonEq and WI-835's check_use_site_requires_eq refuses a NonEq carrier at a parameter whose sort requires Eq — i.e. Pair stopped being a general PRODUCT, which is wrong: a pair of anything is a pair (unlike Set/Map, which genuinely need Eq on keys). Shipped instead with `requires PartialEq[A], requires PartialEq[B]`: Pair[Float, …] loads again, the componentwise eq body has exactly the evidence it needs, and PartialEq[Pair] is conditioned EXACTLY right. The residue this ticket owns: `provides Eq[Pair]` rides that same chain, so it over-claims — lawful equality is asserted where only the components' PARTIAL equality holds. The wanted spelling is `provides PartialEq[Pair] :- PartialEq[A], PartialEq[B]` beside `provides Eq[Pair] :- Eq[A], Eq[B]`. NOT A NEW MECHANISM per 058 §3.8: a per-provision chain is a second contributor to the dictionary's PROVIDER HALF, not a new half, and WI-857 settled the layout that makes it land somewhere. WORK: grammar (a per-clause `:- goals` tail on a provision), per-provision chains carried through SortProvidesInfo, check_provider_requires reading the owning clause's chain rather than the sort's, and dict_layout's provider half built from it. ACCEPTANCE: pair.anthill spells the two provisions separately; Pair[Float, Int64] still loads; a use demanding Eq[Pair[Float, Int64]] is REFUSED naming the unmet component condition; wi858_pair_orderings_test::a_pair_of_floats_still_loads keeps its meaning with its trade-note updated; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. REFERENCE: 058 §3.8, §4; design/058-implementation.md §7 (the conditional-provisions paragraph); stdlib/anthill/prelude/pair.anthill's header."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-08-06T19:14:19Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-877", description: some(value: "GIVE `anthill.prelude.Pair` ITS CANONICAL ORDERING — lexicographic fst-then-snd, the order every neighbouring language gives a pair (Haskell Ord (a,b), Rust Ord for (A,B), Python tuples, std::pair). BLOCKED ON WI-876, and that is the whole reason it is a separate ticket. BUILT AND DRIVEN AT WI-858, THEN WITHDRAWN: Pair providing PartialOrd/Ordered itself works end to end — MEASURED Ordered.compare answering -1 / 1-on-tie / 0 with no bracket anywhere, and SortedSet.empty[T = Pair[Int64, Int64]]() sorting to (1,9)(2,1) with no ordering bracket at all. Nothing in the prelude ordered a Pair before, so it is a capability ADDED, not a choice imposed. It was withdrawn because it costs SEVEN operations in pair.anthill where ONE would do: compare carries the lexicographic logic, and max/min/gt/gte/lt/lte are each one line restating 'call compare, check the sign'. Seven-or-nothing — MEASURED, the LOAD check demands max/min (not resolver builtins, so op_backed finds no backing) and EVAL demands the other four (the spec-op builtins are host-scalar-only, so gt(pair(2,1), pair(1,9)) dies 'expected Ordered scalars of matching type, got Entity and Entity' on a program that loaded clean). Six workaround operations in the standard library for a defect that already has a ticket is the wrong trade. WORK, once WI-876 lands: add `requires Ordered[A]`, `requires Ordered[B]`, `provides PartialOrd[Pair]`, `provides Ordered[Pair]` and the single `compare` body to pair.anthill; delete nothing else. NOTE the shared-chain consequence, which WI-869 owns and which should be re-checked here: those two requires clauses sit on the SAME chain as the PartialEq ones, so every Pair nominally demands ordered components — false for Pair[Term, Term] and unenforced (no use-site check exists for Ordered, unlike Eq's). MEASURED harmless (the slots go unresolved and nothing reads them) but it is a second over-claim beside Eq[Pair]'s. ALSO NOTE what shipping this re-opens: with Pair self-providing, a program declaring a RIVAL pair ordering cannot reach the canonical one — the bare goal is ambiguous and [Ordered = Pair] is refused because Pair is a CONCRETE provider (058 §3.5 check 3, the value decides). Rung 2a (WI-861) closes that with no edit to pair.anthill, so this ticket does not need it but should assert the interaction. ACCEPTANCE: pair.anthill gains the ordering with ONLY compare; a bracket-less Ordered.compare and a bracket-less SortedSet over pairs both work; the full inherited surface (gt/gte/lt/lte/max/min) works with NO further members; wi858_pair_orderings_test's two program-declared orderings still coexist and still tie loudly at a bracket-less compare; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. REFERENCE: stdlib/anthill/prelude/pair.anthill's header; design/058-implementation.md §9; WI-876."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-876", "WI-869"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-08-06T19:14:22Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1033", description: some(value: "MAKE A PROVISION'S CONDITIONS FACT-OBSERVABLE, so the fact path stops reading a conditional provision as unconditional. WI-869 delivered per-provision conditions (`provides X[...] :- goals`, 058 §3.8) but recorded them as a loader-written KB record (`KnowledgeBase::provision_conditions`) with exactly ONE reader: `typing::provider_dict_chain`. So the conditions bind on the DISPATCH/dictionary path and nowhere else. MEASURED consequences: `check_provider_requires` walks SortProvidesInfo FACTS and never sees a condition, so at load `anthill.prelude.Pair` still asserts an unconditional `provides Eq[Pair]`; the reflect `provides(?A, ?S) :- SortProvidesInfo(sort_ref: ?A, spec: ?S)` rule in stdlib/anthill/reflect/typing.anthill reads the same unconditional claim; and `Set[T = Pair[Float, Int64]]` still LOADS (pinned by wi858_pair_orderings_test::a_pair_of_floats_loads_and_that_has_a_recorded_cost). NOTE the second half of that last one is a DIFFERENT gap and not this ticket's: there is no positive use-site check for `requires Eq` at all — MEASURED, `Set[T = <a sort providing nothing>]` loads clean too, and the only written-site refusal is the NonEq one. WORK: a new reflect entity `ProvidesConditionInfo(sort_ref, provided, condition)` — one fact per condition, leaving SortProvidesInfo BYTE-IDENTICAL so no existing reader or reflect rule is touched (SortProvidesInfo's field list is hard-coded in four places: stdlib/anthill/reflect/reflect.anthill, kb/load.rs x2, kb/eq_derive.rs, which is the real reason not to widen it — NOT the false 'named args match as a set' claim WI-869's first cut wrote and then corrected); emit it beside the SortProvidesInfo fact in `load_provides_clause`; `provider_dict_chain` reads facts instead of the side table; `check_provider_requires` consults the owning provision's conditions. ACCEPTANCE: a reflect query over a conditioned provision can read its conditions; `check_provider_requires` refuses a provision whose own conditions cannot hold; the side table and `ProvisionConditions` retire; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. REFERENCE: docs/design/058-implementation.md §16; kb/mod.rs's `ProvisionConditions` doc, which states this scope boundary."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-869"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-08-06T20:11:07Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-870", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. 058 §3.3 composition is wired: an InstanceSelection is now (spec, witness, slots), where slots is what the value bracket bound on the WITNESS own named requirement slots — recursively, so W[S = X[Inner = Y]] composes to any depth. resolve_inner takes a slot_pin for the sub-goal it is resolving; step 0 stays exclusive by construction (a spec key answers the goal the call made, a value slot binding answers a sub-goal of the provider it names). Keyed POSITIONALLY by dictionary-chain index, because 058 own example declares TWO slots of ONE spec whose goals are identical after substitution; the index has one owner (dict_chain_index_of_named_slot) which states the identity — declaration order = SortRequiresInfo fact order = provider_dict_chain prefix — and VERIFIES it, refusing rather than mis-pinning, because a wrong pin computes a wrong answer silently.\n\nTHE HALF THE TICKET DID NOT NAME, and 058 §5 writes it on its own second line: there are TWO producers of a selection, and the σ-read one (selections_from_slot_bindings, WI-844) took sort_functor_of and kept the BASE exactly as selection_witness_sym did. A named slot IS a type parameter (§3.4), so SortedSet.empty[T = …, O = ListOrd[OE = LexFst]]() puts the nested binding in the value TYPE and every later bracket-less call carries it as an ARGUMENT. Fixing only the bracket would have re-dropped the pin one call later. Both producers now read one function; backing that half out alone fails exactly one test.\n\nTWO VALIDATION DECISIONS, each with an exclusive control. (1) Only a NAMED requirement slot becomes a selection — a witness plain parameters share the same bracket, so LexFst[A = Int64] binds a type and selects nothing. (2) §3.5 CHECK 3 IS NOT A SUB-SLOT BUSINESS: it refuses naming a CONCRETE provider because at a call site the argument value already directs dispatch, and a witness requirement slot has no value — it is a dictionary slot the typer resolves. Driven with the identical name in both positions: [Ordered = Pair] is refused, [Ordered = LexFst[OA = Pair]] runs; applying check 3 to the sub-slot fails that arm and nothing else. Check 1 IS shared (check_witness_provides_spec, one owner two callers), so the split is structural, not textual; the binding-precise half stays with the resolution and is worded in the AUTHOR vocabulary (the call bound slot OA of W to X) because the outer wording would send them to the bracket KEY.\n\nA GUARD BUILT THEN DELETED, argument kept at slot_pin_at: a binding for a slot this dispatch does not answer (WI-869 Unavailable) is unreachable — a binder is minted only for a sort-level requires, and a sort-level slot conditions EVERY provision, so strict_mask answers true for exactly the slots an author can write. Untestable check logic replaced by a stated invariant.\n\nMEASURED. Baseline: with three Ordered[Int64] suppliers, [Ordered = LexFst] and [Ordered = LexFst[OA = Descending, OB = Ascending]] produced the IDENTICAL SubGoal tie — the diagnostic advertising the repair that did not work. Back-out matrix, one edit at a time: resolve_inner routing → 7 fail; bracket producer → the same 7; σ-read nested read → 1; slot-flavoured wording → 2; check 3 applied to sub-slots → 1. Three arms pass either way by design and say so at their site. wi858 a_named_slot_bound_in_a_bracket_value_steers_nothing flipped to a positive assertion (renamed …steers_its_sub_goal). Full workspace green: 29 binaries, 4276 tests, 0 failed. Corpus-inert — slots is empty for every bare witness. Docs: proposal §3.3/§5 + Status, design/058-implementation.md §7 and new §18.", at: "2026-08-06T21:14:53Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-870", description: some(value: "A NAMED REQUIREMENT SLOT BOUND IN A BRACKET VALUE IS VALIDATED AND THEN DROPPED — 058 §3.3's composition leg, DRIVEN for the first time at WI-858 and found unwired. §3.3 states the rule: pinning does not reach into the resolution tree, so steering a witness's own sub-goal is written by binding the witness's NAMED slot in the key's VALUE position — `fold[Monoid = ListM[O = MyEq]]` — and 058-implementation.md §7 recorded the leg as the one composition never measured. MEASURED at HEAD with the shipped `PairByFst` (whose element slots ARE named, `requires OA: Ordered[A]`, `requires OB: Ordered[B]`), two arms: (1) THE KEY IS CHECKED — `Ordered.compare[Ordered = PairByFst[NoSuchSlot = X]](…)` is refused, 'anthill.prelude.PairByFst has no type parameter named NoSuchSlot — it declares type parameter(s) A, B, OA, OB', so the value's bracket list is parsed and validated against the witness; (2) THE VALUE STEERS NOTHING — `[Ordered = PairByFst[OA = PairBySnd]]`, a NONSENSE binding (PairBySnd provides no Ordered[Int64] at all), LOADS CLEAN and computes the unbound answer (-1). A SILENT DROP, against the repo's loud-over-silent principle. Root cause located, not guessed: `selection_witness_sym` (kb/typing.rs) reads `sort_functor_of_view` — the BASE identifies the witness, with the comment already noting 'a witness may carry type arguments, §4.5' — and `seed_op_type_args` pushes one spec-keyed InstanceSelection from that base; the arguments reach the type-parameter half and never become a selection for the SUB-GOAL, whose resolution runs with `selected: &[]` by design (resolve_inner's `at_call_goal = stack.is_empty()` gate). WHY IT IS A DEFECT AND NOT A GAP: `TieRepair::SubGoal` PRINTS this exact spelling as the repair — 'give the conditional provider a NAMED requirement slot and bind it in the value position (f[Spec = W[Slot = Chosen]])' — so an author who follows the diagnostic gets the identical error back. MEASURED: with a rival `Ordered[Int64]` declared, `[Ordered = PairByFst[OA = Descending]]` produces the same SubGoal tie as the bare `[Ordered = PairByFst]` control. WORK: carry the bracket value's own slot bindings into the sub-goal resolution keyed by the SELECTED provider (the WI-857 locality rule's channel is the natural carrier — it already threads `local_provider` one level), validate each against the witness's declared slots the way `check_selection_bindings` validates the outer one, and refuse a binding whose value provides nothing at those bindings. ACCEPTANCE: the nonsense binding is REFUSED naming the slot and the non-providing value; a MEANINGFUL binding resolves the sub-goal tier-3 tie the diagnostic advertises; wi858_pair_orderings_test::a_named_slot_bound_in_a_bracket_value_steers_nothing flips to a positive assertion; wi857's locality pins unchanged; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. REFERENCE: 058 §3.3 last paragraph, §5's `SortedSet.empty[…, O = ListOrd[OE = LexFst]]` line; design/058-implementation.md §7 final paragraph."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-08-06T21:15:03Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-860", description: some(value: "Proposal 058 PHASE 8b — the DEFAULTS SUBSTRATE as reflect relations (058 §3.6; the proposal-035 variance pattern, zero new grammar). WORK: (a) a reflect-layer home (sibling section of reflect/typing.anthill, or reflect/instance.anthill): entity DefaultProvider(spec: Symbol, provider: Symbol); rules self_provides(?C, ?S) and default_provider(?S, ?C, ?W) — declared ∪ inferred, consumers read the derived relation only; the declared row derives the CARRIER from the provider's own provision, so CONDITIONALITY COMPOSES FREE (a conditional provision provides only where its chain discharges — ListOrd is default for List[E] exactly where Ordered[E] resolves; assert this as a test). (b) the one_default LOAD check — any two answers coincide — comparing rows at UNIFIABLE carriers: a ground default beside a parametric one for one family is REFUSED (layering defaults by specificity is a boundary-3-style widening needing its own measurement; pin the refusal). NO-DISPLACEMENT NEEDS NO CODE — the inferred row plus one_default refuse an explicit row against a self-providing carrier; assert it as a test, do not write a rule. (c) a DefaultProvider whose provider does not provide the spec is refused (058 §3.5 check 1 re-read as a load check); the provider field is a sort reference in a fact argument (proven surface: fact Covariant(sort: List, param: T), proposal 035). Materialize into a Rust index (the EqDispatchIndex pattern) — the relations are the authority, the hot path reads the index. NOTHING CONSUMES the index yet: rung 2a is phase 8c; this phase is the relations plus the load checks, independently green. ACCEPTANCE: the relations load; one_default refuses a second row naming both declarations; the displacement program is refused BY one_default with the inferred row named; the unifiable ground+parametric pair is refused; the check-1 misuse is refused; the ListOrd conditional-default row loads and is vacuously inert while no rival exists; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. REFERENCE: 058 §3.6; design/058-implementation.md §6."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-859"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-06T22:52:58Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-860", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. 058 §3.6's relations live in `stdlib/anthill/reflect/typing.anthill` beside the variance facts they copy — `entity DefaultProvider(spec, provider)`, `provision_carrier/3`, `self_provides/2`, `default_provider/3` (declared ∪ inferred). `one_default` is NOT the constraint the proposal sketched: it compares rows at OVERLAPPING carriers, a question about the row SET and about term overlap that an equality goal over two answers cannot ask, so it is a Rust load check (`kb/defaults.rs`) beside the materialized `DefaultProviderIndex`. Nothing consumes the index — rung 2a is 8c — so every acceptance drives the RELATION or a REFUSAL, never a dispatch.\n\nSELF_PROVIDES IS A COMPARISON, NOT A SECOND COPY. The rule reads a builtin `dispatch_carrier(?P, ?S, ?C)` over `witness_dispatch_carrier_value` — the same owner `provision_supplier` (dispatch) and the WI-838/859 grouping (load) ask — then asks whether that answer IS the provider, both sides normalized through `extract_sort_ref`. So it inherits NONE of the grouping's coherence policy, which is what WI-859's parting instruction required, and cannot drift from the classifier by construction.\n\nTHE FORK WI-859 LEFT, DECIDED: a carrier owning no member DOES self-provide. `self_provides` is over PROVISIONS — who supplies the dictionary — and a carrier whose spec ops all default supplies one made of those defaults. Gating on `carrier_own_op` would make a default row appear and disappear as members are added, and measured, would silently drop no-displacement for exactly the memberless carriers.\n\nONE OWNER HAD TO BECOME CARRIER-NEUTRAL, AND TWO WRONG CUTS BOTH LOADED CLEAN. A `SortProvidesInfo.spec` reads as a `TermId` through `fact_head_named_args` (load) and as a `Value::Node` OCCURRENCE when the resolver matches the same fact (SLD). Cut 1 gated on `Value::Term`: 93 provisions in, ZERO rows out. Cut 2 routed through `unwrap_spec_view_value`, which drops any binding it cannot read as a `TermId`: EVERY WITNESS IN THE TREE classified as a self-provider. Both readings were internally consistent and neither touched a load. The relation-vs-index AGREEMENT TEST is what showed each — it resolves `default_provider(?S,?C,?W)` over the stdlib plus the Rust bindings (90 rows) and compares to the index as SETS. Two derivations of one relation is the WI-838 shape; that test is what keeps them one relation.\n\nTHE PROPOSAL'S OWN WORDING WAS WRONG AND IS CORRECTED. §3.6 said conditionality composes free because the rule joins the mark with `provides`, which holds only where the chain discharges. The reflect `provides` relation holds UNCONDITIONALLY — WI-1033 says so outright — so `default_provider` answers at `Box[T = E]` for every E. What actually composes is narrower and sufficient: the row carries the carrier AS WRITTEN, so a default can only be consulted for a candidate that already resolved. Proposal amended.\n\nTHE REVIEW FOUND THREE DEFECTS, NOT TIDYING. (1) One wording served three collision shapes and was WRONG for the third: it announced \\\"carrier 'Box[T = Int64]' has 2 default providers\\\" where there are two carriers, and advised a repair already satisfied — `TieRepair`'s exact failure mode, with WI-1012's test hole too (the test checked only that two names appeared). Now `DefaultCollision` with one decision site, decided on the RENDERED carrier so arm and text agree by construction; each arm pinned; collapsing the enum fails 4 tests. (2) `rule DefaultProvider(…) :- …` was a SILENT SKIP — it loads, the relation answers from it, the index and both checks never see it. Measured, then refused as `DerivedDefaultProvider`. (3) The overlap walk was a sixth hand-rolled short-name lookup, label-only, while the SIBLING function had been given the identity-first rung in this same change; `carrier_views_overlap` moved into `typing.rs` and is enrolled in `binding_for_param`'s list. Also: the dedup was coarser than the relation (overlap, not identity) and silently folded a provider's two genuine provisions into one row; and the richer half of both diagnostics sat in `format_at`, which nothing reaches for a post-load pass — measured by running the CLI.\n\nMEASURED CARRIER SHAPES: bare name (`Ref(S)` and the nullary `Fn{S}` both occur) or `Fn{S, named}`. None SortView-wrapped, none with positional args — so no wrapper is peeled and no positional compare exists.\n\nBACK-OUTS, each re-measured after the review: inferred leg → 4 fail; overlap→base-equality → 1; collision enum collapsed → 4; derived-mark refusal → 1; the new `Expr::Apply` display arm → 1 of 2307. Full workspace green (29 binaries, 4288 tests).", at: "2026-08-06T22:53:01Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1026", description: some(value: "The SLD goal path consults none of the spec-op supply routes, so a rule body that names a defaulted spec op DIRECTLY honours neither WI-1010 nor WI-1012. MEASURED during WI-1012 on its own fixture, three spellings x two supply shapes, all silent, none refused: with ONE supplier (the fact route, 'fact Desc[T = Leaf, describe = leafDescribe]', which WI-1010 says must beat the default) 'rule answer(?r) :- Desc.describe(leaf(), ?r)' answers 1 (the DEFAULT - the written binding is invisible), 'describe(leaf(), ?r)' answers [] (nothing at all), 'leaf().describe(?r)' answers 1; with TWO suppliers (own member 7 + fact binding 9) the same three answer 1, [] and 7 (route 1, first match) - three different silent answers for one ambiguous program, where the operation-body path is refused at LOAD. CONTROL, same program, tie reached through an operation body: 7 with one supplier and a load refusal with two, so the defect is the PATH and not the fixture. WHY IT IS NOT WI-1012's: both WI-444 sites (the typer's 'check_apply_iter' block and eval's 'resolve_carrier_override_by_value') sit on the CALL path; 'type_rule_bodies' reaches 'check_apply_iter' only for a DOT ('dispatch_dots_in_occ'), and the dot case resolves the receiver's own member directly, never consulting 'carrier_override_suppliers'. So this is pre-existing and predates both tickets. WORK: decide per spelling. The bare-goal '[]' is likely a separate resolution failure and should be diagnosed first (it answers nothing even with a single unambiguous supplier). For the qualified and dot spellings, the question is whether the SLD goal path should consult 'carrier_override_suppliers' at all, or whether an operation call in a rule body should route through the same reader the eval bridge uses - measure which sites resolve these goals before choosing. BLAST RADIUS IS REQUIRED FIRST: changing what a rule-body goal dispatches to changes program answers, so instrument old-vs-new over the corpus the way WI-1010 did before any arm is written. ACCEPTANCE: the table above re-measured with every row either answering 7 (the supplied impl) or refusing, and a stated reason for any row deliberately left as is. REFERENCE: WI-1012; WI-1010; WI-444; WI-431; docs/design/058-implementation.md S13; the measured table is in the header of wi1012_static_supplier_tie_test.rs."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-07T03:09:29Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1035", description: some(value: "A DOT-spelled call on a defaulted spec op never consults the supplier set, so a 2-supplier TIE is silently first-match — on BOTH the rule-body and the operation-body paths, which is what makes this WI-1012's gap and not WI-1026's. MEASURED on WI-1010's fixture with two suppliers (the carrier's own member = 7 beside 'fact Desc[T = Leaf, describe = otherDescribe]' = 9): 'rule answer(?r) :- leaf().describe(?r)' answers 7, and 'operation probe() -> Int64 = leaf().describe()' answers 7 as well, while the QUALIFIED spelling of the same tie is REFUSED AT LOAD on both paths. Both arms are pinned by WI-1026's 'a_dot_spelled_operation_body_is_silent_on_the_same_tie' — asserting BOTH is the point, since a test on the rule-body arm alone would pass equally if this were a rule-body defect and would license closing it in the wrong place. MECHANISM: the dot resolves 'describe' on receiver sort 'Leaf' BY NAME to the carrier's own member, so 'check_apply_iter' is reached with fn_sym = Leaf.describe — not a spec op at all — and the WI-444 block (which is what compares 'carrier_override_suppliers') is never entered. The rival supplier is invisible because nothing ever asked which spec the member is backing. This violates 058 S3.7 verbatim ('a read that SELECTS one goes loud on the second candidate - NEVER FIRST-MATCH'). WHY IT IS NOT INLINE: the fix is in DOT MEMBER RESOLUTION, which is the WI-907/908/914 name ladder, and it changes operation bodies too — so it needs its own corpus blast-radius measurement over every dot in the tree, not WI-1026's rule-body-only one. WORK: at the point the dot resolves a member on the receiver's sort, ask whether that member backs a spec the carrier provides and whether that spec op has RIVAL suppliers for this carrier; if so, raise the WI-1012 refusal ('supplier_tie_error') rather than taking the member. ACCEPTANCE: both arms of the WI-1026 test above flip from answering 7 to a load refusal, with a stated blast radius over the corpus. REFERENCE: WI-1026; WI-1012; WI-1010; WI-444; docs/design/058-implementation.md S3.7."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-07T05:22:33Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1038", description: some(value: "A DOT on an ABSTRACT-SPEC receiver still takes the receiver sort's own member silently, where the QUALIFIED spelling of the same program is refused at the CALL. MEASURED on a fixture WI-1035 ships as its WI-608 gate control (wi1035_dot_member_supplier_tie_test::an_abstract_spec_receiver_is_not_refused): a spec 'Coll' with no entity of its own, provided by 'Leaf', declaring its own 'describe' member (7) beside 'fact Desc[T = Coll, describe = otherDescribe]' (9). With 'operation viaSpec(c: Coll) -> Int64 = c.describe()' the program loads clean and answers 7; with 'Desc.describe(c)' it loads clean and the CALL returns EvalError::AmbiguousSpecOpDispatch naming carrier 'Coll' and both routes. Same two texts, two spellings, one silent. UNCHANGED BY WI-1035 and deliberately so: WI-608 says an abstract-spec receiver is not a static pin (its runtime value is some concrete provider), so WI-1035's guard returns early there rather than overturn a delivered decision as a side effect - that early return is what its control test drives, and dropping it refuses the fixture AT LOAD, which is the wrong half of the answer. WHY IT IS NOT INLINE: the fix is not another guard. The dot must REACH eval's value-directed reader, which means synthesizing the SPEC op instead of the member for this case - the reroute WI-1035's function doc argues against taking wholesale (it re-types every 'xs.map(f)' in the tree through a spec signature and req_insertion). A reroute NARROWED to the tied abstract case would have zero corpus blast radius by WI-1035's own count, but it is a special case layered on the dot ladder and needs its own altitude decision plus a measurement of what a spec-signature re-type does to the abstract-receiver population, which WI-1035 did not take. WORK: decide between (a) narrowing the reroute to a tie-detected abstract receiver, and (b) making eval's value-directed reader reachable from a statically-pinned member call, then drive both spellings of the fixture above to the SAME verdict. ACCEPTANCE: the two spellings agree, with the agreement asserted from both ends in one test, and a stated blast radius over the abstract-receiver population. REFERENCE: WI-1035; WI-1012; WI-608; docs/design/058-implementation.md S21."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-07T06:12:35Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1039", description: some(value: "A rule-body reflect form's INTERIOR still carries no source span, so a diagnostic raised on a NESTED dot renders '1:1' - a wrong location, not a missing one. WI-1035 closed the ROOT: 'build_body_atom_occurrence_inner' falls back to 'materialize_from_handle' for entity and reflect-form atoms, spans there come from 'kb.term_span', and a rule-body term has no entry, so 'respan_root' now stamps the atom with the parse span the loader is already holding. That reach is the root ONLY - the loader's native walk never descends into a reflect form, so no caller holds a parse span for what is inside one. MEASURED as the shape that reaches it: the receiver of a 'dot_apply' is itself materialized, so the INNER dot of a chained 'a.b().c()' in a rule body keeps SourceSpan{source: SourceId(0), span: 0..0}, which is what WI-1035's own refusal rendered as '1:1' before the root fix. Every rule-body diagnostic keyed on such a node is affected, not only the supplier tie: 'DotDispatchNoMatch', the WI-602 value-precondition refutation, and any type error inside the dispatched form. 'build_body_atom_occurrence''s doc has recorded this loss since WI-246 ('rule-body terms get no term_spans entry, so materialize gave them empty_span') and names 'narrowing these fallbacks ... native entities / structural reflect patterns' as later work WITH NO TICKET - WI-246 is delivered, so nothing owns it. NOT 'create_occurrence': 'convert_term' is hash-consed and 'term_spans' is first-write-wins, so a rule-body term interning equal to another site's term would either be denied its own span or poison that site's - the exact hazard WI-458 gave rule HEADS their own 'rule_head_span' side-table to avoid. A span is per-OCCURRENCE; only the occurrence tree can hold a per-site answer. WORK: either build reflect-form occurrences NATIVELY in 'build_body_atom_occurrence_inner' (the narrowing WI-246 names, which also fixes the entity-arm var-sharing constraint that forced the fallback), or thread a parse-span map through 'materialize_from_handle' so each materialized node can be stamped. ACCEPTANCE: a diagnostic raised on the inner dot of a chained rule-body call reports the inner dot's own line:col, asserted against the offset the needle really has in the fixture (the weaker 'is it numeric / does it differ' assertions both pass on '1:1' - MEASURED in WI-1035). REFERENCE: WI-1035; WI-246; WI-458; docs/design/058-implementation.md S21."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-07T06:12:35Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1037", description: some(value: "A rule body reaching a supplied spec-op implementation whose PARENT SORT declares `requires` still runs the spec's DEFAULT. INDEPENDENTLY DELIVERABLE — the three-part fix below is entirely inside `reduce_op_value`, and the bridge already resolves the impl's own `requires` chain (WI-300 Tier B). WI-1040 (the relational `requires`) is needed only for the narrower case where the ENCLOSING RULE has its own requirement to forward; see `docs/design/requirement-channel.md` §7.\n\nTHE SITE: WI-1026 made a rule-body call on a defaulted spec op honour the typer's pin (`NodeOccurrence::classified_apply_target`, read by `reduce_op_value`), but that reader answers for `CallClass::PinNow` ONLY. `classify_pin_or_apply_within` writes `ConcreteApplyWithin` instead whenever `sort_reads_requirement_slots(impl_sort)` — exactly when the supplied implementation's own sort has `requires` — and that variant means the callee needs a dispatch dictionary in its frame. `reduce_op_value` folds STRUCTURALLY with no frame, so redirecting to it would inline a body whose `requires` slot nothing filled. WHY THE SHARED READER USED TO NAME IT ANYWAY: eval's copy (the one WI-1026 lifted) did list `ConcreteApplyWithin`, but eval's `Expr::Apply` arm matches that variant THREE ARMS EARLIER and routes it to `start_apply_same_sort`, which installs the dispatch_dict — so the branch was DEAD in eval and had never been exercised. It is not dead for the resolver, which calls the reader unconditionally. WI-1026 narrowed the reader to PinNow and recorded this gap rather than shipping a body run without its dictionary.\n\nTWO CORRECTIONS to this ticket's original WORK sentence, both established by reading the code 2026-08-07; the original plan would have shipped a WRONG ANSWER.\n\n(1) \"Let the depth-0 eval bridge take it\" is NOT sufficient and as written is HARMFUL. `bridge_op_to_eval` is handed `op`, and `reduce_op_value` computes `op = occ.classified_apply_target().unwrap_or(functor)` — which for `ConcreteApplyWithin` is `None`, so `op` is the SPEC op. The bridge entry runs the symbol it is given: `call_op_bridged` -> `invoke_op_with_requirements` (`eval/mod.rs:904`) pushes `cached_operation_body(sym)` straight into a frame, and the WI-444 value-directed override (`resolve_carrier_override_by_value`) lives in `dispatch_resolved_operation`, the IN-BODY dispatch path, NOT the entry. So routing to the bridge unchanged would run the spec's DEFAULT inside a frame with real dictionaries installed — today's wrong answer, upgraded to a definite one. The fix must redirect the callee to `ConcreteApplyWithin.fn_target_sym` BEFORE computing `params` and calling the bridge; `resolve_bridge_requirements` then keys on the IMPL's parent chain, which is precisely the `requires` that made the classification `ConcreteApplyWithin`.\n\n(2) \"The bridge already residualizes when a body needs a dict it cannot supply (WI-625 gap 3)\" describes PRE-Tier-B behaviour. Gap 3 was CLOSED by WI-300 Tier B: `call_op_bridged` (`eval/mod.rs:584`) resolves REAL dictionaries at the concrete argument types via `resolve_bridge_requirements` (`typing.rs:14667`), and residualize is only the fallback for `Unresolvable` / `Ambiguous`. So the reachable outcome here is a correct ANSWER, not merely \"delays instead of wrong\" — the acceptance below is stronger than originally filed.\n\nMEASURE FIRST — this ticket asserts two different current behaviours and BOTH can be true, for different fixtures; establish which before writing the arm. A DEFAULTED spec op folds its default (wrong value). An ABSTRACT spec op hits `op_body_node -> None -> return v` at `resolve.rs:6136`, which returns BEFORE the bridge arm, so it never bridges at all and the goal delays (answers `[]`). If the fix routes through the bridge, that early return is a SECOND site to change, not just the `match reduced` arm.\n\nTHE FIX, three parts, all in `reduce_op_value`: (a) redirect the callee to `ConcreteApplyWithin.fn_target_sym` before computing `params`; (b) decline the structural fold for that class rather than folding the spec default; (c) reach the bridge, including from the abstract-op early return at `resolve.rs:6136`. `resolve_bridge_requirements` then builds the impl's own chain at the concrete argument types. The outcome that becomes unreachable is the current one: silently running the spec's default. Only when the ENCLOSING RULE declares a requirement to forward does this need WI-1040's supply path, and that case can follow separately.\n\nACCEPTANCE: a rule body whose supplied impl lives in a `requires`-declaring sort answers THAT IMPL (delay only when the carrier is genuinely unbound), never the spec default; the abstract-spec-op fixture is measured and its behaviour stated at the test site; the WI-1026 tests still pass. Each test says which case fails when the change is backed out — a test that passes both with and without the fix measures nothing here, since the default and the impl must return DIFFERENT values for the assertion to have any content.\n\nREFERENCE: WI-1040; `docs/design/requirement-channel.md`; WI-1026; WI-444; WI-829; `NodeOccurrence::classified_apply_target` carries the reasoning at the site."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-070", description: some(value: "Implement the `Branch` effect — value-level non-determinism in eval. WI-050 stubs it as UnhandledEffect; the runtime is still a placeholder (`eval/effects.rs:42`, `:438` — WI-075 delivered `push_choice`, the SUBSTRATE, not the effect).\n\nSPEC: `docs/proposals/027.2-branch-from-streams.md` (Draft), which SUPERSEDES this ticket's original mechanism sketch. Kept for the record, now superseded: \"(2) an operation returning multiple values pushes alternative frames; the activation stack forks. (3) at resolution points one branch proceeds; on fail() the saved alternative resumes.\" 027.2's shape instead:\n\n(1) `reflect(s: LogicalStream[T]) -> T effects Branch` is the ENTRY POINT — each element of the stream becomes a branch, the empty stream is `fail()`. `branch(a, b)` is `reflect(stream_of(a, b))` and `fail()` is `reflect(empty)`, so the two-way form is the 2-element special case, NOT a separate mechanism. It rides the existing lazy-choice substrate (`push_choice` / `SearchStream`, proposal 033, WI-075 Verified) — n-ary entry point, no new choice substrate.\n\n(2) SOLVERS ARE `reify` HANDLERS, AS FIRST-CLASS VALUES: `all` / `one` (strict iota) / `once` / `beam` (= 027.3 `ScoredBranch`, reused not re-specified) / `oracle`, installed via `provide`. A `Branch` with no enclosing solver is a LOUD load/type error — nondeterminism never leaks past a consumer. Users can define their own solver with no grammar change.\n\n(3) THE ONE GENUINELY-NEW RUNTIME PIECE is the eval-to-SLD SWITCH: suspend the concrete evaluator at the goal, drive SLD, and MULTI-SHOT resume the continuation once per solution. Needs `StepOutcome::Suspend` — today `StepOutcome` is `Done | Continue | Deliver` (`eval/eval.rs:27`) — returning from `run()` with the activation stack intact plus a resume entry; the nearest existing shape is `Done`, which differs only in leaving the stack EMPTY. Plus COORDINATED BINDING: the interpreter keeps its suspended stack and the RESOLVER frame (`ResolverFrame`, `kb/resolve.rs`) holds only a resume handle it calls back, so the resolver never embeds eval types. `Clone` on `Frame`/`ActivationStack` is ALREADY DELIVERED (WI-078 Phase A/a, `eval/frame.rs:164`); the snapshot/resume protocol is WI-069.\n\n(4) Surface desugar `match <solver> case <goal>` / `let <goal> = <solver>`, reusing the existing `case` / `let` binders — no new grammar.\n\nWARNING, from WI-455's OWN feedback: 027.2 and 047 both cite WI-455 as the precedent for `StepOutcome::Suspend`. THAT PRECEDENT DOES NOT EXIST. WI-455 built `StepOutcome::Dispatch`, proved it green, then DROPPED it — an OpRef application now resolves in ONE HOP by construction, so there was no chain left to hand back to `run()`. Introduce `Suspend` on its own merits.\n\nSTATE ACROSS BRANCHES: governed by 047 §8 ranking — a resource ranked BELOW `Branch` survives backtracking, ranked ABOVE it is snapshotted and undone via `register_undo`. `External` admits NEITHER rank, so a `Branch` region may not perform it (054; the co-occurrence gate is DELIVERED, WI-701). Read the world before the search, search over tracked state only, write after the solver commits.\n\nREQUIREMENT DICTIONARIES COME FREE IF WI-1040 LANDS FIRST — a SEQUENCING NOTE, deliberately NOT a dependency edge. Under WI-1040 a dictionary is an ordinary term bound in σ, and each branch has its own σ, so multi-shot resume gets per-branch dictionaries with no per-branch copy and no undo machinery (a dictionary is immutable). If Branch lands first, a dictionary inside a branch region simply re-derives per branch — correct, just wasteful. Design: `docs/design/requirement-channel.md` §7.\n\n027.2 OPEN QUESTIONS to settle when picked up: the coordinated-binding handshake and resume-handle ownership (who holds the suspended stack, who re-enters); whether `reflect` takes the cheap `push_choice` path for the common finite-stream case rather than a general shift/reset; the solver `run` signature vs an incremental Observer interface; nested `provide` scope vs the 047 §8 rank table; `one` degeneracy when more than one solution exists (default: strict iota).\n\nOUT OF SCOPE: the pure-stream layer (052 — DELIVERED as WI-714); the scored solver's internals (027.3, used here as the `beam` instance); general resumable effects beyond the Branch slice (WI-069 owns snapshot/resume as its own capability); effect-row discharge typing (WI-329).\n\nACCEPTANCE: `reflect` over a finite stream branches and a solver reifies the region — DRIVE it (run the operation, assert the collected solutions), not \"it loads clean\"; `fail()` backtracks; a body inside a branch RE-ENTERS search (the nesting case that distinguishes this from 052); a `Branch` with no enclosing solver is a loud error; each test says which case fails when the change is backed out. `cargo-test` green via `rustland/scripts/test.sh`."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-050", "WI-060"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-069", description: some(value: "Implement the `Suspension` effect: first-class continuations on the activation stack. WI-050 stubs `Suspension` as UnhandledEffect. Real implementation: (1) `SuspensionHandle` wraps a saved snapshot of the activation stack + locals + in-flight `AwaitState`s. (2) `operation suspend(k: Function[T, U]) -> U effects Suspension` — when invoked, snapshot the current stack up to the suspend site, return the `SuspensionHandle` to user code, bail out of `run()`. (3) `operation resume(handle: SuspensionHandle, value: T) -> U` — restore the saved stack, deliver the value to the saved continuation, resume `run()`. Uses the heap-allocated activation stack from WI-060 (CPS eval) directly — no VM/bytecode needed. The `ClosureHandle` Rc refcount (WI-055) already makes snapshot+restore safe. Scope: save/restore protocol, suspend/resume builtins, test of continuation capture and round-trip.\n\nSeparate from `Branch` (WI-070 — the dangling \"WI-TODO\" this description used to carry): Branch is value-level non-determinism, a different shape. But the two SHARE this ticket's substrate, and 027.2 names this one explicitly.\n\nWHAT ALREADY LANDED, so this is smaller than filed: `Clone` on `Frame` / `ActivationStack` — the multi-shot half of the protocol — is DELIVERED (WI-078 Phase A/a, `eval/frame.rs:164`), and its doc says the clone semantics are exactly what a snapshot needs (shares immutable `Rc<NodeOccurrence>` sub-trees, deep-copies the mutable `locals` / `buffered` vectors, so a snapshot does not alias the live frame's evolving bindings). `NodeOccurrence` is likewise already shaped for continuation capture (`kb/node_occurrence.rs:1076`).\n\nWHAT IS STILL MISSING, and it is the same piece 027.2 needs: a `StepOutcome::Suspend` variant. Today `StepOutcome` is `Done | Continue | Deliver` (`eval/eval.rs:27`). The nearest existing shape is `Done`, the variant that EXITS the trampoline; `Suspend` differs from it only in leaving the activation stack INTACT instead of empty.\n\nWARNING, from WI-455's OWN feedback: proposals 027.2 and 047 cite WI-455 as the precedent for `StepOutcome::Suspend`. THAT PRECEDENT DOES NOT EXIST — WI-455 built `StepOutcome::Dispatch`, proved it green, then DROPPED it, because an OpRef application now resolves in ONE HOP by construction and there was no chain left to hand back to `run()`. Introduce `Suspend` on its own merits.\n\nCONSUMER: WI-070 (`Branch`) needs exactly the Branch slice of this — suspend at a goal, drive SLD, multi-shot resume once per solution (027.2 §\"The eval↔SLD switch\"). Landing the general capability here first is the cheaper order, but WI-070 may also cut its own narrow slice; decide when one of them is claimed.\n\nACCEPTANCE (retained): a continuation is captured and round-trips — DRIVE it (suspend inside a running operation, resume it, assert the delivered value), not \"it loads clean\"; a multi-shot resume of one handle runs the continuation twice with independent state; each test says which case fails when the change is backed out. `cargo-test` green via `rustland/scripts/test.sh`."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-050", "WI-060"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Open)
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1040", author: "user", content: "STAGING INVARIANT SETTLED (2026-08-07 design review with user; requirement-channel.md sec 2.1 + reworded sec 4): after the typing pass, RUN TIME PERFORMS NO TYPING JUDGMENTS -- selection is a typing-time judgment; the runtime kernel is six operations over ALREADY-RESOLVED dictionaries: fetch (read carried-type tag, index precompiled row) / bind / copy (crossings) / project / compose (conditional provisions) / check. Consequences for this ticket: (a) X is a TYPING-TIME object; the emitted goal is a COMPILED DISPATCH RECORD extending the delivered guard encoding (spec_base, op, witness_args...) with the output slot ?d, carrying attribution (WI-613) + candidate rows + projection paths; it MENTIONS the witness value vars, so delay wakes by ordinary rotation. (b) groundness of X was never the operative condition -- TAG READABILITY is; typing-time narrowing is by ONE-WAY MATCH (only provider vars bind; WI-824 generalized), which makes sec-9 partial dictionaries forced-not-chosen. (c) a runtime TIE is UNREACHABLE, not refused: overlap is refused at typing/load; two rows for one tag at run time is a loud DEFECT. (d) no runtime sub-search over provides, so WI-628 truncation discipline does not apply. (e) CHECK semantics per WI-860 (two derivations of one relation must agree): on a UNIQUE local row a supplied ?d must UNIFY with it -- disagreement is a LOUD ERROR, never a precedence win; supplied decides ONLY where the row is Unresolvable/Ambiguous (WI-855; the WI-843 named-instance case). THIS CORRECTS ACCEPTANCE (c) OF THIS TICKET: a supplied ?d that differs from re-derivation may PASS only on an undecidable row; on a unique row differing must FAIL loudly. (f) ANCHOR RULE: a require[X] with neither a covered body call nor a typed pattern (?x: T) grounding the spec params is REFUSED at typing (guard tier cannot-be-grounded error extended to the named form ?d = require[Eq[T]]). (g) surface: `require` needs a name-resolution home (kernel vocab beside find_dictionary, load.rs:2345); legal positions are bare goal or RHS of top-level = only, nested occurrences refused loudly. (h) WI-869 unfilled-slot read (sec 9.1) proposed settlement: never-promised = structural HOLE (refuse at read), not-yet-known = unbound VAR (delay) -- two distinct leaves, illegal reads distinguishable by shape. STILL OPEN (channel doc sec 10): record encoding + idempotence-gate migration (today pos_args.len()==1) + un-stripping convert-time type-args for WI-613 attribution; ONE sigma-carrier (eval handles vs structural terms, keyed alike via WI-1019 view, term-to-handle materialization at SLD-to-eval); eval-to-SLD handoff mechanism (prove_rule_predicate has no dictionary parameter); bridge resolve_bridge_requirements unify_types -> projection paths or explicit grandfather.", at: "2026-08-07T08:33:36Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-742", author: "user", content: "PROPOSAL 060 (docs/proposals/060-clause-level-requirements-and-typed-heads.md, Draft 2026-08-07) now owns the LANGUAGE RULE this ticket implements: a type-level declaration in a rule clause compiles at typing time to a GENERATED BODY GOAL that at run time only READS (carried-type tags + precompiled rows) -- never judges. This ticket is instance 3 of that rule (`?x: T` on a relational head -> prepended mode-directed `domain(?x, T)`; instances 1-2 are the delivered requires(X) guard and WI-1040 require[X]). ADDITIONAL CONSEQUENCE beyond the original scope, from the 2026-08-07 design session: the annotation is also an ANCHOR for the requirement channel -- `domain(?x, T)` reads ?x carried tag, so T is grounded by projection from the head argument, and a `require[Spec[T]]` in the same clause compiles against it with NO witness call needed (`p(?x: T, ?y) :- ?d = require[Eq[T]], f(?x, ?y, ?d)` becomes legal; without this ticket such a clause is refused by the anchor rule). See proposal 060 sec 3 and requirement-channel.md sec 5 anchor rule. Sequencing note, not a dependency edge: WI-742 and WI-1040 are independently deliverable; only the combined shape above needs both.", at: "2026-08-07T08:44:08Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1040", author: "user", content: "SURFACE NOW OWNED BY PROPOSAL 060 (docs/proposals/060-clause-level-requirements-and-typed-heads.md, Draft 2026-08-07): the generated-body-goal rule, three spellings, position restriction (bare goal or RHS of top-level =, nested refused), and the anchor rule -- which gains a SECOND anchor via WI-742: `?x: T` on a relational head (its domain(?x, T) goal is the projection source), so `p(?x: T, ?y) :- ?d = require[Eq[T]], f(...)` is legal once WI-742 lands, with no witness call. ENCODING (sec-10 item 1) settled in the 2026-08-07 session, three calls: (a) the output rides as a presence-optional NAMED arg `out: ?d` -- the delivered resolver arm reads only positionals (resolve.rs:5088), so no-out goals take the existing path untouched and acceptance (f) holds STRUCTURALLY; out present iff the dictionary is actually threaded (covered calls or author-named); check-only = no out = skip composition. (b) ONE FORM, ONE OWNER: the sweep always emits the extended form; the guard encoding is retired as a separate shape (requires(X) = the no-out case). No persistence migration exists -- the rewritten form is an in-memory contract between sweep and resolver arm; idempotence gate stays arity-based (1 pos arg = unrewritten). (c) the full spec rides in a TYPE-POSITION channel, never general scope resolution -- convert.rs:2799 strips type-args precisely because a bare `T` as a term arg would hit scope resolution with no binding in a free rule (the stated crucial reason); un-stripping must route the decoration where a bare name is a type Var (WI-849 precedent). ALSO SETTLED: the record stores almost nothing -- candidate rows are the KB provides/sort-ops tables (precompiled, shared, keyed by tag; per-goal narrowing is a coherence CHECK not payload), and carrier/projection indices derive at fire time from (spec, op) via OperationInfo/simp_guard_holds_core -- reads, not judgments. No new node kind. The duplicate-spec-base hard error lifts only when attribution lands on un-stripped specs; keep until then.", at: "2026-08-07T08:44:28Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1040", author: "user", content: "TERMINOLOGY: earlier feedback today said \"tags\"/\"rows\"/\"judges\" -- normalized away in proposal 060 + requirement-channel.md. Read as: run time reads the value FULL CARRIED TYPE (value_type_term -- a stored term, never collapsed to its head symbol; the head constructor selects the table entry, the type arguments flow to the sub-dictionary fetches) and performs NO TYPING OPERATIONS (no inference, no type unification, no instance selection).", at: "2026-08-07T08:59:06Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-742", author: "user", content: "TERMINOLOGY: earlier feedback today said \"carried-type tags\"/\"never judges\" -- normalized in proposal 060. Read as: the generated goal reads the value FULL CARRIED TYPE (value_type_term, a stored term -- never collapsed to a head symbol) and performs no typing operations at run time.", at: "2026-08-07T08:59:09Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-742", author: "user", content: "PROPOSAL 060 sec 2.1 (added 2026-08-07, user-approved): the PARAMETER FORM -- in the head of the predicate a rule DEFINES, `name: Type` introduces a typed clause variable with no `?` sigil (`rule adult(p: Person, age: Int) :- person(p), age_of(p, age), gte(age, 18)`), the operation-parameter notation applied at the rule declaration site per 052; body references are bare, typos stay loud unresolved-name errors. A thin CLASSIFICATION layer on top of this ticket: parsing is unchanged (`name: Type` already parses as named_arg, grammar.js:1309); the converter reclassifies only when the resolved head functor (WI-896) is the rule-defined predicate -- an ENTITY-CONSTRUCTOR head keeps named args untouched, and the discriminator is the resolved category, NEVER case convention (entities are commonly lowercase: `palette`, `parent`). MEASURED 2026-08-07 (textual scan of stdlib/examples/anthill-todo): all 8 named-arg fact/rule heads on lowercase functors in the corpus are entity constructors -- the reclassified population is EMPTY; re-measure loader-verified at the classification site when implementing. Everything else in `name: Type` positions stays a named argument (a sort is a legal argument VALUE, 055). Untyped bare `p(x, y)` stays a loud error -- an unresolved bare name is never an implicit variable. ADJACENT CHEAP WIN, independent of this ticket: the unresolved-name diagnostic in rule clauses should hint \"did you mean `?x`?\" for short lowercase unresolved names.", at: "2026-08-07T09:17:45Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1040", author: "user", content: "CITATION UPDATE (2026-08-07, commit 878e9787 rebased in): 058 renumbered the dictionaries section 3.9 -> 3.10 (new 3.9 = positive lawfulness / use-site discharge). Every \"058 sec 3.9\" in this ticket DESCRIPTION and in earlier feedback today means the new sec 3.10. Also corrected in the docs: \"058 sec 4.2\" never existed -- the rule-bodies-out-of-scope statement is 058 sec 3.3 (\"a bracket in a rule body is refused loudly\"). requirement-channel.md and proposal 060 are swept; the ticket description still carries the old numbers.", at: "2026-08-07T09:51:15Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-883", author: "user", content: "RELATED DESIGN LANDED (2026-08-07, commit 878e9787): 058 NEW sec 3.9 \"Lawfulness derives positively -- the missing use-site discharge\" states the rule this ticket is the check half of: the use-site goal Eq[X] (generally Spec[X]) is discharged POSITIVELY by a provision row or, in a generic context, by the enclosing requires as an assumption (Candidate::Assumption) -- never by absence of a NonEq. This ticket owns the check (both halves it names: a call to an unprovided spec op, and a type written at a requires-carrying parameter). NOT covered by this ticket and currently UNTICKETED: sec 3.9 other half -- the congruent positive derivation (eq_derive asserts only the partial side today; the parametric clause rows `provides Eq[Pair[A,B]] :- Eq[A], Eq[B]` are outside WI-664 delivered fixpoint reach), gated on sec 3.9 two measure-first items (pair.anthill:55 use-site state; map.anthill:11 vacuity note archaeology). Sequencing: the check half reads whatever the positive channel derives, so this ticket is deliverable first with the derivation as follow-on.", at: "2026-08-07T09:51:18Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-743", author: "user", content: "PROPOSAL 060 sec 2.2 (2026-08-07, user-approved) SETTLES open question (1) with a third option subsuming both listed ones, and adds a semantic rule: mode (out) does NOT special-case finite ADTs in the resolver -- `domain(?x, T)` dispatches to a MEMBER RELATION T defines, named `domain` (user-chosen name, over value_of; the goal is that member read through the type, hence one name). DERIVED for an all-nullary closed ADT (the finiteness gate unchanged; declaration order, once each -- open question (2) resolved to that presumption), USER-DEFINABLE for any sort (the Palette wrapper is a hand-written `domain` the language gave no name; absorbed into the sort). The implicit typed-head reading and the explicit citable relation are ONE thing. NEW SEMANTIC RULE, user-confirmed: `domain` is DOMAIN-DEFINING, not a generator hint -- where T defines it BOTH modes read it (mode (in) checks conformance AND membership; mode (out) enumerates); a generate-from-subset / accept-anything-conforming split makes the relation answer depend on call mode and is REFUSED. Derived enum case: membership == conformance, no extra cost. Rule-local narrowing stays an ordinary body goal. A sort with NO `domain` keeps the delay/flounder ladder exactly (the String acceptance case unchanged). ABSTRACT T dispatches through the requirement channel (WI-1040, proposal 060 sec 3 anchor); concrete T pins at typing. No 058 sec 3.10 tension: values enumerate by ordinary SLD choice, instances are never chosen. Acceptance sketch unchanged otherwise; the colouring example in 060 sec 2.2 is the driver (uses the sec 2.1 parameter form).", at: "2026-08-07T09:57:29Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1041", description: some(value: "HARDENING (WI-1039/1038 family; /code-review 2026-08-07 findings 4/8/9/10/11 — each verified LATENT: no suite-reachable trigger today, which is why they are ONE ticket; each is a silent mislocation or panic seam whose first trigger arrives with a future edit while the suite stays green). (1) typing.rs:39992 — type_rule_bodies tags every dispatch error Some(n.span.source) unconditionally; an atom carrying the empty-span sentinel (SourceId(0), 0..0) renders <first-file>:1:1 — a WRONG file, where pre-change behaviour was an honest unlocated offset. SourceId(0) is NOT reserved, it is the first registered file. Trigger: term-built rule bodies (term_body_to_nodes -> materialize_from_handle, which has no span table) whose dispatch walk errors; assert_rule is pub API. FIX: keep None for the sentinel — the sibling push 50 lines up is already Option-correct. (2) typing.rs:39987 — the errors/sources parallelism is guarded only by a debug_assert plus the TRUNCATING sources.resize(errors.len()) spelling; every sibling site (38820/38845/39231/39579) uses the structurally non-truncating while-push. A future push into sources inside the walk silently shifts the pairing in release builds — the exact WI-745 defect the change fixed — with no test observing tag alignment. FIX: the sibling spelling, or Vec<(TypeError, Option<SourceId>)>. (3) node_occurrence.rs:442 — carry_typer_stamps_from holds the src borrow guard across the dst borrow_mut of the SAME RefCell, so self==src panics BorrowMutError; verified unreachable through all three current callers, but the method is pub and the precondition is stated nowhere; the panic fires only post-typer on typed corpora, so unit tests over unstamped nodes stay green. FIX: if std::ptr::eq(self, src) return — one line. (4) load.rs:12121 — parse_span_table walk rests on Term::subterms, which returns EMPTY for Term::ParseAux: everything beneath a ParseAux is silently pruned, an undocumented second miss class beside the doc WHAT IT CANNOT COVER paragraph, through the one variant the walk own goes-stale-in-SILENCE comment says it guards. FIX: state the miss class in that paragraph; descend the payload if cheap, else a loud debug assertion. (5) node_occurrence.rs:4601 — match-branch metadata hardcodes branch_span = empty_span() inside visit_fn with br_tid and the caller span table both IN SCOPE, contradicting the consulted-ahead-of-kb.term_span-for-every-node promise; companion latent defect noted by the verifier: the positional reflect encoding silently DROPS branches. FIX: use the in-scope values; record or fix the branch drop. ACCEPTANCE: each of the five gains a driving test (an embedder-shaped assert_rule fixture for 1; unit tests for 2/3/5) or a stated why-not at the site; each test says which case fails when its guard is backed out; until closed the five sites cite this ticket. REFERENCE: /code-review 2026-08-07; WI-1039; WI-745; WI-1026."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1056", description: some(value: "BUG + CAPABILITY (058; WI-1043 residue, MEASURED not predicted): a rule body is NEVER TYPE-CHECKED, and the two populations that follow from it. (1) THE GENERAL Expr::Apply: call_dispatch_shape admits a dot (WI-282) and a spec-op call of either half (WI-1026 defaulted, WI-1043 body-less); every OTHER rule-body call stays untyped, so an arity error, a type mismatch or an unknown functor in a rule body is found by nobody. (2) THE FAILURES WI-1043 DELIBERATELY DOES NOT REPORT: it types body-less spec-op atoms for their dispatch verdict and reports only the two 058 ties (CallDispatch::reports_every_failure), because reporting the rest refuses the corpus. THE MEASUREMENT, taken at the widening (2026-08-08, stdlib + host bindings + examples/ + anthill-todo/ + anthill-testcases/): 231 newly decided call sites; the failures are 41 leaf errors at 7 sites — 27 DotDispatchNoMatch (a nested dot the per-node tolerated arm swallows, typed as part of its enclosing atom), 8 TypeMismatch, 6 UnknownApplyFunctor — and ZERO dispatch verdicts. The 7 sites, each a program that loads and runs today: stdlib/anthill/prelude/bigint.anthill:39 (?P(sub(?n, 1)) in the induction rule — the literal 1 is Int64 where BigInt is expected); stdlib/anthill/prelude/logical_stream.anthill:73 (splitFirst(?a) = some(pair(?first, ?rest)) — the two sides differ by an effect row E = s.E); examples/webots-modelling/lf1/safety_common.anthill:236,277,278,279 and safety_gps.anthill:154 (entity constructors and dots inside = goals). WORK: decide each of the 7 (a source fix, a typer gap, or a stated exemption), then widen — either the error policy at the body-less shape or the gate to the general Expr::Apply, in that order, since the first is the smaller half of the same question. ACCEPTANCE (each test says which case fails when backed out): a rule body with a genuine arity/type error is REFUSED at load naming it (control: today it loads clean); the 7 corpus sites either load clean for a stated reason or are fixed at their source; the WI-1043 suite stays green; the corpus loads clean at every tier. REFERENCE: WI-1043; WI-1026 (which first narrowed the gate for this reason); WI-282; docs/design/058-implementation.md sec 23."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-08T14:24:01Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1057", description: some(value: "BUG (058; WI-1043 residue, CONFIRMED BY DRIVING + one wrong fix already measured): an implementation supplied ONLY by a WI-431 INSTANCE FACT is unreachable from a RULE BODY, on both spellings, while an operation body reaches it. DRIVEN on the wi1043/wi1035 body-less fixture with the fact as the sole supplier (Leaf declares nothing; fact Desc[T = Leaf, describe = otherDescribe]): 'rule answer(?r) :- Desc.describe(leaf(), ?r)' answers [] and so does 'leaf().describe(?r)', while 'operation probe() = Desc.describe(leaf())' answers 9. Pinned by wi1043_bodyless_rule_body_test::a_fact_route_supplier_is_still_unreachable_from_a_rule_body, which FAILS when this is closed — the ticket comes back there. MECHANISM, two early returns and BOTH on the SPELLED functor's missing body: dispatch_spec_op_cached does not read instance-fact op-bindings (WI-431 inc 2), so no typer pin exists and the implementation is discoverable BY VALUE alone; then functional_relation_arity answers None (op_body_node(f) is none), so the goal f(a…, ?r) never becomes the call f(a…); and reduce_op_value would return before bridge_op_to_eval for the same reason. WI-1043 gave the goal shape the PIN (dispatched_relation_arity) — this route has no pin to give it. THE NAIVE FIX IS WORSE AND WAS MEASURED: admitting a body-less spec op to the functional-relation view by its signature arity and bridging to eval bound ?r to the UN-REDUCED CALL and reported it DEFINITE — a wrong answer where there was a missing one, because the WI-938 hook's 'rule-less BODIED op' gate is what guarantees the rewrite reduces. So the work is to make the goal reach eval's value-directed dispatch WITHOUT rewriting to unify() on a call that may not reduce: either resolve the supplier at the goal (a typer pin from the fact binding, which changes operation bodies too) or a bridge whose failure leaves the goal un-rewritten. ACCEPTANCE (each test says which case fails when backed out): the fact-only fixture answers 9 from a rule body in BOTH spellings (control: today [] in both); a body-less spec-op goal whose args are not ground still delays rather than binding a residual (the trap above, driven); the two-supplier tie still REFUSES at load (WI-1043 suite green); wi1027/wi1035 suites green. REFERENCE: WI-1043; WI-1027; WI-1010; WI-431; WI-938; WI-842 (the eval reader that resolves it by value); docs/design/058-implementation.md sec 23."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1056", author: "user", content: "MEASUREMENT CORRECTED (the ticket's own numbers were taken from a CUMULATIVE 4-tier probe, so every count was inflated ~4x). Re-measured per LOAD with counters at the acting arm: 133 newly decided body-less call sites on a whole-corpus load (stdlib + host bindings + examples/ + anthill-todo/ + anthill-testcases/), 28 of them on stdlib + bindings alone; their failures are 19 LEAF ERRORS — 15 DotDispatchNoMatch, 2 TypeMismatch, 2 UnknownApplyFunctor — and ZERO dispatch verdicts. Reported (i.e. with WI-1043's error policy off) those render as 7 LOAD ERRORS in 4 files, which is the list the ticket names and which is unchanged: stdlib bigint.anthill:39, stdlib logical_stream.anthill:73, examples/webots-modelling/lf1/safety_common.anthill:236/277/278/279, safety_gps.anthill:154. Read 133/19 as the population and 7-in-4-files as the work.", at: "2026-08-08T12:30:58Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1043", description: some(value: "BUG (058; /code-review 2026-08-07 finding 2, CONFIRMED BY DRIVING) + SPEC: a rule-body call NAMING a body-less spec op is never typed — the program loads CLEAN and answers [] silently, while the spec claimed REFUSED. DRIVEN: a body-less spec op with TWO suppliers and only rule answer(?r) :- Desc.describe(leaf(), ?r) loads clean and answers []; the refusal the spec cell reported came from the wi1027 fixture probe OPERATION (the load refusal locates at the probe operation line, 19:32) — the exact fixture-contamination class 058-implementation sec 20 itself diagnosed for its own earlier numbers. MECHANISM: expr_needs_call_dispatch requires op_has_runnable_body (typing.rs:41506), so a body-less named call in a rule body never routes to check_apply_iter, and no other refusal path exists; the typing.rs:41398 residue note cites WI-1027, which is DELIVERED — nothing owned the hole until this ticket. THE SPEC CELLS ARE CORRECTED in the same commit that fires this ticket, each citing this number: 058-implementation sec 21 row body-less/instance-fact/rule-body; kernel-language sec Instance coherence and sec Operation override; the wi1035_dot_member_supplier_tie_test.rs header table. WORK: give the body-less rule-body NAMED call the load face WI-1027 gave the operation body — widen the WI-1026 arm (expr_needs_call_dispatch + check_apply_iter) to the body-less half, so ONE supplier pins and dispatches at the call and TWO suppliers refuse at load naming both; or record at the arm, with this number, why it may not. ALSO record the GENERAL-Apply narrowing at the dispatch_calls_in_occ site (rule-body calls that are neither dot, defaulted, nor body-less spec ops stay untyped) as this ticket residue or its own spun-off number. DEPENDS on WI-1042: the arm this widens is the one WI-1042 deduplicates into the shared gate helper — land the helper first so the body-less clause is written once, not a fourth spelling. ACCEPTANCE (each test says which case fails when backed out): the driven two-supplier fixture above REFUSES at load naming both suppliers by supply route (control: backed out, it loads clean answering []); the ONE-supplier body-less fixture answers the supplied impl value as a rule body (control: today it answers [], since the goal is never typed and the abstract-op early return at resolve.rs:6136 returns before the bridge); wi1026/wi1035 suites stay green; the corrected spec cells flip from []-silent back to REFUSED in the closing commit. REFERENCE: /code-review 2026-08-07 finding 2; WI-1027 (Delivered — the operation-body half and the declining-arms record); WI-1035; WI-1034 (the dangling-functor residue, a DIFFERENT population); WI-496."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-1042"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-08T12:41:54Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1043", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED, with the ticket's WORK done in THREE pieces (it named one) and one of its own numbers corrected. (1) THE LOAD FACE, as asked: the walk gate is spec_op_call_parent — the union of the two halves, one clause in defaulted_spec_op_parent's neighbourhood — so a body-less rule-body call reaches check_apply_iter's WI-210 block and WI-1027's tie guard. TWO suppliers REFUSE at load naming both by route, ONE supplier pins. (2) THE GOAL SHAPE, which the ticket's acceptance predicted only obliquely: the pin alone does not answer. functional_relation_arity asks 'has a runnable body?' of the SPELLED functor, so the goal f(a…, ?r) never became the call reduce_op_value folds — the pin was written, carried and never consulted. dispatched_relation_arity reads it; the rule-less clause stays on the spelled functor (Ord.lt's clauses must still win). (3) A PRE-EXISTING REFUSAL OF A LEGAL PROGRAM had to be fixed FIRST: the rule-body requirement check asks sort_provides, which walks the CARRIER's own out-edges, so a WITNESS-supplied carrier (sort Rival provides Desc[T = Leaf], WI-450) read as having no instance and was REFUSED with 'missing requires' — measured on main through the DOT spelling, whose atoms have been typed since WI-282, while the operation-body twin answers 9. carrier_provided_by_witness declines that question; without it the widening would have extended the refusal to the qualified spelling. THE SPEC CELLS ARE FLIPPED BACK TO REFUSED (058 sec 21 row + a new sec 23; kernel-language 'Instance coherence' and 'Operation override'; the wi1035 header table). THE NARROWING THAT LET IT SHIP, and it is the ticket's real cost: an = goal loads as a call on the body-less PartialEq.eq, so this shape is most of what a rule body is made of — 133 newly decided call sites per whole-corpus load, whose failures are 19 leaf errors (15 DotDispatchNoMatch, 2 TypeMismatch, 2 UnknownApplyFunctor) and ZERO dispatch verdicts, rendering as 7 load errors in 4 files, every one a program that loads and runs today. The frame reports only the two TIES, and only for the call it is deciding; the backlog is WI-1056 (which also takes the general-Expr::Apply narrowing the ticket asked to be recorded). TWO DEFECTS OF MY OWN, both found by measurement and not by reasoning: the first cut handed the whole atom to type_check_node and SWALLOWED two delivered rule-body diagnostics (WI-282's member-not-found, WI-602's definite precondition violation) — found as two full-suite failures, fixed by recursing INTO a body-less atom as well as deciding it; and the tie of a call NESTED in an = goal was then reported TWICE (a regression against pre-WI-1043 behaviour for the dot spelling) — found by /code-review, fixed by the (op, span) identity in own_call_tie, driven by a_tie_nested_in_an_eq_goal_is_reported_once. RESIDUE: WI-1057 — an implementation supplied ONLY by an instance fact stays unreachable from a rule body (both spellings answer [], the operation body answers 9), pinned by a test that FAILS when it is closed; the naive fix was driven and is WORSE (it bound ?r to the un-reduced call and reported it DEFINITE). Back-out matrix driven per piece, four reverts. Full workspace green: 29 binaries, 4385 passed, 0 failed.", at: "2026-08-08T12:41:57Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1044", description: some(value: "BUG (058; /code-review 2026-08-07 finding 1, CONFIRMED BY DRIVING): a TOP-LEVEL QUERY on a defaulted spec op folds the spec DEFAULT while the identical goal as a rule body dispatches the supplied implementation — WI-1026 created a rule-body/query divergence for one goal text, and with two suppliers the load refusal is silently BYPASSED on the query path. DRIVEN on the WI-1026 one-supplier fixture: Desc.describe(leaf(), ?r) as a rule body answers Int(7) (the supplied impl); as a top-level query via kb.resolve / the CLI it answers Int(1) (the spec default) — both definite, one goal text, two answers. MECHANISM: query goals and runtime-asserted rules never pass type_rule_bodies, so their occurrences carry NO CallClass; the WI-938 hook materializes an unstamped occurrence (resolve.rs:1293-1320), and reduce_op_value computes op = classified_apply_target().unwrap_or(functor) (resolve.rs:6143) — unstamped falls to the spelled functor and folds op_body_node(spec_op), the default. OWNERSHIP MAP (so this is not re-diagnosed): WI-1034 owns the dangling-functor population, WI-1036 the builtin-clause blast radius, WI-1037 the ConcreteApplyWithin fold — NONE owns the query population; this ticket does. WORK: give the query/materialized population the same classification the typer gives rule bodies — stamp at the WI-938 materialization, or classify on demand in reduce_op_value when no stamp exists, through WI-1042 shared gate helper so the condition is written once — and the same TIE behaviour: a two-supplier carrier REFUSES at the query (loud, naming both candidates by supply route; a query has no load moment, so the call is its load moment). DEPENDS on WI-1037: the stamp CREATES a ConcreteApplyWithin population on the query path (a supplied impl whose parent sort declares requires), which must decline the structural fold and reach the bridge — without WI-1037 the stamp would route those calls into a fold that runs a body with no dictionary. DEPENDS on WI-1042 for the shared gate. ACCEPTANCE (each test says which case fails when backed out): the one-supplier fixture answers Int(7) via top-level query (control: backed out, Int(1)); the two-supplier fixture refuses at the query naming both candidates (control: backed out, silently answers the default); rule-body behaviour unchanged (wi1026 suite green); the WI-938 hook path is the one exercised (assert the goal reached materialization, not a pre-typed rule). REFERENCE: /code-review 2026-08-07 finding 1; WI-1026 sec 20; WI-938; resolve.rs:6143."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-1037", "WI-1042"]), status: Open)
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1042", author: "user", content: "STAMP NUMBERS for item (5): the body-less rule-body residue at typing.rs:41398 and the general-Apply narrowing at dispatch_calls_in_occ both stamp WI-1043 (fired beside this ticket; it owns that population and records the general narrowing as its residue). The other two stamps are as written: carrier_own_op fallback -> this ticket; load.rs WI-246 sentence + loader-synthesized 1:1 -> WI-1041.", at: "2026-08-07T10:52:53Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1036", author: "user", content: "SAFETY CLAIM CORRECTED (/code-review 2026-08-07 finding 3, CONFIRMED against resolve.rs:6143-6147; the doc comment at is_defaulted_spec_op is corrected in the same commit): this ticket recorded blast-radius reasoning rests on an ordering the code does NOT have. reduce_op_value does NOT return early on a builtin before reading a pin — it reads the pin FIRST (op = classified_apply_target().unwrap_or(functor), resolve.rs:6143) and keys the builtin early-return on the PINNED op. So classifying the 60 PartialOrd.gt/gte/lt/lte sites puts the pin in front of the builtin check, which then asks about the impl symbol (Int64.gt / Float.gt) instead of the spelled spec op (PartialOrd.gt). Both happen to be builtin-mapped, so the outcomes may coincide — but that equivalence is a MEASUREMENT this ticket must make, not an ordering to assume; the resolver path IS affected, contrary to the classifying-them-changes-nothing sentence. SEQUENCING: land WI-1042 first — its shared defaulted_spec_op_parent helper is the gate all sites should key on, and its non-operation early-out is the type_params_of_sort cost fix this ticket description says wants fixing FIRST.", at: "2026-08-07T10:52:55Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1037", author: "user", content: "IMPLEMENTATION GUARD (/code-review 2026-08-07 finding 7): if the fix adds a fourth CallClass variant, note that BOTH decode sites read CallClass through catch-alls — classified_apply_target (node_occurrence.rs:608, _ => None) and eval classification match — so a new variant lands SILENTLY in both engines catch-alls with no compile error: eval start_applies the spelled spec op with NO dictionary and the resolver folds the spec default, the precise failure class this ticket exists to remove. Make both decodes exhaustive (or return a typed verdict Pin(sym) | NeedsDict | Unclassified) — the same file carry_typer_stamps_from already made its destructuring exhaustive for exactly this reason (a fifth typer-written cell is a compile error). Also note WI-1044 (fired 2026-08-07) DEPENDS on this ticket: its query-path stamp creates a ConcreteApplyWithin population on the reduce path that must decline the fold and reach the bridge.", at: "2026-08-07T10:52:58Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1040", author: "user", content: "DELIVERY STATE (2026-08-07). Tests: rustland/anthill-core/tests/include/wi1040_require_clause_dictionary_test.rs (9, green). Design rewritten in the same session: requirement-channel.md secs 3, 9, 9.1, 10, 11.\n\nDELIVERED, each with a driving test and a control.\n * SURFACE. `require[X]` as a bare rule-body goal and `?d = require[X]` as the RHS of a top-level `=`; nested occurrences refused loudly at CONVERT with their own sentence. Lowered to `find_dictionary(X_base, out: ?d)`; the bare form mints the output variable at PARSE time (a rule's variables are numbered when the clause closes to De Bruijn, so one introduced later would have no slot).\n * ENCODING as settled: `out` is a presence-optional NAMED arg. Every gate that could have noticed it — the converter's idempotence gate, the sweep's, the resolver arm's argument reads — is POSITIONAL, so acceptance (f) holds STRUCTURALLY; the test that pins it passes either way by design and says so.\n * SWEEP. `record_find_dictionary_grounding` carries `out` through, and gained a FOURTH witness scan for a DEFAULTED spec op (`lookup_spec_op_dispatch` answers only for a body-less one, so a spec op with a default body was no witness at all and its requirement was rejected as ungroundable). Ordered LAST, where the only prior outcome was the hard error — so no rule that loads today can change which witness it picks.\n * FETCH + BIND. `fetch_dictionary` shares the guard verbatim with `find_dictionary_guard` (one carrier decision, one set of carried types), then resolves the provider tree at those types with an EMPTY scope and no selection, and binds. Bind and CHECK are one `unify_values` call.\n * WEAVE. A covered call becomes `Expr::ApplyWithin { functor, args, requirements: [?d] }` — the existing carrier, now with its first OCCURRENCE-side producer. It stays `ViewHead::Opaque`: its faithful term twin is the WRAPPED reflect shape whose head functor is `apply_within` and not the callee, so a transparent head would be a cross-carrier miss (WI-425/WI-815). Two readers read `as_expr()` directly: the WI-938 functional-relation hook and `reduce_op_value`. Woven only where `classified_apply_target` is None (channel doc: emit a goal only where the typer did not already pin).\n * HEADLINE, acceptance (a): a rule variable receiver the typer cannot pin answers 7 (the carrier's supplied impl) with the `require`, and 1 (the spec default) without it. Both numbers reachable in one program.\n\nCORRECTIONS THIS DELIVERY MADE TO ITS OWN DESIGN.\n * `require`'s name-resolution home is the CONVERTER, not the kernel vocabulary. Both legal spellings are rewritten away at convert, so a kernel-vocab entry would be reached only by an ILLEGAL one — turning a loud error into a name that resolves to nothing and then fails silently. Channel doc sec 3 amended.\n * The sec-9 argument for a term (\"a conditional provision makes a PARTIALLY RESOLVED dictionary reachable, which a SmallVec of handles has no inhabitant for\") is STALE and must not be repeated: sec 2.1 relocated narrowing to the typing pass, and sec 4 already says so. After typing there is no partial dictionary — an unreadable carried type DELAYS before composition starts. Sec 9.1's two leaves collapse to one.\n * ONE REPRESENTATION (user design call, this session). Sec 9 rewritten: a dictionary is an ordinary structural first-order value — one functor, one key set, one comparison — and the eval-side arena is to be RETIRED rather than converted into. `?d` binds a `Value::Entity` in the EXACT shape WI-1019's view announces for a handle (`dictionary_view_syms` owns both names), so the two already compare equal. STORAGE is explicitly not decided by the rule, and nothing here interns: hash-consing was an error to reach for (CLAUDE.md's Representation note), since a conditional provision composes over type arguments and interned terms never free. Sec 10 item 2 SETTLED; retirement filed as WI-1045.\n * Sec 10 item 5 (attribution) SETTLED without WI-613's sigma-class matcher: the witness scan already names an OCCURRENCE, and for a spec's own operation the witness and the covered call are the same node — attribution by identity.\n\nINCIDENTAL FIXES, each measured.\n * `subst_var_leaf` panicked on any structured non-Term binding (\"WI-246 not yet implemented\", called unreachable). It stopped being unreachable the moment a rule-body goal could bind a variable to a dictionary; it now splices through `Expr::Spliced`, the carrier that exists for exactly this (WI-714), which every view arm delegates through.\n * `is_unreduced_op_call` counts a surviving `ApplyWithin` as un-reduced. Without it the WI-938 hook bound a result variable to the call node itself — the definite-looking wrong answer that hook's own comment warns about, produced by the one shape it had never seen.\n * A woven call routes to `unify` even when unreduced, which is safe for the same reason the hook says it otherwise is not: `unify_values` delays on an unevaluated call, so the result var is never bound to the call term. Falling through answered NOTHING.\n\nNOT DELIVERED — three boundaries, each PINNED by a test asserting what the code actually does, so closing one must come here and change the assertion.\n * (c) CHECK is unreachable from any surface spelling. The arm is not dead code (bind and check are one `unify` call), but nothing can pre-bind `out`: a second `require` on one spec base is refused by the guard tier (needs the un-stripped spec, sec 10 item 1), and a dictionary passed through a rule head does NOT reach the callee's goal — MEASURED, the callee's variable is a fresh unbound Global, the caller's binding riding in `answer_links`. Reaching it is the automatic call-site synthesis (sec 5) plus sec 10 item 3. Test: `a_clause_dictionary_does_not_cross_a_rule_boundary`.\n * (d) DELAY-and-re-fire. Two halves are in place — the guard suspends on an unreadable carried type, and a woven call with an unbound dictionary delays instead of failing silently — but the CLAUSE still answers nothing when the carrier binds only in a LATER goal. Test: `an_unbound_carrier_answers_nothing_rather_than_delaying`.\n * (e) TIE. Sec 4's \"a runtime tie is unreachable, overlap is refused at typing/load\" is TRUE for a `provides` overlap and FALSE for two SUPPLIERS of one op at one carrier: the same program with a GROUND receiver is refused at load (WI-1026/WI-1035), and through the dictionary it answers the carrier's own member SILENTLY, because `resolve_op_target_checked` reads one sort-ops entry and never consults `spec_op_suppliers_for_carrier` (WI-842). Test: `a_two_supplier_carrier_dispatches_silently_through_the_dictionary`. Same class as WI-1043/WI-1044.\n\nALSO NOT DELIVERED, and stated so it is not read as covered: the automatic call-site synthesis (\"the call site drives it; require[X] is only the explicit form\"); the sec 6 CROSSINGS (`bridge_op_to_eval` supply, `prove_rule_predicate` handoff); the weave for TRANSITIVE and INHERITED witnesses (both bind `?d`, neither is woven — the first needs the crossing, the second a projection into a sub-slot); sec 10 items 1, 3, 4.", at: "2026-08-07T11:58:52Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1040", author: "user", content: "/code-review (high) 2026-08-07 — 6 findings, both HIGH ones MEASURED, all fixed in the same commit; full suite green after (29 binaries).\n\n1 (HIGH, wrong answer). Only ONE covered call per `require[X]` was woven — `GroundedRequirement.covered_call` was a single Option and the witness scan returned on first match. Measured: `require[Desc[T]], Desc.describe(?x,?a), Desc.describe(?x,?b)` gave ?a=1 (the SPEC DEFAULT) and ?b=7. FIX: `collect_covered_calls` — a whole-body DFS over every call to an op of the spec. Test `every_covered_call_in_the_clause_is_woven` (the `eq(?r,?b)` is what turns the regression into NO solution rather than a plausible first value).\n\n2 (HIGH, REGRESSION I introduced). Weaving a GOAL turns it into `Expr::ApplyWithin`, which heads `Opaque` — invisible to builtin dispatch (`get_builtin_view` keys on a Functor head), to the discrim query, and to the WI-938 hook (`functional_relation_arity` is None for a builtin AND for a body-less op — exactly the population the DIRECT witness scan selects). Measured: `requires(PartialEq[T]), eq(?x,?y)` = 1 solution; `require[PartialEq[T]], eq(?x,?y)` = 0. A clause that worked before the weave silently failing after it, on the ordinary typeclass shape. FIX: `collect_covered_calls` gates on `functional_relation_arity(..).is_some()` — a rule-less BODIED operation. So the woven population is NARROWER than the design's \"every covered call\": a body-less or builtin-backed spec op keeps exactly its `requires(X)` behaviour. Widening needs a goal-position reader for a woven call, or the Opaque head's twin problem solved — NOT a quiet transparent head, which would be the WI-425/WI-815 cross-carrier miss. Recorded at the collector, in channel doc sec 11, and in the test header; test `a_body_less_builtin_spec_op_behaves_exactly_as_the_check_only_spelling` compares the two spellings IN ONE KB so asserting only the require side cannot pass while both break.\n\n3 (MEDIUM, stale comment = root cause of 2). term_view.rs's Opaque justification for `Expr::ApplyWithin` said nothing produces it as an occurrence. False since this ticket. Rewritten to name the producer, keep Opaque for the WRAPPED-twin reason, and say where the price is paid.\n\n4 (MEDIUM, lost solutions). The unconditional `ApplyWithin => true` arm in `is_unreduced_op_call` leaked into `op_call_as_occ`, whose contract is BODIED ops (it UNFOLDS the callee's body): `unfold_eq_operand` then picked a woven operand as its case-split subject and bailed at its own `_ => return None`, ABANDONING the WI-580 split the other operand would have served. FIX: `op_call_as_occ` requires `Expr::Apply`.\n\n5 (LOW/MEDIUM, RESIDUE — not fixed, owner recorded at the site). `FindDictFetch::Undecided { detail }` is built and dropped: a resolver builtin has no diagnostic channel (`BuiltinResult` is Success/Bindings/Delay/Failure), so a `require[X]` whose provider tree genuinely cannot be built delays with nothing saying why. Routing it (a WI-737 flounder report is the obvious candidate) is WI-1040 residue; the site now says so instead of `let _ = detail` alone.\n\n6 (LOW, wrong doc). `dictionary_dispatch_target`'s doc still named `construct_requirement(impl_functor = ..)` — the pre-redesign shape — while the code reads `Dictionary(.., impl:)` via `dictionary_view_syms`. Corrected; it is the one place the two crossings are claimed to agree.\n\nThe reviewer independently CHECKED AND CLEARED, so these need not be re-derived: `out` label resolution across converter/loader/resolver; the `debug_assert!(wove)` being unreachable for duplicate or cross-spec require; the eval `ConcreteApplyWithin` arm removal being behaviour-preserving; `witness_sort_goal`'s arg alignment; the minted `__req_out` being freshened per activation.", at: "2026-08-07T12:28:16Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1040", description: some(value: "`require[X]` — bring a requirement dictionary into a rule clause's environment, so body calls that need one can be PASSED it, instead of only checking that it exists. DESIGN: `docs/design/requirement-channel.md`.\n\nSURFACE, NO GRAMMAR CHANGE. `require[X]` already parses in a rule body: WI-311's `application` is the UNIFIED type/term application (it absorbed the old `instantiation_term`), the converter classifies type-vs-term BY POSITION and builds functor + pos_args + named_args (`convert.rs:2312`), and the path `application` in `_non_name_atom_term` in `_atom_term` in `_term` in `_goal` in `rule_body` is open. What is added is an INTERPRETATION, not a production.\n\n  p(?x,?y) :- requires(Eq[T]), eq(?x,?y)       -- today. Checks only\n  p(?x,?y) :- require[Eq[T]], eq(?x,?y)        -- new. In scope for the clause; eq dispatches through it\n  p(?x,?y) :- ?d = require[Eq[T]], f(?x,?d)    -- named, when the author wants to pass it by hand\n\nTHREE SPELLINGS, THREE THINGS, NO OVERLOAD: `requires <T>` DECLARES a slot (sort/op level, `grammar.js:584`, untouched — `requires` is already overloaded three ways in declaration position and needed `prec.dynamic(1)` to break a GLR tie a COMMENT could flip, `grammar.js:590`; do not add a fourth); `require[X]` DENOTES a dictionary in term position; `anthill.kernel.find_dictionary` (`kb/load.rs:2345`) is the KERNEL RELATION both lower to, gaining an output argument.\n\nFORMALIZED AS A RULE TRANSFORMATION DURING TYPING — after elaboration the \"environment\" does not exist at run time at all. Per rule body: (1) each `require[X]` goal becomes `find_dictionary(X, ?d)` with a FRESH `?d` (the author's own variable when written `?d = require[X]`); (2) each body call covered by X carries `?d` in its requirements channel; (3) attribution — which call is covered by which require — is WI-613's σ-class matcher (`find_requires_slot` / `find_requires_location`), reused wholesale. BOTH HALVES EXIST ONE PHASE AWAY: `record_find_dictionary_grounding` (`typing.rs:40046`) already rewrites rule bodies at typing time (walks `kb.live_rule_ids()`, rebuilds the goal list, `set_rule_body_nodes`) — it just rewrites into a CHECKING goal; and `req_insertion::run` (`kb/req_insertion.rs:46`) already performs step 2's weave but walks `kb.op_bodies` ONLY, never rule bodies. This is that weave extended to rules — `requirement-dictionaries.md` §3.3's \"the op-body weave transfers to rules in full\", with its Γ slot replaced by an ordinary logic variable.\n\nTHE CALL SITE DRIVES IT; `require[X]` IS ONLY THE EXPLICIT FORM. A body call to an operation whose sort declares `requires` needs no author-written anything: the transformation reads the callee's dictionary chain (`provider_dict_entries` / `synth_req_names` — the SAME producers `resolve_bridge_requirements` reads, so there is one list, not two) and synthesizes one `find_dictionary` goal per slot — `... op(a,b) ...` becomes `... find_dictionary(X, ?dx), find_dictionary(Y, ?dy), apply_within(fn = op, args = (a,b), requirements = [?dx, ?dy]) ...`. PLACEMENT: immediately BEFORE the call each goal serves. `?dx`/`?dy` are bound FROM THE BODY, so they must be bound before the call reads them — and as late as possible, because resolving them needs the carrier type, which comes from the call's own arguments and may itself only be bound by earlier body goals. Immediately-before satisfies both: no delay in the common case, and the transformation stays local (no whole-body reordering). Placement is not a CORRECTNESS condition — a goal reading an unbound dictionary variable delays until it is bound, which is what keeps a hand-written `require[X]` sound wherever the author puts it; it is what makes the generated case not pay for that safety net. `apply_within` is the EXISTING carrier (`req_insertion` already emits exactly this for op bodies), so no new node kind. An author-written `require[X]` simply PRE-BINDS one of those variables, which the generated goal meets in its BOUND mode (check by unification). Automatic is the default; writing it is how you name or share one.\n\nEMIT A GOAL ONLY WHERE THE TYPER DID NOT ALREADY PIN. Where the 058 §3.2 ladder resolved the witness at compile stage the dict is already built and installed (`ConcreteApplyWithin.dispatch_dict`, WI-415); synthesizing a run-time goal there is a straight pessimization of a solved case. The generated goal is the FALLBACK for what the typer could not pin — in a rule body the common case, because the carrier is often unbound until run time.\n\nNOT A BREACH OF 058 §3.9 (\"instances are never CHOSEN at run time\"), though it looks adjacent: §3.9 forbids CHOOSING, and `find_dictionary` is deterministic — at most one answer, a tie refused. WI-300's guard already resolves from the runtime binding, so a rule body resolving at run time is the established precedent; 058 §4.2 puts rule bodies out of scope for selection precisely because the typer often cannot pin there.\n\nREQUIREMENTS STAY ON THE RIGHT OF `:-`. `rule_heads` is `commaSep1($._goal)` — the same `_goal` as the body — so `require[X]` parses and converts on the left too. But a multi-head rule CONCLUDES each head (each becomes its own rule; the rule must carry a label for a unique citation handle, `load.rs:15613`, `:15623`), so a `require[X]` there would ASSERT the requirement rather than demand it. Considered and rejected on semantics, not on parsing.\n\nCONSEQUENCE: THE RESOLVER GETS NO NEW CONCEPT. After elaboration a rule body is goals and arguments; `ResolverFrame` is untouched. An earlier draft of the design gave it a `requirements: [(Symbol, VarId)]` slot map inherited on push like `assumed_facts` — DROPPED, that was eval's shape imported where it does not apply (eval needs a frame slot because its variables are frame locals; a clause's are clause-scoped already). Likewise NOT Γ (`ResolveConfig.gamma`, `resolve.rs:367`, is a fact overlay global to one resolve call, seeded only by `prove_from_gamma` — not per-activation, not backtrackable).\n\nWHY A DICTIONARY NEEDS NO NEW CARRIER: an arena slot is `{ functor: Option<Symbol>, requirements: Option<SmallVec<[RequirementHandle]>> }` (`eval/requirement_arena.rs:28`) — an impl symbol and its sub-dictionaries, no closure, no host handle, no value. So a dictionary is exactly a first-order term `Dictionary(sub_0 .. sub_n-1, impl: S)`, and WI-1019's `TermView` already presents it as precisely that, totally and losslessly (`kb/term_view.rs:2228`, `:2322`, `:2364`, `:2386`). Matchability is not a property to engineer — a dictionary that could NOT be matched is not constructible. σ already binds `VarId -> Value` (`kb/subst.rs:60`) and `Value::Requirement` is a `Value` (`eval/value.rs:127`).\n\nSEMANTICS of the lowered `find_dictionary(X, ?d)` — a FUNCTION, never a generator: at most one ANSWER, because 058 §3.9 forbids choosing an instance at run time; a tie is REFUSED loudly naming the candidates, never enumerated as two solutions. Unbound `?d` + ground X + unique provider: resolve `provides`, construct, BIND. + no provider: FAIL. Non-ground X: DELAY — the resolver's ordinary delay, replacing the bespoke `FindDictOutcome::Suspend` (`typing.rs:37635`); an unbound var IS the undecided state. BOUND `?d`: CHECK by unification — the mode a caller-supplied dictionary takes (\"run time only copies it along\", 058 §3.9).\n\n058 §3.9 COMPLIANCE, and why the bracket does not breach it: §3.9 permits a dictionary VALUE to fill an ANONYMOUS slot but never a named one (\"a named slot is a type parameter, and a value cannot determine a type\"). The bracket in `require[X]` is on the INPUT side — it names the spec instance being asked for, exactly as 058 §3.3's `f[Spec = W](...)` does. The OUTPUT `?d` is an ordinary logic variable in term position, not a type parameter, and is not addressable in any type. DO NOT read the bracket as making `?d` a type parameter — the one misreading this spelling invites.\n\nCONDITIONAL PROVISIONS DECIDE THE REPRESENTATION (058 §3.8, delivered WI-869). A conditional provision IS a Horn clause over `provides`, so resolving `find_dictionary` over it is ordinary SLD. But it settles what rides in σ: a TERM, not an arena handle. A conditional provision makes a PARTIALLY RESOLVED dictionary reachable — `Eq[Pair[Int64, ?B]]` pins the outer impl while `?B` is unbound, so one sub-dictionary is not yet known. A term represents that natively (`Dictionary(impl: PairEq, d_A, ?d_B)`, refined later); a HANDLE CANNOT, because an arena slot's subs are `SmallVec<[RequirementHandle]>` with no variable inhabitant. The handle stays eval's form, where everything is ground by construction; the boundary converts.\n\nSETTLE BEFORE WRITING THE READ — the one genuine interaction with WI-869. WI-869 made strictness PER-PROVISION: a slot is demanded when it is sort-level or a condition of the provision dispatched, \"otherwise left unfilled, and reading an unfilled slot is refused at the read\". In the term model an unfilled slot is an UNBOUND VARIABLE, so the natural reading of that read is DELAY, not refuse — and a later binding could fill a slot the dispatched provision never promised. Refuse-vs-delay here is a SOUNDNESS decision, not a diagnostic-quality one. Decide it first, and say at the site which of WI-869's four producers the answer is keyed to.\n\nCROSSINGS (the payoff — \"pass it to operations that require dictionaries\"): `bridge_op_to_eval` reads the clause variable's σ-value and installs it into the callee's `eval::Frame::requirements`, where a body's `var_ref(__req_*)` reads it; where the clause has no covering `require`, `resolve_bridge_requirements` (`typing.rs:14667`) re-derives at the concrete argument types as today — supplying beats re-deriving only where re-derivation cannot decide (Unresolvable / Ambiguous, WI-855). `prove_rule_predicate` (`resolve.rs:4788`) hands the eval caller's `frame.requirements` to the goal so a lowered `find_dictionary` CHECKS rather than re-resolves; today they are dropped entirely. FOOTGUN to pin at the site: two scratch interpreters are two arenas, so handles from different crossings must be compared STRUCTURALLY (the WI-1019 view), never by `raw()`. A handle stays valid after `run_in_bridge_interp` drops its interpreter because it owns an `Rc` to its arena (`eval/requirement_arena.rs:90`, `:167`).\n\nRESOLVES an open question elsewhere: `requirement-dictionaries.md` §3.4's \"[Open] whole-rule vs positional requires\" was open only under the Γ substrate. With a clause variable, a goal reading it before `require[X]` binds it sees an unbound var and DELAYS — ordering is a performance question, not a correctness one. Already amended in that doc.\n\nACCEPTANCE — each test DRIVES the capability (resolve the goal, assert the value; \"it loads clean\" is not evidence) and says at its site which case fails when the change is backed out: (a) `require[X]` in a body puts the dictionary in scope and a body spec-op call dispatches through it — assert the supplied impl's value, with the control that impl and spec default return DIFFERENT values; (b) the named form `?d = require[X]` binds and can be passed by hand; (c) a bound `?d` is CHECKED by unification, not re-derived — control: it differs from what re-derivation would pick; (d) a non-ground carrier DELAYS and re-fires once bound; (e) a provider tie is refused loudly and does NOT yield two solutions; (f) the existing arity-1 `requires(X)` form and every WI-300 guard-tier test behave exactly as before. `cargo-test` green via `rustland/scripts/test.sh`.\n\nCONSUMERS: WI-1037 needs only the crossing half (and is otherwise independent — see its description); WI-070 (`Branch`) with WI-069 (`Suspension` snapshot/resume) — jointly 027.2's eval-to-SLD switch — get per-branch dictionaries FOR FREE, since a dictionary is a term bound in σ and each branch has its own σ, and being immutable it needs none of 047 §8's `register_undo` machinery. Sequencing note, deliberately NOT a dependency edge in either direction."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-07T12:28:42Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1045", description: some(value: "Retire the eval-side `RequirementArena`: a dictionary has ONE representation, and the crossing converts nothing. DESIGN: `docs/design/requirement-channel.md` §9 (rewritten 2026-08-07 in the WI-1040 session, which delivered the σ half).\n\nTODAY THERE ARE TWO. σ carries `Dictionary(sub_0 .. sub_n-1, impl: S)` — an ordinary structured value in the shape WI-1019's `TermView` announces (`dictionary_view_syms`, term_view.rs, is the one owner of both names). Eval carries a `RequirementHandle`: a refcounted slot in `RequirementArena` (`eval/requirement_arena.rs`), identity `(arena, raw)`. `eval_requirement_chain_node` converts term -> handle at the SLD->eval edge; `port_resolved_tree` converts a resolved tree -> handle for the bridge.\n\nWHY THIS IS NOT A STYLE QUESTION. Two identities for one thing is a live hazard the channel doc already has to WARN about: two scratch interpreters are two arenas, so one dictionary gets two `raw()`s and every comparison must be routed through the WI-1019 view or it silently answers wrong. Retiring the arena deletes the hazard rather than documenting it, and deletes the boundary at which the two forms could disagree.\n\nMEASURED BEFORE FILING, so the shape is known and not assumed: the arena has NO SETTER (a slot is immutable after `alloc`); `stand_in_requirement` builds no cycle (a bundle over `arity` marker slots), consistent with the operation-call-model no-cycles policy; every dictionary reaching eval is ground by construction. So the thing an arena is protecting is an immutable ground acyclic `(impl symbol, ordered children)` tree — which every ordinary value carrier already holds.\n\nSCOPE: 82 `RequirementHandle` references across 8 files (`eval/{requirement_arena,eval,mod,frame,closure,value}.rs`, `kb/{term_view,typing}.rs`), 32 `Value::Requirement` readers. `Frame::requirements` and `Closure.requirements` change element type; `project(k)` becomes reading child k; `functor()` becomes reading the `impl` field; `arity()` becomes positional arity.\n\nONE SPELLING IS PART OF THE JOB, not a follow-up. The IR construction node is `anthill.reflect.Expr.construct_requirement(impl_functor = .., requirements = ..)` while the view presents `Dictionary(sub.., impl:)` — different functor, different key names for one thing. Collapse them to a single constructor; channel doc §10 item 2 already made that a condition.\n\nSTORAGE IS A SEPARATE DECISION and this ticket does not make it. \"One representation\" is shape and identity — one functor, one key set, one comparison — not which store holds it. Do NOT reach for hash-consing by reflex: CLAUDE.md's Representation note says interning is for persistent heavily-shared structure and is not implied by being matched or indexed (the discrim tree keys on structural `DiscrimKey`s, never on `TermId` identity), and a conditional provision composes over type arguments, so the distinct-dictionary family follows the carried types that actually occur — no static bound, while interned terms live for the KB's lifetime. WI-1040 emits a non-interned `Value::Entity` for exactly this reason.\n\nACCEPTANCE — each test DRIVES the capability (build a dictionary on each side, assert the value), and says at its site which case fails when the change is backed out: (a) a dictionary built by the SLD fetch and one built inside an operation call compare EQUAL with no conversion step anywhere; (b) an operation body's `var_ref(__req_*)` reads the same value the resolver bound, driven end to end; (c) a conditional provision's nested sub-dictionaries project by index identically on both sides; (d) WI-857's `NoProvider` marker slot is still refused at every dispatch read; (e) the refcount tests in `requirement_arena.rs` are DELETED, not ported — say so, since a suite that keeps testing the retired store is how a retirement half-lands. `cargo-test` green via `rustland/scripts/test.sh`."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-07T14:13:48Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1042", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED with two of the ticket's own claims REFUTED BY DRIVING, both recorded at their sites. (1) Item (3) asked for a loud error or an impossibility argument at the dot site's spec_op_parent_sort miss: the shape is REACHABLE and LEGAL — 'sort Leaf provides Plain' where Plain declares no 'sort X = ?' loads clean and reaches it, so Take is the ANSWER and raising would refuse a legal program; as a panic! it fired on that one fixture and on nothing else in 4321 tests. Kept as a driver (a_non_parametric_provided_sort_takes_the_member), not prose. (2) Item (1)'s two spellings are NOT equivalent, and the first justification written for the unification was VACUOUS — 'driven over every operation in the corpus' walks all_operation_params, one entry per OperationInfo FACT, exactly the population where '!operation_has_no_body' and 'op_has_runnable_body' cannot differ. Found by this ticket's own /code-review. The real measurement: with the old body test in the shared gate, 15 tests across 5 suites fail (wi936, wi618, parameterized_provides_block, sql_store_example, wi1042), 14 through is_defaulted_spec_op's rule-body walk type-checking ENTITY-CONSTRUCTOR calls — re-conjoining the strict test there alone restores exactly those 14. The strict read is required, not stronger. The other two sites are safe by a stated precondition each (check_apply_iter sits inside the lookup_operation_info_full bind; the dot site is reached only via an OperationInfo walk). A FOURTH reader was found by the same review: WI-1040's defaulted_spec_op_witness_grounds_soundly asked the gate's question by name while reading the body-AGNOSTIC parent and leaning on caller scan order; it now asks the gate — not observable, stated as such, the change is to the failure mode. COST claim also corrected: the check_apply_iter frame went 5 -> 4 reaches of type_params_of_sort, not 2 -> 1 (lookup_spec_op_dispatch twice + self_receiver_spec_sort remain); collapsing the rest is WI-1011's memo, not a gate question. Gate leg reach MEASURED (stdlib+bindings): 523 calls, name split answers 244, 279 reach the body probe, 55 the parametric leg; old order 466/80. Full workspace 4321 passed / 0 failed.", at: "2026-08-07T15:55:16Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1042", description: some(value: "CLEANUP WITH TEETH (WI-1035/1036 family; /code-review 2026-08-07 findings 5/6/12/14 + the owner stamps of finding 13): ONE OWNER for the defaulted-dispatch gate. (1) typing.rs:41504 is_defaulted_spec_op is a prose-coupled THIRD spelling of the WI-444 gate (check_apply_iter: lookup_spec_op_dispatch().is_none() && spec_op_parent_sort().is_some()) — the equivalence is held only by a doc comment 30k lines away, so when the gate gains a clause the rule-body fire population silently diverges from the op-body pin/refusal population and no test moves (the corpus population is zero). ALSO its two-HashMap-probes-SHIELD claim is wrong for the MAJORITY population: a NON-operation functor (every relational atom head) misses kb.op_record and falls into lookup_operation_info O(N_ops) linear scan over all OperationInfo facts (op_info.rs:197-209), paid in the pre-scan AND the dispatch walk (~745 predicate calls per load measured by the WI-1026 diff instrumentation, which measured only the third clause). FIX: one shared defaulted_spec_op_parent(kb, f) -> Option<Symbol> used by all three sites, with a cheap non-operation early-out; a test that FAILS if the two spellings drift. (2) typing.rs:13027 — dot_member_dispatch_decision defaulted leg is an inline copy of the WI-1012 tie condition with a self-admitted prose-only coupling (nothing enforces that but this sentence); the stated reason not to extract — the pin would walk the suppliers twice — does NOT hold: a helper taking the ALREADY-COMPUTED candidate slice (the exact shape supplier_tie_error / refuse_unarbitrated_supplier_tie already use) serves both sites in one walk. Without it, when check_apply_iter gains a clause the dot spelling silently diverges from the qualified spelling on the same program — the exact spelling-keyed silence WI-1035 was opened to close. (3) typing.rs:12974 — the spec_op_parent_sort-miss early-out silently degrades to Ok(DotMember::Take), skipping the entire WI-1035 tie guard, with no statement of what a None means there and no ticket, unlike every other early-out in the function; it is reached only after find_spec_op_for_provided_sort answered Some (the member DOES back a provided spec), so the miss is an unexpected shape. FIX: loud error, or a recorded impossibility argument at the site — never a silent fallback (CLAUDE.md: prefer a loud error over a silent skip). (4) typing.rs:12838 — the ~100-line contract for dot_member_dispatch_decision (measured table, TIER reasoning, the must-be-written-HERE-too warning) is one contiguous doc block that runs straight into enum DotMember, so rustdoc attaches ALL of it to the enum and the function renders undocumented — the WI-887 comment-placement failure mode reproduced in the file that recorded it. FIX: attach the essay to the function. (5) STAMP LIVE OWNERS at the four unowned gap sites (finding 13; the repo rule: a gap with a reason has no owner — ticket number at the site): the body-less rule-body residue at typing.rs:41398 currently citing Delivered WI-1027; the general-Apply narrowing in dispatch_calls_in_occ (no ticket); the carrier_own_op-disagreement fallback citing Delivered WI-616 at typing.rs:13016 (stamp THIS ticket); load.rs:12050/12081 WI-246 later-work sentence + the loader-synthesized 1:1 residual (stamp WI-1041). The body-less/general-Apply numbers are in this ticket feedback. ACCEPTANCE: one gate helper shared by all three sites; both tie sites read one condition helper; the Take fallback is loud or argued at the site; rustdoc attaches the contract to the function; all four stamps in place; behaviour-neutral — full suite green via rustland/scripts/test.sh, plus the drift test above. REFERENCE: /code-review 2026-08-07; WI-1035; WI-1012; WI-444; WI-653; feedback-typer-no-nongeneric-code."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-07T15:55:23Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1036", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED — DECISION REVERSED MID-TICKET BY DRIVING, and the reversal is the finding. First conclusion (written, then refuted by /code-review high): keep the clause, because classifying the builtin-mapped family buys nothing — the pin's only readers are reduce_op_value and eval's apply, and PartialOrd.gt / Int64.gt / Float.gt are ALL registered builtins so the early-return fires either way. That is true of the STDLIB family and FALSE of the population, and it missed what the clause actually withheld. DRIVEN, both halves failing when the clause is restored: (1) the 058 sec 3.7 TIE REFUSAL — a carrier supplying 'gt' twice (own member + WI-431 instance fact) was REFUSED at load from an operation body and ACCEPTED from a rule body; one program, two verdicts, keyed on where the call is written; without the clause the rule body refuses at its own location with the same message. (2) sec 3.1 DISPATCH BY VALUE IN OPERAND POSITION — 'unify(?r, PartialOrd.gt(p,q))' on a carrier with a supplied override answered an INDEFINITE RESIDUAL; classified, the pin names the override and reduce_op_value folds it to false. A user carrier's own gt is an ordinary bodied operation, so the pin IS readable there. BOTH OF THE TICKET'S STATED REASONS WERE FALSE: reason 1 by its own feedback (the pin is read BEFORE the builtin early-return); reason 2 by driving — a rule-body classification emits NO dispatch rewrite at all (req_insertion::run walks kb.op_bodies_iter(); one classified call emits 1 from an operation body and 0 from a rule body), so the '60 new rewrites' could not happen. THE 60 DOES NOT REPRODUCE: four newly decided sites on a stdlib+bindings load (2 gt, 1 lt, 1 gte, all stdlib rule bodies), five with anthill-testcases, none in examples or anthill-todo, counted at the acting arm of dispatch_calls_in_occ. All four are GOAL-position calls pinned to Int64.gt/Float.gt, both builtins — the corpus gets the refusal, not a dispatch change. RESIDUAL, PINNED WITH ITS OWNER: the same call as a rule-body GOAL still answers [] where the operation body answers the override; deleting the clause does not fix it (measured) — a goal takes BuiltinTag::Gt off its SPELLED functor, no goal-position reader consults a pin, and builtin_cmp fails silently on two entities. WI-879 owns it. ALSO: is_defaulted_spec_op is folded away (it was the gate plus the clause); WI-1042's body-test control re-measured after the deletion, same 15 tests. Full workspace 4326/0.", at: "2026-08-07T18:41:05Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1036", description: some(value: "Decide whether WI-1026's rule-body spec-op dispatch should also cover a BUILTIN-MAPPED defaulted spec op, and build the driver that can observe the answer. WI-1026 routes a rule-body call on a defaulted spec op through 'check_apply_iter' so it pins the carrier's supplied impl (or refuses a tie), gated by 'is_defaulted_spec_op', whose first clause is '!kb.is_builtin(f)'. THAT CLAUSE IS THE WHOLE BLAST RADIUS AND IT WAS MEASURED: with it the arm fires on ZERO sites in the corpus (stdlib+bindings+testcases+examples+anthill-todo); WITHOUT it, on 60 — every one a 'PartialOrd.gt/gte/lt/lte' rule-body goal, newly pinned to 'Int64.gt' / 'Float.gt'. The corpus loads clean either way. IT WAS EXCLUDED FOR TWO REASONS, ONE OF WHICH IS THE POINT OF THIS TICKET. (1) It is inert for WI-1026's own path: a builtin is decided by the interpreter's builtin table, and 'reduce_op_value' returns early on a builtin BEFORE it reads a pin — so classifying these changes nothing the ticket was about. (2) It is NOT inert elsewhere: 'req_insertion::run' emits a dispatch rewrite per classified occurrence ('record_apply_rewrite', spec op -> impl op), so widening emits 60 new rewrites. AND THE SUITE CANNOT DECIDE IT — the full workspace is green BOTH WAYS, 4295/0 with the clause and 4295/0 without, so those 60 rewrites are observed by NO test and a green run is not evidence for landing them. WORK, in this order: build a test that OBSERVES an emitted dispatch rewrite for a rule-body call (there is none today — that absence is the finding); then measure the 60 sites old-vs-new through it; then decide. Deleting the clause is one line, but doing it on a green-both-ways suite would be landing an unobserved behaviour change. ACCEPTANCE: either the clause goes with a driver that fails when it is restored, or it stays with the reason written at the site and here. REFERENCE: WI-1026; 'is_defaulted_spec_op' in kb/typing.rs carries the same numbers."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-07T18:41:17Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-895", description: some(value: "A RULE-BODY GOAL'S UN-IMPORTED FUNCTOR IS SILENTLY INERT — HALF CLOSED by WI-1034; what is left is the ARGUMENT position.\n\nWHAT WI-1034 DELIVERED: a rule-body goal whose functor names nothing is refused at load, named and located (`load::check_rule_body_goals` / `KnowledgeBase::undefined_rule_body_goals`), and `docs/kernel-language.md` §5.3 \"Naming one from elsewhere\" now says so. It shares the head test with the query reader (`undefined_functor`, WI-754/WI-863/WI-878), takes the query walk's DESCENT (top-level atoms + inside `not`; a bare `or`/`push_choice` branch and a quantifier body are left to resolution), and exempts a resolver scoping marker and a functor the rule ASSUMES as a `forall_impl` antecedent. Coverage + the per-piece back-out table: `wi1034_undefined_rule_body_goal_test`.\n\nWHAT IS LEFT, and it is this ticket's ORIGINAL measurement: an un-imported functor in a goal's ARGUMENT — `holds894(ite(true, 10, 20))` — is a DATA slot, is not a goal, is not walked, and still interns bare and never fires as a `[simp]` redex. No diagnostic. Still pinned by `wi894_rule_functor_scope_test::a_rule_body_does_not_yet_refuse_an_unimported_functor` (delete that test when this lands; its doc now says which half it pins).\n\nWHY IT IS A SEPARATE PREDICATE, not a wider walk: WI-1034's probe counted 20 dangling names over the corpus in GOAL position and ~313 with argument positions included — every constructor written in an argument is one. \"This goal can never match\" and \"this redex can never fire\" are different claims with different exemptions; widening `body_goal_children` into data would refuse the entire corpus.\n\nREFERENCE: WI-1034 (the goal half, delivered); WI-894 (the scoping and the operation-body refusal); WI-476 (the bare-intern fallback that swallows it); `docs/kernel-language.md` §5.3. CONSIDER: strengthening the pin to assert the OBSERVABLE inertness (zero solutions) rather than only that the file loads."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1034", description: some(value: "A rule-body goal whose functor names NOTHING answers nothing, silently. MEASURED on WI-1026's fixture: 'rule answer(?r) :- describe(leaf(), ?r)' loads CLEAN and answers [] even with ONE unambiguous supplier present — 'describe' is not in scope at the namespace top level, so it interns to a symbol with no clause, no operation record and no builtin, and the goal can never match. This is NOT a dispatch question (WI-1026's own ordering note predicted it: 'a resolution failure prior to any 058 question'), which is why WI-1026 left it and PINNED the current behaviour in 'a_bare_goal_naming_nothing_still_answers_nothing'. WHY IT IS NOT INLINE: the check is corpus-wide and at least two of its categories are LEGITIMATE, so a naive 'no clauses => refuse' rule is wrong. MEASURED over stdlib+bindings / +testcases / +examples / +anthill-todo, counting GOAL positions only (top-level body atoms plus the arguments of not/or/push_choice — a whole-subtree walk over-counts every constructor in an argument position and reports ~313 instead): 20 dangling goals in stdlib alone, 25 with examples, over 5 distinct names in three categories — (a) 'forall_impl', a RESOLVER SCOPING MARKER that has no clauses by design and is classified specially by the resolver; (b) 'anthill.reflect.typing.DefaultProvider', an ENTITY whose facts the loader asserts after this check would run; (c) genuinely dangling names in examples ('distance_at_step', 'observed_pose_at', 'anthill.examples.lf1.safety.common.RealVelocity'). WORK: decide the predicate (it must know about resolver markers and about functors that acquire facts later), triage the 5 corpus names, then make the rest loud. ACCEPTANCE: a rule-body goal that can never match is refused at load naming it and its span; the three categories above still load; the WI-1026 test above flips from asserting [] to asserting the refusal. REFERENCE: WI-1026; the probe that produced these counts is described in the header of wi1026_rule_body_spec_op_dispatch_test.rs."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-08-07T20:34:10Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1046", description: some(value: "THE BOOLEAN OPERATORS ARE POSITION-DIRECTED IN THE SPEC AND WERE NOT IN FACT. `docs/kernel-language.md` §6.6 (WI-529) states it verbatim: \"`not`, `or`, and `and` each name a dispatched value operation on `Bool` ... inside an operation body (evaluated), but a goal form in a rule body (resolved) ... Resolution is by syntactic position, not by a distinct glyph or operand type.\" WI-529 routed ONE direction (a redirect from the primitives to the Bool ops inside an op body) and left the rule-body direction to the implicit-prelude FALLBACK, which sits BELOW scope resolution — so any name in scope shadows it.\n\nMEASURED, driven, one ordinary import, five rows (`fact l(1)`, `fact r(1)`, `fact empty(99)`):\n  rule pipe(?x)     :- l(?x) | r(?x)          expected 2 | no import 2 | WITH `import anthill.prelude.Bool` 0\n  rule comma(?x)    :- l(?x), r(?x)           expected 1 | 1 | 1            (CONTROL)\n  rule nafTrue(?x)  :- l(?x), not(empty(?x))  expected 1 | 1 | 0\n  rule nafFalse(?x) :- l(?x), not(r(?x))      expected 0 | 0 | 0\n  rule amp(?x)      :- l(?x) & r(?x)          — | 0 | 0\nThe `nafTrue` row is a WRONG ANSWER, not a missing one: negation-as-failure over a goal that fails must SUCCEED. `nafFalse` is why it is the row to drive — it reads 0 under the defect and 0 when correct.\n\nCORRECTION TO THIS TICKET'S ORIGINAL TEXT — one of its two measurements was FALSE. It claimed `rule neg(?x) :- l(?x), not(l(?x) & r(?x))` \"parses clean, loads clean, and `neg` does not resolve\", i.e. a rule vanishing silently. RE-MEASURED with a control: `rule neg(?x) :- l(?x)` — no `&` at all — ALSO does not resolve, and the same body with head `uniq1046` DOES. The cause is the head NAME: `neg` resolves to `anthill.prelude.Numeric.neg`, so per WI-896 the rule concludes about the prelude symbol instead of introducing its own. Documented behaviour, nothing to do with `&`. The real second defect is the import capture above, which the original text did not have.\n\nDELIVERED. (1) `Loader::route_body_goal_boolean`, the mirror of WI-529's `redirect_op_body_boolean`, applied at GOAL POSITIONS: an `in_body_goal` flag rides from each top-level body atom down through the goal slots of the connectives above it and turns off at every data slot, so a goal's ARGUMENT keeps the value reading. (2) Which slots are goals now comes from ONE shared table, `KnowledgeBase::goal_arg_slots`, read by the query walk (WI-863), the rule-body walk (WI-1034) and the loader — which needs the answer while still BUILDING the body and so cannot ask either walk. (3) `&` in a goal position is REFUSED naming the comma (`LoadError::BooleanOperatorInGoalPosition`), since §6.6 says there is no `kernel.and` to route it to. (4) §6.6 updated: the claim was true of one direction only.\n\nBLAST RADIUS ZERO, measured: `Bool.and`/`.or`/`.not` appear in NO rule-body goal position across stdlib+bindings, +testcases, +examples, +anthill-todo. The probe's CONTROL is that the same walk finds `anthill.reflect.not` 3-4x and `anthill.kernel.push_choice` once.\n\nFOUND WHILE TESTING, now pinned: `&` binds LOOSER than `=` (priority 2 vs 3), so `?r = ?a & ?b` is `and(eq(?r, ?a), ?b)` — the `and` is the top-level GOAL. That loaded clean and never fired; it is now loud. Coverage + the three-column back-out table: `wi1046_boolean_goal_routing_test`. REFERENCE: WI-529; WI-1034 (which found this); WI-896 (the `neg` correction); §6.6."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-08-07T21:20:08Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1047", description: some(value: "`|` (DISJUNCTION) ANSWERS NOTHING THROUGH THE `anthill query` CLI WHILE WORKING IN-PROCESS — the same rule, the same file, two different answers depending on which entry point loads it.\n\nMEASURED, one file, no imports (`fact l(1)`, `fact r(2)`, `rule either(?x) :- l(?x) | r(?x)`):\n  in-process (`common::load_kb_with` + `query_unary`)         -> 2 solutions   (CORRECT)\n  `anthill query --path <file> \"demo.q1.either(?x)\"`          -> \"no solutions\"\nCONTROLS, same file, same CLI invocation:\n  `rule both(?x) :- l(?x), r(?y)`                             -> 1 solution    (the comma form is fine)\n  `rule viaPush(?x) :- push_choice(l(?x), r(?x))`             -> 2 solutions   (the PRIMITIVE is fine)\n  `rule viaKernelOr(?x) :- anthill.kernel.or(l(?x), r(?x))`   -> \"no solutions\"\nSo it is not the file, not the query pattern, and not `push_choice`: it is the kernel `or` RULE (`stdlib/anthill/kernel/kernel.anthill:48`, `rule or(?a, ?b) :- push_choice(?a, ?b)`) failing to fire under the CLI. Naming it fully qualified does not help, and the name RESOLVES — an unresolvable functor gets the loud WI-754 report instead (\"does not resolve to a known functor\"), which this does not.\n\nNOT CAUSED BY WI-1046, verified by `git stash`: the same \"no solutions\" is produced by the commit before it. WI-1046's routing degrades safely here — it maps to `anthill.kernel.or` only when that qualified name exists.\n\nWHAT IS ALREADY RULED OUT: `kernel/kernel.anthill` IS in the CLI's embedded set (`anthill-stl/src/stdlib.rs:109`), so a missing FILE is not the explanation. The remaining candidates are that its clause is not asserted under the CLI's load path, or that the CLI's query/resolve configuration does not reach a clause whose body binds goal-valued variables.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS: this is the silent-wrong-answer class, at the entry point users actually type. A disjunctive rule loads clean, is reported by `anthill check`, and answers nothing — with no diagnostic. The in-process suite cannot see it, which is why it survived: every disjunction test in the tree drives the KB directly.\n\nWORK: find which of the two candidates it is (compare the CLI's assembled KB against the in-process one for the `or` clause — `rules_by_functor(anthill.kernel.or)` on both), then fix. ACCEPTANCE: the table above re-measured with the CLI row answering 2; the three CONTROL rows unchanged; a CLI-level test (`anthill-cli/tests/`) driving a disjunctive rule end-to-end, since an in-process test provably cannot catch this; each test says which case fails when the change is backed out. `cargo-test` green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. REFERENCE: WI-1046 (which found it); WI-075 / proposal 033 (`push_choice`); `stdlib/anthill/kernel/kernel.anthill:48`."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-08-07T23:02:30Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1048", description: some(value: "THE REQUIRES-SHADOW LINT (WI-346) FIRES ON A DELIBERATE REFINEMENT AND ADVISES A REPAIR THAT WOULD BREAK IT. These are the stdlib's only two load warnings, printed on every `anthill check` and — since WI-1047 gave `query` the stdlib — on every `anthill query` too:\n\n  warning: operation `map` in `anthill.prelude.FiniteCollection` shadows the inherited\n  `anthill.prelude.Iterable.map` (`FiniteCollection` requires `Iterable`); `requires` does\n  not override — the two are distinct operations. Qualify … or rename `map` to silence.\n  …and the same for `filter`.\n\nTHE STDLIB IS NOT WHAT IS WRONG — MEASURED, driven, on the shape the lint is about. A `List` provides BOTH: `FiniteCollection` explicitly (list.anthill:170) and `Iterable` transitively via `Stream` (WI-495). So `xs.map(f)` is genuinely contested. Annotating the return type makes the choice observable:\n  operation probe(xs: List[T = Int64]) -> Stream[T = Int64, E = {}] = xs.map(lambda x -> x)\n      -> REFUSED: \"expected Stream[...], got FiniteCollection[C = FiniteMappedStream[...], ...]\"\n  operation probe(xs: List[T = Int64]) -> Int64 = xs.map(lambda x -> x).size()\n      -> LOADS CLEAN\nDispatch picks `FiniteCollection.map`, the MORE SPECIFIC one, deterministically — which is exactly what the design says it is for (finite_collection.anthill:52-57, WI-599 \"THIN\": \"UNLIKE Iterable.map/filter (→ lazy maybe-infinite Stream), these RETURN a FiniteCollection, so a pipeline over a finite source stays consumable: `xs.map(f).size()` keeps type-checking\"). There is NO silent wrong answer behind the warning, and the mismatched-expectation case is a LOUD type error naming both types. Renaming `map` — the lint's own advice — would destroy the design.\n\nWHAT IS WRONG IS THE PREDICATE: `check_requires_shadows` (load.rs) compares SHORT NAMES ONLY. It builds `spec_short` from the required spec's own ops and warns on any short-name hit, never looking at signatures — so it cannot tell a deliberate REFINEMENT (different return type; a distinct operation by construction) from an accidental collision (identical signature; the author probably believed `requires` overrides). It already exempts a PROVIDER's own op (\"a legitimate override, not a shadow\", via `sort_provides`); a spec that `requires` another and refines its operation has no such exemption, and a spec hierarchy refining an operation is a normal thing to write.\n\nOPTIONS, in the order they were weighed (a DECISION is needed, which is why this is a ticket and not an inline fix):\n (a) NARROW to same-signature shadows — warn only where the shadowing op's signature MATCHES the spec's, the case where the author probably meant to override. Silences both stdlib warnings with no stdlib edit and keeps the lint's stated purpose. Needs a test that a same-signature shadow STILL warns, else the lint is silently retired.\n (b) An explicit opt-out marker on the refining op (attribute or metadata convention) — keeps the lint's full reach, but needs new surface syntax and every future refinement pays the annotation.\n (c) RETIRE the lint — driving shows dispatch decides correctly and a mismatch is already a loud type error, so it may catch nothing the typer misses. Cheapest; gives up a guard not proven useless outside this one case.\n\nACCEPTANCE: the stdlib loads with ZERO warnings; whichever option is taken, a test DRIVES the case the lint is kept for (under (a): a same-signature shadow still warns; under (c): the removal is justified by showing the typer already reports what it reported); the two dispatch rows above re-measured unchanged, since the point is that the stdlib's behaviour is already right; each test says which case fails when the change is backed out. `cargo-test` green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. REFERENCE: WI-346 (the lint); WI-599 (the FiniteCollection map/filter design); WI-495 (List's transitive Iterable); WI-1047 (which surfaced these on `query`)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-08T04:42:19Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1049", author: "user", content: "DISTINCTNESS DOMAIN — the open question, now MEASURED. Every row below LOADS CLEAN today; the symbol column is what the one name ends up meaning.\n\n  operation foo + operation foo    LOADS CLEAN   one symbol, TWO OperationInfo, order-dependent read  <- THIS TICKET\n  operation foo + rule foo(...)    LOADS CLEAN   kinds=Operation (the rule adds NO kind)              <- MUST STAY LEGAL\n  operation foo + const foo        LOADS CLEAN   kinds=Operation+Const\n  const foo    + const foo         LOADS CLEAN   kinds=Const (second silently merges)\n  entity foo   + operation foo     LOADS CLEAN   kinds=Entity+Operation\n\nTHE RULE ROW IS NOT A REDECLARATION AND MUST NOT BE REFUSED. An operation WITH A BODY is the single source of truth and its equational and relational views are DERIVED from the body by abstract interpretation (WI-580; kernel-language.md lines ~916/~1025, docs/design/abstract-interpreter-and-rules.md §3.3) — nobody hand-writes the twins. A hand-written rule whose head names an operation is a LAW about it (WI-818: a rule is a law, not backing), plus the -family carrier definition-by-cases. MEASURED CONSEQUENCE, and it is the reason the rule row is safe: across the whole stdlib, all 345 operation symbols carry kind  ALONE — not one carries  or . Derivation is why there is nothing to collide with. (Whether a hand-written rule head SHOULD attach to the operation symbol is proposal 044's separate modeling question; do not settle it here.)\n\nSO THE RULE IS NOT 'one name once'. It is: AT MOST ONE  DECLARATION PER NAME PER SCOPE. Two independent reasons it must be scoped that narrowly: the rule row above, and 's DELIBERATE kind accumulation for the sort/namespace case (WI-926/WI-979) — a blanket refusal would break both.\n\nTHE OTHER THREE ROWS ARE THE SAME SILENT-LOSS FAMILY, each needing its own decision and its own evidence, none of them this ticket's blocker:\n  * const+const — a second definition silently lost, structurally identical to the operation case. Likely belongs WITH this ticket if a const carries a per-declaration record the way an operation carries ; CHECK THAT FIRST (there is no  in reflect.anthill, so the analogous instrument may not exist).\n  * operation+const — 's own doc distinguishes the two precisely on being value-denoting, so one symbol that is both makes a bare  and a call  reach different declarations.\n  * entity+operation — the family already has a refusal for the adjacent pair (059 R1 / WI-997 refuses  then ), so this one is a gap in a line that is already drawn.\nCORPUS COST OF REFUSING ALL THREE: ZERO — none of the 345 operation symbols carries Const, Entity, Goal or EquationFunctor.\n\nPROPOSED SPEC SENTENCE (docs/kernel-language.md, §8.7 near the cross-sort ladder): 'An operation name is declared at most once per scope. Anthill has no signature-keyed overloading — a scope maps a name to one symbol, so a second `operation` of the same name does not introduce a second operation; it merges into the first and its signature is lost. Same-named operations on DIFFERENT sorts are not overloading: they are distinct symbols chosen by carrier, per the ladder above. A `rule` whose head names an operation is a law ABOUT it, not a second declaration, and stays legal — an operation with a body is the single source of truth, and its equational and relational views are derived from it (WI-580).'", at: "2026-08-08T07:21:26Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1049", author: "user", content: "DISTINCTNESS DOMAIN — the open question, now MEASURED. Every row below LOADS CLEAN today; the last column is what the one name ends up meaning.\n\n  operation foo + operation foo    one symbol, TWO OperationInfo, order-dependent read   <- THIS TICKET\n  operation foo + rule foo(...)    kinds=Operation — the rule adds NO kind               <- MUST STAY LEGAL\n  operation foo + const foo        kinds=Operation+Const\n  const foo     + const foo        kinds=Const (the second silently merges)\n  entity foo    + operation foo    kinds=Entity+Operation\n\nTHE RULE ROW IS NOT A REDECLARATION AND MUST NOT BE REFUSED — and the reason is symmetric, both halves load-bearing:\n  * an operation WITH a body is the single source of truth, and its equational and relational views are DERIVED from the body by abstract interpretation (WI-580; kernel-language.md ~916/~1025; docs/design/abstract-interpreter-and-rules.md 3.3). Nobody hand-writes the twins.\n  * an operation WITHOUT a body is given meaning BY rules — the [simp] defining equation (an untagged one is inert, CLAUDE.md/WI-881), the eq-family carrier definition-by-cases, and laws generally (WI-818: a rule is a law, not backing).\nSo across both halves a rule whose head names an operation is either derived from it or about it. Never a second declaration of it.\n\nMEASURED CONSEQUENCE, and it is why the rule row is safe: across the whole stdlib all 345 operation symbols carry kind Operation ALONE — not one carries Goal or EquationFunctor. Driven directly: operation foo plus rule foo(...) [simp] in one sort loads clean and the symbol still reads kinds=Operation. A rule attaches clauses under the name without minting a competing declaration. (Whether a hand-written rule head SHOULD attach to the operation symbol is proposal 044's separate modeling question; do not settle it here.)\n\nSO THE RULE IS NOT 'one name once'. THE DISTINCTNESS DOMAIN IS THE DECLARATION RECORD, NOT THE NAME: at most one OperationInfo per symbol. A name may carry one operation declaration plus any number of rules about it. That phrasing is also exactly the instrument — check the records, not the names — and it is why the check must not live in pass 1 where only names exist. Two further reasons it must be scoped this narrowly: the rule row above, and define's DELIBERATE kind accumulation for the sort/namespace case (WI-926/WI-979).\n\nTHE OTHER THREE ROWS ARE THE SAME SILENT-LOSS FAMILY, each needing its own decision and its own evidence, none of them this ticket's blocker:\n  * const+const — a second definition silently lost, structurally identical to the operation case. Belongs WITH this ticket only if a const carries a per-declaration record the way an operation carries OperationInfo; CHECK FIRST — there is no ConstInfo in reflect.anthill, so the analogous instrument may not exist and the fix would differ.\n  * operation+const — SymbolKind::Const's own doc distinguishes the two precisely on being value-denoting, so one symbol that is both makes a bare foo and a call foo(x) reach different declarations.\n  * entity+operation — the family already has a refusal for the adjacent pair (059 R1 / WI-997 refuses entity X then sort X), so this is a gap in a line already drawn.\nCORPUS COST OF REFUSING ALL THREE: ZERO — none of the 345 operation symbols carries Const, Entity, Goal or EquationFunctor.\n\nPROPOSED SPEC SENTENCE (docs/kernel-language.md 8.7, near the cross-sort ladder): \"An operation name is declared at most once per scope. Anthill has no signature-keyed overloading — a scope maps a name to one symbol, so a second `operation` of the same name does not introduce a second operation; it merges into the first and its signature is lost. Same-named operations on DIFFERENT sorts are not overloading: they are distinct symbols chosen by carrier, per the ladder above. A `rule` whose head names an operation is not a second declaration and stays legal: for an operation with a body the equational and relational views are derived from that body (WI-580), and for a body-less one the rules are what give it meaning (WI-818, WI-881).\"\n", at: "2026-08-08T07:22:07Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1050", author: "user", content: "DESIGN NOTE (from the WI-1049 conversation) — THE FIRING SITE IS ALREADY IN THE TYPER, SO THE SUBSTITUTED ROW IS ALREADY IN HAND. This ticket's framing said the ingredient 'exists'; it is stronger than that. docs/design/simp-rewrite-design.md states the engine is 'type-directed, integrated with the typer' with 'the typer over op-body occurrences' as a call site (§1, §4.3), and §6 makes firing conditional on the RECEIVER'S SORT: 'a sort/spec rule applies iff min_sort(receiver) <: S (conformance, transitive)'. So at the moment a rewrite is considered, the carrier is known and sort-conformance is ALREADY being computed. The effect row sigma-instantiates off that same carrier. Checking the SUBSTITUTED row is therefore not a new mechanism at a new place — it is one more condition at a site that already resolves the carrier for a different reason.\n\nThat also answers 'where should this live': DURING TYPING, beside the existing conformance step — not at load-time formation (today), and not in the resolver. Note the doc's own §4.2 caveat: `apply_eq_rules` (resolve.rs) is the OTHER call site running the same rewriter over Term goals at compile time, and §4.4 says the semantic decisions belong to the SHARED CORE so both sites stay consistent. Any effect-row condition must go in that shared core, or the two sites will disagree — which is exactly the class of defect WI-1016/WI-860 record ('both carriers must key alike', 'two derivations must agree').\n\nSTILL OPEN, and the reason a brainstorm was asked for rather than a straight implementation: the relation between RULES and EFFECTS is not settled anywhere as a whole. The pieces are scattered — 054 'Consumers that must decline it' (formation-time refusal, argued for a CONCRETE External row), WI-881/CLAUDE.md ('[simp] is the enablement'; an untagged equation is inert), WI-818 ('a rule is a law, not backing'), WI-580 (an operation WITH a body derives its equational and relational views, so its rules are generated not written), and the row model (045, WI-357/WI-365 grounding at the consumption boundary). Questions they do not jointly answer: may a LAW (untagged, inert) mention an effectful operation at all, given it never rewrites? Does the answer differ for [simp] (rewrites, so duplicates/drops) versus [unfold] versus a bare law cited by `using` in a proof? Is an effect-polymorphic operation's law admissible as a CONDITIONAL equation (fires only where the row grounds to {}) — the shape 054 §3.4.1 already floats for `requires` ('carrying the dictionary as an equation antecedent is a future relaxation')? Settle those before writing the check, or the check will encode one answer by accident.", at: "2026-08-08T08:24:13Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1050", author: "user", content: "SHARPENING FROM THE BRAINSTORM (Q1) — 'THE INSTANTIATED ROW IS {}' AND 'THE INSTANTIATED ROW IS SAFE TO DROP A CALL FOR' ARE DIFFERENT CONDITIONS. This ticket proposes deciding the gate on the SUBSTITUTED row. Before implementing, note that emptiness is not the only axis, because effects are not uniform for this question:\n\n  * CONTROL effects — Error (`operation raise(error: T) -> Nothing`, and `sort Nothing` is UNINHABITED, so on the raising path there is NO VALUE), Branch (027: zero-or-many), non-termination. Rewriting `isEmpty(insert(?c,?x)) -> false` over an Error-carrying `insert` does not merely lose a side effect: it turns a program that RAISES into one that ANSWERS false. A WRONG ANSWER, not a lost effect.\n  * VALUE-PRESERVING effects — allocation, logging, Modify on unrelated state. The LHS always denotes; dropping the call loses the effect but not the answer.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR THIS TICKET, both directions:\n  * it makes the CURRENT refusal of the polymorphic case stronger, not weaker — a row VARIABLE can be instantiated to Error, so today's refusal is protecting against the control-effect case, not just being cautious about an effect running zero times;\n  * and it means a relaxation keyed on 'row grounds to {}' is the SOUND one, while a more permissive 'row contains no control effect' is NOT obviously sound for a rewrite that drops a call (the dropped effect still does not happen). Do not reach for the permissive form as an 'obvious' generalization.\n\nTHE HONEST TARGET for anyone who wants this law over an effectful carrier is a DIFFERENT EQUATION: the effect-preserving form — run the insert, then answer false — where both sides raise identically and nothing is dropped. Whether `<=>` can be given that sequencing reading is open (docs/brainstorms/rules-and-effects.md Q1/Q6). The conditional shape is not a new mechanism either: guarded effect atoms already exist (`effects { s.E, Error[EmptyStream] :- isEmpty(s) }` on Stream.head / List.head), which is the same shape pointed the other way.\n\nSEE docs/brainstorms/rules-and-effects.md — Q1 (which effects remove the value), Q3 (polymorphic as a third answer), Q4 (where the check goes, given firing is already type-directed), Q6 (what a law is FOR). Q6's dichotomy 'executable vs specification' gained a middle term from Q1: even as pure specification a law over a control-effecting op holds only on the defined path, so a law over an effectful operation needs a READING (total / partial / effect-preserving) before it needs a gate. Pick the reading first; what to check follows.", at: "2026-08-08T08:37:59Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1050", author: "user", content: "THE SURFACE CANNOT SPELL THE SOUND LAW — which narrows this ticket and moves the real request elsewhere. Worked out in docs/brainstorms/rules-and-effects.md Q7/Q8.\n\nIn direct style `isEmpty(insert(c,x))` is not an application of isEmpty to a value; when insert is effectful it is `insert(c,x) >>= \\c' -> isEmpty(c')` — the bind is IMPLICIT IN THE NESTING. An effect operation is exactly one that may OMIT that bind: `raise` never invokes the continuation (`Err v >>= f = Err v`), `Branch` may invoke it zero or many times.\n\nAnd the matcher cannot reach it. simp-rewrite-design.md §'Matcher ≠ typer': 'a [simp] rule LHS is a functor-application pattern'. Matching `isEmpty(insert(?c, ?x))` binds ?c and ?x and NOT the continuation — in direct style the continuation is not a subterm, it is the nesting itself. So the RHS can only be built from ?c, ?x and constants, while the unconditionally-sound (fully effect-preserving) form needs to name `\\c' -> ...`. THE ONLY LAWS OF THIS SHAPE THE SYNTAX CAN EXPRESS ARE THE ONES THAT DISCARD THE BIND.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR THIS TICKET — it stays worth doing, and it stays narrow. Deciding the gate on the SUBSTITUTED row can only ever admit the case where the row grounds to {}, i.e. exactly where there is no bind to preserve. That is consistent with all of the above and is the whole of what a row check can buy. Do NOT let the ticket grow into 'admit laws over effectful operations' — that is not a gate change, it is a surface change (a way to write the bind, or a matcher that binds the continuation), and 047's reflect/reify is where it would start since that is the only place the monad is already spelled.\n\nALSO WORTH NAMING ONCE, because this ticket will touch the site: 054's three verbs are one property. DROPS = the bind is invoked zero times (Error, empty Branch); DUPLICATES = more than once (Branch); REORDERS = order-sensitive (state). So 'duplicates, reorders, or drops' is one condition — THE BIND IS INVOKED EXACTLY ONCE, IN PLACE — and it is the same condition 054's decline list needs for its other consumers ('any future memoization or CSE joins the same decline list'). If this ticket introduces a per-effect classification, classify on THAT property rather than enumerating verbs per consumer.", at: "2026-08-08T08:41:36Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1050", author: "user", content: "Q2 (does the tag matter?) — YES, and the axis is WHICH QUANTITY IS KNOWN WHERE. Recorded from the conversation; the proof case is the one with a concrete proposal attached.\n\n  * [simp] — fires DURING TYPING at a redex, so the carrier is known and the row is substitutable (Q4). But Q8: the rewrite must DROP the bind and the surface cannot spell the preserving form, so even with the row known the rewrite is sound only where the row is {} — i.e. where there is no bind to preserve.\n  * [unfold] — fired by the RESOLVER, not the typer. WI-757 measured exactly this asymmetry: keyed on `is_macro` alone, an effectful macro under [unfold] loaded clean and rewrote the effectful call into the program. Whether the carrier is known at that site is the open question for this arm, and it is NOT answered by [simp]'s answer.\n  * a bare law CITED IN A PROOF via `using` — NOTHING IS EXECUTED. 054's whole hazard is about a rewritten PROGRAM: duplicates/reorders/drops are properties of running the call. A proof step runs nothing, so that argument does not reach this use at all. What the proof use needs instead is only that the law be TRUE at the instantiation — and Q1 says a value equation over a CONTROL effect holds only on the defined path, while over an empty row it holds unconditionally.\n\nCONCRETE PROPOSAL for the proof arm: at the citation the carrier is concrete, so for `List` the row grounds to {} and the value equation is unconditionally true — so the citation is sound. Rather than checking that ad hoc, GENERATE THE OBLIGATION: emit an `Obligation` (anthill.realization; kernel-language.md §\"The kernel generates proof obligations — facts of entity type Obligation\") saying 'at this instantiation the row is {}', and let it be discharged at the usual trust levels. That turns the blanket formation refusal into a per-use, per-carrier obligation — which is the conditional-equation shape from Q3, discharged by the proof system instead of by a bespoke check. It also keeps the loud channel Q4 demands: an undischarged obligation is visible, where a silently-non-firing rewrite is not.\n\nVALUE OF A SMALL WORKED EXAMPLE, and it should be part of whatever lands: take the law on `List`, show the obligation generated, show it discharged, and show the same law over an effectful carrier producing an obligation that CANNOT be discharged. That is the non-vacuous form — it drives the mechanism rather than asserting a rule loaded.\n\nSTATE OF THE EXAMPLE, corrected by driving (this ticket's earlier text and my summary both had it wrong): `List` DOES declare its own `insert` — `operation insert(c: List, elem: T) -> List = cons(head: elem, tail: c)` at list.anthill:254, with a body and NO effects clause, so it is pure and passes the gate. What `List` does not declare is `isEmpty`; it imports `Stream.{isEmpty}`, whose row is `s.E` — the RECEIVER's effect parameter, projected. So the law placed inside `List` is refused ONCE, on `Stream.isEmpty` alone, not twice. WI-1049 has been extended to classify that path-dependent projection as Polymorphic rather than Effectful (it read the head only and so mis-called it), and the law is TRUE for List by construction — `insert` prepends a `cons`, so the result is never `nil`.", at: "2026-08-08T08:57:40Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1050", author: "user", content: "DECISION (2026-08-08) — DEFERRED, POST-RELEASE. Do not implement before release.\n\nQ3 is settled the other way from this ticket's framing: EFFECT-POLYMORPHIC => POTENTIALLY EFFECTFUL. Effect-polymorphism translates to effect. There may one day be effect-ELIMINATION rules that discharge the polymorphic case, but they are not known, and until they are, 'may be an effect' and 'is an effect' are ONE CATEGORY for every consumer that must decline. The union is named POTENTIALLY EFFECTFUL and that is what the gate refuses — one verdict, not two.\n\nWhat this ticket proposed (decide the gate on the SUBSTITUTED row) therefore survives only in one shape: A MODE THAT APPLIES [simp] AFTER TYPING, where effects are eliminated and the row is ground. That is the only place the polymorphic arm could earn a different verdict, and it is explicitly future work — NOT before release.\n\nALREADY LANDED, and it is the part that was worth doing now (WI-1049): the two cases are told apart in the DIAGNOSTIC while staying one verdict. `EquationBlock::Effectful` vs `::Polymorphic`, with the shared predicate named 'potentially effectful' in the message and the per-arm sentence explaining which and what to do. That was the actual defect — the gate called `PersistentCollection.insert` effectful when it is not — and it is fixed without touching the verdict. The classification also covers the path-dependent projection spelling (`effects s.E`, Stream.isEmpty).\n\nSO THE REMAINING SCOPE OF THIS TICKET IS THE POST-TYPING SIMP MODE, and nothing else. Everything about wording, classification and the s.E spelling is done. Whoever picks this up after release should re-read docs/brainstorms/rules-and-effects.md first — in particular Q8, which argues the surface CANNOT SPELL the sound (effect-preserving) law at all, so even a post-typing mode can only admit the row-grounds-to-{} case. That bounds the payoff and should be weighed before building it.", at: "2026-08-08T09:04:46Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1051", description: some(value: "DO WE TRACK AN ABSTRACT GAMMA DURING ABSTRACT INTERPRETATION? Open design question, deliberately PRE-OPENED — the answer shapes effect elimination and probably more, but the questions ahead of it (docs/brainstorms/rules-and-effects.md) are not settled and this should not be built first.\n\nTHE QUESTION. WI-580 derives an operation's equational and relational views from its BODY by abstract interpretation. WI-067 (delivered) discharges a guarded effect by REFUTING its guard from Gamma — the flow-sensitive logical environment — plus ground eval and the KB. Gamma's producers today are `if`-branch conditions, `match` arm facts, and match guards (WI-537/WI-550). The question is whether the abstract interpreter should itself carry and propagate a Gamma: an environment of facts about the intermediate values it computes, so that what it LEARNS while interpreting a body becomes available to guard refutation and to any other consumer that reasons about a term's value.\n\nTHE MEASUREMENT THAT RAISES IT (this session, driven):\n\n    operation head(xs: List) -> xs.T effects { Error[EmptyStream] :- isEmpty(xs) }   -- list.anthill:223\n    operation insert(c: List, elem: T) -> List = cons(head: elem, tail: c)           -- list.anthill:254\n\n    operation probe(xs: List[T = Int64], e: Int64) -> Int64 = xs.insert(e).head()\n      -> error: type mismatch in probe.effects (op-effects):\n         expected declared: [], got undeclared effect: Error[T = EmptyStream]\n\n`insert` prepends a `cons`, so `isEmpty(insert(xs,e))` is FALSE, so the guard is refuted, so the Error is not in the row — a guarded atom is 'present iff `guard` is not refuted' (node_occurrence.rs, proposal 048 / WI-478). It does not discharge, and the reason is NOT a missing refutation engine: the expression has no `if` and no `match`, so Gamma has no producer and says nothing about the argument. The fact needed — that `insert(xs,e)` IS a `cons` — is exactly WI-580's body-derived equation for `insert`, which nothing carries into Gamma.\n\nSO THE NARROW VERSION is: can `refute_guard` / `prove_from_gamma` reach a callee's body-derived equation for an operation in ARGUMENT position? And the general version, which is the one worth deciding first: is Gamma a thing the abstract interpreter MAINTAINS as it goes (learning `v = cons(...)` when it interprets `insert(xs,e)`), or does it stay a purely syntactic flow environment fed only by branch conditions?\n\nWHY IT IS BIGGER THAN EFFECT DISCHARGE — and why it is pre-opened rather than open: an abstract Gamma is a shared substrate, so its shape should be chosen against several consumers at once, not retrofitted for one. Known consumers: effect discharge (WI-067 tier 1); the value-precondition check (WI-539/WI-602, which already distinguishes a REFUTED obligation from a FLOATING one — the WI-067/WI-292 polarity, 'act on a DECIDED obligation, never on an UNDETERMINED one'); in-body proofs (WI-538); and the `[simp]` firing site, which is type-directed and already resolves the carrier. Note WI-537's two DEFERRED Gamma producers are the same shape of gap — a `let x = e` binding fact has no indexable Gamma form because a binder ref is `Expr::VarRef` -> `ViewHead::Opaque`, and binder/constructor pattern facts need a reified form (050 open question C). An abstract Gamma would have to answer those too.\n\nRELATED, and possibly the same design: `has_effect` / `hasno_effect` as PREDICATES in the rule vocabulary (rules-and-effects.md Q4a). Guarded effects already condition an EFFECT on a PREDICATE; the dual — conditioning a RULE on the ABSENCE of an effect — has no spelling, and would need the row in a reified, queryable form. That is the same reification an abstract Gamma would want, and the same one Q7's 'both sides of an equation carry the same row' needs. Three consumers, one representation question — decide it once.\n\nBEFORE STARTING: read docs/brainstorms/rules-and-effects.md. Q3 is DECIDED (effect-polymorphic => potentially effectful, one verdict; the post-typing simp mode is WI-1050, post-release). Q8 argues the direct-style surface cannot spell an effect-preserving law at all, which bounds what any of this buys for REWRITING — the payoff is likelier in proofs and in discharge than in simp. REFERENCE: WI-580 + docs/design/abstract-interpreter-and-rules.md §3.3; WI-067 (the two-tier discharge) + proposal 048; WI-537/WI-538/WI-539/WI-550/WI-602 (Gamma substrate and consumers); proposal 050 (local interpretation) open question C; WI-1049/WI-1050."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: PreOpened)
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1051", author: "user", content: "CLARIFICATION on the has_effect / hasno_effect note: the subject is the EFFECT ROW and the question is BINARY — the row is PRESENT (non-empty) or ABSENT (empty). Not 'does it carry effect E'; per-label questions are a different, later thing.\n\nTwo consequences that make it cheaper and more coherent than it first reads:\n  * IT REIFIES A PREDICATE THAT ALREADY EXISTS INTERNALLY. 'Is this row empty?' is exactly what `effect_row_blocking_equations` computes today to decide the [simp] gate. Exposing it is a reification, not a new analysis.\n  * ITS POLARITY REPRODUCES THE Q3 DECISION rather than fighting it. A binary predicate has THREE outcomes in practice — present, absent, and UNDETERMINED — and the undetermined case is precisely the polymorphic row (`E` is neither provably empty nor provably non-empty until a carrier binds it). Under the established polarity, 'act on a DECIDED obligation, never on an UNDETERMINED one' (WI-602; WI-067/WI-292), NEITHER has_effect NOR hasno_effect succeeds there. That is the same refusal Q3 decided, reached from the predicate's own discipline instead of by a special case.\n\nStill open: whether it is a genuine predicate over terms (needing the typer's row as a QUERYABLE VALUE) or sugar for an obligation. Either way it gives the row a REIFIED form — which is also what Q7's 'both sides of an equation carry the same row' needs, and what an abstract Gamma would want. Three consumers, one representation question; design it once. See docs/brainstorms/rules-and-effects.md Q4a.", at: "2026-08-08T09:52:36Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1051", author: "user", content: "CORRECTION to the has_effect/hasno_effect note — THERE ARE TWO PREDICATES, NOT ONE, and the earlier example conflated them.\n\nThe example written first was `hasno_effect(head(insert(?x, ?e)))` with no effect argument. That is wrong for the very case that motivated it: `List.head`'s row is `{ Error[EmptyStream] :- isEmpty(xs), s.E }`, so even once the guard is refuted `s.E` remains. The whole-row claim is FALSE there, while the thing actually wanted — that Error[EmptyStream] is gone — is TRUE.\n\n  (a) PER-LABEL:  hasno_effect(head(insert(?x, ?e)), Error[EmptyStream])\n      Is this effect in the term's row? Drops one atom, leaves the rest alone.\n\n  (b) WHOLE-ROW:  row_empty(insert(?c, ?x))\n      Is the row empty at all?\n\nWHICH CONSUMER WANTS WHICH, so the choice is not made by accident:\n  * guarded-effect discharge (WI-067, the head(insert(x,e)) case) -> (a). It removes ONE atom; the rest of the row must survive.\n  * the [simp] formation gate -> (b). Nothing may be dropped, so the row must be empty. NOTE this is the one that already exists internally as `effect_row_blocking_equations`.\n  * a law's precondition in the Q1 sense -> (b), since the law is about discarding the whole computation.\n  * Q7's 'both sides of an equation carry the same row' -> NEITHER: that needs row EQUALITY, a third thing again.\n\n(b) is expressible as (a) universally quantified, or kept primitive — worth deciding, since (b) is the one already computed.\n\nALSO: the argument is a TERM, ANALYSED, not a value computed. `hasno_effect` asks about a term's TYPING, so it is a REFLECTIVE predicate — nearer anthill.reflect than an ordinary goal. Not alien here (occurrence-valued operations and the Term/KB reflect surface exist), but it decides where the predicate lives and what it may be applied to, and it is the same reification question this ticket already carries.", at: "2026-08-08T09:55:05Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1051", author: "user", content: "THE GUARDED DECLARATION IS ALREADY THE DEFINITION — so the per-label predicate needs no new analysis, and this is the strongest argument for the reification.\n\nFrom the stdlib verbatim (int64.anthill:65):\n    operation div(a: Int64, b: Int64) -> Int64 effects { Error[DivisionByZero] :- eq(b, 0) }\nreads directly as\n    effect_present(div(?a, ?b), Error[DivisionByZero]) :- eq(?b, 0)      -- the declaration, verbatim\n    effect_absent(div(?a, ?b), Error[DivisionByZero])  :- neq(?b, 0)     -- its REFUTATION\n\nMIND THE POLARITY: the absence side carries the REFUTATION of the guard, not the guard. `Err :- eq(b,0)` says the error is present WHEN b = 0, so absence is conditioned on neq(b, 0). Inverting this would license dropping the error exactly where it fires. (The conversation's sketch had it as `:- y = 0`; the correct reading is `:- neq(y, 0)`.)\n\nAnd it must be CONSTRUCTIVE. WI-067 already decided this in the same words — 'drop the guarded element only on a positive proof of ¬G (never NAF)' — and framed the whole mechanism as 'SLD refutation over the effect-row-as-Horn-theory'. So this is not a new predicate so much as A NAME FOR ONE WI-067's DISCHARGE ALREADY COMPUTES.\n\nWHAT THE HORN READING DOES NOT COVER IS EXACTLY THE Q3 BOUNDARY, which is a consistency check on both: a GUARDED atom has a clause; an UNGUARDED atom (s.E, {External}) has none, so there is nothing to refute; a row VARIABLE has none either. Both stay UNDETERMINED, and under never-NAF neither predicate succeeds there. The Horn reading buys the guarded case and leaves the polymorphic case exactly where Q3 put it.\n\nNAMING: has_effect/hasno_effect are placeholders and should not survive. Prefer effect_present / effect_absent — 'present' is already this system's own word for this property ('present iff `guard` is not refuted', node_occurrence.rs, proposal 048), so it keeps one vocabulary rather than minting a second for the same idea. Caveat any name must survive: the pair is NOT a simple positive/negative — effect_absent requires a positive refutation, so it means 'provably cannot occur', and there is a third state (undetermined) where neither holds. A name suggesting plain negation (not_has_effect) would mislead. If the modality should be visible in the name, effect_possible / effect_impossible says it outright at the cost of length.", at: "2026-08-08T09:57:00Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1051", author: "user", content: "NAMING DECIDED: the predicates are effect_present(X, E) / effect_absent(X, E). The has_effect / hasno_effect spelling used earlier in this ticket's description and feedback is superseded — read it as effect_present / effect_absent throughout.\n\nReason recorded once so it is not re-litigated: 'present' is already this system's own word for exactly this property — a guarded atom is 'present iff `guard` is not refuted' (node_occurrence.rs, proposal 048). Reusing it keeps one vocabulary instead of minting a second for the same idea.\n\nAnd the caveat the name must not obscure: the pair is NOT a plain positive/negative. effect_absent requires a POSITIVE REFUTATION of the guard (WI-067: 'never NAF'), so it means 'provably cannot occur', not 'not observed' — and there is a third state, UNDETERMINED, where neither holds, which is exactly the unguarded/polymorphic case. A name implying simple negation (not_effect_present) would mislead about that third state. If the modality should be visible in the name itself, effect_possible / effect_impossible says it outright at the cost of length; effect_present / effect_absent was chosen for vocabulary continuity instead.", at: "2026-08-08T09:58:55Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1050", description: some(value: "CHECK A [simp]/[unfold] RULE'S EFFECT ROWS AFTER EFFECT SUBSTITUTION, NOT AT THE DECLARATION. Today the formation gate (check_simp_effectful_ops -> effect_row_blocking_equations) reads the operation's DECLARED row. For an effect-POLYMORPHIC operation that row is a VARIABLE, so the gate refuses laws that are valid at every carrier the corpus actually has.\n\nMEASURED, driving proposal 054's own example on the real stdlib (rebuild required — the CLI embeds the stdlib via include_str!, so an edit without `cargo build` is invisible):\n\n    -- in sort anthill.prelude.PersistentCollection\n    rule isEmpty(insert(?c, ?x)) <=> false          -- LOADS CLEAN (inert law)\n    rule isEmpty(insert(?c, ?x)) <=> false [simp]   -- REFUSED, twice\n\nRefused naming `PersistentCollection.insert` (row {Effect}) and `Iterable.isEmpty` (row {E}). NEITHER IS EFFECTFUL: `effects Effect = ?` / `effects E = ?` declare row VARIABLES, and list.anthill documents its provision as PURE — 'List satisfies PersistentCollection (pure). Effect left unbound by design ... grounded to {} at the consumption boundary by the typer (WI-357 concrete carrier; WI-365 abstract self-receiver)'.\n\nWHAT WAS ALREADY FIXED, so this ticket is not about wording: WI-1049 taught the predicate to tell the two cases apart (`EquationBlock::Effectful` vs `::Polymorphic`) and the message now says 'is effect-POLYMORPHIC — its row {Effect} is a row VARIABLE, not an effect', and points at the repair. The VERDICT is unchanged. This ticket is about the verdict.\n\nWHY THE CURRENT VERDICT IS DEFENSIBLE — state it before changing it. A rule declared on the SPEC binds EVERY carrier, including ones not yet written. Nothing stops a carrier providing an effectful `insert` (one that logs, or allocates through External). Then the rewrite DROPS the matched call and the effect runs zero times instead of once. At the declaration site the row is genuinely unknown, so refusing is sound. `effect_row_blocking_equations`' doc has always said the polymorphic case is over-refused KNOWINGLY.\n\nTHE PROPOSAL: decide the gate on the SUBSTITUTED row. The ingredient exists — [simp] already requires a STATICALLY KNOWN CARRIER to fire (set.anthill: laws fire 'once the redex's carrier is known to provide Set'), and with the carrier known the spec op's row sigma-instantiates. A rewrite whose instantiated row is {} is sound; one whose row is non-empty is declined, at that redex.\n\n054 HAS A STATED POSITION AGAINST THIS AND IT MUST BE ANSWERED, NOT IGNORED — 'the simp rewriter (fire_simp) checks no effect rows at the firing site — sound today only because effectful ops never *become* simp equations (formation is gated as above). The one real hole is a user-written [simp]-tagged equation rule whose sides mention an External-rowed operation symbol ... The gate for that belongs at load-time tag validation, not the firing site.' NOTE ITS SCOPE: that argument is about an op whose row is CONCRETELY External. The row-VARIABLE case is not discussed in 054 at all; the over-refusal is admitted only in a Rust doc comment. So this is not a reversal of 054's reasoning — it is the case 054 did not consider. Say which in the delivery.\n\nTHE HAZARD TO DESIGN AGAINST, explicitly: moving a check from formation to firing turns one loud load-time refusal into a per-redex decision, and a decision not to fire is SILENT. An author who writes the law expecting it to apply on an effectful carrier would get no rewrite and no diagnostic — the silent-skip the repo forbids. Whatever form this takes must keep a loud channel for 'this redex declined and here is why'.\n\nWHAT DOES NOT WORK, measured, so nobody re-tries it: binding the row at the PROVISION does not help. `fact PersistentCollection[List[T], T, Effect = {}]` loads clean, and the same law is still refused with the same two errors — because the gate keys on the OPERATION symbol's declared row, which is carrier-independent, while purity here is per-carrier. (Whether that binding is even RECORDED or silently dropped is unverified and worth checking on its own — proposal 045 says a closed effect is not a type argument.)\n\nWHAT WORKS TODAY, measured, and is the current repair the message names: declare the equation on a carrier that declares its OWN pure operations — a sort with `operation insert(...) -> ...` / `isEmpty(...)` and no effects clause carries `rule isEmpty(insert(?c, ?x)) <=> false [simp]` cleanly. The gap is that `List` does NOT declare its own `insert`/`isEmpty` — it inherits the spec's — so in the stdlib as it stands there is nowhere to put this law where it would fire.\n\nACCEPTANCE: the law above, declared on `PersistentCollection`, is accepted and FIRES for a pure carrier — driven, asserting the rewritten result, not that it loads; the same law still DECLINES for a carrier whose `insert` has a concrete effect, and that decline is LOUD, not a silent non-firing; the concretely-External case (054's own) keeps its load-time refusal — that is the control that this did not weaken the gate 054 asked for; `wi1049_effect_polymorphic_diagnostic_test` still passes or is updated with its reason; each test says which case fails when the change is backed out. cargo-test green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. REFERENCE: proposal 054 'Consumers that must decline it — loudly' (WI-698/WI-702); WI-1049 (the classification and the message); WI-357/WI-365 (row grounding at the consumption boundary); WI-881 (an untagged equation is inert); proposal 045 (the row model)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: PreOpened)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1049", description: some(value: "A LOAD MUST BE A FUNCTION OF THE PROGRAM, NOT OF ITS SPELLING ORDER — AND FOR A SORT DECLARING ONE OPERATION NAME TWICE, IT IS NOT.\n\nMEASURED, on a sort declaring `q_op(x: T, y: T) -> T` and `q_op(x: T) -> T`:\n  * both declarations resolve to the SAME `Symbol`;\n  * TWO `anthill.reflect.OperationInfo` facts carry that one name;\n  * `op_info::lookup_operation_info` returns whichever it reaches first, so the SAME sort answers arity=2 when the 2-ary is written first and arity=1 when the 1-ary is. The load is CLEAN either way — nothing warns, nothing errors.\nSwapping two declarations that the language says nothing about must not change an answer. That is the property this ticket is about; the refusal below is the instrument.\n\nDO WE SUPPORT OVERLOADING? TWO THINGS WEAR THAT NAME, AND ONLY ONE IS OURS.\n  * ACROSS SORTS — `List.map` / `Stream.map` / `FiniteCollection.map`: YES, and it is not really overloading. These are distinct symbols with distinct qualified names, chosen by CARRIER, and §8.7's ladder specifies the choice. Proposal 011 OQ4.2 calls it 'overloading across sorts'. This is WI-1048's whole subject.\n  * WITHIN ONE SCOPE, keyed on signature: NO, AND IT IS NOT REPRESENTABLE. `SymbolTable::define` (intern.rs ~544) states the invariant in its own comment — 'one name in one scope is still one symbol' (WI-926) — and `by_qualified_name` is a 1:1 map. An operation IS its qualified name. A second declaration therefore does not overload; it silently merges into the first symbol and leaves an orphan `OperationInfo` behind.\n\nMEASURED DEMAND FOR IT: ZERO. 345 operation names across stdlib, stdlib+examples/github-todo and stdlib+anthill-todo — 0 carrying more than one `OperationInfo`. An independent SOURCE scan finds exactly three repeated operation names in the stdlib (`splitFirst`, `collect`, `iterator`) and ALL THREE are cross-sort pairs (`MappedStream`/`FilteredStream`, `FiniteMappedStream`/`FiniteFilteredStream`) — carrier dispatch, not overloading. Nothing in the corpus overloads accidentally or otherwise.\n\nIF IT WERE EVER WANTED, THE KEY IS ARITY, NOT ARGUMENT TYPES — and the key is the cheap part. Arity is known at declaration, needs no type information, and is already materialized (`op_info::declared_arity`). Argument types are not tractable HERE specifically, for reasons the language itself creates: types carry logical variables and unify, there is subtyping and effect-row structure, and dispatch DELAYS on unbound vars — so overload selection would have to run before or during the very unification that determines the argument types (proposal 011 OQ7.1 already names 'overload resolution failure' as the failure mode). A parameter can also be a `Value::Node` arrow with no `TermId` at all (a `denoted` `-Modify[x]` callback), which a type-keyed index cannot key — measured in WI-1048. But EITHER key costs the name->symbol invariant that the whole reflect layer is built on (`OperationInfo(name: Symbol)`, `scope(?name, ?S)`, `KB.operations`), and the carrier dimension already absorbs the demand overloading exists to serve. RECOMMENDATION: do not add it. Refuse the duplicate.\n\nWHY ORDER-INDEPENDENCE ALONE IS NOT ENOUGH: making `lookup_operation_info` deterministic (always first, always last) would restore the property and still leave one written `operation` silently unreachable — a program where a declaration does not exist and nothing says so. Refusal gives BOTH: the same error in either order, and no silent loss.\n\nWANTED: refuse the second declaration at load, LOUDLY. 'Make illegal state unrepresentable', same family as WI-805 (duplicate tuple label), WI-808 (entity field distinct), WI-809 (named-arg distinct) — and it needs their treatment: a sentence in docs/kernel-language.md saying an operation name is declared at most once per scope (the spec is currently SILENT on this; §8.7 specifies only the cross-sort ladder), plus a doc-commented enforcement site.\n\nTHE INSTRUMENT — corrected from this ticket's first draft. Do NOT check 'the symbol table already has this name' in `scan_definitions` pass 1. The prelude bootstrap PRE-DEFINES operation symbols (`kb.symbols.define(\"eq\", \"anthill.prelude.PartialEq.eq\", ...)`, load.rs ~4563, WI-718/WI-967) and the stdlib source then declares `operation eq` into that same scope, so a state-based pass-1 check fires on every pre-registered prelude op. Check instead, as a WHOLE-KB pass beside `check_requires_shadows`: AT MOST ONE `OperationInfo` FACT PER OPERATION SYMBOL. That is immune to the bootstrap by construction, and MEASURED so — pre-registration emits no `OperationInfo`, and `PartialEq.eq` / `Ord.compare` / `Numeric.add` each read exactly 1 after a full stdlib load. It also needs no pass-1 bookkeeping and survives the same file being scanned twice (the CLI embeds the stdlib).\n\nOPEN QUESTION for whoever takes it: what is the distinctness DOMAIN — operations only, or every member name in a sort body (operation vs entity vs sub-sort vs const)? The spec should say, since §6.3's free-standing-entity spelling makes the entity/sort boundary a live question. Note `define` deliberately ACCUMULATES categories for the sort/namespace case (WI-926/WI-979), so the answer is not 'refuse every re-declaration'.\n\nALSO MEASURED, and NOT a defect — recorded so nobody re-investigates: the load is already independent of FILE order. Reversing the stdlib file list yields identical fact counts, rule counts, warnings and errors. WI-321's 4-pass `scan_definitions` is what buys that. The live order-dependence is DECLARATION order within a scope, which is this ticket. A corpus-scale pin for the file-order half does not exist yet and would be cheap.\n\nACCEPTANCE: a sort with two same-named operations is a LOAD ERROR naming the sort, the name, and both spans; the prelude and the full stdlib still load clean (the control that the bootstrap was not caught); a test drives BOTH declaration orders and gets the SAME refusal — the WI-979 idiom, where the two fixtures are identical but for order and that is the whole experiment; each test says which case fails when the change is backed out. `cargo-test` green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. REFERENCE: WI-1048 (found it); WI-926/WI-979 (the one-name-one-symbol invariant and its category accumulation); WI-805/808/809 (the distinctness family); WI-718/WI-967 (bootstrap pre-registration); WI-321 (file-order independence); proposal 011 OQ4.2/OQ7.1."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-08-08T10:54:50Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1052", description: some(value: "`types_definitely_differ` CLAIMS A PROOF IT DOES NOT HAVE: A SORT ALIAS AND ITS EXPANSION READ AS 'DEFINITELY DIFFERENT', SO THE WI-346 SHADOW ADVISORY IS SILENTLY SUPPRESSED ON A GENUINE COLLISION.\n\nWI-1048 gave the requires-shadow lint a two-sided contract: `types_definitely_differ` returning true is a PROOF the two types cannot be reconciled by any instantiation, and everything else is UNDECIDED (fail open, warn). `type_ctor_view` (typing.rs ~22440) implements the read as a head-constructor comparison and does NOT resolve sort aliases, so `IntList` and `List[T = Int64]` compare as different constructors. That is a FALSE PROOF, and it fails CLOSED — the warning the lint exists to emit is dropped.\n\nMEASURED, with a control (found by /code-review during WI-1049):\n\n    sort IntList = List[T = Int64]\n    sort ASpec    sort T = ?   operation a_op(x: T) -> List[T = Int64]  end\n    sort AReq     requires ASpec[T = ACar]\n                  operation a_op(x: ACar) -> IntList = ?   end\n\n  * `anthill check` prints NO shadow warning.\n  * CONTROL: spell the return type `List[T = Int64]` instead of `IntList` — semantically the same type — and the warning appears:\n    'operation `a_op` in `probe.alias.AReq` shadows the inherited …'.\nSo the verdict turns on the SPELLING of a type, which is the property WI-1048 was built to stop turning on (its whole subject is that a name match alone must not decide).\n\nTHE SHAPE OF THE FIX, and the two traps in it. `resolve_sort_alias` (typing.rs ~30884, `&KnowledgeBase`, O(1) off the WI-659 index) already answers 'what does this name expand to', and is used a few lines away in `type_param_sym_of_binding` — so the ingredient is in reach and `type_ctor_view` has exactly ONE call site. Traps: (1) follow the alias only for the BARE-NAME spelling (`Ref`/`Ident`/nullary `Fn`) — an alias head carrying arguments would lose them if the expansion simply replaced the term, and undecided is the safe answer there; (2) bound the chain (alias-to-alias) and say what the bound is, rather than recursing on user input.\n\nWHY IT IS NOT INLINE WITH WI-1049. Relaxing a 'definitely differs' proof makes `requires_shadow_is_confusable` say yes MORE often, so this ADDS warnings — and whether the corpus grows any is unmeasured. If it does, each new warning is a design question about that pair (the FiniteCollection/Iterable family is exactly where WI-1048 had to stop the lint firing), not a mechanical fix. Measure the corpus delta FIRST and report it; a silent warning storm is as bad as the silent suppression.\n\nACCEPTANCE: the fixture above warns, and the control spelling still warns identically — one test, both spellings, asserting the SAME diagnostic (the WI-979 idiom: the two fixtures differ only in how the type is written, and that is the whole experiment); `wi1048_requires_shadow_refinement_test`'s two `*_is_silent` tests still pass — that is the control that this did not simply un-do WI-1048; the corpus warning delta is stated in the delivery (zero, or each new warning named and justified); each test says which case fails when the change is backed out. cargo-test green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. REFERENCE: WI-1048 (the contract and the predicate); WI-346 (the lint); WI-659 (the alias index); WI-1049 (the review that found it)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "ToolPasses(tool: \"cargo-test\", params: none)", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-08-08T11:09:38Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1052", author: "user", content: "RESOLVED INLINE during the WI-1049 session, per the repo rule (the change is smaller than this description).\n\nTHE FIX: `type_ctor_view` (typing.rs) now expands a `SortAlias` at the head before reading the constructor, following ONLY the bare-name spelling (`Ref`/`Ident`/argument-less `Fn`) and bounded by `ALIAS_EXPANSION_LIMIT` = 16. An alias head carrying arguments, and a chain past the bound, both answer UNDECIDED — the fail-open direction WI-1048's contract requires. Both traps this ticket named are taken.\n\nCORPUS WARNING DELTA: ZERO, and it is proven rather than sampled. The fix can only make `types_definitely_differ` return false MORE often, so it can only ADD warnings — monotone. Measured AFTER the fix: `anthill check examples/github-todo` and `anthill check anthill-todo` each print 0 warnings, and `wi345_warnings_channel_test::clean_stdlib_load_carries_no_warnings` is green. After = 0 with a monotone-increasing change means before = 0. POSITIVE CONTROL that the measurement is not vacuous: `anthill check` on the `Pingable`/`Shadower` fixture DOES print the shadow warning, so the zeroes are a real read of a channel that works.\n\nTESTS, in `wi1048_requires_shadow_refinement_test` (the predicate's own suite, whose `load_stdlib_with` helper carries the 'ONE load_all, deliberately' reasoning that duplicating would have lost):\n  * `wi1052_alias_spelled_return_type_still_warns` — FAILS when backed out (loads silent, measured before the fix).\n  * `wi1052_expanded_return_type_warns_identically` — the WI-979 idiom: the two fixtures differ only in whether the return type is written `IntList` or `List[T = Int64]`, and the diagnostic must be byte-identical. Before the fix: alias = [], expanded = one warning.\n  * WI-1048's three `*_is_silent` controls still pass — that is the control that this did not un-do WI-1048.\ncargo-test green via rustland/scripts/test.sh: 29 binaries, 0 failures.", at: "2026-08-08T11:09:41Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1053", description: some(value: "scaland re-derives `getNamedArg` and the string-field decoder privately in FOUR places; rustland has the same primitive factored once in `kb::typing` with 203 call sites.\n\nMEASURED 2026-08-08 while delivering the Scala 3.8.4 bump / language_version work (commit f7951aca), by the /simplify review pass over that diff.\n\nTHE COPIES. Named-arg lookup over a fact's `namedArgs` (`while` loop, `kb.resolveSym(sym) == key`, `Option[TermId]`):\n  * scaland/core/src/main/scala/anthill/codegen/scala/ScalaProfile.scala (private getNamedArg)\n  * scaland/anthill-smt-gen/src/main/scala/anthill/smtgen/Policy.scala:100 (private[smtgen] getNamedArg)\n  * scaland/anthill-smt-gen/src/main/scala/anthill/smtgen/SmtGen.scala:346-355 and :494-507 (inlined twice, not named)\n\nString-literal field decode (`case Term.Const(Literal.StringLit(s)) => Some(s)`):\n  * Policy.scala:86-95 (readStringField), ScalaProfile.scala (stringOf), TacticEmit.scala:75 and :146 (inlined twice)\n\nAnd the body-less-fact walk itself — `tryResolveSymbol -> byFunctor -> if ruleBody(rid).isEmpty -> Term.Fn -> string-field filter -> first match` — is duplicated between `Policy.lookupExplicitPolicy` (:42-69) and `ScalaProfile.findMapping`, ~25 lines each, down to the same comment explaining that the string-literal filter is what skips the loader-synthesised schema-declaration fact.\n\nWHY IT IS NOT JUST TIDINESS. `Policy.scala:98`'s own docstring says it \"Mirrors `anthill_core::kb::typing::get_named_arg`\" — so the tree KNOWS this is a shared primitive, and mirrors the function while not mirroring the thing that makes it worth mirroring: that it lives in `kb` and everybody calls it. The \"skip bodied clauses\" decision is currently made independently at two sites and documented at only one; the retraction and schema-fact rules are likewise duplicated. A change to any of those has two homes and no compiler help finding the second.\n\nDIRECTION. The helpers belong in scaland's `anthill.kb` (the peer of rustland's `kb::typing`), NOT sideways: `anthill-smt-gen` and `anthill-scala-gen` both `dependsOn(core)`, so core cannot call `Policy`'s copy — the lift has to go down. Note `TermView.namedArg` (core/src/main/scala/anthill/kb/TermView.scala:183) already exists but keys on `TermSymbol` and returns `Value`, and its own header says NOTHING READS THROUGH IT YET; making a profile reader its first consumer is a bigger decision than this ticket. The string-keyed reader should sit above it for now.\n\nSCOPE: `getNamedArg`, a string-field variant, and a body-less-facts-by-functor walk in `anthill.kb`; then convert all six call sites and delete the private copies. `unwrapOption` is NOT duplication — ScalaProfile's is scaland's only one (rustland has `unwrap_option` in kb::typing, so it is a parity gap, not a scaland duplicate) — but it may as well move with the others.\n\nACCEPTANCE: one implementation of each helper in `anthill.kb`; zero private copies left in ScalaProfile / Policy / SmtGen / TacticEmit; a test that DRIVES the shared walk (resolve a real fact and read a field off it), not merely one asserting the helpers compile. Say at the test site which assertion fails if a call site is reverted to its private copy. sbt test green.\n\nSOURCE: /simplify review of commit f7951aca."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1020", author: "user", content: "PARTIAL DELIVERY 2026-08-08, commit 0ebec357. The harness EXISTS and works; the ticket's \"at least the prelude closure\" acceptance is NOT met. Left open deliberately.\n\nDELIVERED: `ScalaCompile` (scaland/core/src/test/.../ScalaCompile.scala) runs `dotc.Driver` in-process and fails with the compiler's own diagnostics. Answers to the ticket's three open questions:\n  * HOW: `scala3-compiler % Test` + programmatic Driver, not scala-cli. Hermetic, pinned to `scala3Version`, cannot be absent — so there is no skip-when-unavailable branch, which the ticket specifically warned against. Classpath comes from a `Test / resourceGenerators` file, NOT `java.class.path`: sbt runs tests unforked, so that property is the launcher's classpath and carries no scala3-library.\n  * WHICH: the `eq` closure (PartialEq/Eq/NonEq), compiled as ONE invocation because Eq and NonEq name PartialEq.\n  * EXCLUSIONS: not yet answered, because the closure is not yet wired — see below.\n\nFOUND AND FIXED, a THIRD defect independent of WI-1021 and WI-1022: a sort declaring no operations emitted `trait Eq[T] extends PartialEq[T]:` over a lone `// (no operations)` comment. Scala requires a `:`-opened block to contain a definition, so stdlib's Eq.scala has been shipping as `indented definitions expected, eof found`. The existing WI-170/WI-644 string test was green on it. Fixed in `renderMainSort`'s Algebra branch.\n\nTHE CONTROL IS THE TICKET'S OWN ARGUMENT: back the one-line fix out and the compile test fails naming the compiler's error, while the other 18 tests — including the string-match test over that same file — stay green.\n\nMEASURED, and it resizes the remaining work: compiling each prelude file STANDALONE, 16 of 44 compile and 27 do not. Cross-file refs inflate that (Eq/PartialEq/Ord \"not found\" would resolve in a closure compile), but the real defect families are much wider than WI-1021+WI-1022:\n  * `Missing type parameter for [T1, T2] =>> (T1, T2)` — pair.anthill (WI-1021)\n  * `cannot determine type argument for enum parent` — List, Option (WI-1022 family)\n  * `Too many type arguments for LazyList[A]` / `Iterable[A]` — Stream, Iterable, collection, finite_collection, mutable_collection, combinators\n  * a sort's OWN name unparameterised — Cell, FiniteStream, Relation, LogicalStream\n  * unmapped reflect types (`Term`, `NodeOccurrence`, `Type`) — effects, meta, sort\n  * unbound higher-kinded params (`Not found: type A`/`type M`) — Monad, DelayMonad\n  * a SYNTAX error, `'=' expected, but identifier found` — Numeric.scala:8\n\nWHAT REMAINS FOR THIS TICKET: wire the closure compile over the prelude and name every excluded file with its reason. That cannot land before the defects above are fixed, or the test cannot pass — so the closure ships WITH the fixes, not ahead of them. WI-1021/WI-1022 are now unblocked (they depend on this ticket for the harness, which exists). The Numeric.scala syntax error and the own-name-unparameterised family look like they need their own tickets; the latter may be the same root as WI-1021 (a sort's own name reaching TypeGen without its parameters).\n\nNOTE for whoever finishes it: the harness catches \"does not compile\", NOT \"compiles and means the wrong thing\". Measured: `enum Pair[A, B]: case Pair(...)` — the nested Pair.Pair that §6.3 rules out — compiles clean. Said at the ScalaCompile site.", at: "2026-08-08T11:58:03Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1021", author: "user", content: "UNBLOCKED, and BROADER than the Pair instance. Measured 2026-08-08 by WI-1020's compile harness (commit 0ebec357); WI-1020's harness now exists, so this ticket's acceptance (\"driven by WI-1020's compile harness\") is reachable.\n\nTHE SECOND HALF OF THIS TICKET IS THE BIGGER HALF. The description already notes that `TypeExpr.Simple` \"drops the type ARGUMENTS\" because the `Simple` arm has no bindings to pass on. That is not specific to `Pair`, and not specific to mapPrelude -- it fires for ANY parameterized sort whose own name appears bare in its own members, including sorts that are not in the prelude map at all:\n\n  sort Cell[V]  ->  trait Cell[V]:\n                      def (v: V): Cell        <- `Missing type parameter for anthill.prelude.Cell`\n                      def get(c: Cell): V\n                      def set(c: Cell, value: V): Unit\n\nSame shape measured on FiniteStream, Relation and LogicalStream. So there are TWO independent defects on pair.anthill: the `Pair -> Tuple2` rewrite (this ticket's headline) and the lost type arguments (this ticket's second half), and the second one alone accounts for four more prelude files.\n\nThat matters for choosing between the ticket's two candidate fixes. Option (b) -- drop `case \"Pair\" => \"Tuple2\"` from mapPrelude -- fixes the headline and leaves `Cell` broken, because `Cell` never went through mapPrelude. Option (a) -- tell `TypeGen` the enclosing declaration -- is the plumbing BOTH need, since knowing \"this name is the sort I am inside\" is also what supplies the arguments to re-attach. Recommend doing (a) as the substrate and then deciding (b) on the nominal-vs-host merits separately.\n\nTHE OTHER SUSPECT ENTRIES: this ticket asks to check `List`/`Set`/`Map`/`Stream` at the same time. Measured, `Stream -> LazyList` is live and wrong on ARITY, not just nominality -- anthill `Stream[Element, E]` has two parameters and Scala's `LazyList` has one, so `Iterable` emits `def iterator(c: C): LazyList[Element, E]` and `dotc` reports `Too many type arguments for LazyList[A]`. Same for `Iterable` itself in collection/finite_collection/mutable_collection/combinators. Whatever is decided for `Pair`, an effect-carrying anthill collection type does not have a same-arity Scala counterpart, so the entry cannot be a straight name swap.\n\nSEE ALSO WI-1055, which decides the general policy (fix what the parse IR already knows; REFUSE what needs a KB) and lists this among the fixable group.", at: "2026-08-08T12:03:51Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1021", author: "user", content: "CORRECTION to the previous note: one line lost a word to shell quoting. The Cell emission line should read\n\n    def new(v: V): Cell\n\n(the operation is named \"new\", which scala-gen backticks as a Scala reserved word -- that part is correct and is not the defect). The defect is the return type: bare \"Cell\" where \"Cell[V]\" is required.", at: "2026-08-08T12:04:47Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1054", description: some(value: "scala-gen emits a hyphenated anthill identifier VERBATIM, producing text that is not Scala -- measured, `def zero-val(): T` is a syntax error, and the spec already says what should happen instead.\n\nMEASURED 2026-08-08 by WI-1020's compile harness (commit 0ebec357). `Bootstrap.generate` on stdlib/anthill/prelude/numeric.anthill emits:\n\n  package anthill.prelude\n\n  trait Numeric[T] extends PartialOrd[T]:\n    def add(a: T, b: T): T\n    def sub(a: T, b: T): T\n    def neg(a: T): T\n    def mul(a: T, b: T): T\n    def zero-val(): T\n\n`dotc` 3.8.4 reports `Numeric.scala:8: '=' expected, but identifier found` -- it parses `zero` as the method name, `-` as an operator, and stops. This is the WORST failure class the harness can find: not a type error but a PARSE error, i.e. the emitter produced something that is not the language.\n\nTHE RULE IS ALREADY WRITTEN. docs/scala-forward-mapping.md §1.1 says, in the paragraph describing the output tree: \"Anthill `namespace` segments map directly to the package path; hyphens in identifiers become underscores.\" So this is not an undecided design question -- it is a documented mapping rule with no implementation.\n\nMECHANISM. `Names.scala` handles `_` and nothing else: `scalaTypeName` (:26) and `toCamel` (:35) both branch on `s.contains('_')`, and `scalaFieldName`/the method-name path inherit that. A `-` passes through untouched. Note the repo already knows anthill identifiers contain `-` -- it is why scaland's parser must never re-lex a source slice (WI-950).\n\nDECIDE, and say why: §1.1 says hyphens become UNDERSCORES (`zero_val`), but every other identifier in this backend is camelCased (`to_bigint` -> `toBigint`, pinned by the WI-170 test), so `zeroVal` is the consistent answer and `zero_val` is the documented one. Whichever wins, the OTHER of (code, §1.1) must be updated in the same change -- the current state is that they disagree and neither is enforced.\n\nCHECK THE WHOLE SURFACE, not just operation names: sort names, entity names, field names, and namespace segments can all carry `-`, and each reaches a different `Names` entry point. A hyphen in a namespace segment reaches a PACKAGE name, where the failure mode is different again.\n\nACCEPTANCE: driven by WI-1020's compile harness -- numeric.anthill's emission compiles. A test that a hyphenated name in each position (operation, sort, entity, field) emits a legal Scala identifier, asserting the CHOSEN spelling rather than merely 'not a hyphen'. Say at the test site which assertion fails when the fix is backed out. §1.1 and the code agree at the end of it. sbt test green.\n\nSOURCE: WI-1020's prelude sweep, which found 27 of 44 prelude files do not compile."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Open)
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1020", author: "user", content: "ORDERING, so nobody wires it backwards: WI-1054 (hyphenated identifiers) and WI-1055 (fix-or-refuse) are NOT blocked on this ticket. The harness they need is already committed (0ebec357); what is left here is the prelude CLOSURE test, and that cannot be written until WI-1055 supplies the refusals that give the closure a named exclusion set. So the edge runs the other way in practice: finish WI-1054/WI-1055, then close this. Deliberately left as description text rather than a --depends edge, since an edge blocks claiming.", at: "2026-08-08T12:05:47Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1020", description: some(value: "docs/scala-forward-mapping.md §2.3 promises \"The generated file compiles as-is\" and NOTHING verifies it -- every BootstrapTest assertion is a substring match on the emitted text, which is exactly how the two defects in WI-1021 and WI-1022 survived in stdlib output.\\n\\nMEASURED (2026-08-05, delivering WI-940). Bootstrap.generate on stdlib/anthill/prelude/pair.anthill emits, into src/main/scala/anthill/prelude/Pair.scala:\\n\\n  package anthill.prelude\\n\\n  enum Pair[A, B] extends PartialEq[A], PartialEq[B]:\\n    case Pair(fst: A, snd: B)\\n\\n  trait PairOps[A, B]:\\n    def fst(p: Tuple2): A\\n    def snd(p: Tuple2): B\\n    def eq(a: Tuple2, b: Tuple2): Boolean\\n\\nFed to `scala-cli compile --scala 3.6.3` (with a PartialEq trait as eq.anthill's own emission defines it) this produces SIX errors from two independent mechanisms: four \"Missing type parameter for [T1, T2] =>> (T1, T2)\" (WI-1021) and \"trait PartialEq is extended twice\" plus \"class Pair cannot be instantiated since it has conflicting base types\" (WI-1022). The existing BootstrapTest for this file passes -- it asserts `enum Pair`, `case Pair(fst: A, snd: B)`, `trait PairOps`, `def fst`, `def snd`, and every one of those substrings IS present in a file that does not compile.\\n\\nWHY THIS IS THE ROOT AND NOT A THIRD SYMPTOM. The repo's own rule is that a test for a capability must DRIVE the capability -- \"it loads clean\" is not evidence. A codegen backend's capability is EMITTING COMPILABLE SOURCE, so its driving test compiles the source. String-matching measures that a renderer produced the characters the test author expected, which is a test of the test author. Every future Bootstrap fix (WI-1021, WI-1022, and the WI-940 record collapse itself) is currently pinned the weak way, and each new mapping rule adds another chance for a plausible-looking string to be uncompilable Scala.\\n\\nSCOPE: a test that takes Bootstrap.generate's output for a set of stdlib files, writes the tree, and COMPILES it, failing with the compiler's own diagnostics. Open questions for whoever takes it, all real:\\n  * HOW to invoke the compiler. scala-cli is on the developer's PATH but is not an sbt dependency; `scala3-compiler` as a Test dependency and a programmatic `dotty.tools.dotc.Driver` invocation keeps it hermetic and is the likelier answer. Check what the JVM/Scala version matrix in scaland/build.sbt allows.\\n  * WHICH files. The emitted tree has cross-file references (Pair.scala names PartialEq, declared by eq.anthill), so a single-file compile is not enough -- compile a CLOSURE, and say which one.\\n  * WHAT to do about files that legitimately cannot compile yet. Bootstrap emits abstract `<Sort>Ops` traits with no implementations, which is fine; but a `TypeExpr.Variable` renders as `?` and Denoted/EffectRow render as `Any` placeholders (TypeGen.scala:18-39), and `?` is not a Scala type. Decide whether the harness covers the files that hit those paths or names them as an explicit, listed exclusion -- an unlisted skip would recreate the blindness this ticket closes.\\n\\nACCEPTANCE: a test that compiles Bootstrap's emitted output for at least the prelude closure and fails on a compiler error, with the compiler's message in the failure text. State at the test site which currently-green string-match test it SUBSUMES (if any) and which it does not. Any file excluded from compilation must be named in the test with its reason -- no silent skips. sbt test green.\\n\\nSOURCE: measured while delivering WI-940, whose /simplify-style review pass dumped the actual emitted stdlib output for the first time."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-1055", "WI-1054"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Open)
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1020", author: "user", content: "CORRECTION to the ORDERING note above: the ordering is now a real --depends edge, WI-1020 -> {WI-1054, WI-1055}, not description text. The earlier note undersold it. It IS a technical prerequisite, not soft ordering: with the fix-or-refuse policy decided (WI-1055), the closure test gets its named exclusion set from the refusals, and hyphenated identifiers are in that policy Group A (FIX, not refuse), so numeric.anthill has to genuinely compile before the closure can be green. The direction that would deadlock is the reverse one — WI-1054/WI-1055 must NOT depend on this ticket, since the harness they need is already committed at 0ebec357.\n\nOne overstatement in that note, corrected: \"cannot be written until WI-1055 supplies the refusals\" is too strong in the abstract — a test-side exclusion list would also satisfy the \"no silent skips\" wording. It is the DECISION to refuse rather than exclude that makes WI-1055 a prerequisite, not a logical impossibility.", at: "2026-08-08T12:13:30Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1000", description: some(value: "Implement proposal 059 R3: classify the direct content of every secondary entry with default-deny semantics. A secondary entry may add members and spec claims, never identity.\n\nOPERATION RULE: an operation declared in a secondary entry must be fresh and must have a runnable Anthill body. Refuse a bodyless declaration even if a host mapping could later back it. A `[simp]` equation is not an operation body. Duplicate declarations across main and secondary entries are already refused by WI-1049 and are a control for this ticket, not another implementation path. Ordinary namespaces and bodyless operations in a main entry remain legal.\n\nALLOWED: fresh body-having operations and operation-block sugar; consts; nested sort or enum declarations with bodies, whose own addresses make them main entries; same-entry proofs; `provides` declarations and host realization blocks. The interior of a host `provides` block is recursively classified.\n\nREFUSED: entities; type-parameter declarations; sort-level requires; every rule until WI-1001 can enforce the narrow fresh-head rule; every fact and constraint except the explicit `provides` spelling; foreign-target proofs; and any unclassified production.\n\nCLASSIFY BEFORE IMPLEMENTATION: bodyless type aliases, standalone type-parameter binders, and `describe` declarations. Imports are governed separately by WI-995.\n\nACCEPTANCE: drive a fresh body-having secondary operation through dispatch and assert its value. Refuse a fresh bodyless secondary operation with a diagnostic naming the sort and declaration. Pin controls showing that a main-entry bodyless host-facing operation remains legal, a duplicate declaration is refused by WI-1049, and ordinary namespaces retain their existing content rules. Exercise the same operation rule through operation-block sugar. Run rustland/scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-999", description: some(value: "Implement proposal 059 R4 clause 3: a declaration in a sort scope may not capture a short name that already resolves there unless the new declaration is a legitimate override or a distinguishable refinement.\n\nTHE HAZARD: a main-entry body calls a bare imported operation and answers one value; adding a same-named declaration in the shared sort scope silently repoints the unedited body. The same effect is measured for operations, consts, and nested type declarations, so the check covers every declaration category that can win lookup and applies to the whole sort scope, not only secondary entries.\n\nEXCLUSIONS ARE SEMANTIC. A member operation is allowed when the declaring sort provides, transitively, the spec that owns the captured operation; use `sort_provides` and do not key on whether resolution arrived through requires or import. A requires-only operation is not an override, but WI-1048 established that a confidently distinguishable signature is a deliberate refinement. Reuse `typing::requires_shadow_is_confusable`: the FiniteCollection map and filter refinements must remain legal, while a confusable requires-only shadow remains subject to refusal. Binders and entity variants are outside the declaration categories admitted by R3.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION: run after pass 2, when provider relations are known. Do not assume that the captured symbol has a source declaration or Operation kind; resolver builtins may have no declaration and operation tags may ride another kind. WI-995 is a prerequisite because imports must become file-local before capture is evaluated against the final resolution model.\n\nMIGRATION: after WI-982 removed reflect.KB.nonvar and reflect.KB.ground, the corpus has zero no-override capture sites. The two requires-only FiniteCollection sites are valid refinements and must not be migrated or refused.\n\nACCEPTANCE: drive the operation, const, and nested-type capture controls so the base behavior is observed and the capturing row is refused with both sites named. Drive a provided-spec override and the two WI-1048 refinements as legal controls. Drive a confusable requires-only shadow as refused. Run rustland/scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-995"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1002", description: some(value: "Update docs/kernel-language.md after proposal 059 implementation lands. The kernel spec is canonical; the proposal retains measurements and rejected alternatives.\n\nDOCUMENT: definitions of main entry, secondary entry, ordinary namespace, entry identity by file, member and dispatch surface; classification by address after the finished program is known; R1 single type declaration; R3 default-deny content rules; R4 operation and capture rules; R5 remedies; and R6 order-independent rule-head binding. Record the whole-program classification consequence: an ordinary namespace becomes a secondary entry if another file declares a sort at that address.\n\nCURRENT R4 POLICY: one operation declaration per name and scope, already enforced by WI-1049; an operation introduced by a secondary entry must be fresh and have a runnable Anthill body; a secondary entry never fills a bodyless declaration; and a declaration may not capture an existing meaning unless it is an override or distinguishable refinement. Do not restore the superseded WI-998 fill policy.\n\nSYNC RELATED SPEC TEXT: reconcile section 8.7 scoped instance selection with the whole-program premise, explain how a namespace at a carrier address differs from an unrelated namespace-level operation for backing, update the WI-1048 capture and refinement wording, and record file-local imports from WI-995.\n\nDo not copy measurements or design argument into the canonical spec. Run rustland/scripts/test.sh if executable examples or tests change."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-1001", "WI-999"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1003", description: some(value: "Close the remaining citation, internal-consistency, and grammar-completeness gaps in docs/proposals/059-secondary-entries.md.\n\nRESOLVED ON 2026-08-08: proposal 058 section 3.6 is now described as proposed rather than delivered; proposal 001 and 058 companion mechanisms are identified as separate-address witnesses rather than R2 secondary entries; the measurement banner no longer labels prescriptive rules as measurements; WI-979 and WI-994 attribution is corrected; and the old R4 fill policy was replaced by fresh body-having operations plus WI-1049 duplicate refusal.\n\nREMAINING: reconcile kernel-language section 8.7 scoped instance selection with the proposal whole-program premise; reconcile the broad namespace-level operation backing sentence with attachment at a carrier address; remove any remaining requires or dictionary-layout contradiction; classify bodyless aliases, standalone type binders, and describe declarations with WI-1000; verify every grammar production against the R3 default-deny table; correct any non-parsing example; revisit the census population for realization blocks; state the R2 converse; cover operation-contract proof targets; and state the effect on internal visibility.\n\nKeep implementation ownership in WI-1000, WI-999, WI-1001, and WI-995. This ticket edits the proposal and citations; it does not revive WI-998."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-975", description: some(value: "FOUND WHILE DRIVING WI-956 item 4. Not fixed there: this is a LANGUAGE decision, and WI-956's gate fix is right whichever way it goes.\n\nWHAT LOADS TODAY, clean, no diagnostic:\n\n    namespace n\n      entity Rec(n: Int64)     -- §6.3 sugar: desugars to `sort Rec { entity Rec(n: Int64) }`\n      sort Rec                 -- the SAME name, declared again, with a different body\n        sort T = ?\n        operation peek(x: T) -> T\n      end\n    end\n\n`Rec` ends up `kinds == [Entity, Sort]` with `type_params_of_sort(Rec) == [\"T\"]` and a member `Rec.peek`. Nothing warns that `Rec` was declared twice.\n\nAND IT IS HALF-WIRED. The second declaration's body does not resolve names from its enclosing namespace. MEASURED, three separate shapes:\n  - `operation peek(r: Rec) -> T`         -> \"unresolved name 'Rec' in scope 'peek'\"\n  - `provides Resource[C = Counter, ...]` -> \"unresolved name 'Resource' in scope 'Counter'\", and the same for `Counter` and for the IMPORTED `Int64`\nOnly a body referring exclusively to names declared INSIDE it (like `peek(x: T) -> T` above) loads. So the shape is accepted, but a sort declared this way can carry no `provides`, no member typed by its own sort, and no imported type.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS BEYOND THE ODDITY. This is the shape that made WI-956's `kind_of` bug reachable: `kind_of` reports the FIRST-declared category, so a sort registered `[Entity, Sort]` failed five `kind is Sort` gates and `call_bracket_scopes(Rec.peek)` came back EMPTY. WI-956 fixed the gates (they were asking a display question at a membership site, wrong regardless). The declaration itself is untouched.\n\nTHE DECISION, which the spec should make, not a reader:\n  §6.3 says the sugar and the long form are an EQUIVALENCE -- `entity Account(...)` and `sort Account { entity Account(...) }` denote the same thing. It does not say whether a LATER `sort Account { ... }` EXTENDS that sort, CONFLICTS with it, or is simply undefined behaviour. Pick one:\n  (a) REFUSE -- a duplicate sort declaration is a load error naming both spans. Simplest, and consistent with \"prefer a loud error over a silent skip\".\n  (b) MERGE -- define re-declaration as extension, and then the scope wiring is a real bug to fix (the second body must see its namespace).\n  (c) Something else the spec author intends.\n\nDo NOT pick (b) by default just because it currently half-works.\n\nACCEPTANCE: docs/kernel-language.md §6.3 states the rule; the loader implements it; a test drives the fixture above and pins whichever answer was chosen (if (a), pin the diagnostic text and the two spans; if (b), pin that `provides` and an imported type resolve inside the second body). cargo-test green via rustland/scripts/test.sh.\n\nSOURCE: WI-956 item 4 (see wi956_kind_gate_tests in typing.rs, whose fixture is this shape)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "codex", at: "2026-08-08T13:21:16Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-981", description: some(value: "R4's CAPTURE CLAUSE (proposal 059) is a change to behaviour the spec currently\nPERMITS. Measure the migration before implementing it.\n\nMUST AGREE WITH docs/proposals/059-secondary-entries.md R4, third clause, which\ndefines the rule: no declaration may CAPTURE a name that already resolves in the\nsort's scope. Do not re-decide the policy here; if the migration cost makes the\nrule untenable, amend 059 rather than diverging from it.\n\nTHE HAZARD, MEASURED (all three rows drive `Rec(n: 0).use()` after load):\n\n  namespace lib\n    operation f(x: Int64) -> Int64 = 1\n  end\n  namespace app\n    import lib.f\n    sort Rec\n      entity Rec(n: Int64)\n      operation use(r: Rec) -> Int64 = f(r.n)    -- bare `f` -> the import\n    end\n    ...\n  end\n\n  base, nothing else added                        => 1\n  + `namespace Rec  operation f(...) = 2  end`    => 2   (a SECOND ENTRY)\n  + the same operation in Rec's OWN body          => 2   (the CONTROL)\n\nThe unedited body changed meaning in both cases. Note the control: this is NOT\ncreated by secondary entries, so the fix belongs to the SCOPE, not to secondary entries --\nan entry-local patch would be aimed at the wrong object and would split the one\nshared scope 059 R2 rests on.\n\nALREADY KNOWN, AS A CAUTION: kernel-language.md §6.3 records exactly this for the\nlong form (WI-935) -- \"Mind the short name ... the new member is a second binding\nfor it, and an `import ns.{f}` elsewhere may bind to the member rather than to\nthe namespace-level `f` -- silently, since both are legal (measured)\". 059 R4\nturns that caution into a refusal, for both spellings at once.\n\nALSO COVERED by the same clause, so the measurement must include them:\n  - IMPORTS are shared across all entries of a scope, MEASURED in both\n    directions: an import written in a secondary entry resolves a bare name in the\n    sort's BODY (=7, against a control with the import deleted that fails\n    `unknown functor`), and an import in the body resolves one in the entry (=9).\n    So an import is a capture route exactly as a declaration is.\n  - CONSTS capture too: a secondary entry `const K` shadows an imported `K` (=2,\n    control =1). (A const is NOT a member -- `X.K` and `receiver.K` resolve to\n    nothing in EITHER placement -- it is a name in the sort's scope. See 059 R2.)\n\nTHE MEASUREMENT THIS TICKET IS FOR, and it gates the rest: across stdlib,\nanthill-stl, examples/ and every fixture, count the declarations whose SHORT NAME\nalready resolves in the scope they are declared in -- i.e. the sites the new\nrefusal would reject. Report the count and the sites BEFORE changing behaviour.\nZero means the rule can simply land. Non-zero means it needs a migration and\npossibly a narrower rule (e.g. refuse only where the captured name is reached by\nIMPORT, not by an enclosing declaration), which is an amendment to 059.\n\nWATCH FOR a false positive that would inflate the count: a member legitimately\nsharing a spelling with something in an UNRELATED scope is not a capture. The\nquestion is only whether the name resolves at the declaring scope, by the same\nladder `resolve_in_scope` runs.\n\nACCEPTANCE: the count and the site list are reported first. Then, if the rule\nlands: a capturing declaration is a load error naming both the declaration and\nwhat it would capture; a test drives the three rows above (base => 1, second\nentry => refused, own body => refused) plus one row per capture route (operation,\nconst, import). Say at the test site which rows fail when the fix is backed out.\ncargo-test green via rustland/scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "codex", at: "2026-08-08T13:21:16Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-998", description: some(value: "Implements 059 R4 clauses 1 and 2 — FILL, never DISPLACE — on top of the pass-1\ndeclaration ledger. Spec: docs/proposals/059-secondary-entries.md.\n\nCLAUSE 1 — TWO SUPPLIERS OF ONE MEMBER NAME ARE REFUSED AT LOAD, naming both sites.\n058 §3.1's coexistence-gated-on-nameability: two providers of a SPEC may coexist\nbecause an ambiguous call has a repair the author can write (`[Spec = W]`); two\nmembers of one sort under one name have no such currency — `r.peek()` cannot say\nwhich — so this is the case 058 calls \"coexistence one cannot select out of is a\ntrap, not a feature\". Applies to two secondary entries, and to two same-named\noperations in one main entry.\n\nCLAUSE 2 — A SECONDARY ENTRY MAY NOT REDECLARE A MEMBER THE MAIN ENTRY DECLARES AND\nIMPLEMENTS. 058 §3.6's no-displacement, in its own words: fill silence, never\noverwrite speech.\n\n\"IMPLEMENTS\" IS NOT \"HAS A BODY\", AND GETTING THIS WRONG SILENTLY DISCARDS THE FILL.\nMEASURED: `sort String`'s `isEmpty` is declared body-less in stdlib and mapped to\n`string_is_empty` by an `operation_map` in anthill-stl. A secondary entry supplying\n`operation isEmpty(s: String) -> Bool = false` — wrong for every input — LOADS CLEAN\nAND NEVER RUNS: `\"\".isEmpty()` still answers true and `\"x\".isEmpty()` still false,\nidentical to the control with no secondary entry at all. The author is told nothing.\nThe mechanism: `call_op_sym` and `call_op_bridged` BOTH return a registered builtin\nbefore any body lookup, and an `operation_map` clause arrives there AS a registered\nbuiltin (`register_operation_mappings` -> `register_builtin_sym`).\n\nSO SILENCE MEANS NO IMPLEMENTATION FROM ANY SOURCE — no anthill body, no\n`operation_map`/`const_map` entry for the target language, no registered builtin —\nand a fill over a declaration backed by any of them is REFUSED, naming the backing.\n\nNOT A CORNER CASE: counting `operation_map` entries in anthill-stl, Float has 29,\nString 13, Int64 9, BigInt 7, persistence 6 = 64 members that read as \"silence\" under\na body-only test.\n\n`[simp]` IS A FOURTH CASE AND A DIFFERENT ONE — name it, do not fold it in. A `[simp]`\nequation does not BACK the operation (WI-818/885), but the typer rewrites LHS->RHS\nbefore dispatch, so a filled body is dead at every rewritten call site and live\neverywhere else.\n\nWHERE THE CHECK RUNS. Clause 1 is a pure ledger walk in pass 1. Clause 2 SPLITS: the\nledger decides \"redeclared\", but \"already implemented\" can only be read after the\n`anthill.realization.OperationMapping` facts exist — and the declaration and its\nmapping routinely live in different crates (String's declaration in stdlib, its\nmapping in anthill-stl). So clause 2 needs a post-pass-2 read as well.\n\nWHAT THIS CLAUSE IS FOR, and why the body-less carve-out must survive: it carries\nSPEC-AUTHOR AND IMPLEMENTER AS DIFFERENT HANDS — one writes the sort and its\nsignatures, another (often an AI agent) supplies the bodies in anthill, and the\ninterface it must meet is checkable rather than conventional. The signature stays the\nmain entry's and the filling entry may not change it.\n\nMEASURED BLAST RADIUS: 0 sites for both clauses. Honest but easy — the corpus\ncontains no secondary entries at all, so clause 2 has nothing to bite on yet.\nCONTROL: a fill over a body-less-AND-unimplemented member must still LOAD (that is the\ncapability); a fill over `String.isEmpty` must newly FAIL, and today passes."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-997", "WI-1003"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Rejected(reason: "Superseded by WI-1049 and the 2026-08-08 proposal 059 decision. WI-1049 already refuses every second operation declaration; proposal 059 now refuses bodyless operations in secondary entries instead of allowing another declaration to fill them. Implementation belongs to WI-1000.", at: "2026-08-08T13:21:17Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1058", description: some(value: "BUG + CAPABILITY (058; WI-1056 residue, MEASURED at the widening): the GENERAL Expr::Apply in a rule body is still never typed, and the gap is two missing READINGS rather than a backlog of mistakes. WI-1056 widened the error policy at the body-less spec-op shape (an '=' goal is a call on PartialEq.eq, so most atoms are decided) and the corpus loads clean; what stays out is call_dispatch_shape's narrowing to dot + spec op. THE MEASUREMENT, taken 2026-08-08 by widening call_dispatch_shape's Expr::Apply arm to return Some for every functor and loading stdlib + rustland/anthill-stl/anthill ALONE: 468 errors, 173 distinct, against 2 for the body-less shape on the same load. They split into two populations and NEITHER is a source defect: (1) 220 'expected known operation or arrow-typed variable, got unknown functor' -- overwhelmingly a rule NAME applied to arguments (list_contains, self_provides, provision_carrier, is_entity_of, refines, provides), which in a rule body is a RELATIONAL SUBGOAL and not a value; check_apply_iter's WI-714 relation arm is gated on NOT-in-a-rule-body for exactly that reason, so every subgoal falls straight through to Path 3 and dies as UnknownApplyFunctor. (2) ~150 'expected List[T = Term], got <SomeSort>' -- reflect/?P(...) arguments the walk types structurally against the ho_apply / reflect signatures (74 x LogicalQuery, 13 x Expr, 11 x TypeExtractor, ...). WORK: give the typer the SUBGOAL reading (a rule-name application in a rule body is a goal: check its arity and argument types against the rule's own head, not against an operation signature) and the reflect-argument reading, then widen call_dispatch_shape's Apply arm. ALSO INHERITED: the two exemptions WI-1056 had to state at undecidable_by_this_typer are candidates to RETIRE here -- an equation-introduced functor applied in a rule body (ite) has no signature to check against and its type would have to come from its [simp] clauses; an unresolved dot receiver (WI-282) would need rule-head variable types. ACCEPTANCE (each test says which case fails when backed out): a rule body naming an UNDEFINED functor is refused (control: today WI-1034 catches the dangling GOAL, not a nested apply); a rule-name subgoal with the wrong ARITY is refused naming the rule (control: today it loads clean); the corpus loads clean at every tier -- stdlib, host bindings, examples/, anthill-todo/, anthill-testcases/; the WI-1043 and WI-1056 suites stay green. REFERENCE: WI-1056 (which measured this and states the narrowing at dispatch_calls_in_occ); WI-1043; WI-1026 (which first declined this population); WI-714 (the relation arm and its rule-body gate); WI-1034 (the dangling-functor population, a DIFFERENT one); docs/design/058-implementation.md sec 23."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1056", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. The 7 sites split ONE typer gap / TWO source errors / TWO exemptions -- not the three-way split the ticket predicted, because one of its two 'typer gaps' was refuted by building it. (1) THE TYPER GAP: check_apply_iter's materializer-fallback gate asked is_constructor_symbol where the question is is_entity_constructor; the loader's ApplyOrConstructor drain has always asked the latter, which is why an operation body never noticed and a rule body -- which does not take that drain -- did. (2) SOURCE ERRORS, all invisible because nothing checked them: bigint's induction schema (bare 0/1 where BigInt is declared, all three sites corrected); logical_stream's interleave (LogicalStream[?A] bound T alone and left the WI-714 row to the default -- now shares one row variable across both inputs and the result); and, found only by widening, anthill.kernel.unify(a: T, b: T) written against a NAMESPACE-level 'sort T = ?', so every '<=>' in every program shared ONE symbol as its argument type -- it now carries [T] as struct_eq beside it already did, and the dead sort is deleted. (3) EXEMPTIONS, both 'cannot decide', not 'decline to report': the WI-282 unresolved dot receiver (the whole of what the corpus would newly refuse -- 7 reports at 4 sites, every one an untyped rule-head var), and an equation-introduced functor applied in a rule body, where WI-898 had gated its own diagnostic !in_rule_body and then let a worse one through; its boundary is the IMPORT (an unresolved bare 'ite' is still refused). THE TICKET'S SECOND 'TYPER GAP' WAS WRONG AND I BUILT IT BEFORE FINDING OUT. logical_stream:73 read as a subtype gap -- a same-base PARTIAL type application is refused where the BARE form is accepted, though kernel-language 'Expansion during unification' says both mean the same. Loosening it is UNSOUND: driven, an effectful Stream[T = Int64] then reached a slot declared E = {}. That section governs UNIFICATION (unify_parameterized_view says so in the same comment: 'the subtype twin is the direction that rejects'). Backed out; the site is fixed at source; the asymmetry is WI-1059 with BOTH directions' costs measured (the sound one is 33 stdlib errors, and only after patching both carrier arms -- WI-1016). FOUND BY THE /code-review, not by me: that unsoundness, and the dedup keyed against the whole error accumulator rather than this atom's own recursion (two unlocated errors in different rules could collapse). MY PREDICTED BACK-OUT MATRIX WAS WRONG IN NINE CELLS. Driving it found (a) my shared fixture spelled its sort eponymously, so four rows that say nothing about constructors moved when the ctor gate was reverted, and (b) the rows I marked 'passes both ways by design' FAIL when the exemptions are backed out -- the STDLIB itself stops loading, which is the one failure mode small fixtures cannot see. The table now marks that cascade distinctly. FOUR FIXTURES WERE ILL-TYPED PROGRAMS, not regressions: wi894/wi896 (the kernel.unify shape), wi927 (a bracketed type application in a slot that expects no Type -- refused in an OPERATION body too, always was), wi680 (a bare un-imported 'ite'). THE SECOND HALF IS WI-1058 AND ITS SIZE IS MEASURED: the general Expr::Apply widened reports 468 errors (173 distinct) on stdlib + bindings ALONE against 2 for the body-less shape, and they are two readings the typer lacks -- a rule NAME applied to arguments is a relational subgoal (WI-714's arm is gated !in_rule_body for exactly that reason), and reflect/?P(...) arguments land in List[T = Term] positions. NOT CLAIMED: BigInt.induction is still not driven end-to-end by anything (nothing resolves it; the prove pipeline's phase gamma would need z3), and the test that pins the source fix says so at its site rather than pretending otherwise. Back-out matrix driven, four reverts. Corpus clean at every tier. Full workspace: 29 binaries, 4396 passed, 0 failed.", at: "2026-08-08T14:24:51Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1060", description: some(value: "scaland scala-gen hardcodes a DRIFTED copy of scala_std's type_map, and the fact file that claims to be the source of truth has no reader.\n\nMEASURED 2026-08-08 while delivering WI-1055 (which renamed the table `TypeGen.preludeMapping` and gave each entry a Scala ARITY, so the duplication got bigger rather than smaller).\n\nTHE TWO TABLES. `stdlib/anthill/realization/scala_std.anthill` declares thirteen `TypeMapping` facts and its own header says: 'Codegen reads this from the KB; changing it changes generated code without touching the codegen implementation.' Nothing reads them. `ScalaProfile` is the module built for exactly that seam -- its doc says it 'turns a loaded scala_std profile into the plain values an emitter takes as parameters' -- and it reads only `language_version`.\n\nTHE DRIFT, measured entry by entry:\n - the fact says `Int64 -> Int64`; the code says `Int64 -> Int`. The CODE is right (Scala has no `Int64`, and the emitted `Int` is what WI-1020's compile harness proves compiles). The fact is a WI-068 rename artifact -- the mass Int->Int64 rename hit the host_type column too, which `rust_std.anthill` shows by contrast: it still says `Int64 -> i64`.\n - the fact carries `Duration -> scala.concurrent.duration.Duration` and `Timestamp -> java.time.Instant`; the code has NEITHER, so both fall through to WI-1055's `Placement.Ambient`.\n - the code carries `Unit` and `Nothing`; the fact has neither.\n - the code's per-entry ARITY has no counterpart in the `TypeMapping` schema at all, so it cannot be cross-checked even in principle.\n\nWHY IT WAS NOT FIXED INLINE IN WI-1055, stated so the next person does not redo the reasoning. (a) Bootstrap reads no KB by design (proposal 034), so the fix is the shape `buildSbt` already has -- the CALLER resolves the profile and passes the table in -- which is a `generate` signature change touching every caller and test. (b) The arity column needs a schema decision on `TypeMapping` first. (c) Adding the missing entries to the code to 'catch up' would silently decide WI-1021's open question: `Duration` is ALSO an anthill sort (primitives.anthill declares `sort anthill.prelude.Duration`, and Bootstrap emits it), so a map entry for it picks host-over-nominal for a type whose nominal-vs-host status that ticket is still adjudicating.\n\nSCOPE: give `ScalaProfile` a `typeMap(kb)` reader mirroring `languageVersion`; decide where arity comes from (a `TypeMapping` field, or derived); thread the resolved table into `Bootstrap.generate` as a parameter; delete the hardcoded table. Fix the `Int64` entry in scala_std.anthill in the same change -- deliberately NOT done in WI-1055, because with no reader no test could drive it.\n\nACCEPTANCE: the emitter's type map comes from the KB, asserted by a test that CHANGES a `TypeMapping` fact and sees the emitted Scala change (a table that is merely read but not used keeps passing otherwise). A test that the fact file and any residual hardcoded knowledge agree, or that no hardcoded copy remains. rust_std's own hardcoded `map_primitive_type` (codegen/rust.rs) has the identical shape -- say whether it is in scope or explicitly deferred. sbt test green.\n\nSOURCE: /code-review reuse finding on WI-1055."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-1021"]), status: Open)
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1055", description: some(value: "Bootstrap must REFUSE a declaration it cannot emit correctly, instead of emitting uncompilable Scala -- measured, 27 of 44 prelude files do not compile, and every one of them emitted quietly.\n\nDECIDED 2026-08-08 (rssh): keep Bootstrap minimal -- it stays parse-IR-driven with no KB and no typer (proposal 034) -- and make the constructions it cannot handle a LOAD-TIME REFUSAL rather than a plausible-looking emission. This is the WI-891 inversion applied to scala-gen: cpp-gen turned an unlowerable body from a silent zero into a build failure. `BootstrapError` already exists and already does exactly this for one case (the eponymous-variant-alongside-siblings shape, WI-940), so the mechanism is in place and this ticket widens its remit.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS FOR WI-1020: that ticket requires the emitted prelude CLOSURE to compile, and says \"any file excluded from compilation must be named in the test with its reason -- no silent skips\". A refusal IS that named exclusion. Without this the closure test cannot be written at all, because the files that would fail are precisely the ones nothing refuses.\n\nMEASURED INVENTORY (WI-1020's harness, commit 0ebec357), split by whether the information Bootstrap needs is ALREADY IN THE PARSE IR. This split is the ticket's main content -- the first group is a fix, the second is a refusal.\n\n== GROUP A: information IS present and is dropped. FIX, do not refuse. ==\n\nA1. Operation type parameters are never read. `Operation.typeParams: IndexedSeq[TypeParam]` exists (parse/IR.scala:373); grep for it in OpGen.scala and Bootstrap.scala returns NOTHING. So `operation pure[A](a: A) -> M[A]` emits `def pure(a: A): M[A]` with `A` unbound. Affects monad, delay, and every operation with its own type params.\n\nA2. A sort's HEAD type parameters are not collected. `renderMainSort`'s `typeParams` takes only `Item.AbstractSortItem`, so `sort anthill.prelude.Monad[M[T]]` emits `trait Monad:` with no parameters at all. (`SortWithBody.isTypeParam`, IR.scala:339-345, is how the parser marks these; the loader reads it and Bootstrap does not.)\n\nA3. A sort's OWN name in a member signature loses its type arguments. `sort Cell[V]` emits `trait Cell[V]:` and then `def new(v: V): Cell`. The enclosing sort's parameters are in hand at `renderMainSort`; `TypeGen.render` is simply never told them. Same mechanism as WI-1021's second half -- see the feedback on that ticket; fix once, in the same place.\n\n== GROUP B: information is genuinely absent without a KB. REFUSE. ==\n\nB1. A name that resolves to nothing Bootstrap can see. `Term`, `NodeOccurrence`, `Type`, `Duration` reach the output as bare identifiers that name nothing in the emitted tree (effects, meta, sort). Bootstrap cannot know whether such a name is a sort in another file, an import, or a typo -- that is a resolution question.\n\nB2. `TypeExpr.Variable` renders as `?`, which is not a Scala type (TypeGen.scala:18-21); `Denoted` and `EffectRow` render as `Any` placeholders (:29-39). WI-1020's own text flags these as the open question of what to do about files that legitimately cannot compile. The answer is now: refuse them.\n\nB3. Prelude type-map arity mismatches: anthill `Stream[Element, E]` maps to Scala `LazyList`, which takes ONE parameter, so `Iterable` emits `def iterator(c: C): LazyList[Element, E]` -- `Too many type arguments`. Whether the map entry is wrong or the mapping is unrepresentable is WI-1021's question (it already asks about `List`/`Set`/`Map`/`Stream`); until that is settled a refusal beats an emission.\n\nSCOPE: fix Group A; refuse Group B through `BootstrapError`, naming the declaration and WHY it cannot be emitted. Then WI-1020's closure test becomes writable: generate the prelude, compile what was emitted, and assert the refusal set equals a named, explained list.\n\nA REFUSAL IS NOT A SKIP -- it must name the declaration, its span, and the construct that defeated it, the way the existing `BootstrapError` does. A caller catching it and continuing would recreate exactly the blindness this closes.\n\nWATCH OUT: refusing too much makes the backend useless -- 16 of 44 prelude files compile TODAY, and that number must not go DOWN. State it as a floor in the test.\n\nACCEPTANCE: Group A fixed, driven by the compile harness (monad/cell emissions compile). Group B refused with a located message per case, each pinned by a test asserting the message names the declaration. The count of prelude files that compile is asserted and is >= 16. Say at each test site which assertion fails when that arm is backed out. sbt test green.\n\nSOURCE: WI-1020's prelude sweep."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-08T14:44:23Z"))
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1021", author: "user", content: "WI-1055 (delivered 2026-08-08, commit c5f23975) CLOSED THIS TICKET'S SECOND HALF AND ITS HEADLINE. What is LEFT is only the nominal-vs-host merits.\n\nDONE by WI-1055's A3, in the plumbing this ticket's feedback recommended (option (a) — tell TypeGen the enclosing declaration):\n - The lost type ARGUMENTS. `TypeScope.place` answers `Placement.Enclosing` with the sort's own parameters re-attached, so `sort Cell[V]` emits `def get(c: Cell[V]): V`. cell.anthill's emission now COMPILES (pinned by 'WI-1055 A3: cell.anthill COMPILES').\n - The `Pair -> Tuple2` HEADLINE, as a consequence rather than a separate fix: the enclosing sort answers BEFORE `preludeMapping`, so a file's own name can no longer be captured by an entry meant for its consumers. pair.anthill emits `def fst(p: Pair[A, B]): A` and compiles; pinned by 'WI-1055 A3: a sort's own name is not rewritten through the prelude type map', which also asserts `Tuple2` does not appear.\n\nSo this ticket's candidate (b) — DROP `case \"Pair\" => \"Tuple2\"` — is now a pure question about CONSUMERS, with no compile failure forcing it. Decide it on the merits the description already lays out (nominal sort with named fields and its own `eq` vs structural `Tuple2`; `provides Eq[Pair]` cannot attach to `Tuple2`).\n\nSTILL OPEN AND NOW BLOCKING, the ARITY half this ticket's feedback raised: `Stream[Element, E]` -> one-parameter `LazyList`. WI-1055 REFUSES every such occurrence rather than emitting it (`Placement.Known` carries the Scala arity; a mismatch is a located BootstrapError). That takes iterable.anthill and combinators.anthill OUT of the emitted tree, and the knock-on is measured: `PersistentCollection` and `MutableCollection` then name an `anthill.prelude.Iterable` nothing emits, which is 7 of the 11 errors left in the prelude closure compile. So this ticket is now what unblocks that, and 'an effect-carrying anthill collection type does not have a same-arity Scala counterpart' is the thing to decide.\n\nALSO: `Duration` is deliberately NOT in the code's type map even though `scala_std.anthill` declares `Duration -> scala.concurrent.duration.Duration`, because primitives.anthill declares `sort anthill.prelude.Duration` and Bootstrap EMITS it — adding the entry would silently pick host-over-nominal for a sort this ticket covers. The wider drift between the code table and the fact file is WI-1060, which depends on this ticket for exactly that reason.", at: "2026-08-08T14:44:50Z")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1020", author: "user", content: "WI-1055 delivered (2026-08-08, commit c5f23975). The REFUSAL SET this ticket needs now exists, and the closure is close but NOT yet green — here is exactly what is left, measured.\n\nTHE NAMED EXCLUSION LIST is live and asserted, in BootstrapTest 'WI-1055: the prelude refusal set is a NAMED list, and the compiling count is a floor'. Thirteen prelude files, each paired with the construct that defeated the emitter, so adding a refusal is an edit there rather than a quiet shrinking of the output. Consume that set: what this ticket compiles is its complement.\n\nMEASURED, prelude: 18 -> 24 files compile standalone; 0 -> 13 refused; the whole-closure compile went 415 errors -> 11.\n\nTHE 11 REMAINING, all owned, none of them WI-1055's to fix:\n 1. Numeric.scala:8 '=' expected — `zero-val` is not a Scala identifier. WI-1054.\n 2. 7 errors: PersistentCollection and MutableCollection extend `anthill.prelude.Iterable`, which nothing emits because iterable.anthill is REFUSED on the `Stream[Element, E]` -> one-parameter `LazyList` arity. WI-1021.\n 3. 3 errors: MutableCollection names `anthill.prelude.Modifiable`, declared in effects.anthill, which is REFUSED because it imports `NodeOccurrence`/`Term` from `anthill.reflect` — a package OUTSIDE the prelude closure. Either widen the closure to include stdlib/anthill/reflect.anthill, or keep effects.anthill in the exclusion list and accept that its dependents go with it. That is a scoping decision for THIS ticket.\n\nA CASCADE IS NOT SELF-DETECTING, and it is the one gap to plan for: 2 and 3 are files that would compile if the file DECLARING their sibling had not been refused. Bootstrap is per-file (proposal 034) so it cannot see this; only this ticket, which holds the whole set, can. Expect to compute the transitive closure of the refusal set rather than assuming 'not refused' means 'compiles'.\n\nWHAT MAKES THE MISSING NAMES LOUD: an unplaced same-package name is emitted FULLY QUALIFIED (`anthill.prelude.Iterable`), not bare. A bare mention resolves against Scala's root imports, so an absent sibling CAPTURES instead of failing — measured, field.anthill's bare `Numeric` compiled green against `scala.math.Numeric`. So every cascade now surfaces as 'type X is not a member of anthill.prelude' naming the exact missing declaration, instead of an arity error against the wrong type.\n\nALSO NOTE the floor assertion (>= 16 compiling standalone) already lives in BootstrapTest and is deliberately `>=`, not `==`: WI-1054 and WI-1021 each raise it, and a pinned number would fail on being improved.", at: "2026-08-08T14:45:17Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1059", description: some(value: "BUG (typing; WI-1056 residue, DRIVEN end-to-end and PRE-EXISTING): an UNWRITTEN sort parameter is FLEXIBLE inside the operation body, where it must be RIGID. It is fresh at each call -- 's: Stream[T = Int64]' is 's: Stream[T = Int64, E = ?]' and '?' is a NEW variable per instantiation -- so the operation is universally quantified over it and its BODY may only do what holds for EVERY row. Today the body sees a flexible variable that satisfies any demand. THE PROGRAM, driven, both lines load on main: 'operation takes_pure(s: Stream[T = Int64, E = {}]) -> Int64'; 'operation feed(s: Stream[T = Int64]) -> Int64 = takes_pure(s)' -- which SHOULD be refused at its own declaration, since it claims any row and hands it to something accepting only the empty one; and then 'operation caller(s: Stream[T = Int64, E = {Error}]) -> Int64 = feed(s)', where the same variable is flexible again and binds to {Error}. An effectful stream reaches an operation that declared it wanted none. Each half looks correct alone, which is why neither was ever reported. ROOT CAUSE, LOCATED: rigidify_op_type_params (typing.rs) skolemizes exactly two families -- the operation's OWN [T] params (rec.type_params) and its ENCLOSING sort's (parent_sort_params, WI-942). A parameter of ANOTHER sort left unwritten in a parameter's TYPE (Stream's E in 's: Stream[T = Int64]') is in neither family, so it is never rigidified. A SECOND LEAK, MEASURED SEPARATELY AND SHARPER: 'operation feed[E](s: Stream[T = Int64, E = E]) -> Int64 = takes_pure(s)' ALSO loads -- and there E IS in rec.type_params and IS rigidified. So rigidification alone does not close it: the subtype check accepts a RIGID row against a written '{}'. Fix both, and drive the [E] spelling as its own case -- it is the one that proves the check, not the skolemizer. NOT WHAT THIS IS: an earlier framing of this ticket said the unwritten parameter should be SOLVED FROM THE BODY and become part of the signature. That is wrong and is recorded so it is not re-proposed -- E is fresh per call, so feed is polymorphic in it; the body simply may not assume a value for it. The refusal belongs at feed, not at caller. ALSO SETTLED HERE: docs/kernel-language.md section 'Expansion during unification' now states the rule with its examples, marked intended-but-not-enforced (the house pattern WI-884 uses). A BLUNTER FALLBACK IS MEASURED, in case rigidification proves impractical: making an unwritten parameter simply fail to satisfy a written one costs 33 LOAD ERRORS on stdlib + rustland/anthill-stl/anthill alone (34 with examples/webots-modelling), and only after patching BOTH carrier arms -- types_compatible_term_dispatch's and types_compatible_view_structural's, since patching one alone changes nothing (WI-1016). Prefer rigidification: it refuses the unsound program at the ONE declaration that is wrong, instead of refusing 33 legitimate bare signatures. ACCEPTANCE (each test says which case fails when backed out): 'feed(s: Stream[T = Int64]) = takes_pure(s)' is REFUSED at feed, naming the row it cannot assume (control: today it loads, and so does the caller that exploits it); the [E]-spelled twin is REFUSED for the same reason (control: today it loads DESPITE being rigidified -- this is the second leak); 'feed(s: Stream[T = Int64, E = {}]) = takes_pure(s)' still LOADS; a body that genuinely holds for every row still loads under every spelling; wi1056_rule_body_type_check_test::all_four_spellings_of_one_type_conform_alike still passes -- the four spellings must keep AGREEING, whatever the verdict becomes; the corpus loads clean at every tier -- stdlib, host bindings, examples/, anthill-todo/, anthill-testcases/. REFERENCE: WI-1056 (which measured all four spellings; its own false control is recorded at parameterized_compatible_view); WI-942 (which last widened the rigidifier, to the enclosing sort's params -- this is the next family); WI-374 (signature expansion, inference-only by design); WI-1016 (both carriers must key alike); WI-884 (the intended-but-not-enforced spec pattern); docs/kernel-language.md section 'Expansion during unification'."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-08T18:04:33Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-871", description: some(value: "A COMPONENTWISE PROVIDER'S SECOND REQUIREMENT SLOT IS READ FROM THE FIRST WHEN THE FIRST'S CARRIER IS THE PROVIDER ITSELF — a WI-857 dictionary-slot defect, PRE-EXISTING, surfaced by WI-858 and now reachable from the SHIPPED prelude. REPRODUCER (measured at WI-858's HEAD, and on a purely LOCAL carrier with no prelude and no 058 vocabulary — a two-parameter enum with `requires PartialEq[A], requires PartialEq[B]`, `provides PartialEq[Self]`, and a componentwise `eq` body calling `PartialEq.eq(al, bl)` then `PartialEq.eq(ar, br)`): comparing two values whose FIRST component is itself that carrier and whose SECOND is a primitive dies at eval with `match_failed(occurrence: Node, scrutinee: 7)` — the second component's compare dispatched to the CARRIER's own `eq`, which then matched a primitive against the carrier's entity pattern. CHARACTERIZED BY DRIVING, four cells: A=<carrier>,B=<primitive> FAILS; A=<primitive>,B=<carrier> PASSES; A=B=<carrier> PASSES; A,B both primitive PASSES. So it is not 'nesting' as such — it is the slot the FIRST component's requirement occupies once that requirement resolves to the provider ITSELF. SUSPECT (not confirmed — confirm by driving before fixing): the WI-857 LOCALITY rule (resolve_inner's `local_provider` filter, 058 §3.8) narrows candidates to the enclosing provider whenever that provider offers the spec, and both slots carry the SAME spec at DIFFERENT bindings; the filter is documented as binding-matched (candidates are head-matched first), so the divergence may instead be in how the body's two same-spec reads are INDEXED into the provider half of dict_layout. Whichever it is, it is one owner and the four-cell matrix above is the oracle. IMPACT: `PartialEq.eq(pair(fst: pair(fst: 1, snd: 2), snd: 7), …)` on the shipped `anthill.prelude.Pair` fails at eval; before WI-858 gave Pair a componentwise `eq` the same expression answered correctly via the structural builtin, so this is a user-visible regression for that shape and the reason it is filed rather than absorbed. PINNED at wi858_pair_orderings_test::a_nested_pair_in_the_first_component_is_a_recorded_defect, whose passing arms are the control and whose failing arm PANICS WITH INSTRUCTIONS when the defect is fixed. ACCEPTANCE: all four cells of the matrix pass on both the local carrier and the prelude Pair; the recorded-defect arm is folded into pair_equality_is_componentwise; wi857_dictionary_layout_test unchanged; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh. REFERENCE: design/058-implementation.md §7 (the layout and its one owner, dict_layout); wi857_dictionary_layout_test; stdlib/anthill/prelude/pair.anthill's header."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-08T18:04:34Z"))
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1062", description: some(value: "DECISION (typing; WI-1061 residue, MEASURED end-to-end on the delivered tree): the RETURN position of the unwritten-sort-parameter rule reads TWO WAYS in two delivered documents, and the language has to pick one. THE HOLE, still reachable and pinned by wi1061_unwritten_slot_positions_test::a_return_position_unwritten_row_is_not_yet_rigid / an_effectful_stream_still_reaches_a_pure_slot_through_a_return: 'operation takes_pure(s: Stream[T = Int64, E = {}]) -> Int64' plus 'operation widen(s: Stream[T = Int64, E = {Error}]) -> Stream[T = Int64] = s' plus 'operation exploit(s: Stream[T = Int64, E = {Error}]) -> Int64 = takes_pure(widen(s))' loads clean, and an effectful stream reaches a slot that declared E = {} through the row left unwritten in widen's RETURN. THE TWO READINGS. (1) docs/kernel-language.md section 'Expansion during unification' reads an unwritten parameter as a NEW variable per instantiation WHEREVER it appears, so widen's return promises a stream of EVERY row while the body holds only {Error} -- widen is the wrong declaration. (2) docs/design/type-parameter-scoping.md section 5 reads a bare return as ERASED ('the element/effect tie to l is GONE'), a wart to be fixed by WRITING the type and explicitly NOT a body error; section 4 records normalizing foreign bare refs in signatures as still-open WI-374 scope. Under (2) the wrong site is exploit, which relies on a slot the value's type does not carry -- the same principle that already refuses wi374_expansion_test::bare_value_stays_unusable. WHAT WAS MEASURED, so this is a decision and not an investigation. Reading (1) IS IMPLEMENTED AND WORKS: pass UnwrittenFill::Anonymous and the return type to rigidify_unwritten_sort_params (the walk WI-1061 shipped for parameters, unchanged) and widen is refused at 'widen.return: expected Stream[E = ?E, T = Int64]'. Its COST is 32 tests across nine delivered tickets -- 4 in kb::region::wi353_tests ('expected Cell[V = ?V], got Cell[V = z.V]' on 'foldCell(xs, z: Cell, f) -> Cell') and 28 in wi_tests across WI-374/401/402/405/457/480/491/776/839 -- and they are NOT a batch of small fixes: wi374_expansion_test::foreign_bare_return_op_loads_and_narrows PINS 'makeList() -> List = cons(1, nil)' as loading, with a comment saying the return is deliberately not expanded; and the WI-401/402/457/480/491 escape gate is built on a bare abstract-spec return CONFORMING by provider upcast and only THEN being refused by abstracting_return_error, so materializing the return makes it non-conformable, the gate never fires, and its diagnostics vanish -- those fixtures exist to exercise that shape and cannot simply be updated. The corpus itself is unaffected either way: all seven tiers (stdlib, rustland/anthill-stl/anthill, rustland/anthill-todo/anthill, rustland/anthill-cpp-gen/anthill, examples/, anthill-todo/, anthill-testcases/) load identically with and without the return walk. OPTIONS. (a) Reading (1): enforce the return, retire or re-found the escape gate on a different precondition, rewrite the nine tickets' fixtures, and edit type-parameter-scoping.md sections 4/5 to say the opposite of what they say today. (b) Reading (2): keep erasure, and refuse the CALL instead -- a demand on a slot the argument's type does not carry must not be satisfiable by a fresh flexible var; blast radius NOT measured, and it touches every partial application at every call site. (c) A split neither doc states today (e.g. abstract-spec returns keep erasure, concrete parametric returns quantify) -- note that WI-1056 measured all four spellings as ONE type, so any split must say what it keys on and must not be bare-vs-partial. ACCEPTANCE (each test says which case fails when backed out): the widen chain gets ONE verdict and the two pins in wi1061_unwritten_slot_positions_test flip to assert it (they are the control: both pass today); whichever reading wins, BOTH docs state it and neither contradicts; if reading (1), a test drives that the escape-gate capability is still checked by something (a bare abstract-spec return is still refused, and the diagnostic still names the escape) rather than silently lost; if reading (2), the refusal lands at exploit and wi374_expansion_test stays green unchanged; the corpus loads clean at all seven tiers, measured through 'anthill load' per tier, whose control is that it DOES report the WI-1059/WI-1061 parameter-position refusals. REFERENCE: WI-1061 (the nested half delivered, and the block comment at the return call site in check_operation_bodies that states this scope); WI-1059 (the parameter top level); WI-374 (section 4's open scope item (a), foreign bare refs in signatures); WI-401/WI-402/WI-457/WI-480/WI-491 (the escape gate); docs/kernel-language.md section 'Expansion during unification'; docs/design/type-parameter-scoping.md sections 4-5."), acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test")], depends_on: some(value: nil), status: Open)
fact Tag(workitem: "WI-1062", name: "typing")
fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1061", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED, but SPLIT: the ticket's two positions turned out to be one implementation and one language DECISION, and only the first is here. NESTED IS CLOSED, and wider than the ticket described. rigidify_unwritten_sort_params now recurses through a parameter type's whole structure, not only its top level: a slot nested inside a sort-application binding (the ticket's 'feed(l: List[T = Stream[T = Int64]])'), inside a callback's RESULT ('feed(f: (x: Int64) -> Stream[T = Int64]) = takes_pure(f(1))'), and inside a TUPLE COMPONENT ('feed(p: (s: Stream[T = Int64], n: Int64))'). The last two were NOT in the ticket -- they were found by /code-review on the first cut, which descended through Parameterized bindings only and left every other type carrier falling to '_ => None'; both chains loaded clean and both are the WI-1059 hole verbatim one carrier further in. Each nested slot takes a fresh anonymous Var::Rigid (nothing NAMES a nested slot, so the WI-1059 projection is unavailable), except a SELF-sort reference, which takes the enclosing sort's rigid at every depth. THREE CHILDREN ARE EXCLUDED, each measured, not assumed: an effect-ROW binding and an arrow's effects child (a row's labels are compared by structural identity against the effects a body incurs -- descending into 'Stream[T = Solution, E = Error]' materialized Error's own T, broke rustland/anthill-todo/anthill at 'expected declared: [Error], got undeclared effect: Error[T = ?T]' and took 193 tests with it); and a callback's own PARAMETER, where the quantification faces the OTHER way -- 'f: (a: Cell) -> Unit' says f accepts EVERY instantiation, so the body may pass what it has; rigidifying it failed 9 tests at 'each(l: List[T = Cell], f: (a: Cell) -> Unit)' and 'foldCell', all refusing a body for handing a good value to a callback that declared it takes any. THE RETURN IS NOT CLOSED, and that is a decision, not a line: WI-1062 owns it, with the whole measurement. The walk works there (one more call to the same function; widen is refused at 'widen.return: expected E = ?E') but it contradicts docs/design/type-parameter-scoping.md sections 4-5, which read a bare return as ERASED rather than universally quantified, and it costs 32 tests across nine delivered tickets including the WI-401/402/457/480/491 escape gate, whose mechanism REQUIRES a bare abstract-spec return to conform by provider upcast before being refused. The user chose 'ship nested; hand the return to WI-374's scope'. The hole stays reachable and is PINNED by two tests so it cannot be forgotten. THE DECLARED EFFECTS CLAUSE IS NOT A FURTHER POSITION -- measured: the corpus's effect atoms are row vars, projections, Modify[v] denotations and concrete labels, and where a parametric sort IS written there the effects check is already strict in the SAFE direction ('effects Box' against an incurred 'Box[T = Int64]' is refused today). No ticket needed. EVIDENCE. Tests: wi1061_unwritten_slot_positions_test (7), with a four-column back-out matrix in its header -- every cell RUN, one revert each, and each column fails exactly ONE row because the corpus reaches none of these mechanisms. One earlier draft of that matrix was wrong and is recorded at its site. wi1059_unwritten_param_rigid_test and wi1056::all_four_spellings_of_one_type_conform_alike stay green. Full workspace: 29 binaries, 4409 tests, 0 failures. Corpus: all SEVEN tiers byte-identical to baseline (stdlib, rustland/anthill-stl/anthill, rustland/anthill-todo/anthill, rustland/anthill-cpp-gen/anthill, examples/, anthill-todo/, anthill-testcases/) with the control that 'anthill load' DOES report the WI-1059 refusal. NOTE for whoever measures a tier next: 'anthill-todo/' at the repo root holds WORK ITEMS; the todo CLI's own anthill program is 'rustland/anthill-todo/anthill', a different directory -- measuring the first and calling it the tier missed a real 2-error regression here. Docs: kernel-language.md section 'Expansion during unification' (the enforced positions, the by-depth filler rule, the three exclusions, and the undecided return); type-parameter-scoping.md section 5 now names its disagreement with the spec and points at WI-1062.", at: "2026-08-08T23:13:19Z")
fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1061", description: some(value: "BUG (typing; WI-1059 residue, DRIVEN end-to-end on the delivered tree): the unwritten-parameter rule is enforced ONLY in a PARAMETER's type and ONLY at its TOP LEVEL, so the same soundness hole is still reachable through the other two positions. WI-1059 made an unwritten sort parameter RIGID inside the operation body by materializing the slot as the projection off the value that carries it (`s: Stream[T = Int64]` is checked as `s: Stream[T = Int64, E = s.E]`), which refuses `feed(s: Stream[T = Int64]) = takes_pure(s)` at feed's own declaration. Two positions were deliberately left out and BOTH still load: (1) RETURN POSITION -- 'operation widen(s: Stream[T = Int64, E = {Error}]) -> Stream[T = Int64] = s' plus 'operation exploit(s: Stream[T = Int64, E = {Error}]) -> Int64 = takes_pure(widen(s))' loads clean, and an effectful stream reaches a slot declared 'E = {}' through the unwritten row in the RETURN; (2) NESTED POSITION -- 'takes_pure(l: List[T = Stream[T = Int64, E = {}]])' with 'feed(l: List[T = Stream[T = Int64]]) = takes_pure(l)' and 'caller(l: List[T = Stream[T = Int64, E = {Error}]]) = feed(l)' loads clean for the whole chain. WHY EACH WAS EXCLUDED, and what makes each its own measurement rather than a line to add: the RETURN flips the quantification -- the body must PRODUCE a value good for every instantiation, not consume one -- and there is no carrier VALUE in a return type to project off, so the filler there can only be a fresh anonymous rigid; WI-1059 measured that exact filler in the PARAMETER position at 27 stdlib load errors (all of the shape 'expected List[T = s.T], got List[T = ?T]', none of them wrong programs) and at 82 workspace failures on a later retry, so a fresh rigid needs its own cost measurement before it is used anywhere. A NESTED slot has NO path that names it at all ('s: List[T = Stream]' -- nothing spells the inner row), so it too can only take a fresh rigid, and a return type spelling the same nested bare sort would mint a SECOND unrelated one and desynchronize exactly as the parameter-position experiment did. ACCEPTANCE (each test says which case fails when backed out): the two programs above are REFUSED, each at the declaration that is wrong rather than at its exploiting caller (control: both load clean today); a body that genuinely holds for every instantiation still loads in both positions; wi1059_unwritten_param_rigid_test and wi1056_rule_body_type_check_test::all_four_spellings_of_one_type_conform_alike stay green; the corpus loads clean at every tier -- stdlib, host bindings, examples/, anthill-todo/, anthill-testcases/ -- measured through 'anthill load' per tier, whose control is that it DOES report the WI-1059 refusals. REFERENCE: WI-1059 (the delivered parameter-position half, and the two scope statements at rigidify_unwritten_sort_params that this ticket owns); WI-1056 (the four spellings); WI-942/WI-392 (the two rigid families that came before); docs/kernel-language.md section 'Expansion during unification', whose 'How the slot is named' paragraph states this ticket's scope boundary."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-08T23:13:21Z"))
